const Map<int, String> _yacobEnglish = {
  1001: "In the Name of Allāh [1], the Most Compassionate, the Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. Allāh is the most unique and supreme Name of God. Linguistically, it is a proper and underived Name, belonging exclusively to the One Almighty God, the Creator and Sustainer of the heavens and the earth and all that is within them—the Eternal, Absolute, and Self-Sufficient, to whom alone all worship is due.\nThis Grand Name was used by the prophets Noah, Abraham, Moses, Jesus, and Muhammad ﷺ, among others, all of whom called upon the same One God by this Name, affirming the continuity of pure monotheism throughout divine revelation.",
  1002: "All praise is due to Allāh, Lord [2] of all the worlds.\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. There is no exact or complete equivalent in the English language for the Arabic word Rabb. While it is commonly translated as “Lord,” this translation only captures a portion of its meaning. In reality, Rabb is a richly layered term that includes the meanings of: Creator (the One who brings into existence), Sustainer (the One who keeps all things alive), Provider (the One who supplies every need), Maintainer and Nurturer (the One who causes growth and development), Owner and Master (the One to whom all things belong), Ruler and Controller (the One who governs all affairs), and Guardian (the One who protects and watches over His creation). Thus, the word Rabb conveys an all-encompassing authority, care, and sovereignty that no single English word can fully express.",
  1003: "The Most Compassionate, the Most Merciful. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. The Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said:\n“Allāh created mercy in one hundred parts. He kept ninety-nine parts with Himself and sent down one part to the earth. From that single part, all creatures show mercy to one another—even a mare lifts its hoof away from its foal, fearing that she might harm it.”\nThe remaining ninety-nine portions, preserved with Allāh, signify a mercy so vast that it lies beyond human comprehension. These are reserved for the Day of Resurrection, when believers will experience the fullness of divine compassion, forgiveness, and generosity. The narration powerfully illustrates that all mercy witnessed in the world is but a minute fraction of Allāh’s boundless mercy, hinting at the immeasurable grace awaiting the believers in the Hereafter.",
  1004: "Master of the Day of Judgment.",
  1005: "It is You alone we worship, and You alone we ask for help.",
  1006: "Guide us to the straight Path. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. And keep us firmly upon it—that is, upon Islam, the true religion revealed by Allāh. Here, Islam is affirmed as a complete and divinely ordained way of life, encompassing right belief, sincere worship, moral integrity, and upright conduct. To be kept firmly upon it implies steadfastness and divine support, acknowledging that remaining upon the straight path ultimately depends on Allāh’s guidance and grace.",
  1007: "The path of those upon whom You have bestowed favor [5], not of those who have incurred Your anger [6], nor of the misguided. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The path of those whom You have blessed — namely, the Prophets (such as Noah, Abraham, Moses, Jesus, and Muḥammad, peace be upon them all), along with the affirmers of truth, the martyrs, and the righteous. This phrase expresses a longing to walk the same illuminated path as these honored servants of Allāh.\n[6]. Not the way of those who earned Your anger — those who became deviant, stubborn, or hypocritical.This warning highlights the spiritual danger of rejecting truth once it is known.\n[7]. Nor of those who went astray — those who are ignorant of the truth and worship Allāh based only on conjecture, personal desires, and wishful thinking.\nThis serves as a reminder of how easily one can stray when faith is not grounded in true knowledge.",
  2001: "Alif Lām Mīm [1].\\n\\nFootnotes: [1].The Qur’an is made up of such letters from the Arabic alphabet. The Arabs at the time of revelation were the unmatched masters of the Arabic language and yet when challenged were totally unable to bring anything like the Qur’an. Although these disjointed Arabic letters are referring to the inimitable nature of the Qur’an and its divine source, God only knows best its meaning.",
  2002: "This is the Book [2] about which there is no doubt, a guidance for the righteous.\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. This refers to the Qur’an — the complete, perfect, and final revelation from Allāh — affirming its absolute certainty, truth, and divine origin. It is a source of guidance only for those who approach it with reverence, humility, and mindfulness of their Lord, Allāh.",
  2003: "Those who believe in the unseen [3], establish prayer [4] and spend in charity from what We [5] have provided them.\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. Such as the Existence of God, angels, jinn 'spirits, demons', life after death, Paradise and Hell, etc.\n[4]. Keeping up the Prayer, entails performing it punctually and devoutly, observing the manner set forth by the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ , who said: “Pray as you have seen me Pray”.\n[5]. It is to be noted that the reference of Allāh to Himself as \"We or Us\" in many Qur’ānic verses is necessarily understood in the Arabic language to denote grandeur and power, as opposed to the more intimate singular form \"I or Me\" used in specific instances. This is the “royal” We or Us. In English, its called the plural of majesty. This syntax is used, both in the English and Arabic languages, by emperors, kings and queens. “We, the Emperor of …, grant amnesty to …”. And to Allāh is the best example. He is the King of all kings. He is the Sovereign. To Him belongs the Dominion of the heavens and the earth. Further more the phrase 'a part of what We have provided for them' used the word “min” in Arabic which means some [or part], to make them aware that He will indeed not take from them except a small portion of their wealth, which does not harm them, but they benefit from giving it, and their brothers also benefit from it.",
  2004: "Those who believe in what has been sent down to you [6] and what was sent down before you [7], and have certainty in the Hereafter life.\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. Of the Qur’an and the Sunnah - the teachings of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. It includes everything he said, did, and approved of. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Beware, I have been given the Qur'an and something similar to it along with it, yet the time is coming when a man replete on his couch will say: Keep to the Qur'an; what you find in it to be permissible treat as permissible, and what you find in it to be prohibited treat as prohibited. But indeed, whatever the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ forbids is like what Allāh forbids.”\n[7]. Of the Torah, the Gospel, the Psalms, etc.",
  2005: "They are the ones guided by their Lord, and they are the ones who will succeed.",
  2006: "Indeed, those who persist in disbelief—it is the same for them whether you warn them or not; they will not believe.",
  2007: " Allāh has sealed their hearts and their hearing, and over their eyes is a veil [8]. For them is a great punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. A veil preventing them from accepting the guidance, because of their arrogance and persistence in sin.",
  2008: "Among the people are some who say, \"We believe in Allāh and the Last Day,\" yet they are not truly believers.",
  2009: "They seek to deceive Allāh and those who believe, but they only deceive themselves, though they do not perceive it.",
  2010: "In their hearts is a disease, and so Allāh increases their disease. They will have a painful punishment for their persistent lies.",
  2011: "When it is said to them, ‘Do not spread corruption in the land,’ they say, ‘We are only reformers.’",
  2012: "Indeed, it is they who are the corrupters, though they do not perceive it.",
  2013: "And when they are told, ‘Believe as the people have believed,’ they say, ‘Should we believe as the foolish believe [9]?’ Truly, they are the fools, though they do not realize it.\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. The hypocrites regarded the believers as fools, while in reality they themselves were the fools. To those who reject it, the message of Islam appears foolish; but to those who submit to Allāh and uphold the truth, it is a manifestation of His power, mercy, and grace. Yet the hypocrites act in ways that contradict the very message they claim to accept.",
  2014: "When they meet those who believe, they say, ‘We believe.’ But when they are alone with their devils—[their leaders or instigators]—they say, ‘We are with you; we were only mocking.’",
  2015: "Allāh mocks them and leaves them to wander blindly in their rebellion.",
  2016: "These are the ones who have traded guidance for misguidance, so their bargain brought them no profit, nor were they guided.",
  2017: "Their example is like that of one who kindled a fire, and when it lit up all around him, Allāh took away their light and left them in darkness, unable to see.",
  2018: "Deaf, dumb, and blind—so they will not return [to the right path].",
  2019: "Or their example is like a rainstorm from the sky [10], within which is utter darkness, thunder, and lightning. They press their fingers into their ears at the thunderclaps, fearing death. But Allāh surrounds the disbelievers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. Allāh presents another example to illustrate the state of the hypocrites in relation to the Qur’an and Islam. It is like a heavy rain pouring down upon people in the desert—a storm filled with deep darkness, thunder, and lightning. Terrified, they press their fingers into their ears to block out the thunderclaps, fearing destruction, even though such a gesture cannot save them from danger.\nThis is exactly how the hypocrites react when the Qur’an is recited to them. When they hear its warnings, they shut their ears to avoid being affected by its message. They neither believe in the truth it conveys nor give up the falsehood they cling to. The warnings and revelations in the Qur’an—exposing their reality—strike them like thunderbolts that pierce through their ears.\nAllāh surrounds the deniers with His complete knowledge and power. They cannot escape Him, nor can they ever evade His decree.",
  2020: "The lightning almost snatches away their sight. Whenever it flashes and gives them light, they walk in it; but when darkness covers them, they stand still [11]. If Allāh willed, He could take away their hearing and their sight. Indeed, Allāh is Powerful over all things.\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. This is the condition of the hypocrites: whenever their wealth grows or their children increase, they say, “The religion of Islam is truly from Allāh,” and they remain committed to it—but only for a short time. Yet if a hardship or loss strikes them, they quickly fall back into disbelief, saying, “This misfortune is because of the religion of Muhammad that we followed.”\nWere it not for Allah’s respite and mercy toward them, they would have already been stripped of their hearing and sight—as a consequence of their false professions of faith and their turning away from the truth. And Allāh has the power to do this at any moment He wills.",
  2021: "O people, worship Allāh, your Lord, who created you and those before you, so that you may attain piety.",
  2022: "It is He who made the earth a resting place for you and the sky a canopy, and sent down rain from the sky, then brought forth fruits thereby as provision for you. So do not set up equals to Allāh while you know. [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12].  That there is nothing like Him. So do not set up equals to Allāh in worship, for He is the One who has provided you with all these blessings and alone is worthy of worship.\nA Companion of the Prophet ﷺ said: I asked the Prophet ﷺ, “What is the greatest sin in the sight of Allāh?” He said, “That you set up a rival to Allāh while He alone created you.” I said, “That is indeed a grave sin.” Then I asked, “What comes next?” He said, “To kill your child for fear that he may share your food with you.” I asked, “What comes next?” He said, “To commit adultery with your neighbor’s wife.”",
  2023: "If you are in doubt about what We have revealed to Our servant, then produce a chapter like it, and call upon your supporters besides Allāh—if you are truthful. [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. That is, in your claim that Our servant, the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ, is the author of the Qurʾān. This is a direct challenge to those who doubt its divine origin: to produce even a single chapter comparable to it in its language, meaning, and impact. Despite the eloquence and literary mastery of the Arabs at the time, they were unable to meet this challenge. The verse thus affirms the Qurʾān’s inimitability (iʿjāz) and its divine source.",
  2024: "But if you do not—and you will never be able to—then beware of the Fire whose fuel is people and stones [14], prepared for the disbelievers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. The expression “whose fuel is people and stones” intensifies the warning by portraying Hell not merely as a fire fueled by inanimate matter, but by human beings themselves. The term refers to combustible material used to kindle and sustain fire. Scholars explain that “stones” may refer either generally to rocks that increase the blaze, or more specifically to the stone idols worshiped besides Allāh —implying that false objects of devotion will share the fate of their worshippers (cf. 21:98). The pairing of “people and stones” underscores both accountability and irony: those who reject the divine message become fuel for the very Fire they deny, and the lifeless objects they trusted offer no protection.",
  2025: "Give good news to those who believe and do righteous deeds that they will have gardens beneath which rivers flow. Whenever they are provided with fruits therefrom, they will say, ‘This is what we were given before,’ yet they will be given things similar in appearance [but different in taste]. They will have purified spouses there, and they will abide therein forever.",
  2026: "Indeed, Allāh is not ashamed to present an example—even that of a mosquito or anything beyond it. As for those who believe, they know it is the truth from their Lord. But those who disbelieve say, ‘What does Allāh mean by this example?’ Through it He leads many astray and guides many. Yet He leads none astray through it except the defiantly rebellious –",
  2027: "those who break Allāh’s covenant after it has been confirmed, sever what Allāh has commanded to be joined [15], and spread corruption in the land—it is they who are the losers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said, \"The person who severs the bond of kinship will not enter Paradise\".",
  2028: "How can you disbelieve in Allāh when you were lifeless [16] and He gave you life, then He will cause you to die, then bring you back to life again, and then to Him you will all be returned?\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. These are the stages of life and death that every human being passes through: existence as dormant seeds within their forefathers, then creation and birth, followed by death, then being brought back to life at the resurrection, and finally being held accountable for their deeds.",
  2029: "It is He who created for you all that is on the earth, then He turned to the heaven and fashioned them into seven heavens. and He is All-Knowing of everything.",
  2030: "When your Lord said to the angels, ‘I am placing on earth a successive authority [17],’ they said, ‘Will You place in it one who will spread corruption therein and shed blood, while we glorify You with praise and sanctify You?’ He said, ‘Indeed, I know what you do not know.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. The term “successive authority” (khalīfah) refers to one who succeeds another, generation after generation, entrusted with responsibility upon the earth. It signifies humanity’s role as a steward, exercising authority and moral responsibility in accordance with Allāh’s guidance.",
  2031: "He taught Ādam the names of all things. Then He presented them to the angels and said, ‘Tell Me the names of these, if you are truthful.’",
  2032: "They said, “Glory be to You! We have no knowledge except what You have taught us. Indeed, You are the All-Knowing, the All-Wise.”",
  2033: "He said, ‘O Ādam, inform them of their names.’ When he had informed them of their names, He said, ‘Did I not tell you that I know the unseen of the heavens and the earth, and I know what you reveal and what you conceal?’",
  2034: "And when We said to the angels, ‘Prostrate before Ādam,’ they prostrated, except Iblīs. He refused and was arrogant, and thus became one of the disbelievers.",
  2035: "We said, ‘O Ādam, live with your wife in the Garden and eat freely from it wherever you wish, but do not approach this tree, or you will both become wrongdoers.’",
  2036: "But Satan caused them to slip and brought them out of the state in which they had been. And We said, ‘Descend as enemies to one another, and for you on the earth is a dwelling place and provision for a time.’",
  2037: "Then Ādam received words from his Lord [18], so He accepted his repentance. Indeed, He is the Accepter of repentance, the Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. These “words” refer to supplications taught to Ādam by Allāh, through which he repented, sought forgiveness, and was accepted. The words of repentance are mentioned in the Qurʾān (7:23), when Ādam and Ḥawwāʾ (Eve) prayed: “Our Lord, we have wronged ourselves, and if You do not forgive us and have mercy upon us, we will surely be among the losers.” This highlights that sincere repentance, humility, and seeking Allāh’s mercy are the means to His forgiveness and acceptance. It also contrasts with the concept of inherited or “original” sin, emphasizing instead that each person is responsible for their own deeds and may turn to Allāh in repentance and be forgiven.",
  2038: "We said, ‘Descend from it, all of you. Then, when guidance comes to you from Me, whoever follows My guidance will have no fear, nor will they grieve.’",
  2039: "But those who disbelieve and deny Our signs are the companions of the Fire; they will abide therein forever.",
  2040: "O Children of Israel, remember My favor which I bestowed upon you, and fulfill your covenant with Me, and I will fulfill My covenant with you; and fear Me alone.",
  2041: "Believe in what I have sent down, confirming that which is with you, and do not be the first to disbelieve in it. And do not trade My verses for a small price, and fear Me alone.",
  2042: "Do not mix the truth with falsehood, nor conceal the truth [19] while you know it.\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. About the coming of Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. So believe in the Qur’ān, which confirms your own scriptures, and do not reject it out of pride or worldly interests—especially since your books already foretold the coming of this prophet.",
  2043: "Establish prayer, give the poor-rate, and bow with those who bow [in worship]. [20]\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. Congregational prayer in the mosque is obligatory for all able men. It is not permissible for a man who is capable of attending the congregational prayer in the mosque to pray at home, even if he leads his family in prayer.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n “Whoever hears the call to prayer and does not come, there is no prayer for him, unless he has an excuse.”\nHe was asked, “What counts as an excuse?”\nThe Companion replied: “Fear or illness.”",
  2044: "Do you enjoin righteousness upon the people while you forget yourselves, even as you recite the Scripture? Do you not understand?",
  2045: "Seek help through patience and prayer [21], and indeed it is hard except for the humble,\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. The Children of Israel were held back from true belief due to greed and attachment to worldly life. They were commanded to seek strength through patience—especially fasting, which restrains desires, and through prayer, which instills humility, submission to Allāh, and removes arrogance.",
  2046: "those who are certain that they will meet their Lord, and that to Him they will return.”",
  2047: "O Children of Israel, remember My favor which I bestowed upon you, and that I favored you above all others. [22]\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. This refers to the many favors Allāh bestowed upon the Children of Israel, including guidance through the prophets, deliverance from oppression, provision, and divine revelation. Their being “favored above all others” is understood in the context of their time, meaning they were given distinction over other peoples of that era due to these blessings and responsibilities.",
  2048: "Beware of a Day when no soul will benefit another in the least, nor will intercession be accepted, nor will any compensation be taken, nor will they be aided.",
  2049: "[Remember] when We saved you from the people of Pharaoh, who subjected you to terrible suffering—slaughtering your sons and sparing your women—there was in that a great trial from your Lord.",
  2050: "And when We parted the sea for you and saved you, and drowned the people of Pharaoh while you were looking on.",
  2051: "And when We appointed for Moses forty nights, then you took the calf [for worship] in his absence, while you were wrongdoers.",
  2052: "Yet We pardoned you after that, so that you may be grateful.",
  2053: "[Remember] when We gave Moses the Scripture and the Criterion, so that you may be guided.",
  2054: "And when Moses said to his people, ‘O my people, you have indeed wronged yourselves by taking the calf for worship. So turn in repentance to your Creator and kill the guilty among yourselves—that is best for you in the sight of your Creator.’ Then He accepted your repentance. Indeed, He is the Accepter of Repentance, the Most Merciful.",
  2055: "And when you said, ‘O Moses, we will never believe you until we see Allāh openly,’ then the thunderbolt overtook you while you were looking on.",
  2056: "Then We raised you back to life after your death, so that you might be grateful.",
  2057: "And We shaded you with clouds and sent down to you manna and quails, saying, ‘Eat of the good things We have provided for you.’ They did not wrong Us; rather, they wronged themselves.",
  2058: "And when We said, ‘Enter this town and eat freely from it wherever you wish, but enter the gate bowing humbly and say, “Relieve us of our burdens.” We will forgive your sins and increase the reward of those who do good.’",
  2059: "But the wrongdoers substituted the word with something other than what they had been told, so We sent down upon those who were unjust a plague from the sky because of their persistent disobedience.\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. They were commanded what to do and say, but instead they entered the gate dragging themselves on their rears, and mockingly said, “A grain in a hair,” in place of saying, “Relieve us of our burdens.” They deliberately mocked Allah’s command.\nSo Allāh punished them for their disobedience with a plague sent from the sky, by which seventy thousand of them died.",
  2060: "[Remember] when Moses asked for water for his people, and We said, ‘Strike the rock with your staff,’ then twelve springs gushed forth from it; each tribe knew its own drinking place. ‘Eat and drink from Allāh’s provision, and do not spread corruption in the land.’",
  2061: "And when you said, ‘O Moses, we cannot endure one kind of food. So call upon your Lord for us to bring forth for us from what the earth produces—its herbs, cucumbers, garlic, lentils, and onions.’ He said, ‘Would you exchange what is better for what is inferior? Go down to any town, and you will find what you ask for.’ Then humiliation and misery were imposed upon them, and they incurred the wrath of Allāh. That was because they used to disbelieve in Allāh’s signs and kill the prophets unjustly. That was because they disobeyed and were transgressors.”",
  2062: "Indeed, the believers, and those who were Jews, Christians, and Sabians [a monotheistic group]—whoever believed in Allāh and the Last Day and did righteous deeds—will have their reward with their Lord. They will have no fear, nor will they grieve.",
  2063: "[Remember] when We took your covenant and raised the Mount above you, saying, ‘Hold firmly to what We have given you and observe what is in it, so that you may be mindful of Allāh.’",
  2064: "Yet you turned away after that; had it not been for the favor of Allāh upon you and His mercy, you would surely have been among the losers.",
  2065: "And you know about those among you who transgressed concerning the Sabbath, so We said to them, ‘Be apes, despised.’",
  2066: "So We made it a deterrent punishment for those who witnessed it and those who came after them, and an admonition for the righteous.",
  2067: "[Remember] when Moses said to his people, ‘Indeed, Allāh commands you to slaughter a cow [24],’ they said, ‘Are you mocking us?’ He said, ‘I seek refuge in Allāh from being one of the ignorant.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. This command served as atonement for an unsolved murder. The slaughter of the cow was also meant to detach their hearts from any lingering attachment to calf-worship. Their problem was not only the murder itself, but also their underlying idolatry.",
  2068: "They said, ‘Call upon your Lord to make clear to us what it is.’ He said, ‘He says: It is a cow neither too old nor too young, but of middle age; so do what you are commanded.’",
  2069: "They said, ‘Call upon your Lord to make clear to us its color.’ He said, ‘He says: It is a yellow cow, bright in color, pleasing to the beholders.’",
  2070: "They said, ‘Call upon your Lord to make clear to us what it is. Indeed, all cows look alike to us, and if Allāh wills, we will surely be guided.’",
  2071: "He said, ‘He says: It is a cow neither trained to plow the earth nor water the field—sound and without any blemish.’ They said, ‘Now you have brought the truth.’ So they slaughtered it, though they nearly did not.",
  2072: " [Remember] when you killed a man and disputed over it, Allāh brought to light what you were concealing.",
  2073: "We said, ‘Strike the slain with a part of it.’ Thus Allāh brings the dead to life and shows you His signs, so that you may understand.",
  2074: "Then your hearts became hardened after that, like stones—or even harder. For among stones are those from which rivers gush forth, and others that split open so that water flows out, and others that fall down in awe of Allāh. And Allāh is not unaware of what you do.",
  2075: "Do you, then, hope, O believers, that they will accept your call to faith, when a group among them used to hear the Words of Allāh and then knowingly distort them after fully understanding them?",
  2076: "And when they meet those who believe, they say, ‘We believe.’ But when they are alone with one another, they say, ‘Do you tell them what Allāh has disclosed to you [25], so that they may use it as an argument against you before your Lord? Do you not understand?’\\n\\nFootnotes: [25]. This refers to passages in the Bible that are understood to foretell and affirm the truthfulness of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ.",
  2077: "Do they not know that Allāh is fully aware of what they conceal and what they reveal?",
  2078: "And among them are unlettered people who know nothing of the Scripture except wishful thinking, and they do nothing but speculate. [26]\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. This refers to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, even though his characteristics are described in the Bible.",
  2079: "So woe to those who write the Scripture with their own hands and then say, ‘This is from Allāh,’ in order to sell it for a small price. Woe to them for what their hands have written, and woe to them for what they earn.",
  2080: "And they say, ‘The Fire will not touch us except for a few numbered days.’ Say, ‘Have you taken a covenant from Allāh—then Allāh will never break His covenant—or are you saying about Allāh what you do not know?’\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]. This responds to a claim made by some among the Children of Israel who believed that any punishment in the Hereafter would be brief. The verse challenges this assumption by asking whether they have a genuine divine promise, reminding them that Allāh does not break His promises, and warning against making assertions about Allāh without knowledge.",
  2081: "Indeed, whoever commits evil and is encompassed by his sins—such are the companions of the Fire; they will abide therein forever.",
  2082: "But those who believe and do righteous deeds—such are the companions of Paradise; they will abide therein forever.",
  2083: "And when We made a covenant with the Children of Israel: ‘Worship only Allāh; be good to parents, relatives, orphans, and those in need; speak kindly to people; establish prayer; and give zakah.’ But you turned away—except a few of you—and you still turn away.",
  2084: "And when We made a covenant with you: do not shed each other’s blood or drive one another from your homes. Then you agreed to it and bore witness.",
  2085: "Yet here you are—killing each other and forcing some of your own people out of their homes, supporting one another against them in wrongdoing and hostility. Then when they come to you as captives, you ransom them, even though driving them out was forbidden to you. So do you believe in part of the Scripture and deny the rest? What is the recompense for those of you who do this except disgrace in this worldly life? And on the Day of Resurrection, they will face the most severe punishment. And Allāh is not unaware of what you do.",
  2086: "These are the ones who have traded the Hereafter for the life of this world; so their punishment will not be lightened, nor will they be helped.",
  2087: "We gave Moses the Scripture and sent a succession of messengers after him. We gave Jesus, son of Mary, clear signs and strengthened him with the holy spirit. Yet whenever a messenger came to you with what your souls did not desire, you grew arrogant—some you denied, and others you killed.",
  2088: "They say, ‘Our hearts are sealed.’ Rather, Allāh has cursed them for their disbelief, so little do they believe.",
  2089: "When a Book from Allāh came to them, confirming what they already had—though before that they used to pray for victory over the disbelievers—yet when what they recognized came to them, they rejected it. So the curse of Allāh is upon the disbelievers.",
  2090: "How wretched is what they sold themselves for—that they disbelieve in what Allāh has sent down, out of envy that Allāh should send His bounty upon whom He wills of His servants. So they have incurred wrath [28] upon wrath [29], and for the disbelievers there is a humiliating punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. This refers to their rejection of the Qur’an.\n[29]. This refers to their rejection of the Torah beforehand, as well as their earlier sins.",
  2091: "When it is said to them, ‘Believe in what Allāh has sent down,’ they say, ‘We only believe in what was sent down to us,’ yet they reject what came after it, even though it is the truth confirming what is with them. Say, ‘Then why did you kill the prophets of Allāh before, if you were truly believers?’",
  2092: "And Moses came to you with clear signs, yet you took the calf for worship in his absence—and you were wrongdoers.",
  2093: "And when We took a covenant from you and raised the mountain above you, saying, ‘Hold firmly to what We have given you and obey,’ they said, ‘We hear, but we disobey.’ And the love of the calf was engraved in their hearts because of their disbelief. Say, ‘How wretched is what your faith enjoins upon you, if you are believers.’",
  2094: "Say, ‘If the Home of the Hereafter with Allāh is exclusively yours, apart from all others, then wish for death—if you are truthful.’",
  2095: "But they will never wish for it, ever, because of what their hands have sent ahead; and Allāh is All-Knowing of the wrongdoers.",
  2096: "And you will surely find them the most greedy for life—more than even the pagans. Each one of them wishes to live a thousand years; yet even if he were granted such a long life, it would not save him from the punishment. And Allāh is All-Seeing of what they do.",
  2097: "Say, ‘Whoever is an enemy to Gabriel—it is he who has brought it down [the Qur’an] upon your heart by Allah’s permission, confirming what came before it, and as guidance and good news for the believers.’",
  2098: "Whoever is an enemy to Allāh, His angels, His messengers, and to Gabriel and Michael—then Allāh is indeed an enemy to the disbelievers.",
  2099: "Indeed, We have sent down to you clear signs, and none reject them except those who are defiantly rebellious.",
  2100: "Is it that every time they make a covenant, a group of them casts it aside? Rather, most of them do not believe.",
  2101: "And when a messenger from Allāh came to them, confirming what was with them, a group of the People of the Book cast the Book of Allāh behind their backs, as if they did not know. [30]\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. The “messenger from Allāh ” here is Muhammad ﷺ, whom the People of the Scripture found described in their own Scriptures by signs well-known to their learned ones. His coming did not introduce a new religion, but rather confirmed the truth of what had been revealed before. Yet a faction among them, out of envy and obstinacy, turned away from this messenger though they recognized him, casting aside the Book that enjoined them to follow the final prophet. The phrase “cast the Book of Allāh behind their backs” signifies deliberate rejection after knowledge, not ignorance. Thus the verse censures those who abandoned the covenant entrusted to them and feigned unawareness of what they themselves possessed.",
  2102: "They followed what the devils recited during the reign of Solomon. Solomon did not disbelieve, but the devils disbelieved, teaching people sorcery and what had been sent down to the two angels [31] in Babylon—Harut and Marut. Yet neither of them would teach anyone without first saying, ‘We are only a test, so do not disbelieve.’ Still, they learned from them what could cause separation between a man and his wife. But they could not harm anyone except by Allah’s permission. And they learned what harmed them and did not benefit them. And they certainly knew that whoever purchased it would have no share in the Hereafter. Wretched indeed is what they sold themselves for, if only they knew.\\n\\nFootnotes: [31]. Concerning the phrase \"al-malakayni\" (“the two angels”), there is a well-known variant reading al-malikayn (“the two kings”). The majority of reciters read it as al-malakayni, two angels sent to Babylon as a trial. The alternate reading al-malikayn refers them as “two kings” or “two rulers,” though the dominant interpretation affirms their being angels. Thus the names Hārūt and Mārūt here stand for the two beings through whom the test occurred, whether read as angels or (in the variant) as kings.",
  2103: "If only they had believed and been mindful of Allāh, they would have had a far better reward with Allāh—if only they knew.",
  2104: "O believers, do not say ‘Rā‘inā,’ but say ‘Unẓurnā [32]’, and listen. And for the disbelievers is a painful punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [32]. The Arabic expression rāʿinā, which literally means “consider us” or “give us your attention,” was sometimes distorted by certain Jewish groups into a word of mockery. Because of this offensive misuse, Muslims were prohibited from using the phrase. Instead, the Qur’an instructs believers to say unẓurnā—“look upon us” or “grant us your attention”—a wording free from negative connotations.\nThis verse also highlights a broader principle: believers should avoid imitating non-Muslims in speech, behavior, clothing, celebrations, religious practices, or anything not sanctioned or approved in Islam.",
  2105: "Neither those who disbelieve from the People of the Book nor the polytheists wish that any good be sent down to you from your Lord. But Allāh chooses for His mercy whom He wills, and Allāh is the possessor of great bounty.",
  2106: "We do not abrogate a verse or cause it to be forgotten except that We bring one better than it or similar to it. Do you not know that Allāh is powerful over all things?",
  2107: "Do you not know that to Allāh belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth, and that you have no protector or helper besides Allāh?",
  2108: "Or would you like to question your Messenger as Moses was questioned before? Whoever exchanges faith for disbelief has indeed strayed from the straight path.",
  2109: "Many among the People of the Book wish to turn you back to disbelief after your having believed, out of envy within themselves, after the truth has become clear to them. So pardon and overlook until Allāh brings His command. Indeed, Allāh is powerful over all things.",
  2110: "Establish the prayer and give zakah. Whatever good you send forward for yourselves, you will find it with Allāh. Indeed, Allāh sees all that you do. [33]\\n\\nFootnotes: [33]. Believers are instructed to turn away from fruitless disputes and to focus instead on actions that strengthen faith and yield genuine spiritual benefit.",
  2111: "They say, ‘None will enter Paradise except a Jew or a Christian.’ This is their vain claim. Say, ‘Produce your proof, if you are truthful.’",
  2112: "Rather, whoever submits himself entirely to Allāh and does good will have his reward with his Lord; they will have no fear, nor will they grieve.",
  2113: "The Jews say, ‘The Christians have nothing to stand on,’ and the Christians say, ‘The Jews have nothing to stand on,’ although they both recite the Scripture [34]. Likewise, those [pagans] who do not know say the same. Allāh will judge between them on the Day of Resurrection concerning that over which they used to differ.\\n\\nFootnotes: [34]. When the Christians of Najrān came to the Messenger of God ﷺ, the Jewish rabbis also gathered, and the two groups debated in his presence. One of the rabbis declared to the Christians, “You have no standing whatsoever,” rejecting Jesus and the Gospel. A man from Najrān responded, “You have no standing whatsoever,” denying the prophethood of Moses and the Torah. Yet each group recited its own revealed scripture—the Torah, which contains glad tidings of Jesus’ coming as a messenger to the lost sheep of Israel, and the Gospel, which confirms the truth of Moses and the Torah.\nThis exchange illustrates how deeply entrenched religious rivalries had become. Each community affirmed its own scripture while dismissing the other’s, despite both texts containing signs that pointed toward continuity of God’s guidance. The Qur’anic narrative highlights these disputes to emphasize the need for sincerity, humility, and recognition of truth wherever it appears.",
  2114: "Who is more unjust than one who prevents Allah’s name from being remembered in His mosques and strives for their ruin? Such people should not enter them except in fear. For them is disgrace in this world, and for them is a severe punishment in the Hereafter.",
  2115: "To Allāh belongs the East and the West; wherever you turn, there is the Face of Allāh. Indeed, Allāh is All-Encompassing, All-Knowing.",
  2116: "They say, ‘Allāh has taken a son.’ Glory be to Him! Rather, to Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and the earth; all are obedient to Him.",
  2117: "He is the Originator of the heavens and the earth. When He decrees a matter, He simply says to it, ‘Be,’ and it is.",
  2118: "Those who have no knowledge say, ‘If only Allāh would speak to us or that a sign come to us.’ Those before them said similar words. Their hearts are alike. Indeed, We have made the signs clear for a people who are certain.",
  2119: "Indeed, We have sent you with the truth [35] as a bearer of good news and a warner, and you will not be questioned about the people of the Blazing Fire.\\n\\nFootnotes: [35]. Jesus said, “I will ask Allāh, and He will send you another Helper who will remain with you forever.”\nMany scholars understand this “Helper” to refer to the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ—sent as a mercy to all creation and a guide for humanity.\nWhoever follows his guidance and accepts his message is considered to be showing true love for Allāh; and whoever loves the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ is, in turn, loved by Allāh. As Jesus is also reported to have said, “If anyone loves Allāh, he will keep My word, and Allāh will love him and prepare for him a dwelling in Paradise. But whoever does not love Allāh does not keep My word.”\nJesus further proclaimed: “The Helper—the Spirit of Truth—whom Allāh will send, will teach you all things and remind you of all that has been revealed.”\nScholars interprets this prophecy as referring to Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ, whom Allāh sent to complete the divine message, confirm the truths contained in earlier scriptures, and provide further guidance.\nThe term “Helper” or “Comforter” (Greek: Paraklētos) appears in John 14:16, 26; 15:26; and 16:7–13.\nThese passages are understood to allude to Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ, who is described in the Qur’an as “a messenger confirming what came before him and bringing glad tidings to the believers.”\nThe expression “Spirit of Truth” is seen as pointing to his mission to convey and clarify Allāh’s final revelation.",
  2120: "The Jews and the Christians will never be pleased with you until you follow their religion. Say, ‘Indeed, the guidance of Allāh —that is the true guidance.’ If you were to follow their desires after the knowledge that has come to you, then you would have no protector or helper against Allāh.",
  2121: "Those to whom We have given the \nBook recite it as it should be recited [36]—it is they who believe in it. But whoever disbelieves in it, they are the losers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [36]. Applying the Qur’an in our daily lives is a duty upon every believer. If we fail to act upon it, the Qur’an becomes for us no more than an ordinary book. The first step toward living by Allāh’s Word is simply to read it—with the intention of knowing Allāh, learning His ways, and understanding His purpose for our lives. The next step is to keep His Word alive in our hearts by studying it, memorizing it, and reflecting deeply on what we have read. These four steps—reading, studying, memorizing, and contemplating—enable us to truly apply the Qur’an to our lives.\nIf we want Allāh’s Word to take root within us and bear fruit that pleases Allāh, we must ponder and reflect upon what we read and study. Applying the Qur’an strengthens and illuminates our understanding, and it sharpens our discernment, helping us better distinguish between what is good and what is harmful.",
  2122: "O Children of Israel, remember My favor which I bestowed upon you, and that I favored you above all other peoples.",
  2123: "And beware of a Day when no soul will avail another in the least; no compensation will be accepted from it, nor will intercession benefit it, nor will they be helped.",
  2124: "[Remember] when Abraham was tested by his Lord with certain commands [37], which he fulfilled completely, Allāh said, ‘I will make you a leader for mankind.’ He said, ‘And from among my descendants?’ He said, ‘My covenant does not include the wrongdoers.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [37]. The “words” or commands entrusted to Abraham were a series of divine trials and duties which he fulfilled completely.\nThese included: leaving his family in Makkah in obedience to Allāh’s command, building the Ka‘bah with his son Ishmael, being willing to sacrifice that son, practicing circumcision, and devoting his life to guiding and preaching the truth.\nThe Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said:\n“Five practices are characteristic of al-Fiṭrah — the natural disposition of mankind: (1) Circumcision, (2) Shaving the pubic hair, (3) Trimming the moustache, (4) Clipping the nails, and (5) Removing the hair of the armpits.”\nThese acts represent the continuity of the pure way of Abraham — physical signs of cleanliness and obedience reflecting the inner purity of faith.",
  2125: "And when We made the House a place of refuge and sanctuary for the people, and said, ‘Take the standing place of Abraham as a place of prayer.’ And We enjoined Abraham and Ishmael, ‘Purify My House for those who perform tawaf, those who remain in devotion, and those who bow and prostrate.’",
  2126: "And when Abraham said, ‘My Lord, make this city [of Makkah] a sanctuary and provide its people with fruits—those among them who believe in Allāh and the Last Day.’ He said, ‘As for those who disbelieve, I will grant them enjoyment for a short while, then I will force them into the punishment of the Fire. What an awful destination!’",
  2127: "And when Abraham and Ishmael were raising the foundations of the House, they said, ‘Our Lord, accept this from us, for You are the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.’",
  2128: "Our Lord, make us both submit to You, and from our descendants a nation that submits to You. Show us our rites and turn to us in mercy. Indeed, You are the Accepter of repentance, the Most Merciful.",
  2129: "Our Lord, send among them a messenger from themselves who will recite to them Your verses, teach them the Book and wisdom, and purify them. Indeed, You are the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.",
  2130: "Who would turn away from the creed of Abraham except one who makes a fool of himself [38]? We have indeed chosen him in this world, and in the Hereafter he will surely be among the righteous. [39]\\n\\nFootnotes: [38]. Abdullah ibn Salam—formerly a renowned Jewish scholar in Madinah who embraced Islam during the Prophet Muhammad’s ﷺ time—once invited his two nephews to accept Islam. He reminded them of a passage in the Torah stating: “I will send from the descendants of Ishmael a prophet whose name will be Ahmad; whoever believes in him is guided, and whoever rejects him is condemned.” One of his nephews accepted Islam, while the other refused. It was in this context that Allāh revealed this verse.\n[39]. Abraham is in the highest rank in the Hereafter. He was a prophet and messenger of Allāh, and the forefather of both the Ishmaelite Arabs and the Israelites. In Islam, Abraham stands as a central model of unwavering faith, having fulfilled all of God’s commands and trials with complete devotion.",
  2131: "When his Lord said to him, ‘Submit,’ he said, ‘I have submitted entirely to the Lord of all the worlds.’",
  2132: "And Abraham enjoined this upon his sons, and so did Jacob, saying, ‘O my children, Allāh has chosen this religion [of Islam] for you, so do not die except in submission to Him.’",
  2133: "Or were you present when death approached Jacob, when he said to his sons, ‘What will you worship after me?’ They said, ‘We will worship your God and the God of your fathers—Abraham, Ishmael, and Isaac—the one God, and to Him we submit.’",
  2134: "That was a nation that has passed on. For them is what they earned, and for you is what you have earned. And you will not be questioned about what they used to do.",
  2135: "They say, ‘Be Jews or Christians and you will be rightly guided.’ Say, ‘Rather, we follow the creed of Abraham—the upright monotheist—who was not among the polytheists.’",
  2136: "Say, ‘We believe in Allāh and in what has been sent down to us; and in what was sent down to Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob, and the Tribes; and in what was given to Moses and Jesus; and in what was given to the prophets from their Lord. We make no distinction between any of them, and to Him we submit.’",
  2137: "If they believe as you believe, then they are rightly guided. But if they turn away, then they are only in opposition. So Allāh will suffice you against them, and He is the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.",
  2138: "This is the coloring of Allāh—and who is better than Allāh in coloring [40]? And we are devoted in worship to Him.\\n\\nFootnotes: [40]. “Coloring” (ṣibghah) refers to the mark or imprint of faith that shapes a person inwardly and outwardly, like a dye that permeates cloth. It signifies a transformation that defines one’s identity, character, and way of life.\nIn Islam, this transformation begins with sincere faith: whoever believes in Allāh and His Messenger is guided, and whoever rejects faith bears the loss himself. When a person embraces Islam, they openly bear witness that none deserves worship except Allāh and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger. They also affirm belief in Jesus as a servant of Allāh, His messenger, His word bestowed upon Mary, and a soul created by Him—along with belief in the reality of Paradise and Hell.\nThis declaration marks both a personal and public entry into the Muslim community. It is often accompanied by a full ritual bath (ghusl), symbolizing purity and renewal, and followed by establishing the prayer as a sign of commitment. Yet the true foundation is the Shahādah itself—the pledge to worship Allāh alone and to follow His Messenger ﷺ. It is a profound and transformative commitment that reflects the “coloring of Allāh,” shaping a person’s life in faith, devotion, and obedience.",
  2139: "Say, ‘Do you argue with us concerning Allāh, while He is our Lord and your Lord? Our deeds are for us, and your deeds are for you, and to Him alone we are sincerely devoted.’",
  2140: "Or do you claim that Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob, and the Tribes were Jews or Christians? Say, ‘Do you know better, or does Allāh?’ Who is more unjust than one who conceals a testimony he has from Allāh? And Allāh is never unaware of what you do.",
  2141: "That was a community that has passed on. For them is what they earned, and for you is what you earn. And you will not be questioned about what they used to do.",
  2142: "The foolish among the people [41] will say, ‘What has turned them away from the Qiblah they used to face [42]?’ Say, ‘To Allāh belong the East and the West. He guides whom He wills to a straight path.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [41]. “The foolish among the people” refers to certain pagans, hypocrites, and unbelieving Jews who objected to the change in the direction of prayer. Here, Qiblah denotes the former direction of prayer toward Jerusalem.\n[42]. Before the command revealed in verse 2:144, Muslims prayed facing Jerusalem, which lies to the north of Madinah.",
  2143: "Thus We have made you a just and balanced nation [43], so that you may be witnesses over mankind and the Messenger a witness over you. We only appointed the former direction of prayer you used to face, then turned you from it toward the Ka‘bah in Makkah, to distinguish those who follow the Messenger from those who turn back on their heels. Indeed, it was burdensome except for those whom Allāh guided. And Allāh would never let your faith go to waste. Truly, Allāh is Ever-Gentle and Most Merciful to the people.\\n\\nFootnotes: [43]. “A just and balanced nation” refers to a community that stands between negligence and extremism in all aspects of faith, worship, and conduct. It embodies moderation, fairness, and integrity—avoiding both excess and deficiency. This middle path reflects the Qur’ānic ideal of moral balance, sound judgment, and measured devotion, enabling the Muslim community to serve as witnesses of truth among all peoples, just as the Prophet ﷺ is a witness over his followers.",
  2144: "Indeed, We have seen the turning of your face toward the sky [44], and We will surely turn you to a Qiblah that will please you. So turn your face toward the Sacred Mosque, and wherever you are, turn your faces toward it. And those who were given the Scripture certainly know that it is the truth from their Lord. And Allāh is never unaware of what you do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [44]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ was awaiting God’s command to change the Qiblah—the direction of prayer—so he would look toward the sky in hopeful expectation of the coming revelation.",
  2145: "Even if you were to bring every sign to those who were given the Scripture [45], they would not follow your prayer direction, nor would you follow their prayer direction, nor would they [46] follow one another’s prayer direction. And if you were to follow their desires after the knowledge that has come to you, then you would surely be among the unjust.\\n\\nFootnotes: [45]. Those who were given the Scripture refers to Jews and Christians who, despite possessing earlier revelation, would not accept the divinely mandated change of the Qiblah toward the Kaʿbah, regardless of the signs or proofs presented to them.\n[46]. That is, the Jews who prayed facing the Temple Mount, and the Christians who turned toward the East—each community having its own distinct Qiblah, different from one another and from the direction appointed for the Muslims.",
  2146: "Those to whom We have given the Scripture recognize him as they recognize their own sons [47], yet a group among them conceal the truth while they know.\\n\\nFootnotes: [47]. Because of the descriptions of him ﷺ in the Bible. This means that the learned among the Jews and Christians recognized the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ from their own scriptures with the same certainty and clarity with which a person recognizes their own child.",
  2147: "The truth is from your Lord, so never be among those who doubt.",
  2148: "For each is a direction to which it turns, so compete with one another in good deeds. Wherever you may be, Allāh will bring you all together. Indeed, Allāh is powerful over all things.",
  2149: "From wherever you set out [for prayer], turn your face toward the Sacred Mosque. This is indeed the truth from your Lord. And Allāh is never unaware of what you do.",
  2150: "From wherever you set out [for prayer], turn your face toward the Sacred Mosque; and wherever you may be, turn your faces toward it, so that people [48] will have no argument against you—except those among them who act unjustly. So do not fear them [49], but fear Me, so that I may complete My favor upon you and that you may be rightly guided.\\n\\nFootnotes: [48]. That is, the followers of earlier revelation—Jews and Christians—who had used the former direction of prayer as a point of argument and dispute.\n[49]. The Qur’an frequently repeats the message “Do not fear,” acknowledging fear as a natural human experience while reminding believers that Allāh is their Protector. With His presence and support, there is no need to fear people—only to maintain reverence and trust in Him.",
  2151: "Just as We have sent you a messenger from among you, who recites to you Our verses, purifies you, teaches you the Book and wisdom, and teaches you what you did not know. [50]\\n\\nFootnotes: [50]. “The Book” refers to the Qur’an, while “wisdom” is understood as the insight, understanding, and practical guidance derived from it, including the Prophet’s teachings (Sunnah). The verse highlights the comprehensive role of the Messenger in reciting revelation, purifying hearts, and teaching both the scripture and its proper understanding—knowledge they did not previously possess.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ is the final messenger sent by Allah to all humanity. Acceptance of his message is therefore inseparable from belief in Allah, who sent him. Faith in the prophets and the revelations they brought is a central principle, and those who accept and follow them are promised divine reward.\nThe Prophet ﷺ said, “Every prophet was sent specifically to his own people, whereas I have been sent to all people.” This universal mission underscores the inclusive scope of his message and the finality of his prophethood.\nAccordingly, entry into Islam is marked by the testimony of faith (Shahādah): to affirm that none is worthy of worship except Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. This declaration expresses belief in God’s oneness and acceptance of His final messenger, forming the foundation of a life of faith, guidance, and accountability.",
  2152: "So remember Me, and I will remember you [51]. Be grateful to Me, and do not be ungrateful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [51]. This means that when believers remember Allāh through faith, worship, and obedience, Allāh honors them with His mercy, support, and acceptance. His “remembering” is not like human remembrance; rather, it signifies divine attention, guidance, and reward in response to sincere devotion.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ narrated that Allāh said: “I am as My servant expects Me to be—meaning, I am able to do for him what he believes I can do. And I am with him whenever he remembers Me. If he remembers Me within himself, I remember him within Myself. If he remembers Me in a gathering, I remember him in a gathering better than it. If he draws near to Me by a span, I draw near to him by a cubit; and if he draws near to Me by a cubit, I draw near to him by the length of two outstretched arms. And if he comes to Me walking, I come to him running.”\nThis ḥadīth expresses Allah’s closeness to His servants, His generosity toward those who turn to Him, and His eagerness to reward even the smallest step taken in sincerity.",
  2153: "O believers, seek help through patience and prayer. Indeed, Allāh is with the patient. [52]\\n\\nFootnotes: [52]. Allah’s being “with” the patient means that He supports them with His guidance, strength, and mercy. Patience (ṣabr) here includes perseverance in obedience, restraint from sin, and steadfastness in trials. Those who rely on Allāh through patience and prayer are assured of His nearness and divine assistance.\nRejoicing in hope, enduring in affliction, being devoted and faithful in prayer.",
  2154: "Do not say of those who are slain in the cause of Allāh that they are dead. Rather, they are alive, though you do not perceive it.",
  2155: "We will surely test you with some fear, hunger, and loss of wealth, lives, and crops. But give glad tidings to those who are patient. [53]\\n\\nFootnotes: [53]. Patience (ṣabr) here refers to steadfastness in faith during trials—remaining firm, trusting in Allah’s wisdom, and refraining from complaint or despair. Those who bear hardship with resilience and reliance on Allāh are promised divine reward, comfort, and spiritual elevation.\nBlessed is the one who remains steadfast under trial, for when he has stood the test he will receive much rewards and favors, which Allāh has promised to those who are patient during hardships.\nWhen you meet with various trials consider it all joy, knowing as you do that this tested quality of your faith produces endurance. The various trial you face in life will put your faith to the test. But if you endure patiently the results are positive. You will gain more faith and a stronger relationship with Almighty Allāh.",
  2156: "Those who, when a calamity strikes them, say, ‘Indeed, we belong to Allāh, and to Him alone we shall return.’",
  2157: "They are the ones who receive blessings and mercy from their Lord, and they are rightly guided.",
  2158: "Indeed, Ṣafā and Marwah [54] are among the symbols of Allāh [55]. So whoever performs pilgrimage to the House or performs ʿUmrah—there is no blame upon him for going between them. And whoever does good voluntarily, then surely Allāh is Most Appreciative, All-Knowing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [54]. Ṣafā and Marwah are two small hills located within the Sacred Mosque in Makkah, between which pilgrims perform the ritual of saʿy during Ḥajj and ʿUmrah. Allāh settled Ismāʿīl and his mother Hagar in Makkah, where they found relief through the miraculous spring of the Well of Zamzam. Ismāʿīl is also associated with the construction of the Kaʿbah alongside his father Abraham and is regarded as an ancestor of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ.\n[55]. “Among the symbols of Allāh ” means that Ṣafā and Marwah are sacred rites ordained by God—signs of devotion that commemorate the faith, struggle, and perseverance of Hagar as she sought water for her infant son Ismāʿīl. They serve as enduring reminders of Allah’s mercy: that He responds to supplication, grants relief after hardship, and forgives those who come to Him with sincere hearts—free of idolatry, hypocrisy, and arrogance.",
  2159: "Indeed, those who conceal the clear proofs and guidance We have sent down, after We made them clear for people in the Scripture—those are cursed by Allāh and cursed by those who curse.",
  2160: "Except for those who repent, reform, and make clear what they had concealed—those are the ones to whom I turn in mercy. Indeed, I am the Accepter of repentance, the Most Merciful.",
  2161: "Indeed, those who disbelieve and die as disbelievers—upon them is the curse of Allāh, the angels, and all of humankind.",
  2162: "They will abide in it forever; their punishment will not be lightened, nor will they be given any respite.",
  2163: "Your God is one God; there is no god but Him—the Most Compassionate, the Most Merciful.",
  2164: "Indeed, in the creation of the heavens and the earth, in the alternation of night and day, in the ships that sail the seas carrying what benefits people, in the water that Allāh sends down from the sky—reviving the earth after its death and spreading across it all kinds of creatures—in the directing of the winds, and the clouds made subservient between the sky and the earth—there are surely signs for a people who use their reason.",
  2165: "Yet among the people are those who take others as equals to Allāh, loving them with a love that belongs to Allāh alone. But those who believe are stronger in their love for Allāh. If only the wrongdoers could see—when they behold the punishment—that all power belongs entirely to Allāh, and that Allāh is severe in punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [56]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: ‘Whoever loves for the sake of God, hates for the sake of God, gives for the sake of God, and withholds for the sake of God has completed their faith.’\nWhoever’s love and dislike, loyalty and disavowal are all for Allāh alone will attain closeness to Him. Without living by this principle, a person will never truly experience the sweetness of faith—even if they pray and fast often.\nToday, many people form relationships and express affection purely for worldly reasons, but such attachments will bring no benefit on the Day of Judgment.”",
  2166: "When those who were followed disown those who followed them, and they see the punishment, and all ties between them are severed.",
  2167: "Those who followed will say, ‘If only we had another chance, we would disown them just as they disowned us.’ Thus Allāh will show them their deeds as a source of bitter regret, and they will never escape the Fire.",
  2168: "O people, eat from what is lawful and wholesome on earth [57], and do not follow the footsteps of Satan, for he is a clear enemy to you.\\n\\nFootnotes: [57]. Two conditions govern what may be consumed: it must be lawful, and it must be good—meaning beneficial and not harmful.",
  2169: "He only commands you to commit evil and indecency, and to say about Allāh what you do not know.",
  2170: "When it is said to them, ‘Follow what Allāh has revealed,’ they say, ‘No, we follow what we found our forefathers practicing.’ Even though their forefathers had no understanding whatsoever, nor were they guided?",
  2171: "The example of those who disbelieve is like one who calls out to that which hears nothing but a call and a cry. They are deaf, dumb, and blind—so they do not understand.",
  2172: "O believers, eat from the good things We have provided for you, and be grateful to Allāh, if it is Him alone you worship.",
  2173: "He has only forbidden to you carrion, blood, the flesh of swine, and anything slaughtered in the name of other than Allāh. But whoever is compelled by necessity—neither seeking it nor exceeding limits—there is no sin upon him. Indeed, Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  2174: "Indeed, those who conceal what Allāh has revealed in the Book and exchange it for a small gain consume nothing into their bellies except fire. Allāh will not speak to them on the Day of Resurrection, nor will He purify them, and they will have a painful punishment.",
  2175: "They are the ones who have traded away guidance for misguidance, and forgiveness for punishment. How can they endure the Fire!",
  2176: "That is because Allāh has sent down the Book in truth. And those who dispute about the Book [58] are surely in deep opposition.\\n\\nFootnotes: [58]. Believing in parts of it while disbelieving in others.",
  2177: "Righteousness is not in turning your faces toward the east or the west, but righteousness is to believe in Allāh, the Last Day, the angels, the Book, and the prophets; to give wealth—despite love for it—to relatives, orphans, the needy, the stranded traveler [59], beggars, and for freeing slaves; to establish prayer and give zakah; to fulfill their promises when they make them; and to remain patient in hardship, adversity, and in times of peril. Those are the ones who are true, and those are the ones who are God-conscious.\\n\\nFootnotes: [59]. This principle extends to strangers, travelers in need, refugees, and foreigners who find themselves without support while away from their homeland, as well as residents living in a land not originally their own. Such individuals are often vulnerable and dependent upon the goodwill of those among whom they reside. Islam commands that they be treated with dignity, security, and fairness, not subjected to harm, humiliation, or anxiety.\nThe story of the Hijrah (migration) reminds believers that the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ himself was once a stranger who fled Makkah and was received with generosity and protection in Madinah. This historical reality reinforces the moral duty to show compassion toward those displaced from their homes.\nA resident foreigner or non-Muslim living under covenant (ahl al-dhimmah or muʿāhad) must be granted justice and protection. The Prophet ﷺ said:\n“Whoever kills a covenant-holder shall not smell the fragrance of Paradise, though its fragrance can be perceived from a distance of forty years.”\nHe also said: “Whoever wrongs a covenant-holder, diminishes his rights, burdens him beyond his capacity, or takes from him without his consent—I will be his adversary on the Day of Resurrection.”\nThese teachings establish a clear principle: protection of the vulnerable, justice for minorities, and faithful observance of covenants are integral to Islamic ethics. Kindness toward the stranger is not merely social courtesy—it is a religious obligation rooted in prophetic guidance and accountability before Allāh.",
  2178: "O believers, legal retribution is prescribed for you in cases of murder: the free for the free, the slave for the slave, and the female for the female. But if any pardon is granted by the victim’s kin, then there should be fair follow-up and payment made with good conduct. This is an easing and a mercy from your Lord. But whoever transgresses after that will have a painful punishment.",
  2179: "In legal retribution there is life for you, O people of understanding, so that you may become God-conscious.",
  2180: "It is prescribed for you that when death approaches one of you, if he leaves behind wealth, he should make a bequest for his parents and close relatives in a fair manner [60]. This is a duty upon the righteous. [61]\\n\\nFootnotes: [60]. That is the bequest must not exceed the allowed one-third of the estate.\n[61]. This ruling was later abrogated by the inheritance laws in Q. 4:11, and by the Prophet’s ﷺ statement: “No bequest may be made in favor of an heir.\"\nThose who do not inherit by law may be remembered in a bequest.",
  2181: "So whoever changes it after hearing it, its sin is only upon those who change it. Indeed, Allāh is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.",
  2182: "But whoever fears from a testator any deviation or sin and then reconciles between them—there is no sin upon him. Indeed, Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  2183: "O believers, fasting is prescribed for you [62], just as it was prescribed for those before you, so that you may become mindful of Allāh. [63]\\n\\nFootnotes: [62]. Fasting means abstaining from food, drink, and marital relations from dawn until sunset.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: “Whoever fasts during the month of Ramadan with sincere faith and in hope of Allah’s reward will have all their past sins forgiven.”\n[63]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ would fast either twenty-nine or thirty days each year in the lunar month of Ramadan, depending on the sighting of the moon. He would also spend the nights in prayer, dedicating himself to communion with Allāh. In the same way, through fasting and prayer, a believer draws closer to Allāh and gains deeper insight into His guidance.",
  2184: "It is for a limited number of days. So whoever among you is ill or on a journey—then an equal number of other days. And upon those who can fast only with difficulty is a ransom: feeding one poor person. But whoever volunteers any good—it is better for him. And that you fast is better for you, if only you knew. [64]\\n\\nFootnotes: [64]. The general ruling in this verse was later superseded by the following verse (2:185), with certain exceptions remaining in effect—such as for the very elderly, pregnant women, and others with valid limitations.",
  2185: "The month of Ramadan is that in which the Qur’an was revealed as guidance for mankind, with clear proofs of guidance and the criterion that distinguishes right from wrong. So whoever among you witnesses the month must fast it. But whoever is ill or on a journey—then an equal number of other days. Allāh intends for you ease and does not intend for you hardship, so that you may complete the prescribed number, magnify Allāh for what He has guided you to, and be grateful.",
  2186: "When My servants ask you about Me, then indeed I am near. I respond to the call of whoever calls upon Me. So let them respond to Me and believe in Me, so that they may be rightly guided. [66]\\n\\nFootnotes: [65]. A group of the Prophet’s ﷺ companions once asked him, “Is Allāh, our Lord, near so that we should speak to Him quietly, or is He far away so that we must call out to Him?” Then this verse was revealed.\nAllah’s nearness means that a person does not need to raise their voice or seek an intermediary in order for their supplication to be heard. Allāh listens to and answers the prayers of His servants directly, without the need for any intercession or intermediaries.\n[66]. The Messenger of God ﷺ reported that Allāh said: “Whoever shows hostility to a devoted servant of Mine, I declare war against him. The most beloved deeds by which My servant draws near to Me are those I have made obligatory for him. And My servant continues to draw closer to Me through voluntary acts of worship until I love him. When I love him, I become the hearing with which he hears, the sight with which he sees, the hand with which he acts, and the foot with which he walks. If he asks Me, I grant him; and if he seeks My protection, I protect him. And I do not hesitate in anything as I hesitate to take the soul of the believing servant: he dislikes death, and I dislike to displease him.”",
  2187: "It is permitted for you, on the nights of fasting, to be intimate with your wives. They are a garment for you, and you are a garment for them. Allāh knows that you were betraying yourselves [67], so He turned to you in mercy and forgave you. So now you may approach them intimately [68] and seek what Allāh has ordained for you. Eat and drink until the white thread of dawn becomes distinct from the black thread of night. Then complete the fast until nightfall. But do not approach them while you are in retreat in the mosques. These are the limits set by Allāh, so do not go near them. Thus Allāh makes His verses clear to the people, so that they may become mindful of Him.\\n\\nFootnotes: [67]. This alludes to the quiet struggles of the believers who, in weakness, engaged in what had once been forbidden during the nights of Ramadan. Allāh, in His perfect knowledge of the hearts, was aware of their inner conflict even before they turned back to Him in repentance.\n[68]. In the early days, marital relations were not permitted during the nights before the daily fast, and this proved difficult for some. In moments of human weakness, a few fell into what had been prohibited. Yet Allāh, in His overflowing mercy, accepted their repentance and lifted the hardship from them, making lawful what had once been restricted—a sign of His kindness toward His servants.",
  2188: "Do not consume your wealth among yourselves unjustly, nor offer it as a bribe to the authorities to consume a portion of others’ wealth in sin while you know. [69]\\n\\nFootnotes: [69]. Bribery is clearly condemned in divine revelation. The Messenger of God, Muḥammad ﷺ, stated: “Allāh has cursed the one who gives a bribe and the one who accepts it.” This condemnation is also affirmed in earlier scriptures. In the Book of Proverbs, it is written: “Whoever is greedy for unjust gain brings trouble upon his own household, but the one who hates bribes will live” (Proverbs 15:27). Together, these texts underscore a consistent moral principle across revelations: that bribery corrupts justice, destroys social trust, and ultimately leads to personal and communal harm.",
  2189: "They ask you about the new moons. Say, ‘They are appointed times for the people and for the pilgrimage.’ And righteousness is not in entering houses from their backs, but true righteousness is in one who is mindful of Allāh. So enter houses through their doors, and be mindful of Allāh so that you may succeed.",
  2190: "Fight in the cause of Allāh against those who fight you [70], but do not transgress. Indeed, Allāh does not like the transgressors.\\n\\nFootnotes: [70]. A Companion of the Prophet ﷺ reported that he once asked the Messenger of God ﷺ, “What is the best deed in Islam?” He replied, “To perform the prayers at their appointed times.” The man asked, “What comes next in goodness?” He said, “Honoring your parents, for that is most pleasing to Allāh.” He asked again, “And what comes after that?” The Prophet ﷺ replied, “Striving in the cause of Allāh (jihād).”\nThis verse emphasizes a balanced and principled concept of justice in warfare. The command is to fight only those who initiate hostility against the believers—meaning that combat is a response to aggression, not an act of oppression, revenge, or conquest.\nAt the same time, it establishes clear moral limits: \nDo not transgress beyond what is permitted.\nDo not harm non-combatants, such as women, children, the elderly, monks, or civilians.\nDo not destroy crops, livestock, or places of worship.\nDo not continue fighting once the enemy ceases their aggression.\nThus, fighting in Islam is bound by strict ethical, spiritual, and humanitarian restraints.",
  2191: "And kill them in combat wherever you encounter them, and drive them out from where they drove you out, for persecution is worse than killing. Do not fight them at the Sacred Mosque unless they fight you there. But if they fight you, then fight them—such is the recompense of the disbelievers.",
  2192: "But if they cease, then indeed Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  2193: "Fight them until there is no more persecution and religion is entirely for Allāh. But if they desist, then there is no hostility except against the wrongdoers.",
  2194: "[Retaliation in] the Sacred Month is for [aggression committed in] the Sacred Month, and all violations are subject to just retaliation. So whoever commits aggression against you, respond to him in the same manner as he has aggressed against you. Be mindful of Allāh, and know that Allāh is with with those who are mindful of Him.",
  2195: "Spend in the cause of Allāh, and do not throw yourselves into destruction by your own hands [71]. And do good—indeed, Allāh loves those who do good. [72)\\n\\nFootnotes: [71]. This warning refers to destroying oneself not through direct harm alone, but through abandoning one’s moral duty—by turning away from striving in the Cause of Allāh, or by withholding the wealth that is needed to uphold justice, protect the oppressed, and sustain what is right. For when a person clings to safety, comfort, or fear of loss at the expense of truth and responsibility, that very neglect becomes a path to ruin—spiritually, socially, and ultimately eternally.\nThis frames the verse as a call out of complacency, not into recklessness.\n[72]. Let your heart never grow weary of doing good. For every sincere act is seen by Allāh, and every seed of righteousness will be rewarded at its appointed time. If you do not give up, you will surely reap a harvest—whether in this life, the next, or both. So extend your goodness to all people without exception, but give special care to those who strive in God’s Cause, to those who call others to the Truth with wisdom and patience, and to the wider family of believers who keep alive the worship of God through the congregational prayers in the mosques. For supporting them is supporting the light that guides the world.",
  2196: "Complete the Hajj and the ‘Umrah for the sake of Allāh [73]. But if you are prevented, then offer whatever sacrificial animal is easy to obtain [74], and do not shave your heads until the offering reaches its appointed place. If any of you is ill or has an ailment of the head, then a ransom must be offered—by fasting, charity, or sacrifice [75]. Then when you are secure, whoever performs ‘Umrah followed by Hajj shall offer whatever sacrifice is easy to obtain. But whoever cannot afford it must fast three days during the Hajj and seven when you return—making ten days in all. This is for those whose families do not reside near the Sacred Mosque. Be mindful of Allāh, and know that Allāh is severe in punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [73]. This refers to the two sacred journeys to Makkah: the Hajj, which is the great annual pilgrimage and a pillar of Islam, and the ‘Umrah, the lesser pilgrimage that may be performed at any time of the year. Both are acts of loving obedience, journeys of body and soul toward the House of God, where the believer answers the ancient call of Abraham with humility, longing, and devotion.\n[74]. The sacrificial offering may be a sheep, a cow, or a camel—each according to one’s ability and means. This sacrifice is not merely the shedding of blood, but a symbol of gratitude, surrender, and nearness to Allāh. It represents the pilgrim’s willingness to give up what is precious in obedience to the One to whom all things belong.\n[75]. That is, the one who is required to make expiation may choose one of three paths, each carrying its own spiritual meaning:\n• Fasting for three days, as an act of inward discipline and remembrance of God,\n• Or feeding six poor people of the Sacred Precinct (the sanctified area of Makkah), sharing one’s blessing with the needy in the very land made holy by God,\n• Or slaughtering a sheep or goat and distributing its meat among the poor of the Sacred Precinct, so that one’s mistake is transformed into mercy for others.\nIn every option, the goal is not punishment, but purification—so that the pilgrim returns to God with a heart cleansed, a body humbled, and a soul renewed.",
  2197: "The pilgrimage is in well-known months [76]. So whoever commits himself to the Hajj in them must refrain from intimacy, sinful conduct, and arguing during the pilgrimage. Whatever good you do, Allāh knows it. Take provision, but the best provision is righteousness. And be mindful of Me, O people of understanding.\\n\\nFootnotes: [76]. These are the sacred months of the lunar year in which the Pilgrimage is ordained: Shawwāl, Dhul-Qa‘dah, and Dhul-Ḥijjah. They are not chosen at random, but appointed by Allāh Himself as a sacred season of preparation, journey, and worship—months in which hearts turn toward the House of God and souls ready themselves for the sanctity of standing before Him.",
  2198: "There is no sin upon you in seeking bounty from your Lord. Then, when you depart from ‘Arafāt [77], remember Allāh at the Sacred Monument, and remember Him as He has guided you—for before that you were surely among those who had gone astray.\\n\\nFootnotes: [77]. The word Aravot appears several times in the Hebrew Scriptures, referring to elevated desert plains—vast, open spaces associated with the majesty, nearness, and glorification of God. In Psalm 68:4 it is said: “Extol Him who rides upon the Aravot,” presenting the desert heights as a place of divine manifestation and praise. Likewise, Isaiah 40:3 proclaims: “In the Aravah, make straight a highway for our God,” calling for inner preparation, humility, and spiritual readiness—an idea strikingly similar to the state of repentance, surrender, and prayer lived by the pilgrims at ʿArafāt.\nJoshua 11:2 also mentions the people of the Aravah, the inhabitants of the desert plains. Just as ancient communities once lived and worshipped in these open wilderness spaces, today millions of Muslims gather on the open plain of ʿArafāt—standing, weeping, supplicating, and returning to God in the most intense act of collective repentance on earth.\nʿArafāt itself is an open wilderness—flat land east of Makkah—yet it is known as the Place of Divine Encounter. On this most sacred day of Hajj, pilgrims glorify Allāh in vast multitudes, seeking forgiveness, mercy, and nearness. It is a day that symbolizes both Judgment and Mercy, echoing the prophetic call to repentance and total surrender.\nThe connection between “Aravot” in the earlier scriptures and “ʿArafāt” in Islamic tradition is therefore not merely linguistic, but profoundly spiritual and thematic. Both point to: A sacred desert space,\nA moment of divine presence and revelation,\nAnd an act of deep human humility, submission, and return to God.\nThis harmony across traditions may reflect a shared memory of desert worship—a sacred pattern preserved through generations, where the open wilderness becomes a meeting place between the human soul and the Lord of the worlds.",
  2199: "Then depart from where all the people depart and seek Allah’s forgiveness. Indeed, Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  2200: "When you have completed your rites [78], remember Allāh as you remember your fathers—or with even greater remembrance. And among the people are those who say, ‘Our Lord, give us in this world,’ but they will have no share in the Hereafter.\\n\\nFootnotes: [78]. That is, the sacred rites of Hajj include entering the state of consecration (iḥrām), circling the Ka‘bah (ṭawāf), walking between aṣ-Ṣafā and al-Marwah (sa‘ī), standing at ‘Arafāt in humble supplication, spending the night in remembrance at Muzdalifah, staying in Minā, casting the stones at the appointed pillars as a symbol of rejecting evil, and finally offering the sacrificial animal (hady). Each of these actions is not merely a movement of the body, but an act of obedience, surrender, and renewal of the soul before Allāh.",
  2201: "And among them are those who say, ‘Our Lord, give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter, and protect us from the punishment of the Fire.’",
  2202: "Those will have a share of what they have earned, and Allāh is swift in reckoning.",
  2203: "And remember Allāh during specific numbered days [79]. Then whoever hastens to depart in two days [80]—there is no sin upon him; and whoever delays [81]—there is no sin upon him, for one who fears Allāh. And be mindful of Allāh, and know that to Him you will be gathered.\\n\\nFootnotes: [79]. This refers to the valley of Minā: the 11th, 12th, and 13th of Dhul-Ḥijjah, known as the Days of Tashrīq. During these days, pilgrims are encouraged to frequently remember Allāh by proclaiming the Takbīr, such as: “Allāhu akbar, Allāhu akbar, Allāhu akbar, lā ilāha illā Allāh, wa-Allāhu akbar, Allāhu akbar, wa li-llāhi al-ḥamd”\n(Allāh is the Greatest, Allāh is the Greatest, Allāh is the Greatest; there is no god but Allāh; Allāh is the Greatest, Allāh is the Greatest, and to Allāh belongs all praise.)\n[80]. That is, the 12th of Dhul-Ḥijjah.\n[81]. That is, remaining one additional day, namely the 13th of Dhul-Ḥijjah.",
  2204: "Among the people is he whose speech pleases you in this worldly life, and he calls Allāh to witness what is in his heart—yet he is the most quarrelsome of opponents. [82]\\n\\nFootnotes: [82]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“The most beloved words to Allāh are when a servant says: ‘Glory be to You, O Allāh, and all praise is Yours. Blessed is Your Name, exalted is Your Majesty, and there is no god besides You.’ And the most hateful words to Allāh are when one person says to another, ‘Fear Allah,’ and the other replies, ‘Worry about yourself!’”\nThe Prophet ﷺ also said:\n“The most detestable person in the sight of Allāh is the one who is most quarrelsome.”\nThis is one of the four signs of a true hypocrite. The other three are: lying, breaking promises, and betraying trusts.",
  2205: "And when he turns away, he strives throughout the land to spread corruption, destroying crops and livestock. And Allāh does not like corruption. [83]\\n\\nFootnotes: [83]. The statement “and destroys crops and progeny” conveys a comprehensive image of corruption in the land. It refers to the corruption of the sources of life and the destruction of the means of human survival. Crops (al-ḥarth) signify food production, natural resources, and economic stability, while progeny (al-nasl) denotes the continuity of human and animal life. Accordingly, the verse encompasses all forms of corruption, including environmental degradation, economic disruption, the spread of conflict and warfare, and any activity that leads to the breakdown of the natural and social order.\nThe verse further describes a type of individual whose speech appears pleasing and righteous, yet whose actions are profoundly destructive—outwardly persuasive but inwardly corrupt.\nIn a broader interpretive sense, some scholars extend this concept to include forms of moral and social corruption that undermine the integrity of progeny. This may include sexual immorality and other deviant practices, insofar as they can result in consequences such as confusion of lineage, loss of children’s rights, and the disintegration of family structure. While such acts do not constitute direct physical destruction, they may nevertheless be understood as contributing to the corruption of the social order associated with “progeny.”",
  2206: "When it is said to him, ‘Fear Allah,’ pride in sin takes hold of him. Hell is sufficient for him—and what a terrible resting place.",
  2207: "And among the people is he who sells himself seeking the pleasure of Allāh. And Allāh is Most Kind to His servants.",
  2208: "O believers, enter fully into submission to God [Islam] and do not follow the footsteps of Satan. Indeed, he is your clear enemy.",
  2209: "But if you slip after the clear proofs have come to you, then know that Allāh is All-Mighty, All-Wise.",
  2210: "Do they await anything other than that Allāh should come to them in the shadows of clouds, along with the angels, and then the matter is decided? And to Allāh all matters return.",
  2211: "Ask the Children of Israel how many clear signs We have given them. And whoever exchanges the Favor of Allāh [for disbelief] after it has come to him—then indeed Allāh is Severe in Punishment.",
  2212: "The life of this world is made appealing to those who disbelieve, and they mock those who believe; but those who are mindful of Allāh will be above them on the Day of Resurrection. And Allāh provides for whom He wills without measure.",
  2213: "Mankind was once a single community [before their deviation]. Then Allāh sent prophets as bearers of glad tidings and warners, and sent down with them the Scripture in truth to judge between the people concerning that over which they differed. And none differed over it [the Scripture] except those who were given it, after clear proofs had come to them, out of mutual jealousy. Then Allāh guided those who believed to the truth about which they differed, by His will. And Allāh guides whom He wills to a straight path.",
  2214: "Or do you think that you will enter Paradise without facing what those who passed before you faced? They were afflicted by poverty and hardship and were so shaken that even the Messenger and those who believed with him said, ‘When will the help of Allāh come?’ Indeed, the help of Allāh is near.",
  2215: "They ask you what they should spend. Say, ‘Whatever good you spend should be for parents, relatives, orphans, the needy, and the traveler in need. And whatever good you do—Allāh is All-Knowing of it.’",
  2216: "Fighting has been prescribed for you, though you dislike it. But perhaps you dislike something that is good for you, and perhaps you love something that is bad for you. And Allāh knows, while you do not know.",
  2217: "They ask you about fighting in the Sacred Month [84]. Say, ‘Fighting in it is a grave sin. But preventing others from the path of Allāh, disbelief in Him, preventing access to the Sacred Mosque, and expelling its people from it are far graver in the sight of Allāh. And persecution is worse than killing.’ They will not cease fighting you until they turn you back from your religion, if they are able. And whoever among you turns back from his religion and dies as a disbeliever—those are the ones whose deeds will be lost in this world and the Hereafter. They are the inhabitants of the Fire; they will remain therein forever.\\n\\nFootnotes: [84]. That is, the 1st, 7th, 11th, and 12th months of the Islamic calendar.",
  2218: "Indeed, those who have believed, emigrated, and striven in the cause of Allāh are the ones who hope for Allah’s mercy [85]. And Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [85]. The phrase “striven in the cause of Allāh” translates the Arabic jāhadū fī sabīl Allāh, which encompasses all forms of sincere effort in obedience to God. This includes not only armed struggle when legitimately prescribed, but also striving through worship, patience, charity, knowledge, and moral integrity. The verse highlights that true hope in God’s mercy is attained through faith, sacrifice, and steadfast commitment to His path.\nA believer is therefore called to rejoice in hope, remain patient in hardship, and be constant in prayer—signs of a heart that truly trusts in Allāh: joy in His promise, perseverance in His decree, and closeness to Him through devotion.",
  2219: "They ask you about intoxicants [86] and gambling. Say, ‘In both is great sin and some benefit for people, but their sin is greater than their benefit.’ And they ask you what they should spend. Say, ‘What is beyond your needs [87].’ Thus Allāh makes His signs clear to you, so that you may reflect.\\n\\nFootnotes: [86]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“Among the signs of the Hour are the spread of ignorance, the decline of religious knowledge, the widespread practice of fornication, the drinking of intoxicants in abundance, and the decrease in the number of men while the number of women increases.”\n[87]. That is: from that which exceeds one’s needs.",
  2220: "In this world and the Hereafter. And they ask you about the orphans. Say, ‘Setting right for them is best. And if you mix your affairs with theirs, then they are your brothers. And Allāh knows the corrupter from the one who sets things right. And if Allāh had willed, He could have put you in hardship. Indeed, Allāh is All-Mighty, All-Wise.’",
  2221: "Do not marry women who associate others with Allāh until they believe. A believing slave woman is better than a polytheist, even though she may please you. And do not give your women in marriage to men who associate others with Allāh until they believe. A believing slave is better than a polytheist, even though he may please you. Those invite to the Fire, while Allāh invites to Paradise and forgiveness by His permission. And He makes His verses clear to the people so that they may take heed.",
  2222: "They ask you about menstruation. Say, ‘It is harm, so keep away from your wives during menstruation and do not approach them intimately until they become pure [88]. When they have purified themselves, then go to them as Allāh has commanded you. Indeed, Allāh loves those who constantly turn to Him in repentance, and He loves those who purify themselves.’ [89]\\n\\nFootnotes: [88]. This means refraining from sexual intercourse during menstruation. The Qur’an and Sunnah permit all forms of closeness between spouses at this time except intercourse. The Jews avoided even sitting with a menstruating woman, while some Christians permitted intercourse.\nIslam chose a balanced path: it forbids intercourse but allows affection.\nIt is also prohibited for a man to divorce his wife while she is menstruating. Allāh has decreed that divorce should occur during a state of purity—free from menstruation and postpartum bleeding—and at a time when the husband has not yet resumed intercourse with her. This is the prescribed form of divorce in Islamic law.\nDuring menstruation, women suspend prayer and fasting and maintain personal hygiene. They do not need to make up the prayers missed during this time, but they must make up the fasts missed in Ramadan. When the menstrual period ends, a woman must perform ritual purification (ghusl) and then resume prayer.\n[89]. The verse concludes by highlighting two qualities beloved to Allāh: repentance (tawbah) and purification (ṭahārah). This includes both spiritual purification—turning back to Allāh from sin—and physical cleanliness. Together, they reflect a complete understanding of purity in Islam, encompassing both inner character and outward practice.\nA believer is therefore called to continually repent, purify oneself, and avoid wrongdoing. Through sincere repentance, Allāh forgives sins and purifies the heart. True success lies in striving for purity of both body and soul, seeking nearness to Allāh and His pleasure.",
  2223: "Your wives are a tilth for you [90], so come to your tilth as you will [91], and put forward for yourselves [good deeds] [92]. And be mindful of Allah, and know that you will meet Him. And give glad tidings to the believers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [90]. The metaphor likens the womb to cultivated land: just as seed is sown in the earth in expectation of growth, a man places seed in the womb in hope of offspring. The imagery emphasizes both procreation and care.\n[91]. This indicates that marital intimacy is permissible in any manner, provided it remains within what is lawful—namely, intercourse in the vaginal passage, and not during menstruation or through the anal passage.\n[92]. Marital intimacy is a lawful and meaningful bond between husband and wife, encompassing affection, closeness, and physical fulfillment. It is part of Allāh’s design to strengthen love between spouses and to enable procreation. At the same time, believers are encouraged to intend righteous purposes in their intimacy—such as nurturing righteous offspring, expressing kindness, and strengthening the marital bond—so that even these private acts become deeds rewarded in the Hereafter.",
  2224: "Do not use Allah’s name in your oaths as an excuse to avoid doing good, being mindful of Him, and reconciling between people. And Allāh is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.",
  2225: "Allāh will not hold you accountable for unintentional oaths, but He holds you accountable for what your hearts have earned. And Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Forbearing.",
  2226: "For those who swear to abstain from marital relations with their wives, there is a waiting period of four months [93]. But if they return [to their wives], then indeed Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [93]. This refers to the practice of īlāʾ: when a husband vows to abstain from sexual relations with his wife in order to punish her. Four months is the maximum time this state is allowed to continue. After that, the husband must either return to his wife and restore the marriage, or proceed with divorce so that he no longer wrongs her. If he returns, Allāh is forgiving and merciful.",
  2227: "But if they decide on divorce, then indeed Allāh is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.",
  2228: "Divorced women shall wait for three menstrual periods. It is not lawful for them to conceal what Allāh has created in their wombs, if they believe in Allāh and the Last Day. And their husbands have a greater right to take them back during that period, if they desire reconciliation. And for them are rights similar to the obligations upon them, according to what is fair, though men have a degree of responsibility over them. And Allāh is All-Mighty, All-Wise.",
  2229: " Divorce is twice. Then either retain in a fair manner or release with kindness. It is not lawful for you to take back anything of what you have given them, unless both fear that they cannot keep within the limits of Allāh. But if you fear that they cannot keep within the limits of Allāh, then there is no blame on either of them for what she gives to ransom herself. These are the limits set by Allāh, so do not transgress them. And whoever transgresses the limits of Allāh—those are the wrongdoers.",
  2230: "If he divorces her [for the third time], she is not lawful for him thereafter unless she marries another husband. Then if he divorces her, there is no blame on both of them to return to each other, provided they believe they can uphold the limits of Allāh. These are the limits set by Allāh, which He makes clear to a people who understand.",
  2231: "When you divorce women and they are nearing the end of their waiting period, either retain them honorably or release them graciously. Do not hold them back to harm them and thereby transgress. Whoever does so has wronged himself. And do not make a mockery of Allah’s revelations. And remember the favor of Allāh upon you and what He has sent down to you of the Book and wisdom, by which He instructs you. And be mindful of Allāh, and know that Allāh has full knowledge of all things.",
  2232: "When you divorce women [94] and they complete their waiting period, do not prevent them from remarrying their former husbands if they agree between themselves in a lawful manner. This is an admonition for whoever among you believes in Allāh and the Last Day. That is better and purer for you. And Allāh knows, while you do not know.\\n\\nFootnotes: [94]. Meaning: after either the first or the second pronouncement of divorce.",
  2233: "Mothers may breastfeed their children for two complete years—for those who wish to complete the nursing. Upon the father is their provision and clothing according to what is customary. No soul is burdened beyond its capacity. No mother should be harmed on account of her child, nor a father on account of his child. And upon the heir is the like of that. If both desire weaning by mutual consent and consultation, there is no blame upon them. And if you wish to have your children nursed by others, there is no blame upon you, provided you give what you agreed in fairness. And be mindful of Allāh, and know that Allāh sees what you do.",
  2234: "And those among you who die and leave behind wives—they shall wait for four months and ten days. When they complete their term, then there is no blame upon you for what they do with themselves in a lawful manner. And Allāh is Aware of what you do.",
  2235: "There is no blame upon you for what you hint at in proposing to such women, or what you conceal within yourselves. Allāh knows that you will think of them, but do not make a promise to them in secret, except that you speak a proper word. And do not resolve upon the bond of marriage until the prescribed term has reached its end. And know that Allāh knows what is within yourselves, so beware of Him. And know that Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Forbearing.",
  2236: "There is no blame upon you if you divorce women before you have touched them [95] or specified for them an obligation [dowry]. But provide for them—the wealthy according to his means and the one with limited means according to his means—a provision according to what is fair, a duty upon the doers of good.\\n\\nFootnotes: [95]. That is, before the marriage had been consummated (i.e., through marital relations).",
  2237: "If you divorce them before you have touched them, but after you have fixed a dowry for them, then give them half of what you fixed—unless they remit it, or the one in whose hand is the marriage bond remits it. And to remit is nearer to piety. And do not forget graciousness between yourselves. Indeed, Allāh sees what you do.",
  2238: "Maintain with care the prayers—especially the middle prayer—and stand in devout obedience to Allāh. [96]\\n\\nFootnotes: [96]. Prayer is mentioned in the midst of marital and divorce rulings because of the emotional pain and turmoil that separation often brings. In times of distress, believers are reminded to turn back to Allāh through steadfast prayer. Elsewhere, prayer is described as a deterrent from immorality and wrongdoing.\nIn this verse, believers are commanded to stand before Allāh in complete humility and obedience, for true veneration belongs to Him alone. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: “Whoever loves that people stand for him out of veneration, let him take his seat in Hell.” This warning is against seeking arrogant glorification that belongs only to Allāh.\nActs of worship—such as prostration, ritual bowing as worship, sacrifice, vows, circumbulation, and absolute devotion of fear, hope, and obedience—are due solely to Allāh. Directing these acts to other than Him constitutes idolatry.\nAs for standing in ordinary human customs, such as standing briefly to greet or honor someone without worshipful intent, this is permitted in Islam, so long as it does not involve arrogance, veneration resembling worship, or a demand for submissive glorification.",
  2239: "And if you fear [danger] [97], then pray while walking or riding. But when you are secure, then remember Allāh as He has taught you what you did not know.\\n\\nFootnotes: [97]. That is, fear of an enemy, an imminent danger, or any similar threat to safety.",
  2240: "Those among you who die and leave wives behind should make a bequest for their wives: provision for a year without being turned out [98]. But if they leave of their own accord, then there is no blame upon you for what they do with themselves in a lawful manner. And Allāh is All-Mighty, All-Wise.\\n\\nFootnotes: [98]. Many scholars held that this directive was later superseded in its financial aspect by the rulings of inheritance in 4:12 and by the fixed waiting period set in 2:234, while others maintained that the housing right remains valid if granted through a bequest.",
  2241: "For divorced women is a provision according to what is fair—a duty upon the righteous.",
  2242: "Thus Allāh makes His verses clear to you, so that you may understand.",
  2243: "Have you not considered those who left their homes in thousands, fearing death? Then Allāh said to them, ‘Die,’ and then He brought them back to life [99]. Indeed, Allāh is Gracious to people, but most people are not grateful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [99]. This verse refers to a group of people who fled their towns in large numbers during a plague or a time of war, out of fear of death, thinking they could escape Allāh’s decree. To show them that no one can flee His judgment and to make them reflect on His immense bounty, Allāh caused them to die, and then restored them to life again.",
  2244: "So fight in the cause of Allāh, and know that Allāh is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.",
  2245: "Who is it that will lend Allāh a goodly loan [100], so that He may multiply it for him many times over? Allāh withholds and expands provision, and to Him you will be returned.\\n\\nFootnotes: [100]. The phrase “a goodly loan” (qardan ḥasanan) refers to spending wealth in the cause of Allāh with complete sincerity, seeking nothing in return except His pleasure and reward. It is called a “loan” not because Allāh is in need—exalted is He above all need—but as a metaphor of honor, assurance, and guaranteed recompense. The One who commands the charity is Himself the One who guarantees its multiplied return.\nThis “loan” includes every form of lawful charitable spending for the sake of Allāh, such as supporting the poor, aiding those in hardship, funding lawful community needs, defending the oppressed, strengthening religious institutions, and any expenditure that serves the objectives of faith and justice. It applies to both obligatory charity (such as zakāh in its wider meaning of purification) and voluntary charity (ṣadaqah).\nThe description “goodly” (ḥasan) signifies that the giving must be:\nFrom lawful earnings.\nGiven with sincere intention (ikhlāṣ).\nFree from showing off (riyāʾ).\nFree from reminders, harm, or humiliation to the recipient.\nGiven with a willing heart and trust in Allāh.\nThe multiplication of reward mentioned in the verse is not limited to a fixed number, but is described elsewhere in the Qur’an as increasing to seven hundredfold and beyond, according to Allāh’s wisdom. This guarantee of increase applies both in this world through unseen blessings and in the Hereafter through eternal recompense.\nThe verse also teaches that wealth does not increase or decrease purely by human effort or charity itself, for it is Allāh alone who withholds and expands provision according to His wisdom. Thus, the believer gives without fear of poverty and with certainty of divine replacement.\nIn essence, this verse transforms charity from a financial transaction into an act of devotion, trust, and covenant with Allāh—where the believer gives from what he loves, and Allāh gives in return what no eye has seen and no mind can fully comprehend.",
  2246: "Have you not considered the leaders of the Children of Israel after Moses, when they said to a prophet of theirs, ‘Appoint for us a king so that we may fight in the cause of Allāh? He said, ‘Would you perhaps refrain from fighting if it were prescribed for you?’ They said, ‘Why should we not fight in the cause of Allāh when we have been driven from our homes and our children?’ But when fighting was prescribed for them, they turned away—except for a few of them. And Allāh knows well the wrongdoers.",
  2247: "Their prophet said to them, ‘Indeed, Allāh has appointed Saul as king over you.’ They said, ‘How can he have kingship over us when we are more deserving of it than he, and he has not been given abundance of wealth?’ He said, ‘Indeed, Allāh has chosen him over you and has increased him greatly in knowledge and physical strength.’ And Allāh grants His kingship to whom He wills. And Allāh is All-Encompassing, All-Knowing.",
  2248: "And their prophet said to them, ‘Indeed, a sign of his kingship is that the Ark will come to you, in which is reassurance from your Lord and a remnant of what the family of Moses and the family of Aaron had left, carried by the angels. Indeed, in this is a sign for you, if you are believers.’",
  2249: "When Saul set out with the army, he said, ‘Indeed, Allāh will test you with a river: whoever drinks from it is not of me, and whoever does not taste it is of me—except one who takes a handful with his hand.’ But they drank from it, except for a few of them. Then, when he crossed it with those who believed with him, they said, ‘We have no strength today against Goliath and his forces.’ But those who were certain they would meet their Lord said, ‘How often has a small company overcome a large one by Allah’s permission!’ And Allāh is with those who are patient.",
  2250: "When they went forth to face Goliath and his forces, they said, ‘Our Lord, pour patience upon us, make our steps firm, and grant us victory over the disbelieving people.’",
  2251: "So they defeated them by Allah’s permission, and David killed Goliath. And Allāh gave him kingship and wisdom and taught him what He willed. And had it not been for Allah’s repelling some people by means of others, the earth would have been corrupted. But Allāh is bountiful to all the worlds.",
  2252: "These are the verses of Allāh which We recite to you in truth. And indeed, you are surely one of the messengers.",
  2253: "These messengers—We favored some of them over others: among them are those to whom Allāh spoke directly [101], and some He raised in rank. And to Jesus, son of Mary, We gave clear signs and strengthened him with the holy spirit. Had Allāh willed, those who came after them would not have fought one another after clear proofs had come to them; but they differed—some believed, while others disbelieved. And had Allāh willed, they would not have fought one another, but Allāh does whatever He intends.\\n\\nFootnotes: [101]. The statement that Allāh “spoke directly” refers to certain prophets who were granted the unique honor of direct communication without an intermediary, most notably Moses. Reports also mention that Adam and Muḥammad ﷺ were among those whom Allāh addressed directly, each in a manner befitting his status. Other prophets were raised in rank through distinct favors, such as revelation, miracles, or elevated status among their people. The verse highlights both the unity of the prophetic mission and the diversity of their ranks, all according to Allāh’s wisdom. It also affirms that differences and conflicts among later communities did not arise from a lack of guidance, but from human choice after clear proofs had been given. This underscores both divine sovereignty and human responsibility: Allāh has the power to prevent all differences, yet He allows people to choose, and He does whatever He wills in accordance with His perfect wisdom.",
  2254: "O believers, spend from what We have provided for you before a Day comes when there will be no trade, no friendship, and no intercession. And the disbelievers—they are the wrongdoers.",
  2255: "Allāh —there is no true god except Him, the Ever-Living, the Sustainer of all existence. Neither drowsiness nor sleep overtakes Him. To Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth. Who is it that can intercede with Him except by His permission? He knows what is before them and what is behind them, and they encompass nothing of His knowledge except what He wills. His Kursī [102] extends over the heavens and the earth, and their preservation does not tire Him. And He is the Most High, the Most Great.\\n\\nFootnotes: [102]. The term Kursī (كُرْسِيّ) is mentioned explicitly in the Qur’an and is affirmed as a real created entity whose true nature is known only to Allāh. It is distinct from the ʿArsh (Throne), which is mentioned separately in other verses. The Kursī is described here as extending over the heavens and the earth, indicating the vastness of Allāh’s dominion and the total encompassment of His authority over all creation.\nIt is reported in narrations that:\n“Between the first heaven and the one above it is a distance of five hundred years. Between each of the heavens is a distance of five hundred years. Between the seventh heaven and the Kursī is a distance of five hundred years. Between the Kursī and the water is a distance of five hundred years. The Throne is above the water. Allāh is above the Throne, and nothing whatsoever of our deeds is hidden from Him.”\nThese reports are cited by scholars to illustrate the immensity of the created realms and the utter insignificance of creation in comparison to the dominion of Allāh, while affirming His absolute transcendence beyond all created dimensions.\nIt is also reported that the Messenger of Allāh, Muhammad ﷺ, said:\n“The Kursī, in relation to the Throne, is like an iron ring thrown into a vast open land.”\nThis comparison emphasizes the overwhelming greatness of the Throne and, by extension, the incomprehensible magnitude of Allāh’s dominion over all that exists.\nIt is further narrated that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said: “In Sūrah al-Baqarah there is a verse which is the greatest of all the verses of the Qur’an. It is never recited in a house except that Satan leaves it: Āyat al-Kursī.”\nThis highlights the unique status of Āyat al-Kursī as the fullest single declaration of Allāh’s oneness, life, power, knowledge, sovereignty, and protection.\nEstablishing Allāh over His Throne is as mentioned in the Qur’an—without distortion (taḥrīf), denial (taʿṭīl), asking “how” (takyīf), or likening Him to created forms (tamthīl). It is therefore neither interpreted away as a mere metaphor by default, nor imagined in any physical manner resembling creation.\nThe statement “His Kursī extends over the heavens and the earth” signifies that nothing in existence escapes His authority, governance, or preservation. The following phrase “and their preservation does not tire Him” further affirms that sustaining the entire cosmos causes Allāh no fatigue, effort, or depletion—unlike created beings who are weakened by exertion.\nThis verse therefore unites absolute sovereignty, perfect knowledge, transcendent power, effortless preservation, and boundless dominion in a single supreme declaration of divine majesty.",
  2256: "There is no compulsion in religion [103]. The right path has become clearly distinct from error. So whoever rejects false gods and believes in Allāh has grasped the firmest handhold—one that will never break. And Allāh is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [103]. This verse establishes a fundamental Islamic principle: no one may be coerced into accepting faith. True belief must arise from conviction of the heart, not from force, pressure, or threat. Since the truth has been clearly distinguished from falsehood, coercion becomes both unnecessary and invalid. Faith that is imposed is not regarded as genuine faith in the sight of Allāh.\nThis principle does not mean that Islam is indifferent to guidance or moral responsibility. Rather, it places upon the believers the duty to convey the message of truth with clarity, wisdom, and good instruction, making known what is right and what is wrong. Invitation (daʿwah), explanation, counsel, and moral example are the lawful means of calling others to faith—not compulsion.\nScholars explain that this verse applies to the acceptance of faith itself: no person may be forced to embrace Islam. However, once a person willingly enters Islam, they are then bound by its moral, spiritual, and legal obligations. Thus, freedom of belief does not negate accountability after deliberate acceptance.\nThe verse was revealed in a context where some early Muslims wished to compel their children or dependents to enter Islam, and it came as a decisive prohibition of such coercion. This demonstrates that Islam’s growth was never legislated through forced conversion, but through conviction, evidence, and moral clarity.\nAt the same time, the absence of coercion does not imply the absence of truth. The verse affirms that right guidance is objectively distinct from error, and that human beings are responsible for the choices they make once that distinction is made clear. Whoever freely rejects falsehood and embraces faith is described as having grasped the strongest and most unbreakable spiritual bond.\nThis principle harmonizes perfectly with other Qur’anic commands: “Invite to the way of your Lord with wisdom and good instruction.”\n“You are only a reminder; you are not a controller over them.”\nThus, Islam rejects both forced conversion and forced disbelief. It calls to faith with proof, clarity, patience, and mercy, while leaving the final choice to the conscience of each individual under the ultimate judgment of Allāh.",
  2257: "Allāh is the Protector of those who believe. He brings them out of darknesses into the light. As for those who disbelieve, their protectors are false gods who take them from the light into darknesses [104]. They are the companions of the Fire; there they will remain forever.\\n\\nFootnotes: [104]. In Qur’anic usage, “darknesses” usually refers to paths of disbelief, sin, ignorance, and misguidance, while “light” refers to faith, truth, guidance, and divine revelation. The plural form darknesses indicates the many forms and varieties of misguidance, while light is singular, signifying that truth is one.\nAllāh is described as the source of all true light, meaning that all guidance, clarity, and salvation originate with Him. Satan and false guardians, by contrast, operate within darkness—leading people through deception, desire, arrogance, and false beliefs. Thus, those who reject faith are described as being taken from light into darkness, meaning that they are moved away from truth into layers of misguidance.\nSpiritual blindness in the Qur’an does not mean the absence of physical sight, but rather the inability of the heart to perceive truth. Such people may hear the words of the Qur’an and the teachings of the Prophet ﷺ outwardly, yet fail to comprehend their guidance inwardly due to pride, stubborn denial, or attachment to falsehood.\nThe Qur’an consistently presents revelation as light, and the Sunnah of the Prophet ﷺ as its living explanation. Through these two sources, Allāh rescues believers from moral confusion, spiritual darkness, and destructive paths, and guides them toward certainty, repentance, and upright conduct.\nThe statement that darkness never overcomes light reflects a central Qur’anic truth: falsehood is temporary and unstable, while truth is enduring and victorious by its very nature. Light leads to eternal life in the Hereafter, while persistent darkness leads to eternal loss. For this reason, believers are constantly commanded to seek repentance, renew faith, and place full trust in Allāh, who alone grants true illumination and protection.",
  2258: "Have you not considered the one [105] who disputed with Abraham about his Lord because Allāh had granted him kingship? When Abraham said, ‘My Lord is the One who gives life and causes death,’ he said, ‘I give life and cause death.’ Abraham said, ‘Indeed, Allāh brings the sun from the east, so bring it from the west.’ Then the disbeliever was confounded. And Allāh does not guide the wrongdoing people.\\n\\nFootnotes: [105]. The individual referred to in this verse is not named in the Qur’ān. However, many early commentators identify him with Nimrod (Nimrūd), a powerful ruler traditionally associated with ancient Mesopotamia and often linked in later Jewish and Christian sources to the figure mentioned in the Bible (Genesis 10:8–12) as a mighty king. According to Islamic exegetical tradition, he disputed with Abraham out of arrogance after being granted authority and worldly power.\nThe debate illustrates a contrast between divine reality and human pretension: when Abraham speaks of God’s power over life and death, the ruler responds with a superficial claim, which Abraham then exposes by presenting an undeniable sign—the rising of the sun from the east. Unable to respond, the ruler is left confounded. The verse emphasizes that worldly power can lead to arrogance when it is not accompanied by faith, and that true authority belongs to Allāh alone. It also demonstrates the method of prophetic argumentation: moving from abstract claims to clear, undeniable proofs that expose falsehood.",
  2259: "Or like the one who passed by a town lying in ruins, collapsed upon its roofs [106]. He said, ‘How will Allāh bring this back to life after its death?’ So Allāh caused him to die for a hundred years, then brought him back to life. He said, ‘How long have you remained?’ He said, ‘I have remained a day, or part of a day.’ He said, ‘No—you have remained a hundred years. Look at your food and your drink; they have not changed. And look at your donkey, so that We may make you a sign for the people. And look at the bones—how We assemble them and then clothe them with flesh.’ When it became clear to him, he said, ‘I know that Allāh has power over all things.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [106]. The individual mentioned in this verse is not named in the Qur’ān. Some early commentators identify him as ʿUzayr (Ezra), while others hold that he was an unnamed righteous man. The town is commonly understood to refer to a ruined settlement, often associated in exegetical tradition with Jerusalem after its destruction. The verse illustrates Allāh’s power to resurrect the dead. The man’s question was not one of denial, but of wonder at how life could be restored after complete decay. In response, Allāh caused him to die for a hundred years and then revived him, demonstrating resurrection both through his own experience and through the restoration of his donkey before his eyes. The unchanged state of his food and drink, contrasted with the decay and reassembly of the donkey’s bones, serves as a clear sign that Allāh has full power over time, life, and death. The account ultimately affirms certainty in resurrection and the completeness of divine power.",
  2260: "And when Abraham said, ‘My Lord, show me how You give life to the dead.’ He said, ‘Have you not believed?’ He said, ‘Yes, but so that my heart may be reassured.’ He said, ‘Then take four birds, cut them into pieces, then place a portion of them on every mountain, then call them—they will come to you swiftly. And know that Allāh is All-Mighty, All-Wise.’",
  2261: "The example of those who spend their wealth in the way of Allāh is like a seed that produces seven ears, in each ear a hundred grains. And Allāh multiplies for whom He wills. And Allāh is All-Encompassing, All-Knowing.",
  2262: "Those who spend their wealth in the way of Allāh, then do not follow what they have spent with reminders of it or injury—their reward is with their Lord. They will have no fear, nor will they grieve.",
  2263: "Kind words and forgiveness are better than charity followed by harm. And Allāh is Self-Sufficient, Most Forbearing.",
  2264: "O believers, do not nullify your charity with reminders of it or harm, like the one who spends his wealth to be seen by people while not believing in Allāh and the Last Day. His example is like that of a smooth rock upon which is dust—then a heavy rain falls upon it and leaves it bare. They have no power over anything they have earned. And Allāh does not guide the disbelieving people.",
  2265: "The example of those who spend their wealth seeking Allah’s pleasure and to strengthen their souls is like a garden on high ground. A heavy rain falls upon it, and it yields its fruit in double; and even if a heavy rain does not fall, then a gentle drizzle is enough. And Allāh is All-Seeing of what you do.",
  2266: "Would any of you like to have a garden of date palms and grapevines, with rivers flowing beneath it, in which he has all kinds of fruits—then old age overtakes him while he has weak offspring, and a whirlwind containing fire strikes it, leaving it burned [107]? Thus Allāh makes His signs clear to you, so that you may reflect.\\n\\nFootnotes: [107]. This verse presents a powerful parable illustrating the loss of one’s deeds after investing effort over a lifetime. The garden represents a person’s righteous actions—carefully cultivated, fruitful, and a source of hope—especially in times of greatest need, symbolized by old age and weak dependents. The sudden destruction of the garden by a fiery whirlwind signifies the nullification of those deeds through insincerity, hypocrisy, or harmful conduct such as reminding others of one’s charity or causing them hurt.\nThe imagery emphasizes the severity of losing the reward of good deeds at the moment they are most needed, particularly in the Hereafter. Just as the owner of the garden is left helpless and regretful, so too is the one whose actions are rendered void. The verse thus calls for sincerity, humility, and consistency in righteous deeds, free from pride and harm, so that they remain accepted by Allāh.\nA statement attributed to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (رضي الله عنه) interpreting this verse as referring to a wealthy man who obeys Allāh for a time and then falls into sin until his deeds are nullified. \nThe meaning of the verse is also supported by the Qur’an and authentic Prophetic teachings, which confirm that good deeds may be nullified through showing off, arrogance, injustice, and the loss of sincerity. The parable is intentionally designed to evoke maximum emotional and spiritual impact, forcing the listener to imagine the horror of losing all reward at the moment when it can no longer be recovered.\nThis verse teaches that what ultimately preserves good deeds is not merely outward charity or action, but lifelong sincerity, humility, repentance, and steadfast obedience until death.",
  2267: "O believers, spend in charity from the good things you have earned and from what We have produced for you from the earth. Do not choose the inferior of it to give, while you yourselves would not take it except with your eyes closed. And know that Allāh is Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy.",
  2268: " Satan threatens you with poverty and urges you to immorality, while Allāh promises you forgiveness from Him and grace. And Allāh is All-Encompassing, All-Knowing.",
  2269: " He grants wisdom to whom He wills, and whoever is granted wisdom has been given abundant good [108]. Yet none take heed except people of understanding.\\n\\nFootnotes: [108]. This verse shows that true wisdom (ḥikmah) is not merely intellectual knowledge, but a divine gift granted by Allāh. Wisdom teaches a person how to use knowledge correctly, when to speak, when to act, when to give, and when to withhold. Knowledge without wisdom can lead to arrogance, harm, or misjudgement, whereas wisdom places knowledge in its proper moral and spiritual context.\nAllāh alone is the true source of wisdom. Human knowledge is always limited, imperfect, and subject to error, while divine wisdom is complete, flawless, and perfectly suited to every situation. For this reason, the Qur’an repeatedly connects wisdom with guidance, righteousness, and sound judgment.\nWhoever is granted wisdom has therefore been given “abundant good”, because wisdom governs every other blessing: wealth, speech, actions, intentions, relationships, and decisions of the heart. Without wisdom, blessings may become trials; with wisdom, even hardship becomes a means of elevation.\nThis is why believers are commanded to constantly seek wisdom from Allāh, to act with understanding, and to recognize that true success lies not in how much one knows, but in how well one obeys Allāh with what one knows.",
  2270: "Whatever you spend or make of vows—indeed, Allāh knows it. And the wrongdoers will have no helpers.",
  2271: "If you give charity openly, it is good; but if you give it secretly and give it to the poor, it is better for you [109]. And He (Allāh) will expiate for you some of your sins. And Allāh is fully Aware of all that you do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [109]. Charity given openly is permissible and often beneficial for encouraging others, but charity given in secret is superior in sincerity, protects the giver from showing off (riyāʾ), and preserves the dignity of the poor. It shines more brightly in the sight of Allāh because it is free from reputation, praise-seeking, and social pressure.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“There are seven whom Allāh will shade with His shade on the Day when there will be no shade except His: the just ruler; a young man who grows up worshipping his Lord; a man whose heart is attached to the mosque; two men who love one another for the sake of Allāh and meet and part on that basis; a man who is invited by a woman of rank and beauty to commit adultery but says, ‘I fear Allāh’; a man who gives charity so secretly that his left hand does not know what his right hand gives; and a man who remembers Allāh in private and his eyes overflow with tears.” \nThis confirms that secret charity is among the highest forms of sincerity, placing a person among the elite servants honored with divine shade on the Day of Resurrection. While public charity benefits others, hidden charity purifies the heart itself, and this is why Allāh described it as “better” in this verse.",
  2272: "Not upon you is their guidance, but Allāh guides whom He wills. Whatever good you spend is for yourselves, and you do not spend except seeking the Face of Allāh. And whatever good you spend will be repaid to you in full, and you will not be wronged.",
  2273: "[Charity is] for the poor who are confined for the cause of Allāh—unable to move about in the land. An ignorant person may think them wealthy because of their restraint. You can recognize them by their signs. They do not beg of people [110]. \nAnd whatever good you spend, surely Allāh is Aware of it.\\n\\nFootnotes: [110]. This verse describes those who are bound in the Cause of Allāh, prevented from earning their livelihood, yet maintaining dignity and restraint. Though they possess little, people may mistake them for being wealthy because they refuse to beg. Their true worth is known to Allāh and recognized by the discerning eye.\nAbout the virtue of refraining from asking people, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said to his Companions: “Who will guarantee for me that he will never ask people for anything, and I will guarantee Paradise for him?”\nThis means that whoever commits himself to avoid asking people for worldly assistance—whether great or small—and instead relies upon Allāh alone, demonstrates true tawakkul (trust in Allāh). Such restraint reflects a heart firmly attached to divine provision, not human hands. For this reason, the Prophet ﷺ promised Paradise to the one who upholds this discipline, because reliance upon Allāh, patience, and dignity in poverty are among the highest stations of faith.",
  2274: "Those who spend their wealth by night and by day, secretly and openly, will have their reward with their Lord. No fear shall be upon them, nor will they grieve.",
  2275: "Those who consume interest will not stand [on the Day of Resurrection] except like one whom Satan has confounded by his touch. That is because they say, ‘Trade is just like interest,’ whereas Allāh has permitted trade [111] and forbidden interest [112]. So whoever, when the admonition comes from his Lord, refrains—he may keep what has passed, and his affair rests with Allāh. But whoever returns—they are the companions of the Fire; they will abide therein forever.\\n\\nFootnotes: [111]. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said:\n“The buyer and the seller have the right to keep or return the goods as long as they have not yet parted. If both speak the truth and make clear the defects and qualities of the goods, their transaction will be blessed. But if they conceal defects and lie, the blessing of their transaction will be erased.”\nThis establishes that Islamic commerce is founded upon honesty, transparency, and mutual consent, and that barakah (divine blessing) in wealth is directly tied to truthfulness in trade. Profit in Islam is not merely financial gain, but gain that is purified through integrity and justice.\n [112]. The Prophet ﷺ said: \n“Allāh has cursed the one who consumes ribā (usury), the one who pays it, the one who records it, and the two who witness it.”\nAnd he ﷺ also forbade and cursed:\n- The price of a dog.\n- The earnings of a prostitute.\n- The payment given to a soothsayer.\n- The one who tattoos and the one who is tattooed.\n- The maker of images.\nThese prohibitions demonstrate that ribā (usury) is grouped with the most morally destructive forms of unlawful wealth, and that economic corruption is treated as a spiritual crime in Islam, not merely a financial issue. While lawful trade is permitted and blessed, ribā (usury/interest) corrupts wealth, conscience, and society, and therefore earns a divine curse rather than divine increase.",
  2276: "Allāh destroys interest and gives increase to charities. And Allāh does not like every ungrateful sinner.",
  2277: "Indeed, those who believe and do righteous deeds, establish prayer, and give zakāh will have their reward with their Lord. No fear shall be upon them, nor will they grieve.",
  2278: "O believers, fear Allāh and give up what remains of interest due to you, if you are believers.",
  2279: "But if you do not, then be informed of a war from Allāh and His Messenger. But if you repent [113], you may retain your capital—you do not wrong [114], nor will you be wronged. [115]\\n\\nFootnotes: [113]. That is, to repent sincerely and abandon all remaining interest-based transactions completely, with immediate effect—terminating every outstanding claim to unlawful increase and resolving never to return to ribā (usury) again. True repentance here consists of:\nA. Ceasing the sin,\nB. Regretting past involvement, and\nC. Resolving never to return to it.\nOnly through this complete withdrawal does a person qualify for the divine concession mentioned in the verse.\n[114]. That is, do not wrong others by demanding more than your original capital sum after repentance. Any excess taken beyond the principal constitutes injustice (ẓulm) and a return to ribā (usury), which is strictly forbidden after the divine warning.\n [115]. That is, you will not be wronged by being made to lose any part of your rightful principal. After repentance, the lender is entitled to recover only the original capital—no increase, but also no unjust loss.",
  2280: "And if he [the debtor] is in hardship, then grant him time until ease. But if you give it as charity, that is better for you—if only you knew. [116]\\n\\nFootnotes: [116]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“Whoever would like Allāh to relieve him from the hardships of the Day of Resurrection, let him grant respite to one who is in difficulty, or forgive the debt entirely.”\nThis hadith establishes that showing mercy to a debtor in hardship is directly connected to receiving divine mercy on the Day of Judgment. Granting delay is an act of justice; relinquishing the debt altogether is an act of exceptional virtue. Both are means of earning Allāh’s protection, relief, and forgiveness in the greatest hour of human need.",
  2281: "And fear a Day when you will be returned to Allāh; then every soul will be paid in full for what it has earned, and they will not be wronged.",
  2282: " O believers, when you contract a debt for a fixed term, write it down. Let a scribe write it between you with fairness. The scribe must not refuse to write as Allāh has taught him, so let him write. Let the debtor dictate, and let him fear Allāh, his Lord, and not omit anything from it. But if the debtor is weak, inexperienced, or unable to dictate himself, then let his guardian dictate with fairness. And call two witnesses from among your men; but if two men are not available, then a man and two women from among those acceptable to you as witnesses—so that if one of the two women errs, the other may remind her.\nThe witnesses must not refuse when they are called. Do not be weary of writing it down—whether small or large—together with its due term. That is more just in the sight of Allāh, more reliable as evidence, and more likely to prevent doubt.\nBut if it is an immediate transaction which you conduct among yourselves, then there is no blame on you in not writing it down. Still, take witnesses when you conclude a contract. Let neither scribe nor witness be harmed. If you do so, it is surely wrongdoing on your part. And fear Allāh; and Allāh teaches you. And Allāh is Knowing of all things. [117]\\n\\nFootnotes: [117]. This verse is known as Āyat al-Dayn (the Verse of Debt), and it is the longest verse in the Qur’an. Its extraordinary length reflects the central importance Islam places on justice, clarity, documentation, and the protection of rights in financial dealings. Through this verse, Allāh establishes the foundational legal principles for contracts, testimony, responsibility, and transparency in all matters of debt and trade.",
  2283: "And if you are on a journey and cannot find a scribe, then a pledge in hand. But if one of you entrusts another, then let the one who is entrusted fulfill his trust, and let him fear Allāh, his Lord. And do not conceal testimony. Whoever conceals it—surely his heart is sinful. And Allāh is Knowing of what you do.",
  2284: "To Allāh belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth. Whether you reveal what is within yourselves or conceal it, Allāh will call you to account for it [118]. Then He forgives whom He wills and punishes whom He wills. And Allāh is All-Powerful over all things.\\n\\nFootnotes: [118]. Although this verse states that Allāh will call people to account for what they reveal or conceal within themselves, Allāh showed mercy to the believing community by not holding them accountable for inner thoughts, whispers, or involuntary feelings so long as they do not speak them or act upon them.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“Allāh has pardoned my followers for what crosses their minds, so long as they do not speak of it or act upon it.”\nThus, believers are not taken to account for inner passing thoughts, or involuntary suggestions, but only for deliberate words and intentional actions.",
  2285: "The Messenger has believed in what was revealed to him from his Lord, and so have the believers. All of them believe in Allāh, His angels, His Scriptures, and His messengers, [saying], ‘We make no distinction between any of His messengers.’ And they say, ‘We hear and we obey. We seek Your forgiveness, our Lord, and to You is the final return.’",
  2286: "Allāh does not burden any soul beyond its capacity. It will have what it has earned, and against it what it has incurred.\nOur Lord, do not hold us accountable if we forget or err. Our Lord, do not place upon us a burden like that which You placed on those before us [119]. Our Lord, do not burden us with what we have no strength to bear. Pardon us, forgive us, and have mercy on us. You are our Protector, so help us against the disbelieving people. [120]\\n\\nFootnotes: [119]. This refers to the heavy legal and ritual burdens imposed upon earlier communities, particularly the Children of Israel under the Law of Moses, which included severe penalties, complex ritual obligations, and strict legal constraints placed as consequences for repeated disobedience. It also alludes to forms of excessive ascetic discipline that arose among the followers of Jesus. In contrast, Allāh honored this nation with a religion founded upon ease, balance, and mercy, as the Prophet ﷺ said: “This religion is ease.” Thus the believers ask not to be burdened as previous nations were burdened.\n[120]. Allāh Almighty concludes this Surah by directing His servants how to supplicate Him, just as He taught them in Surah al-Fātihah how to praise Him and ask for guidance. \nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“Whoever recites the last two verses of Sūrat al-Baqarah at night, they will suffice him.”  This means they are sufficient as a protection from evil, a safeguard against Satan, and a source of divine care and blessing. These two verses affirm faith, obedience, repentance, reliance upon Allāh, and the plea for divine support—making them among the most powerful daily remembrances for the believer.",
  3001: "Alif Lām Mīm [1].\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2: Verse 1.",
  3002: "Allāh —there is no god except Him, the Ever-Living, the All-Sustaining.",
  3003: "He sends down to you, [O Prophet], the Book in truth, confirming what came before it; and He sent down the Torah and the Gospel. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The difference between ‘nazzala’ (has been sending down) which is mentioned only with regards to the Qur’an, which signifies that the action took place in portions over a long period of time. Whereas ‘anzala’ (sent down) which is said with regards to the Torah and the Gospel, which happened once and as a whole. \nThe Injīl (commonly rendered in English as the Gospel or Evangel) is the Arabic name for the divine Scripture revealed by Allāh to ʿĪsā (Jesus), the son of Mary.\nThe Injīl is not to be identified with the present-day Biblical “New Testament.” Rather, it is understood—on the testimony of the Qur’an—to have been a complete divine revelation granted directly to Jesus himself, just as the Torah was revealed to Moses and the Psalms to David.\nHowever, the original Injīl was either lost or altered over time, and what remains today in the Bible is a mixture of authentic remnants of revelation and human additions, interpretations, and alterations. The same, to a great extent, applies to the Torah. For this reason, not every word in today’s Bible is the unaltered Word of God, though the Qur’an confirms that certain passages within these scriptures still preserve genuine traces of divine truth.\nTo claim that the entire Bible in its present form is wholly the Word of God is therefore  a serious error concerning the nature of divine revelation and its preservation.\nThe Qur’an alone is affirmed as the only revealed Book whose text has been perfectly preserved, free from distortion, addition, or loss. Every word of the Qur’an is the unaltered, protected Word of God, exactly as it was revealed to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ.",
  3004: "Before, as guidance for the people; and He sent down the Criterion. Indeed, those who disbelieve in the signs of Allāh will have a severe punishment, and Allāh is All-Mighty, Possessor of Retribution.",
  3005: " Indeed, nothing is hidden from Allāh on earth or in heaven.",
  3006: "It is He who shapes you in the wombs as He wills. There is no god except Him, the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.",
  3007: "It is He who has sent down to you the Book. In it are clear and decisive verses—they are the foundation of the Book—and others that are ambiguous. As for those with deviation in their hearts, they follow the ambiguous parts, seeking discord and seeking its interpretation. But no one knows its true meaning except Allāh. And those firmly grounded in knowledge say, ‘We believe in it; all of it is from our Lord.’ And none take heed except people of understanding.",
  3008: "Our Lord, do not let our hearts deviate after You have guided us, and grant us mercy from Yourself. Indeed, You are the Ever-Bestower.",
  3009: "Our Lord, You will surely gather all people for a Day about which there is no doubt. Indeed, Allāh never fails His promise.",
  3010: "Indeed, those who disbelieve—neither their wealth nor their children will avail them in the least against Allāh; they are the fuel of the Fire.",
  3011: "Like the way of the people of Pharaoh and those before them: they denied Our signs, so Allāh seized them for their sins. And Allāh is severe in punishment.",
  3012: "Say to those who disbelieve: ‘You will soon be overcome and gathered into Hell. What an evil resting place!’",
  3013: "There was certainly a sign for you in the two armies that met: one fighting in the cause of Allāh and the other disbelievers. They saw them with their own eyes as twice their number [3]. But Allāh supports with His help whom He wills. Indeed, in this is a lesson for those who have insight.\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. The believers saw the disbelievers to be double their own number preceding the battle of Badr, while, in fact, they were three times their number.",
  3014: "Beautified for people is the love of what they desire: women, children, hoarded treasures of gold and silver, fine horses, livestock, and cultivated lands. That is the enjoyment of the life of this world [4], but with Allāh is the best place of return.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. Righteousness accompanied by contentment is indeed great gain, for a person enters this world with nothing and leaves it with nothing. When one is granted daily sustenance, clothing, shelter, health, and peace, these are sufficient grounds for gratitude and contentment.\nThose who obsessively seek wealth by any means expose themselves to temptation, moral traps, and destructive desires, which ultimately plunge people into ruin and spiritual loss. The uncontrolled love of money becomes a gateway to many forms of corruption, and through this craving many have strayed from faith and inflicted deep suffering upon themselves.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ expressed this principle with perfect clarity, saying:\n“Whoever among you wakes up secure in his home, healthy in his body, and has his provision for the day, it is as if the whole world has been gathered for him.\"\nThis hadith affirms the same universal moral truth: that true wealth lies not in accumulation, but in security, health, daily provision, and inner contentment. When these are present, a person already possesses the essence of worldly well-being.",
  3015: "Say, ‘Shall I inform you of what is better than that [5]? For those who are mindful of Allāh are gardens with their Lord beneath which rivers flow, abiding therein forever, with purified spouses and pleasure from Allāh. Allāh is All-Seeing of His servants,’\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. It is part of human nature to be drawn to worldly attractions and desires. True faith, however, requires resisting these temptations, placing the pursuit of Allāh’s Pleasure above all else, and disciplining the soul through sincere devotion. This path is realized by following the guidance and example of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ—the final Messenger of God—whose life demonstrates how to transcend worldly allure in favor of eternal reward.",
  3016: "those who say, ‘Our Lord, indeed we have believed, so forgive us our sins and protect us from the punishment of the Fire.’",
  3017: "Those who are patient, truthful, devoutly obedient, charitable, and who seek forgiveness before dawn.",
  3018: "Allāh bears witness that there is no god except Him—and so do the angels and those of knowledge—He upholds justice. There is no god except Him, the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.",
  3019: "Indeed, the religion with Allāh is Islam. Those who were given the Scripture did not differ except after knowledge had come to them—out of envy among themselves. And whoever rejects the signs of Allāh, then Allāh is swift in reckoning.\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. The word Islām means “submission” and “surrender” to the will of God. It describes the divinely ordained way of life taught by all the prophets—from Adam to the final Messenger, Muhammad ﷺ. The term carries two essential aspects: yielding oneself to God and devoting that surrender to Him alone, without partners. A Muslim is therefore one who submits wholeheartedly to God in belief, worship, and obedience.",
  3020: "Then if they dispute with you, say, ‘I have submitted myself to Allāh, and so have those who follow me.’ And say to those who were given the Scripture and to the unlettered, ‘Have you submitted?’ If they submit, they are rightly guided; but if they turn away, then your duty is only to convey the message. And Allāh is All-Seeing of His servants.",
  3021: "Indeed, those who disbelieve in the signs of Allāh, kill the prophets unjustly [7], and kill those among the people who enjoin justice—give them tidings of a painful punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. Jesus (peace be upon him) said:\n“O Jerusalem, Jerusalem, you who kill the prophets and stone those sent to you…”\n(Gospel of Matthew 23:37; see also Matthew 23:34–35; Luke 11:51)\nThis statement condemns the repeated rejection and killing of God’s messengers by some of the people of Jerusalem, a theme that is also affirmed in the Qur’an (e.g., 2:61, 3:181). It highlights the tragic historical pattern of opposing divine guidance despite clear signs and repeated warnings.",
  3022: "They are the ones whose deeds have become worthless in this world and the Hereafter, and they will have no helpers.",
  3023: "Have you not seen those who were given a portion of the Scripture? They are called to the Book of Allāh so that it may judge between them, yet a group of them turns away in aversion. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. Referring to the Jews who refused to implement the rulings of Allāh in the Torah or to acknowledge the Prophet Muhammad (ﷺ), whose coming was mentioned in the Bible.",
  3024: "That is because they say, “The Fire will never touch us except for a limited number of days [9].” And they have been deceived in their religion by what they used to invent.\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. This refers to claims—made falsely by some—that if they are punished in the Fire, it will only be for a few days, after which they will surely enter Paradise. The Qur’an rejects this unfounded presumption, clarifying that salvation cannot be attained through wishful thinking but only through sincere faith, obedience, and God’s mercy.",
  3025: "So how will it be when We gather them for a Day in which there is no doubt, and every soul is repaid in full for what it earned, and they will not be wronged?",
  3026: "Say, ‘O Allāh, Owner of Sovereignty, You grant sovereignty to whom You will and take it away from whom You will; You honor whom You will and humble whom You will. In Your hand is all good. Indeed, You are Most Capable of all things.’",
  3027: " You cause the night to enter into the day and the day to enter into the night. You bring the living out of the dead and the dead out of the living. And You give provision to whom You will without measure.",
  3028: "Believers should not take disbelievers as allies instead of believers. Whoever does so has nothing to do with Allāh—unless it is to protect themselves from their harm [10]. And Allāh warns you of Himself, and to Allāh is the final return.\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. This refers to the permission granted—under compulsion or grave danger—to use cautious or conciliatory speech toward disbelievers while the heart remains firm and unwavering in faith. Such outward concession does not invalidate belief so long as the inner conviction, loyalty to Allāh, and rejection of disbelief remain intact. This principle reflects Allāh’s mercy in cases of coercion and severe threat.",
  3029: "Say, ‘Whether you conceal what is in your chests or reveal it—Allāh knows it. And He knows whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth. And Allāh has full power over all things.’",
  3030: "On the Day when every soul will find present whatever good it has done, and whatever evil it has done, it will wish that there were a great distance between itself and that [evil]. Allāh warns you of Himself. Allāh is Kind to His servants.",
  3031: "Say, [O Prophet], ‘If you truly love Allāh [11], then follow me; Allāh will love you and forgive your sins. Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. Genuine love for Allāh is reflected in a believer’s character and conduct. Such a person strives to be patient, loving, kind, and merciful; he does not nurture envy or boastfulness, nor is he arrogant or rude. He does not knowingly persist in falsehood or wrongdoing. Rather, he seeks to follow the Way of Allāh (Islām) with sincerity, taking as his living example the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, and modeling his life upon the Prophet’s guidance in belief, worship, and behavior.",
  3032: "Say, \"Obey Allāh and the Messenger. But if they turn away, then indeed Allāh does not like the disbelievers.",
  3033: "Indeed, Allāh chose Ādam, Noah, the family of Abraham and the family of Imran above all people. [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. Allāh chose them above all people of their respective times:\nĀdam — created directly by Allāh and honored when the angels were commanded to bow before him.\nNūḥ (Noah) — the first messenger sent to humanity after the spread of corruption.\nIbrāhīm (Abraham) — the beloved of Allāh, whose lineage included many prophets, among them Muhammad ﷺ, the final Messenger.\nThe family of ʿImrān — including ʿImrān (Amram) and his wife, their daughter Maryam (Mary), and her son ʿĪsā (Jesus), one of the greatest prophets sent to the Children of Israel.\nThe Bible does not name Mary’s father. The name Joachim comes from early Christian tradition—particularly the Protoevangelium of James—and is not part of the canonical scriptures.\nTheir selection reflects divine favor grounded in faith, patience, sacrifice, and unwavering devotion to Allāh, making them enduring examples for humanity across generations.",
  3034: "They were descendants of one another. Allāh is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.",
  3035: "[Remember] when the wife of 'Imrān said, \"My Lord, Indeed, I have vowed  to dedicate what is in my womb entirely to Your service. So accept it from me. Indeed, You are the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.\"",
  3036: "But when she delivered her, she said: \"My Lord, I have delivered a female - and Allāh knew best what she had delivered - and the male is not like the female, and I have named her Maryam, and I commend her and her offspring into Your Protection from the accursed Satan. [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“Every child of Adam is touched by Satan at the time of his birth, and he cries because of that touch, except Mary and her son [Jesus].”\nThis hadith affirms the unique divine protection granted to Maryam (Mary) and her son ʿĪsā (Jesus) from Satan at birth, in response to the supplication of Mary’s mother (Āl ʿImrān 3:36). It highlights their special status among creation while maintaining the belief that all prophets and the righteous are protected by Allāh’s mercy and care.",
  3037: " So her Lord graciously accepted her with a good acceptance, and caused her to grow up a goodly growing, and entrusted her to the care of Zachariah. Whenever Zachariah entered the sanctuary to see her, he found with her fresh provision [14]. He asked: \"O Maryam, from where does this come to you?\" She said: \"It is from Allāh.\" Indeed Allāh provides for whom He wills without measure.\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. That is, fresh and wholesome fruits that were not of the season in which they normally grow. This points to a miraculous provision granted to Maryam (Mary), indicating that her sustenance came directly from Allāh, beyond ordinary natural means.",
  3038: "There, Zachariah prayed to his Lord, saying: \"My Lord, grant me from Yourself a good offspring. Indeed, You are the Hearer of prayer.\"",
  3039: "So the angels called out to him while he was standing in prayer in the sanctuary: \"That Allāh gives you glad tidings of Yahya, confirming the Word from Allāh [15], honorable, chaste, and a Prophet from among the righteous.\"\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. Yaḥyā is the Qur’anic name of the prophet known in the Christian tradition as John the Baptist. The phrase “the Word of Allāh” refers to ʿĪsā (Jesus), who was conceived by Maryam (Mary) through Allāh’s command “Be” —and he came into existence. This does not mean that Jesus is divine or part of Allāh’s essence; rather, it affirms that his creation occurred directly by divine command, without a human father, just as Adam was created by Allāh’s command alone. The description “chaste” denotes exceptional moral restraint and purity, not celibacy as a religious ideal.",
  3040: "He [16] said, \"My Lord, how can I have a boy when old age has already come upon me, and my wife is barren?\" [The angel] said: \"Thus Allāh does what He wills.\"\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. Zachariah did not direct his request to the angel, nor did he seek anything from him. Being fully aware that Allāh alone is near, responsive, and the One who answers prayers, he turned his supplication directly to Allāh rather than to an intermediary. This reflects the prophetic understanding that angels convey Allāh’s messages but do not possess independent power to grant requests or fulfill prayers.",
  3041: "He said, ‘My Lord, make for me a sign.’ Allāh said, ‘Your sign is that you will not speak to people for three days, except by gestures. And remember Allāh, your Lord, often, and glorify Him in the evening and the morning.’",
  3042: "And [remember] when the angels said, \"O Maryam, indeed, Allāh has chosen you, purified you, and chosen you above all the women of the worlds.\"",
  3043: "O Maryam, be devoutly obedient to Allāh, your Lord, prostrate yourself to Him, and bow down with those who bow down in prayer.",
  3044: "That is from the news of the unseen which We reveal to you. You were not with them when they cast their pens as to which of them should take charge of Maryam, nor were you with them when they were disputing.",
  3045: "When the angels said, “O Maryam, indeed Allāh gives you glad tidings of a Word from Him [17]—his name will be the Messiah, Jesus, son of Maryam—honored in this world and the Hereafter, and among those drawn near to Allāh.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. ʿĪsā (Jesus) was not conceived through any human act of procreation; rather, like Adam, he was brought into existence solely by Allāh’s commanding Word: “Be” —and he came into being. For this reason, he is described as “the Word of Allāh.” This designation affirms the mode of his miraculous creation, not any share in divinity, just as Adam was created by Allāh’s command without parents.",
  3046: "He will speak to the people in the cradle and in adulthood, and he will be one of the righteous.",
  3047: "She said: \"My Lord, how shall I have a son, when no mortal has ever touched me?\" The angel said: \"Thus it is: Allāh creates whatever He wills. When He decrees a matter, He only says to it, ‘Be,’ and it is.",
  3048: "And He will teach him writing, wisdom, the Torah and the Gospel.",
  3049: "And He will send him as a messenger to the Children of Israel [18], [saying], ‘I have come to you with a sign from your Lord: I fashion for you out of clay the form of a bird, then I breathe into it, and it becomes a bird by Allāh’s permission. I heal the blind and the leper, and I bring the dead to life by Allāh’s permission. I also inform you of what you eat and what you store in your homes. Surely in this is a sign for you, if you are believers.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. Every prophet sent to the Children of Israel was commissioned specifically to his own people—the Israelites. None of them, including ʿĪsā (Jesus, peace be upon him), was sent with a universal mission to all nations. Rather, Jesus was an Israelite prophet sent to guide the lost sheep of the House of Israel, as affirmed in both the Qur’an and the Bible.\nIn contrast, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ was sent as a universal messenger—not to one people or nation alone, but to all of humanity, regardless of race, color, tribe, or lineage. His mission extends to every nation and people, and he was sent as a mercy to all the worlds, fulfilling the divine promise of comprehensive and universal guidance.",
  3050: "And I have come confirming what came before me of the Torah, and to make lawful for you some of what was forbidden to you [19]. And I have come to you with a sign from your Lord, so fear Allāh and obey me.\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. This refers to the specific prohibitions placed upon the Children of Israel as a consequence of their disobedience—such as the prohibition of working on the Sabbath, the ban on certain animal fats (including the fat of oxen and sheep), and restrictions regarding some types of fish and birds, such as those lacking fins or claws. These rulings were part of the Mosaic Law and were later lifted under the final revelation brought by the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ.",
  3051: "Indeed, Allāh is my Lord and your Lord [and my God and your God], so worship Him alone; this is the straight Path.",
  3052: "But when ʿĪsā (Jesus) sensed their persistence in disbelief, he said, ‘Who are my supporters in the cause of Allāh?’ The disciples said, ‘We are the supporters of Allāh. We believe in Allāh, so bear witness that we are Muslims [submitters to God].",
  3053: "“Our Lord, we believe in what You have revealed and follow the Messenger, so count us among the witnesses.”",
  3054: "They plotted, and Allāh planned—and Allāh is the best of planners.",
  3055: "[Remember] when Allāh said, ‘O ʿĪsā (Jesus), I will take you and raise you to Myself, and deliver you from those who disbelieve. I will place those who follow you above those who disbelieve until the Day of Resurrection. Then to Me is your return, and I will judge between you concerning that over which you used to differ.\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. The phrase “I will take you to Myself” translates mutawaffīka, which in Qurʾānic usage denotes being taken fully by God, without specifying the exact manner. Exegetes differed as to whether this implies sleep, or divine taking prior to bodily ascension; the wording here preserves the ambiguity of the Arabic. “Deliver you from those who disbelieve” reflects muṭahhiruka, literally “purify you,” understood here as separation and protection from hostile unbelievers rather than moral purification. “Those who follow you” refers to Jesus’s true followers in submission to God, ultimately including those who follow him in Islam, and their being made “above” others is commonly interpreted as dominance in proof, authority, or historical ascendancy, not intrinsic superiority. God’s final judgment is deferred until the Day of Resurrection, when disputed matters will be decisively settled.",
  3056: "As for those who disbelieve, I will punish them severely in this world and the Hereafter, and they will have none to help them.",
  3057: "But as for those who believe and do righteous deeds, He will give them their rewards in full. And Allāh does not like the wrongdoers.",
  3058: "That is what We recite to you of the Verses and the Wise Reminder [Qur’an].",
  3059: "The likeness of Jesus before Allāh is like that of Ādam: He created him from dust; then He said to him, ‘Be,’ and he was.",
  3060: "This is the truth from your Lord, so do not be of the doubters. [21]\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. Doubt is an experience common to all human beings. Even those who believe in Allāh may struggle with it in different ways — though not in their faith itself, but in its details or in moments of weakness. Some are greatly hindered by ordinary doubts; others see them as challenges to overcome; and some even regard them as opportunities for spiritual growth.\nThe Qur’an and Sunnah speak about the causes of doubt and provide examples of people who struggled with it. A useful definition of doubt is: “to lack confidence or to consider something unlikely.”\nThe Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:\n“Satan comes to one of you and says, ‘Who created such-and-such?’ until he says, ‘Who created your Lord?’ If he reaches that stage, one should seek refuge with Allāh and give up such thoughts.”\nWhen Satan tempted Adam and Eve, Allāh had already given them a clear command regarding the tree and had warned them of the consequence of disobedience. Satan planted doubt in their minds when he asked: “Did Allāh really say, ‘You shall not eat from any tree in the garden’?”\nHis aim was to make them lose confidence in Allāh’s command. When they affirmed what Allāh had said — including the warning — Satan followed up with denial and deception. (See Qur’an 2:35–36; 7:20–22; 20:120–121).\nThus, doubt is one of Satan’s tools to make us lose confidence in Allāh’s Word and to consider His judgment unlikely or unreasonable. Yet we cannot place all the blame on Satan. The Qur’an makes it clear that we are also responsible for our own doubts. For example, when Prophet Zakariyyā (Zechariah) was visited by the angel and told that he would be granted a son — Yaḥyā (John) — he expressed amazement, not disbelief. He marveled at Allāh’s power, though he questioned how this could happen since he and his wife were advanced in age. In humility, he asked for a sign, and in response, the angel told him that he would be unable to speak for a period of time. (Qur’an 3:38–41; 19:2–10).\nProphet Zakariyyā’s words expressed wonder, not doubt — an acknowledgment of his human limitations alongside his faith in the limitless power of Allāh. His humility and awe paved the way for divine reassurance and the fulfillment of his prayer.\nSimilarly, others have marveled at Allāh’s power to overcome what seems impossible, such as in the story of ʿUzayr (Ezra). When he wondered how Allāh could bring life to a ruined city, Allāh caused him to die for a hundred years and then resurrected him — showing him His absolute power and control over all things.\n(See Qur’an 2:259).\nWhenever human reasoning overshadows trust in Allāh, sinful doubt arises.\nTrue faith (īmān) means trusting Allāh completely, beyond any doubt — even when His plan appears to go against human logic or experience.",
  3061: "Whoever disputes with you concerning him [Jesus] after the knowledge has come to you [22], then say, ‘Come, let us call our sons and your sons, our women and your women, and ourselves and yourselves, then let us invoke the curse of Allāh upon the liars.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. That is, ʿĪsā (Jesus) is a servant of God and one of His messengers, possessing no share whatsoever in Divinity. He is neither the son of God nor a part of God, but rather a created human being honored with prophethood and revelation. His miraculous birth and exalted status do not alter his essential nature as a servant who worshipped Allāh and called others to worship Him alone.",
  3062: "Indeed, this is the true account [about Jesus]. There is no god except Allāh. And indeed, Allāh—He alone—is the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.",
  3063: "But if they turn away, then indeed Allāh is All-Knowing of those who spread corruption.",
  3064: "Say, ‘O People of the Scripture [Jews and Christians], come to a common word between us and you: that we worship none but Allāh, that we associate no partners with Him, and that we do not take one another as lords besides Allāh. But if they turn away, then say, ‘Bear witness that we are Muslims [submitters to God].’’",
  3065: "O People of the Scripture, why do you dispute about Abraham when the Torah and the Gospel were not revealed until after him? Do you not then understand?",
  3066: "Here you are—disputing about what you have some knowledge of; but why do you dispute about what you have no knowledge of? Allāh knows, and you do not know.",
  3067: "Abraham was neither a Jew nor a Christian, but he was upright in faith, a Muslim, and he was not of the polytheists.",
  3068: "Indeed, the most worthy of Abraham among the people are those who follow him, this Prophet [i.e., Muhammad], and those who believe. And Allāh is the Guardian of the believers.",
  3069: "A group among the People of the Scripture [Jews and Christians] wish to mislead you, but they only mislead themselves—though they do not perceive it.",
  3070: "O People of the Scripture, why do you disbelieve in the signs of Allāh while you bear witness?",
  3071: "O people of the Scripture [Jews and Christians], why do you mix the Truth with falsehood  and conceal the truth [23] knowingly?\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. This refers to the deliberate concealment or obscuring of passages in the earlier scriptures that describe the coming of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, including his characteristics and signs by which he could be recognized. The Qur’an asserts that knowledgeable people among the Jews and Christians were aware of these descriptions yet hid, altered, or withheld them from their communities.\nNumerous early Muslim and Jewish/Christian sources affirm that the prophets foretold the coming of a final messenger, and certain qualities of Muhammad ﷺ can still be traced in existing Biblical texts, despite textual alterations over time.",
  3072: "A group among the People of the Scripture said, ‘Believe in what has been sent down to the believers at the beginning of the day and reject it at its end, so that they might turn back [from their faith].’",
  3073: "And do not believe anyone except those who follow your religion.” Say, “Indeed, the guidance is the guidance of Allāh.” [They say this] lest anyone be given what you have been given or something like it, or dispute with you before your Lord. Say, “Indeed, all bounty is in the Hand of Allāh; He grants it to whom He wills. And Allāh is All-Encompassing, All-Knowing.",
  3074: "He chooses for His mercy [24] whom He wills, and Allāh is the Possessor of immense bounty.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. That is, the Prophethood of Muhammad ﷺ and the divine message he brought. Allāh grants His mercy—guidance, faith, and divine favor—exclusively by His will, not by lineage, status, or communal affiliation.\nAllāh bestows His grace upon all of humankind, and among the greatest and most noble blessings He has conferred is sending the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ and revealing the Qur’an as guidance, thereby showing humanity the Straight Path.\nFrom His mercy also are the beauty and wonders of creation that people witness each day, the timely arrival of what is needed, and the innate conscience (fiṭrah) by which human beings recognize right from wrong. All of these are manifestations of Allāh’s continuous provision, guidance,  mercy and care.",
  3075: "Among the People of the Scripture [Jews and Christians] are those who, if you entrust them with a heap of gold, will return it to you; and among them are those who, if you entrust them with a single gold coin, will not return it to you unless you keep standing over them. That is because they say, ‘There is no blame upon us concerning the unlearned [25],’ and they tell lies about Allāh while they know it.\\n\\nFootnotes: [25]. “The unlearned” renders the Arabic al-ummiyyīn, a term which in the Qur'an commonly refers to those outside the People of the Scripture, particularly non-Jews. In this verse, it reflects a claim attributed to some among them that they bore no moral or legal responsibility in their dealings with such people. Scholars explain that this attitude involved knowingly justifying dishonesty by appealing to a perceived exemption from accountability. The verse explicitly condemns this claim, stating that it amounts to attributing falsehood to Allāh while being fully aware of its falsity.",
  3076: "Indeed, whoever fulfills his covenant and fears Allāh—then surely Allāh loves the righteous. [26]\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. “Fulfills his covenant” encompassing faithfulness to covenants with Allāh as well as honesty and reliability in human agreements. “Fears Allāh ” renders meaning to be consciously mindful of Allāh and to guard oneself against wrongdoing. This verse affirms divine love not on the basis of lineage or religious claim, but on moral integrity, covenantal faithfulness, and God-conscious conduct;  (“the righteous”) are those who embody these qualities consistently. We all find it comforting to consider the reality that there is Allāh, the Creator, the author of life, who is, in his very nature, loving and who loves the righteous.\nThe Qur’an also tells us (over and over) that Allāh is just. Allāh is the judge of the world. And a just judge is a righteous one. A righteous judge does not let the guilty go unpunished. Allāh loves righteousness and justice. He will repay each one according to his works: eternal life to those who by persistence in doing good seek glory, honor and immortality; but wrath and anger to those who are self-seeking and disobey the truth while obeying unrighteousness.",
  3077: "Those who trade the covenant of Allāh and their oaths for a small gain will have no share in the Hereafter. Allāh will neither speak to them nor look at them on the Day of Resurrection, nor will He purify them—and for them is a painful punishment.",
  3078: "And indeed, among them is a group who twist their tongues with the Scripture as they recite it, so that you may think it is from the Scripture, while it is not from the Scripture. They say, ‘It is from Allāh,’ though it is not from Allāh; and they knowingly tell lies about Allāh.",
  3079: "It is not for a human being to whom Allāh has given the Scripture, wisdom, and prophethood to say to the people, ‘Be my worshippers instead of Allāh.’ Rather, he would say, ‘Be devoted servants of your Lord, because you teach the Scripture and study it.’",
  3080: "Nor would he command you to take the angels [27] and the prophets [28] as lords. Would he command you to disbelieve after you have submitted?\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]. Such as the holy spirit—whom some have mistakenly elevated to a divine or semi-divine status. The Qur’an makes it clear that all angels are servants of Allāh: created beings who possess no share in divinity and are never to be worshipped.\n[28]. Such as ʿĪsā (Jesus), peace be upon him, whom some took as a lord besides Allāh. The Qur’an repeatedly affirms that Jesus was a servant of God and one of His greatest messengers, not divine, and that worship belongs to Allāh alone.",
  3081: "And [remember] when Allāh took the covenant of the prophets, [saying], “Whatever I have given you of the Scripture and wisdom—then when there comes to you a messenger confirming what is with you, you must believe in him and support him.” He said, “Do you affirm and accept this binding commitment?” They said, “We affirm.” He said, “Then bear witness, and I am with you among the witnesses.” [29]\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. It is possible that this divine covenant was taken when the offspring of Adam were brought forth from his back, as mentioned in the Qur’an (7:172), when Allāh caused all humanity to testify to His Lordship. It is also understood that this covenant was reaffirmed with each prophet at the time of his mission, obligating them to believe in and support the final Messenger when he would appear. \nThe “messenger” referred to here is the Prophet Muḥammad (ﷺ).",
  3082: "But whoever turns away after this—it is they who are the rebellious [against God].",
  3083: "Do they seek other than the religion of God [Islam], while all that is in the heavens and the earth submits to Him, willingly or unwillingly, and to Him they will be returned [for judgment]?",
  3084: "Say, “We believe in Allāh and in what has been sent down to us, and in what was sent down to Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob, and the tribes, and in what was given to Moses, Jesus, and the Prophets from their Lord; we make no distinction between any of them, and to Him alone we submit.”",
  3085: "Whoever seeks a religion other than Islam [30], it will never be accepted from him, and in the Hereafter he will be among the losers. [31]\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. That is, submission to the will of God (Islām), which brings about inner peace, moral uprightness, and righteous living. True submission to Allāh is not merely outward conformity, but a comprehensive way of life that harmonizes belief, conduct, and character with divine guidance.\n[31]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“By Him in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, there is none from among the Jews or the Christians who hears of me and then dies without believing in the message with which I have been sent, except that he will be among the inhabitants of the Hellfire.”\nThis hadith affirms the universal obligation to believe in the final Messenger once his message has reached a person clearly. Accountability is tied to both the arrival of the proof and the conscious rejection of it, not merely to one’s inherited faith.",
  3086: "How could Allāh guide a people who disbelieved after having believed, bore witness that the Messenger is true, and after clear signs had come to them? For Allāh does not guide the wrongdoing people.",
  3087: "Their recompense is that upon them is the curse of Allāh, the angels, and all people.",
  3088: "They will remain therein forever. Their punishment will not be lightened, nor will they be given respite,",
  3089: "except those who repent thereafter and make amends. Indeed, Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  3090: "Indeed, those who disbelieve after having believed, then increase in disbelief—their repentance will never be accepted, and it is they who are astray.",
  3091: "Indeed, those who disbelieve and die as disbelievers—even an earth full of gold would never be accepted from any of them, even if they sought to ransom themselves with it [32]. For them is a painful punishment, and they will have no helpers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [32]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“An idolater will be asked, ‘If you had as much gold as would fill the earth, would you offer it to ransom yourself from the Hellfire?’ He will reply, ‘Yes.’ Then it will be said to him, ‘You were asked for something far less than that—to associate nothing with Allāh—but you refused.’”\nThis hadith shows that pure monotheism (tawḥīd) is the simplest and most fundamental demand placed upon humanity. Yet those who reject it will find that no ransom, wealth, or worldly possession can save them in the Hereafter. What Allāh required was not gold or sacrifice, but sincere submission and exclusive worship.",
  3092: "You will never attain righteousness until you give from what you love in charity. Whatever you spend, Allāh is All-Knowing of it.\\n\\nFootnotes: [33]. That is, the reward for it is Paradise—the eternal abode of joy, peace, and nearness to Allāh, prepared for those who believe and act righteously.",
  3093: "All food was lawful for the Children of Israel except what Israel [Jacob] forbade himself before the Torah was sent down [34]. Say, “Bring the Torah and recite it, if you are truthful.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [34]. That is, camel meat and its milk. When Jacob (Yaʿqūb), peace be upon him, was afflicted with sciatica, he vowed that if Allah cured him, he would abstain from something he loved. He therefore gave up camel meat and its milk. This was a personal vow, not a divine prohibition, though it was later mistaken by some as a religious restriction.",
  3094: "Whoever fabricates lies about Allāh after that, despite the clear proofs—then it is they who are the wrongdoers.",
  3095: "Say, “Allāh has spoken the truth. So follow the faith of Abraham—upright and devoted to Allāh—and he was not one of the polytheists.”",
  3096: "Indeed, the first House [of worship] established for mankind is the one at Bakkah—full of blessings and guidance for the worlds. [35]\\n\\nFootnotes: [35]. “Bakkah” is the Qurʾānic name used here for the site of the Kaʿbah—identified by the mainstream Islamic tradition with Makkah, often understood more specifically as the sacred precinct immediately surrounding the Kaʿbah. Early lexicographers relate the name to the idea of crowding/pressing together, alluding to the throngs of pilgrims. The phrase “the first House established for mankind” is understood in tafsīr as affirming the Kaʿbah as the earliest divinely appointed sanctuary for public worship, “blessed” and “a guidance for all peoples” indicates both its religious centrality (as a focal point of worship and pilgrimage) and its universal function as a sign directing humanity to God.",
  3097: "In it are clear signs, including the standing place of Abraham. Whoever enters it will be safe. Pilgrimage to the House [of worship in Makkah] is a duty owed to Allāh upon all mankind able to make the journey to it. Whoever disbelieves—then Allāh is in no need of the worlds.",
  3098: "Say, “O People of the Scripture, why do you disbelieve in the signs of Allāh, while Allāh is a Witness to all that you do?”",
  3099: "Say, “O People of the Scripture, why do you hinder those who believe from the path of Allāh, seeking to make it crooked, while you are witnesses?” Allāh is not unaware of what you do. [36]\\n\\nFootnotes: [36]. That is, they sought to divert people from the path of Allāh while knowing the truth themselves and bearing witness to it from their Scriptures. This was not out of ignorance, but deliberate deception and conscious opposition to revealed guidance, which makes their wrongdoing more grave.",
  3100: "O believers, if you obey a group of those who were given the Scripture, they will turn you back into disbelievers after your belief.",
  3101: "How can you disbelieve while Allah’s verses are being recited to you and His Messenger is among you? Whoever holds fast to Allāh is surely guided to a straight path. [37]\\n\\nFootnotes: [37[. That is, by holding fast to the surest and strongest bond—namely, Islām, which represents complete submission to Allāh, steadfast faith, and unwavering adherence to His guidance.",
  3102: "O believers, fear Allāh as He should be feared [38]; and do not die except as Muslims. [39]\\n\\nFootnotes: [38]. By adhering to all that Allāh has commanded and avoiding all that He has forbidden, a believer demonstrates true reverence and consciousness of Him. Allāh is to be obeyed, not disobeyed; remembered, not forgotten; and thanked, not denied. When a person sincerely commits to living by the Qur’an and the teachings of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ with justice, truthfulness, and righteousness, Allāh promises blessing and goodness both in this world and in the Hereafter.\n[39]. That is, complete and sincere submission to the Will of God (Islām)—in belief, worship, conduct, and moral character.",
  3103: "And hold fast, all together, to the rope of Allāh [40], and do not be divided. Remember Allah’s favor upon you: when you were enemies, He brought your hearts together, and by His grace you became brothers. You were on the brink of a fiery pit [41], and He saved you from it. Thus, Allāh makes His signs clear to you, so that you may be guided.\\n\\nFootnotes: [40]. That is, Islam. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: “I am leaving among you two weighty matters. The first is the Book of Allāh —it is the rope of Allāh; whoever holds fast to it is guided, and whoever abandons it has gone astray—and my household,” urging the community to uphold their rights and honor them.\nIn another narration, the Prophet ﷺ said: “I have left among you two things; you will never go astray so long as you hold fast to them: the Book of Allāh and my Sunnah.”\n[41]. That is, tribal rivalries, factional divisions, internal party strife, and the resulting mutual violence and hostility among people.\nOnce the Prophet ﷺ addressed his Anṣār Companions after the Battle of Ḥunayn by saying: “Have I not found you: lost and Allāh guided you by me? Destitute and Allāh made you well-off by me? Divided and Allāh united you by me?”",
  3104: "Let there be a group among you who call to what is good, enjoin what is right, and forbid what is wrong. It is they who are truly successful.",
  3105: "Do not be like those who became divided and differed after clear proofs had come to them. For them is a great punishment.",
  3106: "On the Day when some faces will be bright and others will be dark. As for those whose faces are darkened, it will be said to them, “Did you disbelieve after having believed? Taste the punishment for your disbelief.”",
  3107: "As for those whose faces will be bright, they will be in Allah’s mercy, in Paradise, where they will abide forever.",
  3108: "These are the verses of Allāh, which We recite to you in truth. Allāh does not intend any injustice to the worlds.",
  3109: "To Allāh belongs all that is in the heavens and all that is on the earth, and to Him all matters will return.",
  3110: "You are the best nation raised for humankind: you enjoin what is right, forbid what is wrong, and believe in Allāh. If the People of the Scripture had believed, it would have been better for them. Some of them are believers, but most of them are defiantly rebellious [against God].",
  3111: "They will not harm you beyond minor annoyance. If they fight you, they will turn their backs and flee—then they will find no help.",
  3112: "They are stricken with humiliation wherever they are found, unless under a covenant with Allāh or a treaty with people [42]. They have incurred Allah’s wrath and are marked by humiliation. That is because they disbelieved in Allah’s signs and killed the prophets unjustly. This is due to their disobedience and persistent transgression.\\n\\nFootnotes: [42]. Allāh, Exalted is He, informs that He punished the Jews with humiliation inwardly and destitution outwardly, so they neither settle nor find peace, except by a rope—that is, a covenant—from Allāh and a treaty with people. Thus, the Jews exist only under the authority of the Christians or under the authority and covenant of the Muslims, from whom the jizyah is taken and under whose rule they are humbled. That is, when the People of the Scripture submit to the authority of the Islamic state, they retain their rights, honor, and security, even if they choose not to embrace Islām. This is through the payment of the jizyah, which is given in place of Zakāh and mandatory military service, both of which are obligations upon Muslims. In return, they are granted full protection of life, property, religious practice, and civil rights under the guardianship of the Islamic state.",
  3113: "Yet they are not all the same. Among the People of the Scripture is an upright community who recite Allah’s signs at night, prostrating in prayer.",
  3114: "They believe in Allāh and the Last Day, enjoin what is right and forbid what is wrong, and hasten to good deeds [43]. They are among the righteous.\\n\\nFootnotes: [43]. Just as in a race all competitors run but only one attains the prize, so too must believers strive with purpose and discipline to attain the reward of the Hereafter. This requires true faith, constant good deeds, self-restraint, perseverance, and avoiding distractions. Every serious competitor exercises moderation and self-control in all matters; people do this for fleeting worldly gain, but the believer strives for everlasting success in the Hereafter—for Paradise, whose vastness is like that of the heavens and the earth, prepared for the righteous.",
  3115: "Whatever good they do will never be denied them. Allāh is fully aware of those who are righteous.",
  3116: "Indeed, those who persist in disbelief—their wealth and children will not avail them against Allāh in any way. They are the companions of the Fire, where they will abide forever.",
  3117: "The example of what they spend in this worldly life is like a freezing wind that strikes and destroys the harvest of a people who have wronged themselves. Allāh did not wrong them; rather, they wronged themselves.",
  3118: "O believers, do not take as close allies those outside your own ranks who spare no effort to corrupt you and seek your hardship. Their hatred has already become evident from their mouths, and what their hearts conceal is even worse. We have made the signs clear to you, if you would understand.\\n\\nFootnotes: [44]. That is, taking them as intimate confidants or close allies, disclosing to them sensitive information, secrets, or matters related to your internal affairs. The verse cautions against granting such deep trust to those who may not honor or safeguard your interests.\n[45]. That is, those among the Jews, Christians, and pagan idolaters who harbored hostility toward the believers and sought to undermine them. The warning applies specifically to hostile factions, not to all individuals from these groups.",
  3119: "Here you are—you love them [46], but they do not love you [47], even though you believe in all the Scriptures [48]. When they meet you, they say, ‘We believe,’ but when they are alone, they bite their fingertips in rage against you. Say, ‘Perish in your rage.’ Indeed, Allah knows well what is within the hearts.\\n\\nFootnotes: [46]. That is, because of their outward display of friendship and goodwill, which hides an inner hostility. Their pretense of affection is meant to deceive, not to support or benefit the believers.\n[47]. Since they oppose you in matters of faith, resisting or rejecting the religion of Islām, and therefore cannot be true supporters or protectors of your religious mission.\n[48]. That is, the believers affirm all revelations sent by Allāh, including the previous Scriptures revealed before the Qur’an. This universal belief distinguishes the believers from those who accept some revelations while rejecting others.Partial belief paired with rejection of God’s final message is inconsistent and constitutes disbelief in the divine truth as a whole.",
  3120: "If good touches you, it grieves them; but if adversity befalls you, they rejoice in it. Yet if you remain patient and conscious of Allāh, their schemes will not harm you in the least—for Allāh encompasses all that they do.",
  3121: "And remember when you set out from your household early in the morning to position the believers for battle. Allāh is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.",
  3122: "When two factions among you were about to lose courage—but Allāh made them steadfast [49], as He was their Protector. So in Allāh let the believers put their trust.\\n\\nFootnotes: [49]. That is, Banū Salamah and Banū Ḥārithah—the two groups from among the Anṣār who momentarily wavered before Allāh strengthened their resolve and upheld them in faith.",
  3123: "Allāh had already helped you to victory at Badr when you were weak. So be mindful of Allāh, that you may be grateful.",
  3124: "When you said to the believers, ‘Will it not be enough for you that your Lord supports you with three thousand angels sent down?’",
  3125: "Yes—if you remain patient and mindful of Allāh, and they come upon you suddenly in fury, your Lord will support you with five thousand marked angels.",
  3126: "Allāh made it only as good news for you, so that your hearts might be reassured. And victory comes only from Allāh—the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.",
  3127: "So that He might cut off a portion of those who disbelieved or disgrace them, causing them to turn back utterly disappointed.",
  3128: "It is not for you to decide anything in the matter. He may either accept their repentance or punish them—for they are indeed wrongdoers.",
  3129: "To Allāh belongs all that is in the heavens and on the earth. He forgives whom He wills and punishes whom He wills. Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  3130: "O believers, do not consume usury [50], multiplying it over and over. And be mindful of Allāh, so that you may prosper.\\n\\nFootnotes: [50]. The Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said:\n “Avoid the seven destructive sins.”\nThe Companions asked, “O Messenger of Allāh, what are they?” He replied:\n1. Associating partners with Allāh (shirk);\n2. Practicing sorcery or magic;\n3. Unlawfully killing a soul whose life Allāh has made sacred, except with legal right;\n4. Consuming ribā (usury or interest);\n5. Wrongfully consuming the property of an orphan;\n6. Turning one’s back and fleeing from the battlefield at the time of combat;\n7. Falsely accusing chaste, innocent believing women (or men) of adultery.\nThese sins are described as “destructive” (mubiqāt) because of the profound damage they inflict upon faith, moral integrity, social justice, and the human soul. They lead to severe consequences in this life and grave punishment in the Hereafter unless one sincerely repents and seeks Allāh’s forgiveness.",
  3131: "Beware of the Fire prepared for the disbelievers.",
  3132: "Obey Allāh [51] and the Messenger [52], so that you may receive mercy.\\n\\nFootnotes: [51]. That is, aligning one’s will with the Will of Allāh—doing what Allāh has commanded and refraining from what He has forbidden. It means complete surrender to His authority, basing one’s decisions, intentions, and actions upon His revelation. This is the essence of true submission (Islām).\n[52]. That is, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ—to have genuine faith and full confidence in him, to obey his commands, follow his guidance, and abstain from what he prohibited. Obedience to the Messenger is an integral part of obedience to Allāh.",
  3133: "And hasten to forgiveness from your Lord and to a Paradise as vast as the heavens and the earth, prepared for the righteous,",
  3134: "Those who give in times of ease and hardship [53], who restrain their anger and pardon people [54]—and Allāh loves those who do good.\\n\\nFootnotes: [53]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“Charity is required from every joint of a person each day.”\nHe then explained that helping someone with his means of transport—by assisting him to mount it or by lifting his belongings onto it—is an act of charity. A good word is charity, every step taken toward the congregational prayer is charity, and guiding someone on the road is charity.\nThis hadith shows that charity in Islām is not limited to wealth alone, but includes every form of kindness, assistance, and good conduct—making acts of goodness accessible to every believer, regardless of material means.\n[54]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“The strong person is not the one who overcomes others by physical force, but the strong one is the one who controls himself when he is angry.”\nAnd he ﷺ also said:\n“There is no gulp more greatly rewarded by Allāh than the gulp of anger which a person restrains, seeking the pleasure of Allāh.”\nThese teachings establish that true strength in Islām is moral and spiritual mastery, especially the ability to restrain anger. Such self-control is counted among the most highly rewarded acts of character in the sight of Allāh.",
  3135: "And those who, when they commit a shameful act or wrong themselves, remember Allāh and seek forgiveness for their sins—who can forgive sins except Allāh [55]?—and do not persist knowingly in what they have done.\\n\\nFootnotes: [55]. Allāh’s foremost attributes are His boundless mercy, forgiving love, and perfect patience. He is utterly unique and transcendent, wholly distinct from the false gods worshiped by pagan nations. In His infinite mercy, Allāh sent His final Messenger, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, to guide humanity to the Straight Path—so that people may live honorably and righteously in this world and attain everlasting success in the Hereafter.\nthe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: “There is no Muslim who commits a sin, then performs ablution, prays two rak‘ahs and asks Allāh for forgiveness without Allāh forgiving him”.",
  3136: "Their reward is forgiveness from their Lord and gardens beneath which rivers flow, where they will abide forever. How excellent is the reward of those who do good!",
  3137: "There have been examples before you. So travel through the land and see what was the end of those who denied 'the truth'.\\n\\nFootnotes: [56]. This refers to the divinely established laws and patterns (sunan Allāh) by which victory and defeat alternate among people, sometimes favoring believers and at other times disbelievers. These shifts occur as part of Allāh’s wisdom, justice, and testing. Through such trials, believers are purified, distinguished from others, and strengthened in faith. Despite temporary setbacks, the ultimate deliverance and final success of the believers is assured after they are tested with patience, endurance, and steadfastness.\nWhen the believers were tested by what happened to them on the day of ’Uhud, Allāh Almighty said, consoling them, that there have been divine laws before you in testing our servants through defeat and victory, but the outcome has always been destroying of the disbelievers and giving victory to the believers.",
  3138: "This is a message for all people, a guidance and a reminder for the righteous.",
  3139: "So do not lose heart or grieve [57]—you will have the upper hand if you are believers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [57]. Do not be overwhelmed by anxiety over any matter; rather, in every situation turn to prayer and sincere supplication, accompanied by gratitude and thanksgiving, and present your needs to Allāh. Through this, Allāh grants a peace that surpasses all circumstances, guarding the heart and mind. This inner peace is perfected through faith, reliance upon Allāh, and following the guidance of His last and final Messenger, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ.",
  3140: "If a wound has touched you, a similar wound has touched them. We alternate these days among people so that Allāh may make evident those who believe and take martyrs from among you. And Allāh does not like the wrongdoers.",
  3141: "So that Allāh may purify the believers [58] and wipe out the disbelievers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [58]. That is, Allāh purifies the believing community through trials—separating the sincere from the insincere, exposing hypocrisy, strengthening the hearts of the truthful, and elevating them through patience and perseverance. Tests serve to refine faith, distinguish genuine belief from mere claim, and prepare the believers for greater divine reward.",
  3142: "Or do you think you will enter Paradise before Allāh makes evident those among you who strive and those who remain steadfast?",
  3143: "You had certainly longed for death before you encountered it [59]—and now you have seen it while you were looking on.\\n\\nFootnotes: [59]. This verse serves as a reminder to those believers who did not take part in the Battle of Badr and who had previously expressed a desire to face the enemy and attain martyrdom. Here, the Qur’an alludes to the events of the Battle of Uhud, highlighting that true victory and honor are not achieved through wishful talk alone, but through sincere intention, firm resolve, discipline, and steadfastness at the time of trial. When the moment of testing arrived, faith was distinguished by perseverance and obedience, not by aspiration without endurance.",
  3144: "Muhammad is no more than a messenger; many messengers have passed away before him. So if he were to die or be killed, would you turn back on your heels? Whoever turns back on his heels will not harm Allāh in the least. And Allāh will reward the grateful.",
  3145: "No soul can die except by Allah’s permission, at a destined time. Whoever desires the reward of this world, We will grant him thereof; and whoever desires the reward of the Hereafter, We will grant him thereof. And We will reward the grateful.",
  3146: "How many a prophet fought, and with him many devoted men. They did not lose heart because of what struck them in Allah’s cause, nor did they weaken or yield. And Allāh loves the steadfast.",
  3147: "All they said was, ‘Our Lord, forgive us our sins and our excess in our affairs, make firm our feet, and grant us victory over the disbelieving people.’",
  3148: "So Allāh gave them the reward of this world and the finest reward of the Hereafter—for Allāh loves the doers of good.",
  3149: "O believers, if you obey the disbelievers, they will turn you back on your heels, and you will return as losers. [60]\\n\\nFootnotes: [60]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“Whoever lives with a polytheist and associates with him is like him.”\nThis ḥadīth is a warning, not an unconditional legal ruling. Its primary purpose is to caution Muslims against close, identity-shaping alliances with polytheists—particularly those that involve sharing their religious practices, supporting their beliefs, or adopting their customs in a way that compromises Islamic faith and moral integrity.\nThis does not forbid Muslims from ordinary interactions, coexistence, trade, justice, kindness, diplomacy, or good conduct with non-Muslims. The Qur’ān itself commands righteousness and fairness toward those who do not fight Muslims or expel them from their homes (Q. 60:8).\nThus, the ḥadīth urges believers to:\nSafeguard their religious identity;\nAvoid intimate companionships that may influence them toward disbelief;\nMaintain spiritual integrity while engaging with society.\nIt is, therefore, a principle of moral caution, not a ban on social or civil relations with non-Muslims.",
  3150: "Rather, Allāh is your Protector—and He is the best of helpers.",
  3151: "We will cast terror into the hearts of those who disbelieve because they have associated with Allāh that for which He has sent down no authority. Their abode will be the Fire. How wretched is the abode of the wrongdoers!",
  3152: "Indeed, Allāh fulfilled His promise to you when you were striking them down by His permission—until you lost courage, disputed over the command, and disobeyed after He had shown you what you love. Among you are those who desire this world, and among you are those who desire the Hereafter. Then He turned you away from them so that He might test you, yet He has surely pardoned you. Allāh is full of grace toward the believers.",
  3153: "When you were fleeing uphill, not looking back at anyone, while the Messenger was calling out to you from behind—then Allāh repaid you with sorrow upon sorrow so that you would not grieve over what you had missed nor over what had happened to you. And Allah is All-Aware of what you do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [61]. That is, the fear of your town being destroyed by the victorious enemy, along with the loss of your comrades and fighting companions. Such fears reflect the intense psychological pressure experienced during times of warfare and uncertainty.\n[62]. This refers to the false rumor that the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ had been killed, which spread during the heat of battle and caused confusion, fear, and momentary despair among some of the believers.",
  3154: "Then, after the grief, He sent down upon you a sense of security—drowsiness overtaking a group of you—while another group was concerned only with themselves, thinking of Allāh other than the truth, the thoughts of ignorance. They said, ‘Do we have any say in the matter?’ Say, ‘Indeed, the matter belongs entirely to Allāh.’ They conceal within themselves what they do not reveal to you. They say, ‘If we had any say in the matter, we would not have been killed here.’ Say, ‘Even if you had remained in your homes, those for whom death was decreed would have gone forth to their resting places, so that Allah might test what is within your chests and purify what is in your hearts.’ And Allāh is fully aware of what is within the chests.”",
  3155: "Indeed, those of you who turned back on the day the two hosts met—it was only Satan who caused them to slip because of some of what they had earned. And Allāh has certainly pardoned them. Indeed, Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Forbearing.",
  3156: "O believers, do not be like those who disbelieved and said of their brothers, when they traveled through the land or went out to fight, ‘If only they had stayed with us, they would not have died or been killed.’ Thus Allāh makes it a regret in their hearts. And Allāh gives life and causes death, and Allāh is All-Seeing of what you do.",
  3157: "If you are killed in the cause of Allāh or die, then forgiveness and mercy from Allāh are far better than what they amass.",
  3158: "Whether you die or are killed, you will surely be gathered to Allāh.",
  3159: "So it was by mercy from Allāh that you were lenient with them. Had you been harsh and hard-hearted, they would have dispersed from around you. So pardon them, seek forgiveness for them, and consult them in the matter. Then when you have resolved, put your trust in Allāh. Indeed, Allāh loves those who put their trust in Him.",
  3160: "If Allāh helps you, none can overcome you. But if He forsakes you, who is there to help you after Him? And in Allāh let the believers put their trust.",
  3161: "It is not for a prophet to commit treachery. Whoever does so will come on the Day of Resurrection carrying what he took [63]. Then every soul will be fully repaid for what it has earned, and none will be wronged.\\n\\nFootnotes: [63]. The term ghulūl refers to the unlawful and stealthy misappropriation of property, whether it involves war booty, public funds, or private wealth. In the specific context of this verse, the reference is to the betrayal of trust through the secret embezzlement of spoils of war before their lawful distribution.\n[64]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“Whomever we appoint to a public position, and he conceals from us even a needle or anything smaller than that, it is misappropriation (ghulūl), and he will be brought with it on the Day of Resurrection.”\nA man from the Anṣār stood and said, “O Messenger of Allāh, take back your assignment from me.” The Prophet ﷺ asked, “What is the matter with you?” He replied, “I heard you say such-and-such.”\nThe Prophet ﷺ said:\n“And I say it again: whoever among you is entrusted with a position must render an account for everything—small or great. Whatever is given to him lawfully, he may take. But he must abstain completely from whatever is unlawful.”\nThe Prophet ﷺ also said:\n“By Allāh, none of you misappropriates anything—whether from zakāh, public funds, or war booty—except that he will meet Allāh on the Day of Judgment carrying it on his neck. I will recognize each of you: one carrying a camel groaning, another with a cow lowing, and another with a goat bleating…”\nThese narrations constitute a severe warning against corruption, bribery, embezzlement, and abuse of public trust. Every official, administrator, and leader is commanded to act with absolute honesty and integrity, knowing that accountability before Allāh is unavoidable. Likewise, leaders are obligated to maintain strict oversight over their subordinates. To tolerate corruption, negligence, or exploitation is itself a betrayal. Those who conspire together in plunder and corruption will face compounded accountability on the Day of Judgment.",
  3162: "Is one who follows the pleasure of Allāh like one who incurs the wrath of Allāh, whose abode is Hell? How wretched is the destination!",
  3163: "They are of varying ranks with Allāh, and Allāh is All-Seeing of what they do.",
  3164: "Indeed, Allāh has bestowed a great favor upon the believers when He raised among them a messenger from among themselves, reciting to them His verses, purifying them, and teaching them the Book and wisdom—though before that they were clearly in error. [65]\\n\\nFootnotes: [65]. The first and foremost obligation upon every human being is to know with absolute certainty that there is no true deity worthy of worship except Allāh. He is the First and the Last, the Alpha and the Omega, One without partner, the Creator of the heavens, the earth, and all that lies within and between them. He is the Most Subtle, the All-Aware, and the One who has power over all things.\nThe second fundamental obligation is to believe in the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, and to affirm with full conviction that Allāh sent him to all of humanity as:\nA bearer of glad tidings,\nA warner,\nA caller to God by His permission,\nAnd a radiant lamp of guidance.\nThese two obligations—pure monotheism and faith in the final Messenger—are the foundation of Islām and the basis of all belief, worship, and ultimate salvation.",
  3165: "Why is it that when a calamity struck you—after you had inflicted twice as much—you said, ‘From where is this?’ Say, ‘It is from yourselves.’ Indeed, Allāh has power over all things.",
  3166: "What struck you on the day the two hosts met was by Allah’s permission, so that He might make evident the true believers,",
  3167: "so that He might make evident those who were hypocrites. When they were told, ‘Come, fight in the cause of Allāh or defend yourselves,’ they said, ‘If we had known there would be fighting, we would certainly have followed you.’ On that day they were closer to disbelief than to faith, saying with their mouths what was not in their hearts—but Allāh knows best what they conceal.",
  3168: "Those who said of their brothers while staying behind, ‘If they had obeyed us, they would not have been killed.’ Say, ‘Then avert death from yourselves, if you are truthful.’",
  3169: "Do not think that those who are killed in the cause of Allāh are dead. Rather, they are alive with their Lord, being provided for, [66]\\n\\nFootnotes: [66]. Allāh declares that although the martyrs are slain in this world, their souls remain truly alive, receiving provision and honor in the everlasting abode. Concerning the verse: “Do not think of those who are killed in the way of Allāh as dead. Rather, they are alive, receiving provision from their Lord.” (Qur’an 3:169).\nThe Companions asked the Prophet ﷺ about its meaning, and he explained:\n“Their souls are inside green birds that roam freely in Paradise. They take shelter in lanterns suspended beneath the Throne of Allāh. They roam wherever they wish in Paradise, then return to those lanterns. Allāh asks them, ‘Do you desire anything?’ They reply, ‘O Lord, what more could we desire when we roam freely wherever we wish in Paradise?’ He asks them this three times. When they realize He will continue to ask until they respond, they say, ‘O Lord, we wish that our souls be returned to our bodies so that we may be slain again in Your cause.’ Allāh knows that they have no other wish, so they remain as they are.”\nThe Prophet ﷺ also said:\n“No one who enters Paradise would wish to return to the world, even if he were to possess all that is in it—except the martyr. He longs to return and be killed again ten times over, because of the immense honor and reward he witnesses.”\nThese narrations affirm the exceptional rank, living state, and extraordinary reward of the martyrs, whose sacrifice is compensated with immediate life, provision, freedom, and nearness to the Throne of Allāh.",
  3170: "Rejoicing in what Allāh has given them of His bounty, and rejoicing for those who have not yet joined them—that no fear will be upon them, nor will they grieve.",
  3171: "They rejoice in the favor of Allāh and His bounty, and that Allāh does not let the reward of the believers go to waste.",
  3172: "Those who responded to Allāh and the Messenger after being struck by injury [67]—for those among them who did good and were mindful of Allāh is a great reward,\\n\\nFootnotes: [67]. Refers to the believers who, after being wounded at the Battle of Uhud, responded to the Prophet’s call and pursued the enemy to Ḥamrā’ al-Asad, demonstrating steadfastness despite their injuries.",
  3173: "those to whom people said, ‘Indeed, the people have gathered against you, so fear them.’ But it only increased them in faith, and they said, ‘Allāh is sufficient for us, and He is the best Disposer of affairs.’ [68]\\n\\nFootnotes: [68]. In Allāh alone is true hope, safety, and peace. The believer need not fear any enemy when his trust is firmly placed in his Lord. During moments of hardship and vulnerability, one turns to Allāh in sincere prayer, and from Him alone comes real help, strength, and reassurance.\nIt was narrated that the statement: Hasbunā Allāh wa ni‘ma al-Wakīl (Allāh suffices us, He is the best of Protectors), was said by Abraham when he was thrown in the fire and was said by Muhammad (ﷺ) when it was said: “People have regrouped for you, so fear them”, their belief grew firmer and they said: “Allāh suffices us, He is the best of Protectors”.",
  3174: "So they returned with grace and bounty from Allāh; no harm touched them. They pursued the pleasure of Allāh —and Allāh is the Possessor of great bounty.",
  3175: "It is only Satan who frightens you through his allies. So do not fear them [69], but fear Me, if you are believers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [69]. Stand firm and be courageous—do not give way to fear. Allāh, your Lord, is with you; He will never abandon you nor let you down. So do not be intimidated by your enemies, and do not lose heart. True strength, inner peace, and ultimate victory come from placing unwavering trust in Allāh.",
  3176: "Do not be grieved by those who hasten into disbelief. They cannot harm Allah in the least. Allah intends that He will give them no share in the Hereafter, and for them is a severe punishment.",
  3177: "Indeed, those who exchange faith for disbelief can never harm Allāh in the least, and for them is a painful punishment.",
  3178: "Let not those who disbelieve think that the respite We grant them is good for them. We only grant them respite so that they may increase in sin, and for them is a humiliating punishment.",
  3179: "Allāh would not leave the believers in the state you are in until He distinguishes the impure from the pure. Nor would Allāh disclose to you the unseen; rather, Allāh chooses from among His messengers whom He wills. So believe in Allāh and His messengers. If you believe and are mindful of Allāh, you will have a great reward.",
  3180: "Do not think that those who greedily withhold what Allāh has given them of His bounty—that it is good for them. Rather, it is bad for them: what they withheld will be fastened as a collar around their necks on the Day of Resurrection. To Allāh belongs the inheritance of the heavens and the earth, and Allāh is fully aware of what you do.",
  3181: "Indeed, Allāh has heard the statement of those who said, ‘Allāh is poor, and we are rich [70].’ We will record what they said and their killing of the prophets unjustly, and We will say, ‘Taste the punishment of the burning Fire.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [70]. During the Prophet’s time, when the Jews of Madinah heard the preceding verse encouraging believers to spend in the way of Allāh, they responded mockingly. They said, in effect: “If He asks people to spend in His cause to fulfill His purposes, then He must be poor and in need.” This statement was made in ridicule and denial, and it is to this claim that the following verse responds.",
  3182: "That is because of what your own hands have sent forth—and because Allāh is not unjust to His servants.",
  3183: "Those who said, ‘Indeed, Allāh has commanded us not to believe in any messenger unless he brings us an offering that fire will consume.’ Say, ‘Messengers before me already came to you with clear proofs and with what you demanded. So why did you kill them, if you are truthful?’",
  3184: "But if they deny you, messengers before you were also denied—who came with clear proofs, the Psalms, and the illuminating Book.",
  3185: "Every soul will taste death, and you will be paid your reward in full on the Day of Resurrection. So whoever is spared from the Fire and admitted into Paradise has truly attained success. And the life of this world is nothing but the enjoyment of delusion.",
  3186: "You will surely be tested in your wealth and in yourselves, and you will surely hear much abuse from those who were given the Scripture before you and from those who associate others with Allāh. But if you remain patient and mindful of Allāh, that is among the matters requiring firm resolve.",
  3187: "And remember when Allāh took a covenant from those who were given the Scripture: ‘You must make it clear to the people and not conceal it.’ But they cast it behind their backs and sold it for a small price. How wretched is what they traded!",
  3188: "Do not think that those who rejoice in what they have done and love to be praised for what they have not done will escape the punishment. And for them is a painful punishment. [71]\\n\\nFootnotes: [71]. A companion oftheProphet (may Allāh be pleased with him) reported that this verse was revealed concerning certain hypocrites who would remain behind when the Prophet ﷺ went out on expeditions. They were pleased to stay behind, and upon his return, they would offer excuses and swear to them, seeking praise for what they had not done—namely, their supposed participation and loyalty.",
  3189: "To Allāh belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth, and Allāh has power over all things.",
  3190: "Indeed, in the creation of the heavens and the earth and the alternation of the night and the day, there are certainly signs for people of understanding,",
  3191: "those who remember Allāh while standing, sitting, and on their sides, and reflect on the creation of the heavens and the earth, saying, ‘Our Lord, You did not create this in vain. Glory be to You—so save us from the punishment of the Fire.’",
  3192: "Our Lord, whoever You admit to the Fire—you have indeed disgraced him, and the wrongdoers will have no helpers.",
  3193: "Our Lord, we have heard a caller calling to faith [72], saying, ‘Believe in your Lord,’ so we have believed. Our Lord, forgive us our sins, expiate our misdeeds, and cause us to die among the righteous.\\n\\nFootnotes: [72]. Refers to the one who called to faith—understood as the Prophet Muḥammad (ﷺ), or the call of revelation itself, inviting people to believe in their Lord.",
  3194: "Our Lord, grant us what You have promised us through Your messengers, and do not disgrace us on the Day of Resurrection. Indeed, You never break Your promise.",
  3195: "So their Lord responded to them, ‘Indeed, I will never let the work of any one of you go to waste, whether male or female—you are of one another [73]. Those who emigrated, were driven from their homes, were harmed in My cause, and fought and were killed—I will surely expiate their misdeeds and admit them into gardens beneath which rivers flow, as a reward from Allāh. And with Allāh is the best reward.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [73]. Both men and women are created from one another and are regarded as equal in human worth and spiritual responsibility before Allāh. True believers are not among those who turn back, but among those who remain firm in faith and safeguard their souls. They do not lose courage or abandon the path of Islām. Rather, they endure trials with patience, continue trusting in Allāh, and through this steadfastness preserve their souls and attain salvation and eternal life.",
  3196: "Do not let the apparent prosperity and movement of those who disbelieve throughout the land deceive you.",
  3197: "A brief enjoyment—then their abode is Hell. How miserable is the resting place!",
  3198: "But for those who are mindful of their Lord are gardens beneath which rivers flow, where they will abide forever—an accommodation from Allāh. And what is with Allāh is best for the righteous.",
  3199: "And indeed, among the People of the Scripture are those who truly believe in Allāh and in what has been sent down to you and what was sent down to them—being humble before Allāh. They do not sell Allah’s signs for a small price. Those will have their reward with their Lord. Indeed, Allāh is swift in reckoning.",
  3200: "O believers, be patient, persevere [74], stand firm [75], and fear Allāh—so that you may prosper.\\n\\nFootnotes: [74]. The Qur’an teaches believers to remain patient in the face of life’s trials and hardships, as well as in their dealings with others. It calls them to adorn themselves with compassion, kindness, humility, gentleness, and forbearance in all human interactions. Believers are reminded to show patience even toward those who are difficult to deal with or challenging to love, seeking Allāh’s pleasure through steadfast moral character.\n[75]. Rābitū from ribāt, i.e., the fact of ‘tying up’ one’s war steed at outposts to protect the borders from enemy attack. This also includes the practical meaning of keeping one’s mounts “tethered” at the frontiers, that is, remaining prepared, vigilant, and stationed to guard the borders against enemy attack. It reflects the broader Qur’ānic principle of spiritual patience combined with physical readiness and disciplined preparedness.",
  4001: "O mankind, be mindful of your Lord, who created you from a single soul ˹Ādam˺, and from it created its mate ˹Ḥawwāh / Eve˺, and from the two of them spread many men and women. And be mindful of Allāh, through whom you ask one another, and the ties of kinship. Indeed, Allāh is ever Watchful over you.",
  4002: "Give the orphans their wealth, and do not exchange your bad for their good, nor consume their wealth along with your own. Indeed, that is a grave sin.",
  4003: "If you fear that you will not deal justly with the orphan girls, then marry what seems good to you of other women—two, or three, or four [1]. But if you fear that you will not be just, then marry only one, or those whom your right hands possess. That is closer to your not committing injustice.\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. This verse was revealed concerning a man who serves as both the guardian and potential heir of an orphan girl. In such a situation, she may possess wealth but lack someone to defend her interests, creating a clear imbalance of power. This could tempt the guardian to marry her for personal gain, deprive her of her rightful dowry, or treat her unjustly without accountability.\nThe verse establishes a firm moral and legal safeguard against such abuse. It prohibits exploiting the orphan’s vulnerability and commands the guardian to uphold fairness, integrity, and compassion, ensuring her rights, dignity, and property are fully protected. In doing so, it reinforces a broader principle in Islam: those in positions of trust must protect the vulnerable, not take advantage of them.\nIt then redirects the guardian—if he fears he cannot deal justly with the orphan girl, he should marry other lawful women instead, up to two, three, or four, provided he can maintain fairness among them. If he doubts his ability to be just, he is instructed to marry only one. Thus, the allowance of multiple wives is tied directly to preventing injustice and safeguarding the rights of orphan girls.",
  4004: "Give the women their dowries as a gift; but if they willingly remit to you any part of it, then enjoy it with ease and goodwill.",
  4005: "Do not entrust the weak-minded with your wealth [2]—which Allāh has made a means of support for you—but provide for them from it, clothe them, and speak to them with kindness.\\n\\nFootnotes: [2].  Although this wealth legally belongs to them, Allāh refers to it in the collective sense, reminding us that all wealth ultimately comes from Him. It is not given merely for personal enjoyment, but as a trust to be used responsibly for the welfare of both the individual and the wider community. This wording instills a sense of shared moral responsibility, teaching that wealth must circulate in justice, compassion, and social balance, not be hoarded, misused, or exploited.\nThis principle is powerfully reinforced by the statement of the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ, who said: “Allāh has forbidden for you: to be undutiful to your mothers and fathers; to bury your daughters alive; to withhold the rights of others; and to beg from people. And Allāh has hated for you: excessive gossip, asking trivial or disruptive questions in religious matters, and wasting wealth through extravagance without wisdom or restraint.”\nThis ḥadīth highlights that misuse of wealth is not merely a financial failure, but a moral and spiritual one. Squandering resources through wastefulness reflects ingratitude toward Allāh’s provision and neglect of social responsibility. At the same time, withholding the rights of others — whether financial, social, or ethical — is condemned as a serious injustice. Together, the verse and the ḥadīth establish a comprehensive ethic: wealth must be earned lawfully, used wisely, shared fairly, and protected from abuse.",
  4006: "Test the orphans until they reach the age of marriage. Then, if you discern in them sound judgment, hand over their wealth to them. Do not consume it wastefully or hastily, fearing that they may grow up. Whoever is well-off should abstain; and whoever is poor may take what is reasonable. When you hand over their wealth to them, call witnesses. And Allāh is sufficient as a Reckoner.",
  4007: "Men have a share in what parents and close relatives leave behind, and women have a share in what parents and close relatives leave behind—whether it is little or much—a prescribed share.",
  4008: "When relatives, orphans, and the poor are present at the time of division, give them something from it, and speak to them graciously.",
  4009: "Let those who would fear for their own weak offspring, if they were to leave them behind, fear Allāh and speak upright and just words.",
  4010: "Indeed, those who consume the wealth of orphans unjustly are only consuming fire into their bellies [3], and they will be burned in a blazing Fire.\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. That is, their unlawful consumption of the property of orphans will ultimately become a cause of punishment in the Hereafter. The Qur’an vividly portrays their wrongdoing as if they were consuming fire, emphasizing the severity of exploiting the vulnerable.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: “Beware of the seven deadly sins: And among them he mentioned consuming an orphan’s wealth ”.",
  4011: "Allāh instructs you concerning your children: for the male is the share of two females. If there are daughters, two or more, then for them is two-thirds of what he leaves; and if there is only one, then for her is half. And for his parents—for each of them is a sixth of what he leaves, if he has a child. But if he has no child and his parents inherit him, then for his mother is a third. But if he has siblings, then for his mother is a sixth—after any bequest he makes or debt. Your parents or your children—you do not know which of them is nearer to you in benefit. This is an obligation from Allah. Indeed, Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise.",
  4012: "For you is half of what your wives leave if they have no child; but if they have a child, then for you is a fourth of what they leave—after any bequest they may have made or a debt. And for them is a fourth of what you leave if you have no child; but if you have a child, then for them is an eighth of what you leave—after any bequest you may have made or a debt. If a man or a woman dies leaving neither parents nor children but has a brother or a sister, then for each of them is a sixth; but if they are more than two, they share equally in one-third—after any bequest or debt, without causing harm. This is an ordinance from Allāh. And Allāh is All-Knowing, Most Forbearing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. That is, by making a bequest that exceeds one-third of the total estate, which would unlawfully reduce the fixed shares of the rightful heirs. Islamic law limits personal bequests to a maximum of one-third of the estate and does not allow a bequest in favor of an already-designated heir unless the other heirs willingly consent. This restriction is based on the Prophet’s ruling, “One-third—and one-third is much.”",
  4013: "These are the limits set by Allāh. Whoever obeys Allāh and His messenger, He will admit him into gardens beneath which rivers flow, abiding therein forever. That is the supreme triumph.",
  4014: "But whoever disobeys Allāh and His Messenger and transgresses His limits—He will admit him into the Fire, abiding therein forever, and for him is a humiliating punishment.",
  4015: "Those of your women who commit adultery—call four witnesses from among you to testify against them. If they testify, then confine them to their homes until death claims them or Allāh provides another way for them. [5].\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. This “other way” refers to the later punishment established in Surah 24, verse 2.",
  4016: "As for the two among you who commit it, punish them both. But if they repent and make amends, then leave them alone [6]. Indeed, Allāh is Ever-Accepting of repentance, Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. A person who has sincerely repented should never be humiliated or continually reminded of their past sins. Public shaming or social exclusion can weaken the heart and may even drive someone back into the very wrongdoing they struggled to leave behind. True repentance deserves compassion, privacy, and support. Believers are encouraged to help one another grow, not to trap each other in past mistakes. Remember that Allāh, in His mercy, loves those who turn back to Him sincerely and strives to ease their return to righteousness.",
  4017: "Allāh accepts repentance only from those who commit evil out of ignorance and then repent soon afterward. It is they to whom Allāh turns in forgiveness. And Allāh is All-Knowing, All-Wise.",
  4018: "But repentance is not for those who persist in doing evil deeds until, when death comes to one of them, he says, ‘Now I repent,’ nor for those who die as disbelievers. For them We have prepared a painful punishment.",
  4019: "O believers, it is not lawful for you to inherit women against their will [7], nor to restrain them in order to take back part of what you have given them—unless they commit a clear immorality. And live with them in kindness and honor. If you dislike them, it may be that you dislike something in which Allāh has placed much good.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. This verse addresses a pre-Islamic practice in which, upon a man’s death, his male relatives would claim authority over his widow, treating her as part of the inheritance. They could marry her, give her in marriage to others, or prevent her from remarrying altogether—often against her will.\nThe verse abolishes this injustice by affirming the woman’s right to choose and prohibiting coercion. The phrase “what you have given them” refers to the dowry given at marriage, which a husband is not permitted to reclaim unjustly.",
  4020: "If you wish to replace one wife with another, and you have given one of them a great amount of wealth, do not take anything back from it. Would you take it through slander and manifest sin?",
  4021: "How could you take it back after you have been intimate with one another, and they have taken from you a firm covenant?",
  4022: "Do not marry those women whom your fathers had married, except what has already passed in pre-Islamic times. Indeed, it is an abomination, detestable, and an evil way.",
  4023: "Prohibited to you for marriage are your mothers, your daughters, your sisters, your paternal aunts and maternal aunts, your brothers’ daughters and your sisters’ daughters, your mothers who breastfed you, your foster-sisters, the mothers of your wives, your stepdaughters under your care—born of wives with whom you have consummated marriage; but if you have not consummated, then there is no blame—and the wives of your sons who are from your loins, and that you take two sisters together in marriage, except what has already passed in pre-Islamic times. Indeed, Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  4024: "And married women are prohibited to you—except those whom your right hands possess. This is Allah’s ordinance binding upon you. And lawful for you are those beyond that, provided you seek them with your wealth, in chastity and not in fornication. So for whatever you enjoy from them, give them their due compensation as an obligation. And there is no blame upon you for what you mutually agree upon after the obligation. Indeed, Allāh is All-Knowing, All-Wise.",
  4025: "But if any of you cannot afford to marry free believing women, then marry from among the believing bondwomen whom you possess. Allāh knows best about your faith—you are of one another. So marry them with the permission of their masters and give them their dowries in fairness, provided they are chaste, neither committing fornication nor taking secret lovers. If they commit adultery after marriage, then for them is half the punishment prescribed for free women [8]. This is for those among you who fear falling into sin; but if you are patient, it is better for you [9]. And Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. Their prescribed punishment is fifty lashes. They are not subject to stoning to death, nor to exile. This is because the Qur’an explicitly states that the punishment for married bondwomen who commit sexual immorality is half that of free unmarried women, whose fixed punishment is one hundred lashes. Since stoning cannot be halved and is not mentioned here, it is not applicable in this case. \n[9]. It is advised to refrain from marrying bondwomen who have not yet developed upright character, emotional maturity, or social responsibility, or who have not been fully emancipated, until they are sufficiently prepared for the obligations of marriage. This guidance is inferred from the Qur’anic emphasis on chastity, moral accountability, and readiness for marital responsibility.",
  4026: "Allāh intends to make things clear to you, to guide you to the upright ways of those before you [10], and to accept your repentance. And Allāh is All-Knowing, All-Wise.\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. This refers to the upright religious laws and moral ways practiced by the earlier prophets of Allāh—such as Moses and Jesus—and by their rightly guided followers. These ways were based on devotion to Allāh, adherence to divine law, justice, moral restraint, repentance, and accountability. The verse affirms continuity between Islamic law and the authentic guidance given to earlier prophetic communities.",
  4027: "And Allāh intends to turn toward you in mercy, but those who follow their desires want you to stray into great deviation. [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. That is, those who are deeply immersed in sinful desires seek to draw believers away from the path of Allāh, hoping that they too will abandon moral restraint and become participants in the same corruption. This reflects the recurring human tendency to normalize wrongdoing by spreading it, so that personal guilt may feel shared and therefore lessened. The verse contrasts this destructive impulse with Allāh’s desire to guide people toward repentance, mercy, and moral clarity.",
  4028: "Allāh intends to lighten your burdens, for mankind was created weak.",
  4029: "O believers, do not consume your wealth among yourselves unjustly, except through trade by mutual consent. And do not kill yourselves [12]. Indeed, Allāh is ever Merciful to you.\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. Or one another. The wording also includes self-harm. The Prophet ﷺ warned against taking one’s own life, saying: “Whoever kills himself by strangling will continue to strangle himself in the Fire, and whoever kills himself with a blade will continue to stab himself in the Fire.” This emphasizes that self-destruction is a grave sin and does not bring escape, but rather leads to continued suffering in the Hereafter.",
  4030: "And whoever does that out of hostility and wrongdoing [13], We will make him enter the Fire—and that is easy for Allāh.\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. This refers to the wrongful consumption of people’s property and the unlawful taking of human life. The verse condemns deliberate acts of aggression—whether through theft, deception, exploitation, or intentional killing—rather than actions done mistakenly or under coercion. Such crimes constitute major sins and are linked here with severe accountability in the Hereafter.",
  4031: "If you avoid the major sins from which you are forbidden [14], We will absolve you of your lesser misdeeds and admit you into a noble entrance.\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. Every sin is, in essence, an offense against Allāh. However, Islamic teachings distinguish between two primary categories of sins: major (grave) sins and minor sins. Major sins—often called deadly or grave sins (kabā’ir)—are offenses of such seriousness that they severely corrupt a person’s relationship with their Creator and expose them to divine punishment if committed without repentance. Minor sins, though lesser in severity, still harm one’s spiritual state and moral integrity. Persistent indulgence in minor sins can gradually lead a person into major sins through moral desensitization.\nFor a sin to be classified as a major (deadly) sin, scholars generally identify three necessary conditions:\nA). It involves a matter of grave seriousness.\nB). It is committed with full awareness that it is sinful.\nC). It is committed with deliberate and free consent.\nThe Qur’an and the Sunnah identify many actions as major sins, including but not limited to: associating partners with Allāh (shirk); unjust killing; practicing sorcery; abandoning the prescribed prayer (ṣalāh); refusing to pay zakāh; breaking the fast of Ramaḍān without a valid excuse; failing to perform Ḥajj when one is fully able; disobedience to parents; severing family ties; adultery and fornication; sodomy; consuming intoxicants and drugs; dealing in usury (interest); unjustly consuming the wealth of orphans; theft; highway robbery; bearing false witness; falsely accusing chaste persons; bribery in judgment; men imitating women and women imitating men; arrogance and pride; oppression and tyranny; despairing of Allāh’s mercy; feeling secure from Allāh’s punishment; suicide; betrayal of trusts and oaths; unjust political authority; backbiting and slander; and abandoning the Qur’an through neglect of its recitation and teachings.\nAvoidance of these major sins is a central condition for the divine promise stated in this verse: that lesser faults may be forgiven and the believer admitted to a place of noble honor in the Hereafter.\nThe Prophet ﷺ said: \"Avoid the seven great destructive sins.\" They asked, \"O Allah’s Messenger! What are they?\" He said, \"(1) To associate partners with Allāh, (2) sorcery; (3) to kill a person whom Allāh has forbidden except for a just cause; (4) to consume usury; (5) to eat up the property of an orphan; (6) to turn one’s back to the enemy and fleeing from the battlefield at the time of fighting and (7) to accuse innocent chaste women of immorality.\"",
  4032: "Do not covet what Allāh has granted some of you over others [15]. For men is a share of what they earn, and for women is a share of what they earn. And ask Allāh of His bounty [16]. Indeed, Allāh is All-Knowing of all things.\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. Believers are cautioned against envy and resentment over the worldly advantages granted to others. Allāh, in His wisdom, distributes provisions, opportunities, and talents according to His decree. Envy corrodes the heart, undermines gratitude, and leads to dissatisfaction with Allāh’s will. Instead of wishing for what others possess, believers are encouraged to seek Allāh’s bounty through lawful means, prayer, and righteous effort.\n[16]. Allāh emphasizes justice in financial dealings and warns against exploiting others. Islam upholds that men and women alike must receive their full and fair compensation. Withholding wages, delaying payment, or underpaying workers is considered a sin. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ reinforced this principle, saying: “Give the worker his wages before his sweat dries.” This hadith highlights the ethical urgency of paying employees promptly and treating them with dignity, kindness, and respect. Islamic law consistently condemns financial oppression and regards fair dealing as a core expression of faith.\n[17]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ taught that prayers should be made with firm conviction and full attention. He said: “Ask Allāh while you fully believe that He will answer you, and know that Allāh does not respond to a heart that is heedless or distracted.” This highlights the spiritual importance of sincerity, focus, and confidence in divine response when supplicating.",
  4033: "For each We have appointed heirs to what parents and close relatives leave behind. And to those with whom you have made a covenant, give them their share. Indeed, Allāh is Witness over all things.",
  4034: "Men are caretakers of women by what Allāh has favored some of them over others and by what they spend from their wealth. So righteous women are devoutly obedient, guarding in the unseen what Allāh has commanded to be guarded [18]. And those from whom you fear rebellion—admonish them, abandon them in their beds, and strike them lightly [19]. But if they obey you, then seek no way against them. Indeed, Allāh is Most High, Most Great.\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. I.e., their chastity, and their husband’s wealth and property.\n[19]. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Do not beat the female servants of Allāh.”\nLater, one of the Companions (may Allāh be pleased with him) complained:  “The women have become bold toward their husbands.”\nThe Prophet ﷺ then allowed hitting in cases of real need and only in a way that is light, gentle, and free of cruelty. But soon after, many women came to the households of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ complaining about their husbands. The Prophet ﷺ then said: “Many women have gone around the family of Muhammad complaining of their husbands. Those men are not the best among you.”\nThis shows that those who habitually beat their wives are not examples of good character. Refined manners demand that one avoids hitting altogether, as we see from the conduct of the Prophet ﷺ himself: He never struck anyone with his hand—neither a woman, nor a servant—except in battle for the sake of Allāh.\nHe would only take action against those who overstepped the limits set by Allāh.\nThe Messenger of Allāh ﷺ also said:\n “The believer with the most complete faith is the one with the best character. And the best of you are those who are best to their wives.”\nAlthough Islam has permitted a husband, in exceptional and unavoidable circumstances, to rebuke his wife if she is openly indecent or persistently disobedient, the Qur’ān lays out a wise, graduated process:\nA). Admonish her – Advise her, remind her of Allāh, and counsel her with kindness and sincerity.\nB). Separate in bed – If advice does not help, he may refrain from sharing the bed with her. For a sensible wife, this is a serious warning and a sign that the marriage is in danger.\nC). Light, symbolic hitting – If she still persists, he may resort to a very light, non-injurious form of hitting. He must avoid the face and head. It must not cause harm, injury, or humiliation. He should only do this if he genuinely believes it will help, not out of anger or vengeance.\nIf he knows it will only worsen the situation, it is better to avoid it entirely.\nHe must remember that his wife has feelings and dignity, and that she is like one of the two wheels of the carriage of life. Even if the situation reaches the point of this last step, he must keep her true status in mind and never forget her importance in married life.\nIn the husband’s absence, the wife is responsible not only for safeguarding her own chastity and her husband’s property, but also for respecting his boundaries about who is allowed into the home. She should not admit anyone whom her husband dislikes, no matter how closely related that person may be to her.",
  4035: "If you fear a breach between them, then appoint an arbiter from his family and an arbiter from hers. If they both desire reconciliation, Allāh will bring about harmony between them. Indeed, Allāh is All-Knowing, All-Aware.",
  4036: "Worship Allāh and do not associate anything with Him. And be kind to parents, relatives, orphans, the needy, the near neighbor, the distant neighbor [20], the companion at your side [21], the stranded traveler [22], and those whom your right hands possess. Indeed, Allāh does not like those who are self-conceited and boastful,\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. The “distant neighbor” refers to the neighbor who does not share kinship ties. Exegetes note that the Qur’an distinguishes between the neighbor who is a relative and the neighbor who is not, thereby emphasizing that kindness and consideration extend to all neighbors regardless of family relationship. Some scholars also understood the distinction more broadly as including both the nearby neighbor and the more distant one. The verse thus establishes the wide scope of a Muslim’s social responsibility toward those living around them.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“Whoever believes in Allāh and the Last Day, let him treat his neighbor well. Whoever believes in Allāh and the Last Day, let him honor his guest. And whoever believes in Allāh and the Last Day, let him speak good, or else remain silent.”\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ also said:\n “Angel Gabriel continued to advise me concerning the neighbor until I thought that Allāh would make the neighbor an inheritor.”\nThese statements show the immense emphasis Islam places on good treatment of neighbors, hospitality, and refined speech.\n[21]. The phrase “the companion at your side” has been interpreted in several ways. Many understood it to mean one’s close associate or friend, such as a travel companion, colleague, or anyone who regularly accompanies a person. Others extended it to include one’s spouse, who is literally a constant companion. The expression therefore encompasses those with whom a person shares daily interaction and companionship, highlighting the ethical duty of kindness and good conduct toward them.\n[22] .The “stranded traveler” refers to the traveler who is away from home and temporarily in need of assistance, even if he may be wealthy in his own land. Because travel in earlier times often exposed people to hardship and isolation, the Qur’an repeatedly emphasizes the duty of helping the traveler. The instruction reflects the broader Qur’anic principle of caring for those who find themselves vulnerable due to circumstance.\nThis injunction also applies to strangers, foreigners, and resident aliens belonging to other lands and nations who live among you. You must deal with them with kindness and good conduct, and you must show them love and mercy. Woe to anyone who withholds justice from foreigners, resident aliens, orphans, or widows.",
  4037: "those who are stingy and urge others to be stingy [23], and who conceal what Allāh has given them of His bounty—We have prepared for the disbelievers a humiliating punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. The phrase refers not only to personal miserliness but also to encouraging or pressuring others to withhold charity and assistance. Early commentators explain that such people discourage generosity, warn others against spending in the path of God, and sometimes conceal the blessings or knowledge that Allah has granted them so that others will not benefit. The verse therefore condemns both individual stinginess and the social promotion of miserliness, which undermines compassion, charity, and the welfare of the community.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n “Every day, two angels descend. One of them says, ‘O Allāh, give more to the one who spends in charity,’ and the other says, ‘O Allāh, bring ruin to the wealth of the one who withholds.’”\nThis ḥadīth shows that generosity leads to divine increase, while stinginess leads to the loss and ruin of one’s wealth.",
  4038: "And those who spend their wealth to show off to people, and do not believe in Allāh or the Last Day—whoever takes Satan as a companion, then what a miserable companion he has!",
  4039: "What harm would it be to them if they believed in Allāh and the Last Day and spent from what Allāh has provided them? And Allāh is All-Knowing of them.",
  4040: "Indeed, Allāh does not do injustice even as much as an atom’s weight. And if there is a good deed, He multiplies it and grants from Himself a great reward.",
  4041: "So how will it be when We bring from every people a witness and bring you, [O Prophet], as a witness against them?",
  4042: "On that Day, those who disbelieved and disobeyed the Messenger will wish that the earth were leveled over them, and they will not be able to hide anything from Allāh.",
  4043: "O believers, do not approach prayer while you are intoxicated [24], until you understand what you are saying; nor in a state of major impurity—except while passing through [a mosque]—until you perform a full bath. But if you are ill, or on a journey, or one of you comes from relieving himself, or you have touched women [25] and cannot find water, then purify yourselves with clean earth, wiping your faces and hands with it. Indeed, Allāh is Ever-Pardoning, All-Forgiving.\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. This verse represents an early restriction on prayer while intoxicated. The complete and final prohibition of consuming intoxicants came later in Qur’ān 5:90–91, where alcohol is declared an abomination of Satan’s work and must be entirely avoided.\n[25]. I.e., had sexual intercourse, which results in a state of major ritual impurity (janābah) that requires a full ritual bath (ghusl) before prayer.",
  4044: "Have you not seen those who were given a share of the Scripture, purchasing misguidance and desiring that you stray from the right path?",
  4045: "But Allāh knows your enemies best. And Allāh suffices as a Guardian and suffices as a Helper. [26]\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. Only Allāh —your Creator—truly deserves to be called Lord, Savior, or Redeemer, for all protection and help come from Him alone.",
  4046: "Among the Jews are some who distort words from their proper places, saying, ‘We hear and disobey,’ and ‘Hear—but may you never hear,’ and ‘Rāʿinā,’ twisting their tongues and reviling the religion. Had they instead said, ‘We hear and obey,’ ‘Listen,’ and ‘Look to us,’ it would have been better and more upright for them. But Allāh has cursed them for their disbelief, so only a few of them will believe.",
  4047: "O you who were given the Scripture, believe in what We have sent down, confirming what is with you, before We blot out faces and turn them backward, or curse them as We cursed the Sabbath-transgressors. And Allah’s command is ever fulfilled.",
  4048: " Indeed, Allāh does not forgive associating partners with Him, but He forgives what is less than that for whom He wills. Whoever associates partners with Allāh has indeed fabricated a grave sin.",
  4049: "Have you not seen those who claim purity for themselves [27]? Rather, it is Allāh who purifies whom He wills, and they will not be wronged in the least—not even by the speck on a date stone.\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]. This refers to certain Jews of the Prophet’s time who said, “We are the children of Allāh and His beloved ones.” The Qur’an cites this claim to correct their self-asserted purity, reminding that true purification comes only from Allāh.",
  4050: "See how they fabricate lies against Allāh—this alone is sufficient as a manifest sin. [28]\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. By boasting about themselves and claiming honour without right, they mislead their own souls. One should not exalt oneself with false claims of virtue. Do not be wise in your own eyes; rather, fear Allāh with reverent awe, follow His Messenger, the Prophet  Muhammad ﷺ, and turn wholeheartedly away from every form of evil.",
  4051: "Have you not seen those who were given a share of the Scripture? They believe in superstition and false deities [29], and they say of the disbelievers, ‘These are better guided on the way than the believers.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. This refers to al-jibt and al-ṭāghūt. These terms encompass false objects of belief and devotion—such as idols, superstitions, sorcery, devils, or any authority followed in defiance of Allāh. In Qur’ānic usage, ṭāghūt includes anything that is wrongly elevated to a position of worship or ultimate authority.\nThe statement, “These are better guided,” reflects the attitude of some among the People of the Scripture who, out of hostility, sided with the disbelievers against the Prophet ﷺ. Reports mention that certain individuals praised the Quraysh over the Prophet, despite recognizing the truth, illustrating a distortion of judgment driven by bias rather than sincerity.",
  4052: "They are the ones whom Allāh has cursed, and whoever Allāh curses, you will never find for him any helper.",
  4053: "Do they have a share in the dominion? Then they would not give people even as much as the speck on a date stone.",
  4054: "Or do they envy people for what Allāh has given them of His bounty? We had already given the family of Abraham the Scripture and wisdom, and We granted them a great kingdom.",
  4055: "Some among them believed in him [i.e., Muhammad ﷺ], while others turned away from him. And Hell is sufficient as a blazing Fire.",
  4056: "Indeed, those who disbelieve in Our signs—We will make them enter a Fire. Whenever their skins are burned through, We will replace them with new skins so they may taste the punishment. Indeed, Allāh is All-Mighty, All-Wise.",
  4057: "But those who believe and do righteous deeds—We will admit them into gardens beneath which rivers flow, abiding therein forever. They will have purified spouses therein [30], and We will admit them into a deep, pleasant shade.\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. Purified spouses refers to companions cleansed of all forms of impurity—physical, emotional, and moral. They are free from bodily impurities such as menstruation and excretion, as well as from illness, harm, and every form of imperfection. They are also purified inwardly—free from jealousy, resentment, and all negative traits—embodying complete beauty, harmony, and serenity. This purity reflects the perfection of Paradise, where all discomfort is removed and relationships are marked by peace, affection, and lasting contentment.",
  4058: "Indeed, Allāh commands you to return trusts to their rightful owners, and when you judge between people, to judge with justice [31]. Excellent indeed is what Allāh instructs you. Indeed, Allāh is All-Hearing, All-Seeing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [31]. Believers are instructed to return all trusts to their rightful owners—completely, honestly, and without delay—and to uphold faithfulness and justice in all matters. They are encouraged to pray for righteous and upright leaders: people of firm moral character, unwavering integrity, and sound judgment. The ideal leader is one who embodies justice and integrity, protects the weak, supports the needy, and stands firmly against oppression. Under such leadership, society flourishes, bringing comfort and renewal, like rain that nourishes the earth and spreads prosperity throughout the land.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, the greatest exemplar of leadership, embodied perfect justice, mercy, honesty, and integrity.",
  4059: "O believers, obey Allāh and obey the Messenger, and those in authority among you. If you disagree about anything, then refer it to Allāh and the Messenger, if you truly believe in Allāh and the Last Day. That is best and leads to the finest outcome.",
  4060: "Have you not seen those who claim to believe in what has been sent down to you and what was sent down before you? Yet they seek the judgment of false deities and authorities, though they were commanded to reject them. And Satan desires to lead them far astray.",
  4061: "When it is said to them, ‘Come to what Allāh has sent down and to the Messenger,’ you see the hypocrites turn away from you in aversion.",
  4062: "So how will it be when a disaster strikes them because of what their hands have sent ahead? Then they come to you, swearing by Allāh, ‘We intended nothing but good and reconciliation.’",
  4063: "They are the ones—Allāh knows what is in their hearts. So turn away from them, yet admonish them, and speak to them words that reach deep within themselves.",
  4064: "We sent no messenger except to be obeyed by Allah’s permission. And if, when they had wronged themselves, they had come to you and sought forgiveness from Allāh, and the Messenger had sought forgiveness for them, they would surely have found Allāh Ever-Relenting, Most Merciful.",
  4065: " But no, by your Lord, they do not truly believe until they make you the judge in their disputes, then find within themselves no discomfort regarding what you have decided, and submit in full submission.",
  4066: "If We were to decree for them, ‘Kill yourselves’ or ‘Leave your homes [32],’ they would not do it except for a very few of them. But if they had done what they were admonished with, it would surely have been better for them and more strengthening,\\n\\nFootnotes: [32]. “Kill yourselves” is understood to refer to the guilty among them, as was once commanded to the Children of Israel as a form of repentance. “Leave your homes” refers to migration for the sake of faith, abandoning one’s homeland in obedience to Allāh —similar to what was required of earlier communities.\nThe verse highlights that true faith is demonstrated through obedience: it increases with compliance and diminishes with disobedience.",
  4067: "and We would surely give them from Us a great reward,",
  4068: "and We would certainly guide them to a straight path.",
  4069: "Whoever obeys Allāh and the Messenger will be with those whom Allāh has bestowed favor upon: the prophets, the truthful, the martyrs, and the righteous. What excellent companions they are!",
  4070: "That bounty is from Allāh [33], and Allāh is sufficient as Knower.\\n\\nFootnotes: [33]. This bounty refers to the immense favor Allāh grants to those who obey Him and His Messenger—blessings that extend both in this world and in the Hereafter. Among the greatest of these favors is being raised to the noble company of the prophets, the steadfastly truthful, the martyrs, and the righteous in Paradise, sharing in their honor and companionship as a reward for sincere faith and obedience.",
  4071: "O believers, take your precautions, then march forth in detachments or march forth all together.",
  4072: " Indeed, among you is one who lags behind. Then if a calamity befalls you, he says, ‘Allāh has favored me that I was not present with them.’",
  4073: "But if bounty from Allāh comes to you, he will surely say—as though there had never been any affection between you and him—‘Oh, I wish I had been with them, so I could have attained a great triumph.’",
  4074: "So let those who exchange the life of this world for the Hereafter fight in the cause of Allāh. And whoever fights in the cause of Allāh—whether he is killed or attains victory—We will grant him a great reward.",
  4075: "What is wrong with you that you do not fight in the cause of Allāh, and for the oppressed—men, women, and children—who say, ‘Our Lord, take us out of this town whose people are tyrants, and appoint for us from Yourself a guardian and appoint for us from Yourself a helper.’",
  4076: "Those who believe fight in the cause of Allāh, while those who disbelieve fight in the cause of false deities and authorities. So fight against the allies of Satan. Indeed, the scheme of Satan is ever weak.",
  4077: "Have you not seen those who were told, ‘Restrain your hands, establish prayer, and give zakāh’ [34]? Yet when fighting was prescribed for them, a group of them feared people as they should fear Allāh —or even more. And they said, ‘Our Lord, why have You prescribed fighting for us? If only You would delay it for us for a short while.’ Say, ‘The enjoyment of this world is but brief, and the Hereafter is better for those who are mindful of Allāh. And you will not be wronged even as much as the thread on a date stone.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [34]. In the early period, the believers were commanded to refrain from fighting, as they were not yet prepared or equipped to prevail. Instead, they were instructed to focus on self-discipline—strengthening themselves through steadfast prayer and by giving zakāh, in obedience to Allah—until they were spiritually and materially ready for the struggle ahead.\nZakāh is the prescribed alms in Islam: a fixed portion of one’s wealth given to those in need. It serves not only as social support for the poor, but also as a means of purification and growth, cleansing the giver from greed and fostering compassion and responsibility within the community.",
  4078: "Wherever you may be, death will overtake you—even if you are in fortified towers. When something good happens to them, they say, ‘This is from Allāh.’ But when harm befalls them, they say, ‘This is from you.’ Say, ‘All is from Allāh.’ What is wrong with these people that they hardly understand any statement?",
  4079: "Whatever good befalls you is from Allāh, and whatever harm befalls you is from yourself. We have sent you to mankind as a messenger [35], and Allāh is sufficient as Witness.\\n\\nFootnotes: [35]. Throughout his mission, Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ carried a crystal-clear sense of purpose: to guide humanity to the Straight Path. Through him, all peoples of the earth were shown divine mercy, for he is the last and final Messenger of God—sent to lead humanity from darkness into light. The Prophet ﷺ strove tirelessly to free people from every distraction that pulled them away from devotion to Allāh. He worked to purify their hearts from confusion and inner turmoil so they could receive the spiritual nourishment Allāh has placed throughout His creation for His devoted servants. His sacred mission was to convey God’s message, teach the Qur’an and wisdom, and guide souls toward purification—away from idolatry and every form of sin.",
  4080: "Whoever obeys the Messenger has indeed obeyed Allāh. And whoever turns away, We have not sent you as a keeper over them. [36]\\n\\nFootnotes: [36]. The Prophet’s duty is only to convey the message clearly; guidance and acceptance rest with Allāh alone. This verse emphasizes that the Messenger is not responsible for compelling belief, but for faithfully delivering the revelation.\nThe Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said: “Whoever obeys me has obeyed Allāh, and whoever disobeys me has disobeyed Allāh. And whoever obeys the leader I appoint has obeyed me, and whoever disobeys him has disobeyed me.”",
  4081: "They say, ‘Obedience.’ But when they leave your presence, a group of them spend the night plotting something other than what you say. Yet Allāh records what they plot by night. So turn away from them and put your trust in Allāh —and Allāh is sufficient as Disposer of affairs.",
  4082: "Do they not then reflect upon the Qur’an? If it had been from anyone other than Allāh, they would surely have found within it much contradiction.",
  4083: "When news of security or fear comes to them, they spread it [37]. But if they had referred it to the Messenger or to those in authority among them, those who are able to draw proper conclusions from it would have known it. And had it not been for Allah’s favor upon you and His mercy, you would all have followed Satan—except a few.\\n\\nFootnotes: [37] . This verse teaches a foundational principle of Islamic governance and communal responsibility: news related to public security, danger, or military affairs must not be spread impulsively. When sensitive information reaches ordinary people, they may unintentionally spread rumors or exaggerations, causing fear, instability, or mistrust.\nInstead, Allāh commands that such matters be referred to the Messenger ﷺ and to those in authority—leaders, scholars, and individuals with the expertise to evaluate the situation. They are the ones capable of investigating it properly and determining the correct response.\nThis verse highlights the importance of:\nverification before dissemination,\nconsultation with qualified authorities,\nresponsible handling of sensitive information, and avoiding rumor, panic, and misjudgment. Without Allah’s mercy guiding the believers toward disciplined conduct, people would fall easily into the traps of Satan—rumor, fear, division, and mischief—just as previous communities did.",
  4084: "So fight in the cause of Allāh; you are responsible only for yourself. And urge the believers. It may be that Allāh will restrain the might of those who disbelieve. And Allāh is strongest in might and most severe in punishment.",
  4085: "Whoever intercedes with a good intercession will have a share of it [38], and whoever intercedes with an evil intercession will have a share of its burden. And Allāh is ever Watchful over all things.\\n\\nFootnotes: [38]. This verse teaches a profound spiritual truth: every act of support we offer—every word of encouragement, every prayer, every step taken to help someone in need—echoes in the sight of Allāh and becomes a source of reward for us. When a believer intercedes on behalf of another in a good cause, helps to lift their burden, or opens a path that brings benefit or relief, Allāh grants them a portion of the good that results from their effort. Even if their contribution was small, Allāh multiplies it out of His generosity.\nYet the verse also cautions the believer: just as one gains reward for aiding good, one also shares in the burden of any harm their help create. Whoever supports wrongdoing, spreads harm, or lends their voice or influence to injustice will bear a share of its sin before Allāh. For He, exalted is He, watches over all things—nothing of our actions, intentions, or hidden motivations escapes His perfect knowledge.\nThus, the verse encourages the believer to be ever mindful of the causes they champion and the influence they lend. It invites us to become sources of goodness, bringing relief, justice, and guidance wherever we can, knowing that Allāh —out of His mercy—credits us with every ripple of good that flows from our deeds.\nIt was narrated that whenever a person in need came to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, or when he was asked for something, he would say: “Intercede, and you will be rewarded.”",
  4086: "When you are greeted with a greeting, respond with one better than it or return it in like manner [39]. Indeed, Allāh is ever Reckoner over all things.\\n\\nFootnotes: [39]. It was narrated that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, “As-salāmu ʿalaykum (Peace be upon you).” The Prophet ﷺ returned the greeting, and the man sat down. The Prophet ﷺ said, “Ten.”\nThen another man came and said, “As-salāmu ʿalaykum wa rahmatullaah (Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allāh).” The Prophet ﷺ returned the greeting, and the man sat down. The Prophet ﷺ said, “Twenty.” Then another man came and said, “As-salāmu ʿalaykum wa rahmatullaahi wa barakātuhu (Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allāh and His blessings).” The Prophet ﷺ returned the greeting, and the man sat down. The Prophet ﷺ said, “Thirty.”\nThis shows that the reward of the greeting increases according to its completeness.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ also said:\n“When one of you meets his brother, let him greet him with the greeting of Islam: as-salāmu ʿalaykum. If a tree, a wall, or anything else comes between them, and then they meet again, let him greet him again with as-salāmu ʿalaykum.”\nThis teaches that whenever two people meet, the greeting of peace is prescribed. If they are briefly separated—whether by distance or by an object such as a tree, a car, or a pillar—and then meet again, the greeting should be renewed.\nThe same applies to leaving a gathering and returning shortly afterward. On one occasion, the Prophet ﷺ instructed a man to repeat his prayer because he had not prayed correctly. The man stepped aside to pray nearby, and when he returned, he greeted the Prophet ﷺ and the gathering again.\nThrough this, the Sunnah teaches that the greeting of peace is not a mere formality, but a repeated act of brotherhood, mercy, and mutual recognition among believers.",
  4087: "Allāh —there is no deity except Him. He will surely gather you all for the Day of Resurrection, about which there is no doubt. Who is more truthful than Allāh in speech?",
  4088: "So what is the matter with you that you have become divided into two groups concerning the hypocrites [40], while Allāh has cast them back for what they have earned? Do you wish to guide those whom Allāh has left to stray [41]? Whoever Allāh leaves astray, you will never find for him a path.\\n\\nFootnotes: [40]. That is, those hypocrites who withdrew from the Battle of Uḥud. The believers then differed concerning them: some said, “Let us fight them,” while others said, “No,” hesitating to take action against them.\n[41]. That is, because of his arrogance, deliberate rejection, and persistence in sin. Such a person chooses misguidance through his own actions, so Allāh leaves him to stray as a consequence of what he has earned of sins.",
  4089: "They wish that you would disbelieve as they have disbelieved, so that you would all be alike. So do not take them as allies until they emigrate for the sake of Allāh. But if they turn back, then seize them and kill them wherever you find them, and do not take from among them any ally or helper,",
  4090: "except those who join a people with whom you have a treaty [42], or those who come to you with hearts unwilling to fight you or to fight their own people. If Allāh had willed, He would have given them power over you, and they would have fought you [43]. But if they withdraw from you, do not fight you, and offer you peace, then Allāh has not given you any way against them.\\n\\nFootnotes: [42]. That is, believers are commanded to honor their treaties and pledges, even with those outside the faith. Covenants are binding, and their sanctity must be upheld as long as they are not violated.\n[43]. That is, Allāh could have enabled them to fight alongside your enemies, but He did not will it. Instead, He restrained them and turned them away from you, preventing conflict by His decree.\nThese clearly states the Qur’anic principles of covenant fidelity, divine sovereignty, and the restraint of violence when peace is possible.",
  4091: "You will find others who wish to be safe from you and from their own people. Whenever they are returned to idolatry, they fall into it. So if they do not withdraw from you, nor offer you peace, nor restrain their hands, then seize them and kill them wherever you find them. And against these We have given you a clear authority.",
  4092: "It is not for a believer to kill another believer except by mistake. Whoever kills a believer unintentionally must free a believing slave and pay compensation to the victim’s family, unless they waive it as an act of charity. But if the victim was a believer from a people hostile to you, then the freeing of a believing slave alone is required. And if he belonged to a people with whom you have a treaty, then compensation must be paid to his family along with the freeing of a believing slave. Whoever cannot find one must fast for two consecutive months as a repentance prescribed by Allāh. And Allāh is All-Knowing, All-Wise.",
  4093: "But whoever kills a believer intentionally—his recompense is Hell, abiding therein forever. And Allah’s wrath is upon him, and He has cursed him, and prepared for him a severe punishment. [44]\\n\\nFootnotes: [44]. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said: “A true believer remains within the spacious mercy and allowance of his religion so long as he does not spill blood unlawfully.”\nThis statement underscores the grave reality that intentional murder constricts the soul, shatters moral freedom, and places a person in extreme spiritual danger. As long as life is honored and preserved, the believer remains within the vast mercy of God—but when blood is unjustly shed, that mercy is severely imperiled.",
  4094: "O believers, when you set out in the cause of Allāh, verify carefully [45]. Do not say to one who offers you peace, ‘You are not a believer,’ seeking the gains of this worldly life—for with Allāh are abundant gains. You yourselves were once like that, then Allāh conferred His favor upon you. So verify carefully. Indeed, Allāh is ever All-Aware of all that you do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [45]. That is, before you judge anyone’s faith or label a person as a disbeliever, you are commanded to pause, verify carefully, and restrain yourself. Faith is not to be denied on suspicion, impulse, or worldly motive. Hearts belong to Allāh alone, and the believer is required to act with caution, justice, and humility—especially when life and blood are at stake.",
  4095: "Not equal are the believers who stay behind—except those with a valid excuse—and those who strive in the cause of Allāh with their wealth and their lives. Allāh has preferred those who strive with their wealth and their lives over those who stay behind by a degree. And to all Allāh has promised good, but Allāh has preferred those who strive over those who stay behind with a great reward,",
  4096: "high ranks from Him [46], and forgiveness and mercy. And Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [46]. That is, elevated stations of honor with Allāh—ranks rising one above another—each reflecting a greater measure of nearness, dignity, and reward. These degrees are not signs of pride or superiority among people, but marks of divine generosity bestowed according to sincerity, sacrifice, and devotion.",
  4097: "Indeed, those whose souls the angels take while they were wronging themselves [47]—the angels will ask, ‘What was the matter with you?’ They will reply, ‘We were oppressed in the land.’ The angels will say, ‘Was not Allah’s earth spacious enough for you to emigrate within it?’ Such people—their abode is Hell, and what an evil destination.\\n\\nFootnotes: [47]. That is, by choosing to remain among disbelievers in an environment where one is unable to practice Islam freely, even though one had the means and opportunity to emigrate. Such a choice constitutes a wrong against one’s own soul, for it places comfort, familiarity, or worldly attachment above the preservation of faith and obedience to Allāh. When the path to safeguarding belief is open, neglecting it becomes a moral responsibility, not merely a circumstance.",
  4098: "Except the oppressed among men, women, and children—those who have no means and can find no way out.",
  4099: "For those—it may be that Allāh will pardon them. And Allāh is ever Pardoning, Forgiving.",
  4100: "Whoever emigrates for the cause of Allāh will find on the earth many places of refuge and abundance. And whoever leaves his home, emigrating to Allāh and His Messenger, and then death overtakes him—his reward is assured with Allāh. And Allāh is Ever All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  4101: "When you travel through the land, there is no blame upon you in shortening the prayer [48] if you fear that those who disbelieve may harm you. Indeed, the disbelievers are a clear enemy to you.\\n\\nFootnotes: [48]. That is, during travel the prayers normally consisting of four rakʿahs are shortened to two rakʿahs. The established practice of the Prophet ﷺ and his Companions shows that this concession is linked to the state of travel itself, not to fear or danger. It is a mercy granted by Allāh to ease worship for the traveler, reflecting His desire for facilitation rather than hardship.",
  4102: "And when you are among them and lead them in prayer, let a group of them stand with you, taking their arms. Then when they have prostrated, let them move behind you, and let another group who have not yet prayed come forward and pray with you, taking their precautions and their weapons. Those who disbelieve wish that you would neglect your weapons and your baggage so they may fall upon you in a single assault. But there is no blame upon you, if you are troubled by rain or are ill, that you lay down your weapons—though you must remain vigilant and take your precautions. Indeed, Allāh has prepared for the disbelievers a humiliating punishment.",
  4103: "Then when you have completed the prayer, remember Allāh while standing, sitting, or lying on your sides [49]. Then when you are secure, establish the regular prayer [50]. Indeed, the prayer is prescribed for the believers at fixed times. [51]\\n\\nFootnotes: [49]. Believers are commanded to remember Allāh and mention Him in every state—standing, sitting, or lying down—because constant remembrance nurtures awareness of Him in the heart and draws the servant nearer to Him. This command becomes even more vital in times of hardship, danger, or fighting, for remembrance strengthens the soul, steadies the heart, and provides spiritual power that surpasses reliance on material means alone.\n[50]. That is, once safety and calm are restored, believers must establish the prayer in its complete and prescribed form, observing all its conditions, pillars, and proper timings, just as it is ordinarily required. Concessions exist only for hardship; normal circumstances call for full observance.\n[51]. This refers to the establishment of the fixed times of the five daily prayers after the ascension (Miʿrāj) of the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ. Upon his return, the Angel Jibrīl (Gabriel) came and led him in prayer at the Kaʿbah over two consecutive days. On the first day, Jibrīl prayed each prayer at its earliest time, and on the second day, he prayed each prayer at its latest permissible time. In this way, the time boundaries of the obligatory prayers were clearly defined for the Prophet ﷺ and the believers.\nThe Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said: “Gabriel led me in prayer at the House. He prayed the noon prayer when the sun had passed its zenith; he prayed the afternoon prayer when the shadow of everything was equal to it; he prayed the sunset prayer after the sun had set; he prayed the night prayer when the twilight had disappeared; and he prayed the dawn prayer at the break of dawn while it was still dark.”\nThe Prophet ﷺ also said: “Whoever sleeps through a prayer or forgets it, let him perform it as soon as he remembers.”\nThese narrations establish that prayer is a time-bound obligation, yet Allāh’s mercy remains present for human weakness, forgetfulness, and sleep.",
  4104: "Do not weaken in pursuing the enemy. If you are suffering, they too are suffering as you are suffering—but you hope from Allāh what they do not hope. And Allāh is All-Knowing, All-Wise.",
  4105: "Indeed, We have sent down to you the Book in truth, so that you may judge between people by what Allāh has shown you. So do not be an advocate for the treacherous.",
  4106: "And seek forgiveness of Allāh [52]. Indeed, Allāh is ever All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [52]. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said: “O people, seek repentance from Allāh. Truly, I seek repentance from Him one hundred times a day.”",
  4107: "And do not argue on behalf of those who betray themselves. Indeed, Allāh does not like anyone who is treacherous and sinful.",
  4108: "They seek to conceal themselves from people, but they cannot conceal themselves from Allāh. And He is with them when they spend the night saying words that do not please Him. And Allāh ever encompasses all that they do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [53]. That is, Allāh has full and perfect knowledge of what they are secretly plotting—such as attempting to justify the guilty, conceal wrongdoing, or shift blame onto the innocent. Nothing of their schemes, intentions, or whispered plans is hidden from Him, even if they believe they have concealed them from people.",
  4109: "Here you are—arguing on their behalf in the life of this world. But who will argue with Allāh for them on the Day of Resurrection, or who will be their defender?",
  4110: "Whoever commits evil or wrongs himself, then seeks forgiveness from Allāh, will find Allāh Ever-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  4111: "And whoever commits a sin earns it only against himself. And Allāh is All-Knowing, All-Wise.",
  4112: "But whoever commits an offense or a sin, then casts it upon an innocent person, has surely borne the burden of slander and a manifest sin.",
  4113: "Were it not for Allah’s grace upon you and His mercy, a group of them would have resolved to mislead you. But they mislead none except themselves, and they can cause you no harm at all. And Allāh has sent down to you the Book and wisdom and has taught you what you did not know. And the grace of Allāh upon you is ever great.",
  4114: "There is no good in much of their private counsel, except for one who enjoins charity, goodness, or reconciliation between people. And whoever does that, seeking the pleasure of Allāh, We will grant him a great reward.",
  4115: "But whoever opposes the Messenger after guidance has become clear to him, and follows other than the path of the believers—We will leave him to what he has chosen and consign him to Hell. What an evil destination.",
  4116: "Indeed, Allāh does not forgive associating partners with Him, but He forgives what is less than that for whom He wills. And whoever associates partners with Allāh has indeed gone far astray.",
  4117: "They invoke none besides Him except female deities [54], and they invoke none but a rebellious Satan. [55]\\n\\nFootnotes: [54]. In pre-Islamic Arabia, during the age of ignorance (Jāhiliyyah), many idols were given feminine names and attributes, such as al-Lāt, al-‘Uzzā, and Manāt. These were not women, but carved objects and symbolic representations that were personified with feminine titles and invoked as intercessors or deities.\nThe Qur’anic reference to “female [deities]” addresses this naming and conceptual practice, not women themselves. It critiques the religious inconsistency of attributing divine power to created forms while denying the Creator.\nComparable patterns of assigning feminine identities to divine figures can also be observed in other religious traditions across history, where named female idols or goddesses are venerated as representations of power, fertility, protection, or prosperity. The Qur’an’s critique, however, is religious rather than ethnic or cultural: it rejects all forms of idolatry—male or female—by affirming that worship belongs to Allāh alone.\n[55]. That is, their devotion to false idols is not based on truth or divine authority, but arises from Satan’s deception and manipulation. He entices people by appealing to desires, fears, traditions, and false hopes, gradually leading them away from sincere worship of Allāh. Thus, idolatry is portrayed not merely as an intellectual error, but as the result of moral and spiritual seduction—where Satan redirects acts of worship from the Creator to created forms that possess no real power.",
  4118: "Whom Allāh has cursed—who said, ‘I will surely take for myself an appointed portion of Your servants.’",
  4119: "“I will surely mislead them, arouse in them false hopes, and command them so they will slit the ears of cattle; and I will command them so they will alter the creation of Allāh.” Whoever takes Satan as a protector instead of Allāh has indeed suffered a clear and manifest loss.\\n\\nFootnotes: [56]. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ condemned practices that deliberately alter the natural form created by Allāh for the sake of vanity, superstition, or imitation. He said that Allāh has cursed those who tattoo themselves or others, those who file or alter their teeth for beautification, and those who pluck eyebrows in a manner that distorts the natural form—both the one who performs these acts and the one for whom they are done—because they involve changing the creation of Allāh.\nSuch practices were common in pre-Islamic cultures as expressions of false beliefs, ritual identity, or exaggerated beautification. Islam came to preserve the dignity, balance, and natural disposition (fiṭrah) of the human being, and therefore prohibits intentional bodily alterations that serve no legitimate need and are rooted in vanity or imitation of misguided customs.\nAccordingly, tattoos and similar forms of bodily mutilation or alteration are forbidden in Islam, and believers are commanded to abstain from them in obedience to Allāh, honoring the body as a trust rather than an object of manipulation.\nThis ruling concerns unnecessary, permanent alterations for adornment or superstition. It does not apply to:\n- Medical treatment\n- Necessary surgery\n'- Corrective procedures restoring normal function or appearance.\nThe guiding principle is: Preserving the natural creation of Allāh while allowing necessity, healing, and genuine benefit.",
  4120: "He promises them and arouses in them false hopes, but Satan promises them nothing except delusion.",
  4121: "Those—their abode will be Hell, and they will find no escape from it.",
  4122: "But those who believe and do righteous deeds—We will admit them into gardens beneath which rivers flow, abiding therein forever. This is a true promise from Allāh. And who is more truthful than Allāh in speech?",
  4123: "It is not by your wishful thinking, nor by the wishful thinking of the People of the Scripture. Whoever does evil [57] will be requited for it [58], and will find no protector or helper besides Allāh.\\n\\nFootnotes: [57]. That is, grave sins, such as associating partners with Allāh in worship, murder, sexual immorality, theft, taking or giving interest (usury), consuming intoxicants, lying, or disobeying the Messenger ﷺ in matters of religious command, and similar offenses.\n[58]. Salvation and the attainment of good in both this life and the Hereafter are achieved through firm and sincere belief in Allāh, exclusive worship of Him, without associating partners with Him, and following the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ—Allah’s final Messenger—not through mere wishful thinking.",
  4124: "Whoever does righteous deeds, whether male or female [59], while being a believer—such will enter Paradise and will not be wronged in the least.\\n\\nFootnotes: [59]. Islam makes no distinction between men and women in matters of faith, nor in the religious duties prescribed for them, nor in accountability, reward, or punishment for what they are commanded to do.",
  4125: "And who is better in religion than one who fully submits to Allāh, does good, and follows the way of Abraham—inclining to pure monotheism? And Allāh took Abraham as a close, beloved friend.",
  4126: "To Allāh belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth, and Allāh ever encompasses all things.",
  4127: "They ask you about women. Say, ‘Allāh gives you a ruling concerning them—and concerning what is recited to you in the Book about orphan girls to whom you do not give what has been prescribed for them, though you desire to marry them; and concerning the oppressed among children; and that you deal justly with orphans [60]. And whatever good you do—surely Allāh is ever All-Knowing of it.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [60]. This refers to the obligation to deal justly with orphans—especially orphan girls under one’s care—by safeguarding their rights, including fair treatment, lawful dowries, and inheritance, and by avoiding any form of exploitation or injustice toward them. A believing household must be founded on preserving the dignity and full rights of all its members. Any neglect in this regard can lead to discord and disunity, with the most vulnerable members of the family — and society at large — suffering the most. As the basic building block of a strong, cohesive society, the integrity and wholesomeness of the family must be protected at all costs.",
  4128: "If a woman fears ill-treatment or abandonment from her husband, there is no blame on them if they reconcile between themselves—for reconciliation is best [61]. And human souls are prone to avarice. But if you do good and are mindful of Allāh, then Allāh is surely All-Aware of what you do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [61] . This verse permits a mutually agreed reconciliation between spouses when marital discord arises, including cases of neglect or estrangement. Such reconciliation may involve reasonable concessions made voluntarily by either party in order to preserve harmony and avoid injustice. The verse also acknowledges human self-interest, while encouraging kindness, fairness, and God-conscious conduct—since Allāh is fully aware of all actions and will hold people accountable. Peaceful settlement is better than breaking up and separation.",
  4129: "You will never be able to maintain perfect equality between wives, no matter how eager you may be [62]; so do not incline completely toward one, leaving the other as though suspended. But if you make amends and are mindful of Allah, then Allah is ever All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [62]. A man who has more than one wife will inevitably feel a greater inclination in his heart toward one over another. For this reason, men are reminded to be mindful of Allāh and to exercise moderation and fairness in their conduct. While emotional inclination is beyond human control, it is obligatory for a man to be strictly just and even-handed in fulfilling each wife’s legally prescribed marital rights. If he is unable to maintain such justice, he is commanded to restrict himself to one wife.\nIf a man has more than one wife, he must treat them equally in all practical matters of obligation and conduct, whether the wives are Muslim or from among the People of the Book (i.e., Jewish or Christian). Failing to do so constitutes disobedience to Allāh, and he is required to rectify the injustice done to the wronged wife.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: “Whoever has two wives and inclines toward one of them will come on the Day of Resurrection with half of his body leaning.” This “inclination” refers to favoritism in actions and treatment. A man is not held accountable for the natural inclination of his heart so long as he maintains equality and justice in outward conduct.",
  4130: "But if they separate, Allāh will enrich each of them from His abundance. And Allāh is ever All-Encompassing, All-Wise.",
  4131: "To Allāh belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth. We have enjoined upon those who were given the Scripture before you—and upon you—to fear Allāh. But if you deny, then to Allāh belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth. And Allāh is ever Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy.",
  4132: "To Allāh belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth, and Allāh is sufficient as Disposer of affairs.[63]\\n\\nFootnotes: [63]. That is, Allāh alone is fully sufficient as the Guardian and Manager of all affairs. He oversees, sustains, and governs all creation with perfect wisdom and power, and no being shares in His authority or is independent of Him. Therefore, reliance and trust are to be placed in Him alone.\nWakīl (Al-Wakīl) is one of the most Beautiful Names of Allāh. It means, the Sustainer of the Universe, its Preserver, the Dispenser of its affairs with His Knowledge and Wisdom, and the Witness of everything, Glory be His. This Name entails that Allāh is the Most Trustworthy, the Dependable Advocate of perfect resolution.",
  4133: "If He wills, He can remove you, O people, and bring others in your place. And Allāh is ever All-Powerful to do so.",
  4134: "Whoever desires the reward of this world—then with Allāh is the reward of this world and the Hereafter [64]. And Allāh is ever All-Hearing, All-Seeing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [64]. People who are heedless of Allāh are too eager to gain that which is worldly and think not of the life to come. This verse reminds that all reward ultimately lies with Allāh alone. Whoever seeks worldly gain should know that Allāh is the One who grants success in this life as well as reward in the Hereafter. Therefore, believers are encouraged to align their intentions with what pleases Allāh, striving for good in this world without neglecting the lasting reward of the life to come.",
  4135: "O believers, stand firmly for justice [65], bearing witness for the sake of Allāh, even if it be against yourselves, your parents, or your close relatives. Whether one is rich or poor, Allāh is more worthy of both. So do not follow your inclinations, lest you deviate. And if you distort your testimony or refuse to give it, then indeed Allāh is ever All-Aware of what you do.” [66]\\n\\nFootnotes: [65]. This command requires believers to uphold justice consistently and actively in all matters, without bias or favoritism. Justice here entails truthfulness, fairness, and integrity in judgment and testimony, regardless of personal interest, social status, or family ties. This reinforces the main principle on which the believing community is built: justice to all. \n[66]. This warning applies to both distorting testimony and withholding it when required. Concealing the truth, manipulating evidence, or refusing to bear witness constitutes a grave moral offense, for Allāh is fully aware of all actions and intentions and will hold people accountable for them.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ was asked about the major sins, and he said: \n“They are:\n(a) Associating others with Allāh in worship;\n(b) Being undutiful to one’s parents;\n(c) Killing a person whom Allāh has forbidden to be killed (i.e., murder);\n(d) And false testimony.”",
  4136: "O believers, believe in Allāh, His Messenger, the Book He has sent down to His Messenger, and the Scriptures He sent down before. And whoever denies Allāh, His angels, His Books, His Messengers, and the Last Day has indeed gone far astray.",
  4137: "Indeed, those who believe, then disbelieve, then believe again, and then disbelieve, and thereafter increase in disbelief—Allāh will never forgive them, nor will He guide them to the right path.",
  4138: "Give tidings to the hypocrites of a painful punishment,",
  4139: "those who take the disbelievers as allies instead of the believers—do they seek honor with them? Then indeed, all honor belongs to Allāh. [67]\\n\\nFootnotes: [67]. That is, true honor, power, dignity, and authority belong solely to Allāh. Being the source of all power and honor, Allāh grants them to whom He wills. Seeking strength, protection, or status through allegiance to disbelievers—at the expense of faith and loyalty to the believers—is a form of misplaced reliance, for no lasting honor can be attained except through Allāh \nalone, seeking them from others is ultimately futile.",
  4140: "He has already sent down to you in the Book that when you hear the verses of Allāh being denied and mocked, do not sit with them until they engage in another discourse; otherwise, you would be like them. Indeed, Allāh will gather the hypocrites and the disbelievers all together in Hell.\\n\\nFootnotes: [68]. This command prohibits remaining in gatherings where the verses of Allāh are denied, ridiculed, or mocked, as continued presence implies approval or acceptance of such behavior. The instruction applies when one is unable to stop the wrongdoing; if the discourse changes, the prohibition no longer applies. The verse does not forbid lawful interaction with non-believers in general, but rather warns against moral complicity in disbelief and mockery.\nBlessed is the man who does not walk in the counsel of the wicked, or set foot on the path of sinners, or sit in the seat of mockers.",
  4141: "Those who wait and watch concerning you—if a victory comes to you from Allāh, they say, ‘Were we not with you?’ But if the disbelievers gain a share, they say, ‘Did we not side with you and protect you from the believers?’ So Allāh will judge between you all on the Day of Resurrection, and Allāh will never grant the disbelievers a way against the believers.[69]\\n\\nFootnotes: [69]. “A way” refers to ultimate authority, decisive dominance, or conclusive proof against the believers. This promise does not negate temporary trials, defeats, or oppression in this world, but affirms that Allāh will never grant the disbelievers final victory, lasting authority, or ultimate justification over the believers—especially in the Hereafter, where judgment will be rendered with complete justice.",
  4142: "Indeed, the hypocrites seek to deceive Allāh, but He turns their deceit back upon them. When they stand for prayer, they stand lazily, showing off to the people, and they do not remember Allāh except a little.[70]\\n\\nFootnotes: [70]. That is, their remembrance of Allāh is minimal and insincere. They engage in acts of worship outwardly to be seen by others, not out of genuine faith or devotion. Their hearts are largely heedless, and they remember Allāh only when it serves their worldly interests. This describes a lack of sincerity and presence of heart in worship.",
  4143: "Wavering in between—belonging neither to the believers nor to the disbelievers [71]. And whoever Allāh leaves astray, you will never find for them a way.\\n\\nFootnotes: [71]. This describes the state of hypocrisy: persistent indecision and lack of commitment, oscillating between belief and disbelief for worldly advantage. \nThe hypocrites live in a state of perpetual doubt and constant perplexity, so they are always experiencing a state of dilemma.\nSuch people align themselves wherever benefit appears, without sincere loyalty to faith or truth. When this condition is willfully maintained, Allāh leaves them to their chosen misguidance, and no guidance can be found for them apart from sincere repentance.",
  4144: "O believers, do not take the disbelievers as allies instead of the believers. Do you wish to give Allāh a clear case against yourselves?",
  4145: "Indeed, the hypocrites will be in the lowest depths of the Fire [72], and you will never find for them any helper,\\n\\nFootnotes: [72]. Hell is a place of divine judgment for sinners, with varying degrees of punishment based on several factors in a person’s life. These include the extent to which one surrendered to sin, the influence one had on others to commit sin, and the knowledge of the truth one possessed and rejected. This does not mean that any punishment in Hell will be less than complete, but the Qur’an indicates that some will endure greater suffering or bear a fiercer measure of Allah’s wrath.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: \"The worst people before Allāh on the Day of Judgment will be the double-faced people who appear to some people with one face and to other people with another face.\"",
  4146: "except those who repent, mend their ways, hold fast to Allāh, and are sincere in their faith for Allāh alone—such are with the believers. And Allāh will grant the believers a great reward.",
  4147: "Why would Allāh punish you if you are grateful and believe? For Allāh is ever Appreciative, All-Knowing. [73]\\n\\nFootnotes: [73]. Appreciative means that Allāh fully acknowledges, values, and abundantly rewards even the smallest acts of gratitude and faith. He does not punish without cause; rather, His punishment is only for persistent ingratitude and disbelief. This verse highlights that sincere gratitude and faith are paths to divine mercy, not punishment, and that no good deed goes unnoticed by Him.",
  4148: "Allāh does not like the utterance of evil speech in public except by one who has been wronged [75]. And Allāh is ever All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [74]. “Evil speech ” include abusive speech, foul mouthing, being profane, hurling accusations, slandering, backbiting, insults, and other forms of harmful or indecent language spoken openly. This verse establishes a general prohibition against public verbal wrongdoing, emphasizing dignity, restraint, and moral responsibility in speech.\n[75]. An exception is made for one who has been wronged, allowing the oppressed to speak out in complaint or defense to seek justice or relief. This permission does not justify excess, false accusation, or injustice in return, but is limited to what is necessary and truthful.",
  4149: "If you do good openly or conceal it, or pardon a wrong [76], then indeed Allāh is ever Pardoning, Most Capable.\\n\\nFootnotes: [76]. This is mainly addressed to the one who is wronged. This verse encourages moral excellence by highlighting three virtuous responses: doing good openly to inspire others, concealing good to preserve sincerity, and pardoning personal wrongs to rise above retaliation. Allah’s attribute of being All-Pardoning reflects His willingness to forgive, while Most Powerful affirms that forgiveness is an act of strength, not weakness.\nThis underscores the principle that while harmful speech is generally discouraged in Islam, exceptions are made for those who have been wronged, allowing them to speak out to seek justice. The emphasis is on ensuring that speech serves a constructive purpose, such as rectifying injustice, rather than spreading harm.",
  4150: "Indeed, those who disbelieve in Allāh and His Messengers, and seek to make a distinction between Allāh and His Messengers, saying, ‘We believe in some and disbelieve in others,’ and who wish to take a way in between— [77]\\n\\nFootnotes: [77]. Thinking that such a ‘compromise’ would put them on the road that leads to God’s Pleasure and salvation. Had they really believed in some Messengers of Allāh and listened to them with due care and attention, they would naturally have believed in the rest of the Messengers.\nThis refers to selectively accepting some messengers while rejecting others, claiming a middle position between belief and disbelief. The Qur’an rejects this approach, affirming that true faith requires believing in Allāh and all of His messengers without distinction. Partial belief is treated as disbelief because it denies Allah’s authority to choose and send whom He wills.",
  4151: "It is they who are the true disbelievers, and We have prepared for the disbelievers a humiliating punishment.",
  4152: "But those who believe in Allāh and His Messengers [78], and do not make a distinction between any of them—He will grant them their rewards. And Allāh is ever All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [78]. Whose last is the Prophet Muhammad  ﷺ. That is, true belief requires affirming all of Allah’s messengers without rejecting or diminishing any of them. While their laws differed according to their times, their message of monotheism was one. Allāh promises forgiveness and mercy to those who uphold this complete and undivided faith.",
  4153: "The People of the Scripture ask you to bring down to them a written document from heaven. They had asked Moses for something even greater than that, when they said, ‘Show us Allāh openly.’ So the thunderbolt seized them for their wrongdoing. Then they took the calf for worship after clear signs had come to them; yet We pardoned them, and We granted Moses a clear authority.",
  4154: "And We raised the mountain above them for their breach of the covenant, and We said to them, ‘Enter the gate bowing down in humility [79]’; and We said to them, ‘Do not transgress regarding the Sabbath.’ And We took from them a solemn and binding covenant.\\n\\nFootnotes: [79]. This refers to the command given to the Children of Israel to enter the city gate in a state of humility and submission to Allāh, acknowledging their covenantal obligations. This act symbolized repentance, obedience, and acceptance of divine authority, rather than mere physical posture.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: \"It was said to the children of Israel, ‘Enter the gate (of the town) bowing with humility and say: ‘Repentance’, but they changed the word and entered crawling on their buttocks and said: ‘A wheat grain in the hair’.\" They said so just to ridicule Allah’s order, as they were disobedient to Him. So Allāh punished them severely, most probably with a plague.",
  4155: "So for their breaking of the covenant, their disbelief in the revelations of Allāh, their killing of the prophets without right, and their saying, ‘Our hearts are wrapped’—rather, Allāh has set a seal upon them because of their disbelief, so they do not believe except a few –",
  4156: "and for their disbelief, and for their utterance against Mary—a tremendous slander. [80]\\n\\nFootnotes: [80]. This refers to the grave slander in which some among the Jews falsely accused Mary (Maryam), the mother of Jesus (ʿĪsā), of fornication and unchastity. The Qur’ān condemns this as a tremendous falsehood, affirming her purity, honor, and righteousness.",
  4157: "and for their saying, ‘We killed the Messiah, Jesus, son of Mary, the Messenger of Allāh.’ But they neither killed him nor crucified him—it was only made to appear so to them [81]. Indeed, those who differ about it are in doubt, having no certain knowledge, but only following conjecture. And they certainly did not kill him.\\n\\nFootnotes: [81]. This verse categorically denies that Jesus (ʿĪsā), peace be upon him, was killed or crucified. The phrase “it was made to appear so to them” indicates that the reality of the event was obscured from those who claimed responsibility, while Allāh protected His messenger. This affirms that Jesus was not killed, and that claims to the contrary are based on conjecture rather than certain knowledge. The precise manner of what occurred is known fully only to Allāh.",
  4158: "Rather, Allāh raised him up to Himself [82]. And Allāh is ever All-Mighty, All-Wise.\\n\\nFootnotes: [82]. This affirms that Allāh raised Jesus (ʿĪsā), peace be upon him, to Himself, saving him from being killed or crucified. The raising signifies divine protection and honor. Islamic belief holds that Jesus was not killed, but was taken up by Allāh, and that he will return before the Day of Resurrection by Allah’s will. Allah’s being All-Mighty and All-Wise underscores that this act occurred through His absolute power and perfect wisdom.",
  4159: "There is none among the People of the Scripture but that he will surely believe in him before his death [83]; and on the Day of Resurrection, he will be a witness against them.\\n\\nFootnotes: [83]. \"Before his death\" refers to the death of Jesus (ʿĪsā), peace be upon him, meaning that all People of the Scripture who are alive at the time of his return will believe in him as a true messenger of Allāh before he dies. Another interpretation understands the phrase to mean that each person will come to recognize the truth about Jesus before his or her own death, though this belief will then be of no benefit to them. In either case, Jesus will testify on the Day of Resurrection regarding what they believed and how they responded to the truth.",
  4160: "So for the wrongdoing of the Jews, We prohibited for them certain good things that had previously been lawful for them, and for their frequent hindering of many from the way of Allāh.",
  4161: "And for their taking usury, though they had been forbidden from it, and for their consuming people’s wealth unjustly. We have prepared for the disbelievers among them a painful punishment.",
  4162: "But those firmly grounded in knowledge among them, and the believers, believe in what has been sent down to you and what was sent down before you; who establish prayer, give the poor-rate, and believe in Allāh and the Last Day—these We will grant a great reward.",
  4163: "Surely, We have revealed to you as We revealed to Noah and the prophets after him. And We revealed to Abraham, Ishmael, Isaac, Jacob, and the Tribes; and to Jesus, Job, Jonah, Aaron, and Solomon; and to David We gave the Psalms.",
  4164: "And messengers whose stories We have already related to you, and messengers whose stories We have not related to you. And Allāh spoke to Moses directly.",
  4165: "These messengers were sent as bearers of glad tidings [84] and as warners [85], so that people would have no excuse before Allāh after the coming of the messengers. And Allāh is All-Mighty, All-Wise.\\n\\nFootnotes: [84]. Of eternal Bliss for the believers. That is, messengers were sent to give glad tidings of reward, forgiveness, and Paradise to those who believe and obey Allāh.\n[85]. Of grievous punishment from God for the disbelievers. That is, messengers were also sent to warn of punishment and accountability for disbelief and disobedience, thereby establishing clear proof and removing any excuse of ignorance.",
  4166: "But Allāh bears witness to what He has sent down to you. He sent it down with His knowledge, and the angels also bear witness. And Allāh is sufficient as a Witness.",
  4167: "Indeed, those who disbelieve and hinder others from the way of Allāh have certainly gone far astray.",
  4168: "Indeed, those who disbelieve and do wrong—Allāh will not forgive them, nor will He guide them to a way — [86]\\n\\nFootnotes: [86]. The wrongdoing mentioned here includes acts that accompany disbelief, such as actively opposing the truth and hindering others from the way of Allāh. It refers to persistent injustice and obstruction that prevent others from receiving guidance.",
  4169: "except the way of Hell, abiding therein forever. And that is ever easy for Allāh.",
  4170: "O people, the Messenger has come to you with the truth from your Lord, so believe—it is better for you. But if you disbelieve, then to Allāh belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on earth. And Allāh is ever All-Knowing, All-Wise.",
  4171: "O People of the Scripture, do not go to extremes in your religion [87], and do not say about Allāh anything except the truth. The Messiah, Jesus son of Mary, was only a messenger of Allāh, and His Word which He conveyed to Mary, and a spirit from Him. So believe in Allāh and His Messengers, and do not say, ‘Three.[88]’ Desist—it is better for you. Indeed, Allāh is only One God. Glory be to Him—far exalted is He above having a son. To Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth. And Allāh is sufficient as Disposer of affairs.\\n\\nFootnotes: [87]. “Do not go to extremes” refers to religious excess: exaggerating beliefs, elevating human beings beyond their rightful status, or introducing claims and practices not sanctioned by Allāh. The verse calls the People of the Scripture to moderation, truthfulness, and adherence to pure monotheism, without distortion or overstatement, such as attributing divine qualaties to certain creations of Allāh or revering them excessively.The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: ''Do not exaggerate in praising me as Christians praised Jesus, the son of Mary, for I am only a servant. So, call me the servant of God and His messenger.\" Out of his concern for pristine monotheism and fear that his followers would lapse into associating partners with Allāh like previous nations, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ  warned Muslims against exaggerating in praising him, as Christians did concerning Jesus by giving him divine attributes and calling him God or the son of God, thus falling into idolatry. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ further said, \"Describe me as the servant of God and the carrier of His message, just as He, Exalted be He, described me. Do not transgress the limits by raising me to a station of divinity or lordship as the Christians did with regard to Jesus.\" Divinity is exclusive to Allāh alone.\n[88]. “Do not say, \"Three\" is a prohibition of attributing plurality or division to God—whether expressed as “Trinity” or any formulation that compromises Allah’s absolute oneness. The Qur’an affirms that Jesus (ʿĪsā), son of Mary, is the Messiah and a messenger of Allāh; “His word” means he was created by Allah’s command (“Be”), and “a spirit from Him” means a spirit created by Allāh and bestowed by His will—without implying that Jesus shares in divinity. The verse therefore commands belief in Allāh and His messengers and rejects describing God in triune terms.\nThe Christians claim that God is one being and three persons: God the Father, God the son,  and God the holy spirit.\n They claim that Jesus is the \"son of God\", \"God the son\", \"God incarnate\", or  the second \"person\" in the unholy Trinity. This necessarily reduces God \"Allāh\" to being one third of the Trinity. In truth, nowhere in the Gospels does Jesus make such claims for himself, nor, for that matter, is there any mention of the Trinity. In fact, Jesus always spoke of himself as the \"son of man, and the servant of God and His messenger.\"\nThat is, Jesus’s spirit was created by Allāh. He (Jesus) is a soul just like any other soul but Allāh added him to Himself to honor him. Because Jesus was created by a blow (of air), not by a drop (of sperm), he was called a ‘soul’.” The Qur’an makes it clear that Jesus is just like all other human beings, whose spirit was created by Allāh.",
  4172: "The Messiah would never disdain to be a servant of Allāh, nor would the angels who are near to Him. And those who disdain to worship Him and are arrogant—He will gather them all to Himself.",
  4173: "As for those who believe and do righteous deeds, He will give them their rewards in full and grant them even more from His bounty. But as for those who disdain His worship and act arrogantly—He will punish them with a painful punishment, and they will find for themselves no protector or helper besides Allāh.",
  4174: "O people, there has come to you a clear proof from your Lord, and We have sent down to you a clear Light. [89]\\n\\nFootnotes: [89]. The “clear proof” refers to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ himself—his message, character, and the signs supporting his prophethood—while the “clear Light” refers to the Qur’an, which illuminates the path of faith, guidance, and truth. Together, they leave no excuse for ignorance or misguidance for those who sincerely seek the truth.",
  4175: "As for those who believe in Allāh and hold fast to Him, He will admit them into His mercy and bounty [90], and guide them to Himself along a straight path.\\n\\nFootnotes: [90]. His mercy and bounty refer to Allah’s forgiveness, guidance, and entry into Paradise, along with additional grace beyond what deeds alone would merit. Holding fast to Allāh means clinging firmly to faith, obedience, and reliance upon Him alone. Such people are granted divine mercy in this life and the Hereafter, and are guided to a straight path that leads to Allah’s pleasure.",
  4176: "They ask you for a ruling. Say, ‘Allāh gives you a ruling concerning one who leaves neither parents nor offspring. If a man dies leaving no child but a sister, she shall inherit half of what he leaves, and he shall inherit from her if she has no child. But if there are two sisters, they shall inherit two-thirds of what he leaves. And if there are siblings [91], both male and female, then the male shall receive the share of two females.’ Allāh makes this clear to you so that you do not go astray. And Allāh is All-Knowing of all things.\\n\\nFootnotes: [91]. This verse relates to siblings from the father's side or from both parents, whereas in Verse 12 in this chapter it concerned those from the mother's side alone.\nThat is, siblings who share the same father and mother, or the same father. In such cases of inheritance where the deceased has neither parents nor offspring (kalālah), male siblings receive a share equal to that of two female siblings, in accordance with the Qur’anic principle of proportional distribution tied to financial responsibility. This ruling applies only after any debts and bequests have been settled.",
  5001: "O believers, fulfill your contracts. Lawful for you are livestock animals, except what is recited to you, but hunting is forbidden while you are in the state of ihrām. Allāh judges whatever He wills. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. “Fulfill your covenants” includes not only the covenants one makes with Allāh, but also the agreements, promises, contracts, and obligations undertaken with others—such as pledges, vows, oaths, and all solemn commitments of word and intention. These carry both a spiritual weight, as they are accountable before Allāh, and a moral responsibility, as they uphold trust, integrity, and justice in human relationships.\n“In the state of ihrām” refers to the sacred condition entered during the major pilgrimage (ḥajj) or the lesser pilgrimage (‘umrah), in which certain actions—such as hunting—are prohibited.",
  5002: "O believers, do not violate the sanctity of the rites of Allāh, nor the sacred months [2], nor the sacrificial animals, nor those marked with garlands, nor those heading to the Sacred House, seeking the bounty of their Lord and His pleasure. But when you are released from the state of ihrām, then you may hunt. And do not let the hatred of a people—who once barred you from the Sacred Mosque—lead you to transgress.\nHelp one another in righteousness and piety, and do not help one another in sin and aggression. And fear Allāh; indeed, Allāh is severe in punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The four sacred months are Dhul-Qiʿdah, Dhul-Hijjah, Muḥarram, and Rajab. During these months in particular, violations of sanctity—including fighting—are especially prohibited, emphasizing peace, safety, and reverence. In general, injustice and transgression are forbidden at all times.",
  5003: "Prohibited to you are carrion [3], blood [4], pork, and that which has been slaughtered in the name of other than Allāh; that which has been killed by strangling [5], by a violent blow, by a fall, or by the goring of horns; and that from which wild beasts have eaten—except what you are able to slaughter; and what is slaughtered upon stone altars; and that you seek decisions through divining arrows—this is disobedience [6]. This day those who disbelieve have despaired of your religion, so do not fear them, but fear Me. This day I have perfected for you your religion, completed My favor upon you, and approved for you Islam as your religion.\nBut whoever is compelled by severe hunger [7], without inclining to sin—then indeed, Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. “Carrion” means an animal that has died without lawful slaughter. Fish and other sea animals are permitted even if found dead, based on prophetic reports.\n[4]. “Blood” here refers to flowing/shed blood. Blood that remains in the veins and flesh after proper slaughter is excused and does not make the meat unlawful.\n[5]. Animals killed by strangling or by any method that causes death without the proper outflow of blood are unlawful to eat. Lawful slaughter involves cutting the throat in a prescribed manner so the animal’s blood drains. The purpose is both devotional obedience and avoidance of consuming flowing blood, which is prohibited.\n[6]. Before Islam, some Arabs used marked arrows (or lots) for decision-making and fortune-seeking. A person would draw a lot indicating “do,” “do not,” or a blank/neutral outcome and repeat the draw. The Qur’an forbids this practice as a form of pagan divination.\n[7] If a person is compelled by severe hunger or necessity and has no lawful alternative, they may eat only what is needed to preserve life, without seeking the unlawful out of desire or transgression.",
  5004: "They ask you what is lawful for them. Say, ‘Lawful for you are all pure things [8], and what is caught by your trained hunting animals—training them as Allāh has taught you. So eat of what they catch for you [9], but mention the name of Allāh over it. And fear Allāh; indeed, Allāh is swift in reckoning.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. “Pure things” refers to foods that are lawful, wholesome, and beneficial, causing no harm to the body or the intellect. This includes what is morally and physically sound, as understood through divine law and human well-being.\n[9]. This ruling is clarified by a report from a Companion of the Prophet ﷺ, who said: “O Messenger of Allāh, I set my dog upon game and mention Allah’s name.” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “If you set your dog and mention Allah’s name, and it catches, kills, and eats from the prey, then do not eat it, for it caught it for itself.” The Companion then said: “Sometimes I set my dog, and another dog joins it, and I do not know which of them caught the prey.” He ﷺ replied: “Then do not eat it, for you mentioned Allah’s name over your dog, not the other.\"\nThe Prophet ﷺ ruled that game caught by a trained animal is lawful only if Allah’s name is mentioned and the animal hunts on behalf of its owner. If the animal eats from the prey, or if an untrained or unknown animal joins in, the prey is unlawful.",
  5005: "This day all pure things have been made lawful for you [10]. The food of those who were given the Scripture is lawful for you, and your food is lawful for them.\nAnd lawful for you in marriage are chaste women among the believers and chaste women from among those who were given the Scripture before you—provided that you give them their dowries in lawful wedlock, not committing fornication nor taking them as secret lovers. And whoever denies the faith [11]—his deeds have indeed come to nothing, and in the Hereafter he will be among the losers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. This ruling excludes categories of food explicitly forbidden elsewhere in the Qur’an. The People of the Scripture (Jews and Christians) are regarded in Islamic law as followers of earlier monotheistic revelations. For this reason, their lawful slaughter and, under specific conditions, marriage to their chaste women are permitted to Muslims—unlike idolaters, whose slaughtered animals and marriage alliances are not lawful.\n[11]. “Denying the faith” refers to rejecting belief after it has been made clear, or nullifying faith through disbelief. This may include acts that fundamentally contradict faith—such as the deliberate abandonment of the five obligatory daily prayers or forming prohibited allegiances with disbelievers—depending on belief, intention, and circumstance. The verse warns that faith is the foundation upon which all deeds derive their value",
  5006: "O believers, when you rise for prayer, wash your faces and your hands up to the elbows, and wipe your heads, and wash your feet up to the ankles. If you are in a state of major impurity, then purify yourselves [by taking a bath]. But if you are ill, or on a journey, or one of you has relieved himself [12], or you have had contact with women [13] and cannot find water—then perform tayammum with clean earth [14], and wipe your faces and hands with it. Allāh does not wish to place you in hardship, but He desires to purify you and to complete His favor upon you, so that you may be grateful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. Relieving oneself: This refers to the discharge of urine, feces, or intestinal wind, all of which nullify ritual purity (wuḍūʾ).\n[13]. Contact with women: Many scholars interpret this expression as a euphemism for sexual intercourse. Others understand it to include physical contact. Differences in interpretation have led to differing legal rulings.\n[14]. Tayammum (dry ablution): Tayammum is a form of dry purification performed with clean earth when water is unavailable or harmful to use. It serves as a substitute for wuḍūʾ or ritual bathing (ghusl) in cases of necessity.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ emphasized the spiritual virtue of ritual purification, saying:  “No one preserves wuḍūʾ except a believer.”\nHe ﷺ also said:\n “When a Muslim performs wuḍūʾ and washes his face, every sin committed by the eyes leaves with the water. When he washes his hands, every sin committed by the hands leaves with the water. When he washes his feet, every sin his feet walked toward leaves with the water—until he emerges purified from sins.” This refers to minor sins.",
  5007: "And remember Allah’s favor upon you and His covenant by which He bound you [15], when you said, ‘We hear and we obey.’ And fear Allāh; indeed, Allāh is All-Knowing of what is within the chests.\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. This verse reminds the believers of their binding covenant with Allāh —a covenant that includes obedience to Him and adherence to His divinely ordained laws. It lies at the heart of the believing community, shaping faith, conduct, and collective responsibility.",
  5008: "O believers, be steadfast for Allāh as witnesses in justice [16], and do not let the hatred of a people lead you away from justice. Be just—that is nearer to piety. And fear Allāh; indeed, Allāh is All-Aware of what you do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. That is, to be devoted and steadfast upholders of Allah’s rights, acting sincerely for His sake alone, without seeking fame, recognition, or worldly gain.",
  5009: "Allāh has promised those who believe and do righteous deeds forgiveness and a great reward.",
  5010: "But those who disbelieve and deny Our signs—such are the companions of the Hellfire.",
  5011: "O believers, remember Allah’s favor upon you—when some people [17] intended to stretch out their hands against you, but He withheld their hands from you. So fear Allāh, and in Allāh let the believers put their trust.\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. This refers to an incident in which a group from among the People of the Scripture plotted harm against the believers, but Allāh thwarted their intent. The verse reminds believers to maintain God-consciousness and to recognize that all protection ultimately comes from Allāh. While planning and effort are part of human responsibility, true reliance rests solely in Allah’s wisdom, power, and timing.",
  5012: "Indeed, Allāh took a covenant with the Children of Israel, and We raised among them twelve chiefs [18]. Allāh said, ‘I am with you. If you establish prayer, give the poor-rate, believe in My Messengers and support them [19], and lend to Allāh a goodly loan [20], I will surely remove your misdeeds and admit you into gardens beneath which rivers flow. But whoever among you disbelieves after that has certainly strayed from the right path.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. Twelve chiefs (naqīb): The naqīb was a leader or chief representing a clan. These twelve chiefs were entrusted with overseeing their respective groups, taking pledges of obedience, and guiding their people to uphold Allah’s commandments and remain faithful to the covenant.\n[19]. My messengers: This includes belief in all of Allah’s messengers without distinction, culminating in the final messenger, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ.\n[20]. “Lending to Allāh a goodly loan” refers to giving charity sincerely and generously for the sake of Allāh, with the promise of reward multiplied by Him.",
  5013: "But for their breaking of the covenant, We cursed them and made their hearts hard. They distort words [of the Scripture] from their proper places and have forgotten a portion of what they were reminded of [21]. You will continue to observe betrayal from them—except for a few. So pardon them and overlook. Indeed, Allāh loves the doers of good.\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. This refers to passages in the Torah concerning the description and coming of the final Prophet, Muhammad ﷺ, which were obscured, altered, or neglected over time.",
  5014: "And from those who said, ‘We are Christians,’ We took their covenant, but they forgot a portion of what they were reminded of [22]. So We stirred among them enmity and hatred until the Day of Resurrection. And Allāh will inform them of what they used to do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. This refers to passages in the Gospel concerning the description and coming of the final Prophet, Muhammad ﷺ, which were obscured, altered, or neglected over time.",
  5015: "O People of the Scripture, Our Messenger has come to you, making clear to you much of what you used to conceal of the Scripture, and overlooking much [23]. Indeed, there has come to you from Allāh a Light and a clear Book.\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. “Overlooking much” indicates that not all of what was concealed or altered was exposed. This reflects divine forbearance and mercy, as the Messenger ﷺ clarified essential truths while leaving aside matters that did not require public disclosure. It also highlights that the purpose of revelation is guidance and correction, not mere exposure.",
  5016: "Through it, Allāh guides those who seek His pleasure to the paths of peace, brings them out of the depths of darkness into the light by His will, and guides them to a straight path.",
  5017: "They have certainly disbelieved who say, ‘The Christ, son of Mary, is God.’ \nSay, ‘Who then could prevent Allāh, if He willed that the Christ, son of Mary, his mother, and everyone on earth perish?’ To Allāh belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth and everything between them. He creates whatever He wills, and Allāh is fully capable of all things.",
  5018: "The Jews and the Christians say, ‘We are the children of God and His beloved ones.’ Say, ‘Why then does He punish you for your sins?’ Rather, you are but human beings among those He has created. He forgives whom He wills and punishes whom He wills. To Allāh belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth and all that is between them, and to Him is the final return.",
  5019: "O People of the Scripture, Our Messenger has come to you, making the truth clear after a break between the messengers [24], so that you may not say, ‘No bearer of glad tidings or warner ever came to us.’ Now there has come to you a bearer of glad tidings [25] and a warner [26]. And Allāh is fully capable of all things.\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. “A break between the messengers”:\nThis refers to the period between the mission of Jesus (ʿĪsā) and the sending of the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ, during which no messenger was sent with a universal message. Muḥammad ﷺ was sent as the final messenger to all humanity.\nIn an authentic prophetic narration, the Prophet ﷺ explained that Allāh created people upon a natural state of uprightness, but they gradually went astray. Then Allāh sent him with the Qur’an as a preserved and universal guidance.\n[25] Bearer of glad tidings: That is, one who brings good news of forgiveness, mercy, and eternal bliss for those who believe in Allāh and do righteous deeds.\n[26] Warner: That is, one who warns of punishment for disbelief, associating partners with Allāh, and persisting in wrongdoing.",
  5020: "And when Moses said to his people, ‘O my people, remember the favor of Allāh upon you: He raised prophets from among you, made you sovereign [27], and gave you what He had not given to any other people in the world.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]. That is, He granted you dignity, independence, and authority—possessing what was needed for settled life, such as homes, families, and servants. Some scholars also interpret this as meaning “sovereigns,” i.e., people of independent authority rather than subjugation.",
  5021: "“O my people, enter the Sacred Land which Allāh has decreed for you, and do not turn back, lest you return as losers.”",
  5022: "They said, ‘O Moses, there is a powerful people in this land [28], and we will never enter it unless they leave. But if they leave, then we will enter.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. That is, a people of great physical strength and power who inhabited the land at that time. Scholars describe them as formidable warriors, sometimes portrayed as giants. They are commonly identified as the Canaanites or related peoples of the region.",
  5023: "Two men from among those who feared Allāh [29], upon whom Allāh had bestowed His favor, said, ‘Enter upon them through the gate. Once you enter it, you will surely be victorious. Put your trust in Allāh, if you are truly believers.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. According to Islamic exegesis, these two men are identified as Joshua (Yūshaʿ ibn Nūn) and Caleb (Kālib ibn Yūfannā). They were among the faithful of the Children of Israel who, unlike the majority, trusted in Allah’s promise and encouraged obedience and courage.\nWhen Moses sent scouts into the land, Joshua and Caleb affirmed with conviction that victory was possible through reliance upon Allāh. Their faith and steadfastness stand in contrast to the fear shown by others and exemplify trust in divine assistance.",
  5024: "They said, ‘O Moses, we will never enter it as long as they are there. So go, you and your Lord, and fight; we will remain right here.’",
  5025: "He said, ‘My Lord, I have no authority over anyone except myself and my brother; so separate us from the defiantly disobedient people.’",
  5026: "Allāh said, ‘Then it will be forbidden to them for forty years, during which they will wander through the land. So do not grieve over the defiantly disobedient people.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. That is, the Sacred Land—commonly understood as the region of Greater Palestine, including Jerusalem—was forbidden to them for forty years. During this period, Allāh decreed that they would wander in the wilderness as a punishment for their rebellion and disobedience.",
  5027: "Relate to them, in truth, the story of the two sons of Ādam: when each offered a sacrifice, it was accepted from one [31] but not from the other [32]. The latter said, ‘I will surely kill you.’ The former replied, ‘Allāh accepts only from the righteous.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [31]. Understood in Islamic tradition to refer to the son who offered his sacrifice sincerely and from the best of what he possessed. He is commonly identified as Abel.\n[32]. Understood in Islamic tradition to refer to the son whose offering lacked sincerity and was inferior. He is commonly identified as Cain.",
  5028: "If you raise your hand against me to kill me, I will not raise mine against you to kill you; indeed, I fear Allāh, the Lord of the worlds.",
  5029: "I would rather that you bear the sin [33] against me along with your own, so that you may be among the people of the Fire. And that is the recompense of the wrongdoers.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [33]. Meaning the sin of killing him, which the aggressor alone would bear, in addition to his own sins. This verse, together with the preceding one, exemplifies moral restraint and God-consciousness: Abel refuses violence not out of weakness, but out of fear of Allāh. It establishes a Qur’anic principle that righteousness does not justify aggression and reinforces personal moral accountability.",
  5030: "Then his soul prompted him to kill his brother, so he killed him and became one of the losers. [34]\\n\\nFootnotes: [34]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: “No soul is killed unjustly except that the first son of Adam bears a share of its burden, for he was the first to establish the practice of killing.” This highlights the lasting moral consequence of initiating injustice.",
  5031: " Then Allāh sent a crow scratching in the ground to show him how to bury his brother’s body. He said, ‘Woe to me! Am I so helpless that I cannot be like this crow and bury my brother’s body?’ So he became one of the remorseful.",
  5032: "For that reason, We decreed for the Children of Israel that whoever kills a soul—unless in retribution for murder or for spreading corruption in the land—it is as though he has killed all mankind [35]. And whoever saves a life, it is as though he has saved all mankind. Our messengers came to them with clear signs, yet even after that, many of them still went on to transgress throughout the land.\\n\\nFootnotes: [35]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: “The gravest of sins are: associating partners with Allāh, unjustly taking a human life, disobedience to one’s parents, and bearing false testimony.”",
  5033: "The recompense of those who wage war against Allāh and His Messenger and strive to spread corruption in the land [36] is that they be killed, or crucified, or have their hands and feet cut off on opposite sides, or be exiled from the land [37]. This is a disgrace for them in this world, and in the Hereafter they will have a great punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [36]. That is, armed individuals or groups who engage in violent crimes against civilians, such as murder, robbery, or terrorism, or who wage armed rebellion against lawful public order. This does not refer to mere disbelief or peaceful opposition.\n[37] .This verse outlines prescribed legal penalties for severe crimes such as highway robbery, armed banditry, and organized violence that threaten public safety. Islamic law applies these punishments according to the nature and severity of the crime, under strict judicial procedures and evidentiary standards.",
  5034: "Except those who repent before you overcome them—then know that Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  5035: "O believers, fear Allāh, seek the means of nearness to Him [38], and strive in His cause, so that you may succeed.\\n\\nFootnotes: [38]. That is, seek all lawful means that lead to Allah’s pleasure and nearness—through faith, worship, and righteous deeds performed sincerely for His sake.",
  5036: "Indeed, those who disbelieve—even if they possessed everything on earth and the like of it besides to ransom themselves from the punishment of the Day of Resurrection—it would not be accepted from them, and for them is a painful punishment.",
  5037: "They will wish to come out of the Fire, but they will never come out of it, and for them is an everlasting punishment.",
  5038: "As for the thief, male or female, cut off their hands as a recompense for what they have done—a deterrent from Allāh [39]. \nAnd Allāh is All-Mighty, All-Wise.\\n\\nFootnotes: [39]. Deterrence refers to a penalty imposed as an example, intended to prevent both the offender and others from committing the same crime. It is called deterrence because it discourages wrongdoing by demonstrating the consequences of such actions.\nIn Islamic law, the punishment for theft is subject to strict conditions and due process. Theft must be proven either by a voluntary confession or by the testimony of two reliable eyewitnesses, and it must involve deliberate theft of protected property meeting specific legal criteria. Factors such as necessity, hunger, or ambiguity preclude the application of this penalty.\nDuring the Prophet’s ﷺ time, a woman from the Banū Makhzūm committed theft. When some sought intercession on her behalf, the Prophet ﷺ said: “Are you interceding concerning one of Allah’s prescribed punishments?” He then addressed the people, saying: “Those before you were destroyed because when a noble person stole, they let him go, but when a weak person stole, they carried out the punishment upon him. By Allāh, if Fāṭimah, the daughter of Muhammad, were to steal, I would cut off her hand.”",
  5039: "But whoever repents after wrongdoing and reforms, Allāh will surely accept his repentance. Indeed, Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  5040: "Do you not know that to Allāh belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth? He punishes whom He wills and forgives whom He wills, and Allāh is powerful over all things.",
  5041: "O Messenger, do not let those who rush into disbelief grieve you—those who say with their mouths, ‘We believe,’ while their hearts do not believe, and among the Jews are those who eagerly listen to falsehood, listening for others who have not come to you. They distort words [of the Scripture] from their proper places, saying, ‘If you are given this, take it; but if you are not given it, then beware.’ Whoever Allāh wills to test, you will never have any power to avail him against Allāh. These are the ones whose hearts Allāh does not intend to purify. For them is disgrace in this world, and in the Hereafter they will have a great punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [40]. Purification of the heart refers to spiritual readiness to accept truth, repentance, and sincere obedience to Allāh. Those who were given the Scripture before Islam are called either to follow the Qur’an or to uphold their own Scriptures faithfully, without distortion, acting upon them with sincerity and devotion. A purified heart is essential for drawing near to Allāh, receiving forgiveness, and attaining salvation.\nThe Qur’an frequently speaks of the heart as the center of belief, intention, and moral awareness. Sin hardens and obscures the heart, as Allāh says: “No—rather, what they used to earn has rusted upon their hearts” (Qur’an 83:14). Purification comes through repentance and turning back to Allāh, who cleanses hearts and renews faith.\nTo love Allāh and His Messenger, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, with a pure heart means to have undivided loyalty to Allāh—loving Him above all else and following His Messenger sincerely. Such love manifests in righteous conduct, justice, and peaceful relations with others.",
  5042: "They eagerly listen to falsehood and consume unlawful gain. If they come to you, either judge between them or turn away from them. If you turn away from them, they will not harm you in the least. But if you judge, then judge between them with justice. Indeed, Allāh loves those who are just.",
  5043: "But how is it that they seek your judgment while they have the Torah [41], in which is Allāh’s judgment, yet they turn away after that? Those are not true believers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [41].This verse highlights the inconsistency and insincerity of those who selectively sought judgment. They appealed to the Prophet ﷺ for legal decisions while disregarding the Torah’s own rulings when those rulings conflicted with their interests. The criticism is directed at this selective obedience to divine law, not at sincere followers of revelation.",
  5044: "Indeed, We sent down the Torah, in which is guidance and light. By it, the prophets who submitted to Allāh judged for the Jews, as did the rabbis and the scholars, since they were entrusted with the safeguarding of Allāh’s Scripture and were witnesses to it. So do not fear people, but fear Me, and do not exchange My revelations for a small price [42]. Whoever does not judge by what Allāh has revealed—it is they who are the disbelievers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [42]. Those entrusted with preserving and applying the Scripture of God—the Torah—are warned against allowing fear of influential people, personal desires, or worldly gain to divert them from their responsibility. They are commanded to fear Allāh above all else, to safeguard the Scripture from distortion, and to pronounce Allāh’s judgment faithfully, without regard to social status, power, or material benefit. Divine law is not to be compromised for bribery or worldly advantage.",
  5045: "We ordained for them in the Torah: a life for a life, an eye for an eye, a nose for a nose, an ear for an ear, and a tooth for a tooth—and for wounds, equal retaliation. But whoever remits it as charity, it will be an expiation for him [43]. Whoever does not judge by what Allāh has revealed—it is they who are the unjust.\\n\\nFootnotes: [43]. This verse alludes to the principle known as the law of retaliation or reciprocal justice, expressed in the Torah as ʿayin taḥat ʿayin—“an eye for an eye” (Exodus 21:24). This principle was originally intended to establish proportional justice and to limit excessive retaliation. Over time, however, its application was distorted or misused.\nThe Qur’an reaffirms this principle of proportional justice while simultaneously elevating moral conduct by encouraging forgiveness. The one who is wronged is urged not to act out of anger or vengeance; rather, pardon is presented as a higher moral virtue—beloved by Allāh and rewarded abundantly. To forgive when one has the power to retaliate is described as a mark of true strength, faith, and moral excellence.",
  5046: "And We sent, following in their footsteps, Jesus, son of Mary [44], confirming what came before him of the Torah. And We gave him the Gospel, in which is guidance and light, confirming what came before it of the Torah, and as guidance and admonition for the righteous.\\n\\nFootnotes: [44]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: “I am the nearest of all people to Jesus, the son of Mary. The prophets are paternal brothers—their mothers are different, but their religion is one. And there was no prophet between him and me.”",
  5047: "Let the people of the Gospel judge by what Allāh has revealed therein. Whoever does not judge by what Allāh has revealed—it is they who are the rebellious.",
  5048: "We have sent down to you the Book in truth, confirming what came before it of the Scriptures and as a guardian over them [45]. So judge between them by what Allāh has revealed, and do not follow their desires away from the truth that has come to you. To each of you We have appointed a law and a way. Had Allāh willed, He could have made you one community, but He willed to test you in what He has given you. So compete with one another in good deeds. To Allāh is your return, all together, and He will inform you concerning that over which you used to differ.\\n\\nFootnotes: [45]. “A guardian over them” means that the Qur’an serves as a final authority over the earlier Scriptures—confirming what remains true and valid within them, clarifying what was obscured, and superseding rulings that were altered or abrogated.",
  5049: "And judge between them by what Allāh has revealed, and do not follow their desires. Beware of them, lest they turn you away from some of what Allāh has revealed to you. But if they turn away, then know that Allāh intends to afflict them for some of their sins. And indeed, many among mankind are defiantly rebellious.",
  5050: "Is it the judgment of the times of ignorance that they seek [46]? Who is better than Allāh in judgment for a people who are certain in faith?\\n\\nFootnotes: [46]. Those who refuse to submit to the judgment of Allāh and His Messenger are, in effect, choosing the rule of ignorance—systems of judgment grounded in human desire and speculation rather than divine revelation. Such laws lack the moral authority and guidance found in the revealed Scriptures, which the Qur’an presents as the true source of justice, light, and direction for humanity.\nThe Prophet ﷺ said: \"The most hated persons to Allāh are three: [among them is] a person who wants that the traditions of the pre-Islamic period of ignorance should remain in Islam.\"",
  5051: "O believers, do not take the Jews and Christians as allies [47]; they are allies of one another. And whoever among you takes them as allies is indeed one of them [48]. Surely Allāh does not guide the unjust people.\\n\\nFootnotes: [47]. Referring to those among the Jews and Christians who were hostile to the Muslim community or engaged in opposition to it, particularly in matters of political allegiance and conflict. This verse does not address relations with those who are peaceful or just.\n[48]. That is, whoever gives them loyalty or support in opposition to the believers, or aligns with them in a way that compromises faith and allegiance to Islam.",
  5052: "So you see those in whose hearts is a disease rushing to align themselves with them, saying, ‘We fear that some misfortune may befall us.’ But perhaps Allāh will bring victory or a command from Himself, and they will regret what they had concealed within themselves.",
  5053: "And those who believe will say, ‘Are these the ones who swore by Allāh with their most solemn oaths that they were indeed with you?’ Their deeds have become worthless, so they have become losers. [49]\\n\\nFootnotes: [49]. This refers to the hypocrites whose outward claims of faith were not matched by sincerity of heart. Though they swore solemn oaths, their lack of true belief and integrity rendered their deeds void. By prioritizing self-interest and abandoning obedience to Allāh, their hypocrisy was exposed, and all that they strove for came to nothing. Such is the extent of their loss.",
  5054: "O believers, whoever among you turns back from his religion [50]—Allāh will bring forth a people whom He loves [51] and who love Him [52]: humble toward the believers, firm against the disbelievers, striving in the way of Allāh and not fearing the blame of any blamer. That is the bounty of Allāh, which He grants to whom He wills. And Allāh is All-Encompassing, All-Knowing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [50]. That is, turning away from obedience and loyalty to Allāh and His Messenger. In this context, aligning oneself in opposition to the believing community is treated as a serious breach of faith.\n[51]. Allah’s love is earned through sincere faith, obedience, humility, patience, and striving in His cause. Those whom Allāh loves are distinguished by their character and commitment, not merely by outward claims.\n[52]. Loving Allāh entails sincere submission to His commands and following the guidance of His final Messenger, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. True love of Allāh is demonstrated through obedience, not words alone.",
  5055: "Indeed, your true guardian is none but Allāh, His Messenger, and the believers—those who establish prayer and give zakāh while bowing in submission.",
  5056: "Whoever takes Allāh, His Messenger, and the believers as guardians—then surely the party of Allāh are the victorious.",
  5057: "O believers, do not take as guardians those who mock your religion and make a jest of it—whether from among those who were given the Scripture before you or from among the idolaters. And fear Allāh, if you are truly believers.",
  5058: "When you make the call to prayer [53], they mock and ridicule it; that is because they are a people who do not understand.\\n\\nFootnotes: [53]. Prayer is singled out here because it is the most visible and defining act of worship of the believing community, distinguishing faith in practice as well as belief. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ emphasized its centrality, saying: “The covenant that stands between us and them is the prayer; whoever abandons it has committed disbelief.” He also said: “Between a person and disbelief and idolatry is the abandonment of prayer.” These statements highlight the foundational role of prayer in Islam and its function as a clear marker of faith.",
  5059: "Say, ‘O People of the Scripture, do you resent us for anything other than that we believe in Allāh, in what has been sent down to us, and in what was sent down before [54], while most of you are defiantly disobedient?’\\n\\nFootnotes: [54]. That is, belief in all divine revelations sent by Allāh—including the Qur’an and the earlier Scriptures such as the Torah, the Gospel, and the Psalms.",
  5060: "Say, ‘Shall I inform you of those who are worse in recompense with Allāh? They are those whom Allāh has cursed and upon whom His wrath has fallen—those whom He transformed into apes and pigs, and those who worship false deities. Such are worse in position and farther astray from the straight path.’",
  5061: "When they come to you, they say, ‘We believe,’ though they entered with disbelief and departed with it. And Allāh knows best what they conceal.",
  5062: "You see many of them hastening into sin and aggression, and consuming what is unlawful [55]. Evil indeed is what they have been doing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [55]. “What is unlawful” include bribery, usury, injustice, and other forms of illicit gain. The verse underscores that religious claims are rendered meaningless when contradicted by unethical behavior.",
  5063: "Why do their rabbis and scholars not forbid them from sinful speech and consuming what is unlawful? Evil indeed is what they have been doing.",
  5064: "The Jews say, ‘The Hand of Allāh is chained [56].’ Rather, their own hands are chained, and they are cursed for what they say. On the contrary, both His Hands are outstretched; He gives as He wills. What has been sent down to you from your Lord will surely increase many of them in arrogance and disbelief. We have cast among them enmity and hatred until the Day of Resurrection. Whenever they kindle the fire of war, Allāh extinguishes it. They strive to spread corruption in the land, and Allāh does not like the corrupters.\\n\\nFootnotes: [56]. This statement exemplifies a pattern of defiance in which certain individuals among the Jews went beyond sinful conduct to utterances that misrepresented or demeaned the divine attributes—specifically by implying miserliness or limitation in Allāh. The verse condemns such speech as a grave offense and instead affirms Allāh’s absolute generosity and sovereignty.\nThe Prophet Muhammad (ﷺ) said: “Indeed, the Right Hand of Allāh is full, and spending cannot diminish it. It incessantly spends day and night. Suppose how much He must have spent since He created the heavens and earth, yet that did not decrease anything of what is in His Right Hand”.",
  5065: "If the People of the Scripture had believed and been mindful of Allāh, We would have surely expiated their misdeeds and admitted them into Gardens of Bliss.",
  5066: "If only they had upheld the Torah and the Gospel and what has been sent down to them from their Lord [57], they would have been provided for from above them and from beneath their feet. Among them is a moderate community, but many of them—evil is what they do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [57]. That is, if they had truly upheld and followed the Torah and the Gospel as originally revealed, along with what has now been sent down to them from their Lord—the Qurʾān—they would have received abundant provision from all directions. The expression “from above them and from beneath their feet” signifies a comprehensive and uninterrupted flow of blessings. The verse also acknowledges that among them are some who are upright and moderate, though many persist in wrongdoing.",
  5067: "O Messenger, convey what has been sent down to you from your Lord. If you do not do so, then you have not conveyed His message. And Allāh will protect you from the people. Indeed, Allāh does not guide the persistently disbelieving people.\\n\\nFootnotes: [58]. This verse underscores the Prophet Muhammad’s ﷺ absolute duty to convey the divine message in its entirety, without fear, concealment, or compromise. It affirms that partial delivery of revelation would amount to failing the trust of prophethood. At the same time, Allāh reassures His Messenger of divine protection against harm, emphasizing that opposition or hostility cannot obstruct the fulfillment of God’s will. The verse highlights the balance between human responsibility in conveying truth and divine sovereignty in granting protection and guidance.",
  5068: "Say, ‘O People of the Scripture, you are not upon true guidance until you uphold the Torah, the Gospel, and what has been sent down to you from your Lord. Surely, what has been sent down to you from your Lord will cause many of them to increase in transgression and disbelief. So do not grieve over the disbelieving people.’",
  5069: "Indeed, those who believe, and those who were Jews, Sabians, or Christians—whoever believes in Allāh and the Last Day and does righteous deeds—will have no fear, nor will they grieve. [59]\\n\\nFootnotes: [59]. This verse affirms that salvation is not based on communal labels or ancestry, but on sincere faith in Allāh, belief in the Last Day, and righteous conduct. It refers to those among earlier religious communities who truly believed in Allāh and lived righteously in accordance with the revelation available to them before the advent of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. Once the final revelation has come and its truth made clear, belief entails accepting it as part of faith in Allāh. Thus, the verse emphasizes divine justice and mercy, while maintaining the Qur’anic principle that accountability corresponds to knowledge and responsibility.",
  5070: "We certainly took a covenant from the Children of Israel and sent them messengers. Whenever a messenger came to them with what their souls did not desire, some they denied and others they killed.",
  5071: "They thought there would be no trial [60], so they became blind and deaf. Then Allāh turned to them in forgiveness, yet again many of them became blind and deaf. And Allāh is All-Seeing of what they do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [60]. They deceived themselves by selfishly assuming there would be no consequences for their grave offenses. This self-deception rendered them blind and deaf to seeing and hearing the truth of Islam, leaving them unable to find a path to it. Even after Allāh turned to them in mercy and guided them for a time, many of them still fell back into their former ways.",
  5072: "They have certainly disbelieved who say, ‘The Christ, son of Mary, is God.’ But the Christ said, ‘O Children of Israel, worship Allāh—my Lord and your Lord. Indeed, whoever associates anything with Allāh, Allāh has forbidden Paradise for him, and his abode will be the Fire. And for the wrongdoers there will be no helpers.’",
  5073: "They have certainly disbelieved who say, ‘God is one of three [61].’ There is no god except One God [62]. If they do not desist from what they say, those among them who disbelieve will surely be afflicted with a painful punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [61]. It is a mistaken belief that Allāh consists of three equal persons within one Godhead—God the Father, God the Son, and the Holy Spirit. This idea, commonly described as a “mystery,” is itself difficult to define or comprehend. Aside from a disputed and widely rejected passage in the King James Version (1 John 5:7–8), along with a few ambiguous references, there is no clear scriptural basis for a triune God or for the doctrine of the Trinity. The term “Trinity” does not appear in any version of the Bible.\nHistorically, the doctrine developed gradually. At the Council of Nicaea in 325 CE, it was declared that God the Father and Jesus Christ were of the “same substance,” though distinct. Later, the Council of Constantinople (381 CE) affirmed the divinity of the holy spirit. The Council of Chalcedon (451 CE) further defined Jesus Christ as both fully divine and fully human. By this stage, belief in a triune God had become established doctrine, independent of explicit biblical wording.\nSome historians also trace trinity-like concepts to earlier ancient belief systems, including traditions associated with Nimrod, Semiramis, and Tammuz. Even the Roman Catholic Church acknowledges the late doctrinal formulation. The New Catholic Encyclopedia states:\n“The formulation ‘one God in three persons’ was not solidly established, nor fully assimilated into Christian life and its profession of faith, prior to the end of the fourth century. … Among the Apostolic Fathers, there had been nothing even remotely approaching such a mentality or perspective.”— 1967, Vol. XIV, p. 299\n[62]. Allāh has never had any offspring, nor is there any deity besides Him.",
  5074: "Will they not then turn to Allāh in repentance and seek His forgiveness? And Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  5075: "The Christ, son of Mary, was only a messenger; messengers had passed on before him. His mother was a woman of truth. They both used to eat food [63]. See how We make the signs clear to them; then see how they are misled.\\n\\nFootnotes: [63]. Both of them were dependent upon sustenance, a clear indication of their created nature and proof that they were not divine. Allāh Almighty, by contrast, is utterly self-sufficient—He stands in need of nothing, while all that exists is in constant need of Him.",
  5076: "Say, ‘Do you worship, besides Allāh, that which has no power to harm or benefit you, while Allāh alone is All-Hearing, All-Knowing?’",
  5077: "Say, ‘O People of the Scripture, do not go to extremes in your religion beyond the truth, and do not follow the desires of a people who went astray before [64], led many astray, and have strayed from the straight path.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [64]. That is, their religious leaders—priests and pastors—who themselves went astray and, through their authority and influence, led others astray as well.",
  5078: "Those among the Children of Israel who disbelieved were cursed on the tongue of David and Jesus, son of Mary. That was because they disobeyed and persisted in transgression. [65]\\n\\nFootnotes: [65]. This refers to passages such as Psalms 28:21–22 and 31:33, and Gospel of Matthew 12:34 and 23:13–38, in which David and Jesus, respectively, denounced the disobedience and corruption of their people. In these passages, they openly expose hypocrisy, moral decay, and persistent rebellion against God’s commandments.",
  5079: "They did not forbid one another from wrongdoing. How wretched indeed was what they used to do.",
  5080: "You see many of them allying themselves with the idolaters. How wretched is what their own souls have put forward for them—that Allāh’s wrath is upon them, and in the punishment they will abide.",
  5081: "If they had truly believed in Allāh, the Prophet, and what was revealed to him, they would not have taken them as allies; but many of them are defiantly disobedient.",
  5082: "You will surely find that, among all people, the most hostile to the believers are the Jews and those who associate others with Allāh. And you will surely find that the nearest in affection to the believers are those who say, ‘We are Christians.’ This is because among them are priests and monks, and because they are not arrogant.",
  5083: "When they hear what has been revealed to the Messenger, you see their eyes overflowing with tears because they recognize the truth. They say, ‘Our Lord, we have believed, so record us among those who bear witness.’ [66]\\n\\nFootnotes: [66]. “Those who bear witness”:\nThat is, those who bear witness to the truth—namely, the oneness of Allāh and the truth of His messengers.\nThe Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said:\n“Whoever testifies that there is no god except Allāh alone, without partner; that Muḥammad is His servant and messenger; that Jesus is the servant of Allāh and His messenger, His Word bestowed upon Mary, and a spirit from Him; and that Paradise and Hellfire are true—Allāh will admit him into Paradise according to his deeds.”\nJesus likewise described himself as a servant when he said:\n“The Son of Man did not come to be served, but to serve.”\n— Gospel of Mark 10:45\nThus, Jesus exemplified complete servanthood: he lived among the Children of Israel as a human being and a messenger of Allāh, fully submitting to and carrying out the divine will. In both the Qurʾānic and biblical sense, the servants of Allāh are those who worship Him sincerely, obey His commands, and are entrusted with guidance.",
  5084: "Why should we not believe in Allāh and in the truth that has come to us, and hope that our Lord will admit us among the righteous? [67]\\n\\nFootnotes: [67]. That is, the Prophets, and those who affirmed their message, followed their guidance, and remained steadfast upon the truth they were sent with, in belief, word, and deed.",
  5085: "Thus Allāh rewarded them for what they had said [68] with gardens beneath which rivers flow, where they will abide forever. That is the reward of the doers of good.\\n\\nFootnotes: [68] That is, for their sincere acknowledgment and acceptance of the truth, and for their steadfast commitment to God’s true religion, Islam.",
  5086: "But those who disbelieve and deny Our signs are the companions of the Hellfire.",
  5087: "O believers, do not forbid the good things that Allāh has made lawful for you, and do not transgress; indeed, Allāh does not like the transgressors.\\n\\nFootnotes: [69]. That is, these divine laws remain permanently binding and must not be treated lightly or set aside, as earlier nations did when they neglected and violated what Allah had ordained.",
  5088: "Eat of what Allāh has provided for you, lawful and good, and be mindful of Allāh in whom you believe.",
  5089: "Allāh does not hold you accountable for your unintentional oaths [70], but He holds you accountable for the oaths you deliberately bind yourselves to. The expiation for such an oath is to feed ten needy people with food similar to what you normally feed your families, or to clothe them, or to free a slave. Whoever cannot afford this must fast for three days. That is the expiation for your oaths when you swear. So be mindful of your oaths [71]. Thus Allāh makes His verses clear to you, so that you may be grateful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [70] That is, oaths uttered unintentionally, out of habit, or without sincere intent—such as casual speech—are not subject to accountability.\n[71] That is, do not swear excessively or falsely. Honor your oaths and commitments, and do not misuse the Name of Allāh, your Lord. Allāh does not overlook the deliberate abuse of His Name or the violation of solemn oaths.",
  5090: "O believers, intoxicants [72], gambling [73], stone altars, and divining arrows are nothing but abominations—the work of Satan. So keep away from them, that you may succeed.\\n\\nFootnotes: [72]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“Allāh has cursed intoxicants, its drinker, the one who serves it, the one who sells it, the one who buys it, the one who presses it, the one for whom it is pressed, the one who carries it, and the one to whom it is carried.”\nThis ḥadīth demonstrates that the prohibition of intoxicants extends not only to consumption, but to every role involved in their production, distribution, and use, highlighting the seriousness of the harm they cause to individuals and society.\n[73]. Gambling is the act of wagering money or valuables on games of chance, in which gain or loss depends largely on luck rather than productive effort. Its forms are many, including lotteries, sports betting, animal races or fights, casino games, card rooms, and modern online gambling.\nWhile some may experience temporary gains, gambling often leads to addiction, financial ruin, broken families, and social harm. Many people continue gambling in the hope of recovering losses, sometimes risking essential income meant to support their families. When livelihoods are lost in this way, the consequences can be devastating.\nFor this reason, Islam prohibits gambling in all its forms. Wealth in Islam is meant to be earned through honest labor and lawful means. Ill-gotten or hasty wealth—such as that gained through gambling or “get-rich-quick” schemes—quickly disappears, whereas lawful earnings acquired gradually are blessed with growth.\nHuman beings are commanded to work, cultivate the earth, and act as responsible servants of Allāh. All provision ultimately comes from Allāh, and wealth is a trust meant to be used responsibly and shared through charity—not squandered through chance and speculation.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ also said:\n“Whoever swears an oath and says in it, ‘By such-and-such,’ should say, ‘There is no god worthy of worship except Allāh.’ And whoever says to his companion, ‘Come, let us gamble,’ must give something in charity.”\nThis teaching underscores the seriousness of both misusing oaths and even inviting others to gamble, and it establishes charity as a means of expiation and moral correction.",
  5091: "Satan only seeks to sow enmity and hatred among you through intoxicants and gambling, and to turn you away from the remembrance of Allāh and from prayer. Will you not then refrain?",
  5092: "Obey Allāh and obey the Messenger, and be cautious. If you turn away, then know that the duty of Our Messenger is only to convey the message clearly.",
  5093: "There is no blame upon those who believe and do righteous deeds for what they consumed before its prohibition, so long as they remain mindful of Allāh, believe, and do righteous deeds; then they grow in mindfulness and faith; then they persist in piety and excellence. And Allāh loves the doers of good.",
  5094: "O believers, Allāh will surely test you with some game within reach of your hands and your spears, so that Allāh may make evident those who fear Him in the unseen [74]. Whoever transgresses thereafter will have a painful punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [74]. This refers to restraining oneself from sin when it is hidden from the sight of others—especially when the temptation is close, easy, and appealing. Such restraint is a true measure of faith and reverent fear of Allāh.",
  5095: "O believers, do not kill game while you are in a state of consecration. Whoever among you kills it deliberately must offer compensation equivalent to it, as determined by two just men among you, to be presented as an offering at the Kaʿbah. Or, as expiation, he may feed the needy or fast an equivalent number of days, so that he may taste the consequence of his deed. Allāh has pardoned what is past, but whoever repeats it—Allāh will exact retribution from him. And Allāh is All-Mighty, Possessor of Retribution.",
  5096: "Lawful to you is the game of the sea and its food, as provision for you and for travelers. But the game of the land is forbidden to you while you are in a state of consecration [iḥrām for pilgrimage]. And be mindful of Allāh, to whom you will all be gathered.",
  5097: "Allāh has made the Ka‘bah—the Sacred House in Makkah—a sanctuary [75] and a source of security and benefit for the people, along with the sacred month, the offerings, and the garlanded sacrificial animals. This is so that you may know that Allāh knows whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth, and that Allāh is All-Knowing of everything.\\n\\nFootnotes: [75]. The term qiyām conveys the meanings of stability, uprightness, and the careful maintenance of affairs. When Allāh declares the Kaʿbah to be a qiyām for the people, it signifies that it serves as a central pillar by which both religious and worldly life are sustained and protected. Spiritually, it is the focal point of worship, where believers establish prayer and perform ḥajj and ʿumrah, fulfilling their devotion to God. Socially and economically, it functions as a secure gathering place, facilitating trade, cooperation, and the strengthening of communal ties— all within the sacred protection ordained by Allāh. Through this, order, security, and benefit are preserved for humanity.",
  5098: "Know that Allāh is Severe in punishment, and that Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  5099: "The Messenger’s duty is only to convey the message clearly, and Allāh knows whatever you reveal and whatever you conceal.",
  5100: "Say, ‘The bad [76) and the good [77] are not equal, even if the abundance of the bad impresses you. So fear Allāh [78], O people of understanding, that you may succeed.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [76]. The bad: all that is bad with respect to things, deeds, beliefs, persons, and foods.\n[77]. The good: all that is good with respect to things, deeds, beliefs, persons, and foods.\n[78]  Fear Allāh: that is, abstain from all sins and evil deeds that He has forbidden, and love Allāh by performing all the good deeds that He has ordained.",
  5101: "O believers, do not ask about things which, if made clear to you, would cause you hardship [79]. If you ask about them while the Qurʾān is being revealed, they will be made clear to you. Allāh has pardoned what is past, and Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Forbearing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [79]. That is, questions—especially contrived or excessive ones—whose answers would expand legal obligations and restrictions, thereby causing unnecessary hardship for the believers.",
  5102: "A people before you asked such questions; then they became disbelievers because of it. [80]\\n\\nFootnotes: [80]. That is, the excessive and obstinate questioning of earlier peoples—particularly some among the Children of Israel—ultimately led them to reject the commands of their prophets. Rather than deepening their faith, their persistent demands and unnecessary inquiries undermined their submission to Allāh. When they were confronted with obligations they disliked, they refused to comply, and this rejection caused some of them to fall into disbelief. This serves as a solemn warning to later believers not to repeat the same error by burdening themselves with needless questions that may weaken obedience and sincerity.",
  5103: "Allāh has not ordained such things as Bahīrah, Saʾibah, Wāṣilah, or Ḥām [81], but those who disbelieve invent lies against Allāh, and most of them do not understand.\\n\\nFootnotes: [81]. These refer to pre-Islamic practices in which certain animals were dedicated to idols and declared sacred, with specific restrictions placed upon their use, without any divine authority.",
  5104: "When it is said to them, ‘Come to what Allāh has revealed and to the Messenger,’ they reply, ‘What we found our forefathers following is enough for us’—even though their forefathers knew nothing and were not guided.",
  5105: "O believers, you are responsible for your own selves. Those who go astray will not harm you so long as you remain rightly guided [82]. To Allāh is your final return—all of you together—and He will inform you of what you used to do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [82] This verse establishes the principle of personal responsibility in faith and conduct: every believer is accountable for his own guidance and sincerity. Once a person has fulfilled his duty of advising others, calling to the truth, and forbidding wrongdoing, the persistence of others in error will not harm him—so long as he himself remains firm upon right guidance. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: \"When people see wrongdoing and do not prevent it, it is likely that Allāh will encompass them all with punishment.\"",
  5106: "O believers, when death approaches one of you at the time of making a bequest, let the testimony be given by two just men from among you—or two others from outside your community if you are traveling through the land and death befalls you. If you doubt them [83], detain them both after the prayer [84] and let them swear by Allāh: ‘We will not exchange our testimony for any price, even for the sake of a close relative, nor will we conceal the testimony of Allāh; otherwise, we would surely be among the sinners.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [83]. “If you doubt their testimony”\nThis clause refers to any suspicion or uncertainty concerning the honesty, accuracy, or reliability of the witnesses. The verse acknowledges that even upright individuals may be subject to doubt, whether due to error, bias, external pressure, or the sensitive nature of matters such as wills and inheritance, which often involve family disputes and financial interests. In such cases, the prescribed response is not punishment, but careful verification of the testimony, ensuring justice for the deceased and fairness among the heirs. The verse emphasizes that doubt should be resolved through solemn oaths and conscious accountability before Allāh, thereby reinforcing the moral and spiritual gravity of bearing witness.\n[84]. “Detain them both after the prayer”\nThe witnesses are to be temporarily detained after a congregational prayer—traditionally understood to be the ʿAṣr prayer—in order to administer the oath in a formal and solemn setting. This procedure serves several important purposes:\nPublic and ritual solemnity — the oath is taken in a context of heightened reverence and communal awareness.\nDeliberation and protection from coercion — witnesses are given time to reflect carefully and swear truthfully, free from haste or undue influence.\nMoral accountability — linking the oath to prayer underscores that testimony is ultimately given before Allāh, not merely as a legal formality.\nThis instruction reflects the prudence of Islamic law, which integrates spiritual consciousness, ethical responsibility, and procedural safeguards to uphold justice. Witnesses are required to be truthful, doubts must be addressed methodically, and the entire process is grounded in God-consciousness and communal integrity.",
  5107: "Then if it is discovered that the two have incurred guilt, let two others stand in their place—chosen from those with the stronger claim—and let them swear by Allāh, saying, ‘Our testimony is truer than their testimony, and we have not transgressed; for then we would surely be among the wrongdoers.’",
  5108: "That is more likely to ensure that they [85] give true testimony, or fear that their oaths may be countered by others afterward. So be mindful of Allāh and listen attentively. And Allāh does not guide the defiantly rebellious people.\\n\\nFootnotes: [85]. “That” refers to the two original witnesses. The procedure described is more likely to ensure that they give testimony truthfully, as it places them under solemn oath and accountability before Allāh. It also serves as a deterrent against false testimony, since the possibility of their oaths being challenged and replaced by others increases the risk of exposure. In this way, the system safeguards justice by encouraging honesty and preventing wrongdoing.",
  5109: "The Day when Allāh will gather the messengers and say, ‘What response were you given?’ They will say, ‘We have no knowledge; indeed, You alone are the All-Knower of the unseen.’",
  5110: "When Allāh will say, ‘O Jesus, son of Mary [86], remember My favor upon you and upon your mother—when I strengthened you with the holy spirit, so that you spoke to the people in the cradle and in maturity; and when I taught you writing, wisdom, the Torah, and the Gospel; and when you fashioned from clay the form of a bird by My permission, then breathed into it and it became a bird by My permission; and when you healed the blind and the leper by My permission, and brought the dead to life by My permission; and when I restrained the Children of Israel from you when you came to them with clear proofs—yet those who disbelieved among them said, “This is nothing but manifest magic.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [86]. Among all the messengers of God, Jesus is singled out here and questioned in particular, for those most deserving of admonition are the people who claim to follow him—namely, the Christians. Whereas other communities rejected their prophets outright, this community went further by making the gravest and most unfounded claims about God Himself: attributing to Him a son, and constructing an entire creed upon that assertion.\nThe divine favors enumerated in this passage make clear that Jesus was nothing more than a human messenger of God—honored, aided, and supported by Him, yet entirely dependent upon His permission in all that he did. Every miracle attributed to Jesus is explicitly qualified as occurring “by My permission,” leaving no room for any claim to divinity.\nThis reality is echoed even within Christian scripture itself. In Gospel of Matthew 7:22–23, Jesus is reported to have said:\n“Many will say to me on that day, ‘Lord, Lord, did we not prophesy in your name, and cast out demons in your name, and do many mighty works in your name?’\nAnd then I will declare to them, ‘I never knew you; depart from me, you who practice lawlessness.’”\nThis passage stands as one of the clearest testimonies within the Bible that Jesus will repudiate those who elevate him beyond his true station or worship him, for he neither claimed divinity nor commanded such devotion. Rather, it affirms his authentic role: a prophet, servant, and messenger of God—calling people to worship Allāh alone.",
  5111: "And when I inspired the disciples, ‘Believe in Me and in My Messenger,’ they said, ‘We believe—so bear witness that we are Muslims.’",
  5112: "When the disciples said, “O Jesus, son of Mary, can your Lord send down to us a table [spread with food] from heaven?” He said, “Fear Allāh, if you are truly believers.”",
  5113: "They said, ‘We wish to eat from it, so that our hearts may be reassured, that we may know you have spoken truthfully to us, and that we may be among its witnesses.’",
  5114: "Jesus, son of Mary, said, ‘O Allāh, our Lord, send down to us a table from heaven, that it may be for us a festival—for the first of us and the last of us—and a sign from You. And provide for us, for You are the best of providers.’",
  5115: "Allāh said, ‘Indeed, I am sending it down to you. But whoever disbelieves thereafter among you—I will surely punish him with a punishment such as I have not inflicted upon anyone among all peoples.’",
  5116: "When Allāh will say, ‘O Jesus, son of Mary [87], did you say to the people, “Take me and my mother as two gods besides Allāh?”’ He will say, ‘Glory be to You! It is not for me to say what I have no right to say. Had I said it, You would surely have known it. You know what is within myself, but I do not know what is within Yourself. Indeed, You alone are the All-Knower of the unseen.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [87]. This verse serves as a solemn admonition to those who claim to follow Jesus yet have distorted his message. Although Jesus himself is directly addressed, the purpose is not to question him—rather, it is to establish his testimony against those who falsely attributed divinity to him and to his mother. As with every messenger, Jesus will bear witness concerning his own people on the Day of Resurrection.\nThe exchange highlights Jesus’ complete innocence of such claims, his absolute submission to Allāh, and the falsehood of elevating any created being to divine status. It also affirms a central Qur’anic principle: all messengers called to the worship of Allāh alone, and none ever sanctioned the worship of themselves.",
  5117: "I never said to them anything except what You commanded me: ‘Worship Allāh, my Lord and your Lord.’ I was a witness over them as long as I remained among them; but when You took me up [88], You Yourself were the Watcher over them. And You are a Witness over all things.\\n\\nFootnotes: [88]. This refers to Allāh taking Jesus up to heaven in both body and spirit. From that moment onward, Jesus no longer bore responsibility for the beliefs or actions of those who came after him. Ultimate supervision, judgment, and testimony belong solely to Allāh, who witnesses all things at all times.",
  5118: "If You punish them, they are Your servants; but if You forgive them, then surely You alone are the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.",
  5119: "Allāh will say, ‘This is the Day when the truthful [89] will benefit from their truthfulness [90].’ For them are gardens beneath which rivers flow, where they will abide forever. Allāh is pleased with them, and they are pleased with Him. That is the great achievement.\\n\\nFootnotes: [89]. The truthful are those whose lives are consistently grounded in truth—truth in belief, truth in speech, and truth in action. Their sincerity is not selective or occasional, but steadfast and enduring. In the Qur’anic expression, truthfulness is a defining mark of spiritual excellence and a core virtue of genuine faith. It reflects integrity of heart and loyalty to the commands of Allāh, and it distinguishes those whose outward actions align with their inner conviction.\n[90]. Will benefit from their truthfulness\nOn this Day, the reward of truthfulness will fully manifest. The sincerity and integrity these believers upheld in the world will not be lost or forgotten; rather, it will be the very cause of their salvation and honor. Their truthfulness will grant them entrance into gardens beneath which rivers flow, the pleasure of Allāh, and everlasting contentment. This affirms a central Qur’anic principle: moral and spiritual integrity leads not only to divine approval in the Hereafter, but to ultimate success and eternal bliss.",
  5120: "To Allāh belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth and whatever is in them, and He has power over all things.",
  6001: "All praise is due to Allāh, who created the heavens and the earth and made the darknesses and the light; yet those who disbelieve equate others with their Lord. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. This verse opens this chapter with a declaration of absolute praise and gratitude to Allāh — the One who brought the heavens and the earth into existence and ordered the alternation of darkness and light. It is a call to acknowledge Him as the sole Creator and Sustainer of all that exists.\nThe mention of “darknesses” in the plural and “light” in the singular is significant. It states that truth is one and clear, whereas falsehood is many and confusing. The light symbolizes divine guidance, revelation, and truth, while darknesses represent ignorance, disbelief, and deviation.\nDespite these clear signs of divine power and wisdom, those who disbelieve still make equals with their Lord — worshiping others alongside Him or attributing divine qualities to created beings. This exposes the depth of human ingratitude and spiritual blindness: after recognizing His creation, they still deny His sole right to be worshiped. Thus, the verse establishes two core truths of Islamic belief:\n1. Tawḥīd al-Rubūbiyyah — that Allāh alone is the Creator, Sustainer, and Controller of the universe.\n2. Tawḥīd al-Ulūhiyyah — that only He deserves to be worshiped, with no partners or intermediaries.",
  6002: "He is the One who created you from clay [2], then set a fixed span for your life. And with Him is another appointed time [for resurrection]—yet you still doubt.\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. That is, Adam, the father of humanity, was created from clay—earth that was gathered, processed, and formed.",
  6003: "He is Allāh, the only true God, in the heavens and on the earth [3]. He knows what you conceal and what you reveal, and He knows whatever you earn.\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. That is, He alone is rightfully deserving of worship by all who inhabit the heavens and the earth.",
  6004: "No sign ever comes to them from their Lord without them turning away from it.",
  6005: "They denied the truth [4] when it came to them, but soon the news of what they used to ridicule will reach them.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. “The truth” refers to the revealed message sent by God through the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. “The news” denotes the eventual reality and consequences of what they used to ridicule, which will become clear to them.",
  6006: "Have they not seen how many generations We destroyed before them [5]—whom We had established on the earth more firmly than We have established you? We sent down abundant rain upon them and made rivers flow beneath them; yet We destroyed them for their sins and raised up after them other generations.\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. This verse reminds humanity of the fate of past nations who were more powerful, wealthy, and established on earth than the Quraysh or any later people. Despite their strength, knowledge, and flourishing civilizations, when they persisted in arrogance and sin, denying their prophets and Allah’s guidance, they were destroyed. The abundant rain and flowing rivers symbolize worldly prosperity — yet even such blessings could not save them from divine retribution. This serves as a warning that power and comfort are not signs of divine favor if they are accompanied by ingratitude and rebellion against Allāh.",
  6007: "Even if We had sent down to you [O Prophet] a written scripture on parchment that they could touch with their own hands, the disbelievers would still say, “This is nothing but obvious magic.”",
  6008: "They say, “Why has an angel not been sent down to him?” If We had sent down an angel, the matter would have been settled, and they would be given no respite.",
  6009: "And if We had made him an angel [i.e., the messenger], We would have made him appear as a man, and We would have left them in the confusion they are already in.",
  6010: "And indeed, messengers before you were ridiculed, but those who mocked them were overtaken by what they used to ridicule.",
  6011: "Say, “Travel through the land and see how those who denied met their end.”",
  6012: "Say, “To whom belongs whatever is in the heavens and the earth?” Say, “To Allāh.” He has decreed mercy upon Himself. He will surely gather you for the Day of Resurrection—there is no doubt about it. Those who have doomed themselves will not believe.",
  6013: "To Him belongs all that rests in the night and the day; He is the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.",
  6014: "Say, “Should I take a protector other than Allāh —the Originator of the heavens and the earth—while He feeds and is not fed?” Say, “Indeed, I have been commanded to be the first to submit [to Allāh] and not to be among the polytheists.",
  6015: "Say, “Indeed, I fear the punishment of a tremendous Day, should I disobey my Lord.”",
  6016: "Whoever is spared the punishment on that Day has indeed been granted mercy; that is the clear triumph.",
  6017: "If Allāh touches you with harm, none can remove it but Him; and if He touches you with good, He is capable of all things.",
  6018: "He is the Supreme over His servants, and He is the All-Wise, the All-Aware.",
  6019: "Say, “Whose testimony is greatest?” Say, “Allāh is Witness between me and you. This Qur’an has been revealed to me so that I may warn you by it and all whom it reaches. Do you truly bear witness that there are other gods besides Allāh?” Say, “I do not bear witness.” Say, “He is but one God, and I am indeed free of what you associate with Him.”",
  6020: "Those to whom We gave the Scripture recognize him [6] as they recognize their own sons. But those who have doomed themselves will not believe.\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. “Recognize him” refers to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, whose description and signs were known to the People of the Scripture, as clearly as they recognize their own sons.",
  6021: "Who is more unjust than one who fabricates lies about Allāh or rejects His revelations? Truly, the wrongdoers will never succeed.",
  6022: "On the Day We gather them all together, We will say to those who associated others with Allāh, ‘Where are the partners you used to claim as equals with Allāh?’",
  6023: "Then they will have no excuse but to say, ‘By Allāh, our Lord, we did not associate partners with Him.’",
  6024: "See how they will lie against themselves, and how what they used to fabricate will vanish from them.",
  6025: "Among them are those who listen to you [7], but We have placed coverings over their hearts so they do not understand, and deafness in their ears. Even if they were to see every sign, they would still not believe. When they come to you arguing, the disbelievers say, ‘This is nothing but legends of the former peoples.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. Meaning, they hear the words of the Prophet ﷺ, but without sincerity or reflection. Their hearts are veiled and their hearing is spiritually impaired, so they neither comprehend nor believe, even when clear signs are presented.",
  6026: "They prevent others from it [the Qur’an] and keep away from it themselves; they destroy none but themselves, though they do not realize it.",
  6027: "If only you could see when they are made to stand before the Fire, and they say, ‘If only we could be sent back, we would not deny the revelations of our Lord, and we would be among the believers.’",
  6028: "Rather, what they used to conceal has now been revealed to them. Even if they were sent back, they would surely return to what they were forbidden. Indeed, they are liars.",
  6029: "They say, ‘There is nothing beyond our life in this world, and we will not be resurrected.’",
  6030: "If only you could see them when they are made to stand before their Lord. He will say, ‘Is this not the truth?’ They will say, ‘Yes indeed, by our Lord.’ He will say, ‘Then taste the punishment for what you used to deny.’",
  6031: "Indeed, the true losers are those who deny the meeting with Allāh. When the Hour comes upon them suddenly, they will cry out, ‘Oh, how great is our regret for what we neglected!’ And they will carry their burdens on their backs. Truly, how evil is the burden they bear.",
  6032: "The life of this world is nothing but play and amusement, but the home of the Hereafter is far better for those mindful of Allāh. Will you not then understand?",
  6033: "We know that what they say grieves you. It is not you they deny; rather, it is the revelations of Allāh that the wrongdoers reject.",
  6034: "Messengers before you were denied, but they patiently endured rejection and harm until Our help came to them. None can change the words of Allāh. And there has certainly come to you some of the news of the messengers.",
  6035: "If their denial is hard on you, then seek—if you can—a tunnel into the earth or a stairway into the sky to bring them a sign. But if Allāh had willed, He would have gathered them all upon guidance. So do not be among the ignorant.",
  6036: "Only those who truly listen will respond. As for the dead, Allāh will resurrect them; then to Him they will be returned.",
  6037: "They say, ‘Why has no sign been sent down to him from his Lord?’ Say, ‘Indeed, Allāh is able to send down a sign, but most of them do not know.’",
  6038: "There is no creature on earth nor any bird that flies with its wings but that they are communities like you. We have left nothing out of the Book [8]; then to their Lord they will all be gathered.\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. “The Book” refers to the Preserved Tablet (al-Lawḥ al-Maḥfūẓ), in which all things are comprehensively recorded by Allāh.",
  6039: "Those who reject Our revelations are deaf and mute, in layers of darkness. Whomever Allāh wills, He leaves astray; and whomever He wills, He guides to a straight path.",
  6040: "Say, ‘Tell me—if the punishment of Allāh were to come upon you, or the Hour were to overtake you, would you call upon anyone other than Allāh, if you are truthful?’",
  6041: "No—rather, it is Him alone you would call upon. Then He would remove what you called upon Him for, if He willed, and you would forget whatever partners you associate with Him.",
  6042: "We certainly sent messengers to nations before you, then We afflicted them with hardship and adversity so that they might humble themselves. [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. The hardships were meant as a means to bring people back to humility and reliance upon Allāh.",
  6043: "Why, then, did they not humble themselves when Our punishment came to them? Instead, their hearts became hardened, and Satan made their deeds seem appealing to them. [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [ 10]. Their failure to humble themselves despite hardship is attributed to hardness of heart and the deceptive influence of Satan.",
  6044: "Then when they forgot what they had been reminded of, We opened for them the doors of every good thing. But when they rejoiced in what they were given, We seized them suddenly—then they were plunged into despair. [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. This describes a gradual test: ease and abundance are given after neglect, leading to complacency before sudden punishment.",
  6045: "Thus the root of those who did wrong [by disbelief] was cut off. And all praise is due to Allāh, the Lord of the worlds.",
  6046: "Say, ‘Tell me—if Allāh were to take away your hearing and your sight and seal your hearts, who is there besides Allāh that could restore them to you?’ See how We diversify the verses; yet they still turn away.",
  6047: "Say, ‘Tell me—if the punishment of Allāh were to come upon you suddenly or openly, would any be destroyed except the wrongdoing people?’",
  6048: "We send messengers only as bearers of good news and as warners. So whoever believes and reforms—there will be no fear upon them, nor will they grieve.",
  6049: "But those who deny Our verses—the punishment will touch them because of their persistent disobedience.",
  6050: "Say, ‘I do not tell you that I possess the treasures of Allāh, nor that I know the unseen, nor that I am an angel. I only follow what is revealed to me.’ Say, ‘Are the blind and the seeing equal? Will you not then reflect?’",
  6051: "Warn by it those who fear being gathered before their Lord—when they will have no protector or intercessor besides Him—so that they may be mindful of Allāh. [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. “Warn by it” refers to the Qur’an. It addresses those who are conscious of being gathered before their Lord and are therefore receptive to guidance—maintaining a steady commitment to what is right by following the Qur’an and the Sunnah, which helps them remain upright.",
  6052: "Do not drive away those who call upon their Lord morning and evening, seeking His Face. You are not accountable for them in anything, nor are they accountable for you in anything. If you were to drive them away, you would be among the wrongdoers.",
  6053: "Thus We have tested some of them by means of others, so that they may say, “Are these the ones whom Allāh has favored among us?” Does not Allāh know best those who are truly grateful?",
  6054: "When those who believe in Our verses come to you, say, ‘Peace be upon you. Your Lord has decreed mercy upon Himself: whoever among you does wrong out of ignorance, then repents after that and reforms—He is indeed All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.’",
  6055: "Thus We make the verses clear, so that the path of the criminals may become evident. [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [ 13]. Refers to clarifying truth so that the way of those who persist in wrongdoing becomes distinct and recognizable.",
  6056: "Say, ‘Indeed, I have been forbidden to worship those you call upon besides Allāh.’ Say, ‘I will not follow your desires; for if I did, I would go astray and would not be among the rightly guided.’",
  6057: "Say, ‘Indeed, I am upon clear proof from my Lord, yet you deny it. I do not have what you seek to hasten. Judgment belongs only to Allāh: He declares the truth, and He is the best of judges.’",
  6058: "Say, ‘If what you seek to hasten were within my power [14], the matter would have already been decided between me and you. But Allāh knows best the wrongdoers.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [ 14]. Emphasizes that the timing of punishment is not in the Prophet’s control but rests solely with Allah’s knowledge and wisdom.",
  6059: "With Him are the keys of the unseen—none knows them except Him. He knows what is on the land and in the sea. Not a leaf falls but that He knows it, nor a grain in the darknesses of the earth, nor anything moist or dry, except that it is in a clear record. [15]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. Refers to the Preserved Tablet. The verse emphasizes that the keys of the unseen belong to Allāh alone—perfect knowledge of what is hidden, including the Hour, the sending down of rain, and what is in the wombs. No soul knows what it will earn tomorrow or in which land it will die.",
  6060: " He is the One who takes your souls by night and knows what you do by day [16]. Then He revives you by day so that an appointed term may be fulfilled. To Him is your return, and He will inform you of what you used to do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. “Taking the soul by night” refers to sleep, often described as a lesser form of death, while waking is described as revival.",
  6061: "He is supreme over His servants, and He sends guardians over you. When death comes to one of you, Our messengers take him, and they do not fall short.",
  6062: "Then they will be returned to Allāh, their true Lord. Indeed, judgment belongs to Him alone, and He is the swiftest of reckoners.",
  6063: "Say, ‘Who saves you from the darknesses of the land and sea when you call upon Him humbly and in secret, saying, “If He saves us from this, we will surely be among the grateful”?’",
  6064: "Say, ‘It is Allāh who saves you from it and from every hardship, yet you still associate others with Him in worship.’",
  6065: "Say, ‘He has the power to send upon you a punishment from above you or from beneath your feet, or to divide you into factions and make you taste the violence of one another.’ See how We diversify the signs, so that they may understand.",
  6066: "But your people deny it, though it is the truth. Say, ‘I am not a guardian over you.’",
  6067: "For every matter there is an appointed time, and you will soon come to know. [17]\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. That is, nothing is concealed except that it will be brought to light, and nothing is hidden except that it will be made known.",
  6068: "When you see those who engage in vain discourse about Our verses, withdraw from them until they turn to another topic. And if Satan causes you to forget, then once you remember, do not remain with the wrongdoing people. [18]\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. Refers to avoiding gatherings where Allah’s revelations are mocked, distorted, or treated disrespectfully, until the conversation changes.",
  6069: "Those who are mindful of Allāh are not responsible for them [i.e., the disbelievers] in any way; their duty is only to remind, so that they may become mindful of Allāh.",
  6070: "Leave those who take their religion as a game and a pastime, and whom the worldly life has deluded. But continue to remind with it, lest a soul be destroyed for what it has earned. It will have no protector or intercessor besides Allāh, and no ransom—however great—will be accepted. Such are the ones ruined by what they earned. For them is a drink of boiling water and a painful punishment for their denial.",
  6071: "Say, ‘Shall we call upon, besides Allāh, that which can neither benefit us nor harm us, and be turned back on our heels after Allāh has guided us—like one whom the devils have led astray upon the earth, confused, while he has companions calling him to guidance, “Come to us”?’ Say, ‘Indeed, the guidance of Allāh is the only true guidance, and we have been commanded to submit to the Lord of all worlds,’",
  6072: "and to establish prayer and fear Him. And He is the One to whom you will all be gathered.",
  6073: "And He is the One who created the heavens and the earth in truth. The Day He says, ‘Be,’ it is—His word is the truth. To Him belongs the dominion on the Day the Trumpet is blown. And He is the Knower of the unseen and the seen, and He is the All-Wise, the All-Aware.",
  6074: "And [remember] when Abraham said to his father Āzar, ‘Do you take idols as gods? Indeed, I see you and your people in manifest error.’",
  6075: "Thus We showed Abraham the dominion of the heavens and the earth so that he might be among those of certain faith.",
  6076: "When the night fell over him, he saw a star and said, ‘Is this my Lord?’ But when it set, he said, ‘I do not like those that set.’[19]\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. That is, he did not truly take the star as his lord. Rather, he said this as part of a reasoned argument meant to expose the falsehood of worshiping celestial bodies, since anything that sets and disappears cannot be worthy of divinity. This illustrates Prophet Abraham’s method of reasoning—a lesson in using reflection and intellect to arrive at faith in the Ever-Present Creator.",
  6077: "When he saw the moon rising, he said, \"Is this my lord?\" But when it set, he said, \"If my Lord does not guide me, I will surely be among the people who are astray.\"",
  6078: "When he saw the sun rising, he said, ‘Is this my Lord? This is greater.’ But when it set, he said, ‘O my people, I am free from what you associate with Allāh.’",
  6079: "Indeed, I turn my face toward the One who originated the heavens and the earth, inclining toward truth, and I am not of those who associate others with Allāh.",
  6080: "His people argued with him. He said, ‘Do you argue with me concerning Allāh while He has guided me? I do not fear what you associate with Him, unless my Lord wills something. My Lord encompasses all things in knowledge. Will you not then take heed?’",
  6081: "And how should I fear what you associate with Him, while you do not fear associating with Allāh that for which He has sent down no authority? Which of the two parties, then, has more right to security—if you know?",
  6082: "Those who believe and do not mix their faith with injustice [20]—for them there is true security, and they are rightly guided.\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. This means that they do not mix their faith with shirk—associating others with Allāh in worship—as clarified by the Prophet ﷺ. When this verse was revealed, some of the Prophet’s Companions said, “Which of us has not wronged himself?” The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ replied, “It is not what you think. It refers to what Luqmān said to his son: ‘My son, do not associate anything with Allāh. Indeed, associating partners with Him is a grave injustice’” (Qur’an 31:13).\nThus, “injustice” here primarily refers to idolatry, though it also includes all forms of wrongdoing. Scholars note that sins weaken faith and diminish one’s sense of security, but the verse specifically contrasts pure monotheism with associating partners with Allāh.",
  6083: "That was Our argument which We granted to Abraham against his people. We raise in rank whom We will. Indeed, your Lord is All-Wise, All-Knowing.",
  6084: "We granted him Isaac and Jacob; each of them We guided. And Noah We guided before. And among his descendants were David, Solomon, Job, Joseph, Moses, and Aaron. Thus do We reward the doers of good.",
  6085: "And Zachariah, John, Jesus, and Elijah—all were among the righteous.",
  6086: "And Ishmael, Elisha, Jonah, and Lot—We favored each of them above all people of their time.",
  6087: "And from among their fathers, their descendants, and their brothers, We chose them and guided them to a straight path.",
  6088: "That is the guidance of Allāh by which He guides whom He wills of His servants. And if they had associated others with Him, all that they used to do would have been nullified.",
  6089: "Those are the ones to whom We gave the Scripture, sound judgment, and prophethood. But if these disbelieve in it, then We have entrusted it to a people who do not disbelieve in it. [21]\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. Refers to others—such as later believers—who accept and uphold the message if some reject it. “Sound judgment” (ḥukm) includes sound understanding, wisdom, and the ability to judge rightly in accordance with divine guidance. This includes the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ and those who follow him.",
  6090: "They are the ones whom Allāh has guided—so follow their guidance. Say, ‘I ask of you no payment for it; it is only a reminder for the worlds.’",
  6091: "They did not truly revere Allāh when they said, ‘Allāh has not revealed anything to any human being.’ Say, ‘Who then sent down the Scripture that Moses brought as a light and guidance for the people, which you make into separate sheets, revealing some of it and concealing much? And you were taught what neither you nor your forefathers had known.’ Say, ‘Allāh,’ then leave them to amuse themselves in their vain discourse.\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. That is, they selectively disclosed portions of the Scripture that served their interests while concealing other passages—especially those affirming divine revelation and foretelling later prophets. Among what they sought to hide was the prophethood and messengership of Muhammad ﷺ.",
  6092: "And this is a Book We have sent down, full of blessings and confirming what came before it, so that you may warn the Mother of Cities and those around it. Those who believe in the Hereafter believe in it and are steadfast in maintaining their prayers.",
  6093: "Who could be more unjust than one who fabricates a lie about Allāh, or claims, ‘It has been revealed to me,’ when nothing has been revealed to him, or says, ‘I can produce something like what Allāh has revealed’? If only you could see the wrongdoers at the moment of death, as the angels reach out to them, saying, ‘Give up your souls! Today you will be repaid with a humiliating punishment for what you used to say against Allāh other than the truth and for your arrogance toward His revelations.’",
  6094: "Indeed, you have come to Us alone, just as We created you the first time, and you have left behind all that We bestowed upon you. And We do not see with you your intercessors—those whom you claimed as partners with Allāh. Indeed, all ties between you have been severed, and all that you used to claim has abandoned you.",
  6095: "Indeed, it is Allāh who splits the grain and the date-stone. He brings forth the living from the dead and the dead from the living. That is Allāh —your Lord—so how are you turned away?",
  6096: "He is the One who cleaves the daybreak, and made the night for rest, and the sun and the moon for calculation. That is the determination of Allāh, the All-Mighty, the All-Knowing.",
  6097: "And He is the One who made the stars for you, so that you may be guided by them through the darknesses of the land and the sea. We have made the signs clear for a people who know. [23]\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. The break of day, the sun, the moon, and the stars are among the many cosmic signs meant to be contemplated and reflected upon, leading to belief in the Oneness of God—that there is only one God, Allāh —and to affirmation that Muhammad ﷺ is His final Messenger. Monotheism also concerns the nature of God: that He is One, not composite, not triune, and has no partners whatsoever.\nThe stars are not only means of navigation but also signs of divine wisdom, order, and purpose. Those who truly “possess knowledge” perceive in them evidence of the Creator’s guidance, both in the physical world and in spiritual understanding. Allāh created the stars for three purposes: (a) as adornment for the heavens, (b) as missiles against devils, and (c) as signs by which people are guided. Whoever interprets them otherwise has erred, wasted his effort, and claimed knowledge of that which he does not possess.",
  6098: "He is the One who originated you from a single soul [Adam], then for you is a place of settlement and a place of deposit. Indeed, We have made the signs clear for a people who understand.",
  6099: "He is the One who sends down water from the sky, and by it We bring forth vegetation of every kind. From it We bring forth greenery, from which We produce clustered grain. And from the palm trees—from their sheaths—come forth clusters of dates hanging within reach. And gardens of grapes, olives, and pomegranates—similar in form, yet different in taste. Look at their fruit when it begins to bear and as it ripens. Indeed, in that are signs for a people who believe.",
  6100: "Yet they attribute to Allāh partners—the jinn—even though He created them. And they falsely ascribe to Him sons and daughters without knowledge. Exalted is He above what they describe.",
  6101: "He is the Originator of the heavens and the earth. How could He have a son when He has no consort? He created all things, and He is All-Knowing of everything.",
  6102: "That is Allāh, your Lord—there is no deity except Him, the Creator of all things. So worship Him alone, for He is the Disposer of all things.",
  6103: "No vision can encompass Him, yet He encompasses all vision; and He is the Most Subtle, the All-Aware.",
  6104: "Indeed, clear insights have come to you from your Lord. Whoever sees, it is for his own soul; and whoever is blind, it is against it. And I am not a keeper over you.",
  6105: "Thus do We diversify the verses, so that they may say, ‘You have studied,’ and so that We may make it [i.e., the Qur’ān] clear for a people who know.",
  6106: "Follow [O Prophet] what has been revealed to you from your Lord. There is no deity except Him, and turn away from the polytheists.",
  6107: "If Allāh had willed, they would not have associated others with Him. We have not appointed you over them as a keeper, nor are you a trustee over them.",
  6108: "Do not revile those whom they worship besides Allāh, lest they revile Allāh in ignorance and out of spite [24]. Thus We have made every people’s deeds appear fair to them. Then to their Lord is their return, and He will inform them of what they used to do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. This verse establishes an important principle in calling to Allāh: that truth must be conveyed with wisdom and restraint. Reviling the objects of others’ worship does not lead to guidance but instead provokes them, causing them to respond by reviling Allāh out of ignorance and spite.\nAllah’s statement that He has made the deeds of every people appear fair to them means that, as a test, He allows people to perceive their own beliefs and practices as good and justified—either by leaving them to their desires after they turn away from guidance, or by permitting Satan to embellish their deeds when they persist in falsehood. This is not approval of their actions, but a consequence of human choice and divine wisdom.\nThrough this, people are tried, moral responsibility is established, and accountability is made complete. Then, to Allāh alone is their final return, where He will inform them of the reality of what they used to do and judge them with perfect justice.",
  6109: "They swear by Allāh their most solemn oaths that if a sign were to come to them, they would surely believe in it. Say, ‘The signs are only with Allāh.’ And what will make you realize that even if it were to come, they would still not believe?",
  6110: "We will turn their hearts and their sight away, just as they did not believe in it the first time, and We will leave them in their transgression, wandering blindly.",
  6111: "Even if We had sent down the angels to them, and the dead had spoken to them, and We had gathered all things before them, they would still not believe—unless Allāh willed—but most of them are ignorant.",
  6112: "Thus We have appointed for every prophet an enemy—devils from among mankind and jinn—who inspire one another with alluring speech as deception. And if your Lord had willed, they would not have done it; so leave them and that which they invent,",
  6113: "so that the hearts of those who do not believe in the Hereafter may incline toward it, be pleased with it, and persist in what they do.",
  6114: "Shall I seek a judge other than Allāh, while it is He who has sent down to you the Book, explained in detail? And those to whom We gave the Scripture know that it has been sent down from your Lord in truth, so never be among those who doubt.",
  6115: "The word of your Lord has been perfected in truth and justice. None can change His words, and He is the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.",
  6116: "If you obey most of those on the earth, they will lead you astray from the path of Allāh. They follow nothing but conjecture, and they do nothing but lie.",
  6117: "Indeed, your Lord knows best who strays from His path, and He knows best those who are rightly guided.",
  6118: "So eat of that over which Allah’s name has been mentioned [25], if you truly believe in His revelations.\\n\\nFootnotes: [25]. Refers to lawful meat over which the name of Allāh is invoked at the time of slaughter. It includes permissible animals slaughtered in accordance with prescribed guidelines. Meat from animals that die on their own, are improperly slaughtered, or over which Allah’s name is not mentioned is considered unlawful.",
  6119: "Why should you not eat of that over which the name of Allāh has been mentioned [26], when He has explained in detail to you what He has forbidden—except when you are compelled [27]? And indeed, many lead others astray through their desires without knowledge. Your Lord knows best the transgressors.\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. That is, upon slaughtering it.\n[27]. That is, in cases of dire necessity, what is normally prohibited becomes permissible, but only to the extent required by the need.",
  6120: "Avoid all sin, whether openly or in secret. Indeed, those who commit sin will be repaid for what they used to do.",
  6121: "Do not eat of that over which the name of Allāh has not been mentioned, for indeed it is grave disobedience. And the devils inspire their allies to dispute with you; and if you were to obey them, you would indeed be idolaters.",
  6122: "Is one who was dead, then We gave him life and made for him a light by which he walks among the people, like one who is in darknesses from which he can never emerge? Thus it has been made appealing to the disbelievers what they used to do.",
  6123: "Thus We have placed in every town its leading criminals to plot therein, but they plot only against themselves without realizing it.",
  6124: "When a sign comes to them, they say, ‘We will never believe until we are given the like of what was given to the messengers of Allāh.’ Allāh knows best where to place His message. Those who committed crimes will be afflicted with abasement before Allāh and a severe punishment for what they used to conspire.",
  6125: "Whoever Allāh wills to guide, He opens his heart to Islam; and whoever He wills to leave astray, He makes his heart tight and constricted [28], as if he were climbing into the sky. Thus does Allāh place defilement upon those who do not believe.\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. That is, as a result of his arrogance and continued persistence in sin.",
  6126: "This is the straight path of your Lord. Indeed, We have explained the verses in detail for a people who take heed.",
  6127: "For them is the Home of Peace with their Lord, and He is their Protector because of what they used to do.",
  6128: "On the Day He gathers them all together, [saying], ‘O assembly of jinn, you have led many humans astray.’ Their human allies will say, ‘Our Lord, we benefited from one another, and we have reached our appointed term which You set for us.’ He will say, ‘The Fire is your abode, wherein you will remain forever—except as Allāh wills.’ Indeed, your Lord is All-Wise, All-Knowing.",
  6129: "Thus We make some wrongdoers allies of others because of what they used to do.",
  6130: "“O assembly of jinn and mankind! Did there not come to you messengers from among you, reciting to you My verses and warning you of the meeting of this Day of yours?” They will say, “We bear witness against ourselves.” And the worldly life deluded them [29], and they will bear witness against themselves that they were disbelievers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. Refers to how attachment to worldly life led them to ignore the warnings of the messengers, resulting in their self-condemnation on the Day of Judgment. They were enticed and distracted by its fleeting pleasures, which turned them away from the truth and led them into misguidance.",
  6131: "That is because your Lord would not destroy towns for wrongdoing until their people had been forewarned.",
  6132: "Each will have ranks according to what they have done. And your Lord is not unaware of what they do. [30]\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. The different ranks that people will occupy in the Hereafter—whether in Hell or Paradise—will correspond to their deeds. The more good one performs, the higher the rank they will attain in Paradise; the more evil one commits, the more severe their position in Hell will be.",
  6133: "Your Lord is Self-Sufficient and full of mercy. If He wills, He can do away with you and replace you with whomever He wills [31], just as He originated you from the descendants of another people.\\n\\nFootnotes: [31]  That is, Allāh can replace one people with another if they persist in disbelief and wrongdoing. This serves as a warning that succession and favor are not guaranteed but are granted according to His will and wisdom. Yet He grants them respite to reflect and mend their ways; His allowing people to continue in rebellion is not to be mistaken for any lack of ability.",
  6134: "Indeed, what you have been promised will surely come to pass, and you cannot escape.",
  6135: "Say, ‘O my people, work according to your way; indeed, I am working as well. Soon you will know who will have the ultimate abode. Indeed, the wrongdoers will never prosper.’",
  6136: "They assign to Allāh a share from the crops and livestock He has created, saying, “This is for Allāh,” as they claim, “and this is for our partners [idols].” But what is set aside for their partners does not reach Allāh, while what is set aside for Allāh ends up reaching their partners. Evil indeed is their judgment. [32]\\n\\nFootnotes: [32]. It was a pagan heathen practice to allocate two shares from their livestock: a share for Allāh, to be spent on the poor and the needy, and a share for idols, to be spent on them. If a portion of Allāh’s share was mixed with that of their idols to be spent on them, they would not deduct it, saying that Allāh is in no need of it. Yet if a portion of their idols’ share fell with that of Allāh’s, they would readily take it and add it to their idols’ share.",
  6137: "Likewise, their partners [i.e., devils or false deities] have made the killing of their children seem appealing to many of the polytheists, in order to bring about their ruin and to confuse them in their religion. Had Allāh willed, they would not have done so. So leave them and what they invent.",
  6138: "They say, “These livestock and crops are forbidden; none may eat from them except those whom we permit,” as they claim. And there are livestock whose backs are forbidden [from use], and livestock over which they do not mention the name of Allāh—fabricating lies against Him. He will surely recompense them for what they used to invent.",
  6139: "They also say, “What is in the wombs of these livestock is exclusively for our males and forbidden to our females. But if it is born dead, then they all share in it.” He will surely recompense them for what they attribute. Indeed, He is All-Wise, All-Knowing.",
  6140: "Lost indeed are those who killed their children in foolishness, without knowledge, and forbade what Allāh had provided for them—fabricating lies against Allāh. They have truly gone astray and were not rightly guided.",
  6141: "And He is the One who brings forth gardens, trellised and untrellised, and date palms, and crops of diverse kinds, and olives and pomegranates—similar [in form] yet different [in taste]. Eat of their fruit when they bear fruit, and give its due on the day of harvest, and do not be excessive. Indeed, He does not like those who are excessive.",
  6142: "And among the livestock are those for carrying burdens [33] and those [too small or low] for other use. Eat of what Allāh has provided for you, and do not follow the footsteps of Satan; indeed, he is to you a clear enemy.\\n\\nFootnotes: [33]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said, \"While a man was riding a cow, it turned towards him and said, 'I have not been created for this purpose (i.e. riding), I have been created for ploughing.\" The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ went on, \"A wolf caught a sheep, and when the shepherd chased it, the wolf said, 'Who will be its guard on the day of wild beasts, when there will be no shepherd for it except me?' \"[It has been written that] a wolf also spoke to one of the companions of the Prophet near Madinah. He said, \"I was amongst my sheep. Suddenly a wolf caught a sheep and I shouted at it. The wolf sat on its tail and addressed me, saying, 'Who will look after it (i.e. the sheep) when you will be busy and not able to look after it? Do you forbid me the provision which Allāh has provided me?' \" He added, \"I clapped my hands and said, 'By Allāh, I have never seen anything more curious and wonderful than this!' On that the wolf said, 'There is something (more curious) and wonderful than this; that is, Muhammad, God's messenger ﷺ, in those palm trees, inviting people to God 'i.e. Islam'.' \"Then he went to God's messenger and informed what happened and embraced Islam.",
  6143: "[They are] eight [animals in four] pairs: of sheep, two, and of goats, two [34]. Say, “Has He forbidden the two males, or the two females, or what the wombs of the two females contain? Inform me with knowledge, if you are truthful.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [34]. The verse refers to four pairs so far: sheep and goats, each consisting of a male and a female. The pagan Arabs used to claim that certain animals were forbidden for consumption based on their sex or the circumstances of their birth. For example, they would say, “Only males may eat this,” or “This offspring is sacred and dedicated to our idols.”\nThrough this verse, Allāh exposes the falsehood of these invented prohibitions and challenges them to produce any genuine knowledge or proof for such claims.",
  6144: "And of the camels, two, and of the cattle, two. Say, “Has He forbidden the two males, or the two females, or what the wombs of the two females contain? Or were you witnesses when Allāh commanded you this?” Then who is more unjust than one who invents a lie against Allāh to mislead people without knowledge? Indeed, Allāh does not guide the wrongdoing people.",
  6145: "Say, “I do not find in what has been revealed to me anything forbidden for one who would eat it, except that it be carrion, or spilled blood, or the flesh of swine—for indeed, it is impure—or that which is [slaughtered in] disobedience, dedicated to other than Allāh. But whoever is compelled by necessity, neither desiring it nor transgressing the limit—then indeed, your Lord is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.”",
  6146: "And to those who are Jews, We forbade every animal with an undivided hoof [35]; and of the cattle and the sheep, We forbade to them their fat—except what adheres to their backs, or the entrails, or what is joined with bone. Thus We recompensed them for their transgression, and indeed, We are truthful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [35]. That is, animals with undivided hooves or claws, having nails on their toes—such as camels, ostriches, ducks, geese, and similar species.",
  6147: "But if they deny you, then say, “Your Lord is the Possessor of vast mercy, yet His punishment cannot be averted from the guilty people.",
  6148: "Those who associate others with Allāh will say, “If Allāh had willed, neither we nor our forefathers would have associated anything with Him, nor would we have declared anything unlawful.” Likewise, those before them denied the truth until they tasted Our punishment. Say, “Do you have any knowledge that you can present to us? You follow nothing but assumption, and you do nothing but guess.”",
  6149: "Say, “With Allāh alone is the conclusive argument. If He had willed, He would have guided you all.”",
  6150: "Say, “Bring your witnesses who testify that Allāh has forbidden this.” Then if they testify, do not testify with them. And do not follow the desires of those who deny Our signs, who do not believe in the Hereafter, and who set up equals with their Lord.",
  6151: "Say, “Come, I will recite to you what your Lord has forbidden for you: that you associate nothing with Him [36]; that you show kindness to your parents; that you do not kill your children out of poverty—We provide for you and for them; that you do not approach immoral acts, whether open or hidden; and that you do not take a life which Allāh has made sacred, except by right. This is what He has instructed you, so that you may understand.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [36]. Monotheism is the foundation of every virtue, whereas polytheism—associating others with Allāh in worship—is the root of all evil.\nThese verses outline a set of universal prohibitions that are not confined to a particular divine law, but are shared across all revealed traditions due to their essential role in preserving human life and social order. Foremost among these is associating partners with God (shirk), followed by the neglect of dutifulness and kindness to parents. Alongside these are the prohibition of immoral acts, the sanctity of human life—including the forbiddance of killing children out of fear of poverty—the protection of the property of orphans, the obligation of fairness in measure and weight, justice in speech, faithfulness to covenants, and adherence to the straight path that prevents division in religion.\nThe sequence of these prohibitions indicates that disobedience and disrespect toward parents is among the gravest of sins after polytheism. This is because the very foundation of human society rests upon the family unit, which is sustained by mutual compassion between parents and children. Parents are naturally inclined to care for and nurture their children during their weakness, driven by innate affection. In contrast, their need for their children’s care arises in old age and incapacity, when the children are at the height of their strength.\nIf filial ingratitude and harshness toward parents become widespread—especially at the time when parents most need care—it undermines this essential bond of mercy. This, in turn, weakens the motivation for procreation and responsible upbringing, discourages the formation of families, and erodes the meaning of parenthood. The result is the emergence of generations lacking bonds of kinship and compassion, leading to the disintegration of social cohesion. Ultimately, society becomes fragmented and corrupted in a way that no legal system or customary practice can fully repair, resulting in the loss of well-being in both this world and the Hereafter.\nIncluded among the prohibited immoralities are adultery, sodomy, and false accusation against chaste women and men. What is meant by “that which is apparent and that which is hidden” is, most likely, the distinction between public and secret forms of such acts—such as open fornication and the taking of lovers or illicit companions in secret. Permitting such indecencies removes their sense of shame and moral gravity; and once their repulsiveness is nullified, they spread widely. This is because they appeal strongly to human desire, which resists deprivation from its most intense pleasures and inclines toward what most powerfully excites it.\nAs these acts become widespread, they lead to the disruption of lineage and the collapse of the family structure. With the breakdown of the family, the larger human society itself begins to disintegrate, since it is built upon the stability and integrity of the household.",
  6152: "And do not approach the property of the orphan except in the best manner, until he reaches maturity. And give full measure and weight with justice—We do not burden any soul beyond its capacity. And when you speak, be just, even if it concerns a close relative. And fulfill the covenant of Allāh. This is what He has instructed you, so that you may remember.",
  6153: "And this is My straight path, so follow it; and do not follow other ways, lest they separate you from His way. This is what He has instructed you, so that you may become mindful. [37]\\n\\nFootnotes: [37]. Whoever follows this Straight Path attains righteousness and is counted among the righteous.",
  6154: "Then We gave Moses the Book, completing [Our favor] upon those who do good, and as a detailed explanation of all things, and as guidance and mercy, so that they might believe in the meeting with their Lord.",
  6155: "And this is a blessed Book which We have revealed, so follow it and be mindful of Allāh, that you may receive mercy.",
  6156: "[We revealed it] lest you say, “The Scripture was only sent down to two groups before us [38], and we were unaware of what they studied.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [38]. The “two groups” refers to earlier scriptural communities, especially the Jews and Christians, who were given the Torah and the Gospel. The verse addresses the claim that the Arabs had no access to or knowledge of previous revelations.",
  6157: "Or lest you say, “If only the Scripture had been sent down to us, we would have been better guided than they.” But now there has come to you a clear proof from your Lord, and guidance and mercy. So who is more unjust than one who denies the signs of Allāh and turns away from them? We will recompense those who turn away from Our signs with the worst punishment, for their turning away.",
  6158: "Do they await anything except that the angels should come to them, or that your Lord should come, or that some of the signs of your Lord should come? On the Day when some of the signs of your Lord do come, no soul will benefit from its faith if it had not believed before or had not earned any good through its faith. Say, “Wait, then; indeed, we too are waiting.”",
  6159: "Indeed, those who have divided their religion and become sects [39]—you have nothing to do with them. Their affair is only with Allāh; then He will inform them of what they used to do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [39]. This refers to those who divided their religion into sects and factions, splitting what was originally a unified revelation into differing beliefs and practices. In its immediate context, it addresses earlier scriptural communities—such as the Jews and Christians—who became divided among themselves. More broadly, it serves as a warning against sectarianism and fragmentation in religion, in contrast to the unity and integrity intended in divine guidance.",
  6160: "Whoever comes with a good deed will have ten times its like, but whoever comes with an evil deed will not be recompensed except with its equivalent—and they will not be wronged.",
  6161: "Say, “Indeed, my Lord has guided me to a straight path—an upright religion—the way of Abraham, inclining to the truth; and he was not among those who associate others with Allāh.”",
  6162: "Say, “Indeed, my prayer, my sacrifice, my living and my dying are all for Allāh, Lord of all worlds. [40]\\n\\nFootnotes: [40]. This verse teaches that true worship is comprehensive: in response to Allāh’s love and mercy, believers devote their entire lives to Him—ritual, conduct, gratitude, and service—so that every action becomes an act of devotion.",
  6163: "He has no partner. This I have been commanded, and I am the first of those who submit to Allāh.”",
  6164: "Say, “Shall I seek a lord other than Allāh, while He is the Lord of all things?” No soul earns except against itself, and no bearer of burdens shall bear the burden of another. Then to your Lord is your return, and He will inform you concerning that over which you used to differ.",
  6165: "And He is the One who has made you successors upon the earth and raised some of you above others in degrees, that He may test you in what He has given you. Indeed, your Lord is swift in punishment, yet He is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  7001: "Alif Lām Mīm Ṣād. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2: Verse 1.",
  7002: "This is a Book revealed to you—so let there be no distress in your heart because of it—that you may warn by it and as a reminder to the believers.",
  7003: "Follow what has been revealed to you from your Lord, and do not follow any allies besides Him. Little do you take heed. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The term “allies” (awliyāʾ) includes protectors, patrons, or anyone given loyalty and devotion besides Allāh. It may refer to influences such as devils, as well as religious authorities—such as rabbis, monks, priests, pastors, or similar figures—when they are followed in ways that go against divine guidance. This command forbids taking others as objects of devotion alongside Allāh and warns against altering or adding to His revelation or the teachings of His Messenger, Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ, as such actions lead to misguidance and falsehood.",
  7004: "How many a town have We destroyed [3], and Our punishment came upon them by night or while they were resting at midday.\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. This reflects Allāh’s justice: punishment comes only after He has clearly warned people and made them aware of their wrongdoing through His message.",
  7005: "Yet their only cry, when Our punishment came upon them, was: “Indeed, we were wrongdoers.”",
  7006: "We will surely question those to whom the messengers were sent, and We will surely question the messengers.",
  7007: "Then We will surely relate to them [their deeds] with knowledge, for We were never absent.",
  7008: "The weighing of deeds on that Day will be true and just. So those whose scales are heavy—it is they who will be the successful.",
  7009: "But those whose scales of deeds are light—it is they who have lost themselves, for they used to wrong Our verses. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. This affirms that Allāh’s guidance is always accompanied by clear evidences; belief or denial thereafter rests with the individual.",
  7010: "We have certainly established you upon the earth and provided for you therein means of livelihood—yet little do you give thanks.",
  7011: "We have certainly created you, then fashioned you; then We said to the angels, “Prostrate to Ādam,” so they prostrated—except Iblīs. He was not among those who prostrated.",
  7012: "Allāh said, “What prevented you from prostrating when I commanded you?” He said, “I am better than him. You created me from fire, and You created him from clay.”",
  7013: "He said, “Then descend from it, for it is not for you to be arrogant therein. So get out; indeed, you are among the disgraced.”",
  7014: "He said, “Grant me respite until the Day they are resurrected.”",
  7015: "He said, “Indeed, you are among those who are given respite.”",
  7016: "He said, “Since You have led me astray [5], I will surely sit in wait for them on Your straight path.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. This is Iblīs’s claim. His misguidance resulted from his own arrogance and refusal to obey Allāh’s command, not from any injustice on Allāh’s part. By asserting that Allāh had already decreed his misguidance, Satan became the first to misuse the concept of predestination as a justification for disobedience, attempting to rationalize his rebellion against the divine command.",
  7017: "Then I will surely come to them from before them and from behind them, from their right and from their left—and You will not find most of them grateful.”",
  7018: "He said, “Get out from here, disgraced and expelled. Whoever among them follows you—I will surely fill Hell with all of you together.”",
  7019: "“O Ādam, dwell—you and your wife—in the Garden, and eat from wherever you wish; but do not approach this tree, lest you be among the wrongdoers.”",
  7020: "Then Satan whispered to them to expose what had been concealed from them of their private parts. He said, “Your Lord has only forbidden you this tree lest you become angels or become among the immortal.”",
  7021: "And he swore to them both, “Indeed, I am a sincere adviser to you both.” [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. Satan reinforced his deception with an oath, claiming sincerity in order to gain their trust. By swearing falsely in Allāh’s name, he revealed the depth of his deceit and the extent of his enmity toward humankind. This act illustrates how falsehood, when cloaked in apparent sincerity, can mislead even those who are pure in intention—a timeless warning against deceptive counsel and misplaced trust.",
  7022: "So he led them both astray through deception. When they tasted of the tree, their nakedness became apparent to them, and they began fastening together leaves from the Garden to cover themselves. Then their Lord called out to them, “Did I not forbid you from that tree and tell you that Satan is to you a clear enemy?”",
  7023: "They said, “Our Lord, we have wronged ourselves; if You do not forgive us and have mercy upon us, we will surely be among the losers.” [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. Allāh accepted their repentance and forgave their transgression. This refutes the notion of original sin: Adam and his wife bore responsibility only for their own mistake, which was forgiven, and no inherited sin was passed on to their descendants.",
  7024: " He said, “Descend as enemies to one another [8]. And for you on the earth is a place of settlement and provision for a time.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. This enmity refers primarily to the enduring hostility between humankind and Satan, who vowed to mislead them, as well as to the moral struggle and conflict that would arise among humans in earthly life.",
  7025: "He said, “There you will live, and there you will die, and from it you will be brought forth.”",
  7026: "O children of Ādam, We have provided you with clothing to cover your nakedness and as adornment. But the garment of righteousness—that is best [9]. That is among the signs of Allāh, so that you may remember.\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. Rather than striving merely to adorn ourselves with the fashions of this world, we are called to clothe ourselves with the garments of the spirit—love, compassion, kindness, humility, gentleness, and patience. On our shared journey to Allāh, following the Truth brought by Prophet Muhammad ﷺ from our Creator, we are urged to show patience and grace toward one another. Through this inner adornment, hearts are transformed and purified, leading to true and lasting peace.",
  7027: "O children of Ādam, do not let Satan tempt you as he drove your parents out of the Garden, stripping them of their clothing to expose their nakedness. Indeed, he and his tribe see you from where you do not see them. Indeed, We have made the devils allies of those who do not believe.",
  7028: "When they commit an indecent act, they say, “We found our forefathers doing it, and Allāh has commanded us.” Say, “Indeed, Allāh does not command indecency. Do you say about Allāh what you do not know?”",
  7029: "Say, “My Lord has commanded justice. Direct your faces toward Him at every place of prayer, and call upon Him with sincere devotion to Him. Just as He created you first, so you will return.”",
  7030: "A group He has guided, and another has justly deserved to go astray. They take the devils as allies instead of Allāh, yet they think themselves rightly guided.",
  7031: "O children of Ādam, wear your adornment at every place of worship; and eat and drink, but do not be excessive. Indeed, He does not like those who are excessive.",
  7032: "Say, “Who has forbidden the adornment of Allāh which He has brought forth for His servants, and the good and lawful provisions?” Say, “They are for those who believe in the life of this world, and exclusively for them on the Day of Resurrection.” Thus We make the verses clear for a people who know.",
  7033: "Say, “My Lord has only forbidden indecent acts—whether open or hidden—sin [10], oppression without right, associating with Allāh that for which He has sent down no authority, and speaking about Allāh without knowledge.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. This includes acts such as drinking alcohol, taking drugs, and other intoxicants or behaviors that impair reason and lead to moral and social harm.",
  7034: "And for every nation is an appointed term. When their term comes, they can neither delay it nor advance it by a single moment.",
  7035: "O children of Ādam, if messengers come to you from among yourselves, reciting My verses to you—then whoever is mindful and reforms, there will be no fear concerning them, nor will they grieve.",
  7036: "But those who deny Our verses and are arrogant toward them—they are the companions of the Fire; they will remain therein forever.",
  7037: "Who, then, is more unjust than one who invents a lie against Allāh or denies His signs? They will receive their allotted share of the decree until, when Our messengers come to take their souls, they will say, “Where are those you used to call upon besides Allāh?” They will say, “They have departed from us,” and they will testify against themselves that they were disbelievers.",
  7038: "He will say, “Enter among the nations of jinn and humankind that passed before you into the Fire.” Every time a nation enters, it curses the one before it, until, when they are all gathered therein, the last of them says about the first, “Our Lord, these led us astray, so give them double punishment in the Fire.” He will say, “For each is double, but you do not know.”",
  7039: "Then the first of them will say to the last, “You had no advantage over us, so taste the punishment for what you used to earn.”",
  7040: "Indeed, those who deny Our verses and are arrogant toward them—the gates of Heaven will not be opened for them, nor will they enter Paradise until a camel passes through the eye of a needle. Thus do We repay the wicked.",
  7041: "For them there will be a bed of fire from Hell, and above them coverings of fire. Thus do We repay the wrongdoers.",
  7042: "But those who believe and do righteous deeds—We do not burden any soul beyond its capacity—they are the companions of Paradise, abiding therein forever.",
  7043: "We will remove whatever is in their chests of resentment [11]. Beneath them rivers will flow, and they will say, “All praise is due to Allāh, who has guided us to this. We would never have been guided had Allāh not guided us. Indeed, the messengers of our Lord came with the truth.” And they will be called, “This is the Paradise you have been made to inherit for what you used to do.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. All harmful thoughts and toxic feelings will be removed from their hearts, allowing them to live in complete peace, free of resentment or ill will toward one another—especially despite their differing ranks in Paradise.",
  7044: "And the companions of Paradise will call out to the companions of the Fire, “We have found what our Lord promised us to be true. Have you found what your Lord promised to be true?” They will say, “Yes.” Then a caller will proclaim among them, “The curse of Allāh is upon the wrongdoers",
  7045: "those who turn others away from the way of Allāh and seek to make it crooked, while they disbelieve in the Hereafter.",
  7046: "And between them is a barrier, and on the heights are men who recognize each group by their marks. They will call out to the companions of Paradise, “Peace be upon you.” They have not yet entered it, though they long for it.",
  7047: "And when their eyes are turned toward the companions of the Fire, they say, “Our Lord, do not place us among the wrongdoing people.”",
  7048: "And the people of the heights will call out to men whom they recognize by their marks, saying, “Neither your gathering nor your arrogance has availed you anything.” [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. Allāh abhors arrogance. It is incumbent upon believers to love what He loves and to detest what He detests. Thus, humility is commanded, for Allāh resists the proud and bestows His grace upon the humble; whoever exalts himself shall be humbled, and whoever humbles himself shall be exalted.",
  7049: "“Are these the ones about whom you swore that Allāh would not grant them mercy?” Then it will be said, “Enter Paradise; no fear will be upon you, nor will you grieve.”",
  7050: "The companions of the Fire will call out to the companions of Paradise, “Pour upon us some water or from what Allāh has provided you.” They will say, “Indeed, Allāh has forbidden both to the disbelievers.",
  7051: "Those who took their religion as amusement and play, and were deceived by the life of this world [13]—so today We will forget them [14] just as they forgot the meeting of this Day of theirs, and because they used to deny Our verses.\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. A believer must not love this world or its attractions more than Allāh and His Messenger, Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ. When worldly attachment dominates the heart, true love for Allāh and His Messenger is diminished. The pleasures of this world will pass away, but whoever seeks the pleasure of Allāh and follows His Messenger will attain lasting and eternal joy.\n[14] That is, Allāh will abandon them to the punishment of the Fire, just as they abandoned belief in the meeting of this Day and denied His signs.",
  7052: "Indeed, We have brought them a Book [this Qur’an] which We have detailed with knowledge, as guidance and mercy for a people who believe.",
  7053: "Are they waiting for anything except its fulfillment [15]? On the Day its fulfillment comes, those who had ignored it before will say, “Indeed, the messengers of our Lord came with the truth. Are there any intercessors to intercede for us, or can we be sent back so that we might do other than what we used to do?” They have surely lost themselves, and what they used to fabricate has forsaken them. [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. That is, the actual realization of what the Qur’an foretells—namely the coming of the Resurrection and the ensuing judgment and punishment.\n[16]. Referring to their false deities and claims—worshipping others besides Allāh.",
  7054: "Indeed, your Lord is Allāh, who created the heavens and the earth in six days, then rose over the Throne [17]. He covers the night with the day, each pursuing the other swiftly; and the sun, the moon, and the stars are subjected by His command. Indeed, His is the creation and the command. Blessed is Allāh, the Lord of the worlds.\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. The term istawā literally means “rose over ” in a manner befitting Allāh’s Majesty—without resemblance to His creation and without inquiring into its modality. We affirm that Allāh istawā (rose over the Throne) in a way befitting His Majesty—without resemblance, denial, distortion, or asking how.",
  7055: "Call upon your Lord humbly and in secret; indeed, He does not like the transgressors.",
  7056: "Do not spread corruption on the earth after it has been set right, but call upon Him in fear and hope. Indeed, the mercy of Allāh is near to those who do good.",
  7057: "He is the One who sends the winds as glad tidings before His mercy, until, when they carry heavy clouds, We drive them to a dead land. Then We send down water from it and bring forth thereby fruits of every kind. Thus We bring forth the dead, so that you may remember.",
  7058: "The good land produces its vegetation by the permission of its Lord, while the barren land yields only sparse growth. Thus We set forth the signs for a people who are grateful.",
  7059: "Indeed, We sent Noah to his people. He said, “O my people, worship Allāh alone; you have no deity other than Him. I truly fear for you the punishment of a great Day.”",
  7060: "The chiefs of his people said, “Indeed, we see you in clear error.”",
  7061: "Noah said, O my people, there is no error in me, but, I am a messenger from the Lord of all worlds.”",
  7062: "I convey to you the messages of my Lord, give you sincere counsel, and I know from Allāh what you do not know.",
  7063: "Are you then surprised that a reminder has come to you from your Lord through a man from among yourselves, so that he may warn you, that you may fear Allāh, and that you may receive mercy?",
  7064: "But they denied him, so We saved him and those with him in the Ark, and We drowned those who denied Our signs. Indeed, they were a people blind [to the truth].",
  7065: "And to ʿĀd We sent their brother Hūd. He said, “O my people, worship Allāh alone; you have no deity other than Him. Will you not then fear Him?”",
  7066: "The chiefs among his people who disbelieved said, “Indeed, we see you as foolish, and we think you are one of the liars.”",
  7067: "He said, “O my people, there is no foolishness in me; rather, I am a messenger from the Lord of the worlds.”",
  7068: "I convey to you the messages of my Lord, and I am to you a trustworthy adviser.",
  7069: "Are you then surprised that a reminder has come to you from your Lord through a man from among yourselves, so that he may warn you? And remember when He made you successors after the people of Noah and increased you greatly in stature; so remember the favors of Allāh, that you may prosper.",
  7070: "They said, “Have you come to us so that we should worship Allāh alone and abandon what our forefathers used to worship? Then bring us what you threaten us with, if you are among the truthful.”",
  7071: "He said, “Abomination and wrath have already fallen upon you from your Lord. Do you dispute with me over mere names you and your forefathers have invented, for which Allāh has sent down no authority? Then wait; indeed, I am with you among those who wait.”",
  7072: "So We saved him and those with him by a mercy from Us, and We destroyed those who denied Our signs; they were not believers.",
  7073: "And to Thamūd We sent their brother Ṣāliḥ. He said, “O my people, worship Allāh alone; you have no deity other than Him. There has come to you a clear proof from your Lord. This is the she-camel of Allāh—a sign for you—so let her graze in Allāh’s land, and do not harm her, lest a painful punishment seize you.”",
  7074: "And remember when He made you successors after ʿĀd and settled you in the land: you build palaces on its plains and carve homes in the mountains. So remember the favors of Allāh, and do not spread corruption throughout the land.",
  7075: "The arrogant leaders among his people said to those who were deemed weak—those who believed—“Do you truly know that Ṣāliḥ has been sent by his Lord?” They said, “Indeed, we are believers in what he has been sent with.”",
  7076: "Those who were arrogant said, “We certainly disbelieve in what you believe in.”",
  7077: "Then they hamstrung the she-camel and defied the command of their Lord, saying, “O Ṣāliḥ, bring upon us what you threaten us with, if you are indeed one the messengers.”",
  7078: "Then the earthquake seized them, and they lay lifeless in their homes.",
  7079: "So he turned away from them and said, “O my people, I have indeed conveyed to you the message of my Lord and given you sincere counsel, but you do not like sincere advisers.”",
  7080: "And [We sent] Lūṭ when he said to his people, “Do you commit such indecency as none among all creation has ever preceded you in?”",
  7081: "Indeed, you approach men with desire instead of women. Rather, you are a people who transgress.",
  7082: "But the response of his people was only that they said, “Expel them from your town; indeed, they are people who seek to keep themselves pure.”",
  7083: "So We saved him and his family—except his wife; she was among those who remained behind.",
  7084: "And We rained upon them a rain [18]; so see how the end of the guilty was.\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. A rain of burning stones—described in other passages as stones of fire or baked clay (often understood as brimstone)—sent from above, which utterly destroyed them. The expression conveys both the suddenness and overwhelming severity of divine judgment upon those who persist in grave wrongdoing.",
  7085: "And to Midian We sent their brother Shuʿayb. He said, “O my people, worship Allāh alone; you have no god other than Him. There has come to you a clear proof from your Lord. Give full measure and weight, and do not withhold from people what is rightfully theirs [19], and do not spread corruption on the earth after it has been set right. That is better for you, if you are believers.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. That is, by unjust practices such as unfair or illegal taxes, fraudulent levies, and exploitative interest.",
  7086: "And do not lie in wait on every path, threatening and turning away from the way of Allāh those who believe in Him, seeking to make it crooked. Remember when you were few and He made you many. And consider how the end of the corrupters was.",
  7087: "If a group among you believe in that with which I have been sent, and another group do not believe, then be patient until Allāh judges between us. And He is the best of judges.",
  7088: "The arrogant leaders among his people said, “We will surely drive you out, O Shuʿayb, along with those who believe with you from our town, unless you return to our religion.” He said, “Even if we are unwilling?”",
  7089: "We would indeed be inventing a lie against Allāh if we were to return to your religion after Allāh has saved us from it. And it is not for us to return to it unless Allāh—our Lord—so wills [20]. Our Lord encompasses all things in knowledge. In Allāh we place our trust. Our Lord, judge between us and our people in truth, for You are the best of judges.\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. That is, all creatures are subject to the will of Almighty Allāh: nothing occurs except by His will, and His knowledge encompasses all things—past, present, and future.",
  7090: "The chiefs among his people who disbelieved said, “If you follow Shuʿayb, you will indeed be losers.”",
  7091: "Then the earthquake seized them, and they lay lifeless in their homes.",
  7092: "Those who denied Shuʿayb were as though they had never lived there; those who denied Shuʿayb—it was they who were the losers.",
  7093: "So he turned away from them and said, “O my people, I have conveyed to you the messages of my Lord and given you sincere counsel. How then could I grieve over a disbelieving people?”",
  7094: "We sent no prophet to any town except that We seized its people with poverty and hardship, so that they might humble themselves before Allāh.",
  7095: "Then We replaced the hardship with ease, until they multiplied, and they said, “Hardship and ease also touched our forefathers.” So We seized them suddenly while they did not perceive it.",
  7096: "If the people of the towns had believed [21] and been mindful of Allāh, We would have opened for them blessings from the heavens and the earth; but they denied, so We seized them for what they used to earn.\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. That is, they believed by following the messengers of God who were sent to them.",
  7097: "Do the people of the towns feel secure that Our punishment will not come upon them by night while they are asleep?",
  7098: "Or do the people of the towns feel secure that Our punishment will not come upon them by day while they are at play?",
  7099: "Do they then feel secure from the plan of Allāh [22]? None feel secure from the plan of Allāh except the people who are losers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. That is, Allāh draws them on gradually through blessings and respite, then seizes them suddenly with His mighty power.",
  7100: "Has it not become clear to those who inherit the land after its former people that, if We so willed, We could afflict them for their sins? But We seal their hearts, so they do not hear.",
  7101: "Those towns—We relate to you some of their news. Their messengers came to them with clear proofs, but they would not believe in what they had already denied before. Thus does Allāh seal over the hearts of the disbelievers. [23]\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. Their persistent rejection was not due to a lack of evidence, but to an entrenched denial of the truth. The phrase “Allāh seals over their hearts” signifies that, as a consequence of continued disbelief and refusal to accept guidance, they become spiritually unreceptive—unable to perceive or respond to the truth.\nThese accounts illustrate a recurring pattern: when truth is rejected out of pride or defiance, hearts are sealed and societies decline. The stories of Noah, ʿĀd, Thamūd, Lūṭ, and Midian serve as enduring reminders—calling people to reflect, repent, and return to sincere faith.",
  7102: "We did not find most of them faithful to any covenant; indeed, We found most of them to be lawbreakers.",
  7103: "Then, after them, We sent Moses with Our signs to Pharaoh and his chiefs, but they wrongfully rejected them. So consider how the end of the corrupters was.",
  7104: "Moses said, “O Pharaoh, indeed I am a messenger from the Lord of all the worlds.”",
  7105: "It is incumbent upon me to say nothing about Allāh except the truth. Indeed, I have come to you with a clear proof from your Lord; so send with me the Children of Israel.",
  7106: "Pharaoh said, “If you have come with a sign, then bring it forth, if you are truthful.”",
  7107: "So Moses threw down his staff, and suddenly it became a clearly visible serpent.",
  7108: "And he drew out his hand, and it was white, radiant to the beholders.",
  7109: "The chiefs among the people of Pharaoh said, “Indeed, this is a learned magician.”",
  7110: "He wants to drive you out of your land. So what do you advise?",
  7111: "They said, “Delay him and his brother, and send agents to the cities,”",
  7112: "So that they may bring you every learned magician.",
  7113: "The magicians came to Pharaoh and said, “Will there be a reward for us if we are the ones who prevail?”",
  7114: "Pharaoh said, “Yes—and you will surely be among those brought near.”",
  7115: "They said, “O Moses, either you throw, or we will be the ones to throw first.”",
  7116: "He said, “You throw first.” So when they threw, they bewitched the people’s eyes, struck terror into them, and produced mighty magic.",
  7117: "We inspired Moses, “Throw down your staff.” So at once it swallowed up what they had falsified.",
  7118: "So the truth prevailed, and what they had been doing was rendered void.",
  7119: "So they were defeated right there and utterly humiliated.",
  7120: "The magicians fell down in prostration,",
  7121: "and said, “We have believed in the Lord of the worlds,",
  7122: "the Lord of Moses and Aaron.”",
  7123: "Pharaoh said, “Have you believed in him before I gave you permission? Indeed, this is a plot you have devised in the city to drive out its people. But you will soon know.”",
  7124: "I will surely cut off your hands and your feet on opposite sides; then I will crucify you all.",
  7125: "They said, “Indeed, to our Lord we will return.”",
  7126: "You only take vengeance on us because we believed in the signs of our Lord when they came to us. Our Lord, pour upon us patience and cause us to die as Muslims.",
  7127: "The chiefs among the people of Pharaoh said, “Will you leave Moses and his people to cause corruption in the land and abandon you and your gods?”\nHe said, “We will kill their sons and spare their women; and indeed, we have dominance over them.”",
  7128: "Moses said to his people, “Seek help from Allāh and be patient. Indeed, the earth belongs to Allāh; He grants it to whomever He wills of His servants. And the good outcome is for the righteous.”",
  7129: "They said, “We were oppressed before you came to us and after you came to us.”\nHe said, “Perhaps your Lord will destroy your enemy and make you successors in the land; then He will see how you act.”",
  7130: "Indeed, We afflicted the people of Pharaoh with years of famine and a shortage of fruits, so that they might take heed.",
  7131: "So when good came to them, they said, “This is ours.” But when evil befell them, they saw an evil omen in Moses and those with him. Indeed, their fortune is with Allāh, but most of them do not know.",
  7132: "They said, “No matter what sign you bring us to bewitch us, we will not believe in you.”",
  7133: "So We sent upon them the flood [24], the locusts, the lice, the frogs [25], and the blood [26]—distinct signs; yet they were arrogant and were a sinful people.\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. The flood: A widespread calamity involving destructive flooding, accompanied by the death of livestock and damage to crops, disrupting agriculture and food supplies.\n[25]. Frogs that swarmed across the land and into homes, wells, and daily life, overwhelming the people of Egypt and rendering normal living impossible.\n[26]. The blood: The transformation of Egypt’s water sources—such as rivers, canals, and reservoirs—into blood, making the water undrinkable and unusable.",
  7134: "And when the punishment fell upon them, they said, “O Moses, call upon your Lord for us by what He has promised you. If you remove the punishment from us, we will surely believe you, and we will send with you the Children of Israel.”",
  7135: "But when We removed the punishment from them until a term they were to reach, they immediately broke their promise.",
  7136: "So We exacted retribution from them and drowned them in the sea, because they denied Our signs and were heedless of them.",
  7137: "We caused the people who had been oppressed to inherit the eastern and western lands that We had blessed [27]. And the good word of your Lord was fulfilled for the Children of Israel because of what they had patiently endured. And We destroyed what Pharaoh and his people used to produce and what they used to build.\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]. That is, the blessed lands of the Levant (al-Shām)—the region historically known as Greater Syria, encompassing present-day Palestine, Jordan, Syria, and Lebanon. In the Qur’an, this land is repeatedly described as blessed because of its prophetic legacy, fertility, and central role in the unfolding of divine guidance.",
  7138: "And We led the Children of Israel across the sea; then they came upon a people devoted to their idols. They said, “O Moses, make for us a god just as they have gods [28].” He said, “Indeed, you are an ignorant people.” [29]\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. A Companion of the Prophet ﷺ reported: We set out from Makkah with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ toward Hunayn, and we were new to Islam at that time. The polytheists had a tree under which they would gather, seeking blessing, and upon which they would hang their weapons. It was known as Dhāt Anwāṭ.\nWhen we passed by a large green tree, we said, “O Messenger of Allāh, make for us a Dhāt Anwāṭ as they have a Dhāt Anwāṭ.”\nThe Messenger of Allāh ﷺ replied, “By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, you have said exactly what the people of Moses said: ‘Make for us a god just as they have gods.’ Indeed, you are a people who act ignorantly. You will surely follow the ways of those before you—step by step.”\nThis narration illustrates the subtle and dangerous nature of shirk (associating partners with Allāh). A person may believe an act brings him closer to Allāh, while in reality it distances him from His mercy and leads toward His displeasure. Seeking blessing, protection, or benefit from trees, stones, objects, or places—regardless of intention—is a form of idolatry when such acts are directed to other than Allāh. Sincere striving in the path of Allāh therefore requires vigilance in preserving pure monotheism (tawḥīd) and rejecting all practices that compromise it.\n[29] That is, a people ignorant of the Majesty and Greatness of Allāh and of what is obligatory upon them—namely, to worship Him alone without partner, as the only true God and Lord of all existence.\nThe ignorance referred to here is not a lack of intelligence or reasoning ability, but moral and religious ignorance—a failure to grasp and uphold pure monotheism despite having witnessed clear signs of divine power and guidance. Such ignorance manifests in imitation of false practices, attachment to created things, and neglect of Allāh’s exclusive right to worship.",
  7139: "Indeed, what they are devoted to is doomed, and what they do is worthless. [30]\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. Blessing (barakah) is determined solely by Allāh. No one may designate, alter, or add sources of blessing except through revelation. Whatever Allāh and His Messenger ﷺ have identified as means of blessing is accepted, and whatever has no basis in revelation is not permissible.\nPermissible means of seeking blessing mentioned in the revealed sources include:\na) Objects\nZamzam water\nThe Black Stone\nb) Times\nThe month of Ramadan\nThe Night of Decree (Laylat al-Qadr)\nThe first ten days of Dhul-Hijjah\nc) Places\nThe Sacred Mosque in Mecca\nThe Prophet’s Mosque in Medina\nThe Sacred Mosque in Jerusalem\nd) Actions\nPrayer\nFasting\nCharity\nAll acts of obedience legislated in Islam.\nWhat is not established in revelation as a means of blessing is forbidden, including:\nPersons: seeking blessing through saints or pious individuals.\nTimes: such as the Night of Ascension or the night of the Prophet’s birth\nPlaces: graves, shrines, or similar locations\nActions: religious innovations and newly invented practices.\nThe blessings of Allāh can only be attained through obedience to Allāh and obedience to Muhammad ﷺ.\nThis is realized by:\nFulfilling what is obligatory,\nAvoiding what is forbidden,\nHastening to repentance and seeking forgiveness whenever one falls short.",
  7140: "He said, “Shall I seek for you a god other than Allāh, while He has favored you above the peoples [of your time]?”",
  7141: "And [remember] when We saved you from the people of Pharaoh, who were afflicting you with the worst torment—killing your sons and sparing your women. And in that was a great trial from your Lord.",
  7142: " We appointed for Moses thirty nights and completed them with ten, so the term of his Lord was fulfilled as forty nights. And Moses said to his brother Aaron, “Take my place among my people, set things right, and do not follow the way of the corrupters.”",
  7143: "When Moses came at Our appointed time and his Lord spoke to him, he said, “My Lord, let me see You.” He said, “You will not see Me [31], but look at the mountain; if it remains in its place, then you will see Me.” But when his Lord appeared to the mountain, He rendered it level, and Moses fell unconscious. Then when he recovered, he said, “Glory be to You! I repent to You, and I am the first of the believers.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [31]. No one can see Allāh in this worldly life. Human perception in the present world is limited and cannot bear the direct vision of the Creator. This is affirmed by the statement to Moses, “You will not see Me” (Qur’an 7:143), which establishes the impossibility of seeing Allāh in this life.\nHowever, the believers will see Allāh in the Hereafter, as clearly affirmed in the Qur’an and authentic Sunnah. Allāh says:\n“Faces on that Day will be radiant, looking toward their Lord.” (Qur’an 75:22–23)\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“You will see your Lord as you see the full moon—without crowding or difficulty.”\nThis vision will occur without modality, comparison, or comprehension of His essence—without asking how and without resemblance to created beings. It is the greatest reward of Paradise, granted exclusively to the believers as an honor and delight beyond all worldly pleasures.\nThus, the verse concerning Moses does not deny the vision of Allāh absolutely; rather, it affirms: Impossibility in this world, due to human limitation. Certainty in the Hereafter, by divine grace and promise.",
  7144: "He said, “O Moses, I have chosen you above the people by My messages and by speaking to you. So hold fast to what I have given you, and be among the grateful.”",
  7145: " We wrote for him on the Tablets an admonition and a detailed explanation of all things. So take hold of them with firmness, and command your people to take what is best of it. Soon I will show you the dwelling of the defiantly disobedient.",
  7146: "I will turn away from My signs those who are arrogant upon the earth without right. Even if they see every sign, they will not believe in it; and if they see the path of guidance, they will not take it as a path. But if they see the path of error, they will take it as a path. That is because they denied Our signs and were heedless of them.",
  7147: "As for those who deny Our signs and the meeting of the Hereafter—their deeds have become void. Are they recompensed except for what they used to do?",
  7148: "And the people of Moses, after him, made from their ornaments a calf—an image with a lowing sound [32]. Did they not see that it could neither speak to them nor guide them to any path? Yet they took it for worship, and they were wrongdoers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [32]. This lifeless body lacked even the most basic attributes of divinity. It could neither speak to them nor benefit them in any way, nor guide them to what was right. The verse appeals to simple reason: that which possesses no power, knowledge, or guidance can never be worthy of worship.",
  7149: "When they were overcome with regret and saw that they had gone astray, they said, “If our Lord does not have mercy upon us and forgive us, we will surely be among the losers.”",
  7150: "When Moses returned to his people, angry and grieved, he said, “How evil is what you have done after me! Did you hasten the command of your Lord?” And he cast down the Tablets and seized his brother by the head, pulling him toward himself. He said, “O son of my mother, the people overpowered me and nearly killed me. So do not let the enemies rejoice over me, and do not place me among the wrongdoing people.”",
  7151: "Moses said, “My Lord, forgive me and my brother [33], and admit us into Your mercy. You are the Most Merciful of the merciful.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [33]. Moses immediately turns from righteous anger to humility, seeking forgiveness from Allāh both for himself—due to his harsh reaction—and for his brother Aaron, who was innocent but caught amid the turmoil. This verse beautifully balances justice and compassion: after condemning the sin and addressing wrongdoing, Moses humbly returns to supplication, entrusting the matter to divine mercy.\nIt teaches that even justified anger must ultimately give way to prayer, humility, and compassion, and that true leadership is marked not only by firmness against error, but by repentance, mercy, and concern for unity.",
  7152: "Indeed, those who took the calf for worship will be afflicted with wrath from their Lord and humiliation in the life of this world. Thus do We recompense the inventors of falsehood.",
  7153: "But as for those who commit evil deeds and then repent and believe, indeed your Lord is, thereafter, All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  7154: "When the anger subsided in Moses, he took up the Tablets; and in their inscription was guidance and mercy for those who fear their Lord.",
  7155: "And Moses chose from his people seventy men for Our appointed meeting. When the earthquake seized them, he said, “My Lord, if You had willed, You could have destroyed them before—and me as well. Would You destroy us for what the foolish among us have done? This is only Your trial, by which You lead astray whom You will and guide whom You will. You are our Protector, so forgive us and have mercy on us; You are the best of forgivers.”",
  7156: "“Ordain for us good in this world and in the Hereafter; indeed, we have turned back to You.” He said, “My punishment—I afflict with it whom I will, but My mercy encompasses all things. So I will ordain it for those who fear Me, give the prescribed alms, and believe in Our revelations,",
  7157: "Those who follow the Messenger—the unlettered Prophet—whom they find written with them in the Torah [34] and the Gospel [35]—he enjoins upon them what is right and forbids them what is wrong, makes lawful for them what is pure and forbids them what is impure, and relieves them of their burden and the shackles that were upon them. So those who believe in him, honor him, support him, and follow the light sent down with him—it is they who are the successful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [34]. Deuteronomy 18:18\n“A prophet like Moses… from among their brethren\" that is Muhammad ﷺ, a law-bearing prophet from Ishmael’s line	\nDeuteronomy 33:2	\nGod shines from Sinai, Seir, and Paran	Three stages of revelation: Moses, Jesus, Muhammad	\nIsaiah 42	\nServant from Kedar brings justice: That is Muhammad ﷺ from Makkah (descendant of Kedar)	\n[35]. John 14–16	\nPromise of the “Comforter\" Foretelling Prophet Ahmad ﷺ	\nSong of Solomon 5:16	\n“Mahmadim” = “The Praised One”	Linguistic echo of Muhammad ﷺ",
  7158: "Say, “O mankind, indeed I am the Messenger of Allāh to you all. To Him belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth; there is no god except Him. He gives life and causes death. So believe in Allāh and His Messenger, the unlettered Prophet, who believes in Allāh and His words, and follow him, so that you may be rightly guided.” [36]\\n\\nFootnotes: [36]. Whoever sincerely declares, “There is no god worthy of worship except Allāh, and Muḥammad is His servant and Messenger,” affirming it with the tongue and believing it wholeheartedly, has entered Islam and laid the foundation for salvation. Such a person is promised deliverance from eternal punishment and admission into Paradise by Allāh’s mercy, provided that this faith is accompanied by sincerity, obedience, and repentance.\nTrue belief is not mere speech, but conviction of the heart expressed through worship, moral conduct, and submission to Allāh’s commands. While sins may bring accountability and punishment, sincere faith ultimately leads to forgiveness, and no one who truly affirms monotheism will remain eternally in the Hellfire.\nThis verse therefore calls not only to verbal testimony, but to living faith—belief, following the Messenger ﷺ, and walking the path of guidance that leads to success in this world and the Hereafter.",
  7159: "And among the people of Moses is a community that guides by the truth and by it establishes justice.",
  7160: "And We divided them into twelve tribes. And We inspired Moses when his people asked him for water: “Strike the rock with your staff.” So twelve springs gushed forth from it, and each tribe knew its drinking place. And We shaded them with clouds and sent down upon them manna and quails: “Eat of the good things We have provided for you.” They did not wrong Us, but they were wronging themselves.",
  7161: "When it was said to them, “Dwell in this city [of Jerusalem] and eat from it wherever you will, and say, ‘Absolve us,’ and enter the gate bowing humbly; We will forgive you your sins, and We will increase those who do good.”",
  7162: "But those among them who did wrong changed the words to something other than what had been said to them, so We sent down upon them a punishment from the sky because of their wrongdoing.",
  7163: "Ask them about the town [of Eila] that was by the sea—when they transgressed regarding the Sabbath: when their fish came to them openly on their Sabbath day, but on the day they did not observe the Sabbath, they did not come to them. Thus We tested them because of their persistent disobedience.",
  7164: "When a group among them said, “Why do you admonish a people whom Allāh is about to destroy or punish with a severe punishment [37]?” They said, “As an excuse before your Lord, and perhaps they may fear Him.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [37]. Although we cannot force others to abandon sinful behavior, we are still responsible to advise with compassion and wisdom. Speaking the truth is not meant to humiliate or coerce, but to fulfill moral responsibility and leave the door open for repentance. As the Qur’an teaches elsewhere, guidance belongs to Allāh, while our duty is sincere counsel.\nThis verse shows that admonition serves two purposes: it absolves the believer of negligence before Allāh, and it may awaken conscience in others. True counsel is therefore offered with humility, patience, and mercy, following the example of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, whose character embodied truthfulness, compassion, and restraint. It teaches that even when change seems unlikely, speaking the truth with wisdom and love remains an act of faith and obedience.",
  7165: "When they forgot what they had been reminded of, We saved those who forbade evil and seized those who did wrong with a wretched punishment because of their persistent disobedience.",
  7166: "When they persisted in what they had been forbidden, We said to them, “Be apes, despised.”",
  7167: "And when your Lord declared that He would surely send against them, until the Day of Resurrection, those who would afflict them with the worst punishment. Indeed, your Lord is swift in punishment; and He is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  7168: "And We divided them throughout the earth into communities: among them are the righteous, and among them are others besides that. And We tested them with good and bad, so that they might return.",
  7169: "Then they were succeeded by generations who inherited the Scripture, taking the gains of this lower life and saying, “We will be forgiven.” And if similar gains come to them, they will take them [38]. Was not the covenant of the Scripture taken from them—that they would not say about Allāh except the truth—and they had studied what was in it? And the abode of the Hereafter is better for those who fear Allāh. Will you not then understand?\\n\\nFootnotes: [38]. That is, unlawful gains and fleeting pleasures of this worldly life—such as wealth, status, or advantage acquired through dishonesty, compromise of religious principles, or distortion of divine guidance. The verse criticizes not lawful enjoyment itself, but the pursuit of worldly benefit at the expense of truth, integrity, and obedience to Allāh.\nThese people exploited their religious knowledge for material gain, reassuring themselves with empty claims of forgiveness while persisting in the same wrongdoing whenever opportunity arose. Such behavior reflects a dangerous misuse of divine mercy—treating it as permission rather than a call to repentance.\nThe Qur’an contrasts these temporary gains with the abiding reward of the Hereafter, reminding that true success lies not in what is quickly consumed, but in what endures with Allāh.",
  7170: "But those who hold fast [39] to the Scripture and establish the prayer—indeed, We do not let the reward of the righteous be lost.\\n\\nFootnotes: [39]. That is, to take hold of the Scripture firmly and adhere to it in belief, practice, and conduct—not merely to possess it or recite it. Holding fast implies commitment, obedience, and perseverance: learning its guidance, upholding its commands, avoiding what it forbids, and remaining steadfast even when doing so conflicts with personal desire or worldly gain.\nThus, “holding fast” contrasts with those who exploited the Scripture for advantage; it describes those who live by revelation, not those who merely inherit or claim it.",
  7171: "And when We raised the mountain above them as if it were a canopy, and they thought it would fall upon them: “Hold firmly to what We have given you and remember what is in it, so that you may be mindful of Allāh.”",
  7172: "And [remember] when your Lord brought forth from the loins of the Children of Ādam their descendants and made them testify concerning themselves, [saying], “Am I not your Lord?” They said, “Yes—indeed, we testify.” [We did so] lest you should say on the Day of Resurrection, “Indeed We were of this unaware,”",
  7173: "or lest you should say, “It was only our forefathers who associated others with Allāh before, and we were descendants who came after them. Will You then destroy us for what the falsifiers have done?”",
  7174: "Thus We make the verses clear, so that they may return to Allāh.",
  7175: "And recite to them the story of the one [40] to whom We gave Our signs, but he turned away from them; so Satan pursued him, and he became one of the deviants.\\n\\nFootnotes: [40]. Many early commentators identify this individual as Balaam son of Beor, a learned man from among the Children of Israel who was known for his knowledge of divine revelation. According to these reports, he was granted insight and understanding but chose worldly desire over obedience, deliberately turning away from the guidance he possessed.\nThe Qur’an, however, does not name him, emphasizing instead the moral lesson: that knowledge without sincerity can become a cause of ruin. When a person abandons divine guidance by choice, the protection it affords is withdrawn, leaving them vulnerable to misguidance.\nThus, the verse serves as a timeless warning—the gravest loss is not ignorance, but knowingly turning away after understanding the truth.",
  7176: "If We had willed, We could have elevated him by them; but he clung to the earth and followed his own desire. So his likeness is that of a dog: if you chase it, it pants, or if you leave it, it pants. Such is the likeness of the people who deny Our signs. So relate the stories, that they may reflect.",
  7177: "How evil is the example of the people who deny Our signs and used to wrong themselves.",
  7178: "Whoever Allāh guides is rightly guided; and whoever He allows to go astray—those are the losers.",
  7179: "We have certainly created for Hell many of the jinn and humankind. They have hearts with which they do not understand, eyes with which they do not see, and ears with which they do not hear [41]. They are like livestock—rather more astray [42]. It is they who are the heedless.\\n\\nFootnotes: [41]. That is, they fail to use the faculties of reason, perception, and moral discernment that Allāh has endowed them with to recognize the truth and respond to His guidance. Their hearts, eyes, and ears are not defective in themselves; rather, they are deliberately neglected, rendering them ineffective for understanding and accepting the message of Allāh and the way of submission to Him (Islam).\n[42]. They become absorbed in gratifying their base desires and worldly impulses, with little concern for accountability in the Hereafter. In this regard, they resemble livestock, which follow instinct without moral responsibility. Indeed, they are described as more astray, because humans are endowed with intellect and choice—yet these individuals willingly abandon those higher capacities.",
  7180: "And to Allāh belong the Most Beautiful Names, so call upon Him by them [43]. And leave those who deviate concerning His names [44]; they will be recompensed for what they used to do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [43]. Believers are encouraged to find solace, strength, and inner peace in Allāh through His Most Beautiful Names (al-asmāʾ al-ḥusnā). Each Name reflects a perfect divine attribute—mercy, power, wisdom, care, life, and justice—and calling upon Allāh through them nurtures hope, patience, trust, and resilience. Remembering Allāh by His Names helps steady the heart, restore perspective, and protect one’s moral and spiritual well-being in times of hardship and trial.\n[44]. IThat is, to misuse, distort, deny, or apply meanings to Allāh’s Names that are not befitting His majesty. Deviation (ilḥād) regarding the Divine Names includes denying them, stripping them of their meanings, likening Allāh to His creation, inventing attributes without revelation, or using His Names in irreverent or misleading ways.\nAmong the authentic Names by which Allāh has described Himself are Allāh, Ar-Raḥmān (the Most Compassionate), Ar-Raḥīm (the Most Merciful), Al-Ḥayy (the Ever-Living), and Al-Qayyūm (the All-Sustaining), among many others. These Names affirm His absolute perfection, transcendence, and uniqueness.\nThe Qur’an emphasizes that Allāh is the proper and supreme Name of the One true God, a Name of majesty that encompasses\nall divine attributes. Linguistically related Semitic terms such as El, Eloah, and Elohim were used by earlier prophets, including Moses and Jesus, to refer to the same singular God—whom Muslims call Allāh (Allāhumma in supplication).\nIslam affirms that Allāh is absolutely unlike His creation and rejects attributing to Him descriptions implying human limitations, physicality, or lineage. For this reason, Muslims refrain from using relational or metaphorical terms that may compromise divine transcendence, adhering instead to the Names and attributes revealed by Allāh Himself.\nThe seriousness of respecting God’s Names is echoed in earlier scripture as well:\n“You shall not misuse the name of the LORD your God…” (Exodus 20:7)\nThe Qur’an repeatedly emphasizes the centrality of Allāh’s Names, as in:\n“He is Allāh—there is no god worthy of worship except Him… He has the Most Beautiful Names. Whatever is in the heavens and the earth glorifies Him, and He is the Almighty, the All-Wise.”\n— Qur’an 59:22–24\nThus, believers are commanded to invoke Allāh by His revealed Names, and to avoid all forms of distortion or irreverence regarding them, trusting that those who persist in such deviation will be held accountable by Allāh alone.",
  7181: "And among those We have created is a community [45] that guides by the truth and establishes justice by it.\\n\\nFootnotes: [45]. That is, the followers of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, who uphold the truth revealed to him and strive to establish justice through it. Many scholars explain that this verse refers primarily to the Muslim community (ummah), as they were entrusted with preserving and conveying divine guidance after earlier communities failed in that duty.\nAt the same time, the wording of the verse is broad, indicating that in every age Allāh raises people who guide by the truth and judge with justice. The Muslim ummah is distinguished in that it was explicitly commissioned to carry this responsibility universally—calling to truth, upholding justice, and standing as witnesses over humanity.",
  7182: "But those who deny Our signs—We will gradually lead them from where they do not know. [46]\\n\\nFootnotes: [46]. That is, by granting them repeated favors and apparent ease, such as wealth, success, or prolonged respite, which they mistakenly interpret as approval. Instead of leading them to gratitude and repentance, these favors only increase them in arrogance, heedlessness, and sin, until they are taken suddenly and decisively.\nThis gradual process—known as istidrāj—is not a sign of divine pleasure, but a form of hidden punishment, wherein continued blessings harden the heart and deepen accountability.",
  7183: "And I grant them respite; indeed, My plan is firm. [47]\\n\\nFootnotes: [47]. The Prophet ﷺ, explained the meaning of divine respite, saying, “Indeed, Allāh grants respite to the wrongdoer, but when He seizes him, He does not let him escape.” He ﷺ then recited the words of Allāh: “Such is the seizing of your Lord when He seizes the towns while they are doing wrong. Indeed, His seizing is painful and severe.” — Qur’an 11:102\nThis ḥadīth clarifies that delay in punishment is not neglect or approval, but part of a deliberate divine plan. When accountability finally comes, it is decisive and unavoidable, perfectly aligned with justice.",
  7184: "Have they not reflected? There is no madness in their companion; he is only a clear warner.",
  7185: "Have they not looked into the dominion of the heavens and the earth and all that Allāh has created, and that perhaps their appointed term has drawn near? Then in what statement after this will they believe?",
  7186: "Whoever Allāh lets go astray—there is no guide for him; and He leaves them to wander blindly in their transgression. [48]\\n\\nFootnotes: [48]. That is, as a consequence of their persistent wrongdoing and deliberate rejection of the truth. When people repeatedly turn away from guidance after it has been made clear, Allāh withdraws His aid and leaves them to the path they have chosen, resulting in confusion, moral blindness, and continued transgression.\nThis misguidance is therefore not arbitrary, but the outcome of sustained defiance and refusal to respond to divine signs.",
  7187: "They ask you about the Hour—when will it occur? Say, “Its knowledge is only with my Lord. None will disclose its time except Him. It weighs heavily upon the heavens and the earth. It will not come upon you except suddenly.” They ask you as if you are well aware of it. Say, “Its knowledge is only with Allāh, but most people do not know.”",
  7188: "Say, “I do not possess for myself any benefit nor harm, except what Allāh wills. And if I knew the unseen, I would have acquired much good, and no harm would have touched me. I am only a warner and a bearer of good news to a people who believe.” [49]\\n\\nFootnotes: [49]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ is not a worker of miracles at will, nor does he possess knowledge of the unseen except what Allāh grants him. He is nothing more than a conveyor of God’s message. People are therefore called to listen, reflect, and believe—but only those who are mindful will truly benefit from him.",
  7189: "He is the One who created you from a single soul and made from it its spouse, that he might find repose with her. Then when he covers her, she carries a light burden and continues with it. But when it grows heavy, they both call upon Allāh, their Lord: “If You give us a righteous one, we will surely be among the thankful.”",
  7190: "But when He gives them a righteous one, they associate partners with Him concerning what He has given them [50]. So exalted is Allāh above all that they associate with Him.\\n\\nFootnotes: [50]. That is, they came to love the child with a love that rivaled their love for Allāh. But Allāh is far too exalted for anything to be loved as an equal alongside Him.",
  7191: "Do they associate with Allāh those who create nothing, while they themselves are created?",
  7192: "They cannot help them, nor can they help themselves.",
  7193: "If you call them to guidance, they will not follow you; it is the same whether you call them or remain silent.",
  7194: "Indeed, those whom you call upon besides Allāh are servants like yourselves. So call upon them and let them respond to you, if you are truthful.",
  7195: "Do they have feet with which they walk, or hands with which they grasp, or eyes with which they see, or ears with which they hear [51]? Say, “Call upon your idols, then conspire against me and give me no respite.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [51]. This outright challenge highlights the powerlessness of their gods. \nA closely parallel critique of idolatry appears in the Hebrew Bible. Psalm 115:4–7 states: “Their idols are silver and gold, the work of human hands. They have mouths, but do not speak; eyes, but do not see. They have ears, but do not hear; noses, but do not smell. They have hands, but do not feel; feet, but do not walk; nor do they make a sound in their throat.” Similarly, Psalm 135:15–17 declares: “The idols of the nations are silver and gold, the work of human hands. They have mouths, but do not speak; eyes, but do not see; they have ears, but do not hear, nor is there any breath in their mouths.” These passages closely parallel Qurʾān 7:195 in imagery and argument, reflecting a shared prophetic denunciation of idolatry.",
  7196: "Indeed, my protector is Allāh, who has sent down the Book [52], and He protects the righteous.\\n\\nFootnotes: [52]. The Qur’an, the Gospel, the Torah, the Psalms, among others.",
  7197: "But those whom you call upon besides Him cannot help you, nor can they help themselves.",
  7198: "If you call them to guidance, they do not hear; and you see them looking at you, while they do not see.",
  7199: "Be gracious, enjoin what is right, and turn away from the ignorant.",
  7200: "If an evil whisper comes to you from Satan, then seek refuge with Allāh [53]. Indeed, He is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [53]. That is, from being provoked into rage or driven into sinful conduct.",
  7201: "Indeed, those who fear Allāh—when an evil whisper from Satan touches them—remember Him, and at once they see clearly.",
  7202: "But their brothers—the devils—draw them deeper into error, and they do not cease.",
  7203: "When you do not bring them a sign [54], they say, “Why have you not contrived it?” Say, “I only follow what is revealed to me from my Lord. This is an insight from your Lord, and guidance and mercy for a people who believe.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [54]. The Qur’an itself is the greatest sign, yet they rejected it blindly without reflection or insight.",
  7204: "When the Qur’an is recited, listen to it attentively and remain silent, that you may be shown mercy.",
  7205: "And remember your Lord within yourself, with humility and fear, without raising your voice, morning and evening, and do not be among the heedless.",
  7206: "Indeed, those who are near your Lord are not too proud to worship Him. They glorify Him, and to Him they prostrate.",
  8001: "They ask you about the spoils of war. Say, “The spoils belong to Allāh and the Messenger. So fear Allāh, set matters right among yourselves, and obey Allāh and His Messenger, if you are believers.”",
  8002: "The believers are only those whose hearts tremble when Allāh is mentioned, and when His verses are recited to them, it increases them in faith, and upon their Lord they rely; [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. Those who place their hope and trust entirely in Allāh find their strength renewed and their needs provided for. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: “If you were to rely upon Allāh with true reliance, He would provide for you as He provides for the birds: they leave their nests hungry in the morning and return in the evening full.”",
  8003: "those who establish the prayer and spend from what We have provided for them.",
  8004: "These are the true believers. For them are high ranks with their Lord, forgiveness, and an honorable provision.",
  8005: "Just as your Lord brought you out of your home in truth [2], even though a group among the believers were averse to it; [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. That is, for the expedition to Badr, undertaken to uphold justice and truth, though its full consequences were not yet apparent to the believers.\n[3]. That is, some believers felt unease and reluctance—particularly in light of the earlier disagreement concerning the distribution of the war booty.",
  8006: "They disputed with you concerning the truth after it had become clear, as if they were being driven toward death while they were looking on. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. This vivid simile describes the intense fear and reluctance some believers felt when facing armed confrontation at Badr, despite the truth of God’s command having become clear. It reflects natural human apprehension in the face of mortal danger, not disbelief or hypocrisy.",
  8007: "When Allāh promised you one of the two groups [5]—that it would be yours—and you wished that the unarmed one would be yours. But Allāh intended to establish the truth by His words and to cut off the root of the disbelievers,\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. That is, one of the two enemy groups: either the trade caravan or the armed band of Meccan fighters.",
  8008: "so that He might establish the truth and abolish falsehood, even if the criminals disliked it.",
  8009: "When you called upon your Lord for help [6], and He responded to you: “I will reinforce you with a thousand angels, following one another in succession.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. During the time of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, a hypocrite used to harm the believers. Some of them said to one another, “Come, let us seek the help of Allāh’s Messenger against this hypocrite.” The Prophet ﷺ replied: “No one is to seek help from me; rather, help is to be sought from Allāh alone.”\nThis response demonstrates complete reliance upon Allāh (tawakkul) and clarifies that ultimate aid and protection belong solely to Him. Cultivating a habit of seeking supernatural help from created beings—especially in matters that only Allāh controls—diverts reliance away from the Creator and places it upon creation, which can lead to idolatry.\nAccordingly, asking the deceased to supplicate to Allāh, to fulfill needs, to cure illness, to grant provision, or to avert harm is regarded as polytheism, for it assigns divine powers to created beings. This resembles the idolatry committed by Christians who invoke Mary and Jesus Christ, believing them to possess knowledge of people’s circumstances or the ability to intervene independently. Help and assistance are to be sought from Allāh alone, for He alone has the power to grant them.",
  8010: "Allāh made it only as glad tidings and to reassure your hearts by it. And victory is only from Allāh. Indeed, Allāh is All-Mighty, All-Wise.",
  8011: "When He caused drowsiness to overcome you as reassurance from Him, and sent down water upon you from the sky to purify you by it, to remove from you the impurity of Satan, to strengthen your hearts, and to make your feet firm thereby.",
  8012: " When your Lord inspired the angels: “I am with you [7], so strengthen those who believe. I will cast terror into the hearts of those who disbelieve and wage war against them; so strike them at their necks and strike from them every fingertip.” [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. The phrase “I am with you” in the Qur'an expresses divine support and assurance, not physical presence. It indicates that Allāh is aiding, guiding, and supporting the angels in their role of strengthening the believers during the Battle of Badr. In this context, it conveys reassurance, victory, and moral support rather than a literal or physical sense of being “with” them.\n[8]. This verse refers specifically to the Battle of Badr, an early conflict in which Muslims from Medina were outnumbered by forces from Mecca. The statement describes divine support given during this battle, including strengthening the believers and weakening enemy morale. The command to “strike their necks” and “fingertips” reflects conventional battlefield language of the time, indicating decisive combat against enemy fighters. It is context-specific and not a general instruction for interactions outside of warfare.",
  8013: "That is because they opposed Allāh and His Messenger. And whoever opposes Allāh and His Messenger—indeed, Allāh is severe in punishment.",
  8014: "That is for you, so taste it; and for the disbelievers is the punishment of the Fire.",
  8015: "O believers, when you meet the disbelievers advancing in battle, do not turn your backs to them.",
  8016: "Whoever turns his back to them on that day—unless it is for a tactical maneuver or to join another group—has indeed incurred the wrath of Allāh, and his refuge is Hell—what a miserable destination!",
  8017: "You did not kill them, but Allāh killed them; and you did not throw when you threw, but Allāh threw [9]—so that He might test the believers with a gracious test from Him [10]. Indeed, Allāh is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. That is, Allāh caused the dust to reach the eyes and nostrils of the opposing soldiers, disorienting them and hindering their advance.\n[10]. That is, so that the believers might recognize and appreciate the favor bestowed upon them by Allāh.",
  8018: "That is so—and indeed, Allāh will thwart the schemes of the disbelievers.",
  8019: "If you seek the decision, then the decision has come to you. If you desist [11], it will be better for you; but if you return [to war], We will return. Your forces will not avail you at all, however numerous they may be—for Allāh is with the believers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. That is, to desist from associating partners with Allāh, denying His message, and persisting in hostility toward the believers.",
  8020: "O believers, obey Allāh and His Messenger, and do not turn away from him while you hear him.",
  8021: "And do not be like those who say, “We have heard,” while they do not hear.",
  8022: "Indeed, the worst of living creatures in Allāh’s sight are the deaf and the dumb—those who do not understand. [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. This refers to the disbelievers who, although possessing the faculties of hearing and speech, do not use them to perceive the truth or to affirm it. Their description as “deaf and dumb” is therefore not literal, but denotes their deliberate turning away from hearing the truth and speaking it, resulting in a lack of true understanding.",
  8023: "If Allāh had known any good in them, He would have made them hear; and if He had made them hear, they would have turned away in aversion.",
  8024: "O believers, respond to Allāh and the Messenger when he calls you to that which gives you life [13]. And know that Allāh intervenes between a person and his heart, and that to Him you will all be gathered.\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. That is, responding to the call to strive against the forces of evil—most immediately through participation in the lawful struggle when commanded—by which faith is preserved and true life is safeguarded.",
  8025: "Beware of a trial that will not strike exclusively those among you who have wronged [14]; and know that Allāh is severe in punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. That is, its effects may extend to the entire community, afflicting both the righteous and the wicked, when wrongdoing is tolerated or left unopposed.",
  8026: "And remember when you were few and oppressed in the land, fearing that people might snatch you away. Then He gave you refuge, strengthened you with His help, and provided you with good things—so that you might be grateful.",
  8027: "O believers, do not betray Allāh and the Messenger, nor betray your trusts knowingly.",
  8028: "And know that your wealth and your children are only a trial [15], and that with Allāh is a great reward.\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. That is, because of attachment to wealth and children, one may be tempted to violate Allāh’s commands concerning them, placing personal desire above obedience.",
  8029: "O believers, if you fear Allāh, He will grant you a criterion, and remove from you your misdeeds and forgive you. And Allāh is the Possessor of great bounty.",
  8030: "And [remember] when those who disbelieved conspired against you to imprison you or kill you or expel you. They plan, and Allāh plans—and Allāh is the best of planners.",
  8031: "When Our verses are recited to them, they say, “We have heard. If we wished, we could say something like this. This is nothing but the tales of the former peoples.”",
  8032: "And [remember] when they said, ‘O Allāh, if this is indeed the truth from You, then rain down upon us stones from the sky or bring upon us a painful punishment.’",
  8033: "But Allāh would not punish them while you, [O Prophet], are among them, nor would He punish them while they seek forgiveness.",
  8034: "But why should Allāh not punish them, when they hinder others from the Sacred Mosque, though they are not its rightful guardians? Its true guardians are only the righteous, but most of them do not know.",
  8035: "And their prayer at the House [i.e., the Kaʿbah] was nothing but whistling and clapping. So taste the punishment for what you used to disbelieve.",
  8036: "Indeed, those who disbelieve spend their wealth to hinder others from the way of Allāh. They will continue to spend it, but it will become a source of regret for them; then they will be overcome. And those who disbelieve will be gathered to Hell,",
  8037: "so that Allāh may distinguish the impure from the pure, and place the impure one upon another, heaping them all together, then cast them into Hell. It is they who are the losers.",
  8038: "Say to those who disbelieve: if they desist, what has passed will be forgiven for them; but if they return, then the fate of the former peoples has already been set as a precedent.",
  8039: "And fight them until there is no more persecution [16], and the religion is wholly for Allāh alone. But if they desist [17], then indeed Allāh is All-Seeing of what they do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. That is, believers are commanded to fight until hostile persecution and coercion are ended, and until people are free to worship Allāh alone without interference, intimidation, or enforced idolatry. The objective is not violence for its own sake, but the removal of oppression so that sincere worship and peace may prevail.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: “Whoever dies without having fought in the cause of Allāh, nor having resolved to do so, dies with a trait of hypocrisy within him.”\nThis refers not to one who lacked opportunity, but to one who harbors no readiness or resolve to defend the faith when required. Refraining from struggle out of cowardice or indifference—when duty calls—was characteristic of the hypocrites. Thus, a believer is distinguished by inward willingness to uphold truth and justice, even if circumstances never allow actual \nparticipation.\n[17]. That is, if they cease idolatry, persecution, and hostility toward the believers, and refrain from aggression.",
  8040: "But if they turn away, then know that Allāh is your Protector—an excellent Protector and an excellent Helper.",
  8041: "And know that whatever spoils of war you gain, one-fifth of it belongs to Allāh, the Messenger [18], his near relatives [19], the orphans, the needy, and the stranded traveler—if you truly believe in Allāh and in what We sent down upon Our Servant on the Day of Distinction, the day when the two armies met. And Allāh is over all things All-Powerful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. That is, to be administered in the cause of Allāh for communal welfare, public needs, and lawful purposes determined by divine guidance, not for personal use.\n[19]. That is, the clans of Banū Hāshim and Banū ʿAbd al‑Muṭṭalib, who are prohibited from receiving zakāh and were therefore allotted a share from this fifth.",
  8042: "[Remember] when you were on the nearer side of the valley, and they were on the farther side, while the caravan was below you. Had you made an appointment, you would surely have failed to keep it. But it was so that Allāh might accomplish a matter already decreed—so that those who were to perish would perish upon clear evidence, and those who were to live would live upon clear evidence. And indeed, Allāh is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.",
  8043: "[Remember] when Allāh showed them to you in your dream as few in number. Had He shown them to you as many, you would surely have lost heart and disputed over the matter—but Allāh saved you. Indeed, He is All-Knowing of what is within the hearts.",
  8044: "And [remember] when, as you met, He made them appear few in your eyes and made you appear few in their eyes, so that Allāh might accomplish a matter already decreed. And to Allāh all matters are returned.",
  8045: "O believers, when you encounter a hostile force, remain steadfast and remember Allāh much, so that you may be successful.",
  8046: "Obey Allāh and His Messenger, and do not dispute among yourselves, lest you lose heart and your strength depart. And remain steadfast—indeed, Allāh is with those who are steadfast.",
  8047: "Do not be like those who came forth from their homes in arrogance and to be seen by people, turning others away from the way of Allāh. And Allāh encompasses all that they do.",
  8048: "And [remember] when Satan made their deeds seem appealing to them and said, ‘No one among the people can overcome you today, and I am your protector.’ But when the two armies came within sight of one another, he turned on his heels and said, ‘Indeed, I disown you. Indeed, I see what you do not see; indeed, I fear Allāh. And Allāh is severe in punishment.’",
  8049: "[Remember] when the hypocrites and those in whose hearts was a disease said, ‘These people have been deceived by their religion.’ But whoever puts his trust in Allāh —then indeed Allāh is All-Mighty, All-Wise.",
  8050: "If only you could see when the angels take the souls of those who disbelieve, striking their faces and their backs, [saying], ‘Taste the punishment of the Burning Fire.’",
  8051: "That is for what your own hands have sent forth—and because Allāh is never unjust to His servants.",
  8052: "Their example is as that of the people of Pharaoh and those before them: they disbelieved in the signs of Allāh, so Allāh seized them for their sins. Indeed, Allāh is All-Strong, severe in punishment.”",
  8053: "That is because Allāh would never change a blessing He has bestowed upon a people until they change what is within themselves. And indeed, Allāh is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.",
  8054: "Their example is as that of the people of Pharaoh and those before them: they denied the signs of their Lord, so We destroyed them for their sins and drowned the people of Pharaoh—and all were wrongdoers.",
  8055: "Indeed, the worst of living creatures in the sight of Allāh are those who persist in disbelief, and so they do not believe,",
  8056: "Those with whom you made a treaty—yet they break their pledge every time, and they do not fear Allāh.",
  8057: "So when you encounter them in battle, make of them a decisive example, so that those behind them may be scattered and take heed.",
  8058: "But if you fear treachery from any people, then renounce their treaty to them openly, on equal terms. Indeed, Allāh does not like the treacherous.",
  8059: "And let not those who disbelieve think they have escaped. Indeed, they can never thwart [the will of] Allāh.",
  8060: "Prepare against them whatever force you are able [20], and steeds of war, by which you may deter the enemy of Allāh and your enemy, and others besides them whom you do not know—Allāh knows them [21]. Whatever you spend in the cause of Allāh will be repaid to you in full, and you will not be wronged.\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said from the pulpit: “Prepare against them whatever force you can—indeed, force lies in shooting; indeed, force lies in shooting; indeed, force lies in shooting.”\nThis ḥadīth explains the nature of the strength commanded in this verse. At the time, archery was the dominant form of ranged military power; however, the wording establishes a general principle, not a limitation to a specific weapon. The command encompasses all effective means of preparedness and ranged capability appropriate to any era, place, or technology.\nThe Prophet ﷺ also said: “Lands will be opened for you, and Allāh will suffice you against your enemies, but none of you should neglect practicing archery.”\nAnd when he passed by people practicing shooting, he said: “Shoot, O sons of Ishmael, for your father was an archer.”\nThis emphasizes disciplined preparedness and skill as ongoing responsibilities, even in times of security.\n[21] That is, other adversaries—including hypocrites and hostile forces—not presently engaged in open conflict but known to Allāh and liable to pose a threat in the future. The verse underscores the need for vigilance against unforeseen dangers, not aggression against the uninvolved.",
  8061: "If they incline to peace, then incline to it and rely upon Allāh. Indeed, He is the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. That is, those disbelievers who are actively at war with the believers, not peaceful or neutral parties.",
  8062: "If they intend to deceive you, then sufficient for you is Allāh; it is He who supported you with His help and with the believers.",
  8063: "And He brought their hearts together. Had you spent all that is on the earth, you could not have brought their hearts together—but Allāh brought them together. Indeed, He is All-Mighty, All-Wise.",
  8064: "O Prophet, Allāh is sufficient for you and for those believers who follow you.",
  8065: "O Prophet, urge the believers to fight. If there are among you twenty who are steadfast, they will overcome two hundred; and if there are among you a hundred, they will overcome a thousand of those who disbelieve, for they are a people who do not understand.",
  8066: "Now Allāh has lightened [the burden] for you, knowing that there is weakness among you. So if there are among you a hundred who are steadfast, they will overcome two hundred; and if there are among you a thousand, they will overcome two thousand, by the permission of Allāh. And Allāh is with the steadfast.",
  8067: "It is not befitting for a Prophet to take captives until he has thoroughly subdued the land. You seek the fleeting gains of this world, but Allāh desires for you the Hereafter. And Allāh is All-Mighty, All-Wise.",
  8068: "Had it not been for a decree from Allāh that had already gone forth [23], a great punishment would surely have befallen you for what you took [of ransom].\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. That is, a prior divine decree:\nthat spoils of war and ransom would be made lawful for them; that Allāh does not punish people for a matter before its ruling has been clearly explained; and that He would not punish the Companions who took part in the Battle of Badr, due to their unique merit and precedence.",
  8069: "So consume what you have taken as spoils, lawful and good, and be mindful of Allāh. Indeed, Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  8070: "O Prophet, say to those captives in your hands: if Allāh knows any good in your hearts [24], He will grant you better than what was taken from you and will forgive you. And Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. That is, a sincere inclination and genuine willingness to embrace Islam.",
  8071: "But if they intend to betray you, they have already betrayed Allāh before, so He empowered you over them. And Allāh is All-Knowing, All-Wise.",
  8072: "Indeed, those who believed and emigrated and strove with their wealth and their lives in the cause of Allāh, and those who gave them shelter and support—these are allies of one another. But those who believed and did not emigrate—you have no duty of protection toward them until they emigrate. Yet if they seek your help in the matter of faith, then it is your duty to help them—except against a people with whom you have a treaty. And Allāh is All-Seeing of what you do.",
  8073: "As for those who disbelieve are allies of one another. If you do not do so, there will be turmoil in the land and great corruption. [25]\\n\\nFootnotes: [25]. This verse further clarifies the bond among the believers, urging them to uphold mutual loyalty and collective responsibility, and to abandon alliances with the disbelievers—who are themselves firmly allied with one another against the believers.\nThat is, if the believers fail to maintain unity, mutual support, and a just social order, the inevitable result will be widespread turmoil: persecution of the vulnerable, the breakdown of security, and the spread of injustice and corruption throughout society.",
  8074: "But those who believed and emigrated and strove in the cause of Allāh, and those who gave shelter and support—they are the true believers; for them is forgiveness and a noble provision.",
  8075: "And those who believed afterward, emigrated, and strove along with you—they are of you. But blood relatives are more entitled to one another [in inheritance] according to the Book of Allāh [26]. Indeed, Allāh is All-Knowing of everything.\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. This ruling applies only to Muslim relatives; others may receive a share through bequest. See 4:11.",
  9001: "This is a declaration of disassociation from Allāh and His Messenger to those polytheists with whom you had made a treaty, and who then broke it.",
  9002: "So travel freely throughout the land for four months [1], but know that you can never escape Allāh, and that Allāh will surely disgrace the disbelievers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. They were given the opportunity to repent, or—if they did not wish to do so—to seek protection and be granted safe passage to a place of security.",
  9003: "And [this is] an announcement from Allāh and His Messenger to the people on the day of the greater pilgrimage: that Allāh is disassociated from the polytheists, and so is His Messenger. So if you repent, it is better for you; but if you turn away, then know that you cannot escape Allāh. And give tidings to those who disbelieve of a painful punishment.",
  9004: "Except for those polytheists with whom you made a treaty and who have not failed you in anything nor supported anyone against you—so fulfill their treaty to them until the end of its term. Indeed, Allāh loves the righteous.",
  9005: "Then when the sacred months have passed [2], kill the polytheists wherever you encounter them in battle [3]; capture them, besiege them, and lie in wait for them at every place of ambush. But if they repent, establish prayer, and give zakah, then let them go on their way. Indeed, Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The sacred months are the periods of truce in the Islamic calendar—Muḥarram, Rajab, Dhū al-Qaʿdah, and Dhū al-Ḥijjah—during which fighting was traditionally prohibited unless hostilities were initiated by the opposing side.\n[3]. This directive applies specifically to combatant polytheists who violated their treaty obligations, initiated hostilities, and posed an active military threat to the Muslim community. It does not apply to non-combatants or to those who upheld their treaties.",
  9006: "If any of the polytheists seeks your protection, then grant him protection so that he may hear the Word of Allāh [4]; then escort him to his place of safety. That is because they are a people who do not know.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. That is, the Qur’an. Their hostility and belligerence are attributed to ignorance of its message and guidance rather than deliberate, informed opposition. The subsequent verses clarify who these individuals were and the actions that led to such retaliatory measures.",
  9007: "How can there be for the polytheists a treaty with Allāh and His Messenger—except those with whom you made a treaty at the Sacred Mosque? So long as they are upright toward you, be upright toward them. Indeed, Allāh loves the righteous.",
  9008: "How can there be [such a treaty], when—if they were to gain the upper hand over you—they would respect neither kinship nor any covenant toward you? They seek to please you with their words, but their hearts reject it, and most of them are defiantly disobedient.",
  9009: "They have traded away the verses of Allāh for a small gain and have averted others from His path—how evil is what they have been doing.",
  9010: "They respect neither bonds of kinship nor any covenant of protection toward a believer—it is they who are the transgressors.",
  9011: "But if they repent, establish prayer, and give zakah, then they are your brothers in faith. We make the verses clear in detail for a people who know. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. After the death of the Prophet ﷺ, when Abu Bakr became his successor, some Arab tribes apostatized and others refused to pay zakah while still professing Islam. ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb objected, saying:\n“O Abu Bakr! How can you fight these people when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said: ‘I have been commanded to fight the people until they testify that there is no god but Allāh; whoever says it has protected his life and property from me, except by right, and his reckoning is with Allāh?”\nAbu Bakr replied: “By Allāh, I will fight anyone who differentiates between prayer and zakah, for zakah is a right due on property by Allah’s command. By Allāh, if they were to withhold even a young goat they used to give to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, I would fight them for withholding it.”\nʿUmar later said:\n“By Allāh, it was only that Allāh opened Abu Bakr’s heart to this decision, and I came to realize that it was the correct one.”\nThis report illustrates the practical application of the principle stated in Qur’an 9:11: “…if they repent, establish prayer, and give zakah, then they are your brothers in faith.”\nAbu Bakr understood this verse to mean that prayer and zakah together constitute the public obligations of Islam, and that refusing zakah while claiming faith was not merely a private lapse but a breach of communal covenant.",
  9012: "But if they break their oaths after their treaty and defame your religion, then fight the leaders of disbelief—for indeed, they hold no oaths binding—so that they may desist.",
  9013: "Will you not fight a people who broke their oaths, sought to drive out the Messenger, and initiated hostilities against you first? Do you fear them? But Allāh has more right that you should fear Him, if you are believers.",
  9014: "Fight them; Allāh will punish them by your hands, disgrace them, grant you victory over them, and heal the hearts of a believing people. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. That is, those believers who had been subjected to oppression and persecution, whose suffering is acknowledged and whose hearts are comforted through the restoration of justice.",
  9015: "And He will remove the anger from their hearts. And Allāh turns in mercy to whom He wills; for Allāh is All-Knowing, All-Wise.",
  9016: "Do you think that you will be left untested, while Allāh has not yet made evident those among you who strive in His cause and do not take others besides Allāh, His Messenger, and the believers as intimates? Allāh is fully aware of what you do.",
  9017: "It is not for the polytheists to tend the mosques of Allāh while bearing witness against themselves in disbelief [7]. Their deeds are rendered worthless, and in the Fire they will abide forever.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. That is, they manifested disbelief through both words and actions: they installed idols in and around the Sacred Mosque— even within the Kaʿbah itself—invoked them in supplication, and performed acts of devotion in their names, thereby associating them with Almighty Allāh.",
  9018: "The mosques of Allāh are only to be maintained by those who believe in Allāh and the Last Day, establish prayer, give the prescribed alms, and fear none but Him. It is they who are most worthy to be among the rightly guided. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. The Last Day (Arabic: al-yawm al-ākhir) refers to the Day of Resurrection and Final Judgment, when all people will be raised from the dead and held accountable before Allāh. In the Qurʾān, the expression summarizes belief in the final resurrection, judgment, reward, and punishment. Faith in “Allāh and the Last Day” frequently appears as a paired formula expressing the essential foundations of belief.\nThe Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said: “Whoever builds a mosque seeking only the pleasure of Allāh, Allah will build for him a house in Paradise.”",
  9019: "Do you consider the providing of water to the pilgrims and the maintenance of the Sacred Mosque equal to one who believes in Allāh and the Last Day and strives in the cause of Allāh? They are not equal in the sight of Allāh. Allāh does not guide the wrongdoing people.",
  9020: "Those who have believed, emigrated, and strove in the cause of Allāh with their wealth and their lives are greater in rank in the sight of Allāh. It is they who are the triumphant.",
  9021: "Their Lord gives them glad tidings of mercy from Him, and of His good pleasure, and of gardens wherein they will have everlasting bliss.",
  9022: "They will abide therein forever. Indeed, with Allāh is a great reward.",
  9023: "O believers, do not take your fathers or your brothers as close allies if they prefer disbelief over faith. And whoever among you takes them as allies—it is they who are the wrongdoers.",
  9024: "Say, ‘If your fathers, your sons, your brothers, your spouses, your kin, the wealth you have acquired, the trade whose decline you fear, and the homes you cherish are more beloved to you than Allāh, His Messenger, and striving in His cause, then wait until Allāh brings about His command. And Allāh does not guide the defiantly disobedient people.’",
  9025: "Indeed, Allāh has granted you victory on many battlefields, and on the Day of Ḥunayn—when your great numbers pleased you, but availed you nothing. The earth, despite its vastness, became constricted for you; then you turned back in retreat. [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. At the Battle of Ḥunayn, the Prophet ﷺ went forth with his noble Companions. Some among them felt confident of victory because of their large numbers and said, “We will not be defeated today because of our numbers.” As a result, they were made to experience an initial retreat. This retreat, however, did not include all the believers. Many of the steadfast early Emigrants (Muhājirūn) and Helpers (Anṣār) remained firm, and above all, the Prophet ﷺ himself did not retreat. He stood resolute, calling the believers back until Allāh granted victory.\nThis verse thus serves as a profound lesson in humility, emphasizing that victory comes through divine support, not through numbers or material strength alone.",
  9026: "Then Allāh sent down His tranquility upon His Messenger and upon the believers, and sent down forces you did not see, and punished those who disbelieved. Such is the recompense of the disbelievers.",
  9027: "Then, after that, Allāh accepts the repentance of whom He wills. And Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  9028: "O believers, indeed the polytheists are impure, so let them not approach the Sacred Mosque after this, their final year. And if you fear poverty, Allāh will enrich you from His bounty, if He wills. Indeed, Allāh is All-Knowing, All-Wise.",
  9029: "Fight those from among the People of the Scripture who do not believe in Allāh or in the Last Day, who do not forbid what Allāh and His Messenger have forbidden [10], and who do not follow the religion of truth[11]—until they pay the jizyah [12] willingly , while being in submission.\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. That is, acts which Allāh and His Messenger have declared unlawful, such as usury, consumption of pork, intoxicants, and sexual immorality, among others.\n[11]. That is, Islam, the religion of truth. This refers to those who, despite possessing earlier revelation, depart from pure monotheism through doctrines and practices inconsistent with Islamic belief—such as the doctrine of the Trinity, the veneration of the cross, or the belief that Jesus Christ is the incarnation or human manifestation of the One true God, Allāh.\n[12]. Jizyah: a protection tax levied on able-bodied non-Muslim citizens under Islamic governance in exchange for security, exemption from military service, and the safeguarding of their religious and civil rights.",
  9030: "The Jews say, ‘Ezra is the son of Allāh [13],’ and the Christians say, ‘The Messiah is the son of Allāh.’ These are but words from their mouths, echoing the claims of those who disbelieved before them [14]. May Allāh condemn them—how they are turned away from the truth.\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. The statement that “Ezra (ʿUzayr) is the son of God” is understood by early Muslim exegetes as referring to a particular group among the Jews who held or expressed such a belief. Ezra is a respected figure in Jewish tradition, known for his role in preserving and teaching the Torah after the Babylonian exile. However, this specific claim is not found in mainstream Jewish doctrine. The Qur’ānic wording is generally interpreted as addressing certain expressions or beliefs that were seen as elevating a human figure beyond the bounds of pure monotheism.\nIslamic faith consistently emphasizes that all prophets and righteous individuals—including Ezra (ʿUzayr) and Jesus (ʿĪsā)—are human servants and messengers of Allāh, not divine or semi-divine beings.\n[14].  The phrase “echoing the claims of those who disbelieved before them” is understood to refer to earlier religious traditions in which divine offspring or familial relationships were attributed to God—such as pre-Islamic beliefs that described angels as daughters of God. The Qur’ān presents such ideas as part of a recurring pattern in human history: the tendency to exalt created beings beyond their proper status, thereby compromising the principle of absolute monotheism (tawḥīd).\nThis verse critiques claims of divine sonship and reaffirms a central teaching of Islam: that Allāh is absolutely one, without partner, offspring, or equal. It also highlights a recurring religious pattern—where reverence for honored figures can develop into beliefs that conflict with strict monotheism.",
  9031: "They have taken their rabbis and monks as lords [15] besides Allāh, and the Messiah, son of Mary—though they were commanded to worship only One God. There is no deity except Him. Exalted is He above what they associate with Him.\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. By obeying them rather than obeying Allah’s commandments.\n ‘Adiyy ibn Hātim al-Tā’iyy, who was Christian before embracing Islam, narrated that he came to the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) while he was reciting this verse, and he said to the Messenger: “We do not worship them!” He asked: “Do they not deem unlawful what Allāh allowed and you follow them making it unlawful, and declare lawful what Allāh forbade and you follow them making it lawful?” ‘Adiyy said: “Indeed!” then he (ﷺ) said: “That is their worship!”\nIn Matthew 15:1–9 (Jesus rebukes hypocrisy in their worship) 'Then some Pharisees and teachers of the law came to Jesus from Jerusalem and asked, “Why do your disciples break the tradition of the elders? They don’t wash their hands before they eat!” Jesus replied, “And why do you break the command of Allāh for the sake of your tradition? For Allāh said, ‘Honor your father and mother,’ and ‘Anyone who curses their father or mother is to be put to death.’\nBut you say that if anyone declares that what might have been used to help their father or mother is ‘devoted to God,’ they are not to honor their father or mother with it. Thus you nullify the word of Allāh for the sake of your tradition.\nIsaiah 29:13 (Quoted by Jesus in Matthew)\n “These people draw near to Me with their mouths and honor Me with their lips, but their hearts are far from Me. Their worship of Me is made up only of rules taught by men.”\nJeremiah 8:8 (Corruption of religious scholars) “How can you say, ‘We are wise, for we have the law of Allāh,’ when actually the lying pen of the scribes has handled it falsely?”",
  9032: "They seek to extinguish the Light of Allāh with their mouths, but Allāh refuses except to perfect His Light, even though the disbelievers hate it.",
  9033: "It is He who sent His Messenger with guidance and the religion of truth, so that He may make it prevail over all religion, even though the polytheists hate it.",
  9034: "O believers, indeed many of the rabbis and monks consume the wealth of people unjustly and turn others away from the path of Allāh. And those who hoard gold and silver and do not spend it in the cause of Allāh —give them tidings of a painful punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. This serves as a timely moral reminder to the believers that wealth is a trust from Allāh, urging them to spend generously and responsibly in His cause, and warning against hoarding or allowing material attachment to distract from ethical duty and spiritual accountability.",
  9035: "On the Day when it will be heated in the Fire of Hell [17], and their foreheads, flanks, and backs will be seared with it, [it will be said], ‘This is what you hoarded for yourselves—so taste what you used to hoard.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. This refers to hoarded gold, silver, and wealth from which the obligatory zakāt (the prescribed alms) was not paid. Such wealth, withheld from its divinely mandated rights, becomes a cause of punishment in the Hereafter. The Prophet ﷺ explained this clearly, saying: “Anyone to whom Allāh has given wealth and he does not pay its zakāt, his wealth will be made to appear for him on the Day of Resurrection as a bald-headed poisonous male snake with two venomous glands. It will encircle his neck and bite his cheeks, saying, ‘I am your wealth; I am your treasure.’” He ﷺ also warned that the greatest losers are the wealthy who withhold their obligations, except those who give generously in every direction—front, back, right, and left—though such people are few. Likewise, whoever owns camels, cattle, or sheep and does not give their due zakāt will find those animals returned to him on the Day of Judgment in full strength, trampling him with their hooves and butting him with their horns repeatedly until judgment is concluded. This clarifies that the punishment described in the verse is not for wealth itself, but for miserliness, hoarding, and neglect of God’s prescribed social and moral duties tied to wealth.",
  9036: "Indeed, the number of months with Allāh is twelve months, in the ordinance of Allāh since the day He created the heavens and the earth; of these, four are sacred [18]. That is the upright religion, so do not wrong yourselves during them [19]. And fight the polytheists all together as they fight you all together. And know that Allāh is with the righteous.\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. That is, the four sacred months are the 1st, 7th, 11th, and 12th months of the Islamic lunar calendar (Muḥarram, Rajab, Dhū al-Qaʿdah, and Dhū al-Ḥijjah), which Allāh has designated as inviolable since the creation of the heavens and the earth.\n[19]. That is wrongdoing is forbidden especially during these sacred months, and indeed during all months of the year in general. Fighting and the waging of wars are prohibited in these months even with disbelievers, except in cases of lawful self-defense.",
  9037: "Indeed, the postponement [of the sacred months] is but an increase in disbelief [20], whereby the disbelievers are led further astray. They declare it lawful one year and sacred another, in order to match the number that Allāh has made sacred—thus making lawful what Allāh has forbidden. The evil of their deeds has been made appealing to them, and Allāh does not guide the disbelieving people.\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. This refers to a pre-Islamic pagan custom in which the Arabs altered the sacred months—by delaying them or swapping them—to make fighting permissible when it suited their interests. If they wished to wage war during a sacred month, they would declare it non-sacred and transfer its sanctity to another month, thereby preserving the number of sacred months while nullifying their purpose and effectively making lawful what Allāh had forbidden.",
  9038: "O believers, what is the matter with you that, when you are told, ‘Go forth in the cause of Allāh,’ you cling heavily to the earth? Are you satisfied with the life of this world rather than the Hereafter? Yet the enjoyment of this worldly life, compared with the Hereafter, is but little.",
  9039: "If you do not go forth for war, He will punish you with a painful punishment and replace you with another people; and you will not harm Him at all. And Allāh has power over all things.",
  9040: "If you do not support him, then Allāh has already supported him—when those who disbelieved drove him out, as one of two [21], while the two were in the cave. He said to his companion, ‘Do not grieve; indeed Allāh is with us.’ Then Allāh sent down His tranquility upon him, supported him with forces you did not see, and made the word of the disbelievers the lowest [22], while the Word of Allāh is the highest [23]. And Allāh is All-Mighty, All-Wise.\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. “The second” refers to the Prophet’s companion Abu Bakr, who was with him in the cave during the Hijrah.\n[22]. That is, the claims, slogans, and assertions of disbelief raised against the truth.\n[23]. That is, the declaration of pure monotheism: “Lā ilāha illā Allāh” (there is no true deity worthy of worship except Allāh).",
  9041: "Go forth for war whether lightly or heavily equipped [24], and strive hard in the cause of Allāh [25] with your wealth and your lives; that is best for you, if you only knew.\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. That is, whether young or old, mounted or on foot, in ease or in hardship—under all circumstances and conditions.\n[25]. That is, striving in the moral way that leads to peace, through commitment, sacrifice, and righteous effort in the cause of Allāh.",
  9042: "Had it been a near gain and an easy journey, they would have followed you. But the distance seemed too far for them. They will swear by Allāh, “If we had been able, we would surely have gone out with you.” destroying themselves [through false oaths]—and Allāh knows that they are liars.",
  9043: "May Allāh pardon you—why did you give them permission [to remain behind], before it became evident to you who were truthful and you knew the liars?",
  9044: "Those who believe in Allāh and the Last Day do not ask your permission to be excused from striving with their wealth and their lives in the cause of Allāh. And Allāh is All-Knowing of the righteous.",
  9045: "Only those who do not believe in Allāh and the Last Day ask your permission [26]; their hearts are filled with doubt, so they waver in it.\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. That is, to seek exemption from fighting and striving in the cause of Allāh without a valid excuse.",
  9046: "Had they truly intended to go forth for war, they would have made preparations for it. But Allāh disliked their going out, so He caused them to lag behind, and it was said to them, “Remain with those who stay behind.” [27]\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]. i.e., those excused from going forth for war—such as the weak, women, and children.",
  9047: "Had they gone forth with you, they would have added nothing but confusion among you, hastening about in your ranks, seeking to stir up discord. And among you are those who would listen to them. And Allāh is All-Knowing of the wrongdoers.",
  9048: "They had already sought to stir up dissension before and had turned matters against you, until the truth came and the command of Allāh prevailed, while they were averse.",
  9049: "And among them is one who says, ‘Grant me permission and do not put me to trial.’ Indeed, they have already fallen into trial [28]. And Hell will surely encompass the disbelievers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. That is, by avoiding their obligations and duties, they themselves fell into trial and moral failure.",
  9050: "If good befalls you, it grieves them; but if misfortune strikes you, they say, ‘We had already taken our precautions,’ and they turn away rejoicing. [29]\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. That is, the hypocrites claim to have protected themselves by remaining behind from jihād—i.e., refraining from going to war undertaken to establish justice, peace, and order—mistaking avoidance of duty for wisdom and safety.",
  9051: "Say, ‘Nothing will ever befall us except what Allāh has decreed for us. He is our Protector, and in Allāh alone let the believers place their trust.’ [30]\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. Regardless of the trials believers may face, true contentment, strength, and success is in maintaining complete trust and reliance upon Allāh and His decree.",
  9052: " Say, ‘Do you expect anything for us except one of the two best outcomes—victory or martyrdom? But we expect that Allāh will afflict you with punishment from Himself or at our hands. So wait; we too are waiting with you.’",
  9053: "Say, ‘Whether you spend willingly or unwillingly, it will never be accepted from you, for you have been a defiantly disobedient people.’",
  9054: "Nothing prevents their spending from being accepted except that they disbelieve in Allāh and His Messenger, come to prayer only lazily [31], and spend only unwillingly.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [31]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ emphasized the importance of sincerity in worship, stating, “The most burdensome prayers for the hypocrites are the dawn (Fajr) and night (‘Ishā’) prayers. If they knew the reward contained in them, they would attend them even if they had to crawl.”\nThis statement highlights that reluctance toward congregational prayer—especially prayers performed when social recognition is minimal—is a distinguishing sign of hypocrisy, whereas true faith is marked by consistency and devotion regardless of public visibility.",
  9055: "So do not let their wealth or their children impress you. Allāh only intends to punish them through these things in the life of this world and let their souls depart while they are disbelievers.",
  9056: "They swear by Allāh that they are with you, but they are not truly from among you; rather, they are a people who are afraid.",
  9057: "If they could find a refuge, or caves, or a place to hide, they would rush toward it headlong.",
  9058: "Among them are those who criticize you concerning the distribution of charity. If they are given a share of it, they are pleased; but if they are not given any of it, they immediately become angry.",
  9059: "If only they had been content with what Allāh and His Messenger gave them and said, ‘Allāh is sufficient for us. Allāh will give us from His bounty, and so will His Messenger. Indeed, to Allāh alone we turn with hope.’",
  9060: "Alms (zakah) are only for the poor, the needy, those appointed to administer it, those whose hearts may be drawn toward Islam, for freeing those in bondage, for those burdened by debt, for the cause of Allāh, and for the stranded traveler—an obligation ordained by Allāh [32]. And Allāh is All-Knowing, All-Wise.\\n\\nFootnotes: [32]. Zakah in Islam is not merely an act of voluntary charity or personal benevolence; it is a binding social obligation grounded in divine justice. Its foundation lies in the belief that Allāh did not err in His creation, nor did He fail to provide sufficient resources for humanity to live with dignity. There is, in principle, enough wealth in the world for all, but injustice arises when wealth is hoarded by some while others are left deprived.\nThus, the wealthy can justify their prosperity before Allāh only by fulfilling their obligation to share, directing their wealth toward those whom Allāh Himself has designated as rightful recipients. In doing so, they do not display extraordinary virtue, but merely meet the minimum standard of righteousness required of them as members of the believing community.\nTo withhold zakah while possessing the means is therefore a sin, whereas giving it restores moral balance, purifies the soul from greed, and elevates one’s standing in the sight of Allāh. Zakah functions both as a mechanism of social equity and as a means of spiritual purification, reinforcing justice, compassion, and communal responsibility.",
  9061: "Among them are those who offend the Prophet and say, ‘He listens to everyone.’ Say, ‘He listens for your good. He believes in Allāh, trusts the believers, and is a mercy to those among you who believe.’ And those who offend the Messenger of Allāh —for them is a painful punishment.",
  9062: "They swear by Allāh to you in order to please you, but it is Allāh and His Messenger whose pleasure they should seek, if they are believers.",
  9063: "Do they not know that whoever opposes Allāh and His Messenger—for him is the Fire of Hell, abiding therein forever? That is the ultimate disgrace.",
  9064: "The hypocrites fear that a chapter may be revealed about them, exposing what is in their hearts. Say, ‘Go on mocking. Allāh will surely expose what you fear.’",
  9065: "If you question them, they will surely say, ‘We were only talking and joking.’ Say, ‘Was it Allāh, His verses, and His Messenger that you were mocking?’ [33]\\n\\nFootnotes: [33]. During the Expedition of Tabūk, a man remarked in a gathering: “I have never seen anyone more gluttonous, more prone to lying, or more cowardly than these reciters of ours.” Another man present replied, “You are lying! You are indeed a hypocrite, and I will report this to the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ).”\nWhen this reached the Prophet ﷺ, verses of the Qur’an were revealed concerning the incident. The narrator relates: “I saw the man clinging to the strap of the Prophet’s she-camel, pleading, ‘O Messenger of Allāh! We were only chatting and passing time.’ Meanwhile, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ was reciting: ‘Was it Allāh, His verses, and His Messenger that you were mocking?’”",
  9066: "Make no excuses. You have disbelieved after your belief. If We pardon one group of you, We will punish another group because they were criminals.",
  9067: "The hypocrite men and hypocrite women are of one another: they enjoin what is evil, forbid what is good, and withhold their hands [from giving]. They forgot Allāh [34], so He forgot them [35]. Indeed, the hypocrites—it is they who are the defiantly disobedient.\\n\\nFootnotes: [34]. That is, they neglected the devotion, obedience, and remembrance that were due to Him.\n[35]. That is, Allāh abandoned them to punishment in the Hereafter, withdrawing His mercy from them.",
  9068: "Allāh has promised the hypocrite men and women, and the disbelievers, the Fire of Hell, wherein they will abide forever. It is enough for them. Allāh has cursed them, and for them is an enduring punishment.",
  9069: "[You are] like those who came before you: they were stronger than you in power and more abundant in wealth and children. They enjoyed their share, and you have enjoyed your share just as those before you enjoyed theirs. You have indulged in idle talk just as they did. Their deeds have come to nothing in this world and the Hereafter, and it is they who are the true losers.",
  9070: "Has the news of those before them not reached them—the people of Noah, ʿĀd, Thamūd, the people of Abraham, the people of Midian, and the overturned cities? Their messengers came to them with clear proofs. Allāh did not wrong them; rather, they wronged themselves.",
  9071: "The believing men and believing women are allies of one another. They enjoin what is right, forbid what is wrong, establish prayer, give zakah, and obey Allāh and His Messenger. It is they upon whom Allāh will have mercy. Indeed, Allāh is All-Mighty, All-Wise.",
  9072: "Allāh has promised the believing men and women gardens beneath which rivers flow, wherein they will abide forever, and splendid dwellings in Gardens of everlasting bliss. But the pleasure of Allāh is even greater. That is the supreme achievement.",
  9073: "O Prophet, strive against the disbelievers and the hypocrites, and be firm with them. Their abode is Hell, and wretched is that destination.",
  9074: "They swear by Allāh that they said nothing, yet they certainly spoke words of disbelief, disbelieved after accepting Islam, and resolved upon what they could not attain [36]. They resented only that Allāh and His Messenger had enriched them from His bounty. So if they repent, it will be better for them; but if they turn away, Allāh will punish them with a painful punishment in this world and the Hereafter, and they will have no protector or helper on earth.\\n\\nFootnotes: [36]. That is, to assassinate the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ. This refers to a plot devised by a group of hypocrites to murder the Prophet during his return from the Tabūk Expedition. Their plan was uncovered and ultimately thwarted, as reported in early exegetical and historical sources. The verse thus exposes both their concealed disbelief and their failed act of violent treachery against the Messenger of Allāh.",
  9075: "Among them are those who made a covenant with Allāh, saying, ‘If He grants us from His bounty, we will surely give in charity and be among the righteous.’",
  9076: "But when He granted them from His bounty, they became miserly and turned away in aversion.",
  9077: "As a result, He caused hypocrisy to take root in their hearts until the Day they meet Him, because they broke their promise to Allāh and because they used to lie.",
  9078: "Do they not know that Allāh knows their secrets and their private counsel, and that Allāh is the All-Knower of the unseen?",
  9079: "Those who criticize the believers who give charity willingly, and mock those who find nothing to give except their own effort—Allāh will mock them in return, and for them is a painful punishment.",
  9080: "Whether you [O Prophet] seek forgiveness for them or do not seek forgiveness for them—even if you were to seek forgiveness for them seventy times—Allāh will never forgive them. That is because they disbelieved in Allāh and His Messenger, and Allāh does not guide the defiantly disobedient people.",
  9081: "Those who stayed behind rejoiced in remaining behind the Messenger of Allāh and disliked striving in the cause of Allāh with their wealth and their lives. They said, ‘Do not march forth in the heat.’ Say, ‘The Fire of Hell is far more intense in heat’—if only they understood.",
  9082: "So let them laugh a little and weep much as recompense for what they used to earn.",
  9083: "So if Allāh brings you back to a group of them and they ask your permission to go forth with you, say, ‘You will never go forth with me, nor will you ever fight an enemy with me. You were content to remain behind the first time, so now remain with those who stayed behind.’",
  9084: "Never offer the funeral prayer for any of them who dies, nor stand by his grave. They disbelieved in Allāh and His Messenger and died while defiantly disobedient.",
  9085: "Do not let their wealth or their children impress you. Allāh intends only to punish them through these things in this worldly life and to let their souls depart while they are disbelievers.",
  9086: "Whenever a chapter is revealed, saying, ‘Believe in Allāh and strive alongside His Messenger,’ the affluent among them ask you for exemption, saying, ‘Let us remain with those who stay behind.’",
  9087: "They were content to remain with those who stayed behind, and their hearts were sealed, so they do not understand.",
  9088: "But the Messenger and those who believed with him strove with their wealth and their lives. It is they who will have all that is good, and it is they who are truly successful.",
  9089: "Allāh has prepared for them gardens beneath which rivers flow, wherein they will abide forever. That is the great success.",
  9090: "Some of the desert Arabs came with excuses, seeking exemption, while those who lied to Allāh and His Messenger stayed behind. A painful punishment will strike those among them who disbelieved.",
  9091: "There is no blame on the weak, the sick, or those who have nothing to spend, so long as they are sincere to Allāh and His Messenger. There is no blame on those who do good. Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  9092: "Nor is there any blame on those who came to you asking for mounts, and you said, ‘I have nothing on which to carry you.’ They turned away with their eyes overflowing with tears, grieving that they could not find anything to spend in the cause of Allāh.",
  9093: "Blame is only upon those who seek permission from you though they are rich. They were content to remain with those who stayed behind, and Allāh has sealed their hearts, so they do not understand.",
  9094: "They will make excuses to you when you return to them. Say, ‘Make no excuses; we will never believe you. Allah has already informed us about your affairs. Allāh and His Messenger will see your deeds. Then you will be returned to the Knower of the unseen and the seen, and He will inform you of what you used to do.’",
  9095: "They will swear to you by Allāh, when you return to them, so that you may turn away from them. So turn away from them. Indeed, they are impure, and their abode is Hell—a recompense for what they used to earn.",
  9096: "They swear to you so that you may be pleased with them. But even if you are pleased with them, Allāh is never pleased with defiantly disobedient people.",
  9097: "Some of the desert Arabs are more entrenched in disbelief and hypocrisy, and more likely to be ignorant of the limits Allāh has revealed to His Messenger [37]. Allāh is All-Knowing, All-Wise.\\n\\nFootnotes: [37]. That is because they lived far from the centers of knowledge and guidance, and did not regularly mix with the knowledgeable Companions of the Messenger ﷺ. Their distance from learning, instruction, and communal religious life made them more prone to ignorance of the limits and rulings revealed by Allāh.",
  9098: "Among the desert Arabs are some who regard what they spend as a burden and await calamities to befall you. Upon them will fall the evil turn. Allāh is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.",
  9099: "Yet among the desert Arabs are those who believe in Allāh and the Last Day, and who regard what they spend as a means of drawing nearer to Allāh and gaining the prayers of His Messenger. Truly, it is a means of nearness for them. Allāh will admit them into His mercy. Indeed, Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  9100: "As for the foremost pioneers among the Emigrants [38] and the Helpers [39, and those who followed them with excellence—Allāh is pleased with them, and they are pleased with Him. He has prepared for them gardens beneath which rivers flow, wherein they will abide forever. That is the supreme success.\\n\\nFootnotes: [38]. The Emigrants (al-Muhājirūn): Those believers who emigrated from Makkah to Madinah for the sake of their faith, leaving behind their homes, wealth, families, and social status in obedience to Allāh and in support of His Messenger ﷺ. Their migration was an act of sacrifice, sincerity, and prioritizing faith over worldly attachment.\n[39]. The Helpers (al-Anṣār): The believers from Madinah who welcomed, aided, and supported the Prophet ﷺ and the Emigrants. They shared their homes, wealth, and lives with them, giving precedence to others over themselves out of faith, love and devotion.",
  9101: "Some of the desert Arabs around you are hypocrites, as are some of the people of Madinah. They persist in hypocrisy. You do not know them, but We know them. We will punish them twice; then they will be returned to a tremendous punishment.",
  9102: "And there are others who admitted their sins: they mixed a righteous deed with another that was bad. It may be that Allāh will accept their repentance. Indeed, Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  9103: "Take alms [zakat] from their wealth to purify them and help them grow by it [40], and pray for them. Indeed, your prayer is a source of comfort for them. And Allāh is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [40]. That is, to increase their wealth, refine their character, multiply their righteous deeds, and grant them reward both in this world and in the Hereafter.",
  9104: "Do they not know that Allāh accepts repentance from His servants and receives their charities [41], and that Allāh is the Accepter of Repentance, the Most Merciful?\\n\\nFootnotes: [41]. That Allāh accepts charity sincerely given for His sake, rewards it, and causes it to be a means of purification, forgiveness, and nearness to Him.",
  9105: "Say, ‘Do deeds. Allāh will see your deeds [42], and so will His Messenger and the believers. Then you will be returned to the Knower of the unseen and the seen, and He will inform you of what you used to do.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [42]. Having been granted renewed opportunity through the acceptance of their repentance, they are now commanded to demonstrate sincerity through righteous action—supporting the cause of Allāh and aiding those in need—knowing that deeds are observed and will be fully accounted for. Charity and good works are a means of restoring what was previously lost.",
  9106: "And there are others [43] deferred until Allah’s command: He will either punish them or turn to them in mercy. And Allāh is All-Knowing, All-Wise.\\n\\nFootnotes: [43]. That is, others whose true state was not yet clear—neither openly hypocritical nor clearly sincere. Their case was deferred to Allah’s judgment alone: He may punish them if they persist in wrongdoing, or accept their repentance if they turn back sincerely. This underscores that final accountability and mercy rest entirely with Allāh.",
  9107: "And there are those who built a mosque to cause harm, foster disbelief, divide the believers, and serve as an outpost for one who had previously waged war against Allāh and His Messenger. They will surely swear, ‘We intended nothing but good.’ But Allāh bears witness that they are surely liars.",
  9108: "Never stand in it at all. A mosque founded upon piety from the first day is more deserving for you to stand in. In it are men who love to purify themselves, and Allāh loves those who purify themselves.[44]\\n\\nFootnotes: [44]. “A mosque founded upon piety” refers to the Mosque of Qubā’, which was established upon sincerity, devotion, and obedience to Allah from its very first day. It stands in contrast to the mosque built by the hypocrites for harm, division, and disbelief.\n“In it are men who love to purify themselves” refers to the sincere believers who strive for both spiritual and physical purity. They purify their hearts from disbelief, hypocrisy, and sin, and they also maintain cleanliness in worship and daily life through acts of purification such as ablution and personal hygiene. Because of their love for purity inwardly and outwardly, Allah praised them and declared His love for those who purify themselves.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“Purity is half of faith.”\nThis highlights the great importance of cleanliness and spiritual purification in Islam, both outwardly in practice and inwardly in character and faith.",
  9109: "So is one who laid the foundation of his building [45] upon piety from Allāh and seeking His pleasure better, or one who laid the foundation of his building [46] on the edge of a crumbling cliff, so it collapsed with him into the Fire of Hell? And Allāh does not guide the wrongdoing people.\\n\\nFootnotes: [45]. The foundation of his building upon piety: this refers to deeds, intentions, or institutions established upon taqwā (God-consciousness) and sincerity, seeking Allah’s pleasure alone—such as the Mosque of Qubāʾ, which was founded upon obedience and pure intention.\n[46]. Founded on the edge of a crumbling precipice:\nA metaphor for actions or institutions built on hypocrisy, corruption, or false motives, such as Masjid al-Dirār (the mosque of harm). Though outwardly impressive, such foundations are spiritually unstable and destined to collapse, leading to ruin in the Hereafter.",
  9110: "The building [47] they constructed will never cease to be a source of doubt in their hearts until their hearts are torn apart [48]. And Allāh is All-Knowing, All-Wise.\\n\\nFootnotes: [47]. The mosque of harm (Masjid al-Ḍirār), built with corrupt intentions to undermine the believers.\n[48].That is, until their death.",
  9111: "Indeed, Allāh has purchased from the believers their lives and their wealth in exchange for Paradise. They fight in the cause of Allāh —killing [49] and being killed. It is a true promise binding upon Him in the Torah, the Gospel, and the Qur’an. And who is more faithful to His covenant than Allāh? So rejoice in the bargain you have made with Him. That is the supreme achievement.\\n\\nFootnotes: [49]. The phrase “killing [in the cause of Allāh]” does not refer to aggression or compulsion in religion, but to striving in defense of truth, justice, and peace.\nIt describes believers who fight only when compelled, to protect faith, stop oppression, and uphold moral order — never for worldly gain or power.\nThus, “they kill” means they resist injustice, defending the innocent and safeguarding freedom of belief. Their struggle is rooted in mercy and restraint — a means of ensuring peace and dignity for all, under the guidance of divine law.\nThe Prophet ﷺ said, “Allāh laughs at two men, one of them killed the other, yet they both entered Paradise. This one fought in the cause of Allāh and was killed, then his killer repented and was also martyred.”",
  9112: "[They are] the repentant, the worshippers, those who praise Allāh, those who fast, bow, and prostrate, who enjoin what is right and forbid what is wrong, and who observe the limits set by Allāh. Give good news to the believers.",
  9113: "It is not for the Prophet and the believers to seek forgiveness for the polytheists, even if they are close relatives, once it has become clear to them that they are companions of Hellfire.",
  9114: "Abraham’s seeking forgiveness for his father was only because of a promise he had made to him. But when it became clear to him that he was an enemy of Allāh, he dissociated himself from him. Indeed, Abraham was tender-hearted and forbearing.",
  9115: "Allāh would not let a people go astray after He has guided them until He makes clear to them what they must avoid [50]. Indeed, Allāh is Knowing of all things.\\n\\nFootnotes: [50]. This verse was revealed to reassure the believers who feared that they might be held accountable for having previously sought forgiveness for their deceased polytheist relatives, before the divine ruling on the matter had been made clear.",
  9116: "Indeed, to Allāh belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth. He gives life and causes death. And besides Allāh, you have no protector or helper.",
  9117: "Allāh has indeed turned in mercy to the Prophet, the Emigrants, and the Helpers who followed him in the hour of hardship, after the hearts of some of them had almost wavered. Then He turned to them in mercy. Indeed, He is Kind and Merciful to them.",
  9118: "And [He turned in mercy] to the three [51] who were left behind [52]—until the earth, vast as it is, seemed narrow to them, and their souls felt constricted, and they realized that there is no refuge from Allāh except in Him. Then He turned to them in mercy so that they might repent. Indeed, Allāh is the Accepter of Repentance, the Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [51]. These are Kaʿab Ibn Mālik, Hilāl Ibn Umayyah, and Murārah Ibn Al-Rabīʿ, who were sincere companions but, for one reason or another and without valid excuses, failed to join the Messenger ﷺ in his campaign to Tabuk.\n[52]. Whose case was deferred for Decree of Allāh.",
  9119: "O believers, fear Allāh and be with the truthful. [53]\\n\\nFootnotes: [53]. “The truthful” refers to those who are sincere in faith, steadfast in obedience, and truthful in their words, intentions, and actions. They are the ones whose hearts and tongues align in devotion to Allāh — unlike the hypocrites, whose speech and deeds contradict their claims of belief.\nBeing “with the truthful” means keeping their company, following their example, and embodying their sincerity in all affairs of faith and conduct.\nThe Prophet Muhammad (ﷺ) said, \"Truthfulness leads to righteousness, and righteousness leads to Paradise. And a man keeps on telling the truth until he becomes a truthful person. Lying leads to wickedness, and wickedness leads to the Hellfire, and a man may keep on telling lies till he is written before Allāh, a liar.\"\nTelling the truth is a vital habit for Muslims  to live out as they are surrendering their lives to Allāh. They need always to live the truth, to cease and put off falsehood, and to speak truthfully to all people.  We must strive for truth in our words and actions.\nWhatever is true, whatever is honorable, whatever is right, whatever is pure, whatever is lovely, whatever is of good repute, if there is any excellence and if anything worthy of praise, dwell on these things.",
  9120: "It was not for the people of Madinah and the desert Arabs around them to remain behind from the Messenger of Allāh, nor to prefer themselves over him. That is because they are not afflicted by thirst, fatigue, or hunger in the way of Allāh, nor do they take any step that enrages the disbelievers, nor inflict any harm upon an enemy, except that it is recorded for them as a righteous deed. Indeed, Allāh does not allow the reward of the doers of good to go to waste.",
  9121: "Nor do they spend anything, small or great, nor cross any valley, except that it is recorded for them, so that Allāh may reward them according to the best of what they used to do. [54]\\n\\nFootnotes: [54]. Every step, sacrifice, or expense made sincerely for Allah’s cause — no matter how minor — is counted as a righteous deed in His record. This highlights Allah’s perfect justice and boundless generosity: He not only records every action but rewards each person according to the best of their deeds, not merely the average of them. Even the smallest effort — a short walk, a drop of water, a moment of fatigue — done with sincere intention, becomes an act of worship in His sight. It reminds believers that no good deed is ever wasted, and Allah’s mercy magnifies sincere actions beyond measure.",
  9122: "It is not for the believers to go forth all together. Rather, a group from every faction should go forth to gain understanding in the religion and to warn their people when they return to them, so that they may be cautious.",
  9123: "O believers, fight the disbelievers who are closest to you, and let them find firmness in you [55]. And know that Allāh is with the righteous.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [55].  This command addresses active hostilities in the historical and legal context of armed conflict. It does not sanction indiscriminate violence; rather, it refers to disbelievers who are in a state of war with the Muslim community, as clarified by other Qur’anic passages and prophetic practice.\nMuhammad, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ  said, \"Fight for the Cause of Allāh and in His name, against the disbelievers in Allāh. Fight them, but do not breach your contracts or truces, do not mutilate, and do not kill a child....\" And he (ﷺ) said: \"Do not kill a woman or slave.\"\nThis is also in compliance with the directions and the recommendation of the succeeding Muslim Caliphs to their \nmilitary leaders upon commissioning them for Jihad, saying, \"Listen and obey the following commands and instructions: Do not betray any one (if you give a pledge). Do not steal from the war booties. Do not\n breach your pledge of allegiance. Do not mutilate the body of the killed enemy fighters or deceased. Do not kill a child\n or a minor. Do not kill an elderly man or woman. Do not uproot a date palm tree (or any other trees) and do not burn them either. Do not cut or destroy a fruit tree. Do not slaughter a female sheep, a cow or a camel except for your (required) food. You surely will pass by some people who isolate themselves and are secluded for worship of Allāh as monks and else, thus leave them alone and do not disturb them. You will, surely, pass by a group of people who shaved the hair in the center of their heads, and left the surrounding hair long braids: it is allowed to fight and kill these people as they are the warriors of the enemies who carry their weapons against you. Go ahead, with the name of Allāh. \"\nThe prisoners of war are not to be tortured, humiliated, or mutilated. They are not to be imprisoned in tight claustrophobic prison cells without adequate food and drink thus causing them to die.\"\nContrary to these wise teachings in Islam, Christians reject the belief that the Bible has been corrupted. However, they cannot explain disturbing verses in the Bible such as: Samuel 15:3 which says, 'Now go, attack the Amalekites and totally destroy all that belongs to them. Do not spare them; put to death men and women, children and infants, cattle and sheep, camels and donkeys.’\nNumbers 31:17-18, says 'Now kill all the boys. And kill every woman who has slept with a man, but save for yourselves every girl who has never slept with a man.\"\nThat is from their scripture, but it is not for sure from the original scripture of God.\nThey say it is from God, but it is not really from God.\nThe truth is that their scriptures have been altered and corrupted by human hands.",
  9124: "Whenever a chapter is sent down, there are among them those who say, ‘Which of you has this increased in faith?’ As for those who believe, it increases them in faith, and they rejoice.",
  9125: "But as for those in whose hearts is a sickness [of doubt], it increases them in filth upon their filth, and they die while they are disbelievers.",
  9126: "Do they not see that they are being tested once or twice every year [56]? \nYet they neither repent nor take heed.\\n\\nFootnotes: [56]. They are repeatedly given reminders and wake-up calls each year through trials, events, and revelations, yet they pay little heed and fail to respond with repentance or reflection.",
  9127: "Whenever a chapter is sent down, they look at one another, saying, ‘Is anyone watching you?’ Then they slip away. Allāh has turned their hearts away because they are a people who do not understand.",
  9128: "Indeed, there has come to you a Messenger from among yourselves. Your suffering weighs heavily upon him; he is deeply concerned for you and kind and merciful to the believers.",
  9129: "But if they turn away [57], say, ‘Sufficient for me is Allāh; there is no deity except Him. On Him I have relied, and He is the Lord of the Great Throne.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [57]. This refers to the idolaters and hypocrites who deliberately refrain from belief and remain obstinate in their rejection of your message.",
  10001: "Alif Lām Rā [1]. These are the Verses of the Wise Book [2].\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2: verse 1.\n[2]. That is, the Qur’an—whose verses are characterized by perfect wisdom, just and sound rulings, and clear guidance to the Straight Path.",
  10002: "Is it so surprising to people that We revealed to a man from among themselves, saying, “Warn the people and give good news to those who believe that they will have a sincere footing and an honourable status with their Lord [3]”? But the disbelievers say, “Indeed, this is clearly a manifest sorcerer.” [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. The phrase “a sincere footing” refers to the good deeds that believers have sent forth in advance, securing for them a firm, honorable standing with Allāh. In this verse, it signifies a state of felicity and divine acceptance already decreed by Allāh and inscribed in the Preserved Tablet—a reward for their faith and righteous actions, and for believing in Muḥammad ﷺ, who guided them to the path leading to Paradise. Thus, the believers establish for themselves a noble foundation and a secure position for the life to come—the true and everlasting life of the Hereafter.\nThe verse was revealed in response to people’s astonishment that a human messenger—Muḥammad ﷺ—could receive divine revelation. Instead of reflecting upon the wisdom of this choice, the disbelievers rejected the message out of arrogance and denial.\n[4]. Unable to rationally confront the clarity, depth, and transformative power of the Message of Islam, the disbelievers dismissed it as sorcery, claiming that it bewitched people and turned them away from their former beliefs. This accusation was not based on evidence, but was a means of denying the truth while avoiding submission to it.",
  10003: "Indeed, your Lord is Allāh, who created the heavens and the earth in six Days, then established Himself over the Throne, directing all affairs. None can intercede except by His permission. That is Allāh, your Lord—so worship Him alone. Will you not then take heed?",
  10004: "To Him is your ultimate return, all together—the promise of Allāh, truly binding. Indeed, He originates creation, then brings it back again, so that He may justly reward those who believe and do righteous deeds. But as for those who disbelieve, for them is a drink of scalding water and a painful punishment for their persistent disbelief.",
  10005: "It is He who made the sun a radiant light and the moon a reflected light [5], and determined for it phases, so that you may know the number of years and the reckoning of time. Allāh did not create all of this except with truth and purpose. He makes the signs clear for people who have knowledge.\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The sun is described as radiant, generating its own light, while the moon is a reflected light, drawing from the sun. Its phases allow humans to measure years and reckon time. This distinction reflects Allah’s perfect design and wisdom, and He makes such signs clear for those who seek knowledge, guiding them to understand His purpose and order in creation.",
  10006: "Indeed, in the alternation of the night and day, and in all that Allāh has created in the heavens and the earth, there are signs for people who are mindful of Allāh.",
  10007: "Indeed, those who do not hope to meet Us, and are satisfied with the life of this world and content with it, and those who are heedless of Our signs—",
  10008: "Those—their abode will be the Fire, because of what they used to earn.",
  10009: "As for those who believe and do righteous deeds—their Lord will guide them by their faith; rivers will flow beneath them in Gardens of Bliss.",
  10010: "Their prayer therein will be, “Glory be to You, O Allāh,” and their greeting therein will be, “Peace.” And the conclusion of their prayer will be, “All praise be to Allāh, Lord of the worlds.”",
  10011: "If Allāh were to hasten evil [6] for mankind as they seek to hasten good, they would have been ruined. But We leave those who do not hope to meet Us to wander blindly in their transgression.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. Referring to the evil and punishment that people invoke upon themselves or others out of anger, denial, or mockery—such as challenging the Prophet ﷺ to bring immediate punishment. Allāh, in His mercy and wisdom, does not hasten retribution as people hasten worldly benefit, for doing so would lead to their destruction.",
  10012: "When harm touches a person, he calls upon Us—lying on his side, sitting, or standing. But when We remove the harm from him, he goes on as though he had never called upon Us about the harm that struck him. Thus, what they used to do is made fair-seeming to the extravagant.",
  10013: "Indeed, We destroyed generations [7] before you when they persisted in wrongdoing. Their messengers came to them with clear proofs, but they were not such as to believe. Thus do We recompense the guilty people.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. That is, the people of Noah, ʿĀd, Thamūd, the people of Abraham, the dwellers of Midian, and the overturned cities (of Lot), whose destruction followed their persistence in wrongdoing, idolatry, and the denial of their messengers after the coming of clear proofs.",
  10014: "Then We made you successors on the earth after them, that We might see how you would act.",
  10015: "When Our clear verses are recited to them, those who have no hope of meeting Us say, “Bring a Qur’an other than this—or change it.” Say [O Prophet], “It is not for me to change it of my own accord; I only follow what is revealed to me. Indeed, I fear the punishment of a tremendous Day if I were to disobey my Lord.”",
  10016: "Say, “Had Allāh so willed, I would not have recited it to you, nor would He have made it known to you. I have lived among you for a lifetime before this [8]. Will you not then use your reason?”\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. That is, for many years—traditionally understood as forty—before the revelation of the Qur’an, during which he lived among them and was known for truthfulness, honesty, and integrity, without having recited or claimed any revelation, underscoring that this message was not of his own making.",
  10017: "Who is more unjust than one who invents a lie against Allāh or denies His signs? Indeed, the guilty will never prosper.",
  10018: "They worship besides Allāh those who can neither harm nor benefit them, saying, “These are our intercessors with Allāh.” Say, “Do you presume to inform Allāh of that which He does not know in the heavens or on the earth?” Glory be to Him, exalted is He above all that they associate with Him in worship.",
  10019: "Mankind was but a single community [9], then they differed. Had it not been for a Word from your Lord that had already gone forth, the matter would have been settled between them concerning that over which they differ.\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. That is, mankind was originally united upon one religion—Islamic monotheism (tawḥīd). This refers to an initial state in which humanity affirmed the oneness of Allāh and worshipped Him alone, as exemplified by the Original faith of Adam and those who followed him. Disagreement later arose through deviation, innovation, and the rejection of prophetic guidance, leading to difference in belief and practice.\nIt is reported that “the period between Adam and Noah was ten centuries, during which people remained upon the right path of Islam. Thereafter, they fell into disagreement concerning matters of faith, and Allāh sent prophets as bearers of glad tidings and as warners.”",
  10020: "They say, ‘Why has no sign been sent down to him from his Lord [7]?’ Say, ‘The knowledge of the unseen belongs to Allāh alone. So wait; indeed, I too am waiting with you.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. That is, they demanded a miraculous sign of their own choosing—such as an extraordinary physical miracle—to confirm his prophethood. The Qur’an repeatedly responds that the timing and nature of such signs belong solely to Allāh, not to the messenger, and that signs are sent in accordance with divine wisdom, not human challenge. Their demand was not made in sincere search of guidance, but as a condition for belief or a pretext for rejection, as similar signs had been denied by earlier peoples despite clear proof.",
  10021: "When We let people taste mercy after adversity has struck them, at once they begin plotting against Our verses. Say, “Allāh is swifter in planning.” Indeed, Our angelic messengers record all that you scheme.",
  10022: "It is He who enables you to travel on land and sea, until when you are in ships and they sail with a pleasant wind and rejoice in it, suddenly a violent wind strikes, and waves come upon them from every side, and they realize that they are completely surrounded. Then they cry out to Allāh alone [11], being sincere to Him in devotion, saying: “If You deliver us from this, we will surely be among the grateful.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. The journey of human life is naturally vulnerable and unstable, much like a sea voyage in which people are carried along without certainty as to when conditions may suddenly turn against them. Safety amid such uncertainty is not self-secured; rather, it is by Allāh’s grace that people are preserved from the trials, dangers, and perils that confront them throughout life.",
  10023: "But when He delivers them, at once they rebel on the earth without right [12]. \nO people, your rebellion is only against yourselves—a brief enjoyment of the life of this world; then to Us will be your return, and We will inform you of what you used to do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. That is, through disbelief in Allāh, associating false deities or partners with Him, and persisting in sinful conduct and moral transgression in defiance of divine guidance.",
  10024: "The example of this worldly life is like rain that We send down from the sky, which is absorbed by the plants of the earth from which people and cattle eat. Then, when the earth has taken on its adornment and is beautified, and its people think they have control over it, Our command comes to it by night or by day, and We make it like a clean-mown harvest, as though it had never flourished the day before [13]. Thus We explain the signs in detail for people who reflect.\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. This verse vividly illustrates the fragile and temporary nature of worldly life through a powerful example. Like vegetation that flourishes after rain but is suddenly destroyed, human life and its pleasures appear stable and beautiful for a short time, yet can come to an abrupt end through death. The purpose of this image is to awaken reflection: the life of this world is fleeting and deceptive in its appeal, whereas the Hereafter is the true and lasting life.",
  10025: "Allāh invites to the Home of Peace [14] and guides whom He wills to a straight path.\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. The “Home of Peace” refers to Paradise, the eternal abode prepared by Allāh for the believers. It is described as a place of complete peace and safety—free from fear, grief, death, hardship, and sin—where people enjoy lasting contentment and nearness to Allāh. It is called the Home of Peace because it is secure from all forms of harm and because it is granted by Allāh, who is Himself the source of peace.",
  10026: "For those who do good is the best [reward], and even more [15]. Neither darkness nor humiliation will cover their faces. They are the people of Paradise; they will abide therein forever.\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. “The best [reward]” refers to Paradise, while “and even more” is understood to mean the supreme blessing of beholding the Honorable Face of Allāh. This vision is regarded as the greatest joy granted to the believers—surpassing all other delights of Paradise—through which they experience complete contentment, nearness to Allāh, and the fulfillment of faith, hope, and love. \nIn that joy-filled moment when we see Allāh and know Him as He knows each of us, the Muslims. He will infuse us with the faith, hope, and love we need to stand firm, until we gaze at our Loving and Living God - Allāh.",
  10027: "As for those who commit evil deeds, the recompense of an evil deed is the like thereof. Humiliation will cover them [16]; there will be none to protect them from Allāh. It will be as though their faces are covered with patches of the darkest night. These are the inhabitants of the Fire; they will abide therein forever.\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. This refers to the Day of Resurrection, when those who fabricated lies against Allāh or denied His truth will be visibly disgraced, their faces darkened as a sign of humiliation and loss. This outward darkness reflects their inner corruption and the consequences of their falsehood, standing in stark contrast to the brightness and honor granted to the righteous.",
  10028: "On the Day when We gather them all together, We will say to those who associated others with Us in worship, “Remain where you are, you and your associate-gods!” Then We will separate them, and their associate-gods will say, “It was not us that you used to worship!” [17]\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. This disavowal highlights the complete collapse of false objects of worship on the Day of Resurrection. Those who were invoked besides Allāh—whether idols, beings, or imagined intermediaries—will deny any claim to divinity or devotion, exposing the worshippers’ error and leaving them without support or excuse. The scene underscores that all acts of worship were misdirected and that ultimate accountability rests solely with Allāh.",
  10029: "Allāh is sufficient as a witness between us and you that we were indeed unaware of your worship of us.",
  10030: "There, every soul will realize what it had sent forth before. They will be returned to Allāh, their True Master, and whatever they used to invent will vanish from them.",
  10031: "Say, “Who provides for you from the heaven and the earth? Who controls your hearing and your sight? Who brings forth the living from the dead, and the dead from the living? Who directs every matter?” They will say, “Allāh [18].” Say, “Will you not then fear Him?”\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. These matters—creating life, restoring it after death, providing sustenance, and governing all affairs—are among the clearest and most powerful signs of Allāh’s Lordship. They demonstrate that He alone possesses absolute power, knowledge, and authority, and therefore He alone is truly worthy of worship, without any partners or intermediaries.",
  10032: "Such is Allāh, your True Lord. What is there beyond the truth except falsehood? So how are you turned away?",
  10033: "Thus the Word of your Lord has been proven true against those who persist in rebellion—that they will never believe. [19]\\n\\nFootnotes: [19] That is, by Allāh’s established decree, those who persist in rebellion and willful disobedience are not guided. This does not mean they are forced into disbelief; rather, their continued rejection of truth and refusal to respond to guidance results in Allāh leaving them to the path they have chosen.",
  10034: "Say, “Is there any of your associate-gods who originates creation and then brings it back to life [20]?” Say, “Allāh originates creation and then brings it back. How then are you turned away?”\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. This demonstrates the complete inability and unworthiness of false gods to be worshipped. They neither create nor restore life, nor do they possess any power, knowledge, or authority of their own. Their helplessness exposes the futility of directing worship toward them and affirms that true devotion belongs to Allāh alone.",
  10035: "Say, “Is there any of your associate-gods who can guide to the Truth?” Say, “It is Allāh who guides to the Truth. So is the One who guides to the Truth more worthy to be followed, or one who cannot find the way unless he is guided? What is wrong with you? How do you judge?”",
  10036: "Yet most of them follow nothing but assumption. Indeed, assumption avails nothing against the Truth. Indeed, Allāh is All-Knowing of all that they do.",
  10037: "This Qur’an could not have been produced by anyone other than Allāh. Rather, it is a confirmation of that which came before it [of divine scriptures] and a detailed explanation of the Book. There is no doubt about it—it is from the Lord of all worlds.",
  10038: "Or do they say, “He invented it”? Say, “Then bring a chapter like it, and call upon whoever you can other than Allāh [for help], if what you say is true.”",
  10039: "Rather, they denied that which they had not encompassed in knowledge and whose fulfillment had not yet come to them. Thus did those before them deny [the truth]. See, then, how was the end of the wrongdoers.",
  10040: "Among them are those who believe in it [this Qur’an], and among them are those who do not believe in it. Your Lord knows best those who spread corruption.",
  10041: "If they deny you, then say, “For me are my deeds, and for you are your deeds [21]. You are free of what I do, and I am free of what you do.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. This indicates that when clear truth has been presented and argument no longer benefits, there is no need to continue debating. At that point, each person is left to their own responsibility and choice, and the matter is entrusted to Allāh, who will judge between people with complete justice.",
  10042: "Among them are those who [pretend to] listen to you. Will you then make the deaf hear, even though they do not use reason?",
  10043: "And among them are those who [pretend to] look at you. But can you guide the blind, even though they do not [attempt to] see?",
  10044: "Indeed, Allāh does not wrong people in the least [22]; rather, it is people who wrong themselves.\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. This affirms the perfect justice of Allāh. He does not wrong anyone in any way, nor does He punish without cause. Any loss, misguidance, or suffering people experience is the result of their own choices—through disbelief, wrongdoing, or rejection of guidance. Thus, responsibility lies with human beings themselves, not injustice on the part of Allāh.",
  10045: "On the Day when He gathers them [after resurrection], it will be as though they had remained [in this world] only for an hour of a day, recognizing one another [23]. Truly lost are those who denied the meeting with Allāh, for they did not follow guidance.\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. At that moment, they will fully realize the seriousness of their fatal error. Their brief recognition of one another will not bring comfort or help; rather, it will lead only to mutual blame, reproach, and regret, as each seeks to distance himself from responsibility.",
  10046: "Whether We show you, [O Prophet], some of what We warn them of, or take you in death [before that] [24], to Us is their return. And Allāh is a Witness to all that they do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. That is, whether the Prophet ﷺ witnesses some of the punishment or consequences they were warned of during his lifetime, or whether he passes away before that occurs. In either case, their final return is to Allāh alone, and His judgment will inevitably take place. They will not be able to escape His punishment, for no one can evade the justice or decree of Allāh.",
  10047: "For every nation there is a messenger. When their messenger comes, judgment will be passed between them with justice [25], and they will not be wronged.\\n\\nFootnotes: [25]. This judgment occurs only after a messenger has been sent and the message made clear. People are not held accountable until guidance has reached them. When judgment comes—whether in this world or on the Day of Resurrection—it is carried out with complete justice, and no one is wronged in the least. With the sending of the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ, truth is clearly distinguished from error: those who accept the message are guided and saved, while those who knowingly reject it bear the consequences of their denial.",
  10048: "They say, “When will this promise come to pass [26], if you are indeed truthful?”\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. That is, the promised punishment or judgment which they were warned of for rejecting the truth. Their question was not asked in sincere search of understanding, but in doubt and mockery, demanding that the warning be hastened. The Qur’an repeatedly emphasizes that such matters occur only at the time appointed by Allāh, according to His wisdom, and not at human demand.",
  10049: "Say, “I have no power to harm or benefit myself except as Allāh wills. For every nation there is an appointed term; when their time comes, they can neither delay it for a moment nor advance it.”",
  10050: "Say, “Have you considered: if His punishment were to come upon you by night or by day, what part of it would the wicked wish to hasten?",
  10051: "Is it then, when it has befallen, that you believe in it? Now?—when you had already been seeking to hasten it on!”",
  10052: "Then it will be said to those who associated others with Allāh in worship, “Taste the eternal punishment! Are you being recompensed for anything except what you used to earn?”",
  10053: "They ask you, “Is it true [27]?” Say: “Yes, By my Lord!. It is certainly true, and you cannot escape it.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]  That is, they ask whether the promised punishment, resurrection, and final judgment are truly real. Their question is not one of sincere inquiry, but of doubt and challenge. The verse affirms with certainty that what they were warned of will indeed come to pass and that no one will be able to escape Allāh’s decree.",
  10054: "If every soul that wronged [28] possessed all that is on earth, it would offer it as ransom. They will conceal their regret when they see the punishment. Judgment will be passed between them with justice, and they will not be wronged.\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. That is, by worshipping others besides Allāh and dying in that state without repentance. This verse highlights the complete futility of ransom on the Day of Judgment: no amount of wealth or possession—even everything on earth—can avert punishment once judgment has arrived. The statement “they will conceal their regret” points to silent remorse and helpless despair, for there will be no opportunity left to repent, make amends, or undo past deeds. Judgment at that time will be carried out with perfect justice, and no one will be wronged in the least.",
  10055: "Indeed, to Allāh belongs whatever is in the heavens and the earth. Indeed, the promise of Allāh is true, but most of them do not know. [29]\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. That is, the promise of resurrection, judgment, and recompense made by Allāh is absolutely true and will inevitably be fulfilled. Allāh reigns supreme over all creation, and nothing can prevent Him from carrying out His decree or bringing punishment upon those who persist in disbelief. Yet most people remain heedless of this reality—distracted by worldly life or doubtful of the Hereafter—and therefore live as though they will not be held accountable.",
  10056: "It is He who gives life and causes death, and to Him you will be returned.",
  10057: "O people, there has already come to you an admonition from your Lord—a healing for what is in the hearts, and guidance and mercy for the believers.",
  10058: "Say, “In the bounty of Allāh and in His mercy—in that let them rejoice [30]. It is far better than all that they accumulate.” [31]\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. That is, in the bounty and mercy of Allāh—namely the Qur’an, true guidance, faith, forgiveness, and the promise of salvation. These are the greatest gifts Allāh bestows upon humanity, and they are the proper cause for joy, gratitude, and inner contentment.\nThe source of true and lasting joy is from Allāh and by following His last and final messenger, prophet Muhammad ﷺ. It  enables us to shine Allah’s light as we strive to bring joy to others, a joy that offers hope and strength to face trials.\n[31]. That is, better than all worldly possessions people gather, such as wealth, status, and material gain. These things are temporary and soon pass away, whereas Allāh’s bounty and mercy are lasting and bring benefit in this life and the Hereafter.\nBelievers always need to remember Allah’s great bounty by which He guided them to Islam and gave them the gift of the Qur’an to which any worldly gain is not even remotely comparable. When the righteous prosper the city rejoices. When those who truly live by Islamic faith begin to influence a community, then this signals good news because it means Allah’s justice is taking hold. Greed diminishes. The poor find support. The oppressed are protected. Whenever Islam flourishes, then there’s joy and blessing in the city.",
  10059: "Say [to the pagans], “Have you considered what Allāh has provided for you as sustenance, and how you have made some of it unlawful and some lawful?\" Say, \"Has Allāh given you permission, or do you invent lies against Allāh?\"",
  10060: "What do those who invent lies about Allāh suppose on the Day of Resurrection? Indeed, Allāh is gracious towards people, but most of them do not give thanks.",
  10061: "In whatever matter you [O Prophet] may be engaged, whatever portion of the Qur’an you may recite, and whatever work you people may do, We are Witnesses over you when you undertake it. Nothing is hidden from your Lord—not even an atom’s weight in the earth or in the heaven, nor anything smaller than that or greater, but it is all in a clear Record.",
  10062: "Indeed, the allies of Allāh—there will be no fear upon them, nor will they grieve. [32]\\n\\nFootnotes: [32]. The “allies of Allāh” are those who are close to Him through true faith and obedience. They believe in Him, remain mindful of Him, and strive to live according to His guidance. Because of this relationship, they will have no fear of what lies ahead and no grief over what has passed, for they are assured of Allāh’s protection, mercy, and reward.\nThis assurance frees believers from anxiety over worldly concerns, such as provision and livelihood. While those who reject faith are preoccupied with such matters, the allies of Allāh trust in His care. When they seek His pleasure through obedience and avoid wrongdoing, Allāh suffices them and provides for their needs according to His wisdom.",
  10063: "[They are] those who believe and are mindful of Allāh.",
  10064: "For them are glad tidings in this worldly life and in the Hereafter. There is no change in the promise of Allāh. That is the great success.",
  10065: "Do not let their words grieve you. Indeed, all honor and power belong to Allāh.He is the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.",
  10066: "Indeed, to Allāh belongs whoever is in the heavens and whoever is on the earth. Those who invoke others besides Allāh do not truly follow any associate-gods. They follow nothing but assumption, and they do nothing but lie.",
  10067: "It is He who has made for you the night, that you may rest therein, and the day bright. Indeed, in this are signs for people who listen.",
  10068: "They say, “Allāh has taken for Himself a son.” Glory be to Him! He is the Self-Sufficient. To Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth. You have no evidence for this claim. Do you say about Allāh what you do not know?",
  10069: "Say, “Indeed, those who invent lies about Allāh will never prosper.”",
  10070: "[It is only] a brief enjoyment in this world; then to Us will be their return. Then We shall make them taste the severe punishment for their persistent disbelief.",
  10071: "Recite to them the story of Noah, when he said to his people, ‘O my people, if my presence among you and my reminding you of the signs of Allāh are burdensome to you, then I have placed my trust in Allāh. So gather your resolve, along with your associate-gods, and let your plan be clear to you. Then carry it out against me, and give me no respite.’",
  10072: "But if you turn away, I have not asked you for any reward. My reward is only with Allāh, and I have been commanded to be among the Muslims. [33]\\n\\nFootnotes: [33]. That is, to be among those who submit themselves fully to God in faith, obedience, and sincerity. The term Muslims here is used in its original, universal sense, referring to all who surrender their will to God —such as the prophets and their true followers—long before the specific community of Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ.",
  10073: "Yet they denied him, so We delivered him and those who were with him in the Ark, and made them successors, while We drowned those who rejected Our signs. So observe how was the end of those who were warned.",
  10074: "Then after him We sent messengers to their people. They came to them with clear proofs, but they were not to believe in what they had already denied before. Thus We seal over the hearts of the transgressors.",
  10075: "Then after them, We sent Moses and Aaron with Our signs to Pharaoh and his chiefs, but they behaved arrogantly, for they were a guilty people.",
  10076: "So when the Truth came to them from Us, they said, “Indeed, this is clear magic.”",
  10077: "Moses said, “Do you say this about the Truth when it has come to you? Is this magic? Magicians will never prosper.”",
  10078: "They said, “Have you come to turn us away from what we found our forefathers following, so that greatness in the land may belong to you two [Moses and Aaron]? But we are not believers in you.”",
  10079: "Pharaoh said, ‘Bring me every skilled magician.’",
  10080: "When the magicians came, Moses said to them, “Cast whatever you are going to cast.”",
  10081: "So when they had cast [their ropes and staffs], Moses said, “What you have brought is mere magic. Allāh will surely nullify it, for Allāh does not set right the work of the corrupters.”",
  10082: "Allāh establishes the Truth by His words, however much the criminals may hate it.",
  10083: "But none believed in Moses except some youths from his people, out of fear of Pharaoh and their own chiefs, lest they persecute them. Indeed, Pharaoh was a tyrant in the land, and he was truly among the transgressors.",
  10084: "Moses said, “O my people, if you truly believe in Allāh, then place your trust in Him alone [34], if indeed you are Muslims.”\n[35]\\n\\nFootnotes: [34]. That is, place your complete trust in Allāh alone. He is perfectly wise, merciful, and good, and all His plans and decrees are for the best, even when their outcomes are not immediately apparent.\n[35]. That is, if you have truly surrendered yourselves to God and submitted fully to His will, obeying Him with sincerity and reliance.",
  10085: "They said, “Upon Allāh alone do we rely. Our Lord, do not subject us to the persecution of the wrongdoing people, [36]\\n\\nFootnotes: [36]. This is a plea for protection from a trial that might weaken their faith under severe oppression.",
  10086: "and deliver us, by Your mercy, from the disbelieving people.”",
  10087: "We inspired Moses and his brother, “Appoint houses for your people in Egypt, make your houses places of worship facing the Qiblah, establish prayer, and give glad tidings to the believers.”",
  10088: "Moses said, “Our Lord, You have given Pharaoh and his chiefs splendor and wealth in the life of this world, and our Lord, they lead people astray from Your path. Our Lord, obliterate their wealth and harden their hearts, so that they will not believe until they see the painful punishment.”",
  10089: "Allāh said [to Moses and Aaron], “Your prayer has been accepted, so remain steadfast, and do not follow the way of those who do not know.”",
  10090: "And We caused the children of Israel to cross the sea. Then Pharaoh and his soldiers pursued them in tyranny and hostility, until, when drowning overtook him, he said, “I believe that there is no god except Allāh, in whom the children of Israel believe, and I am one of the Muslims.” [37]\\n\\nFootnotes: [37]. This declaration of belief came only when Pharaoh was overtaken by drowning and had no possibility of escape. Such belief, expressed at the moment of certain destruction, is not accepted, because it is compelled by sight and despair rather than sincere faith. True belief must be made freely, before death and before the unseen realities become fully apparent. Pharaoh’s confession therefore did not save him, but instead stands as a final testimony against himself.",
  10091: "‘Only now [do you believe], when before this you disobeyed and were among the corrupters?’",
  10092: "So today We will preserve your body [38], so that you may be a sign for those who come after you. Yet indeed, many people remain heedless of Our signs.\\n\\nFootnotes: [38]. That is, Pharaoh’s dead body was preserved so that the Children of Israel could see with certainty that he had indeed perished, and so that it might serve as a lesson for later generations. It stands as a lasting warning that worldly power, arrogance, and tyranny cannot protect anyone from divine justice. Though Pharaoh claimed lordship and absolute authority, he was reduced to a lifeless body—preserved not as an honor, but as a sign that truth ultimately prevails and falsehood collapses. Yet, despite such clear signs, many people remain heedless and fail to take heed.",
  10093: "Indeed, We settled the children of Israel in a blessed dwelling place [39], and provided them with good things [40]. They did not differ until knowledge had come to them [41]. Your Lord will judge between them on the Day of Resurrection concerning that over which they used to differ.\\n\\nFootnotes: [39]. That is, a blessed land identified as the Levant, an environment suitable for settlement, stability, and prosperity after deliverance from oppression.\n[40]. That is, Allāh provided them with wholesome and lawful provisions, such as when He shaded them with clouds and sent down manna and quails, sustaining them generously during their journey and settlement.\n[41]. That is, their disagreement arose only after clear knowledge had come to them—namely, after the Torah was revealed—showing that their division was due not to ignorance, but to willful dispute, envy, and deviation from revealed guidance.",
  10094: "If you are in doubt about what We have revealed to you [42], then ask those who read the Scripture before you. The truth has certainly come to you from your Lord, so never be among the doubters.\\n\\nFootnotes: [42]. This statement is intended to affirm the certainty of the Qur’an and to challenge those who denied it. The meaning is that the truth of this revelation can be confirmed by those who possess earlier Scriptures, as they recognize its harmony with the message brought by the previous prophets.\nThe coming of Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ was foretold in earlier revelations—the Torah and the Gospel—and his name and description were known to the people of Scripture. Thus, this verse highlights the continuity of divine revelation and removes all grounds for doubt concerning the Qur’an’s divine origin.",
  10095: "And never be among those who deny the signs of Allāh, lest you be among the losers.",
  10096: "Indeed, those against whom the Word of your Lord has been fulfilled will never believe,",
  10097: "Even if every sign were to come to them until they see the painful punishment.",
  10098: "There was no town that believed and then benefited from its faith—except the people of Jonah. When they believed, We removed from them the punishment of disgrace in this worldly life and allowed them to enjoy themselves for a time.",
  10099: "Had your Lord willed, everyone on earth would have believed—all of them together. Would you then compel people to become believers?",
  10100: "No soul can believe except by Allah’s permission, and He places impurity upon those who do not reason.",
  10101: "Say, “Look at what is in the heavens and the earth.” But signs and warnings do not benefit a people who do not believe.",
  10102: "Are they waiting for anything except the fate of those who passed away before them? Say, ‘Then wait; indeed, I am with you among those who wait.’",
  10103: "Then We deliver Our messengers and those who have believed. Thus it is binding upon Us to save the believers.",
  10104: "Say, “O people, if you are in doubt about my religion, then I do not worship those whom you worship besides Allāh. Rather, I worship Allāh alone—the One who causes you to die—and I have been commanded to be among the believers.”",
  10105: "So set your face toward the true faith, upright, and never be among those who associate others with Allāh.",
  10106: "Do not call upon besides Allāh that which can neither benefit you nor harm you, for if you do so, you will surely be among the wrongdoers.",
  10107: "If Allāh touches you with harm, none can remove it except Him [43]; and if He intends good for you, none can withhold His grace. He bestows it upon whomever He wills among His servants. And He alone is the All-Forgiving, the Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [43]. This verse affirms that harm and benefit are entirely in the Hand of Allāh alone. In this regard, it is reported that a group of people came to the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ to pledge their allegiance. He accepted the pledge from nine of them but declined it from one man. When asked about this, the Prophet ﷺ said, “He is wearing an amulet.” The man then removed and broke it, after which the Prophet ﷺ accepted his pledge and said: “Whoever hangs an amulet has committed polytheism.”\nThe reason for this is that amulets are often worn with the belief that they can ward off harm or bring benefit independently of Allāh. Such beliefs contradict true reliance upon Allāh and His exclusive power over harm and good. Therefore, making, wearing, buying, or displaying amulets, talismans, crosses, or similar objects—whether on the body, in clothing, in homes, or on vehicles—with the belief that they offer protection or blessing, falls under prohibited practices connected to idolatry. True protection and benefit come only from Allāh.",
  10108: "Say, “O people, the truth has already come to you from your Lord. Whoever accepts guidance does so only for his own benefit, and whoever goes astray does so only against himself. And I am not a keeper over you.”",
  10109: "Follow what is revealed to you, [O Prophet], and be patient until Allāh passes His Judgment, for He is the Best of judges.",
  11001: "Alif Lām Rā [1]. This is a Book whose verses have been perfected and then fully explained, from One who is All-Wise, All-Aware.\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2: verse 1.",
  11002: "Do not worship except Allah alone. Indeed, I have been sent to you from Him as a warner and a bearer of glad tidings.",
  11003: "And seek forgiveness from your Lord, then turn to Him in repentance. He will grant you good provision for an appointed term and bestow His grace upon everyone of virtue. But if you turn away, then indeed I fear for you the punishment of a tremendous Day.",
  11004: "To Allāh is your ultimate return, and He is capable of all things.",
  11005: "Indeed, they fold up their chests to hide from Him [2]. But even when they cover themselves with their garments, He knows what they conceal and what they reveal. Indeed, He is All-Knowing of what is within the hearts.\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. This refers to people who inwardly conceal disbelief, doubt, or hostility toward the truth. “Folding up their chests” is a figurative expression describing their attempt to hide their true thoughts and intentions from Allāh, either by suppressing them inwardly or by outwardly covering themselves. The verse makes clear that such concealment is futile, for Allāh knows what is hidden in the heart just as He knows what is openly expressed.",
  11006: "There is no moving creature on the earth except that its provision rests with Allāh. He knows its dwelling place and its place of storage. Everything is recorded in a clear Record. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. That is, Allah has complete and perfect knowledge of every living creature—where it resides during its life and where it is kept or placed at every stage of its existence. Many scholars explain that “its dwelling place” refers to where a creature lives and moves upon the earth, while “its place of storage” includes where it is kept before birth in the womb, where it is sustained during life, and where it is laid to rest in the grave after death. Some also include places where its provision is stored or assigned. This expression emphasizes that every stage of life—from conception in the womb to burial in the grave—is fully known to Allah, and nothing about any creature’s life, sustenance, or destiny is hidden from Him. \nAll of this is recorded “in a clear Record” — that is, in the Preserved Tablet (al-Lawḥ al-Maḥfūẓ), which contains all events that have occurred and will occur in the heavens and the earth. Allah’s knowledge encompasses them all, His Pen has recorded them, His will has brought them into effect, and His provision extends over them entirely. Therefore, hearts should find peace in the sufficiency of the One who has guaranteed their provision and whose knowledge fully encompasses their beings and their conditions.",
  11007: "It is He who created the heavens and the earth in six days—while His Throne was upon the water—in order to test you as to which of you is best in deeds. Yet if you say, “You will surely be resurrected after death,” those who disbelieve will say, “This is nothing but plain magic.”",
  11008: "If We delay the punishment from them for a fixed term, they will surely say, “What is holding it back?” Indeed, on the Day it comes upon them, it will not be averted from them, and they will be encompassed by what they used to ridicule.",
  11009: "If We let man taste mercy from Us and then withdraw it from him, he becomes despairing and ungrateful.",
  11010: "But if We let him taste prosperity after hardship has afflicted him, he will surely say, ‘My suffering is gone.’ Indeed, he becomes exultant and boastful.",
  11011: "Except those who are patient and do righteous deeds—for them will be forgiveness and a great reward.",
  11012: "Perhaps you would abandon some of what has been revealed to you [O Prophet] [4], and your chest feels constricted by it, because they say, “Why has a treasure not been sent down to him, or an angel come with him?” You are only a warner [5], and Allāh is the Disposer of everything.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. This refers to the fact that some people would reject or resist certain commands, warnings, or teachings revealed to the Prophet ﷺ because they found them difficult or burdensome to accept or follow. Their reluctance was not due to a lack of clarity in the message, but rather because the teachings challenged their personal desires, established traditions, or vested interests. In this context, Allāh consoles the Prophet ﷺ by saying that he might feel inclined to withhold part of what has been revealed to him or feel distressed by the reactions of the disbelievers. This distress arose from the mockery, denial, stubbornness, and persistent disbelief of the polytheists, as well as their demands for extraordinary signs—such as asking for great wealth to be sent down to him or for an angel to descend from the sky to testify to his prophethood.\nAllah reassures the Prophet ﷺ that he should not feel burdened or constrained by such reactions. His duty is only to convey what has been revealed to him and to warn people against shirk, disbelief, and disobedience. Guidance and acceptance, however, are in Allah’s hands.\n [5]. The Prophet ﷺ is charged only with conveying Allāh’s message clearly and faithfully. He is not responsible for forcing belief upon people, nor for satisfying their arbitrary demands for miracles or material signs. Guidance lies with Allāh alone, and the Prophet’s duty is limited to warning and delivering glad tidings.",
  11013: "Or do they say, “He has fabricated it”? Say, “Then bring ten fabricated chapters like it, and call upon whomever you can besides Allāh, if you are truthful.”",
  11014: "But if they do not respond to you, then know that it has been revealed only with the knowledge of Allāh, and that there is no god except Him. Will you then submit yourselves to Him? [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. That is, after having been convinced by such clear and decisive evidence. True salvation lies in sincerely believing that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allāh and that Muḥammad ﷺ is His Messenger. Mere knowledge about Allāh is not sufficient; rather, salvation requires deep and genuine faith, affirmed by submission to Allāh and lived out through obedience to the Qur’an and the teachings of Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ, the final Messenger of God. Such faith brings security and success in this world and the Hereafter.",
  11015: "Whoever seeks the life of this world and its adornment, We shall repay them in full for their deeds therein, and they will not be deprived of their due in it.",
  11016: "They are the ones for whom there will be nothing in the Hereafter except the Fire. Worthless are the deeds they did in this world, and vain is all that they used to do. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. That is, although they may have performed deeds that appeared outwardly good in this worldly life, those deeds have no value in the Hereafter because they were not done with sincere faith in Allāh or with the intention of seeking His pleasure. Their actions were motivated by worldly gain, status, or praise, and therefore their reward was confined to this world alone. Since faith and sincerity are the foundation for the acceptance of deeds, whatever is done without them becomes void and yields no lasting benefit in the Hereafter.\nThe Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said: “Allāh would not deal a believer unjustly even with regard to a single good deed. He grants him reward for it in this worldly life and recompenses him for it in the Hereafter. As for the disbeliever, he is given the reward for his good deeds in this world, but when he comes to the Hereafter, there will be nothing left for him to be rewarded for.”",
  11017: " So can one who stands upon a clear proof from his Lord—recited by a witness from Him [8], and preceded by the Scripture of Moses as a guide and a mercy—be like those who deny the truth? Such people believe in it. But whoever among the factions disbelieves in it, the Fire will be his promised end. So do not be in doubt about it. Indeed, it is the truth from your Lord, yet most people do not believe.\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. The “witness from Him” has been explained by scholars in several complementary ways. Many interpret it as the Qur’an itself, which bears witness to its own truth through its clarity, coherence, and inimitable nature. Others hold that it refers to the Angel Jibrīl (Gabriel), who conveyed the revelation from Allāh to the Prophet ﷺ. Some scholars also understood it as the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ himself, whose character, truthfulness, and life bore witness to the message he delivered.\nThese interpretations are not contradictory; rather, they collectively emphasize that the message is supported by divine testimony—through revelation, the messenger who conveyed it, and the angel who brought it—leaving no excuse for denial.",
  11018: "Who is more unjust than one who fabricates a lie against Allāh? Such people will be presented before their Lord, and the witnesses will say, “These are the ones who lied against their Lord.” Indeed, the curse of Allāh is upon the wrongdoers,",
  11019: "those who hinder others from the path of Allāh and seek to make it appear crooked, while they themselves disbelieve in the Hereafter.",
  11020: "They will have no escape on earth, nor will they have any protectors besides Allāh. The punishment will be doubled for them, for they could neither bear to listen to the truth nor see it. [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. This does not refer to physical deafness or blindness, but to willful rejection. They deliberately refused to listen attentively to the truth or to reflect upon the signs that pointed to it. Out of arrogance and stubbornness, they closed their ears and eyes to guidance, even when it was made clear to them. Because they rejected the truth themselves and also hindered others from it, their punishment is described as being doubled.",
  11021: "They are the ones who have ruined themselves, and whatever they used to fabricate will vanish from them.",
  11022: "Without doubt, they will be the greatest losers in the Hereafter.",
  11023: "Indeed, those who believe, do righteous deeds, and humble themselves before their Lord—they are the companions of Paradise; they will abide therein forever.",
  11024: "The example of the two groups is that of the blind and deaf and the seeing and hearing. Are they equal? Will you not then reflect?",
  11025: "We certainly sent Noah to his people, saying, ‘Indeed, I am a clear warner to you,’",
  11026: "that you worship none but Allāh. I truly fear for you the punishment of a painful Day.",
  11027: "But the chiefs of those who disbelieved among his people said, ‘We see you as nothing but a human being like ourselves, and we see that none follow you except the lowest among us, acting without thought or deliberation. Nor do we see in you any superiority over us; rather, we think you are liars.’",
  11028: "Noah said, “O my people, tell me: if I stand upon a clear proof from my Lord, and He has bestowed upon me mercy from Himself, but it has been made obscure to you—should we compel you to accept it while you are averse to it?”",
  11029: "O my people, I do not ask you for any wealth in return for this message—my reward is only with Allāh. And I will never drive away those who have believed [10]; they will surely meet their Lord. Rather, I see that you are a people behaving ignorantly.\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. The leaders and elite of society, driven by pride and arrogance, looked down upon the poor and socially weak believers. They considered faith to be a mark of inferiority and demanded that Prophet Noah (peace be upon him) expel these humble followers in order to gain their approval. A similar attitude was shown toward Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ, whose early followers also included many of the poor and marginalized. This footnote highlights a recurring pattern in history: the powerful often reject the truth not because it lacks evidence, but because accepting it would require humility and equality with those they despise.",
  11030: "O my people, who would protect me against Allāh if I were to drive them away? Will you not then take heed?",
  11031: "I do not tell you that I possess the treasures of Allāh, nor do I know the unseen, nor do I claim to be an angel. Nor do I say of those whom you look down upon that Allāh will never grant them any good. Allāh knows best what is within their souls. If I were to say otherwise, then indeed I would be among the wrongdoers.",
  11032: "They said, “O Noah, you have argued with us and prolonged the argument excessively. So bring upon us what you threaten us with, if you are truthful.”",
  11033: "He said, “Only Allāh can bring it upon you, if He wills, and you will not be able to evade it.”",
  11034: "My advice will not benefit you—even if I sincerely wished to advise you—if Allāh wills to let you go astray. He is your Lord, and to Him you will be returned.",
  11035: "Or do they say, ‘He has fabricated it?’ Say, ‘If I have fabricated it, then upon me is my guilt, and I am free of the crimes you commit.’",
  11036: "It was revealed to Noah, “None of your people will believe except those who have already believed. So do not grieve over what they have been doing.” [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. That is, Allāh informed Noah (peace be upon him) that those who had persistently rejected the message—through denial, mockery, and ridicule—would no longer be guided to faith. Their continued rejection caused their hearts to become sealed, leaving no openness to guidance. This revelation was meant to console Noah and lift the burden of grief from him, making it clear that he had fully discharged his duty of conveying the message. What followed was a matter of divine judgment, not any failure on the part of the Prophet.",
  11037: "Construct the Ark under Our watchful eyes and by Our inspiration, and do not plead with Me concerning those who have wronged, for they will surely be drowned.",
  11038: "As he was building the Ark, whenever the chiefs of his people passed by him, they ridiculed him. He said, “If you ridicule us now, then we too will ridicule you, just as you ridicule us.",
  11039: "You will soon know upon whom a punishment will come to disgrace him, and upon whom a lasting punishment will descend.",
  11040: "Thus it continued until, when Our command came and the oven overflowed [12], We said, ‘Load into the Ark a pair of every kind, along with your family—except those against whom the decree has already passed—and those who have believed.’ But none had believed with him except a few.\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. The “oven” (al-tannūr) is mentioned as the sign by which Noah (peace be upon him) would know that the Flood had begun. When it “overflowed,” it indicated that the divine command had arrived and that the waters were bursting forth. Many commentators understand this as a literal oven or furnace associated with Noah’s household from which water began to gush—an extraordinary sign marking the start of the punishment. Others interpret it more broadly as a reference to the earth’s springs erupting and water bursting forth from the ground. In either case, the meaning is that the Flood began suddenly and decisively by Allāh’s command, leaving no doubt that the promised judgment had commenced.",
  11041: "Noah said, “Embark therein; in the Name of Allāh it sails and comes to rest. Indeed, my Lord is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.”",
  11042: " As it sailed with them through waves like mountains, Noah called out to his son—who had kept himself apart—“O my son, embark with us and do not be among the disbelievers.”",
  11043: "He said, “I will take refuge on a mountain that will protect me from the water.” Noah said, “There is no protection today from the command of Allāh, except for the one upon whom He has mercy.” Then a wave came between them, and he was among the drowned.",
  11044: "Then it was said, “O earth, swallow up your water; and O sky, withhold your rain!” So the water subsided, the matter was settled, and the Ark came to rest upon mount Jūdī. And it was said, “Away with the wrongdoing people!”",
  11045: "And Noah called out to his Lord, saying, “My Lord, my son is of my family, and indeed Your promise is true, and You are the Most Just of judges.”",
  11046: "Allāh said, ‘O Noah, he is not of your family. Indeed, his conduct was unrighteous. So do not ask Me about that of which you have no knowledge. Indeed, I admonish you lest you be among the ignorant.’",
  11047: "Noah said, “My Lord, I seek refuge in You from asking about that of which I have no knowledge. Unless You forgive me and have mercy upon me, I will be among the losers.”",
  11048: "It was said, ‘O Noah, disembark in peace from Us, with blessings upon you and upon nations descended from those with you. But other nations We will grant enjoyment for a time; then a painful punishment from Us will touch them.’",
  11049: "These are among the stories of the unseen that We reveal to you, [O Prophet]. Neither you nor your people knew them before this. So be patient. Indeed, the best outcome belongs to the righteous.",
  11050: "And to the people of ʿĀd We sent their brother Hūd. He said, ‘O my people, worship Allāh—you have no god other than Him. You are nothing but fabricators of falsehood.’",
  11051: "O my people, I ask of you no reward for this. My reward is only with the One who created me. Will you not then understand?",
  11052: "O my people, seek forgiveness from your Lord, then turn to Him in repentance. He will send down abundant rain upon you from the sky and increase you in strength upon strength. And do not turn away as criminals.",
  11053: "They said, ‘O Hūd, you have not brought us any clear proof, and we will not abandon our gods at your word, nor will we believe in you.’",
  11054: "We only say that some of our gods have afflicted you with evil [13].’ He said, ‘Indeed, I call Allāh to witness—and you bear witness as well—that I am free from all that you associate as partners\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. They held a superstitious belief that one of their false gods had struck Hūd (peace be upon him) with madness because he spoke against them and rejected their worship. This accusation reflects their ignorance and desperation, as they had no evidence to counter his message and resorted instead to mockery and baseless claims. It also shows how deeply rooted their belief in false deities was, leading them to attribute illness or misfortune to imagined divine retaliation.",
  11055: "with Him. So plot against me, all together, then give me no respite.",
  11056: "Indeed, I have put my trust in Allāh—my Lord and your Lord. There is no moving creature but that He holds it by its forelock. Indeed, my Lord is upon a straight path.” [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. The expression “He holds it by its forelock” is a figurative way of describing Allāh’s absolute power and control over every living creature. Just as holding an animal by its forelock signifies complete mastery and direction, this phrase affirms that no being can move, act, or escape except by Allāh’s will.\nThe statement “my Lord is upon a straight path” means that Allāh’s actions are always just, wise, and perfectly balanced. He never acts unjustly or arbitrarily; rather, His governance is founded upon truth, fairness, and absolute justice. He is a God of faithfulness, free from any wrongdoing—upright, wise, and always upon the right course in all matters.",
  11057: " But if you turn away, then I have already conveyed to you the message with which I was sent. My Lord will replace you with another people, and you will not harm Him in the least. Indeed, my Lord is Watchful over all things.",
  11058: "When Our command came, We saved Hūd and those who believed with him by a mercy from Us, and We delivered them from a severe punishment.",
  11059: "Such were the people of ʿĀd: they rejected the signs of their Lord, disobeyed His messengers, and followed the command of every stubborn tyrant.",
  11060: "They were pursued in this world by a curse, and on the Day of Resurrection as well. Indeed, ʿĀd disbelieved in their Lord. So away with ʿĀd—the people of Hūd!",
  11061: "And to the people of Thamūd, We sent their brother Ṣāliḥ. He said, “O my people, worship Allāh; you have no god other than Him. It is He who brought you forth from the earth and made you settle therein to cultivate and develop it. So seek His forgiveness, then turn to Him in repentance. Indeed, my Lord is Ever-Near, All-Responsive.”",
  11062: "They said, “O Ṣāliḥ, we truly had great hopes in you before this. Do you forbid us from worshiping what our forefathers worshiped? Indeed, we are in serious doubt about that to which you call us—deeply suspicious.”",
  11063: "He said, “O my people, tell me: if I stand upon a clear proof from my Lord and He has bestowed mercy upon me from Himself—who could protect me from Allāh if I were to disobey Him? You would not increase me except in loss.”",
  11064: "O my people, this she-camel of Allāh is a sign for you. So let her graze freely in Allāh’s land, and do not harm her, lest an imminent punishment seize you.",
  11065: "But they hamstrung her. So he said, “Enjoy yourselves in your homes for three days. That is a promise that will not be belied.”",
  11066: "When Our command came, We saved Ṣāliḥ and those who believed with him, by a mercy from Us, and delivered them from the disgrace of that Day. Indeed, your Lord is the All-Strong, the All-Mighty.",
  11067: "The mighty blast seized those who had wronged, and they lay lifeless within their homes,",
  11068: "as though they had never lived or prospered there. Indeed, Thamūd disbelieved in their Lord. So away with Thamūd!",
  11069: "Indeed, Our 'angelic' messengers came to Abraham with glad tidings. They said, “Peace.” He replied, “Peace,” and he did not delay in bringing them a roasted calf.",
  11070: "But when he saw that their hands did not reach the food, he felt uneasy about them and was fearful of them [15]. They said, “Do not be afraid. We have been sent to the people of Lot.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. Abraham (peace be upon him) regarded their refusal to eat his food as a troubling sign. In his culture, accepting food was a clear gesture of peace, trust, and goodwill, while refusing it could suggest hostility or harmful intent. Traditionally, if a guest declined such an act of hospitality, it was understood that he did not come in peace and might harbor ill will toward the host. For this reason, Abraham became uneasy and fearful, suspecting that they might have come with malicious purposes. This reaction reflects the customs of hospitality of the time and does not indicate any lack of faith; rather, it shows a natural human response to an unfamiliar and potentially threatening situation.",
  11071: "And his wife was standing nearby, and she laughed [16]. Then We gave her glad tidings of Isaac, and after Isaac, of Jacob.\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. Sarah (peace be upon her) was standing nearby, observing from a place of modest concealment. Her laughter has been explained by scholars in more than one way. Some say she laughed out of relief and surprise when she saw that the guests did not eat the food, and then learned they were angels and not a threat. Others explain that she laughed out of gladness upon hearing that the people of Lot—known for their grave corruption—were to be destroyed. Both explanations highlight her human reaction to sudden relief and divine justice, and neither interpretation conflicts with the dignity of her character.",
  11072: "She said, “Oh, woe to me! Shall I bear a child while I am an old woman, and this my husband is an old man? Indeed, this is a wondrous thing!”",
  11073: "They said, “Do you marvel at the decree of Allāh? May the mercy of Allāh and His blessings be upon you, O people of the house. Indeed, He is Praiseworthy, Most Glorious.”",
  11074: "When the fear had departed from Abraham and the glad tidings 'of Isaac’s birth' had reached him, he began to plead with Us concerning the people of Lot.",
  11075: "Indeed, Abraham was most forbearing, tender-hearted [17], and ever turning to Allāh in repentance.\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. This describes Abraham’s deeply compassionate nature. The term indicates one who sighs or groans in supplication, out of sorrow for people and awe and fear of Allāh. Abraham (peace be upon him) was tender-hearted and deeply concerned for others, even those who were sinful, that is why he pleaded for the people of Lot. True compassion requires more than good intentions; it involves patience in hardship, faithfulness in prayer, and sharing in the grief of others. Abraham’s character reflects a heart softened by devotion to Allāh—one that responds to human suffering with mercy, humility, and constant turning back to his Lord. Yet divine punishment ultimately befalls only those who persistently reject the truth after it has been made clear.",
  11076: "[The angels said], “O Abraham, turn away from this. Indeed, your Lord’s decree has already come to pass, and there will come upon them a punishment that cannot be averted.”.",
  11077: "When Our 'angelic' messengers came to Lot, he was deeply distressed for them and felt overwhelmed on their account [18], saying, “This is a dreadful day.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. Lot (peace be upon him) was distressed because he feared for the safety of his guests. He knew that the men of his town were prone to violent and immoral behavior, including attempting sexual assault against male visitors. As their host, Lot felt morally and socially responsible to protect his guests from harm and humiliation. His anguish reflects both his sense of honor and his helplessness in the face of his people’s persistent corruption and aggression.",
  11078: "And his people came rushing toward him, and they had long been accustomed to committing evil deeds. He said, “O my people, here are my daughters—they are purer for you [19]. So fear Allāh, and do not disgrace me concerning my guests. Is there not among you a single right-minded man?”\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. The phrase “my daughters” does not mean Lot (peace be upon him) was offering his own biological daughters for them. Rather, scholars explain that he was referring to the women of his community, whom he regarded as his “daughters” in the sense that a prophet is like a father to his people. He was urging them toward lawful marriage with women, which is pure and permissible, instead of their corrupt and unlawful practice. This statement was a desperate moral appeal, directing them to what Allāh had made lawful, and exposing the unnaturalness and gravity of their sin.",
  11079: "They said, “You already know that we have no claim to your daughters, and you know very well what we want.”",
  11080: "Lot said, “If only I had strength against you, or could take refuge in a mighty support.” [20]\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. The phrase “a mighty support” refers to powerful protection that Lot (peace be upon him) lacked at that moment. Some scholars explain that he meant a strong clan, tribe, or worldly protector who could restrain his people and defend his guests. Others interpret it as an expression of yearning for divine help and refuge in Allāh. These meanings are not contradictory: Lot felt the absence of worldly means while remaining inwardly reliant upon Allāh.\nThis understanding is supported by the statement of the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ: “May Allāh have mercy on Lūṭ; he was seeking refuge in a true and mighty support,” meaning Allāh Himself. Thus, although Lot expressed human vulnerability, the ultimate source of strength and protection was always divine, and the angels soon reassured him that Allāh’s support was already present and His decree had been set.",
  11081: "[The angels] said, “O Lot, we are messengers from your Lord—they can never reach you. So set out with your family during the dark part of the night [21], and let none of you look back—except your wife; she will surely be struck by the same punishment that will strike them. Indeed, their appointed time is the morning—and is not the morning near?”\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. The command to depart “during the dark part of the night” emphasizes secrecy, urgency, and divine protection. Lot (peace be upon him) and his family were instructed to leave very late at night, specifically at the time of saḥar—the final part of the night when dawn is about to break. Leaving at this time would prevent the people of Lot from noticing their escape and attempting to stop them. It also symbolizes withdrawal from a land steeped in corruption just before divine punishment descended. This instruction highlights that salvation required strict obedience to Allāh’s command, swift action, and complete trust in His protection.",
  11082: "When Our command came, We turned their cities upside down, making their highest part their lowest, and We rained upon them clustered stones of baked clay, piled one upon another.",
  11083: "Marked by your Lord—and it is not far from the wrongdoers. [22]\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. “Marked by your Lord” means that the punishment was precisely designated and carried out by Allāh, not random or indiscriminate. Each stone and every aspect of the destruction occurred by divine command and perfect knowledge. The statement “it is not far from the wrongdoers” serves as a continuing warning: such punishment is not limited to past nations, but remains close to any people who persist in wrongdoing, injustice, and rebellion against Allāh after clear guidance has come to them. Divine justice may be delayed, but it is never absent.",
  11084: "And to the people of Midian, We sent their brother Shuʿayb. He said, “O my people, worship Allāh—you have no god other than Him. Do not give short measure or weight [23]. Indeed, I see you in prosperity, yet I fear for you the punishment of an all-encompassing Day.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. While the cardinal sin of the people of Lot was sexual immorality driven by lust, the major sin of the people of Shuʿayb was greed. They were notorious for economic corruption—cheating in measurement and weight, engaging in usury (interest), imposing unjust taxes, and exploiting people in trade.\nThis verse specifically condemns giving less than what is due when measuring or weighing goods. The people of Midian were known for widespread dishonesty in business, including fraud, deception, and unjust enrichment at the expense of others. Shuʿayb (peace be upon him) denounced these practices as serious moral and religious violations, emphasizing that true worship of Allāh must be reflected in integrity, fairness, and justice in all dealings. Economic injustice is not a minor sin in Islam; it corrodes trust, destabilizes society, and invites divine punishment when persisted in after clear warning.",
  11085: "O my people, give full measure and weight with justice. Do not deprive people of what is rightfully theirs, nor spread corruption upon the earth.",
  11086: "What remains lawfully from Allāh is best for you [24], if you are true believers. And I am not a keeper over you.\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. “What remains lawfully from Allāh” refers to the honest and permissible profit that remains after fulfilling people’s rights—by giving full measure and weight and avoiding fraud, usury, and injustice. Although such lawful gain may appear small when compared to dishonest earnings, it is far better and more blessed. It brings peace of conscience, lasting benefit, and divine approval. This phrase reminds believers that true provision comes from Allāh, and that trusting Him by adhering to what is lawful leads to real prosperity in this life and in the Hereafter. Lawful earnings, even if modest, are purer and more enduring than wealth acquired through deception or wrongdoing.",
  11087: "They said, “O Shuʿayb, does your prayer order you that we should abandon what our forefathers worshipped, or refrain from doing whatever we wish with our wealth? Surely you are the forbearing, the right-minded!” [25]\\n\\nFootnotes: [25]. This statement was made mockingly and sarcastically, not as genuine praise. By calling Shuʿayb “forbearing” and “right-minded,” they intended the opposite—ridiculing him as foolish and misguided for prohibiting their idolatry and dishonest dealings.",
  11088: "He said, “O my people, have you considered: if I stand upon clear proof from my Lord, and He has provided me with good provision from Himself? I do not wish to do behind your backs what I forbid you from doing. I seek nothing but reform to the best of my ability [26]. My success comes only from Allāh—upon Him I rely, and to Him I turn in repentance.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. Reform (iṣlāḥ) means restoring what has been corrupted—morally, socially, and spiritually—by bringing it back into harmony with Allāh’s guidance. True reform is not driven by power, personal gain, or self-righteousness, but by sincere intention (ikhlāṣ) and obedience to Allāh. The prophets consistently emphasized that their call to reform was accompanied by integrity in conduct: they practiced what they preached and sought no benefit for themselves. Sincerity ensures that reform is pursued with humility, patience, and reliance upon Allāh, recognizing that success does not come from human effort alone, but from divine assistance. Without sincerity, calls for reform become hypocrisy; with sincerity, even limited efforts are blessed and accepted by Allāh.",
  11089: "O my people, do not let your opposition to me lead you into sin, lest there befall you what befell the people of Noah, or the people of Hūd, or the people of Ṣāliḥ. And the people of Lot are not far from you.",
  11090: "Seek forgiveness from your Lord, then turn to Him in repentance. Indeed, my Lord is Most Merciful, Most Affectionate. [27]\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]. “Most Merciful, Most Affectionate” translates two distinct divine attributes: al-Raḥīm and al-Wadūd. Al-Raḥīm emphasizes Allāh’s mercy expressed through forgiveness, care, and compassion toward His servants—especially those who repent and return to Him. Al-Wadūd conveys a deeper meaning of loving kindness and affection: Allāh not only forgives, but lovingly accepts and draws near to those who turn back to Him sincerely. Together, these names affirm that repentance is not met with harshness or rejection, but with mercy, acceptance, and divine love.\nAllāh manifests His love and affection in countless ways: in the sun that rises each morning, in the song of birds, in the sweetness of water that quenches thirst, in the beauty of colors and changing seasons, and in every act of mercy, forgiveness, generosity, hope, and peace. Above all, He revealed His greatest sign of love by sending His final Messenger—the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ—as a mercy to all creation.\nTrue love for Allāh and His Messenger is steadfast and enduring. It does not fade, fall silent, or surrender. As Scripture says, many waters cannot quench love, nor can rivers sweep it away. Every trace of love that endures within us is but a faint reflection of His boundless love for His creation—for Allāh alone is the eternal source of all love that is pure, unwavering, and true.",
  11091: "They said, “O Shuʿayb, we do not understand much of what you say, and indeed we see you as weak among us. Were it not for your clan, we would certainly have stoned you. For you hold no power among us.”",
  11092: "He said, “O my people, is my clan more esteemed by you than Allāh, that you have cast Him behind your backs in neglect? Indeed, my Lord encompasses all that you do.”",
  11093: "O my people, carry on in your own way; indeed, I too am carrying on. Soon you will know who will be visited by a punishment that will disgrace him, and who is the liar. So watch; indeed, I am watching with you.",
  11094: "When Our command came, We saved Shuʿayb and those who believed with him by a mercy from Us. But the mighty blast seized those who had wronged, and they lay lifeless within their homes,",
  11095: "as though they had never lived or prospered there. So away with Midian, just as it was with Thamūd.",
  11096: "Indeed, We sent Moses with Our signs and a manifest authority.",
  11097: "To Pharaoh and his chiefs; but they followed Pharaoh’s command—and Pharaoh’s command was not rightly guided.",
  11098: "He will precede his people on the Day of Resurrection, leading them into the Fire—how miserable indeed is the place to which they are led.",
  11099: "They were pursued by a curse in this world, and on the Day of Resurrection as well. How wretched is the cursed gift they are given!",
  11100: "These are some of the stories of the towns that We relate to you, [O Prophet]—some are still standing, and others have been harvested.",
  11101: "We did not wrong them, but they wronged themselves. So their gods—those whom they invoked besides Allāh —were of no avail to them when the command of your Lord came. And they did not increase them in anything except ruin.",
  11102: "Such is your Lord’s seizure when He seizes the towns while they persist in wrongdoing. Truly, His seizure is painful and severe.",
  11103: "Indeed, in this is a sign for whoever fears the punishment of the Hereafter. That is a Day for which all people will be gathered, and a Day that will be witnessed.",
  11104: "We do not delay it except for an appointed term.",
  11105: "When that Day comes, no soul will speak except by His permission. Some among them will be wretched, and others blessed.",
  11106: "As for those who are wretched, they will be in the Fire; for them there will be sighing and groaning within it.",
  11107: "They will abide therein as long as the heavens and the earth endure —except as your Lord wills [28]. Indeed, your Lord is the All-Accomplisher of whatever He wills.\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. “Except as your Lord wills” refers to the divine exception understood by many scholars as God’s will to bring out the disobedient among the monotheists from the Fire after a period of their remaining in it, and to admit them into Paradise by His mercy. This exception does not negate the permanence of punishment for the unbelievers, but affirms God’s absolute sovereignty and freedom to act as He wills.",
  11108: "As for those who are blessed [on that Day], they will be in Paradise; abiding therein as long as the heavens and the earth endure, except as your Lord wills [29]—a gift unceasing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. “Except as your Lord wills” indicates an exception for those sinful believers who may enter Hellfire for a time because of their sins. After this period, Allāh—by His mercy—will bring them out of the Fire and admit them into Paradise. This shows that entry into Paradise for the believers is ultimately a lasting and unending gift, even if some are first punished before being forgiven.",
  11109: "So do not be in doubt as to what these [pagans] worship. They worship nothing except what their forefathers worshiped before. And indeed, We will surely pay them in full their due share, undiminished.",
  11110: "We certainly gave Moses the Scripture, but differences arose therein. Had a Word not preceded from your Lord, the matter would have been judged between them. And indeed, they are in a disquieting doubt about it [this Qur’an].",
  11111: "Indeed, to each, your Lord will surely repay all their deeds in full. Indeed, He is All-Aware of what they do.",
  11112: "So remain steadfast upon the right path as you have been commanded—along with those who have turned in repentance with you—and do not transgress. Indeed, He is All-Seeing of whatever you do.",
  11113: "Do not incline toward the wrongdoers, lest the Fire touch you. You have no protectors besides Allāh; then you will not be helped.",
  11114: "Establish prayer at the two ends of the day [30], and during the early hours of the night [31]. Indeed, good deeds do away with misdeeds. That is a reminder for those who are mindful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. “The two ends of the day” is understood by many scholars to refer to the morning, the noon, and afternoon prayers (Fajr, Duhr and ‘Asr), or more broadly to the daytime prescribed prayers, marking the beginning and close of the day in worship.\n[31]. “The early hours of the night” refers to the night prayers, especially Maghrib and ‘Ishā’, and may also include additional voluntary prayers offered shortly after Ishā.",
  11115: "Be patient, for indeed Allāh does not let the reward of those who do good go to waste.",
  11116: "So why were there not, among the generations before you, people of a remnant of good sense—those who forbade corruption in the land—except for a few whom We saved among them? But the wrongdoers pursued the luxuries they were given therein, and they were wicked.",
  11117: "And your Lord would never destroy the towns unjustly, while their people were acting righteously [and striving to set things right].",
  11118: "If your Lord had willed, He could have made all mankind a single community—but they will not cease to differ.",
  11119: "Except for those upon whom your Lord has bestowed mercy [32]—and for that He created them [33]. And the Word of your Lord will be fulfilled: “I will certainly fill Hell with jinn and humans all together.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [32]. “Except for those upon whom your Lord has bestowed mercy” refers to those whom Allāh guides to Islam and favors with faith and righteousness. Many scholars explain that, although people differ in belief and conduct, those who receive Allāh’s mercy are guided to single Him out in worship and to turn away from associating any equals or partners with Him. They are thus excepted from blameworthy division, for they are directed to the truth and the Oneness of God. Allāh’s mercy here includes guidance, forgiveness, and salvation, all granted by His will and wisdom.\n[33]. This difference among people exists so that they may be tested, to make clear who chooses the path of guidance and who chooses the path of misguidance. Through this test, people are held accountable for their beliefs and actions, and each person’s choice becomes evident according to Allāh’s wisdom and justice.",
  11120: "Each of these stories of the messengers We relate to you [O Prophet] to strengthen your heart. In this there has come to you the truth, and an admonition, and a reminder for the believers.",
  11121: "Say [O Prophet] to those who do not believe, “Carry on with your course; indeed, we are doing so as well.",
  11122: "And wait; indeed, we too are waiting.\"",
  11123: "To Allāh belongs the unseen of the heavens and the earth, and to Him all matters are returned entirely. So worship Him alone, and put your trust in Him—for your Lord is not unaware of what you do.",
  12001: "Alif Lām Rā. [1]\nThese are the verses of the clear Book. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2: verse 1.\n[2]. The Clear Book is the Qur’an: a revelation whose verses are exact in language, perfect in judgment, and whole in meaning. Its truth stands manifest, illuminating what is unclear and bringing clarity to all that is uncertain or hidden.",
  12002: "Indeed, We have sent it down as an Arabic Qur’an—complete and coherent—so that you may understand.",
  12003: "We relate to you [O Prophet] the best of stories through Our revelation of this Qur’an, though before it you were unaware of them.",
  12004: "When Joseph said to his father, “O my father, indeed I saw eleven stars, and the sun and the moon—I saw them prostrating to me.”",
  12005: "He said, “O my son, do not relate your vision to your brothers, lest they devise a plot against you. Indeed, Satan is a clear enemy to humankind.”",
  12006: "Thus will your Lord choose you, teach you the interpretation of dreams and events, and complete His favor upon you and upon the family of Jacob—just as He completed it before upon your forefathers Abraham and Isaac. Indeed, your Lord is All-Knowing, All-Wise.",
  12007: "Indeed, in the story of Joseph and his brothers there are lessons for those who inquire.",
  12008: "When they said [to one another], “Joseph and his brother are more beloved to our father than we are, even though we are a group. Indeed, our father is in clear error.”",
  12009: "“Kill Joseph, or cast him away to another land, so that your father’s full attention may be devoted to you alone, and afterward you may become righteous people.”",
  12010: "One of them said, “Do not kill Joseph. Rather, cast him into the depths of the well [3], so that a passing caravan may pick him up—if you are going to do anything.” [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. The Qur’an does not identify which of Joseph’s brothers made this proposal. Muslim exegetes generally describe him as the most restrained among them, seeking to avert murder while still appeasing the group. Some later commentators—drawing on biblical tradition—identify him as Reuben, the eldest brother, though this identification is extraneous to the Qur’anic text and not required for its interpretation. The verse emphasizes moral gradation among the brothers, rather than individual naming.\n[4]. In the Torah narrative, Reuben proposes casting Joseph into the pit in order to spare his life, while Judah later proposes selling him to passing traders.",
  12011: "Joseph’s brothers said—after agreeing to remove him—“O our father, why do you not trust us with Joseph, while we are surely sincere toward him?”",
  12012: "Send him with us tomorrow so that he may enjoy himself and play. We will surely protect him.",
  12013: "Jacob said, “Indeed, it grieves me that you should take him away, and I fear that a wolf might devour him while you are heedless of him.”",
  12014: "They said, “If a wolf were to eat him while we are a strong group, then we would surely be losers!”",
  12015: "Then when they took him away and agreed together to place him into the depths of the well, We inspired him: “You will surely inform them of this affair of theirs, while they are unaware.”",
  12016: "Joseph’s brothers came to their father at nightfall, weeping.",
  12017: "They said, “Father, we went off racing one another and left Joseph with our belongings, and a wolf ate him—but you will not believe us, even if we are truthful.”",
  12018: "They brought his shirt, stained with false blood [5]. Jacob said, “No—your souls have enticed you into a matter. So beautiful patience is most fitting. Allāh is the One whose help is sought against what you describe.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The shirt functions as a recurring symbol in the Joseph narrative. Here it is presented stained with “false blood”, signaling deliberate deception rather than mere error. Scholars note that the intact shirt itself exposes the brothers’ lie, since it shows no sign of being torn by an animal. The image sets side by side outward evidence with inner falsehood, a theme that recurs later in the sūrah when Joseph’s shirt again serves as decisive proof—this time in the establishment of truth rather than its concealment.",
  12019: "A caravan came by, and they sent their water-drawer. He lowered his bucket into the well and exclaimed, “Oh, what good news! Here is a boy!” They concealed him as merchandise, but Allāh is All-Knowing of all that they do.",
  12020: "They sold him for a low price, a few silver coins, and they were indifferent toward him. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. The phrase “they were indifferent toward him” indicates that Joseph was sold for a pittance—whether by his brothers to the caravan, or by the wayfarers later in Egypt—by those who were unaware of his true worth. The expression conveys deliberate lack of concern rather than mere ignorance. Exegetes explain that the sellers regarded Joseph as expendable, either out of fear of discovery or because they wished to be rid of him quickly. The verse thus underscores the striking contrast that one chosen for divine favor is undervalued by those blind to his worth, a theme that recurs throughout the narrative.",
  12021: "The man in Egypt [7] who bought him said to his wife, “Make his stay honorable. Perhaps he may be of benefit to us, or we may adopt him as a son.” Thus We established Joseph in the land, so that We might teach him the interpretation of discourses and dreams. Allāh is predominant over His affair, but most people do not know.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. The Qur’an refers to Joseph’s purchaser simply as “the man in Egypt”, later described by the title al-ʿAzīz, a high official or chief administrator. The Qur’anic text does not name him. In the Torah narrative, he is identified as Potiphar, an officer of Pharaoh, but this name is extraneous to the Qur’anic account and is not required for its interpretation. The Qur’an’s restraint shifts attention away from individual identity toward divine agency, emphasizing that Joseph’s establishment in Egypt unfolds according to Allah’s purpose rather than human design.",
  12022: "When he reached his prime, We granted him sound judgment and knowledge. Thus do We reward the doers of good.",
  12023: "But the woman [8] in whose house he was sought to seduce him. She locked the doors firmly and said, “Come to me!” He said, “God forbid! Truly, He is my Lord, who has made my stay excellent. Indeed, the wrongdoers will never prosper.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. The Qur’an does not name the woman, referring to her only as “the woman in whose house he was.” She is later identified by her social position as the wife of al-ʿAzīz, a high-ranking Egyptian official. Extra-Qur’anic tradition, drawing on the Torah and later narrative literature, names her Zuleikha, but this name is absent from the Qur’anic text. The Qur’an’s restraint keeps the focus on moral testing and personal integrity, rather than on individual characterization.",
  12024: "She certainly resolved to seduce him, and he would have inclined to her, had he not seen the proof of his Lord. Thus it was, so that We might avert from him evil and indecency. Indeed, he was among Our sincere servants.",
  12025: "They both raced to the door, and she tore his shirt from behind [9]. They found her master [i.e., her husband] at the door. She said, “What should be the punishment for one who intended evil toward your wife, except that he be imprisoned or suffer a painful punishment?”\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. That Joseph’s shirt was torn from behind serves as concrete evidence of the encounter. It signifies that Joseph was fleeing from sin while the woman pursued him, reversing her accusation. The shirt thus operates as a recurring narrative symbol in the sūrah, transforming from an instrument of deception earlier into a means of revealing truth.",
  12026: "Joseph said, “She was the one who tried to seduce me.” Then a member of her household testified: “If his shirt is torn from the front, she is telling the truth and he is lying.",
  12027: "But if his shirt is torn from behind, then she is lying and he is telling the truth.”",
  12028: "When he saw that Joseph’s shirt was torn from the back, he said, “This is clearly part of your scheming [O women]. Your scheming is truly formidable.”",
  12029: "Joseph, let this go. And you [O wife]—ask forgiveness for your sin; you are clearly in the wrong.",
  12030: "[Some] women in the city said, “The wife of al-ʿAzīz [10] is trying to seduce her young servant. Love for him has pierced her heart! Indeed, we see her to be clearly in the wrong.\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. i.e., Potiphar. al-ʿAzīz: Literally “the Mighty” or “the Powerful.” often interpreted as the treasurer or chief minister, traditionally understood as Joseph’s master. Unlike later Jewish and Christian traditions, the Qur’an does not provide his personal name.",
  12031: "When she heard of their gossip, she invited them, prepared a banquet for them, and gave each of them a knife. Then she said to Joseph, “Come out before them.”\nWhen they saw him, they were overwhelmed that they cut their hands in astonishment, exclaiming, “Glory be to Allāh! This is no human being; this is none but a noble angel.”",
  12032: "She said, “This is the one about whom you blamed me. I did seek to seduce him, but he resolutely resisted. If he does not do what I command, he will surely be imprisoned and utterly humiliated.”",
  12033: "He said, “My Lord, prison is dearer to me than what they are inviting me to. If You do not turn their scheming away from me, I might incline toward them and become among the ignorant.” [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. The term al-jāhilīn (lit. “the ignorant”) in Qur’anic usage often refers not merely to those lacking knowledge, but to those who act without moral restraint or spiritual awareness. Joseph’s prayer reflects his recognition of human vulnerability and his reliance upon divine protection.\nThis noble prophet, Joseph, did not rely on his own strength. Rather, he sought refuge in Allāh, recognizing that without divine protection even the most righteous can falter.\nMuhammad, the Messenger of God ﷺ, said: “I have not left behind me a temptation more harmful to men than women.”\nHe also said: “Beware of the world, and beware of the temptation of women.”\nThese statements are not condemnations of women, but warnings about human desire, reminding both men and women to preserve modesty and avoid situations that lead to sin.\nThe Prophet ﷺ also said:\n“No man should be alone with a woman who is not his maḥram, for whenever a man and a woman are alone together, the third among them is Satan.”\nBelievers are encouraged to strengthen their hearts through worship — prayer, fasting, and supplication. The Prophet ﷺ said:\n“O young men, whoever among you can afford marriage, let him marry; and whoever cannot, let him fast, for it will be a shield for him.”\nModern forms of temptation require equal vigilance. Screens, social media, and private messaging can become new forms of seclusion.\nAllāh elevates those who resist temptation. Joseph was imprisoned for choosing chastity, yet he was later raised to authority in Egypt and became a lasting model of sincerity.\nThe Prophet ﷺ said: “Whoever leaves something for the sake of Allāh, Allāh will replace it with something better.”",
  12034: "So his Lord answered him and turned their scheming away from him. Indeed, He alone is the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.",
  12035: "Then it appeared to them—after they had seen the signs of his innocence—that they would surely imprison him for a time. [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. Although the officials had recognized the clear signs of Joseph’s innocence, they chose imprisonment to suppress scandal and preserve social reputation. The phrase “for a time” suggests an unspecified but temporary detention, reflecting a decision driven more by political and social pressure than by justice.",
  12036: "Two young men entered the prison with him. One of them said, ‘I saw myself in a dream pressing wine.’ And the other said, ‘I saw myself in a dream carrying bread upon my head, from which birds were eating. Tell us its interpretation. Indeed, we consider you to be among those who do good.’",
  12037: "Joseph said, “No food comes to you that you are provided with except that I will inform you of its interpretation before it arrives. This is part of what my Lord has taught me [13]. Indeed, I have left the faith of a people who do not believe in Allāh and who disbelieve in the Hereafter.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. Joseph emphasizes that his ability to interpret dreams is not the result of human expertise but divine teaching. In the Qur’an, prophetic knowledge is presented as a gift from Allāh, granted to guide others toward faith and moral awareness. This statement also serves as an introduction to Joseph’s invitation to monotheistic belief.",
  12038: "I follow the faith of my forefathers — Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob. It is not right for us to associate anything with Allāh [14]. This is from Allāh’s favor upon us and upon humanity [15], yet most people are not grateful. [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. This verse explains the reason behind this great favor: guiding people to pure monotheism and worship of Allāh alone without associating partners with Him. It emphasizes that monotheistic guidance is both a special blessing granted to the prophets and a universal mercy intended for all humanity. In the Qur’an, divine guidance is portrayed as a gift extended to every people through prophetic teaching.\n[15]. This favor highlights that faith is an honor rather than a mere inheritance. Joseph inherited the message of his forefathers, not privilege; he lived its principles even while imprisoned. Pure monotheism remains the central message of every prophetic mission.\n[16]. That is, they are heedless of this great favour and do not believe and worship only Allāh and Him Alone.\nThe Qur’an frequently highlights human ingratitude as a moral and spiritual failing. Gratitude in Islam involves recognizing divine blessings and responding through faith, obedience, and ethical conduct.\nGratitude sustains faith. When people stop thanking Allāh, they easily fall into shirk (associating others with Allāh in worship).\nTrue gratitude is not in words only but in actions as well and in heart. The thankful heart sees faith itself as the greatest blessing.",
  12039: "O my two fellow prisoners, are many different lords better, or Allāh—the One, the All-Subduer? [17]\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. The verse presents a logical argument for monotheism by contrasting multiple competing deities with Allah’s absolute unity and sovereignty. The divine name “the All-Subduer” emphasizes Allāh’s complete authority over creation, highlighting the coherence and supremacy of monotheistic belief.\nEven in the darkness of prison, Prophet Joseph (peace be upon him) speaks with dignity, gentleness, and clarity of faith. He does not begin by condemning or rebuking his fellow prisoners for their beliefs; instead, he invites them to think rationally. His tone is friendly — “O my two fellow prisoners ” — showing empathy before presenting truth. This models the dawah (invitation) method of the prophets: kindness before correction.\nHe poses a question rather than a command: “Are many lords better, or Allāh —the One, the All-Subduer?”\nThis rhetorical question appeals to the intellect, not emotion. It asks: Can multiple, conflicting gods bring harmony, power, and justice? Or is it more rational to believe in One Lord, absolute in power, free of partners and equals?\nBy this simple question, Joseph exposes the absurdity of polytheism and points to the logical and spiritual peace found in tawḥīd — the oneness of God.\nAllāh alone has irresistible power and control over all creation. Even kings, rulers, and jailers—who seem mighty—are under His command. Joseph, though imprisoned, recognizes that true power lies not with those who hold the keys to cells, but with the One who controls the destiny of all.\nDespite his unjust imprisonment, Joseph’s heart is not filled with bitterness or despair; it is filled with da‘wah and purpose. He uses even his misfortune as an opportunity to guide others. This shows that a believer’s mission of spreading truth is not limited by circumstance — even hardship can become a platform for guidance.",
  12040: "You worship nothing besides Him except mere names that you have named — you and your forefathers—for which Allāh has sent down no authority. Judgment belongs to none but Allāh. He has commanded that you worship none but Him. That is the upright religion, but most people do not know. [18]\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. People’s ignorance often leads them to associate partners with Allāh in worship. The Qur’anic term “the upright religion” describes a faith founded upon pure monotheism and divine guidance. The verse contrasts authentic revelation with beliefs shaped by distortion, inherited superstition, and cultural tradition. It highlights a recurring Qur’anic theme: religious truth is determined by divine authority rather than social convention or ancestral practice.",
  12041: " Joseph said, “O my two fellow prisoners, as for one of you, he will serve wine to his master [19]; but as for the other, he will be crucified, and the birds will eat from his head. The matter about which you asked has been decided.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. That is the king.\nThe interpretation reveals that the dream of pressing wine symbolized restoration to royal service. In ancient Near Eastern royal courts, serving wine to a ruler was a position of trust and high responsibility, indicating that the prisoner would be pardoned and restored to his former status.",
  12042: "Then he said to the one he knew would be saved of the two, “Mention me to your master.” But Satan caused him to forget to mention him to his master, so Joseph remained in prison for several years.",
  12043: "The king said, “Indeed, I saw in a dream seven fat cows being eaten by seven lean ones, and seven green ears of grain and seven others dry. O nobles, tell me the interpretation of my dream, if you can interpret dreams.”",
  12044: "They said, “These are nothing but mixed dreams, and we do not know the interpretation of such dreams.” [20]\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. The phrase “confused dreams” describes dreams believed to be chaotic mixtures of thoughts without clear symbolic meaning. By dismissing the king’s dream in this way, the advisors reveal their lack of true interpretive knowledge, preparing the narrative for the divinely guided interpretation that follows.\nThis moment becomes a pivotal turning point in the story of Joseph. What appeared insignificant ultimately led to his rise to prominence, reflecting how divine wisdom often unfolds gradually through events that seem ordinary or accidental.",
  12045: "The one who was saved of the two young prisoners — and who remembered Joseph after a time—said, “I will inform you of its interpretation, so send me.”",
  12046: " He said, “Joseph, O man of truth [21], explain to us about seven fat cows being eaten by seven lean ones, and seven green ears of grain and seven others dry—so that I may return to the people and that they may know.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. The expression “man of truth” translates the Arabic al-ṣiddīq, describing one known for absolute honesty, integrity, and reliability. The title reflects Joseph’s reputation for moral excellence and truthful character.\nThis shows a complete reversal from Joseph’s forgotten years in prison — now, his name is remembered in the royal court with honor.\nThe cupbearer calls him the truthful — affirming that Joseph’s past interpretation was fulfilled exactly, proving his sincerity and connection to Allāh.\nThe same man who once neglected Joseph now seeks his wisdom.\nWhen Allāh wills to elevate His servant, He brings honor even from those who once forgot.\nJoseph doesn’t rush to demand freedom or vindication — he focuses solely on service, teaching, and revelation.\nHis wisdom will soon rescue not only Egypt, but his own family from famine.\nIt wasn’t wealth, status, or connections that restored Joseph’s name — it was his truthfulness.\nAllāh makes the truthful shine when the time is right. Truth may sleep in a prison, but it will awaken in a palace when Allāh decrees.",
  12047: "Joseph said, “You will sow for seven years continuously, and whatever you harvest—leave it in its ears, except for a small portion that you eat.”",
  12048: "Then after that will come seven difficult years which will consume what you have stored in advance for them, except for a little of what you preserve.",
  12049: "Then after that will come a year in which people will be granted relief with abundant rain, and in which they will press [olives and grapes]. [22]\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. The phrase “they will press” refers to extracting oil and juice from agricultural produce such as olives and grapes, symbolizing the return of agricultural prosperity following the years of famine.\nThese verses reveal a wise economic model: Preparation in prosperity, moderation in hardship, gratitude in relief.\nJoseph doesn’t merely interpret a dream — he delivers a national survival plan grounded in revelation and reason.\nThe transition from “they will consume” to “they will press” mirrors human spiritual renewal: just as the land revives after drought, the heart revives after patience and faith. Trust in Allāh includes using reason, planning, and strategy. Joseph's wisdom teaches us that reliance on Allāh never negates preparation. Allāh teaches us steadfastness in famine and gratitude in abundance.\nThe verse ends not in despair but in relief and abundance — a Qur’anic pattern of hope following adversity.\n“For indeed, with hardship comes ease.” — (Qur’an 94:6)",
  12050: "The king said, “Bring him to me [23].”\nBut when the messenger came to Joseph, he said, “Go back to your master and ask him: What is the case of the women who cut their hands? Indeed, my Lord is All-Knowing of their scheming.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. To highlight the remarkable moral strength of Joseph, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said, out of humility and self-effacement despite being the greatest of the prophets:\n“Had I remained in prison as long as Joseph did, I would have answered the caller.”\nThis statement emphasizes the extraordinary patience and integrity demonstrated by Joseph. While most people in such a desperate situation would rush to accept immediate release, Joseph chose to secure his freedom only after his innocence was publicly established, thereby preserving his honor and reputation.",
  12051: "The king said, “What was your affair when you sought to seduce Joseph?” They said, “Allāh forbid [24]! We knew nothing improper about him.” The chief minister’s wife said, “Now the truth has come to light. It was I who sought to seduce him, and indeed he is truthful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. The phrase ḥāsha li-llāh is an exclamation expressing astonishment and the absolute rejection of wrongdoing or the idea that Joseph could be regarded as accused. In this context, it reflects the women’s acknowledgment of Joseph’s innocence and moral integrity, reinforcing the public vindication he sought before accepting release from prison.",
  12052: "That was so he would know that I did not betray him in secret, and that Allāh does not guide the schemes of the treacherous.”",
  12053: "Yet I do not declare myself free [from blame]; for indeed the soul prompts to evil—except those upon whom my Lord has mercy. Indeed, my Lord is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  12054: "The king said, \"Bring him to me; I will appoint him exclusively for myself [25].\" Then, when he spoke to him, he said, \"Indeed, you are today in a highly esteemed position with us and fully trusted.\"\\n\\nFootnotes: [25]. The king’s statement indicates his intention to elevate Joseph to a position of personal trust and close advisory authority, recognizing his innocence, his great trustworthiness, and his noble character. In ancient royal courts, such personal appointment signified exceptional confidence and marked Joseph’s transition from imprisonment to political authority and leadership.",
  12055: "Joseph said, “Appoint me over the storehouses of the land [26]; indeed, I am a trustworthy guardian and knowledgeable.”\n[27]\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. The phrase “storehouses of the land” refers to the management of Egypt’s agricultural reserves and economic resources. Joseph requests responsibility for overseeing the storage and distribution of grain during the predicted years of famine, reflecting administrative competence and long-term strategic planning.\n[27]. The Arabic expression ḥafīẓ ʿalīm combines moral integrity with professional expertise. Ḥafīẓ denotes reliability, trustworthiness, and careful guardianship, while ʿalīm indicates knowledge, skill, and administrative competence. Together, they highlight that effective leadership requires both ethical character and practical capability—qualities especially essential for one entrusted with managing public wealth and resources.",
  12056: "Thus We established Joseph in the land, to settle therein wherever he willed. We bestow Our mercy upon whom We will, and We never allow the reward of those who do good to be lost. [28]\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. Allāh presents in this verse a lesson drawn from the life of Joseph: patience combined with righteousness leads to divine establishment. What Joseph lost through injustice, Allāh restored with honor and authority. Every believer who endures wrongdoing with integrity shares in this divine principle.\nThe verse emphasizes a central Qur’anic theme: sincere righteousness and moral excellence are never lost in the sight of Allāh. Although reward may be delayed, divine justice ensures that acts of integrity, patience, and faith ultimately receive their rightful outcome.",
  12057: "But the reward of the Hereafter is far better [29] for those who believe and are mindful of Allāh.\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. This verse concludes the rise of Joseph, reminding believers that no matter how great worldly success may be, it remains insignificant compared to the reward of the Hereafter. It redirects hearts from material triumph to spiritual permanence—from the fleeting splendor of this world to the everlasting gardens of eternity.\nEarthly blessings are manifestations of divine mercy, but the reward of the Hereafter is the true fruit of faith and righteousness. Believers are therefore encouraged to appreciate divine favors in this life while keeping their focus firmly upon the Hereafter, with gratitude, humility, and mindful devotion.",
  12058: "The brothers of Joseph came [30] then entered upon him, and he recognized them, while they did not recognize him.\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. Joseph’s brothers came to Egypt seeking grain during the years of famine. Having last seen Joseph as a young boy, they failed to recognize him after his rise to political authority, while Joseph immediately recognized them. The moment introduces a powerful dramatic contrast that prepares the unfolding reconciliation narrative.",
  12059: "When he had furnished them with their rations, Joseph said, “Bring me a brother of yours from your father. Do you not see that I give full measure and that I am the best of hosts?”",
  12060: "But if you do not bring him to me, there will be no more measure [of grain] for you from me, nor will you come near me. [31]\\n\\nFootnotes: [31]. Joseph places a firm condition on future provisions, requiring his brothers to bring their youngest brother, Benjamin, on their next visit. The condition serves both administrative and personal purposes, forming part of Joseph’s measured test to examine their sincerity and to facilitate reunion with his full brother.\nJoseph links continued provision to Benjamin’s return, pressing his brothers to demonstrate their truthfulness and responsibility. The tone is firm yet practical—a ruler safeguarding scarce resources while enforcing a condition necessary to uncover past wrongdoing. The episode illustrates a balance between mercy, as Joseph had already supplied them generously, and accountability, showing that wise leadership often requires clear limits to ensure justice and eventual reconciliation.",
  12061: "They said, “We will persuade his father to let him go with us, and we will surely do so.”",
  12062: "Joseph said to his servants, “Place their merchandise back into their saddlebags, so that they may know upon their return to their family [that we did not purchase it from them] — perhaps they will return.”",
  12063: "When they returned to their father, they said, “O our father, further measure [of grain] has been denied to us [unless we take Benjamin with us]. So send our brother with us, that we may receive our measure, and we will surely guard him.”",
  12064: "Jacob said, “Shall I entrust him to you except as I entrusted you with his brother, Joseph, before? But Allāh is the best Guardian, and He is the Most Merciful of the merciful.”",
  12065: "When they opened their saddlebags, they found their merchandise had been returned to them. They said, “O our father! What more could we desire? This is our merchandise returned to us. We can bring provisions for our family, guard our brother well, and receive an extra camel-load of grain. That is an easy measure.”",
  12066: "Jacob said, “I will never send him with you until you give me a solemn pledge before Allāh that you will surely bring him back to me, unless you are surrounded and overpowered.” When they gave him their solemn pledge, he said, “Allāh is Witness over what we say.”",
  12067: "And Jacob said, ‘O my sons, do not enter through one gate, but enter through different gates. Yet I cannot avail you in anything against Allāh. Judgment belongs only to Allāh. Upon Him I rely and place my trust, and upon Him let those who trust place their trust.’",
  12068: "When they entered as their father had instructed them, it did not avail them against Allāh at all; it only fulfilled a need within Jacob’s heart [32]. And indeed, he was endowed with knowledge because of what We had taught him, but most people do not know.\\n\\nFootnotes: [32]. That is, Jacob instructed them to enter through different gates out of concern for their safety and to avoid attracting envy or the evil eye; however, this precaution could not avert anything decreed by Allāh.",
  12069: "When they entered upon Joseph, he drew his brother close to himself and said, ‘Indeed, I am your brother, so do not grieve over what they used to do.’",
  12070: "When Joseph had furnished them with their provisions, he placed the measuring bowl [33] in his brother’s saddlebag.Then a caller called out, ‘O caravan! You are surely thieves!’\\n\\nFootnotes: [33]. The bowl was a royal measuring vessel used for distributing grain and may also have served as a drinking cup.",
  12071: "They said, turning toward them, “What is it that you are missing?”",
  12072: "They said, “We are missing the king’s measuring bowl, and whoever brings it will have a camel-load [of grain]; and I stand surety for it.”",
  12073: "They said, “By Allāh, you already know that we did not come to spread corruption in the land, and we are not thieves.”",
  12074: "What then shall be the penalty for the one who did it, if you are lying?",
  12075: "They said, “The penalty for this is that the one in whose bag it is found shall himself be the recompense for it [34]. Thus do we recompense the wrongdoers.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [34]. That is, the offender would be placed in servitude to the one from whom he had stolen. The brothers describe a penalty consistent with their own customary law, in which theft was punished by detention or servitude rather than corporal punishment. By articulating this rule themselves, they unknowingly establish the legal basis for what follows, demonstrating how divine wisdom operates through existing human conventions without injustice.",
  12076: "So he began searching their bags before his brother’s bag; then he brought it out from his brother’s bag. Thus did We plan for Joseph; he could not have taken his brother under the king’s law unless Allāh so willed. We raise in degrees whom We will, but above every possessor of knowledge is the All-Knowing One. [35]\\n\\nFootnotes: [35]. This verse affirms a central Qur’anic principle: while human beings may possess knowledge and authority, all such knowledge remains limited and subordinate to divine wisdom. Joseph’s plan succeeded not through deception or injustice, but through divine permission operating within existing legal frameworks. The closing statement reminds readers that no matter how learned a person may be, ultimate knowledge belongs to Allāh alone.",
  12077: "They said, “If he steals, then a brother of his stole before him [36].” But Joseph kept it within himself and did not reveal it to them. He said [inwardly], “You are in a worse position, and Allāh knows best what you describe.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [36]. The brothers allude to a past allegation against Joseph, reviving an old claim to deflect blame from themselves and diminish Benjamin’s perceived innocence. Some early reports mention that Joseph, while young boy, took a golden idol belonging to his maternal grandfather and broke it in order to prevent its worship. This act, rooted in devotion to monotheism, was later misconstrued as theft. The brothers’ remark thus reflects moral projection and lingering guilt rather than established wrongdoing. Joseph’s decision to remain silent demonstrates restraint, wisdom, and trust in divine justice.",
  12078: "They said, “O chief minister, he has an elderly and vulnerable father, so take one of us in his place. Indeed, we see you as a doer of good.”",
  12079: "Joseph said, “Allāh forbid that we should take anyone except the one in whose possession we found our property; for then we would surely be unjust.”",
  12080: "Then, when they despaired of him, they withdrew to confer privately. The eldest of them [37] said, “Do you not know that your father has taken from you a solemn pledge by Allāh? And before that, you failed in your duty regarding Joseph. So I will not leave this land until my father permits me, or Allāh decides for me—and He is the best of judges.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [37]. The “eldest” refers to the brother who assumes moral leadership at this moment. Although he is not named in the Qur’an, many early exegetes identify him as Reuben (Rūbīl), the eldest son of Jacob. He is portrayed here as the most conscientious among the brothers, openly acknowledging their past failure regarding Joseph and refusing to abandon responsibility. His stance reflects emerging repentance and a decisive shift from earlier deception toward accountability and submission to divine judgment.",
  12081: "Go back to your father and say, “O our father, your son has committed theft. We only testify to what we know, and we were not aware of the unseen.”",
  12082: "Ask [the people of] the town in which we were, and the caravan with which we came; and indeed, we are telling the truth.",
  12083: "Jacob said, “Rather, your souls have enticed you to a matter. So beautiful patience is most fitting. Perhaps Allāh will bring them all back to me. Indeed, He alone is the All-Knowing, the All-Wise.”",
  12084: "He turned away from them and said, “O my sorrow for Joseph!” And his eyes became white from grief, while he was suppressing his anguish.",
  12085: "They said, ‘ByAllāhh, you will never cease remembering Joseph until you waste away with illness or perish.’",
  12086: "Jacob said, “I only complain of my anguish and sorrow to Allāh, and I know from Allāh what you do not know.”",
  12087: "O my sons, go and search diligently for Joseph and his brother, and do not despair of Allah’s mercy. Indeed, none despairs of Allah’s mercy except a disbelieving people.",
  12088: "So when they entered upon Joseph, they said, “O chief minister, hardship has afflicted us and our family, and we have come with merchandise of little value. So give us full measure [of grain] and be charitable to us. Indeed, Allāh rewards the charitable.”",
  12089: "Joseph said, “Do you realize what you did to Joseph and his brother when you were ignorant?”",
  12090: "They said, “Is it really you, Joseph?”\nHe said, “I am Joseph, and this is my brother. Allāh has indeed been gracious to us [38]. Indeed, whoever fears Allāh and is patient—Allāh does not allow the reward of the doers of good to go to waste.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [38]. Joseph points out that both he and his brother experienced a complete reversal of fortune after enduring oppression. He attributes his reunion, honor, and relief entirely to Allāh’s grace, not to his own intelligence or endurance. After years of betrayal, enslavement, false accusation, and imprisonment, his first response is gratitude rather than reproach. The statement affirms a central Qur’anic principle: true success is not the result of worldly power or personal merit alone, but a gift from Allāh granted to those who combine faith (taqwā) with patience (ṣabr).",
  12091: "They said, “By Allāh, Allāh has indeed favored you over us [39], and we have surely been sinners.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [39]. The brothers openly acknowledge that Allāh has favored Joseph over them, not merely in worldly position, but in moral character, patience, and divine selection. This admission marks the collapse of their earlier envy and denial and represents their first unqualified confession of wrongdoing. The favor they recognize is shown to be a result of Joseph’s faith, steadfastness, and trust in Allāh rather than lineage or power. The verse highlights a core Qur’anic principle: true honor and elevation come from righteousness and divine grace, not from rivalry or human schemes.",
  12092: "He said, \"No blame will be upon you today [40]. May Allāh forgive you, and He is the Most Merciful of the merciful.\"\\n\\nFootnotes: [40]. Joseph’s declaration, “No blame will be upon you today,” signifies complete absolution—without reproach, retaliation, or humiliation. It marks the emotional and moral climax of the narrative and reflects prophetic mercy at its highest expression. Joseph does not assert personal superiority or demand apology; rather, he entrusts judgment and forgiveness to Allāh, praying for their pardon. This moment illustrates a central Qur’anic ethic: true forgiveness arises from strength, faith, and moral clarity, not from weakness. Reconciliation reaches its perfection when mercy is coupled with supplication for divine forgiveness.\nThis scene also prefigures the example of Muhammad ﷺ, who—centuries later—uttered the very same words when he forgave the Quraysh tribe at the Conquest of Makkah: 'No blame will be upon you today'. Just as Joseph forgave his brothers at the height of his power, so too did the Prophet ﷺ forgive his enemies when he held absolute victory.\nThe Qur’an thus teaches that true power lies not in revenge, but in mercy inspired by faith. The truly strong are those who forgive when they are fully able to punish. Like Joseph and the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, greatness is measured by restraint, compassion, and trust in Allāh. Forgiveness heals generational pain: Joseph’s words bring an end to decades of family guilt, restoring trust and unity.\nMercy invites divine mercy. As the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ taught: “Show mercy to others, and the Most Merciful will show mercy to you.” Joseph’s forgiveness was not weakness—it was the perfection of strength. He remembered neither the pit nor the prison, but Allāh’s purpose through them all. When the heart is filled with faith, even wounds become doors to mercy.",
  12093: "Take this shirt of mine and cast it over my father’s face—he will regain his sight. Then bring to me your family, all together.”",
  12094: "When the caravan departed [from Egypt], Jacob said, “Indeed, I sense the scent of Joseph—if only you would not think me senile.”",
  12095: "They said, “By Allāh, you are certainly still in your old delusion.”",
  12096: "Then, when the bearer of glad tidings came, he cast the shirt over Jacob’s face, and he regained his sight. Jacob said, “Did I not tell you that I know from Allāh what you do not know?”",
  12097: "They said, “O our father, ask forgiveness for our sins—we have indeed been sinners.”",
  12098: "Jacob said, “I will ask forgiveness for you from Allāh, my Lord. Indeed, He alone is the All-Forgiving, the Most Merciful.”",
  12099: "When they entered upon Joseph, he drew his parents close to himself and said, “Enter Egypt, if Allāh wills, safe and secure.”",
  12100: "Joseph raised his parents upon the throne, and they all fell down before him in prostration [41]. And he said, “O my father, this is the interpretation of my dream of long ago. My Lord has indeed made it come true. He was truly kind to me when He brought me out of prison and brought you all from the desert [42], after Satan had sown enmity between me and my brothers. Surely, my Lord is Most Subtle in what He wills. Indeed, He is the All-Knowing, the All-Wise.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [41]. The prostration was a gesture of honor and respect customary at that time, not an act of worship. Worship in Islam is due to Allāh alone. This moment fulfills Joseph’s childhood dream (12:4), in which the sun, moon, and stars symbolized his parents and brothers. The prostration signifies acknowledgment of Joseph’s God-given position and the completion of divine promise.\n[42]. “The desert” refers to the pastoral region of Canaan, where Jacob and his family lived as shepherds, in contrast to the settled and politically powerful life of Egypt. Joseph attributes the family’s reconciliation entirely to Allāh’s kindness, deliberately minimizing human blame by noting Satan’s role in stirring enmity. This reflects prophetic humility and a Qur’anic ethic of forgiveness that prioritizes healing over recrimination.\nThroughout revelation, the desert appears as a place of divine preparation. Many prophets—including Abraham, Ishmael, Moses, Jesus, and Muhammad ﷺ—experienced formative periods away from worldly security. In the desert, reliance upon Allāh is stripped of distractions and purified of illusion. It is there that trust is refined, patience is learned, and faith is strengthened. True safety, therefore, is not determined by location, but by reliance upon Allāh—who remains with His servants wherever they may be.",
  12101: "[Joseph said], “My Lord, You have indeed granted me authority and taught me the interpretation of discourses and dreams. O Originator of the heavens and the earth, You are my Protector in this world and the Hereafter. Let me die in submission to You [as a Muslim] and join me with the righteous.” [43]\\n\\nFootnotes: [43]. Joseph’s supplication comes at the height of his worldly success, revealing profound spiritual insight. Rather than asking for continued power, longevity, or prosperity, he asks for a righteous end—to die in submission to Allāh and to be joined with the righteous. By “the righteous,” Joseph refers to his forefathers—such as Abraham and Isaac—as well as all prophets and messengers who lived in faith and obedience.\nThis reflects a core Qur’anic principle: true success is not measured by authority or achievement, but by faithfulness at life’s end. The prayer also affirms that Islam—submission to Allāh—was the faith of all prophets, long before the formal establishment of later religious communities. Joseph’s request demonstrates humility, gratitude, and a deep awareness that the greatest honor is a good ending (ḥusn al-khātimah)—to finish life well—rather than worldly rank or power.",
  12102: "That is from the news of the unseen which We reveal to you, O Muhammad. You were not with them when they agreed upon their decision, while they were plotting.",
  12103: "But most of mankind—no matter how eagerly you desire it—will not be believers.",
  12104: "You ask them for no reward for it. It is only a reminder to all the worlds.",
  12105: "How many a sign there are in the heavens and the earth which they pass by, while they turn away from them.",
  12106: "And most of them do not believe in Allāh except while associating others with Him in worship.",
  12107: "Do they then feel secure that an overwhelming punishment from Allāh will not come upon them, or that the Hour will not come upon them suddenly while they are unaware?",
  12108: "Say, “This is my way. I call to Allāh with clear insight—I and those who follow me [44]. Glory be to Allāh, and I am not of the polytheists.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [44]. “Those who follow me” refers to all who sincerely adhere to the Prophet’s path by calling to Allāh upon knowledge, clarity, and conviction—not blind imitation, emotion, or conjecture. The phrase “with clear insight” emphasizes that true daʿwah is grounded in sound understanding, evidence, and wisdom. This verse establishes that the mission of Prophet Muhammad ﷺ is shared, in principle, by his true followers: to invite others to Allāh with sincerity, humility, and unwavering commitment to pure monotheism. It also affirms that calling to Allāh is itself an act of worship that must be rooted in knowledge and free from all forms of idolatry. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Whoever guides others to goodness will have a reward similar to that of those who act upon it.” This highlights the immense virtue and responsibility of calling others to righteousness with wisdom and insight.",
  12109: "We did not send before you except men from among the people of the towns, to whom We revealed. Have they not traveled through the land and observed how was the end of those before them? And the Home of the Hereafter is far better for those who fear Allāh. Do you not then understand?",
  12110: "[They continued] Until, when the messengers despaired and thought that they had been utterly rejected, Our help came to them; then We saved whom We willed, and Our punishment is not averted from the guilty people.",
  12111: "In their stories there is certainly a lesson for those of understanding. It was never a narration fabricated; rather, it is a confirmation of what came before it [45], a detailed explanation of all things, and a guidance and mercy for a people who believe.\\n\\nFootnotes: [45]. “What came before it” refers to the divine revelations sent to earlier prophets, including the Torah, Psalms, and Gospel in their original, revealed forms. The Qur’an confirms their essential truths, shared monotheistic message, and moral teachings, while also clarifying, correcting distortions, and completing divine guidance. This verse affirms that the Qur’an is not fiction, legend, or borrowed narrative, but revealed truth—consistent with prior revelation and authoritative over it. Thus, the stories related in the Qur’an serve not merely as historical accounts, but as guidance, explanation, and mercy for those who believe and reflect.",
  13001: "Alif Lām Mīm Rā [1]. These are the verses of the Book. What has been sent down to you [O Prophet] from your Lord is the truth, yet most people do not believe.\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2: verse 1.",
  13002: "Allāh is the One Who raised the heavens without visible pillars [2], then rose over the Throne [3]. He subjected the sun and the moon, each running its course for an appointed term. He governs all affairs and makes the signs clear, so that you may be certain of meeting your Lord.\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. “Without visible pillars” highlights Allāh’s absolute power and wisdom in creation. The heavens are upheld not by material supports perceptible to human sight, but by divine command and decree. Some scholars noted that the phrase allows for the possibility of unseen supports known only to Allāh, while others understood it as a rhetorical affirmation that the cosmos stands solely by His will. In either case, the verse directs attention away from physical causation to recognition of the Creator’s sustaining authority.\n[3]. “He rose over the Throne” affirms Allāh’s supreme authority, dominion, and governance over all creation. This attribute is accepted as it appears in revelation—without likening Allāh to creation, without imagining modality or form, and without denial—following the methodology of the early Muslim scholars. The Throne represents the greatest of created entities, and Allāh’s rising over it signifies His absolute loftiness and transcendence, in a manner befitting His majesty.",
  13003: "He is the One Who spread out the earth, set upon it firm mountains and rivers, and made in it two pairs of every fruit. He covers the day with the night. Surely in this are signs for people who reflect. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. The purpose of these signs is to lead people to worship Allāh alone, without associating any partners with Him in worship. The verse invites reflection upon the signs of Allāh manifest in creation: the spreading of the earth, the stability provided by mountains, the life-giving flow of rivers, the pairing found in fruits, and the alternation of day and night. These phenomena point to deliberate design, balance, and wisdom—not randomness.\nThe Qur’an repeatedly emphasizes that such signs are not merely to be observed, but to be reflected upon, guiding the mind and heart toward recognition of the Creator, affirmation of His oneness, and certainty of His power and purpose. Only those who reflect thoughtfully perceive these signs as evidence of divine truth.",
  13004: "And on the earth are neighboring plots of land, and gardens of grapevines, crops, and date palms—some growing from a single root and others from separate roots. They are watered with the same water, yet We make some of them excel others in taste. Surely in this are signs for people who reflect and reason.",
  13005: "If you are astonished [5], then astonishing indeed is their saying: “When we have become dust, will we truly be brought into a new creation?” These are the ones who disbelieved in their Lord. They will have shackles around their necks, and they are the companions of the Fire, wherein they will remain forever.\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. This verse addresses the pagans who denied the Prophet’s message of Islamic monotheism and took besides Allāh idols that neither benefit nor harm. The verse employs rhetorical astonishment to reverse the object of wonder: the true cause for amazement is not Allāh’s ability to recreate, but the denial of resurrection despite the abundant signs of divine power evident in creation.\nThe address consoles the Prophet by portraying such disbelief as irrational, exposing the inconsistency of those who acknowledge initial creation yet reject re-creation. In Qur’anic rhetoric, this form of astonishment functions as a subtle rebuke, calling people to reflection and reason while highlighting the moral and intellectual failure inherent in denying the Hereafter.",
  13006: "They ask you to hasten the punishment before the good, even though exemplary punishments have already passed before them. Yet surely your Lord is full of forgiveness toward people despite their wrongdoing, and surely your Lord is severe in punishment.",
  13007: "Those who disbelieve say, “Why has no sign been sent down to him from his Lord?” You are only a warner, and for every people there is a guide.",
  13008: "Allāh knows what every female bears, and how the wombs diminish or increase [6]. And everything with Him is according to a precise measure.\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. The three matters mentioned in the verse — “what every female bears” and “how the wombs diminish or increase” — may be understood as referring to three functions of the womb during pregnancy. “What every female bears” refers to the fetus which the womb receives and preserves. “How the wombs diminish” refers to menstrual blood retained and redirected as nourishment for the fetus, while “how they increase” refers to blood discharged outward, such as postnatal bleeding and the spotting or redness sometimes observed during pregnancy. Most exegetes, however, interpret “how the wombs diminish” as the shortening of the normal term of pregnancy from the usual nine months, and “how they increase” as any extension beyond it.",
  13009: "He is the Knower of the unseen and the seen, the All-Great, the Most Exalted.",
  13010: "It is all the same to Him whether any of you conceals his speech or speaks it openly, and whether one hides in the darkness of night or walks openly by day.",
  13011: "For each person there are successive angels before and behind him, guarding him by Allāh’s command [7]. Indeed, Allāh does not change the condition of a people until they change what is within themselves [8]. But when Allāh wills harm for a people [9], there is no repelling it, and besides Him they have no protector.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. “Successive angels” refers to the angels appointed by Allāh to accompany each human being. They alternate in shifts by day and night, demonstrating the continuity of divine care and oversight. According to prophetic reports, there are four angels in each shift: two guardians—one positioned in front and another behind—who protect the person from harm by Allāh’s command, and two recorders—one on the right recording good deeds and another on the left recording misdeeds.\nThese angels operate solely by divine permission and do not prevent what Allāh has decreed. Their presence reflects a balance between protection, accountability, and human responsibility, underscoring that while a person is constantly guarded and observed, ultimate authority and judgment belong to Allāh alone.\n[8]. As long as people recognize Allāh’s favors and respond with gratitude and obedience, His mercy remains upon them. But when they persist in disobedience and ingratitude, they should not be deceived by the respite they are granted, for it is not a sign of approval. Rather, it is a delay born of wisdom, and Allāh’s command may come suddenly at any moment. This reflects a recurring Qur’anic principle: lasting change—whether toward mercy or punishment—is tied to what people choose within themselves.\n[9]. That is, because of their sins. Divine punishment is never arbitrary; it is the consequence of persistent wrongdoing after clear signs and repeated opportunities for repentance.",
  13012: "It is He who shows you lightning, causing fear and hope [10], and raises up the heavy clouds [with rain].\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. Lightning inspires both fear and hope: fear of destruction, thunder, and potential harm, and hope for rainfall, relief, and life-giving provision. The verse highlights how Allāh instills contrasting emotions through a single natural phenomenon, reminding humanity of His power and mercy simultaneously. In Qur’anic discourse, such signs are meant to awaken humility, gratitude, and reliance upon Allāh—fear restraining arrogance, and hope nurturing trust and supplication.",
  13013: "The thunder glorifies Him with praise, and so do the angels in awe of Him. He sends thunderbolts and strikes with them whom He wills, yet they dispute about Allāh, while He is severe in might.",
  13014: "To Him alone belongs all true and sincere supplication. Those whom they call upon besides Him do not respond to them in any way—except like one who stretches out his hands toward water [from afar, calling it] to reach his mouth, but it never reaches it [11]. The supplication of the disbelievers is nothing but error.\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. This parable illustrates the utter futility of directing supplication to anything besides Allāh. It likens such prayer to a person stretching out his hands toward water from a distance, hoping it will reach his mouth—yet it never does. Likewise, false deities possess no power to hear, respond, or benefit. The image emphasizes that misplaced supplication is inherently ineffective, not merely unanswered. In Islam, supplication (duʿāʾ) is itself an act of worship, and directing it to other than Allāh constitutes misguidance, for it seeks help from that which has no power to give or withhold.",
  13015: "To Allāh prostrates [and submits] all who are in the heavens and the earth, willingly or unwillingly, as do their shadows in the mornings and the evenings.",
  13016: "Say, ‘Who is the Lord of the heavens and the earth?’ Say, ‘Allāh.’ Say, ‘Have you then taken besides Him protectors who possess no power even to benefit or harm themselves?’ Say, ‘Are the blind and the seeing equal? Or are darkness and light equal?’ Or have they ascribed to Allāh partners who created like His creation, so that the two creations appeared alike to them? Say, ‘Allāh is the Creator of all things, and He is the One, the All-Conquering.’ [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. “The One, the All-Conquering” affirms Allāh’s absolute oneness and irresistible authority. Al-Wāḥid negates any partner or equal in creation or control, while al-Qahhār signifies complete dominion over all things, subduing every force by His will. Together, these names refute the notion of rival creators or protectors and establish that all power, benefit, and harm belong solely to Allāh.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: 'It is a right upon Allāh that nothing is raised in this world except He lowers it.'\nEvery high thing must come down. Every stronghold of disbelievers shall be broken. Allāh is the only One true God and has no second. He is  the Victorious, the All-Conquering.\n Let Allāh’s glory fill the universe. Let His power overflow. He sent us his messenger, Muhammad ﷺ, after Jesus, and revealed to him the Qur’an, which is the fullness of God's divine revelation and so there will be no more prophets giving public revelation after the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ.",
  13017: "He sends down water from the sky, and the valleys flow according to their measure, while the torrent carries rising foam upon its surface. Likewise, from what they melt in the fire to make ornaments or utensils, there emerges a foam similar to it. Thus does Allāh set forth the example of truth and falsehood: as for the foam, it passes away as scum; but that which benefits people remains on the earth. Thus does Allāh set forth examples 'for those who reflect'. [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. This powerful parable contrasts truth and falsehood through two familiar natural processes. Truth is likened to water that remains and benefits people, while falsehood is compared to foam or scum that rises briefly before dissolving and disappearing; likewise, the slag produced from molten metals is discarded while the pure, useful metal endures. Rainwater brings life yet produces foam that quickly vanishes, whereas the beneficial water remains. In both examples, falsehood is conspicuous but fleeting, while truth is enduring, beneficial, and firmly established by Allāh’s will.\nThe verse teaches that although falsehood may appear dominant or impressive for a time, it has no permanence. What is rooted in truth continues to benefit people and remains. The parable reassures believers and calls skeptics to reflection: lasting value lies not in outward appearance or noise, but in substance, benefit, and divine approval.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ illustrated this same principle when he said: “The example of the guidance and knowledge with which Allāh has sent me is like abundant rain falling upon the earth…” He explained that some land absorbs the rain and produces vegetation, some retains water and benefits others, while some barren land neither holds water nor yields growth. The first represents those who understand, benefit from, and teach divine guidance; the last represents those who turn away from it. Thus, true guidance—like rain and pure metal—endures and gives life, while falsehood leaves no lasting trace.",
  13018: "For those who respond to their Lord [14] is the best 'reward'; but those who do not respond to Him [15]—if they possessed all that is in the earth, and the like thereof with it, they would surely offer it as ransom to redeem themselves. Those will have an evil reckoning, and their abode is Hell. How wretched indeed is that resting place!\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. “Those who responded to their Lord” refers to those who accepted Allāh’s call with faith, obedience, and submission. Responding to Allāh entails believing in His oneness, following His guidance, and acting upon His commands—both inwardly and outwardly—and obeying His Messenger, Muhammad ﷺ. Such a response is not merely verbal, but is manifested through righteous conduct, patience, and moral commitment. For these people is “the best reward”—Paradise and the pleasure of Allāh.\n[15]. “Those who did not respond to Him” are those who rejected or ignored Allāh’s guidance despite clear signs, and who disobeyed His Messenger ﷺ. Their failure to respond reflects deliberate denial, arrogance, or heedlessness. The verse emphasizes that no worldly possession—no matter how vast—can ransom a soul on the Day of Judgment. Their “evil reckoning” signifies severe accountability, and their final abode is Hell, underscoring the irreversible consequence of rejecting divine truth.",
  13019: "Is one who knows that what has been sent down to you from your Lord is the truth like one who is blind [16]? Only those endowed with understanding will take heed,\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. That is, a believer and a disbeliever.\nThe contrast here is between one who possesses knowledge rooted in recognition of divine truth and one who is spiritually blind. Blindness in this verse is not physical, but a blindness of the heart—willful ignorance and refusal to acknowledge revelation despite clear evidence. The Qur’an repeatedly emphasizes that true knowledge leads to humility, faith, and obedience, while blindness results in denial and loss. Only those endowed with understanding (ulū al-albāb)—people of sound intellect and reflective hearts—are able to perceive this distinction and respond rightly to the truth.",
  13020: "those who fulfill the covenant of Allāh and do not break the pledge,",
  13021: "and those who uphold whatever Allāh has commanded to be upheld [17], stand in awe of their Lord, and fear an evil reckoning,\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. Literally, “whatever Allāh has commanded to be joined.” The expression is general. Scholars most commonly interpret it as referring to maintaining ties of kinship (ṣilat al-raḥim), but it also encompasses all relationships and bonds that Allāh has commanded to be upheld — including family ties, covenants, faith, obedience, and social obligations. The wording intentionally remains broad rather than restricting the meaning to one specific category.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: \"The one who maintains ties of kinship is not the one who reciprocates. The one who maintains ties of kinship is the one who, when his relatives cut him off, maintains ties of kinship.\"\nOne of the early righteous predecessors was asked about the complete man of perfect virtue and noble character. He replied:\n“The complete man is the one who honors his parents, maintains ties of kinship, treats his brothers generously, possesses good character, safeguards his religion, manages his wealth wisely, spends from his surplus, speaks well, and keeps to his home.”",
  13022: "and those who are patient, seeking the Face of their Lord, establish prayer, spend from what We have provided for them—secretly and openly—and repel evil with good [18]—for them is the good end of the abode.\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. Literally, “they repel (or ward off) evil with good.” The verb conveys the sense of actively pushing back or countering harm. Scholars understand this as responding to wrongdoing with forgiveness, patience, and virtuous conduct rather than retaliation. The expression encompasses moral discipline, restraint in the face of provocation, and overcoming hostility through righteous character. It emphasizes not returning evil for evil, but choosing instead to respond with kindness and compassion, thereby promoting goodness in the face of harm. Through consistent upright conduct, one may positively influence others and soften hearts. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ exemplified this principle throughout his life, responding to persecution and hostility with patience, mercy, and integrity.",
  13023: "Gardens of Eden they will enter—along with those who were righteous among their parents, their spouses, and their offspring. The angels will enter upon them from every gate,",
  13024: "“Peace be upon you for what you patiently endured. How excellent is the final home.”",
  13025: "But those who break the covenant of Allāh after its ratification, and sever the bonds Allāh has commanded to be joined, and spread corruption in the land—for them is the Curse, and for them is the evil abode.",
  13026: "Allāh extends provision for whom He wills and restricts it. They rejoice in the worldly life, yet the life of this world, compared to the Hereafter, is nothing but fleeting enjoyment.",
  13027: "Those who disbelieve say, ‘Why has a sign not been sent down to him from his Lord?’ Say, ‘Indeed, Allāh leaves astray whom He wills and guides to Himself those who turn back to Him.’",
  13028: "Those who believe and whose hearts find rest in the remembrance of Allāh. Surely, in the remembrance of Allāh do hearts find rest. [19]\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. Literally, “their hearts become tranquil in the remembrance of Allāh.” The verb conveys a sense of calm assurance, settledness, and inner stability after agitation. Remembrance (dhikr) encompasses mindful awareness of Allāh, invocation, recitation of the Qur’an, prayer, and conscious reflection upon Him. The repetition in the verse underscores that true and lasting tranquility is not found in worldly attainment, but in sustained remembrance and orientation toward the Divine. For believers in Allāh and His Messenger, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, remembrance becomes a refuge — a sacred retreat amid life’s turbulence. In turning to Allāh, seeking Him, invoking Him, and gathering in His houses of worship where His Name is remembered, hearts are renewed and steadied. The verse affirms that nearness to Allāh — through worship, supplication, and recitation — is the enduring source of inner peace.",
  13029: "Those who believe and do righteous deeds—for them is bliss and a beautiful return.",
  13030: "Thus have We sent you to a nation before which other nations had passed away, so that you may recite to them what We have revealed to you [O Prophet], while they disbelieve in the Most Compassionate [20]. Say, “He is my Lord; there is no god except Him. In Him I place my trust, and to Him is my return.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. Al-Raḥmān (rendered here as “the Most Compassionate”) is a Divine Name derived from the root denoting vast, all-encompassing mercy. It signifies an especially intense and universal form of compassion. In the early Makkan context, some objected to or resisted this Name, despite its affirmation of God’s boundless mercy. The verse thus highlights the paradox that they reject the One whose defining attribute is mercy itself. Its use here underscores both divine compassion and the gravity of denying the One who is universally merciful.\nRecognition of al-Raḥmān calls the believer to conscious submission to the will of Allāh and sincere adherence to His final Messenger, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. True faith expresses itself not only in conviction, but in righteous deeds, upright character, and service to others. In living by divine mercy—reflecting it in conduct, worship, and compassion toward creation—the believer embodies the meaning of this Name and finds purpose, renewal, and spiritual direction.",
  13031: "Even if there were a Qur’an by which mountains were moved, or the earth split asunder, or the dead made to speak [21]—rather, to Allāh belongs the matter entirely. Have those who believe not realized that, if Allāh had willed, He could have guided all mankind? But those who disbelieve—calamity will not cease to strike them for what they have done, or it will settle near their dwellings, until the promise of Allāh comes to pass. Indeed, Allāh never fails in His promise.\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. That is, even if a revelation possessed such extraordinary power, it would not be other than this Qur’an. The verse presents a powerful rhetorical conditional: even if a scripture could move mountains, split the earth, or cause the dead to speak, it would not compel belief. The imagery underscores that guidance does not depend upon the display of extraordinary signs, but upon the will of Allāh and the receptivity of the human heart. Scholars explain that the disbelievers demanded miraculous proofs; the verse responds that ultimate authority over all affairs belongs entirely to Allāh. Faith is therefore not produced by spectacle alone, but by divine guidance granted to those who turn toward Him.",
  13032: "Indeed, messengers before you were mocked; but I granted respite to those who disbelieved [22], then I seized them—how severe was My punishment!\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. The expression “I gave respite” (amlaytu) denotes granting time and extension despite persistent disbelief. It does not imply approval or neglect, but a deliberate postponement of judgment. The Qur’an frequently presents divine respite as a manifestation of both mercy and justice: mercy, in that space is given for repentance and reflection; and justice, in that eventual accountability becomes more evident and decisive. The delay of punishment should not be mistaken for its absence. When it comes, it comes with full measure and according to divine wisdom.",
  13033: "Is He who watches over every soul, knowing what it earns, like others? Yet they ascribe partners to Allāh. Say, “Name them! Or are you informing Him of something He does not know on the earth? Or is it nothing but empty speech?” Rather, their plot has been made to seem fair to those who disbelieve, and they have been turned away from the path. Whomever Allāh leaves astray—there is no guide for him.",
  13034: "For them is a punishment in this worldly life, but the punishment of the Hereafter is surely more severe, and they will have no protector against Allāh. [23]\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. The term rendered here as “protector” (wāq) carries the sense of one who shields, guards, or defends from harm. The expression “they will have no protector against Allāh” signifies that none can shield them from His judgment or avert His decree. It underscores the absolute sovereignty of Allāh and the futility of relying upon intermediaries, false deities, or worldly power for ultimate protection. On the Day of Judgment, no barrier will stand between a person and divine accountability except by His mercy.\nThe Qur’an consistently affirms that ultimate salvation and deliverance belong to Allāh alone. This proclamation continues the essential monotheistic message conveyed in earlier revelations, which likewise emphasize that no savior exists apart from the One true God, Allāh. As expressed in the former Scriptures: “I, I am the Lord, and besides Me there is no savior” (Isaiah 43:11); “There is no God apart from Me — a righteous God and a Savior; there is none but Me” (Isaiah 45:21); and “You shall acknowledge no God but Me, no Savior except Me” (Hosea 13:4). The unity of this declaration across prophetic history reinforces the Qur’anic affirmation that authority, protection, and salvation rest solely with Allāh.",
  13035: "The likeness of the Garden promised to those who fear Allāh: rivers flow beneath it; its fruit and its shade are everlasting. That is the final outcome of those who fear Allāh, but the outcome of the disbelievers is the Fire.",
  13036: "Those to whom We gave the Scripture rejoice in what has been revealed to you, [O Prophet]. But among the factions are those who deny part of it. Say, “I have only been commanded to worship Allāh alone and associate none with Him. To Him I call, and to Him is my return.”",
  13037: "Thus We have sent it down as an Arabic judgment, decisive and clear. But if you were to follow their desires after what has come to you of knowledge [24], you would have no protector or defender against Allāh. [25]\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. That is, the reference is to certain among the People of the Scripture who opposed or selectively rejected aspects of the revelation. The term rendered here as “desires” (ahwā’, plural of hawā) denotes not merely natural inclinations, but capricious impulses, subjective whims, and passions that deviate from revealed guidance. In Qur’anic usage, hawā often signifies desire unchecked by divine instruction — personal inclination elevated above truth. The warning underscores the danger of subordinating revealed knowledge to personal preference, inherited bias, or communal pressure.\n[25]. The two terms translated as “protector” (walī) and “defender” (wāq) carry related but distinct nuances. Walī conveys the sense of a close ally, patron, or guardian who offers support and authority. Wāq derives from a root meaning to shield or guard, indicating one who protects and saves from harm or punishment. Their pairing emphasizes that none can serve as either ally or shield against Allāh’s decree. No relationship, power, or intercession can avert His judgment apart from His mercy. The Qur’anic message consistently affirms that ultimate salvation rests solely with Allāh, a theme echoed throughout earlier prophetic teachings: deliverance is beyond human power and lies entirely within the will of the One God.\nThe disciples of Jesus once asked, “Who then can be saved and who saves?” Jesus looked at them and said, “With man (i.e., Jesus referring himself and all mankind) this is impossible, but with Allāh all things are possible.\" He is the only Savior. Allāh saves, no one else can save.",
  13038: "We have certainly sent messengers before you and appointed for them wives and offspring [26]. It was not for any messenger to bring a sign except by Allāh’s permission. For every appointed term there is a written decree.\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. These are basic human needs. The messengers were human beings, not angels. Like other people, they lived ordinary human lives: they married, had families, and experienced the responsibilities and realities of human existence. Their humanity does not diminish their prophethood; rather, it affirms that they were sent as relatable guides who lived among their communities and understood human needs, struggles, and social life firsthand.",
  13039: "Allāh erases and confirms whatever He wills [26], and with Him is the Master Record. [27]\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. The expression “Allāh erases and confirms whatever He wills” affirms His absolute sovereignty over decree and unfolding events. The verb yamḥū (erases) conveys removal, effacement, or cancellation, while yuthbit (confirms) signifies establishment and making firm. Scholars explain that this may refer to changes within created circumstances, decreed outcomes, or recorded matters known to the angels, all according to divine wisdom. The verse emphasizes that apparent change does not imply uncertainty; rather, all alteration occurs within the scope of His prior knowledge and will.\n[27]. The phrase rendered here as “the Master Record” translates Umm al-Kitāb, literally “the Mother of the Book.” In Arabic usage, umm can denote the source, foundation, or principal reference of something. The expression refers to the ultimate and preserved source of divine decree and knowledge — commonly identified as the Preserved Tablet (al-Lawḥ al-Maḥfūẓ) with Allāh, in which all matters are established. While specific decrees may be erased or confirmed within created reality according to His wisdom, the Preserved Tablet represents the unchanging foundation of divine knowledge from which all determinations proceed.",
  13040: "Whether We show you [O Prophet] part of what We warn them, or cause you to die before that, your duty is only to convey the message, and upon Us is the reckoning.",
  13041: "Do they not see that We are reducing the land from its edges [29]? Allāh judges—none can reverse His decree. And He is swift in reckoning.\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. The phrase “reducing the land from its edges” has been interpreted in several complementary ways by scholars. Some understand it as a reference to the gradual loss of territory and power among those who oppose the truth, as lands once under their control diminish over time. Others interpret it more broadly as the steady decline of worldly dominance, the passing of generations, or the progressive unfolding of divine judgment in history. The imagery conveys gradual but certain reduction — a reminder that no earthly authority remains permanent, and that all affairs ultimately return to the decree of Allāh.",
  13042: "Those before them had already plotted, but to Allāh belongs all planning. He knows what every soul earns, and soon the disbelievers will know for whom is the final outcome of the abode.",
  13043: "Those who disbelieve say, “You are not a messenger.” Say, “Allāh is sufficient as a witness between me and you, and so is he who possesses knowledge of the Scripture [30].”\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. The phrase “he who possesses knowledge of the Scripture” has been understood in several ways by scholars. Many interpreted it as referring to learned people among the People of the Scripture who recognized the truth of the Prophet’s message through their scriptures. Others held that it refers more generally to anyone granted knowledge of revelation. In either case, the verse affirms that both divine testimony and informed recognition from people of scripture stand as witnesses to the Prophet’s truthfulness.",
  14001: "Alif Lām Rā [1]. This is a Book We have revealed to you so that you may bring mankind out of darknesses [2] into the light, by the permission of their Lord, to the path of the All-Mighty, the Praiseworthy.\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2:  verse 1.\n[2]. The metaphor of darkness and light teaches that faith is not merely a claim upon the tongue, but a reality reflected in one’s conduct. Whoever professes belief in Allāh and in His final Messenger, Muhammad ﷺ, yet continues to walk in the darkness of disobedience and injustice, deceives himself. True faith manifests in obedience, compassion, and steadfast adherence to Allāh’s commands while avoiding what He has forbidden.\nWalking in the light means loving one another for the sake of Allāh, supporting one another in righteousness, and striving for purity of heart and action. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Purification is half of faith. ‘Al-ḥamdu lillāh’ fills the scale. ‘Subḥān Allāh’ and ‘al-ḥamdu lillāh’ fill what is between the heavens and the earth. Prayer is a light; charity is a proof; patience is a radiant illumination; and the Qur’an is either a proof for you or against you. Every person goes out in the morning and trades his soul—either freeing it or destroying it.”\nThis ḥadīth reinforces the Qur’anic teaching: faith illuminates the heart through remembrance, prayer, charity, patience, and moral integrity. The Qur’an itself becomes a witness—either in one’s favor or against him—depending on how he responds to it.\nThus, the believer is called to walk in the light of guidance—remembering Allāh often, doing good, and upholding justice—so that Allāh may purify his heart and guide him to the everlasting light of His mercy.",
  14002: "Allāh—to whom belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is in the earth. And woe to the disbelievers from a severe punishment.",
  14003: "Those who prefer the life of this world over the Hereafter, and avert others from the path of Allāh, seeking to make it crooked—such are far astray.",
  14004: "We did not send any messenger except in the language of his people, so that he might make the message clear to them. Then Allāh leaves astray whom He wills and guides whom He wills. And He is the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.",
  14005: "We certainly sent Moses with Our signs: ‘Bring your people out of darknesses into the light [3], and remind them of the Days of Allāh.’ Indeed, in this are signs for everyone steadfast and grateful. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. Light in the Qur’an consistently symbolizes guidance, knowledge, faith, mercy, and divine revelation, whereas darkness represents misguidance, sin, ignorance, and despair. The command to bring people “out of darkness into light” signifies the transition from disbelief and oppression to faith and moral clarity.\n“The Days of Allāh” refers to the decisive moments in history when Allāh’s power, justice, mercy, or punishment became manifest—such as deliverance from oppression, the destruction of tyrants, or great divine favors bestowed upon a people. In the context of Moses, this includes the rescue of the Children of Israel from Pharaoh and the signs shown to them thereafter. The expression encompasses both days of blessing and days of retribution, reminding people that history unfolds under divine authority and moral order.\n[4]. The pairing of patient and grateful describes the balanced believer. Patience is manifested in hardship, trial, perseverance in obedience, and restraint from sin; gratitude is shown in times of blessing, ease, and provision through acknowledgment of Allāh’s favors and their proper use. Together, they reflect the two enduring states of human life—difficulty and prosperity. Only those who endure with patience and respond with gratitude truly perceive the signs of Allāh and benefit from them.\nA thankful heart approaches its Lord with humility, praise, and reverence—recognizing that all goodness comes from Him alone. Gratitude is not merely spoken, but lived through obedience and trust. For Allāh is Most Generous and Most Faithful to His promises; His mercy endures, and His guidance continues for those who remain steadfast and grateful.",
  14006: "And [remember] when Moses said to his people, “Recall Allāh’s favor upon you when He saved you from the people of Pharaoh, who afflicted you with a dreadful torment—slaughtering your sons and sparing your women [5]. And in that was a tremendous trial from your Lord.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The sparing of women refers to their being kept alive for servitude and humiliation after the systematic killing of male infants. This policy was intended to weaken and subjugate the community. The verse frames both suffering and deliverance as part of a divine trial, testing patience and gratitude.",
  14007: "And [remember] when your Lord proclaimed, ‘If you are grateful, I will surely give you more; but if you are ungrateful, indeed, My punishment is severe.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. True gratitude to Allāh is the servant’s conscious response to divine grace. It manifests upon the tongue through words of praise, within the heart through love and mindful remembrance, and upon the limbs through humble obedience. Genuine thankfulness rests upon five foundations: submission to Allāh, love for Him, recognition of His blessings, praise for them, and refraining from using His gifts in disobedience to Him.\nThese principles together form the essence of gratitude. When one of them is absent, the fullness and beauty of thankfulness are diminished. Thus, gratitude in Islam is not merely speech, but a comprehensive state of the heart and a disciplined way of living.",
  14008: "And Moses said, ‘If you disbelieve—you and all who are on earth together—indeed, Allāh is Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy.’",
  14009: "Have there not come to you the stories of those before you—the people of Noah, ʿĀd, and Thamūd, and those after them? None knows them except Allāh. Their messengers came to them with clear proofs, but they put their hands to their mouths and said, ‘Indeed, we disbelieve in that with which you have been sent, and we are in grave doubt concerning that to which you invite us.’",
  14010: "Their messengers said, ‘Is there any doubt about Allāh, the Originator of the heavens and the earth? He calls you so that He may forgive you your sins and grant you respite until an appointed term.’ They said, ‘You are but human beings like us. You wish to turn us away from what our forefathers used to worship. Then bring us a clear authority, if you are truthful.’",
  14011: "Their messengers said to them, “We are only human beings like you, but Allāh bestows His favor upon whom He wills of His servants. It is not for us to bring you any proof except by Allāh’s permission. And upon Allāh alone let the believers place their trust.”",
  14012: "Why should we not place our trust in Allāh, when He has guided us to our ways? We will surely remain patient over whatever harm you cause us. And upon Allāh let all who trust place their trust. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. This verse joins two foundational virtues: patience (ṣabr) and reliance upon Allāh (tawakkul). Patience here refers to steadfast endurance in the face of harm, persecution, and hardship without abandoning obedience or resorting to injustice. Reliance upon Allāh means entrusting one’s affairs to Him with confidence in His wisdom and decree while continuing to act responsibly and lawfully. Together, these qualities form the spiritual strength of the believer: patience restrains despair, and reliance dispels fear. The repetition—“upon Allāh alone”—emphasizes exclusivity in trust, affirming that ultimate support, protection, and outcome belong solely to Him.\nThrough faith, the believer places full confidence in Allāh alone. This trust deepens prayer, strengthens resolve, and cultivates serenity in the heart. It brings peace in knowing that Allāh directs every path with wisdom and never abandons those who rely upon Him. The lives of the prophets and the righteous testify to this truth: they trusted Allāh through trials and hardship, and He granted them steadfastness, dignity in this world, and salvation in the Hereafter.",
  14013: "Those who disbelieved said to their messengers, ‘We will surely drive you out of our land unless you return to our religion [8].’ Then their Lord revealed to them: ‘We will surely destroy the wrongdoers.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. The demand to “return to our religion” reflects coercion rather than sincere dialogue. The prophets had never adhered to the false religion of their people; the expression therefore signifies forced conformity to prevailing beliefs and social order. It reveals a recurring historical pattern: when truth challenges entrenched tradition and entrenched power, opposition shifts from reasoned debate to intimidation—threatening exile, social pressure, or violence.\nBehind such persecution lies the influence of Satan, who incites hostility toward faith and stirs hearts against those who uphold truth. Yet the believer is not to fear oppression or even death, for life and death are in the hand of Allāh alone. Harm may afflict the body, but the soul remains under divine protection. Persecution is temporary; divine justice is certain. Allāh has decreed both the beginning and the end of every trial.\nThus believers are instructed not to fear those who threaten or harm them, but to fear Allāh alone—He who holds dominion over body and soul, and in whose hand is the final outcome.",
  14014: "And We will surely cause you to dwell in the land after them. That is for those who fear standing before Me [for reckoning] and fear My warning.",
  14015: "They sought victory, and every obstinate tyrant was doomed.",
  14016: "Hell awaits him, and he will be made to drink oozing pus.",
  14017: "He will sip it, yet he will scarcely swallow it. Death will approach him from every side, yet he will not die. And beyond him lies a severe punishment.",
  14018: "The example of those who disbelieve in their Lord: their deeds [9] are like ashes blown away by a furious wind on a stormy day. They will have no power over anything they earned. That is the furthest straying.\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. The comparison of deeds to ashes scattered by a violent wind illustrates their utter loss and weightlessness on the Day of Judgment. Actions performed without true faith and sincerity toward Allāh lack enduring value, no matter how impressive they may appear in this world. Just as ashes cannot be gathered once dispersed, such deeds yield no lasting benefit in the Hereafter. The verse underscores a central Qur’anic principle: righteous action must be rooted in correct belief and sincere intention to have eternal worth.",
  14019: "Have you not seen that Allāh created the heavens and the earth in truth? If He wills, He can do away with you and bring forth a new creation.",
  14020: "And that is not difficult for Allāh. [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. The phrase “not difficult for Allāh” translates the expression bi-ʿazīz, which means that nothing is beyond His power, nothing can resist His will, and nothing is hard for Him to do. In this context, it refers to His ability to remove one people and replace them with another. The verse affirms Allāh’s complete sovereignty and independence: He does not need creation in any way. His power is effortless, unlimited, and always exercised with perfect wisdom.",
  14021: "They will all stand before Allāh, and the weak will say to those who were arrogant, ‘We were your followers, so can you spare us from any part of Allāh’s punishment?’ They will reply, ‘If Allāh had guided us, we would have guided you. It makes no difference now whether we despair or remain patient—there is no escape for us.’",
  14022: "Satan will say, when the matter has been decided, ‘Indeed, Allāh promised you the true promise, and I too promised you, but I failed you. I had no authority over you except that I called you, and you answered me. So do not blame me; blame yourselves. I cannot help you, nor can you help me. Indeed, I reject your associating me with Allāh before. Indeed, the wrongdoers will suffer a painful punishment.’",
  14023: "Those who believed and did righteous deeds will be admitted into gardens beneath which rivers flow, abiding therein forever by the permission of their Lord. Their greeting there will be: ‘Peace.’",
  14024: "Have you not considered how Allāh sets forth a parable? A good word is like a good tree [11], whose root is firmly fixed and whose branches rise into the sky, [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. The “good word” is most commonly interpreted as the testimony of faith (lā ilāha illā Allāh) and, by extension, every word and belief rooted in truth, sincerity, and remembrance of Allāh. It represents sound faith established in the heart, expressed upon the tongue, and manifested through righteous action. Just as a healthy tree begins with a strong seed, true faith begins with a pure and sincere word.\nAllāh’s Word—the Qur’an—is a source of guidance, reassurance, and light. It brings tranquility in times of anxiety and clarity in moments of confusion. The believer lives by this good word—reflecting upon its promises, its descriptions of Allāh’s wisdom and sovereignty, and its call to moral excellence.\nThe parable also reminds us of the moral power of speech. A harsh word can wound and burden the heart, while a good and gentle word uplifts, heals, and brings joy. Words shape hearts, strengthen bonds, and draw a person nearer to the pleasure of Allāh. The Qur’an commands: “Speak good to people” (2:83), and describes the righteous as those who are “guided to goodly words and to the path of the Praiseworthy” (22:24). The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said, “A good word is charity.”\nThus the “good word”—faith in Allāh, truthful speech, and kind encouragement—is like a living tree: its roots are firm in conviction, its branches rise in hope, and its fruits benefit all who draw near. The believer therefore chooses words that heal rather than harm, that illuminate rather than darken, reflecting the life-giving nature of true faith.\n[12]. The image of the tree whose branches reach high into the sky symbolizes elevation, growth, and visible benefit. Its height reflects the nobility of faith and the honor it brings, while its upward reach suggests aspiration toward what is pure and lofty. Just as a tree draws nourishment through firm roots and then extends outward and upward, true belief rises through righteous deeds, noble character, and beneficial influence upon others. The believer whose faith is firmly rooted becomes a source of goodness, stability, and hope—steadfast in conviction and expansive in mercy.",
  14025: "yielding its fruit at all times by the permission of its Lord. And Allāh sets forth parables for mankind so that they may take heed.",
  14026: "And the example of a bad word is like a bad tree—uprooted from the surface of the earth, with no stability. [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. The “bad word” refers to disbelief, falsehood, and every corrupt statement rooted in denial of truth. It is commonly understood as the opposite of the testimony of faith—namely, words of polytheism, hypocrisy, or rejection of divine guidance. The parable of the “bad tree” illustrates instability and lack of permanence: it has no firm roots, no nourishment, and no lasting fruit. Just as an uprooted tree cannot stand or benefit anyone, false belief and harmful speech lack foundation and ultimately perish. What is not grounded in truth may appear for a time, but it cannot endure or produce lasting good.\nThe parable also reflects the condition of the heart. People are known by their words and deeds, for every statement and action is the fruit of what lies within. A diseased heart produces harmful speech and destructive behavior, just as a corrupt tree bears bitter fruit. But a sound heart yields goodness—words of kindness, acts of mercy, and deeds of righteousness that bring light and healing to others. As the heart is, so is the life that grows from it.\nWhen the heart is nourished by faith, strengthened through remembrance of Allāh, and purified by repentance, it flourishes with goodness and stability. But when it is left to arrogance, envy, or heedlessness, it weakens and withers. Thus the parable teaches that true reform begins within: roots determine fruit, and foundations determine destiny.",
  14027: "Allāh keeps firm those who believe with the firm word of faith in the life of this world and in the Hereafter [14]. And Allāh leaves the wrongdoers astray. Allāh does whatever He wills.\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. The “firm word” is widely understood to mean the testimony of faith (lā ilāha illā Allāh — “there is no deity worthy of worship except Allāh”). It signifies the believer’s unwavering conviction in the oneness of Allāh and trust in His promise. Allāh grants steadfastness upon this word during times of trial, at the moment of death, and in the questioning of the grave. The verse teaches that true stability is a divine gift: faith rooted in the heart and expressed through righteous action is strengthened and preserved by Allāh in both this life and the Hereafter. Firmness upon the truth is therefore not merely personal determination, but support granted by divine grace.",
  14028: "Have you not seen those who exchanged Allāh’s favor for disbelief and led their people to the abode of ruin? [15]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. “Exchanged Allāh’s favor for ingratitude” refers to those who were granted divine blessings—such as guidance, security, provision, and the sending of a messenger—yet chose disbelief and rejection instead. The expression portrays ingratitude as a deliberate exchange: they traded faith for denial and obedience for rebellion. In the Qur’anic sense, ingratitude is not merely a failure to give thanks, but the rejection, misuse, or distortion of Allāh’s blessings.\nWhen divine favor is met with arrogance rather than gratitude, it results not in increase, but in loss and ruin. The verse carries a direct warning to leaders and nations who receive revelation, prosperity, or stability, yet replace thankfulness with denial and use their blessings to spread corruption or disbelief. Leadership without gratitude becomes a source of destruction—for the individual and for the society that follows.\nIt also applies broadly to anyone who uses wealth, authority, or knowledge in ways that oppose their Giver. When those in power lead others astray, moral corruption spreads, producing social collapse in this world and accountability in the Hereafter. Thus the verse reminds us that blessings carry responsibility, and gratitude is the safeguard that preserves them.",
  14029: "Hell—they will enter it and burn. Wretched indeed is that dwelling place. [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. Jahannam (Hell) is the name given in the Qur’an to the abode of punishment prepared for those who persist in disbelief and corruption. The term conveys severity, depth, and blazing fire. It is described as a place of humiliation, regret, and unending consequence for those who knowingly rejected divine guidance. In this context, it represents the ultimate outcome of exchanging Allāh’s favor for ingratitude—an abode earned through deliberate denial and misuse of His blessings. The verse underscores both the certainty and the gravity of that end.",
  14030: "They set up equals with Allāh to mislead others from His path. Say, ‘Enjoy yourselves, for indeed your destination is the Fire.’",
  14031: "Tell My servants who believe to establish prayer and spend from what We have provided them, secretly and openly, before a Day comes when there will be neither trade nor friendship. [17]\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. The phrase “neither trade nor friendship” emphasizes the complete absence of worldly means of rescue on the Day of Judgment. “No trade” signifies that salvation cannot be bought, negotiated, or exchanged for wealth. “No close friendship” indicates that even the strongest bonds—family ties, alliances, and loyal companionship—will not avail a person against divine judgment. Each soul will stand accountable on its own. The verse therefore urges believers to act now—through prayer and charity—before the Day arrives when neither wealth nor relationships can alter one’s destiny.",
  14032: "It is Allāh who created the heavens and the earth, and sent down water from the sky, bringing forth fruits thereby as provision for you. And He has subjected to you the ships to sail upon the sea by His command, and subjected to you the rivers.",
  14033: "He has subjected to you the sun and the moon, each moving along its appointed course, and has subjected to you the night and the day. [18]\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. The repeated phrase “He has subjected for you” (sakhkhara lakum) emphasizes that the sun, moon, night, and day operate under Allāh’s command for the benefit of humanity. Their continuous and precise movement reflects divine order and wisdom, not randomness or independence. The sun and moon regulate time, seasons, and sustenance, while the alternation of night and day provides rest and activity—both essential for life. The verse underscores a central Qur’anic theme: the vast forces of the universe are not objects of worship, but signs of the One who governs them. Their submission to His will contrasts with human beings, who are given the choice to submit willingly through faith and obedience.",
  14034: "He has given you from all that you asked of Him [19]. And if you were to count the favors of Allāh, you could never encompass them [20]. Indeed, humankind is truly unjust and ungrateful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. The phrase “He has given you from all that you asked of Him” does not mean that every request is granted exactly as desired. Rather, Allāh has provided humanity with every essential need for life, growth, and guidance—whether requested or not. Many blessings are given before we ask, and many come in forms better than what we sought. The verse highlights His generosity and sufficiency.\n[20]. The inability to “enumerate the favors of Allāh” points to the vastness of His blessings, including life, provision, guidance, intellect, security, and spiritual opportunity. The verse describes humanity as “unjust and ungrateful” because people often misuse blessings and fail to acknowledge their Giver. It is both a reminder of divine generosity and a warning against heedlessness.\nAlthough the Qur’an does not formally divide Allāh’s grace into categories, scholars have described its different manifestations. Common grace is the universal mercy given to all creation, such as life, provision, and the natural order. Saving grace is the gift of guidance and faith. Purifying grace is the mercy through which hearts are refined by worship and repentance. Sustaining grace is the strength and patience granted during hardship. Complete grace is the perfection of divine favor in Paradise.\nAll of life is surrounded by Allāh’s grace—in creation, guidance, purification, perseverance, and eternal reward. Every blessing, seen or unseen, reflects His mercy. Recognizing this grace leads to gratitude, which is at the heart of faith.",
  14035: "And [remember] when Abraham said, ‘My Lord, make this city secure and keep me and my sons away from worshiping idols.’ [21]\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. The “city” referred to here is Makkah, which at that time was a barren valley without an established settlement. Abraham’s prayer for its security came before its development and reflects his awareness that it would become a sacred center of worship. He asked Allāh to grant it safety and stability—blessings essential for both worldly life and religious devotion.\nHe then prayed for protection from idol worship, even for himself and his sons. This supplication reflects profound humility and awareness of the danger of idolatry. Although Abraham was a prophet and the foremost champion of pure monotheism, he still sought divine protection from falling into idolatry. This teaches that no one should feel secure from spiritual deviation without Allāh’s help. His prayer also highlights the responsibility of parents to seek not only material security for their children, but the preservation of their faith. The request for the city’s safety alongside protection from idol worship shows that true security is both physical and spiritual: a land is only truly safe when its people are safeguarded from false worship and moral corruption.",
  14036: "My Lord, they have led many among the people astray [22]. So whoever follows me is of me; and whoever disobeys me—indeed, You are Forgiving and Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. The pronoun “they” refers to the idols and false objects of worship that had become widespread among Abraham’s people. Although idols possess no power of their own, they become a cause of misguidance when people attribute divinity to them and build systems of belief around them. Abraham acknowledges the reality that many were led astray through attachment to false worship. Yet his response is marked by compassion and humility: he distinguishes between those who follow him in pure monotheism and those who disobey, while entrusting their ultimate judgment to Allāh’s mercy. His appeal—“You are Forgiving, Most Merciful”—reflects prophetic concern for guidance rather than personal vengeance, and recognition that even those who err remain dependent upon divine mercy.",
  14037: "Our Lord, I have settled some of my offspring in an uncultivated valley near Your Sacred House, our Lord, that they may establish prayer [23]. So make the hearts of people incline toward them [24], and provide them with fruits, that they may be grateful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. Abraham explains that his purpose in settling his family in the barren valley was not for trade, comfort, or worldly gain, but “that they may establish prayer.” This shows that worship was the true foundation of that settlement. The Sacred House (the Kaʿbah) was to become a center of pure monotheism and devotion. The verse teaches that spiritual commitment must come before material security; when prayer is established, divine provision follows.\nPrayer is specifically mentioned because it is the greatest and most comprehensive act of worship. It nurtures faith, disciplines the soul, and connects the servant directly to Allāh. For this reason, it is described in the Qur’an as a safeguard against wrongdoing and a key to all good. When prayer is upheld, the rest of life is guided and ordered around obedience to Allāh.\n[24]. The phrase “make the hearts of people incline toward them” refers to placing love, attachment, and longing in the hearts of others for Abraham’s descendants and for the Sacred House. History bears witness to the fulfillment of this prayer: people from every land travel to Makkah with deep love and devotion. The request also reflects a subtle wisdom—Abraham asked that the hearts of some people incline toward them, meaning those guided to faith. True attachment to sacred places and righteous people is itself a gift from Allāh.",
  14038: "Our Lord, You know what we conceal and what we reveal. Nothing is hidden from Allāh in the earth or in the heaven.",
  14039: "All praise is due to Allāh, who granted me Ishmael and Isaac in my old age. Indeed, my Lord is the Hearer of supplication.",
  14040: "My Lord, make me steadfast in establishing prayer—and likewise from my offspring. Our Lord, accept my supplication.",
  14041: "Our Lord, forgive me, my parents, and the believers on the Day the Reckoning is established.",
  14042: "Never think that Allāh is unaware of what the wrongdoers do. He only delays them for a Day when eyes will stare in horror. [25]\\n\\nFootnotes: [25]. This verse reminds believers that divine justice is certain, though not always immediate. Allāh’s delay is not neglect; it is part of His perfect wisdom and timing. When oppression appears unchecked, this promise offers reassurance: all deeds are seen, all records are preserved, and a Day will surely come when every injustice meets its consequence. For the oppressor, it is a solemn warning; for the oppressed, it is a promise of vindication.\nThe phrase “eyes will stare in horror” describes a state of overwhelming terror on the Day of Judgment. It portrays eyes fixed and unblinking, unable to turn away from the terrifying reality unfolding before them. The imagery conveys shock, helplessness, and the sudden realization that accountability can no longer be delayed.",
  14043: "Rushing forward with necks outstretched, their heads raised high, their gaze fixed and unblinking, and their hearts hollow within. [26]\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. This verse portrays the psychological reality of divine justice. Those who once lived with arrogance and defiance are stripped of composure—physically rushing, mentally stunned, and spiritually hollow. Their posture reflects inner collapse: movement without direction, eyes fixed in terror, hearts emptied of hope and certainty.\nIt is a profound reversal:\nOnce proud — now humiliated.\nOnce boastful — now speechless.\nOnce heedless — now staring in horror.\nFor the believer, these words are not meant merely to terrify, but to awaken. They remind us that the heart filled with remembrance of Allāh in this life will not be empty on that Day. The Qur’an says: “The Day when neither wealth nor children will benefit, except one who comes to Allāh with a sound heart.” (26:88–89)\nThus the verse teaches that true preparation is inward: a living heart today prevents a hollow heart tomorrow.",
  14044: "Warn the people of the Day when the punishment will come upon them, and the wrongdoers will say, ‘Our Lord, grant us a brief respite; we will answer Your call and follow the messengers.’ [It will be said], ‘Did you not swear before that you would never pass away?’",
  14045: "You dwelt in the dwellings of those who wronged themselves, and it became clear to you how We dealt with them. And We set forth for you examples.",
  14046: "They had indeed devised their schemes, but with Allāh rests their plotting, even though their schemes were such as to make mountains vanish. [27]\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]. The expression “their plots were such as might cause mountains to vanish” is a powerful Qur’anic image emphasizing the intensity and gravity of their schemes. In Arabic rhetoric, mountains often symbolize firmness, stability, and strength. To say that plots could make mountains vanish is to portray them as seemingly overwhelming and formidable. Some commentators understand it as hyperbolic imagery—describing how destructive or far-reaching their conspiracies appeared—while others interpret it as a reference to attempts to undermine the very foundations of faith and revelation.\nYet the verse affirms that no matter how vast or threatening human schemes may seem, they remain fully encompassed by Allāh’s knowledge, decree, and power. What appears immovable to human perception is effortless before Him. The imagery therefore reassures believers: even the most formidable opposition cannot overturn divine truth or frustrate Allāh’s will.",
  14047: "So never think that Allāh will fail in His promise to His messengers. Indeed, Allāh is All-Mighty, Possessor of Retribution. [28]\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. This verse affirms the certainty of Allāh’s promise to His messengers: that truth will ultimately prevail, falsehood will perish, and divine justice will be fulfilled. Although victory may be delayed and trials prolonged, Allāh never breaks His promise. His support may come in this world through protection, establishment, or vindication—or in the Hereafter through complete justice and eternal reward.\nThe name “All-Mighty” (al-ʿAzīz) signifies irresistible power: nothing can prevent His decree or oppose His will. “Possessor of Retribution” (Dhū Intiqām) affirms that He justly repays persistent wrongdoing after warning and respite. His retribution is not driven by anger or injustice, but by perfect wisdom and moral order.\nFor believers, this verse is reassurance: divine help is certain, even if unseen. For wrongdoers, it is a warning: delay is not escape, and every promise of accountability will be fulfilled.",
  14048: "On the Day when the earth will be replaced by another earth, and the heavens as well [29], and all will appear before Allāh—the One, the All-Conquering. [30]\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. The transformation of the earth and the heavens signifies a complete cosmic renewal on the Day of Resurrection. The present order of creation—known to humanity with its familiar structures, landscapes, and physical laws—will be replaced by a new order suited for judgment and eternity.\nThe Qur’an describes the earth being flattened and cleared, and the heavens split apart—not annihilated into nothingness, but re-created in a form prepared for divine reckoning. On that Day, the earth will be replaced with another—pure, pristine, and white—and the heavens will likewise be transformed.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“People will be gathered on the Day of Judgment upon a reddish-white land, pure and clean, like a flawless loaf made of fine white flour—without any landmarks or features.”\nThis dramatic transformation shows that the Hereafter is not a continuation of worldly life, but an entirely new realm governed by perfect justice and permanence. The verse reminds humanity that the world they depend upon is temporary, while the coming reality is decisive and everlasting.\n[30]. Allāh will execute His full justice —His complete and perfect retribution upon the wicked.\nThe names “the One, the All-Conquering” (al-Wāḥid al-Qahhār) highlight Allāh’s absolute uniqueness and irresistible authority at the moment when all creation stands exposed before Him. Al-Wāḥid affirms that no partner, rival, or intercessor shares in His sovereignty. Al-Qahhār conveys complete dominion—every power subdued, every tyrant humbled, every soul brought under His authority. On that Day, all claims of independence, false gods, and worldly hierarchies vanish. Only the sovereignty of Allāh remains manifest, and every creature stands equally dependent before Him.",
  14049: "You will see the wicked that Day bound together in chains. [31]\\n\\nFootnotes: [31]. The image of the wicked “bound together in chains” conveys humiliation, confinement, and helplessness on the Day of Judgment. Those who once moved freely in arrogance and defiance will now be restrained and powerless. The binding together may also indicate shared guilt—leaders and followers alike joined in consequence for their cooperation in wrongdoing. The scene reflects a complete reversal: those who united in sin in this life are united in punishment in the Hereafter. It emphasizes that no alliance, status, or power can shield a person when divine justice is manifest.",
  14050: "Their garments will be of pitch, and the Fire will engulf their faces.",
  14051: "So that Allāh may recompense every soul for what it has earned. Indeed, Allāh is swift in reckoning.",
  14052: "This is a message for all people, so that they may be warned by it, know that He is but One God, and that people of understanding may take heed.",
  15001: "Alif, Lām, Rā. [1] These are the verses of the Book — and a clear Qur’an. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2: Verse 1.\n[2]. Both “the Book” and “the clear recitation” mentioned here refer to one and the same reality: the Qur’an itself — the Scripture as it exists in written form, and as it is recited in revealed verses. Their dual mention is intended to draw special attention to the powerful evidences the Qur’an contains for the truthfulness of its message.",
  15002: "Often, those who disbelieve will wish they had been Muslims. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. Upon their death and On the Judgment Day.\n“Muslims” here denotes those who sincerely submit themselves to God — specifically by accepting His final revelation and following His last Messenger, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. “The disbelievers” at the beginning of the verse refers to those who knowingly rejected the truth after it had been clearly presented to them.",
  15003: "Leave them to eat and enjoy themselves, and be distracted by false hope [4]; they will soon know. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4].  “False hope” refers to the delusion of prolonged worldly life, imagined security, and the assumption that accountability will never come. It includes becoming absorbed in ambitions, desires, and worldly pursuits that distract a person from the true purpose of life, rendering them heedless of the truth of Islam and negligent of the reality of death and the Hereafter. One of the greatest purposes for which Allāh created mankind — and among the greatest of life’s tests — is that they affirm His Oneness and worship Him alone without associating partners with Him.\n[5]. The phrase “they will soon know” conveys the certainty of a future realization — that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allāh and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allāh — when the truth becomes fully manifest and the consequences of rejection are unmistakably clear.",
  15004: "We never destroyed any town except that it had a known and appointed decree.",
  15005: "No nation can hasten its appointed time, nor can it delay it.",
  15006: "They say, “O you to whom the Reminder has been sent down — you are truly possessed.”",
  15007: "Why do you not bring us the angels, if you are indeed truthful?”",
  15008: "We do not send down the angels except with the Truth [6]; and then they would not be granted respite.\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. “With the Truth” signifies that angels are sent only in accordance with God’s decisive decree and just purpose — whether to convey revelation or to execute judgment upon the disbelievers. Their visible descent in response to defiant demands would not function as a sign for negotiation or persuasion, but would instead signal the arrival of final reckoning, after which no further respite would be granted.",
  15009: "Indeed, it is We who have sent down the Reminder, and We will surely preserve it. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. This verse affirms the divine preservation of the Qur’an. God Himself guarantees its protection from distortion, alteration, addition, or omission — both in its wording and in its essential message. Unlike previous scriptures entrusted to human custodianship, the Qur’an remains safeguarded by divine decree, preserved through continuous memorization, careful transmission, and written record across generations. More than fourteen centuries have passed, and not a single word of it has been altered, despite persistent efforts by its opponents to undermine or challenge it.",
  15010: "We certainly sent messengers before you among the factions of earlier peoples.",
  15011: "No messenger came to them except that they mocked him.",
  15012: "Thus We let it [denial] enter the hearts of the guilty;",
  15013: "They will not believe in it, although the precedent of the former peoples has already been established.",
  15014: "Even if We were to open for them a gate into heaven and they continued ascending through it,",
  15015: "They would certainly say, “Our eyes have only been dazzled — rather, we are a people bewitched.”",
  15016: "We have placed constellations in the heaven and adorned it for the onlookers.",
  15017: "And We have protected it against every accursed devil,",
  15018: "except one who steals a hearing—then a bright, blazing flame pursues him.",
  15019: "As for the earth — We spread it out, placed upon it firm mountains, and caused everything to grow in precise measure.",
  15020: "And We have placed therein means of livelihood for you — and for those whose sustenance you do not provide.",
  15021: "There is nothing but that its treasures are with Us, and We do not send it down except in a known and measured decree.",
  15022: "And We send fertilizing winds, then send down water from the sky and give it to you to drink — and you are not its keepers.",
  15023: "Indeed, it is We who give life and cause death, and We alone are the Inheritors.",
  15024: "We certainly know those who have preceded you, and We know those who will come afterward. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. Allāh knows every generation — those who lived before, those living now, and those yet to come. Not a single soul exists except that Allāh knows its appointed term and its place. Others interpret the verse to mean that Allāh knows those who advance toward good deeds and those who hold back from them.",
  15025: "Indeed, your Lord — He alone will gather them [for Judgment]. Surely, He is All-Wise, All-Knowing.",
  15026: "We have created man from sounding clay, from dark, altered mud. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8] “Sounding clay” refers to dry clay that produces a sound when struck, while “dark, altered mud” describes clay that has been shaped and changed over time. The verse highlights the humble earthly origin of man, in contrast to the jinn who were created from fire.",
  15027: "And the jinn [9] We created before that from a scorching fire.\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. The jinn are a distinct creation of God, created before man from a scorching, penetrating fire (nār as-samūm). They are not humans, nor a species of humans, but a separate order of beings. Like humans, they possess intellect, free will, and moral responsibility. Among them are those who are righteous and those who are wicked.",
  15028: "And [remember] when your Lord said to the angels, “Indeed, I am creating a human being from sounding clay—from dark, altered mud.”",
  15029: "So when I have fashioned him and breathed into him of My spirit [10], then fall down before him in prostration.\\n\\nFootnotes: [10] “My spirit” does not imply that Adam shares in divinity. Rather, it refers to a created spirit that God honored by attributing it to Himself — a form of distinction and elevation. The spirit is a creation of God, bestowed upon Adam as the source of life and consciousness.",
  15030: "So the angels prostrated — all of them together,",
  15031: "except Iblīs — he refused to be with the prostrating ones.",
  15032: "He said, “O Iblīs, what is the matter with you that you are not among those who prostrated?”",
  15033: "He said, “I am not one to prostrate to a human You created from sounding clay — from dark, altered mud.”",
  15034: "He said, “Then get out of it — for you are indeed accursed.”",
  15035: "And indeed, the curse shall be upon you until the Day of Recompense.",
  15036: "Iblīs said, “My Lord [11], grant me respite until the Day they are resurrected.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. This shows that Satan believes in Allah’s existence and power, but lacks obedience and submission.\nIt’s a faith of recognition, not of surrender — the faith of arrogance.\nIblīs asks to live until humanity is resurrected — i.e., until the Day of Judgment. His intention is not repentance, but revenge: to mislead and tempt the offspring of Adam until the end of time.\nHe did not ask for pardon, nor mercy, but only delay — that he might continue his mission of deceit and temptation against the children of Adam.\nThis reveals a profound truth: Even one accursed may call upon Allāh, but only repentance earns mercy; arrogance earns only delay, not forgiveness.\nArrogance can speak the language of worship, but without humility it brings only ruin. True faith requires submission, not mere recognition.",
  15037: "He said, “You are indeed among those granted respite, [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. Allāh grants Iblīs the delay he asked for, not as mercy, but as part of His decree and wisdom until the appointed time, to complete the test of faith for humankind. \nThus began the era of temptation, struggle, and choice — where truth and falsehood run side by side until the Day of Judgment.\nThis shows that Allāh may grant requests even from the wicked — not as favor, but to fulfill divine purpose.\nIt also shows His absolute sovereignty: even Iblīs’s lifespan is by Allah’s permission, not by his own power.",
  15038: "until the Day of the appointed and known time.” [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13] The “appointed time” refers to a moment fixed by divine decree and known only to Allāh. It indicates that Iblīs’ respite is limited and does not extend indefinitely; it lasts only until the time determined by Allāh.",
  15039: "He said, “My Lord, since You have let me to go astray, I will make sins appealing to them on earth [14], and I will mislead them all.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. “Make it appear fair” refers to the act of beautifying wrongdoing — presenting sin, disbelief, and disobedience as attractive, justified, or harmless. This is one of Satan’s primary methods: not coercion, but deception through persuasion and false embellishment. His statement that God “let” or “caused” him to go astray reflects a defiant attempt to shift blame for his own rebellion. In this, Satan set the precedent for using predestination as a pretext for disobeying Allah’s command.",
  15040: "Except Your sincere and devoted servants among them.",
  15041: "Allāh said, “This is a straight path that leads to Me.”",
  15042: "Indeed, as for My servants—you have no authority over them, except those who follow you from among the deviators.",
  15043: "And Hell is certainly the promised place for them all.",
  15044: "It has seven gates [15]; for each gate is an allotted portion of them — among Satan’s followers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [15] The “seven gates” are understood to represent divisions or levels within Hell, each assigned to different categories of its inhabitants according to their deeds and degree of disbelief or wrongdoing. The number emphasizes order and proportion in divine judgment.",
  15045: "Indeed, the righteous will be in gardens and springs.",
  15046: "“Enter it in peace and security.” [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. “In peace and security” signifies complete safety from fear, grief, harm, or expulsion. The people of Paradise enter in a state of tranquility, assured that their bliss is eternal. They are safe and secure from all that might diminish their happiness or disturb their sense of security.",
  15047: "We will remove whatever rancor is in their hearts [17]; they will be brothers, upon raised couches, facing one another.\\n\\nFootnotes: [17] “Rancor” (ghill) refers to any resentment, jealousy, bitterness, or hidden ill-feeling that may exist between people in worldly life. In Paradise, Allāh removes all such negativity from their hearts, purifying their inner states completely. Their relationships are marked by perfect harmony, sincerity, and brotherhood, free from rivalry or enmity.",
  15048: "No fatigue will ever touch them therein [18], nor will they ever be expelled from it.\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. “No fatigue” includes physical exhaustion, emotional strain, or any form of hardship. The bliss of Paradise is untainted by the drawbacks of toil, decline, or uneasiness about the future. Unlike worldly life — where comfort is mixed with effort, anxiety, and the fear of loss — the inhabitants of Paradise experience uninterrupted ease and complete security. Nor will they ever be expelled from it; their joy is eternal and free from all apprehension.",
  15049: "Inform My servants that I am truly the All-Forgiving, the Most Merciful,",
  15050: "and that My punishment is indeed the painful punishment.",
  15051: "And inform them about the guests of Abraham. [19]\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. Abraham (Ibrāhīm) is one of the major prophets in Islam. The name Ibrāhīm is commonly associated with meanings related to fatherhood and multitude. In Arabic:\n“ab” (أب) = father\n“hamon” may be expressed as (ثلة) or (عدد كثير من الناس) = multitude, many people\nAbraham’s original name was Abram, meaning “exalted father,” and it was later changed to Abraham, commonly understood to mean “father of many” or “father of a multitude.”\nThe “guests of Abraham” refers to the angels who came to him in human form. They brought him glad tidings of a son and later informed him of the impending punishment of the people of Lot. Their visit illustrates both divine mercy toward the righteous and divine justice upon the corrupt.",
  15052: "When they entered upon him and said, ‘Peace,’ he said, ‘Indeed, we are fearful of you.’ [20]\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. Since they came at a time when guests were not usually expected, he doubted their motives and wanted them to assure him of peace, which they promptly did.",
  15053: "They said, ‘Do not fear. We give you glad tidings of a knowledgeable son [Isaac].’",
  15054: "He said, ‘Do you give me glad tidings even though old age has overtaken me? Then what glad tidings are these?’",
  15055: "They said, “We have given you glad tidings in truth, so do not be among the despairing.”",
  15056: "Abraham said, “Who would despair of the mercy of his Lord except those who are astray?”",
  15057: "He said, “So what is your mission, O messengers?”",
  15058: "They said, “We have been sent to a guilty people,",
  15059: "except the household of Lot—We will surely save them all,",
  15060: "except his wife; We have  decreed that she will be among those remain behind. [21]\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. Despite being married to the prophet Lot, his wife did not believe in his message and sympathized with the corrupt practices of her people. This verse underscores a central Qur’anic principle: salvation is determined by faith and obedience, not by familial relationship. Her association with a prophet did not shield her from divine judgment.",
  15061: "When the messengers came to the household of Lot,",
  15062: "he said, “You are unknown people.”",
  15063: "They said, “Rather, we have come to you with that [punishment] about which they were doubting.”",
  15064: "We have come to you with the truth, and we are certainly truthful.",
  15065: "So set out with your family during a portion of the night, and follow behind them. Let none of you look back, and proceed where you are commanded.",
  15066: "We conveyed this decree to him: that the last remnant of these people would be cut off by morning.",
  15067: "And the people of the city came rejoicing.",
  15068: "Loṭ said, “These are my guests, so do not bring shame on me.”",
  15069: "Fear Allāh, and do not disgrace me.”",
  15070: "They said, “Did we not forbid you from [protecting] anyone?”",
  15071: "He said, ‘These are my daughters—if you seek lawful marriage.’ [22]\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. “These are my daughters, if you seek..” The Arabic expression in kuntum fāʿilīn (“if you are doers”) leaves the object unstated, with the intended meaning supplied by the context—namely, the men’s sexual intent toward Lot’s guests. Many exegetes understand “my daughters” not as a reference to his biological children, but to the women of his community, since a prophet is described elsewhere as being like a father to his people (cf. 33:6). The statement is thus read as a redirection toward lawful marriage rather than illicit assault.",
  15072: "By your life [O Muhammad], they were wandering blindly in their intoxication. [23]\\n\\nFootnotes: [23.] The Arabic laʿamruka is an emphatic oath meaning “By your life.” Scholars understand this as God swearing by the life of the Prophet Muhammad—an expression unique in the Qur’an. The phrase lafī sakratihim (“surely in their intoxication”) conveys a state of overwhelming delusion or moral stupor; while sakrah literally denotes intoxication, it is widely interpreted here as metaphorical. It reflects the immediate narrative context—namely, their obsessive sexual intent—rather than an explicit term in the Arabic. The verb yaʿmahūn signifies wandering blindly in confusion and heedlessness.",
  15073: "So the blast seized them at sunrise.",
  15074: "So We turned their cities upside down [24], making the highest part the lowest, and rained down upon them stones of baked clay.\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. The Arabic reads fa-jaʿalnā ʿāliyyahā sāfilahā, literally, “We made its upper part its lower,” an emphatic expression denoting total overturning and destruction. The singular feminine pronoun refers collectively to the towns of Lot’s people. The wording suggests not merely collapse but complete inversion. The term sijjīl is commonly understood to mean “baked” or “hardened clay,” describing stones sent as divine punishment.",
  15075: "Indeed, in this are signs for those who discern.",
  15076: "And indeed, they are situated on a well-traveled road.” [25]\\n\\nFootnotes: [25]. The phrase wa-innahā la-bi-sabīlin muqīm (“and indeed it is upon an established road”) refers collectively to the towns of Lot’s people, traditionally situated along the route to the Levant, in the region of the present-day Dead Sea. The expression sabīl muqīm denotes a fixed, well-known, or frequently traveled highway. Exegetes understood this to indicate that the ruins lay along a major trade route, visible to subsequent travelers, thereby reinforcing the narrative as an observable sign and enduring warning.",
  15077: "Indeed, in that is a sign for the believers.",
  15078: "And the people of the Thicket [26] were wrongdoers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. The term Aṣḥāb al-Aykah (“companions of the Thicket” or “People of the Wood”) refers to a community associated with the people of Midian, to whom the prophet Shuʿayb was sent (cf. 7:85; 26:176–189). The word aykah denotes a wooded area or dense grove. The description la-ẓālimīn (“surely wrongdoers”) emphasizes moral corruption and injustice, particularly in matters of commerce and social conduct as described elsewhere.",
  15079: "So We took vengeance from them, and both are situated along a manifest road. [27]\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]. The dual pronoun in innahumā (“indeed, the two of them”) is generally understood to refer to the ruins of the people of Lot and the People of the Thicket (associated with Midian). The expression bi-imāmin mubīn literally means “upon a clear/manifest path.” Although imām often signifies a leader or guide, it can also denote a clearly marked road. In this context, exegetes understood it to indicate that the remains of both communities lay along a well-known trade route, plainly visible to travelers, thereby serving as an enduring and observable warning.",
  15080: "Indeed, the people of the Stone Valley [Thamūd] also rejected the messengers.",
  15081: "We granted them Our signs, yet they kept turning away from them.",
  15082: "And they were carving from the mountains houses, secure.",
  15083: "Then the violent blast struck them at dawn.",
  15084: "So all that they had earned did not avail them at all.",
  15085: "We have not created the heavens and the earth and all that is between them except in truth. And indeed, the Hour is surely coming. So forgive them with a gracious forgiveness. [28]\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. The command “so pardon with a gracious pardon” has been described by exegetes as forgiveness in which there is no blame, reproach, or lingering resentment. It denotes overlooking offense with a pure heart—free from bitterness or vindictiveness. Such forgiveness entails patience, restraint, and moral composure toward those who oppose or mistreat one.\nThis injunction, however, does not imply passivity in the face of injustice. Islamic teaching consistently upholds the defense of the innocent and the obligation to stand firmly for truth and justice. Later revelations permitted armed struggle (jihād) under defined circumstances—not as aggression, but as a regulated means of resisting persecution and upholding moral order. Thus, the verse calls for personal magnanimity while affirming that justice ultimately belongs to Allāh and, where necessary, to lawful means of defense.",
  15086: "Indeed, your Lord—He alone—is the All-Creating, the All-Knowing.",
  15087: "We have certainly given you the seven oft-repeated [verses] and the great Qur’an.\n[29]\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. The phrase sabʿan mina al-mathānī (“seven of the oft-repeated”) is widely understood to refer to Sūrat al-Fātiḥah, the opening chapter of the Qur’an. It consists of seven verses and is recited repeatedly in the daily prayers, hence its description as “oft-repeated.” The term al-mathānī derives from a root meaning “to repeat” or “to recite again.” While some exegetes proposed broader interpretations—such as the seven long chapters or a category of recurrent revelation—the dominant view identifies it specifically with al-Fātiḥah. The verse thus highlights its special status alongside the Great Qur’an as a whole.",
  15088: "Do not look longingly at what We have allowed some of them to enjoy, nor feel distressed for them, and lower your wing [in humility] to the believers.",
  15089: "And say, \"Indeed, I am the clear warner.\"",
  15090: "Just as We had sent down [punishment] upon the confederates bound by oath to oppose the truth. [30]\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. The Arabic kamā anzalnā ʿalā al-muqtasimīn literally reads, “as We sent down upon the ones who divided.” The term al-muqtasimīn derives from taqāsamū, which can mean to divide among themselves or to apportion. Some exegetes understood this to refer to earlier communities—particularly certain Jews and Christians—who accepted parts of revelation while rejecting or altering others, thereby fragmenting divine guidance and dividing into sects and factions.\nOthers interpreted taqāsamū in the sense of forming a pact or swearing a collective oath (cf. 27:49), identifying al-muqtasimīn as confederates who bound themselves together in opposition to the Prophet and his message. The verse thus carries a broader warning against fragmenting revelation—whether by selective acceptance, distortion, or organized opposition. The object of what was “sent down” is left implicit, and is commonly understood to refer to punishment or judgment.",
  15091: "Those who made the Qur’an into [different] fragments. [31]\\n\\nFootnotes: [31]. The phrase jaʿalū al-Qurʾāna ʿiḍīn literally means “they made the Qur’an into pieces.” The term ʿiḍīn denotes fragments or separated portions. Exegetes differed regarding its precise referent: some held that it describes those who selectively accepted certain passages while rejecting others; others understood it to refer to opponents who labeled parts of the Qur’an as poetry, magic, or tales of the ancients, thereby fragmenting its unity. The verse thus warns against dividing or distorting revelation rather than upholding it in its entirety.",
  15092: "So, by your Lord, We will surely question them all.",
  15093: "About all that they used to do.",
  15094: "So proclaim openly what you are commanded, and turn away, [O Prophet], from the polytheists.",
  15095: "Indeed, We are sufficient for you against the scoffers.",
  15096: "Those who set up alongside Allāh another god — they will soon know.",
  15097: "And We surely know that your chest is tightened by what they say.",
  15098: "So glorify your Lord with praise, and be among those who prostrate to Him.",
  15099: "and worship your Lord until the certainty [of death] comes to you. [32]\\n\\nFootnotes: [32]. The command to “worship your Lord until there comes to you the certainty” has been widely understood as a call to lifelong devotion, since “the certainty” is interpreted by exegetes as referring to death—the inevitable end appointed for every soul. The verse thus enjoins steadfast adherence to revelation until life’s final moment. Following the Qur’an and the Sunnah—the teachings and example of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ—until the end of one’s life represents the most complete and noble conclusion to one’s earthly journey. A faithful and graceful perseverance through life’s trials bears witness to the truth and beauty of Islam as a religion of submission, peace, and constancy in worship. Such a life leaves behind a legacy of integrity and inspiration: the remembrance of one who worshipped Allāh alone, without associating partners with Him, and who took as his living example the final Messenger. In this way, faith, worship, and righteous conduct render a person’s life itself a testimony—that Islam is not only a creed to affirm, but a way of life to be lived with steadfast devotion until the very end.",
  16001: "The command of Allāh is coming [1], so do not seek to hasten it; Glory be to Him, and Highly Exalted is He above what they associate with Him. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. The “Command of Allāh” refers to the inevitable fulfillment of His decree — particularly the coming of the Day of Judgment, when truth will prevail, falsehood will perish, and every soul will face the consequence of its deeds.\nFor the idolaters and deniers of truth, this Day will come swiftly, even if they mock its delay.\n[2]. The verse immediately warns against polytheism — the gravest of all sins, and the only one for which forgiveness is denied if unrepented. After mentioning the imminence of the Hour, Allāh draws attention to this offense to show that associating partners with Him is not only irrational but a direct violation of His majesty. The verses that follow expand on this warning — calling humankind to pure monotheism: to worship Allāh alone, without partner, rival, or intermediary.",
  16002: "He sends down the angels with the inspiration of His command upon whom He wills of His servants, [saying], “Warn that there is no god except Me; so fear Me.”",
  16003: "He created the heavens and the earth for a true purpose. Exalted is He above what they associate with Him. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. The clearest and most indisputable sign of Allah’s supreme Power is His creation —particularly the vast creation of the heavens and the earth, the mightiest realms within human perception, which none besides Him is able to bring into existence or sustain.",
  16004: "He created man from a sperm-drop; then behold [4], he is a manifest opponent. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. The expression “then, behold” (Arabic: fa-idhā) conveys suddenness and striking contrast. It highlights the unexpected transformation from a humble biological origin (nuṭfah, a minute drop of fluid) to a being capable of self-awareness, speech, and argumentation. The rhetorical force underscores irony: from insignificance to bold disputation.\n[5]. “A manifest opponent” translates khaṣīmun mubīn. Khaṣīm refers to one who argues, disputes, or litigates; mubīn means clear, evident, or openly displayed. Together they describe a person who openly contends and argues against the truth—often understood as disputing divine revelation or denying resurrection (cf. 36:77). In context, it refers particularly to the idolaters and deniers who reject the Hereafter, argue against the Truth, and show ingratitude for Allah’s favors. The phrase underscores the human propensity toward defiance and opposition despite originating from a humble beginning.",
  16005: "And the livestock He created for you; in them is warmth and other benefits, and from them you eat.",
  16006: "And for you [O people] there is beauty in them when you bring them [home] to rest, and when you send them out [to pasture].",
  16007: "And they carry your heavy loads to a land which you could not reach except with great difficulty to yourselves. Indeed, your Lord is Ever-Kind, Most Merciful.",
  16008: "And [He created] horses, mules, and donkeys for you to ride and as adornment. And He creates other things you do not know. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. “And He creates other things you do not know” broadens the scope beyond the animals specifically mentioned. Commentators understood this to include creatures and means of benefit unknown to people at the time—whether other animals, natural phenomena, or future forms of transport and utility. The phrase emphasizes the vastness and continuity of divine creative power, extending beyond present human knowledge and experience. It also serves as a reminder that human awareness is limited, while Allāh’s creative act is ongoing and unrestricted.",
  16009: "It is upon Allāh to show the moderate, balanced path [7]. Yet among it are deviant [8]. Had Allāh willed, He could have guided you all aright.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. The Straight Path in Islam is one that is free from all extremes and excesses.\nIt represents pure Islamic Monotheism — guidance for all humankind — showing what is lawful and unlawful, good and evil.\nWhoever accepts this guidance does so for his own benefit, and whoever turns away does so only to his own loss and ruin.\n[8]. The deviant paths refer to those systems and beliefs that have strayed from true monotheism, such as paganism, Judaism, and Christianity, which altered or deviated from the original message of their prophets.",
  16010: "It is He who sends down water from the sky: from it you drink, and by it grow the trees in which your livestock graze.",
  16011: "By it He causes crops, olives, palm trees, grapevines, and all kinds of fruits to grow for you. Indeed, in this is a sign for people who reflect. [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. The verse draws attention to the many forms of vegetation that Allāh brings forth through rain: crops for nourishment, olives for oil and food, palm trees for sustenance and shelter, grapevines for fruit and cultivation, and countless other fruits that benefit humanity. The phrase “for you” emphasizes that these blessings were created for human benefit and survival, reflecting divine mercy and provision. The diversity, order, and continual renewal of plant life point to wisdom, power, and purposeful design in creation. The verse concludes by stating that “in this is a sign for people who reflect,” indicating that these natural phenomena are not merely ordinary processes, but signs meant to awaken thought, gratitude, and recognition of the Creator. Reflection upon the balance of nature, the dependence of life upon water, and the abundance produced from the earth leads the thoughtful person to awareness of Allāh’s sustaining care and creative power.\nTruly, the blessings and favors of Allāh are beyond limit — encompassing every age, every people, and every heart that turns to Him in faith.",
  16012: "He has subjected to you the night and day, the sun and moon, and the stars are subjected by His command. Indeed, in this are signs for a people who understand. [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. The verse describes how Allāh has “subjected” the night and day, the sun and moon, and the stars for the benefit of humanity. The alternation of night and day provides rest and activity, while the sun and moon regulate light, time, seasons, and growth. The stars also serve purposes of beauty, navigation, and cosmic order. The repeated idea of subjection emphasizes that these vast heavenly bodies do not operate independently or randomly; rather, they function according to the command and decree of Allāh. Their precision, harmony, and consistency are signs of divine wisdom and power.\nThe verse concludes by stating that “in this are signs for people who understand,” indicating that reflection upon the order of the universe should lead thoughtful people to recognize the Creator. The Qur’an frequently calls attention to the natural world as evidence of divine authority, mercy, and purposeful design. The regular movement of the heavens, upon which human life depends, is presented not merely as a physical phenomenon, but as a sign inviting contemplation, gratitude, and faith.",
  16013: " And whatever He has spread out for you on the earth in varying colors—indeed, in this is a sign for people who take heed.",
  16014: "He is the One who subjected the sea, so that you may eat from it tender meat and bring forth from it ornaments you wear. And you see the ships sailing through it, so that you may seek His bounty and be grateful.",
  16015: "And He has set firm mountains in the earth, lest it shift beneath you, and made rivers and pathways, so that you may be guided,",
  16016: "And landmarks—and by the stars they find their way. [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. “And by the stars they are guided” refers to navigation by the stars, particularly for travelers in deserts and at sea. The “stars” indicates the constellations and prominent celestial bodies used for determining direction, such as the Pole Star. Together with landmarks, the verse points to natural terrestrial and celestial signs that facilitate human travel and orientation. The wording underscores divine provision in creation: guidance is embedded in the physical world, enabling movement, trade, and settlement, while also alluding more broadly to the theme of guidance itself.\nThroughout the sūrah, a wide range of blessings are presented in a deliberate and interconnected manner. These include the creation of the universe and human beings, the benefits of livestock, rain and agriculture, and the alternation of night and day along with the sun and the moon. The diversity of creation, the blessings of the sea, and the stabilizing role of mountains all point to a carefully ordered world. Roads and navigation facilitate human movement, while the revival of dead earth signifies renewal and life. Everyday provisions such as milk and honey—especially noted for its healing—further demonstrate divine care. Human life is also enriched through family, provision, and the faculties of hearing, sight, and understanding.\nIn addition, Allāh provides homes, shelter, shade, and protection, ensuring both comfort and security. Above all, guidance through revelation completes these blessings, accompanied by warnings to awaken human responsibility. All of these signs culminate in a central principle:\n“And whatever blessing you have—it is from Allāh.”\nThus, every blessing—whether material or spiritual—points toward a single purpose: that human beings recognize their dependence upon their Creator and respond with sincere gratitude.",
  16017: "Is then He who creates [i.e., Allāh] like one who does not create? Will you not then take heed?",
  16018: "If you tried to count Allah’s blessings, you could never fully number them [12]. Truly, Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. “You could never fully number them” translates lā tuḥṣūhā, meaning “you would not be able to enumerate or encompass them.” Although niʿmat Allāh (“the blessing of Allah”) appears in the singular, it functions collectively, referring to the entirety of His favors—outward and inward, material and spiritual. The verse underscores both the immeasurable abundance of divine beneficence and the limits of human capacity to comprehend or repay it. It implies that Allāh alone deserves the deepest gratitude, for His blessings permeate every dimension of life: forgiveness of sins, healing of hearts and bodies, the gift of love and compassion, and the countless provisions embedded within creation. Even when not every sorrow or trial finds full relief in this life, gratitude renews the heart, for divine mercy and love remain constant. The closing affirmation—All-Forgiving, Most Merciful—reminds that despite human shortcomings in thankfulness, His pardon and compassion are ever-present.",
  16019: "Allāh knows whatever you conceal and whatever you reveal.",
  16020: "But those whom they call upon [13] besides Allāh create nothing, and they themselves are created.\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. “Those whom they call upon besides Allāh ” refers to the false deities invoked in place of Allāh —whether idols carved by human hands, celestial beings, angels, prophets, saints, or imagined powers. The verse presents a stark contrast: while Allāh alone creates, these objects of worship create nothing; rather, they themselves are created. The argument is both religious and rational—what is brought into existence, fashioned, or dependent cannot possess divine authority. The wording exposes the inconsistency of attributing power, benefit, or intercession to beings that are themselves contingent and powerless.",
  16021: "They are dead, not living, and they do not know when they will be resurrected.",
  16022: "Your God is One God. Yet those who do not believe in the Hereafter—their hearts reject the truth, and they are arrogant. [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. “Their hearts reject the truth, and they are arrogant ” translates qulūbuhum munkirah wa hum mustakbirūn. The phrase conveys scornful refusal to believe in the Oneness of God and to accept the Truth. The term munkirah denotes inward rejection or deliberate refusal to acknowledge what is true; the denial is rooted in the heart rather than mere intellectual uncertainty. Mustakbirūn describes those who act with arrogance or self-exaltation—consciously elevating themselves above submission. The verse thus links disbelief in the Hereafter with pride: the heart rejects accountability, and the ego resists humility before Allāh, the One and only true God.",
  16023: "Without doubt, Allāh knows whatever they conceal and whatever they reveal. Indeed, He does not like the arrogant.",
  16024: "When it is said to them, “What has your Lord sent down?” they say, “Legends of the ancients.”",
  16025: "So that they may bear their burdens in full on the Day of Resurrection, and some of the burdens of those whom they lead astray without knowledge [15]. Indeed, evil is what they bear.\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. The Prophet (ﷺ) said: “Whoever calls to misguidance will incur as much sin as that of whoever follows him.”.",
  16026: "Those before them had also plotted, but Allāh struck their buildings at the foundations, so the roof collapsed upon them from above, and the punishment came upon them from where they did not expect.",
  16027: "Then on the Day of Resurrection He will disgrace them and say, “Where are My so-called partners whom you used to associate with Me in worship and for whose sake you opposed [the prophets and believers]?” Those who were given knowledge will say, “Indeed, disgrace today and evil are upon the disbelievers.”",
  16028: " Those whom the angels take their souls in death while they are wronging themselves—then they will offer submission, saying, “We were not doing anything wrong.” But the angels will reply, “Yes indeed—you were. Surely Allāh is fully aware of what you used to do.”",
  16029: "So enter the gates of Hell, abiding therein forever. How miserable indeed is the dwelling place of the arrogant.",
  16030: "When it is said to those who are mindful of Allāh, “What has your Lord sent down?” they reply, “All that is good.” For those who do good in this world is good, and surely the Home of the Hereafter is far better. And excellent indeed is the abode of the righteous.",
  16031: "Gardens of Eden they will enter, beneath which rivers flow. There they will have whatever they desire. Thus does Allāh reward the righteous.",
  16032: "Those whose souls the angels take while they are good and pure [16], saying, “Peace be upon you. Enter Paradise for what you used to do.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. “While they are good and pure” denotes those who are spiritually sound, morally upright, and free from corruption in faith and conduct. The term suggests purity of belief alongside integrity of character at the time of death. In contrast to those earlier described as “wronging themselves” (16:28), these souls depart in a state of righteousness and are received with reassurance rather than rebuke. The angels’ greeting—“Peace be upon you”—signifies safety, divine approval, and freedom from fear or grief. The command, “Enter Paradise for what you used to do,” affirms the principle of just recompense: their admission is a gracious reward corresponding to their deeds.",
  16033: "Are the disbelievers waiting for anything other than the angels to come to them, or for your Lord’s command to be carried out? So did those before them. Allāh did not wrong them, but they wronged themselves.",
  16034: "So the evil consequences of what they had done struck them, and they were surrounded by the very thing they used to mock.",
  16035: "Those who associate partners with Allāh say, “Had Allāh willed, we would not have worshipped anything besides Him—neither we nor our fathers—nor would we have forbidden anything apart from Him.” Thus did those before them. Is there upon the messengers except the clear conveyance [of the message]?",
  16036: "Indeed, We sent into every community a messenger, [saying], “Worship Allāh and shun false deities.” Then among them were those whom Allāh guided, and among them were those upon whom misguidance was justified. So travel through the land and observe how was the end of the deniers.",
  16037: "Even though you are eager for their guidance, indeed Allāh does not guide those whom He leaves to stray [17], and they will have no helpers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. “Allāh does not guide the one He lets go astray” expresses a recurring Qur’anic principle concerning guidance and misguidance. Divine guidance is granted to those who are open to truth and willing to respond, while those who persistently reject clear signs are left to the course they have chosen. The phrasing does not suggest arbitrary misguidance; rather, it reflects a just consequence of sustained denial. The closing clause—“and for them there are no helpers”—emphasizes that once deprived of divine guidance, no external power can rescue or intercede on their behalf.",
  16038: "They swear by Allāh their most solemn oaths that Allāh will not resurrect those who die. Yes indeed- It is a true promise upon Him [18], but most of the people do not know.\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. “It is a true promise upon Him” affirms the certainty of resurrection. The expression signifies a promise that Allāh has assured and established by His own will and decree. It reflects the consistency of divine justice and wisdom—resurrection is necessary for the fulfillment of accountability and recompense. The concluding phrase, “but most of the people do not know,” points to widespread ignorance or denial of this foundational truth despite its clear proclamation.\nThis meaning is reinforced in a well-known prophetic narration. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ related that Allāh said: “The son of Adam has accused Me of lying, and he has no right to do so; and he has insulted Me, and he has no right to do so. His accusing Me of lying is his saying: ‘He will not recreate me as He created me at first,’ whereas the first creation is no easier for Me than recreating him. And his insulting Me is his saying: ‘Allāh has taken a son,’ while I am the One, the Self-Sufficient; I beget not, nor was I begotten, and there is none comparable to Me.”\nThe hadith underscores two central religious principles affirmed in the verse: the absolute power of Allāh to resurrect after death, and that He is above any human traits. Denial of resurrection is thus portrayed not merely as doubt, but as a rejection of divine power and truthfulness.",
  16039: "So that He may make clear to them what they used to differ about, and so that those who disbelieved may know that they were liars.",
  16040: "Indeed, Our word to a thing, when We will it, is only that We say to it, “Be,” and it is. [19]\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. Allah's Absolute Glory and Power is highlighted here. The realization of His ability to create are carried out by His word, 'Be' and it is.\n“Be, and it is” translates kun fa-yakūn, a concise Qur’anic formula expressing the immediacy and absolute efficacy of divine will. The phrase does not imply speech in a human or temporal sense; rather, it signifies that whenever Allah wills something, its existence follows without resistance, delay, or effort. The construction emphasizes exclusivity—“Our word… is only”—indicating that no intermediary or struggle is involved in creation. The verse affirms God’s unrestricted creative power, applying equally to the original creation and to resurrection, both of which occur solely by His command.",
  16041: "Those who emigrated for the sake of Allāh after being persecuted—We will surely grant them a good dwelling in this world [20], and the reward of the Hereafter is far greater, if only they knew,\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. “We will certainly settle them a good place in the world” translates a promise of gracious settlement (lanubawwiʾannahum fī al-dunyā ḥasanah), signifying secure residence, honor, and provision after hardship. The verse is commonly understood to refer to the early believers who emigrated (Hijrah) from persecution, particularly from Makkah to Madinah. It affirms that sacrifice made for the sake of Allāh is not lost: worldly stability may follow affliction, yet the reward of the Hereafter surpasses any temporal compensation. The clause “if they only knew” underscores that the true magnitude of divine recompense exceeds present human understanding.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“Islam began as something strange, and it will return to being strange as it began, so glad tidings are for the strangers.”\nIn other narrations, the Prophet ﷺ explained: “They are those who rectify what the people have corrupted.”\nAnd in another wording: “They are a small group of righteous people among many who oppose them.”",
  16042: "those who were patient and relied upon their Lord. [21]\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. Ṣabr (“patience”) signifies steadfast endurance in the face of hardship, persecution, and faithful obedience to Allāh. Tawakkul (“reliance”) denotes complete trust and dependence upon Allāh, recognizing Him as the ultimate disposer of all affairs. The pairing of patience and reliance reflects a central Qur’anic ethic: perseverance amid difficulty combined with unwavering confidence in divine wisdom and support. Those described here trust that their Protector and Savior is none but Allāh alone. Placing one’s trust in Allāh is the foundation of all righteous deeds; no sincere act can truly exist or reach completion without it. Indeed, reliance upon Allāh represents one of the highest expressions of sincere faith.",
  16043: "We did not send before you except men to whom We revealed. So ask the people of the Reminder [22], if you do not know.\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. “The people of the Reminder” translates ahl al-dhikr, commonly understood as those who possess previous revelation and scriptural knowledge. In its immediate context, the phrase answers objections to human messengers by affirming that all earlier prophets were likewise human beings who received revelation. More broadly, it establishes the principle that matters of revealed knowledge should be referred to those grounded in it. It also affirms the continuity of divine revelation through human messengers and the legitimacy of seeking guidance from qualified scholars when one lacks knowledge.",
  16044: "[We sent them] with clear proofs and revealed scriptures. And We have sent down to you the Reminder [23] so that you may clarify to the people what has been sent down to them, and so that they may reflect.\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. “The Reminder” translates al-dhikr, a term used in the Qur'an to denote divine revelation, particularly the Qur’an itself. The word conveys the sense of a message that reminds humanity of truths already embedded in their natural disposition: the oneness of God, moral responsibility, and the reality of the Hereafter. The Qur’an is called a “Reminder” because it continually brings to attention what people need for the betterment of their lives—guidance in faith, conduct, and social order—while repeatedly reminding them of Allāh Almighty and the Last Day. It is also understood as a source of honorable mention and elevated repute for those who uphold it.\nThe verse further affirms the Prophet’s role not merely as a transmitter but as an explainer—clarifying, exemplifying, and expounding what has been revealed. The concluding phrase, “that they may reflect,” emphasizes that revelation calls for thoughtful contemplation and conscious response, not mere passive hearing.",
  16045: "Do those who devise evil plots feel secure that Allāh will not cause the earth to swallow them, or that punishment will not come upon them from where they do not perceive?",
  16046: "Or that He may seize them with punishment in the midst of their movements or travels, leaving them no escape?",
  16047: "Or that He will seize them in gradual diminution while they are in fear. Indeed, your Lord is Most Gentle, Most Merciful.",
  16048: "Have they not considered all that Allāh has created—how their shadows incline to the right and to the left, prostrating to Allāh in humility?",
  16049: "To Allāh prostrates [24] whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth of living creatures—and the angels as well—and they are not arrogant\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. “Prostrates” (Arabic: yasjudu) signifies submission and obedience to divine authority. In Qur’anic usage, prostration is not confined to the physical act of worship performed by rational beings; rather, it encompasses the universal submission of all creation to Allah’s will. The prostration of rational beings consists of conscious worship, humility, and surrender to Allāh Almighty. The prostration of non-rational beings, however, lies in their conformity to the divine order and their execution of His command without resistance.\nScholars have described prostration to Allāh as being of two kinds. The first is a prostration of necessity (sujūd al-qahr), which reflects the inherent subjection of all created things to His dominion and is a manifestation of His perfect attributes of power and sovereignty. This type is universal, encompassing all creatures—believers and unbelievers, righteous and rebellious alike. The second is a prostration\nof willingness (sujūd al-ṭāʿah), which consists of conscious, voluntary worship and is particular to Allah’s believing servants, the angels, and others who submit in faith and obedience. The verse thus affirms both the universal lordship of Allāh and the special honor of those who submit willingly without arrogance.",
  16050: "They fear their Lord above them, and they do what they are commanded. [25]\\n\\nFootnotes: [25]. The verse describes the angels, emphasizing two defining qualities: reverent fear and absolute obedience. “They fear their Lord above them” expresses awe, humility, and consciousness of divine majesty—not fear born of uncertainty, but of profound recognition of His greatness and authority. The phrase “above them” affirms divine transcendence and supreme dominion. “They do what they are commanded” underscores their complete compliance with Allah’s will; they neither hesitate nor disobey. Together, these qualities portray perfect servitude—reverence coupled with unwavering execution of divine command.\nThis verse is also among the passages associated with prostration (sajdah al-tilāwah). It is reported that when the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ recited the Qur’an and came upon a verse of prostration such as this, he would prostrate, and those present with him would likewise prostrate in devotion. During this prostration, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would say:\n“My face has prostrated to the One who created it and fashioned for it hearing and sight by His might and power. O Allāh, record this prostration for me as a reward, erase from me a sin because of it, make it a treasure for me with You, and accept it from me as You accepted it from Your servant David.”\nThe practice highlights the believer’s humility before the Lord whose angels themselves exemplify perfect obedience.",
  16051: "Allāh said, “Do not take two gods [26]. He is but One God, so fear Me alone.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. This emphasizes pure monotheism (tawḥīd)—the belief in the absolute Oneness of God —and firmly rejects any form of associating partners with Him. The divine command, “Do not take two gods,” is not confined to literal dualism; rather, it is a rebuke against polytheism in all its forms, calling humanity to direct all reverence, obedience, and fear to Allāh alone.\nAllāh—God, the One and Only—is not triune. He is Unique, without partners or equals. To Him alone belongs the Dominion, and to Him alone is all praise.\nIt is noteworthy that the word “Trinity” is never mentioned even once in the Bible. The only reference often cited is the account of Jesus’ baptism in Matthew 3:13, 16–17, where the three are clearly distinct—thus negating the idea of “three in one.” The concept further collapses in Matthew 24:35–36: “Heaven and earth shall pass away, but my words shall not pass away. But of that day and hour knoweth no man, no, not the angels of heaven, but my Lord only.”\nOther translations even include the phrase, “Not even me,” referring to Jesus himself. This distinction confirms that Allāh alone is All-Knowing and All-Powerful, while His prophets—Moses, Jesus, and Muhammad (peace be upon them all)—were His chosen messengers and servants, not divine beings. God is One, a single, indivisible Being. To worship Jesus, his mother Mary, or any other creation as Allāh is to violate the very core of monotheism and directly contradict the First and Second Commandments found in Exodus 20:3–6 (KJV): “Thou shalt have no other gods before Me. Thou shalt not make unto thee any graven image, or any likeness of any thing that is in heaven above, or that is in the earth beneath, or that is in the water under the earth.”\nThis command clearly forbids idolatry—a practice sadly seen in some churches that display painted images of “Jesus” or statues of the crucifixion. Such portrayals contradict the spirit of divine revelation and the purity of worship that belongs to Allāh alone, the Ever-Living, the Eternal, Who neither begets nor is begotten.",
  16052: "To Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and the earth, and constant devotion is due to Him alone. Will you then fear anyone other than Allāh?",
  16053: "Whatever blessing you have—it is from Allāh. Then when adversity touches you, it is to Him alone that you cry out. [27]\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]. The verse affirms two foundational realities: every blessing originates solely from Allāh, and in moments of hardship, human beings instinctively turn to Him alone. The term niʿmah (blessing) encompasses all forms of favor—health, wealth, safety, guidance, and inner tranquility. None of these arise from human power or merit, nor from independent secondary causes; rather, they are manifestations of Allah’s mercy and generosity.\nWhen adversity or suffering strikes, the heart naturally turns away from all others and cries only to Him—the One who truly hears and responds. This instinctive appeal reveals the innate recognition of tawḥīd (the Oneness of God) embedded within every soul. Even those who associate partners with Him call upon Him alone in times of distress. Thus, the verse exposes both Allah’s benevolence in prosperity and His sovereignty in adversity, teaching gratitude in ease and reliance in hardship. It calls the believer to conscious thankfulness for blessings and sincere dependence upon Allāh in every circumstance.",
  16054: "Then when He removes the adversity from you, at once a group among you associates others with their Lord. [28]\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. This exposes a recurring flaw in human behavior: ingratitude after relief.\nWhen struck by hardship, people turn sincerely to Allāh alone, crying out for help—acknowledging, even if momentarily, that only He can save them. But once the calamity is lifted and ease returns, a group among them reverts to associating others with their Lord—whether through idolatry, misplaced reliance, or ingratitude.\nIt is a moral and spiritual contrast: In distress, faith awakens; in comfort, heedlessness returns.\nThis is both a rebuke and a reminder—to maintain exclusive devotion to Allāh not only in times of need, but also in times of ease.",
  16055: "Thus they show ingratitude for what We have given them [29]. So enjoy yourselves—for soon you will know.\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. “Showing ingratitude” reflects the Qur’anic concept of kufr al-niʿmah—denial or misuse of divine blessings. In this context, ingratitude manifests in attributing blessings to others besides Allāh or failing to acknowledge Him as their sole source. The command “Enjoy yourselves” is not approval, but a rhetorical warning, conveying temporary indulgence before inevitable accountability. The concluding phrase—“for soon you will know”—signals the certainty of consequence: worldly enjoyment is fleeting, and ultimate knowledge will come through the realization of judgment.",
  16056: "They assign a portion of what We have provided them to those who know nothing [30]. By Allāh, you will surely be questioned about what you used to invent.\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. “Those who know nothing” refers to idols or false deities that possess neither benefit nor harm. This is a Qur’ānic rebuke of idolatry and ingratitude. Despite receiving every blessing from Allāh, people dedicate portions of His provision—whether in offerings, wealth, or sacrifices—to objects or beings that neither create, sustain, nor comprehend. The phrase underscores the utter futility of such devotion: these so-called deities are unable to respond.\nThe verse further condemns the fabrication of religious claims without divine authority. The oath “By Allah” intensifies the warning, affirming inevitable accountability for what was falsely attributed to Him. In contrast to powerless idols, Allāh alone is All-Knowing, the true Provider and Sustainer. The passage thus exposes both the irrationality of idolatry and the moral gravity of ingratitude toward the One from whom all provision flows.",
  16057: "They attribute daughters to Allāh—glory be to Him!—while for themselves they prefer what they desire [i.e., sons]. [31]\\n\\nFootnotes: [31]. This exposes the irrational contradictions and injustices of the pagan Arabs’ beliefs. They claimed that the angels were the “daughters of  God ”, yet at the same time considered daughters inferior to sons for themselves.\nAllāh refutes this absurdity with the majestic declaration: “Glory be to Him!” —\nan expression of absolute transcendence and freedom from all human attributes, limitations, and false associations.\nThis highlights two layers of error:\nA. Religious error — ascribing offspring to Allāh, Who is far above such imperfection.\nB. Moral hypocrisy — disdaining for themselves what they falsely assign to the Creator.\nThrough this rebuke, the Qur’an affirms that Allāh is beyond gender, lineage, or need.\nHe neither begets nor is begotten, and nothing in creation is comparable to Him.",
  16058: "When one of them is informed of [the birth of] a baby girl, his face darkens with gloom, while he suppresses his anguish inwardly.",
  16059: "He hides himself from the people because of the “bad” news he has received. Should he keep her in humiliation, or bury her in the dust? Evil indeed is their judgment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [32]. This completes the powerful depiction of pre-Islamic ignorance and moral decay.\nWhen told of the birth of a daughter, the man’s face darkens, and now, as described here, he withdraws in shame, struggling with his misguided pride. He hides himself from society, torn between two cruel choices:\nA. To keep her alive in humiliation, treating her as a burden and a source of disgrace, B. To bury her alive in the dust, committing an unspeakable act of injustice and cruelty.\nThe Qur’an concludes with a piercing judgment: “Unquestionably, evil is what they judge.”\nThis closing statement is both a divine condemnation and a moral awakening—it exposes how utterly corrupt human values become when detached from the guidance of Allāh.",
  16060: "Those who do not believe in the Hereafter possess the attribute of evil, while to Allāh belongs the highest attribute; and He is the All-Mighty, the All-Wise\\n\\nFootnotes: [33]. This draws a profound contrast between the falsehood of disbelief and the perfection of divine reality. Those who deny the Hereafter are characterized by corrupt reasoning, distorted values, and moral blindness — their worldview and character reflect “the example of evil.” They attribute to creation what belongs to the Creator, fabricate lies about Allāh, and chase fleeting worldly illusions while denying eternal truth. In contrast, to Allāh belongs “the Highest Attributes” meaning the perfect qualities of majesty, purity, mercy, and wisdom, free from every imperfection and beyond all comparison.",
  16061: "If Allāh were to take people to task immediately for their wrongdoing, He would not leave a single living being on earth. But He grants them respite until an appointed term. When their term comes, they can neither delay it nor bring it forward, even for a moment.",
  16062: "They attribute to Allāh what they themselves dislike [34], and their tongues utter the lie that for them is the best [35]. Without a doubt, theirs is the Fire, and they will be abandoned to it. [36]\\n\\nFootnotes: [34]. That is, they claimed that the angels were the daughters of Allāh, while they themselves disliked having daughters. The verse exposes their inconsistency and moral hypocrisy: they ascribe to Allāh what they themselves consider inferior or undesirable. (see also 16:57–59; 37:149–153; 43:16–19).\n[35]. The Arabic term al-ḥusnā literally means “the best” or “the finest outcome.” It reflects their claim that, despite their disbelief and false attributions, they would receive the most favorable end—whether understood as divine favor, prosperity, or a blessed destiny in the Hereafter. The expression is deliberately general, emphasizing their presumptuous confidence in a good outcome.\n[36]. The term mufraṭūn derives from the root f-r-ṭ, which conveys the sense of being sent ahead, left behind, or abandoned. In this context, it signifies that they will be given over to the Fire—left to it without relief or rescue. The word thus carries the idea of judicial abandonment rather than mere neglect.",
  16063: "By Allāh, We certainly sent messengers to nations before you, but Satan made their deeds seem fair to them [37]. So he is their ally today [38], and for them is a painful punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [37]. The Arabic verb zayyana (“to adorn” or “make seem fair”) conveys the idea that Satan caused their wrongful deeds—such as disbelief, polytheism, rejection of the Messenger, and other evil acts—to appear attractive and justified in their eyes, thereby leading them to persist in error and misguidance.\n[38]. The Arabic term walī denotes a protector, patron, ally, or one who exercises authority over another. In this context, it indicates that Satan has become their ally and patron in misguidance, having been followed and obeyed by them. The expression carries an unexpected influence: instead of Allāh being their true Protector and Patron, they have taken Satan as their patron—a choice that leads only to punishment.",
  16064: "We have sent down to you the Book only so that you may clarify to them that over which they differ [39], and as guidance and mercy for a people who believe.\\n\\nFootnotes: [39]. That is, matters of belief and practice concerning which earlier communities fell into dispute—particularly the worship of Allāh alone (pure monotheism), the reality of the Resurrection, and the rulings of the Divine Law. The verse underscores the Qur’an’s function as a clarifying and decisive revelation in resolving religious disagreement.",
  16065: "Allāh sends down rain from the sky and revives the earth thereby after its death. Indeed, in this is a sign for people who listen.",
  16066: "Indeed, there is a lesson for you in the livestock: We give you drink from what is within their bellies—between digested matter and blood—pure milk, wholesome for those who drink. [40]\\n\\nFootnotes: [40]. The verse draws attention to the transformation by which milk is produced within the animal’s body—emerging pure and wholesome despite originating from substances associated with digestion and blood. The imagery emphasizes divine power and wisdom in bringing forth nourishment from unlikely origins.",
  16067: "And from the fruits of the date-palms and grapevines you derive intoxicants and wholesome provision [41]. Indeed, in this is a sign for a people who reason.\\n\\nFootnotes: [41]. The word sakar refers to intoxicating drink, particularly wine made from dates or grapes. At the time of revelation, intoxicants had not yet been prohibited; later verses (e.g., 2:219; 5:90–91) forbade them. The verse distinguishes between intoxicants and “good provision” (rizqan ḥasanan), which many exegetes understand as lawful and wholesome sustenance. This distinction subtly alludes to the presence of both harmful and beneficial aspects in certain things, inviting reflection on their proper use and moral evaluation.",
  16068: "Your Lord inspired the bees [42], saying: “Make homes in the mountains, the trees, and in the trellises that people construct.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [42]. The verb awḥā (“inspired”) here denotes a form of divine guidance instilled in the natural disposition of the bees, rather than prophetic revelation. The feminine imperative (ittakhidhī) reflects the Arabic grammatical convention in which “bee” (naḥl) is treated as feminine, and it may also allude to the predominance of female worker bees in hive activity.",
  16069: "Then eat from every kind of fruit and follow the paths of your Lord made easy for you [43]. From their bellies emerges a drink of varying colors wherein is healing for mankind [44]. Indeed, in this is a sign for a people who reflect.\\n\\nFootnotes: [43]. The word dhululan conveys the sense of paths made easy, smooth, and compliant. It reflects the divinely ordered instinct by which bees follow routes prepared for them, in complete submission to the natural law established by their Lord. The bees thus humbly follow the pathways Allāh has inspired for them—paths that render their lives orderly, beneficial, and perfectly aligned with His design. By contrast, human beings are endowed with choice; the verse subtly invites reflection on how different human life would be were people likewise to humble themselves and follow the path of Allāh rather than pursue misguided ways of their own making.\n [44]. The “drink” refers to honey. The phrase “in it is healing for people” is understood to indicate that honey possesses beneficial and therapeutic properties, without implying that it is a cure for every ailment. The verse highlights divine wisdom in placing nourishment and remedy within the natural world. Among the things described in the Qur’an as possessing healing are honey (16:69) and the Qur’an itself (17:82; 41:44).",
  16070: "Allāh created you, then takes you in death. And among you is one who is returned to the frailest stage of life [45], so that after knowledge he knows nothing. Indeed, Allāh is All-Knowing, All-Powerful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [45]. The phrase ardhal al-ʿumur literally means “the most abject (or lowest) stage of life.” It refers to extreme old age, when physical strength declines and mental faculties may weaken, such that a person who once possessed knowledge may lose memory and awareness. The expression underscores human frailty and the cyclical nature of life, contrasting human limitation with the enduring knowledge and power of Allāh.",
  16071: "Allāh has favored some of you over others in provision. Yet those who are favored will not share their provision with those whom their right hands possess so that they would be equal in it. Do they then deny the favor of Allāh? [46]\\n\\nFootnotes: [46]. The verse employs a rhetorical argument. Just as people who possess wealth are unwilling to make their dependents equal partners in their provision, how then can they attribute partners to Allāh in His dominion and favors? The point exposes the inconsistency of associating others with Allāh while recognizing human distinctions in ownership and authority. Denying “the favor of Allāh” thus refers to rejecting His exclusive lordship and beneficence.",
  16072: "And Allāh has made for you spouses from among yourselves, and through them He has granted you children and grandchildren, and provided for you good and wholesome things. Do they then believe in falsehood and deny the favor of Allāh? [47]\\n\\nFootnotes: [47]. Allāh reminds humanity that He created for them partners bringing companionship, comfort, and continuation of lineage, a sign of divine mercy and continuity of life — both a worldly joy and a means of maintaining faith and community. Despite all these gifts, some people believe in falsehood — idols, human ideologies, or false deities — and deny Allah’s grace, the very source of all these blessings.The verse exposes the contradiction of those who enjoy Allah’s favors yet turn away from His oneness. Gratitude demands both acknowledgment of the Oneness of God and worship of the true Benefactor, Allāh.",
  16073: "Yet they worship besides Allāh those who possess no power to provide for them anything from the heavens or the earth, nor are they able to do so. [48]\\n\\nFootnotes: [48]. The verse continues the argument from the preceding passages concerning divine provision. While Allāh alone grants sustenance from the heavens and the earth—through rain, crops, livestock, and all means of livelihood—the objects of their worship possess no power to bestow even the slightest provision. The rhetorical contrast exposes the irrationality of directing worship toward beings that neither own nor control what only Allāh provides.",
  16074: "So do not set up equals with Allāh [49]. Indeed, Allāh knows, and you do not know. [50]\\n\\nFootnotes: [49]. The word equals means rivals, peers, partners, or associates, i.e., anything made comparable to Allāh in worship, love, or obedience. It can refer to idols, saints, celestial bodies, or even desires that people elevate above divine truth. Since Allāh alone provides, sustains, gives life and death, and controls the universe, it is irrational to treat anyone or anything as His equal.\nLift up your eyes and look to the heavens: Who created all these other than Allāh? This verse dismisses the idea that any gods could be compared with Allāh - the true God. As there is nothing similar to Him, nor He resembles anything or comparable to Him.\n[50]. Out of ignorance and a failure to recognize Allāh’s true greatness, they assert similarities between Him and their false deities. The closing statement underscores the limitation of human understanding in contrast to divine knowledge. Since Allāh alone possesses perfect and complete knowledge, any attempt to define, compare, or liken Him to created beings is inherently flawed.",
  16075: "Allāh presents an example: a bonded slave who possesses no power at all, and one whom We have granted good and wholesome provision from Ourselves, so he spends from it, secretly and openly. Are the two equal? All praise is due to Allāh — but most of them do not understand. [51]\\n\\nFootnotes: [51]. No sane person would equate the two. Just as these two men cannot be equal — one powerless and owning nothing, while the other possesses ability, freedom, and provision — then how could they equate in worship the Creator and Owner of the entire universe with lifeless idols or false gods that possess neither benefit nor harm?\nAllāh alone is the Sovereign Provider and Disposer of all affairs; from Him alone come every favor, blessing, and grace. None possesses them besides Him, and there is no god worthy of worship except Him.\nThough this example is clear and self-evident, many people remain blind to it because of blind tradition, pride, or heedlessness.",
  16076: "Allāh sets forth the example of two men: one is mute and unable to do anything, a burden to his master. Wherever he sends him, he accomplishes nothing good. Can he be compared to one who enjoins justice and walks upon a straight path? [52]\\n\\nFootnotes: [52]. The “mute” and incapable man in the parable symbolizes one devoid of power, guidance, and benefit—whether understood as an idol, a false deity, misguided leaders, or individuals lacking moral direction. Being unable to produce good, he represents dependence and ineffectiveness. By contrast, “the one who commands justice and is upon a straight path” symbolizes truth, moral integrity, and divine guidance—ultimately pointing to Allāh, who alone perfectly commands justice, and secondarily to those who embody His guidance in conduct and action.\nThe broader lesson affirms that Allāh alone guides to truth and justice and is uniquely worthy of worship; there is none comparable to Him, and none deserving of devotion besides Him. The parable also carries a moral dimension: faith is not merely affirmation but must translate into action. True belief manifests in commanding good, upholding justice, and walking steadfastly upon the path of Allāh. The contrast invites personal reflection: is one a burden upon others, or a source of benefit?\nIn this connection, it is reported from the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ that “the best of people are those most beneficial to others,” highlighting the ethical imperative of living faith through service and justice.",
  16077: "To Allāh belongs the unseen of the heavens and the earth. The matter of the Hour is but like the blink of an eye—or even nearer. Indeed, Allah is powerful over all things.",
  16078: "Allāh brought you forth from your mothers’ wombs knowing nothing, and He gave you hearing, sight, and hearts so that you might be grateful. [53]\\n\\nFootnotes: [53]. The term rendered “understanding” translates al-afʾidah, literally “hearts.” In Qur’anic usage, the heart is not merely the seat of emotion but the center of perception, reflection, and moral awareness. The verse highlights the gradual endowment of human faculties—hearing, sight, and inner understanding—as signs of divine favor, intended to lead to gratitude and recognition of Allāh’s lordship. These faculties are entrusted to human beings so that they may reflect upon their creation, come to know Allāh as their Lord, give thanks to Him, and devote themselves to His worship alone.",
  16079: "Do they not see the birds made subservient in the open sky? None holds them aloft except Allāh. Indeed, in that are signs for a people who believe.",
  16080: "Allāh has made for you from your homes a place of rest, and from the hides of livestock He has made for you dwellings that you find light on the day of your journey and the day of your stay. And from their wool, fur, and hair He provides furnishings and benefits for a time.\\n\\nFootnotes: [54]. The verse reflects the pastoral and nomadic context of early Arabian life, in which dwellings made from the hides of livestock were easily assembled, transported, and re-erected during travel and settlement. The reference to wool, fur, and hair highlights the practical uses of animal products for furnishings and daily necessities. The phrase “for a time” underscores the temporary nature of worldly comforts in contrast to the permanence of the Hereafter.",
  16081: "Allāh has made for you, from what He created, shades; and from the mountains He has made for you shelters. And He has made for you garments that protect you from the heat [and cold] and garments that protect you from your own might. Thus does He complete His favor upon you, so that you may submit to Him.",
  16082: "But if they turn away, your duty is only to convey the message clearly.",
  16083: "They recognize the favors of Allāh, yet they deny it — and most of them are ungrateful.",
  16084: "On the Day when We raise up from every nation a witness [55], those who disbelieved will not be granted permission to offer excuses, nor will they be allowed to make amends.\\n\\nFootnotes: [55]. The “witness” is understood to refer to the prophet sent to each community, who will testify concerning his people’s response to the message. Some exegetes also include the angels or the believers as additional witnesses. The verse emphasizes the certainty of divine judgment and the accountability of every community before its appointed messenger.",
  16085: "When the wrongdoers see the punishment, it will not be lightened for them, nor will they be reprieved.",
  16086: "When those who associated partners with Allāh see their so-called partners, they will say, “Our Lord, these are the partners we used to invoke besides You.” But they will reply to them, “You are indeed liars.”",
  16087: " On that Day, they will submit fully to Allāh, and all that they used to invent will vanish from them.",
  16088: "Those who disbelieved and turned others away from the path of Allāh — We will increase them in punishment upon punishment for the corruption they used to spread.",
  16089: "And on the Day when We raise up among every nation a witness over them from themselves, and bring you as a witness over these, We have sent down to you the Book as clarification for everything, and as guidance and mercy and glad tidings for the Muslims. [56]\\n\\nFootnotes: [56]. The “witness” refers to the prophet sent to each nation, who will testify concerning his people’s response to the message. The Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ is brought as a witness over his own community and, by extension, over those to whom his message reached. The description of the Book as “an explanation of everything” (tibyānan li-kulli shayʾ) signifies its comprehensive clarification of matters necessary for guidance—belief, law, ethics, and ultimate accountability—rather than an exhaustive account of every worldly detail. It is described as guidance, mercy, and good news specifically for the Muslims because they are the ones who accept, follow, and benefit from its teachings.",
  16090: "Indeed, Allāh commands justice, excellence, and giving to close relatives [57], and He forbids indecency, wrongdoing, and oppression. He admonishes you so that you may take heed.\\n\\nFootnotes: [57]. This verse is regarded as one of the most comprehensive ethical statements in the Qur’an. It brings together three foundational commands—justice (ʿadl), excellence (iḥsān), and care for close relatives—and contrasts them with three prohibitions: indecency, wrongdoing, and oppression. Justice represents fairness and moral balance; excellence calls for going beyond obligation in benevolence and upright conduct; and giving to relatives emphasizes the strengthening of family and social bonds.\nThe “close relatives” include degrees of kinship beginning with parents, then offspring, followed by brothers and sisters, paternal uncles and aunts, maternal uncles and aunts, and then more distant relatives according to proximity. The verse thus establishes a structured ethic of responsibility that radiates outward from the immediate family to the broader community, forming a complete framework\nfor personal and social righteousness.",
  16091: "Fulfill the covenant of Allāh when you enter into a pledge [58], and do not break oaths after confirming them, for you have made Allāh a guarantor over you. Indeed, Allāh knows all that you do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [58]. The “covenant of Allāh” includes both formal agreements made in His name and the broader moral and religious commitments binding upon believers. Oaths were taken seriously in pre-Islamic and Islamic society, and invoking Allāh as a guarantor (kafīl) renders the pledge a matter of sacred accountability. The term signifies that Allāh stands as overseer and surety over the agreement; thus, breaking such oaths is not merely a social violation but a breach of one’s responsibility before Him.",
  16092: "And do not be like the woman who unraveled her spun thread after it had become strong, reducing it to loose strands, by taking your oaths as a means of deceit between you because one nation is more prosperous than another. Allāh only tests you thereby, and He will surely make clear to you on the Day of Resurrection that over which you used to differ. [59]\\n\\nFootnotes: [59]. The image of a woman unraveling thread after it has been firmly spun serves as a parable for the folly and moral ruin of breaking solemn commitments after confirming them. The phrase “into loose strands” renders the Arabic ankāthan, referring to fibers undone and scattered after having been tightly bound together. “Deceit” translates dakhala, conveying the sense of treachery and bad faith—using oaths as a means of manipulation, especially when one party is stronger, wealthier, or more numerous than another.\nThe verse warns against swearing falsely or breaking treaties and contracts for worldly advantage or political gain. Such situations are presented as a divine test of integrity: whether one will remain faithful to a binding pledge or abandon it in favor of a more appealing alliance with a stronger party. Allāh declares that all disputes, betrayals, and hidden motives will ultimately be exposed and clarified on the Day of Resurrection.",
  16093: "Had Allāh willed, He would have made you one community [of believers] [60]; but He leaves astray whom He wills and guides whom He wills. And you will surely be questioned about what you used to do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [60]. “One community” refers to unity in faith and guidance. The verse affirms that Allāh has the power to make all humanity adhere to one belief; however, in His wisdom He allows diversity in human response—guiding some and leaving others astray according to His perfect knowledge and justice. Allāh, through His boundless power and infinite wisdom, could have made all people believers, but such compelled faith would negate the reality of moral testing and accountability. Genuine faith arises through conscious choice, and thus human beings remain responsible for their deeds, as emphasized by the certainty of questioning on the Day of Judgment.",
  16094: "Do not take your oaths as a means of deceit among yourselves, lest a foot slip after having been firmly established, and you taste the evil consequences for turning others away from the path of Allah; and for you in the Hereafter is a great punishment.",
  16095: "Do not trade the covenant of Allāh for a small, fleeting gain. Indeed, what is with Allāh is far better for you — if only you knew.",
  16096: "Whatever is with you will be exhausted, but whatever is with Allāh will endure. And We will surely reward those who were patient according to the best of what they used to do.",
  16097: "Whoever does righteous deeds, whether male or female, while being a believer — We will surely grant them a good and wholesome life [61], and We will surely reward them according to the best of what they used to do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [61].  “A good and wholesome life” (ḥayātan ṭayyibah) has been understood to include inner contentment, lawful provision, tranquility of heart, and a life characterized by faith and moral integrity. It does not necessarily imply worldly wealth or ease, but rather a life enriched by divine blessing and spiritual satisfaction. The verse affirms that true well-being is rooted in faith and righteous action, while ultimate recompense awaits in the Hereafter.",
  16098: "So when you recite the Qur’an, seek refuge in Allāh from the accursed Satan.",
  16099: "Indeed, he has no influence over those who believe and rely upon their Lord. [62]\\n\\nFootnotes: [62].  “Authority” translates sulṭān, which denotes power, dominion, or compelling influence. The verse affirms that Satan has no coercive control over true believers who rely upon their Lord. His influence is limited to suggestion, whispering, and temptation; he cannot compel obedience. Protection from his insinuations is secured through faith and sincere reliance upon Allāh.",
  16100: "His influence extends only over those who take him as their protector and who, through him, associate partners with Allāh.",
  16101: "When We replace one verse [63] with another—and Allāh knows best what He sends down—they say, “You are but a fabricator.” But most of them do not know.\\n\\nFootnotes: [63]. This refers to occasions when Allāh replaced an earlier command or verse with another—always for reasons of wisdom, mercy, and circumstance.\nFor example, gradual revelation helped believers adjust spiritually and socially (such as the prohibition of wine revealed in stages). It highlights that revelation was dynamic, guiding humanity step by step, not a sudden imposition of all laws at once. The disbelievers, unaware of divine wisdom, accused the Prophet ﷺ of “making up” verses when they heard one law replaced by another.\nBut Allāh responds by affirming that He alone knows best what He sends down—meaning, revelation follows divine knowledge, not human reasoning.",
  16102: "Say, “The holy spirit [64] has brought it down from your Lord in truth, to strengthen those who believe, and as guidance and glad tidings for the Muslims.\" [65]\\n\\nFootnotes: [64]. “The holy spirit” (Rūḥ al-Qudus) is understood to refer to the angel Jibrīl (Gabriel), who conveyed revelation to the prophets by Allāh’s command. The term emphasizes purity and sanctity, underscoring that the Qur’an is of divine origin and not the product of human invention.\n[65]. The Qur’an is described as guidance and glad tidings specifically for “the Muslims” — those who submit to the will of God — because they are the ones who accept its truth and benefit from its instruction. While its message is universal, its guidance applies effectively to those who respond in faith and obedience.",
  16103: "We certainly know that they say, “It is only a human being who teaches him.” [66]\nYet the language of the one they refer to is foreign, whereas this is a clear Arabic language.\\n\\nFootnotes: [66]. The disbelievers alleged that the Prophet ﷺ was being instructed by a human teacher, possibly a foreigner residing in Makkah. The verse refutes this claim by pointing out the obvious contradiction: the person they alluded to spoke a foreign (aʿjamī) tongue, whereas the Qur’an is delivered in clear and eloquent Arabic. The argument highlights the linguistic inimitability of the Qur’an and exposes the weakness of their accusation.",
  16104: "Indeed, those who do not believe in the revelations of Allāh, Allāh will not guide them, and for them is a painful punishment.",
  16105: "Only those who do not believe in the revelations of Allāh fabricate lies, and it is they who are the liars.",
  16106: "Whoever disbelieves in Allāh after having believed — except one who is compelled while his heart remains firm in faith [67] — but whoever willingly opens his heart to disbelief, upon them is the wrath of Allāh, and for them is a great punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [67]. This verse distinguishes between outward compulsion and inward conviction. It was revealed in connection with early believers who were persecuted and forced to utter words of disbelief, among them ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir. The exception applies to one who is coerced while his heart remains firm and tranquil in faith; such a person is not held blameworthy. By contrast, “whoever opens his chest to disbelief” refers to one who willingly and inwardly embraces rejection of faith. The verse thus establishes a foundational principle in Islamic law: coercion removes moral culpability when faith remains sincere within the heart.",
  16107: "That is because they chose the life of this world over the Hereafter, and Allāh does not guide the people who disbelieve.",
  16108: "Those are the ones upon whose hearts, hearing, and sight Allāh has set a seal [68], and they are the heedless.\\n\\nFootnotes: [68]. “To set a seal” signifies the consequence of persistent disbelief and rejection. It denotes a state in which the faculties of perception—heart, hearing, and sight—become spiritually unresponsive to truth. The sealing is not arbitrary; rather, it reflects the result of deliberate preference for falsehood and heedlessness, as described in the preceding verses.",
  16109: "Without doubt, it is they who are the losers in the Hereafter.",
  16110: "As for those who emigrated after being persecuted, then strove hard and remained steadfast — surely, your Lord, after that, is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  16111: "On the Day when every soul will come pleading for itself, and every soul will be fully recompensed for what it did, and they will not be wronged.",
  16112: "Allāh sets forth an example: a town that was secure and tranquil, its provision coming to it abundantly from every place. But it became ungrateful for Allāh’s favors, so Allāh made it taste enveloping hunger and fear for what they used to do. [69]\\n\\nFootnotes: [69]. The “town” is understood by many exegetes to refer to Makkah, which had enjoyed security and abundant provision but responded with ingratitude and rejection of the Prophet ﷺ. The phrase “the garment of hunger and fear” translates libās al-jūʿ wa’l-khawf, a metaphor suggesting that hunger and fear enveloped them completely, as a garment covers the body. The verse illustrates the moral principle that prosperity accompanied by ingratitude and disbelief leads to deprivation and insecurity as a consequence of one’s deeds. It serves as a direct warning to the people: they are being warned against Divine Retribution in this worldly life as much as they have just been warned against the one in the Hereafter.",
  16113: "Indeed, a messenger from among themselves came to them [70], but they denied him; so the punishment seized them while they were wrongdoers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [70]. Many exegetes understand this to refer specifically to Makkah and the Prophet ﷺ, following the parable of the secure town mentioned in the previous verse. Others view it more generally as a recurring divine pattern: when a community rejects a messenger sent from among themselves, after having received clear warning, punishment follows as a consequence of their wrongdoing. The verse reinforces the principle that accountability comes only after guidance has been conveyed.",
  16114: "So eat of what Allāh has provided for you, lawful and wholesome, and be grateful for the favor of Allāh, if it is Him alone that you worship.",
  16115: "He has only forbidden you carrion, blood [71], the flesh of swine, and that which has been dedicated to other than Allāh [72]. But whoever is compelled by necessity — neither desiring it nor exceeding due limits — then surely Allāh is Most Forgiving, Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [71]. “Blood” refers to flowing blood that is drained from an animal at slaughter. Consuming blood as food was practiced in some pre-Islamic cultures, but it is explicitly prohibited in Islamic law. Blood that remains naturally within properly slaughtered meat is not included in this prohibition.\n[72]. “That which has been dedicated to other than Allāh” refers to animals over which a name other than Allāh’s was invoked at the time of sacrifice, or which were offered as part of idolatrous rites. The prohibition safeguards pure monotheism in acts of worship, including ritual slaughter, affirming that devotion and sacrifice belong to Allāh alone.",
  16116: "Do not say falsely, as your tongues describe, “This is lawful and this is unlawful,” thereby inventing lies against Allāh. Indeed, those who fabricate lies against Allāh will never succeed.",
  16117: "It is but a brief enjoyment, and for them is a painful punishment.",
  16118: "And upon the Jews in particular, We prohibited what We had previously related to you [O Prophet] [73]. We did not wrong them; rather, they wronged themselves.\\n\\nFootnotes: [73]. This refers to dietary restrictions previously mentioned in the Qur’an (cf. 6:146), including certain animals and fats that were made unlawful for the Jews. These prohibitions were not arbitrary but imposed as a consequence of their transgression and disobedience. The verse emphasizes that such restrictions were a form of just recompense; Allāh did not wrong them — rather, they wronged themselves through their actions.",
  16119: "As for those who commit evil out of ignorance, then repent afterward and reform, surely your Lord, after that, is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  16120: "Indeed, Abraham was a nation [in himself] [74], devoutly obedient to Allāh, upright, and he was not among the polytheists.\\n\\nFootnotes: [74]. That is, a leading paragon and shining example. Describing Abraham as “a nation” (ummah) signifies that he embodied within himself the qualities of an entire community devoted to truth. He stood alone in pure monotheism amid widespread idolatry, serving as a model of faith, obedience, and unwavering devotion. The term highlights both his moral stature and his role as a foundational exemplar for later believers.",
  16121: "He was grateful for His blessings; He chose him and guided him to a straight path [i.e., Islam].",
  16122: "We granted him good in this world, and indeed, in the Hereafter he will surely be among the righteous.",
  16123: "Then We revealed to you [O Prophet] to follow the faith of Abraham — inclining solely to truth — and he was not among the polytheists.",
  16124: "Indeed, the Sabbath was only instituted for those who differed concerning it [75], and indeed your Lord will surely judge between them on the Day of Resurrection concerning that over which they used to differ.\\n\\nFootnotes: [75]. The Sabbath was prescribed specifically for the Children of Israel, to dedicate a set day solely for worshiping Allāh apart from their daily livelihood. According to exegetical reports, Friday had originally been enjoined upon them, but they deviated from it, and the Sabbath was subsequently ordained. Disagreement arose among them concerning its proper observance and restrictions. The verse clarifies that this ordinance was not part of Abraham’s original monotheistic way, but a later command tied to a particular community and its circumstances. Final judgment regarding their disputes and conduct belongs to Allāh on the Day of Resurrection.",
  16125: "Invite [all people] to the way of your Lord with wisdom and gracious exhortation, and argue with them in the best manner. Indeed, your Lord knows best who strays from His path, and He knows best those who are rightly guided.",
  16126: "If you retaliate, then retaliate with the like of that you were afflicted with. But if you patiently indure, it is surely best for those who are patient. [76]\\n\\nFootnotes: [76]. This verse establishes the principle of proportional justice: retaliation is permitted only to the extent of the harm suffered, without excess. However, patience and restraint are declared superior. The verse thus balances lawful retribution with moral excellence, encouraging forgiveness and forbearance as higher virtues while upholding justice. Believers are therefore urged to rid themselves of bitterness, rage, anger, quarrels, slander, and every form of malice, and to cultivate kindness, compassion, and forgiveness toward one another, just as they seek Allāh’s forgiveness for their own sins.",
  16127: "So be patient, for your patience is only through Allāh. Do not grieve over them [77], nor be distressed by their schemes.\\n\\nFootnotes: [77]. The Prophet ﷺ is consoled not to grieve over the rejection of his people nor to be distressed by their plotting. True patience is sustained only through reliance upon Allāh, reminding the believer that guidance and ultimate justice belong to Him alone.",
  16128: "Indeed, Allāh is with those who fear Him, and those who excel in goodness. [78]\\n\\nFootnotes: [78]. Those who excel in their worship of their sole Creator — Allāh — are those who also strive to do good deeds.\nTherefore, be ever mindful of Allāh and keep your hearts focused on Him alone.\nYou must cleanse yourselves of all that He has forbidden: sexual immorality, impurity, lust, evil desires, greed, theft, and deceit.\nFor because of such sins, the wrath of Allāh descends upon those who persist in wrongdoing. Likewise, remove from your lives all traces of anger, rage, malice, slander, lying, and filthy speech.These corrupt the soul and darken the heart. Instead, follow the example of the Prophet — Muḥammad ﷺ, the final Messenger of God — and clothe yourselves with the virtues he embodied: compassion, kindness, humility, gentleness, and patience. Bear with one another in mercy, and forgive each other, if any of you has a grievance against another. Forgive as Allāh forgive you out of His boundless mercy.\nAnd above all these virtues, embrace love —for love binds them all together in perfect harmony and unity.\nThis verse beautifully captures the balance of Islam: Purity of worship through devotion and worship to Allāh alone, and purity of character through compassion and forgiveness toward others.\nTo worship Allāh truly is to reflect His mercy in our actions. Faith in the heart must blossom as goodness in the world.",
  17001: "Glory be to Allāh, Who took His servant [1] by night from the Sacred Mosque [in Makkah] to the Farthest Mosque [in Jerusalem], whose surroundings We have blessed [2] —so that We might show him some of Our signs. Indeed, He alone is the All-Hearing, the All-Seeing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. This refers to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, and it fulfills a divine prediction found in Psalm 118, revealed many centuries earlier to Prophet David.\nThat Psalm foretold the coming of the world’s final Prophet and Messenger — Muhammad ﷺ — and his blessed entry into Jerusalem. King David, who was given the Psalms (Zabūr), recorded Allah’s prophecy about how His final Messenger would one day be welcomed with open gates. As stated in Psalm 118:19, “Open for me the gates of the righteous; I will enter and give thanks to Allāh.” Later in Psalm 118:26, the blessing upon that Messenger is described: “Blessed is he who comes in the Name of Allāh; from the House of Allāh we bless you.” And in verses 21–23, the Psalm predicts the rejection and later exaltation of that chosen Messenger: “The stone the builders rejected has become the cornerstone.”\nThis refers to Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, who became the cornerstone of divine guidance after earlier nations had turned away.\nThe Night Journey (Al-Isrāʾ wa’l-Miʿrāj)\nAccording to authentic Sunnah, the Prophet ﷺ was taken by night on the miraculous steed al-Burāq — from the Sacred Mosque in Makkah to the Farthest Mosque in Jerusalem (Masjid al-Aqṣā).\nThis blessed event is known as al-Isrāʾ (the Night Journey), forming the first part of his heavenly ascension. Upon arriving in Jerusalem, the Prophet ﷺ prayed within Masjid al-Aqṣā alongside the earlier Prophets — Abraham, Moses, Jesus, and others — peace be upon them all.\nThe Angel Gabriel (Jibrīl) led him forward, and the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ led the other Prophets in prayer, signifying his role as the leader and Seal of the Prophets.\nAfterward, the Prophet ﷺ ascended through the heavens (al-Miʿrāj), where he witnessed the great signs of Allāh, met with previous Messengers, and was brought into the divine presence — a journey of honor, nearness, and revelation.\nThe Night Journey fulfills divine prophecy linking Makkah, Jerusalem, and Heaven.\nThe Psalms of David foretold the coming of the world’s final Messenger, Muhammad ﷺ.\nThe Prophet’s leadership in prayer with all Prophets symbolizes the unity of divine guidance.\nHis ascension (Miʿrāj) demonstrates that true servitude to Allāh leads to the highest spiritual nearness.\n[2]. The “surroundings” refers to the blessed land around Jerusalem—often understood as the region of Levant [al-Shām]—blessed through prophetic history, revelation, and abundant spiritual and worldly benefits. The phrase underscores the sacred status of the place and its connection to many prophets.",
  17002: "We gave Moses the Scripture and made it a guidance for the Children of Israel, [saying], “Do not take anyone besides Me as your Disposer of affairs.” [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. “Disposer of affairs” translates wakīl, which denotes one entrusted with managing, protecting, and overseeing one’s matters. The verse commands exclusive reliance upon Allāh, affirming that ultimate trust, guardianship, and dependence belong to Him alone. A believer is therefore not to entrust his ultimate affairs to anyone besides Allāh, while still making lawful use of worldly means. As the Qur’an elsewhere declares: “Put your trust in Allāh — and Allāh is sufficient as a Trustee.” (cf. 33:3)",
  17003: "O descendants of those whom We carried with Noah [in the Ark] — indeed, he was a grateful servant. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. Noah is described as shakūr (deeply grateful), highlighting gratitude as a defining trait of the righteous and the proper response to divine deliverance. By recalling Prophet Noah (peace be upon him), the Children of Israel are reminded of their ancestral connection to those saved in the Ark and are encouraged to follow his example in devotion, obedience, and thankfulness to Allāh.",
  17004: "And We decreed for the Children of Israel in the Scripture: “You will surely cause corruption in the land twice [5], and you will surely rise to extreme arrogance.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. For their disobedience, arrogance, and transgression, they spread corruption in the land—even to the point of slaying prophets and violating sacred trusts. The “corruption” refers to moral, social, and political wrongdoing manifested in defiance of divine guidance, oppression, and the breach of covenantal obligations. Exegetes identify two major periods of transgression in the history of the Children of Israel, each followed by divine punishment. The verse illustrates the recurring principle that arrogance and corruption ultimately lead to downfall.",
  17005: "Then, when the time of the first promise came, We sent against you servants of Ours possessing great military strength, and they swept through your homes. And it was a promise fulfilled. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. The “servants of Ours” refers to a powerful foreign force raised against them as a means of divine retribution. Exegetes differ regarding their precise historical identity, with many identifying them as the Babylonians under Nebuchadnezzar, though the Qur’ān does not specify. The emphasis lies on divine justice rather than historical detail.\n[7]. “A promise fulfilled” indicates that the punishment occurred exactly as decreed. The phrase underscores the certainty of divine judgment and the inevitability of consequences following corruption and arrogance.",
  17006: "Then We gave you back the turn over them and supported you with wealth and sons, and made you greater in number. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. The verse reflects the cycle of downfall and restoration: when the Children of Israel repented, Allāh lifted their affliction and revived their fortunes — a reminder that divine favor follows gratitude and obedience.",
  17007: "If you do good, you do good for yourselves; and if you do evil, it is against yourselves. Then, when the time of the second promise came [9], We sent them to bring grief upon your faces, to enter the Temple as they entered it the first time, and to utterly destroy all that they overpowered.\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. The “second promise” refers to the second major period of corruption and the corresponding punishment foretold earlier. Early exegetes commonly identify the first destruction with the Babylonians under Nebuchadnezzar (6th century BCE) and the second with the Romans under Titus (70 CE), who destroyed Jerusalem and its Temple. However, the Qur’an does not explicitly name these powers. The emphasis of the verse is not on historical detail but on the moral principle that persistent corruption and arrogance inevitably lead to downfall.",
  17008: "Your Lord may still have mercy upon you. But if you return to wrongdoing, We will return to punishment [10]. And We have made Hell a prison for the disbelievers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. After this severe punishment, Allāh granted them another opportunity by restoring their strength and sovereignty. But He also warned that if they returned to their former ways of rebellion and corruption, the consequences would return as well. The way of Allāh in dealing with nations does not change. Sin and defiance lead only to ruin and humiliation, while repentance, obedience, and gratitude bring security, prosperity, and blessing. This verse highlights a constant moral law: a community’s condition rises or falls according to its faithfulness to divine guidance.",
  17009: "Indeed, this Qur’an guides to that which is most upright [11] and gives glad tidings to the believers who do righteous deeds that they will have a great reward.\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. “That which is most upright” translates aqwam, meaning the straightest, most just, and most balanced path. The Qur’an guides in matters of belief, worship, morality, and social order, offering the soundest way of life in both spiritual and worldly affairs. A believer should therefore keep the Qur’an close—reciting it regularly, reflecting upon its meanings, and striving to live by its teachings. Through sincere engagement with it, one finds true success, stability, and blessing in this life and the next.",
  17010: "And that We have prepared for those who do not believe in the Hereafter a painful punishment.",
  17011: "Man invokes harm as he invokes good, for man is ever impulsive and hasty. [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. The verse highlights human impulsiveness — people often invoke things without foresight, sometimes asking for what would harm them out of impatience, anger, or despair. The Arabic term ʿajūl (“ever hasty”) emphasizes a deep-seated tendency in human nature to act or speak without reflection. In moments of frustration, a person may call down harm upon himself or others just as readily as he calls for good. Yet Allāh, in His mercy, does not hasten to answer such harmful invocations. The description contrasts human rashness with divine wisdom and patience.",
  17012: "We made the night and the day two signs [13]. Then We darkened the sign of the night and made the sign of the day radiant, so that you may seek the bounty of your Lord and know the number of years and the reckoning of time. And We have explained all things in detail.\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. The “two signs” refer to the alternating phenomena of night and day. The dimming of the night and the brightness of the day point to divine wisdom in the natural order—night for rest and reflection, and day for work and seeking provision. The verse highlights both spiritual reflection and practical benefit, as the cycle of time enables human beings to measure years and regulate their affairs. In this precise and orderly design is a clear sign affirming Allāh’s Oneness, His perfect Knowledge, and His absolute Power.",
  17013: "We have bound every person’s fate to his neck [14]; and on the Day of Resurrection We shall bring forth for him a record which he will find laid open.\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. The phrase “bound to his neck” signifies that every person’s deeds and their consequences are inseparably attached to him. The Arabic word ṭāʾir originally referred to an omen or one’s lot, but here it denotes the outcome of one’s actions—good or evil—firmly linked to the individual. The imagery conveys personal responsibility: no one can escape his record, nor can he transfer it to another. On the Day of Resurrection, each person will confront his own deeds openly and unmistakably.",
  17014: "“Read your record. Today, you yourself are sufficient as a reckoner against you.”",
  17015: "Whoever is guided is guided only for his own soul; and whoever goes astray goes astray only against it. No bearer of burdens shall bear the burden of another [15]. And never do We punish until We send a messenger.\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. This verse establishes two foundational principles: individual moral responsibility and divine justice. Every person bears the consequence of his own deeds, and no one carries the sin of another. Moreover, Allāh does not punish a people until clear guidance has reached them through a messenger, affirming that accountability follows the establishment of proof.\nThis verse refutes the doctrine of “original sin.” The Qur’an affirms that every soul is accountable only for its own deeds — “No bearer of burden will bear the burden of another.” Each person is born pure and free from inherited guilt, and divine judgment is based solely on individual belief and action. In contrast to the Christian concept of inherited sin from Adam, this verse emphasizes personal moral responsibility and divine justice, teaching that no one suffers for another’s fault nor redeems another’s sin.This highlights divine justice and personal responsibility: Guidance and misguidance return to the individual; one’s choices determine one’s fate. This is a universal Qur’anic principle (repeated in 6:164, 35:18, etc.), rejecting collective punishment or inherited guilt.",
  17016: "When We intend to destroy a town, We command its affluent ones [16], but they defiantly rebel therein; so the Word [of punishment] becomes due against it, and We destroy it with utter destruction.\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. The term mutrafūhā refers to those given ease, luxury, and influence within a society. Exegetes explain that when guidance and warning are given, these privileged elites often lead in corruption and defiance. Some interpret “We command” as a command of guidance which they reject; others understand it as a decree allowing them to persist in arrogance until destruction becomes deserved. The verse highlights a recurring pattern: moral decay often begins with those in positions of power and wealth, leading to collective ruin.",
  17017: "How many generations have We destroyed after Noah! Your Lord is sufficient as All-Aware and All-Seeing of the sins of His servants.",
  17018: "Whoever desires this immediate [life] [17], We hasten for him therein whatever We will—to whomever We please. Then We assign to him Hell; he will enter it, condemned and banished.\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. “The immediate [life]” translates al-ʿājilah, referring to the fleeting worldly life, so called because of its nearness and quick passing. The verse clarifies that worldly success is not necessarily a sign of divine approval. Allāh grants provision in this life according to His wisdom and decree—sometimes even to those who ultimately reject faith. What is decisive, however, is the outcome in the Hereafter. Those who seek only worldly gain without regard for accountability may receive what Allāh wills for them here, but their ultimate end is loss.\nWorldly blessings—such as wealth, comfort, and relationships—are not evil in themselves; they are part of Allāh’s creation and a test. Their appeal can lead either to gratitude and obedience or to heedlessness and excess. When pursued without regard for divine guidance, they may become a source of spiritual harm. But when enjoyed within the lawful bounds set by Allāh, with mindfulness and gratitude, they become means of reward and stability. Thus, the believer lives in the world without being consumed by it—benefiting from its blessings while remaining anchored to the Hereafter.",
  17019: "But whoever desires the Hereafter and strives for it with the striving it deserves, while being a believer—then it is they whose effort is ever appreciated. [18]\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. Allāh will accept their good deeds, reward them generously by multiplying their recompense, and forgive their shortcomings. He does not let their sincere efforts go unnoticed. Rather, He appreciates even the smallest act done with true faith, increases its reward many times over, and overlooks their mistakes out of mercy.",
  17020: "To each We extend—these as well as those—from the gift of your Lord. And the gift of your Lord is not restricted. [19]\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. This verse clarifies that worldly provision is granted to both believers and unbelievers. Allāh’s generosity in this life is not confined to those who have faith. Material wealth, health, and opportunity are part of His universal bounty. However, the true distinction between people lies not in what they receive in this world, but in their standing in the Hereafter.\nThis worldly distribution is not a measure of divine approval, but part of God’s justice and wisdom — a test for gratitude and patience alike. The phrase “the gift of your Lord is not restricted ” signifies that His mercy and sustenance are boundless, extending to all creation in this life, though the eternal reward is reserved only for the believers in Allāh and the Hereafter life.",
  17021: "See how We have favored some over others [in this world]; but surely the Hereafter is far greater in ranks and far greater in distinction.",
  17022: "Do not set up with Allāh any other god, lest you sit condemned and forsaken.",
  17023: "And your Lord has decreed that you worship none but Him, and that you show the utmost kindness to your parents [20].\nIf one or both of them reach old age in your care, do not even say to them ‘uff,’ nor rebuke them, but speak to them with gracious and noble words,\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. The command to worship Allāh alone is immediately followed by kindness to parents, highlighting the immense status of filial devotion in Islam. Iḥsān implies excellence in conduct—kindness, gentleness, patience, gratitude, and enduring care. Even when a parent has been harsh or difficult, the believer is called to respond with dignity and mercy, maintaining respect while upholding what is right. Honoring parents includes speaking well of them, praying for them, serving them in their need, and setting an example of compassion for one’s own children. Through such conduct, the values of gratitude and family integrity are preserved across generations.\n[21]. “Uff” is the slightest expression of irritation or contempt. The verse forbids even this minimal sign of disrespect toward one’s parents—let alone anything more severe. It calls for patience, humility, and gentle speech, especially in their old age, when they may be most vulnerable. The command establishes a standard of reverence and self-restraint, requiring that service to parents be marked by dignity and compassion.",
  17024: "and lower to them the wing of humility out of mercy [22], and say, “My Lord, have mercy upon them as they raised me when I was small.” [23]\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. This expression is a metaphor. It describes showing parents the utmost humility and tenderness—like a bird lowering its wing gently over its young in protection and care. The image conveys softness, mercy, and loving attentiveness. A believer is commanded to approach his parents not with pride or irritation, but with gentleness, humility, and heartfelt compassion.\n[23]. Parents, in their old age, may reach a stage of weakness similar to the helplessness of a child in early life. Just as they once cared patiently for their child when he was small and dependent, the child is now called to respond with patience, mercy, and gratitude toward them in their time of need.",
  17025: "Your Lord knows best what is within yourselves. If you are righteous, then surely He is ever Forgiving to those who continually turn back to Him in repentance.",
  17026: "And give the relative his rightful due, as well as the needy and the stranded traveler [24]. But do not squander your wealth wastefully. [25]\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. In Islam, you are no longer foreigners or strangers, but fellow citizens among God’s people — the Muslims — who follow His final Messenger, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. You are no longer regarded as outsiders or aliens, but as equal members of the believing community, sharing the same rights, blessings, and responsibilities.\nIslam removes all barriers of nationality, ethnicity, and social status. The mosque stands as a symbol of this unity — a place where all believers, including immigrants, belong and are valued. Just as Allāh has welcomed believers into His House, Muslims are called to welcome others into their hearts and communities, treating them not as outsiders, but as brothers and sisters in faith.\nLoving immigrants means recognizing their equal dignity and rights. It calls upon believers to build communities where newcomers feel valued and embraced — communities that reflect the unity, mercy, and love that come from being part of God’s people. This love transcends cultural and national boundaries, embodying the inclusive spirit of the Qur’an and the prophetic example.\n[25]. Wastefulness (isrāf) means spending wealth in disobedience to Allāh — on sinful, harmful, or vain purposes — or using resources in ways that are inappropriate and excessive. True moderation in Islam means spending wisely, with gratitude and purpose, avoiding extravagance or misuse of the blessings Allāh has entrusted to us.",
  17027: "Indeed, the wasteful are brothers of Satan [26], and Satan is ever ungrateful to his Lord. [27]\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. Islam is a religion of balance and moderation. It does not encourage wastefulness, nor does it approve of stinginess. A believer is taught to spend responsibly—neither extravagantly nor miserly—but in a thoughtful and measured way. Wealth is a trust from Allāh, and it should be used wisely, with gratitude and awareness of accountability.\n[27]. Satan is described as deeply ungrateful because he rebelled against Allāh’s command instead of submitting in obedience. Ingratitude is shown not only by denying blessings but by misusing them and defying the One who granted them. Those who follow the path of rebellion resemble Satan in this ingratitude, turning away from divine guidance rather than responding with humility and obedience.",
  17028: "And if you must turn away from them, while seeking the mercy of your Lord that you hope for [28], then at least speak to them with gentle and gracious words.\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. That is, if you have nothing available to give them at the moment. If you are unable to help financially because you yourself are waiting for provision from Allāh, you are not blamed. However, you must still respond kindly and respectfully, offering gentle words instead of turning them away harshly.",
  17029: "Do not keep your hand chained to your neck [through stinginess], nor extend it completely open [through reckless spending] [29], lest you become blameworthy and left destitute.\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. This is a metaphor. A hand “chained to the neck” represents miserliness and refusal to give, while a hand “fully extended” symbolizes excessive and wasteful spending. The verse teaches moderation—neither stinginess nor extravagance—so that one does not fall into blame or poverty. Islam encourages balanced generosity rooted in wisdom and responsibility.",
  17030: "Indeed, your Lord expands provision for whom He wills and restricts it. Surely He is ever All-Aware and All-Seeing of His servants.",
  17031: "Do not kill your children out of fear of poverty. We provide for them and for you. Indeed, killing them is a tremendous sin. [30]\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. In Islam, the unborn child possesses essential sanctity and is considered a living trust from Allāh. Terminating a pregnancy without a valid and lawful reason is regarded as a grave sin, akin in moral weight to the act condemned in this verse — the killing of children out of fear of hardship.\nIslamic jurists agree that life begins with conception, and that after 120 days (four months)—when the soul (rūḥ) is believed to be breathed into the fetus—abortion becomes an act of unjustified killing unless the mother’s life is in clear danger. Even before this stage, abortion is not permitted except for serious and legitimate reasons, and only after consultation with qualified scholars and physicians.\nThis teaching reflects Islam’s profound respect for life, its recognition of divine providence, and its rejection of fear-based or convenience-based motives for ending a pregnancy. Allāh is the Provider of sustenance for both parent and child; hence, believers are called to trust His wisdom and mercy rather than succumb to despair.\nA Companion of the Prophet ﷺ reported:\n“I asked, ‘O Messenger of Allāh, what is the greatest sin?’\nHe replied, ‘That you set up rivals with Allāh, though He alone created you.’\nI then asked, ‘What comes next?’\nHe said, ‘That you kill your child out of fear that he will share your food.’”",
  17032: "Do not even approach adultery [31]. Indeed, it is a grave indecency and an evil way.\\n\\nFootnotes: [31]. We must avoid all forms of impurity — in thought, word, and deed — whether in private or in our interactions with others, both within and outside of marriage. Fornication, adultery, and homosexual acts are all considered major sins in Islam.\nWe should also avoid anything that leads to such immoral behavior: private or mixed gatherings of unrelated men and women in closed places, seductive gestures or glances, provocative entertainment such as music and dancing between the sexes, exposure to indecent images, and the consumption of alcohol — all of which weaken modesty and open the door to temptation.\nIn contrast to such corruption, this verse calls believers to chastity, purity, and self-restraint, both inside and outside marriage. In Islamic law, adultery committed by a married person is regarded as a most serious crime and, when proven by the strict evidentiary requirements of the Sharī‘ah, is punishable by stoning to death — a penalty meant to protect the sanctity of family and preserve the moral fabric of society.",
  17033: "Do not kill any soul which Allah has made sacred, except by right [32]. And whoever is killed unjustly, We have given his heir authority [33], but let him not exceed the limits in retribution. Indeed, he has been supported by the law.\\n\\nFootnotes: [32]. That is, except in cases permitted by law—such as the execution of a lawful judicial sentence, in a just war, or in legitimate self-defense. Human life is sacred and may only be taken with lawful justification under due legal process. The verse affirms the inviolability of life and prohibits vigilantism, personal revenge, and unlawful killing. Justice must be carried out through legitimate authority, not through individual impulse.\n[33]. The “heir” (walī) refers to the closest legal relative of the victim. He is granted the right to seek justice through lawful retribution (qiṣāṣ) or to pardon. However, he must not exceed the limits by taking more than one life or acting unjustly. “He is supported” means the law upholds his right to justice.",
  17034: "Do not approach the wealth of the orphan except in the best manner, until he reaches maturity. And fulfill every covenant, for every covenant will be called to account. [34]\\n\\nFootnotes: [34]. The orphan’s wealth is to be protected and managed responsibly until he reaches maturity and sound judgment. “In the best manner” means preserving, investing, and safeguarding it without exploitation. The command to fulfill covenants extends to all promises, contracts, and trusts, whether with Allāh or with people. Every commitment carries moral accountability.",
  17035: "Give full measure when you measure, and weigh with a just and accurate balance. That is most fair and best in outcome. [35]\\n\\nFootnotes: [35]. The verse commands honesty and integrity in trade and transactions. “Full measure” and “just balance” symbolize fairness in all dealings. “Best in outcome” indicates that justice brings lasting benefit, both in this life and in the Hereafter.",
  17036: "Do not pursue that of which you have no knowledge. Indeed, the hearing, the sight, and the heart — all of them will be called to account. [36]\\n\\nFootnotes: [36]. This verse forbids speaking, judging, or acting without knowledge. It warns against speculation, false testimony, rumor, and baseless accusation. The “heart” (fuʾād) refers to the inner faculty of understanding, reflection, and intention. Every human faculty—what one hears, sees, and inwardly believes—will be subject to accountability before Allāh.\nThe Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said: “Part of a person’s excellence in Islam is leaving what does not concern him.” This teaching reinforces the verse’s call to restraint, mindfulness, and responsible speech.",
  17037: "Do not walk upon the earth with arrogance. Indeed, you will never pierce the earth, nor will you ever reach the mountains in height. [37]\\n\\nFootnotes: [37]. The verse condemns arrogance and self-importance. The imagery reminds humans of their physical and existential limitations: no matter how proudly one walks, he cannot split the earth beneath him nor rival the height of mountains. Pride is therefore irrational, for true greatness belongs only to Allāh.",
  17038: "All of that evil is detestable in the sight of your Lord.",
  17039: "This is part of the wisdom your Lord has revealed to you. And, [O mankind], do not set up with Allāh any other god, lest you be cast into Hell, blameworthy and banished. [38]\\n\\nFootnotes: [38]. In these verses (17:23–39), the Qur’an outlines ten foundational moral commandments that together form a comprehensive framework for faith, family life, and social order. These teachings emphasize exclusive devotion to Allāh; kindness and respect toward parents; the protection of children and future generations; the preservation of honor through chastity and the avoidance of sexual immorality; the sanctity of human life; safeguarding the rights of orphans and other vulnerable members of society; faithfulness to promises and covenants; fairness and honesty in economic dealings; refraining from speaking without knowledge; and humility in conduct. Collectively, they present a divine blueprint for a balanced and just civilization grounded in faith, justice, mercy, and moral integrity.",
  17040: "Has your Lord favored you with sons while taking for Himself daughters from among the angels [39]? Indeed, you are uttering a most grievous statement.\\n\\nFootnotes: [39]. The pre-Islamic Arabs falsely claimed that the angels were daughters of Allāh, while they themselves preferred sons and regarded daughters as inferior. The verse exposes the inconsistency and injustice of attributing to Allāh what they disliked for themselves. It highlights the contradiction in deeming daughters a lesser blessing for themselves, yet ascribing them to the Lord of Majesty and Power.\nThe verse firmly rejects any notion of divine offspring and affirms that Allāh is exalted far above human attributes, gender, or lineage. He is One, Eternal, and absolutely Independent of all creation.",
  17041: "We have certainly varied the teachings in this Qur’an so that they may take heed, yet it increases them in nothing but aversion.",
  17042: "Say, “If there had been other gods along with Him, as they claim, then they would surely have sought a way to the Lord of the Mighty Throne.” [40]\\n\\nFootnotes: [40]. This verse presents a rational argument for divine oneness. If multiple gods truly existed, they would seek supremacy, rivalry, or dominance over one another. The orderly harmony of creation proves that ultimate authority belongs to One alone. The “Lord of the Mighty Throne” signifies absolute sovereignty, supreme dominion, and unrivaled power.",
  17043: "Glorified is He, and highly exalted above what they claim! [41]\\n\\nFootnotes: [41]. “Glory be to Him” (Subḥānahu) declares Allāh’s absolute transcendence from imperfection and false attribution. The phrase “exalted above what they say” affirms that He is beyond all human projection, limitation, or comparison. The repetition emphasizes His supreme majesty and incomparability.",
  17044: "The seven heavens, the earth, and all that is within them glorify Him [42]. There is not a single thing except that it glorifies Him with His praise, though you do not understand their glorification. Indeed, He is Ever-Forbearing, All-Forgiving.\\n\\nFootnotes: [42]. All creation glorifies Allāh in a manner appropriate to its nature, even if humans cannot perceive or comprehend it. This universal glorification reflects the order, harmony, and submission inherent in creation. The closing attributes—Forbearing and Forgiving—remind humanity that despite their heedlessness of this reality, Allāh remains patient and merciful.",
  17045: "When you recite the Qur’an, We place between you and those who do not believe in the Hereafter a concealed barrier. [43]\\n\\nFootnotes: [43]. The “concealed barrier” refers to a spiritual barrier that prevents hardened hearts from benefiting from the Qur’an. Their inability to perceive its truth is not due to lack of clarity but due to their own rejection of the Hereafter. The barrier is unseen yet real, reflecting the inner condition of disbelief.",
  17046: "We have placed coverings over their hearts lest they understand it, and heaviness in their ears [44]. And when you mention your Lord alone in the Qur’an, they turn their backs in aversion.\\n\\nFootnotes: [44]. The “coverings” and “heaviness” represent spiritual obstruction, not physical incapacity. Their rejection of the Hereafter results in an inner barrier that prevents true understanding. When pure monotheism is proclaimed—mentioning Allāh alone without intermediaries—their aversion becomes most evident, exposing their resistance to exclusive devotion.",
  17047: "We know best what they listen for [45] when they listen to you, and when they confer in secret—when the wrongdoers say, “You follow none but a man bewitched.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [45]. The verse exposes their insincere listening. They did not listen the Qur’an to understand but to find fault and to mock. Their secret discussions reveal their accusation that the Prophet ﷺ was “bewitched,” meaning deluded or influenced by sorcery. The Qur’an counters this by affirming that Allāh fully knows their motives and schemes.",
  17048: "See how they set forth false comparisons about you. Thus they have gone astray and cannot find a way. [46]\\n\\nFootnotes: [46]. “To set forth examples” refers to their attempts to label the Prophet ﷺ as bewitched, mad, or merely human in a way meant to discredit him. Their flawed comparisons led them further into error. Having rejected clear guidance, they are unable to find a path to the truth.",
  17049: "They say, ‘What! When we have become bones and crumbled remains, shall we truly be resurrected as a new creation?’ [47]\\n\\nFootnotes: [47]. The disbelievers mock the idea of resurrection, imagining it impossible after bodily decay. Their objection reflects material reasoning limited to worldly experience, failing to recognize the power of the One who created them in the first place.",
  17050: "Say, ‘Even if you were stones or iron,’ [48]\\n\\nFootnotes: [48]. The verse responds to their denial of resurrection by emphasizing divine power. Even if they were to become as hard and lifeless as stone or iron—symbols of strength and permanence—Allāh would still be able to resurrect them. Nothing is beyond His power.",
  17051: "Or any other creation that seems impossible in your minds to restore. They will say, ‘Who will bring us back to life?’ Say, ‘The One who created you the first time.’ Then they will shake their heads at you in disbelief and say, ‘When will that be?’ Say, ‘Perhaps it is near.’",
  17052: "On the Day He calls you, you will respond with His praise and think that you had remained in the world only a short while.",
  17053: "Tell My servants to speak what is best [49], for Satan incites discord among them. Indeed, Satan is a clear enemy to mankind. [50]\\n\\nFootnotes: [49]. That is: speak kindly, reasonably, truthfully, beautifully, and beneficially — whether to friend, enemy, or relative.\nOne who thinks that words are spoken and simply fade away is mistaken. Words do not disappear; they leave lasting impressions upon the heart. What truly revives hearts are beautiful conversations, gentle responses, kind words, warm smiles, sincere affection, and thoughtful gestures.\nEvery person carries unseen burdens within. The wise are those who bear their struggles with patience yet still manifest the beauty of their character in the way they treat others.\nLet our motto be: Do good in a way that embraces hearts with beauty, for our hearts are deserving of beauty.\nKind words uplift the soul, while harsh ones weigh heavily upon it. True beauty lies in gentle speech, gracious conduct, and patient kindness — even amidst life’s hidden trials.\n[50]. Be vigilant against Satan’s subtle incitements. Stand firm in the Islamic faith, remain steadfast upon obedience to Allāh, and follow His final Messenger, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. Exercise patience, for patience protects the heart from discord and preserves unity among believers.",
  17054: "Your Lord knows you best. If He wills, He will show you mercy; and if He wills, He will punish you. And We have not sent you as a guardian over them.",
  17055: "Your Lord knows best all who are in the heavens and the earth. We have certainly favored some prophets above others, and We gave David the Psalms.",
  17056: "Say, ‘Call upon those [51] you claimed as gods besides Him. They have no power to remove harm from you, nor to shift it elsewhere.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [51]. This refers to those falsely invoked besides Allāh — whether idols, celestial beings, saints, prophets, or any created entity to whom divine power is attributed. The verse challenges the assumption that such beings possess independent authority. They have no power to remove harm, avert misfortune, or redirect it in any way.\nThe expression “whom you claim” indicates that their supposed divinity is merely an assertion without proof. Ultimate control over benefit and harm belongs solely to Allāh. No created being shares in His sovereignty, nor can any intercede or act independently of His will.\nThis verse affirms the absolute oneness of divine authority and reminds believers that reliance, supplication, and hope must be directed to Allāh alone.",
  17057: "Those whom they call upon [ 52] themselves seek a means of nearness to their Lord, each striving to be the closest, hoping for His mercy and fearing His punishment. Indeed, the punishment of your Lord is ever to be feared.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [52]. That is, those beings whom some people invoke besides Allāh — whether prophets such as Jesus Christ, angels such as the holy spirit, saints, righteous believers, or any other created being.\nThe verse clarifies that all of them are themselves servants of Allāh. They seek nearness to their Lord, hope for His mercy, and fear His punishment. Therefore, they possess no independent authority, nor do they share in divinity or control over benefit and harm.",
  17058: "There is no city but that We will destroy it before the Day of Resurrection or punish it with a severe punishment [53]. That is inscribed in the Record. [54]\\n\\nFootnotes: [53]. That is, no community persists indefinitely in its present state. The verse refers either to destruction through calamities, decline, or disappearance, or to severe punishment as a consequence of persistent wrongdoing and rejection of divine guidance.\nThe destruction mentioned does not necessarily imply total annihilation of every individual, but rather the downfall of a society whose corruption has become entrenched. Such punishment unfolds in accordance with divine justice and wisdom, after warning and opportunity for reform have been given.\nThus, the verse affirms the moral accountability of nations and the certainty that societies are not beyond divine reckoning.\n[54]. The “Record” refers to the divine decree — often identified as the Preserved Tablet (al-Lawḥ al-Maḥfūẓ).",
  17059: "Nothing prevents Us from sending signs except that the former peoples denied them. We gave Thamūd the she-camel as a visible sign [55], but they wronged her. We do not send signs except as a warning.\\n\\nFootnotes: [55]. The she-camel was the miraculous sign granted to the people of Thamud through their prophet Salih. According to the Qur’anic account, they demanded a tangible miracle as proof of his prophethood. By God’s command, a she-camel emerged as a clear and visible sign. They were instructed to allow her to graze freely and to share the water with her on appointed days.\nInstead of honoring the sign, they defied the command and hamstrung (killed) the she-camel, thereby committing a grave act of rebellion. Their wrongdoing was not merely against an animal, but against a manifest sign of God. As a consequence, divine punishment befell them.\nThe narrative illustrates that miracles do not compel faith in hearts hardened by arrogance; rather, they increase accountability and serve as a decisive warning.",
  17060: "And [remember, O Prophet] when We told you, ‘Indeed, your Lord encompasses all people [56].’ We did not make the vision We showed you [57] except as a test for the people, nor the accursed tree mentioned in the Qur’an. We warn them, but it only increases them in great transgression.\\n\\nFootnotes: [56]. “Your Lord encompasses all people” means that Allāh’s knowledge, authority, and power fully surround humanity. Nothing escapes His awareness, nor can anyone evade His decree. The statement reassures the Prophet that opposition and hostility are within divine control and occur only by Allāh’s wisdom and permission.\n[57]. The “vision” refers to the extraordinary experience shown to the Prophet — understood as the Night Journey and Ascension (al-Isrāʾ wa’l-Miʿrāj). It was a physical and spiritual journey by which God honored His Messenger and manifested clear signs of His power. This event served as a test for the people: those firm in faith accepted it with conviction, while others responded with doubt and mockery.\n[58]. The “accursed tree” mentioned in the Qur’an refers to the tree of Zaqqum, described as growing in Hell and serving as food for its inhabitants (see 37:62–68; 44:43–46). It is called “accursed” because it is associated with punishment and divine wrath. Its mention became a trial for some disbelievers who mocked its description, while for believers it reinforced the reality of accountability and the Hereafter.",
  17061: "And [remember] when We said to the angels, \"Prostrate to Ādam [59],\" they all prostrated except Iblīs. He said, \"Shall I prostrate to one You created from clay?\"\\n\\nFootnotes: [59]. The command to “prostrate to Adam” was not an act of worship directed to Adam, but an act of obedience to Allāh and a gesture of honor toward Adam. \nWorship belongs to Allāh alone; the prostration here was a special command signifying Adam’s elevated status as the first human and as Allāh’s appointed vicegerent on earth.\nAll the angels obeyed immediately, demonstrating their complete submission to Allāh’s will. Iblīs, however, refused out of arrogance, objecting to Adam’s creation from clay. His refusal was not a matter of misunderstanding, but of pride and rebellion, marking the beginning of his enmity toward humankind.\nThe narrative emphasizes that true nobility lies not in one’s origin, but in obedience to Allāh.",
  17062: "He said, “Do You see this one whom You have honored above me? If You grant me respite until the Day of Resurrection, I will surely lead [60] his descendants astray — except a few.\" [61]\\n\\nFootnotes: [60]. The expression لَأَحْتَنِكَنَّ ذُرِّيَّتَهُ (la-aḥtanikanna dhurriyyatahu) \"I will surely lead...\" in the verse comes from a root that conveys the idea of placing a bridle in an animal’s mouth — specifically controlling it from the palate or jaws. It is a vivid metaphor meaning “to take firm control,” “to subdue,” or “to lead astray.”\nIn this context, Iblīs is declaring his intent to seize control of Adam’s descendants and lead them wherever he wills, as a rider directs and dominates his mount. The image suggests manipulation, domination, and the steering of others into misguidance.\nThus, the statement expresses Satan’s vow to exert strong influence over humanity, drawing them into sin and rebellion — except for a small number who remain faithful and guided.\n[61]. The phrase “except a few” refers to those among the descendants of Adam who remain sincere in faith and steadfast in obedience to Allāh. They are the servants who resist Satan’s whispers, guard themselves against temptation, and seek refuge in Allāh.\nElsewhere, Satan himself acknowledges that he will have no authority over Allah’s devoted servants (cf. 15:40; 38:83). Thus, “the few” are not defined by number alone, but by their sincerity, humility, and reliance upon their Lord. They are protected not by their own strength, but by divine guidance and grace.",
  17063: "Allāh said, “Be gone! Whoever among them follows you — Hell will be your recompense, a full recompense.”",
  17064: "“Entice whoever you can among them with your voice [62]; rally against them with your cavalry and infantry; share with them in wealth and children [63], and make promises to them.” But Satan promises them nothing except delusion.\\n\\nFootnotes: [62]. “With your voice” refers to every form of temptation and incitement by which Satan misleads human beings — whether through whispers, deceptive suggestions, false ideologies, alluring speech, or any call to disobedience toward Allāh. It includes all avenues through which falsehood is beautified and sin is encouraged.\nThis has been understood by some early scholars to encompass inciting speech, illicit songs, corrupting forms of entertainment, and any other expression that draws people away from remembrance of Allāh and obedience to Him. The “voice” thus symbolizes subtle persuasion and moral influence, rather than literal sound alone.\n[63]. This phrase reveals the many ways Satan becomes a “partner” with people in their wealth and offspring — not by direct ownership, but by influencing how they earn, spend, and raise them.\nThis “partnership” occurs when Satan tempts humans to misuse blessings that should be means of worship.\nA). In their wealth: When they earn it through forbidden means, such as usury, theft, gambling, bribery, deceit, or dishonest trade. When they spend it wastefully or sinfully, such as on luxury, pride, intoxicants, or immoral entertainment. When they withhold charity and neglect the poor, forgetting that wealth is a trust from Allāh. When they hoard or boast of their possessions, making wealth a source of arrogance instead of gratitude.\nB). In their children: When parents neglect their moral and spiritual upbringing, leaving them to grow up without faith or prayer.\nWhen they expose them to corrupt influences, leading them toward disbelief, vice, or rebellion or fornication, adultery, or homosexuality, etc.. When people dedicate their children to un-Islamic causes or prioritize worldly success over righteousness. When they follow pagan or superstitious customs, such as invoking jinn or fortune-tellers for protection. Thus, Satan becomes a “partner” in one’s wealth when it is gained or spent contrary to divine guidance, and in one’s children when they are raised without remembrance of Allāh.This partnership is not physical but moral and spiritual — a share in the misuse of what Allāh entrusted to humanity.",
  17065: "Indeed, My servants — you have no authority over them. Sufficient is your Lord as a Guardian. [64]\\n\\nFootnotes: [64]. The verse draws a clear line between those vulnerable to Satan’s influence and those protected by faith and sincerity. Satan’s power is not coercive, only suggestive. He can tempt and whisper, but cannot compel the true servants of Allāh who hold firmly to faith. This divine protection extends to those who are sincere in servitude, those purified in belief, remembrance, and reliance on Allāh.\n“Your Lord is Enough as a Guardian” \nThe word Wakīl means One who manages, protects, and provides those who rely on Him. It conveys both trust and protection — that is, Allāh is sufficient as a Guardian, Protector, and Disposer of affairs. This statement seals the dialogue between Allāh and Iblīs with assurance that no believer who places his trust in Allāh can ever be overcome by Satan.",
  17066: "Your Lord is the One who causes ships to sail for you through the sea, so that you may seek of His bounty. Indeed, He is ever Merciful to you.",
  17067: "When adversity strikes you at sea, those you call upon vanish except Him. But when He delivers you to land, you turn away. Mankind is ever ungrateful.",
  17068: "Do you feel secure that He will not cause the land to swallow you, or send against you a storm of stones? Then you would find no protector for yourselves.",
  17069: "Or do you feel secure that He will not send you back into it once again, and then send against you a raging wind and drown you for your disbelief? Then you would find no one to defend you against Us.",
  17070: "We have certainly honored the children of Ādam, carried them on land and sea [65], provided them with good provisions, and favored them above many of those We have created—with a marked preference.\\n\\nFootnotes: [65]. “Carried them on land and sea” refers to the means by which Allāh has enabled human beings to travel, move, and sustain themselves across the earth. This includes transport by animals, vehicles, and ships, as well as the natural laws that make navigation and travel possible.\nThe phrase highlights divine facilitation: humanity does not move independently of Allāh’s enabling power. Whether by land or across the sea, all means of conveyance are ultimately subject to His control. It is a reminder that mobility, trade, exploration, and livelihood are signs of divine generosity and mercy.",
  17071: "On the Day We will call every people with their leader, whoever is given his record in his right hand — they will read their record and will not be wronged in the least, not even by the thread of a date-stone.",
  17072: "But whoever is blind from 'the truth' in this [life] will be blind in the Hereafter and further astray from the right way.",
  17073: "And indeed, they were about to tempt you away from what We revealed to you, so that you might fabricate something else against Us [66]; and then they would surely have taken you as a close friend.\\n\\nFootnotes: [66]. This verse refers to attempts by some of the Prophet’s opponents in Mecca to pressure him into softening or altering aspects of the revelation so it would be more acceptable to them. They sought compromise—such as easing condemnation of their idols or adjusting certain commands—in exchange for reconciliation, social acceptance, or political support. The Qur’an clarifies that such proposals were a trial and that acceptance would have meant attributing to Allāh what He did not reveal.",
  17074: "Had We not made you stand firm, you would have almost inclined to them a little.",
  17075: "Then, We would have made you taste double [punishment] in life and double after death; then you would have found no helper against Us.",
  17076: "Indeed, they were about to force you out of the land [of Makkah] in order to expel you from it; but had they done so, they would not have remained there after you except for a little while.",
  17077: "Such was Our established way for the messengers We sent before you, and you will never find any change in Our way.",
  17078: "Establish prayer from the decline of the sun until the darkness of the night [67], and also the recitation at dawn. Indeed, the recitation at dawn is ever witnessed. [68]\\n\\nFootnotes: [67]. “From the decline of the sun until the darkness of the night” refers to the prescribed times of the daily prayers beginning from midday. The “decline of the sun” marks its descent from its highest point (zenith), corresponding to the ẓuhr prayer, and extends through the afternoon and evening prayers. “The darkness of the night” refers to the deepening night, encompassing the time of maghrib and ʿishāʾ.\nThus, the verse alludes collectively to the obligatory daily prayers, while distinguishing the dawn prayer separately for special mention.\n[68]. “The recitation at dawn is ever witnessed” means that the dawn prayer is attended and observed, particularly by the angels. According to prophetic tradition, the angels of the night and the angels of the day gather at the time of the dawn prayer, bearing witness to those who establish it.\nThe phrase also emphasizes the special virtue and spiritual clarity associated with the early morning recitation of the Qur’an.",
  17079: "And during part of the night, pray with it as an additional act of devotion for you; it may be that your Lord will raise you to a praiseworthy station. [69]\\n\\nFootnotes: [69]. The “Praiseworthy Station” (maqāman maḥmūdan) is widely understood to refer to the Station of Intercession (al-Maqām al-Maḥmūd) granted uniquely to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ on the Day of Resurrection. At that moment, when humanity will be overwhelmed by fear and waiting for judgment, he will be permitted to intercede before Allāh on behalf of creation. This intercession will relieve their distress and mark the beginning of the Reckoning.\nIt is called “praiseworthy” because all creation will praise and acknowledge the honor bestowed upon him in that position. The promise of this station reflects both the Prophet’s elevated rank and the immense mercy of Allāh toward humanity.\nThe verse thus links night devotion with spiritual elevation, showing that steadfast worship is a means to divine honor and nearness.",
  17080: "And say, “My Lord, cause me to enter with a truthful entrance and to exit with a truthful exit, and grant me from Yourself a supporting authority.”",
  17081: "And say, “Truth has come, and falsehood has vanished. Indeed, falsehood is ever bound to perish.” [70]\\n\\nFootnotes: [70]. This verse declares a universal principle: falsehood has no lasting foundation. Though it may appear powerful or widespread for a time, it is essentially unstable and destined to fade. The expression “ever bound to vanish” reflects that falsehood, by its very nature, cannot endure in the presence of truth.\nHistorically, the Prophet ﷺ recited this verse upon entering Mecca at its conquest, when the idols surrounding the Kaʿbah were being removed. It is reported that there were around three hundred and sixty idols placed around it, and he struck them with a stick in his hand, causing them to fall, while reciting: “Truth has come, and falsehood has vanished. Indeed, falsehood is ever bound to vanish.”\nThis moment symbolized the triumph of divine guidance over idolatry. Yet the verse is not confined to that historical event; it expresses a timeless reality — wherever truth is established with sincerity and firmness, falsehood ultimately collapses.",
  17082: "We send down of the Qur’an that which is a healing and a mercy [71] for the believers, but it increases the wrongdoers only in loss.\\n\\nFootnotes: (71]. It is reported that some of the Companions of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ passed by an Arab tribe whose chief had been bitten by a snake. One of the Companions recited Sūrat al-Fātiḥah over him as ruqyah (supplication for healing), and the man recovered as though nothing had afflicted him. They were given a flock of goats in return. When the matter was brought to the Prophet ﷺ, he approved their action and said, “How did you know that it is a ruqyah? You have done rightly. Divide it and allot a share for me.”\nIt is also narrated that when the Prophet ﷺ fell ill, he would recite the Muʿawwidhāt (Sūrat al-Falaq and Sūrat al-Nās) and blow over himself. When his illness became severe, his wife would recite them over him and wipe his body with his hand, seeking blessing thereby.",
  17083: "When We bestow favor upon man, he turns away and distances himself; but when harm touches him, he is ever despairing.",
  17084: "Say, “Each works according to his own manner [72], but your Lord knows best who is best guided in the way.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [72]. That is, each person acts according to his inner disposition, intention, and chosen path. The verse implies that those who reject the message will persist in their way, while the Prophet remains steadfast upon his. Ultimate judgment as to who is truly guided belongs to Allāh alone.",
  17085: "They ask you [O Prophet] about the spirit. Say, “The spirit is of the command of my Lord [73], and you have not been given of knowledge except a little.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [73]. “The Spirit” (al-rūḥ) is generally understood to refer to the human soul — the immaterial reality by which life is given and consciousness is sustained. Some early commentators also suggested that it could refer to the angel Jibrīl (Gabriel), though the majority view relates it to the nature of the soul itself.\nThe reply does not provide a detailed explanation; rather, it affirms that the Spirit belongs to the realm of divine command — a matter of the unseen whose full reality is known only to Allāh. The concluding statement underscores the limitation of human knowledge, reminding that even profound questions about existence remain within the bounds set by divine wisdom.",
  17086: "If We willed, We could surely take away that which We revealed to you; then you would find for yourself no guardian or advocate against Us concerning it.",
  17087: " Except as a mercy from your Lord [74]. Indeed, His favor upon you is ever great.\\n\\nFootnotes: [74]. That is, the Qur’an remains preserved and protected only by the mercy of Allāh. Though He has full power to remove revelation, He sustains it out of grace toward His Messenger and as mercy for humanity. The verse affirms both divine sovereignty and divine compassion: the preservation of the Qur’an is not owed to human effort, but to Allāh’s enduring favor. It also highlights the immense honor granted to the Prophet ﷺ, whose mission and revelation are manifestations of that great divine grace.",
  17088: "Say, “Even if all mankind and jinn were to gather together to produce the like of this Qur’an, they could never produce its like, even if they supported one another.” [75]\\n\\nFootnotes: [75]. This verse presents the Qur’an’s open challenge to humanity and jinn: to produce anything comparable to it. The challenge is not merely literary, but encompasses its linguistic excellence, depth of meaning, coherence, transformative power, and enduring guidance. Elsewhere, the Qur’an narrows the challenge — first to ten chapters (11:13), then to a single chapter (2:23) — yet declares that it cannot be met.\nThe verse affirms the Qur’an’s inimitability (iʿjāz): even if all created beings combined their efforts, they would be unable to produce its like. Its preservation and uniqueness thus stand as signs of its divine origin.",
  17089: "We have certainly presented to the people in this Qur’an every kind of example, yet most people refuse except disbelief.",
  17090: "They say, “We will never believe in you until you cause a spring to gush forth for us from the earth,",
  17091: "or until you have a garden of date-palms and grapevines, and make rivers gush forth abundantly through its midst,",
  17092: "or you make the sky fall upon us in fragments, as you claim, or bring Allāh and the angels before us face to face,",
  17093: "or until you have a house of gold, or you ascend into the sky. And we will never believe in your ascension unless you bring down to us a book that we may read.” Say, “Glory be to my Lord! Am I anything but a human messenger?”",
  17094: "Nothing prevented the people from believing, once guidance had come to them, except that they said, ‘Has Allāh sent a mortal man as a messenger?’",
  17095: " Say, ‘If there had been angels upon the earth, walking about in security, We would surely have sent down to them from heaven an angel as a messenger.’",
  17096: "Say, “Allāh is sufficient as a Witness between me and you. Indeed, He is ever All-Aware and All-Seeing of His servants.”",
  17097: "Whoever Allāh guides is truly guided; and whoever He lets go astray—you will never find for them any protectors besides Him. We will gather them on the Day of Resurrection upon their faces, blind, mute, and deaf. Their abode will be Hell. Whenever it subsides, We will increase for them its blaze.",
  17098: "That is their recompense because they disbelieved in Our revelations and said, “When we have become bones and crumbled remains, shall we indeed be resurrected as a new creation?”",
  17099: "Do they not see that Allāh—who created the heavens and the earth—is able to create the like of them? He has appointed for them a fixed term about which there is no doubt. Yet the wrongdoers refuse all but disbelief.",
  17100: "Say, “If you were to possess the treasuries of my Lord’s mercy, you would surely withhold them for fear of spending. For man is ever stingy.”",
  17101: "We certainly gave Moses nine clear signs [76]. Ask the Children of Israel: when he came to them, Pharaoh said to him, ‘O Moses, I truly think that you are bewitched.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [76]. This is where Allāh describes the confrontation between Moses and Pharaoh. It continues the theme of arrogance and denial, connecting the Prophet Muhammad’s ﷺ experience with that of earlier messengers who were rejected despite clear proofs.These “nine signs\" given to Moses are mentioned across the Qur’an and identified by scholars as: 1. The staff turning into a serpent. 2. The radiant hand. 3. The flood.\n4. The locusts. 5. The lice. 6. The frogs.\n7. The blood. 8. The drought and famine.\n9. The shortage of fruits.\nEach sign was a divine warning and proof, yet Pharaoh’s arrogance only deepened.",
  17102: "Moses said, “You certainly know that none has sent down these signs except the Lord of the heavens and the earth as clear proofs. And I truly think, O Pharaoh, that you are doomed.”",
  17103: "So he intended to expel them from the land, but We drowned him and those with him altogether.",
  17104: "And We said after him [i.e., Pharaoh] to the Children of Israel, “Dwell in the land. Then, when the promise of the Hereafter comes, We will bring you forth all together.”",
  17105: "We have sent it down in truth, and in truth it has descended; and We have sent you only as a bearer of good news and a warner. [77]\\n\\nFootnotes: [77]. Allāh sent Prophet Muhammad ﷺ to straighten what had become crooked in belief, and to guide mankind to the right path — that they may bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allāh. Through him, Allāh opened blind eyes, deaf ears, and sealed hearts — bringing insight where there was ignorance, and faith where there was denial.\nHe sent him to mend the broken-hearted, to proclaim freedom to those enslaved by falsehood, to lead people out of darkness into light, and to comfort those who mourn.\nBy the Qur’an and the Sunnah, Allāh heals hearts, revives souls, and restores humanity to truth, mercy, and peace.\nHumanity had deviated into idolatry, superstition, and arrogance; Islam came to “set right” what was distorted in faith and morality. The Prophet’s message illuminated minds and awakened hearts that were previously closed to truth.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ freed people from oppression, ignorance, and the tyranny of desires, guiding them to the inner freedom of servitude to Allāh alone.\nHe comforted the sorrowful, supported the oppressed, and uplifted every broken heart through kindness and faith.\nThis portrays the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ as: A reformer of faith — restoring pure monotheism. A spiritual physician — healing blindness of the heart.\nA liberator — freeing people from bondage to sin and false gods.\nA comforter — bringing mercy, hope, and inner peace.\nA messenger of truth — revealing divine guidance in both Qur’an and Sunnah.\nThrough him, Allāh’s mercy touched every aspect of human life — belief, morality, emotion, and society — transforming the world from darkness into divine light.",
  17106: "And it is a Qur’an which We have revealed in stages, so that you may recite it to the people over time; and We have sent it down progressively.",
  17107: "Say, “Believe in it or do not believe. Indeed, those who were given knowledge before it [78], when it is recited to them, they fall upon their faces in prostration,\\n\\nFootnotes: [78]. The verse refers to those among the People of the Scripture who recognize the truth of revelation when it is recited. The posture described — falling upon the face in prostration — is a gesture of deep humility known in earlier prophetic traditions. In the Hebrew Bible, Abraham “fell on his face” before Allāh (Genesis 17:3), and similar expressions of prostration are attributed to other prophets. The New Testament likewise describes Jesus praying while falling upon his face (Matthew 26:39).\nIn Islam, the religion of all the prophets, prostration (sujūd) remains a central act of worship, embodying humility before the One True God, Allāh.",
  17108: "and they say, ‘Glory be to our Lord! Indeed, the promise of our Lord is sure to be fulfilled.’",
  17109: "They fall down upon their faces in prostration, weeping, and it [i.e., the Qur’ān] increases them in humility.",
  17110: "Say, “Call upon Allāh or call upon the Most Compassionate [79]. Whichever Name you call upon, to Him belong the Most Beautiful Names. Do not raise your voice in prayer nor lower it too much, but seek a way in between.” [80]\\n\\nFootnotes: [79]. In the Semitic languages of earlier prophets, the Name of God appears in closely related forms. In Hebrew scripture, the term Elah (אֱלָהּ / אֵלָה) is used for God, while in Aramaic — the language spoken by Jesus (peace be upon him) — the form Alaha (ܐܠܗܐ) appears. In Arabic, the revealed Name is Allāh (الله).\nThese names share the ancient Semitic triliteral root ʾ-L-H, associated with divinity and worship. The linguistic continuity across Hebrew, Aramaic, and Arabic reflects their shared Semitic heritage.\nThe English word god, by contrast, is a Germanic term that can be applied generically. In Semitic usage, however, the divine Name functions as a proper and uniquely referential designation for the One God.\nThe form “Jehovah” arose in medieval Europe when the consonants YHWH (the Tetragrammaton in Hebrew scripture) were combined with the vowel markings of Adonai (“Lord”). Ancient Hebrew was written without vowels, and the original pronunciation of YHWH is not preserved with certainty.\nThe related forms Elah, Alaha, and Allāh demonstrate the deep linguistic connections among the Semitic languages in which earlier revelations were conveyed.\n[80] This instruction was revealed during the early Makkan period. The Prophet ﷺ would recite the Qur’an aloud while leading prayer. When the pagans heard him, they would revile the Qur’an and the One who sent it down. He was therefore instructed not to raise his voice so loudly that it would provoke hostility, nor to recite so quietly that his companions could not hear. The verse thus establishes a principle of moderation in recitation — avoiding excess while preserving clarity and reverence.",
  17111: "And say, ‘All praise is due to Allāh, who has not taken a son, nor has any partner in His dominion, nor any protector out of weakness. And magnify Him greatly.’",
  18001: "All praise be to Allāh [1], Who has sent down the Book upon His servant and has made in it no crookedness. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. It is a glorification of Allāh, the Almighty, for all His favors — both hidden and apparent — whether related to faith or to worldly life. The greatest of all these blessings is His revelation of the Qur’an to His most honored servant, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ.\n[2]. He has made it perfectly straight and flawless — a true and clear guide, free from contradiction or deviation — for all humankind and jinn.",
  18002: "[A Book] upright and steadfast, to warn of a severe punishment from Allāh, and to give glad tidings to the believers who do righteous deeds that they will have an excellent reward [Paradise].",
  18003: "Abiding therein forever.",
  18004: "And to warn those who say, \"Allāh has taken for Himself a son.” [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. This refers to those who have deviated by ascribing offspring to Allāh — such as the Christians who claim that Jesus is the son of God, and the pagans who allege that the angels are the daughters of God. Exalted is He, far above what they ascribe to Him. To claim that Allāh has a child is to imply resemblance between the Creator and the creation — suggesting partnership in divinity and a need for companionship to fulfill emotional or physical desires. Yet the Lord of the heavens and the earth is utterly free from all such imperfection. He is the Self-Sufficient, upon Whom all depend; He neither begets nor is begotten, and there is none comparable to Him. Thus, this stands as a clear warning against associating any being with Allāh, affirming the absolute oneness of God and transcendence of the Almighty — beyond need, or offspring.",
  18005: "They have no knowledge of it, nor did their forefathers. Grave is the word that comes out of their mouths! They speak nothing but falsehood.",
  18006: "Perhaps you would consume yourself with sorrow over them, if they do not believe in this message.",
  18007: "Indeed, We have made whatever is upon the earth an adornment for it, so that We may test which of them is best in deeds. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. This worldly life is the arena of toil and testing, while the Hereafter is the abode of reward or retribution — Paradise for the faithful, and Hell for the faithless.\nParadise is pure and good, and only the pure and good shall enter it. Allāh, the Most Good, accepts nothing but what is good.\nTherefore, it is by divine wisdom that Allāh tries His servants with hardships, so that the believer may be distinguished from the unbeliever, and the truthful from the liar.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“Indeed, the world is sweet and green, and Allāh has made you successors in it, so He will see how you act. So beware of the world, and beware of women. For the first trial of the Children of Israel was concerning women.”\n“Beware of the world” means: do not be deceived by its beauty, wealth, status, and pleasures in a way that leads you away from obedience to Allāh and accountability in the Hereafter.\n“Beware of women” does not mean women are evil or inferior. Rather, it warns men specifically against moral temptation (fitnah) through illicit desire and sexual misconduct — just as the Qur’an warns women about guarding modesty and avoiding similar temptation.",
  18008: "And indeed, We shall surely make all that is upon it a barren, level ground. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5] This verse reminds us that everything beautiful and attractive in this world is temporary. The earth and all its adornments are part of a test to see how people live and what choices they make. Since this life does not last, a person should use it wisely—doing good deeds and preparing for what comes after death, which is everlasting.",
  18009: "Or did you think that the Companions of the Cave [6] and the Inscription were among the most wondrous of Our signs?[7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. The “Companions of the Cave” refers to a group of young believers who withdrew to a cave to preserve their faith. Their story is recounted in the verses that follow. Many scholars have noted similarities between this account and the late antique Christian tradition known as “The Seven Sleepers of Ephesus,” though the Qur’an presents the narrative with its own distinctive religious emphasis and message.\n[7]. The verse implies that the story of the Companions of the Cave, though remarkable, is not the greatest of Allah’s signs. His signs are countless and far more wondrous.",
  18010: "When the youths sought refuge in the cave [8], they said, \"Our Lord, grant us mercy from Yourself and prepare for us a right course in our affair.\" [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. The cave served as a place of refuge for the youths, who withdrew from a society hostile to their faith. Their retreat reflects a conscious choice to preserve belief over conformity to prevailing disbelief.\n[9]. They asked Allāh for a means of which the end is firmness on the path to the Truth, Islam, being spared from the idolaters, gaining useful knowledge and acting upon it, so that their religious and worldly affairs would be on the right side.\nThe phrase “prepare for us a right course” translates rashad, which denotes sound judgment, right guidance, and a rightly directed outcome. The youths ask not only for mercy and protection, but for clarity and firmness in managing their situation according to divine guidance.",
  18011: "So We sealed their ears [10] in the cave, causing them to sleep for a number of years.\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. The ears are specifically mentioned because hearing is the sense most likely to disturb sleep; sealing them signifies a deep and protected slumber. According to early historical traditions, the youths withdrew to escape persecution during the reign of a Roman ruler, often identified in Christian sources as Emperor Decius (249–251 C.E.). The Qur’an, however, does not specify the ruler or the date, focusing instead on the spiritual dimension of their story.",
  18012: "Then We raised them up [11], so that We might make known which of the two groups was more precise in calculating the period they had remained.\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. “Raised them up” here means that they were awakened from their prolonged sleep. The phrase reflects Qur’anic usage in which awakening from sleep is sometimes described in terms similar to resurrection, foreshadowing the theme of revival after death.",
  18013: "We relate to you their story in truth. Indeed, they were youths who believed in their Lord, and We increased them in guidance. [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. The Qur’an highlights that they were youths, emphasizing that sincere faith and moral courage are not confined to age or status. Youth is often a period of strength, conviction, and decisive commitment. Throughout Islamic history, many distinguished companions of the Prophet ﷺ — such as Zayd ibn Thābit, Musʿab ibn ʿUmair, Usāmah ibn Zayd, and ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās (may Allāh be pleased with them) — embraced faith and carried great responsibility at a young age. The verse thus underscores that steadfast belief, when met with sincerity, is rewarded with increased guidance.",
  18014: "We strengthened their hearts when they stood up [13] and said, \"Our Lord is the Lord of the heavens and the earth. We will never call upon any deity besides Him; if we did, we would indeed have spoken an outrageous falsehood.\"\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. According to traditional accounts, the youths stood before a ruling authority who had commanded the worship of idols. \nThe expression “We strengthened their hearts” (literally, “We bound their hearts”) signifies divine support that grants firmness, courage, and steadiness in times of trial. It reflects the Qur’anic principle that sincere faith is met with increased resolve and guidance from Allāh.",
  18015: "These, our people, have taken deities besides Him. Why do they not bring forward a clear proof for them? Who, then, is more unjust than one who fabricates a lie against Allāh?",
  18016: "When you have withdrawn from them and from what they worship besides Allāh, then seek refuge in the cave. Your Lord will spread out for you of His mercy and will prepare for you ease in your affair.",
  18017: "You would have seen the sun, when it rose, inclining away from their cave toward the right; and when it set, passing away from them toward the left, while they lay within a spacious hollow of it [14]. That was among the signs of Allāh. Whoever Allāh guides is rightly guided; but whoever He leaves astray, you will never find for him a protecting guide.\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. The positioning of the cave allowed sunlight to pass without directly striking them, preserving their bodies during their prolonged sleep. Being situated within a spacious hollow also suggests that they received sufficient air and ventilation. These details underscore divine care and protection, and are presented as among the subtle signs of Allāh’s providence.",
  18018: "You would have thought them awake, though they were asleep. We turned them to the right and to the left [15], while their dog lay with its forelegs outstretched at the entrance. Had you looked upon them, you would surely have turned away in flight and been filled with dread of them.\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. Their being turned from side to side reflects divine preservation, preventing their bodies from deteriorating during their prolonged sleep. In human experience, those confined to bed for extended periods must be repositioned to avoid harm from continuous pressure on the body. The verse thus underscores that their condition remained under Allāh’s direct care and protection.",
  18019: "Thus We awakened them so that they might question one another. One of them said, \"How long have you remained?\" They said, \"We have remained a day, or part of a day.\" They said, \"Your Lord knows best how long you have remained. So send one of you with this silver coin of yours to the city, and let him see which food is purest; then let him bring you provision from it. And let him be discreet and make no one aware of you.\"",
  18020: "For if they discover you, they will stone you or force you back into their religion, and then you will never succeed.",
  18021: "Thus We caused them to be discovered, so that they might know that the promise of Allāh is true and that there is no doubt about the Hour. When they disputed among themselves concerning their affair, they said, \"Build over them a structure; their Lord knows best about them.\" But those who prevailed in their matter said, \"We will surely build over them a place of worship.\" [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. The word masjid literally means “a place of prostration” or worship. The verse reports the intention of those who prevailed in the matter to build a place of devotion over them. The Qur’an recounts their statement without explicitly approving or disapproving the practice, while emphasizing the greater sign demonstrated through the youths’ story — the reality of resurrection and the fulfillment of Allāh’s promise.\nOne of the wives of the Prophet ﷺ told him that, while she was in Abyssinia, she saw a church adorned with many images. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: “When a righteous man or a saint among them dies, they build a place of worship over his grave and set up images within it. They are the worst of all creatures before Allāh, for they combined two evils — worshipping at graves and making graven images.” The Prophet ﷺ also warned: “Beware! Those who came before you used to turn the graves of their prophets into places of worship. Beware! Do not take graves as places of worship. I strictly forbid you to do so.”\nThese words of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ serve as a clear caution against exaggeration in veneration, which can lead to forms of idolatry. Islam teaches pure monotheism (tawḥīd) — worship directed only to Allāh without intermediaries or sacred shrines. Building structures of devotion over graves and decorating them with images often began as acts of respect, but in time led to misguidance and imitation of earlier nations. Thus, the Prophet ﷺ firmly prohibited such practices to preserve the purity of faith and to protect the believers from gradually drifting into association of partners with Allāh.",
  18022: "They will say, \"Three; their dog was the fourth.\" And they will say, \"Five; their dog was the sixth,\" guessing at the unseen. And they will say, \"Seven; and their dog was the eighth.\" Say, \"My Lord knows best their number; none knows them except a few. So do not argue about them except with what is apparent, and do not consult any of them concerning them.\" [17]\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. The differing numbers reflect speculation among earlier communities. Scholars observed that the Qur’anic phrasing subtly distinguishes the final statement — “Seven; and their dog was the eighth” — from the earlier guesses. The first two are followed by the expression “guessing at the unseen,” while the third is not, which some scholars understood as an implicit indication that this number is closer to the truth. Nevertheless, the verse redirects attention away from such details toward the moral and religious lessons of the story.",
  18023: "And never say of anything, \"Indeed, I will do that tomorrow,\"",
  18024: "Except [by saying], \"If Allāh wills.\" And remember your Lord when you forget [18], and say, \"Perhaps my Lord will guide me to what is nearer to right guidance than this.\"\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. Whenever a person realizes that he forgot to say In shā’ Allāh — “if Allāh wills” — regarding a future intention, he should say it as soon as he remembers. And whenever one forgets something, let him remember Allāh and say: “Lā ilāha illallāh” — there is no deity worthy of worship but Allāh alone. For through the remembrance of Allāh, the heart regains clarity, and what was forgotten is often recalled.",
  18025: "They stayed in their cave for three hundred years, plus nine more. [19]\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. The People of the Cave remained asleep for 300 solar years, which is equal to approximately 309 lunar years. A solar year (based on the sun) is slightly longer than a lunar year (based on the moon), and over three centuries this difference amounts to about nine years. Many scholars mention this to explain the wording of the verse.\nThis verse also reminds us that Allāh is fully in control of time itself. He carefully managed the condition of these young believers for centuries — preserving their bodies, protecting them from harm, and awakening them at the exact moment He willed. His care over them is a sign of His complete authority over creation.\nNothing in the universe runs independently of Him. He does not hand over ultimate control to anyone. The story teaches that life, death, sleep, awakening, and even the passage of centuries are all under Allāh’s command. Preserving the youths for so long serves as a powerful sign of His ability to resurrect the dead and bring people back to life after long periods of time.",
  18026: "Say, “Allāh knows best how long they stayed. He alone knows the unseen of the heavens and the earth. How perfectly He sees and hears! They have no protector besides Him, and He shares His authority with no one.”",
  18027: "Recite what has been revealed to you from the Book of your Lord. No one can change His words, and you will never find, apart from Him, any refuge. [20]\\n\\nFootnotes: 20. This verse commands the Prophet to faithfully recite and follow the revelation given to him without compromise or alteration. The phrase “No one can change His words” affirms that Allāh’s decrees, promises, and revelations are final and cannot be overturned, corrected, or defeated by anyone. His words are not subject to revision, pressure, or negotiation.\nThe final phrase, “you will never find, apart from Him, any refuge,” emphasizes complete reliance upon Allāh. The Arabic word multahad refers to a place of escape, shelter, or protection. It teaches that no authority, power, or support system can ultimately protect a person from the consequences of truth or falsehood except Allāh Himself.\nIn context, this verse follows the reminder that Allāh alone possesses full knowledge of the unseen and shares His authority with no one. It therefore calls the Prophet — and by extension all believers — to remain firm\nupon revelation, trusting that divine truth is protected and that ultimate security lies only with Him.",
  18028: "And restrain yourself with those who call upon their Lord in the morning and the evening, seeking His Face [21]. And do not let your eyes pass beyond them, desiring the adornment of the worldly life. And do not obey one whose heart We have made heedless of Our remembrance, who follows his desire, and whose affair is excessive.\\n\\nFootnotes: 21. This verse instructs the Prophet to remain patient and committed to the company of sincere believers — even if they are poor, socially weak, or overlooked by society. The phrase “restrain yourself” suggests deliberate self-discipline: staying firmly aligned with those devoted to Allāh, despite social pressure to prefer the wealthy or influential.\nThose described as “calling upon their Lord morning and evening” represent consistent worshippers whose devotion is sincere and continuous. The expression “seeking His Face” means seeking Allāh’s pleasure alone — acting purely for Him, without desire for status, recognition, or worldly gain.\nThe warning “do not let your eyes pass beyond them” cautions against being distracted by the outward glitter of wealth and power. The Qur’an acknowledges the attraction of worldly beauty but teaches that spiritual worth is not measured by material success.\nThe verse then contrasts this group with those whose hearts have become heedless of Allāh’s remembrance. When a person neglects divine guidance, they begin to follow personal desires instead. Over time, this leads to imbalance and excess (furut), meaning their priorities and actions become distorted and morally unrestrained.\nOverall, the verse teaches that true honor lies in devotion, not status; in remembrance, not display; and in disciplined faith, not unchecked desire.",
  18029: "Say, the truth is from your Lord. So whoever wills—let him believe; and whoever wills—let him disbelieve [22]. Indeed, We have prepared for the wrongdoers a Fire whose canopy will encompass them. If they cry out for relief, they will be relieved with water like molten metal that roasts faces. Wretched is the drink and evil the resting place.\\n\\nFootnotes: 22. This verse affirms that the message of Islam is the truth from God, not a human invention or negotiation. The statement “whoever wills—let him believe; and whoever wills—let him disbelieve” does not suggest that disbelief is acceptable without consequence. Rather, it emphasizes moral freedom and accountability. Faith cannot be forced; each person chooses their response to the truth and bears responsibility for that choice.\nThe verse immediately describes the outcome for persistent wrongdoing. The image of a Fire whose “canopy” surrounds its inhabitants conveys total enclosure — there is no escape. The word suggests something like a tent or surrounding wall, emphasizing confinement from all sides.\nThe description of relief turning into punishment — “water like molten metal that roasts faces” — intensifies the warning. What is normally a source of comfort becomes a source of suffering. This vivid imagery underscores the seriousness of rejecting truth after it has been made clear.\nThe verse balances freedom and consequence: human beings are free to choose belief or disbelief, but they are not free from the results of that choice. Divine justice follows human decision.",
  18030: "Indeed, those who believe and do righteous deeds — We do not waste the reward of one who excels in good. [23]\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. After describing the consequences of disbelief, this verse shifts to reassurance and hope. It affirms that those who believe and live righteously will never lose the reward of their efforts. Allāh’s justice is not only about accountability for wrongdoing, but also about preserving every act of sincere goodness.\nThe phrase “We will not let go to waste” emphasizes that no sincere deed is overlooked — no prayer, no act of charity, no patience in hardship, and no hidden act of integrity escapes divine knowledge. Even when good actions appear unnoticed or unappreciated in this world, they remain fully preserved with Allāh.\nThe expression “anyone who excels in good” (al-muḥsinīn) refers not merely to those who perform outward deeds, but to those who act with sincerity, excellence, and awareness of Allāh. It includes both correct action and purity of intention.\nTogether with the previous verse, this passage presents a clear moral balance: rejection of truth carries consequence, but sincere faith and righteous effort are never lost. Divine justice ensures that every choice — good or bad — is accounted for with perfect fairness.",
  18031: "They shall have Gardens of Eden,\nbeneath which rivers flow. There they will be adorned with bracelets of gold and clothed in green garments of fine silk and rich brocade, reclining upon elevated couches. What an excellent reward — and what a beautiful resting place. [24]\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. Since we have these promises, believers, let us purify ourselves from everything that corrupts body and soul, and strive to perfect our righteousness in conscious awareness of Allāh (taqwā).",
  18032: "Set forth for them an example of two men [25]: to one of them We made two gardens of grapevines, and We surrounded both with date-palms, and placed between them fields of crops.\\n\\nFootnotes: [25]. This verse introduces a parable — a teaching story meant to convey deeper spiritual lessons. The command “Set forth for them an example” shows that what follows is not simply a historical report, but a moral illustration. The story contrasts two men — one a believer and the other a disbeliever — in order to explore themes of wealth, gratitude, arrogance, and faith.\nThe description of the gardens highlights abundance and stability. Vineyards suggest luxury and long-term investment, date-palms represent lasting provision, and cultivated crops indicate steady, ongoing sustenance. Together, these elements depict layered prosperity — beauty, security, and continuous income.\nThe parable invites readers to reflect on how material success shapes the human heart. Wealth itself is not condemned. Rather, the story examines whether prosperity leads a person toward gratitude and humility, or toward pride and forgetfulness of Allāh.",
  18033: "Each of the two gardens produced its full yield and did not fail in anything thereof, and We caused a river to gush forth through them.",
  18034: "And he had [abundant] fruit. So he said to his companion, while conversing with him, “I am greater than you in wealth and superior in followers.”",
  18035: "He entered his garden, while he was wronging himself. He said, “I do not think that this will ever perish.”",
  18036: "And I do not think that the Hour will be established. And if I am returned to my Lord, I will surely find better than this as a place of return.",
  18037: "His companion said to him, while conversing with him, “Do you disbelieve in the One who created you from dust, then from a drop of sperm, then fashioned you into a well-proportioned man?”",
  18038: "But as for me — He is Allāh, my Lord, and I do not associate anyone with my Lord. [26]\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. This statement is the believing companion’s clear declaration of tawḥīd — the oneness of Allāh. After hearing the other man boast about his wealth and question the Hereafter, he responds by affirming his loyalty and devotion to Allāh alone. His words shift the focus from material success to spiritual truth.\nThe phrase “I do not associate anyone with my Lord” rejects every form of shirk (associating partners with Allāh), whether through worship, reliance, pride in worldly power, or belief that anything exists independently of Him. It is a reaffirmation that all blessing, authority, and destiny belong solely to Allāh.\nIn the flow of the parable, this verse highlights the contrast between arrogance and humility. While the wealthy man places confidence in his possessions and status, the believer anchors himself in faith and exclusive devotion to his Lord. The verse teaches that true security lies not in wealth or social power, but in unwavering monotheism.",
  18039: "If only, when you entered your garden, you had said, “Whatever Allāh wills shall be [27]; there is no power except through Allāh [28].” Even though you see me less than you in wealth and children.\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]. When a man once said to the Prophet ﷺ, “With the will of Allāh and (also) with your will,” the Prophet ﷺ corrected him, saying: “Have you made me an associate with Allāh? Rather, it is what Allāh Alone wills.”\nThis teaches that all will and power belong solely to Allāh, and that even the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ disclaimed any share in divine authority, reaffirming pure  monotheism.\n[28]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said, \"Shall I not teach you a word that is one of the treasures of Paradise? It is to say, \"Lā ḥawla wa lā quwwata illā billāh’ — ‘There is no ability or power except through Allāh.\"\nThis phrase expresses total dependence upon Allāh — acknowledging that no one can change, move, or act without His permission. It is both a shield of humility and a key to divine strength.",
  18040: "Yet it may be that my Lord will give me better than your garden and send upon it a thunderbolt from the sky, so that it becomes a barren, slippery ground.",
  18041: "Or its water may sink deep, and you will never be able to reach it.",
  18042: "And his fruits were encompassed [by ruin] [29], so he began turning his hands over what he had spent on it, while it had fallen upon its trellises, and he said, “Oh, woe to me! Would that I had not associated anyone with my Lord.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. The phrase “his fruits were encompassed” conveys total destruction. The Arabic expression suggests that his produce was surrounded and overtaken by ruin from every side — leaving nothing intact. What had once appeared secure, flourishing, and permanent was wiped out completely.\nThe image of him “turning his hands” describes a physical gesture of shock and regret — wringing or flipping his hands in despair over the wealth he had invested. The garden, once a symbol of pride and self-sufficiency, now lay collapsed upon its trellises, its very supports broken.\nHis cry — “Would that I had not associated anyone with my Lord” — reveals that his downfall was not merely economic but spiritual. His error was not wealth itself, but arrogance and subtle polytheism — trusting in material power, status, and permanence\ninstead of acknowledging dependence on Allāh. The verse shows how quickly worldly security can vanish, and how regret often comes only after the loss has occurred.",
  18043: "He had none to help him against Allāh, nor could he help himself. [30]\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. This concludes the downfall of the arrogant man with stark simplicity:\nAll his wealth, followers, and influence vanished, leaving him alone and powerless before Allāh.\nThe phrase “no one to help him” shows that worldly allies — family, friends, or status — are meaningless when divine judgment comes.\n“Nor could he help himself” completes the picture: even self-reliance collapses when one stands without faith.\nThe message is clear: Human power is fragile; divine power is absolute.\nWealth and support cannot rescue anyone from the decree of Allāh, but faith, humility, and remembrance can protect the soul even when everything else perishes.",
  18044: "There, the authority belongs to Allāh, the True God. He is best in reward and best in outcome.",
  18045: "Set forth for them the example of the worldly life: like water which We send down from the sky, and the plants of the earth mingle with it; then it becomes dry debris, scattered by the winds. Allāh is ever Powerful over all things.",
  18046: "Wealth and children are the adornment of the life of this world. But the enduring righteous deeds are better with your Lord in reward and better in hope. [31]\\n\\nFootnotes: [31]. The “everlasting good deeds” refer to all actions that please Allāh, the Almighty — whether they are the five daily prayers or words of remembrance of Him. Unlike the fleeting adornments of worldly life, these deeds endure forever for those who perform them, and they are called “good” because they are carried out in a manner that pleases Allāh.\nThe Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said, “To say: Subḥānallāh (Glory be to Allāh), Al-ḥamdu lillāh (All praise and gratitude be to Allāh), Lā ilāha illallāh (There is no god but Allāh), and Allāhu Akbar (Allāh is the Greatest) — these are among the everlasting good deeds.”",
  18047: "And [beware of] the Day when We will remove the mountains, and you will see the earth laid bare. We will gather them all together, leaving not a single one behind.",
  18048: "They will be brought before your Lord in rows. He will say: “You have come to Us just as We created you the first time [32]. Yet you claimed We would never appoint a time for you to meet Us.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [32]. The statement “just as We created you the first time” emphasizes that resurrection is not a new or difficult act for Allāh — it is a return to the One who created humanity from nothing. Just as the first creation required no assistance, the second creation is equally effortless for Him.\nThe prophetic teachings explain that people will be resurrected as they were first created: naked, uncircumcised, and alone — stripped of all worldly distinctions and protections. This imagery highlights complete vulnerability and equality before divine judgment. No wealth, status, family ties, or social rank will accompany them.\nThe phrase also underscores individual accountability. Each person will stand before Allāh without the outward supports that once gave them confidence. The mention of an “appointed time” further affirms that resurrection is a fixed and certain meeting, divinely decreed. What some denied in worldly life will become an undeniable reality.",
  18049: "The record of deeds will be laid open before them, and you will see the guilty trembling at what it contains. They will cry out, “Woe to us! What kind of record is this? It leaves nothing small or great without counting it!” They will find everything they did brought before them. And your Lord does not wrong anyone.",
  18050: "And [remember] when We said to the angels, “Prostrate before Ādam,” and they all prostrated except Iblīs. He was one of the jinn, and he rebelled against the command of his Lord. Will you then take him and his offspring as protectors [33] instead of Me, even though they are your enemies? What a miserable exchange [34] this is for the wrongdoers!\\n\\nFootnotes: [33]. The demons could inhabit idols or sacred objects and communicate through them. Out of fear or superstition, the pagans sought protection from the harm of the jinn by appraising them, invoking their names, or offering sacrifices for them. Such acts — seeking refuge, devotion, reliance, and sacrifice — are forms of worship that belong to Allāh alone.\nThis verse warns against giving loyalty, obedience, or spiritual allegiance to Iblīs and his offspring. To treat unseen beings as protectors, intermediaries, or sources of power is to misplace trust and elevate created beings to a position that belongs only to the Creator.\n[34]. Satan, together with his fellow demons, continues to lead people astray from the Straight Path of Islam — deceiving them through false beliefs, desires, and distractions from the remembrance of Allāh.\nThe phrase “wretched is the exchange” refers to the disastrous trade made by those who turn away from Allāh and instead align themselves with Iblīs and his offspring. It is described as an “exchange” because they abandon divine guidance, protection, and mercy in favor of deception and enmity.\nThe verse highlights the irrationality of this choice: they replace the guardianship of the Creator with allegiance to a declared enemy. Instead of seeking refuge in the One who grants life and forgiveness, they follow one who seeks only to mislead and destroy. It is a loss both in this world — through moral corruption — and in the Hereafter — through accountability and punishment.",
  18051: "I did not make them witnesses to the creation of the heavens and the earth, nor to the creation of their own selves. And I would never take those who mislead others as helpers. [35]\\n\\nFootnotes: [35]. This verse refutes the idea that Satan or other unseen beings possess any share in divine authority. Allāh makes clear that they were not present at the creation of the heavens and the earth, nor even at their own creation. They have no knowledge of the unseen, no creative power, and no role in sustaining the universe.\nThe phrase “Nor would I ever take those who lead astray as helpers” emphasizes that misguidance has no place in divine governance. Allāh does not rely on rebellious or corrupt beings to manage His creation. The statement also exposes the contradiction in seeking guidance, protection, or support from those whose very nature is to mislead.\nIn context, the verse reinforces a central theme: ultimate authority, knowledge, and power belong to Allāh alone. To turn to Satan or his followers for protection or direction is not only misguided — it is fundamentally irrational.",
  18052: "And [beware of] the Day, He will say, “Call upon those you claimed to be My partners.” They will call upon them, but they will not respond to them [36]; and We will place between them a destructive barrier.\\n\\nFootnotes: [36]. The false deities they worshipped besides Allāh will completely forsake them on the Day of Judgment — abandoning them at the very moment they need them most. On that Day, those they invoked will disown their followers, declaring their innocence of the worship directed to them. All ties between the worshippers and their idols will be severed, and both will face the reality that none has power or intercession except Allāh alone. And both will share the same doom.",
  18053: "And the guilty will see the Fire and realize that they are destined to fall into it. And they will find no way to escape from it.",
  18054: "We have certainly presented in this Qur’an every kind of example for people [37]. Yet the human being is more argumentative than anything else. [38]\\n\\nFootnotes: [37]. The phrase “We have presented every kind of example” means that the Qur’an presents guidance in many forms — parables, historical accounts, warnings, promises, vivid imagery, logical arguments, and moral illustrations. These varied examples are meant to reach different minds and hearts, leaving no room for confusion. The repetition and diversity of expression reflect divine mercy: the message is clarified from multiple angles so that people may understand and reflect.\n[38]. The description of man as “most quarrelsome” points to the human tendency to argue, dispute, and resist truth — even when clear evidence is presented. While this trait can appear in anyone, it is especially characteristic of persistent disbelievers who reject guidance despite repeated clarification. Instead of sincere reflection, debate is often driven by pride, ego, or blind attachment to inherited beliefs.\nThe verse does not condemn thoughtful questioning or honest inquiry. Rather, it criticizes stubborn argumentation that aims to deflect, delay, or avoid submission to truth. It highlights a recurring human weakness: choosing contention over humility and self-examination.",
  18055: "Nothing prevents people [39] from believing when guidance comes to them, and from seeking forgiveness from their Lord, except that fate of the former peoples should come to them, or that the punishment come before them face to face.\\n\\nFootnotes: [39]. The phrase “Nothing prevents people from believing” indicates that the obstacle is not a lack of evidence or clarity. Guidance has already come, and the path has been made clear. The real barrier lies within the human heart — pride, denial, attachment to worldly comfort, or refusal to change.\nThe verse explains that some people delay faith and repentance until they witness undeniable signs, such as the destruction that befell earlier nations or punishment unfolding before their eyes. Yet true faith requires trust in revelation before such visible consequences occur. Waiting for overwhelming proof often means waiting too long.\nThis passage highlights a recurring human tendency: postponing repentance until confronted with crisis. It reminds readers that sincere belief and seeking forgiveness should arise from humility and reflection — not from fear after punishment becomes unavoidable.",
  18056: "We do not send the Messengers except as bearers of glad tidings and warners [40]. And those who disbelieve argue by falsehood to refute thereby the truth, and they take My revelations and what they are warned of as mockery.\\n\\nFootnotes: [40]. This verse defines the essential role of the Messengers: they are sent as bearers of glad tidings and as warners. They bring hope — promises of mercy, forgiveness, and reward for those who believe and live righteously — and they deliver warning — reminders of accountability and consequence for persistent wrongdoing. Their mission is to convey the message clearly, not to force belief.\nThe verse also describes a common reaction among disbelievers: instead of engaging sincerely with the message, they argue using false claims, distorted reasoning, or deliberate misrepresentation in order to undermine the truth. Some go further by treating divine revelations and warnings as objects of ridicule. Such mockery reflects not intellectual strength, but resistance rooted in pride and denial.\nThe passage highlights a contrast: revelation is delivered with clarity and balance — hope and warning — while rejection often manifests as argumentation and contempt rather than thoughtful reflection.",
  18057: "Who is more unjust than one who is reminded of the signs of his Lord but turns away from them and forgets what his hands have put forward? Indeed, We have placed over their hearts coverings lest they understand it, and in their ears deafness. And if you call them to guidance, they will never then be guided.",
  18058: "Yet your Lord is Most Forgiving, full of mercy. If He were to punish them immediately for what they have done, He would have hastened their punishment. Instead, they have an appointed time from which they will find no escape.",
  18059: "Those towns [41] We destroyed them when they persisted in wrongdoing, and We set a fixed time for their destruction.\\n\\nFootnotes: 41. The “towns” refers to earlier communities that were destroyed after persistent rejection of guidance and continued wrongdoing. Throughout the Qur’an, past nations such as ʿĀd, Thamūd, and others are mentioned as historical examples of moral decline followed by divine judgment. Their destruction was not sudden or unjust; it came only after repeated warnings, clear signs, and ample opportunity to repent.\nThe verse emphasizes two principles: first, punishment follows sustained injustice, not isolated mistakes; second, destruction occurs at a divinely appointed time. Allāh grants respite out of mercy, allowing space for reflection and repentance. But when corruption becomes entrenched and the appointed time arrives, accountability is inevitable.\nThe mention of these towns serves as a warning and a reminder: societies, like individuals, are accountable for collective injustice. History is presented not merely as record, but as moral lesson.",
  18060: "And [remember] when Moses [42] said to his young servant, “I will not stop until I reach the meeting point of the two seas [43], even if I travel for ages.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [42]. The Prophet ﷺ narrated that Moses once stood to address the Children of Israel and was asked whether anyone was more knowledgeable than he was. Believing that he had been given the greatest knowledge available to him, he replied in the negative. Allāh then revealed to him that a servant of His — al-Khiḍr — had been granted knowledge that Moses did not possess. Moses was instructed to seek him at the place where a fish would miraculously come to life and make its way into the sea — this sign would mark their meeting point.\nMoses (Mūsā, peace be upon him) is among the greatest prophets in Islamic tradition, one who received revelation and spoke directly with Allāh. Yet even he was taught that divine knowledge is vast and distributed according to Allah’s wisdom. This episode underscores a profound spiritual lesson: no matter one’s rank, learning, or closeness to revelation, humility remains essential. The pursuit of knowledge is lifelong, and even a prophet continues to grow in understanding under Allāh’s guidance.\n[43]. The “meeting of the two seas” refers to a location known with certainty only to Allāh, where Moses was destined to meet a servant endowed with special knowledge (traditionally identified as al-Khiḍr). The Qur’an does not specify the exact geographical site, and scholars have differed in their views. Among the suggested locations is the region where the Gulf of Aqaba and the Gulf of Suez meet, near the Sinai Peninsula. Others have proposed different possibilities, but no definitive proof establishes one view over another.\nThe primary focus of the verse is not geography, but determination and perseverance in the pursuit of knowledge. Moses’ resolve to travel “for ages” reflects his humility and commitment to learning, even at personal cost. The journey symbolizes that seeking deeper understanding requires patience, effort, and willingness to go beyond one’s comfort.",
  18061: "So when they reached the meeting of the two seas, they forgot their fish, and it took its way into the sea, slipping away.",
  18062: "Then when they had passed beyond it, Moses said to his young servant, “Bring us our meal; we have encountered in this our journey [44] much fatigue.\"\\n\\nFootnotes: [44]. There are six forms of chivalry. Three of them are for when you are at home and three others are for when you are travelling. The three for when you are at home are: reciting the Qur’an, regularly attending the congregational prayer and promoting God's Mosques and making friends for the sake of Allāh. The three for when you are travelling are: to give to others from your own travel provisions, being good-tempered and joking regarding things other than acts of disobedience to Allāh.",
  18063: "He said, “Did you see when we sought shelter at the rock? Indeed, I forgot the fish — and none made me forget it except Satan [45] — that I should mention it; and it took its path into the sea amazingly.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [45]. The young attendant attributes his forgetfulness to Satan, reflecting the Qur’anic teaching that Shayṭān seeks to distract and cause people to forget what is important. This does not mean Satan has independent power to control human beings, but that he whispers, distracts, and diverts attention. Human forgetfulness, however, still occurs within the will and decree of Allāh.\nIn this case, the lapse was not random. What appeared to be a simple mistake was part of a greater divine plan. The forgotten fish was the very sign Moses had been given to locate the servant of Allāh he was seeking. The verse therefore teaches two lessons: first, that believers should recognize and guard against spiritual distraction; and second, that even small events — including moments of forgetfulness — unfold within divine wisdom and purpose.",
  18064: "Moses said, “That is exactly what we were looking for!” So they turned back, retracing their footsteps.",
  18065: "So they found one of Our servants [46], whom We had granted mercy from Us and taught him knowledge from Our Ourselves.\\n\\nFootnotes: [46]. The servant mentioned here is identified as al-Khiḍr, a righteous servant of Allāh endowed with special knowledge. The Qur’an does not explicitly name him, focusing instead on his qualities: servanthood, divine mercy, and knowledge granted directly from Allāh. This emphasis shifts attention away from identity and toward spiritual rank.\nThe phrase “mercy from Us” suggests a special favor — guidance, protection, and nearness to Allāh. The expression “knowledge from Our Presence” indicates knowledge not acquired through ordinary means, but granted directly by divine teaching. It reflects a form of insight into matters of unseen wisdom and divine decree.\nThe verse highlights an important lesson: true honor lies in servitude to Allāh. Even one who possesses extraordinary knowledge is described first and foremost\nas a servant. Knowledge and mercy are gifts from Allāh, not sources of pride.",
  18066: "Moses said to him, “Shall I follow you [47], so that you may teach me from what you have been taught of right guidance?”\\n\\nFootnotes: [47]. Moses’ request, “May I follow you?”, reflects remarkable humility. Although he was a prophet and recipient of revelation, he does not command or assume authority. Instead, he politely asks permission to accompany the servant of Allāh and to learn from him. The language shows respect, restraint, and eagerness for growth.\nThe phrase “from what you have been taught” acknowledges that all knowledge ultimately comes from Allāh. Moses recognizes that this servant’s knowledge is a divine gift, not a personal achievement. By asking to learn “some” of it, he demonstrates modesty and awareness that knowledge is vast and cannot be fully encompassed by any single individual.\nThis verse teaches an enduring lesson: true scholarship is grounded in humility. Rank, experience, or previous knowledge should never prevent a person from seeking deeper understanding. Even a prophet models the proper etiquette of learning — sincerity, patience, and respect for the teacher.",
  18067: "He replied, “You will not be able to have patience with me.”",
  18068: "How can you have patience over what you have not encompassed in knowledge?",
  18069: "Moses said, “You will find me patient, if Allāh wills, and I will not disobey you in any matter.”",
  18070: "He said, \"Then if you follow me, do not ask me about anything until I myself mention it to you.\"",
  18071: "So they both set out, until when they had embarked on a boat, he made a hole in it. Moses said, “Have you made a hole in it to drown its people? You have certainly done a dreadful thing!”",
  18072: "He said, “Did I not say that you would not be able to have patience with me?”",
  18073: "Moses said, “Do not blame me for what I forgot [48], and do not impose hardship upon me in my matter with you.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [48]. Moses attributes his objection to forgetfulness, not defiance. Earlier, he had pledged patience, but in the shock of seeing what appeared to be a wrongful act, he reacted instinctively. His request, “Do not blame me for what I forgot,” reflects humility and self-correction rather than argument.\nSome commentators note that this forgetfulness was not a failure of faith, but a natural human response to something outwardly troubling. The Qur’an consistently portrays prophets as human — capable of emotion, concern, and momentary oversight — while still guided and sincere.\nThe verse also highlights the etiquette of seeking knowledge. A sincere student admits error and asks for understanding instead of persisting in pride. Moses’ response models accountability, modesty, and a desire to continue learning despite his lapse.\nThe Prophet ﷺ said: \"The first excuse given by Moses, was that he had forgotten, while they were in the ship. Then a sparrow came and sat over the edge of the ship and dipped its beak once in the sea. Al-Khidr said to Moses, \"My knowledge and your knowledge, compared to Allâh’s Knowledge is like what this sparrow has taken out of the sea.\"",
  18074: "Then they both proceeded until they met a boy, he killed him [49]. Moses said, “Have you killed an innocent soul who has killed no one? You have certainly done a deplorable thing!”\\n\\nFootnotes: [49]. This is the second and more intense test of Moses’ patience. Unlike the boat, where the harm appeared indirect, the killing of a boy looks like a clear violation of justice. Moses therefore responds strongly, describing the child as an “innocent soul” — meaning someone who had committed no crime deserving death. His reaction reflects the prophetic commitment to the sanctity of life and the outward rulings of the Sharīʿah, which protect life and forbid killing without right.\nAt this point in the story, Moses is judging by what is visible. Al-Khiḍr’s actions, however, are tied to knowledge and wisdom that Moses has not yet been shown. The Qur’an delays the explanation to teach a central lesson: some events cannot be understood by appearances alone, and divine wisdom may be hidden behind matters that seem disturbing in the moment.\nThis verse also highlights the difference between human responsibility and divine decree. Human beings are accountable to act according to clear guidance and justice, while Allāh’s knowledge encompasses what people do not know — including future consequences and unseen realities.",
  18075: "He said, “Did I not tell you that you would not be able to have patience with me?”",
  18076: "Moses said, “If I ask you about anything after this, then you need not keep me in your company. You would then have every right to part from me.”",
  18077: "So they both proceeded until, when they came to the people of a town, they asked its people for food, but they refused to host them. Then they found therein a wall wanting to collapse, so he set it straight [50]. He said, “If you wished, you could have taken for it a payment.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [50]. This verse subtly highlights the dignity of skill and craftsmanship. Allāh, Exalted is He, has honored the human being with intelligence, understanding, and the ability to build, repair, and create. These capacities are not merely worldly talents — they are divine gifts, entrusted to humanity as part of their responsibility on earth.\nFrom the earliest prophets, this gift was evident. Allāh made iron pliable for Prophet Dāwūd (David), enabling him to shape it with ease — a sign of divine favor and mastery of craft. Prophet Zakariyyā (peace be upon him) worked as a carpenter, earning his livelihood through his own hands. The Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ praised such self-reliance, saying: “The best food a person eats is from the work of his own hands.” Honest labor, therefore, is not inferior to worship — it can itself be an act of worship when done sincerely.\nProphet ʿĪsā (Jesus), peace be upon him, also lived humbly and worked with his hands. Both Prophet Mūsā (Moses) and Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ tended flocks in their early lives — an occupation that cultivates patience, care, and leadership.\nIn this story, al-Khiḍr demonstrates practical skill when he repairs the collapsing wall in a town whose people had refused them hospitality. His act combines craftsmanship with wisdom and hidden mercy. It shows that excellence in work and sincerity in intention can coexist.\nThe broader lesson is timeless: honest labor is honorable, and mastery of one’s craft reflects gratitude to the Creator who granted the ability. Whatever profession a person pursues, it should be approached with sincerity, excellence (ihsān), and skill — for benefit arises only through dedication and responsible effort.",
  18078: "He said, “This is the time for us to part. Now I will explain to you the meaning of what you were unable to bear patiently.”",
  18079: "As for the ship, it belonged to poor people who worked at sea, so I intended to damage it slightly [51]. There was a king behind them who was seizing every sound ship by force.\\n\\nFootnotes: [51]. The action that looked harmful (damaging the boat) was actually a protective measure: by making the vessel temporarily unserviceable, al-Khiḍr prevented a tyrant from confiscating it and causing permanent loss to its poor owners.\nThe verse highlights a recurring Qur’anic theme: outwardly harmful events may conceal wise mercy when seen from Allāh’s larger perspective.",
  18080: "As for the boy, his parents were believers, and we feared that he would overburden them with transgression and disbelief. [52]\\n\\nFootnotes: [52]. The phrase “we feared” reflects knowledge granted by Allāh concerning the boy’s future — not speculation or personal judgment. According to commentators, the boy would have grown up to persist in disbelief and rebellion, causing deep spiritual harm and distress to his believing parents. The fear mentioned here is therefore rooted in divinely revealed knowledge, not human assumption.\nThis episode does not justify taking life based on anticipated wrongdoing. In Islamic law, no one is punished for what they might do in the future. Rather, this was a unique act carried out by a servant acting under direct divine instruction, outside the ordinary legal framework that governs human conduct.\nThe deeper lesson concerns divine wisdom and unseen mercy. What appeared to be a tragedy concealed protection for the parents’ faith and future well-being. It reminds believers that Allāh’s knowledge encompasses outcomes beyond human perception, and that events which seem painful may contain hidden mercy known only to Him.",
  18081: "So we intended that their Lord would replace him with someone better than him — purer in character and closer in compassion.",
  18082: "As for the wall, it belonged to two orphan boys in the city. Beneath it was a treasure that belonged to them, and their father had been a righteous man. So your Lord willed that they would reach maturity and uncover their treasure — as a mercy from your Lord. I did not do any of this on my own initiative [53]. This is the meaning of what you could not bear patiently.\\n\\nFootnotes: [53]. The statement “I did not do any of this on my own initiative” makes clear that these actions were not personal decisions, but carried out under divine instruction. Al-Khiḍr — the servant mentioned in this narrative — is identified in Islamic scholarship as a righteous servant of Allāh endowed with special, divinely granted knowledge. The Qur’an does not explicitly call him a prophet, and scholars have differed on whether he was a prophet or a righteous servant (walī). What is certain from the text is that he acted based on revelation or direct divine guidance, not personal judgment.\nThis clarification is essential: the events described here are exceptional and tied to specific knowledge granted by Allāh. They do not establish general principles for human behavior. Ordinary human beings remain bound by revealed law and are accountable according to what is outwardly known.\nThe verse also conveys two enduring lessons. First, the righteousness of a parent can become a source of protection and blessing for their children. The treasure was preserved because their father had been righteous — suggesting that piety can benefit future generations. Second, divine wisdom often lies beyond immediate appearances. What seemed like an unnecessary act — repairing a wall in a town that refused hospitality — was in reality a hidden mercy safeguarding vulnerable orphans.\nThe story concludes by reaffirming a central truth: human knowledge is limited, while Allāh’s knowledge encompasses the unseen past, present, and future. Patience is often required before wisdom becomes clear.",
  18083: "They ask you about Dhul-Qarnayn [54]. Say, “I will relate to you something of his story as a reminder.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [54]. Dhul-Qarnayn (ذو القرنين) is a title meaning “the one of two horns” (often understood as an emblem of power or reach). The Qur’an does not give his personal name; instead, it presents him as a God-fearing ruler whom Allāh enabled on earth and who traveled widely, ruling with justice and building a barrier against Gog and Magog (18:84–98). \nScholars have long differed over his historical identity. Some modern writers connected him with Alexander in legendary form; others argued for figures such as Cyrus or certain ancient Arabian/Yemeni kings. Because the Qur’an does not confirm an identity, the safest approach is to treat “Dhul-Qarnayn” as a Qur’anic figure presented for moral instruction, not as a biography lesson.\nThe phrase “I will relate to you something of his story as a reminder” also signals selectivity: only what is spiritually beneficial is narrated—highlighting leadership, justice, humility before Allāh, and responsible use of power.",
  18084: "Indeed, We established him firmly in the land [55], and gave him a means to every things. [56]\\n\\nFootnotes: [55]. The phrase “We established him firmly in the land” indicates that Dhul-Qarnayn was granted authority, stability, and influence over a vast territory. This establishment was not merely political power, but divinely granted strength, security, and capacity to govern. The Qur’an attributes his position directly to Allāh, reminding readers that all authority ultimately comes from Him. Power, when viewed correctly, is a trust (amānah), not a personal achievement.\n[56]. The expression “We gave him a means to every thing” refers to the resources, knowledge, pathways, and capabilities necessary to achieve his objectives. The word sabab (means) can include material tools, strategic knowledge, access routes, and practical skills. It does not imply unlimited or divine power, but rather that Allāh provided him with what was needed to fulfill his mission.\nTogether, these two statements emphasize a central lesson: success arises from divinely granted means combined with responsible effort. Dhul-Qarnayn’s journeys were not random conquests, but purposeful undertakings made possible through resources and guidance bestowed by Allāh.",
  18085: "So he pursued a course. [57]\\n\\nFootnotes: [57]. The phrase “So he pursued a course” indicates that Dhul-Qarnayn made active use of the means granted to him. The word sabab (means, pathway, route) suggests both physical routes of travel and the practical resources or strategies necessary to accomplish a mission. In this context, it refers to the path that led toward the west, marking the first of his recorded journeys.\nThis brief statement highlights an important principle: divine provision does not replace human effort. Allāh granted Dhul-Qarnayn authority and access, but he still had to plan, travel, and act. Achievement in the Qur’anic perspective combines divine enablement with responsible initiative.\nThe verse also introduces a sequence of purposeful expeditions, each illustrating how power should be exercised — with justice, restraint, and awareness of accountability before Allāh.",
  18086: "Until, when he reached the far western horizon — where the sun sets — he saw it as though sinking into a dark, murky spring. There he found a people. We said, “O Dhul-Qarnayn, you may either punish them or treat them with kindness.”",
  18087: "He said, “As for the one who does wrong [58], we will punish him. Then he will be returned to his Lord, who will punish him with a severe punishment.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [58]. The phrase “the one who does wrong” includes those who commit injustice and oppression, and especially those who persist in disbelief and associate partners with Allāh in worship (idolatry). In the Qur’anic sense, wrongdoing is not limited to social injustice; it also includes spiritual injustice — denying the truth after it has been made clear and directing acts of devotion to other than Allāh.\nDhul-Qarnayn distinguishes between wrongdoing and righteousness, demonstrating that authority must be exercised with moral discernment. He recognizes his role in administering worldly justice — “we shall punish him” — yet he affirms that ultimate judgment belongs to Allāh — “then he will be returned to his Lord.” This prevents tyranny, reminding rulers that their authority is limited and accountable.\nThe verse teaches that worldly punishment is not the final measure of justice. Even if someone avoids human consequences, they remain subject to divine reckoning. Thus, Dhul-Qarnayn’s response models responsible leadership grounded in awareness of Allāh’s supreme sovereignty.",
  18088: "But as for the one who believes and does righteous deeds, he will have the best reward, and we will speak to him with ease and kindness in our command.",
  18089: "Then he pursued another course. [59]\\n\\nFootnotes: [59]. The phrase “Then he pursued another course” repeats the earlier expression, emphasizing purposeful movement. The word sabab (means, path, route) again suggests that Dhul-Qarnayn made active use of the resources and access granted to him. His journeys were deliberate and directed, not random exploration.\nIn this instance, the narrative shifts from the western horizon to another direction — traditionally understood as the east. The repetition of the phrase highlights a pattern: divine enablement followed by responsible action. Authority alone does not define leadership; what matters is how one uses the means provided.\nThe structure of the passage also signals that each journey will present a new moral situation, testing how power is exercised in different contexts.",
  18090: "Until, when he reached the far eastern horizon — where the sun rises — he found it rising over a people for whom We had made no shelter or covering against it. [60]\\n\\nFootnotes: [60]. The phrase “for whom We had made no shelter or covering against it” describes a people living in an exposed region, without natural barriers such as mountains, forests, or substantial dwellings to shield them from the sun. Some commentators suggested that they may have lived in open plains or constructed minimal structures, while others understood it simply as a description of a harsh climate where the sun’s heat was direct and intense.\nThe verse does not imply primitiveness or inferiority, but rather emphasizes environmental conditions. Just as Dhul-Qarnayn encountered different societies in the west, he now meets a community shaped by distinct geographical circumstances. The Qur’an presents these scenes to illustrate the breadth of his travels and the diversity of human habitation across the earth.\nAs in the previous account, the focus is not on geography alone, but on how authority interacts with varied peoples and conditions. The narrative highlights exposure — both physical exposure to the sun and the broader theme that Allāh’s knowledge encompasses all lands and peoples, whether sheltered or uncovered.",
  18091: "Thus it was; and We had encompassed whatever he had in knowledge.",
  18092: "Then he pursued another course.",
  18093: "Until, When he came to a valley between two mountain barriers, he found there a people who could scarcely comprehend a word. [61]\\n\\nFootnotes: [61]. The phrase “a people who could scarcely comprehend a word” indicates a significant language barrier between them and Dhul-Qarnayn. It does not imply intellectual deficiency, but rather difficulty in communication due to linguistic difference or isolation. The expression suggests that they spoke a distinct language or dialect unfamiliar to outsiders.\nThe mention of the two mountain barriers further implies that this community lived in a remote and geographically enclosed region. Their isolation may have limited cultural exchange, making communication with travelers especially difficult.\nThe verse highlights the diversity of humanity — in language, geography, and social development. It also sets the stage for their request for protection, showing that despite linguistic barriers, justice and cooperation can transcend differences.",
  18094: "They said, “O Dhul-Qarnayn, indeed Gog and Magog are corrupters in the land [62]. So shall we assign for you a payment that you may make between us and them a barrier?”\\n\\nFootnotes: [62]. Gog and Magog (Yaʾjūj and Maʾjūj) are described in the Qur’an as two groups from among the descendants of Adam who spread corruption in the land. Their exact identity and location are not definitively established in revelation. Some scholars placed them in the far northern regions beyond Palestine, while others differed. The Qur’an focuses less on geography and more on their moral characterization as a destructive force.\nAccording to prophetic traditions, Dhul-Qarnayn was enabled by Allāh to construct a massive barrier between two mountain ranges to restrain them. This barrier remained intact by divine will. The Prophet ﷺ described that they continue attempting to break through it, but they will not succeed until the time decreed by Allāh near the end of days.\nAuthentic ḥadīth narrations state that their release will occur after the descent of ʿĪsā (Jesus, peace be upon him) and after he slays the False Messiah (al-Dajjāl). Gog and Magog will then emerge in overwhelming numbers, spreading devastation across the earth. Some narrations mention that they will pass by Lake Tiberias and consume its waters.\nJesus (peace be upon him) and the believers with him will retreat to Mount Ṭūr, seeking refuge and praying for deliverance. Allāh will then send a sudden affliction — described in ḥadīth as worms or parasites striking their necks — causing their destruction. Thereafter, Allāh will send rain to cleanse the earth of their remains, restoring purity and peace.\nThese accounts form part of Islamic belief in the end times. The central lesson is not geographical speculation, but affirmation of divine control over history: even the most overwhelming forces remain subject to Allāh’s decree, and ultimate victory belongs to Him.",
  18095: "He said, “What my Lord has established me with is far better than your tribute. So support me with manpower, and I will construct a strong dam between you and them.”",
  18096: "“Bring me blocks of iron.” Then, when he had filled the gap between the two mountain walls, he said, “Blow!” And when he had made it glow like fire, he said, “Bring me molten copper to pour over it.”",
  18097: "Thus they could not climb over it, nor could they pierce through it.",
  18098: "He said, “This is a mercy from my Lord. But when my Lord’s promise comes to pass, He will level it to the ground. And the promise of my Lord is always true.”",
  18099: "On that Day, We will let them surge over one another like waves. Then the Trumpet will be blown, and We will gather them all together. [63]\\n\\nFootnotes: [63]. The phrase “We will let them surge against one another like waves” portrays a scene of overwhelming chaos and unrest. Some commentators understood it to refer to Gog and Magog spreading across the earth in vast numbers after their release. Others viewed it more broadly as a description of humanity in turmoil near the end of time. The imagery of waves suggests confusion, disorder, and unstoppable movement.\nThe mention of “the Trumpet” refers to the blast that will signal the end of the world and the beginning of resurrection. At its sounding, worldly life will come to an end, and all creation will be gathered for judgment.\nThe verse moves from earthly events to ultimate reality. No matter how powerful or chaotic events appear in history, they culminate in divine reckoning. The final gathering leaves no one absent — rulers and subjects, oppressors and oppressed — all stand equally before Allāh.",
  18100: "On that Day We shall present Hell to the disbelievers in full display.",
  18101: "Those whose eyes were veiled from My reminder [64], and who could not bear to hear [the truth].”\\n\\nFootnotes: 64. The phrase “whose eyes were veiled from My reminder” describes spiritual blindness, not physical impairment. It refers to those who turned away from revelation and refused to reflect upon its message. The “reminder” (dhikr) includes divine revelation, signs in creation, and every call to truth that directs a person back to Allāh.\nLikewise, “they could not bear to hear” does not mean they were physically deaf, but that they were unwilling to listen with sincerity. Their hearts were closed by pride, attachment to false beliefs, or deliberate rejection. In Qur’anic language, hearing implies receptive listening — the kind that leads to understanding and response.\nThe verse highlights a recurring theme: persistent refusal to see and hear truth results in spiritual veiling. Guidance is available, but it benefits only those who approach it with humility and openness.",
  18102: "Do those who disbelieve think they can take My servants as protectors instead of Me? Indeed, We have prepared Hell as a dwelling for the disbelievers.",
  18103: "Say, “Shall We inform you who are the greatest losers in their deeds?”",
  18104: "They are those whose efforts are lost in the pursuit of worldly life, while they think they are doing good. [65]\\n\\nFootnotes: 65. This verse describes a profound spiritual loss: people who exert effort, invest energy, and believe themselves to be successful — yet their striving ultimately amounts to nothing in the sight of Allāh. Their loss is not due to lack of activity, but misdirected intention and false foundations. Scholars explain that this applies foremost to those who reject faith while performing deeds they consider righteous. Without correct belief and sincere intention for Allāh, actions lose their eternal value. Good works disconnected from truth may yield worldly benefit, reputation, or satisfaction, but they do not secure reward in the Hereafter.\nThe verse also carries a broader warning for believers: sincerity and alignment with divine guidance are essential. A person may feel confident in their actions, yet true success depends on conformity to revelation and purity of intention. It is therefore not effort alone that matters, but whether that effort is rooted in faith and directed toward pleasing Allāh.",
  18105: "They are the ones who rejected the signs of their Lord and denied the meeting with Him. So their deeds have become worthless [66], and on the Day of Resurrection We will assign them no weight.\\n\\nFootnotes: [66]. For the acceptance of the righteous deeds it is stipulated that the following two basic conditions must be fulfilled: (a) The intentions while doing such deeds must be totally for Allah’s sake only without any show-off or to gain praise or fame. \n(b) Such a deed must be performed in accordance with the Sunnah of God's Messenger Muhammad bin ‘Abdullâh ﷺ, the last of the Prophets and the Messengers of God. Muhammad, God's Messenger ﷺ said: \"If somebody innovates something which is not present in our religion (of Islâmic Monotheism), then that thing will be rejected.\"  He also said: \"Whoever performs a 'good' deed which we have not ordered anyone to do 'or is not in accord with our religion of Islâmic Monotheism', then that deed will be rejected and will not be accepted.\"",
  18106: "That is their recompense—Hell—because they disbelieved and took My signs and My messengers in mockery. [67]\\n\\nFootnotes: [67]. The verse identifies two intertwined causes of their punishment: disbelief and mockery. It was not mere ignorance, but a conscious rejection of divine revelation and a deliberate ridiculing of the signs of Allāh and His messengers. Mockery here represents a hardened state of heart — treating sacred truth as something trivial or laughable rather than worthy of reflection and reverence.\nIn the Qur’anic worldview, ridicule of revelation is more than a verbal insult; it is a form of spiritual arrogance. It reflects pride, resistance to accountability, and a refusal to humble oneself before divine guidance. Such an attitude closes the door to repentance, because one who mocks the truth rarely seeks to understand it sincerely.\nThe verse also underscores the dignity of the messengers. To mock them is, in reality, to mock the One who sent them. The severity of the recompense corresponds to the gravity of rejecting and ridiculing clear signs after they have been plainly conveyed.\nAt the same time, the Qur’an consistently presents guidance with clarity. The disbelievers choose the path they follow. They have been shown, in detail, both the path of ruin and the path of eternal reward — throughout this chapter and the Qur’an as a whole — so that they might reflect and open their hearts to faith. Accountability therefore rests upon conscious choice after knowledge has been made clear.",
  18107: "Indeed, those who believe and do righteous deeds will have the Gardens of Paradise as their dwelling place [68],\\n\\nFootnotes: [68]. The term “Gardens of Paradise” translates Jannāt al-Firdaws, which in Islamic tradition refers to the highest and most excellent level of Paradise. The word Firdaws conveys the meaning of a lush, elevated garden — abundant, enclosed, and full of beauty. In prophetic teachings, believers are encouraged to ask Allāh for al-Firdaws, as it is described as the loftiest rank in Paradise and closest to the Divine Throne.\nThe verse uses the word nuzul (lodging or reception), which originally refers to what is prepared to welcome an honored guest. This suggests that Paradise is not merely a reward earned, but a gracious reception from Allāh — a prepared place of honor, hospitality, and eternal rest.\nThe pairing of faith and righteous deeds throughout the Qur’an emphasizes that belief is not merely inward conviction, nor are deeds sufficient without faith. True success combines sincere faith with upright action, resulting in a permanent and noble abode in the Hereafter.",
  18108: "Abiding therein forever, never wishing to leave it.",
  18109: "Say, “If the sea were ink for writing the words of my Lord [69], the sea would be exhausted before the words of my Lord were exhausted — even if We were to add another sea like it to replenish it.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [69]. The phrase “the words of my Lord” refers to the limitless knowledge, wisdom, decrees, and speech of Allāh. It includes revelation, divine commands, and the unfolding realities of creation itself. The verse uses a powerful image: even if the vast oceans were turned into ink, they would be exhausted before the fullness of Allāh’s words could be written.\nThis imagery emphasizes divine infinity. Human language, writing, and comprehension are finite, while Allāh’s knowledge has no boundary. The comparison does not suggest that Allāh’s words are physically written in such a manner; rather, it conveys that His knowledge and wisdom are beyond enumeration and completion.\nThe closing of the sūrah with this image reminds the reader of humility. However much one learns, reflects, or writes, it remains only a drop from an endless ocean of divine knowledge.",
  18110: "Say, [O Muhammad], “I am only a human being like you [70]. It has been revealed to me that your God is only One God [Allāh alone]. So whoever hopes to meet his Lord, let him do righteous deeds and never associate anyone in the worship of his Lord.\" [71]\\n\\nFootnotes: [70]. The statement “I am only a human being like you” affirms the humanity of the Prophet ﷺ and all the messengers before him. Noah, Abraham, Moses, Jesus, and Muhammad — peace be upon them all — were honored servants of Allāh, yet they were human beings, not divine. They did not possess independent knowledge of the unseen except what Allāh revealed to them. Their greatness lay in their obedience, sincerity, and faithful delivery of revelation — not in any share of divinity.\nThe central message conveyed through all prophets was the oneness of Allāh (monotheism): that God is One, without partner, rival, or equal. Worship, reliance, supplication, and devotion belong to Him alone. No prophet, saint, angel, or created being is to be worshiped or treated as a partner alongside Him.\n[71]. The Prophet Muhammad (ﷺ), the last and final Messenger of God, reported that Allāh said: “I am the One most free of need from having partners. Whoever performs a deed while associating others with Me, I abandon him and his act of associating partners with Me.”\nA Companion of the Prophet narrated:\n“The Messenger of God (ﷺ) once came out to us while we were discussing the Antichrist (Ad-Dajjāl) and said: ‘Shall I not tell you of something I fear for you even more than the Antichrist?’ We said: ‘Yes, O Messenger of God.’ He said: ‘Hidden polytheism — when a man stands to pray and beautifies his prayer because he sees another person watching him.’",
  19001: "Kāf Hā Yā ʿAin Ṣād. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2: verse 1.",
  19002: "This is a reminder of your Lord’s mercy to His servant, Zachariah.",
  19003: "When he called out to his Lord in private.",
  19004: "He said, “My Lord, my bones have grown weak, and my head shines with white hair; yet I have never been disappointed in my prayer to You, my Lord.”",
  19005: "I fear what my relatives may do after me, and my wife [2] is barren; so grant me from Yourself an heir,\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The statement “my wife is barren” reflects both biological impossibility and human limitation. Prophet Zakariyyā (peace be upon him) and his wife were advanced in age, and she had never borne children. From a worldly perspective, there was no apparent means for them to have offspring. Yet his supplication was directed to Allāh, whose power is not bound by natural causes.\nThe verse highlights a recurring Qur’anic theme: divine ability surpasses human expectation. What appears impossible within ordinary experience is entirely possible for Allāh. Zakariyyā’s prayer was not driven by personal desire alone, but by concern for the preservation of faith and righteous leadership after him.\nThus, the mention of barrenness intensifies the miracle that follows. The birth of Yaḥyā (John, peace be upon him) becomes a sign of divine mercy — demonstrating that when hope in worldly means fades, reliance upon Allāh remains the believer’s refuge.",
  19006: "who will inherit from me and from the family of Jacob [3], and make him, my Lord, pleasing to You.\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. That is, he will inherit from me and from the family of Jacob the legacy of religious knowledge and prophethood. The inheritance referred to here is not material wealth, for the prophets do not leave behind worldly riches to be inherited. Rather, as the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“We, the Prophets, do not leave behind inheritance in the form of wealth; what we leave behind is charity.”\nThus, Zakariyyā’s plea was for a righteous heir to continue the mission of Islamic faith, knowledge, and guidance — a successor who would preserve the divine message and serve Allāh in a way that is pleasing to Him.",
  19007: "“O Zakariyyā, We bring you glad tidings of a son whose name is Yaḥyā [4]; never have We given this name to anyone before.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. Yaḥyā is identified in the New Testament as John (John the Baptist), son of Zechariah. The Qur’anic name derives from the Arabic root ḥ-y-y, meaning “to live.” Some scholars note that the name may carry the sense of “he lives” or “one given life,” possibly alluding to the spiritual renewal he would bring. The verse also states that no one had previously been given this name, which scholars understand either as literal uniqueness of the name or as indicating his unmatched character and mission.",
  19008: "He said, “My Lord, how can I have a son when my wife is barren and I have reached such extreme old age?”",
  19009: "He said, “So it will be. Your Lord says, \"It is easy for Me — I created you before, when you were nothing at all.\"",
  19010: "Zachariah said, “My Lord, grant me a sign.” He said, “Your sign is that you will not speak to people for three nights, though you are perfectly sound.”",
  19011: "So he came out to his people from the sanctuary and signaled to them, “Glorify Allāh morning and evening.”",
  19012: "“O Yaḥyā, hold fast to the Scripture.”\nAnd We granted him wisdom while he was still a boy.",
  19013: "And compassion from Us, and purity — and he was devout.",
  19014: "and dutiful to his parents — he was neither domineering nor disobedient.",
  19015: "And peace be upon him the day he was born, the day he dies, and the day he is raised to life again.",
  19016: "And mention in the Book Mary, when she withdrew from her family to a place toward the east.",
  19017: "She drew a veil apart from them; then We sent to her Our Spirit [5], and he appeared to her as a well-proportioned man.\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. “Our Spirit” (Arabic: rūḥanā) is traditionally understood to refer to the angel Gabriel, elsewhere described as the bearer of divine revelation (cf. 2:97; 26:193). The expression denotes a spirit sent by Allāh and does not imply a part of the Divine Essence. In this verse, the Spirit appears to Mary in the form of a well-proportioned human being.",
  19018: "She said, “I seek refuge in the Most Compassionate from you, [do not approach me]  if you truly fear Allāh.”",
  19019: "He said, “I am but a messenger from your Lord, that I may grant you a pure son.”",
  19020: "She said, “How can I have a son when no man has touched me, nor have I been unchaste?”",
  19021: "He said, “So it will be. Your Lord says, \"It is easy for Me — that We may make him a sign for the people and a mercy from Us. And it is a matter already decreed.\"",
  19022: "So she conceived him, and withdrew with him to a far-off place. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. That is, she withdrew to Bethlehem—an outlying town situated roughly ten kilometers south of Jerusalem—seeking seclusion from the people so she could give birth away from their sight. She sought a remote place out of modesty, fear of reproach, and to devote herself fully to the command of her Lord.",
  19023: "The pains of labor drove her to the trunk of a palm tree. She said, “Oh, would that I had died before this and had been completely forgotten!”",
  19024: "Then he called out to her from below her [7], “Do not grieve; your Lord has placed a stream beneath you.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. The identity of the caller is interpreted in two main ways:\n(A) Jibrīl (Gabriel) — comforting her and instructing her.\n(B) ʿĪsā (Jesus, as an infant) — speaking miraculously to reassure his mother.\nThe wording indicates a gentle, reassuring voice coming from a place just beneath her, soothing her fear and reminding her of the mercy of her Lord, who caused a fresh stream to flow beneath her for nourishment and relief.",
  19025: "Shake the trunk of the palm tree toward you; it will drop upon you fresh, ripe dates. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. The command to shake the palm tree highlights both divine provision and human participation. Though Mary was in a weakened state after childbirth, she is instructed to act, after which Allāh caused fresh dates to fall. Commentators note that dates are especially nourishing and strengthening, particularly for a woman after labor. The image conveys comfort, sustenance, and gentle reassurance in a moment of distress.\nSome exegetes further observe that the mention of fresh, ripe dates suggests that the birth took place during a warm season, since dates ripen and are harvested in late summer. As Mary labored, she was told to shake the palm tree, and fresh dates fell for her to eat—an event associated with the natural season of ripening. On this basis, some have inferred that the birth of Jesus occurred during the warmer months rather than in winter. While the Qur’an does not specify an exact date, the imagery of freshly ripened dates points toward the summer harvest season rather than the cold of December.",
  19026: "So eat, drink, and be comforted. And if you see any human being, say, “I have vowed a fast to the Most Compassionate, so I will not speak today to any human being.”",
  19027: "Then she brought him to her people, carrying him. They said, “O Mary, you have certainly done a grievous thing.”",
  19028: "O sister of Aaron [9], your father was not a wicked man, nor was your mother unchaste.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. Mary (Maryam) lived many generations after Prophet Moses (Mūsā) and his brother Aaron (Hārūn).\nSo, this “Aaron” mentioned here cannot literally be the brother of Moses.\nInstead, Mary was a descendant of the family of ʿImrān, the priestly lineage descending from Aaron.\nThus, the name Hārūn (Aaron) in this verse is symbolic of lineage and virtue, not of direct sibling relationship.\nA companion of the Prophet said:\nWhen I went to the people of Najrān, they said: “You read in your Qur’an, ‘O sister of Aaron,’ yet Moses came long before Jesus!”\nI returned to the Prophet ﷺ and told him what they said. The Prophet ﷺ replied:\n“They used to name their people after the prophets and righteous men who had gone before them.”\nMeaning: “sister of Aaron” was an honorary or genealogical title, referring to her descent from the family of Aaron or resemblance to his piety — not literal sisterhood.\nIn ancient Semitic usage (Arabic, Hebrew, Aramaic), calling someone “brother” or “sister” of a past figure could mean:\nDescent or family lineage (i.e., from the tribe or clan of so-and-so), or Moral likeness (i.e., a woman as pious as Aaron).\nHence “O sister of Aaron!” likely meant:\n“O woman of the lineage or devotion of Aaron (the priestly family).”",
  19029: "So she pointed to him. They said, “How can we speak to a child in the cradle?”",
  19030: "Jesus said, \"Indeed, I am a servant of Allāh. He has given me the Scripture and made me a prophet. [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. The verbs “has given” and “made” are expressed in the past tense in Arabic, though Jesus speaks here as an infant. Scholars explain that the past tense conveys certainty and divine decree, indicating that his prophethood and reception of the Scripture were already ordained by Allāh and thus spoken of as accomplished.",
  19031: "He has made me blessed wherever I may be, and has enjoined upon me prayer and almsgiving so long as I live.",
  19032: "and dutiful to my mother — He has not made me tyrannical or wretched.",
  19033: "And peace be upon me the day I was born, the day I die, and the day I am raised to life again.",
  19034: "That is Jesus, son of Mary — the statement of truth about which they dispute.",
  19035: "It is not befitting for Allāh to take a son unto Himself. Glory be to Him! When He decrees a matter, He only says to it, “Be,” and it is.",
  19036: "Indeed, Allāh is my Lord and your Lord, so worship Him. This is a straight path.”",
  19037: "Yet the factions differed among themselves [11]. So woe to those who disbelieve from the witnessing of a tremendous Day.\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. The “factions” refer to Christian groups who differed concerning the nature and status of Jesus. Among the people, some exaggerated regarding him until they claimed divinity for him or ascribed to him sonship. Others disputed or denied aspects of his mission. Yet those who remained upon his original teaching held that he was but a human being—honored with prophethood, a servant of God, and His messenger sent to guide the Children of Israel. This verse alludes to such sectarian divisions, particularly disputes over whether Jesus was a servant and messenger of God or possessed a divine nature. It affirms that Jesus was neither divine nor the son of God, but a servant and messenger of Allāh, and it warns against both exaggeration in elevating him beyond his human status and denial of his prophethood and mission.",
  19038: "How clearly they will hear and see on the Day they come to Us! But today the wrongdoers are in clear error.",
  19039: "Warn them of the Day of Regret, when the matter is decided, while they are heedless and do not believe.",
  19040: "Indeed, it is We who inherit the earth and all who are upon it, and to Us they will all be returned.",
  19041: "And mention in the Book Abraham. He was indeed a man of truth, a prophet.",
  19042: "When he said to his father, “O my dear father, why do you worship that which neither hears nor sees, nor can benefit you at all?”",
  19043: "“O my dear father [12], indeed there has come to me of knowledge what has not come to you; so follow me — I will guide you to a straight path.” [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. That is, Āzar (also known as Terah), the father of Abraham. He served as the chief priest under King Nimrod Shaddād of Babylon and Nineveh and was a skilled master sculptor of idols.\n[13]. That is, Islam — the path of right belief in Allāh, free from the worship of images and idols, devoted solely to Him. It is the way of true worship, lawful livelihood, truthful speech, upright conduct, righteous effort and perseverance, mindful awareness, and sincere concentration upon all that draws one closer to Allāh.",
  19044: "O my dear father, do not worship Satan. Indeed, Satan has ever been rebellious against the Most Compassionate.",
  19045: "O my dear father, indeed I fear that a punishment from the Most Compassionate may touch you, and that you may become an ally of Satan.",
  19046: "He said, “Are you turning away from my gods, O Abraham? If you do not desist, I will surely stone you; so leave me for a long while.”",
  19047: "Abraham said, “Peace be upon you. I will seek forgiveness for you from my Lord. Indeed, He has ever been gracious to me.”",
  19048: "And I will withdraw from you and from whatever you invoke besides Allāh, and I will call upon my Lord. Perhaps I will not be wretched in calling upon my Lord.”",
  19049: "So when he withdrew from them and from what they worshipped besides Allāh, We granted him Isaac and Jacob, and made each of them a prophet.",
  19050: "We bestowed upon them of Our mercy, and made for them an exalted and truthful reputation. [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. This has been fulfilled in how Jews, Christians, and Muslims all revere Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob as righteous prophets.\nA “tongue of truth” doesn’t just mean fame — it means lasting, sincere remembrance grounded in truth, not in myth or exaggeration. Their honor endures because their mission was pure — calling humanity to worship Allāh alone.\nThis shows that true honor comes from sincerity and obedience, not power or lineage. When one lives truthfully for Allāh, Allāh ensures their remembrance remains truthful and noble through time.",
  19051: "And mention in the Book the story of Moses. Indeed, he was chosen, and he was a messenger and a prophet. [15]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. A messenger (rasūl) is one sent by Allāh with a specific mission, often accompanied by revelation and a distinct law (sharīʿah) to guide and reform a people—such as Moses, Jesus, and Muhammad (peace be upon them). A prophet (nabī), on the other hand, is one who receives revelation and is tasked with guiding people in accordance with an existing divine law, without necessarily bringing a new one—such as Jacob, Isaac, and Solomon (peace be upon them). According to traditional understanding, prophets are more numerous than messengers.",
  19052: "We called him from the right side of the Mount [Sinai], and We brought him near in private conversation.",
  19053: "And We bestowed upon him, out of Our mercy, his brother Aaron as a prophet.",
  19054: "And mention in the Book the story of Ishmael. Indeed, he was true to his promise, and he was a messenger, a prophet.",
  19055: "He used to enjoin upon his family prayer and almsgiving, and he was well-pleasing to his Lord.",
  19056: "And mention in the Book the story of Idrīs [Enoch]. Indeed, he was a man of truth, a prophet.",
  19057: "And We raised him to a lofty station. [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. Allāh, exalted is He, granted Enoch—Idrīs عليه السلام—a singular distinction, raising him to a lofty abode and delivering him from the grasp of death. Allāh caused him to ascend to the heavens and placed him in an honored station. This elevation was a recompense for his truthfulness, steadfastness, and abundant devotion. Thus his ascension stands as a sign of Allah’s absolute power, a manifestation of His grace toward the righteous, and a testimony to the honour He bestows upon His chosen servants.",
  19058: "Those are the ones upon whom Allāh bestowed His favor — from among the prophets of the descendants of Ādam, and of those We carried with Noah, and of the descendants of Abraham and Israel [Jacob], and of those We guided and chose. When the verses of the Most Compassionate were recited to them, they fell down in prostration, weeping. [17]\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. The description of the prophets falling in prostration and weeping reflects the depth of their humility, reverence, and responsiveness to divine revelation. Their prostration signifies complete submission and devotion, while their weeping expresses awe, gratitude, and profound spiritual awareness. This verse presents their reaction as a model of sincere faith—contrasting sharply with those who hear revelation yet remain heedless.\nThe Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said: “Two eyes will not be touched by the Fire: an eye that has wept out of fear of Allāh, and an eye that has spent the night standing guard in the cause of Allāh.”\nWhen a servant reflects upon the majesty of Allāh and His absolute power over creation, then considers his own shortcomings and failure to fulfill Allāh’s rights as they ought to be fulfilled, his heart trembles and his eyes shed tears—hoping for His mercy and fearing His punishment. Such sincere tears are a mark of humility and true awareness. The one who weeps in this manner is promised protection from the Hellfire.",
  19059: "Then there came after them generations who neglected prayer and pursued their desires; so they will soon meet their ruin.",
  19060: "Except those who repent, believe, and do righteous deeds — they will enter Paradise and will not be wronged in the least.",
  19061: "Gardens of Eden, promised by the Most Compassionate to His servants—though unseen. Surely His promise will come to pass.",
  19062: "There they will hear no idle talk—only greetings of peace. And they will have their provision there, morning and evening. [18]\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. The phrase “They will not hear therein any vain talk” emphasizes the moral and emotional purity of Paradise. Laghw refers to idle, meaningless, hurtful, or sinful speech — words that cause harm, regret, or spiritual emptiness. In contrast, the inhabitants of Paradise will hear only peace — words of safety, harmony, and mutual goodwill. Their environment will be free from gossip, insult, argument, or anxiety.\nThe expression “morning and evening” does not necessarily imply the existence of earthly time cycles in Paradise. Rather, it conveys continuity, regularity, and ease. Just as daily provision in this world follows a familiar rhythm, the sustenance of Paradise is constant and assured — without effort, fear of shortage, or interruption.\nTogether, the verse highlights two dimensions of eternal bliss: inner tranquility and outward abundance. Paradise is not merely a place of physical enjoyment, but a realm of perfected character, pure speech, and complete peace.",
  19063: "Such is the Paradise We grant as an inheritance to those of Our servants who were truly devout.",
  19064: "And we [19] do not descend except by the command of your Lord. To Him belongs all that is before us, all that is behind us, and all that is in between. Your Lord is never forgetful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. The “we” in this verse refers to the angels, specifically understood in context to include the angel Jibrīl (Gabriel), who was entrusted with conveying revelation to the Prophet ﷺ. The verse was revealed in response to a period when revelation had paused, and the Prophet ﷺ longed for its continuation. It reassures him that the angels do not descend at their own will, but only by the command of Allāh.\nThe statement affirms complete divine sovereignty. The angels are obedient servants who act solely under instruction. Their coming and going, like all events in creation, occur according to divine wisdom and timing. What may appear as delay is not neglect.\nThe closing phrase — “your Lord is never forgetful” — comforts the believer. Allāh does not overlook, abandon, or forget His servants. Every moment unfolds with knowledge and purpose, even when the wisdom behind it is not immediately apparent.",
  19065: "Lord of the heavens and the earth and whatever is between them. So worship Him alone and be steadfast in His worship. Do you know of anyone comparable to Him?",
  19066: "Yet man says, “When I am dead, will I truly be brought forth alive?”",
  19067: "Does man not remember that We created him before, when he was nothing?",
  19068: "By your Lord, We will surely gather them and the devils. Then We will bring them around Hell, fallen upon their knees.",
  19069: "Then We will surely drag out from every sect those who were most insolent in rebellion against the Most Compassionate.",
  19070: "For surely, We know best who is most deserving of the Fire.",
  19071: "There is none of you but will pass over it [20]. This is a decree of your Lord—inevitable and binding.\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. That is be exposed to it. However, the people of Paradise will not be harmed thereby. The phrase “pass over it” (wāriduhā) refers to crossing the Bridge over Hell (Ṣirāṭ) on the Day of Judgment.\nEvery person will approach it:\nThe righteous will cross safely, their speed proportional to their faith and deeds.\nThe wicked will fall into it for punishment.\nThis universal passage demonstrates that none escapes divine justice, yet none who believe sincerely will be wronged — for Allāh’s mercy will deliver them in the end.\nProphet Muhammad ﷺ said: “Everyone will pass over Hell, then they will be saved according to their deeds — some swiftly as lightning, some like wind, some walking, and some crawling.”",
  19072: "Then We will deliver those who were devout, and leave the wrongdoers therein—fallen on their knees.",
  19073: "When Our clear verses are recited to them, those who disbelieve say to those who believe, “Which of the two parties is better in station and superior in gathering?”",
  19074: "How many a generation before them\ndid We destroy—greater in wealth and more splendid in appearance?",
  19075: "Say, “Whoever persists in misguidance—let the Most Compassionate prolong his span. Until, when they see what they were promised—whether punishment or the Hour—they will know who is worse in position and weaker in forces.”",
  19076: "Allāh increases those who are guided in guidance. And lasting righteous deeds are better with your Lord in reward and better in return. [21]\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. The “lasting righteous deeds” (al-bāqiyāt al-ṣāliḥāt) refer to all good actions that endure in reward — foremost among them are the words of remembrance (dhikr): Subḥān Allāh, Al-Ḥamdu li-llāh, Lā ilāha illā Allāh, Allāhu Akbar.\nThey also encompass every act of sincerity and obedience that draws a person nearer to Allāh.\nThese deeds never perish, while worldly wealth and power vanish; hence, they are “better in reward and better in return.”\nWhat Allāh reminds us here is that we should not get caught up in the pursuit of worldly rewards at the expense of the Hereafter. We should work for success in both, keeping in mind that the rewards of this world are transient and those of the Hereafter are eternal. Allāh emphasizes the quality of permanence, drawing our attention to how it contrasts with worldly blessings like wealth and children. Once this contrast is established strongly in our minds, we are then informed of the way we can attain these permanent blessings – by engaging in acts of righteousness. Nothing causes worry for human beings more than the loss of what we have, the loss of wealth, of health, and the inevitable loss of youth. There is no stronger context, then, for us to be reminded of the enduring value of our good deeds.",
  19077: "Have you seen the one who rejected Our signs and said, “I will surely be given wealth and children [in the next life]?\"",
  19078: "Has he looked into the unseen? Or has he taken a covenant with the Most Compassionate?",
  19079: "No indeed! We will record what he says and prolong for him the punishment extensively.",
  19080: "We will inherit from him what he claims [22], and he will come to Us alone.\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. A clear statement of divine ownership and reversal. Allāh says, in effect: The wealth and children he claims as his own — We will inherit them after him.\nJust as people inherit from the dead, Allāh is the ultimate Owner and Inheritor.\nHis claim — “I will surely be given wealth and children” — is shown to be empty: he will lose everything, and the true ownership returns to Allāh.\nOn the Day of Resurrection, every soul will stand before Allāh without wealth, family, or power. This underlines human helplessness and accountability — all worldly pride collapses at death.\nEverything we possess — wealth, status, even family — is a trust that returns to its true Owner.\nThat is, Allāh will take back all that the disbeliever prided himself upon — his wealth, family, and worldly power.\nHe will leave this world empty-handed, just as he entered it.\nThis exposes the futility of arrogance and worldly attachment, reminding that the true inheritance belongs only to Allāh, the Everlasting Inheritor, and that every soul will stand before Him alone, accountable for its deeds.",
  19081: "They have taken, besides Allāh, false gods, hoping they would be a source of strength and glory for them.",
  19082: "No indeed! They will deny their worship—and turn against them as adversaries.",
  19083: " Do you not see that We have sent the devils against the disbelievers—driving them on with relentless agitation?",
  19084: "So do not be impatient concerning them; We are only counting out for them a number [of days].",
  19085: "On the Day We will gather the righteous\nbefore the Most Compassionate—as an honored delegation.",
  19086: "And We will drive the guilty to Hell like a thirsty herd.",
  19087: "They will have no power of intercession—except those who have taken a covenant from the Most Compassionate.",
  19088: "They say, “The Most Compassionate—has taken a son.”",
  19089: "You have certainly uttered a most outrageous claim.",
  19090: "The heavens almost rupture because of it, the earth splits apart, and the mountains fall down in devastation.",
  19091: "Because they claim that the Most Compassionate has a son [23].\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. The Arabic verb daʿaw here means “to assert falsely” or “to attribute.”\nIt does not mean to call upon in worship, but rather to ascribe or claim falsely something about Allāh.\nThus, the verse literally means: “Because they falsely claim that Allāh the Most Compassionate has a son.”\nThe Qur’an deliberately uses the Most Compassionate here — the Name that emphasizes boundless mercy and absolute perfection.\nIt’s as if to say: How could the One whose mercy and power are infinite have any need of offspring or partner?\nThe title heightens the absurdity and offense of this false claim.\nAll creation recognizes Allah’s transcendence and oneness. Only the arrogant and heedless attribute human limitations to Him.\nThe verse calls believers to preserve pure monotheism in thought, word, and heart — affirming that the Creator, Allāh, is One only and is far above all comparison and need.",
  19092: "It does not befit the Most Compassionate to take a son.",
  19093: "There is none in the heavens and the earth but will come to the Most Compassionate as a servant.",
  19094: "Indeed, He has counted them all and numbered them precisely.",
  19095: "And each of them will come to Him on the Day of Resurrection alone. [24]\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. Every soul will come before Allāh alone and accountable, leaving behind all worldly possessions, titles, and relationships. This verse emphasizes that true equality and justice will be fully realized on the Day of Resurrection.\nNo intercession, lineage, or claim will avail anyone except by the permission of Allāh, the Most Compassionate.",
  19096: "Those who believe and live righteously—the Most Compassionate will bless them with love—from Him [and within the hearts of believers]. [25]\\n\\nFootnotes: [25]. The Arabic word wudd denotes deep affection, loving acceptance, and heartfelt goodwill. Scholars explain that this love includes: (1) Allah’s love for the believers; (2) love placed for them in the hearts of others—especially among the righteous; and (3) their acceptance and honor in the unseen and on earth. Thus, the verse promises not only divine approval but also enduring spiritual affection and moral esteem.",
  19097: "So We have made the Qur’an easy in your tongue, that you may give glad tidings to the righteous and warn a contentious people with it.",
  19098: "How many a generation before them have We destroyed—do you perceive any of them, or hear even a faint sound from them?",
  20001: "Ṭā Hā. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2: verse 1.",
  20002: "We have not send down the Qur’an to you that you should be distressed. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. This verse was revealed as a comfort to the Prophet ﷺ during a time when he exerted himself intensely in worship and in concern for his people. He would stand long hours in prayer and feel deep sorrow over the rejection of the message. Allāh reassures him that the Qur’an was not sent to burden or exhaust him, nor to cause emotional distress.\nThe word used in the verse conveys hardship, strain, or suffering. Revelation is not meant to overwhelm, but to guide and bring mercy. While the message carries responsibility, it is not intended to break the spirit of the one who carries it.\nThe verse also offers a broader lesson: religion in its essence is a path of balance, mercy, and guidance. Though it includes discipline and accountability, its ultimate purpose is ease of heart, clarity of direction, and nearness to Allāh — not despair or undue hardship.",
  20003: "But as a reminder for those who fear [Allāh].",
  20004: "A revelation sent down from the One who created the earth and the high heavens.",
  20005: "The Most Compassionate rose over the Throne [3].\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. In a manner that suits His Majesty. We affirm the attribute as revealed, while maintaining His transcendence beyond human comprehension without asking how. The verse beautifully combines compassion and majesty — He is Ar-Raḥmān, infinitely merciful, yet enthroned in perfect authority, overseeing the entire creation with justice and care.",
  20006: "To Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on earth—whatever is between them, and whatever is beneath the soil.",
  20007: "If you speak [the word] aloud [or not], He surely knows the secret and even what is more hidden.",
  20008: "Allāh — there is no deity except Him; to Him belong the most beautiful Names.",
  20009: "Has the story of Moses reached you?",
  20010: "When he saw a fire, he said to his family, “Stay here, for indeed I have spotted a fire; perhaps I may bring you a flaming brand from it, or find at the fire some guidance.” [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. The phrase “find at the fire some guidance” carries both immediate and thematic meaning. In its narrative context, it refers to physical direction — guidance from someone near the fire or orientation in the wilderness. Yet the wording subtly anticipates the greater guidance about to unfold: the divine revelation granted to Moses at that very place. The Qur’anic style often layers practical and spiritual meanings, allowing the outward event to foreshadow a deeper, transformative encounter.",
  20011: "Then, when he came to it, he was called, “O Moses!”",
  20012: "Indeed, I am your Lord. So remove your sandals — you are in the sacred valley of Ṭuwā.",
  20013: "I have chosen you [as My messenger], so listen to what is being revealed [to you]:",
  20014: "“Indeed, I am Allāh — there is no deity except Me — so worship Me alone, and establish prayer for My remembrance.",
  20015: "The Hour is surely coming — I almost keep it hidden — so that every soul may be recompensed for what it strives for.",
  20016: "So do not let one who does not believe in it and follows his low desires avert you from it, lest you perish.” [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. Moses عليه السلام—though raised in Pharaoh’s palace and had a promising future, fled Egypt and was living in obscurity as a shepherd when Allāh called him. In such a state, he seemed to possess nothing of worldly rank or means, yet Allāh chose him and entrusted him with His message. Thus Allāh demonstrates that He raises whom He wills, and that the worth of His servant lies not in worldly station, but in sincerity and obedience; for Allāh brings forth great matters from the simplest of means.\n Moses must have thought he had nothing to offer Allāh, but he learned that Allāh can use anyone and anything for His glory. We don’t need much to be used by Allāh. We can simply serve Him with the talents He has given us.",
  20017: "What is that in your right hand, O Moses?”",
  20018: "He said, “It is my staff. I lean on it, and with it I beat down leaves for my sheep, and I have other uses for it as well.”",
  20019: "Allāh said, “Throw it down, O Moses.”",
  20020: "So he threw it down, and suddenly it became a serpent, moving swiftly.",
  20021: "Allāh said, “Take it, and do not fear; We shall restore it to its former state.”",
  20022: "Put your hand under your arm; it will come out [shining] white, without blemish — as another sign,",
  20023: "so that We may show you some of Our greatest signs.”",
  20024: "Go to Pharaoh, indeed, he has transgressed.” [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. The Arabic verb ṭaghā (طغى) literally means “to overflow” or “to exceed proper bounds,” and is used for rebellion, arrogance, and tyrannical overreach. It conveys more than ordinary wrongdoing; it implies willful defiance of divine limits and oppressive excess in power. The term is frequently associated in the Qur’an with figures who become insolent through authority or wealth. Here it characterizes Pharaoh not merely as sinful, but as having arrogantly surpassed all moral and spiritual limits.",
  20025: "Moses said, “My Lord, open my heart for me [7],\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. i.e., grant me firmness of heart, serenity and assurance, and endow me with contentment, courage, and all that strengthens the servant in carrying out the command of his Lord.",
  20026: "and make my task easy for me,",
  20027: "and untie the knot from my tongue [8],\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. Literally, “a knot from my tongue.” The expression evokes a physical obstruction to fluent speech and is commonly understood as a request for clarity, eloquence, and freedom from hesitation in articulation. The phrase preserves vivid imagery while conveying Moses’ plea for effective communication of his message.",
  20028: "So that they may understand my speech;",
  20029: "And appoint for me a helper from my family:",
  20030: "Aaron, my brother,",
  20031: "Strengthen me through him,",
  20032: "And let him share in my mission,",
  20033: "So that we may glorify You much. [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. This phrase is part of Prophet Mūsā’s (Moses’) supplication when he asked Allāh to appoint his brother Hārūn (Aaron) as his helper. His request was not for personal strength or worldly advantage, but for spiritual purpose: “so that we may glorify You much.” It reveals that the ultimate aim of leadership and cooperation in inviting to God’s religion is increased remembrance of Allāh.\nThe word nusabbihaka (to glorify You) refers to declaring Allāh’s perfection and transcendence above all shortcomings. It includes praise, worship, obedience, and constant remembrance. By asking for support in order to glorify Allāh more frequently, Moses shows that strength and partnership are means toward devotion, not self-promotion.\nThe verse teaches that collective effort in the path of truth should lead to greater remembrance, gratitude, and humility before Allāh. True success is measured not by influence or status, but by how much it increases sincere worship and awareness of Him.",
  20034: "And remember You much. [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. This phrase completes Prophet Moses's supplication for support. After asking for assistance so that they might glorify Allāh much, he adds, “and remember You much.” Glorification (tasbīḥ) emphasizes declaring Allāh’s perfection, while remembrance (dhikr) includes continual awareness of Him — through prayer, supplication, obedience, gratitude, and reliance.\nBy linking leadership with remembrance, Moses demonstrates that the purpose of strength and partnership is not worldly dominance, but sustained devotion. The mission he was entrusted with required patience, courage, and clarity — all of which are strengthened by constant remembrance of Allāh.\nThe verse highlights a broader spiritual principle: the heart remains steady in trials through frequent remembrance. True success in any endeavor depends not merely on strategy or ability, but on maintaining conscious connection with Allāh.",
  20035: "Indeed, You are ever Watchful over us.”",
  20036: "Allāh said, “You have indeed been granted your request, O Moses.”",
  20037: "And We had already bestowed favor upon you once before,",
  20038: "When We inspired your mother with what was revealed,",
  20039: "“Place him in the casket, then cast it into the river, and let the river throw it upon the shore; it will be taken by an enemy of Mine and an enemy of his, and I cast upon you love from Me, that you might be brought up under My [watchful] Eye; [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. The phrase “under My Eye” is an expression of Allah’s perfect care, protection, and watchfulness. It affirms the divine attributes mentioned in revelation in a manner befitting Allah’s majesty, without likening them to created forms and without inquiring into their modality (bilā kayf). Thus, the attribute is affirmed as revealed while maintaining that its true nature is known only to Allāh and is unlike human attributes.\nThe expression conveys that Moses was under the constant knowledge, supervision, and safeguarding of Allāh. Even as an infant placed in apparent danger, he remained fully within divine care.\nThe statement “I cast upon you love from Me” indicates that Allāh placed acceptance and affection for Moses in the hearts of those around him — including within the household of Pharaoh, who was his declared enemy. This formed part of the divine plan: the very tyrant who sought to kill the Israelite boys would unknowingly raise the one who would later be his downfall.\nThe verse thus illustrates a profound lesson in divine wisdom: what appears as vulnerability may conceal protection, and what appears as threat may become the very means of salvation. Allah’s decree unfolds with precision, even through circumstances that outwardly seem perilous.",
  20040: "When your sister came forward and said, “Shall I guide you to someone who will take care of him?” So We returned you to your mother, so that her eyes might be comforted and she would not grieve. Then you killed a man [12], but We saved you from distress and tested you with many trials. You remained for years among the people of Midian; then you came here according to a destined decree, O Moses.\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. The reference is to the incident in which Moses unintentionally killed an Egyptian while intervening in a fight between an Israelite and an Egyptian (cf. Qur’an 28:15–16). Scholars note that the act was not deliberate murder; Moses immediately repented and sought Allāh’s forgiveness, after which Allāh delivered him from the resulting danger and subsequent hardships.",
  20041: "And I have prepared you for Myself. [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. This verse follows Allāh’s review of Mūsā’s entire life journey:\nProtection as an infant.\nReunion with his mother.\nDeliverance from Pharaoh after the accidental killing.\nYears of hardship and growth in Midian.\nFinally, divine calling at Mount Ṭūr.\nAll of these experiences, Allāh now reveals, were not random — they were part of a divine plan of preparation.\n“I have produced you for Myself”\nThe verb iṣṭanaʿtuka means “I have fashioned, prepared, or shaped you.”\nIt implies careful formation, as a craftsman shapes something with precision for a noble purpose.\n“This means: I raised you under My care and protection, purified you by trials, and chose you exclusively to call to Me.”\nThough this verse is specific to Moses, its spirit applies to anyone whom Allāh guides through hardship: Every trial is a refinement, every delay a preparation — for something greater and nearer to Him.\n You are not forgotten in your trials; you are being prepared — lovingly, purposefully — for what Allāh has created you to become.",
  20042: "Go, you and your brother, with My signs, and do not slacken in remembering Me.",
  20043: "Go, both of you, to Pharaoh — indeed, he has exceeded all limits.",
  20044: "Then speak to him with gentle speech, that perhaps he may be reminded or fear.",
  20045: "They said, “Our Lord, we truly fear that he may hasten to punish us or that he may transgress all bounds.”",
  20046: "He said, “Fear not; indeed, I am with you both — I hear and I see.”",
  20047: "Then go to him, both of you, and say, “Indeed, we are the two messengers of your Lord. So send the Children of Israel with us and do not punish them. We have come to you with a sign from your Lord, and peace be upon whoever follows the guidance.”",
  20048: "It has already been revealed to us that punishment is upon whoever denies and turns away.",
  20049: "Pharaoh said, “So who is the Lord of you both, O Moses?”",
  20050: "Moses said, “Our Lord is the One who gave everything its form, then guided it.”",
  20051: "He said, “Then what is the case of the former generations?”",
  20052: "Moses said, “Its knowledge is with my Lord, in a record. My Lord neither errs nor forgets.”",
  20053: "Who made the earth a cradle for you, and set therein pathways for you, and sent down water from the sky — thereby bringing forth pairs of varied plants.",
  20054: "Eat and graze your livestock. Indeed, in this are signs for people of sound intellect.",
  20055: "From the earth We created you, and into it We shall return you, and from it We shall bring you forth once again. [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. This verse recalls humanity’s origin from the earth and its eventual return to it — “for dust you are, and to dust you will return.” The Qur’an frequently reminds that Allāh created human beings from earthly matter and will restore them from it at the resurrection. The passage underscores human mortality and dependence upon Allāh while also affirming the Qur’anic doctrine of bodily resurrection after death.",
  20056: "We certainly showed Pharaoh all of Our signs, but he denied them and refused.",
  20057: "He said, “Have you come to drive us out of our land by your magic, O Moses?”",
  20058: "We will surely bring you similar magic. So set an appointment between us and you that neither we nor you will fail, in a neutral place.",
  20059: "Moses said, “Your appointment is the Day of the Festival, and let the people be gathered in the forenoon.”",
  20060: "So Pharaoh turned away, marshaled his plot, and then came.",
  20061: "Moses said to them, “Woe to you! Do not fabricate a lie against Allāh, lest He annihilate you with punishment. And whoever invents lies has surely failed.”",
  20062: "Then they disputed their matter among themselves and kept their counsel secret.",
  20063: "They said, “These two are indeed magicians who want to drive you out of your land by their magic and do away with your exemplary way of life.”",
  20064: "So marshal your plot, then come forth in ranks. And whoever prevails today has surely succeeded.",
  20065: "They said, “O Moses, either you throw, or we will be the first to throw.”",
  20066: "He said, “Rather, you throw.” Then suddenly their ropes and staffs appeared to him by their magic as though they were moving [like snakes].",
  20067: "Then Moses felt a fear within himself.",
  20068: "We said, “Do not fear. Indeed, you are the uppermost.”",
  20069: "Throw what is in your right hand; it will swallow up what they have made. What they have made is only the plot of a magician, and the magician will never prosper—wherever he may go.",
  20070: "Then the magicians fell in prostration and said, “We believe in the Lord of Aaron and Moses.”",
  20071: "Pharaoh said, “Have you believed in him before I gave you permission? Surely he is your leader who taught you magic. I will surely cut off your hands and feet on opposite sides, and I will surely crucify you on the trunks of palm trees. Then you will surely know which of us is harsher in punishment and more enduring.” [15]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. That is Pharaoh was comparing himself with Allāh—the Lord of Moses—when he said, “which of us is more severe in punishment and more lasting.”\nThis reveals Pharaoh’s extreme arrogance and delusion, placing himself in rivalry with the Creator.\nFrom this account we learn enduring lessons: tyrants seek control even over belief and use false accusations to protect their power; guidance can transform hearts instantly, as seen when the magicians embraced truth; true faith produces courage in the face of threats; and arrogance blinds people to clear evidence. Worldly power is temporary, while divine justice is certain. When falsehood cannot answer truth with proof, it resorts to intimidation. Yet unity upon truth is powerful—those who stand together in faith can shake even the mightiest oppressor.",
  20072: "They said, “We will never prefer you to the clear signs that have come to us, nor to the One who created us. So decide whatever you will. You can only judge in this present worldly life.”",
  20073: "We have believed in our Lord so that He may forgive us our sins and the magic you compelled us to. And Allāh is better and more enduring.",
  20074: "Indeed, whoever comes to his Lord as a guilty one, for him is Hell: he will neither die therein nor live.",
  20075: "But whoever comes to Him as a believer, having done righteous deeds—for them are the highest ranks.",
  20076: "Gardens of Eden beneath which rivers flow, where they will dwell forever. Such is the reward for those who purify themselves.",
  20077: "Indeed, We revealed to Moses, “Set out with My servants by night, and strike for them a dry path through the sea. Do not fear being overtaken, nor be apprehensive.”",
  20078: "Then Pharaoh pursued them with his forces, and there covered them from the sea what covered them. [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. That is not merely drowning in water, but also other forms of punishment and suffering known only to Allāh —such as anguish, regret, and pains of the heart—which He decreed for them.”\nThe expression “there covered them from the sea what covered them” is a rhetorical form of emphasis in Arabic. Instead of describing the scene in detail, the verse leaves the extent of the destruction unstated — intensifying its impact. The repetition conveys overwhelming and complete engulfment, beyond the need for elaboration.\nAfter the sea had been parted for Moses and the Children of Israel, Pharaoh arrogantly pursued them with his army. When they entered the parted sea, the waters returned upon them, drowning Pharaoh and his forces. The same sea that served as a path of salvation for the believers became a means of destruction for the oppressors.\nThe verse illustrates a recurring Qur’anic theme: divine justice unfolds decisively. Arrogance and tyranny may seem powerful for a time, but they collapse when confronted by the decree of Allāh. What appears as strength can be turned into sudden ruin, while those who trust in Him are delivered according to His wisdom.",
  20079: "Thus Pharaoh led his people astray and did not guide them.",
  20080: "O Children of Israel, We rescued you from your enemy, and made an appointment with you [17] on the right side of the Mount, and sent down manna and quail for you.\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. The “appointment” refers to the covenant made at Mount Ṭūr (Sinai), where Prophet Mūsā (Moses, peace be upon him) was summoned to receive revelation. It was a sacred meeting in which divine law and guidance were given to the Children of Israel after their deliverance from Pharaoh. The mention of the “right side” of the mountain indicates a specific, honored location associated with that encounter.\nThe verse reminds the Children of Israel of successive favors: rescue from oppression, the gift of revelation, and sustained provision in the wilderness. Manna and quails were special provisions sent to them during their wandering — food granted without cultivation or toil, demonstrating divine care even in hardship.\nBy recalling these blessings, the Qur’an emphasizes gratitude and covenantal responsibility. Deliverance and provision were not ends in themselves, but signs calling for obedience, trust, and faithful adherence to divine guidance.",
  20081: "Eat from the good things We have provided for you, but do not exceed the limits therein [18], or My anger will fall upon you. And anyone upon whom My anger falls is truly ruined.\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. The command “do not exceed the limits therein” warns against transgression in response to blessing. The Children of Israel were provided with pure and lawful sustenance, yet they were cautioned not to misuse it through ingratitude, arrogance, greed, or disobedience. Transgression may take the form of excess, waste, unlawful consumption, or rebellion against the One who provides.\nThe Qur’an consistently links provision with accountability. Blessings are not merely privileges; they are tests of character. Gratitude preserves favor, while excess and ingratitude invite divine displeasure. The phrase “My anger will fall upon you” signifies withdrawal of divine favor and the consequences that follow rebellion.\nThe closing warning — “whoever My anger falls upon is truly ruined” — emphasizes the seriousness of spiritual loss. Material abundance cannot protect one from ruin if it is accompanied by disobedience. True safety lies not in provision itself, but in remaining within the limits set by Allāh.",
  20082: "Indeed, I am surely forgiving to whoever repents, believes, does righteous deeds, then continues to follow guidance.",
  20083: "[Allāh said], \"What made you hasten from your people, O Moses?\"",
  20084: "He said, “They are following in my footsteps, and I hastened to You, my Lord, so that You may be pleased.”",
  20085: "He said, “But indeed, We have tested your people after your departure, and the Sāmirī has led them astray.”",
  20086: "So Moses returned to his people, angry and grieved. He said, \"O my people, did your Lord not promise you a good promise? Did the appointment seem too long for you? Or were you seeking your Lord’s anger to fall upon you—so you broke your promise to me?\"",
  20087: "They said, “We did not break our promise to you on our own. Rather, we were burdened with loads of the people’s jewelry, so we threw them [into the fire]—and the Samaritan did the same.”",
  20088: "Then he produced for them a calf—a mere body, having a lowing sound—and they said, “This is your god and the god of Moses, but he forgot.”",
  20089: "Did they not see that it could not respond to them, nor possess any power to harm or benefit them?",
  20090: "Yet Aaron had already said to them beforehand, “O my people, you are only being tested by this [idol]. Your Lord is indeed the Most Compassionate. So follow me and obey my command.”",
  20091: "They said, “We will not stop being devoted to it until Moses returns to us.”",
  20092: "He said, “O Aaron, what prevented you—when you saw them going astray—”",
  20093: "from following me? Did you then disobey my command?”",
  20094: "Aaron said, “O son of my mother, do not seize me by my beard or by my head [19]. I feared that you would say, \"You have caused division among the Children of Israel and did not observe my word.\"\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. The Old Testament law restricted certain forms of shaving among Israelites. For example, Leviticus 21:5 states: “They shall not make bald spots on their heads, nor shave off the edges of their beards, nor make any cuts on their body.” This command was part of the laws given to the Israelites, prohibiting mourning and ritual practices associated with surrounding pagan cultures.\nIn ancient Israelite society, beards were a normal and honorable feature of adult men. Their removal could signify shame or humiliation. This is illustrated in 2 Samuel 10:4–5 (cf. 1 Chronicles 19:4–5), where the servants of King David had half of their beards shaved by the Ammonite king Hanun, an act regarded as deeply humiliating. David subsequently instructed them to remain in Jericho until their beards had grown back, underscoring the cultural importance of the beard as a symbol of dignity.\nIn Islamic teachings, growing the beard is explicitly commanded in several authentic hadiths of Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. Among them are the statements: “Trim the mustache and let the beard grow,” and “Be different from the pagans: let your beards grow and trim your mustaches.” These narrations are commonly understood as part of maintaining a distinct Muslim identity. Additionally, in a well-known hadith on fitrah (natural disposition), the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ included “trimming the mustache and letting the beard grow” among the practices that align with human natural inclination.",
  20095: "Moses said, \"What is your matter, O Sāmirī?”",
  20096: "He said, “I saw what they did not see, so I took a handful from the messenger’s traces and cast it. Thus my soul enticed me.” [20]\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. This verse records the confession of al-Sāmirī, the individual who led the Children of Israel into the worship of the golden calf during the absence of Moses. His statement, “I saw what they did not see,” has been interpreted by many early commentators to indicate that he perceived something hidden from others—commonly understood as seeing the angel Gabriel. According to this explanation, he took a handful of earth from the place where the angel’s mount had stepped and cast it into the molten calf, which—by divine test—produced a sound that misled the people.\nThe phrase “my soul enticed me” points to the internal origin of his wrongdoing. Rather than fully accepting responsibility, he attributes his actions to self-deception and desire. This Qur’anic expression conveys how the human soul can embellish falsehood, making misguidance appear justified or even appealing.\nThe verse thus conveys a broader moral lesson: deviation often begins with arrogance—believing oneself to possess unique or privileged insight—followed by rationalization. When the soul is not governed by humility and obedience, it can lead a person to justify even serious error.\nWithin the Qur’anic narrative, Aaron is portrayed as a faithful prophet who opposed the worship of the golden calf and called his people back to monotheism. By contrast, in the Biblical account (Exodus 32), Aaron is described as participating in the making of the calf. This distinction reflects a fundamental principle in Islam: prophets are safeguarded from major sins such as idolatry and moral corruption, thereby preserving their spiritual and moral integrity. Such Biblical portrayals of prophets committing grave transgressions are understood as later distortions of earlier revelation rather than authentic teachings from God.",
  20097: "Moses said, “Begone, then. In this life your lot will be to say, \"No touching [21].\" And indeed, you have an appointed time you will not fail to meet. Now look at your god to which you remained devoted: we will surely burn it and scatter it into the sea completely.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. The expression lā misās (lit. “no touching”) is understood by early commentators as a form of social banishment or affliction imposed upon al-Sāmirī. It has been interpreted either as a physical condition—such that he could neither touch nor be touched without harm—or, more broadly, as a lasting state of isolation in which he was compelled to warn others to avoid contact with him. The phrase thus signifies both a worldly punishment and a mark of disgrace.\nHuman beings are created for connection, not isolation. We need more than occasional contact — we need meaningful, heart-to-heart relationships. True connection is built through presence: spending time with family, gathering with brothers and sisters in faith, studying the Book of Allāh and the authentic Sunnah together, and sharing life’s experiences openly and sincerely.\nSimple acts carry great weight — sitting together, sharing meals, speaking face to face, shaking hands, embracing, looking one another in the eye, and giving one another unhurried attention. These moments nurture affection, strengthen bonds, and cultivate mercy within families and communities.\nConnection is not merely exchanging messages or maintaining distant communication. It is intentional closeness — caring, listening, supporting, and being truly present. In a world that often replaces presence with convenience, we are reminded to preserve genuine companionship and family ties.\nLet us therefore strive not only to remain “in contact,” but to remain connected — through care, shared time, and sincere love for the sake of Allāh.",
  20098: "Your God is only Allāh—there is no deity except Him. He encompasses all things in knowledge.",
  20099: "Thus We relate to you some of the accounts of what has gone before; and indeed We have given you from Us a Reminder [this Qur’an].",
  20100: "Whoever turns away from it will bear a burden on the Day of Resurrection.",
  20101: "They will remain therein forever. How evil for them is that burden on the Day of Resurrection.",
  20102: "On the Day the Trumpet is blown, We will gather the wicked, that Day, blue-eyed. [22]\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. The word (zurqā) translated as “blue-eyed” carries connotations of extreme fear, shock, and distress. Scholars explained that the eyes may appear bluish due to terror, dryness, or loss of vitality — a sign of horror and humiliation on the Day of Resurrection. In Arabic usage, the term can also imply blindness or a lifeless gaze.\nThe description is not meant to praise physical features, but to portray the visible effects of dread and accountability. The wicked, who once turned away confidently from truth, will stand transformed by fear and helplessness.\nThe verse uses vivid imagery to convey that the Resurrection is not merely a spiritual reckoning, but a reality that affects the whole being. What was once denied or mocked will become undeniable, and its impact will be seen in their very faces.",
  20103: "They will whisper among themselves, “You remained no more than ten [days on earth].\"",
  20104: "We know best what they say, when the most perceptive among them will say, \"You remained no more than a day [on earth].\"",
  20105: "They ask you about the mountains. Say, “My Lord will scatter them completely.”",
  20106: "And He will leave it a smooth, level plain.",
  20107: "You will see therein neither crookedness nor unevenness.",
  20108: "On that Day they will follow the Caller—there will be no deviation from him. All voices will be humbled before the Most Compassionate, and you will hear nothing but a whisper. [23]\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. The “Caller” is understood by many commentators to refer to the angel who will summon humanity to the place of gathering on the Day of Resurrection. At that moment, there will be no hesitation, resistance, or deviation. Every soul will respond, compelled by the reality of divine command.\nThe phrase “none will deviate from him” conveys complete order and submission. In worldly life, people may ignore calls to truth or turn away in arrogance. On that Day, however, no one will have the ability to refuse or delay.\nThe image of voices being humbled before the Most Compassionate portrays overwhelming awe and silence. The loud debates, prideful speech, and denial of the past will vanish. Only the faintest whisper — described as a soft murmur or the sound of light footsteps — will be heard. The scene reflects total humility before divine majesty and the certainty of accountability.",
  20109: "On that Day no intercession will benefit—except from one to whom the Most Compassionate has granted permission and whose word He approves.",
  20110: "He knows whatever is before them and whatever is behind them, but they do not encompass Him in knowledge.",
  20111: "All faces will be humbled before the Ever-Living, the All-Sustaining, and failed indeed is the one who bears iniquity. [24]\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. The word ẓulman here broadly denotes wrongdoing, injustice, or moral transgression—encompassing sins, evil deeds, and acts of oppression toward others. In Qur’anic usage, the term reaches its gravest sense in reference to polytheism (shirk), described elsewhere as “a tremendous injustice” (cf. 31:13), because it violates the exclusive right of Allāh to be worshipped. At the same time, the word also includes injustices committed against people, such as oppression, violation of rights, and moral corruption. The expression thus carries a comprehensive moral warning: ultimate loss awaits those who persist in either form of injustice—against Allāh or against His creation.",
  20112: "But whoever does righteous deeds while being a believer will have no fear of injustice nor deprivation.",
  20113: "Thus We have sent it down as an Arabic Qur’an, and We have varied the warnings within it so that they may become mindful or it may awaken in them remembrance.",
  20114: "So exalted is Allāh, the King, the Truth. And do not hasten with the Qur’an before its revelation to you is completed [25], and say, “My Lord, increase me in knowledge.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [25]. This directive addresses the Prophet Muhammad’s eagerness to repeat the Qur’anic revelation as it was being delivered to him. Out of his deep concern to preserve the words precisely, he would hasten to recite along with the angel. He was therefore instructed not to rush the recitation until the revelation had been fully conveyed to him. Instead, he was to listen attentively and allow the revelation to be completed, with the assurance that Allāh would preserve it in his memory. The verse thus reflects both the Prophet’s earnest devotion to the Qur’an and the divine guidance governing the manner of its reception.",
  20115: "We had already charged Ādam [not to eat from the tree], yet he forgot, and We found in him no firm resolve.",
  20116: "When We said to the angels, “Prostrate yourselves before Ādam,” they all prostrated—except Iblīs, who refused.",
  20117: "We said, “O Ādam, this is an enemy to you and to your wife [26]; so do not let him drive you both out of the Garden, or you will be wretched.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [26].  Ḥawwāh, the wife of Ādam.\nThe names Ādam and Ḥawwāh (Eve) are commonly understood today as personal names, but in their original languages, they also carry descriptive meanings.\nIn Arabic, the word Ādam is associated with meanings such as “human,” “mankind,” or “from the earth (dust).” It reflects the origin and nature of humanity.\nSimilarly, the name Ḥawwāh is linked to the meaning “life” or “living one.” As stated in the Book of Genesis 3:20:\n“The man called his wife Ḥawwāh (Eve), because she was the mother of all living.”\nWhen early translators rendered the Hebrew scriptures into Greek (the Septuagint, around the 3rd century BCE), they often transliterated these words—preserving their sound rather than fully translating their meaning. Thus:\nḤawwāh → Greek → Latin → Eve in English\nĀdam → Adam\nOver time, these forms became widely used as proper names.\nWhile Ādam can refer to humanity in general in some contexts (e.g., Genesis 1:26: “Let us make mankind…”), it is also used as a specific individual in later passages.\nLikewise, Ḥawwāh functions both as:\nA personal name, and\nA word reflecting her role as the source of life (mother of all living)\nOn the Word “Eden”\nThe word Eden is not merely a name; it is associated with meanings such as “delight,” “bliss,” or a place of beauty and abundance.”\nIn the Qur'an and Sunnah: Ādam is the first human and prophet, created from the earth\nḤawwāh is his spouse, created as his companion\nTheir names reflect both personal identity and meaningful description, highlighting their roles and origins.",
  20118: "Indeed, you are assured that you will neither hunger therein nor be unclothed.",
  20119: "nor will you suffer from thirst or from scorching heat.”",
  20120: "But Satan whispered to him, saying, “O Ādam, shall I show you the tree of immortality and a kingdom that will never decay?”",
  20121: "So they both ate from the tree, then their nakedness became apparent to them, and they began to cover themselves with leaves from the Garden. Thus Ādam disobeyed his Lord and erred. [27]\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]. The Qur’anic account presents Adam and his wife as jointly responsible for eating from the tree, without singling out the woman for blame. In contrast, the Biblical narrative in Genesis 3 places primary emphasis on Eve’s role in the transgression and her subsequent influence on Adam. The Qur’an consistently avoids attributing the lapse to Eve alone, instead portraying the event as a shared human error followed by repentance and divine mercy. This distinction reflects a broader Qur’anic emphasis on individual moral responsibility and the absence of inherited guilt.",
  20122: "Then his Lord chose him, accepted his repentance and guided him aright.",
  20123: "He said, “Descend from here, both of you, all together, as enemies to one another. Then when guidance comes to you from Me, whoever follows My guidance will neither go astray nor suffer.”",
  20124: "But whoever turns away from My Reminder will have a constricted life [28], and We will raise him blind on the Day of Resurrection.\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. The phrase maʿīshatan ḍankan literally denotes a life of constriction, hardship, and distress. It signifies a condition of tightness and difficulty imposed as a consequence of turning away from Allāh’s Book. Many early exegetes interpreted this “constricted life” specifically as the punishment of the grave, in which the grave is narrowed upon the person and he is subjected to torment in recompense for his neglect of divine guidance. They cited this verse among the evidences for the reality of the punishment of the grave (cf. 6:93; 32:21; 40:46). Other commentators understood the expression more broadly, holding that the constricted life includes the distress, anxiety, and hardship experienced in this world, as well as the suffering of the intermediate state and the Hereafter. The wording of the verse is general and does not restrict the constriction to a single stage, thus encompassing all forms of divinely decreed hardship that befall one who turns away from Allāh’s remembrance.",
  20125: "He will say, “My Lord, why have You raised me blind while I used to see?”",
  20126: "He will say, “Thus Our revelations came to you, but you forgot them; and thus today you will be forgotten.” [29]\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. The statement “today you will be forgotten” does not imply that Allāh forgets in the human sense. Allāh is never forgetful. Rather, it signifies being abandoned to the consequences of one’s own choices — deprived of mercy, care, and relief on the Day of Judgment.\nIn worldly life, the individual “forgot” the verses of Allāh — meaning he neglected, ignored, or deliberately turned away from revelation and guidance. Forgetfulness here refers to willful disregard, not mere lapse of memory. He lived as though divine reminders carried no weight.\nThe response on the Day of Resurrection reflects perfect justice: as he disregarded the verses when they came to him, he will now be disregarded in his distress. The verse highlights a profound principle — accountability mirrors action. Mercy is granted to those who respond to guidance, while deliberate neglect leads to spiritual abandonment.",
  20127: "Thus We recompense whoever is excessive and does not believe in the verses of his Lord. And the punishment of the Hereafter is certainly harsher and more lasting.",
  20128: "Does it not guide them how many generations before them We destroyed, through whose dwelling places they now walk? Indeed, in this are signs for those of sound intellect.",
  20129: "Had it not been for a prior decree from your Lord [30] and an appointed term, their punishment would certainly have been inevitable. [31]\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. The “prior decree” refers to Allāh’s previously determined judgment to defer immediate punishment and grant respite to the deniers. In Qur’anic usage, such a decree reflects divine wisdom in allowing time for reflection, repentance, and the establishment of clear proof before final accountability. The delay does not imply neglect but measured divine forbearance.\n[31]. That is, their punishment would have been inevitable and immediate. The verse indicates that were it not for the prior divine decree and the fixed appointed term, the consequences of their denial would have overtaken them without delay. The expression underscores both the certainty of divine judgment and the mercy inherent in its postponement.",
  20130: "So be patient with what they say, and glorify your Lord with His praise before the sun’s rising and before its setting; and glorify Him in the hours of the night, and at the ends of the day [32], so that you may be pleased.\\n\\nFootnotes: [32]. The command to glorify Allāh “before the rising of the sun and before its setting … in the hours of the night, and at the ends of the day” has been understood by many scholars to allude to the prescribed daily prayers. The early morning (Fajr), late afternoon (‘Aṣr), evening (Maghrib), night (‘Ishā’), and the prayer around midday (Ẓuhr) are encompassed within these expressions. Thus, remembrance is woven throughout the entire day.\nThe phrase “glorify your Lord with His praise” combines two acts of devotion: declaring Allāh’s perfection (tasbīḥ) and expressing gratitude and praise (ḥamd). Together, they cultivate humility, reliance, and gratitude.\nThe verse also connects worship with emotional resilience. The Prophet ﷺ was instructed to remain patient in the face of mockery and rejection, and to find strength in consistent remembrance. The closing words — “so that you may be pleased” — indicate that contentment and inner peace arise from steady devotion. Remembrance is not merely ritual; it is nourishment for the heart in times of trial.\nThe Prophet  ﷺ said: \"The five (daily) prayers and from one Friday prayer to the (next) Friday prayer, and from Ramadhan to Ramadhan are expiations for the (minor sins) committed in between (their intervals) provided one shuns the major sins.\"",
  20131: "Do not let your eyes long for what We have given some of them to enjoy—the splendor of this worldly life by which We test them. And the provision of your Lord is far better and more lasting.",
  20132: "Enjoin your household to establish prayer and be steadfast in it. We do not ask you for provision—We provide for you. And the best outcome is for righteousness. [33]\\n\\nFootnotes: [33]. The gift of righteousness nurtures distinct spiritual qualities in the heart. First, it instills reverent awareness of Allāh — recognizing Him as a Loving, Kind, and Merciful Lord. This awareness is not based on fear alone, but on gratitude and trust in His wisdom and care.\nSecond, it cultivates deep love for Allāh. Such love inspires a person to seek His pleasure willingly, even when it requires sacrifice. Acts of devotion are no longer driven merely by obligation, but by longing to draw nearer to Him.\nThird, righteousness produces loving obedience to the guidance found in the Qur’an and the authentic Sunnah. A believer sees divine commands not as restrictions, but as expressions of mercy — guiding humanity toward what is pure, balanced, and ultimately fulfilling.\nWhen these dispositions take root, prayer is no longer a burden but a meeting with the One who is loved. Obedience — even when challenging — is embraced with trust, because it reflects divine truth and leads to lasting happiness. In this way, righteousness transforms worship from routine into relationship, and discipline into devotion.",
  20133: "They say, “Why does he not bring us a sign from his Lord?” Has there not come to them a clear proof confirming what was in the previous Scrolls? [34]\\n\\nFootnotes: [34]. The demand for a “sign” reflects a recurring pattern among those who resist guidance. Instead of responding sincerely to the message already presented, they ask for additional miracles as a condition for belief. Yet the Qur’an itself — in its unmatched language, depth of meaning, and transformative power — stands as a clear and sufficient proof.\nThe phrase “a clear proof confirming what was in the previous Scrolls” highlights the continuity of divine revelation. The Qur’an affirms the essential truths found in earlier scriptures: belief in One God, moral accountability, prophethood, and the call to righteousness. Its message is not isolated or novel, but part of a consistent and enduring divine tradition.\nMoreover, the Qur’an serves as a lasting miracle — unlike earlier signs that were confined to a particular time and people. Its preservation, eloquence, and profound guidance remain accessible across generations. It also confirms what was foretold in the earlier Books, including the coming of Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ, thereby linking his prophethood to the broader history of revelation.\nThe verse therefore suggests that the issue is not a lack of evidence, but reluctance to accept it. When hearts are closed, even extraordinary signs fail to produce faith. True belief begins not with demanding more proof, but with openness to the guidance already given.",
  20134: "Had We destroyed them with a punishment before this, they would certainly have said, “Our Lord, if only You had sent us a messenger so we could have followed Your revelations before being humiliated and disgraced.”",
  20135: "Say, “Each is waiting, so wait. You will soon come to know who are the people of the straight path and who are rightly guided.”",
  21001: "The time of the people’s reckoning has drawn near, yet they remain heedless, turning away. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. Among the most widespread and destructive illnesses of the human condition is heedlessness — a spiritual blindness that causes a person to forget Allāh, the One who created him, fashioned him, and sustains him at every moment. It is a neglect that leads to forgetting the true purpose of existence: to worship Allāh alone, to live in obedience to Him, and to prepare for the eternal life that follows death.\nAs time advances and the world dazzles with its outward adornments, many drift further from the path of truth. They become absorbed in the fleeting attractions of worldly life — its comforts, ambitions, status, and pleasures — until their hearts grow attached to what fades and distant from the remembrance of their Lord. The modern age, with its relentless pace and endless distractions, has only intensified this condition. Men and women are swept along by constant stimulation and preoccupation, until the remembrance of Allāh becomes rare upon their tongues and faint within their hearts.\nAllāh describes such people as possessing sharp awareness of worldly affairs — skilled in trade, technology, strategy, and calculation — yet heedless of the Hereafter, the life that truly endures. They know how to navigate the complexities of this world, but remain unaware of the path that leads to lasting salvation. Their knowledge serves their temporary existence, but neglects their eternal destiny.\nHeedlessness, therefore, is not ignorance of information; it is forgetfulness of purpose. And the cure lies in remembrance — conscious awareness of Allāh, reflection upon His signs, and living with the end in mind.",
  21002: "No reminder comes to them from their Lord, newly revealed, but they listen to it while they play.",
  21003: "Their hearts are heedless [2]. And the wrongdoers confer secretly, saying, “Is this man anything but a human like yourselves? Will you follow the magic while you see?”\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. There will be terrible times in the last days. Most people will be lovers of themselves, lovers of wealth, boastful and proud, disobedient and abusive, ungodly and ungrateful…\nIngratitude to Allāh means turning away from His guidance, neglecting His commands, and rejecting His messengers—foremost among them the Seal of the Prophets, Muḥammad ﷺ. When gratitude departs from the heart, humility fades, obedience weakens, and sins become beautified to the soul.\nFor this reason, the Qur’ān repeatedly summons mankind to self-examination, to look within their own hearts and measure the reality of their faith. For the diseases of the heart—heedlessness, arrogance, self-admiration, ingratitude—are more destructive than any outward calamity. They slowly consume the soul until it becomes hardened, unresponsive to reminders, and indifferent to divine warnings.\nThus, the believer must strive to renew his remembrance of Allāh, guard his heart from heedlessness, and cleanse his soul from the stains of worldly excess. For only through the constant remembrance of Allāh does the heart find life, clarity, and true success.",
  21004: "The Prophet said, “My Lord knows every word spoken in the heaven and the earth, and He is the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.”",
  21005: "Yet they say, “Confused dreams! Rather, he has fabricated it. Rather, he is just a poet. So let him bring us a sign like those sent before.”",
  21006: "Not a single town We destroyed before them ever believed [3]. So will they believe?\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. That is, none of the nations whom We destroyed before them ever embraced true faith, despite the clear Signs shown to them. Their eyes beheld the proofs, their ears heard the warnings, yet their hearts remained sealed, their souls unmoved. Thus, when they persisted in denial after the truth had been made manifest, the decree of destruction overtook them. So how then shall these people believe—while their hearts are harder, their arrogance greater, and their turning away more obstinate than those who perished before them?",
  21007: "We did not send before you except men to whom We revealed; so ask the people of the Scripture if you do not know.",
  21008: "We did not make them mere bodies that did not eat food, nor were they immortal.",
  21009: "Then We fulfilled Our promise to them: We saved them and whoever We willed, and destroyed the transgressors.",
  21010: "We have certainly sent down to you a Book in which is your reminder [4]. Will you not then reason?\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. It is a nobility and a badge of honour for those who believe in it with certainty, submit to its guidance, and adhere to its noble injunctions.\nFor indeed, the Qur’ān is not merely a scripture, but a source of elevation—\nit raises the believer in rank, increases him in light, purifies his heart, and ennobles his character.\nThose who approach it with faith and obedience find that their dignity is strengthened, their understanding deepened, and their path illuminated.\nSuch are the ones whom Allāh honours by attaching them to His Book, for no honour surpasses being among the people of the Qur’ān—the people of Allāh and His chosen ones.",
  21011: "How many a wrongdoing town We destroyed, and raised up after it another people.",
  21012: "When they felt Our punishment, at once they ran away from it.",
  21013: "\"Do not run away. Return to the luxuries you once enjoyed and to your homes [5], so that you may be questioned.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. This statement is addressed to them in the manner of rebuke and derision, for they had persisted in denial despite the clearest proofs. Thus it is not an honorific address, but a form of sarcasm and mockery—that they may realize, when the truth is unveiled, the wretchedness of their obstinacy and the futility of their false hopes.",
  21014: "They cried, \"Woe to us! We were truly wrongdoers.\"",
  21015: "And their cry did not cease until We made them like a harvested field, stilled and extinguished.",
  21016: "We did not create the heavens and the earth and all that is between them in idle play. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. That is without purpose. That is, you were not created in vain, nor brought into existence without wisdom or an appointed end. Rather, Allāh—exalted is He—created you with a noble purpose, that you may worship Him, obey His commands, and prepare for the Day of Return. Thus, whoever imagines that life is mere play and diversion has been deceived by the glitter of this worldly realm, for Allāh does nothing without perfect wisdom, nor does He create idly or in jest. Every breath you take, every moment granted to you, is measured and recorded, intended to lead you either to felicity through faith and righteousness or to loss through heedlessness and denial.",
  21017: "If We had wished to take a pastime [7], We would surely have taken it from what is with Us—were We ever to do so. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. That is a “pastime” refers—according to the early exegetes—to a spouse or offspring. This wording is employed as a rhetorical rebuke, negating in the most decisive manner the false claims of those who ascribed to Allāh a wife, a child, or any form of partner.\n[8]. That is, were it conceivable—far exalted is He above such a notion—that Allāh would take a pastime, it would be from His own Presence, not from His creation. But such a matter is utterly impossible for Allāh, Glorified and Most High, for He is absolutely transcendent, sanctified from every imperfection, partner, likeness, need, or dependency. Thus the verse is a declaration of His pure monotheism and absolute freedom from the fabrications of the polytheists.",
  21018: "Rather, We hurl the truth against falsehood, and it smashes it—at once it vanishes. And woe to you for what you attribute [to God, of a partner or son].",
  21019: " To Him belongs whoever is in the heavens and the earth. And those [angels] who are with Him are not too proud to worship Him, nor do they tire.",
  21020: "They glorify Him night and day, never ceasing.",
  21021: "Or have they taken gods from the earth who can resurrect the dead? [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. The question is rhetorical: these supposed gods are utterly powerless and incapable of resurrecting or giving life to the dead—so how could they be taken as deities despite such obvious impotence? How foolish are the minds that accept for themselves gods made from the very earth, while abandoning Allāh, the Magnificent in His essence and attributes. Allāh alone possesses full authority over His servants—their life and their death—so is it fitting for them to turn to any besides Him?",
  21022: "Had there been gods besides Allāh in the heavens and the earth, both would have been ruined. Exalted is Allāh, Lord of the Throne, far above what they ascribe to Him.",
  21023: "He is not questioned about what He does, but they will all be questioned.",
  21024: "Or have they taken other gods besides Him? Say, “Bring your proof. This is a reminder for those with me and a reminder for those before me [10].” Yet most of them do not know the truth, so they turn away.\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. The Prophet ﷺ is commanded to challenge the pagans to produce any proof for the existence or authority of their alleged gods. No such proof exists. The Qur’an revealed to him—and the earlier scriptures such as the Torah and the Gospel—are all in agreement that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allāh alone.\nThus Allāh presents another argument for His oneness: those who associate partners with Him claim that their idols can benefit or harm. They are told to produce evidence for this claim. Yet neither the Book revealed to the Prophet nor the scriptures of earlier nations contain any support for their assertion. Rather, all divine revelations consistently call to the exclusive worship of Allāh. Most of these polytheists, however, merely imitate their forefathers, arguing without knowledge and remaining in misguidance because of their turning away from the truth.",
  21025: "We did not send before you any messenger without revealing to him: “There is no deity worthy of worship except Me, so worship Me alone.” [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. This verse establishes a central truth shared by all prophets throughout history: every messenger—from Adam to Muhammad—was sent with the same core message of pure monotheism (tawḥīd). Their teachings may have differed in laws and details suited to their communities, but the foundation was always the same: that none is worthy of worship, devotion, or ultimate obedience except Allāh alone. The verse also refutes the idea that earlier prophets taught polytheism or endorsed the worship of intermediaries; rather, all of them called their people back to the worship of the One God. By addressing Muhammad directly, the verse reinforces the continuity and unity of divine revelation across ages and nations.",
  21026: "Yet they say, “The Most Compassionate has taken a son.” Glory be to Him! No—they are only honored servants. [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. That is, those whom the misguided claim to be “sons” or “daughters” of God — such as ʿĪsā (Jesus, peace be upon him), or the angels who were falsely regarded as divine offspring. Exalted is Allāh far above what they ascribe to Him. Such claims arise only from ignorance, conjecture, and inherited myths of earlier nations. In truth, all created beings — prophets, angels, and the righteous — are but honored servants of Allāh, sustained by His grace, subject to His command, possessing neither share nor partner in His Lordship or divinity. How then could they be taken as His offspring when He is the Eternal, the Self-Sufficient, the One free of need for spouse, child, or companion?",
  21027: "They do not precede Him in speech, and they act only by His command.",
  21028: "He knows what is before them and what is behind them, and they do not intercede except for those He approves. And they stand in awe of Him.",
  21029: "If any of them were to say, “I am a god besides Him [13],” We would repay him with Hell. Thus do We reward the wrongdoers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. This verse establishes a universal principle: no created being—whether prophet, angel, or any other—has ever claimed divinity beside Allāh. In particular, it refutes the claims made by some communities about figures such as Jesus, the holy spirit, or the angels.\nThe Qur’an consistently teaches that Jesus (ʿĪsā) never claimed to be God nor invited people to worship him; rather, he proclaimed servanthood and called people to worship Allāh alone (see 5:116–118). Likewise, the holy spirit—understood in Islamic teaching as Gabriel (Jibrīl)—is a noble angel who conveys revelation and never claimed divine status. All angels, no matter how honored or close to Allāh, are described as obedient servants who speak only when He commands (21:27), fear Him with reverence (21:28), and would never dare claim a share in divinity.\nThus, this verse clarifies that anyone—even hypothetically—who claimed to be a god besides Allāh would deserve punishment. Since neither Jesus, nor Gabriel, nor any prophet or angel ever made such a claim, the verse highlights that false attributions of divinity come from people, not from the servants of Allāh themselves.",
  21030: " Do those who disbelieve not see that the heavens and the earth were once joined together, then We split them apart? And We made every living thing from water. Will they not believe?",
  21031: "We have placed firmly set mountains upon the earth so it would not shift beneath them, and We made within it broad pathways so that they might find their way.",
  21032: "And We have made the sky a protected canopy, yet they turn away from its signs.",
  21033: "It is He who created the night and the day, the sun and the moon—each floating in its orbit.",
  21034: "We did not grant any human being before you immortality. So if you were to die, would they then live forever?",
  21035: "Every soul will taste death. We test you with hardship and ease as a trial [14]. And to Us you will all be returned.\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. Every soul created in this world will inevitably taste death, no matter how long it lives. Human life itself is a test—through divine commands and prohibitions, and through the shifting conditions of hardship and ease. Allāh tests people with adversity and prosperity alike—illness and health, poverty and wealth, humiliation and honor, life and death—to reveal who is best in deeds. Death is the inescapable end of every living being, so the wise remain mindful of it and prepare for what follows. Many, however, become heedless during times of blessing and neglect the duty of gratitude. Whoever shows patience in hardship and gratitude in times of favor, seeking the pleasure of his Lord, may look forward to Allah’s reward when all return to Him alone for reckoning and recompense on the Day of Resurrection.",
  21036: "But when the disbelievers see you, they take you only in mockery, saying, “Is this the one who speaks against your gods?” Yet they themselves reject the very mention of the Most Compassionate. [15]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. This verse exposes a deep contradiction in the behavior of the pagans. They fiercely defend their idols—objects carved from stone or wood that could neither hear nor answer—yet they openly reject the remembrance of Allāh, the Most Compassionate. Their anger toward the Prophet of Islam ﷺ is not because he spoke truth, but because his message threaten their traditions, pride, and social order. Notably, they feel insulted on behalf of powerless idols, but show no reverence for the One who created them, sustained them, and showered them with His mercy. This reflects a spiritual blindness: valuing tradition over truth, and defending false gods while turning away from the remembrance of the True God, Allāh.",
  21037: "Man is created of haste. I will show you My signs, so do not ask Me to hasten them.",
  21038: "They say, \"When will this promise be fulfilled—if you are truthful?\"",
  21039: "If only the disbelievers knew the time when they will be unable to ward off the Fire from their faces or their backs, and they will not be helped.",
  21040: "Rather, it will come upon them suddenly and stun them. They will be unable to repel it, nor will they be given any respite.",
  21041: "Indeed, messengers before you were mocked, but those who scoffed at them were encompassed by what they used to ridicule.",
  21042: "Say, “Who can guard you, night or day, from the Most Compassionate?” Yet they turn away from their Lord’s remembrance.",
  21043: "Or do they have gods who can defend them against Us? They cannot even help themselves, nor can they be protected from Us.",
  21044: "Rather, We allowed these and their forefathers to enjoy themselves until life grew long for them [16]. Do they not see that We come to the land, reducing it from its borders? Then is it they who will prevail?\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. They—and their forefathers before them—were deluded because Allāh granted them respite and worldly ease, including abundant wealth, children, and long lives. This prolonged enjoyment led them to persist in disbelief and misguidance, assuming they would never be punished.\nThe verse also points to a clear sign: the steady diminishing of their land from its borders. This has been understood to include the gradual expansion of Islam and the weakening of disbelief, as territories increasingly came under the authority of the believers. In this way, Allah’s promise is fulfilled that His forces will ultimately prevail, while the disbelievers will be subdued and brought low.",
  21045: "Say, “I warn you only by revelation.” But the deaf do not hear the call when they are warned.",
  21046: "If even the slightest breath of your Lord’s punishment were to touch them, they would surely say, “Woe to us! We were truly wrongdoers.”",
  21047: "We will set up the just scales on the Day of Resurrection, so no soul will be wronged in the least. Even if it be the weight of a grain of mustard seed, We will bring it forth. Sufficient are We as Reckoners. [17]\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. This verse affirms the perfect precision of the divine scales by which deeds—great and small—will be weighed on the Day of Resurrection. No soul, whether believer or disbeliever, will be wronged in the least: no good deed will be diminished, and no evil deed will be increased. Even if a deed is as small as the weight of a mustard seed—the tiniest and most insignificant of things—Allāh will bring it forth and present it so that its doer may be recompensed accordingly.\nThis meaning is reinforced elsewhere: “Whoever does an atom’s weight of good will see it, and whoever does an atom’s weight of evil will see it” (cf. 99:7–8). Likewise, the record of deeds will omit nothing, small or great, but will enumerate all precisely (cf. 18:49).\nThe statement “Sufficient are We as Reckoners” affirms that Allāh alone perfectly knows the deeds of His servants, preserves and records them, measures their true weight, and delivers their full and just recompense.",
  21048: "We certainly gave Moses and Aaron the Criterion, a light and a reminder for the righteous. [18]\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. The “Criterion” (al-Furqān) refers to the revelation given to Moses and Aaron—understood by most commentators to be the Torah—which distinguishes truth from falsehood. It is described as both a “light,” illuminating the path of guidance, and a “reminder,” awakening hearts to faith and obedience. These benefits are especially realized by the righteous (al-muttaqūn), who are receptive to divine guidance and mindful of their duty to Allāh.",
  21049: "Those who fear their Lord unseen and are in awe of the Hour. [19]\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. That is, they fear their Lord even when they are unseen by others—when alone and hidden from the eyes of people, with none observing them but Allah, Exalted is He. In such private moments—when the soul is most prone to heedlessness and the restraint felt before others is absent—the sincerity of a servant is truly tested. Many restrain themselves in public yet permit themselves what displeases Allāh when in seclusion. Thus, this verse praises those whose fear of Allāh remains constant in both public and private, whose hearts remain watchful even when no human eye sees them, and whose inward state accords with their outward righteousness.",
  21050: "And this is a blessed Reminder We have sent down. Will you then deny it?",
  21051: "We had already given Abraham his sound judgment before, for We knew him well [worthy of guidance].",
  21052: "When he said to his father and his people, “What are these images to which you are so devoted?”",
  21053: "They said, “We found our forefathers worshipping them.”",
  21054: "He said, \"You and your forefathers have been in manifest error.\"",
  21055: "They said, “Have you come to us with the truth, or are you merely jesting?”",
  21056: "He said, “Rather, your Lord is the Lord of the heavens and the earth, who originated them, and I am among the witnesses to this.”",
  21057: "He said [inwardly], “By Allāh, I will surely scheme against your idols after you have turned away.”",
  21058: "So he broke them into pieces, except the largest of them, so that they might return to it.",
  21059: "They said, \"Who has done this to our gods? He is surely one of the wrongdoers.\"",
  21060: "They said, “We heard a youth called Abraham speaking against them.”",
  21061: "They said, 'Bring him then before the eyes of the people, so that they may testify.'",
  21062: "They said, \"Are you the one who did this to our gods, O Abraham?\"",
  21063: "He said, “Rather, this one—their largest—did it. So ask them, if they can speak.”",
  21064: "So they turned to themselves and said, \"Surely you yourselves are the wrongdoers.\"",
  21065: "Then they relapsed into their stubbornness and said, “You already know that these do not speak.”",
  21066: "He said, \"Do you then, instead of Allāh, worship [idols] that can neither benefit you nor harm you at all?",
  21067: "Fie on you and on what you worship besides Allāh! Have you then no sense?",
  21068: "They said, \"Burn him and support your gods, if you are going to act.\"",
  21069: "We said: \"O fire, be cool and safe for Abraham.\"",
  21070: "They intended an evil scheme against him, but We made them the worst losers.",
  21071: "And We saved him and Lot and brought them to the land We have blessed for all people. [20]\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. I.e., the Levant region.That is, al-Shām—the blessed land that Allāh distinguished with abundant goodness, noble prophets, and many signs throughout the ages. It encompasses the areas known today as Syria, Palestine, Jordan, and Lebanon. The early scholars frequently referred to it as al-arḍ al-mubārakah (the blessed land), for in it lived many of Allāh’s messengers, and to it descended revelation, tranquility, and divine mercy. Hence, when the Qur’ān alludes to this region, it often carries the connotation of a land honoured by Allāh, elevated in rank, and chosen for guidance to mankind.",
  21072: "And We gave him Isaac and Jacob as an additional gift, and We made each of them righteous.",
  21073: "And We made them leaders, guiding by Our command. We inspired them to do good deeds, establish prayer, and give alms. And they were devoted worshippers of Us.",
  21074: "And to Lot We bestowed sound judgment and knowledge, and We saved him from the town that committed shameful deeds. Indeed, they were an evil people—rebellious.",
  21075: "And We admitted him into Our mercy, for he was of the righteous.",
  21076: "And [remember] Noah, when he called out to Us before that, We answered him and saved him and his household from the great affliction.",
  21077: "And We helped him against the people who denied Our signs. Indeed, they were an evil people, so We drowned them all.",
  21078: "And [remember] David and Solomon, when they gave judgment concerning the field into which certain people’s sheep had strayed by night. And We were witness to their judgment.",
  21079: "So We caused Solomon to understand it, and to each of them We gave sound judgment and knowledge. And with David We subjected the mountains and the birds to glorify Allāh. And We were ever the Doer.",
  21080: "And We taught him the art of crafting coats of armor for you, to protect you in battle. Will you then be grateful?",
  21081: "And to Solomon We subjected the wind—stormy and swift—running by his command to the land We had blessed. [21] And We are Knowing of all things.\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. That is the Levant region.",
  21082: "And among the devils were those who dived [into the seas] for him and did other work besides [22]. And We kept watch over them.\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. Allāh further informs that He subjected the devils to Solomon’s service. Among them were those who dived into the sea to extract precious gems for him, while others performed various tasks such as construction, craftsmanship, and other forms of labor. They were unable to refuse what was commanded of them. Allāh kept them under strict watch, preventing them from disobedience or corruption in the duties they were assigned.",
  21083: "And [remember] Job, when he cried out to his Lord, “Adversity [23] has struck me, and You are the Most Merciful of the merciful.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. That is, the grievous afflictions with which Allāh tried His servant Job—of illness, the loss of offspring and wealth, and the withdrawal of worldly comforts.\nThese trials were not a mark of divine displeasure, but rather a purification and elevation, for Allāh tries His most beloved servants with that which reveals the purity of their patience and the truthfulness of their reliance upon Him. Thus Job remained steadfast, never uttering a word of complaint against his Lord, but bearing all with serene acceptance and humble submission. His example stands as an enduring proof that calamities are not always punishment, but may instead be a means by which Allāh raises His servants to the loftiest ranks of gratitude, patience, and nearness to Him.",
  21084: "So We answered his prayer, removed the adversity that had afflicted him, and restored his family to him along with twice their number—as a mercy from Us and a reminder for those who worship Us.",
  21085: "And [remember] Ishmael, Idris [Enoch], and Dhu’l-Kifl [Ezekiel]—all of them were among the steadfast.",
  21086: "We admitted them into Our mercy; indeed, they were among the righteous.",
  21087: "And [remember] the Man of the Whale [Jonah], when he departed in anger, thinking We would not restrict him. Then, in the depths of darkness [24], he cried out [to Allāh], “There is no god except You. Glory be to You! Indeed, I was among the wrongdoers.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. I.e., from the encompassing darknesses that enveloped him: the darkness of the night, the darkness of the vast sea, and the darkness of the belly of the great fish. These three layers of obscurity surrounded Prophet Yūnus (Jonah) عليه السلام from every side, leaving no apparent means of deliverance. Yet, within such overwhelming despair, he turned to Allāh with sincere humility, uttering his great supplication. His deliverance therefrom stands as a sign that no matter how deep the darkness of one’s circumstance, the light of divine mercy is nearer than one imagines, and that the heartfelt call of a repentant servant is never lost upon the Lord of the worlds.",
  21088: "So We answered his prayer and saved him from distress. Thus do We save the believers.",
  21089: "And [remember] Zachariah, when he cried out to his Lord, “My Lord, do not leave me alone [without an heir], for You are the Best of inheritors.”",
  21090: "So We answered his prayer, granted him Yahya [John], and cured his wife for him [so she could bear a child]. Indeed, they used to hasten to good deeds and call upon Us in hope and awe, and they were humble before Us.",
  21091: "And [remember Mary], who guarded her chastity [25] — We breathed into her through Our angel [Gabriel], and We made her and her son a sign for all people. [26]\\n\\nFootnotes: [25]. The word chastity here refers to refraining from all illicit sexual conduct and guarding one’s purity in thought, word, and action. In the case of Mary (peace be upon her), it also refers to her vow of purity and complete devotion to Allāh.\nIslam places great emphasis on protecting personal dignity and safeguarding society from moral harm. The Sunnah teaches young men to treat older women as they would their mothers and younger women as they would their sisters — with sincerity, modesty, and respect. A simple principle guides this ethic: treat every woman as you would want others to treat your own mother or sister.\nA well-known narration illustrates this beautifully. A young man once approached the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ and asked permission to commit adultery. Those around him were shocked, but the Prophet ﷺ responded with gentleness. He drew the young man near and asked him, “Would you like this for your mother?” The man replied, “No, by Allāh.” The Prophet said, “And people do not like it for their mothers.” He repeated the question regarding his daughter, sister, and aunts, and each time the young man rejected the idea. The Prophet ﷺ then placed his hand upon him and prayed: “O Allāh, forgive his sin, purify his heart, and guard his chastity.” Thereafter, the young man no longer inclined toward such sin.\nThe Qur’anic teaching on chastity is clear: any sexual relationship outside the bond of marriage is a grave transgression. Yet chastity begins in the heart. From the heart arise intentions and desires, whether pure or corrupt. The Prophet ﷺ warned that spiritual harm can begin internally, teaching that the eyes, ears, tongue, hands, feet, and heart each have their share in wrongdoing when they incline toward what is forbidden.\nModern challenges — such as pornography, immodest display, and casual intimacy outside marriage — directly contradict the Qur’anic principle of chastity. A chaste person establishes personal boundaries, avoids environments that inflame desire, and cultivates self-restraint. Regular prayer, fasting, remembrance of Allāh, recitation of the Qur’an, and mindful reflection purify the heart and strengthen resolve.\nAlthough many societies may neglect or dismiss the value of chastity, it remains central in Islam. Outward modesty alone is not sufficient; Allāh looks to the heart and intention. True purity begins within. When chastity becomes a sincere aim, Allāh grants strength, protection from sin, and the sweetness of nearness to Him.\n[26]. This verse highlights the infinite power of Allāh. He created ʿĪsā (Jesus, peace be upon him) without a father, and Mary (peace be upon her) conceived and bore him without any human partner. Their miraculous story stands as a sign for all humanity — a reminder that the Creator is not bound by natural laws or customary causes.\nWhat appears impossible within human experience is effortless for Allāh. The birth of Jesus is therefore not merely a historical event, but a sign pointing to divine omnipotence. It affirms that creation occurs by His command alone — “Be,” and it is.",
  21092: "Indeed, this community of yours is one community [27], and I am your Lord—so worship Me alone.\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]. The verse primarily addresses the prophets collectively. The phrase “one community” highlights the essential unity of the religion brought by all prophets—submission to Allāh (Islam). By extension, the address is also understood to include the people, in light of the preceding mention of the prophets. Scholars explain that the messengers referenced are the community’s leaders and exemplars, whose guidance is to be followed and whose path is to be emulated. Since the Lord is One, the prophetic message is one, and the religion is one, the command to worship Allāh alone follows as the natural and necessary consequence of this unity.",
  21093: "But the people have divided their religion among themselves into sects; all will return to Us.",
  21094: "whoever does righteous deeds while being a believer—his effort will never be denied, and We are surely recording it.",
  21095: "It is impossible for any community We have destroyed to return. [28]\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. This verse serves as a stern warning against persisting in denial and disbelief, for such persistence may lead to a destruction after which there is no return and no opportunity for redress. The explicit declaration that they will not return—despite its apparent obviousness—appears intended to confront and unsettle the deniers, underscoring that none of them will escape the impending punishment, nor will they be granted the chance to repent once the decree has taken effect.",
  21096: "Until, when Gog and Magog are released, they will surge down from every elevation. [29]\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. Gog and Magog (Yaʾjūj and Maʾjūj) are described in the Qur’an and Prophetic reports as a destructive people who will be released near the end of time (see 18:94–99). According to hadith-based accounts, they will spread widely across the land, causing great corruption. Allāh —Majestic and Exalted—will then decree their destruction, striking their vast multitudes with a sudden affliction so that they perish in great numbers. It is further reported that birds then rain will be sent to remove their remains and cleanse the earth of their corruption. These events are presented as among the major signs preceding the Final Hour and as a manifestation of Allah’s overwhelming power and decisive dominion.",
  21097: "And the true promise will draw near [30] and suddenly the eyes of those who disbelieved will stare in horror: “Woe to us! We were indeed heedless of this—rather, we were wrongdoers.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. The “true promise” refers to the assured coming of the Resurrection and the Final Judgment. The sudden fixing of the disbelievers’ eyes conveys shock and terror at the realization of what they had long denied. Their confession of heedlessness and wrongdoing reflects the Qur’anic theme that regret at that moment will no longer avail them.",
  21098: "Indeed, you and whatever you worship besides Allāh are fuel for Hell; you will surely come to it.",
  21099: "Had these truly been gods, they would not have entered it. And all of them will remain there forever.",
  21100: "For them therein is groaning, and they will not hear therein. [31]\\n\\nFootnotes: [31]. They will hear nothing except their own cries and the furious roaring of the Fire; nothing will reach them that offers comfort or relief. Exegetes explain that the intensity of the punishment will so overwhelm them that they will remain wholly absorbed in their own anguish, unable to perceive anything beyond it.",
  21101: "Indeed, those for whom the best has already been decreed by Us will be kept far away from it. [32]\\n\\nFootnotes: [32]. The “best” refers to the divine promise of ultimate good—namely Paradise—by virtue of which such people will be kept far from Hell. This verse was also understood as a response to the claim of some polytheists that Jesus and the angels who had been worshipped would enter the Fire. Allāh clarifies that those whom He has already known to be among the people of ultimate felicity—such as Jesus (peace be upon him)—will be kept far removed from it.",
  21102: "They will not hear its faintest hissing, and they will abide forever in what their souls desire.",
  21103: "The greatest horror will not grieve them [33], and the angels will receive them, saying, “This is your Day which you were promised.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [33]. The “greatest horror” is commonly understood to refer to the overwhelming fright of the Day of Resurrection, from which the righteous will be granted complete security.",
  21104: "On the Day when We roll up the sky like the rolling of a scroll of records. Just as We originated the first creation, so We will bring it back—a promise binding upon Us; surely We will fulfill it.",
  21105: "Indeed, We have written in the revealed Scriptures, [34] after the Preserved Record, [35] that the land will be inherited by My righteous servants.\\n\\nFootnotes: [34]. That is the earlier revealed Scriptures—such as the Psalms, the Torah, and the Gospel—as well as the Qur’an. The verse affirms a recurring divine principle found across revelation: ultimate inheritance belongs not to the arrogant or oppressive but to the righteous and patient. Thus, Psalm 37 states, “Evildoers will be cut off, but those who hope in Allāh will inherit the earth” (37:9), and, “The righteous will inherit the land and dwell in it forever” (37:29). Similarly, Proverbs 2:21 declares, “The upright will dwell in the land, and the blameless will remain in it,” and in the Gospel it is said, “Blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth” (Matthew 5:5).\nThe qualities highlighted in these texts—gentleness, patience, humility, restraint from anger, and forgiveness—are likewise highly praised in Islam. A mild-tempered person shows emotional discipline, is slow to retaliate, and inclines toward mercy rather than harshness; such traits reflect inner strength, not weakness. Islamic teaching affirms that true honor belongs to those who embody righteousness and gentleness. Accordingly, the promised inheritance is understood not merely as worldly dominion, but as lasting security, dignity, and ultimate reward in the Hereafter. In its highest and final sense, the “earth” is fulfilled in the everlasting abode of Paradise, the homeland of peace for the faithful.\nThe Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said, “Allāh is Gentle and loves gentleness.” Gentleness thus attracts divine love, fosters harmony among people, and leads to enduring success in this world and the next.\n[35]. The “Preserved Record” (al-Dhikr) is interpreted by many exegetes as the Preserved Tablet (al-Lawḥ al-Maḥfūẓ), the divine register in which all decrees are recorded.",
  21106: "Indeed, in this is a clear message for a people who are devoted in worship.",
  21107: "We have not sent you,[36] except as a mercy to all the worlds. [37]\\n\\nFootnotes: [36]. The address is to the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ. \nWhoever sincerely utters with his tongue and affirms in his heart that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allāh, and that Muḥammad is the servant and Messenger of Allāh, shall be saved from the Fire and admitted into Paradise.\nThis declaration of monotheism is the gateway to salvation, for it gathers within it the essence of faith: singling out Allāh alone for worship, and affirming the truth of His final Messenger, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. When a servant truly believes this testimony—outwardly and inwardly—it becomes a fortress of protection in this world and the next, and a cause for eternal bliss by the permission of Allāh.\n[37]. The love, mercy, and compassion of the Messenger of Allāh, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ for creation were of a purity unmatched by the sentimental claims of modern humanists. His compassion surpassed that of any mother for her child, for he was nurtured, refined, and commissioned by Allāh—Lord of the heavens and the earth—as a Mercy to all beings, human and jinn alike.\nHe lived not for his own comfort, nor for worldly gain, but for the guidance, rectification, and happiness of the conscious creation, and for the harmony and righteousness of existence. His entire life—his tears, his prayers, his battles, his patience, and his sacrifices—were offered in selfless devotion for the salvation of others. Thus, he is the embodiment of divine mercy manifested in human form, the beacon of guidance for all who seek the pleasure of Allāh",
  21108: "Say, “It is only revealed to me that your God is One God. Will you then submit to Him?” [38]\\n\\nFootnotes: [38]. That is, will you submit yourselves in sincere obedience and surrender to Allāh alone, thereby becoming Muslims. To be Muslim is to yield oneself wholly to Allāh —placing one’s trust in Him, doing what is right, and finding peace in His remembrance. It entails entrusting to Him one’s desires, concerns, and the unfolding affairs of life. Through such surrender, Allāh grants guidance and inner tranquility. For the believer, true success lies in allowing Allah’s guidance to shape one’s path. Whoever relies upon Him sincerely will find that He brings about what is best and most fitting in the end.",
  21109: "But if they turn away, then say, “I have warned you all equally,[39] and I do not know whether what you are threatened with is near or far.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [39]. That is, the warning has been conveyed to all of you plainly and without distinction, so that none may claim ignorance or unfairness. The Prophet’s duty is only to deliver the message clearly and equally to all.",
  21110: "Surely He knows what is spoken openly, and what you conceal.",
  21111: "And I do not know—perhaps it is a trial for you and an enjoyment for a time.[40]\\n\\nFootnotes: [40]. That is, the delay of the threatened punishment may itself be a test for you, while you are granted a limited period of worldly enjoyment before the final reckoning. The Prophet is instructed to convey that the postponement of punishment—despite your deserving it—may serve as a trial: either that you persist in your transgression until the appointed time of destruction, thereby increasing your punishment, or that you return to the truth and thus are saved from Allah’s punishment.",
  21112: "He said, “My Lord, judge in truth [41]. And our Lord is the Most Compassionate—the One whose help is sought against what you describe.” [42]\\n\\nFootnotes: [41]. At the close of the proclamation and the culmination of the call to a people whom the gentleness of the caller only increased in obstinacy and persistence in misguidance, the final report is entrusted to the All-Decisive, the All-Knowing, with an appeal for swift judgment between the caller and those he called. The plea, “judge in truth,” is a supplicatory expression filled with confidence in the promised victory—fulfilled on the Day of Badr, when the banner of triumph was raised over the polytheists. Be yourself upon the truth and monotheism so that you may rightly say, ‘Judge in truth,’ for the one who is upon falsehood cannot say, ‘Judge in truth.’” It is of the mercy of the Most Compassionate that He aids His allies against the allies of Satan—those who threaten the believers with dominance and openly persist in idolatry and turning away from the call.\n[42). That is, the false claims you attribute to Allāh —such as alleging that He has taken a son or daughters, accusing His Messenger, Muhammad ﷺ, of being a sorcerer or a poet, or claiming that the Qur’an is nothing but magic. Against these and similar fabrications the help of the Most Compassionate is sought.",
  22001: "O mankind, fear your Lord. Indeed, the quake of the Hour is a tremendous thing.",
  22002: "On the Day you see it, every nursing mother will be distracted from the child she was nursing, and every pregnant woman will deliver her burden. You will see people as though intoxicated, though they are not intoxicated—but the punishment of Allāh is severe.",
  22003: "Among the people is he who disputes concerning Allāh without knowledge and follows every rebellious devil.",
  22004: "It has been decreed against him: whoever takes him as an ally, he will lead him astray and guide him to the punishment of the blazing Fire.",
  22005: "O people, if you are in doubt concerning the Resurrection, then know that We created you from dust [1], then from a sperm-drop, then from a clinging clot, then from a lump of flesh—formed and unformed [2]—so that We may make this clear to you [3]. We settle in the wombs whatever We will for an appointed term; then We bring you forth as infants, then [We raise you] so that you may reach maturity. And among you is he who dies early, and among you is he who is returned to the most abject stage of old age, so that after having known, he knows nothing. You see the earth barren, then when We send down water upon it, it stirs, swells, and brings forth every kind of beautiful plant.\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. I.e., Your father, Adam.\n[2]. That is, some developing forms are fully formed and proceed toward a sound embryo, while others remain incomplete and end in miscarriage. The phrase highlights the differing outcomes within the stages of human development by the will and decree of Allāh.\n[3]. This refers to the origin of your creation, and to Our perfect power—making clear that resurrecting you anew is no more difficult for Us than your first creation.\n The verse draws the human being’s attention to his own beginning, reminding him that he was once nothing—not even a thing mentioned—until Allāh fashioned him with His Will, shaped him in due proportion, breathed into him of His spirit, and granted him intellect, hearing, and sight.\nThus, the One who brought mankind forth from non-existence into existence, without model or precedent, is surely able—indeed, more able—to bring them back again after they have turned to dust. The Qur’ān repeatedly emphasizes this point:\n “And He is the One who originates creation, then repeats it; and that is even easier for Him.”\nThe intended meaning is not that resurrection is “easy” in comparison to creation—since nothing is difficult for Allāh—but rather that if the human mind accepts the first creation, then it must all the more readily accept the second.\nThe early scholars would say: “He who was able to begin creation without effort is surely able to restore it after its dispersal.”\nIn this way, the Qur’ān appeals to the intellect, awakening the heart from heedlessness, and reminding the human being that resurrection is a certainty, not a matter of speculation. For the One who said “Be!” and it came to be, requires no further exertion to return His servants to life for judgment.",
  22006: "That is because Allāh alone is the Absolute Truth, He gives life to the dead, and He has full power over all things.",
  22007: "And that the Hour is coming—there is no doubt about it—and Allāh will surely resurrect all who are in the graves.",
  22008: "Yet among the people is he who argues about Allāh without knowledge, guidance, or an enlightening scripture.",
  22009: "Twisting his neck [in arrogance] [4], that he may lead others astray away from the path of Allāh. For him is disgrace in this world, and We shall make him taste on the Day of Resurrection the punishment of the burning Fire.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. The Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said:\n“Whoever has even an atom’s weight of pride in his heart shall not enter Paradise.”\nA man asked, “O Messenger of Allāh, what of a person who likes his clothes and shoes to be fine?” He ﷺ replied:\n“Allāh is Most Beautiful and loves beauty. Pride is to reject the truth and to belittle people.”\nIn this noble ḥadīth, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ lays bare the essence of arrogance—a disease of the heart that veils one from guidance and estranges the servant from his Lord. True pride, the forbidden kind, is not in adorning oneself with lawful garments, nor in appreciating the beauty Allāh has permitted. Rather, it lies in two grave matters:\nA). Turning away from the truth when it reaches one—out of stubbornness, self-admiration, or disdain.\nB). Looking down upon the servants of Allāh, deeming oneself superior in worth, lineage, wealth, or status.\nThus, arrogance is a veil that prevents the heart from humility, gratitude, and receptivity to divine guidance. Beauty—when accompanied by gratitude and humility—is from the blessings of Allāh and among the adornments He has allowed His servants. But the moment a servant allows his heart to be inflated by self-importance, or begins to diminish others, he steps into the realm of the condemned. For this reason, the Prophet ﷺ warned that even the smallest trace of such pride bars a person from Paradise until he is purified of it.",
  22010: "[He will be told], “This is because of what your two hands have sent forth, and Allāh is never unjust to His servants.”",
  22011: "Among the people is he who worships Allāh on the edge [5]: if good befalls him, he is content with it; but if a trial afflicts him, he turns back headlong [to ingratitude]. He loses both this world and the Hereafter—that is indeed the clear loss.\\n\\nFootnotes: [5] Literally, “on an edge” (ʿalā ḥarf), an Arabic expression describing one who stands on the brink or margin of something without firm footing. It depicts a person whose commitment to faith is unstable and conditional—he worships so long as matters proceed favorably, but at the first trial he slips away. The imagery evokes someone poised on the edge of a precipice, ready to fall back at any moment, thus illustrating the shallowness and insecurity of such devotion.",
  22012: "He calls upon, instead of Allāh, that which can neither harm him nor benefit him. That is truly the far straying. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. Image-worship — attributing divine honor, devotion, or ultimate reliance to anything created — is regarded in Islam as idolatry (shirk) and a denial of the Creator’s exclusive right to worship. Worship belongs to Allāh alone, the One who created, sustains, and governs all existence.\nWhen a person turns away from Allāh, he risks falling into spiritual confusion, directing acts of devotion toward created beings instead. Throughout history, people have elevated prophets, angels, jinn, saints, natural forces, trees, rivers, mountains, stones, and other created things to a status of reverence that belongs only to the Creator.\nIslam distinguishes clearly between respect and worship. Prophets and righteous people are honored, but never worshiped. Natural creation is appreciated as a sign of divine artistry, but never treated as divine itself. True guidance restores the heart to its proper orientation: gratitude toward creation, but devotion to the Creator alone.",
  22013: "He calls upon one whose harm is closer than his benefit—surely miserable is the protector, and miserable is the companion.",
  22014: "Allāh will surely admit those who believe and do righteous deeds into gardens beneath which rivers flow. Indeed, Allāh does whatever He wills.",
  22015: "Whoever thinks that Allāh will not support him [the Prophet] in this world and the Hereafter—let him stretch a rope to the ceiling, then cut it off, and see whether his plan can remove what enrages him. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. The Arabic phrase “let him stretch a rope to the sky, then cut it off” is a vivid idiom of frustration and futility. Exegetes explain it as a sarcastic challenge: if someone is so enraged by Allah’s support of His Messenger, let him attempt the most extreme measure—even to the point of strangling himself—then see whether this removes his anger. The expression does not primarily command the act but exposes the utter helplessness of the denier and the inevitability of Allah’s decree. It is a rhetorical declaration that Allāh will unfailingly support and assist His Prophet ﷺ, whether the opponents like it or not. Allāh will never cease to help His Messenger Muhammad ﷺ and uphold his religion, no matter what the deniers attempt.",
  22016: "Thus We have sent down [this Qur’an] as clear verses, and Allāh guides whom He wills.",
  22017: "Indeed, those who believe [the Muslims] [8], the Jews [9], the Sabians, the Christians [10], the Zoroastrians [11], and those who associate others with Allāh —indeed, Allāh will judge between them on the Day of Resurrection. Allāh is surely Witness over all things.\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. Muslims affirm the absolute oneness of God (tawḥīd). Allāh is One—unique, without partner, offspring, or equal. He is not human, nor has He ever been human. Accordingly, Islamic religion rejects doctrines such as the Trinity, original sin, and salvation through atonement.\nIn Islam, salvation is attained through sincere belief in the One God, faith in His messengers, repentance, righteous conduct, and a life characterized by moral integrity, charity, and obedience. Each person is accountable for his or her own deeds, and forgiveness is granted directly by Allāh to those who turn to Him sincerely.\n[9]. Islamic belief also differs from certain portrayals found in Jewish tradition. Islam teaches that prophets are protected from major moral failings; therefore, narratives depicting prophets committing grave sins—such as adultery or drunkenness—are not accepted in Islamic religion.\nIslam further holds that the Torah in its present form has undergone alterations over time and does not fully preserve the original revelation given to Moses. While Judaism does not recognize Jesus or Muhammad as prophets, Muslims believe in all of God’s prophets, His angels, His revealed scriptures, the Last Day, and divine decree.\n[10]. From the Islamic perspective, contemporary Christianity does not maintain pure monotheism and does not recognize Muhammad ﷺ as the final messenger of God. Islam teaches that rejecting any one of God’s prophets constitutes rejection of them all.\nIslam also preserves a comprehensive framework of worship and law, including the five daily prayers, fasting in Ramadan, almsgiving (zakāt), and pilgrimage (ḥajj), along with prohibitions such as gambling, alcohol consumption, and usury. Dietary regulations and moral guidelines similarly shape daily life. Muslims understand these practices as expressions of submission to God and His commandments.\n[11]. I.e., Zoroastrians; fire-worshippers.",
  22018: "Do you not see that to Allāh prostrates whoever is in the heavens and whoever is on the earth— and the sun, the moon, the stars, the mountains, the trees, the creatures, and many of the people; but many have the punishment justified upon them. And whoever Allāh humiliates, none can honor him. Indeed Allāh does whatever He wills.",
  22019: "These are two adversaries [12] disputing about their Lord. As for the disbelievers, garments of fire will be cut out for them, and scalding water will be poured over their heads.\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. The two adversaries” refers to two opposing parties locked in dispute over their Lord—namely, the people of faith and the people of disbelief, obedience versus rebellion. Many exegetes understand the verse more specifically as pointing to the believers and disbelievers who confronted one another, and some relate it to the two rival groups that met at the Battle of Badr. The wording of the verse, however, is general and extends to every such confrontation in which people contend about Allāh: those who affirm and submit in contrast to those who deny and persist in resistance.",
  22020: "With it will be melted all that is in their bellies and their skins.",
  22021: "And for them are iron maces. [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. The Arabic maqāmiʿ refers to heavy iron clubs or maces used for striking and driving back. Exegetes explain that the inhabitants of the Fire are beaten with them whenever they attempt to escape, emphasizing the severity and inescapability of the punishment.",
  22022: "Whenever they intend to come out of it out of anguish, they will be returned into it, and [it will be said to them], “Taste the punishment of the Burning Fire!”",
  22023: "Allāh will surely admit those who believe and do righteous deeds into gardens beneath which rivers flow. There they will be adorned with bracelets of gold and pearls, and their garments there will be silk.",
  22024: "They were guided to the finest speech [14] and to the path of the One worthy of all praise. [15]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. The “finest speech” refers foremost to the pure word of faith—lā ilāha illā Allāh (“there is no god but Allāh ”)—and, more broadly, to all speech characterized by truth, praise, remembrance, and gratitude. It encompasses righteous words, sincere supplications, and every expression that brings a person nearer to Allāh.\n[15]. “The One who is Most Praiseworthy” translates al-Ḥamīd, one of the Divine Names of Allāh in Islam, meaning the One who is perfectly and eternally worthy of all praise. The phrase ṣirāṭ al-Ḥamīd (“the path of the Praiseworthy”) is understood to refer to Islam—the path that leads to Allāh and to His pleasure—namely, the straight path of faith, obedience, and righteousness.",
  22025: "Indeed, those who disbelieve and hinder others from the path of Allāh and from the Sacred Mosque in Makkah—which We have made equally accessible to all people, whether resident or visitor—and whoever intends within it any wrongdoing or deviation [16], We will make him taste a painful punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. The phrase “intends therein wrongdoing or deviation” refers to any deliberate inclination toward sin, injustice, or violation of the sanctity of the Sacred Mosque and its precinct. Exegetes note that the wording is especially severe: even the firm intention to commit wrongdoing in the sacred sanctuary is treated as a grave offense, reflecting the heightened holiness and inviolability of the Haram in Makkah.",
  22026: "And ˹remember˺ when We appointed for Abraham the site of the Sacred House and its precinct, ˹saying,˺ “Do not associate anything with Me, and purify My House for those who circumambulate it, and for those who stand in prayer, and those who bow and prostrate.”",
  22027: " And proclaim the pilgrimage to the people: they will come to you on foot and on every lean mount, arriving from every distant pathway.",
  22028: "So that they may witness benefits [17] for themselves and mention Allah’s Name during the appointed days over the sacrificial animals He has provided for them [18]. Then eat from them and feed the distressed poor.\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. The “benefits” are understood to include both worldly and spiritual gains associated with the pilgrimage—such as forgiveness, reward, trade, social gathering, and the strengthening of faith and unity among believers.\n[18]. The phrase refers to the sacrificial livestock (bahīmat al-anʿām)—namely camels, cattle, sheep, and goats—offered during the prescribed days of Ḥajj and ʿĪd al-Aḍḥā. Mentioning Allah’s Name over them refers to the ritual pronouncement at the time of slaughter, acknowledging that the provision comes from Him alone.",
  22029: "Then let them complete their cleansing, fulfill their vows, and circumambulate the Ancient House [the Kaʿbah]. [19]\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. The command to “complete their cleansing” translates tafath, which exegetes explain as the removal of the physical untidiness associated with the state of iḥrām—such as shaving the hair, trimming the nails, and performing the post-ritual grooming following the sacrifice. The “Ancient House” (al-bayt al-ʿatīq) refers to the Kaʿbah in Makkah.",
  22030: "That is so. Whoever honors the sacred ordinances of Allāh —it is best for him with his Lord. The livestock have been made lawful for you, except what has been recited to you [20]. So shun the filth of idols and avoid false speech. [21]\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. Mentioned in Qur’an 5:3. This refers to the specific categories of meat that Allāh has prohibited—such as carrion, spilled blood, the flesh of swine, and animals slaughtered improperly, without the Name of Allāh being invoked, or those dedicated to idols.\n[21]. “The filth of idols” translates rijs min al-awthān, referring to the spiritual impurity and abomination of idolatry. \n “Lying speech” covers all kinds of untrue or dishonesty speech—lying, false testimony and deceit., especially in matters of faith and worship.  Scholars also connect it to the chants and invocations of idolaters, as well as any words that promote false beliefs.",
  22031: "Being devoted exclusively to Allāh, not associating anything with Him in worship. For whoever associates partners with Allāh is like one who falls from the sky—then the birds snatch him away, or the wind sweeps him off to a distant place.",
  22032: "That is so. And whoever honors the symbols of Allāh [22]—such honor arises from the piety of the hearts.\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. “The symbols of Allāh ” (shaʿāʾir Allāh) refers to the sacred rites, places, and acts associated with worship—especially the rituals of Hajj, such as the sacrificial animals, the Sacred Mosque, and Mount ‘Arafah. It also includes all signs that remind one of Allāh, such as the Friday (Jumu‘ah) and Eid prayers, and the call to prayer. Honoring these symbols reflects reverence, humility, and genuine God-consciousness rooted in the heart.",
  22033: "There are benefits for you in them until an appointed time. Then their place of sacrifice is the Ancient House. [23]\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. The “Ancient House” (al-bayt al-ʿatīq) refers to the Kaʿbah in Makkah. The verse indicates that the sacrificial animals designated for pilgrimage rites ultimately reach their place of slaughter within the sacred precinct (ḥaram) associated with the Kaʿbah, after benefiting their owners (e.g., through riding or milk) up to the prescribed time.",
  22034: "For every community We have appointed a sacrificial rite [24], so that they may pronounce the Name of Allāh over the livestock He has provided for them. So your God is One God—therefore submit to Him. And give good news to the humble.\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. The “sacrificial rite” (mansak) refers to the prescribed act of ritual sacrifice appointed for each religious community. Exegetes explain that while the specific forms differed among earlier peoples, the underlying purpose was the same: to remember Allāh and invoke His Name over the animals He provided, thereby affirming gratitude, devotion, and monotheistic worship.",
  22035: "Those whose hearts tremble when Allāh is mentioned [25], who patiently endure whatever afflicts them, who establish prayer, and who spend in charity from what We have provided for them.\\n\\nFootnotes: [25]. The phrase describing hearts that “tremble” when Allāh is mentioned (wajilat qulūbuhum) conveys a rich spiritual state that scholars have understood to include reverent awe, loving devotion, and hopeful reliance upon Allāh. Islamic spirituality often describes true worship as being grounded in this triad—love (maḥabbah), fear or awe (khawf), and hope (rajāʾ) in Allāh. Together, these inward qualities form the balanced essence of sincere devotion, safeguarding the believer from despair on the one hand and complacency on the other.",
  22036: "As for the sacrificial animals [camels, cattle, and others], We have made them among the symbols of Allāh for you—in them there is much good for you. So mention the Name of Allāh over them as they are lined up for sacrifice; then, once they fall lifeless on their sides, eat from them and feed the one who is content and does not beg, and the one who openly begs. Thus We have subjected them to you so that you may be grateful.",
  22037: "It is neither their meat nor their blood that reaches Allāh, but it is your piety that reaches Him. Thus He has subjected them to you so that you may magnify Allāh for what He has guided you to. And give good news to the doers of good.",
  22038: "Indeed, Allāh defends those who believe [26]. Surely Allāh does not like anyone who is treacherous and ungrateful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. The word “defends” conveys the sense of continual protection and the repelling of harm. Scholars note that this includes both worldly support and the ultimate divine safeguarding of the believers. The description “treacherous and ungrateful” combines betrayal toward others with ingratitude toward Allāh, highlighting a deeply condemned moral state.\nIn light of this, believers are reminded of their responsibility to stand firm in justice and to support the oppressed. This includes striving to educate and develop themselves—gaining awareness of current affairs, history, law, and other beneficial knowledge that enables them to serve as capable and responsible members of their communities. In doing so, they may become a voice for the voiceless and advocates for those who are neglected or wronged.",
  22039: "Permission ˹to fight back˺ is granted to those who are being fought, because they were wronged. And surely Allāh is fully capable of granting them victory.",
  22040: "Those who have been driven from their homes for no reason other than saying, “Our Lord is Allāh.” Had Allāh not repelled some people by means of others, monasteries, churches, synagogues, and mosques—wherein Allah’s Name is much mentioned—would surely have been destroyed. Allāh will certainly help those who help His cause. Indeed, Allāh is All-Strong, Almighty.",
  22041: "They are those who—if We grant them authority in the land—establish prayer, give alms, enjoin what is right, and forbid what is wrong. And to Allāh belongs the ultimate outcome of all matters.",
  22042: "If they deny you, ˹O Muhammad˺, then before them the people of Noah also denied, as did the peoples of ʿĀd and Thamūd.",
  22043: "As well as the people of Abraham and the people of Lot.",
  22044: "And the people of Midian, and Moses was also denied. So I gave the disbelievers respite; then I seized them—so how was My punishment!",
  22045: "How many a town We have destroyed while it was steeped in wrongdoing—now lying in ruins upon its roofs. And how many a deserted well and lofty palace have been left in ruin! [27]\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]. This verse paints a vivid picture of once-prosperous civilizations reduced to desolation. The “deserted well” symbolizes abandoned sources of life, sustenance, and community, while the “lofty palace” represents former power, wealth, and grandeur now left empty. Together, these images underscore a central Qur’anic theme: no society—no matter how mighty or advanced—endures when it persists in injustice and wrongdoing.",
  22046: "Have they not traveled through the land so that they may have hearts to understand and ears to hear? For it is not the eyes that become blind, but it is the hearts within the chests that are blind.",
  22047: "They ask you to hasten the punishment. But Allāh will never fail in His promise. And truly, a day with your Lord is like a thousand years of your reckoning. [28]\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. The statement that “a day with your Lord is like a thousand years of your reckoning” emphasizes that divine time is not measured according to human standards. What may appear to humans as delay in the fulfillment of God’s promise is not delay from the divine perspective. Similar language appears in the Bible in the words attributed to Peter: “With the Lord a day is like a thousand years, and a thousand years are like a day” (2 Peter 3:8). In that passage, Peter responds to skeptics who questioned why the promised judgment had not yet occurred, explaining that “the Lord is not slow in keeping his promise, as some understand slowness”. The idea conveyed in both texts is that God—Allāh—acts according to His own wisdom and timing, and that what humans perceive as delay may in fact be part of a divine purpose and plan.",
  22048: "How many a town I granted respite while it was steeped in wrongdoing; then I seized it. And to Me is the final return.",
  22049: "Say, “O mankind, I am only a clear warner to you.” [29]\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. Warnings are signs—words or events—that alert us to potential harm or danger. Yet the warnings most often ignored are those given by Allāh and His Messenger Muhammad ﷺ. These warnings are not meant to restrict us, but to protect, guide, and benefit us.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ cautioned humanity against polytheism, sinful behavior, and all forms of evil. He warned against false and hypocritical religious figures, leaders who present themselves as righteous while spreading teachings that contradict Allah’s guidance. If any part of their message conflicts with the Qur’an or the authentic Sunnah, it is counterfeit and must be avoided. Allah’s warnings—and the Prophet’s ﷺ warnings—are expressions of divine mercy. Sin creates a barrier between us and Allāh, distancing us from His closeness and His blessings. That is why we are instructed to stay away from sin: for our own spiritual safety. The Prophet ﷺ also warned us about Satan, his deceptions, and the subtle traps that can imprison a person day by day. He cautioned against becoming materialistic, allowing wealth or possessions to take priority over Allāh. We are warned against hypocritical judgment. A person will be judged by the same measure they use on others. If we cannot apply a standard to our own lives, we have no right to impose it on someone else. The Prophet ﷺ also warned against pride, for arrogance prevents a person from accepting truth and submitting to Allāh. True faith requires humility, for Allāh loves the humble and opposes those who are proud.",
  22050: "Those who believe and do righteous deeds will have forgiveness and an honorable provision.",
  22051: "But those who strive to undermine Our verses—they are the people of the Hellfire.\n[30]\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. The phrase describing those who “strive to undermine” the divine verses includes those who actively discourage people from following the Prophet ﷺ. It refers to those who exert themselves to turn others away from the signs of the Qur’an and from the evidences of God’s oneness, while imagining that they can frustrate Allah’s will or escape His power. Such people are the companions of the Fire, bound to it and abiding therein. Whoever supposes that by opposing Allāh or His Messenger—or by preventing others from following the truth—he can evade divine punishment has gravely erred; his efforts are rendered futile, and he becomes deserving of the punishment of Hell if he dies in that state.",
  22052: "We did not send before you any messenger or prophet except that, when he recited, Satan attempted to interfere with his recitation [by casting into it some misunderstanding]. But Allāh nullifies whatever Satan casts; then Allāh firmly establishes His revelations. And Allāh is All-Knowing, All-Wise. [31]\\n\\nFootnotes: [31]. This verse teaches that whenever a messenger or prophet delivered revelation, Satan attempted to cause confusion, opposition, or misunderstanding among the listeners. He does not alter revelation itself, for Allāh protects His message completely. Instead, Satan seeks to distract hearts, inspire doubts, or create distorted interpretations—but Allāh always cancels and overcomes these attempts.\nSatan’s “casting” refers to his efforts to mislead people, stir whispers, confuse audiences, or inspire false ideas in the minds of the disbelievers.\nAllāh nullifies Satan’s influence by clarifying the truth, strengthening the believers, exposing falsehood, and firmly establishing His revelation in its pure form.\nThis process is part of divine wisdom: it tests hearts, distinguishes sincerity from hypocrisy, and shows that guidance ultimately belongs to Allāh.\nIn short, Satan may try to confuse people around revelation, but he can never corrupt revelation itself. Allah’s guidance remains perfectly preserved and precisely established.",
  22053: "So that He may make what Satan casts a trial for those whose hearts are diseased [with doubt] and those whose hearts are hardened. And indeed, the wrongdoers are in extreme dissension. [32]\\n\\nFootnotes: [32]. That is, from their obstinate resistance to the truth of Islam. This verse explains the divine wisdom in allowing Satan to attempt interference around revelation: it serves as a test. Those whose hearts are diseased or hardened fall into confusion, doubt, or hostility, while those with sincere and receptive hearts are guided even more firmly. In this way, Satan’s attempts expose the true spiritual condition of people, revealing who sincerely seeks the truth and who persists in falsehood.",
  22054: "So that those who have been given knowledge may know that this ˹Qur’an˺ is the truth from your Lord, and so they may believe in it and their hearts may humbly submit to it. Indeed, Allāh guides those who believe to a straight path.",
  22055: "Yet those who disbelieve will continue in doubt about it ˹the Qur’an˺ until the Hour comes upon them suddenly or the punishment of a barren Day overtakes them. [33]\\n\\nFootnotes: [33]. The phrase refers to a time after which there will be no night — understood by many scholars as the Day of Resurrection. Unlike worldly days that alternate with night and offer cycles of rest and renewal, that Day will usher in a final and unchanging reality.\nThe description of it as a “barren Day” conveys a powerful image. It is a day stripped of relief, joy, or hope for those who rejected faith. Just as barren land produces no crops, that Day will yield no new opportunity for repentance or rectification. Deeds will have ended, choices will have been sealed, and the time for action will have passed.\nMost commentators interpret the term as referring to the Day of Judgment, called “barren” because no good can be produced then, and no repentance will benefit once the Final Hour has arrived. Others explain that it may also include severe worldly punishments — events so overwhelming that they leave no escape, comfort, or avenue of recovery.\nIn either case, the expression underscores finality: a moment when the consequences of belief or disbelief become irreversible.",
  22056: "Sovereignty on that Day will belong to Allāh alone. He will judge between them. Then those who believed and did righteous deeds will be in Gardens of Bliss.",
  22057: "But those who disbelieved and denied Our revelations—for them will be a humiliating punishment.",
  22058: "As for those who emigrated in the cause of Allāh and were then killed or died—Allāh will surely grant them a goodly provision. Indeed, Allāh is the best of providers.",
  22059: "He will surely admit them to an entrance with which they will be well pleased ˹i.e., Paradise˺. And Allāh is surely All-Knowing, Most Forbearing.",
  22060: "That is so. Whoever retaliates only in proportion to the harm he has suffered, and is then wronged again—Allāh will surely help him. Indeed, Allāh is surely Oft-Pardoning, All-Forgiving.",
  22061: "That is because Allāh causes the night to merge into the day and the day to merge into the night, and indeed Allāh is All-Hearing, All-Seeing.",
  22062: "That is because Allāh is the Absolute Truth, while whatever they invoke besides Him is falsehood. And Allāh is the Most High, the Most Great.",
  22063: "Do you not see that Allāh sends down water from the sky, and the earth becomes green? Indeed, Allāh is Most Subtle, All-Aware.",
  22064: "To Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth. And indeed, Allāh is the Self-Sufficient, the Praiseworthy.",
  22065: "Do you not see that Allāh has subjected to you whatever is on the earth and the ships sailing on the sea by His command? He holds back the sky from falling upon the earth except by His permission. Indeed, Allāh is surely Kind and Merciful to humankind.",
  22066: "He is the One who gave you life, then He will cause you to die, and then He will bring you back to life. Indeed, man is truly ungrateful.",
  22067: "For every community We have appointed rites they are to observe [34]. So let them not dispute with you about this matter. And call ˹all people˺ to your Lord. Indeed, you are surely upon straight guidance.\\n\\nFootnotes: [34]. The term “rites” (manāsik) refers to the prescribed forms of worship and ritual practices appointed for each religious community. While the specific details of these rites and laws differed among earlier peoples, all prophets came with the same essential message: to worship the One God alone, to do good, and to shun evil. The variation among communities therefore lay in their particular legal frameworks, not in the core principles of monotheistic faith and righteousness.",
  22068: "But if they dispute with you, then say, “Allāh knows best what you do.”",
  22069: "Allāh will judge between you on the Day of Resurrection concerning that over which you used to differ.",
  22070: "Do you not know that Allāh knows whatever is in the heavens and the earth? Indeed, all of that is recorded in a Book. Indeed, that is easy for Allāh.",
  22071: "Yet they worship, besides Allāh, that for which He has sent down no authority and of which they have no knowledge. The wrongdoers will have no helper.",
  22072: "When Our clear verses are recited to them, you recognize disgust on the faces of those who disbelieve—they almost attack those who recite Our verses. Say, “Shall I inform you of something worse than that [35]? The Fire! Allāh has promised it for those who disbelieve. And what a miserable destination.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [35]. Than the rage you feel against the believers. This describes how the disbelievers showed open anger and aggression when hearing the verses. Their hostility almost escalated into physical attack, but what awaits them—if they persist—is far worse: the punishment of the Fire.",
  22073: "O people, a parable is presented—so listen to it carefully: Those you invoke besides Allāh cannot create even a fly, even if they were all to gather together for it. And if the fly were to snatch something away from them, they could not recover it from it. How weak the seeker and the one sought!\n[36]\\n\\nFootnotes: [36]. The phrase “the seeker and the one sought” captures the utter powerlessness of both parties in idol worship. The “seeker” refers to the worshipper who calls upon false deities, while the “one sought” refers to the idol or object of worship. The parable exposes the futility of such devotion: the worshipper is weak in relying on what cannot benefit him, and the idol is weak in its complete inability to create, protect, or respond. Scholars highlight that the expression is deliberately concise and emphatic, underscoring the absolute impotence of both the worshipper and the worshipped besides Allāh.",
  22074: "They have not esteemed Allāh as He ought to be esteemed [37]. Indeed, Allāh is truly All-Strong, All-Mighty.\\n\\nFootnotes: [37]. The expression “they have not esteemed Allāh as He ought to be esteemed” is an emphatic Qur’anic phrase denoting a failure to properly recognize, honor, and revere Allāh as His true majesty deserves. Scholars explain that this includes both deficient belief and deficient obedience. Foremost among its manifestations is directing acts of worship to others besides Him—such as the veneration of Jesus and his mother Mary in ways that attribute to them divine qualities, or the devotion of idolaters to their idols and intermediaries. The wording therefore conveys not merely lack of respect, but a profound underestimation of Allah’s absolute uniqueness, sovereignty, and greatness.",
  22075: "Allāh chooses messengers from the angels and from among the people. Indeed, Allāh is All-Hearing, All-Seeing.",
  22076: "He knows whatever is before them and whatever is behind them. And to Allāh all matters are returned.",
  22077: "O believers, bow down, prostrate yourselves, worship your Lord, and do good so that you may succeed.",
  22078: "Strive in the cause of Allāh with the striving due to Him. He has chosen you and has not placed upon you any hardship in the religion—the faith of your forefather Abraham. It is Allāh who named you Muslims before and in this ˹Qur’an˺ as well, so that the Messenger may be a witness over you and that you may be witnesses over humanity [38]. So establish prayer, give alms, and hold firmly to Allāh. He is your Protector—what an excellent Protector and what an excellent Helper!\\n\\nFootnotes: [38]. Once, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ was sitting with a group of his companions when two funeral processions passed by. For each one, the Prophet asked who the deceased was. They named the first man and said he was someone who loved Allāh and His Messenger, was eager to obey Allah’s commands, and kept away from sin. They described him as a righteous, pious man whose faith was unquestionable.\nThen the second funeral passed. When they named the deceased, they added that he had been a man of bad character—abusive, neglectful of his religious duties, and careless about his behavior.\nAfter hearing their descriptions, the Prophet ﷺ commented on both with the same words: “He has secured it.”\nThe companions were surprised and asked, “What has each of them secured?”\nThe Prophet ﷺ replied that the man they praised had secured Paradise, while the man they spoke critically of had secured Hell. Then the Prophet ﷺ added:\n“You (the righteous believers) are Allah’s witnesses on earth.”",
  23001: "Successful indeed are the believers [1],\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. The word translated as “successful ” (aflaḥa) signifies true and lasting success — attaining one’s ultimate goal, being saved from loss, and securing eternal well-being. Success is not measured by wealth, status, or worldly achievement, but by faith, righteousness, and salvation in the Hereafter.\nThe “believers” are those who affirm the oneness of Allāh, accept His messengers, and live in conscious obedience to Him. Their faith is not merely verbal; it is reflected in their hearts, their actions, and their character. The verses that follow describe their qualities: humility in prayer, avoidance of vain talk, commitment to charity, guarding their chastity, fulfilling trusts, and maintaining their prayers. Belief, therefore, is both inward conviction and outward conduct.\nThe verse opens with strong emphasis, declaring with certainty that such people have already attained success. Their prosperity is present in the peace and guidance that faith brings, and future in the eternal reward promised to them.\nThus, the Qur’an redefines success: it is not the accumulation of the world, but alignment with the divine purpose. The truly successful are those whose faith shapes their lives and secures their Hereafter.",
  23002: "those who are humbly submissive in their prayers. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. That is they pray with humility, focus, and presence of heart—fully aware before Allāh. Attending congregation and praying with all solemnity and full submissiveness strengthens one’s connection with Allāh. Prayer is about expressing love, gratitude, hope, love, fear to Allāh and finding meaning in life’s challenges and peace.",
  23003: "and those who turn away from vain talk [3],\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. Idle talk refers to futile speech, obscenity, gossip, falsehood, disputes without benefit, and anything that distracts the heart from remembrance of Allāh.\nBelievers guard their time and speech, avoiding words that bring no good—choosing instead what uplifts, benefits, or draws them closer to Allāh.\nLet no bad words proceed out of your mouth, but that which is good, that builds, encourages, and strengthens others.",
  23004: "and those who pay the prescribed charity. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. I.e., Zakat, the obligatory alms. \nIt is the mandated purification of wealth, given annually to specified categories of recipients, and considered one of the pillars of Islam. Muslims are obligated to pay 2.5% of the total value of their wealth less any liabilities in Zakat provided they reach the Nisab. The Nisab is the minimum amount that a Muslim must have before being obliged to pay Zakat. The Nisab was set by the Prophet Muhammad at a rate equivalent to 85 grams of gold or 609 grams of silver or its equivalent in money, taking into consideration the one lesser value will be the Nisab either the silver or the gold. Zakat is taken from the rich and given to the poor. Whoever cares for the poor lends to Allāh, who will pay back the sum in full. Giving to the poor, is another way someone can express their faith and is a way in which they can put “love for your brother as you love for yourself ” into action. It is unselfishly wishing others to be happy; to be delighted; to offer our affection and help freely without wanting anything in return. Eligible Muslims pay Zakat once a year, and it is due as soon as one lunar (Islamic) year passed.\nGiving zakah trains the believer’s heart away from greed, supports the vulnerable, and affirms gratitude for Allah’s provision.",
  23005: "and those who guard their private parts, [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. This is the command of Allāh—Most High, Lord of Majesty and Perfection—that you safeguard yourselves from faḥishah (sexual immorality), and that each soul learn to restrain its own limbs, governing the body with honor, dignity, and noble discipline, not surrendering it to the blind urges of desire as do the peoples who know not Allāh nor fear His reckoning.\nFor Allāh has not summoned His servants to impurity, corruption, or the pursuit of lusts, but to purification, righteousness, and exalted conduct. Thus, let none among you transgress the limits of his Lord in this matter, neither violating the honor of another nor betraying the trust of his own soul. Whoever dares to disregard this command, let him know that he has not merely disregarded man, nor violated a social custom, but has opposed Allāh Himself, the One who created him, sustained him, and commanded him with wisdom.\nChastity is the dignity of the body, the ornament of character, the fortress of modesty, the tranquility of homes, the source of purity, and the light of both intention and deed. Through it, the believer safeguards his honor, protects his soul from disgrace, and preserves the sanctity of others. It is by chastity that hearts remain sound, families remain whole, societies remain upright, and the path to Allāh remains clear.\nThus, the believers guard their private parts, confining their desires within the limits made lawful by Allāh, seeking thereby His Pleasure and fearing His Wrath. They honor their own purity as a trust from their Lord, just as they honor the dignity of His servants—male and female alike. In this lies the perfection of faith, the refinement of manners, and the path to salvation in this world and the next.",
  23006: "except with their wives or those whom their right hands possess—for then they are free from blame;",
  23007: "But whoever seeks beyond that—such are the transgressors.",
  23008: "And those who are faithful to their trusts [6] and their covenants. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6].The word amaanah - trust - is general in meaning and encompasses all the duties of Islam. Everything that Allāh has enjoined upon His servant is an amaanah (trust), and the person must carry them out in the fullest sense. That also includes things that are entrusted to one by people, such as wealth, secrets and the like. The individual must pay attention to both matters and fulfill both trusts. The one who fulfills his duty towards Allāh and towards people will be rewarded, but the one who neglects his duty towards Allāh and towards people deserves punishment.\n[7]. Promise, pledge. Whenever a person makes a promise, he or she must fulfill it; if he or she commits him/herself to something, whether it concerns other people or Allāh the Almighty, and then falls short of delivering on it, that is considered a breach of the pledge. The righteous believers never break their covenants, which is rather typical of immoral people and hypocrites. Islam warns against breaching covenants, even with enemies, unless they breach first. Treachery is a grave major sin, particularly when it comes from a person who is in a position of authority. That is because his infringement harms many people and he is not forced to do that because fulfilling it instead is within his capacity. There is abundant proof indicating the obligation of fulfilling covenants and the prohibition of breaching it. Keeping one’s word is a hallmark of true faith; every promise is treated as a sacred trust before Allāh.",
  23009: "and those who carefully maintain their prayers. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. By offering them at their proper times with consistency and care.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ was asked, \"What deeds are the best?\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"(1) To perform the (daily obligatory) prayers at their (early) stated fixed times, (2) to be good and dutiful to one's own parents, (3) and to participate in Jihad in Allah's Cause.\"",
  23010: "Those are indeed the inheritors,",
  23011: "who will inherit al-Firdaws [9]; they will abide therein forever.\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. Al-Firdaws is the highest and most excellent level of Paradise, described as the loftiest garden and the ultimate reward for the righteous.",
  23012: "Indeed, We created man [10] from an extract of clay.\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. The phrase “man” here refers to the human being in general, beginning with the creation of Adam from clay. The term “extract” (sulālah) denotes something drawn out or refined from a substance, indicating the origin of human creation from earthly material.",
  23013: "Then We placed him as a drop of fluid in a secure resting place ˹the womb˺. [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. The “drop of fluid” (nuṭfah) refers to the fertilizing fluid from which human development begins (the zygote stage). The “secure resting place” (qarār makīn) is understood to be the womb, where the embryo is safely sustained and developed by God’s design.",
  23014: "Then We made the drop into a clinging form, then We made the clinging form into a lump of flesh, then We made the lump into bones, and We clothed the bones with flesh. Then We brought him forth as another creation. So blessed is Allāh—the Best of creators. [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. The stages described—nuṭfah (drop), ʿalaqah (clinging form), and muḍghah (lump)—outline successive phases of early human development. Scholars understood the final phrase “another creation” to refer to the distinct emergence of the fully formed human being, often associated with the breathing of the soul.\n“So blessed is Allāh, the best of creators” is an expression of exaltation and awe, affirming the perfection and majesty of Allāh in His act of creation. The phrase “blessed” (tabāraka) conveys abundance in goodness, greatness, and enduring perfection—declaring Him far above any deficiency or limitation.\nDescribing Him as “the best of creators” does not imply that others create in the same absolute sense, but rather distinguishes His divine act of creation—bringing into existence from nothing—with complete power, wisdom, and precision. Any human “creation” is merely shaping or assembling what He has already brought into being, whereas Allāh alone is the true Originator.\nThis statement invites reflection upon the complexity, beauty, and harmony of creation as signs of His unmatched skill and wisdom. It calls the believer to recognize His supreme authority, to glorify Him, and to respond with gratitude and humility before the One whose creative power is perfect and without equal.",
  23015: "Then after that, you will surely die.",
  23016: "Then indeed, on the Day of Resurrection, you will be raised.",
  23017: "And We have certainly created above you seven pathways [13], and We are never unaware of the creation.\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. The “seven pathways” (sabʿa ṭarāʾiq) is commonly understood to refer to the seven heavens layered above the earth.",
  23018: "And We send down water from the sky in due measure, then We settle it in the earth—and surely We are able to take it away.",
  23019: "So with it We produce for you gardens of date palms and grapevines—in them you have abundant fruits, and from them you eat.",
  23020: "And ˹We produce˺ an olive tree that springs forth from Mount Sinai, which yields oil and a relish for those who eat.",
  23021: "Indeed, there is a lesson for you in the cattle. We give you to drink from what is in their bellies, and you derive many benefits from them, and from them you eat.",
  23022: "And upon them and upon the ships you are carried. [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. The verse draws attention to the great means of transportation granted by Allāh as blessings for humanity. On land, animals such as camels and livestock have long carried people and their burdens across vast distances. At sea, ships enable travel and trade across oceans — made possible by the natural laws of buoyancy, wind, and balance that Allāh has placed within creation.\nBy extension, this blessing includes all other means of transport developed through the knowledge and abilities Allāh has granted humankind. Travel through the air by aircraft, and modern forms of land and sea transport, are likewise manifestations of divine mercy. Though humans design and build these vehicles, the materials, physical laws, intelligence, and resources that make them possible originate from Allāh.\nThus, whether journeying across deserts, sailing through the sea, or flying through the sky, humanity moves by means that are ultimately gifts from the Creator. What may appear as human achievement is, at its foundation, facilitation from Allāh — signs of His mercy and provision for mankind.",
  23023: "Indeed, We sent Noah to his people, and he said, “O my people, worship Allāh; you have no deity other than Him. Will you not then fear ˹Him˺ and shun evil?”",
  23024: "The chiefs of those who disbelieved among his people said, “This is nothing but a man like yourselves. He only wishes to gain superiority over you. Had Allāh willed, He could have sent down angels. We have never heard of such a thing among our forefathers.”",
  23025: "He is nothing but a man possessed, so wait and watch him for a while.\" [15]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. The accusation that he was “possessed” reflects a common reaction of disbelieving communities toward their prophets. When unable to refute the message itself, they resorted to attacking the messenger’s character. By claiming madness or possession, they sought to discredit him and discourage others from listening.\nThe Arabic phrase “bihi jinnah” literally suggests that there is some form of madness in him — often implying influence from unseen forces. In pre-Islamic culture, extraordinary speech or behavior was sometimes attributed to demons or jinn (spirits). Rather than acknowledging divine revelation, they attempted to explain it away as psychological disturbance or supernatural possession.\nSuch accusations reveal not weakness in the prophet, but resistance in those who heard him. Throughout the Qur’an, prophets are described as people of sound mind, integrity, and moral excellence. The charge of madness is shown to be a strategy of denial — a way of avoiding serious engagement with the truth being presented.",
  23026: "Noah said, “My Lord, grant me help, for they have denied me.”",
  23027: "So We inspired him, ˹saying,˺ “Build the Ark under Our watchful Eyes and according to Our revelation. Then when Our command comes and the oven bursts forth, take on board a pair of every kind and your family—except those against whom the decree has already been passed. Do not plead with Me concerning those who have wronged, for they will surely be drowned.”",
  23028: "Then when you and those with you have settled in the Ark, say, \"All praise is due to Allāh, who has saved us from the wrongdoing people.\"",
  23029: "And say, “My Lord, grant me a blessed landing place, for You are the Best of accommodators.”",
  23030: "Indeed, in this are signs, and indeed We are ever testing.",
  23031: "Then We raised up after them another generation ˹the people of ʿĀd˺.",
  23032: "So We sent among them a messenger from themselves ˹Hūd, saying,˺ “Worship Allāh; you have no deity other than Him. Will you not then fear ˹Him˺ and shun evil?”",
  23033: "But the prominent leaders of his people—those who disbelieved, denied the meeting of the Hereafter, and whom We had given luxury in the life of this world—said, “This is nothing but a mortal like yourselves. He eats what you eat and drinks what you drink.",
  23034: "If you follow a mere mortal like yourselves, then you will surely be losers.",
  23035: "Does he promise you that when you have died and become dust and bones, you will indeed be brought forth?",
  23036: "Far-fetched, utterly far-fetched is what you are being promised!",
  23037: "There is nothing but our worldly life: we die and [others are born and] live, and we will not be resurrected.",
  23038: "He is nothing but a man who has fabricated a lie against Allāh, and we will not believe in him.",
  23039: "Hūd said, “My Lord, help me, for they have denied me.”",
  23040: "He said, “In a little while, they will surely become regretful.”",
  23041: "So the Blast seized them in all justice, and We made them like plant stubble—so away with the wrongdoing people.",
  23042: "Then We raised up after them other generations.",
  23043: "No nation can advance its term, nor can they delay it.",
  23044: "Then We sent Our messengers in succession. Whenever a messenger came to a nation, they rejected him. So We made some of them follow others ˹in destruction˺ and made them mere tales of the past. So away with a people who do not believe.",
  23045: "Then We sent Moses and his brother Aaron with Our signs and a clear authority.",
  23046: "To Pharaoh and his chiefs, but they acted arrogantly and were a people given to self-exaltation. [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. That is, they showed arrogance toward their Lord—rejecting His command, denying His signs, and exalting themselves above the rank of His messengers. Their pride led them to consider themselves too great to follow Moses, even though he came to them with clear proofs and gentle admonition. Such was their conceit that they viewed obedience as beneath them and the truth as inferior to their own imagined greatness.",
  23047: "They said, “Should we believe in two mere mortals like ourselves, while their people are subservient to us?”",
  23048: "So they denied them both and were among those who were destroyed.",
  23049: "We certainly gave Moses the Scripture so that they might be guided.",
  23050: "And We made the son of Mary—ʿĪsā [Jesus]—and his mother a sign, and We sheltered them both on a high, elevated ground, having a place of rest and a flowing spring. [17]\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. That is, Allāh made ʿĪsā (Jesus) and his mother Mary a Sign for all people—His creating Jesus without a father, by His Word, ‘Be,’ and he was; and His protecting Mary from the slander of her people. Their very existence is a manifest proof of His perfect Power and Sovereign Will. The “high ground” is said by many early exegetes to be a blessed, elevated place endowed with calm, fertile soil and a running spring—some identified it as Jerusalem, others as Damascus, and still others as a location near Bethlehem. Allāh knows best.",
  23051: "O Messengers, eat from the lawful and wholesome things and do righteous deeds. Indeed, I am fully aware of all that you do. [18]\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. This verse establishes a foundational principle: purity in provision is closely tied to righteousness in action. The command to “eat from what is good and lawful” (ṭayyibāt) refers not only to food that is lawful (ḥalāl), but also to what is wholesome, clean, and ethically obtained. Spiritual integrity begins with lawful sustenance.\nBy addressing the messengers directly, the verse honors their elevated status while also demonstrating that even the most righteous servants of Allāh are commanded to uphold moral discipline in both livelihood and conduct. The linkage between lawful consumption and righteous deeds teaches that outward actions and inward spirituality are interconnected.\nThe closing statement — “I am fully aware of all that you do” — serves as both reassurance and warning. Allāh’s knowledge encompasses every deed, intention, and source of provision. Nothing escapes His awareness. Thus, purity in what one consumes and sincerity in what one does are essential elements of a life lived in conscious accountability before Him.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ described a man on a long journey—dusty, disheveled, and raising his hands toward the sky as he calls, “O Lord, O Lord.” Yet, the Prophet explained, this man’s supplication is unlikely to be answered because his food, his drink, and his clothing are all from unlawful sources, and his body has been nourished with what is prohibited. The Prophet then remarked: How, then, can his prayer be answered?\nThis teaches that consuming what is unlawful drains the strength of one’s supplication and weakens its impact.",
  23052: "Indeed, this religion of yours is one unified religion, and I am your Lord—so fear Me alone.",
  23053: "But they broke their unity, dividing into factions, each party rejoicing in what it has. [19]\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. That is, the people who had been commanded to unite upon one religion and a single way—especially the followers of the Messiah—broke into divisions after clear guidance had come to them. Each group adhered to its own scriptures and claims: some, like Jews, asserted exclusive adherence to the Torah while rejecting the Gospel and the Qur’an; others, like Christians, claimed adherence to the Gospel while denying the Criterion (the Qur’an). Thus they divided into factions, sects, creeds, and customs, turning the originally unified religion into competing religious identities. Every party became satisfied and self-admiring in what it possessed of beliefs and opinions, assuming itself to be upon the truth while neglecting their reality. This fragmentation arose when they departed from the pure message of Islām—the straight path Allāh sent to unite hearts upon His obedience—and instead followed their desires rather than divine guidance. The verse therefore warns against sectarianism in matters of religion and calls to unity upon the true religion of God—Islam—and adherence to His final Messenger, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ.",
  23054: "So leave them in their confusion for a while.",
  23055: "Do they think that, by what We provide them of wealth and children— [20]\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. Allāh declares that these nations originally had one unified religion—a single path of truth, guidance, and devotion to Him alone and that is Islam. All the Prophets were sent with the same core message: worship Allāh, obey Him, and uphold righteousness. Unity in Islamic faith was the natural state of humanity. But over time, people fractured this unity. They introduced divisions, disagreements, and innovations, forming sects and factions. These included the various groups found among the Jews, the Christians, and other communities who split their religion into competing interpretations and doctrines. Instead of returning to divine guidance, each group became pleased with what it held, even when it contradicted the original message.\nBecause of this stubbornness and self-satisfaction, Allāh tells the Prophet to leave them in their confusion for a while. This is not abandonment, but a statement of divine wisdom: when people insist on error after truth has reached them, they are allowed to wander in the very confusion they chose, until the consequences of their choices become clear.\nYet many of them misread Allāh’s patience and provision. Seeing their wealth, children, and prosperity grow, they assumed it was a sign of divine approval and favor. They thought, “If we were wrong, why would Allāh continue to give us more?” But they failed to realize that worldly abundance is not proof of guidance. Sometimes it is a test, a delay, or even a means by which a person is gradually led to accountability.\nAllāh’s message in these verses is sharp and merciful at the same time:\nUnity is the original path; division comes from human desires.\nConfusion is the natural outcome when people prefer sectarianism over revelation.\nWorldly success does not equal divine pleasure. The reminder is meant to awaken hearts, not condemn them—to show that true guidance lies not in numbers, wealth, or sectarian pride, but in sincere submission to Allāh and the religion He originally revealed, Islam.",
  23056: " We hasten good things for them? No! Rather, they do not perceive. [21]\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. Their misguidance, and that the increase in wealth, children, or worldly pleasure is not a sign of divine favor. Rather, it is a form of a gradual and subtle drawing of them toward destruction, as they become more deluded by worldly comfort and less receptive to guidance.",
  23057: "But those who stand in awe of their Lord are truly fearful,",
  23058: "and those who truly believe in the verses of their Lord,",
  23059: "and those who do not associate anything with their Lord,",
  23060: "and those who give whatever they give [22] while their hearts tremble in awe—knowing that they will return to their Lord,\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. That is, they spend in charity, offer righteous deeds, and perform acts of obedience—yet their hearts remain fearful and humbled, lest their deeds be rejected, or that they may fall short of what Allāh deserves of sincerity and devotion. This trembling of the heart is not due to doubt in Allāh’s promise, nor suspicion of His Mercy, but rather out of profound reverence, modesty, and recognition of their own shortcomings. It is reported from ʿĀʾishah (may Allāh be pleased with her) that when she asked the Prophet ﷺ whether this verse referred to those who steal, commit adultery, and drink wine while fearing their return to Allāh, he replied: “No, O daughter of al-Ṣiddīq. Rather, it refers to those who fast, pray, and give charity—yet they fear that their deeds will not be accepted.” Such is the state of the righteous: combining good works with fear, and obedience with humility, thus perfecting the balance between hope and dread, which is the hallmark of the believers.",
  23061: "They hasten to good deeds, and they are foremost in them.",
  23062: "We never burden a soul beyond its capacity. And with Us is a Record that speaks the truth, and they will not be wronged.",
  23063: "Rather, their hearts are in deep confusion about this ˹Qur’an˺, and they have other evil deeds besides that which they are doing.",
  23064: "Until, when We seize their affluent ones with punishment, they immediately cry out for help.",
  23065: "Do not cry out for help today. Indeed, you will not be helped by Us.",
  23066: "Indeed, My verses were recited to you, but you used to turn back on your heels.",
  23067: "Boasting arrogantly about it ˹the Sacred House˺, spending the night in idle talk while turning away from the truth. [23]\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. That is, they behaved arrogantly toward people without right because of their association with the Sacred House, boasting, “We are its custodians; none can overcome us within it.” They would gather around it at night, engaging in foul and improper speech. Their talk included offensive and contemptuous words regarding this Qur’an. Such was the way of the deniers: turning away from the Qur’an and urging one another to do the same. Because they combined all these vile traits, the punishment rightfully fell upon them. And when it came upon them, they had no helper to support them and no rescuer to save them, and they were reproached for these disgraceful deeds.",
  23068: "Have they not, then, reflected on the Words ˹of Allah˺? Or has there come to them what did not come to their forefathers of old?",
  23069: "Or did they not recognize their Messenger, so they deny him?",
  23070: "Or do they say, \"He is possessed\"? Rather, he has brought them the truth, but most of them are averse to the truth.",
  23071: "Had the Truth followed their lowly desires, the heavens and the earth and all who are within them would certainly have been corrupted. Rather, We have brought them their Reminder, yet they turn away from their Reminder ˹this Qur’an˺.",
  23072: "Or do you ask them for a tribute? But the bounty of your Lord is best, and He is the Best of providers.",
  23073: "And you are surely calling them to a straight path.",
  23074: "But those who do not believe in the Hereafter are deviating from the straight path.",
  23075: "Even if We were to show them mercy and remove whatever hardship afflicted them, they would still persist in their transgression, blindly wandering.",
  23076: "And We have already seized them with punishment ˹as a warning˺, but they did not humble themselves to their Lord, nor did they implore Him in humility.",
  23077: "Until, when We open for them a gate of severe punishment, behold—they are in despair.",
  23078: "It is He who granted you hearing, sight, and understanding—yet how little thanks you give. [24]\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. The faculties of hearing, sight, and understanding are singled out because they are the primary means by which human beings perceive truth and recognize divine guidance. Despite being endowed with these great blessings, many people fail to show proper gratitude to Allāh—either by denying His signs or by not using these faculties in obedience and reflection.",
  23079: "It is He who multiplied you throughout the earth, and to Him you will be gathered.",
  23080: "And it is He who gives life and causes death, and to Him belongs the alternation of night and day [25]. Will you not then understand?\\n\\nFootnotes: [25]. The alternation of night and day is presented in the Qur’an as a clear sign of Allāh’s perfect power and governance over the universe. Through this constant and orderly cycle, human beings are reminded of His ability to regulate time, sustain life, and bring about resurrection. Since Allāh alone possesses such complete authority and control, He alone is worthy of worship without any partners. The sign is therefore meant to awaken reflection, gratitude, and recognition of His Oneness.",
  23081: "Rather, they say the same as what the former people said.",
  23082: "They say, “When we have died and become dust and bones, will we truly be resurrected?”",
  23083: "We have been promised this—we and our forefathers before. This is nothing but ancient tales of the former peoples.",
  23084: "Say, “To whom belongs the earth and whoever is in it, if you truly know?”",
  23085: "They will say, “˹It belongs˺ to Allāh.” Say, “Will you not then take heed?”",
  23086: "Say, “Who is the Lord of the seven heavens and the Lord of the Mighty Throne?”",
  23087: "They will say, \" To Allāh. Say, \"Will you not then fear Him [and shun evil]?\"",
  23088: "Say, “In whose hand is the dominion of everything—and who grants protection while none can protect against Him—if you truly know?”",
  23089: "They will say, “˹It all belongs˺ to Allāh.” Say, “Then how are you so deluded?”",
  23090: "Rather, We have brought them the truth, and they are certainly liars.",
  23091: "Allāh has never taken a son, nor has there ever been any god alongside Him. Had there been, each deity would have taken what it created and some would have sought to dominate others. Glory be to Allāh—far above what they describe.",
  23092: "˹He is the˺ Knower of the unseen and the seen. Exalted is He—far above whatever they associate with Him.",
  23093: "Say, \"My Lord, if You should show me what they are warned of,",
  23094: "My Lord, then do not place me among the wrongdoing people.”",
  23095: "Indeed, We are surely able to show you what We have warned them.",
  23096: "Repel evil with what is best. We know best what they describe.",
  23097: "And say, “My Lord, I seek refuge in You from the incitements of the devils,",
  23098: "And I seek refuge in You, my Lord, lest they be present with me.”",
  23099: "Until, when death comes to one of them, he says, “My Lord, send me back,",
  23100: "So that I may do good in what I left behind.” No! It is only a word he speaks. And before them is a barrier until the Day they are resurrected. [26]\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. The “barrier” (barzakh) refers to the intermediate state between death and resurrection. It signifies that once death arrives, return to worldly life is impossible; the soul remains in this unseen realm until the Day of Resurrection.",
  23101: "So when the Trumpet is blown [27], there will be no kinship between them on that Day, nor will they ask about one another. [28]\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]. The Trumpet refers to the great blast blown by an angel on the Day of Resurrection. At the first blowing, all who are on the earth will perish; then it will be blown a second time, and all will be raised for judgment.\n[28]. On the Day of Resurrection, worldly ties and family loyalties will offer no benefit; each person will be wholly concerned with their own fate, worried and anxious about his own plight.",
  23102: "So those whose scales are heavy ˹with good deeds˺—they are the successful.",
  23103: "But those whose scales are light—they are the ones who have lost their souls, abiding in Hell ˹Jahannam˺ forever.",
  23104: "The Fire will scorch their faces, leaving them grim and disfigured, their lips drawn back [and their teeth gnashing]. [29]\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. A similar depiction of the fate of the wicked appears in the Gospel according to Gospel of Matthew (13:42–43): “And throw them into the fiery furnace. In that place there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth. Then the righteous will shine like the sun in Allāh’s kingdom.” These words are attributed to Jesus (ʿĪsā, peace be upon him) after explaining the Parable of the Weeds, describing the final judgment in which the wicked are cast into punishment while the righteous are honored and made radiant in the kingdom of Allāh. This reflects a theme also emphasized in the Qurʾān concerning the ultimate separation between the people of Paradise and the people of the Fire.",
  23105: "\"Were My verses not recited to you, yet you used to deny them?”",
  23106: "They will say, “Our Lord, our misery overcame us, and we were a people astray.”",
  23107: "“Our Lord, take us out of it; and if we ever return ˹to evil˺, then indeed we would be wrongdoers.”",
  23108: "He will say, “Be despised therein and do not speak to Me.”",
  23109: "Indeed, there was a group of My servants who used to say, “Our Lord, we have believed, so forgive us and have mercy upon us. You are the Most Merciful of those who show mercy.”",
  23110: "But you took them in ridicule, until they made you forget My remembrance, and you used to laugh at them.",
  23111: "Indeed, I have rewarded them this Day for their patience—truly, they are the successful. [30]\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. That is, for their steadfastness upon faith, their endurance of harm in the path of Allāh, and their perseverance upon obedience despite trials and tribulations. Among the greatest forms of patience is to remain firm when one is insulted, mocked, or wronged for the sake of Allāh — for such was the way of the Prophets and the righteous before them. Whoever reflects upon the lives of the Messengers will come to know that they were met with denial, abuse, and slander, yet they bore all of it with forbearance, seeking only the pleasure of their Lord. Thus Allāh Most High honours them on the Day of Resurrection with the glad tidings of victory and salvation, as a recompense for their steadfast hearts, their unwavering trust in Him, and their refusal to abandon the truth despite what befell them.",
  23112: "He will say, “How many years did you stay on the earth?”",
  23113: "They will say, “We stayed a day or part of a day—so ask those who keep count.”",
  23114: "He will say, “You stayed only a little while—if only you had known.”",
  23115: "Did you think that We created you in vain and that you would not be returned to Us ˹for judgment˺? [31]\\n\\nFootnotes: [31]. This verse confronts a fundamental question: did you imagine that you were created without purpose, wisdom, or accountability? Allāh — Majestic and Exalted — is far above creating His servants in vain or neglect. His creation is governed by perfect knowledge and precise wisdom.\nHuman beings were created for a noble purpose: to know their Lord, worship Him alone, obey His commands, and refrain from what He has prohibited. Life is not random, nor is existence meaningless. When a person reflects upon his origin, his faculties of intellect and speech, and the countless signs spread throughout creation, he recognizes that nothing in the universe is without order and intent.\nHow then could man — endowed with reason, guidance, and moral responsibility — be left without return or reckoning? This verse rebukes heedlessness and denies the illusion of purposeless living. It reminds the believer of his true station: that life is a test, death is a passage, and the return to Allāh is certain. On that Day, every soul will be recompensed fully for what it earned — without injustice, without neglect, and without escape.",
  23116: "So exalted is Allāh, the True King. There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him, Lord of the Noble Throne.",
  23117: "Whoever calls upon another god besides Allāh—for which he has no proof—his reckoning is only with his Lord. Indeed, the disbelievers will never succeed. [32]\\n\\nFootnotes: [32]. This verse establishes a foundational principle of divine justice: no belief, no act of worship, and no object of devotion is valid unless supported by clear evidence from Allāh. Since no proof exists for the worship of any being besides Him—neither revelation, nor sound reason, nor innate disposition—such devotion is deemed falsehood and will bring its adherent to account on the Day of Resurrection.\nCalling upon others besides Allāh includes:\nIdolatry in its various forms—invoking idols, saints, ancestors, celestial bodies.\nAttributing divine qualities to created beings, such as knowledge of the unseen, power to benefit or harm, or the right to legislate.\nSeeking deliverance, forgiveness, or answered prayers from anyone besides Allāh.\nThe verse emphasizes:\nA. No evidence exists for any claimant who worships or supplicates to other than Allāh. All such worship is baseless conjecture.\nB. Accountability is certain—each person will stand before his Lord alone, without helper or intercessor except by His permission.\nC. Ultimate failure is the destiny of those who persist in associating others with Allāh, for polytheism nullifies deeds and bars one from salvation.\nThus, the verse serves both as a warning to those who call upon others and a reassurance to the believers that truth is established by evidence, and that all falsehood will perish before the judgment of the Almighty.",
  23118: "Say, “My Lord, forgive and have mercy, for You are the Most Merciful of those who show mercy.”",
  24001: "This is a chapter We have sent down and made obligatory; in it We have revealed clear verses so that you may remember.",
  24002: "The [unmarried] woman and the [unmarried] man guilty of fornication—flog each of them with a hundred lashes [1], and let no compassion for them prevent you from enforcing Allah’s law [2], if you truly believe in Allāh and the Last Day. And let a group of the believers witness their punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. Under Islamic law, the charge of fornication must be proven either by the individual’s own confession or by the testimony of four trustworthy eyewitnesses who actually saw the act taking place.\nAs for married adulterers, the traditional punishment of stoning is established in the Prophetic Sunnah.\nWithin the Bible, the legal basis for capital punishment specifically for adultery appears in several passages.\nIn Leviticus 20:10–12, it states: “If a man commits adultery with another man’s wife— with the wife of his neighbor— both the adulterer and the adulteress must be put to death.”\nSimilarly, Deuteronomy 22:22–24 commands: “If a man is found lying with a woman who is married to another man, both of them shall die…,”\nand further describes stoning in certain cases involving a betrothed virgin.\nThus, the criminalization of adultery and the severe penalties associated with it have historical precedent in both Jewish and early Christian scripture, and in Christian understanding these laws remain part of the divine legislation that Jesus referred to when he said: “Do not think that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets; I have not come to abolish them but to fulfill them.” (Matthew 5:17)\n[2]. This means: Do not allow personal sympathy to lead you to compromise or neglect a ruling established by Allāh, for His law safeguard the wellbeing and moral order of society as a whole.",
  24003: "The fornicator does not marry except a fornicatress or a polytheist woman; and the fornicatress—none marries her except a fornicator or a polytheist man. Such marriages are forbidden to the believers.",
  24004: "As for those who accuse chaste women [or men] of adultery but fail to produce four witnesses—flog them with eighty lashes and do not ever accept their testimony. They are the transgressors.",
  24005: "Except for those who repent afterward and mend their ways; for surely Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  24006: "As for those who accuse their wives of adultery and have no witnesses except themselves: the testimony of one of them shall be four oaths by Allāh that he is indeed telling the truth, [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3] This procedure applies when a husband accuses his wife of adultery but has no witnesses except himself. He must swear four times by Allāh that he is telling the truth, in order that he be spared the prescribed punishment for false accusation.",
  24007: "and the fifth [oath] is that the curse of Allāh be upon him if he is lying.",
  24008: "But the punishment will be averted from her if she testifies four times by Allāh that he is indeed among the liars.",
  24009: "And the fifth [oath] is that the wrath of Allāh be upon her if he is indeed among the truthful. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. When the mutual Curse (liʿān) between the spouses is completed, they are separated permanently, and the child in question is no longer attributed to the husband who initiated the liʿān. The apparent wording of the verses indicates that the specific formula of the oaths is required from both parties, in the prescribed order, without omission or substitution. It also indicates that imprecation is specific to the husband when he accuses his wife (not vice versa), and that resemblance of the child is not considered once imprecation occurs—just as resemblance is not considered where lawful marital presumption (firāsh) applies. Resemblance is only taken into account where no stronger legal presumption exists.",
  24010: "Had it not been for Allah’s grace upon you and His mercy—and that Allāh is Ever-Accepting of repentance, All-Wise—[you would have been punished].",
  24011: "Indeed, those who brought forth the slander [5] are a group from among you. Do not think it bad for you; rather, it is good for you [6]. Each of them will bear the sin he has earned, and the one who assumed the greatest share of it [7] will have a tremendous punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. This slander refers to the false accusation made against ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ.\n[6]. It ultimately proved beneficial, as it distinguished the true believers from the hypocrites, cleared ʿĀ’ishah of all wrongdoing, and brought down revealed guidance on how to deal with such incidents.\n[7]. That is, ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy, the leader of the hypocrites in Madinah at the time.",
  24012: "Why did the believing men and women, when they heard it, not think well of their own and say, “This is an obvious slander”?",
  24013: "Why did they not bring four witnesses for it? Since they did not bring the witnesses, they are—in Allah’s sight—the liars.",
  24014: "Had it not been for Allah’s grace upon you and His mercy in this world and the Hereafter, you would certainly have been touched with a grievous punishment for what you had engaged in.",
  24015: "When you were passing it along with your tongues and uttering with your mouths what you had no knowledge of, thinking it trivial—while in Allah’s sight it was grave. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. Words have the power to bring life or cause harm, and those who use them will experience their effects. What you say can either help or hurt—it matters more than you think.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ emphasized this when he said to Mu'adh ibn Jabal:\n“Shall I not tell you of the foundation of it all?” Then he took hold of his tongue and said, “Restrain this.”\nWhen Muʿādh asked if people would be held accountable for what they say, the Prophet ﷺ replied:\n“Take heed, O Muʿādh! Are people thrown into the Fire on their faces except because of what their tongues have brought forth?”\nLet your speech be good, thoughtful, and full of grace. Speak kindly and wisely, and respond in a way that is appropriate and respectful.",
  24016: "Why did you not, when you heard it, say, “It is not for us to speak of this. Glory be to You! This is a tremendous slander.”",
  24017: "Allāh warns you never to return to anything like this, if you are believers.",
  24018: "Allāh makes clear to you the verses, and Allāh is All-Knowing, All-Wise.",
  24019: "Indeed, those who love that indecency should spread among the believers will have a painful punishment in this world and the Hereafter. And Allāh knows, while you do not know.",
  24020: "Were it not for Allah’s grace upon you and His mercy—and that Allāh is Ever Gracious, Most Merciful—[you would have been ruined].",
  24021: "O believers, do not follow the footsteps of Satan. Whoever follows the footsteps of Satan—then indeed he commands indecency and evil. Had it not been for Allah’s grace upon you and His mercy, none of you would ever have been purified. But Allāh purifies whom He wills [9]. Allāh is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. The Word of God—the Qur’an—keeps our hearts clean by pointing out the areas that require confession, correction, and abandoning of sin. It helps remove the stains that cloud the heart. Both the Qur’an and the Sunnah of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ call us to obey Allāh. A pure heart is, by its very nature, an obedient heart. To obey Allah’s Word sincerely is to obey Him with a heart that is pure. There is a deep connection between obedience and purity. True obedience means doing what is pleasing to Allāh and staying away from idolatry and sin. In our time, idolatry appears in many subtle forms. Alongside the old and familiar types, modern life produces new ones: materialism, greed, excess luxury, obsession with wealth, gadgets, appearance, or lifestyle. Any of these can become an idol—anything that distracts the heart from Allāh and pollutes its purity. We are warned to avoid every object of devotion that can corrupt the heart. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“The lawful is clear and the unlawful is clear, and between them are doubtful matters unknown to most people. Whoever avoids the doubtful matters safeguards his faith and his honor, and whoever falls into them falls into the unlawful. It is like a shepherd who grazes his flock near a restricted sanctuary—they will eventually stray into it. Every king has a sanctuary, and the sanctuary of Allāh is what He has forbidden. There is a piece of flesh in the body: if it is sound, the whole body is sound; and if it is corrupt, the whole body is corrupt. Indeed, it is the heart.”\nThe Prophet ﷺ was once asked, “Who are the best people?” He replied: “Those with a heart that is clean and a tongue that is truthful.” They asked, “We know what a truthful tongue is, but what is a clean heart?” The Prophet ﷺ said: “A heart that is God-fearing and pure, free from sin, wrongdoing, and envy.” They asked, “Who displays such a heart?” He said: “One who holds this world lightly and loves the Hereafter.” They asked, “And who shows that sign?” The Prophet ﷺ said:\n “A believer with good character.”",
  24022: "Let not those among you who are blessed with bounty and ample means swear that they will not give to their relatives, the needy, and those who emigrated in the way of Allāh. Let them pardon and overlook. Do you not love that Allāh should forgive you [10]? For Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. This verse was revealed after the slander against ʿĀ’ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), in which Allāh Himself declared her innocence. Verses descended from the seven heavens affirming that she was free from all wrongdoing.\nAmong those who took part in spreading the false accusation was Mistah ibn Uthāthah, a close relative of Abu Bakr, whom Abu Bakr used to support financially. When Allāh revealed the verses absolving our mother ʿĀ’ishah, Abu Bakr—deeply hurt by Mistah’s involvement—swore that he would no longer maintain him.\nIt was then that Verse 22 of this chapter was revealed, commanding the believers not to withhold charity from relatives, the needy, or the emigrants for Allah’s sake, and instructing them to pardon and overlook, ending with the divine appeal:\n“Do you not love that Allāh should forgive you?” The moment Abu Bakr heard this, he responded with beautiful sincerity:\n “By Allāh, we do indeed want that Allāh should forgive us.”\nAnd he resumed supporting Mistah just as he had before. This example teaches a profound lesson: a person who does not forgive others will not be forgiven. When you forgive those who wrong you, Allāh—who is far more generous—will forgive you. Allāh wants His servants to embody mercy and forgiveness. Forgiving others brings peace to the heart, while holding onto resentment leads only to stress and emotional pain. Forgiveness softens the heart, calms the mind, and even improves one’s physical well-being. In this way, Allāh guides us toward what is spiritually and emotionally healthy.",
  24023: "Indeed, those who falsely accuse chaste, innocent, believing women [11] are cursed in this world and the Hereafter, and they will have a tremendous punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. Although the verse mentions women, the ruling is not limited to them. It also applies to men who are falsely accused of committing unlawful sexual relations. Women are mentioned here because they were the ones targeted in the specific incident behind the revelation, but the principle covers both genders equally. Islam prohibits slander against any innocent person—man or woman—and considers such accusations a major sin that destroys honor, spreads corruption in society, and invites severe consequences both in this world and the Hereafter.",
  24024: "On the Day their tongues, hands, and feet will testify against them for what they used to do.",
  24025: "On that Day Allāh will repay them in full their true due, and they will know that Allāh is the Evident Truth.",
  24026: "Corrupt words belong to corrupt people, and corrupt people are drawn to corrupt words; and good words belong to good people, and good people are drawn to good words [12]. They are free from what others say about them. For them is forgiveness and an honorable provision. [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. Another widely accepted interpretation of this verse is:\n“Evil women are for evil men, and evil men are for evil women; and good women are for good men, and good men are for good women. They are innocent of what others say about them. For them is forgiveness and an honorable provision.”\nThis means that righteous people are far above the slander spoken against them.\n[13]. “The innocent” refers primarily to ʿĀ’ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, as well as anyone pure and upright like her who is falsely accused. In reality, bad words and deeds arise from bad people, and bad people are naturally drawn toward what is corrupt. Likewise, good words and deeds belong to good people, and good people are inclined toward what is pure and righteous.",
  24027: "O believers, do not enter houses other than your own until you seek permission and greet their occupants [14]. That is better for you, so that you may take heed.\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said:\n“Three types of people are under Allah’s protection. If they live, they will be granted sufficient provision; and if they die, they will enter Paradise. The first is the one who, when entering or leaving his home, greets its inhabitants with the greeting of peace—As-salāmu ‘alaykum. The second is the one who goes out to the mosque for the congregational prayer. And the third is the one who goes out to strive in the cause of Allāh. These three are under Allah’s guarantee.”",
  24028: "If you do not find anyone therein, do not enter it until permission is granted to you. And if you are told, “Go back,” then go back—that is purer for you. Allāh is All-Knowing of what you do.",
  24029: "There is no blame on you for entering uninhabited houses [15] in which there is benefit for you [16]. And Allāh knows what you reveal and what you conceal.\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. This refers to public places such as travelers’ inns, guesthouses, lodges, and charitable hostels—places not used as private residences.\n[16]. Meaning any form of benefit found there, such as rest, shelter, storage of belongings, or access to necessary commodities.",
  24030: "Tell the believing men to lower their gaze [17] and guard their private parts. That is purer for them. Indeed, Allāh is All-Aware of what they do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. Lowering the gaze is an act of inner cleansing. It protects the heart from desires that begin with a single look and grow into temptation. When a believer turns his eyes away from what is forbidden—such as looking at a woman with lust who is not his wife—he is guarding the purity of his own soul before he is guarding the honor of others. Every sinful glance plants a seed in the heart, and the heart is the king of the body. If it becomes stained, the whole being becomes stained.\nThis teaching is not new to Islam. The earlier revelation—the Gospel—echoes the same truth. Jesus (peace be upon him) taught his followers that purity begins within: “Whoever looks at a woman with lust has already committed adultery with her in his heart.” (Matthew 5:27–28)\nThus the Qur’an and the previous Scriptures agree that the eyes must be disciplined in order for the heart to remain sound. The believer who trains his gaze to look only at what is pure finds ease, lightness, and serenity within. Lowering the gaze is not a restriction—it is a protection, a shield for the heart, and means of drawing close to Allāh.",
  24031: "And tell the believing women to lower their gaze [18] and guard their private parts, and not to display their adornment except what must [necessarily] appear [19]. Let them draw their veils over their chests, and not reveal their hidden adornment except to their husbands, their fathers, their husbands’ fathers, their sons, their husbands’ sons, their brothers, their brothers’ sons, their sisters’ sons, their women, those whom their right hands possess, male attendants who have no [sexual] desire, or children who are unaware of women’s private aspects. Let them not stamp their feet so as to reveal what they conceal of their adornment. Turn to Allāh in repentance, all of you, O believers, so that you may succeed.\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. This refers to lowering the gaze when in the presence of men who are not close relatives—those with whom marriage is permissible. Modesty of the eyes protects the purity of the heart and preserves respectful boundaries between men and women.\n[19]. That is, the parts of a woman’s appearance that may naturally and unavoidably become visible—such as her outer garments or what appears out of necessity—commonly understood by many scholars to include the face and the palms of the hands. Everything beyond that is to be covered in the presence of non-maḥram men (those with whom marriage is permissible). The hidden adornment is not to be shown except to the categories mentioned in the verse—foremost the husband, who may see from his wife what no one else may. Some of the adornment ordinarily visible in the private domestic sphere—such as the face, neck, hands, and forearms—may be seen by her fathers, her husbands’ fathers, her sons, her husbands’ sons, her brothers, her brothers’ sons, her sisters’ sons, and her Muslim women (according to many scholars, not non-Muslim women), as well as those whom their right hands possess and young children who are not yet aware of women’s private aspects.",
  24032: "Arrange the marriage of the unmarried among you, and the righteous among your bondmen and bondwomen. If they are poor [20], Allāh will enrich them out of His bounty—for Allāh is All-Encompassing, All-Knowing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. The mention of poverty is not meant to discourage marriage; rather, it is meant to remove hesitation. Allāh Himself promises to enrich those who marry, providing for them from His vast bounty. This verse is a reassurance for individuals and families who fear financial strain that Allāh will “compensate them from His riches, for sustenance is in His hand alone.” This verse encourages marriage even in cases of limited means, because marriage brings blessings and expands provision. However, this divine promise is not a call to passivity. Reliance upon Allāh in Islam is always paired with striving. Scholars emphasize that the one who marries must still make the necessary effort toward earning lawful sustenance, trusting that Allāh will bless those efforts and open doors that were previously unseen. True reliance is never to abandon effort, but to do one’s best while recognizing that all provision ultimately comes from Allāh.\nMarriage often becomes a cause for increased provision, because Allāh aids the one who seeks purity, stability, and responsibility through lawful means. \nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“Three are entitled to the help of Allāh: the one who marries seeking chastity, the one who strives in Allah’s cause, and the slave who works to free himself.”\nThis confirms that Allāh supports those who take righteous steps, even when worldly resources appear limited.\nThus, the verse combines two essential truths:\nA. Allāh enriches whom He wills, especially those who marry with sincere intention.\nB. Human beings must take rightful means, seeking sustenance with trust, confidence, and effort.\nThrough this balance of effort and trust, a believer’s life is filled with both responsibility and divine blessing.\nThough they are empty-handed, Allāh will never forsake them. He will never turn them away, He will never abandon them. But He will enrich them from His bounty; for Allāh is Ample-Giving, All-Knowing",
  24033: "Let those who cannot afford marriage remain chaste until Allāh enriches them from His bounty. And those among your bondservants who seek a written contract for their freedom — grant it to them if you know good in them, and give them from the wealth Allāh has given you. And do not compel your slave girls into prostitution [21] if they desire chastity, seeking the temporary gains of worldly life. But if anyone compels them, then indeed, after their compulsion, Allāh is Forgiving and Merciful. [22]\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. This refers to any form of sexual exploitation—whether forcing women into sexual slavery or compelling them into prostitution for financial gain. Islam categorically forbids all such practices, as they violate human dignity, deny a woman’s right to chastity, and treat her as an object of profit.\n[22]. In cases where women are coerced, the sin falls entirely upon the one who compelled them—the exploiter or trafficker—not upon the victims. Allāh, in His mercy, absolves the oppressed of blame and extends His forgiveness and compassion to them.",
  24034: "We have certainly sent down to you clear verses, and examples from those who passed before you, and an admonition for those mindful of Allāh.",
  24035: "Allāh is the Light of the heavens and the earth [23]. The example of His Light [24] is like a niche within which is a lamp; the lamp is within a glass; the glass is as if it were a radiant star, lit from the oil of a blessed olive tree, neither of the east nor of the west [25]. Its oil would almost glow even without being touched by fire. Light upon light. Allāh guides to His Light whom He wills. Allāh sets forth parables for the people, and Allāh is Knowing of all things.\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. Allāh is the One who illuminates the heavens and the earth with His guidance. Through His light, He calls those who are confused, lost, or in spiritual darkness to the clarity of faith. His light removes doubts, sins, blind spots, and obstacles, allowing the heart to see truth as it really is. When Allāh shines His light into a person’s heart, their entire path becomes illuminated. The Qur’an explains that whoever follows Allāh and His Messenger will never walk in darkness but will be given nūr—inner light, guidance, and clarity.\nThrough Allah’s light, the believer finds protection, direction, and stability. Instead of wandering aimlessly, we turn to Him, trusting that He will light the way forward.\n[24]. The parable of Allah’s Light is interpreted by many scholars as referring to faith and the Qur’an within the heart of a believer. When faith settles into the heart, it becomes a radiant lamp; the Qur’an serves as its fuel; and the believer’s purified heart becomes like clear glass—transparent, receptive, and glowing with guidance. This inner light then guides one’s thoughts, actions, and entire way of life.\n[25]. Meaning: the tree from which this pure oil comes is situated in an open, elevated place—such as on a hill—where it receives sunlight from morning until evening. Because it is not shaded by eastern or western barriers, its oil is exceptionally pure and bright, symbolizing the clarity and strength of divine guidance.",
  24036: "[This radiant light is] in houses Allāh has permitted to be raised and in which His Name is mentioned—glorifying Him therein in the morning and the evening—",
  24037: "men whom neither trade nor commerce distract from the remembrance of Allāh, from establishing prayer, or from giving the prescribed charity. They fear a Day when hearts and eyes will be overturned.",
  24038: "So that Allāh may reward them according to the best of what they have done and increase them from His bounty. Allāh provides for whom He wills without measure.",
  24039: " But those who disbelieve—their deeds are like a mirage in a barren plain which the thirsty person thinks is water, until, when he comes to it, he finds nothing, but finds Allāh there, who pays him his account in full. Allāh is Swift in reckoning.",
  24040: "Or their deeds are like layers of darkness in a deep sea, covered by waves upon waves, above which are clouds—darknesses, one above another. When he stretches out his hand, he can hardly see it. Whoever Allāh does not grant light—he has no light. [26]\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. Some of the supplications of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ included the following: “O Allāh, place light in my heart, light in my sight, light in my hearing, light on my right, light on my left, light above me, light below me, light in front of me, light behind me—make light for me.”\nIn this profound supplication, the Prophet ﷺ asked Allāh to fill every part of his being with light—his heart, his senses, and all directions around him. This “light” refers to clarity, guidance, the unveiling of truth, and protection from misguidance. By invoking light for his limbs, his perception, and his surroundings, he sought divine illumination in his thoughts, actions, choices, and circumstances. It is a prayer to live in constant awareness of Allāh, safeguarded from confusion and error, and guided toward what is right in every situation and at every moment.",
  24041: "Do you not see that whoever is in the heavens and the earth glorifies Allāh, and the birds with wings outspread? Each has known its prayer and its glorification. Allāh is All-Knowing of what they do.",
  24042: "To Allāh belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth, and to Allāh is the final return.",
  24043: "Do you not see that Allāh drives the clouds, then joins them together, then heaps them up, after which you see the rain emerging from within them? He sends down hail from mountains of clouds in the sky, causing it [rain] to fall upon whom He wills and turning it away from whom He wills. The flash of its lightning nearly takes away the sight.",
  24044: "Allāh alternates the night and the day. Indeed, in this is a lesson for people of vision. [27]\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]. Meaning that Allāh causes the night and the day to follow one another in perfect succession—lengthening one, shortening the other, and constantly rotating them for the benefit of His creation. This continuous alternation is a sign of His power, wisdom, and precise control over the universe.",
  24045: "Allāh has created every living creature from water. Some of them crawl on their bellies, some walk on two legs, and some walk on four. Allāh creates whatever He wills. Indeed, Allāh is Capable of all things.",
  24046: "We have certainly sent down clear verses. And Allāh guides whom He wills to a straight path.",
  24047: "They say, “We believe in Allāh and in the Messenger, and we obey.” But after that, a group of them turns away. Such are not believers.",
  24048: "When they are called to Allāh and His Messenger so that the Messenger may judge between them, behold a group of them turns away.",
  24049: "But if the right is in their favor, they come to him in submission. [28]\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. The Arabic mudhʿinīn conveys willing compliance and ready submission, often with the nuance of yielding to a judgment one finds favorable. The verse criticizes the hypocrites for accepting the Prophet’s judgment only when it serves their interests.",
  24050: "Is there a sickness in their hearts? Or do they doubt? Or do they fear that Allāh and His Messenger will treat them unjustly? No—rather, they themselves are the unjust.",
  24051: "The only response of the believers, when they are called to Allāh and His Messenger so that the Messenger may judge between them, is to say, “We hear and we obey.” And they are the successful.",
  24052: "Whoever obeys Allāh and His Messenger, and fears Allāh and is mindful of Him—such indeed are the successful.",
  24053: "They swear by Allāh with their most solemn oaths that if you were to command them, they would surely march forth. Say, “Do not swear. Known obedience is [what is required]. Indeed, Allāh is All-Aware of what you do.”",
  24054: "Say, “Obey Allāh and obey the Messenger.” But if you turn away, then his duty is only what he has been charged with, and your duty is what you have been charged with. If you obey him, you will be rightly guided. And the Messenger’s duty is only clear conveyance [of the message].",
  24055: "Allāh has promised those among you who believe and do righteous deeds that He will surely grant them succession on the earth, just as He granted it to those before them; and He will firmly establish for them the religion He has chosen for them; and He will replace their fear with security while they worship Me alone, associating nothing with Me. But whoever disbelieves after this—it is they who are the rebellious. [29]\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. Al-fāsiqūn describes those who deliberately break away from obedience to Allāh. The term encompasses conscious rebellion after clear guidance, such as denying Allah’s favor or refusing to live in accordance with His ordinances. In this context, it underscores the gravity of disbelief after the promise of divine support has been made manifest.",
  24056: "Establish prayer, give the poor-rate, and obey the Messenger, so that you may receive Allah’s mercy.",
  24057: "Do not think that the disbelievers can escape on earth. Their abode is the Fire. Indeed, what a miserable destination.",
  24058: " O believers, let those whom your right hands possess and those among you who have not yet reached puberty ask your permission [to enter] at three times: before the dawn prayer, when you lay aside your clothes at midday, and after the ‘Isha’ prayer. These are three times of privacy for you. There is no blame on you or on them after these [periods] to move about freely, some of you attending to others [30]. Thus Allāh makes His verses clear to you. For Allāh is All-Knowing, All-Wise.\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. Meaning that outside these three private times, there is no blame on either you or them if they come into your presence without asking permission to enter, since daily interaction and movement among one another is natural and unavoidable within a household.",
  24059: "When the children among you reach puberty, let them ask permission [to enter], as those before them asked permission. Thus Allāh makes His verses clear for you. For Allāh is All-Knowing, All-Wise.",
  24060: "As for elderly [menopausal] women [31] who no longer expect to marry, there is no blame upon them if they lay aside their outer garments [32], provided they do not display any adornment. But if they choose to remain modest, it is better for them. Allāh is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [31]. Referring to women who are past the age of child-bearing and no longer expect to marry.\n[32]. Meaning their outer garments—such as a cloak, shawl, or face veil—while still maintaining modesty and without displaying any beauty or adornment.",
  24061: "There is no blame on the blind, nor on the lame, nor on the sick, nor on yourselves if you eat in your own homes [33], or in the homes of your fathers, your mothers, your brothers, your sisters, your paternal uncles, your paternal aunts, your maternal uncles, your maternal aunts, or in the homes whose keys you possess, or in the homes of your friends. There is no sin upon you whether you eat together or separately. But when you enter houses, greet one another with a greeting of peace—a blessed and good greeting from Allāh [34]. Thus Allāh makes His verses clear to you, so that you may understand.\\n\\nFootnotes: [33]. “your own homes” is understood by exegetes to include the homes of one’s children, since a child’s property is considered closely connected to the parent.\n[34]. That is, to greet the occupants by saying as-salāmu ʿalaykum (“peace be upon you”), the greeting of peace taught by Allāh. The phrase “greet one another” reflects the Qur’anic expression that treats the believing community as a single body; thus greeting others is described as greeting yourselves.",
  24062: "The believers are only those who believe in Allāh and His Messenger and who, when they are with him on a collective matter, do not depart until they have sought his permission. Indeed, those who ask your permission—those are the ones who truly believe in Allāh and His Messenger. So when they ask your permission regarding some affair of theirs, grant permission to whom you will among them and ask Allāh to forgive them. Indeed, Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  24063: "Do not treat the call of the Messenger among you as you would the call of one another [35]. Allāh certainly knows those of you who slip away, hiding behind others [36]. So let those who oppose his command beware, lest a trial afflict them or a painful punishment befall them. [37]\\n\\nFootnotes: [35]. Believers are instructed not to address the Messenger as they would one another. That is, he should not be called merely by name (e.g., “O Muhammad”), but with respectful titles such as “O Prophet” or “O Messenger of Allāh.”\n[36]. That is, those who quietly withdraw from the Prophet’s gatherings without permission, concealing themselves behind others. The verse warns against undermining communal discipline and respect for the Messenger’s authority.\n[37]. The verse warns of the serious consequences of opposing the Messenger’s command. Exegetes note that such defiance may result in fitnah—including affliction, turmoil, dissension, or the casting of hypocrisy or polytheism into the heart—or in a painful punishment in the Hereafter.",
  24064: "Unquestionably, to Allāh belongs whatever is in the heavens and the earth. He certainly knows the state you are in [38]. And on the Day they are returned to Him, He will inform them of what they have done. Allāh has full knowledge of all things.\\n\\nFootnotes: [38]. That is, Allāh fully knows the true state of your hearts—whether faith, disbelief, or hypocrisy—as well as the inner condition of your souls and the reality of your conduct, both inwardly and outwardly.",
  25001: "Blessed is the One who sent down the Criterion [1] to His servant [2], so that he may be a warner to all the worlds.\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. Referring to the Qur’an—called al-Furqān (the Criterion) because it distinguishes truth from falsehood, guidance from misguidance, and right from wrong.\n[2]. That is the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, Allah’s devoted servant and messenger through whom the Qur’an was revealed.",
  25002: "He to whom belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth; who has taken no son [3], and has no partner in His sovereignty; and who created everything and determined it with precise measure.\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ narrated that Allāh Almighty said: “The son of Adam denies Me, though he has no right to do so; and he reviles Me, though he has no right to do so. As for his denial of Me, it is his saying: ‘He will not recreate me after He first created me.’ Yet the first creation is no easier for Me than recreating him. And as for his reviling Me, it is his saying: ‘Allāh has taken a son,’ while I am the One, the Eternal Refuge. I neither beget nor am begotten, and there is none comparable to Me.”\nThis sacred narration affirms Allah’s absolute oneness and perfection, and refutes every claim of Him taking a child or partner.",
  25003: "Yet they have taken besides Him gods that create nothing while they themselves are created; they have no power to harm or benefit themselves, nor do they control death, or life, or resurrection.",
  25004: "The disbelievers say, “This Qur’an is nothing but a lie he has fabricated, and other people have helped him with it.” So they have indeed come with an injustice and a falsehood.",
  25005: "And they say, “[These are] legends of the former peoples, which he has had written down, they are dictated to him morning and evening.”",
  25006: "Say, “It was sent down by the One who knows the secret in the heavens and the earth. Indeed, He is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.”",
  25007: "And they say, “What is the matter with this Messenger that he eats food and walks in the marketplaces? Why is not an angel sent down to him to be a warner alongside him?”",
  25008: "Or a treasure is cast down to him [from heaven], or he has a garden from which he eats?” The wrongdoers say, “You follow none but a man bewitched.”",
  25009: "Look how they strike comparisons for you, [O Prophet] [4]—thus they have gone astray and are unable to find a way.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. That is the disbelievers coined false descriptions for the Prophet ﷺ—calling him a sorcerer, a poet, a liar, or someone bewitched—none of which bore any truth, but were only attempts to mislead others and turn them away from the message.",
  25010: "Blessed is He who—if He willed—could grant you, [O Prophet], far better than that: gardens beneath which rivers flow, and palaces.",
  25011: "But they deny the Hour, and We have prepared for whoever denies the Hour a blazing fire.",
  25012: "When it sees them [5] from a distant place, they will hear its furious raging and roaring.\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The verse describes Hell as if it “sees” the disbelievers and reacts with fury. This personification intensifies the imagery, portraying Hell not as a passive place, but as a terrifying reality prepared for those who rejected truth. The language conveys immediacy and inevitability — as though the Fire itself recognizes its inhabitants.\nThe terms translated as “raging” and “roaring” suggest violent anger and a fierce, blazing sound. Exegetes note that such expressions evoke the terrifying noise of a blazing fire — like the explosive crackling and roaring of flames when they surge upward.\nThis vivid description serves as a warning. The punishment is not distant or abstract; it is real, powerful, and overwhelming. The verse awakens the heart by appealing to the senses — hearing its roar even before reaching it — reminding the reader of the seriousness of accountability before Allāh",
  25013: "When they are cast into it in a cramped place—chained together—they will cry out there for destruction.",
  25014: "Do not cry out today for a single destruction—but cry out for many.",
  25015: "Say, “Is that better, or the everlasting Paradise which has been promised the righteous? It is a reward for them and a final destination.”",
  25016: "They will have there whatever they desire, abiding therein forever. That is a promise your Lord has taken upon Himself.",
  25017: "On the Day when He gathers them all together—along with whatever they used to worship besides Allāh—He will say, “Was it you who led these servants of Mine astray, or did they themselves stray from the right path?”",
  25018: "They will say, “Glory be to You! It was never for us to take guardians besides You [6]. But You allowed them—and their forefathers—to enjoy themselves until they forgot the Reminder, and they became a ruined people.” [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. That is they had no right to take us as deities or protective lords, nor to make us objects of worship.\nThis explains why the speakers in the verse declare that it was never appropriate for people to worship them.\n[7]. According to a reported saying of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, \"Whoever dies associating others with Allāh will enter Hell.\"",
  25019: "Thus those you claimed as gods will deny your claims [8], and you will be unable to ward off [the punishment] or find any help. Whoever among you commits iniquity [9], We will make him taste a grievous punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. That is, the false gods or beings they worshipped will disown their worshippers on the Day of Judgment.\nThis explains why the verse says “they deny you in what you say.”\n[9]. That is whoever commits the ultimate wrongdoing—associating others with Allāh —and dies persisting in it.”",
  25020: "We did not send any messengers before you, [O Muhammad], except that they too ate food and walked in the marketplaces. We have made some of you a test for others—will you be patient? Your Lord is All-Seeing.",
  25021: "Those who do not expect to meet Us say, “If only angels were sent down to us, or if only we could see our Lord!” They have become arrogant within themselves and have grown greatly defiant.",
  25022: "On the Day they see the angels [10], there will be no good news on that Day for the guilty, and [the angels] will say, \"[This is] absolutely forbidden to you.\" [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. This refers to the occasions when a person beholds the angels: at the moment of death, in the intermediate state after death, or upon resurrection on the Day of Judgment.\n[11]. The phrase ‘Absolutely forbidden’ is understood as a declaration that entry into Paradise is completely denied to those who reject the Islamic faith. Paradise is reserved only for those who affirm ‘lā ilāha illā Allāh’—meaning, ‘none is worthy of worship except Allāh.’\nTherefore, on the Day the guilty see the angels, they are told that access to mercy, safety, and Paradise is barred to them because they denied Allāh and rejected His messengers—whose final messenger is the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ.”",
  25023: "We will turn to whatever deeds they performed and reduce them to scattered dust. [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. That is, their deeds lacked sincerity and were not grounded in faith in God’s Oneness; consequently, they will be rendered null on the Day of Judgment. Thus the good works of the disbelievers—such as prayer and charity—will not avail them in the Hereafter because of their disbelief and denial of the truth of Islam.",
  25024: "On that Day, the people of Paradise will be in the best abode and the finest resting place.",
  25025: "On the Day the sky will split open with the clouds, and the angels will be sent down in successive descent.",
  25026: "All dominion on that Day belongs to the Most Compassionate [13], and it will be a difficult Day for the disbelievers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. Al-Raḥmān (“the Most Compassionate”) is one of Allah’s names denoting His boundless mercy. The verse declares that on the Day of Judgment all dominion will belong exclusively to Him. While in this world Allāh grants limited authority to kings, rulers, and others, on that Day all such delegated power will cease and His absolute sovereignty will be fully manifest.",
  25027: "On that Day the wrongdoer will bite his hands [in regret], saying, “Oh, if only I had taken the path alongside the Messenger!” [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. The disbeliever regrets this on the Day of Judgment because he realizes—when the truth is unmistakable—that following the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ would have led him to guidance, protection from sin, and ultimately salvation. He sees that rejecting the Prophet’s message caused him to miss the path to Allāh’s mercy, the support of revelation, and entry into Paradise. This regret reflects the recognition that worldly pride, stubbornness, or imitation of misguided companions prevented him from accepting the truth when he had the chance.",
  25028: "Oh, woe to me! Would that I had not taken so- and-so as a close friend.",
  25029: "He truly led me away from the Reminder—the Qur’an—after it had reached me. Satan is ever a betrayer of man.",
  25030: "And the Messenger [15] said, “My Lord, my people have abandoned this Qur’an.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. The “Messenger” here refers to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. His complaint is that his people persisted in abandoning the Qur’an—by refusing to believe in it, listen to it, reflect upon it, act according to it, or convey it to others. Exegetes note that such abandonment includes denying its truth, neglecting its recitation, failing to live by its guidance, or preferring other matters over it despite access to it. The verse therefore contains a serious warning for those who forsake the Qur’an and do not act upon it.",
  25031: "Thus We have assigned for every Prophet an enemy from among the wicked. Yet sufficient is your Lord as Guide and Helper. [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. This emphasizes that opposition to prophets is a recurring pattern in human history; those who persist in wrongdoing naturally resist divine guidance. Despite this hostility, Islamic teaching holds that Allāh alone is fully sufficient to guide, support, and protect His prophets and believers against every form of opposition. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ when he was rejected and persecuted by the deniers, he prayed to His Lord, Allāh, saying: \"To You, my Lord, I complain of my weakness, lack of support and the humiliation I am made to receive. Most Compassionate and Merciful God, Allāh. You are the Lord of the weak, and you are my Lord. To whom do You leave me? To a distant person who receives me with hostility? Or to an enemy You have given power over me? As long as you are not displeased with me, I do not care what I face. I would, however, be much happier with Your mercy. I seek refuge in the light of Your face by which all darkness is dispelled and both this life and the life to come are put in their right course against incurring your wrath or being the subject of your anger. To You I submit (O Allah), until I earn Your pleasure. Everything is powerless without your support\".",
  25032: "The disbelievers say, “Why was the Qur’an not sent down to him all at once?” Thus We revealed it to strengthen your heart, and We have recited it in measured stages. [17]\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. The Qur’ān was sent down over the span of twenty-three years—munajjaman, in separated portions—according to divine wisdom. It descended in response to events, questions, needs, and circumstances faced by the Prophet ﷺ and the early believing community, so that their hearts might be strengthened, their certainty increased, and their faith nurtured step by step. By revealing the Qur’ān gradually, Allāh educated, purified, and disciplined the believers over time, allowing the rulings, admonitions, and spiritual lessons to settle firmly in their souls. This method also served as a continual miracle, linking revelation to living reality and displaying the active providence of the Lord of the worlds.",
  25033: "They never come to you with any argument [18] except that We bring you the truth and the best explanation.\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. This refers to any argument, challenge, or analogy the disbelievers present in an attempt to oppose the Prophet or find fault with the Qur’an.",
  25034: "Those who will be gathered on their faces into Hell will be in the worst position and farthest astray from the right path.",
  25035: "We certainly gave Moses the Scripture and appointed his brother Aaron as a helper alongside him.",
  25036: "We said, “Go, both of you, to the people who rejected Our signs.” Then We destroyed them completely.",
  25037: "And the people of Noah—when they denied the messengers—We drowned them and made them an example for mankind. We have prepared for the wrongdoers a painful punishment. [19]\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. Although the people of Noah directly denied only their own prophet, the Qur’an expresses this as denial of “the messengers” in the plural. This reflects the principle that the prophets share one essential message—the call to God’s Oneness—so whoever rejects one messenger has, in effect, rejected them all. The statement “We made them an example for mankind” means a lasting lesson and warning for others to reflect upon. As indicated elsewhere (69:11–12), the preservation of the Ark and the survival of Noah’s followers were meant to serve as a reminder of Allah’s saving grace and a sign for later generations, that they might remember His favor in rescuing the believers from drowning and making subsequent peoples descend from those who affirmed the truth.",
  25038: "And [We destroyed] ‘Ād, Thamūd, the people of the Rass [20], and many generations in between them.\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. The identity of the “people of the Rass” (Aṣḥāb al-Rass) is not definitively known. The word rass in Arabic can refer to a well, pit, or buried structure. Many commentators held that they were a community who rejected their prophet and destroyed him—some reports say by casting him into a well—after which they were themselves destroyed. Because the Qur’an does not specify their exact location or time, the term primarily serves to recall another historical example of a people who denied the truth and were consequently punished.",
  25039: "To each We presented examples, and each We utterly destroyed.",
  25040: "They have certainly passed by the town [of Sodom] that was rained upon with the rain of evil [of brimstones]. Have they not seen it? Rather, they do not expect resurrection.",
  25041: "When they see you, [O Prophet], they take you only in mockery, saying, “Is this the one whom Allāh has sent as a messenger?”",
  25042: "He would have almost led us astray from our gods, had we not remained steadfast in holding to them. But they will soon know, when they see the punishment, who is most astray from the right path.",
  25043: "Have you seen the one who takes his own desires as his god? Would you then be responsible for him?",
  25044: "Or do you think that most of them even hear or understand [21]? They are but like cattle [22]—no, they are even further astray from the right path.\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. That is they do not truly listen or think for themselves; they simply follow others blindly without reflection or understanding.\n[22]. This comparison refers to cattle or sheep that follow their leaders without awareness or questioning—highlighting the unthinking, unreflective nature of their behavior.",
  25045: "Have you not seen how your Lord stretches out the shadow? If He had willed, He could have made it still. Then We made the sun a guide to it.",
  25046: "Then We gradually draw it back to Us—an easy, gentle drawing-in. [23]\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. That is, the shadow is gradually withdrawn by the rising and movement of the sun. Had Allāh willed, He could have made it fixed and still, unaffected by the sun; rather, He made the sun a sign through which the changing conditions of the shadow are recognized. The shadow therefore contracts little by little as the sun rises—whenever its elevation increases, the shadow diminishes. This phenomenon is among the evidences of Allah’s power and majesty, and that He alone is worthy of worship without partner.",
  25047: "It is He who made the night as a cover for you [24], and sleep for rest, and made the day for rising again [to seek your livelihood].\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. The night is described metaphorically as a “cover” or “garment” because it envelops people with darkness, providing concealment, calm, and protection. Just as clothing covers the body, the night covers the earth, facilitating rest and privacy. The imagery highlights Allah’s mercy in regulating the natural cycle of rest and activity for human well-being.",
  25048: "He is the One who sends the winds as heralds before His mercy, and We send down from the sky water that is purifying.",
  25049: "so that We may revive dead land with it, and give drink with it to what We have created—livestock and many people.",
  25050: "We have certainly varied it [the water] among them so that they may remember [25], but most people refused all except ingratitude.\\n\\nFootnotes: [25]. That is, Allāh varied and distributed the rain among different regions and at different times—sometimes sending abundant rainfall and at other times withholding it—so that people might reflect upon His power and recognize His favor. This alternation serves as a reminder of human dependence upon divine mercy; yet despite these clear signs, most people persist in ingratitude.",
  25051: "Had We willed, We could have sent to every single town a warner. [26]\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. That is, Allāh had the power to send a separate warner to every town or community. Instead, He sent Muhammad ﷺ as a universal messenger to all peoples, thereby increasing both the scope of the message and the responsibility of conveying it. The verse highlights divine wisdom in appointing one final messenger whose call is meant to reach every land.",
  25052: "So do not obey the disbelievers, but strive against them with [this Qur’an]—a great striving. [27]\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]. The phrase “a great striving” (jihādan kabīran) here refers primarily to striving through the Qur’an—by conveying its message, clarifying the truth, and refuting falsehood. In this Makkan context, the command emphasizes intellectual, spiritual, and rhetorical struggle rather than armed conflict. The verse highlights the weighty responsibility of upholding and proclaiming the revelation with perseverance and resolve.",
  25053: "It is He who merges the two seas: one is palatable and sweet, and the other is salty and bitter. And He sets between them a barrier and an inviolable partition.",
  25054: "And He is the One who created the human being from water, then made for him ties of lineage and bonds of marriage. And your Lord is Ever All-Powerful.",
  25055: "Yet they worship besides Allāh that which neither benefits them nor harms them. The disbeliever is ever a helper [of Satan] against his Lord. [28]\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. The Arabic term ẓahīr denotes a supporter, ally, or one who lends backing and assistance. In this context, it describes the disbeliever as effectively aiding falsehood against his own Lord by persisting in disbelief and idolatry. Exegetes explained that such a person becomes a supporter of Satan and the forces of opposition to divine guidance. The wording underscores the gravity of rejecting the truth: by doing so, one is not merely negligent but actively aligned against God’s command.",
  25056: "We have not sent you, [O Muhammad], except as a bearer of good news and a warner. [29]\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: “No one has a greater sense of honor than Allāh, and no one is more willing to accept excuses than Allāh. For this reason, He sent the messengers as bearers of glad tidings and warners. And no one loves to be praised more than Allāh, because of what He has promised of Paradise.” This hadith clarifies that the Prophet’s role as a bringer of good news and a warner is itself a manifestation of divine mercy—giving people both encouragement and warning so they may be rightly guided. Accordingly, the messengers give glad tidings of the immediate and ultimate reward for those who obey Allāh, and they warn of the immediate and ultimate punishment for those who disobey Him. This necessarily entails clarifying the commands and prohibitions through which the good news and the warning are realized.",
  25057: "Say, “I do not ask you for any reward for it—except that whoever wills may take a path to his Lord.” [30]\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. By spending in the way of Allāh.\nThis verse highlights the sincerity of the Prophet ﷺ and the voluntary nature of faith. He declares that he seeks no payment, status, or worldly reward for delivering the message. His mission is not driven by personal gain, but by obedience to Allāh and concern for humanity.\nThe phrase “that whoever wills may take a way to his Lord” emphasizes human responsibility and choice. Guidance is presented clearly, but it is not forced. Each person must decide whether to follow the path that leads to nearness to Allāh. The “way” refers to faith, repentance, obedience, and righteous living — the means by which one draws closer to the Creator.\nThus, the only “reward” desired is that people benefit themselves by choosing guidance. The Prophet’s role is to convey; the decision rests with the individual. This underscores a central Qur’anic principle: accountability is personal, and the path to Allāh remains open to all who sincerely seek it.",
  25058: "Place your trust in the Ever-Living—the One who never dies—and glorify Him with praise. He is sufficient and perfectly aware of the sins of His servants.",
  25059: "[He is the One] who created the heavens and the earth and everything between them in six Days, then rose over the Throne—the Most Compassionate. So ask about Him One who is fully aware. [31]\\n\\nFootnotes: [31]. The phrase “rose over the Throne” (istawā ʿalā al-ʿarsh) is affirmed in the Qur’an as one of the attributes of Allāh in a manner befitting His majesty. The scholars upheld the wording as revealed without likening Allāh to His creation, without inquiring into the manner (bilā kayf), and without denying the meaning. The expression signifies Allah’s supreme authority, dominion, exaltedness and loftiness over creation, while His essence remains utterly unlike anything in the created realm (cf. 42:11). Thus the verse affirms divine transcendence and exaltedness.",
  25060: "Yet when it is said to them, “Prostrate yourselves to the Most Compassionate,” they say, “Who is the Most Compassionate? Shall we prostrate to what you command us?” And it increases them in aversion.",
  25061: "Blessed is He—Allāh —who has placed in the sky [great] constellations and set within it a [radiant] lamp [the sun] and a luminous moon.",
  25062: "He is the One who made the night and the day follow one another—for whoever wishes to take heed or to give thanks.",
  25063: "And the servants of the Most Compassionate are those who walk humbly upon the earth, and when the ignorant address them, they respond with [words of] peace. [32]\\n\\nFootnotes: [32]. That is, they respond with gentle and mild words, avoiding harshness or improper speech.”\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“Allāh loves the one who is righteous, self-sufficient, and unnoticed.”\nThis refers to a person who has strong faith in Allāh, does good deeds purely out of love for Him, avoids sins, does not ask from others but earns their livelihood through honest work, and remains humble—seeking no fame or recognition. Such a person acts sincerely for Allāh alone.\nThe Prophet ﷺ embodied perfect mercy and gentleness. He never sought personal revenge; he only acted when the limits set by Allāh were violated. And whenever he was given a choice between two matters, he would choose the easier of the two—so long as it was not sinful.\nThrough his patience, forbearance, and compassion, the Prophet ﷺ transformed hearts, showing that true strength lies not in retaliation but in mercy, restraint, and patience for the sake of Allāh.",
  25064: "They are those who spend [part of] the night prostrating themselves before their Lord and standing [in prayer]. [33]\\n\\nFootnotes: [33]. That is, they devote a portion of the night to voluntary prayer, standing and prostrating before their Lord in humility and devotion. The verse highlights the virtue of night worship (qiyām al-layl), which the Qur’an and Prophetic teachings repeatedly praise as a mark of sincere faith. Performing prayer at night—when most people are asleep—reflects deeper mindfulness, sincerity, and closeness to Allāh.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: “The best of you are those who, when they are seen, make others remember Allāh —the Mighty, the Majestic.” This hadith illustrates the spiritual effect of sincere worship: those who regularly humble themselves before their Lord—especially in the quiet of the night—develop a purity of character and presence that naturally reminds others of Allāh. Their devotion shapes their manners, speech, and conduct so that even their outward demeanor inspires remembrance of Him.",
  25065: "Those who say, “Our Lord, turn away from us the punishment of Hell. Indeed, its punishment is ever inescapable.”",
  25066: "Indeed, it is miserable as a place of settlement and residence.",
  25067: "Those who, when they spend, are neither wasteful nor miserly, but maintain a balanced course between the two.",
  25068: "Those who do not invoke any other god besides Allāh, nor kill the soul which Allāh has made sacred except with due right, nor commit adultery. Whoever does that will meet the penalty of sin.",
  25069: "The punishment will be doubled for him on the Day of Resurrection, and he will remain therein forever, humiliated.",
  25070: "Except for those who repent, believe, and do righteous deeds—Allāh will replace their evil deeds with good ones. And Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  25071: "Whoever repents and does righteous deeds—he has truly turned to Allāh in sincere repentance.",
  25072: "[They are] those who do not witness falsehood, and when they pass by what is vain or idle, they pass by with dignity. [34]\\n\\nFootnotes: [34]. This verse praises those servants of the Most Compassionate [Allāh] who neither give false testimony nor attend gatherings founded upon falsehood. This includes staying away from assemblies of vain talk, indecent entertainment, and places where Allāh is disobeyed. Among such settings are gatherings of idle amusement, music that arouses heedlessness, mixed entertainment between unrelated men and women, councils of backbiting, slander, mockery, and all gatherings where time is wasted in what has no benefit for one’s faith or character. Instead, when they encounter such scenes, they pass by with dignity—neither participating nor approving—preserving their hearts from corruption and their conduct from what does not befit people of righteousness.",
  25073: "Those who, when reminded of the verses of their Lord, do not fall upon them as though they were deaf and blind.",
  25074: "And those who say, “Our Lord, grant us in our spouses and children the joy of our eyes, and make us exemplary leaders for the righteous.” [35]\\n\\nFootnotes: [35]. The expression “the joy of our eyes” (qurrat aʿyun) is an Arabic idiom denoting deep, abiding happiness and emotional comfort. Literally referring to the cooling of the eyes—an image associated in Arabic with relief, tranquility, and delight—it signifies loved ones whose righteousness brings peace and contentment to the heart. Thus the supplication is not merely for family affection, but for spouses and children whose faith and upright conduct become a source of spiritual joy in this life and the Hereafter.",
  25075: "These will be rewarded with the [lofty] chamber [in Paradise] for their patience, and they will be received therein with greetings and [words of] peace.",
  25076: "Abiding therein forever. Excellent indeed is the place of settlement and residence. [36]\\n\\nFootnotes: [36]. This means that they will dwell in those lofty chambers of Paradise forever, never fearing that their bliss will diminish or that they will be removed from it. The mention of abiding eternally indicates the perfection of Divine favor and complete security from extinction, for the delights of Paradise neither fade nor change. Then Allāh praises that noble abode, saying: ‘What an excellent place to settle and reside.’ That is, how blessed is the place where they find rest and tranquility, and how honorable is the dwelling in which they remain with permanence and dignity. The word settle points to comfort and ease, while reside points to stability, honor, and everlasting enjoyment. All of this is part of the reward prepared for those who were patient, steadfast, and sincere in their devotion.",
  25077: "Say, [O Prophet], “My Lord would not care for you were it not for your supplication [37]. But since you have denied [38], the punishment has become inevitable.” [39]\\n\\nFootnotes: [37]. That is, your faith, worship, and sincere devotion to Allāh alone—this being the true purpose for which you were created. Without turning to Him in supplication and belief, a person has no standing before their Lord.\n[38] That is, you denied the Qur’an and the Messenger ﷺ, rejecting the truth that was sent to guide you.\n[39]  That is, the recompense for your denial in the Hereafter—along with what may befall you in this world. This verse serves as the sūrah’s closing address, exposing the utter loss of those who reject faith. Allāh commands His Prophet to declare that divine regard for people is tied to their worship, supplication, and humble turning to Him. Without faith and devotion, human beings hold no true worth before their Lord; it is through worship that they attain honor, nearness, and divine care.\nBut since the deniers persisted in rejection and refused to call upon Allāh, the verse announces that the punishment has become inevitable—meaning that the consequence of disbelief has now become binding and unavoidable unless they repent. The wording conveys both certainty and imminence of the decree. Thus the sūrah concludes with a final warning: those who turn away from faith cut themselves off from mercy, leaving only the consequence of their own denial.",
  26001: "Ta-Sîn-Mîm. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2: verse 1.",
  26002: "These are the Verses of the Clear Book. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. “The Clear Book” refers to the Great Qur’an — a Scripture distinguished by perfect clarity in its wording, precision in its rulings, and purity in its meanings. It is a Book in which the truth is made manifest, falsehood is unmasked, and matters that are ambiguous or obscure are illuminated. Its guidance is evident for those who seek it, and its proofs stand clear for every heart that reflects.",
  26003: "Perhaps you would grieve yourself to death because they are not believers. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. This verse addresses the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ with gentle reproach and consolation. His compassion for the people was so immense, his longing for their guidance so deep, that he would nearly exhaust himself with sorrow when they rejected faith. The Arabic expression “bākhiʿun nafsaka” conveys the image of overwhelming grief—almost to the point of harming oneself—out of concern that they persist in disbelief.\nYet Allāh reminds him that guidance is solely in His Hand. The duty of the Messenger is to convey the truth with clarity and patience; as for the hearts of people, they are under the dominion of the Almighty. Thus, this verse teaches:\nThe extraordinary mercy and tenderness of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, whose compassion extended even to those who opposed him. The Prophet’s purity of concern—not for worldly gain, but for their salvation in the Hereafter.\nA divine reminder that one must not be crushed by sorrow over what lies beyond one’s control, for guidance is granted only by Allāh’s will.\nIt is a lesson for all callers to truth: deliver the message with sincerity and wisdom, but do not allow grief to consume you over those who choose the path of heedlessness.",
  26004: "If We willed, We could send down upon them a sign from the sky to which their necks would remain humbly bowed in submission.",
  26005: "No new reminder comes to them from the Most Compassionate except that they turn away from it [in aversion].",
  26006: "So they have certainly denied [this Qur’an]; but soon there will come to them the news of what they used to mock.",
  26007: "Do they not look at the earth and see how many noble pairs We have caused to grow therein? [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. The phrase “noble pairs” (zawj karīm) refers to the many fine and beneficial varieties of plants that Allāh causes to grow from the earth. The word zawj (pair) in Qur’anic usage often denotes kinds, species, or complementary counterparts, while karīm conveys the meanings of noble, excellent, or abundant in benefit and beauty. The expression thus highlights both the diversity and the inherent goodness of the vegetation produced by divine power—serving as a sign of Allah’s wisdom, provision, and creative mastery.",
  26008: "Indeed, in that is a sign. Yet most of them are not believers.",
  26009: "And indeed, your Lord—He is the All-Mighty, the Most Merciful.",
  26010: "And [remember] when your Lord called Moses, [saying], “Go to the wrongdoing people,",
  26011: "The people of Pharaoh. Will they not fear Allāh [and shun evil]?\"",
  26012: "He said, “My Lord, indeed I fear that they will deny me,",
  26013: "And my chest tightens, and my tongue does not flow freely, so send for Aaron [to assist me].",
  26014: "And they have a charge [of murder] against me, so I fear they may kill me.”",
  26015: "He said, “No. Go, both of you, with Our signs. Indeed, We are with you, listening.”",
  26016: "So go to Pharaoh and say, “Indeed, we are messengers of the Lord of all worlds,",
  26017: "Send with us the Children of Israel.”",
  26018: "Pharaoh said, “Did we not raise you among us as a child, and you remained among us many years of your life?",
  26019: "And you committed the deed you did, and you are truly ungrateful.”",
  26020: "He said, “I did it then, when I was among those astray [and unaware of Allah’s guidance].”",
  26021: "So I fled from you when I feared you. Then my Lord granted me wisdom and made me one of the Messengers.",
  26022: "Is this the favor you remind me of—while you have enslaved the Children of Israel? [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. When a person lives among you as a stranger or outsider, do not wrong them nor treat them with harshness. Rather, the one who seeks refuge or residence in your land is to be honored, protected, and shown kindness just as you would show to one of your own.\nTreat them with fairness, dignity, and compassion, for the foreigner is not to be made to feel inferior nor unsafe. Honor them as you honor your own people, and show them goodwill as you would hope to receive if you were in their place.\nThis is among the noble manners that Allāh commands: showing mercy to the weak, justice to all, and goodness to every soul that enters your company.\nThus, let none among you resemble Pharaoh—who exalted himself in pride, enslaved the Children of Israel, and oppressed those whom he was commanded to treat with justice. Rather, be among those who uphold dignity, show mercy, and honour every soul that Allāh has made. For Allāh the Most High loves equity and hates tyranny, and He does not bless the hand that behaves with cruelty. Therefore treat the stranger with gentleness, the needy with compassion, and the vulnerable with protection; and let humility guide your dealings, that you may be unlike Pharaoh and more akin to the righteous whom Allāh has praised.",
  26023: "Pharaoh said, “What is the Lord of the worlds?”",
  26024: "Moses said, “He is the Lord of the heavens and the earth and whatever is between them—if you would be certain.”",
  26025: "Pharaoh said to those around him, “Do you not hear him?”",
  26026: "Moses said, “He is your Lord and the Lord of your former forefathers.”",
  26027: "Pharaoh said, “Your messenger who was sent to you is truly mad.”",
  26028: "Moses said, “He is the Lord of the East and the West and all that is between them—if you would use reason.”",
  26029: "Pharaoh said, “If you take any god other than me, I will surely place you among the imprisoned.”",
  26030: "Moses said, “Even if I were to bring you something clear and convincing?”",
  26031: "Pharaoh said, “Then bring it—if you are truthful.”",
  26032: "So he cast down his staff, and at once it became a manifest serpent.",
  26033: "And he drew out his hand, and at once it was bright white to the beholders.",
  26034: "Pharaoh said to the chiefs around him,\n“This is indeed a skilled magician.”",
  26035: "He seeks to drive you out of your land by his magic. So what do you propose?",
  26036: "They said, “Delay him and his brother, and dispatch gatherers to the cities,",
  26037: "who will bring you every skilled magician.”",
  26038: "So the magicians were gathered for the appointed time of a known day [the day of the festival],",
  26039: "And it was said to the people, “Will you assemble?”",
  26040: "so that we may follow the magicians—if they are the victors?”",
  26041: "When the magicians came, they said to Pharaoh, \"Will we truly have a reward if we are victorious?\"",
  26042: "He said, \"Yes—and you will surely be among those brought near to me.\"",
  26043: "Moses said to them, “Cast whatever you are about to cast.”",
  26044: "So they threw down their ropes and staffs and said, \"By the might of Pharaoh, indeed it is we who are victorious.\"",
  26045: "Then Moses threw down his staff, and at once it swallowed what they had falsified.",
  26046: "So the magicians fell down in prostration.",
  26047: "They said, \"We have believed in the Lord of all worlds.\"",
  26048: "the Lord of Moses and Aaron.\"",
  26049: "Pharaoh said, “You have believed in him before I gave you permission. He is surely your leader who taught you magic. But you will soon come to know. I will surely cut off your hands and feet on opposite sides, and I will surely crucify you all.”",
  26050: "They said, “No harm. Indeed, we will surely return to our Lord.",
  26051: "Indeed, we hope that our Lord will forgive us our sins, since we are the first of the believers.”",
  26052: "And We inspired Moses, saying, \"Travel by night with My servants; indeed, you will be pursued.\"",
  26053: "So Pharaoh sent gatherers into the cities,",
  26054: "Indeed, \"these are but a small faction,",
  26055: "And indeed, they are provoking us,",
  26056: "And indeed, we are all fully vigilant.”",
  26057: "So We drove them out of gardens and springs,",
  26058: "and treasures and a stately residence,",
  26059: "Thus We caused the Children of Israel to inherit it.",
  26060: "So they pursued them at sunrise.",
  26061: "Then when the two hosts sighted each other, the companions of Moses said, \"We are surely overtaken.\"",
  26062: "Moses said, “No! Indeed, my Lord is with me; He will guide me.”",
  26063: "Then We inspired Moses, \"Strike the sea with your staff.\" So it parted, and each part became like a towering mountain.",
  26064: "Then We drew the others near,",
  26065: "and We saved Moses and all who were with him.",
  26066: "Then We drowned the others.",
  26067: "Indeed, in that is a sign. Yet most of them are not believers.",
  26068: "And indeed, your Lord—He is the All-Mighty, the Most Merciful.",
  26069: "Recite to them the story of Abraham.",
  26070: "when he said to his father and his people, \"What is it that you worship?\"",
  26071: "They said, \"We worship idols and remain devoted to them.\"",
  26072: "He said, \"Do they hear you when you call on them?\"",
  26073: "Or can they benefit or harm you?\"",
  26074: "They said, \"Rather, we found our forefathers doing the same.\"",
  26075: "He said, \"Have you considered what you have been worshipping—\"",
  26076: "you and your forefathers before you?",
  26077: "Indeed, they are my enemies—except the Lord of the worlds, [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. The Arabic uses the singular collective ʿaduww (“an enemy”), which in context denotes total hostility rather than a single foe. Rendering it as the plural “enemies” reflects the idiomatic sense in English while preserving Abraham’s decisive disavowal of the idols he and his people had worshipped. This excludes the Lord of the worlds alone, since their devotion had been directed to idols alongside their worship of Allāh.",
  26078: "The One who created me, and He alone guides me,",
  26079: "and He is the One who feeds me and gives me drink,",
  26080: "and when I am ill, He is the One who heals me [7],\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. These are the words of the Prophet Abraham, who acknowledged his own inability to heal himself and affirmed that true healing belongs to Allāh alone. Illness is attributed to himself (“when I am ill”), while healing is attributed exclusively to his Lord, reflecting refined etiquette and pure monotheism.\nSins, too, is a form of spiritual disease whose cure lies in divine forgiveness—something only Allāh can grant. This meaning is illustrated in the well-known report of the boy and the king in the Sunnah: when the king’s courtier asked the boy to cure his blindness, the boy replied, “I cure no one; rather, Allāh —Exalted is He—alone cures. If you believe in Allāh, I will supplicate to Him and He will heal you.” The boy’s supplication was thus merely a means through which healing occurred, while the true healer remained Allāh alone.\nAccordingly, attributing an action such as healing to an apparent means (like a person, medicine, or supplication) is linguistically and religiously valid, provided one understands that the means has no independent power but functions only by Allah’s will. The same principle applies to Jesus, who—by Allah’s permission—healed the blind and the lepers and raised the dead. These miracles were not from himself; rather, they occurred solely through the power and permission of Allāh, his Lord.",
  26081: "And He is the One who will cause me to die [8], and then bring me back to life;\\n\\nFootnotes: [8] . Abraham here affirms that both death and restoration to life occur solely by Allah’s will. The verbs are in the future sense, expressing firm certainty in the divine decree over life, death, and resurrection. By attributing these ultimate transitions exclusively to his Lord, Abraham completes the sequence of divine favors (creation, guidance, provision, healing) and moves from present care to the final realities of mortality and the Hereafter. This statement reinforces a central Qur’anic principle: while human beings may observe the apparent causes of death and life, the true giver of death and the One who restores life is Allāh alone.\nThe Prophet Muhammad (ﷺ) said:\n“Whoever accompanies the funeral of a believer, with faith and seeking reward, and remains with it until the burial is completed, will return with two qirāṭs of reward—each qirāṭ being like the size of Mount Uhud. And whoever performs only the funeral prayer and returns before the burial will return with one qirāṭ of reward.”",
  26082: "and the One who I hope will forgive my sin on the Day of Recompense.\"",
  26083: "My Lord, grant me wisdom and join me with the righteous.",
  26084: "and grant me an honorable mention among later generations. [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. Muslims regularly invoke blessings upon Abraham and Muhammad in their daily prayers—especially in the final sitting (tashahhud)—in accordance with the Prophet’s instruction. This enduring practice reflects Abraham’s elevated rank and his standing as a foremost model of pure monotheism.\nIn this light, his supplication to be granted “a truthful reputation among later generations” was fulfilled by Allāh in the most magnificent way. The followers of Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) continue to mention Abraham with reverence, send blessings upon him on daily basis, and remember him as one of the greatest prophets.\nThis widespread and lasting remembrance across generations stands as a clear manifestation of Abraham’s prayer being answered, demonstrating how Allāh raises the honor and legacy of His sincere servants far beyond their earthly lifetimes.",
  26085: "and include me among the inheritors of the Garden of Bliss.",
  26086: "And forgive my father, for he is among those who are astray.",
  26087: "and do not disgrace me on the Day they are resurrected.",
  26088: "the Day when neither wealth nor children will benefit anyone,",
  26089: "except for those who come to Allāh with a sound heart. [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. A “sound heart” (qalb salīm) is one that is free from shirk (associating partners with Allāh) and from the diseases of pride, hypocrisy, rancor, and moral corruption. It is a heart that is inwardly whole, sincere, and rightly oriented toward its Lord. Because the heart is the center of intention and character, its condition shapes a person’s faith, conduct, and relationships: a corrupted heart gives rise to harm and spiritual ruin, whereas a sound and wholesome heart brings clarity, balance, and moral health.\nThe Qur’an and Sunnah repeatedly warn against destructive inner traits such as spite, vindictiveness, arrogance, persistent quarrelling, dishonesty, injustice, and betrayal. In contrast, they praise sincerity, humility, compassion, faithful loyalty, and protective love for others’ well-being. Thus, the “sound heart” is not merely free of corruption but is actively adorned with truthfulness, sincerity, and love of what is pleasing to Allāh. It is this heart—pure in faith and upright in character—that will avail its possessor on the Day of Judgment.",
  26090: "Paradise will be brought near to the righteous.",
  26091: "and Hellfire will be laid bare for those who went astray,",
  26092: "and it will be said to them, \"Where are what you used to worship,",
  26093: "Besides Allāh? Can they help you, or even help themselves?”",
  26094: "So they are hurled into it—both they and those who went astray,",
  26095: "and the soldiers of Iblīs all together.",
  26096: "They will say, while they argue within it:",
  26097: "By Allāh, we were indeed in clear error,",
  26098: "when we made you equal to the Lord of all the worlds.",
  26099: "None led us astray except the wicked [11].\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. Corrupt rulers, dishonest religious figures, false preachers, and harmful companions can exert a powerful influence over those who trust or follow them. Their authority, charisma, or personal closeness often enables them to shape people’s beliefs, attitudes, and behavior—sometimes subtly and without immediate awareness.\nSuch figures may mislead others through distorted teachings, emotional manipulation, persuasive example, or by gradually normalizing sin and injustice. Many people follow them out of trust, habit, admiration, fear, or the desire to belong. Over time, this misplaced loyalty can harden the heart, cloud moral judgment, and draw a person far from truth and righteousness.\nFor this reason, those who misguide others bear a compounded burden of guilt: they themselves are astray, and they lead others into the same path. On the Day of Judgment, those who were misled will clearly perceive the consequences of following leaders and companions who were corrupt, heedless, or driven by worldly ambition. At that moment, no false authority, friendship, or claimed intercession will avail them—each soul will face the outcome of what it chose and whom it chose to follow.",
  26100: "So now we have no intercessors at all,",
  26101: "nor an intimate friend.",
  26102: "If only we had another chance to return, we would be among the believers.\"",
  26103: "Indeed, in that is a sign. Yet most of them are not believers.",
  26104: "And indeed, your Lord—He is the All-Mighty, the Most Merciful.",
  26105: "The people of Noah rejected the messengers. [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. Although the people of Noah directly rejected only their own prophet, the Qur’an expresses their crime as rejecting “the messengers” in the plural. This reflects a core Qur’anic principle: denying any one messenger is, in reality, a denial of the entire chain of divine revelation, since all prophets called to the same fundamental message of monotheism and obedience to Allāh. Thus, whoever rejects a single true messenger implicitly rejects those who came before and after him—including the final messenger, Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him).",
  26106: "When their brother Noah said to them, \"Will you not fear Allāh [and shun evil]?\"",
  26107: "I am truly a trustworthy messenger to you.",
  26108: "So fear Allāh, 'love Him', and obey me.",
  26109: "I ask you for no reward; my reward is only with Allāh, the Lord of all worlds.",
  26110: "So fear Allāh, love Him, and obey me.",
  26111: "They said, \"Should we believe in you when the lowliest among us follow you?\"",
  26112: "He said, \"How could I know what they used to do?\"",
  26113: "Their reckoning is only with my Lord—if only you were aware.",
  26114: "I will never drive away the believers.",
  26115: "I am only a clear warner. [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13] of the punishment for anyone who associates partners with Allāh and does evil.",
  26116: "They said, “Noah, if you do not desist, you will surely be stoned to death.”",
  26117: "He said, “My Lord, indeed my people have rejected me.",
  26118: "So judge decisively between me and them, and save me and the believers with me.”",
  26119: "So We saved him and those with him in the laden Ark. [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. By the time of Noah, corruption and disbelief had become widespread among his people, and very few remained faithful. Noah himself stood out for his unwavering devotion—worshiping, loving, and obeying Allāh with steadfast sincerity. This constancy appears closely tied to the deep gratitude that characterized his faith, a quality that distinguished him and the small band of believers who were ultimately saved from the great flood. The Qur’an elsewhere describes Noah as a “grateful servant,” highlighting thankfulness as a defining trait of true faith.\nIn contrast, his society had become morally and spiritually corrupted, which is why the divine judgment of the flood was sent. Ingratitude toward Allāh often lies at the root of disbelief and sin, and it seems to have been a key pathway through which Satan misled the people of that era—while Noah remained protected through sincere devotion and thankfulness.\nThis pattern of grateful obedience is seen repeatedly among the prophets. Abraham—the patriarch of many later nations—was likewise distinguished by profound gratitude and submission to Allāh. His willingness to sacrifice his beloved son Ismail in obedience to the divine command powerfully illustrates the depth of his faith; yet Allāh, in His mercy, spared Ismail, demonstrating how He honors and rewards sincere devotion. Even when Abraham sought reassurance, his questions arose from a heart firmly anchored in faith and gratitude.\nThe same spirit is reflected in Moses and the faithful who followed him, in Jesus and his devoted disciples, and finally in Muhammad and his companions. Across generations, the Qur’an consistently portrays gratitude to Allāh as a central mark of the sincere believer and a key source of steadfastness in the face of widespread misguidance.",
  26120: "Then We drowned the rest.",
  26121: "Indeed, in that is a sign. Yet most of them are not believers.",
  26122: "And indeed, your Lord—He is the All-Mighty, the Most Merciful.",
  26123: "The people of Ād rejected the messengers,",
  26124: "When their brother Hūd said to them, “Will you not fear Allāh [and shun evil]?\"",
  26125: "I am truly a trustworthy messenger to you;",
  26126: "So fear Allāh, 'love Him', and obey me:",
  26127: "I ask you for no reward; my reward is only with Allāh the Lord of all worlds.",
  26128: "Do you build on every height a monument, idly amusing yourselves?",
  26129: "And you build massive structures as though you will live forever.",
  26130: "And when you strike, you strike as tyrants.",
  26131: "So fear Allāh, love Him, and obey me.",
  26132: "Fear and love the One who has provided you with what you know,",
  26133: "Who provided you with livestock and children,",
  26134: "and gardens and springs.",
  26135: "Truly, I fear for you the punishment of a momentous Day.",
  26136: "They said, \"It is the same to us whether you admonish us or are not among the admonishers.",
  26137: "This is nothing but the fables of the ancients.",
  26138: "And we will never be punished.”",
  26139: "So they rejected him, then We destroyed them. Indeed, in that is a sign—yet most of them are not believers.",
  26140: "And indeed, your Lord—He is the All-Mighty, the Most Merciful.",
  26141: "The people of Thamūd rejected the messengers,",
  26142: "When their brother Ṣāliḥ said to them, “Will you not fear Allāh [and shun evil]?\"",
  26143: "I am truly a trustworthy messenger to you;",
  26144: "So fear Allāh, 'love Him', and obey me.",
  26145: "I ask you for no reward; my reward is only with Allāh, the Lord of all worlds.",
  26146: "Will you be left secure in what is here?",
  26147: "in gardens and springs,",
  26148: "and crops and palm trees with tender fruit.",
  26149: "and you carve houses out of the mountains, proudly.",
  26150: "So fear Allāh, 'love Him', and obey me.",
  26151: "Do not obey the command of the transgressors.",
  26152: "those who spread corruption in the land and do not reform.",
  26153: "They said, \"You are but one who has been bewitched!",
  26154: "You are but a human like us. So bring a sign if you are one of the truthful.\"",
  26155: "Ṣāliḥ said, \"This is a she-camel. She has her turn to drink, and you have yours on an appointed day.\"",
  26156: "Do not harm her, or the punishment of a great Day will overtake you.",
  26157: "So they hamstrung her, and became remorseful.",
  26158: "So the punishment overtook them. Indeed, in that is a sign—yet most of them are not believers.",
  26159: "And indeed, your Lord—He is the All-Mighty, the Most Merciful.",
  26160: "The people of Lūṭ rejected the messengers,",
  26161: "When their brother Lot said to them: \"Will you not fear Allāh [and shun evil]\"",
  26162: "I am truly a trustworthy messenger to you;",
  26163: "So fear Allāh, 'love Him', and obey me.",
  26164: "I ask you for no reward; my reward is only with Allāh, the Lord of all worlds.",
  26165: "Do you approach males [15] from among all people?\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. That is, with sexual desire. Islamic tradition preserves several reports attributed to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ that address sexual conduct and the boundaries established in Islamic law. In some narrations, same-sex acts—whether between two men or two women—are described as grave sins, reflecting the broader legal and moral principle that sexual intimacy is permitted only within marriage between a man and a woman.\nAnother well-known report strongly condemns both bestiality and sodomy, stating: “Cursed is the one who has intercourse with an animal, and cursed is the one who commits the act of sodomy,” with the warning repeated for emphasis—a common prophetic teaching method used to underscore seriousness.\nTaken together, these narrations form part of the Islamic moral framework governing sexual behavior. They highlight the emphasis placed in the tradition on preserving moral boundaries, protecting human dignity, and restricting sexual relations to the lawful marital bond.",
  26166: "Leaving what your Lord created for you as wives? Rather, you are a transgressing people. [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. This refers to crossing the limits that Allāh has set regarding what is lawful and what is unlawful. These boundaries are meant to protect human dignity, family structure, and moral conduct. When a community abandons the lawful forms of intimacy that Allāh created for them—such as the natural bond between husband and wife—and instead pursues actions that He has prohibited, they are described as having exceeded those limits. In this verse, the people of Lot are called “transgressing” because they moved beyond the moral boundaries established by Allāh, choosing behaviors that violated His guidance and the natural order He created.",
  26167: "They said, “If you do not desist, O Lot, you will surely be expelled.”",
  26168: "Lot said, \"Indeed, I am one of those who abhor your deeds.",
  26169: "My Lord, save me and my family from what they do.”",
  26170: "So We saved him and all his family,",
  26171: "Except for an old woman who was among those who remained behind. [17]\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. The “old woman” refers to the wife of the Prophet Lot. Although she lived among the believers, she did not share their faith and instead sided inwardly with the corrupt people of her town. Because of her disbelief and disloyalty to the divine message, she was excluded from the salvation granted to Lot and his family. Her case illustrates an important Qur’anic principle: close family ties to a prophet do not benefit a person without personal faith and obedience. Each individual is accountable before Allāh for his or her own belief and actions.",
  26172: "Then We destroyed the rest.",
  26173: "And We rained upon them a rain [of brimstones]. Evil indeed was the rain of those who had been warned.",
  26174: "Indeed, in that is a sign—yet most of them are not believers.",
  26175: "And indeed, your Lord—He is the All-Mighty, the Most Merciful.",
  26176: "The dwellers of the thicket [18] rejected the messengers,\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. The “dwellers of the thicket” (Aṣḥāb al-Aykah) refers to a people associated in Islamic tradition with the community of Midian to whom the Prophet Shuʿayb was sent. The term aykah literally means a dense thicket or wooded grove, likely describing a prominent geographical feature of their region. Many scholars held that the people of the thicket were the same as, or closely related to, the people of Midian, though the Qur’an preserves the distinct designation here. The expression highlights both their location and their collective identity while maintaining the Qur’an’s characteristic precision in naming past communities.",
  26177: "When Shu’aib said to them, \"Will you not fear Allāh [and shun evil]?\"",
  26178: "I am truly a trustworthy messenger to you;",
  26179: "So fear Allāh, 'love Him', and obey me.",
  26180: "I ask you for no reward; my reward is only with Allāh, the Lord of all worlds.",
  26181: "Give full measure, and do not be among those who cause loss.",
  26182: "and weigh with the just balance.",
  26183: "Do not deprive people of their due, nor spread corruption in the land.",
  26184: "Fear and love the One who created you and the former generations.",
  26185: "They said, “You are only bewitched!",
  26186: "You are but a human like us, and we indeed think you are one of the liars.",
  26187: "So cause a fragment of the sky to fall upon us if you are truthful.\"",
  26188: "Shu‘ayb said, “My Lord knows best whatever you do.”",
  26189: "So they rejected him, and the punishment of the Day of the shadowing cloud seized them. Indeed, it was the punishment of a great Day.",
  26190: "Indeed, in that is a sign—yet most of them are not believers.",
  26191: "And indeed, your Lord—He is the All-Mighty, the Most Merciful.",
  26192: "And indeed, this is a revelation from the Lord of the worlds.",
  26193: "The trustworthy Spirit [Gabriel] brought it down.",
  26194: "Upon your heart [O Muhammad], so that you may be one of the warners.",
  26195: "in a clear Arabic language.",
  26196: "And indeed, it is [19] in the Scriptures of the former peoples.” [20]\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. This refers to the Qur’an itself and its revelation to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ.\n[20]. That is the earlier divine Scriptures, such as the Torah and the Gospel, which contain references to the coming of the final and last messenger, the Prophet Muhammad, and the core message of monotheism.",
  26197: "Was it not a sign for them that the scholars of the Children of Israel knew it? [21]\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. This refers to the learned individuals among the Children of Israel who possessed knowledge of earlier Scriptures—such as the Torah and the Psalms—which contained descriptions and signs of the coming of the last and final messenger, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. Their awareness of these prophecies served as further confirmation of the truth of the Qur’anic message.",
  26198: "If We had revealed this [Qur’an] to some of the non-Arabs,",
  26199: "and he recited it to them, they would not have believed in it.”",
  26200: "Thus We caused it [disbelief] to enter the hearts of the wicked.",
  26201: "They will not believe in it until they see the painful punishment.",
  26202: "which will come upon them suddenly while they are unaware.",
  26203: "So they will say, \"Will we be granted respite?\"",
  26204: "So is it Our punishment that they seek to hasten?",
  26205: "Have you then considered if We let them enjoy themselves for years,",
  26206: "Then there came to them what they had been warned of,",
  26207: "What benefit will what they once enjoyed in worldly life be to them?",
  26208: "We never destroyed any town except that it had warners.",
  26209: "as a reminder, and We have never been unjust.",
  26210: "And this [Qur’an] has not been brought down by the devils, [22]\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. This verse refutes the accusation of the pagan Arabs that the Qur’an was inspired by devils or soothsayers. In pre-Islamic Arabia, poets and fortune-tellers were often thought to receive information through jinn. The Qur’an firmly rejects this notion, affirming that its source is purely divine. As clarified in the following verses, devils neither have the ability nor the access to bring down revelation of this kind. Rather, the Qur’an was conveyed by the trustworthy angel Gabriel by the command of Allāh.",
  26211: "It is not fitting for them, nor are they able. [23]\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. That is, the devils are neither suited nor permitted to bring down the Qur’an, nor do they possess the ability to do so. Divine revelation is guarded and delivered only by the trustworthy angel Gabriel by Allah’s command. This verse further refutes the claim that the Qur’an originated from satanic or jinn sources, emphasizing both the impossibility and the incapacity of the devils in this matter.",
  26212: "Indeed, they are completely barred from hearing. [24]\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. That is, the devils are completely cut off from access to the heavenly realm and are unable to overhear divine revelation. As indicated elsewhere in the Qur’an (e.g., 72:8–9), attempts by the devils to eavesdrop on the heavenly assembly are thwarted by flaming projectiles. This verse reinforces the Qur’an’s assertion that revelation is securely protected and cannot be infiltrated, altered, or transmitted by satanic or jinn forces.",
  26213: "So do not call upon any other god alongside Allāh, lest you be among the punished.",
  26214: "And warn your closest relatives. [25]\\n\\nFootnotes: [25]. That is, warn your nearest relatives about the approaching punishment of Allāh and urge them to turn back to Him before it is too late. This command marked an early stage in the Prophet Muhammad’s mission, directing him to begin his public warning with those closest to him in kinship. It highlights the principle that the call to truth starts with one’s immediate circle before extending more broadly to society. The verse also underscores personal accountability: even the Prophet’s own relatives were not exempt from the obligation to believe and respond to the message.",
  26215: "and lower your wing to the believers who follow you.",
  26216: "But if they disobey you, then say, \"Indeed, I am free of what you do.\"",
  26217: "And put your trust in the All-Mighty, the Most Merciful.",
  26218: "Who sees you when you stand [for prayer],",
  26219: "and your movements among those who prostrate.",
  26220: "Indeed, He is the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.",
  26221: "Shall I inform you about whom the devils descend upon?",
  26222: "They descend upon every sinful liar,",
  26223: "They lend an ear [26], and most of them are liars [27].\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. This refers to devils attempting to eavesdrop on the heavenly realm to steal snippets of information before being driven away by shooting flames, as described in Qur’an 72:8–9.\n[27]. I.e., the soothsayers and fortune tellers. Even when they pass on what they overhear, they mix it with many lies—only a tiny fragment is true, while most of what they convey is falsehood.",
  26224: "As for the poets [28], they are followed by the misguided.\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. Islam encourages poetry that is wholesome, uplifting, and spiritually beneficial—poetry that inspires reverence for Allāh, deepens one’s awareness of Him, and motivates obedience, virtue, and moral conduct. Such poetry was appreciated by the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ and by the early Muslims.\nWhat Islam cautions against is poetry (and songs) that promote indecency, immoral desires, and behaviors that violate the boundaries set by Allāh and His Messenger. Poetry that glamorizes sin, encourages lust, or leads hearts away from righteousness is considered blameworthy.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“It is better for one of you to have his belly filled with pus that destroys his body than to have it filled with poetry.”\nThis hadith does not condemn poetry as an art form. Rather, it warns against being consumed by corrupt or harmful poetry—allowing it to dominate one’s mind and lead one away from the remembrance of Allāh.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ did not approve of poets who used their craft to promote worldliness, pride, immorality, or the pursuit of fleeting desires. However, he praised and encouraged poets whose verses exalted Allāh, defended truth, expressed wisdom, and guided people toward righteousness.",
  26225: "Have you not seen that they wander in every valley [aimlessly]? [29]\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. This refers to their habit of speaking without restraint or sincerity—telling lies freely, praising some people excessively, and defaming others unjustly. Such poets shift from one claim to another, driven by emotion or personal gain rather than truth or integrity.",
  26226: "and that they say what they do not do,",
  26227: "Except those who believe, do righteous deeds, remember Allāh often, and defend themselves after being wronged. But the wrongdoers [30] will soon know to what return they will return. [31]\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. This refers to those who commit wrongdoing, injustice, or oppression—whether through speech or action—especially individuals who misuse their words, influence, or poetry to harm others, spread falsehood, or oppose the truth.\n[31]. That is, they will soon come to know the end that awaits them—either in this world through the consequences of their actions and divine justice, or in the Hereafter when they face the final outcome of their deeds.\nThe Qur’anic warning also carries an enduring moral call: believers are commanded to uphold righteousness and justice in their lives. This includes standing for what is right, seeking justice, defending the oppressed, supporting the orphan, and advocating for the vulnerable. Faith in Islam is not merely professed—it must be lived through active commitment to truth, mercy, and justice.",
  27001: "Ṭā Sīn [1]. These are the verses of the Qur’an and a clear Book. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2: verse 1.\n[2]. Both the Book and the verses of the Qur’an mentioned here refer to one and the same thing, the Qur’an itself; the Book that was to be and then present recited Verses. Its double mention, as given here, is meant to draw special attention to the evidences it provides to the Truthfulness of the Message.\nIt is a Book that is perfectly clear in its message, guidance, and purpose. It provides unmistakable evidence of divine truth, distinguishing right from wrong, and revealing matters with clarity and certainty.",
  27002: "a guidance and glad tidings for the believers,",
  27003: "those who establish the prayer, give zakah [the alms due], and are certain of the Hereafter.",
  27004: "Indeed, those who do not believe in the Hereafter—We have made their deeds seem appealing to them, so they wander blindly.",
  27005: "Those are the ones who will have the worst punishment, and in the Hereafter they will be the greatest losers.",
  27006: "Indeed, you are receiving the Qur’an from the All-Wise, All-Knowing.",
  27007: "[Remember] when Moses said to his family [3], \"Indeed, I have perceived a fire. I will bring you news from it, or bring you a burning brand so that you may warm yourselves.\"\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. I.e., his wife, during his journey from Midian back to Egypt.",
  27008: "When he came to it, he was called: Blessed is the One who is at the fire [4] and those around it [5]. Glory be to Allāh, Lord of all worlds.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. The phrase “Blessed is the One at the fire” refers to the sacred nature of the moment when Mūsā (Moses, peace be upon him) was called. The One “at the fire” is Allāh — not in a physical or spatial sense, for He is exalted and above the heavens — but in the sense that the divine call issued from that place. The expression indicates the sanctity of the encounter.\n[5]. The “those around it” are understood to include Moses himself and, according to many scholars, the angels present at that blessed scene.\nScholars also explained that what Moses saw was not ordinary fire. It was described as light — a radiant, glowing manifestation: the light of the Lord of the worlds. The fire did not burn; rather, it illuminated. It was a sign that drew Moses toward a divinely appointed moment of communication.\nThe verse concludes with “Glory be to Allāh, Lord of all worlds,” affirming His transcendence. Though He spoke to Moses, He remains utterly unlike His creation. The blessing lay not in a physical flame, but in the divine light and revelation that occurred there.",
  27009: "“O Moses, indeed it is I—Allāh, the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.",
  27010: "Cast your staff.” But when he saw it writhing like a serpent, he turned and fled without looking back. “O Moses, do not fear. Indeed, the messengers do not fear in My presence.”",
  27011: "Except whoever does wrong, then replaces good after evil—indeed, I am the All-Forgiving, the Most Merciful.",
  27012: "Put your hand into your bosom [of your robe]; it will come out white, without blemish. Among nine signs to Pharaoh and his people. Indeed, they are a defiantly disobedient people.",
  27013: "But when Our clearly visible signs came to them, they said, \"This is clear magic.\"",
  27014: "They denied them out of injustice and arrogance, even though their souls were convinced of them. So see how the end of the corruptors was.",
  27015: "We certainly granted David and Solomon knowledge, and they said, \"All praise is for Allāh, who has favored us over many of His believing servants.\"",
  27016: "Solomon inherited David and said, \"O people, we have been taught the speech of birds, and we have been given of everything. Indeed, this is the evident bounty.\"",
  27017: "Solomon’s armies of jinn, humans, and birds were gathered for him, and they were driven in ordered ranks.",
  27018: "Until, when they came upon the valley of the Ants, an ant said, \"O ants, enter your dwellings, lest Solomon and his armies crush you while they are unaware.\"",
  27019: "So he smiled, laughing at her speech [6], and said, \"My Lord, inspire me to be grateful for Your favor which You have bestowed upon me and upon my parents, and enable me to do righteous deeds that please You. And admit me, by Your mercy, among Your righteous servants.\"\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. This verse highlights the gentle humanity of Prophet Solomon, who responded with a smile and laughter. It is a reminder to appreciate moments of lightness and to meet life with warmth and good cheer.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ likewise embodied kindness and approachability. He cared deeply for the needy and the poor, visited the sick to inquire about their well-being, and greeted people—whether familiar or strangers—with the greeting of peace, often accompanied by a sincere and welcoming smile.\nHe said: “Smiling at your brother is an act of charity. Enjoining what is right and forbidding what is wrong is charity. And guiding a lost person is also charity.”\nThis teaching reflects the Islamic emphasis on small acts of kindness and social care as meaningful forms of worship and righteousness.",
  27020: "He inspected the birds and said, “Why is it that I do not see the hoopoe? Or is he absent?”",
  27021: "I will surely punish him with a severe punishment, or I will slaughter him, unless he brings me clear proof.",
  27022: "So he remained away only a short time. Then, when he came, he said, “I have encompassed what you have not encompassed, and I have come to you from Sheba with sure news.”",
  27023: "I found a woman ruling over them [7]; she has been given of all things, and has a great throne.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. The woman referred to here is identified as Bilqīs, the Queen of Sheba (Saba’), who ruled a prosperous kingdom in southern Arabia. Her mention in the Qur’an highlights both her political authority and the strength of her administration. The statement that she “has been given of everything” indicates that she possessed abundant worldly resources — wealth, power, organization, and stability — necessary for a flourishing kingdom.\nHer “great throne” symbolizes not only material splendor but also established sovereignty and influence. In ancient times, a throne represented legitimate rule and centralized authority. The description underscores the impressive worldly stature of her kingdom before her encounter with Prophet Sulaymān (Solomon, peace be upon him).\nThe narrative that follows does not criticize her for being a woman ruler; rather, it focuses on the spiritual transformation that occurs when she recognizes the truth. Her story ultimately illustrates humility, wisdom, and willingness to submit to Allāh once guidance becomes clear.",
  27024: "I found her and her people prostrating to the sun instead of Allāh. Satan has made their deeds appear fair to them and has averted them from the way, so they are not guided.",
  27025: "How can they not prostrate to Allāh [8], who brings forth what is hidden in the heavens and the earth and knows whatever you conceal and whatever you reveal?\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. This verse rebukes the people of Sheba for directing their prostration to the sun instead of to Allāh, the true Creator. Prostration is the highest outward expression of humility and worship. It signifies submission, reverence, and acknowledgment of divine sovereignty. Such devotion belongs to Allāh alone.\nThe phrase “who brings forth what is hidden in the heavens and the earth” refers to His power to reveal what is concealed — whether physical realities such as rain, vegetation, and buried treasures, or unseen matters known only to Him. Nothing remains hidden from His knowledge. He also knows what people conceal within their hearts and what they openly declare with their tongues.\nBy describing these divine attributes, the verse contrasts the helplessness of created objects — like the sun — with the absolute power and knowledge of the Creator. The One who brings forth the hidden and knows the secrets of all beings alone deserves worship. Prostration, therefore, is not merely a physical act, but a recognition of ultimate authority and divine perfection.",
  27026: "Allāh—there is no god except Him—the Lord of the Great Throne.",
  27027: "Solomon said, “We will see whether you have told the truth or are among the liars.”",
  27028: "Go with this letter of mine and deliver it to them. Then turn away from them and see what response they return.",
  27029: "She said, “O eminent ones, indeed a noble letter has been sent to me.” [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. The speaker here is the Queen of Sheba 'Bilqīs' when she had read Solomon’s letter.",
  27030: "It is from Solomon, and it reads: “In the Name of Allāh—the Most Compassionate, the Most Merciful.”",
  27031: "Do not exalt yourselves over me, but come to me in submission.\" [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. That is, come in peace and humility—not in defiance or pride; that is, as true believers who submit themselves to Allāh.",
  27032: "She said, “O chiefs, advise me in my affair. I do not decide any matter unless you are present.”",
  27033: "They said, “We are possessors of strength and of mighty prowess, but the decision is yours, so consider what you will command.”",
  27034: "She said, “Indeed, when kings enter a town, they ravage it and make the honored among its people humiliated—and so they do.”",
  27035: "I will send them a gift and see what response the envoys return.",
  27036: "So when the envoys came to Solomon, he said, “Do you provide me with wealth? What Allāh has given me is better than what He has given you. Rather, it is you who exult in your gift.”",
  27037: "Return to them, for we will surely come to them with armies they cannot resist, and we will drive them out from there humiliated and utterly subdued.”",
  27038: "He said, “O chiefs, which of you will bring me her throne before they come to me in submission?”",
  27039: "A powerful jinn said, “I will bring it to you before you rise from your place, and indeed I am strong and trustworthy for it.”",
  27040: "Then one who had knowledge of the Scripture said, “I will bring it to you before your gaze returns to you.” When Solomon saw it set before him, he said, “This is by the favor of Allāh, my Lord, to test me whether I will be grateful or ungrateful. Whoever is grateful is only grateful for his own benefit, and whoever is ungrateful—then indeed my Lord is Self-Sufficient, Most Generous.”",
  27041: "He said, “Disguise her throne so we may see whether she will be guided or be among those who are not rightly guided.”",
  27042: "When she arrived, she was asked, “Is your throne like this?” She replied, “It is as though it were the same.” [Solomon said:] “We were given knowledge before her, and we were already Muslims.”",
  27043: "What she used to worship instead of Allāh had averted her, for indeed she was from a disbelieving people.",
  27044: "It was said to her, “Enter the palace.”\nWhen she saw it, she thought it was a wide expanse of water, so she lifted her garments and uncovered her shins. Solomon said, “It is a palace whose floor is made smooth with glass.” She said, “My Lord, I have wronged myself, and I submit with Solomon to Allāh, the Lord of all worlds.”",
  27045: "We certainly sent to Thamūd their brother Ṣāliḥ, saying, “Worship Allāh alone.” But suddenly they became two groups, disputing with one another. [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. That is they split into two opposing factions—believers and disbelievers—who began disputing with one another after Ṣāliḥ’s call.",
  27046: "Ṣāliḥ said, “O my people, why do you seek to hasten punishment before the good? Why do you not seek Allāh’s forgiveness so that you may be shown mercy?”",
  27047: "They said, “We take you and those with you as a bad omen [12].” He said, “Your omen is with Allāh; rather, you are a people being tested.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. Taking omens — interpreting events, sights, or occurrences as signs of inevitable good or bad luck — was a common practice in pre-Islamic Arabia. Islam rejects this belief because it attributes independent influence to created things rather than to Allāh alone.\nThe Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ strongly warned against this practice, saying:\n“Taking omens is polytheism; taking omens is polytheism; taking omens is polytheism.”\nHe repeated it three times for emphasis. Then he added:\n“Every one of us may feel something of it, but Allāh removes it through trust in Him.”\nThis shows that fleeting feelings of superstition may occur in the heart, but a believer overcomes them through reliance (tawakkul) upon Allāh.\nWhen omens were mentioned in his presence, the Prophet ﷺ clarified:\n“The best type is a good omen, and it does not turn a believer back from his purpose.”\nA “good omen” here refers to optimistic interpretation — such as hearing a positive word and taking encouragement from it — without believing that it independently controls events.\nHe also taught a supplication for anyone troubled by such thoughts:\n“O Allāh, none brings good except You, none averts evil except You, and there is no might and no power except with Allāh.”\nThus, Islam replaces superstition with trust in divine decree. Harm and benefit come only by Allāh’s will, and nothing in creation possesses independent power. True faith rests in reliance upon Him alone.",
  27048: "There were in the city nine kinsmen who spread corruption in the land and did not reform.",
  27049: "They said, “Swear together by Allāh that we will attack him and his family by night, then we will say to his heir, ‘We did not witness the destruction of his family, and indeed we are truthful.’”",
  27050: "They plotted a plot, and We planned a plan while they were unaware.",
  27051: "So observe how the end of their plot was: We destroyed them and their people altogether.",
  27052: "These are their houses, lying desolate because of their wrongdoing. Indeed, in this is a sign for a people who know.",
  27053: "And We saved those who believed and feared Allāh.",
  27054: "And remember Lot, when he said to his people, “Do you commit indecency while you see?”",
  27055: "Do you indeed approach men with lust instead of women? Rather, you are a people who act ignorantly. [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. That is behaving in ignorance by neglecting what Allāh requires of you—such as preserving faith, maintaining purity, and upholding moral integrity—and by refusing to refrain from sinful and shameful acts. Their actions reflected a deliberate disregard for guidance and a conscious persistence in wrongdoing.",
  27056: "Yet the only response of his people was that they said, “Expel Lot’s family from your town—they are a people who keep themselves pure.”",
  27057: "So We saved him and his family, except his wife—we destined her to be among those who remained behind and perished.",
  27058: "And We rained upon them a rain [of brimstone]. So evil was the rain of those who had been warned.",
  27059: "Say, “All praise be to Allāh, and peace be upon His servants whom He has chosen. Is Allāh better, or what they falsely associate with Him [in worship]?”",
  27060: "Is He [not better] who created the heavens and the earth and sends down for you water from the sky, by which We bring forth gardens of delightful beauty? You could never cause their trees to grow. Is there any deity with Allāh? Rather, they are a people who ascribe equals to Him.",
  27061: "Is He [not better] who made the earth a stable ground, set rivers within it, placed firm mountains upon it, and made between the two seas a barrier? Is there any deity with Allāh? Rather, most of them do not know.",
  27062: "Is He [not better] who answers the desperate one when he calls upon Him, removes the harm, and makes you inheritors of the earth? Is there any deity with Allāh? Little do you remember.",
  27063: "Is He [not better] who guides you through the darknesses of the land and sea, and who sends the winds as heralds of His mercy? Is there any deity with Allāh? Exalted is Allāh above whatever they associate with Him [in worship].",
  27064: "Is He [not better] who originates creation, then restores it, and who provides for you from the sky and the earth? Is there any deity with Allāh? Say, \"Produce your evidence, if you are truthful.\"",
  27065: "Say, “None in the heavens and the earth knows the unseen except Allāh, and they do not perceive when they will be resurrected.”",
  27066: "Rather, their knowledge does not reach the Hereafter. Rather, they are in doubt about it; rather, they are blind to it.",
  27067: "The disbelievers said, “When we and our forefathers have become dust, will we really be brought forth [alive]?”",
  27068: "We have certainly been promised this—we and our fathers before. This is nothing but the tales of the former peoples.",
  27069: "Say, “Travel through the land and see how the end of the evildoers was.”",
  27070: "Do not grieve over them, nor be in distress over what they plot.",
  27071: "They say, “When will this promise be fulfilled, if you are truthful?”",
  27072: "Say, “Perhaps some of what you seek to hasten has drawn close behind you.”",
  27073: "Indeed, your Lord is bountiful toward the people, but most of them are not grateful.",
  27074: "Your Lord surely knows whatever their hearts conceal and what they reveal.",
  27075: "There is nothing hidden in the heavens and the earth except that it is in a clear Record. [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. This refers to the Preserved Tablet (al-Lawḥ al-Maḥfūẓ), the perfect and eternal Record with Allāh in which all things are written — past, present, and future. Nothing hidden in the heavens or the earth escapes it, for it contains the full knowledge of everything that will take place, exactly as Allāh has decreed.",
  27076: "Indeed, this Qur’an recounts to the Children of Israel most of what they differ about.",
  27077: "And indeed it is guidance and mercy for the believers.",
  27078: "Indeed, your Lord will decide between them by His judgment, and He is the All-Mighty, the All-Knowing.",
  27079: "So put your trust in Allāh; indeed, you are upon the clear truth.",
  27080: "Indeed, you cannot make the dead hear, nor can you make the deaf hear the call when they turn away retreating. [15]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. That is, those who stubbornly reject the message are like the dead and the deaf—they cannot benefit from guidance when they deliberately turn away.",
  27081: "Nor can you guide the blind out of their error. You can only make hear those who believe in Our revelations, so they submit as Muslims. [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. All people are called to submit themselves to Allāh —meaning to yield to Him in faith, obedience, and trust. True submission to Allāh alone strengthens the heart, and when a person is firmly committed to Him, the devil retreats. By relying on Allāh and following His guidance, a believer is empowered to resist temptation and remain steadfast upon the right path.",
  27082: "When the Word is fulfilled against them [17], We will bring forth for them a beast from the earth that will speak to them, saying that the people have no certainty in Our signs. [18]\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. This refers to the divine decree of punishment or the arrival of the Last Hour, when denial can no longer benefit the disbelievers.\n[18]. The Beast (dābbat al-arḍ) is one of the major signs of the Last Day. It will appear near the end of time when corruption becomes widespread among the people. Allāh will cause this great creature to emerge from the earth. It will speak to humanity with clear speech, admonishing them and testifying to their lack of certainty in Allah’s revelations. It will also mark the people—distinguishing the believers from the disbelievers by a visible sign.\nThe Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:\n“The Last Hour will not come until ten signs appear before it:\nthe rising of the sun from its place of setting;\nthe emergence of the Beast;\nthe coming forth of Gog and Magog;\nthe appearance of the Dajjāl (the Antichrist);\nthe descent of Jesus, son of Mary;\nthe smoke;\nand three major land-collapses: one in the East, one in the West, and one in the Arabian Peninsula.\nThe final of these will be a fire that will emerge from Yemen, from the lower part of Aden, driving the people to their place of gathering.",
  27083: "[Warn of] the Day when We will gather from every nation a group of those who denied Our revelations, and they will be driven in ranks.",
  27084: "Until, when they arrive, He will say, “Did you deny My revelations without fully comprehending them? Or what were you doing?”",
  27085: "The Word [of punishment] will befall them for their wrongdoing, so they will not speak.",
  27086: "Do they not see that We made the night for them to rest therein and the day illuminating? Indeed, in this are signs for a people who believe.",
  27087: "And beware of the Day when the Trumpet will be blown, and all who are in the heavens and on the earth will be stricken with terror—except those whom Allāh wills to spare. And all will come to Him humbled.",
  27088: "You will see the mountains, thinking them to be solid, while they pass like clouds. This is the work of Allāh, Who has perfected all things [19]. Indeed, He is All-Aware of what you do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. Allāh has endowed human beings with remarkable skill, imagination, and craftsmanship. He has gifted them the ability to design, build, and produce works of beauty and benefit — whether through engraving, embroidery, weaving, painting, architecture, carpentry, landscaping, cooking, writing, engineering, or any other form of artistry and skilled work. Every trade that involves precision, creativity, and care is a reflection of the talents Allāh has placed within His servants.\nWhen a believer practices any profession sincerely, with excellence, integrity, and gratitude, he or she is working in devotion to the Lord of the universe. In Islam, Allāh is the First, the Best, and the Perfect. Human beings do not create out of nothing, but they use the abilities Allāh has granted them to shape, build, imagine, and produce. These gifts are a trust.\nThe Qur’an and Sunnah draw our attention to those who use their skills not only to produce what is beneficial or beautiful, but also to direct hearts toward the majesty of Allāh, to uphold goodness, and to teach others. In this way, human creativity becomes a form of worship, gratitude, and remembrance of the One Who perfected all things.",
  27089: "Whoever comes with a good deed [20] will have better than it, and they will be secure from the terror of that Day.\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. The “good deed” here refers first and foremost to faith in the Oneness of Allāh (Monotheism)—the foundation of all righteousness—together with the sincere good works a person performs. Any righteous deed built upon true faith is accepted, multiplied, and elevated by Allāh. Without belief in the Oneness of God, however, no deed has lasting value. Thus, whoever comes on the Day of Judgment with sincere faith and righteous actions will find their reward increased many times over.",
  27090: "And whoever comes with an evil deed [21] will be hurled on their faces into the Fire. “Are you recompensed except for what you used to do?”\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. The “evil deed” here refers foremost to polytheism and disbelief, which are the gravest of sins, along with every other evil deed a person commits. Such actions persist without repentance and lead to punishment on the Day of Judgment, for Allāh only repays each soul according to what it earned.",
  27091: "Say, “I have only been commanded to worship the Lord of this city, who has made it sacred [22]—and to Him belongs everything—and I am commanded to be among the Muslims.” [23]\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. “who has made it sacred”\nThe Arabic verb ḥarramahā (حرّمها) literally means “He declared it forbidden.” In Qur’anic usage, however, taḥrīm in relation to a place signifies declaring it sacred and inviolable (ḥaram). The “city” referred to here is Mecca, home of the Kaʿbah, which was designated a sanctuary by God. Within the sacred precinct (al-ḥaram), certain acts—such as bloodshed, hunting, and uprooting vegetation—are prohibited. The verse thus emphasizes both divine sovereignty over the city and its special sanctified status, grounding its sacredness not in tribal custom but in divine decree.\n[23]. “those who submit themselves [to Him] as Muslims”\nThe term al-muslimīn derives from the root S-L-M, connoting peace, wholeness, and surrender. Literally, it means “those who submit” or “those who surrender themselves.” In the Qur’anic context, the word does not function merely as a later communal label but describes a spiritual posture: conscious submission to God’s will. Many translators retain “Muslims” as a proper designation, while others render it dynamically as “those who submit” or “those devoted to Him” to preserve the verbal force of the participle. The phrase here underscores personal alignment with a broader community defined by obedience and surrender to Allāh.",
  27092: "And to recite the Qur’an [24]. Whoever is guided is guided only for his own soul, and whoever goes astray—then say, “I am only one of the warners.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. The Qur’an provides guidance for every person who seeks to live in a way that pleases Allāh. It offers direction for all aspects of life, so that whenever a believer is faced with a dilemma, he can turn to it for clarity and wisdom. Acting according to the Qur’an leads to salvation, especially when followed together with the example of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ.\nThe Qur’an and the Sunnah guide, protect, and keep a believer away from sin. Through Allah’s words, one gains understanding, inner strength, and firmness in faith. The Qur’an brings healing to the heart and body; it is a shield, a source of light, and a weapon against falsehood and temptation.\nWithin it lies the path to salvation, descriptions of the downfall of sinners, and the happiness of the believers. Its teachings are binding, its commands are obligatory, its truths are certain, and its stories are real. Read it to become wise, believe in it to be safe, and live by it to be righteous.",
  27093: "Say, “All praise be to Allāh. He will soon show you His signs, and you will recognize them. And your Lord is not unaware of what you do.”",
  28001: "Ṭā Sīn Mīm. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2: verse 1.",
  28002: "These are Verses of the Clear Book. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The “Clear Book” refers to the Qur’an—a scripture whose verses are precise, eloquent, and unmistakable in both language and legal guidance. Its meanings are firmly established, free from contradiction or ambiguity. It is a Book grounded in truth, illuminating every matter it addresses and clarifying issues that might otherwise remain uncertain, unclear, or open to confusion.",
  28003: "We recite to you some of the account of Moses and Pharaoh in truth for a people who believe.",
  28004: "Indeed, Pharaoh exalted himself in the land and made its people into factions, oppressing one group among them—the Children of Israel—slaughtering their sons while sparing their women. Indeed, he was one of the corrupters.",
  28005: "But We intended to show favor to those who had been oppressed in the land and to make them the leaders and the inheritors [of the land]. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. Referring to the blessed land of the Levant, to which the Children of Israel were brought after Pharaoh’s destruction.\nEven as they witnessed tyrants drawing their swords against them, the believers placed their trust in Allāh, confident that He would set matters right. Life can be harsh and deeply unfair, yet the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ taught a message that echoes the wisdom found in Psalm 37:\n “Do not be distressed by those who do evil, nor envy those who do wrong; for they will soon fade like grass and wither like green plants. Trust in Allāh and do good; remain steadfast in your land and enjoy His security. Take delight in Allāh, and He will grant the desires of your heart. Commit your path to Allāh; trust in Him, and He will act on your behalf. He will cause your righteousness to shine like the dawn and your justice like the midday sun. Be still before Allāh and wait patiently for Him; do not be troubled when people succeed through wrongdoing or carry out their wicked schemes. Turn away from anger and abandon rage; do not be consumed by worry—for it only leads to harm. Those who persist in evil will be destroyed, but those who place their hope in Allāh will inherit the land. This ancient reassurance—whether in the Psalms or the teachings of the Prophet Muhammad —reminds believers that oppression is temporary, while divine justice is certain and enduring.",
  28006: "and to establish them in the land [4] and to show Pharaoh, Haman, and their hosts what they feared.” [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. “establish them in the land”\nThe verb numakkina (نمكّن) derives from the root m-k-n, which conveys firmness, stability, and secure establishment. It signifies more than the mere granting of temporary power; it implies being firmly rooted and endowed with enduring authority. In the context of the Children of Israel in Egypt, the expression marks a divinely ordained reversal: those who had been oppressed would be securely established in the very land in which they had been subjugated.\n[5]. Namely, that Pharaoh feared his kingdom would eventually be overthrown by a newborn male from among the Children of Israel, as had been foretold to him—prompting his brutal policy of killing their infant sons. The phrase mā kānū yaḥdharūn literally means “that which they used to fear” or “that which they sought to guard against.” Pharaoh ordered the killing of Israelite male infants out of fear that a child from among them would one day threaten his rule (cf. 28:4). In the end, the very outcome he sought to prevent—his downfall through Moses—came about through the very people he had tried to suppress. The wording reflects a recurring Qur’anic theme: the divinely ordained reversal of power, whereby the schemes of the oppressor become the means of their own undoing.",
  28007: "We inspired the mother of Moses: “Suckle him [for now], but when you fear for him, cast him into the river. Do not be afraid, nor grieve. We will certainly return him to you and make him one of the messengers.”",
  28008: "Then the family of Pharaoh picked him up, only to become for them an enemy and a cause of grief. Indeed, Pharaoh, Haman, and their soldiers were sinners.",
  28009: "Pharaoh’s wife said, “A comfort to the eye for me and for you. Do not kill him; perhaps he will benefit us, or we may take him as a son.” While they were unaware. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. The expression qurratu ʿayn (literally, “coolness of the eye”) is an Arabic idiom denoting deep joy, comfort, and emotional relief. In early usage, something that brings “coolness” to the eye is a source of lasting delight and reassurance. Pharaoh’s wife thus expresses immediate affection and hope for the child. The closing phrase,  (“while they did not perceive”), introduces dramatic notably: they were unaware that the very child they chose to spare would become the means of Pharaoh’s downfall. The wording reinforces the Qur’anic theme of divine providence operating beyond human perception.",
  28010: "The heart of Moses’ mother became empty [of all else]. She nearly disclosed him, had We not reassured her heart so that she would be among the believers.",
  28011: "She said to his sister [7], “Follow him.” So she watched him from a distance, while they were unaware.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. i.e., Miriam",
  28012: "We had already forbidden to him the wet nurses before that. Then his sister said, “Shall I guide you to a household that will care for him and be compassionate toward him?”",
  28013: "So We returned him to his mother so that she might find comfort and not grieve, and that she might know that Allah’s promise is true. Yet most of them do not know.",
  28014: "When he reached his full strength and maturity, We gave him sound judgment and knowledge. Thus do We reward the doers of good.",
  28015: "And he entered the city at a time when its people were unaware [8], and found therein two men fighting—one from his people and the other from his enemy. The one from his people sought his help against the one from his enemy. So Moses struck him and killed him. He said, “This is of Satan’s work; indeed, he is a clear enemy who leads astray.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. The phrase “at a time of unawareness on the part of its people.” It denotes a moment when the inhabitants were inattentive or off guard—commonly understood as a midday rest or a period when public activity was minimal. The wording suggests that Moses entered the city at a time when his presence would attract little notice. More broadly, the expression contributes to the narrative tension: the encounter occurs quietly and unexpectedly, setting in motion events that would profoundly alter Moses’ life.",
  28016: "He said, “My Lord, I have wronged myself, so forgive me.” So He forgave him [9]. Indeed, He is the All-Forgiving, the Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said—relating from his Lord, Allāh, blessed and exalted be He: “O My servants—human and jinn—all of you are astray except those whom I guide. So ask Me for guidance, and I will guide you. O My servants, all of you are hungry except those whom I feed. So ask Me for food, and I will feed you.\nO My servants, all of you are unclothed except those whom I clothe. So ask Me for clothing, and I will clothe you.\nO My servants, you commit sins night and day, but I forgive all sins. So ask Me for forgiveness, and I will forgive you.”\nThis ḥadīth teaches that what only Allāh can do—such as giving guidance, forgiveness, provision, or control over the unseen—may not be sought from anyone but Him. It is not permissible to ask anyone besides Allāh to perform actions that are exclusively His—such as: “Forgive me.”\n“Send us rain.” “Grant us victory.”\n“Guide our hearts.”\nThese matters belong solely to Allāh, glorified and exalted is He.\nA person must turn to Allāh in both ease and hardship, seeking His help in all matters, for He alone has power over everything. However, if a person seeks help from another human being in something that a human is capable of doing, then this is permissible—just as the Israelite asked Moses for assistance in a matter Moses was able to help with. As long as the one being asked is alive, present, and capable, there is no objection to asking for such assistance. Yet, even then, it remains better and more virtuous to rely upon Allāh and ask Him first in every circumstance.\nFor indeed, Allāh is Able to do all things.",
  28017: "He said, “My Lord, because of the favor You have bestowed upon me, I will never be a supporter of the criminals.”",
  28018: "He found himself in the city fearful and vigilant; when suddenly the one who had sought his help the previous day cried out to him for help. Moses said to him, “You are indeed clearly misguided.”",
  28019: "So when he wanted to strike the one who was an enemy to them both, he said, “Moses, do you want to kill me [10] as you killed a man yesterday? You only want to be a tyrant in the land, and you do not want to be among the reformers.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. That is the Israelite—or possibly the Copt—thought that Moses intended to strike him next.",
  28020: "A man came from the farthest part of the city, rushing. He said, “O Moses, the chiefs are plotting against you to kill you, so leave. Indeed, I am sincerely advising you.”",
  28021: "Then he left the city, fearful, watching cautiously. He said, “My Lord, deliver me from the wrongdoing people.”",
  28022: "When he set out toward Midian, he said, “Perhaps my Lord will guide me to the right path.”",
  28023: "When he came to the water of Midian, he found there a crowd of men watering their flocks, and apart from them he noticed two women holding their animals back [11].  He asked, “What’s the matter with you?” They said, “We do not water until the shepherds leave, and our father is an old man.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. The two women are commonly identified in later tradition as Zipporah and her younger sister, daughters of the righteous man Jethro (known in Islamic tradition as Shuʿayb), though the Qur’an itself does not name them or their father. The phrase tadhūdān (“holding back” or “keeping away”) indicates that they were restraining their flock at a distance, waiting until the male shepherds had finished watering. Their conduct suggests modesty and reserve in a setting dominated by men. Their explanation—“our father is an old man”—clarifies that economic necessity compelled them to undertake this task despite the difficulty. The scene underscores their vulnerability and introduces Moses’ first recorded act of assistance during his exile, marking a significant turning point in his life narrative.",
  28024: "So he watered their flocks for them, then withdrew [12] to the shade and said, “My Lord, I am truly in need of whatever good You may send down to me.” [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. The verb تَوَلَّىٰ إِلَى الظِّلِّ literally means “he turned aside to the shade” or “withdrew to the shade.” The phrasing conveys a quiet withdrawal after helping the two women, suggesting both physical movement and a moment of humble retreat. Exegetes often note the adab (noble restraint) reflected here: Moses does the good deed and then steps away without seeking recognition. The verse thus illustrates a broader ethical lesson—do not wait for praise for every good deed nor seek applause for acts of courage. Rather, serve with sincerity: help those in need, comfort the distressed, support the weak, and lift up those who stumble. Give charity quietly and turn away so as not to expose the vulnerability of the needy. Such was the nobility of Moses (peace be upon him): he offered help, then withdrew without seeking gratitude—and Allāh rewarded him with something far better. Always remember: one is dealing with Allāh, the Most Generous.\n[13]. The expression إِنِّي … فَقِيرٌ literally means “I am poor/in need.” In Qur’anic usage, faqīr conveys deep dependence and vulnerability before Allāh, not merely financial poverty. The phrase لِمَا أَنْزَلْتَ إِلَيَّ مِنْ خَيْرٍ (“for whatever good You may send down to me”) is deliberately open-ended, reflecting Moses’ humility and complete reliance on any provision Allāh may grant. Exegetes note the immediacy of the divine response that follows in the narrative: having left Egypt with nothing and arrived in Midian in a state of need, Moses soon receives from Allāh a home, a livelihood, and a wife. The verse thus powerfully illustrates how sincere turning to Allāh in moments of vulnerability is met with generous provision.",
  28025: "Then one of the two women [14] came to him, walking modestly [15]. She said, “My father is inviting you so that he may reward you for watering our flocks.” When Moses came to him and told him his story, the man said, “Do not fear—you are delivered from the wrongdoing people.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. I.e., Zipporah. The Qur’an itself does not name the woman; it simply refers to her as “one of the two.” Exegetes generally identify the two women as daughters of the righteous man of Midian (called Jetro), though the Qur’anic text remains deliberately non-specific.\n[15]. The phrase walking modestly conveys modesty, dignity, and moral reserve rather than mere timidity. The Qur’anic wording portrays a composed and respectful manner of conduct. From this expression, scholars often draw broader ethical guidance about cultivating ḥayāʾ (modesty): one should carry oneself with humility, avoid seeking fame or status, and refrain from self-display or showing off. This includes dressing and behaving modestly, not belittling others, supporting the weak and oppressed, and avoiding actions that expose or embarrass people—especially fellow believers.\nIf one remains unknown or unnoticed, this may itself be a blessing. The Prophet ﷺ said: “No one humbles himself for the sake of Allāh except that Allāh raises him.”. The verse thus reflects both outward propriety and an inward state of sincere, God-conscious humility.",
  28026: "One of the two daughters said, “Dear father, hire him. Indeed, the best one you can hire is the strong and trustworthy.”",
  28027: "[Later, the father] said, “I wish to marry you to one of my two daughters on the condition that you work for me for eight years. But if you complete ten, that will be a favor from you. I do not intend to make things difficult for you. You will find me—Allāh willing—among the righteous.” [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: “Righteousness is good character, and sin is what troubles your heart and what you dislike others finding out about.”\nTo strive for righteousness is to cultivate a relationship with Allāh grounded in obedience, sincerity, and moral excellence. It means developing noble character and manners, taking the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ as the model of exemplary conduct.\nFor Allāh has not called us to impurity, but to righteousness and purity of heart. When believers commit themselves to righteousness, they set themselves apart from the corruption and moral decay of the world, becoming lights of integrity, mercy, and virtue.",
  28028: "Moses said, “Let that be the agreement between you and me: whichever of the two terms I complete, there will be no claim against me. Allāh is Witness over what we say.”",
  28029: "When Moses had completed the term and was traveling with his family, he spotted a fire from the direction of the mountain. He said to his family, “Stay here. I have spotted a fire; perhaps I may bring you some news from it [17], or a burning brand from it so that you may warm yourselves.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. The phrase “some news” reflects Moses’ practical expectation that the distant fire might indicate nearby travelers or habitation from whom he could obtain directions or assistance during the night journey. In a wilderness setting, a visible fire often signaled human presence and potential safety. The narrative carries a notably subtle: the “news” he anticipated was not ordinary information but the beginning of divine revelation. The wording thus gently prepares the reader for the transition from a seemingly routine search for guidance to the momentous encounter with Allah’s guidance at the mountain.",
  28030: "But when he came to it, he was called from the right side of the valley, in the blessed place, from the tree: “O Moses, indeed I am Allāh, Lord of the worlds.”",
  28031: "“Throw down your staff.” But when he saw it writhing as if it were a snake, he turned and fled without looking back. He was called, “O Moses, approach and do not fear—surely you are among the secure.”",
  28032: "Put your hand into the opening of your garment; it will come out radiant white, without any blemish. And draw your arm close to yourself to calm your fear. These are two proofs from your Lord to Pharaoh and his chiefs. Indeed, they are a rebellious people.",
  28033: "Moses said, “My Lord, I have killed one of them, so I fear they may kill me.”",
  28034: "My brother Aaron is more eloquent than I am. So send him with me as support to strengthen and affirm me. For I fear that they will deny me.”",
  28035: "Allāh said, “We will strengthen your arm through your brother, and We will grant you both authority, so they will not reach you. Through Our signs, you and whoever follows you will be victorious.”",
  28036: "When Moses came to them with Our clear signs, they said, “This is nothing but invented magic. We have never heard of this among our forefathers.”",
  28037: "Moses said, “My Lord knows best who has come with guidance from Him and who will have the good end in the Home [of Bliss]. Indeed, the wrongdoers will never prosper.”",
  28038: "Pharaoh said, “O chiefs, I know of no god for you other than me. So kindle for me, O Haman, a fire over the clay [to bake bricks], and build for me a lofty tower so that I may look at the God of Moses. And indeed, I think he is a liar.”",
  28039: "He and his soldiers acted arrogantly in the land without right, and they thought they would never be returned to Us.",
  28040: "So We seized him and his troops and hurled them into the sea. So see how the end of the wrongdoers was.",
  28041: "We made them leaders who call to the Fire [18], and on the Day of Resurrection they will not be helped.\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. That is, they were made leaders in misguidance—examples whose pattern of tyranny would be followed by later oppressors. The verse conveys both their role in actively calling others to ruin and their becoming precedents whose destructive ways others would imitate.",
  28042: "We made a curse follow them in this world, and on the Day of Resurrection they will be among the despised.",
  28043: "We certainly gave Moses the Scripture after We had destroyed the earlier generations, as clear insights for the people and as guidance and mercy, so that they might take heed.",
  28044: "You, [O Muhammad], were not on the western side [of the mountain] when We conveyed the command to Moses, nor were you among those who witnessed it.",
  28045: "But We raised up generations [after Moses], and a long time passed over them. And you were not [O Prophet] dwelling among the people of Midian, reciting to them Our revelations—rather, We were sending messengers. [19]\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. Allah’s sending of messengers throughout history reflects His mercy and concern for humanity. He does not abandon people to confusion, but continually provides guidance so that the path to truth and salvation remains open to all. In this light, the mission of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ comes as the completion of that long chain of mercy. He was sent as the final messenger, not to a single people but to all nations, so that divine guidance might reach every heart and every land. The verse thus reminds the reader that revelation is an expression of Allah’s care for His creation—guidance extended to humanity until the very end.",
  28046: "You were not at the side of the Mount when We called out [to Moses]. But it is a mercy from your Lord that you may warn a people to whom no warner had come before you, so that they might take heed. [20]\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. When the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ was asked, “Was there ever a day worse for you than the day of Uḥud?” he replied:\n“I suffered greatly at the hands of the pagans, and the hardest thing I ever endured from them was the day of al-‘Aqabah. I presented myself to their chief, but he did not respond to what I hoped for. I left, deeply distressed, not knowing where I was going, until I found myself at Qarn al-Tha‘ālib. There I looked up and saw a cloud shading me. Within it was Gabriel, who called me and said: ‘Allāh has heard what your people said to you and how they responded to you. He has sent to you the Angel of the Mountains—so that you may command him as you will.’\nThen the Angel of the Mountains greeted me and said: ‘Muhammad, Allāh has heard what your people have said to you. I am the Angel of the Mountains, and your Lord has sent me to you so that you may instruct me regarding them. If you wish, I will bring together the two mountains of Makkah to crush them.’ But the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said: ‘Rather, I hope that Allāh will bring forth from their descendants people who will worship Allāh alone, associating nothing with Him.",
  28047: "So that they would not say, should a calamity strike them because of what their own hands have sent ahead, “Our Lord, why did You not send us a messenger so we might follow Your revelations and be among the believers?”",
  28048: "But when the Truth came to them from Us, they said, “Why has he not been given the like of what was given to Moses?” Did they not disbelieve in what Moses was given before? They said, “Two works of magic supporting one another.” And they said, “Indeed, we disbelieve in both.” [21]\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. I.e., they accused both revelations of being mutually supporting “magic.” They were referring to the revelation given to Moses (the Torah) and the Qur’an, claiming that each merely reinforced the other. The charge reflects a recurring Qur’anic pattern: when confronted with consistent divine guidance across generations, deniers reinterpret that very consistency as evidence of fabrication rather than truth.",
  28049: "Say, “Then bring a scripture from Allāh that offers better guidance than these two, so that I may follow it, if you are truthful.”",
  28050: "But if they do not respond to you, then know that they follow their desires. Who is more astray than one who follows his own desires without guidance from Allāh? Indeed, Allāh does not guide the wrongdoing people.",
  28051: "We have certainly conveyed the message to them time and again, so that they may take heed. [22]\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. The verb وَصَّلْنَا conveys repeated and sustained delivery. That is, the divine message was not sent once and left unexplained; rather, it was conveyed to them again and again through revelation, reminders, and successive messengers. The wording underscores Allah’s mercy and the removal of every excuse—guidance was made to reach them in a continuous and unmistakable manner so that they might reflect and take heed.",
  28052: "Those to whom We gave the Scripture before this [Qur’an]—they believe in it. [23]\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. I.e., among the People of the Scripture were those who, upon hearing the Qur’an, recognized in it the same divine truth found in earlier revelation and therefore believed in it. Exegetes often cite Jewish scholars such as ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām and others who, being well grounded in previous scripture, were able to discern this continuity of guidance. The verse highlights that sincere familiarity with earlier revelation can prepare the heart to recognize the Qur’an’s authenticity when it appears.",
  28053: "When this [Qur’an] is recited to them, they say, “We believe in it; indeed, it is the truth from our Lord. Indeed, even before it we were Muslims.” [24]\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. The phrase “we were, before it, Muslims,” that is, “in submission.” In this context, it refers to individuals—often understood to be among the People of the Scripture —who recognized the truth of the Qur’an upon hearing it because they were already devoted to sincere submission to Allāh. The term Muslim here retains its primary Qur’anic sense of one who submits to Allāh. The statement underscores continuity in divine revelation: those who were genuinely faithful to earlier scripture find in the Qur’an a confirmation of what they already believed.",
  28054: "They will be given their reward twice for their patience. They repel evil with good [25], and they spend from what We have provided them.\\n\\nFootnotes: [25]. The expression “repel evil with good” reflects a central Qur’anic ethic: responding to harm, hostility, or wrongdoing not with retaliation, but with patience, forgiveness, and righteous conduct. Scholars explain that this includes meeting offense with forbearance, harshness with gentleness, and wrongdoing with acts of goodness. Such moral restraint transforms conflict, softens hearts, and distinguishes those who truly embody divine guidance.",
  28055: "When they hear vain talk, they turn away from it and say, “For us are our deeds, and for you are your deeds. Peace be upon you; we do not seek [the ways of] the ignorant.” [26]\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. I.e., they respond to offensive or meaningless speech with dignified restraint rather than argument or retaliation. The phrase “Peace be upon you” here is not merely a greeting but a courteous disengagement—an expression of goodwill coupled with a refusal to be drawn into fruitless dispute. Scholars note that “we do not seek the ignorant” means they neither desire the company of the foolish nor wish to engage in the kind of behavior characterized by ignorance. The verse thus portrays a hallmark of spiritual maturity: rising above provocation through composure, restraint, and principled distance.",
  28056: "You cannot guide whomever you like, but Allāh guides whomever He wills, and He knows best those who are rightly guided.",
  28057: "They say, “If we follow the guidance with you, we will be snatched from our land.” Have We not established for them a secure sanctuary [in Makkah] [27] to which are brought fruits of every kind as provision from Us? But most of them do not know.\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]. I.e., the Sacred Sanctuary of Makkah (the ḥaram), which Allāh made a place of security despite the surrounding region’s frequent instability and tribal conflict. The verse reminds the Quraysh that the very safety and prosperity they enjoyed—along with the steady flow of goods and fruits brought to Makkah from distant lands—were themselves signs of divine provision. Their fear of losing security by following the Prophet ﷺ is thus exposed as misplaced, since the One who established their sanctuary is fully able to preserve it.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ declared:\n\"Allāh has sanctified Makkah; its thorny plants must not be cut, its game must not be hunted, and lost property must not be taken except to make public announcement of it.”",
  28058: "How many towns have We destroyed that grew arrogant in their way of life. So these are their dwellings, not inhabited after them except briefly—and We alone were the Inheritors.",
  28059: "Your Lord would never destroy the towns until He had sent to their central town a messenger reciting to them Our verses. And We never destroy towns except when their people are wrongdoers.",
  28060: "Whatever you have been given is only the enjoyment of the worldly life and its adornment. But what is with Allāh is far better and more lasting. Will you not then understand?",
  28061: "Is one whom We have promised a good promise—which he will surely meet—like one to whom We gave the enjoyment of the worldly life, then on the Day of Resurrection he will be among those brought forth [for punishment]?",
  28062: "And [beware of] the Day when He will call them and say, \"Where are those whom you claimed to be My partners in worship?\"",
  28063: "Those against whom the Word has come to pass will say, “Our Lord, these are the ones we led astray; we led them astray as we ourselves went astray. We disown them before You. It was not us they worshiped.”",
  28064: "Then it will be said, “Call upon your associate-gods!” So they will call upon them, but they will not respond to them. And they will see the punishment—wishing they had been guided.",
  28065: "On the Day He will call them and say, “How did you respond to the messengers?”",
  28066: "Then all arguments will be obscured to them on that Day [28], and they will not ask one another.\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. The expression literally conveys that the “reports” or “answers” will become obscured or inaccessible to them. In the overwhelming shock of the Day of Resurrection, they will find themselves unable to produce excuses or coherent replies. The verse thus portrays a moment of utter helplessness: the arguments they once relied upon in worldly life will fail them completely, leaving them speechless and unable even to consult one another.",
  28067: "But as for the one who repents, believes, and does righteous deeds—such a person may be among the successful.",
  28068: "Your Lord creates whatever He wills and chooses; the choice is not theirs [29]. Glory be to Allāh, exalted is He above what they associate with Him.\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. I.e., the ultimate choice and decree belong solely to Allāh. The verse responds to those who presumed they could dictate who should receive prophethood or divine favor. It affirms that just as creation is exclusively Allah’s act, so too is selection—both proceeding from His perfect knowledge and wisdom. Human preference or objection carries no weight against the divine will.\nIn this spirit of submitting one’s choice to divine wisdom, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ used to teach his companions the prayer of istikhārah (seeking the best choice) in all matters, just as he taught them a sūrah from the Qur’an. He said: “If any of you is concerned about a decision, let him pray two units of non-obligatory prayer, then supplicate:\nO Allāh, I seek Your guidance through Your knowledge, and I seek strength through Your power, and I ask You from Your immense grace. You are capable while I am not; You know while I do not; and You are the Knower of the unseen. O Allāh, if You know that this matter (to be named) is good for me in my religion, my livelihood, and the outcome of my affairs (in this world and the next), then decree it for me, facilitate it for me, and bless it for me.\nBut if You know that this matter is bad for me in my religion, my livelihood, and the outcome of my affairs (in this world and the next), then turn me away from it and turn it away from me, and decree for me what is good wherever it may be, and make me content with it.",
  28069: "Your Lord knows what their chests conceal and what they reveal.",
  28070: "He is Allāh—there is no god except Him. To Him belongs all praise in this world and in the Hereafter. His is the judgment, and to Him you will all be returned.",
  28071: "Say, “Tell me, if Allāh were to make the night perpetual over you until the Day of Resurrection, what god other than Allāh could bring you light? Will you not then listen?” [30]\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. The word sarmadā denotes something continuous, unending, and without interruption. The verse invites the listener to imagine a perpetual night extending until the Day of Resurrection—a scenario that would suspend normal life and human activity. By posing this clear conjectural, the Qur’an draws attention to the often-overlooked blessing of the natural alternation between night and day. The rhetorical question underscores human dependence on Allāh alone for light, order, and the basic rhythms that sustain life.",
  28072: "Say, “Tell me, if Allāh were to make the day perpetual over you until the Day of Resurrection, what god other than Allāh could bring you the night in which you rest? Will you not then see?” [31]\\n\\nFootnotes: [31]. As in the previous verse, the word sarmadā conveys the sense of something continuous and unending. Here the listener is asked to imagine a perpetual daytime lasting until the Day of Resurrection. Just as endless night would disrupt human life, unbroken daylight would likewise deprive people of the rest and stillness essential for physical and psychological well-being. The verse highlights the divine wisdom in the alternation of night and day: light for activity and livelihood, and darkness for rest and restoration. The closing appeal—“Will you not then see?”—invites reflection on these everyday signs of Allah’s mercy that are often taken for granted.\nSleep itself is one of Allah’s subtle blessings. It restores physical strength, renews mental clarity, and refreshes the spirit. The Qur’an indicates that sleep serves not only bodily recovery but also carries spiritual significance. Islamic teachings describe sleep as a temporary separation of the soul from the body—akin, though not identical, to the state of death. Awakening from sleep is therefore viewed as a minor resurrection, as the soul is returned to the body.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ would say upon awakening: “Praise be to Allāh, who has restored us to life after causing us to die, and to Him is the Resurrection.”",
  28073: "And out of His mercy He has made for you the night and the day, so that you may rest in it, seek His bounty, and so that you may give thanks.",
  28074: "And on the Day He will call them and say, “Where are those you claimed to be My partners?”",
  28075: "We shall bring forth from every nation a witness and say [to the polytheists], “Bring your proof.” Then they will know that the truth belongs to Allāh alone, and whatever they used to invent will vanish from them.",
  28076: "Indeed, Qārūn [Korah] was from the people of Moses, but he oppressed them. We had granted him such treasures whose keys alone would have weighed down a band of strong men. When his people said to him, “Do not exult. Indeed, Allāh does not like the exultant. [32]\\n\\nFootnotes: [32]. Qārūn (Korah) was a wealthy man from the people of Moses who became arrogant and oppressive because of his riches. The description of his treasure keys weighing down a band of strong men is a clear Qur’anic overstatement emphasizing the immense scale of his wealth. His people’s warning, “Do not exult,” is therefore a rebuke against boastful pride and self-satisfied arrogance—not against lawful joy or gratitude. The verse warns that unchecked prosperity can lead to spiritual corruption when it breeds arrogance instead of thankfulness.\nPride is a path to ruin: when arrogance takes root, disgrace soon follows, whereas humility opens the door to wisdom. Allāh detests every proud and arrogant heart. Wealth that breeds arrogance is a form of poverty; knowledge that leads to conceit is ignorance; a position that inspires tyranny is moral decay; and power that produces oppression is weakness in disguise. True wealth, honor, and knowledge belong to the humble.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ—though the greatest of creation—was among the most humble of people. He would milk his own goat, mend his sandals, and sew his clothes. He even raced playfully with his wife ʿĀʾishah and would gently wipe away the tears of his wife Ṣafiyyah, comforting her with tenderness and compassion.\nOn one occasion, when his companions divided the tasks of preparing a sheep for a meal—one saying, “I will slaughter it,” and another, “I will skin it”—the Prophet ﷺ said, “And I will gather the firewood,” thereby demonstrating humility through action, not merely words.",
  28077: "Seek the Home of the Hereafter with what Allāh has granted you, but do not forget your share of this world. Do good as Allāh has been good to you, and do not seek to spread corruption in the land. Indeed, Allāh does not like the corrupters.",
  28078: "He said, “I was only given it because of knowledge I possess.” Did he not know that Allāh had destroyed before him generations mightier than him in strength and greater in accumulation [of wealth]? And the guilty will not be questioned about their sins. [33]\\n\\nFootnotes: [33]. I.e., their guilt will be so evident on the Day of Judgment that no formal questioning for information will be necessary. The verse does not negate the broader Qur’anic teaching that people will be interrogated about their deeds; rather, it emphasizes the overwhelming clarity of the criminals’ wrongdoing and the immediacy of their condemnation. Exegetes explain that this refers to a moment of exposure and disgrace in which excuses are futile and denial impossible.",
  28079: "So he came out before his people in his splendor. Those who desired the life of this world said, “O would that we had the like of what Qārūn [Korah] has been given! He is indeed a man of great fortune.”",
  28080: "But those who had been given knowledge said, “Woe to you! Allah’s reward is far better for whoever believes and does righteous deeds, and none will attain it except the patient.”",
  28081: "So We caused the earth to swallow him and his home. There was none to help him against Allāh—nor could he defend himself.",
  28082: "Those who had wished for his position just the day before began to say, “Oh! Indeed Allāh expands provision for whom He wills among His servants and restricts it. Had Allāh not conferred favor on us, He would have made the earth swallow us as well. Oh! Indeed, the disbelievers will not succeed.”",
  28083: "That Home of the Hereafter—Paradise—We shall assign it to those who do not seek self-exaltation on the earth nor corruption, and the good outcome is for the righteous.",
  28084: "Whoever comes with a good deed will have something better than it; and whoever comes with an evil deed—those who commit evil will not be recompensed except for what they used to do.",
  28085: "Indeed, He who has ordained the Qur’an for you will surely bring you back to your place of return [34]. Say, “My Lord knows best who has come with guidance and who is in manifest error.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [34]. That is, He will return you to Makkah in this life—fulfilling His promise of restoring you to your homeland—and He will return you to Paradise in the Hereafter, granting you the ultimate place of return and reward.",
  28086: "You were not expecting this Book to be sent down to you. Rather, it is a mercy from your Lord. So never be a supporter of the disbelievers. [35]\\n\\nFootnotes: [35]. I.e., the Prophet ﷺ had not anticipated receiving revelation or bearing the burden of prophethood; the Qur’an was bestowed upon him purely as an act of divine mercy. The instruction that follows is not a warning that he had actually supported disbelief or made concessions to it, but rather a firm directive reinforcing his complete disassociation from the disbelievers’ cause. More broadly, the verse teaches that those honored with divine guidance must never lend moral, ideological, or practical support to falsehood or to those who knowingly oppose the truth.",
  28087: "Do not let them turn you away from Allah’s verses after they have been sent down to you. Instead, call all people to your Lord, and never be among the polytheists.",
  28088: "Do not call upon any other god alongside Allāh. There is no deity except Him. Everything will perish except His Face. His is the judgment, and to Him you will all be returned. [36]\\n\\nFootnotes: [36]. The phrase “except His Face” is a Qur’anic expression conveying the permanence of Allāh in contrast to the perishability of all created things. Literally rendered “His Face,” the term wajh in Arabic can denote the face, countenance, or presence, but in religious usage it signifies Allāh’s enduring Essence or Being. Exegetes variously understood the phrase to mean that only Allāh Himself remains, or that only what is done sincerely for His sake endures in lasting value. The wording affirms absolute divine permanence and sovereignty: all existence is transient except the Eternal.",
  29001: "Alif Lām Mīm. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2: verse 1.",
  29002: "Do people think that they will be left to say, “We believe,” [2] and that they will not be tested?\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. This refers to the truthfulness of their claim when they say, “There is no god but Allāh, and Muhammad is His servant and messenger.” The testing of our faith is actually for our own benefit.\nBelievers should face every trial with patience, gratitude, and trust in Allāh, knowing that the testing of faith nurtures perseverance. Too often we overlook the link between hardship and spiritual growth, yet our temporary difficulties can bring about lasting spiritual strength and reward. As the Qur’an repeatedly teaches, our trials—though heavy when we endure them—are in reality a means of purification, elevation, and strengthening of character.\nSuffering produces perseverance, and perseverance refines the believer. When one allows perseverance to complete its work, it leads to maturity, spiritual completeness, and a heart that lacks nothing essential. Thus, every test is an opportunity for growth, and every hardship carries within it the seeds of a greater eternal reward.",
  29003: "We certainly tested those before them, so Allāh will surely make evident those who are truthful [3], and He will surely make evident the liars.\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. Blessed and truly successful is the one who remains steadfast in trials and perseveres through temptations. For when a believer endures the test and proves sincere and faithful, he receives the noble reward from Allāh—the reward that He has promised to those who love Him, obey Him, and rely upon Him with full trust. Such perseverance is a mark of true faith, and its fruit is both honor in this world and everlasting reward in the Hereafter.\nThat is, Allāh—exalted is He—tests His servants in order to distinguish the sincere from the false, as He did with the nations before them. And Allāh knows best who is rightly guided.",
  29004: "Or do those who commit evil deeds think that they can escape Us? How evil is what they judge!",
  29005: "Whoever hopes for the meeting with Allāh [4]—then indeed, the term set by Allāh is surely coming. And He is the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. It is related in the authentic Sunnah that the Prophet ﷺ said: “Allāh Almighty says: Whoever comes with a good deed will have the reward of ten like it, and even more. Whoever comes with an evil deed will be recompensed for one evil deed like it, or he will be forgiven. Whoever draws near to Me by a handspan, I draw near to him by an arm’s length; and whoever draws near to Me by an arm’s length, I draw near to him by a fathom. Whoever comes to Me walking, I will come to him running. And whoever meets Me with sins filling the earth, without associating any partners with Me, I will meet him with as much forgiveness.”\nThis ḥadīth, transmitted as a divine saying (ḥadīth qudsī), explains the meaning of Allāh’s trials and the virtue of sincerity. It teaches that the servant’s efforts, though small, are multiplied by Allāh’s grace, and that His mercy far outweighs His wrath. The believers are therefore encouraged to draw near to their Lord in obedience, for whoever seeks Allāh sincerely will find Him ready with mercy and forgiveness.\nThus, whoever gives precedence to Allāh in his life—placing faith, obedience, and remembrance before all other concerns—will witness Allāh arranging his affairs, guiding his steps, and bringing about good in all circumstances. Whether one is new on the path to Allāh or has been steadfast for many years, the duty of seeking closeness to Allāh never ends. For knowledge of Allāh, love of Him, and reliance upon Him are ranks that continually increase, and the successful are those who constantly strive to know their Lord better.",
  29006: "Whoever strives does so only for his own benefit. Indeed, Allāh has no need of all the worlds. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. Allāh—glorified and exalted is He—is absolutely free of need. He is the One who is Self-Sufficient, Self-Existent, and Independent of all creation. Nothing benefits Him, and nothing harms Him; rather, all things stand in need of Him at every moment.\nAlthough Allāh has no need for His servants, He is Most Merciful toward them, and He wills good for them. He loves those who believe, obey, and draw near to Him with sincerity, and He rewards them out of His bounty and grace. Thus, His love is the love of Majesty and Generosity, not a need or dependency—far above is He from all imperfection. And it is from His mercy that He invites His servants to a relationship of faith, worship, and nearness to Him.",
  29007: "As for those who believe [6] and do righteous deeds, We will surely expiate from them their evil deeds and reward them according to the best of what they used to do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. That is, those who believe in the Oneness of Allāh—pure Islamic monotheism—and in His Messenger Muhammad ﷺ, and who remain firm upon their faith. They do not abandon their belief because of the harm, hostility, or persecution they face from the pagans and unbelievers. Rather, they hold fast to their faith with patience and steadfastness, trusting in Allāh and seeking His reward.",
  29008: "We have enjoined upon man to be good to his parents [7]. But if they both strive to make you associate with Me what you have no knowledge of, do not obey them. To Me is your return; then I will inform you of what you used to do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. This refers to Allāh’s repeated command that children show excellence, kindness, and dutifulness toward their parents. A child must obey his parents in all matters that do not involve disobedience to Allāh, for obedience to them is among the greatest acts of righteousness and is highly pleasing to the Lord.\nIn the Qur’an and Sunnah, honoring one’s parents is placed immediately after the worship of Allāh, highlighting its immense status. Showing love, respect, soft speech, humility, and service to one’s parents is a mark of true faith.\nBut as for children who belittle, disrespect, or reject the rightful authority and guidance of their parents, such behavior leads only to ruin and loss. Allāh has warned strongly against arrogance or harshness toward parents, saying: “Do not even say to them a word of irritation, nor repel them, but speak to them noble words” (Qur’ān 17:23).\nThus, honoring parents brings blessings, ease, and divine mercy, while mistreating them brings hardship and regret. Success in this world and the next is tied to righteousness toward one’s parents.",
  29009: "Those who believe and do righteous deeds, We will surely admit them among the righteous [in Paradise].",
  29010: "Among the people are those who say, “We believe in Allāh,” but when they are harmed in the cause of Allāh, they deem the persecution of people as equal to the punishment of Allāh. Yet when a victory comes from your Lord, they will surely say, “Indeed, we were with you.” Is not Allāh most knowing of what is within the chests of all people?",
  29011: "Allāh will surely make evident those who truly believe, and He will surely make evident the hypocrites. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. That is, Allāh tests His servants with ease and hardship, prosperity and poverty, in order to distinguish the sincere from the false, and the righteous from the wicked. This distinction is for their sake—not because Allāh needs to learn anything new, for He knew their states long before testing them. Rather, the trial manifests outwardly what is within the chests, so that the truthful may be honored for their truthfulness and the hypocrites exposed for their hypocrisy. Thus, testing is a means of purification, elevation, and the establishment of proof against the people.",
  29012: "Those who disbelieve say to those who believe, “Follow our way, and we will bear the burden of your sins.” But they will never bear any of their sins at all. Indeed, they are surely liars.",
  29013: "They will surely bear their own burdens, and burdens [9] along with their burdens. And they will surely be questioned on the Day of Resurrection about what they used to fabricate.\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. That is, they will bear an additional burden for those whom they misled and caused to go astray—sins which they invited others toward and encouraged them to commit. Yet this extra burden will not diminish the burden of the followers in the least, for every soul will carry its own load of sin. Thus, the leaders in misguidance bear their own sins as well as the sins of those they led astray, while each follower also bears the full weight of his own wrongdoing.",
  29014: "Indeed, We sent Noah to his people, and he remained among them for a thousand years minus fifty. Then the flood overtook them while they were wrongdoers [10].\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. That is, they persisted in wrongdoing by associating others with Allāh in worship—remaining steadfast upon polytheism, refusing His oneness, and rejecting the call of His messenger. Their injustice was their obstinate devotion to false gods and idols, despite clear proofs being presented to them.",
  29015: "So We saved him and those who were with him in the Ark, and made it a sign for all peoples.",
  29016: "And [remember] Abraham when he said to his people, \"Worship Allāh alone and be mindful of Him; that is best for you, if only you knew.\"",
  29017: "You worship nothing but idols besides Allāh and fabricate falsehood. Indeed, those whom you worship besides Allāh have no power to provide for you. So seek provision from Allāh, worship Him alone, and be grateful to Him. To Him you will all be returned.",
  29018: "And if you deny [the Truth], then many nations before you also denied [11]. And the Messenger is responsible only for conveying the message clearly.\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. That is, if you deny this message, you are not the first to do so; many nations before you likewise rejected their prophets and were destroyed for their wickedness. When corruption and disbelief prevail in a people, sin spreads throughout the land, and moral restraint is lost. Those who turn away from the guidance of Allāh are left to their desires—following lusts, whims, and self-indulgence—and their society descends into disorder and ruin.\nBut as for those who hold fast to righteousness and obey the commands of Allāh, they are the ones who ultimately succeed, both in this world and the next. For a nation that upholds the guidance of Allāh, establishes justice, and obeys His laws will find blessing, harmony, and true happiness.",
  29019: "Have they not considered how Allāh begins the creation, then repeats it? [12] Indeed, that is easy for Allāh.\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. That is, Allāh—exalted is He—who brought creation into existence the first time, will raise it once more after death for the Resurrection. He will revive the dead from their graves, gather them together, and bring them forth for the Day of Judgment. There, every soul will be recompensed for what it earned: the righteous will be rewarded with mercy and paradise, and the wrongdoers will face the consequences of their deeds. Thus, the One who originated creation without precedent has no difficulty in resurrecting it and restoring it anew.",
  29020: "Say, “Travel through the land and see how He began the creation; then Allāh will produce the later creation. Indeed, Allāh is over all things competent.”",
  29021: "He punishes whom He wills and shows mercy to whom He wills, and to Him you will all be returned [for judgment].",
  29022: "You cannot escape Him on earth nor in the heaven. And you have no protector or helper besides Allāh.",
  29023: "And those who disbelieve in the signs of Allāh and the meeting with Him—it is they who have despaired of My mercy, and for them is a painful punishment.",
  29024: "The only response of his people was, “Kill him or burn him.” But Allāh saved him from the fire. Indeed, in this are signs for a people who believe.",
  29025: "He said, “You have only taken idols instead of Allāh out of mutual affection among yourselves in this worldly life [13]. But on the Day of Resurrection, you will disown one another and curse one another. Your abode will be the Fire, and you will have no helpers.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. Their devotion to idols was not based on proof or guidance, but on the social ties and inherited loyalties that bound them together. Every community had its chosen idol, around which their unity and collective identity were built. Idol-worship also linked them to their forefathers; they claimed that these gods were the traces and traditions of their ancestors, and this false appeal to heritage strengthened their attachment.\nMoreover, the leaders and chiefs among the polytheists encouraged the masses to worship these idols, using them as a means of controlling and rallying their followers. Thus the worship of idols became a chain of mutual attachment—between people and their forefathers, and between leaders and followers.\nBut on the Day of Resurrection, all these flimsy and rotten bonds will be severed. Each will turn against the other; followers will renounce their leaders, leaders will disown their people, and every person will attempt to cast his sin upon someone else. They will curse one another and repudiate each other’s deeds, for all worldly ties built on falsehood will collapse before the truth.",
  29026: "So Lot believed in him, and Abraham said, “Indeed, I am migrating to my Lord. Surely, He is the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.”",
  29027: "We granted him Isaac and Jacob, and placed prophethood and the Scripture among his descendants, and gave him his reward in this world, and indeed, in the Hereafter he will surely be among the righteous.",
  29028: "And [remember] Lot, when he said to his people, “Indeed, you commit an indecency such as no one among all peoples has ever committed before you.” [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. That is, among all the creatures of Allāh, was there anyone before you who committed this abhorrent and unnatural act? No nation, community, or people had ever engaged in such corruption prior to the people of Lot. Thus their sin was unprecedented in its perversity, blatant defiance, and moral degeneration, and this is why the Qur’ān emphasizes that no one among all the worlds had ever committed such an indecency before them.",
  29029: "Indeed, you approach men [sexually], cut off the way [15], and commit evil in your assemblies. But the response of his people was only that they said, “Bring upon us the punishment of Allāh, if you are of the truthful.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. Meaning: you cut off the way—either by committing highway robbery, attacking travelers, and spreading fear along the roads; or, as some scholars explained, by “cutting off offspring,” since you approached men instead of women, thereby abandoning the natural means through which progeny is continued.",
  29030: "Lot said, “My Lord, support me against the corrupt people.”",
  29031: "When Our angel-messengers came to Abraham with good news, they said, “We are to destroy the people of this town, for its people are persistent wrongdoers.”",
  29032: "He said, “But Lot is there! [16]” They said, “We know best who is there. We will surely save him and his family—except his wife; she will be among those who remain behind [to perish].”\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. In Islam, Lut (Lot) is regarded as a prophet of Allāh and a righteous servant sent to guide his people away from immorality and corruption.\nThe Qur'an honors him as truthful, upright, and innocent of the accusations found in the Bible. In Book of Genesis 19, it is stated that after the destruction of Sodom and Gomorrah, Lot’s daughters made him drunk with wine and slept with him while he was unaware, and they became pregnant. Although the Biblical text describes the event rather than approving it, Islam rejects this account and considers prophets protected from such immoral acts.\nAccording to the Qur’an:\nLut called his people to worship Allāh and live morally.\nHis people rejected his message and persisted in wrongdoing.\nAllāh saved Lut and the believers, while the corrupt cities were destroyed.\nThus, in Islam, Lut is remembered as a noble, pure, and obedient prophet.",
  29033: "When Our angel-messengers came to Lot, he was distressed for them and felt straitened by their arrival. But they said, “Do not fear [17], nor grieve; we shall surely deliver you and your family—except your wife; she is among those who will remain behind [to perish].”\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. Whenever Allāh reassures His prophets and righteous servants, He commands them not to fear, for His protection is with them. This pattern—“Do not fear… for We are with you” or “Fear not… for We shall save you”—is repeatedly found throughout the Qur’ān. Allāh’s nearness, support, and protection are the greatest cures to fear.\nThus, when the angels came to Lot in a form that caused him distress, they calmed him with the Divine message: “Fear not, nor grieve.” Likewise, Allāh said to Moses and Aaron, “Do not fear, for I am with you, hearing and seeing,” and to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said, “Do not grieve; indeed Allāh is with us.”\nHow comforting it is for the believer to know that wherever life leads, he is under the watch of a Lord who is Near and Responsive. Even in the darkest trials, the one who trusts in Allāh need not fear any evil, for Allāh is with him—guiding, protecting, and supporting him.",
  29034: "Indeed, We will bring down upon the people of this town a punishment from the sky, for their defiantly disobedient conduct.",
  29035: "We have surely left therein a clear sign for a people who understand.",
  29036: "And to Midian We sent their brother Shuʿaib. He said, “My people, worship Allāh, and hope for the Last Day, and do not go about in the land spreading corruption.”",
  29037: "But they rejected him, so the earthquake seized them, and they became corpses in their homes, fallen prone.",
  29038: "And ʿĀd and Thamūd—[We destroyed them as well], which is clear to you from their ruined dwellings. Satan made their deeds seem fair to them and kept them from the path, though they were people of insight.",
  29039: "And [We also destroyed] Korah, Pharaoh, and Hāmān—Moses surely came to them with clear signs, but they acted arrogantly in the land [18], yet they could not escape [Us].\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. Their arrogance led to their ruin. For whenever a people exalt themselves unjustly, Allāh brings them low; but those who humble themselves before Him, He elevates and honors. Thus their pride blinded them to the truth and prevented them from accepting the clear signs that came to them.",
  29040: "So each of them We seized for their sin: among them were those whom We sent a violent storm [19]; among them were whom We seized by a blast [20]; among them were whom We caused the earth to swallow [21]; and among them those whom We drowned [22]. It was not Allāh who wronged them, but it was they who wronged themselves.\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. That is, the people of Lot, upon whom a storm of stones was sent as punishment for their persistent indecency and defiant disobedience.\n[20]. Referring to Thamūd and the people of Shuʿaib, who were seized by a mighty blast after rejecting their prophets and persisting in corruption.\n[21]. That is, Korah (Qārūn), who was swallowed by the earth as punishment for his arrogance, greed, and rebellion against Allāh.\n[22]. Referring to the people of Noah and Pharaoh and his followers, who were drowned after prolonged disbelief, oppression, and rejection of divine warnings.",
  29041: "The parable of those who take protectors besides Allāh is that of a spider that builds a house for itself—yet the frailest of houses is the spider’s house, if only they knew.",
  29042: "Indeed, Allāh knows that whatever they invoke besides Him is nothing at all. He is the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.",
  29043: "These parables We set forth for the people, but none understand them except the people of knowledge.",
  29044: "Allāh created the heavens and the earth for a true purpose. Indeed, in this there is a sign for the believers.",
  29045: "Recite [O Prophet] what has been revealed to you of the Book [23], and establish prayer. Indeed, prayer restrains one from indecency and wrongdoing [24]. The remembrance of Allāh is truly greater [25]. And Allāh knows whatever you do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. This command emphasizes the recitation of the Qur’an together with reflection and adherence. The Qur’an is the Word of Allāh and His final guidance to humanity, containing wisdom, clarity, mercy, and light. Through its recitation the believer gains insight; through belief in it, safety and salvation; and through acting upon it, righteousness. The Qur’an nourishes the heart, strengthens the spirit, and directs one to the straight path. Part of fulfilling this command also includes establishing the prayer regularly and, when possible, in congregation—especially in the mosque—joining the community of those who submit themselves to Allāh.\n[24]. One of the greatest benefits of prayer is its role in restraining a person from indecency and wrongdoing. Through sincere and consistent prayer, the heart is purified, desires are disciplined, and resistance to temptation is strengthened. In prayer, the servant seeks Allāh’s aid in avoiding sinful choices and in being guided toward what is right. Thus, prayer serves as both a shield against moral corruption and a source of strength for obedience.\n[25]. The remembrance of Allāh (adh-dhikr) is among the greatest acts of worship. It may be performed with the heart, the tongue, or both, and includes glorifying Allāh, praising Him, seeking His forgiveness, and engaging in any act that draws one closer to Him. Though often simple in wording, its reward is immense and its effect upon the soul profound. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Two words that are light on the tongue, heavy on the scale, and beloved to the Most Merciful: Subḥānallāhi wa biḥamdih, Subḥānallāhil-ʿAẓīm.” Remembrance strengthens faith, brings tranquility to the heart, and draws the servant nearer to his Lord.",
  29046: "Do not argue with the People of the Scripture except in the best and most gracious manner—except with those among them who act unjustly. And say, “We believe in what has been revealed to us and what has been revealed to you. Our God and your God is One, and to Him we submit.” [26]\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. This verse affirms the shared foundation of monotheism between Muslims and the People of the Scripture, while also distinguishing between pure monotheism and beliefs that deviate from it. Although Jews and Christians originally received revelation, many among them later introduced doctrines inconsistent with the absolute oneness of God. In Islam, monotheism is preserved in its pure form: worship is directed to Allāh alone, without partners, intermediaries, or deified beings. For this reason, Muslim places of worship remain sanctified exclusively for the remembrance of Allāh, whereas churches may contain practices or symbols and images contrary to the pure monotheism.\nMuslims also affirm the prophethood of ʿĪsā (Jesus) عليه السلام—an honored Messenger who was sent to the Children of Israel with clear signs. The Jews deny him, just as the Christians deny the final Messenger, Muḥammad ﷺ, even though he was foretold in earlier scriptures. Since Allāh has now sent His final revelation through Muḥammad ﷺ, it becomes obligatory upon all people, including the People of the Scripture [Jews and Christians), to follow him; for rejecting a Messenger after evidence has come is equivalent to rejecting the truth.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: “By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, you will not enter Paradise until you believe, and you will not truly believe until you love one another.” True love and brotherhood are realized through obedience to Allāh: worshipping none but Him, believing in His Messenger, and walking in humility, unity, and compassion with the believers.",
  29047: "Thus We have sent down to you [O Prophet] the Book. So those to whom We gave the Scripture believed in it [27], and among these [pagans] are those who believed in it. And none rejects Our verses except the disbelievers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]. Such as ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām. Those among the People of the Scripture who possessed sincere knowledge recognized the Qur’an as truth, for they found in their own Scriptures descriptions of the final Revelation and the prophethood of Muḥammad ﷺ. The Torah and the Gospel contained signs of the coming Messenger—his qualities, his community, and the nature of the revelation given to him. When the Qur’an was revealed, it corresponded perfectly with what they already knew, and thus those who were honest and learned among them believed in it.",
  29048: "You did not recite any Scripture before it, nor did you write it with your right hand. Otherwise, the falsifiers would have had cause for doubt.",
  29049: "Rather, this [Qur’an] is clear verses [preserved] in the hearts of those who have been granted knowledge. None rejects Our verses except the wrongdoers.",
  29050: "They say, “Why is it that no signs have been sent down to him from his Lord?” Say, “The signs are only with Allāh, and I am only a clear warner.”",
  29051: "Is it not enough for them that We have sent down to you the Book, which is recited to them? Indeed, in this is mercy and a reminder for people who believe. [28]\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. The Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said:\n“No envy is justified except in two cases: a person whom Allāh has taught the Qur’an, and he recites it by night and by day; and a person whom Allāh has given wealth, and he spends it in the way Allāh commands.”\nThe “envy” mentioned here does not refer to blameworthy jealousy (ḥasad), but to ghibṭah—a praiseworthy longing. Ghibṭah means to admire a blessing given to another and to wish for something similar, without desiring that the other person lose that blessing.\nThese two cases represent the highest forms of divine favor because they unite a gift from Allāh with righteous action. The first is knowledge of the Qur’an accompanied by consistent recitation and living by its guidance, which naturally inspires others. The second is wealth that is used rightly—through generosity, charity, and support of good causes—transforming material resources into a means of spiritual elevation.\nWhile Islam discourages destructive envy, it encourages this form of virtuous aspiration, in which one seeks blessings that lead to obedience to Allāh, admiration of righteousness, and imitation of good deeds. Such ghibṭah elevates the soul rather than corrupting it.",
  29052: "Say, “Allāh is sufficient as a Witness between me and you. He knows whatever is in the heavens and the earth. Those who believe in falsehood and disbelieve in Allāh—it is they who are the losers.”",
  29053: "They ask you [O Prophet] to hasten the punishment. Had it not been for an appointed term, the punishment would surely have come to them. And it will surely come upon them suddenly, while they do not perceive.",
  29054: "They ask you to hasten the punishment. Yet Hell [Jahannam] will certainly encompass the disbelievers.",
  29055: "On the Day when the punishment will cover them from above them and from beneath their feet, and He will say, “Taste [the punishment] for what you used to do.”",
  29056: "O My servants who believe, indeed My earth is spacious—so worship Me alone.",
  29057: "Every soul will taste death; then to Us you will all be returned.",
  29058: "As for those who believe and do righteous deeds—We will surely settle them in lofty chambers of Paradise, beneath which rivers flow, abiding therein forever. How excellent is the reward of the workers [of good].",
  29059: "Those who are patient, and who place their trust in their Lord [Allāh] alone.",
  29060: "How many a creature does not carry its own provision. Allāh provides for it and for you. And He is the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.",
  29061: "If you ask them, “Who created the heavens and the earth and subjected the sun and the moon?” they will surely say, “Allāh.” So how are they then turned away [from the truth]?",
  29062: "Allāh extends the provision to whom He wills of His servants or restricts it. Indeed, Allāh has perfect knowledge of all things.",
  29063: "If you ask them, “Who sends down water from the sky and thereby gives life to the earth after its death?” they will surely say, “Allāh.” Say, “All praise be to Allāh.” But most of them do not understand.",
  29064: "The life of this world is nothing but amusement and play. But the Home of the Hereafter—that is indeed the [everlasting] life, if only they knew.",
  29065: "When they board ships [and find themselves in danger], they call upon Allāh alone, devoting their worship sincerely to Him. But when He delivers them safely to land, at once they associate others with Him [in worship].",
  29066: "So that they may show ingratitude for the favors We have bestowed upon them. Let them enjoy themselves—soon they will know [their evil end at death].",
  29067: "Have they not seen that We have made a safe sanctuary—Makkah—while people are being snatched away from all around them [29]? Do they then believe in falsehood and deny the grace of Allāh?\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. That is, while the surrounding regions were plagued by raids, warfare, abductions, and insecurity, the sanctuary of Makkah was granted safety by Allāh. Even before Islam, its inviolability was widely recognized, and its people were spared the violence common elsewhere in Arabia. This security was a clear divine favor, meant to lead them to gratitude and faith—yet many instead denied the truth while enjoying the protection Allāh bestowed upon them.",
  29068: "Who is more unjust than one who fabricates a lie against Allāh or rejects the truth when it comes to him? Is there not a  home in Hell for the disbelievers?",
  29069: "As for those who strive in Our cause, We will surely guide them to Our paths [30]. Indeed, Allāh is with the doers of good.\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. That is, those who sincerely strive for the sake of Allāh—by struggling against their own souls, remaining steadfast in faith, obeying His commands, avoiding sin, enduring hardship, and persevering in righteousness—will be guided by Him to the many paths that lead to His pleasure. This striving (jihād) is not limited to physical struggle, but includes every sincere effort made to uphold truth and draw nearer to Allāh. Divine guidance is granted in proportion to sincerity and perseverance, and it leads to increased clarity, firmness upon the truth, and success in both this world and the Hereafter. The verse concludes by affirming that Allāh is with those who excel in goodness, supporting them, aiding them, and granting them victory and guidance.",
  30001: "Alif Lām Mīm. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qurʾān chapter 2: verse 1.",
  30002: "The Byzantines have been defeated [2].\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The statement “The Byzantines have been defeated” refers to the early seventh-century conflict between the Byzantine Empire and the Sassanian Persian Empire. Byzantine forces suffered major defeats around 613–614 CE, including the loss of territories in Syria and Palestine. The verse introduces a prophecy that the Byzantines would soon regain victory, a development later associated with the campaigns of Emperor Heraclius.",
  30003: "In the nearer [and lowest] land [3]; and after their defeat, they [4] will overcome [5],\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. This refers to regions of the Levant close to Arabia, some of which include notably low-lying terrain.\n[4]. “They” refers to the Romans (Byzantines), who were adherents of earlier revealed scriptures and whose initial defeat is mentioned in the preceding verse.\n[5]. “Will overcome” renders the future verb sayaghlibūn, indicating a reversal of fortunes and predicting a subsequent victory—understood here as the Romans’ eventual defeat of the Persians, who at that time followed Zoroastrianism.",
  30004: "Within a few years. To Allāh belongs the whole matter, before and after [6]. On that day the believers will rejoice,\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. This verse refers to the Byzantine defeat by the Sasanian Persians in the early seventh century (c. 613–615 CE), followed by the Byzantine recovery and victory under Emperor Heraclius around 622–628 CE. The Qurʾānic statement emphasizes divine sovereignty over historical events: the expression “before and after” indicates that ultimate authority over victory and defeat rests with Allāh at every stage of history, rather than with human power or circumstance.",
  30005: "At the victory granted by Allāh. He gives victory to whom He wills. For He is the All-Mighty, the Most Merciful.",
  30006: "This is Allāh’s promise. Allāh never fails in His promise, but most people do not know.",
  30007: "They know only what is apparent of the life of this world [7], but they are heedless of the life to come.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. The “superficial aspects of the world” refer not to the physical world itself, but to a way of life shaped by material desire, sensuality, and pride, lived in disregard of divine guidance. Worldly life is presented as a probationary stage and an opportunity for moral testing, not something to be escaped. The verse criticizes heedless living, not the world as such, which also contains virtue and righteousness. According to Islamic teaching, balance, true happiness, and inner strength are attained through obedience to Allāh and adherence to the Qurʾān and the Sunnah, by which worldly life becomes a means of spiritual growth rather than moral loss.",
  30008: "Do they not reflect within themselves? Allāh did not create the heavens and the earth and everything between them except in truth and for an appointed term. And indeed many people deny the meeting with their Lord.",
  30009: "Have they not traveled throug the land to see how was the end of those before them? They were greater than them in strength, cultivated the earth, and developed it more than they developed it. Their messengers came to them with clear Proofs. Allāh did not wrong them, but they wronged themselves. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. That is, by transgressing the rights of their Creator. The verse calls for reflection on earlier civilizations that surpassed later peoples in strength, agriculture, and material development, yet perished after rejecting their messengers. Their downfall is attributed not to injustice on Allāh’s part, but to their own wrongdoing, underscoring moral accountability over material power.",
  30010: "Then the end of those who did evil was the worst consequence, because they denied the revelations of Allāh and used to mock them.",
  30011: "It is Allāh who originates creation, then will repeat it; then to Him you will be returned [for Judgment].",
  30012: "On the Day the [Final] Hour comes, the guilty will despair.",
  30013: " They will have no intercessors from among their associate-gods, and they will disavow their associate-gods.",
  30014: "On the Day the Hour comes, on that Day they will be separated. [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. That is, humanity will be decisively separated according to faith and deeds, commonly understood as the division between believers and disbelievers and between the inhabitants of Paradise and Hell.",
  30015: "As for those who believed and did righteous deeds, they will be in a Garden, rejoicing.",
  30016: "But as for those who disbelieved and rejected Our revelations and the meeting of the Hereafter, they will be brought forth for punishment.",
  30017: "  So glory be to Allāh in the evening [10] and in the morning. [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. “The evening” refers to the period from sunset onward and is commonly understood to include prescribed acts of glorification, remembrance, and prayer—namely the Maghrib (sunset) and ʿIshāʾ (night) prayers.\n[11]. “The morning” denotes the time from dawn onward and is associated with regular glorification, remembrance, and prayer at the start of the day—specifically the Fajr (early morning) prayer.",
  30018: "And to Him belongs all praise in the heavens and the earth; and [glorify Him] in the afternoon [12] and at noon. [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. That is, the ʿAṣr (afternoon) prayer.\n[13]. That is, the Ẓuhr (midday) prayer. In a well-known prophetic statement, the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ compared the five daily obligatory prayers to bathing in a river five times a day, explaining that just as no dirt would remain after such washing, so too do the prescribed prayers efface sins through Allāh’s mercy.",
  30019: "He brings forth the living from the dead and brings forth the dead from the living, and He revives the earth after its death. Thus you will be brought forth.",
  30020: "Among His signs is that He created you from dust, and then—behold—you are human beings spread throughout the earth.",
  30021: "And among His signs is that He created for you spouses from among yourselves, so that you may find tranquility in them; and He placed between you affection and mercy. Indeed, in this are signs for a people who reflect.",
  30022: "And among His signs is the creation of the heavens and the earth, and the diversity of your languages and colors. Indeed, in this are signs for those who have knowledge.",
  30023: "Among His signs is your sleep by night and your seeking of His bounty by day. Indeed, in this are signs for a people who listen.",
  30024: "And among His signs is that He shows you lightning, inspiring fear and hope, and sends down water from the sky, by which He revives the earth after its death. Indeed, in this are signs for a people who understand.",
  30025: "And among His signs is that the heaven and the earth stand firm by His command. Then, when He calls you with a single call from the earth, behold, you will come forth.",
  30026: "And to Him belongs whoever is in the heavens and the earth. All are obedient to Him.",
  30027: "It is He who originates creation, then will repeat it—and that is even easier for Him. To Him belongs the most exalted similitude in the heavens and the earth. And He is the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.",
  30028: "He sets forth for you an example from among yourselves: do you have, among those whom your right hands possess, any partners in what We have provided for you, such that you are equal in it and fear them as you fear one another [within a partnership]? Thus We explain the verses for a people who understand. [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. That is, if you would not accept equality or partnership with those under your own authority, how can you ascribe partners to Allāh from among His creation?",
  30029: "In fact, those who do wrong follow their own desires without any [divine] knowledge. So who can guide one whom Allāh has left astray? For such there are no helpers. [15]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. That is, due to arrogance and persistence in sin, which result in the loss of divine guidance.",
  30030: "So direct your face toward the religion, upright—inclining to truth—the natural disposition [16] of Allāh upon which He has created all people. There is no change in Allāh’s creation. That is the upright religion, but most people do not know.\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. The natural inborn inclination of man to worship his Creator alone, prior to the corruption of his nature by external influences.",
  30031: "Turning back to Him [in repentance], fear Him, establish the prayer, and do not be of the polytheists.",
  30032: "Of those who split up their religion and became dissenting sects [17]—each sect rejoicing in what they have. [18]\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. That is, those who fragmented their religion by abandoning its unity and introducing divisions based on partisan allegiance, innovation, or selective adherence. The verse addresses religious fragmentation as a general phenomenon, in which communities divide into competing groups rather than adhering to a unified, divinely grounded standard of truth—understood as Islam.\n[18]. Each group is depicted as taking pride in its own beliefs, interpretations, or inherited customs, remaining satisfied with what it possesses and unwilling to engage in self-critique or return to a shared, unifying truth—namely, Islam.",
  30033: "When harm touches people, they cry out to their Lord, turning back to Him [in repentance]. Then, when He lets them taste mercy from Him, at once a group among them associates others with their Lord,",
  30034: "Thus they show ingratitude for what We have given them. So enjoy yourselves—for soon you will come to know.",
  30035: "Or have We sent down upon them an authority that speaks in favor of what they used to associate with Him?",
  30036: "When We let people taste Our mercy, they rejoice in it. But if harm befalls them because of what their own hands have sent forth [of sins], behold, they fall into utter despair.",
  30037: "Do they not see that Allāh extends provision for whom He wills and restricts it? Indeed, in this are signs for a people who believe.",
  30038: "So give the relatives their due right, and the destitute, and the stranded traveler. That is best for those who seek the Face of Allāh, and it is they who are the successful.",
  30039: "Whatever you give in usury [interest] to gain more through the wealth of people does not increase [in reward] with Allāh. But whatever you give in zakāh, seeking the Face of Allāh—it is they who will receive manifold reward.",
  30040: "It is Allāh who created you, then provided for you, then will cause you to die, then will bring you back to life [for judgment]. Is there any of your associate-gods who does any of that? Glory be to Him, and exalted is He above what they associate with Him [in worship].",
  30041: "Corruption has appeared on land and sea because of what people’s own hands have earned [of sins], so that He may let them taste part of what they have done, that they might return [to righteousness].",
  30042: "Say, “Travel through the land and observe how the end of those before you was; most of them were polytheists.”",
  30043: "So set your face toward the upright religion [of Islam] before there comes from Allāh a Day that cannot be averted. On that Day, they will be divided.",
  30044: "Whoever disbelieves, then his disbelief is against himself. And whoever acts righteously—they are preparing for themselves [eternal homes in Paradise].",
  30045: "So that He may reward those who believe and do righteous deeds out of His favor. Indeed, He does not like the disbelievers.",
  30046: "And among His signs is that He sends forth the winds bearing good tidings [of rain], so that He may let you taste of His mercy, and that ships may sail by His command, so that you may seek of His bounty and be grateful.",
  30047: "Indeed, We sent messengers before you to their peoples, and they came to them with clear proofs. Then We exacted retribution upon those who persisted in committing crimes, for it is incumbent upon Us to help the believers.",
  30048: "It is Allāh who sends forth the winds, which raise clouds; then He spreads them across the sky as He wills and makes them fragments, so you see rain emerging from within them. Then, when He causes it to fall upon whom He wills of His servants, at once they rejoice.",
  30049: "Although, before it was sent down upon them—indeed, just before that—they were in utter despair.",
  30050: "So observe the effects of Allāh’s mercy—how He revives the earth after its death. Indeed, He is surely the One who gives life to the dead, and He has full power over all things.",
  30051: "If We were to send upon their crops a [damaging] wind, and they saw them turn yellow, they would thereafter become ungrateful. [19]\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. That is, they would lapse into ingratitude and denial of Allāh’s favor after having previously acknowledged His mercy, illustrating human inconsistency in times of hardship.",
  30052: "So you cannot cause the dead to hear, nor can you make the deaf hear the call when they turn their backs, retreating. [20]\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. This describes those who are spiritually unreceptive. “The dead” and “the deaf” do not denote physical incapacity, but a deliberate refusal to listen or respond to guidance; “turning their backs” underscores willful rejection rather than mere inability.",
  30053: "Nor can you guide the blind [of heart] out of their misguidance. You can make none hear except those who believe in Our verses, for it is they who submit.",
  30054: "It is Allāh who created you in a state of weakness; then after weakness He granted you strength; then after strength He made weakness and gray hair. He creates whatever He wills, and He is the All-Knowing, the Most Powerful.",
  30055: "On the Day the Hour [of Doom] comes, the guilty will swear that they had remained [in their graves] no more than an hour. Thus were they deluded [from the truth]. [21]\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. That is, in worldly life they held similar false assumptions, believing that resurrection and accountability would never occur. This habitual denial shaped their perception, so that even on the Day of Judgment they underestimate the reality of their stay and the gravity of what has taken place.",
  30056: "But those who were given knowledge and faith will say, “You have indeed remained, by the decree of Allāh, until the Day of Resurrection. So this is the Day of Resurrection, but you did not know.”",
  30057: "So on that Day their excuses will not benefit those who committed wrongdoing [22], nor will they be allowed to make amends.\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. That is, through associating partners with Allāh in worship (polytheism) and through denial of the Day of Resurrection and Judgment. These acts represent fundamental forms of injustice, as they involve both the violation of Allāh’s exclusive right to worship and the rejection of ultimate moral accountability.",
  30058: "We have certainly set forth for the people every kind of example in this Qurʾān. Yet if you [O Prophet] bring them any sign, those who disbelieve will surely say, “You are nothing but falsifiers.”",
  30059: "Thus does Allāh set a seal upon the hearts of those who do not know. [23]\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. That is, those who persist in ignorance by willfully rejecting truth after it has been made clear. The sealing of the heart signifies a consequence of deliberate denial and obstinate refusal to reflect, rather than an arbitrary deprivation of guidance.",
  30060: "So be patient. Indeed, the promise of Allāh is true. And do not let those who have no certainty [in the Hereafter] cause you to be taken lightly. [24]\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. That is, do not allow those who lack firm conviction in the Hereafter to undermine resolve, belittle your mission, or draw you into doubt, haste, or impatience, leading to deviation from right conduct. The expression conveys the sense of being unsettled, pressured, or treated lightly as a result of the skepticism, ridicule, or denial of those who themselves are devoid of certainty. The verse thus emphasizes steadfastness upon the path of truth, remaining unaffected by external opposition or disparagement.",
  31001: "Alif Lām Mīm. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qurʾān chapter 2: verse 1.",
  31002: "These are the verses of the Wise Book. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. That is, the Qurʾān, whose verses are characterized by wisdom, sound judgment, and guidance to the straight path.",
  31003: "A guidance and a mercy for the doers of good,",
  31004: "Those who establish prayer, pay zakah 'the poor-rate', and have firm belief in the Hereafter.",
  31005: "Those are upon guidance from their Lord, and it is they who are successful.",
  31006: "Among the people are those who purchase idle talk [3] to mislead others from the path of Allāh without knowledge, and to make a mockery of it. For such there will be a humiliating punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. “Idle talk” (lahw al-ḥadīth) has been interpreted by early and later exegetes as encompassing speech, narratives, entertainment, or activities that distract from remembrance of God and are used deliberately to divert others from divine guidance. This includes false stories, frivolous discourse, or cultural practices promoted as alternatives to revelation. The phrase “to mislead without knowledge” emphasizes both ignorance and moral irresponsibility, while “making a mockery of it” points to ridicule or trivialization of God’s signs. The verse condemns not mere amusement, but intentional misuse of speech and culture to obstruct faith and understanding.",
  31007: "When Our verses are recited to him, he turns away in arrogance, as though he had not heard, as though there were deafness in his ears. So give him tidings of a painful punishment. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. This verse portrays deliberate arrogance and willful rejection rather than physical inability. The repeated comparison “as though he had not heard” and “as though there were deafness in his ears” emphasizes conscious disregard for divine revelation. The command to “give tidings” (bashshirhu) is employed coincidentally, as the term normally denotes good news, here intensifying the warning by contrasting expectation with outcome. The verse underscores moral accountability for rejecting guidance out of pride rather than ignorance.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:  \"Everyone of my nation that is all the people will enter Paradise except those who refuse.\" It was said: \"Who does refuse, O Messenger of God?\" He said: \"He who obeyed me, shall enter Paradise, and who disobeyed me, indeed he has refused.''",
  31008: "Indeed, those who believe and do righteous deeds—they will have the Gardens of Bliss.",
  31009: "Abiding therein forever—this is the true promise of Allāh. And He is the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.",
  31010: "He created the heavens without pillars that you can see, and He cast firm mountains into the earth lest it should shake with you, and He spread throughout it moving creatures of every kind. And We send down water from the sky, then cause to grow therein vegetation of every noble kind.",
  31011: "This is Allāh’s creation. So show Me what those you invoke besides Him have created [5]. Indeed, the wrongdoers are in clear error.\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. This verse presents a rhetorical challenge highlighting the inability of any beings worshipped besides Allāh to create or originate anything. By contrasting divine creation with the complete creative impotence of false objects of worship, it exposes the irrationality of associating partners with Allāh and affirms exclusive divine authorship of the heavens, the earth, and all that exists between them. The reference to creation serves as an appeal to reflection on observable reality as evidence of divine power, mercy, and sovereignty.",
  31012: "And indeed, We granted wisdom to Luqmān, [saying], “Be grateful to Allāh.” Whoever is grateful is grateful only for his own benefit; and whoever is ungrateful—then surely Allāh is Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy.",
  31013: "When Luqmān said to his son, while advising him, “O my son, do not associate anything with Allāh. Indeed, associating others with Allāh is surely a grievous iniquity.” [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. That is, associating partners with Allāh (shirk) is described as a “grievous iniquity” because it violates the fundamental principle of divine oneness and directs worship away from its rightful object. In fact, it is regarded as the greatest form of injustice, as it equates created beings with the Creator. Luqmān’s counsel to his son illustrates the importance of early moral and religious instruction, emphasizing monotheism and establishing the prayer as the foundation of faith and ethical conduct\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ once saw a man with a brass ring on his hand and asked him, “What is this?” the man replied, “To overcome the weakness of old age.” He said, “Remove it, for, it can only add to your weakness. Should death overtake you while you are wearing it, you would never succeed.”  The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ also said: “Whoever wears talisman or an amulet (or cross for protection or cure) would never see his wish fulfilled by God. And whoever hangs a sea shell (for protection) would never get peace and rest.” The Prophet ﷺ is reported to have said: “Whoever wears a talisman (or cross for protection) has committed polytheism.” \nThe Prophet saw a man with a piece of twine on his hand (as a protection or cure from fever) so he cut the twine and read the Verse: “Most of them believe in God while still practicing idolatry.”",
  31014: "We have enjoined upon man kindness toward his parents. His mother bore him in weakness upon weakness, and his weaning is in two years. Be grateful to Me and to your parents. To Me is the final return.",
  31015: "But if they strive to make you associate with Me that of which you have no knowledge, then do not obey them. Yet keep company with them in this world with kindness, and follow the way of those who turn back to Me [in repentance]. Then to Me is your return, and I will inform you of what you used to do.",
  31016: "O my dear son, even if it be the weight of a mustard seed, hidden within a rock or in the heavens or in the earth, Allāh will bring it forth. Indeed, Allāh is Most Subtle, All-Aware. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. This verse affirms Allāh’s complete and perfect knowledge, extending to even the smallest deeds, regardless of how concealed or remote they may be. The imagery underscores moral accountability, emphasizing that no action—however insignificant or hidden—escapes divine awareness or judgment. The divine attributes “Most Subtle” (al-Laṭīf) and “All-Aware” (al-Khabīr) highlight both the precision of Allāh’s knowledge and His intimate awareness of all things.",
  31017: "O my dear son, establish the prayer [8]; enjoin what is right [9], forbid what is wrong [10]; and bear patiently whatever befalls you. Indeed, that is among matters requiring firm resolve.\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. The command to “establish the prayer” emphasizes regular, disciplined worship as a foundational religious duty. Prophetic tradition underscores the importance of early moral and religious instruction, encouraging parents to train children in prayer from a young age in order to cultivate lifelong commitment and responsibility.\nThe Prophet ﷺ said, \"Command your children to pray when they are seven years old, and beat them for (not offering) it when they are ten, and separate them (boys and girls) in beds.\"  \n[9]. “That which is right and just” (al-maʿrūf) refers to Islamic monotheism and all actions, beliefs, and conduct recognized as morally sound and virtuous by divine revelation and ethical reason.\n[10]. “That which is wrong” (al-munkar) denotes disbelief in the oneness of God, polytheism in all its forms, and all actions or beliefs that are morally corrupt, unjust, or contrary to divine guidance.",
  31018: "Do not turn your cheek away from people in scorn, nor walk upon the earth exultantly. Indeed, Allāh does not like any self-conceited, boastful person.",
  31019: "Be moderate in your pace and lower your voice [11], for the harshest of all sounds is surely the braying of donkeys.\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. Moderation in pace and speech signifies humility, self-control, and good manners. Lowering the voice discourages arrogance, harshness, and ostentation in social conduct. The comparison to the braying of donkeys employs vivid imagery to censure loud, coarse speech, emphasizing that refinement of character includes restraint in behavior and expression.",
  31020: "Do you not see that Allāh has made subservient to you whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth, and has completed and lavished upon you His favors, both outward and inward? Yet among the people is one who disputes about Allāh without knowledge, or guidance, or an enlightening Scripture.",
  31021: "When it is said to them, “Follow what Allāh has sent down,” they say, “Rather, we follow that upon which we found our fathers doing.” Even if Satan were calling them to the punishment of the blazing Fire?",
  31022: "Whoever submits his face to Allāh while being a doer of good has indeed grasped the firmest and most trustworthy handhold [12]. And to Allāh belongs the ultimate outcome of all matters.\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. That is, exclusive devotion to Allāh without associating any partners with Him, meaning that no other deities are taken besides Allāh. The expression “the firmest handhold” (al-ʿurwat al-wuthqā) is a metaphor for unshakeable faith and complete reliance upon Allāh, grounded in sincere submission and righteous conduct, expressed through faithful obedience to His commands in belief, word, and deed. It denotes a bond that does not break, providing security and guidance under all circumstances.",
  31023: "But whoever disbelieves—do not let their disbelief grieve you. To Us is their return; then We shall inform them of what they have done. Indeed, Allāh is All-Knowing of what is within the chests.",
  31024: "We let them enjoy for a little while, then We shall drive them to a harsh punishment.",
  31025: "If you ask them, “Who created the heavens and the earth?” they will surely say, “Allāh.” Say, “All praise is due to Allāh.” Yet most of them do not know.",
  31026: "To Allāh belongs whatever is in the heavens and the earth. Indeed, Allāh is the Self-Sufficient, the Praiseworthy.",
  31027: "If all the trees on earth were pens, and the sea were ink—replenished after it by seven more seas—the Words of Allāh would not be exhausted. Indeed, Allāh is the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.",
  31028: "The creation and resurrection of all of you are only like that of a single soul. Indeed, Allāh is All-Hearing, All-Seeing.",
  31029: "Do you not see that Allāh causes the night to merge into the day and the day to merge into the night, and that He has subjected the sun and the moon, each pursuing its course until an appointed time, and that Allāh is All-Aware of what you do?",
  31030: "That is because Allāh—He is the Absolute Truth, and what they invoke besides Him is falsehood, and because Allāh is the Most High, the Most Great.",
  31031: "Do you not see that the ships sail through the sea by Allāh’s grace, so that He may show you some of His signs? Indeed, in this are signs for everyone who is steadfast and grateful.",
  31032: "When waves cover them like canopies, they cry out to Allāh, devoting their religion sincerely to Him. But when He delivers them safely to the land, some of them take a moderate course [while others persist in denial]. None rejects Our verses except every treacherous, ungrateful one.",
  31033: "O people, be mindful of your Lord, and fear a Day when no father will avail his son in anything, nor will a son avail his father in anything. Indeed, the promise of Allāh is true, so let not the worldly life deceive you, nor let the Deceiver deceive you concerning Allāh.",
  31034: "Indeed, Allāh alone has knowledge of the Hour [of Doom] [13]. He sends down the rain, and He knows what is in the wombs [14]. No soul knows what it will earn tomorrow, and no soul knows in which land it will die. Indeed, Allāh is All-Knowing, All-Aware.\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. That is, the exact timing of the Last Hour is known exclusively to Allāh. This knowledge is withheld from all creation, emphasizing human limitation and the necessity of moral readiness rather than speculative certainty.\nJesus Christ, God's messenger to the Israelites, said: “But of that day or that hour [of God’s coming execution of judgment] no one knows, not even the angels in heaven, nor me, but only Allāh.”\n[14]. This refers to Allāh’s complete knowledge of what is in the wombs, encompassing not only physical attributes but also destiny, lifespan, provision, and character. The verse affirms divine omniscience beyond empirical or predictive knowledge.",
  32001: "Alif Lām Mīm. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2: verse 1.",
  32002: "The revelation of this Book—there is no doubt about it—is from the Lord of the worlds.",
  32003: "Or do they say, “He has fabricated it”? Rather, it is the Truth from your Lord, so that you may warn a people to whom no warner has come before you, that they may be guided.",
  32004: "It is Allāh Who created the heavens and the earth and whatever is between them in six days, then rose over the Throne. You have no besides Him any protector or intercessor. Will you not then take heed?",
  32005: "He regulates the affair from the heaven to the earth with wisdom and precision; then it ascends to Him in a Day whose measure is a thousand years by your reckoning.",
  32006: "Such is Allāh—the Knower of the unseen and the seen, the All-Mighty, the Most Merciful,",
  32007: "Who perfected all that He created [2] and began the creation of man from clay.\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. That is, Allāh created everything in due proportion, harmony, and suitability for its purpose. The reference to the creation of man from clay recalls humanity’s humble material origin, underscoring both divine power in creation and the intentional design underlying human existence.\nThe Prophet Muhammad, peace and blessings of Allāh be upon him, has taught us that we should perfect every single action we do. He said: “Allāh loves that if one does a job he perfects it.” The word ‘aml, translated in this hadith as job, can literally be any action that we perform. Thereby, one can turn any ordinary work into an act of worship that Allāh loves as long as you put all your effort into perfecting it and as long as one does it for the sake of Allāh.",
  32008: "Then He made his progeny from an extract of a lowly fluid.",
  32009: "Then He fashioned him in due proportion and breathed into him of His spirit. And He made for you hearing, sight, and intellect. Little is it that you give thanks.",
  32010: "They say, “When we have disintegrated within the earth, shall we indeed be recreated anew?” Rather, they deny the meeting with their Lord.",
  32011: "Say, “The Angel of Death who has been entrusted with you will take your souls, then to your Lord you will be brought back.”",
  32012: "If only you could see when the guilty hang their heads before their Lord, [saying], “Our Lord, we have now seen and heard, so send us back [to the world]; we will do righteous deeds. Indeed, we are now certain.”",
  32013: "If We had willed, We would have given every soul its guidance; but the Word from Me has come to pass, “I will surely fill Hell with jinn and mankind all together.”",
  32014: "So taste [the punishment] for having forgotten the meeting of this Day of yours. Indeed, We have [now] forgotten you [3]. And taste the abiding punishment for what you used to do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. The expression “We have forgotten you” does not imply forgetfulness on the part of Allāh, who is free from all deficiency. Rather, it signifies abandonment and the withdrawal of mercy as a form of just recompense, corresponding to their deliberate neglect of the Day of Judgment. It thus denotes requital in kind, not literal forgetfulness.",
  32015: "Only those believe in Our verses who, when they are reminded of them, fall down in prostration and glorify their Lord with praise, and they are not arrogant.",
  32016: "They rise from their beds to pray to their Lord with both fear and hope, and they give from what We have provided them.",
  32017: "So no soul knows what has been kept hidden for them of comfort for the eyes [4], as a reward for what they used to do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. “Comfort for the eyes” (qurrati aʿyun) is a Qurʾānic expression denoting the highest joy, delight, and contentment. The verse emphasizes that the reward prepared for the righteous in the Hereafter lies beyond human knowledge and imagination, concealed until its full unveiling as recompense for faith and righteous deeds—described elsewhere as that which no eye has seen, no ear has heard, and no human heart has conceived.",
  32018: "Can a believer be compared to someone who lives in rebellion against God? They are certainly not equal.",
  32019: "As for those who believe and do righteous deeds, for them are the Gardens of Abode, as a gracious welcome for what they used to do.",
  32020: "But as for those who rebelled, their abode is the Fire. Whenever they seek to escape from it, they will be returned to it and told, “Taste the punishment of the Fire that you used to deny.”",
  32021: "We will surely let them taste the nearer punishment before the greater punishment [5], so that they may return [in repentance].\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. “The nearer punishment” is commonly understood by exegetes as worldly afflictions—such as hardship, loss, fear, or calamity—experienced prior to the punishment of the Hereafter. It serves as a warning and a means of awakening, intended to prompt reflection and repentance before the final and greater punishment.",
  32022: "Who is more unjust than one who is reminded of the verses of his Lord, then turns away from them? We will certainly take vengeance upon the guilty. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. The verse identifies the gravest form of injustice as deliberate rejection after clear reminder. Turning away from the divine signs signifies conscious refusal rather than ignorance. The reference to “vengeance” denotes just retribution exercised by Allāh alone against persistent wrongdoing, affirming divine justice and moral accountability rather than personal retaliation.",
  32023: "We certainly gave Moses the Scripture, so do not be in doubt about his meeting [7]; and We made it [the Torah] a guidance for the Children of Israel.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. The phrase “his meeting” has been interpreted as referring either to the Prophet Muḥammad’s encounter with Moses during the night of al-Mi‘rāj (ascent), or more generally to the certainty of receiving revelation in continuity with earlier messengers. The statement thus reassures the Prophet of the authenticity and divine origin of revelation, situating the Qurʾān within the same line of guidance as the Torah.",
  32024: "And We appointed from among them leaders who guided by Our command, because they showed patience and had certainty in Our verses.",
  32025: "Indeed, it is your Lord alone who will judge between them on the Day of Resurrection concerning all that they used to differ about.",
  32026: "Has it not guided them how many generations We destroyed before them, in whose habitations they now walk? Indeed, in this are signs. Will they not then listen?",
  32027: "Do they not see that We drive water to barren land and bring forth thereby crops from which their cattle and they themselves eat? Will they not then see?",
  32028: "They say, \"When will this Judgment Day be fulfilled, if you are truthful?\"",
  32029: "Say, “On the Day of Judgment, the faith of those who disbelieved will not benefit them, nor will they be given any respite.”",
  32030: "So leave them and wait [8]; they too are waiting.\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. This command signifies disengagement rather than abandonment of the mission. “Waiting” refers to awaiting the unfolding of divine judgment and outcome, affirming that ultimate resolution belongs to Allāh alone. The statement underscores certainty in divine justice while contrasting patient reliance upon Allāh with the opponents’ anticipation of a contrary outcome.",
  33001: "O  Prophet, fear Allāh [1] and do not obey the disbelievers and the hypocrites. Indeed, Allāh is ever All-Knowing, All-Wise.\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. To “fear Allāh” usually means having deep respect, reverence, and awareness of Allāh. It’s about honoring Allāh and trying to live righteously. It denotes consciousness of Allāh expressed through obedience, moral restraint, and awareness of divine accountability. It does not signify fear in a purely emotional sense, but mindful reverence that governs belief, intention, and conduct, and calls for steadfast adherence to divine guidance.",
  33002: "Follow what has been revealed to you from your Lord. Indeed, Allāh is fully aware of all that you do.",
  33003: "Put your trust in Allāh alone, for Allāh is sufficient as a Trustee.",
  33004: "Allāh has not placed two hearts within any man. Nor has He made your wives—whom you declare unlawful through ẓihār (likening them to your mothers’ backs) [2]—your actual mothers [3], nor has He made your adopted sons your real sons [4]. That is merely a saying of your mouths. But Allāh speaks the truth, and He guides to the straight way.\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The phrase refers to the pre-Islamic formula, “You are to me like the back of my mother,” by which a man declared his wife permanently unlawful to himself without formally divorcing her. This practice, known as ẓihār, was a customary form of marital suspension in pre-Islamic Arabia. The Qurʾān condemned this declaration as false and sinful, requiring expiation rather than dissolution of marriage (see Qurʾān 58:3–4).\n[3]. Ẓihār did not make a wife one’s actual mother, nor did it legally transform the marital relationship. The verse abolishes the legal fiction underlying this practice, affirming that verbal declarations cannot alter biological or moral realities. Islam retained marriage while regulating ẓihār through prescribed expiation, thereby correcting an unjust custom rather than recognizing it as a valid form of divorce.\n[4]. The verse also addresses pre-Islamic adoption practices in which adopted children were legally treated as biological offspring, including lineage, inheritance, and marital prohibitions. The Qurʾān abolishes this legal assimilation, affirming that lineage cannot be altered by naming or custom. While formal adoption that obscures a child’s true parentage is prohibited, caring for, sponsoring, and raising orphans or children in need remains strongly encouraged, with full moral merit, while preserving their original identity and legal lineage.",
  33005: "Call them by [the names of] their own fathers; that is more just in the sight of Allāh. But if you do not know their fathers, then they are your brothers in faith and your protégés. There is no sin upon you for what you do unintentionally by mistake, but only for what your hearts deliberately intend. And Allāh is Ever All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  33006: "The Prophet has a greater claim over the believers than their own selves [5], and his wives are [like] their mothers. Blood relatives are more entitled to one another [in inheritance] according to the Decree of Allāh than the [other] believers and the emigrants—except that you may do some good [by way of bequest] to your close friends [6]. This is written in the Book [the Preserved Tablet].\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. That is, the Prophet ﷺ has a stronger claim to the obedience, loyalty, and welfare of the believers than they have over themselves. This reflects his authority as Messenger and leader, whose guidance takes precedence over personal desires or judgments in matters of faith and conduct.\n[6]. This refers to the abrogation of an earlier ruling that permitted inheritance ties between Muslims based on faith and migration, particularly between the emigrants (Muhājirūn) and the helpers (Anṣār). The final ruling restricts inheritance to blood relatives, while allowing non-heirs to receive a voluntary bequest, up to one-third of the estate, in accordance with Islamic law.",
  33007: "And [remember] when We took from the prophets their covenant—and from you, [O Muhammad], and from Noah, Abraham, Moses, and Jesus, son of Mary. We took from them a solemn covenant.",
  33008: "So that He may question the truthful about their truthfulness [7]. But for the disbelievers, He has prepared a painful punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. This questioning is not for the purpose of acquiring knowledge, but to make truthfulness manifest and establish justice. It has been interpreted as referring to Allāh questioning the prophets regarding what they conveyed of the divine message and the response they received. The term “the truthful” may also encompass those who believed in the prophets’ message and faithfully upheld and conveyed it to others. In all cases, the verse emphasizes accountability, sincerity, and the public affirmation of truth on the Day of Judgment.",
  33009: "O believers, remember Allāh’s favor upon you when armies came against you; then We sent against them a fierce wind and forces that you could not see. Allāh is ever Seeing of what you do.",
  33010: "When they came at you from above you and from below you, when the eyes swerved and the hearts rose to the throats, and you assumed about Allāh diverse assumptions. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. The phrase “diverse assumptions about Allāh” refers to the differing inner responses revealed during the ordeal: some believers remained firm in trust and reliance upon Allāh, while others were overtaken by doubt, fear, or skepticism. The verse thus highlights how severe trials expose the sincerity or weakness of faith, distinguishing steadfast belief from wavering conviction.",
  33011: "There, the believers were tested and shaken with a severe shaking.",
  33012: "When the hypocrites and the weak-hearted said, “Allāh and His Messenger have promised us nothing but false hopes.”",
  33013: "When a group of them said, “O people of Yathrib [Madinah], you cannot remain here, so return,” and another group sought the Prophet’s permission to leave, claiming, “Our homes are exposed,” though they were not—they sought only to escape.",
  33014: "And if [the enemy] had entered upon them the town from all its surrounding quarters, and they had then been urged to sedition [9], they would have complied with it, except for a little hesitation.\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. That is, if the hypocrites had been urged to commit fitnah—understood here as apostasy from Islam, betrayal of the believers, or siding with the enemy—they would have readily complied. The verse thus exposes the fragility of their faith and their willingness to abandon commitment under pressure.",
  33015: "They had already pledged to Allāh before, not to turn their backs—And the covenant with Allāh will surely be questioned about. [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. That is, the covenant made with Allāh—whether through explicit pledge or implicit commitment—entails moral and religious accountability. Individuals will be questioned regarding their faithfulness to such pledges, emphasizing the seriousness of promises made to Allāh and the certainty of reckoning for their fulfillment or breach.\nThe Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said:\n“Pledge to me that you will uphold six matters, and I will guarantee for you Paradise: be truthful when you speak; do not break your promises or covenants; fulfill what you are entrusted with; lower your gaze from what is forbidden; guard your chastity; and do not harm others, whether physically or verbally.”",
  33016: "Say, “Fleeing will not benefit you if you flee from death or from being killed; even then, you would not be granted enjoyment except for a little while.”",
  33017: "Say, “Who is it that can protect you from Allāh if He intends harm for you, or intends mercy for you?” They will find for themselves, besides Allāh, neither a protector nor a helper.",
  33018: "Indeed, Allāh knows those among you who discourage others [from fighting in the way of Allāh], and those who say to their brethren, “Come to us,” yet they come to the fighting only a little.",
  33019: "They are stingy toward you [11]. Then, when fear comes, you see them looking at you, their eyes rolling like one fainting at the throes of death. But when the fear passes, they lash you with sharp tongues, begrudging of any good. Such people have not truly believed, so Allāh has rendered their deeds worthless. And that is easy for Allāh.\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. That is, they are reluctant to offer support, sacrifice, or assistance to the believers. This miserliness is not limited to wealth but includes withholding effort, loyalty, and solidarity in times of trial.",
  33020: "They think that the confederates have not yet withdrawn [12]. If the confederates were to return, they would wish to be out in the desert among the Bedouins, asking about your news. Even if they were among you, they would not fight except a little.\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. That is, they remain in a state of fear and suspicion even after the danger has passed, revealing their lack of trust in Allāh and absence of true resolve. Their continued anxiety exposes their insincerity and reluctance to participate in struggle, preferring distance and self-preservation over commitment to the believers.",
  33021: "Indeed, in the Messenger of Allāh you have an excellent example for whoever hopes in Allāh and the Last Day, and remembers Allāh often.",
  33022: "When the believers saw the confederates [advancing against them], they said, “This is what Allāh and His Messenger promised us [13], and Allāh and His Messenger have spoken the truth.” And it only increased them in faith and submission.\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. That is, the believers recognized the fulfillment of earlier divine assurances that trials, hardship, and confrontation with enemies would occur before victory. Rather than weakening them, this confirmation strengthened their faith, trust in Allāh, and willing submission to His decree.",
  33023: "Among the believers are men who have been true to the covenant they made with Allāh. Among them are those who have fulfilled their vow [to the point of death], and among them are those who still await, and they have not changed in the least.",
  33024: "So that Allāh may reward the truthful for their truthfulness [14], and punish the hypocrites if He wills, or accept their repentance. Indeed, Allāh is Ever All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. That is, their truthfulness in faith, intention, and steadfastness in honoring the covenant made with Allāh. The verse affirms that sincerity is rewarded, while the fate of the hypocrites remains subject to divine justice and mercy—either punishment or repentance accepted—underscoring Allāh’s perfect balance between accountability and forgiveness.",
  33025: "Allāh has repelled those who disbelieved in their rage; they gained no advantage. And Allāh sufficed the believers in fighting [15]. Allāh is Ever All-Strong, All-Mighty.\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. That is, Allāh alone brought about the defeat and withdrawal of the enemy—by sending against them a fierce wind and unseen forces—without the believers having to engage in decisive combat. The verse thus affirms divine intervention and support, emphasizing that victory ultimately derives not from human strength, but from Allāh’s power and decree.",
  33026: "And He brought down from their strongholds those of the People of the Scripture who aided the pagans, and He cast terror into their hearts: a group you killed, and a group you took captive.",
  33027: "He caused you to inherit their land, their homes, and their wealth—and also land you had not yet set foot upon [16]. Allāh has power over all things.\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. That is, land which you had not yet entered or set foot upon; this is commonly understood as a reference to the land of Khaybar, which was taken in subsequent conquests after the events described here.",
  33028: "O Prophet, say to your wives: “If you desire the worldly life and its adornment, then come; I will provide for you and release you in a fitting manner.”",
  33029: "But if you seek Allāh, His Messenger, and the Home of the Hereafter, then indeed Allāh has prepared for those who do good among you a great reward.",
  33030: "O wives of the Prophet, whoever among you commits a manifest immorality—the punishment will be doubled for her [in the Hereafter]. And that is easy for Allāh.",
  33031: "But whoever among you obeys Allāh and His Messenger and acts righteously—We will grant her a double reward, and We have prepared for her a noble provision.",
  33032: "O wives of the Prophet, you are not like any other women [17]: if you fear Allāh, do not be soft in speech, lest one in whose heart is a disease should be moved with desire; rather, speak in a dignified and appropriate manner.\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. Allāh addresses the wives of the Prophet ﷺ, affirming that they are not like other women due to their elevated spiritual and moral status. They are instructed to uphold dignity, restraint, and propriety in speech and behavior. The verse emphasizes that the household of the Prophet ﷺ serves as noble examples to others and holds a more exalted status, bearing greater responsibility in embodying righteousness, modesty, and exemplary conduct.",
  33033: "Remain settled in your homes and do not display yourselves [18] as was the display of finery in the former Age of Ignorance [19]. Establish prayer, give zakāh, and obey Allāh and His Messenger. Indeed, Allāh intends only to remove all impurity from you, O People of the Household, and to purify you with a complete purification.\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. The Arabic term tabarruj refers to conspicuous public display of beauty, adornment, or behavior intended to attract attention. In the social context of pre-Islamic Arabia, this could include revealing dress, ostentatious adornment, or public conduct designed to draw notice. This refers to the outward display of a woman’s adornment, finery, and bodily form, including clothing, beautification, and physical features, when appearing before non-maḥram men—that is, men who are not among her close male relatives and to whom marriage would be legally permissible under Islamic law. The prohibition addresses intentional public display that may attract attention or provoke desire, as described by the Qur’anic concept of tabarruj (ostentatious display).\n[19]. Instead, women are instructed to observe the Islamic dress code (ḥijāb) and standards of modesty, which include covering the body appropriately, maintaining dignified conduct, and avoiding behavior or appearance that resembles the immodest display characteristic of the pre-Islamic Age of Ignorance (Jāhiliyyah). This guidance aims to preserve personal dignity, moral integrity, and social harmony in accordance with Qur’anic ethics.",
  33034: "Remember what is recited in your homes of the verses of Allāh and wisdom [20]. Indeed, Allāh is ever Most Subtle, All-Aware.\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. The Wisdom (al-Ḥikmah) refers to the Sunnah—the recorded sayings, actions, and tacit approvals of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. Alongside the Qur’an, the Sunnah constitutes the second primary source of guidance and knowledge in Islam, serving to explain, clarify, and practically implement Qur’anic teachings. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ is presented in the Qur’an as the perfect and exemplary model for all believers, whose character, conduct, and obedience to Allāh provide a comprehensive framework for moral, spiritual, and social life.",
  33035: "Indeed, the men and women who submit to Allāh, the believing men and believing women, the obedient men and obedient women, the truthful men and truthful women, the patient men and patient women, the humble men and humble women, the men and women who give charity, the men and women who fast, the chaste men who guard their private parts and the chaste women, and the men and women who remember Allāh often—Allāh has prepared for them forgiveness and a great reward.",
  33036: "It is not for a believing man or a believing woman—when Allāh and His Messenger have decided a matter—to have any choice in their affair [21]. Whoever disobeys Allāh and His Messenger has indeed gone clearly astray.\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. That is, a believing man or woman has no discretion to seek judgment from any authority other than Allāh and His Messenger ﷺ, nor the freedom to choose whether to accept or reject their decision once it has been made. True faith requires complete submission and obedience, and departing from this principle constitutes clear misguidance, as affirmed by the verse.",
  33037: "And [remember, O Prophet] when you said to the one upon whom [22] Allāh had bestowed favor and upon whom [23] you had bestowed favor: “Keep your wife to yourself and fear Allāh,” while you were concealing within yourself what Allāh was to disclose, and you feared the people—whereas Allāh has a greater right that you should fear Him. Then when Zayd had fulfilled what he needed of her [and divorced her] [24], We married her to you [25], so that there would be no difficulty for the believers concerning the wives of their adopted sons once they have fulfilled what they needed of them. And Allah’s command is ever fulfilled.\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. That is, Zayd ibn Harithah, whom Allāh favored by guiding him to Islam and honoring him with faith, righteousness, and close companionship with the Prophet ﷺ.\n[23]. This also refers to Zayd ibn Harithah, whom the Prophet ﷺ had favored by manumitting him from slavery, caring for him, and treating him with honor and affection prior to the abolition of pre-Islamic adoption practices.\n[24]. Meaning: when Zayd had completed the lawful and necessary process of divorce, thereby fully dissolving the marital bond and concluding all marital rights and obligations.\n[25]. That is, Zaynab bint Jahsh, whom Allāh caused the Prophet ﷺ to marry after she had completed her prescribed waiting period (ʿiddah) following the divorce, in order to establish a clear legal ruling and remove any hardship for the believers.",
  33038: "There is no blame upon the Prophet concerning what Allāh has ordained for him [26]. Such was the pattern of Allāh with those [prophets] who passed away before. The command of Allāh is a decree firmly determined.\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. That is, the Prophet ﷺ bears no fault, sin, or reproach for carrying out what Allāh has lawfully decreed and commanded for him, even if it contradicts prevailing customs or social expectations. This principle reflects the established Sunnah (way) of Allāh with His prophets and messengers throughout history, who faithfully implemented divine commands despite public criticism or opposition. Allāh’s decree is final, binding, and precisely measured, leaving no room for human objection once His will has been made manifest.",
  33039: "Those who convey the messages of Allāh [27] and fear Him, and fear none except Allāh. Allāh is sufficient as a Reckoner.\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]. That is, the prophets and their sincere followers who faithfully and honestly convey Allah’s message to the people without concealment, distortion, or compromise. They fear Allāh alone, maintaining true God-consciousness (taqwā), and do not allow fear of people, authority, or social pressure to deter them from proclaiming the truth. The verse affirms that Allāh is sufficient as a Reckoner, fully aware of all intentions and actions, and the ultimate judge over those who convey and receive His message.",
  33040: "Muhammad is not the father of any of your men, but he is the Messenger of Allāh and the Seal of the Prophets [28]. Allāh is ever All-Knowing of everything.\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. The Seal of the Prophets means that Prophet Muhammad ﷺ is the final and last prophet, after whom no prophet or messenger will come. His prophethood completes and seals the chain of divine revelation begun with earlier prophets. This verse also clarifies that the Prophet’s ﷺ relationship to the believers is spiritual and religious, not biological lineage, thereby affirming the abolition of pre-Islamic adoption practices that equated adopted sons with natural sons. Allah’s complete knowledge encompasses His wisdom in establishing final prophethood and legislating for the guidance of humanity.",
  33041: "O believers, remember Allāh with much remembrance. [29]\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. That is, believers are commanded to constantly remember and praise Allāh through the heart, tongue, and actions. This includes verbal remembrance (dhikr) such as glorification, supplication, and recitation; inward remembrance through mindfulness and God-consciousness (taqwā); and practical remembrance by obedience to Allah’s commands and adherence to His guidance. The phrase “much remembrance” (dhikran kathīrā) emphasizes frequency, consistency, and sincerity, encouraging remembrance in all circumstances—ease and hardship, privately and publicly.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: \"The example of the one who remembers (praises) his Lord (Allāh) in comparison to the one who does not remember is that of a living one compared to a dead one\". Muhammad, God’s Messenger ﷺ, also said: \"Whoever says 'Subhânallâhi wa bihamdihi' 'Glory be to Allāh and all praise be to Him', one hundred times a day, will be forgiven his (minor) sins.\" He also said: \"Whoever says: Lâ ilâha illallâhu wahdahû lâ sharîka lahû, lahul-mulku wa lahul-hamdu wa Huwa ‘alâ kulli shai’in Qadîr,' 'None has the right to be worshipped but Allāh (Alone) Who has no partner; to Him belongs the kingdom, and for Him are all the praises, and He has full power to do everything,' one hundred times will get the same reward as given for manumitting ten slaves; and one hundred good deeds will be written in his account, and one hundred sins will be deducted from his account, and it (his saying) will be a shield for him from Satan on that day till night, and nobody will be able to do a better deed except the one who does more than he.\"",
  33042: "And glorify Him morning and evening.",
  33043: "It is He who bestows His blessings upon you, and His angels [invoke blessings for you], so that He may bring you out of darknesses into the light [30]. And He is ever Merciful to the believers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. This verse affirms that Allāh bestows His mercy, grace, and blessings upon the believers, while His angels invoke prayers of forgiveness, mercy, and elevation on their behalf. The phrase “darknesses” refers to all forms of ignorance, disbelief, sin, confusion, and moral deviation, whereas “the light” signifies faith, guidance, knowledge, and righteous conduct. Through divine mercy and angelic supplication, Allāh guides the believers away from spiritual and moral darkness into clarity, faith, and obedience. The verse concludes by emphasizing Allah’s enduring mercy and compassion toward the believers, both in this world and in the Hereafter.",
  33044: "Their greeting on the Day they meet Him will be, “Peace.” And He has prepared for them an honorable reward.",
  33045: "O Prophet, indeed We have sent you as a witness, a bearer of good news, and a warner, [31]\\n\\nFootnotes: [31]. That is, Allāh sent the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ with multiple divinely assigned roles: as a witness over humanity by conveying the message faithfully and testifying to people’s response on the Day of Judgment; as a bearer of good news to the believers, promising forgiveness, mercy, and Paradise for faith and righteous deeds; and as a warner to those who reject faith, cautioning them against disbelief, injustice, and the consequences of disobedience. Together, these roles encompass the Prophet’s ﷺ comprehensive mission of guidance, accountability, mercy, and moral instruction for all people.\nAllāh sent the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ into a world marked by oppression, injustice, and conflict, to guide humanity from darkness into light, from servitude to creation to worship of the Creator, and from injustice to the justice of Islam. His mission was to proclaim the truth, establish justice, teach the ignorant, liberate the oppressed, and restore human dignity. He exemplified this mission not only through words but through action, showing special care for the poor, the weak, and the marginalized. The Qur’an repeatedly emphasizes compassion and social responsibility, commanding believers to uphold justice and mercy. Following the Prophet ﷺ entails embodying these values—serving others with love, righteousness, and commitment—as a divinely entrusted responsibility.",
  33046: "and as one who calls to Allāh by His permission, and as an illuminating lamp.[32]\\n\\nFootnotes: [32]. This verse describes the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ as a caller to Allāh, meaning that his mission was to invite humanity to faith, obedience, and guidance only by Allah’s permission and authority, not by personal power or desire. He is also described as an “illuminating lamp\", signifying that his guidance dispels the darkness of ignorance, disbelief, and moral confusion, just as a lamp provides light in darkness. Through his message, character, and practice, the Prophet ﷺ illuminated the path of truth, righteousness, mercy, and justice, enabling humanity to see clearly the way to Allāh and upright living.",
  33047: "Give good news to the believers that they will have a great bounty from Allāh.",
  33048: "Do not obey the disbelievers and the hypocrites, overlook their harm, and place your complete trust in Allāh. Allāh is sufficient as a Trustee.",
  33049: "O believers, when you marry believing women and then divorce them before you have touched them [33], there is no waiting period for you to count for them. So give them a suitable provision and release them in a fitting and gracious manner.\\n\\nFootnotes: [33]. This verse clarifies that if a marriage is dissolved before consummation, the woman is not required to observe a waiting period (ʿiddah). Nevertheless, the husband is obligated to provide a suitable gift or provision and to release her with dignity, kindness, and fairness, reflecting Islam’s emphasis on compassion and justice even at the time of separation.",
  33050: "O Prophet, indeed, We have made lawful for you your wives to whom you have given their dowries; those whom your right hand possesses from among those Allāh has granted you; the daughters of your paternal uncles, the daughters of your paternal aunts, the daughters of your maternal uncles, and the daughters of your maternal aunts who migrated with you; and a believing woman if she offers herself to the Prophet, if the Prophet wishes to marry her—this being exclusively for you and not for the rest of the believers. We know well what We have ordained for them regarding their wives and those whom their right hands possess, so that there may be no hardship upon you. Allāh is ever Oft-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  33051: "You, [O Prophet], may postpone [the turn of] whom you will of them, and you may take to yourself whom you will. And anyone whom you desire of those whom you had set aside—there is no blame upon you to take her again. That is more suitable so that their eyes may be comforted, they may not grieve, and all of them may be content with what you give them. Allāh knows what is in your hearts. And Allāh is Ever-Knowing, Most Forbearing",
  33052: "It is not lawful for you [O Prophet] to marry women after this, nor to exchange them [34] for other wives [35], even though their beauty may please you—except those whom your right hand possesses. Allāh is ever Watchful over all things.\\n\\nFootnotes: [34]. That is, your present wives. After the specific permissions previously granted to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ were completed, he was no longer permitted to contract new marriages. This restriction was unique to him and served to define and conclude the special marital rulings that applied exclusively to the Prophet ﷺ.\n[35]. Meaning: he was not permitted to divorce any of his existing wives in order to marry others in their place, regardless of beauty, social status, or personal preference. This ruling upheld the honor, security, and dignity of the Mothers of the Believers, ensuring stability within the Prophet’s household. The verse concludes by affirming that Allāh is fully aware of all actions and intentions, reinforcing accountability and divine oversight.",
  33053: "O believers, do not enter the houses of the Prophet unless permission is given to you for a meal—without waiting for it to be prepared. But when you are invited [36], then enter; and when you have eaten, then disperse, without lingering for conversation. Indeed, that would annoy the Prophet, yet he is shy to ask you [to leave], but Allāh is not shy of stating the truth. And when you ask his wives for anything, ask them from behind a screen. That is purer for your hearts and for their hearts. It is not lawful for you to annoy the Messenger of Allāh, nor to ever marry his wives after him. Indeed, that would be a grave offense in the sight of Allāh.\\n\\nFootnotes: [36]. This instruction establishes etiquette (ādāb) for interacting with the household of the Prophet ﷺ. Believers are commanded to respect privacy, avoid inconvenience, and refrain from overstaying or engaging in unnecessary conversation. The verse highlights the Prophet’s ﷺ natural modesty and patience, while affirming that Allāh openly clarifies what is right. The command to address the Prophet’s wives from behind a screen (ḥijāb) serves to protect mutual purity of hearts, honor their unique status as the Mothers of the Believers, and preserve moral boundaries. The prohibition against harming the Prophet ﷺ or marrying his wives after him underscores their permanent honor and the sanctity of the Prophet’s household, declaring any violation a serious offense before Allāh.\nThe Prophet ﷺ said: “The rights of a believer over another are six.” It was said: What are they, O Messenger of God? He said: “If you meet him, greet him; if he invites you, accept the invitation; if he asks for advice, give him sincere advice; if he sneezes and praises Allāh (by saying Al-Hamdulillaah), say Yarhamukallaah (may Allāh have mercy on you); if he falls sick, visit him; and if he dies, attend his funeral.”",
  33054: "Whether you reveal a matter or conceal it, indeed Allāh is ever All-Knowing of all things.",
  33055: "There is no blame upon women [if they appear unveiled] before their fathers [37], or their sons, or their brothers, or their brothers’ sons, or their sisters’ sons, or their fellow women, or those whom their right hands possess. Fear Allāh. Indeed, Allāh is ever a Witness over all things.\\n\\nFootnotes: [37]. Or their paternal uncles or maternal uncles.\nThis verse clarifies the categories of maḥram (close male relatives) and others before whom a woman is not required to observe full covering, due to the absence of lawful sexual interest and the presence of familial trust. These include fathers, sons, brothers, nephews (from both brothers and sisters), as well as women of the community and those whom their right hands possess. The ruling balances modesty with practicality, ensuring ease within family and private settings while maintaining moral boundaries. The verse concludes with a reminder to fear Allāh, affirming that all conduct—public and private—is under His constant witness and accountability.",
  33056: "Indeed, Allāh sends His blessings upon the Prophet, and His angels [invoke blessings for him]. O believers, ask Allāh to bestow His blessings upon him and greet him with a worthy greeting. [38]\\n\\nFootnotes: [38]. This verse establishes the divine command to send blessings and salutations (ṣalāh and salām) upon the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. Allāh’s blessing signifies mercy, honor, and exaltation of rank, while the angels’ blessings represent continual prayers for forgiveness and elevation. Believers are commanded to participate by invoking Allāh’s blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ and greeting him with peace and reverence. This act expresses love, obedience, gratitude, and recognition of his prophetic role, and it strengthens spiritual connection, faith, and remembrance. Sending blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ is a continuous act of worship and a distinguishing practice\nA companion of the Prophet said:  We asked Allâh’s Messenger ﷺ saying, ‘O Allâh’s Messenger! How should one (ask Allâh to) send blessings upon you and the members of the family of the prophet ﷺ, for Allāh has taught us how to greet you?’ He said, ‘Say Allahumma Salli ‘alâ Muhammadin Wa‘âlâ âli Muhammadin, Kamâ Sal-laita ‘alâ Ibrâhîma wa ‘ali âli Ibrahîma, Innaka Hamidun Majid. Allahumma bârik ‘alâ Muhammadin wa ‘alâ âli Muhammadin Kamâ bârakta ‘alâ Ibrâhîma wa ‘alâ âli Ibrâhîm, Innaka Hamidun Majid.’ [O Allâh! Send Your prayers (Graces, Honours and Mercy) upon Muhammad and on the family or the followers of Muhammad, as You sent Your prayers (Graces Honour and Mercy) on Abraham and on the family or the followers of Abraham, for You are the Most Praise-Worthy, the Most Glorious. O Allâh! Send Your Blessings upon Muhammad and upon the family or the followers of Muhammad as You sent your Blessings on Abraham and upon the family or the followers of Abraham, for You are the Most Praise-Worthy, the Most Glorious.’.",
  33057: "Indeed, those who offend Allāh [39] and His Messenger [40] are cursed by Allāh in this world and in the Hereafter, and He has prepared for them a humiliating punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [39]. Offending Allāh includes disbelief, rejection of His commands, denial of His signs, distortion of His revelation, and persistent disobedience, as well as attributing to Him what is unbefitting. Such actions constitute a direct violation of divine sanctity and authority.\n[40]. Offending the Messenger refers to insulting, harming, mocking, disobeying, or undermining the honor, message, or authority of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, whether through words, actions, or deliberate disrespect. This includes causing harm to his person, his family, or his mission, or opposing the guidance he conveyed. Since the Messenger ﷺ conveys Allāh’s revelation, harming him is inseparably linked to harming Allāh’s cause.",
  33058: "Those who abuse believing men and believing women [41] without cause have indeed taken upon themselves slander and a manifest sin.\\n\\nFootnotes: [41]. This verse condemns the act of harming, accusing, or abusing believing men and women unjustly, whether through false allegations, verbal abuse, mockery, defamation, or any form of wrongdoing without valid cause. Such behavior constitutes slander (buhtān) and a clear, manifest sin, as it violates personal dignity, spreads injustice, and disrupts social harmony. The verse underscores Islam’s strong protection of honor and reputation, emphasizing accountability for those who wrong others through words or actions.",
  33059: "O Prophet, tell your wives, your daughters, and the believing women to draw their cloaks closely around themselves [42]. That is more proper so that they may be recognized [43] and not be harassed [44]. Allāh is Ever-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [42]. The command to “draw their cloaks closely around themselves” refers to wearing the outer garment (jilbāb) in a manner that covers the body modestly when appearing in public. This includes concealing the form and adornment, distinguishing public dress from private attire, and upholding dignity and modesty in accordance with Islamic ethical standards.\n[43]. “That they may be recognized” means recognized as respectable, believing women who observe modesty and moral conduct. The purpose is not social status, but clear identification that commands respect and discourages inappropriate behavior from others.\n[44]. “And not be harassed” indicates protection from verbal, physical, or moral harm, including insults, advances, or exploitation. The verse addresses a social reality and provides a preventative measure to safeguard women’s dignity and security, while placing moral responsibility on society to respect and protect them.",
  33060: "If the hypocrites [45], those in whose hearts is a sickness [46], and the rumor-mongers [47] in Madinah do not desist, We will surely incite you against them; then they will not remain your neighbors therein except for a short while. [48]\\n\\nFootnotes: [45]. The hypocrites (al-munāfiqūn) are those who outwardly profess faith while inwardly concealing disbelief, working to undermine the Muslim community through deceit, fear-mongering, and disunity.\n[46].  “Those in whose hearts is a sickness” refers to individuals afflicted with moral corruption, such as lewd intentions, lust, or malicious desire, which manifest in harmful behavior, especially toward women and the vulnerable.\n[47]. The rumor-mongers are those who deliberately spread false reports, panic, and destabilizing gossip, particularly in times of social tension, with the aim of weakening communal trust and security.\n[48]. The verse warns that persistent subversion, immorality, and incitement will not be tolerated indefinitely. If such behavior continues, Allāh grants authority to confront it decisively, resulting in the offenders’ removal from the community. This serves as a measure to protect social order, moral integrity, and public safety, not as arbitrary punishment.",
  33061: "Accursed wherever they are found, they shall be seized and killed decisively. [49]\\n\\nFootnotes: [49]. This verse functioned primarily as a strong deterrent and warning to hypocrites and agitators who sought to undermine the Muslim community. Despite their harmful actions, Muhammad ﷺ did not execute any of them so long as they openly professed Islam, in order to prevent social disorder, false accusations, and greater harm to the community. The threat of severe punishment was intended to restrain persistent subversion and moral corruption, not to authorize indiscriminate violence. Any enforcement, where applicable, was subject to legitimate authority, clear evidence, and considerations of public interest, reflecting the Prophet’s ﷺ commitment to justice, restraint, and societal stability.",
  33062: "Such has been the way of Allāh with those who passed on before, and you will never find any change in the way of Allāh.",
  33063: "People ask you, [O Prophet], about the Hour. Say, “Its knowledge is only with Allāh.” And what will make you realize? Perhaps the Hour is near.",
  33064: "Indeed, Allāh has cursed the disbelievers and has prepared for them a blazing Fire. [50]\\n\\nFootnotes: [50]. The curse of Allāh signifies exclusion from divine mercy and favor as a consequence of persistent disbelief and rejection of truth after it has been made clear. The “blazing Fire” refers to the punishment of the Hereafter, prepared for those who knowingly deny faith and persist in wrongdoing. The verse underscores divine justice and accountability, warning against arrogance and heedlessness while affirming that recompense corresponds to one’s chosen path.",
  33065: "They will abide therein forever. They will find no protector and no helper.",
  33066: "On the Day when their faces will be turned over in the Fire, they will say, “Oh, if only we had obeyed Allāh and obeyed the Messenger.”",
  33067: "They will say, “Our Lord, indeed we obeyed our leaders and our elders, and they led us astray from the right Way.",
  33068: "Our Lord, give them a double punishment and curse them with a mighty curse.”",
  33069: "O believers, do not be like those who slandered Moses; but Allāh cleared him of what they said [51]. And he was honorable in the sight of Allāh.\\n\\nFootnotes: [51]. This verse refers to the false accusations and slander directed against Moses (peace be upon him) by some among his people. Allāh Himself vindicated Moses by exposing their lies and affirming his innocence, integrity, and prophetic honor. The verse warns believers against harming or defaming Allah’s messengers or righteous servants through false speech, suspicion, or disrespect, and affirms that true honor comes from Allāh, who ultimately defends the innocent and upholds justice.\nThis meaning is illustrated in an authentic narration in which the Prophet ﷺ explained that Moses was extremely modest and used to cover his body fully. Some of the Children of Israel falsely accused him of having a physical defect. Allāh cleared Moses of these claims by causing his clothes to be taken away while he was bathing, so that his people saw him and realized he was free of any defect. Thus Allāh vindicated him publicly, as stated in this verse, and confirmed his honorable status in His sight.",
  33070: "O believers, fear Allāh [52] and speak words that are right and just.\\n\\nFootnotes: [52]. Fear Allāh (ittaqullāh) means to maintain constant God-consciousness (taqwā)—being mindful of Allāh in intentions, speech, and actions, and avoiding anything that incurs His displeasure. “Speak words that are right” (qawlan sadīdā) refers to truthful, just, upright, and responsible speech, free from falsehood, exaggeration, injustice, or harm. The verse links inner piety with outward speech, teaching that sincere faith must manifest in honesty, integrity, and moral accountability in what one says.",
  33071: "He will then amend your deeds and forgive your sins. Whoever obeys Allāh and His Messenger has indeed attained a great achievement. [53]\\n\\nFootnotes: [53]. This verse affirms the divine outcome of taqwā (God-consciousness) and upright speech: Allāh rectifies one’s actions, accepts righteous deeds, and forgives sins. “A great achievement” refers to true success in both this life and the Hereafter, encompassing Allāh’s pleasure, salvation from punishment, and entry into Paradise. The verse emphasizes that obedience to Allāh is inseparable from obedience to His Messenger ﷺ, and that such obedience constitutes the highest form of success attainable by a believer.",
  33072: "Indeed, We offered the Trust [54] to the heavens, the earth, and the mountains, but they declined to bear it [55] and feared it. Yet man undertook to bear it. Indeed, he is ever unjust and ignorant. [56]\\n\\nFootnotes: [54]. The Trust (al-Amānah) refers to the comprehensive moral, religious, and legal responsibility entrusted to human beings. It includes faith, free will, accountability, obedience to Allāh, fulfillment of obligations, and adherence to divine commands and prohibitions. Accepting the Trust signifies accepting responsibility for one’s choices and actions before Allāh.\n[55]. The heavens, the earth, and the mountains are described as declining to bear the Trust out of awe and fear of failing to fulfill its weighty responsibility, not out of disobedience. This portrayal emphasizes the immense seriousness and gravity of moral accountability, which even the greatest creations refrained from undertaking.\n[56]. “Ever unjust and ignorant” describes the human tendency—when acting without divine guidance—to wrong oneself through sin and transgression and to behave in ignorance of the consequences of moral failure. This is not an absolute condemnation of humanity, but a warning about human weakness when detached from revelation, repentance, and obedience, all of which elevate humans above injustice and ignorance.",
  33073: "So that [57] Allāh may punish the hypocrite men and hypocrite women, and the polytheist men and polytheist women [58]; and that Allāh may turn in mercy to the believing men and believing women [59]. Allāh is Ever-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [57]. “So that” indicates the divine purpose and outcome of humanity’s acceptance of the Trust (al-Amānah): that moral accountability be established, truth distinguished from falsehood, and justice carried out according to belief and action.\n[58]. The hypocrites are those who outwardly profess faith while inwardly concealing disbelief, whereas the polytheists openly associate partners with Allāh. Both groups—men and women alike—are held equally accountable for rejecting or betraying the Trust, and thus face punishment as a consequence of conscious disbelief and moral corruption.\n[59]. In contrast, Allāh turns in mercy and repentance to the believing men and women—those who uphold faith, sincerity, and obedience. This includes acceptance of repentance, forgiveness of sins, and bestowal of divine compassion. The verse concludes by affirming that Allah’s mercy predominates, as He is Ever-Forgiving and Most Merciful toward those who believe and return to Him.",
  34001: "All praise be to Allāh alone—to whom belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is in the earth. His is all praise in the Hereafter. He is the All-Wise, the All-Aware.",
  34002: "He knows whatever penetrates into the earth and whatever comes forth from it, whatever descends from the heaven and whatever ascends to it. And He is the Most Merciful, the Most Forgiving.",
  34003: "Those who disbelieve say, “The Hour will never come upon us.” Say, “Yes indeed—by my Lord, it will surely come upon you.” He is the Knower of the unseen; not even the weight of an atom escapes His knowledge in the heavens or in the earth, nor anything smaller than that or greater, except that it is in a clear Register.",
  34004: "So that He may reward those who believe and do righteous deeds. Those, for them is forgiveness and an honorable provision. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. This verse states the purpose of divine knowledge, resurrection, and judgment: to reward those who combine faith (īmān) with righteous deeds. Forgiveness signifies the removal of sins and their consequences, while “an honorable provision” refers to the noble and everlasting sustenance of Paradise, marked by dignity, peace, and nearness to Allāh. The verse highlights that true success lies not in mere belief alone, but in belief expressed through righteous conduct.",
  34005: "But those who strive against Our verses, seeking to undermine them—those will have a punishment of painful torment.",
  34006: "Those who have been given knowledge [2] see that what has been sent down to you, [O Muhammad], from your Lord is the truth, and that it guides to the path of the All-Mighty, the Praiseworthy.\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. Those who have been given knowledge refers to people of sound understanding and insight—among the Muslims and the true believers from the People of the Scripture—who recognize the truth of divine revelation through sincere knowledge and reflection. This includes those from earlier scriptures who embraced faith upon recognizing the Qur’an as confirmation of previous revelation. Their knowledge enables them to see that what has been sent down to Muhammad ﷺ from his Lord is the absolute truth and that it guides to the path of Allāh, the All-Mighty and the Praiseworthy. The verse highlights that genuine knowledge leads to faith, humility, and guidance, not arrogance or denial.",
  34007: "Those who disbelieve say, [mockingly], “Shall we point out to you a man who will tell you that when you have been utterly torn apart and completely disintegrated, you will indeed be in a new creation? [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. This statement reflects the mockery and denial of resurrection by the disbelievers, who considered bodily decay and disintegration into dust as proof that re-creation is impossible. The phrase “utterly torn apart” emphasizes complete physical decomposition, leaving nothing recognizable. Their ridicule targets the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ and the core Islamic belief in resurrection after death, which the Qur’an repeatedly affirms as a fundamental reality grounded in Allāh’s absolute power and knowledge. The verse exposes the flawed reasoning of disbelief, which measures divine capability by human limitations and ignores the One who created in the first instance.",
  34008: "Has he fabricated a lie against Allāh, or is there madness in him? Rather, those who do not believe in the Hereafter will be in punishment and in farthest error. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. This verse records the false accusations made by the disbelievers against the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, oscillating between accusing him of deliberate fabrication and madness, reflecting their inability to confront the truth of his message. Allāh decisively rejects both claims, declaring instead that the real error lies with those who deny the Hereafter. Their rejection places them in active punishment and in extreme misguidance, as denial of resurrection undermines moral accountability, justice, and divine wisdom. The verse exposes how disbelief often resorts to character attacks when rational argument fails.",
  34009: "Have they not considered what is before them and what is behind them of the heaven and the earth? If We willed, We could cause the earth to swallow them, or cause fragments of the sky to fall upon them. Indeed, in this there is a sign for every servant who turns back to Allāh in repentance.",
  34010: "Indeed, We bestowed favors upon David: “O mountains, echo Our praises with him, and you birds as well.” And We made iron pliable for him, [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. This verse highlights the special divine favors granted to David (peace be upon him). Allāh enabled the mountains and birds to join him in glorifying Allāh, symbolizing the harmony of creation in obedience to divine command. Making iron pliable for David refers to a miraculous ability granted to him—allowing him to shape iron without fire or excessive force—through which he produced armor and tools. The verse reflects the union of spiritual devotion (praise of Allāh) and practical skill, showing that divine favor encompasses both worship and constructive worldly benefit.",
  34011: "\"Make full coats of mail, measure precisely the links, and work righteousness [6]. Indeed, I am Seeing of all what you do.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. “Work righteousness” renders is a recurring Qurʾānic imperative that joins ethical conduct with skilled action. In this context, it links technical craftsmanship with moral responsibility, indicating that excellence in material production must be accompanied by upright intention and conduct. The command follows the instruction on armor-making to emphasize that skill, precision, and labor are subject to ethical accountability under divine observation.\nPursuing righteousness leads to a life marked by integrity, steadfastness, and devotion to Allāh rather than self. It is expressed through just actions, truthful and reliable speech, and a heart firmly centered on what pleases Him. From a righteous heart flows goodness—words that encourage, uplift, and edify rather than deceive, manipulate, or harm. Because words carry weight, they should always reflect grace and truth, avoiding gossip and choosing mercy toward others as we desire mercy ourselves.\nRighteousness also requires rejecting evil and refusing to participate in wicked ways. It means loving what Allāh loves and hating\nwhat He hates, honoring those who fear Him, and showing respect, encouragement, and grace to fellow believers. A righteous person keeps their word, holds themselves accountable regardless of cost, and cannot be swayed by bribery or personal gain.\nGenerosity is another hallmark of righteousness. Trusting Allāh as the Provider allows one to give freely, without interest or expectation of return. Ultimately, steadfast righteousness is sustained by daily reliance on Allāh in every action and interaction.",
  34012: "And to Solomon [We subjected] the wind: its morning course was a month’s journey, and its evening course was a month’s journey. We caused a spring of molten brass to flow for him. And among the jinn were those who worked before him by the command of his Lord. Whoever among them deviated from Our command—We shall make him taste the punishment of the blazing Fire.",
  34013: "They made for him whatever he wanted —places of worship [7], images, vast basins like reservoirs, and massive fixed cooking cauldrons. \"Act in gratitude, O family of David\". Yet only a few of My servants are grateful.\" [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. “Places of worship ” translates maḥārīb, which in this context refers to elevated or designated spaces for worship, devotion, or governance. Scholars describe them as places set aside for prayer and remembrance, not merely architectural structures, emphasizing Solomon’s kingdom as one oriented toward devotion alongside power.\n[8]. This statement underscores a recurring Qurʾānic theme: gratitude is not merely verbal acknowledgment, but sustained obedience, humility, and righteous action. Despite abundant divine favor, true gratitude—expressed through faithful conduct—remains rare among people.\nIn a culture driven by consumption and constant distraction, believers are called to guard their time and priorities so they are not drawn away from devotion to Allāh. While rest and recreation have their place, excessive focus on entertainment and material gain can prevent meaningful contribution. Allāh calls His servants to be active laborers—serving their mosques, communities, and those around them. By choosing contribution over consumption, even through small acts of service, believers fulfill Allāh’s purpose and reflect His light in a world in need.",
  34014: "Then, when We decreed his death, nothing informed them of his death except a termite gnawing at his staff. When he collapsed, the jinn realized that had they known the unseen, they would not have remained in humiliating suffering.",
  34015: "There was truly a sign for the people of Sheba in their homeland: two flourishing orchards, one to the right and one to the left. \"Eat from the provision of your Lord and give thanks to Him—a good land and a Most Forgiving Lord.\"",
  34016: "But they turned away, so We sent upon them a devastating flood [9], and We replaced their two gardens with two other gardens bearing bitter fruit, tamarisk, and only a few sparse lote trees.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. The “devastating flood” refers to the collapse of the Maʾrib dam, which had sustained Sheba’s prosperity. Its destruction signified the withdrawal of divine favor after persistent ingratitude. The replacement of lush orchards with bitter, sparse vegetation symbolizes not only environmental ruin but also moral consequence: when gratitude is abandoned, blessing is diminished. The verse highlights a Qurʾānic principle that prosperity is sustained through thankfulness and obedience, not merely infrastructure or power.",
  34017: "This is how We repaid them for their ingratitude—and do We ever punish anyone except those who are persistently ungrateful?",
  34018: " 'Before their downfall', We placed between them and the towns We had blessed [10] many visible towns, and We precisely determined the stages of travel between them: \"Travel through them safely, by night and by day.\"\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. “The towns We had blessed” is understood to refer to regions of sustained prosperity and security, often identified with Greater Syria (al-Shām). The placement of visible towns at measured intervals provided safe passage, ease of travel, and economic stability, reflecting an extensive divine favor. This infrastructure of security and accessibility underscores that prosperity included not only fertile land, but also protected movement and social order—blessings later withdrawn due to persistent ingratitude.",
  34019: "But they said, \"Our Lord, lengthen the distances between our journeys.\" And they wronged themselves, so We made them tales [of the past] and scattered them in complete scattering. Indeed, in this are signs for every one who is steadfastly patient and deeply grateful.",
  34020: "Indeed, Iblīs found true his assumption about them [11], so they followed him—except for a group of the believers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. I.e., the people of Sheba or humankind in general.\nSatan's “conjecture” refers to his claim that most people would succumb to misguidance and fail to remain grateful and obedient to Allāh (cf. Qurʾān 7:16–17). The verse clarifies that Satan possesses no coercive power; his success lies only in persuasion and human choice. The exception—“a group of the believers”—highlights that faith, patience, and gratitude enable resistance to satanic influence, reinforcing moral responsibility rather than predestination.",
  34021: "And he had no authority over them, except that We might make evident those who believe in the Hereafter from those who are in doubt about it [12]. Your Lord is Preserver over all things.\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. This verse affirms that Iblīs possesses no coercive authority over humanity. His influence serves only as a means of distinction, exposing what already lies within the human heart. Those firmly convinced of the Hereafter resist misguidance, while doubt renders others vulnerable. The test is not for divine knowledge—Allāh already knows all—but for manifestation and moral responsibility, so belief and disbelief become evident through choice and action.\nTo avoid coming under Satan’s influence or control, you need to learn how he manipulates people, so that you are not ignorant of his designs. By reading the Qur’an and the Sunnah of the Prophet Muhammad, you can obtain vital knowledge of Satan’s tactics and thus avoid becoming a victim. Get rid of any equipments associated with demon activity. That includes crosses, amulets, charms, printed matter, videos, music, and computer applications that promote spiritism or divination.",
  34022: "Say, \"Invoke those you claim [to be gods] besides Allāh. They own not even the weight of an atom in the heavens or on the earth, nor do they share in either of them. And He has no helper among them. [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. The verse negates every possible basis for associating partners with Allāh: ownership, shared authority, and assistance. By systematically denying each claim, it establishes that all power, control, and governance belong exclusively to Allāh, rendering all objects of worship besides Him entirely powerless.\nWith all such claims negated, nothing remains except intercession—which itself is entirely subject to Allāh’s permission. Thus, intercession holds no independent power and benefits only those whom Allāh allows, affirming His absolute sovereignty and exclusive authority.",
  34023: "“No intercession shall benefit with Him except for one to whom He has granted permission—until, when fear is removed from their hearts [14], they will say, \"What has your Lord said?\" They will say, \"The Truth.\" And He is the Most High, the Most Great.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. This verse depicts the awe and reverence surrounding divine speech. When Allāh issues a decree, the angels are overcome with fear and humility until it is lifted, after which they affirm the truth of His command. The scene emphasizes that even the highest created beings possess no independence before Allāh; authority, speech, and intercession operate solely by His permission. Thus, the verse affirms Allāh’s absolute transcendence and majesty while clarifying that intercession is neither automatic nor autonomous.",
  34024: "Say, \"Who provides for you from the heavens and the earth?\" Say, \"Allāh.\" And indeed, either we or you [15] are upon right guidance, or in clear error.\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. The verse affirms that provision itself testifies to Allāh’s lordship, and that acknowledgment of this reality logically leads to recognizing where true guidance lies. It models a Qurʾānic method of dialogue: firm in truth, yet measured and inviting reflection.\nThe statement —“either we or you”—refer to the believers and the pagans. While presented as a fair comparison to invite reflection rather than confrontation, the conclusion is not uncertain: the rightly guided are the believers, and those in manifest error are the pagans. The verse thus combines confidence in the truth with a measured, reasoned invitation to acknowledge it.",
  34025: "Say, \"You will not be questioned about what we committed of wrongdoing, nor will we be questioned about what you do.\" [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. This verse emphasizes personal moral responsibility. Each group is accountable for its own deeds, not for the actions of others. The wording maintains a calm and non-confrontational tone, reinforcing the Qurʾānic method of dialogue: truth is asserted without hostility, leaving judgment to Allāh. It also subtly affirms confidence in the believers’ position, as accountability ultimately rests on divine justice.",
  34026: "Say, \"Our Lord will gather us together, then He will judge between us with truth [and justice], for He is the All-Knowing Judge.\"",
  34027: "Say, \"Show me those whom you have associated with Him as partners.\" No indeed! Rather, He is Allāh—the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.”",
  34028: "We have not sent you [O Muhammad] except as a bearer of good news and as a warner to all people [17], but most people do not know. [18]\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. “To all people” affirms the universality of the Prophet’s mission. Unlike earlier messengers sent to specific communities, Muhammad is sent to all humanity, across time and place. His message of guidance, accountability, and mercy is therefore not limited by ethnicity, geography, or era.\nIf you declare with your mouth, “that there is no god except Allāh and that Mohammad is Allah's servant and messenger,” and believe that in your heart, you will be saved from the Hellfire and admitted to heaven 'Paradise'.\n[18]. “Most people do not know” points to widespread ignorance—not a lack of access to truth, but a failure to recognize, reflect, or respond to it. This ignorance often stems from heedlessness, inherited assumptions, or attachment to worldly concerns rather than sincere inquiry.",
  34029: "They say, \"When will this promise be fulfilled, if you are truthful?\"",
  34030: "Say, \"There is for you an appointed Day which you can neither delay by a moment nor advance.\"",
  34031: "Those who disbelieve say, \"We will never believe in this Qur’an, nor in what came before it.\" But if only you could see when the wrongdoers are made to stand before their Lord, casting blame upon one another. Those who were deemed weak will say to those who were arrogant, \"Had it not been for you, we surely would have been believers.\"",
  34032: "Those who were arrogant will say to those who were deemed weak, \"Was it we who turned you away from guidance after it had come to you? Rather, you yourselves were guilty.\"",
  34033: "Those who were considered weak will say to the arrogant, \"No—it was your constant plotting night and day, when you urged us to disbelieve in Allāh and to associate partners with Him.\" When they see the punishment, they will hide their regret, and We will place chains around the necks of the disbelievers. Are they repaid for anything other than what they themselves used to do?",
  34034: "We never sent a warner to a town except that its affluent ones said, \"Indeed, we are disbelievers in what you have been sent with.\"",
  34035: "They also said, \"We possess greater wealth and more children, and we will not be punished.\"",
  34036: "Say, \"Indeed, my Lord extends provision for whom He wills or straitens it, but most people do not know.\"",
  34037: "It is not your wealth nor your children that bring you nearer to Us in rank [19]; rather, whoever believes and does righteous deeds—for them there is a multiplied reward for their righteousness, and they will dwell in the upper chambers [of Paradise], safe and secure.\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. That is, earns the good Pleasure of Allāh.\nNearness to Allāh is not determined by material possessions or family status, but by faith (īmān) and righteous action (ʿamal ṣāliḥ). Wealth and children are tests, not measures of honor. The “double reward” signifies divine generosity—reward exceeding the apparent value of deeds—while the “upper chambers” symbolize elevated rank, honor, and lasting security in Paradise. The verse thus redefines true success as spiritual closeness and eternal peace rather than worldly abundance.",
  34038: "But those who strive against Our verses, seeking to undermine them—those will be brought into the punishment.",
  34039: "Say, \"My Lord grants abundant provision to whom He wills and limits it 'for whom He wills'. Whatever you give in charity, He will replace it; and He is the Best of providers.\"",
  34040: "On the Day He gathers them all [20], He will  say to the angels, \"Was it you that they used to worship?\"\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. This question is not posed for information, for Allāh knows all things. Rather, it serves to expose false worship publicly and absolve the angels of any claim to divinity. By addressing the angels directly, the verse dismantles the beliefs of those who venerated angels or other created beings, demonstrating that such worship was unfounded and unauthorized. The scene underscores that all forms of devotion directed to created beings will be repudiated on the Day of Judgment, and that worship belongs to Allāh alone.",
  34041: "They will say, \"Glory be to You! You are our Protector, not them. Rather, they used to worship the jinn; most of them were believers in them.\"",
  34042: "Today, none of you has the power to benefit or harm another. And We will say to those who associated others with Allāh, \"Taste the punishment of the Fire which you used to deny.\"",
  34043: "When Our clear verses are recited to them, they say, \"He is only a man trying to divert you from what your forefathers worshipped.\" They also say, \"This is nothing but a fabricated falsehood.\" And when the truth comes to them, the disbelievers say, \"This is only clear magic.\"",
  34044: "We had not given them any scriptures that they could study, nor had We sent to them before you any warner.",
  34045: "Those before them also rejected [the truth], though these have not attained even a tenth of what We gave them. Yet they rejected My messengers—so how severe was My reproach!",
  34046: "Say [O Prophet], \"I only admonish you to one thing: that you stand for Allāh, in pairs and individually, then reflect. Your companion is not possessed; he is only a warner to you before a severe punishment.\"",
  34047: "Say, \"Whatever payment I may have asked of you—it is for yourselves. My reward is only with Allāh, and He is a Witness over all things.\"",
  34048: "Say, \"Indeed, my Lord hurls the truth [against falsehood], and He has full knowledge of the unseen.\"",
  34049: "Say, \"The truth has come, and falsehood [21] can neither begin nor restore [anything].\"\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. “Falsehood” (al-bāṭil) refers to all forms of idolatry, disbelief, and fabricated claims opposed to divine truth. The statement that it can neither begin nor restore signifies its total impotence: falsehood cannot initiate guidance, sustain life, or revive what has perished. Once truth is established, falsehood loses all creative, corrective, and redemptive power. The verse thus proclaims the finality and permanence of truth, affirming that error survives only in the absence of revelation.",
  34050: "Say, \"If I go astray, I only go astray against myself; and if I am rightly guided, it is by what my Lord reveals to me. Indeed, He is All-Hearing, Ever-Near.\"",
  34051: "If only you could see when they are seized with terror—there will be no escape—and they will be taken from a place nearby. [22]\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. “From a place nearby” emphasizes the suddenness and inescapability of divine seizure. This denotes how swiftly and effortlessly judgment overtakes the deniers—without distance, delay, or refuge. The terror described is not anticipatory fear, but shock at an unavoidable reality. The verse reinforces a Qurʾānic theme: when accountability arrives, power, flight, and preparation are no longer possible.",
  34052: "Then they will say, \"Now we believe in it.’ But how can they attain faith from such a distant place?” [23]\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. “From such a far away place” signifies the impossibility of accepting faith after the time of choice has passed. The belief expressed at the moment of judgment—when the unseen has become seen and accountability is unavoidable—no longer carries moral value. Faith must be chosen freely in the realm of testing, not compelled by certainty at the moment of reckoning. The verse thus underscores a central Qurʾānic principle: repentance and belief are only valid before the arrival of final judgment.",
  34053: "While they had disbelieved in it before [24], casting [their conjectures] at the unseen from a distant place. [25]\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. “They disbelieved in it before” refers to their prior, deliberate rejection of the truth during worldly life—the time of moral choice and accountability. Their later profession of belief arises only after certainty and judgment have overtaken them, when faith no longer carries any value.\n[25]. “From a distant place” is a metaphor for ignorance and remoteness from guidance. To “hurl guesses at the unseen” signifies speaking without knowledge—making assertions about Allāh, revelation, and the Hereafter based on conjecture rather than evidence. The image contrasts sharply with true faith, which is grounded in revelation and sincere reflection, not speculation from afar.",
  34054: "And a barrier will be placed between them and what they desire [26], as was done with those like them before. Indeed, they were in disturbing doubt. [27]\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. “What they desire” refers to what is now sought but no longer attainable: faith, repentance, escape from punishment, or return to worldly life. This barrier marks the final separation between desire and possibility—a consequence of delaying belief until the time of choice had passed.\n[27]. “Disquieting doubt” describes a state of persistent uncertainty that unsettles rather than sincerely seeks truth. It is not honest questioning, but hesitation mixed with denial—doubt that prevents commitment and ultimately leads to loss. Such doubt differs from sincere inquiry, which the Qurʾān encourages; here it denotes skepticism that resists resolution despite clear signs.",
  35001: "All praise be to Allāh, Originator of the heavens and the earth, who appointed the angels as messengers with wings—two, three, or four [1]. He adds to creation whatever He wills. Indeed, Allāh has full power over all things.\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. The mention of angels with “wings—two, three, or four” affirms both the reality and diversity of the angelic realm. The numbers indicate variation, not limitation, as suggested by the following statement: “He increases in creation whatever He wills.”  This highlights Allāh’s unrestricted creative power—no form, capacity, or function in creation is fixed beyond His will. The verse thus opens the sūrah by establishing Allāh as the absolute Originator, Sustainer, and Governor of both the seen and unseen worlds.",
  35002: "Whatever Allāh opens for people of His mercy, none can withhold it; and whatever He withholds, none can release thereafter. He is the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.",
  35003: "O people, remember the favors of Allāh upon you. Is there any creator other than Allāh who provides for you from the heaven and the earth? There is no god except Him. So how are you deluded?",
  35004: "If they reject you, [O Muhammad], then messengers were rejected before you. To Allāh return all matters.",
  35005: "O people, indeed the promise of Allāh is true. So do not let the life of this world deceive you [2], nor let the chief deceiver [Satan] deceive you concerning Allāh. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. “The life of this world” refers to its attractions—wealth, status, pleasure, and false security—that can distract from the Hereafter. The warning is not against lawful enjoyment, but against being deceived into thinking worldly success equals divine approval or lasting safety.\nOne should not be overly attached to worldly (non-spiritual) things. Believers are to love Allāh and His messenger Muhammad ﷺ more than any one else, and love also one another, not the world or possessions. Those who love the world whole heartedly, cannot truly love Allāh. These highlight the contrast between love of the world and love of Allāh. Jesus, Allah’s Messenger to Israelites, offered an example of this contrast when He was tempted by Satan in the wilderness. Satan told Jesus He could rule all the kingdoms of the world  - if He would bow down to him. Jesus replied, \"Be gone, Satan. For it is written: You shall worship Allāh your God and him only shall you serve and worship\".\n[3]. “The chief deceiver” refers to Satan (Iblīs), whose primary strategy is to distort understanding of Allāh—His mercy, justice, and promise—leading people to delay repentance, deny accountability, or presume forgiveness without obedience.\nO Allāh, deliver me from the devil’s deception. Saving God, give me grace to live for You and die for you, to pray to You only and to make my sacrifices and vows to You only. There is none like You O Allāh, and there is no god besides You.",
  35006: "Indeed, Satan is an enemy to you, so consider him as an enemy [4]. He only invites his party that they may be among the inmates of the Blazing Fire.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. Satan is to be looked upon as an enemy, and to be opposed as such, and to be watched and guarded against; the whole armour of God should be taken and made use of, particularly the weapon of prayer, the word of God 'the Qur’an', and the shield of faith; and also the grace of humility.\nThis verse establishes Satan as a declared and permanent enemy of humanity. The command to “take him as an enemy” is not merely descriptive but practical: it requires vigilance, resistance, and refusal to follow his whispers. Satan’s influence is not neutral—his sole aim is to lead people toward destruction. The verse also clarifies that he has no power to compel; he only invites, and people choose whether to respond. Accountability therefore remains entirely human, while enmity toward Satan is a necessary condition for spiritual safety.",
  35007: "Those who disbelieve will have a severe punishment; but those who believe and do righteous deeds will have forgiveness and a great reward.",
  35008: "Is one whose evil deeds have been made to appear fair to him, so that he sees them as good, [like one who is rightly guided]? Surely Allāh leaves astray whom He wills and guides whom He wills. So do not let your soul be consumed with regrets over them. Indeed, Allāh is All-Knowing of what they do.",
  35009: "It is Allāh who sends the winds, so they stir up clouds; then We drive them to a dead land and revive the earth thereby after its death. Thus is the Resurrection.",
  35010: "Whoever seeks honor—then to Allāh belongs all honor. To Him ascend the good words, and righteous deeds raise them. But those who plot evil deeds [5] will have a severe punishment, and the plotting of such people will perish.\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. This verse redefines honor (ʿizzah) as something granted solely by Allāh, not achieved through wealth, status, or manipulation. “Good words” are commonly understood as declarations of faith, remembrance, and truth, while “righteous deeds” give those words weight and acceptance. Speech without action is incomplete, and action without sincerity lacks ascent. In contrast, those who seek power through deception and wrongdoing will find their schemes nullified. The verse thus establishes a moral law: what is sincere rises, what is corrupt collapses.\nAllāh commanded us that we maintain true justice; show mercy and compassion to one another, that we do not oppress the widow or the orphan, the stranger, the foreigner or the poor, and that we do not plot evil against each other.",
  35011: "Allāh created you from dust, then from a drop of semen; then He made you in pairs. No female conceives nor gives birth except with His knowledge. And no one is granted a long life, nor is anything diminished from his lifespan, except that it is in a Register. Indeed, that is easy for Allāh.",
  35012: "The two bodies of water are not alike: one is fresh and sweet, pleasant to drink, and the other is salty and bitter. Yet from each you eat fresh, tender meat and extract ornaments which you wear. You see the ships plowing through it, that you may seek of His bounty and that you may be grateful.",
  35013: "He merges the night into the day and merges the day into the night, and He has subjected the sun and the moon—each running for an appointed term. Such is Allāh, your Lord; to Him belongs all dominion. And those whom you invoke besides Him do not possess even the membrane of a date seed.”",
  35014: "If you invoke them, they do not hear your supplication; and even if they were to hear, they would not respond to you. And on the Day of Resurrection they will disavow your associating them [with Allāh]. And none can inform you like One who is All-Aware.",
  35015: "O people, you are the ones in need of Allāh, while Allāh is the Self-Sufficient, the Praiseworthy.",
  35016: "If He so wills, He can remove you and bring about an entirely new creation.",
  35017: "And that is easy for Allāh. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. This implies that Allāh, our God, is all powerful, that he is able to do everything that he pleases, nothing is hard for him and nothing is hidden from Him.\nThis concluding statement emphasizes that replacement, re-creation, resurrection, and judgment place no burden or limitation upon Allāh. What appears impossible or unimaginable to human beings is effortless for Him. The verse reinforces a recurring Qurʾānic principle: divine power is not constrained by scale, number, or complexity, and therefore accountability, resurrection, and renewal are certain.",
  35018: "No bearer of burdens will bear the burden of another [7]. If a heavily burdened soul calls for its burden to be carried, nothing of it will be carried—even if he be a close relative. You can only warn those who fear their Lord unseen and establish prayer. Whoever purifies himself, he only purifies for his own benefit. And to Allāh is the final return.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. From the Justice of Allāh is that He will not count others who are innocent, as guilty for the sins of their fathers through ‘ancestral’ or ‘original’ sin.\nThis verse establishes the principle of individual moral responsibility. No one can transfer guilt, accountability, or consequences to another—family ties, status, or closeness offer no exemption. Even sincere appeals at the time of reckoning cannot shift one’s burden. The verse then clarifies who truly benefits from warning: those who possess reverent fear of Allāh in the unseen and demonstrate it through prayer. Self-purification (tazkiyah) yields benefit only to the one who undertakes it, underscoring that guidance, worship, and repentance are personal choices. The verse concludes by directing all outcomes to the final return to Allāh, where justice is complete and exact.",
  35019: "Those who are blind [to the truth] and those who see are not equal.",
  35020: "Nor are the darknesses and the light [equal].",
  35021: "Nor are the shade and the scorching heat [equal].",
  35022: "Nor are the living and the dead equal. Indeed, Allāh causes to hear whom He wills, but you cannot make hear those who are in the graves. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. The four examples given by Allāh in verses 19-22 are those of the believer and unbeliever, disbelief and belief, Paradise and Hellfire, and those receptive to guidance and those unreceptive.\n“Those who are in the graves” is a metaphor for people whose hearts are spiritually dead—closed to guidance and unmoved by truth. The verse does not deny the Prophet’s role in conveying the message; rather, it clarifies the limits of human agency. Guidance is ultimately an act of divine grace, not persuasion alone. Just as the physically dead cannot respond to sound, those entrenched in disbelief cannot respond to truth unless Allāh revives their hearts. The verse thus consoles the Prophet while affirming that responsibility is in conveying the message, not in causing acceptance.",
  35023: "You are only a warner. [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. This verse clearly defines the Prophet’s mission: to warn, not to compel belief or guarantee acceptance. Guidance belongs to Allāh alone, while the Messenger’s responsibility is faithful conveyance of the message. The statement consoles the Prophet by removing the burden of outcomes and reinforces a central Qurʾānic principle—accountability lies with the hearer, not the messenger.",
  35024: "Indeed, We have sent you, [O Muhammad], with the truth, as a bearer of good news and a warner [10]. There is no nation except that a warner has passed among them.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. This verse affirms two foundational principles. First, the mission of Muhammad is grounded “with the Truth”—divinely revealed, not self-derived. Second, divine justice is universal: no people are held accountable without prior warning. Throughout history, Allāh has sent messengers or warners to every nation, ensuring that guidance precedes judgment. The verse thus reinforces both the credibility of the Prophet’s message and the fairness of divine accountability.\nSending Prophet Muhammad ﷺ as God's last and final messenger is great blessing. Allāh sent the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ into the world to convey His message. We are  grateful to our prophet who, at the end of His call to Islamic Monotheism, was able to look to heaven and say, “I have brought you glory on earth by finishing well the mission you gave me to do. The Prophet Muhammad’s mission was to fulfill God's plan on the earth, to seek and save the pagans, the lost Jews and Christians and guide them all to the straight Path of Islam.",
  35025: "If they reject you, [O Muhammad], then those before them also rejected. Their messengers came to them with clear proofs, scriptures, and the enlightening Book.",
  35026: "Then I seized those who disbelieved—so how terrible was My reproach.",
  35027: "Do you not see that Allāh sends down rain from the sky, and by it We produce fruits of varying colors? And among the mountains are bands of white and red, of different hues, and others intensely black.",
  35028: "And among people, beasts, and livestock are likewise various colors. Only those of His servants who possess knowledge truly fear Allāh. Indeed, Allāh is All-Mighty, Most Forgiving.",
  35029: "Indeed, those who recite the Book of Allāh [11], establish prayer, and spend from what We have provided for them—secretly and openly—hope for a trade that will never suffer loss.\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. “Recite the Qur’an” entails more than verbal recitation; it includes belief, reflection, and living by its guidance. The verse presents a complete pattern of devotion:\n- Recitation (connection to revelation),\n- Prayer (consistent worship), and\nCharity (social responsibility and trust in Allāh).\nReciting the Qur’an is a spiritual discipline, which means that we need to be disciplined in how we approach it. If you lack the desire to recite the Qur’an, pray and ask that Allāh would give you a desire for His word. Then, build a time into your day to recite his Book, meditate it, and make this time a priority. You should then find people to hold you accountable to a reciting plan. Maybe even start a reciting plan with someone or group.",
  35030: "So that He may repay them in full their rewards and increase them from His bounty. Indeed, He is Forgiving, Most Appreciative.",
  35031: "What We have revealed to you, [O Muhammad], of the Book is the Truth, confirming what came before it [12]. Indeed, Allāh is fully aware of and sees His servants.\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. Of the Torah, the Psalms, the Gospel, etc.\nThis verse establishes both the authenticity and continuity of the Qur’an. It is “the Truth” in itself and also confirms previous revelations—affirming their original divine source while correcting distortions that arose over time. By concluding with All-Aware and All-Seeing, the verse reassures that Allāh knows the sincerity, struggle, and response of every servant to revelation. Acceptance or rejection of the truth does not go unnoticed, and divine judgment is grounded in perfect knowledge and sight.",
  35032: "Then We caused those whom We chose from among Our servants to inherit the Book. Among them are those who wrong themselves [13], among them are those who take a middle course [14], and among them are those who are foremost in good deeds by the permission of Allāh. That is the great bounty.\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. “Those who wrong themselves” refers to believers who accept the truth but fall short in practice—neglecting obligations, committing sins, or failing to live fully by the guidance of the Book. Their wrongdoing is against their own souls, not against Allāh, and does not exclude them from faith.\n[14]. “Those who follow a middle course” are believers who fulfill obligations and avoid major sins, maintaining consistency without excess or neglect. They represent balance and steadiness in faith and practice.",
  35033: "Gardens of Eden—they will enter them all. They will be adorned therein with bracelets of gold and pearls, and their garments therein will be of silk.",
  35034: "They will say, \"All praise be to Allāh, who has removed from us all grief [15]. Indeed, our Lord is surely All-Forgiving, Most Appreciative.\"\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. Grief, sickness, suffering  and sadness are part and parcel of this imperfect world but it is not attributed to Allāh. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: “And evil is not attributable to You O Allāh ”. Thus Allâh’s decree by itself has no evil whatsoever, because it is coming from perfect mercy and wisdom. Evil may, however, result from some of His decrees. Despite this, evil in His decree is not pure evil. It is rather evil in one respect and good in another, or it is evil in one case and good in another. Thus corruption in the land resulting from drought, disease, poverty, and fear is evil, but it is good in another respect. Allâh, the Exalted said: “Corruption has appeared on the land and sea for what men’s hands have earned. Allâh has ordained this for men, so that they may taste some of what they have done, in order that they may turn back [from evil] .” \n“All grief” encompasses every form of sorrow, fear, regret, fatigue, and anxiety experienced in worldly life—whether from hardship, loss, sin, or the uncertainty of outcome. Its complete removal signifies not only emotional relief, but absolute security and contentment, with no possibility of grief returning. The pairing of All-Forgiving and Most Appreciative highlights divine generosity: Allāh forgives shortcomings while abundantly rewarding sincere effort. Paradise is thus entered not by perfection, but by mercy—followed by honor and lasting joy.",
  35035: "[They will say:] \"He who has settled us in the Home of Everlasting Abode out of His bounty—where no exhaustion or weariness will ever touch us.\"",
  35036: "But those who disbelieve—for them is the Fire of Gehinnom. Death will not be decreed for them so that they may die, nor will its punishment be lightened for them. Thus do We recompense every ungrateful one.",
  35037: "They will cry out therein, \"Our Lord, take us out; we will do righteous deeds other than what we used to do.\" [It will be said], \"Did We not grant you a life long enough for whoever would take heed to take heed therein? And did a warner not come to you? So taste [the consequence]; for the wrongdoers will have no helper.\"",
  35038: "Indeed, Allāh is the Knower of the unseen of the heavens and the earth. Surely, He has full knowledge of what is within the chests.",
  35039: "It is He who made you successors upon the earth. So whoever disbelieves—upon him is his disbelief. The disbelief of the disbelievers does not increase them with their Lord except in detestation, and their disbelief does not increase them except in loss.",
  35040: "Say, \"Have you considered your associate-gods whom you invoke besides Allāh? Show me what they have created of the earth, or do they have any share in the heavens? Or have We given them a Book so that they stand upon clear proof from it?\" Rather, the wrongdoers promise one another nothing but delusion.",
  35041: "Indeed, Allāh holds the heavens and the earth lest they vanish. And if they were to vanish, none could hold them after Him. Surely, He is Ever-Forbearing, Most Forgiving.",
  35042: "They swore by Allāh their strongest oaths that if a warner came to them, they would surely be better guided than any of the previous nations. But when a warner came to them, it only increased them in aversion.",
  35043: "Out of arrogance in the land and evil plotting. But evil plotting does not encompass except its own people. Then do they await anything except the way of those who passed before? You will never find any change in Allāh’s way, nor will you find any alteration in Allāh’s way. [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. This verse identifies arrogance and deliberate scheming as the true reasons for rejecting guidance, not lack of evidence. The principle stated is decisive: evil plots ultimately recoil upon their authors. By invoking “the way of the former peoples,” the verse situates present denial within a consistent historical pattern—nations that combined pride with opposition to truth met the same end. The repeated assertion that there is no change or alteration in Allāh’s way affirms the constancy of divine justice: mercy follows repentance, and ruin follows persistent arrogance. History is thus not random, but morally ordered.",
  35044: "Have they not traveled through the land to see what was the end of those before them? They were stronger than them in power. But nothing can escape Allāh in the heavens or on the earth. Indeed, He is All-Knowing, Most Powerful.",
  35045: "If Allāh were to take people to task for what they have committed, He would not leave upon the earth a single living creature. But He grants them respite for an appointed term. Then, when their term comes, surely Allāh is Ever-Seeing of His servants.",
  36001: "Yā Sīn. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2: verse 1.",
  36002: "By the Wise Qur’an. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. This is an oath sworn by Allāh to emphasize what follows. Describing the Qur’an as “Wise” (al-ḥakīm) signifies that it is perfectly ordered, precise in judgment, sound in guidance, and free from contradiction. Its wisdom is evident in its teachings, laws, narratives, and moral direction. By swearing an oath by the Qur’an itself, Allāh highlights its authority, truthfulness, and decisive role as the foundation of the Prophet’s message and the proof of his prophethood.",
  36003: "You [O Muhammad] are truly one of God’s messengers.",
  36004: "On a straight Path. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. The “straight path” signifies clarity, balance, and unwavering truth—free from deviation or excess. It affirms that the message brought by the Prophet is sound in belief, upright in practice, and divinely guided in purpose. Coming immediately after the affirmation of prophethood, this phrase establishes that both the messenger and the message are rightly guided.",
  36005: "It is a revelation from the Almighty, the Most Merciful.",
  36006: "So that you may warn a people whose forefathers were not warned, so they are heedless.",
  36007: "The word has already been justified against most of them [4], so they do not believe.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. “The word” refers to Allāh’s just decree, which becomes established against a people after persistent rejection of guidance. It is not arbitrary predestination, but a confirmation of consequence following repeated denial and heedlessness. As a result, their hearts become unreceptive to faith. The verse therefore affirms both human responsibility and divine justice, clarifying that disbelief is the outcome of sustained refusal after warning.",
  36008: "Indeed, We have placed shackles around their necks, reaching up to their chins, so their heads are forced upward.",
  36009: "We have set a barrier before them and a barrier behind them, and We have covered their vision—so they are unable to see. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The barriers and the covering of vision are metaphorical, describing spiritual blindness rather than physical obstruction. After persistent rejection of guidance, perception itself becomes blocked: the past offers no lesson, the future no warning, and the present no clarity. This condition is not arbitrary, but the consequence of continued refusal until the heart becomes unreceptive. The verse illustrates a Qurʾānic principle: when truth is repeatedly resisted, the ability to recognize it is eventually lost.",
  36010: "It is the same for them whether you warn them or do not warn them—they will not believe. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. This verse does not diminish the duty of warning; rather, it describes the condition of a specific group whose persistent rejection has rendered them unresponsive. Warning remains a proof against them and a mercy for others. The verse emphasizes that guidance is not produced by repetition alone—when the heart is sealed by continued refusal, even clear warning no longer penetrates. The Prophet’s responsibility remains conveyance; acceptance lies with Allāh and the willingness of the listener.",
  36011: "You can only warn those who follow the Reminder and fear the Most Gracious though unseen. So give them good news of forgiveness and a noble reward.",
  36012: "Indeed, We give life to the dead, and We record what they put forward and the traces they leave behind [7]. Everything We have counted in a clear register.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. “Their tracks (traces)” refers to the lasting effects of a person’s actions—good or evil—that continue after death. This includes influences such as guidance or misguidance imparted to others, ongoing charity or harm, and any enduring consequences set in motion during one’s life. The verse thus expands accountability beyond individual acts to their ripple effects over time. The “clear Record” that is the Preserved Tablet denotes the comprehensive divine register in which nothing is omitted—actions, intentions, and consequences alike—underscoring perfect justice and certainty of recompense.\nThe Prophet ﷺ said: \"Among the good deeds that continue to benefit a believer after death are: a knowledge that he taught and disseminated, a righteous child who lived after him and supplicate for him, a Qur'an book that he left as inheritance, a masjid that he built, a house that he built for the wayfarers, a stream that he ran, or a charity that he gave from his wealth during his healthy lifetime so that it would reach him (in rewards) after death.\" This indicate that a righteous child benefits his deceased parents with supplication. It is further demonstrated here that he can benefit them by spending sadaqah, as well as doing other charitable deeds, on their behalf. What benefits him also is teaching and disseminating the knowledge, printing a useful and beneficial book that remains after him, or establishing a charity, the rewards of these things continue to reach him as long as they continue to exist.",
  36013: "Give them an example: the people of a town [8], when messengers were sent to it.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. The “people of the town” are presented as a parable, not merely a historical reference. Although some reports identify the town as Antioch, the Qurʾān deliberately leaves it unnamed so that the lesson remains universal, not confined to a particular location or era. The emphasis is not on geography, but on the recurring human response to divine warning—acceptance, rejection, and consequence. By introducing this example immediately after affirming resurrection and accountability (36:12), the verse prepares the reader to witness how denial of messengers leads to loss, while sincerity and courage in faith lead to honor.",
  36014: "When We sent two messengers to them and they rejected them both, We reinforced them with a third, and they said, \"Indeed, we have been sent to you.\"",
  36015: "They said, \"You are merely humans like us. The Most Compassionate has not sent down anything —you are only telling lies.\"",
  36016: "They said, \"Our Lord knows that we have truly been sent to you.\"",
  36017: "Our responsibility is simply to deliver the message plainly.",
  36018: "They said, \"We consider you a bad omen [9]. If you do not stop, we will surely stone you, and you will suffer a painful punishment from us.\"\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. “We augur an evil omen from you” reflects a superstitious mindset, blaming messengers for misfortune rather than confronting one’s own wrongdoing. Such thinking shifts responsibility away from moral choice and onto the bearers of truth. The threat of stoning marks a turning point in the narrative: when argument fails, intimidation and violence replace reason. The verse illustrates a recurring pattern in the Qurʾān—truth is first denied, then mocked, and finally opposed with force—revealing the moral collapse that follows rejection of guidance.\nThe Prophet ﷺ said: \"There is no passing of disease except with God's Permission, nor is there any bad omen (whether from men or) from birds, nor is there any bad omen in Safar (or in any month). Whenever any of you sees something he assumes as bad omen, he should pray: ‘O Allāh, my Lord, none but You bring good things. None but you prevent evil things. There is no power and no strength except in You.\"",
  36019: "They said, \"Your misfortune lies within yourselves. Is it because you were reminded? No—you are a people who have transgressed all bounds.\"",
  36020: "Then a man [10] came running from the far edge of the city, saying, \"My people, follow the messengers!\"\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. Although some reports identify the man as Habīb the carpenter, the Qurʾān deliberately leaves him unnamed, emphasizing character over identity. \nHis urgency (“rushing”) reflects sincerity, courage, and compassion for his people. Coming from the farthest end of the city suggests both physical distance and moral contrast—while the city’s leaders reject the truth, an ordinary man responds with faith and selfless concern. He stands as a model of moral courage, speaking for truth despite social risk, and demonstrates that guidance often finds its strongest advocates among the humble rather than the powerful.",
  36021: "Follow those who ask nothing of you in return and who are rightly guided.",
  36022: "Why should I not worship the One who created me, when to Him you will all be returned?",
  36023: "Should I take besides Him other gods? If the Most Merciful were to will harm for me, their intercession would be of no use at all, nor could they rescue me.",
  36024: "Indeed, then I would surely be in clear error.",
  36025: "Indeed, I have believed in your Lord, so listen to me. [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. But they killed him.This is a public and courageous declaration of faith. By saying “your Lord”, the speaker affirms that the One he believes in is also the Lord of those opposing him, removing any sense of division or exclusivity. The appeal “so listen to me” expresses urgency and sincere concern, not self-assertion. This open confession likely came at great personal risk, highlighting the Qurʾānic ideal of bearing witness to truth despite the cost.",
  36026: "It was said [to him] [12], \"Enter Paradise.\" He said, \"If only my people knew.\" [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. After he was martyred for the sake of Allāh. “It was said [to him]” indicates a direct divine honor granted immediately after his martyrdom. The command “Enter Paradise” reflects acceptance without delay, signifying sincerity of faith and the high rank of one who bears witness to truth despite danger. The immediacy here is a sign of exceptional favor.\n[13]. His words—“If only my people knew”—reveal pure compassion, not resentment. Even after being killed for his faith, his concern remains for his people’s guidance and salvation. This response exemplifies the highest moral character: wishing good for others even after suffering at their hands. The verse presents him as a model of selfless faith, mercy, and concern for humanity.",
  36027: "For how my Lord has forgiven me and placed me among the honored ones.",
  36028: "We did not send down upon his people after him any host from the heaven, nor were We to send any down.",
  36029: "It was no more than a single shout—then behold! They were extinguished.",
  36030: "How great is the sorrow for humanity! No messenger ever comes to them without their mocking him.",
  36031: "Do they not realize how many generations We destroyed before them—people who will never return to them?",
  36032: "Yet all of them will surely be brought present before Us [for Judgment].",
  36033: "And a sign [14] for them is the dead land: We give life to it and bring forth from it grain, from which they eat.\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. The dead land serves as a visible proof of resurrection. Just as barren earth is revived by rain and yields sustenance, so too can Allāh revive the dead after they have decayed. The sign is deliberately ordinary and recurring—something people witness repeatedly—so that denial of resurrection becomes unreasonable. By linking revival directly to daily provision, the verse also reminds humanity that the One who sustains life now is fully able to restore it again.",
  36034: "And We made within it gardens of date palms and grapevines, and caused to burst forth therefrom springs.",
  36035: "So that they may eat of the fruit thereof, and what their hands have madet it [15]. Will they not then be grateful?\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. The phrase “what their own hands have made” translates wa-mā ʿamilat-hu aydīhim, which admits two complementary readings. It may refer to what people cultivate and process through their labor—highlighting human effort within divine provision. It may also be read as “that which their hands did not make,” emphasizing that the fruit itself is not truly produced by human hands, but granted entirely by Allāh. Both meanings reinforce the same truth: whether through effort or without it, all provision ultimately comes from Allāh, making gratitude the only fitting response.",
  36036: "Glory be to Allāh, who created all pairs: from what the earth grows, from themselves, and from that which they do not know.",
  36037: "A sign for them is the night: We strip from it the day, and behold—they are in darkness.",
  36038: "The sun runs to its appointed resting place. That is the decree of Allāh, the All-Mighty, the All-Knowing.",
  36039: "As for the moon, We have set measured phases for it, until it becomes like an old palm stalk.",
  36040: "It is not for the sun to catch up with the moon, nor does the night outstrip the day. Each is swimming in its own orbit.",
  36041: "And another sign for them is that We carried their offspring in the laden Ark. [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. The “laden Ark” refers to the vessel of Noah, filled with those whom Allāh saved from the flood. Calling later generations “their offspring” emphasizes that human survival itself is a result of divine rescue—their very existence traces back to that deliverance. The Ark is thus a dual sign: a reminder of mercy, in that life was preserved, and of warning, in that rejection led to destruction. By placing this sign alongside cosmic and natural signs (36:33–40), the verse links history and creation as equally clear evidences of Allāh’s power and care.",
  36042: "And We created for them from its like that which they ride. [17]\\n\\nFootnotes: [17] “From its like” refers to means of transport modeled after the Ark, extending the sign beyond Noah’s time. This includes ships and vessels by which people travel across water, and by extension other conveyances granted by divine enabling. The verse emphasizes that human mobility and commerce—often taken for granted—are also forms of divine provision. Just as the Ark was a means of salvation, later modes of transport remain signs of Allāh’s mercy, knowledge, and control over the forces of nature.",
  36043: "If We had willed, We could have drowned them—no one would have answered their cries, and they would not have been rescued.",
  36044: "Except by a mercy from Us and an enjoyment for a time.",
  36045: "When it is said to them, \"Beware of what is before you and what is behind you, so that you may be shown mercy,\"",
  36046: "Yet no sign from among the signs of their Lord ever comes to them except that they turn away from it.",
  36047: "When it is said to them, \"Spend from what Allāh has provided for you,\" those who disbelieve say to those who believe, \"Should we feed one whom Allāh would have fed if He willed? You are only in clear error.\"",
  36048: "They say, \"When will this Promise [of Resurrection and Reckoning] come to pass, if you are truthful?\"",
  36049: "They await nothing but a single shout that will seize them while they are disputing.",
  36050: "Then they will not be able to make any bequest, nor will they be able to return to their families.",
  36051: "The Trumpet will be blown, and at once they will rush out of their graves toward their Lord. [18]\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. The “Trumpet” refers to the divine blast that signals the Resurrection. The term translated as “sepulchres” (graves) emphasizes the state of death and stillness prior to revival. The sudden hastening portrays shock and inevitability—people rise not leisurely, but compelled toward their Lord for judgment. The verse underscores that resurrection is instant, universal, and irresistible, marking the transition from worldly time to final accountability.",
  36052: "“They will say, \"Oh, woe to us! Who has raised us up from our resting place?\" [It will be said], \"This is what the Most Compassionate promised, and the messengers spoke the truth.\"",
  36053: "It will only be a single shout, so behold, they will all be brought before Us.",
  36054: "So today no soul will be wronged in the least, and you will not be recompensed except for what you used to do.",
  36055: "Indeed, the companions of Paradise, that Day, will be busy in joyful engagement.",
  36056: "They and their spouses will be in cool shade, reclining comfortably on couches.",
  36057: "For them therein are fruits and whatever they ask for.",
  36058: "“Peace!\"—a greeting from a Most Merciful Lord. [19]\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. This verse signifies the greatest blessing of Paradise: direct address from Allāh Himself. “Peace” (salām) here is not merely a greeting, but a divine declaration of safety, acceptance, and eternal security—freedom from fear, grief, and punishment. That it comes as a word underscores intimacy and honor, while its attribution to the Most Merciful emphasizes that this final assurance flows from compassion, not mere recompense. This divine greeting surpasses all physical pleasures of Paradise, for it confirms complete reconciliation, nearness, and everlasting contentment with the Lord.\nIn this light, it is reported that whenever Muhammad, the Messenger of God ﷺ, completed his prayer, he would not remain seated long except to say: “O Allāh, You are Peace, and from You is peace. Blessed are You, O Possessor of Majesty and Honor.” This supplication reflects the Qurʾānic truth that all peace originates with Allāh, and that the ultimate peace is found in His presence and approval.",
  36059: "Stand apart this Day, O guilty ones!",
  36060: "Did I not enjoin upon you, O children of Ādam, that you should not worship Satan? Indeed, he is to you a clear enemy.",
  36061: "And that you worship Me alone—this is the Straight Path.",
  36062: "He has already led vast masses of you astray [20]. Did you not then have any sense?\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. This verse highlights Satan’s rebellion and fall, and further indicates his success in persuading large numbers of people to follow him in that rebellion. While believers are not encouraged to fixate on Satan, there is great benefit in recognizing that, despite whatever influence he may still possess, his power is limited. He has no authority over the servants of God except to the extent that they allow him. Awareness of Satan’s reality also serves as a warning of his ability to deceive. Yet believers are not meant to be ignorant of his methods, for he is ever ready to mislead and entrap the unprepared and the undiscerning. Satan remains constant in his nature, but the believer’s protection lies in keeping their focus firmly on Allāh. Through reliance upon Him, victory is assured, and the likelihood of falling prey to Satan’s schemes is greatly diminished.",
  36063: "This is Jahannam [Hell] that you were warned about.",
  36064: "Enter it, [O deniers], and burn today as recompense for what you used to deny.",
  36065: "Today We will set a seal upon their mouths; their hands will speak to Us, and their legs will testify to what they used to commit.",
  36066: "Had We willed, We would surely have effaced their eyes; then they would race to the path—so how could they see?",
  36067: "And had We willed, We could have paralyzed them in their places, so they would be unable to move forward or turn back.",
  36068: "Whoever We grant a long life, We reverse him in creation—will they not understand? [21]\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. That is, when a person is granted a long lifespan, the natural course of human development is reversed: strength gives way to weakness, clarity to frailty, and independence to dependence. This gradual decline serves as a tangible sign of Allāh’s power over creation and a reminder of human vulnerability, intended to prompt reflection and understanding.",
  36069: "We have not taught him [Muhammad] poetry, nor is it fitting for him. It is only a Reminder and a clear Qur’an.",
  36070: "So that he may warn whoever is alive and that the Word be justified against the disbelievers.",
  36071: "Do they not see that We have created for them, from what Our hands [22] have wrought, livestock—so they are their owners?\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. The expression “Our Hands” signifies divine power and direct agency. It is also affirmed that Allāh possesses two Hands, as mentioned in the Qur’an and prophetic reports, without likeness to creation and without asking how (bilā kayf). Adam’s creation is uniquely described elsewhere as being fashioned “with My two Hands”, distinguishing his creation from that of other beings.",
  36072: "And We have made them subservient to them—from them they ride, and from them they eat.",
  36073: "And for them therein are benefits and drinks; will they not then be grateful?",
  36074: "Yet they have taken deities besides Allāh, hoping that they might be helped.",
  36075: "They are not able to help them, nor can those whom they worship besides Allāh; rather, all will be brought forth into the punishment, each disavowing the other.",
  36076: "So do not let their words grieve you. Indeed, We know what they conceal and what they reveal.",
  36077: "Does man not see that We created him from a mere drop of semen? Then behold—he becomes an open disputant. [23]\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. “An open disputant” describes one who argues defiantly and persistently. The verse highlights that a human—originating from a negligible substance—nonetheless challenges divine truth openly, often disputing resurrection and accountability.",
  36078: "He sets forth an example for Us and forgets his own creation, saying, \"Who will give life to the bones when they are decayed?\"",
  36079: "Say, “He will give life to them—the One Who brought them into existence the first time—and He is All-Knowing of every creation.”",
  36080: "The One who produces fire for you from the green tree—so you kindle from it.",
  36081: "Is not the One who created the heavens and the earth able to create the like of them? Yes indeed—He is the All-Creating, the All-Knowing.",
  36082: "His command, when He wills anything, is only to say to it, \"Be,\" and it is.",
  36083: "So glory be to Allāh, in Whose Hand is the dominion of all things, and to Him you will all be returned.",
  37001: "By those [angels] arrayed in ranks,",
  37002: "and by those [angels] who drive [the clouds] forcefully,",
  37003: "And those who recite Allāh’s words . [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. In these three verses, Allāh takes an oath by three categories of angels in order to emphasize the statement that follows.",
  37004: "Your God is indeed One,",
  37005: "Lord of the heavens and the earth and whatever is between them, and Lord of the sun’s risings.",
  37006: "Indeed, We have adorned the nearest sky with an adornment of stars,",
  37007: "And as a protection against every rebellious devil.",
  37008: "They are unable to eavesdrop on the exalted assembly [of angels], and they are pelted from every side,",
  37009: "as a repulsion; and for them is a perpetual punishment.",
  37010: "Except one who stealthily snatches [a word], then a piercing flame follows him.",
  37011: "So ask them, “Are they stronger in creation, or those whom We have created?”\n[2]. Indeed, We created them [3] from a sticky clay.\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. Those whom We have created: the heavens, the earth, angels, and other mighty creations mentioned earlier in the Sūrah.\n[3]. Sticky clay: clay that clings and holds together, emphasizing the humble and malleable origin of humanity.",
  37012: "Rather, you are astonished, while they mock,",
  37013: "and when they are reminded, they pay no heed.",
  37014: "and whenever they see a sign, they scoff,",
  37015: "and they say, \"This is nothing but clear magic.",
  37016: "When we are dead and become dust and bones, shall we indeed be resurrected?",
  37017: "and our earlier forefathers as well?",
  37018: "Say, “Yes—and you will be utterly humiliated.”",
  37019: "It is only a single shout [4], and at once they will be staring [in dread].\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. That is the second blowing of the Trumpet, causing all to rise from the dead for resurrection and Judgment. It is a decisive divine blast by which the dead are raised instantly, emphasizing the suddenness and irresistible power of Allāh’s command—without delay, repetition, or effort.",
  37020: "They will say, \"Woe to us, this is the Day of Recompense\".",
  37021: "“This is the Day of Judgment which you used to deny.”",
  37022: "[O angels], “Gather together those who associated others with Allāh, and their fellows [5], and whatever they used to worship,”\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. Azwājahum literally means “their pairs” or “their counterparts or fellows.” It is those of the same kind as them—namely, their companions, peers, or associates who shared their beliefs, actions, and path of wrongdoing. It does not refer to spouses specifically, but to those united with them in idolatry and disbelief, who will be gathered together for judgment.",
  37023: "Besides Allāh, so lead them all to the path of Hell.",
  37024: "Stop them, for they are to be questioned.\"",
  37025: "\"Why do you not help one another?”",
  37026: "No—today they have completely surrendered.",
  37027: "They will confront one another, hurling reproaches.",
  37028: "[The followers] will say, “You used to approach us from the right hand.” [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. From the power and authority to mislead us.",
  37029: "[The misleaders] will say, “Rather, you yourselves were not true believers.”",
  37030: "and we had no power over you; rather, you were a transgressing people.",
  37031: "Thus the Word of our Lord has come into effect against us. Indeed, we shall taste [the punishment].",
  37032: "So we led you astray, for we ourselves were astray.\"",
  37033: "Indeed, on that Day they will all share in the punishment.",
  37034: "Indeed, this is how We deal with the guilty.",
  37035: "Indeed, whenever it was said to them, “There is no god except Allāh,” they acted arrogantly.",
  37036: "And they say, “Shall we abandon our gods for a mad poet?”",
  37037: "Rather, he came with the Truth and affirmed the messengers.",
  37038: "Indeed, you who deny the truth will surely taste the painful punishment,",
  37039: "and you will not be recompensed except for what you used to do,",
  37040: "except the sincere servants of Allāh.",
  37041: "They will have a known provision.",
  37042: "fruits—and they will be honored.",
  37043: "In gardens of bliss,",
  37044: "Seated on thrones, facing one another. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. “Facing one another” (Arabic: mutaqābilīn) conveys harmony, equality, and mutual affection among the inhabitants of Paradise. Exegetes explain that they sit on raised couches in a way that none turns his back on another, reflecting the absence of resentment or rivalry. The image symbolizes complete fellowship and peace among them.",
  37045: "They will be served a cup drawn from a flowing spring. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. The Arabic word kaʾs literally means “cup,” but in classical Arabic it typically refers to a cup containing wine. The Qur’an describes the drink indirectly through the vessel rather than naming it outright, a stylistic feature that emphasizes the scene of cups being circulated among the people of Paradise. The phrase “from a flowing spring” (min maʿīn) highlights the purity and freshness of the drink. Exegetes understood this as a reference to the wine of Paradise, which the following verses describe as delightful and free from the harmful effects of earthly intoxicants.",
  37046: "white and delicious for those who drink.",
  37047: "In which there is no harm, nor will they be intoxicated by it.",
  37048: "and with them will be maidens restraining their gaze [9], with lovely eyes.\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. Restraining their gaze denotes chastity, bashfulness, and dignified modesty. It signifies that they look only toward their mates, expressing exclusivity, devotion, and purity of affection—rather than timidity or passivity,",
  37049: "as if they were pristine pearls.",
  37050: " Then they will turn to one another, mutually asking.",
  37051: "A speaker among them will say, \"Indeed, I had a companion [in the world],",
  37052: "[Mockingly] saying, \"Do you truly believe",
  37053: "That when we have died and become dust and bones, shall we indeed be brought to judgment?”",
  37054: "He will say [10], \"Would you like to take a look [at his fate]?\"\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. To his companions in Paradise.",
  37055: "So he looks down and sees him in the midst of the blazing Fire.",
  37056: "He will say, \"By Allāh, you almost ruined me;",
  37057: "Had it not been for the grace of my Lord, I would have been among those brought forth [to Hell].\"",
  37058: "“So we will never die?",
  37059: "Except for our first death, nor are we going to be punished?\"",
  37060: "Indeed, this is truly the great success.",
  37061: "For the like of this, let the workers work.",
  37062: "Is that a better accommodation, or the [hellish] tree of Zaqqūm?",
  37063: "Indeed, We have made it a trial for the wrongdoers.",
  37064: "It is indeed a tree that grows from the depths of Hell.",
  37065: "Its emerging fruit is as if it was heads of the devils. [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. Another meaning: \"Its emerging fruit are like the heads of Cobras serpents, 'extremely ugly disgusting to eat, harming others, harms one's own self'.\"\nThe simile does not require prior knowledge of what devils look like; it draws on instinctive horror imagery common in Arabic rhetoric—what is imagined as most repulsive and terrifying.",
  37066: "They will indeed eat from it, filling their bellies with it.",
  37067: "Then indeed, for them there will be, on top of it, a mixture of scalding water.",
  37068: "Then, their return will surely be to the Hell.",
  37069: "Indeed, they found their forefathers astray.",
  37070: "So they rushed to follow in their footsteps.",
  37071: "Indeed, most of the former peoples went astray before them.",
  37072: "And We had certainly sent warners among them.",
  37073: "Then observe how was the end of those who were warned,",
  37074: "except the sincere servants of Allāh.",
  37075: "Indeed, Noah called to Us—and We are the best of responders.",
  37076: "And We saved him and his household from the great anguish.",
  37077: "And We made his descendants the only survivors.",
  37078: "And We preserved for him an honorable mention among later generations.",
  37079: "Peace upon Noah among all peoples.",
  37080: "Thus do We reward the doers of good.",
  37081: "Indeed, he was one of Our believing servants.",
  37082: "Then We drowned the others.",
  37083: "Indeed, Abraham was among those who followed his path.",
  37084: "When he came to his Lord with a sound heart, [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. Sound heart refers to an inner state that is spiritually whole and uncorrupted—free from false belief, hypocrisy, and moral disease. In Qur’anic usage, the “heart” denotes the inner self, encompassing faith, intention, and moral orientation. It is a heart devoted wholly to Allāh, free from idolatry, arrogance, envy, and malice. One who possesses a sound heart is content with the divine decree, and Allāh is pleased with him. Such a person enjoys a tranquil and trusting spirit, marked by confidence, hope, and inner serenity.",
  37085: "when he said to his father and his people, \"What is it that you worship?\"",
  37086: "Is it false gods besides Allāh that you seek to worship?",
  37087: "So what do you think of Allāh, the Lord of the worlds?",
  37088: "Then he cast a glance toward the stars,",
  37089: "and said, \"Indeed, I am sick.\" [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. I am sick (Arabic: saqīm) literally denotes illness or weakness. Qur’anic commentators have offered differing explanations: some understand it as physical illness; others as inner distress or aversion toward his people’s idolatry; and others as a deliberate statement made to excuse himself from participation in their religious festival. The wording of the verse remains intentionally broad, allowing the expression to encompass physical, emotional, or situational meaning without specifying one to the exclusion of the others.\nIt is narrated that Abraham (peace be upon him) did not utter what is described as an untrue statement except on three occasions, two of which were for the sake of Allāh—when he said, “Indeed, I am sick,” and when he said, “Rather, the biggest of them did it,” referring to the chief idol. The third occurred when Abraham and his wife Sarah were traveling and passed through the land of a tyrant. When the ruler inquired about Sarah, Abraham said, “She is my sister,” meaning his sister in faith.\nThese statements are not regarded as outright falsehoods, but as ambiguous or indirect expressions. The description “he did not lie” is therefore understood metaphorically, emphasizing Abraham’s truthfulness and intention rather than literal speech.",
  37090: "So they turned away from him, retreating.",
  37091: "Then he turned to their gods [idols] and said [mockingly], \"Will you not eat? [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. Will you not eat of the offerings before you? is a rhetorical, mocking challenge directed at the idols. In the ancient Near Eastern milieu—and in the Qur’anic portrayal of Abraham’s dispute—idols were commonly presented with offerings (including food), and their inability to respond or benefit from such offerings exposes their impotence. Abraham’s question therefore functions as satire: by speaking to them as though they were living beings who could eat, he highlights the contradiction of worshiping objects that can neither act, hear, nor defend themselves.",
  37092: "What is wrong with you—why do you not speak?",
  37093: "Then he turned upon them, striking them with his right hand.",
  37094: "So they advanced toward him, rushing.",
  37095: "He said, \"Do you worship what you yourselves carve?",
  37096: "While it is Allāh who created you and what you make?\"",
  37097: "They said, \"Build for him a pyre, then cast him into the blazing fire.\"",
  37098: "So they devised a plot against him, but We made them the lowest.",
  37099: "And he said, 'I am going to my Lord [15];  He will guide me.\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. I am going to my Lord expresses Abraham’s resolve to depart from his people and homeland in obedience to Allāh. The phrase does not imply physical movement toward Allāh, but signifies migration (hijrah) undertaken for the sake of faith, turning away from idolatry and persecution toward a life ordered by divine guidance. The concluding statement, “He will guide me,” reflects Abraham’s reliance upon Allāh for direction and protection in an uncertain journey, entrusting his path and outcome entirely to Him.",
  37100: "Lord, grant me righteous offspring.\"",
  37101: "So We gave him the glad tidings of a forbearing son [Ishmael].",
  37102: "When he reached the age to work alongside him, Abraham said, \"O my son, I have seen in a dream that I should sacrifice you. Consider, then—what do you think?\" He replied, \"O my dear father, do as you are commanded. You will find me, if Allāh wills, among the patient.\"",
  37103: "So when they had both submitted, and he laid him down upon his brow,",
  37104: "We called out to him [saying], \"O Abraham,",
  37105: "You have already fulfilled the vision; thus indeed do We reward the doers of good.",
  37106: "Indeed, this is the manifest test.",
  37107: "And We ransomed him with a great sacrifice. [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. A great sacrifice refers to the divinely provided offering by which Abraham’s son was redeemed at the moment of complete submission. Prophetic tradition identifies this offering as a ram sent by Allāh as a substitute, underscoring the principle of divine ransom and mercy. The description “great” signifies not merely the physical sacrifice, but the magnitude of the trial, the sincerity of obedience demonstrated by both Abraham and his son Ishmael, and the enduring religious significance of this act, which is commemorated in the ritual sacrifice performed during the Pilgrimage in Makkah.",
  37108: "And We preserved for him an honorable mention among later generations.",
  37109: "Peace be upon Abraham.",
  37110: "Thus do We reward the doers of good.",
  37111: "Indeed, he was one of Our believing servants.",
  37112: "And [in time] We gave him the glad tidings of Isaac [17], a prophet from among the righteous.\\n\\nFootnotes: [17] The announcement of Isaac’s birth occurs after the account of the sacrifice, which indicates that the son involved in the sacrificial trial was the firstborn, Ishmael, and not Isaac. This sequencing is a key internal indicator within the Qur’anic narrative and stands in contrast to later Jewish and Christian traditions that identify Isaac as the intended sacrifice. Further support is found in Qur’an 11:71, where Sarah is informed of the forthcoming birth of Isaac, followed thereafter by the birth of his son Jacob. This confirms that Isaac’s birth—and thus his prophetic lineage—belongs to a later stage in Abraham’s life, subsequent to the trial described here.",
  37113: "And We blessed him and Isaac. But among their descendants are those who do good, and those who are openly unjust to themselves.",
  37114: "We certainly bestowed Our favor upon Moses and Aaron,",
  37115: "and We saved both of them and their people from the great distress [18],\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. The great distress (Arabic: al-karb al-ʿaẓīm) refers to the severe affliction endured by Moses, Aaron, and their people under Pharaoh’s tyranny. This includes prolonged oppression, forced labor, the systematic persecution of the Israelites, and the existential threat posed by Pharaoh’s pursuit, culminating in the events of the Exodus. The phrase underscores both the intensity and the comprehensive nature of the trial from which Allāh granted deliverance.",
  37116: "and We helped them, so they were victorious,",
  37117: "and We gave them both the clear Scripture,",
  37118: "and guided both of them to the straight Path [of Islam].",
  37119: "and We preserved for them both an honorable mention among later generations.",
  37120: "Peace be upon Moses and Aaron.",
  37121: "Indeed, thus do We reward the doers of good.",
  37122: "Indeed, they were both of Our believing servants.",
  37123: "Indeed, Ilyas [Elijah] was one of the messengers [of Allāh],",
  37124: "when he said to his people, \"Will you not fear [Allāh, and shun evil]?",
  37125: "Do you invoke Ba’al [19], and forsake the Best of creators,\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. Baʿal was a prominent Canaanite deity worshiped in the Levant, commonly associated with fertility, rain, and agricultural prosperity. The term baʿal in Semitic languages can also mean “lord” or “master,” which may have contributed to its misuse as an object of worship. Elijah’s rebuke confronts this cult directly, exposing the contradiction of invoking a fabricated deity while abandoning Allāh, described here as “the Best of creators,” a phrase that affirms Allāh’s absolute perfection and sole creative power in contrast to powerless idols.",
  37126: "Allāh, your Lord and Lord of your earliest forefathers?",
  37127: "But they rejected him, so they will certainly be brought [to the Hell].",
  37128: "Except the sincere servants of Allāh.",
  37129: "And We preserved for him an honorable mention among later generations.",
  37130: "Peace be upon Ilyas [Elijah].",
  37131: "Indeed, thus do We reward the doers of good.",
  37132: "Surely he was one of Our believing servants.",
  37133: "Indeed, Lot was one of the messengers [of Allāh].",
  37134: "when We saved him and his family all—",
  37135: "except an old woman [20] among those who lagged behind.\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. An old woman refers to the wife of Lot, who, despite her association with a prophet, did not share his faith or align herself with his mission. Qur’anic commentators explain that she sympathized with or supported the wrongdoing of her people and therefore remained among those destined for punishment. Her being described as “among those who lagged behind” emphasizes that salvation is determined by faith and conduct rather than family ties, age, or social position.",
  37136: "Then We destroyed the others.",
  37137: "Indeed, you pass by [their ruined town] in the morning [21],\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. Their ruined town refers to the remnants of the settlements of Lot’s people, traditionally identified with towns in the region of the Dead Sea. The verse addresses the Qur’an’s first audience by appealing to direct, observable evidence: these ruins lay along well-traveled routes and were passed by regularly, “in the morning and at night.” The reference underscores that the fate of earlier communities was not remote or legendary, but a visible historical reality meant to provoke reflection, moral awareness, and restraint. Then will you not reflect and fear that the same fate that struck them may strike you too?",
  37138: "and at night—will you not then reason?",
  37139: "Indeed, Yunus [Jonah] was one of the messenger [of Allāh],",
  37140: "when he fled to the laden ship,",
  37141: "then he drew lots with them and he lost—the lot fell upon him. [22]\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. Casting lots was a customary maritime practice in antiquity, employed to determine responsibility or selection in moments of crisis. The phrase “he lost” does not indicate moral failure or wrongdoing, but simply that the lot fell upon Jonah, marking him as the one to be cast overboard. Qur’anic commentators emphasize that this outcome occurred by divine decree, serving as the means through which Jonah would undergo trial, reflection, and eventual repentance, rather than as a punishment in the ordinary sense.",
  37142: "So the whale gobbled him, while he was blameworthy. [23]\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. \"Blameworthy\" denotes being in a state deserving reproach, not moral corruption or disbelief. Early Qur’anic commentators explain that Jonah was reproached for departing from his people before receiving explicit divine permission, rather than for any act of rebellion against Allāh. The term affirms prophetic accountability while preserving prophetic integrity, presenting Jonah's experience as a moment of trial and correction that led to repentance, growth, and renewed mission.",
  37143: "Had it not been that he was of those who glorify Allāh,",
  37144: "He would have certainly remained in its belly until the Day of Resurrection [24].\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. This statement is expressed in a counterfactual form, indicating what would have occurred had Jonah not been among those who continually glorified Allāh. It emphasizes that his deliverance was not due to circumstance, but to divine mercy responding to sincere remembrance and repentance. The verse is not meant to suggest a literal biological survival in the fish until the Day of Resurrection, but to underscore the gravity of the situation and the decisive role of worship in averting prolonged punishment.",
  37145: "But We cast him onto the open shore while he was ill. [25]\\n\\nFootnotes: [25]. \"while he was ill\" refers to Jonah's physical weakness and exhaustion following his ordeal in the belly of the fish. Qur’anic commentators describe him as being frail, emaciated, and in need of care, highlighting his human vulnerability after divine rescue. The verse marks a transition from trial to restoration, preparing for the subsequent mention of divine provision and healing.\nThe Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allāh be upon him) said: “Feed the hungry, visit the sick and free the captives.”\nHe also said, “There is no one who visits a (sick) person in the morning but seventy thousand angels send blessings upon him until evening comes, and if he visits him in the evening, seventy thousand angels send blessings upon him until morning comes.”",
  37146: "And caused to grow over him a tree of gourds. [26]\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. A tree of gourds (Arabic: yaqṭīn) denotes a rapidly growing, broad-leafed plant of the gourd family, such as squash or pumpkin. Such plants provide ample shade, gentle nourishment, and natural protection from insects, making them particularly suited to Jonah’s weakened condition following his ordeal. The verse thus illustrates a moment of tender divine care, marking the transition from hardship and correction to healing and restoration.",
  37147: "We sent him to a hundred thousand [of his people], or more.",
  37148: "So they believed, then We let them enjoy for a while.",
  37149: "So ask them, \"Does your Lord have daughters, while they have sons?\" [27]\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]. This verse addresses a belief held by some pagans who claimed that the angels were the daughters of God, while at the same time regarding sons as preferable for themselves. The Qur’an exposes the inconsistency and injustice of this claim: attributing to Allāh what they themselves considered undesirable, while reserving honor for their own lineage. The argument is rhetorical, highlighting the incoherence of such beliefs and reaffirming that Allāh is transcendent above lineage, gender, and human projections.",
  37150: "Or did We create the angels as females while they were witnesses?",
  37151: "Unquestionably, indeed they—out of their falsehood—surely say:",
  37152: "\"Allāh has begotten,\" but they are indeed liars.",
  37153: "Has He chosen daughters instead of sons?",
  37154: "What is the matter with you, how do you judge? [28]\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. This verse rebukes the claim that God has offspring—specifically the assertion that the angels are His daughters while sons are reserved for themselves. The expression is a forceful rhetorical challenge that combines astonishment with moral censure. “What is the matter with you?” questions their state of mind, while “how do you judge?” exposes the soundness of their reasoning and ethical discernment. Together, the phrasing highlights the irrationality and injustice of such beliefs, emphasizing that they stem not from knowledge or revelation, but from distorted assumptions and inherited falsehoods.",
  37155: "Will you not then take heed?",
  37156: "Or do you have a clear authority?",
  37157: "Then bring your scripture, if you are truthful.",
  37158: "They have claimed a lineage between Him and the jinn, yet the jinn themselves know that those who make such claims will surely be brought forth [for punishment].",
  37159: "Glory be to Allāh above what they describe.",
  37160: "Except the sincere servants of Allāh.[29]\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. Who do not share in that sin refers to the sincere servants of Allāh (Arabic: al-mukhlaṣīn), a term denoting those whose faith and worship are purified from shirk (associating partners with God), hypocrisy, and self-interest, and who are devoted to Allāh alone. The verbal form also carries the meaning of being chosen and purified by Allāh Himself, highlighting both their sincerity in devotion and the divine favor bestowed upon them. This exception clarifies that the false descriptions and claims mentioned in the preceding verses do not apply to those whose belief and worship are sound, sincere, and grounded in true knowledge of Allāh.",
  37161: "So you and whatever you worship—",
  37162: "none of you can seduce anyone away from Him,",
  37163: "Except those who are destined to [enter and] burn in the Hellfire.",
  37164: "[The angels say], there is none among us but has a known position,",
  37165: "Indeed, we are the ones who stand in ranks [for prayer],",
  37166: "and indeed, we are the ones who glorify Allāh.",
  37167: "And [the pagans] used to say,",
  37168: "\"If only we had a scripture from the earlier generations,",
  37169: "We would have been the sincere servants of Allāh.\"",
  37170: "Yet they disbelieved in it, so they will soon come to know. [30]\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. They will soon come to know refers to the consequences of their disbelief in the Qur’an and in the message of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. This Qur’anic expression denotes inevitable realization through consequence rather than mere intellectual awareness. Qur’anic commentators explain that it points to the moment when the truth they denied becomes unmistakably clear—whether at death, in the Hereafter, or upon witnessing divine punishment. The phrase serves as a solemn warning: delayed acknowledgment does not avert accountability, and certainty arrives when repentance is no longer possible.",
  37171: "Our Word has already preceded for Our servants, the messengers,",
  37172: "Indeed, they are the ones who will be helped.",
  37173: "and indeed, Our soldiers will surely be the victors.",
  37174: "So turn away from them for a while,",
  37175: "and watch them; soon they will see.",
  37176: "Do they seek to hasten Our punishment?",
  37177: "But when it [31] descends upon their land, wretched will be the morning of those who were warned.\\n\\nFootnotes: [31]. It refers to the divine punishment whose arrival they mockingly sought to hasten in the preceding verse. The pronoun recalls the threatened chastisement decreed by Allāh, whether understood as a specific historical punishment that befell earlier communities or, more broadly, as the inevitable consequence of persistent denial. Its sudden “descent” into their territory emphasizes that punishment comes by divine command, at a time and in a manner beyond human control or expectation.",
  37178: "So turn away from them for a while,",
  37179: "and watch them; soon they too will see.",
  37180: "“Glory be to your Lord [32], the Lord of Might, above what they describe.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [32]. Your Lord is addressed to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, affirming his special relationship with Allāh while also encompassing all believers who follow his message. The phrase “The Lord of might” denotes absolute power, honor, and invincibility—Allāh’s complete transcendence above weakness, dependence, or false attribution. The verse serves as a final declaration of divine exaltation, refuting every claim previously mentioned and affirming that Allāh is far above all descriptions fashioned by human conjecture or false belief.",
  37181: "Peace be upon the messengers.",
  37182: "And praise be to Allāh, Lord of all worlds.",
  38001: "Sād [1]. By the Qur’an, full of reminder, [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2: verse 1.\n[2]. The phrase dhī al-dhikr describes the Qur’an as a source of remembrance, admonition, and honor. It encompasses reminding humanity of divine truth, moral responsibility, and ultimate accountability, while also signifying the Qur’an’s noble status and enduring authority. The oath underscores the seriousness and certainty of what follows in the sūrah affirming that the matter is not as the pagans assume that Allāh has partners with Him.",
  38002: "Yet those who disbelieve are entrenched in pride and dissension.",
  38003: "How many a generation have We destroyed before them, so they cried out [to Us], when it was too late to escape.",
  38004: "They are astonished that a warner has come to them from among themselves. The disbelievers say, \"This is a sorcerer, a liar.\" [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. \"A warner\" that is Prophet Muhammad. “Sorcerer” (Arabic sāḥir) was a common accusation leveled against prophets, implying deception through illusion rather than divine revelation. “Liar” translates kadhdhāb, an intensive form indicating deliberate and habitual falsehood. The phrase reflects the Meccan leaders’ rejection of the Qur’anic message and their denial of the Prophet’s claim to divine authority.\nMuhammad ﷺ performed many miracles, for he was God’s final messenger to all humankind. Yet this did not convince the pagans, who declared that they would rather die than allow anyone to change their lives or the ancestral customs of idol worship they had inherited. Although the Prophet opened blind eyes and deaf ears to the Truth through the greatest miracle—the Qur’an, which God revealed to him—the eminent leaders among the deniers persisted in their devotion to idols, saying:",
  38005: "Has he made the gods into one God? Indeed, this is surely a strange thing.",
  38006: "The eminent ones among them went forth, saying, \"Go on, and remain steadfast to your gods. Indeed, this is something intended [against you].\" [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. “Something intended” implies a deliberate and calculated scheme. The Meccan leaders interpreted the Prophet’s call to monotheism as an intentional attempt to undermine their religion, social order, and inherited traditions, and therefore urged one another to remain steadfast in the worship of their gods.",
  38007: "We have not heard of this in the latter faith [5]. This is nothing but an invention.\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. “The latter faith” refers either to the most recent established monotheistic religion known to the Meccan disbelievers—commonly understood as Christianity—or to the later-established religious traditions of the pagan Arabs themselves. By this claim, they argued that the Prophet’s call to exclusive monotheism had no precedent even in earlier revealed scriptures.",
  38008: "Has the Reminder been sent down to him alone from among us?” Rather, they are in doubt about My Reminder; indeed, they have not yet tasted My punishment.",
  38009: "Or do they possess the treasuries of the mercy of your Lord, the All-Mighty, the Great Bestower?",
  38010: "Or do they have the dominion of the heavens and the earth and whatever is between them? Then let them ascend by any means. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. A rhetorical challenge implying that if the disbelievers truly possessed dominion, they could attempt to ascend the pathways of the heavens to control or block divine decree—preventing revelation from reaching the Prophet *Muhammad ﷺ. The statement underscores the impossibility of obstructing God’s will.",
  38011: "A host [7]—there—destined to be defeated among the confederates. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. “A host” translates the Arabic jund, used here in a deliberately minimizing sense. The verse is commonly understood as alluding to the defeat of the pagan forces of Mecca at Badr, demonstrating that even a seemingly powerful host is easily overcome by God’s decree.\n[8]. “The confederates” renders al-aḥzāb, referring broadly to the allied armies of earlier peoples who rejected their messengers and were ultimately defeated. The term situates the Meccan opposition within a recurring historical pattern of denial followed by divine retribution.",
  38012: "Long before them, the people of Noah rejected their messenger—so did ʿĀd, and Pharaoh, the possessor of the stakes. [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. “The possessor of the stakes” is interpreted as referring to Pharaoh’s military might, instruments of torture, or—more broadly—his firmly established and oppressive power.",
  38013: "So did Thamūd, the people of Lot, and the dwellers of the wooded thicket—those are the confederates. [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. “The dwellers of the wooded thicket” are commonly identified with the people of Midian associated with the prophet Shuʿayb. The phrase refers to a region characterized by dense vegetation and is used here to group together earlier communities who collectively opposed their messengers.",
  38014: "Each of them denied the messengers, and so My punishment came due.",
  38015: "And these await nothing but a single blast [of the Horn]; there will be no reprieve for it. [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. The “single blast” alludes to the blast of the Horn—i.e., the blowing of the Trumpet by the angel Isrāfīl—signaling the final judgment, which will occur without pause or reprieve.",
  38016: "They say [in mockery], “Our Lord, hurry for us our share [of punishment] before the Day of Reckoning.”",
  38017: "Be patient with what they say, and remember Our servant David, the man of great strength—indeed, he constantly turned back [to Allāh].",
  38018: "We subjected the mountains to him, glorifying [Allāh] in the evening and at sunrise.",
  38019: "And the birds as well, assembled together; all echoing praises with him. [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. The birds are depicted as joining David in glorification, reinforcing the motif of creation participating in worship. “Assembled together” emphasizes their readiness and obedience, while “echoing praises” conveys responsive, harmonious glorification.",
  38020: "We strengthened his kingdom and gave him wisdom and decisive speech.",
  38021: "Has there come to you [O Prophet] the tiding of the litigants, when they climbed over the wall of the chamber [of David]?",
  38022: "When they entered upon David, he was frightened of them. They said, “Fear not. [We are] two litigants; one of us has acted wrongfully toward the other. So judge between us with truth, do not exceed it, and guide us to the straight path.”",
  38023: "Indeed, this is my brother: he has ninety-nine ewes, while I have only one ewe; yet he said, “Entrust her to me,” and he overpowered me in speech.",
  38024: "David said, “He has certainly wronged you by demanding your ewe to add to his own ewes. Indeed, many partners act wrongfully toward one another, except those who believe and do righteous deeds—and few indeed are they.” Then David realized that We had tested him, so he sought his Lord’s forgiveness, fell down bowing, and turned to Him in repentance.",
  38025: "So We forgave him, and indeed, he has from Us nearness and an excellent place of return. [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“The most beloved prayer to Allāh is the prayer of David (Dāwūd), and the most beloved fasting to Allāh is the fasting of David.\nHe used to sleep half the night, stand in prayer for one third of it, and then sleep for one sixth of it.\nAnd he used to fast one day and break the fast the next day.”\nThe hadith praising the devotional practice of David in Islam illustrates a key ethical principle in Islamic spirituality: balanced and continuous worship. By describing David’s pattern of alternating fasting and structuring the night between rest and prayer, prophet Muhammad ﷺ encouraged believers to pursue devotion without neglecting physical well-being, thus promoting moderation, discipline, and perseverance in acts of worship.",
  38026: "“O David, We have made you a ruler on earth [14]; so judge between people with justice and do not follow desire, lest it lead you astray from Allāh’s way. Indeed, those who go astray from Allāh’s way will have a severe punishment for having forgotten the Day of Reckoning.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. “A ruler on earth” renders khalīfah fī al-arḍ, denoting one entrusted with authority and responsibility to uphold justice among people. In this context, it emphasizes moral stewardship and accountability before Allāh rather than hereditary succession or unrestricted political power. Historically, David’s rule is associated with Jerusalem, which he established as the capital of his kingdom.",
  38027: "We did not create the heavens and the earth and whatever is between them in vain. That is the assumption of those who disbelieve. So woe to those who disbelieve—from the Hellfire.",
  38028: "Or should We make those who believe and do righteous deeds like those who spread corruption on the earth? Or should We make the pious like the wicked?",
  38029: "This is a blessed Book We have sent down to you, so that they may reflect upon its verses, and that those endowed with understanding may take heed.",
  38030: "And to David We gave Solomon—an excellent servant. Truly, he was oft-returning [to Allāh].",
  38031: "When, one evening, well-trained racehorses, standing poised, were displayed before him.",
  38032: "He said, ‘I loved worldly things over remembering my Lord,’ until the sun slipped out of sight. [15]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. “The love of [worldly] things” renders ḥubb al-khayr, a Qur’anic expression for valued possessions or wealth—understood here as the horses. “The veil” (al-ḥijāb) refers to the horizon at sunset. Exegetes commonly explain that this preoccupation continued until the sun set, causing him to miss the afternoon (ʿAṣr) prayer, which in turn prompted his immediate self-reproach.",
  38033: "He said, ‘Bring them back to me,’ then he began 'gently' stroking their legs and necks.",
  38034: "We certainly tested Solomon and placed a body on his throne [16]; then he turned back to Allāh.\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. The phrase “a body” is left unspecified in the Qur’anic wording, and exegetes offer varied explanations. Some identify it as a lifeless form placed upon Solomon’s throne, understood as a sign of abasement or loss; others relate it to a child or offspring connected to a trial; and others to an impostor figure, often appearing in narratives in which a jinn or devil temporarily assumes Solomon’s place—accounts sometimes linked to the well-known “ring” reports. A further line of interpretation understands the “body” as Solomon’s own weakened state, such as illness or physical debility, cast upon the throne as a vivid image of human frailty.\nMany commentators note that the narrative details in these accounts derive from extra-Qur’anic material (including Isra’iliyyāt), and that the verse itself does not specify the nature of the “body,” emphasizing instead the reality of the trial and Solomon’s turning back [to Allāh].\nIn this connection, a Prophetic report relates that Solomon once intended that each of his wives would bear a son who would strive in Allāh’s cause, but he neglected to say “if Allāh wills,” and only one child was born, lifeless or deformed; Solomon then sought Allāh’s forgiveness.",
  38035: "He said, ‘My Lord, forgive me and grant me a kingdom that no one after me will possess. Indeed, You are the Bestower.’",
  38036: "So We subjected the wind to him, blowing gently at his command wherever he desired.",
  38037: "And the devils—every builder and diver,",
  38038: "and others fettered in chains.",
  38039: "“This is Our gift; so grant or withhold, without reckoning.”",
  38040: "Indeed, he has with Us nearness and an excellent place of return.",
  38041: "And remember Our servant Job, when he called to his Lord, “Indeed, Satan has touched me with exhaustion and suffering.”",
  38042: "“Strike [the ground] with your foot—here is [spring for a] cool bath and drink.”",
  38043: "We restored to him his family and the like of them with them—a mercy from Us and a reminder for those endowed with understanding.",
  38044: "“Take in your hand a bundle and strike with it [17], and do not break your oath. Truly, We found Job patient—an excellent servant. Surely, he was ever-returning to Us.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. This instruction addresses an oath Job had sworn, traditionally understood as a vow to strike his wife if Allāh restored him to health, following her reproach of him for persevering in his faith during prolonged suffering. He is commanded to fulfill the oath symbolically by striking with a bundle of grass, thereby avoiding harm while remaining truthful to his word. The episode illustrates how divine mercy resolves moral difficulty without negating ethical or legal commitment, and reflects the principle that Allāh does not intend hardship for His servants.",
  38045: "Remember Our servants—Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob—men of strength and insight.",
  38046: "We certainly chose them for an exclusive quality—the remembrance of the Home of the Hereafter. [18]\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. The phrase “an exclusive quality” indicates a pure and singular distinction granted by Allāh. “The remembrance of the Home of the Hereafter ” refers to constant awareness of the Hereafter, shaping intention and action. The verse thus portrays Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob as marked by an enduring orientation toward the ultimate abode rather than worldly attachment. Exegetes further explain that they not only remained mindful of the Hereafter themselves, but also called others to remember it, inviting people to obey Allāh and to perform righteous deeds in preparation for the life to come.",
  38047: "Indeed, they are truly among the chosen—the best in Our sight.",
  38048: "Remember also Ishmael, Elisha, and Dhū al-Kifl [Ezekiel]—each among the best.",
  38049: "This is a reminder. Indeed, for the righteous there is an excellent place of return:",
  38050: "Gardens of Eden, with gates wide open for them.",
  38051: "They will be reclining there in comfort, freely calling for plenty of fruit and drink.",
  38052: "With them will be companions of modest gaze, all of similar age.",
  38053: "This is what you have been promised for the Day of Reckoning.",
  38054: "This is truly Our provision—never ending.",
  38055: "So it is. And for the tyrants, there will certainly be an evil return.",
  38056: "Hell, where they will burn—what a dreadful place to rest.",
  38057: "This is what awaits them—so let them taste it: scalding water and putrid fluid.",
  38058: "and other similar punishments, in pairs.[19]\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. The phrase “in pairs” is understood here to denote multiple varieties or contrasting forms of punishment rather than simple numerical pairing. It refers to punishments of similar kind presented in differing types or extremes, emphasizing multiplicity and severity rather than symmetry alone.",
  38059: "They will say, ‘Here is a crowd rushing in with you—there is no welcome for them. They too will burn in the Fire.’",
  38060: "They will say, ‘No—you are not welcome. It was you who brought this upon us. What a miserable place to stay!’",
  38061: "They will say, ‘Our Lord, whoever caused this for us—give him double punishment in the Fire.’",
  38062: "And they will say, ‘Why do we not see men whom we used to regard among the evil ones?’",
  38063: "Did we ridicule them, or have our eyes failed to see them?",
  38064: "That is the truth—the mutual quarreling of the people of the Fire.",
  38065: "Say, ‘I am only a warner [20], and there is no god except Allāh —the One, the All-Subduing.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [20].  “A warner” renders nadhīr, emphasizing the prophetic role of proclamation and warning rather than compulsion. The statement defines the mission of Prophet Muhammad ﷺ as conveying divine truth and alerting people to accountability, while affirming that ultimate authority and judgment belong to Allāh alone.\nSuch warning is understood as an expression of care and mercy, intended to protect people from moral and spiritual harm. Accordingly, the Prophet ﷺ warned against associating partners with Allāh, violence, sexual immorality, injustice, harmful speech, theft, and ungodliness, while calling instead to righteousness, lawful means of living, and moral responsibility. He also warned of the danger posed by Satan, urging resistance through obedience and nearness to Allāh. Central to this warning is the principle that allegiance belongs to Allāh alone, and that humility before Him—rather than selfishness or arrogance—is the proper human response, for divine favor is associated with humility and submission.",
  38066: "The Lord of the heavens and the earth and everything between them—the Almighty, the All-Forgiving.",
  38067: "Say, \"This is momentous news.",
  38068: "from which you turn away.\"",
  38069: "I had no knowledge of the exalted assembly [of angels] when they were disputing.”",
  38070: "It has only been revealed to me that I am a clear warner.",
  38071: "When your Lord said to the angels, ‘I am about to create a human being from clay,’ [21]\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. “Clay” renders ṭīn, a term used in the Qur’an to describe the earthly material from which the first human was created. Elsewhere, human creation is described using related expressions such as dust (turāb), sticky clay (ṭīn lāzib), and molded clay (ṣalṣāl), which are generally understood as referring to different stages or aspects of the same created substance, emphasizing humanity’s humble origin and dependence upon Allāh.\nAllāh is our Lord; we are the clay, and He is our Creator—we are all the work of His hands.\nThe Prophet ﷺ said: “Allāh created Adam in His image, and his height was sixty cubits. When He created him, He said, ‘Go and greet that group of angels… and listen to how they greet you, for that will be your greeting and the greeting of your descendants.’ So he said, ‘Al-salāmu ʿalaykum (peace be upon you),’ and they replied, ‘Al-salāmu ʿalayka wa raḥmatullāh (peace be upon you and the mercy of Allāh),’ adding, ‘wa raḥmatullāh.’ Everyone who enters Paradise will be in the form of Adam, but mankind has continued to decrease in stature since then.”\nThis hadith has been discussed by scholars, who affirm it in a manner that befits Allāh’s majesty, without likening Him to His creation. His attributes are unique and incomparable, just as His essence is beyond comparison.",
  38072: "So when I have formed him and breathed into him of My spirit, then fall down in prostration before him.”",
  38073: "So the angels all prostrated together,",
  38074: "Except Iblīs—he exalted himself and turned ungrateful.",
  38075: "Allāh said, ‘O Iblīs, what prevented you from prostrating to the one I created with My own two hands? Are you proud or are you of the exalted ones’",
  38076: "He said, ‘I am better than him. You created me from fire, and You created him from clay.’",
  38077: "Allāh said, ‘Then get out of here—you are accursed.’",
  38078: "And My curse will be upon you until the Day of Judgment.”",
  38079: "He said, ‘My Lord, then grant me respite until the Day they are raised up.’",
  38080: "Allāh said, ‘Then you have been given respite,’",
  38081: "until the Day of the appointed time.”",
  38082: "Iblīs said, ‘By Your might, I will surely mislead them all,’",
  38083: "except Your sincere servants among them.”",
  38084: "Allāh said, ‘The truth it is—and the truth I speak—’",
  38085: "I will surely fill Hell with you and all those who follow you.”",
  38086: "Say, O Muhammad, ‘I ask of you no payment for this ˹message˺, nor am I of those who invent or pretend.’",
  38087: "It is only a Reminder for all people;",
  38088: "And you will surely come to know its news after a time.” [22]\\n\\nFootnotes: [22] “Its news” (nabaʾahu) denotes not just any report, but something momentous, weighty, and consequential. “After a while” (baʿda ḥīn) refers to an unspecified period, emphasizing certainty of occurrence rather than immediacy of timing.",
  39001: "This Book is sent down from Allāh —the Almighty, the All-Wise.",
  39002: "Indeed, We have sent down to you the Book in truth, so worship Allāh alone, devoting your religion sincerely to Him.",
  39003: "Unquestionably, sincere devotion belongs to Allāh alone. As for those who take protectors besides Him, saying, ‘We only worship them so that they may bring us nearer to Allāh —Allāh will surely judge between them regarding what they differ about. Indeed, Allāh does not guide one who is a liar [1] and ungrateful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. “Liar” translates كَاذِب (kādhib), indicating one who makes a false claim—such as attributing a son to God, or professing devotion to Him while directing worship elsewhere. “Ungrateful” translates كَفَّار (kaffār), an intensive form denoting persistent ingratitude, disbelief, or willful rejection of divine truth.",
  39004: "If Allāh had wanted to take a son [2], He could have chosen whatever He willed from His creation. Exalted is He above that.\nHe is Allāh —the One, the Supreme, the All-Dominant.\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. This conditional statement does not affirm sonship but negates it by emphasizing divine will and absolute sovereignty; it responds to claims attributing offspring to Allāh, whether a son or daughters.",
  39005: "He created the heavens and the earth with true purpose. He wraps the night over the day and wraps the day over the night.\nHe has subjected the sun and the moon—each moving on for a fixed term.\nTruly, He is the Almighty, the Ever-Forgiving.",
  39006: "He created you from a single being, then from it made its mate. And He provided for you eight pairs of livestock [3]. He creates you in your mothers’ wombs—stage after stage—within three layers of darkness [4]. That is Allāh, your Lord; to Him belongs all dominion.There is no god but Him—so how are you turned away?\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. The “eight pairs” refer to four types of livestock—sheep, goats, cattle, and camels—each consisting of male and female (cf. Qurʾān 6:143–144).\n[4]. “Three layers of darkness” is commonly understood as referring to the abdominal wall, the uterine wall, and the amniotic membranes surrounding the fetus.",
  39007: "If you disbelieve, then indeed Allāh has no need of you, and He does not approve ingratitude for His servants. But if you are grateful, He is pleased with it for you.\nNo bearer of burdens will bear the burden of another [5]. Then to your Lord is your final return, and He will inform you of what you used to do. Indeed, He is All-Knowing of what is within the chests.\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. This principle affirms individual moral responsibility: no person bears the sin or accountability of another. It excludes the concept of inherited or ancestral sin and emphasizes that forgiveness is attained through sincere repentance and seeking Allāh’s forgiveness, as exemplified by Adam and his mate after their disobedience.",
  39008: "When adversity touches man, he calls upon his Lord, turning to Him. Then when He grants him a favor from Himself, he forgets what he had been calling upon Him for before, and sets up equals to Allāh to mislead others from His path. Say, “Enjoy your disbelief for a little while; indeed, you are among the people of the Fire.”",
  39009: "Is one who is devoted in worship during the night, prostrating and standing, wary of the Hereafter and hoping for the mercy of his Lord, like one who is not? Say, “Are those who know and those who do not know alike?” Only people of understanding take heed.",
  39010: "Say [6], “O My servants who believe, be mindful of your Lord. For those who do good in this world, there is good. And Allāh’s earth is spacious. Indeed, only those who are patient will be given their reward in full, without measure.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. The command “Say” indicates that Allāh instructs the Prophet to convey these words directly on His behalf, emphasizing that the address and promise originate from Allāh, not from the Prophet himself.",
  39011: "Say, “I have been commanded to worship Allāh alone, devoting myself sincerely to Him.”",
  39012: "And I have been commanded to be the first of those who submit [to Allāh].”",
  39013: "Say, “Indeed, I fear—if I were to disobey my Lord—the punishment of a tremendous Day.”",
  39014: "Say, “It is Allāh alone whom I worship, devoting myself sincerely to Him.”",
  39015: "So worship whatever you please instead of Him. Say, “Indeed, the true losers are those who will lose themselves and their families on the Day of Judgment.”\nIndeed, that is the clear loss.",
  39016: "They will have above them canopies of Fire, and beneath them canopies. By that Allāh warns His servants. So fear Me, O My servants.”",
  39017: "But those who avoid false gods, lest they worship them, and turn in repentance to Allāh—for them are glad tidings. So give good news to My servants,",
  39018: "Those who listen to what is said and follow the best of it—those are the ones whom Allāh has guided, and those are the people of understanding. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. “Listen to what is said” refers broadly to attentively hearing words, teachings, and discourse presented to them, without prejudice or blind imitation. “Follow the best of it” indicates discerning acceptance of what is most true, upright, and beneficial, in accordance with divine guidance. Early exegetes explain this as listening with reflection and choosing what accords with revelation and sound reason. Those described are thus characterized by both receptivity and discernment, which is why they are called “the people of understanding”. \nThe Qur’an tells us to be doers of the word of Allāh and not hearers only. Obeying Allāh should be not just something we do, but something that flows from us. By His help, we can live out His Word in our world.\nBlessed are they that hear the Word of Allāh, the Qur’an, and keep it.",
  39019: "Is one against whom the word of punishment has come true—can you guide him? Can you save one destined for the Fire?",
  39020: "But those who are mindful of their Lord—for them are lofty chambers, above which are chambers built one above another, beneath which rivers flow. This is the promise of Allāh. Allāh does not fail in His promise.",
  39021: "Do you not see that Allāh sends down water from the sky and channels it through the earth as springs? Then He brings forth thereby crops of various colors; then they wither, and you see them turning yellow; then He turns them into dry debris. Indeed, in this is surely a reminder for those of sound understanding.",
  39022: "Is one whose heart Allāh has opened to Islam, so that he is upon a light from his Lord, like one who is not? Woe to those whose hearts are hardened against the remembrance of Allāh—those are in clear error. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. “Allāh has opened his heart to Islam” is an expression denoting inner expansion, receptivity, and readiness to accept faith and guidance. “A light from his Lord” refers to divinely granted clarity, insight, and guidance that direct belief and conduct. By contrast, “hearts hardened against the remembrance of Allāh” describes spiritual rigidity and resistance to admonition, resulting in estrangement from guidance. The verse thus contrasts inward illumination with inward hardness, identifying the latter as “manifest error.”\nScholars often note that persistent rejection of admonition leads to increased hardness of heart, reinforcing misguidance rather than repentance.",
  39023: "Allāh has sent down the best statement: a Book—this Qur’an—consistent, oft-repeated, causing the skins of those who fear their Lord to shiver; then their skins and their hearts soften at the remembrance of Allāh. That is Allāh’s guidance; He guides by it whom He wills.\nAnd whoever Allāh leaves astray—for him there is no guide. [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. Due to his unbelief and obstinacy. Divine guidance is granted to those who are receptive to truth, while misguidance follows persistent rejection. Thus, one whom Allāh leaves astray finds no guide apart from Him.",
  39024: "Is one who will shield himself with his face [10] from the terrible punishment on the Day of Resurrection like one who is secure? It will be said to the wrongdoers, “Taste what you used to earn.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. “Shield himself with his face” reflects a vivid Qur’anic image of utter helplessness and humiliation. Exegetes explain that on the Day of Resurrection the condemned will be unable to protect themselves with their hands, as one normally would, because their hands and feet will be bound and chained to their necks. Exposed in this state, they are forced to face the punishment directly. The expression thus emphasizes both the severity of the punishment and the complete absence of any means to avert it.",
  39025: "Those before them denied, so punishment came upon them from where they did not perceive.",
  39026: "So Allāh made them taste disgrace in this worldly life, and the punishment of the Hereafter is far greater—if only they knew.",
  39027: "Indeed, We have set forth for the people every kind of example in this Qur’an, so that they may take heed—",
  39028: "An Arabic Qur’an, without any crookedness, so that they may be mindful of Allāh.",
  39029: "Allāh sets forth an example: a slave owned by several quarrelsome partners, and a slave devoted wholly to one master [11]. Are they equal in comparison?\nAll praise be to Allāh. Yet most of them do not know.\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. This parable contrasts divided and exclusive allegiance. The slave owned by multiple quarrelsome partners represents one who serves competing authorities or objects of devotion, resulting in confusion and unrest, while the slave owned by a single master represents sincere, undivided devotion, exemplified by those who worship Allāh alone. The rhetorical question underscores that the two are not equal, illustrating the Qur’anic call to worship Allāh without partners.",
  39030: "You will certainly die, and they too will die;",
  39031: "then, on the Day of Judgment, before your Lord, you will dispute with one another.",
  39032: "Who, then, is more unjust than one who invents lies about Allāh [12] and denies the truth [13] when it comes to him? Is there not in Hell an abode for the disbelievers?\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. “Invents lies about Allāh” refers to attributing false statements, doctrines, or claims to Allāh—whether by inventing revelation, misrepresenting divine commands, or ascribing to Him what He has not affirmed, such as claiming that He has a son, daughters, or any partners. Such fabrication is among the gravest forms of injustice, as it distorts truth at its very source.\n[13]. “The truth” refers foremost to divine revelation and guidance sent by Allāh, including the Qur’an and the message it conveys—namely, that God has no partner, no parent, no child, no spouse, and no equal. Denying the truth “when it comes to him” emphasizes willful rejection after clarity and evidence have been made manifest, rather than ignorance or misunderstanding.",
  39033: "But the one who has brought the Truth [14] and those who believed in it—those are the truly pious.\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. “The one who has brought the Truth” is understood by many scholars to refer primarily to the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ, as the bearer and conveyer of divine revelation. Others note that the wording also allows for a broader sense, encompassing anyone who conveys the truth of revelation faithfully. “Those who believed in it” refers to those who accepted the message with sincerity and acted upon it. Together, the verse links the transmission of truth with belief in it, identifying both as the defining qualities of the truly pious.",
  39034: "They will have whatever they desire [15] from their Lord. That is the reward of the doers of good.\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. “Whatever they desire” signifies the completeness and generosity of divine reward, extending from the time of death through entry into Paradise—being saved from the torment of the grave and the severities of the Day of Resurrection and Judgment, and ultimately admitted to Paradise. This reward encompasses all forms of blessing—spiritual, moral, and physical—granted in the presence of their Lord. Exegetes note that this promise is not limited to specific requests but indicates fulfillment beyond expectation, in a manner befitting divine wisdom and mercy. The phrase “with their Lord” further emphasizes honor, nearness, and acceptance, rather than mere possession.",
  39035: "So that Allāh may remit from them the worst of what they did, and reward them according to the best of what they used to do.",
  39036: "Is Allāh not sufficient for His servant? Yet they seek to frighten you with those [they worship] besides Him. Whoever Allāh leaves astray—for him there is no guide.",
  39037: "And whoever Allāh guides—none can mislead him. Is not Allāh All-Mighty, Possessor of retribution?",
  39038: "If you ask them, “Who created the heavens and the earth?” they will surely say, “Allāh.” Say, “Then tell me: those whom you invoke besides Allāh—if Allāh intends harm for me, can they remove His harm? Or if He intends mercy for me, can they withhold His mercy?” Say, “Allāh is sufficient for me; upon Him the reliant place their trust.”",
  39039: "Say, “O my people, go on as you are; and so will I. Soon you will come to know—”",
  39040: "—to whom will come a punishment that will disgrace him [in this life], and upon whom will descend a lasting punishment [in the next life].\"",
  39041: "Indeed, We have sent down to you the Book [this Qur’an] for all mankind in truth. So whoever is guided—it is for his own benefit; and whoever goes astray—he goes astray only against himself. You are not a keeper over them.",
  39042: "Allāh takes the souls at the time of their death, and those that do not die during their sleep. Then He keeps those for whom He has decreed death, and sends the others back for an appointed term. Indeed, in this there are surely signs for a people who reflect.",
  39043: "Or have they taken intercessors besides Allāh? Say, “Even though they have no power over anything, nor do they understand?”",
  39044: "Say, “All intercession belongs to Allāh alone. To Him belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth; then to Him you will all be returned.”",
  39045: "When Allāh alone is mentioned, the hearts of those who do not believe in the Hereafter recoil; but when those [they worship] besides Him are mentioned, behold—they rejoice. [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. The phrase “those besides Him” in Qur’an refers to any beings or entities invoked, revered, or relied upon alongside Allāh, whether as intermediaries, intercessors, or objects of devotion. This include figures such as Jesus Christ, Mary, the holy spirit, angels, prophets, saints, jinn, or other created beings.\nThe verse underscores a key religious contrast: an aversion to the exclusive mention of Allāh alone versus a sense of comfort or satisfaction in mediated or shared forms of devotion. This distinction reflects a central Qur’anic critique—namely, that associating others with Allāh in acts of worship or reliance constitutes a departure from pure monotheism (tawḥīd), which the Qur’an consistently affirms as the foundation of true belief.",
  39046: "Say: O Allāh—Originator of the heavens and the earth, Knower of the unseen and the seen—you will judge between Your servants regarding what they used to differ about.",
  39047: "If those who associate others with Allāh possessed everything on earth—and the like of it along with it—they would surely offer it as ransom to save themselves from the severe punishment on the Day of Resurrection. And there will appear to them from Allāh what they never had expected.",
  39048: "And the evil consequences of what they earned will become clear to them, and what they used to mock will surround them.",
  39049: "When hardship touches a person, he calls upon Us. Then when We grant him a favor from Us, he says, ‘I was given it because of my knowledge.’ Rather, it is a test—but most of them do not know.",
  39050: "Those before them had already said the same, but what they had earned did not benefit them.",
  39051: "So the evil consequences of what they earned struck them. And those who do wrong among these—upon them too will fall the evil consequences of what they earn, and they will not escape.",
  39052: "Do they not know that Allāh expands provision for whom He wills and restricts it? Indeed, in that are signs for a people who believe.",
  39053: "Say, “O My servants who have sinned excessively, do not despair of Allah’s mercy. Indeed, Allāh forgives all sins—He alone is the All-Forgiving, the All-Merciful.” [17]\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. The command “Say” indicates that Allāh instructs the Prophet to convey these words directly on His behalf, emphasizing that the address and promise originate from Allāh, not from the Prophet himself.\n“Do not despair of Allāh’s mercy” affirms the vastness of divine forgiveness and addresses those who have committed excesses against themselves through sin. Scholars explain that this verse calls sinners to repentance and hope, assuring that no sin is too great to be forgiven when one turns back sincerely to Allāh. While the statement “Allāh forgives all sins” is comprehensive in scope, Qur’anic teaching understands it to be conditioned upon repentance and abandonment of wrongdoing, particularly with respect to persistent disbelief or associating partners with Allāh. The verse thus balances boundless mercy with moral responsibility, discouraging both despair and complacency.\nIn this connection, it is narrated that some individuals among the pagans—who had committed numerous murders and had excessively indulged in immorality—came to the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ and said: “What you call to is indeed good, but if you inform us that there is expiation for our past deeds, then we would accept it.” Thereupon this verse was revealed, affirming the possibility of forgiveness through sincere repentance.\nThe Prophet ﷺ reports also that Allāh Almighty says, \"O children of Adam! As long as you call upon Me, putting your hope in Me, I will forgive you for what you have done, and I will not mind. O children of Adam! If your sins were to reach the clouds of the sky and then you sought My forgiveness, I would forgive you. O children of Adam! If you were to come to Me with sins filling the whole world and then you came to Me without associating partners with Me, I would certainly match your sins with forgiveness.\"",
  39054: "Turn back to your Lord and submit to Him before the punishment comes upon you—then you will not be helped.",
  39055: "Follow the best of what has been sent down to you from your Lord [18], before the punishment comes upon you suddenly while you are unaware,\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. “The best of what has been sent down” refers to adhering fully and sincerely to divine revelation—namely, the Qur’an and the Prophetic Sunnah—and to its most upright commands, guidance, and ethical teachings. The expression encourages believers not merely to hear revelation, but to follow it in the best and most complete manner, prioritizing obedience, moral excellence, and consistency. The warning that punishment may come “suddenly” underscores the urgency of responding to guidance without delay, as heedlessness may persist until accountability arrives unexpectedly.",
  39056: "lest a soul should say [19], ‘Oh, my regret for what I neglected regarding Allāh, and for being among those who mocked [the Truth]’,\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. On the Day of Resurrection and Judgment.",
  39057: "or it will say, ‘If Allāh had guided me, I would have been among the righteous’,",
  39058: "or it will say, when it sees the punishment, ‘If only I had another chance, I would be among those who do good.’",
  39059: "No indeed. My signs had already come to you, but you denied them—you were arrogant and were among the ungrateful.",
  39060: "On the Day of Resurrection, you will see those who lied against Allāh [20] —their faces darkened. Is Hell not a fitting dwelling for the arrogant?\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. “Those who lied against Allāh” refers to individuals who falsely attribute statements, beliefs, or claims to Allāh—such as inventing revelation, denying His messages, or ascribing to Him partners, offspring, or qualities He has not affirmed. Lying against Allāh is among the gravest forms of wrongdoing, as it corrupts truth at its source and leads others astray. The darkening of faces symbolizes humiliation, exposure, and moral disgrace on the Day of Judgment, in contrast to the honor granted to the truthful and humble.",
  39061: "But Allāh will save those who were mindful of Him by their success—no harm will touch them, nor will they grieve.",
  39062: "Allāh is the Creator of everything, and He is in charge of everything.",
  39063: "To Him belong the keys of the heavens and the earth. Those who disbelieve in Allah’s signs—it is they who are the losers.",
  39064: "Say, ‘Is it other than Allāh that you order me to worship, O ignorant ones?’",
  39065: "It has already been revealed to you and to those before you that if you associate others with Allāh, your deeds will surely be rendered worthless, and you will surely be among the losers.",
  39066: "Rather, worship Allāh alone, and be among the grateful.",
  39067: "They did not appraise Allāh with due appraisal. On the Day of Resurrection, the whole earth will be in His grasp, and the heavens will be rolled up in His right hand [21]. Glorified and exalted is He, far above what they associate with Him.\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. The expression “they did not appraise Allāh with His due appraisal” conveys a failure to recognize Allāh’s true majesty and worth, as they worshiped alongside Him weak and powerless created beings. The phrases “in His grasp” and “in His right hand” are translated literally, in accordance with the wording of the Qurʾān, without attempting metaphorical reinterpretation. The verse affirms Allāh’s absolute sovereignty and transcendence, concluding with a declaration of His exaltedness above all associations made with Him.\nIt is narrated that a Jewish scholar came to God’s Messenger and said: “O Muḥammad, we learn that Allāh will place the heavens upon one finger, the earths upon one finger, the trees upon one finger, the water and the dust upon one finger, and all other created beings upon one finger. Then He will say: ‘I am the King; where are the tyrants, and where are the arrogant ones today?’” The Prophet ﷺ smiled until his premolar teeth became visible, affirming the scholar’s statement, and then recited: “They did not appraise Allāh with His due appraisal, and on the Day of Resurrection the whole earth will be in His grasp.”",
  39068: "The Trumpet will be blown, and all who are in the heavens and all who are on the earth will swoon, except whom Allāh wills. Then it will be blown again, and behold, they will be standing, looking on.",
  39069: "The earth will shine with the light of its Lord, and the record will be laid open; the prophets and the witnesses will be brought forth, and judgment will be passed between them in truth, and they will not be wronged.",
  39070: "Every soul will be repaid in full for what it has done, and He knows best what they do.",
  39071: "Those who disbelieved will be driven to Hell in groups until, when they come to it, its gates will be opened, and its keepers will say to them, ‘Did there not come to you messengers from among yourselves, reciting to you the revelations of your Lord and warning you of the meeting of this Day of yours?’ They will say, ‘Yes.’ But the word of punishment has come true against the disbelievers.",
  39072: "It will be said, ‘Enter the gates of Hell, to remain there forever.’ How wretched is the dwelling of the arrogant.",
  39073: "But those who were mindful of their Lord will be led to Paradise in groups, until, when they come to it and its gates are wide open, its keepers will say to them, ‘Peace be upon you. You have done well, so enter it, abiding forever.’",
  39074: "They will say, ‘All praise be to Allāh, who has fulfilled His promise to us and made us inherit the land so we may settle in Paradise wherever we will. How excellent is the reward of those who strive [in goodness].’",
  39075: "You will see the angels surrounding the Throne, glorifying their Lord with praise. Judgment will be passed in truth, and it will be said, ‘All praise be to Allāh, Lord of the worlds.’ [22]\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. The declaration “All praise be to Allāh, Lord of the worlds” (al-ḥamdu lillāhi rabbi l-ʿālamīn) concludes the scene of final judgment, affirming that ultimate praise belongs to Allāh alone after justice has been fully established among people. \nThe verse mirrors the opening formula of the Qurʾān (1:2), forming a rhetorical closure that underscores divine sovereignty, perfect justice, and the fulfillment of Allāh’s decree.",
  40001: "Ḥāʾ Mīm. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2: verse 1.",
  40002: "This Book is sent down by Allāh—the Almighty, the All-Knowing.",
  40003: "The Forgiver of sin, the Accepter of repentance, severe in punishment, and abounding in bounty. There is no god but Him; to Him is the final return.",
  40004: "No one disputes Allah’s signs except the disbelievers, so do not let their movement throughout the land deceive you.",
  40005: "Before them, the people of Noah denied, as did the factions after them. Every nation plotted against its messenger to seize him, and argued with falsehood to suppress the truth. So I seized them—how severe was My punishment!",
  40006: "Thus has the Word of your Lord come true against those who disbelieved: that they are the companions of the Fire.",
  40007: "Those [angels] who bear the Throne and those around it glorify their Lord with praise, believe in Him, and seek forgiveness for the believers, saying: “Our Lord, You encompass all things in mercy and knowledge, so forgive those who repent and follow Your path, and protect them from the punishment of the Fire.”",
  40008: "Our Lord, admit them into the Gardens of Eden that You have promised them, along with the righteous among their parents, their spouses, and their children. Truly, You are the Almighty, the All-Wise.",
  40009: "And protect them from the evil consequences. For whoever You protect from the evil consequences on that Day—You have shown him mercy. And that is the supreme success.",
  40010: "Indeed, those who disbelieve will be called out, “Allah’s hatred of you—when you were called to faith but rejected it—was greater than your hatred of yourselves.”",
  40011: "They will say, ‘Our Lord, You caused us to die twice [2] and gave us life twice [3]. So we have confessed our sins—is there any way out?’\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. This is commonly understood to refer to non-existence before creation as the first state of lifelessness, and death after worldly life as the second. Other interpretations understand the two deaths as worldly death and a subsequent death after resurrection, or as death in the grave (barzakh) prior to final resurrection. The verse itself does not specify the sequence in detail, emphasizing instead Allāh’s absolute control over life and death.\n[3]. The two lives are commonly understood as worldly life and life after resurrection on the Day of Judgment. Some commentators view the pairing of life and death as describing a complete cycle—non-existence → life → death → resurrection—highlighting accountability and the reality of the Hereafter rather than outlining a precise successive sequence.",
  40012: "This is because when Allāh alone was called upon, you rejected faith; but when partners [4] were associated with Him, you believed. So judgment belongs entirely to Allāh —the Most High, the Most Great.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. That is, any beings or entities invoked, revered, obeyed, or relied upon alongside Allāh. This includes, among others, Jesus Christ, his mother Mary, the holy spirit, angels, prophets, saints, demons, idols, or any created beings to whom divine qualities, intercessory power independent of Allāh, or acts of worship are attributed. The Qur’an consistently condemns such associations (shirk) as a denial of exclusive devotion to Allāh alone.",
  40013: "He is the One who shows you His signs and sends down provision from the sky for you. Yet only those who turn back to Him take heed.",
  40014: "So call upon Allāh alone, devoting your worship sincerely to Him—even though the disbelievers dislike it.",
  40015: "He is exalted in rank, Lord of the Throne. He sends down revelation by His command to whoever He wills among His servants, so that they may warn of the Day of Meeting.",
  40016: "The Day they are exposed—nothing about them is hidden from Allāh. To whom belongs the dominion today [5]? To Allāh —the One, the All-Subduing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. Sovereignty (al-mulk) denotes absolute dominion, authority, and ownership—power that is unrestricted, unshared, and final. On the Day of Judgment, all apparent worldly authority, kingship, and control are stripped away, revealing that true sovereignty has always belonged to Allāh alone. The rhetorical question and immediate answer emphasize the collapse of all false claims to power and the exclusive reality of divine rule.\nThe Prophet ﷺ said that all creation will perish on that Day, and nothing will remain except Allāh—the Ever-Living, the Eternal. Then Allāh will proclaim, “To whom belongs all authority today? Where are the kings of the earth?” When no one responds, He will answer Himself: “It belongs to Allāh—the One, the Supreme.”\nThis report vividly illustrates that all claims to power, kingship, and sovereignty collapse on the Day of Judgment, leaving only the absolute and exclusive dominion of Allāh.",
  40017: "On that Day, every soul will be repaid for what it has earned. There will be no injustice that Day. Indeed, Allāh is swift in reckoning.",
  40018: "Warn them of the imminent Day, when hearts will be at the throats, choked with anguish. The wrongdoers will have no close friend, nor any intercessor who will be heeded.",
  40019: "Allāh knows even the most subtle glances of the eyes and whatever the hearts conceal.",
  40020: "Allāh judges with truth, while those they call upon besides Him cannot judge at all. Indeed, Allāh alone is the All-Hearing, the All-Seeing.",
  40021: "Have they not traveled through the land and seen how those before them met their end? They were greater than them in strength and left stronger traces in the land, yet Allāh seized them for their sins, and they had no one to protect them from Allāh.",
  40022: "This was because their messengers came to them with clear proofs, but they disbelieved, so Allāh seized them. Indeed, He is All-Strong, severe in punishment.",
  40023: "Indeed, We sent Moses with Our signs and clear authority.",
  40024: "to Pharaoh, Haman, and Korah. But they said, “He is a sorcerer and a liar.”",
  40025: "When he brought them the truth from Us, they said, “Kill the sons of those who believe with him, and spare their women.” But the scheming of the disbelievers leads only to ruin.",
  40026: "Pharaoh said, “Let me kill Moses—and let him call upon his Lord! I fear he will change your religion or spread corruption throughout the land.”",
  40027: "Moses said, “I seek refuge in Allāh —my Lord and your Lord—from every arrogant person who does not believe in the Day of Reckoning.”",
  40028: "A believing man from Pharaoh’s family [6], who was concealing his faith, said, “Would you kill a man simply for saying, ‘My Lord is Allah,’ when he has brought you clear proofs from your Lord? If he is lying, his lie is upon him; but if he is truthful, some of what he warns you of will befall you. Indeed, Allāh does not guide one who is a transgressor and a habitual liar.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. Many exegetes identify this believing man as Ḥizqīl (also rendered Hizqil), described as a close relative of Pharaoh—often said to be his cousin—and his treasurer. He is portrayed as a member of Pharaoh’s inner court who concealed his faith out of prudence until this moment of open counsel.\nThe Qur’an does not name him explicitly, directing attention instead to his courage, reasoned argument, and moral clarity under tyranny. His anonymity in the Qur’anic text underscores that faithful witness and truth-telling, rather than status or lineage, are what grant lasting significance.",
  40029: "He said, “O my people, yours is the dominion today, prevailing in the land; but who will help us against Allah’s punishment if it comes upon us?” Pharaoh said, “I show you only what I see, and I guide you only to the path of right conduct.”",
  40030: "And the believing man said, “O my people, I truly fear for you a day like the Day of the confederates.” [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7].  “The Day of the confederates” refers collectively to the days on which earlier nations—confederated in opposition to their messengers—were destroyed after persisting in disbelief. The expression does not denote a single historical day, but rather a recurring pattern of divine judgment against powerful groups united in falsehood. The believer’s warning thus invokes historical precedent, cautioning that present dominance offers no protection from Allāh’s punishment.",
  40031: "Like the fate of the people of Noah, ʿĀd, Thamūd, and those after them. Allāh does not intend injustice for His servants.",
  40032: "O my people, I truly fear for you the Day of calling out to one another.",
  40033: "The Day you will turn away, fleeing—there will be no protector for you against Allāh. And whoever Allāh leaves astray, for him there is no guide.",
  40034: "Joseph had already come to you before with clear proofs, yet you continued to doubt what he brought you. Then, when he died, you said, “Allāh will never send a messenger after him.” Thus Allāh leaves astray whoever is a transgressor and given to doubt.",
  40035: "Those who dispute Allah’s signs without any authority given to them—how greatly detestable it is in the sight of Allāh and of those who believe. Thus Allāh seals the heart of every arrogant tyrant.",
  40036: "Pharaoh said, “O Haman, build for me a lofty tower, so that I may reach the ways—”",
  40037: "the ways of the heavens, so that I may look upon the God of Moses—though indeed, I think he is a liar.” Thus the evil of his deed was made appealing to Pharaoh, and he was turned away from the path. And Pharaoh’s scheme led only to ruin.",
  40038: "And the believing man said, “O my people, follow me; I will guide you to the path of right conduct.” [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. This statement deliberately echoes Pharaoh’s claim in verse 40:29, “I do not guide you except to the path of right conduct.” The contrast is intentional: Pharaoh asserts guidance based on personal authority and subjective judgment, whereas the believing man offers guidance grounded in submission to Allāh and objective moral truth. His invitation—“follow me”—is not a call to personal allegiance, but to the path he himself follows: God’s true religion (Islam), with which Moses was sent. This path is marked by humility, reasoned counsel, and accountability before Allāh. The verse thus exposes the difference between coercive leadership that claims infallibility and faithful leadership that invites reflection, conscience, and choice.",
  40039: "O my people, this worldly life is nothing but a temporary enjoyment, but the Hereafter is the eternal home.",
  40040: "Whoever does an evil deed [9] will not be recompensed except with its like. But whoever does righteousness—whether male or female—while being a believer, those will enter Paradise, where they will be provided for without measure.\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. This verse establishes a fundamental principle of divine justice and mercy. Evil is repaid only with its equivalent, never with excess or injustice, affirming that Allāh does not wrong His servants. By contrast, righteous deeds—when joined with true faith—are rewarded with provision “without measure” indicating generosity beyond calculation, not limited by strict measure or proportionality. The contrast highlights that while punishment is governed by exact justice, reward flows from overflowing grace and mercy.",
  40041: "O my people, why is it that I call you to salvation, while you call me to the Fire?",
  40042: "You call me to disbelieve in Allāh and to associate with Him what I have no knowledge of, while I call you to the Almighty, the All-Forgiving.",
  40043: "There is no doubt that what you call me to has no response in this world or in the Hereafter. And that our return is to Allāh, and that the transgressors are the people of the Fire.",
  40044: "You will soon remember what I am telling you. I entrust my affair to Allāh. Indeed, Allāh is All-Seeing of His servants.",
  40045: "So Allāh protected him from the evils of what they plotted, and the worst punishment engulfed the people of Pharaoh.",
  40046: "The Fire—they are exposed to it morning and evening. And on the Day the Hour is established, it will be said, “Admit the people of Pharaoh into the most severe punishment.” [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. This verse is a key Qur’anic text cited by scholars as evidence for punishment in the intermediate state (al-barzakh), prior to the Day of Resurrection. The exposure to the Fire “morning and evening” occurs before the Hour is established, indicating ongoing chastisement in the grave. By contrast, the command “Admit Pharaoh’s people into the most severe punishment” refers to the final punishment after resurrection and judgment. The verse thus clearly distinguishes between pre-Resurrection punishment and the ultimate punishment of the Hereafter, demonstrating continuity and escalation rather than a single moment of reckoning.",
  40047: "And when they argue with one another in the Fire, the weak will say to those who were arrogant, “Indeed, we were your followers—so can you relieve us of any share of the Fire?”",
  40048: "Those who were arrogant will say, “Indeed, we are all in it. Allāh has already judged between His servants.”",
  40049: "Those in the Fire will cry out to the keepers of Hell: ‘Call upon your Lord to ease the punishment for us—even if only for a day.’",
  40050: "The keepers will say, “Did your messengers not come to you with clear proofs?” They will reply, “Yes.” They will say, “Then call upon your Lord. But the supplication of the disbelievers is nothing but in vain.”",
  40051: "Indeed, We will surely support Our messengers and those who believe in this worldly life and on the Day when the witnesses stand.",
  40052: "On the Day when the excuses of the wrongdoers will not benefit them: for them is a curse, and theirs is the evil home.",
  40053: "We certainly gave Moses guidance and made the Children of Israel inherit the Scripture,",
  40054: "as guidance and a reminder for people of sound understanding.",
  40055: "So be patient. Indeed, the promise of Allāh is true. Seek forgiveness for your sin and glorify your Lord with praise in the evening and at dawn. [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. Patience (ṣabr), prayer (ṣalāh), seeking forgiveness (istighfār), and the remembrance and glorification of Allāh (dhikr and tasbīḥ) are repeatedly presented in the Qur’an as primary means of spiritual resilience and moral steadfastness. Through patience, believers endure trials without despair; through prayer, they remain connected to divine guidance; through seeking forgiveness, they are continually purified and renewed; and through glorifying Allāh, they maintain awareness of His presence and sovereignty. Together, these practices cultivate inner strength, clarity of purpose, and perseverance upon the path of truth, especially in times of hardship and testing.",
  40056: "Indeed, those who argue about Allāh’s signs without any authority given to them harbor nothing in their chests but a craving for greatness that they will never attain. So seek refuge in Allāh. Truly, He alone is the All-Hearing, the All-Seeing.",
  40057: "Indeed, the creation of the heavens and the earth is far greater than that of mankind; yet most people do not know.",
  40058: "The blind and the seeing are not equal, nor are those who believe and do righteous deeds equal to those who do evil. Yet little do you take heed.",
  40059: "Indeed, the Hour is surely coming—there is no doubt about it. Yet most people do not believe.",
  40060: "Your Lord says, “Call upon Me, and I will answer you [12]. Indeed, those who disdain My worship [13] will enter Hell, abased.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. “Call upon Me; and I will answer you” is a divine invitation to supplication (duʿāʾ), affirming the nearness, responsiveness, and mercy of Allāh toward His servants. It assures that no sincere call goes unheard—whether the response comes in the form of what is asked, something better, or protection from harm. This promise encourages reliance upon Him alone, fostering hope, humility, and trust in His wisdom. It emphasizes that turning to Allāh in every need, great or small, is not only permitted but beloved to Him.\nThis assurance calls for certainty of faith in supplication—that one turns to Allāh with conviction, trusting in His promise and believing that He responds in the best and most fitting way. In this sense, the believer approaches prayer with a heart anchored in trust, not doubt.\nA similar expression is found in earlier scripture, as in the Book of Jeremiah (33:3): “Call to Me and I will answer you, and will tell you great and hidden things that you have not known,” highlighting a shared theme of divine responsiveness and the unveiling of what lies beyond human perception.\n[13]. That is, those who do not invoke Allāh alone and who reject His Oneness (tawḥīd)—refusing to worship Him sincerely—are those who will enter the Hellfire. It refers to individuals who turn away in arrogance, unwilling to humble themselves before Allāh through worship and supplication.\nHere, supplication is closely linked to worship itself, indicating that neglecting to call upon Him may arise from pride, self-sufficiency, or denial of dependence upon the Creator. Such disdain is not mere omission, but a deeper rejection of one’s need for Allāh and a refusal to submit to Him.\nThe consequence—entering Hell in a state of humiliation—reflects a fitting reversal: those who exalted themselves and refused humility in this life will be brought low in the Hereafter, bearing the outcome of their arrogance and rejection.",
  40061: "It is Allāh Who made the night for you to rest therein and the day bright. Indeed, Allāh is full of bounty to the people [14], but most people do not give thanks. [15]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. That is, Allāh’s bounty encompasses His countless favors bestowed upon humanity, including the alternation of night and day, rest and activity, and the means of life and sustenance. These blessings are granted universally—to believers and disbelievers alike—as part of His mercy and generosity. They reflect Allāh’s wise ordering of creation for human benefit and well-being, calling people to recognize His favor and respond with faith and righteousness.\nProsperity and well-being are tied not merely to material ease, but to upright conduct—righteousness, godliness, faith, patience, love, and moral integrity—through which divine favor is truly realized.\n[15]. That is, most people fail to show gratitude to Allāh for His favors—whether by acknowledging Him alone, praising Him, or obeying Him. Ingratitude includes both denial of the Giver and neglect of obedience, despite the clarity and constancy of His blessings.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ exemplified gratitude through action. He would stand in prayer for long hours at night until his feet became swollen. When his Companions remarked that Allāh had forgiven his past and future shortcomings, he replied:\n“Should I not be a grateful servant?”\nThis shows that gratitude is not confined to the heart alone, but must be manifested in belief, speech, prayer, conduct, and action. Although the Prophet ﷺ was not \ncommanded to worship to this degree, his devotion was a complete expression of thankfulness to Allāh.\n He also said: “Whoever does not thank people has not truly thanked Allāh.”\nThus, true gratitude is comprehensive—acknowledging Allāh inwardly and outwardly, and translating thankfulness into righteous deeds.",
  40062: "Such is Allāh, your Lord—the Creator of all things. There is no true god but Him. So how then are you deluded? [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. That is, Allāh alone is your Lord and the Creator of all things, without partner or equal. Since everything besides Him is created, nothing else deserves worship. The rhetorical question “How are you deluded?” expresses astonishment and rebuke: how can people be turned away from the truth of pure monotheism (tawḥīd) after such clear and manifest signs? This delusion arises from following false beliefs, inherited traditions, or desires in place of divine guidance.",
  40063: "This is how those who used to deny Allāh’s signs become deluded.",
  40064: "It is Allāh who made the earth a resting place for you and the sky a canopy. He formed you and perfected your forms, and provided you with good and wholesome things. That is Allāh, your Lord—so blessed and exalted is Allāh, Lord of the worlds.",
  40065: "He is the Ever-Living—there is no true god but Him. So call upon Him alone, devoting your worship sincerely to Him [17]. All praise be to Allāh, Lord of all creation.\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. Ikhlāṣ (sincerity) means devoting all acts of worship—outward and inward—exclusively to Allāh alone, free from associating partners with Him, seeking the praise of others, or dividing one’s devotion. The phrase “devoting your worship sincerely to Him” signifies that faith, supplication, obedience, love, fear, and hope are directed solely to Allāh. True sincerity requires purity of intention and constancy in action, so that worship is performed for Allāh alone, not for worldly gain, reputation, or self-display. The Qur’an makes it plain that there is only ONE God, in  Arabic and Hebrew Allāh, Eli, Elahom. \"I am Allāh. That is my name; I give my glory to no one else, Nor my praise to carved images.\" He said:  \"I am Allah! There is no one worthy of worship but I, so worship Me alone, and establish the prayer for My Remembrance.\" \nAllāh said to Moses: “This is what you are to say to the Israelite’s, ‘Allāh the God of your forefathers, the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob, has sent me to you.’  There is no other god besides Allāh, A righteous God and a Savior; There is none except Him.",
  40066: "Say, “I have been forbidden to worship those you call upon besides Allāh, after clear proofs have come to me from my Lord. I have been commanded to submit myself fully to Allāh, Lord of all creation.”",
  40067: "It is He who created you from dust, then from a semen-drop, then from a clinging clot; then He brings you forth as infants, then allows you to reach full strength, then to grow old—though some of you are taken earlier—so that you may reach a fixed term, and so that you may come to understand. [18]\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. This verse outlines the successive stages of human creation and life through which Allāh manifests His power, wisdom, and decree: origin from dust, development in the womb, birth, growth to maturity, aging, and death. Not all reach every stage, for each soul has an appointed term known only to Allāh. Recalling these stages is meant to prompt reflection and understanding—namely, recognition of Allāh’s lordship over life and death, the transience of human existence, His Oneness and absolute power, and the certainty of resurrection and return to Him—so that people may become true believers.",
  40068: "It is He who gives life and causes death. When He decrees a matter, He only says, “Be!”—and it is.",
  40069: "Have you not seen those who dispute concerning the signs of Allāh—how they are turned away!",
  40070: "Those who deny the Book and the message We sent with Our messengers—they will soon come to know. [19]\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. “The Book” refers primarily to the Qur’an and, by extension, to all revealed scripture. “That with which We sent Our messengers” includes the divine messages, commands, prohibitions, proofs, and warnings entrusted to every messenger. Central to this message is the call to worship none but Allāh alone with sincere devotion, to reject all false deities, and to affirm the realities of resurrection after death, Paradise, and Hell. Thus, denial of this Book entails denial of the entire chain of revelation and the fundamental truths conveyed by all messengers.",
  40071: "When iron collars are placed around their necks and chains bind them,\nthey will be dragged—",
  40072: "through scalding water; then in the Fire they will be burned.",
  40073: "Then it will be said to them, “Where are those you used to associate as partners—",
  40074: "“Besides Allāh?” They will say, “They have vanished from us. Rather, we were not invoking anything before.” Thus Allāh leaves the disbelievers astray.",
  40075: "This is because you used to rejoice on the earth without right and behave arrogantly.",
  40076: "Enter the gates of Jahannam [Hell], abiding therein forever. How evil is the abode of the arrogant.",
  40077: "So be patient. Surely the promise of Allāh is true. Whether We show you some of what We threaten them with, or cause you to die—to Us they will all return.",
  40078: "We have indeed sent messengers before you. Among them are those whose stories We have related to you, and among them are those whose stories We have not related to you. It was not for any messenger to bring a sign except by Allāh’s permission. When the command of Allāh comes, the matter will be judged in truth, and there the followers of falsehood will be utterly ruined.",
  40079: "It is Allāh who created livestock for you—some to ride, and some to eat.",
  40080: "And in them are other benefits for you, and that you may attain through them a need within your chests [20]. And upon them and upon ships you are carried.\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. That is, through livestock Allāh enables people to fulfill deeply felt needs and aims held within their hearts—such as travel, transport of goods, livelihood, trade, migration, and the pursuit of provision and safety. The phrase “a need within your chests” points to necessities and purposes that may not always be spoken aloud but are known inwardly to each person. By mentioning both livestock and ships, the verse highlights Allāh’s provision of means for movement and benefit across land and sea.",
  40081: "He shows you His signs—so which of Allāh’s signs will you deny?",
  40082: "Have they not traveled through the land and seen what was the end of those before them? They were more numerous than them and stronger in power and in the traces they left upon the earth, yet all that they used to earn was of no avail to them.",
  40083: "When their messengers came to them with clear proofs, they took pride in the knowledge they possessed, and they were encompassed by the very thing they used to mock.",
  40084: "But when they saw Our punishment,\nthey said, “We believe in Allāh alone, and reject those we used to associate with Him.”",
  40085: "But their faith was of no benefit to them once they saw Our punishment. This is Allāh’s established way with His servants—and there the disbelievers will be utterly ruined.",
  41001: "Ḥāʾ Mīm. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2: verse 1.",
  41002: "This is a revelation from the Most Compassionate, the Most Merciful,",
  41003: "a Book whose verses are clearly explained—a perfect Qur’an in Arabic—\nfor people who seek knowledge,",
  41004: "as a bringer of good news and a warner;\nyet most of them turn away, so they do not listen.",
  41005: "They say, “Our hearts are closed to what you are calling us to. There is deafness in our ears, and between us and you is a barrier. So go on with what you are doing—we will do the same.”",
  41006: "Say, “I am only a human being like you.\nIt is revealed to me that your God is one God. So be upright toward Him and seek His forgiveness. Woe to those who associate others with Him—”",
  41007: "Those who do not give the purifying alms [2], and who are disbelievers in the Hereafter.\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. Zakāh literally means purification and growth. It is the obligatory alms prescribed by Allāh upon eligible wealth, by which both the wealth and the giver are purified—from greed, miserliness, and moral corruption. Zakāh is not a voluntary charity, but a religious obligation and a fundamental pillar of Islam. Its neglect reflects not merely social failure, but a deeper spiritual and doctrinal deficiency, especially denial of accountability in the Hereafter. Thus, refusal to give zakāh is linked in the Qur’an to disbelief in resurrection and judgment.",
  41008: "Indeed, those who believe and do righteous deeds—for them is a reward that will never be cut off.",
  41009: "Say, “Do you really disbelieve in the One who created the earth in two days [3] and do you set up equals with Him? That is the Lord of all creation.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. That is, on Sunday and Monday, according to early exegetical reports. The expression highlights order and divine decree in creation. It is fully consistent with other verses describing the creation of the heavens and the earth in six days, which refer to the total creative process across multiple stages, of which the creation of the earth in two days is one part.",
  41010: "He placed upon it firm mountains rising above it, and He blessed it, and measured within it its sustenance in four days—equal for those who ask. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. That is, the four days include the first two days already mentioned. The remaining two days are understood—according to early exegetical reports—to be Tuesday and Wednesday. Thus, the verse describes the completion and provisioning of the earth within four days in total, not an additional four beyond the initial two.",
  41011: "Then He turned to the heaven while it was still smoke and said to it and to the earth, “Come into being, willingly or unwillingly.” They said, “We come willingly.”",
  41012: "So He completed them as seven heavens in two Days [5], and inspired in each heaven its affair. We adorned the lower heaven with lanterns and as a protection. This is the Decree of the All-Mighty, the All-Knowing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. That is, during the later stages of creation—traditionally understood as the fifth and sixth days (Thursday and Friday)—Allāh completed the formation of the heavens, including the stars and celestial bodies. The creation of living beings followed, culminating in the creation of Adam, peace be upon him, which occurred in the last hour of Friday, as reported in authentic narrations. This sequence reflects the ordered completion of creation, with humanity created last and placed within a prepared and structured cosmos.\nHumans were created in God’s image.The “image” that God referred to is obviously not the physical attributes of God.\nHumans, different from the “unreasoning animals” were created with the ability to reason and to have spiritual attributes. They had qualities such as love, a sense of justice and wisdom. They had the ability to understand why they existed and their Creator’s purpose toward them. So they, unlike the animals, were given the capacity for spirituality. This is the “image” that humans were created in. However, human beings may degrade themselves morally if they abandon truth and guidance, acting instead on impulse and desire—thus falling below the standard for which they were created.",
  41013: "But if they turn away, then say,\n“I have warned you of a thunderbolt\nlike the thunderbolt of ʿĀd and Thamūd.”",
  41014: "When the messengers came to them\nfrom before them and from behind them,\n[saying], “Worship none but Allāh,” they said, “If our Lord had willed, He would surely have sent down angels. We therefore reject the message you have been sent with.”",
  41015: "As for [the people of] ‘Aad, they were arrogant in the land without right, and they said, \"Who is mightier than us in strength?\" Did they not see that Allāh—who created them—is mightier than them in strength?\nYet they used to reject Our signs.",
  41016: "So We sent upon them a furious wind\nin days of evil omen [for them], that We might make them taste the punishment of disgrace in the life of this world; but surely the punishment of the Hereafter is more disgracing, and they will not be helped.",
  41017: "As for Thamūd, We guided them,\nbut they preferred blindness over guidance.\nSo the thunderbolt of the punishment of humiliation seized them for what they used to earn [of sins].",
  41018: "And We saved those who believed\nand were mindful of God [Allāh].",
  41019: "And [consider] the Day when the enemies of Allāh will be gathered to the Fire, and driven forward in ordered ranks.",
  41020: "Until, when they come to it, their hearing, their sight, and their skins will testify against them concerning what they used to do.",
  41021: "They will say to their skins, “Why have you testified against us?” They will say, “Allāh has caused us to speak—He who causes everything to speak. And He is the One who created you the first time, and to Him you are returned.”",
  41022: "And you did not seek to conceal yourselves lest your hearing, your sight, and your skins testify against you; rather, you assumed that Allāh did not know much\nof what you were doing.",
  41023: "That assumption of yours which you assumed about your Lord has brought you to ruin; so you became among the losers.",
  41024: "Even if they endure it patiently, the Fire will still be their home; and if they ask for pardon, it will not be granted to them.",
  41025: "We assigned for them companions [6], who made fair-seeming to them what was before them and what was behind them; and the sentence was justified against them among nations of jinn and humankind that had passed on before them. Indeed, they were losers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. That is, evil companions—from among devils of jinn and humans—whom Allāh allowed to accompany them because of their own inclinations and choices. These companions include shayāṭīn, corrupt peers, and misleading influences who encourage disbelief and sin. They made wrongdoing appear fair and acceptable, beautifying both past actions and future consequences, until falsehood felt normal and truth burdensome. Their assignment was not coercion but a consequence of persistent rejection, in accordance with the divine principle that those who turn away from guidance are left to what they choose.",
  41026: "Those who disbelieve say, “Do not listen to this Qur’an, and make noise during it [7], so that you may prevail.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. The word al-ghaw denotes clamor, loud noise, and deliberate disruption. It refers to shouting, heckling, whistling, or speaking over the Qur’an while it is being recited, with the aim of preventing attentive listening and understanding. This tactic was used intentionally by the disbelievers, not out of confusion but out of fear that the Qur’an’s message might persuade if heard calmly. Thus, al-ghaw represents active obstruction of truth, substituting noise for argument when refutation was impossible.",
  41027: "So We will surely make those who disbelieve taste a severe punishment,\nand We will surely recompense them\naccording to the worst of what they used to do.",
  41028: "That is the recompense of the enemies of Allāh: the Fire. For them therein is the home of eternity—a recompense for what they used to deny of Our signs.",
  41029: "And those who disbelieved will say,\n“Our Lord, show us those who led us astray\nfrom among the jinn and humankind,\nso that we may place them under our feet, that they may be among the lowest.” [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. This plea reflects regret, anger, and misplaced blame in the Hereafter. The disbelievers seek to identify those—from among jinn and humankind—who actively misled them: devils, false leaders, corrupt influencers, and companions who promoted disbelief and sin. Their desire to place them “under our feet” signifies extreme humiliation and vengeance, wishing them to be among the lowest ranks of Hell. Yet this appeal does not absolve them of responsibility, for the Qur’an repeatedly affirms that each soul chose its own path and bears its own burden. The scene exposes the collapse of false loyalties and the futility of blame once judgment has come.",
  41030: "Indeed, those who say, “Our Lord is Allāh,” then remain steadfast—the angels descend upon them, [saying], “Do not fear, nor grieve; but rejoice in the good news of Paradise which you have been promised.”",
  41031: "We are your supporters in this worldly life and in the Hereafter. There you will have everything your souls desire, and whatever you ask for, it will be yours.",
  41032: "A gracious welcome from Allāh,\nthe All-Forgiving, the Most Merciful.",
  41033: "Who is better in speech than one who calls to Allāh, does righteous deeds, and says, “Indeed, I am one of the Muslims”?",
  41034: "Good and evil are not the same.\nRepel evil with what is best [9], and then one who was once your enemy may become a close and devoted friend. [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. Repelling evil with what is best means responding with faith-driven excellence in character and action. True faith is manifested through humility, patience, forgiveness, and generosity, including helping the poor, the sick, and the needy without pride or expectation of return. The Qur’an and the Sunnah teach believers how to live, speak, spend, and respond in all circumstances—how to show kindness to neighbors, maintain ties of kinship, and meet the needs of others sincerely. Such conduct is the outward expression of inward faith, for faith must be accompanied by righteous action. Thus, believers are commanded to restrain anger, forgive harm, and respond to mistreatment with goodness. Do not repay evil with evil, but always pursue what is good, both among yourselves and toward all people.”\n[10]. Do not repay evil with evil or insult with insult. Instead, respond with prayer and blessing, for this is the calling through which one attains true blessing.\nPrayer plays a central role in transforming hostility into closeness. Through prayer, believers entrust their emotions, grievances, and relationships to Allāh, seeking His guidance and mercy for themselves and others. By submitting one’s will and feelings to Allāh, the heart is softened and purified, making forgiveness and reconciliation possible. For this reason, the command to repel evil with good is closely connected to prayer—especially praying for those who cause harm. When one prays for an adversary, resentment gives way to compassion, and enmity can be transformed into sincere friendship by Allāh’s grace.",
  41035: "But none attains it except those who are patient, and none attains it except one endowed with a great share [of good].",
  41036: "If a whisper from Satan provokes you, then seek refuge in Allāh. Indeed, He is the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing. [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. The term nazgh refers to a sudden satanic provocation or incitement—a subtle stirring of anger, resentment, pride, or impulse toward wrongdoing. It is not a sustained temptation but a momentary jab or irritation intended to derail moral restraint, especially when one is striving to repel evil with good. The Qur’an therefore prescribes an immediate remedy: seeking refuge in Allāh, which cuts off the provocation at its source. By turning to Allāh—who is All-Hearing of the plea and All-Knowing of the inner struggle—the believer is protected from acting on impulse and is restored to clarity and composure.",
  41037: "Among Allāh’s signs are the night and the day, the sun and the moon. Do not bow down to the sun or the moon, but bow down to Allāh who created them, if you truly worship Him alone.",
  41038: "But if they turn away in arrogance, then those with your Lord glorify Him night and day and never tire.",
  41039: "And among His signs is that you see the earth lifeless; then, when We send down water upon it, it stirs and swells—indeed, the One who gives it life is surely the Giver of life to the dead. Indeed, He has power over all things.",
  41040: "Indeed, those who twist the meaning of Our verses are not hidden from Us. Is one who will be cast into the Fire better, or one who comes secure on the Day of Resurrection? Do whatever you will—indeed, He is All-Seeing of what you do.",
  41041: "Those who reject the Reminder [12] when it comes to them are surely lost—and indeed, it is a Mighty Book:\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. The term al-Dhikr (“the Reminder”) is one of the Qur’an’s own names for itself, signifying that it calls humanity to remember Allāh, the truth of revelation, moral accountability, and the Hereafter. It is both an admonition and a source of guidance, reviving hearts that heed it. The description kitābun ʿazīz (“a Mighty Book”) denotes that the Qur’an is honored, invincible, and unassailable—protected by Allāh from being overcome, corrupted, or nullified. Its authority cannot be diminished by denial, distortion, or opposition; rather, rejection only results in loss for the rejecters, while the Qur’an itself remains exalted and preserved.",
  41042: "Falsehood cannot approach it, neither from before it nor from behind it. It is a revelation from One All-Wise, All-Praiseworthy.",
  41043: "Nothing is said to you [O  Prophet] except what was said to the messengers before you [13]. Your Lord is truly the Lord of forgiveness and the Lord of painful punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. This verse consoles the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ by placing his experience within the established pattern of prophethood. Every messenger before him faced denial, mockery, false accusations, and persecution from their people, despite bringing clear proofs and sincere counsel. Such opposition is therefore not a sign of falsehood or failure, but a recurring response to divine truth that confronts entrenched beliefs, arrogance, and worldly interests. For this reason, knowledge of the history of the prophets strengthens believers during times of slander and persecution in the cause of Allāh, reminding them that endurance upon truth is part of the prophetic path. The verse further affirms that the ultimate outcome rests with Allāh alone, who combines vast forgiveness for those who repent with painful punishment for those who persist in obstinate rejection, thereby upholding perfect justice.",
  41044: "Had We made it a Qur’an in a foreign language, they would have said, “Why are its verses not explained in detail? A foreign scripture and an Arab messenger?”\nSay, “For those who believe, it is guidance and healing. [14]” And those who do not believe—in their ears is deafness, and it is blindness for them. Those are being called from a far-off place. [15]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. Describing the Qur’an as “guidance and healing” highlights its dual function for those who believe. It provides guidance by distinguishing truth from falsehood and directing belief, conduct, and purpose toward what pleases Allāh. It is also a healing—not primarily of physical ailments, but of the heart and soul—curing doubt, ignorance, despair, arrogance, and moral corruption.\nThis healing manifests only in receptive hearts. The Qur’an benefits those who approach it with faith, humility, and sincerity, while those who reject it deprive themselves of its remedy. True healing also requires effort: it involves recognizing one’s need for Allāh, remaining open to His guidance, and learning to live with what cannot be changed—finding peace in submission and in the knowledge that one remains a beloved servant of God.\n[15]. The expression “those are being called from a far-off place” is a metaphor for extreme spiritual distance and disconnection. Although the message reaches them clearly, their hearts are so unreceptive that responding becomes as difficult as hearing a call from afar.\nThe failure lies not in the message, but in their deliberate turning away. Their rejection creates a barrier that prevents them from benefiting from guidance, even when it is plainly presented.",
  41045: "We certainly gave Moses the Scripture, but it was disputed. And had it not been for a word that had already gone forth from your Lord, judgment would surely have been decided between them. And indeed, they are in troubling doubt about it.",
  41046: "Whoever does righteousness—it is for his own benefit; and whoever does evil—it is against himself. Your Lord is never unjust to His servants.",
  41047: "To Him alone belongs the knowledge of the Hour. No fruits emerge from their sheaths, nor does any female conceive or give birth, except with His knowledge.\nAnd on the Day He will call out to them,\n“Where are My partners?” they will say,\n“We declare to You: none of us is a witness.”",
  41048: "Whatever they used to invoke before will vanish from them, and they will know for certain that there is no escape for them.",
  41049: "Man does not tire of supplicating for good, but when hardship touches him, he becomes utterly despairing and despondent. [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. This verse describes a common human weakness, particularly evident in the denier and the spiritually heedless. People persistently supplicate for what they perceive as good—ease, wealth, health, and success—yet when hardship or loss touches them, they fall into despair and despondency, losing hope in Allāh’s mercy and wisdom. The paired terms emphasize total inner collapse: despair of relief and hopelessness about the future. This reaction reflects weak faith and poor understanding of divine decree, for true belief produces steadiness—gratitude in ease and patience in adversity—while despair is condemned in the Qur’an as incompatible with trust in Allāh.",
  41050: "But if We let him taste mercy from Us\nafter hardship that touched him, he will surely say, “This is mine. I do not think the Hour will ever come; and even if I am returned to my Lord, surely I will have the best with Him.” But We will surely inform those who disbelieve of what they did, and We will surely make them taste a harsh punishment.",
  41051: "When We bestow favor upon man, he turns away and draws aside; but when evil touches him, he becomes persistent in supplication.",
  41052: "Say, “Have you considered: if it is from Allāh and you then disbelieve in it—who is more astray than one in deep and distant opposition?”",
  41053: "We will show them Our signs in the horizons and within themselves, until it becomes clear to them that it is the truth [17]. Is it not enough that your Lord is a Witness over all things?\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. “Allāh’s signs in the horizons” refer to the external evidences of His truth and power—visible in the universe, nature, history, and the unfolding order of creation—while “within themselves” points to the internal signs found in human conscience, intellect, moral awareness, and lived experience. Together, these outward and inward signs continually confirm the truth of revelation, until denial becomes untenable. The verse indicates a progressive clarification over time: as knowledge, reflection, and events accumulate, the truth of the Qur’an becomes manifest to those willing to reflect. Ultimately, beyond all created signs, Allāh Himself is the supreme Witness, whose testimony encompasses and surpasses every proof.",
  41054: "Indeed, they are in doubt about the meeting with their Lord. Indeed, He encompasses all things.",
  42001: "Ḥāʾ Mīm.",
  42002: "Ayyn Sīn Qāf. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2: verse 1.",
  42003: "Thus does He reveal to you, and to those before you—Allāh, the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.",
  42004: "To Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth.\nAnd He is the Most High, the Most Great.",
  42005: "The heavens are almost torn apart\nfrom above them. The angels glorify their Lord with praise and seek forgiveness for those on the earth. Indeed, Allāh is the All-Forgiving, the Most Merciful. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The description of the heavens “nearly tearing apart” expresses the overwhelming majesty and awe of Allāh, not a physical rupture. It portrays creation’s profound responsiveness to divine greatness, especially in contrast to human disbelief, arrogance, and idolatry on earth. Similar imagery appears elsewhere in the Qur’an to emphasize how even the vast heavens react with reverence to what violates divine oneness or challenges God’s majesty (cf. 19:90–91). The verse thus magnifies Allāh’s transcendence while exposing the gravity of denial—yet it is immediately followed by mention of divine mercy, as the angels seek forgiveness for those on earth.",
  42006: "As for those who take protectors besides Him [3]—Allāh is a Watcher over them [4], and you are not responsible for them.\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. “Protectors” (awliyāʾ) refers to those taken as objects of reliance, allegiance, or devotion besides Allāh—such as idols, saints, leaders, powers, or systems—whose protection, authority, or intercession is sought independently of Him. Taking such protectors compromises exclusive reliance and worship (tawḥīd), even if they are claimed only as intermediaries.\n[4]. “Allāh is a Watcher over them” translates ḥafīẓ, meaning that Allāh is their Guardian, Overseer, and Preserver in the sense of complete awareness, authority, and accountability—not approval or protection from consequence. Though they may seek guardians besides Him, they remain fully within Allāh’s knowledge, control, and judgment.",
  42007: "Thus We have revealed to you an Arabic Qur’an, so that you may warn the Mother Town [5] and all around it [6], and warn of the Day of Gathering—about which there is no doubt. One group will be in Paradise, and another in the blazing Fire.\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. “The Mother Town” (Umm al-Qurā) is an honorary title for Makkah, reflecting its supreme religious status as the site of Allāh’s first House of worship established on earth (the Kaʿbah) and the focal point of monotheistic devotion. It is called the “Mother” because of its foundational role in faith, guidance, and religious history, from which the message spread outward. Some scholars also note its central position among human settlements, reinforcing its symbolic role as the spiritual heart of the world.\n[6]. That is, all other people beyond Makkah. The phrase “those around it” initially refers to the surrounding regions of Arabia, but by extension encompasses all of humanity, indicating that although the Qur’an was revealed in Arabic and first addressed to Makkah, its message and warning are universal, not confined to a single place or people.",
  42008: "Had Allāh willed, He could have made them a single community; but He admits whom He wills into His mercy. And the wrongdoers [7]—there is for them neither protector nor helper.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. “The wrongdoers” refers to those who commit injustice against themselves by rejecting faith, associating partners with Allāh, and persisting in disbelief and idolatry. Such wrongdoing deprives them of divine mercy, leaving them without any protector or helper on the Day of Judgment.",
  42009: "Or have they taken protectors besides Him? But Allāh—He alone is the Protector. He gives life to the dead, and He has power over all things.",
  42010: "Whatever you may disagree about, its judgment belongs to Allāh. Such is Allāh—my Lord. In Him alone I place my trust, and to Him I continually return.",
  42011: "He is the Originator of the heavens and the earth. He has made for you pairs from among yourselves, and from the cattle—pairs—by which He multiplies you.\nThere is nothing like Him, and He is the All-Hearing, the All-Seeing.",
  42012: "To Him belong the keys of the heavens and the earth. He expands provision for whom He wills, and He restricts it. Indeed, He is All-Knowing of all things.",
  42013: "He has ordained for you the same faith which He enjoined upon Noah, and that which We have revealed to you, [O Muhammad], and what We enjoined upon Abraham, Moses, and Jesus [8], saying, “Establish the religion and do not be divided therein.” Hard upon the pagans is that to which you call them. Allāh chooses for Himself whom He wills, and guides to Himself whoever turns to Him.\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. This verse names the five greatest prophets of firm resolve (ulū al-ʿazm): Noah, Abraham, Moses, Jesus, and Muhammad—peace and blessings of Allāh be upon them all. Their mention together highlights the unity of divine religion throughout history. Each was commanded to establish pure monotheism (Islam in its universal sense) and to call their people to worship Allāh alone, though the specific laws and regulations given to them differed according to time and circumstance. The core essence of faith—belief in One God, obedience to Him, and moral accountability before Him—remains constant and unchanged across all prophetic missions.",
  42014: "They did not become divided except after knowledge had come to them out of mutual envy among themselves. Had not a Word preceded from your Lord till an appointed term, the matter would certainly have been settled between them. And indeed, those who inherited the Scripture [9] after them are in disquieting doubt about it.\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. That is, the Jews and the Christians.",
  42015: "So to that call, and remain steadfast as you have been commanded. Do not follow their desires, but say, “I believe in whatever Scripture Allāh has sent down, and I am commanded to act justly between you. Allāh is our Lord and your Lord. For us are our deeds, and for you are your deeds. There is no dispute between us and you. Allāh will gather us all, and to Him is the final return.”",
  42016: "Those who argue about Allāh after His call has been accepted—their argument holds no weight before their Lord. Upon them is wrath, and they will suffer a severe punishment. [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. “After acceptance” means after the call to Allāh has been clearly conveyed and responded to, when the truth has become manifest and people have already believed. At this point, continued argument is no longer sincere inquiry but obstinate opposition, and such objections carry no standing before Allāh.",
  42017: "It is Allāh who sent down the Book with truth and the balance. What will make you know? Perhaps the Hour is near.",
  42018: "Those who do not believe in it seek to hasten it, while those who believe are apprehensive of it and know that it is the truth. Indeed, those who dispute about the Hour are in far-reaching error.",
  42019: "Allāh is Subtle and Gentle with His servants [11]. He provides abundantly for whom He wills. He is the All-Powerful, the All-Mighty.\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. Al-Laṭīf (the Subtle, the Most Gentle) denotes Allāh’s hidden kindness and delicate care, by which He brings benefit to His servants through ways they may not perceive. His subtlety includes granting provision and restricting it for whom He wills, according to His wisdom, so that both ease and hardship serve a higher purpose. Nothing in His dealings is random or harsh; even what appears difficult is governed by mercy, knowledge, and perfect judgment.",
  42020: "Whoever seeks the harvest of the Hereafter—We increase their harvest. And whoever seeks only the harvest of this world—We give them some of it [12], but in the Hereafter they will have no share at all.\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. This metaphor draws on the image of sowing and harvest: one who plants sparingly reaps little, while one who plants generously reaps an abundant return. It emphasizes that outcomes—whether worldly or in the Hereafter—correspond directly to intention and effort.",
  42021: "Or do they have partners who have legislated for them a religion that Allāh has not authorized? Were it not for a decisive word already given [13], judgment would certainly have been passed between them. And indeed, the wrongdoers will suffer a painful punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. That is, a prior divine decree postponing final judgment until its appointed time. Were it not for this decree, Allāh would have already judged between them decisively in this world.",
  42022: "You will see the wrongdoers fearful of what they have earned, and it will surely come upon them. But those who believe and do righteous deeds will be in the meadows of the Gardens, having whatever they wish with their Lord—that is the great bounty.",
  42023: "That is the good news which Allāh gives to His servants who believe and do righteous deeds. Say, “I ask of you no reward for it, except affection [14] for close kin.” Whoever performs a good deed, We increase its goodness for him. Indeed, Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Appreciative.\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. Lit. “affection in/for the near kin” (al-mawaddata fī l-qurbā). This phrase has been understood in several ways: \n(a) that the Prophet asks no recompense except that his people show due affection and respect toward those closely related to him—often understood as his household; (b) that he asks them to uphold the bonds of kinship in general, maintaining family ties and not severing them; or (c) that, at minimum, they refrain from opposing or harming him, given the shared kinship and social ties among them. The wording is deliberately concise and admits more than one of these meanings. At its core, the phrase points to enduring values of love, faithfulness, mercy, and mutual honor—qualities that draw people closer to Allāh and lead to divine favor and a good standing in His sight.",
  42024: "Or do they say, “He has invented a lie against Allāh”? Yet if Allāh willed, He could seal your heart. Allāh abolishes falsehood and establishes the truth by His words. Indeed, He is All-Knowing of what is within the chests.",
  42025: "It is He who accepts repentance from His servants and pardons sins, and He knows all that you do.",
  42026: "He responds to those who believe and do righteous deeds [15], and increases them out of His bounty. And for the disbelievers is a severe punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. The verb yastajību (“He answers”) denotes divine response and acceptance, not merely verbal reply. It includes God’s responding to faith and righteous action—often through granting prayers, providing guidance, forgiveness, and increasing reward—according to His wisdom. The increase “out of His bounty” emphasizes that what is given exceeds what is strictly earned. In this sense, God is attentive to the calls of the righteous: He hears their supplication and watches over them with care.",
  42027: "If Allāh were to enlarge provision for all His servants, they would surely transgress on the earth; but He sends it down in due measure, as He wills. Indeed, He is All-Aware and All-Seeing of His servants.",
  42028: "It is He who sends down rain after they have despaired, and spreads His mercy. And He is the Guardian, the Praiseworthy.",
  42029: "Among Allāh’s signs is the creation of the heavens and the earth, and whatever living beings He has dispersed within them. He is fully capable of gathering them all together whenever He wills.",
  42030: "Whatever calamity befalls you is because of what your hands have earned—and yet He pardons much of it.",
  42031: "You cannot escape Him on earth, and you have no protector nor helper besides Allāh.",
  42032: "And among His signs are the ships sailing in the sea like towering mountains.",
  42033: "If He wills, He can still the wind, and they remain motionless upon the sea. Indeed, in this are signs for everyone who is patient and grateful.",
  42034: "Or He may cause them to perish because of what they have earned—and yet He pardons much of it.",
  42035: "So that those who dispute Our signs may know that they have no escape.",
  42036: "Whatever you are given is merely the fleeting enjoyment of this worldly life, but what is with Allāh is far better and more lasting for those who believe and place their trust in their Lord,",
  42037: "those who avoid major sins and indecencies, and who, when angered, forgive. [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. The verse praises restraint and forgiveness at moments of anger; it does not commend revenge, oppression, or violence. In dealings with others, believers are called to tolerance, self-control, and moral restraint—being deliberate in speech and slow to anger. This ethic reflects divine attributes themselves, for Allāh is forbearing, abundant in mercy, and faithful. Forgiveness is repeatedly linked to divine pardon: those who forgive others are themselves forgiven by Allāh.\nThis principle is exemplified in the conduct of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, who embodied restraint even when personally threatened. During the expedition of Dhāt al-Riqāʿ, a man drew the Prophet’s sword and asked, “Who will protect you from me?” The Prophet calmly replied, “Allāh.” The sword fell from the man’s hand, and when the Prophet took it and asked the same question, the man pleaded for forgiveness. The Prophet did not retaliate, but released him, and the man later testified to his companions: “I have come to you from one of the best of mankind.” Such conduct illustrates that forgiveness here refers to restraint in personal grievance and moral response—not the abandonment of justice, but the elevation of character.",
  42038: "those who respond to their Lord [17], establish prayer, conduct their affairs by mutual consultation, and spend out of what We have provided for them;\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. That is, to believe that He is the One True God and to worship none but Him. “Respond to their Lord” denotes willing acceptance of Allāh’s call through faith and obedience. It includes responding to revelation, submitting to divine guidance, and acting upon what Allāh commands. This response is not merely verbal assent, but a lived commitment expressed through worship, ethical conduct, and communal responsibility.",
  42039: "and those who, when injustice afflicts them, defend themselves justly. [18]\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. That is, restoring one’s rights in order to preserve honor and dignity, particularly where the oppressor does not merit remission or where forgiveness would not serve a just interest. This refers to legitimate self-defense in response to injustice or oppression, not retaliation marked by excess or wrongdoing. The Qur’an affirms the right to respond to tyranny while placing clear moral limits on such response: it must be proportionate, just, and free of transgression. The verses that follow emphasize that although measured self-defense is permitted, forgiveness and restraint remain the higher moral ideal when reconciliation is possible.",
  42040: "The recompense of an offense is an offense like it; but whoever pardons and makes reconciliation, his reward is with Allāh. Indeed, He does not like the wrongdoers.",
  42041: "Whoever defends themselves after being wronged—there is no blame against them.",
  42042: "Blame lies only upon those who oppress people and transgress in the land without right; for them there will be a painful punishment.",
  42043: "Yet whoever is patient [19] and forgiving—indeed, that is among the matters requiring firm resolve. [20]\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. Patience in the Qur’anic sense does not mean passive endurance or resignation to injustice. Rather, it denotes moral steadiness, gentleness of character, and self-restraint, especially in moments of hardship, anger, or provocation. It includes bearing with others in a spirit of mercy and love while remaining committed to what is right.\n[20]. Firm resolve refers to strength of will and moral determination in choosing what is right despite personal cost. By describing patience and forgiveness as matters of firm resolve, the verse elevates them as deliberate and courageous ethical choices, not signs of weakness. Such conduct is characteristic of those who seek Allāh’s reward, and it is granted only to those whom Allāh has endowed with great merit.",
  42044: "Whoever Allāh leaves to stray, there is no protector for them after Him. And you will see the wrongdoers, when they behold the punishment, saying, “Is there any way back?” [21]\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. That is, a return to worldly life or an opportunity to reverse their fate after judgment has become manifest. The question expresses despair rather than hope, for the Qur’an repeatedly affirms that once punishment is seen, repentance and return are no longer possible..",
  42045: "You will see them exposed to the Fire, humbled by disgrace, looking on with furtive glances. The believers will say, “The true losers are those who have lost themselves and their families on the Day of Resurrection.” Indeed, the wrongdoers will be in an everlasting punishment.",
  42046: "They will have no protectors to help them besides Allāh. And whoever Allāh leaves to stray—there is no way forward for them.",
  42047: "Respond to your Lord [22] before a Day comes from Allāh that cannot be averted. On that Day you will have no refuge, nor will you be able to deny it.\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. “Respond to your Lord” denotes turning back to Allāh in faith, repentance, and obedience—affirming pure Islamic monotheism—before the onset of the Day of Judgment. It includes accepting divine guidance, abandoning wrongdoing, and acting upon what Allāh has commanded while the opportunity for return and forgiveness remains.",
  42048: "But if they turn away, We have not sent you [O Prophet] as a guardian over them. Your duty is only to convey [the message clearly] [23]. And indeed, when We let a person taste mercy from Us, they rejoice in it; but if harm afflicts them because of what their hands have put forth, then indeed, the human being is utterly ungrateful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. That is, the Prophet’s responsibility is limited to conveying the divine message clearly and faithfully. Guidance and acceptance rest with Allāh alone; the Prophet is not charged with compelling belief, controlling outcomes, or being accountable for people’s responses.\nThe message of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ was complete and clear, centered upon the Oneness of God, his prophethood, the Hereafter, and the ethical demands of mercy, justice, love, and forgiveness. He called people to love Allāh and to show compassion toward others, especially neighbors and the vulnerable. His message offered hope to the marginalized and transformed lives, yet many still chose to turn away from the truth.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ illustrated his concern for humanity by saying: “The example of me and you is like that of a man who kindled\na fire, and moths and insects began to fall into it. I keep holding you back from the Fire, but you keep slipping from my hand.” This highlights his role as a sincere warner and guide—not an enforcer of belief.",
  42049: "To Allāh belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth. He creates whatever He wills: He grants daughters to whom He wills, and sons to whom He wills.",
  42050: "Or He grants them both sons and daughters, and leaves whoever He wills childless. Indeed, He is All-Knowing, All-Powerful.",
  42051: "It is not for any human that Allāh should speak to them except by revelation, or from behind a veil, or by sending a messenger to reveal, by His permission, whatever He wills. Indeed, He is Most High, All-Wise.",
  42052: "Thus We have revealed to you a revelation from Our command. You did not know what the Scripture was, nor what faith was; but We made it a light by which We guide whom We will of Our servants. And indeed, you guide to a straight path—",
  42053: "the path of Allāh —to whom belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth. Indeed, to Allāh all matters return.",
  43001: "Ḥāʾ Mīm. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2: verse 1.",
  43002: "By the Clear Book. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The Clear Book refers to the Qur’an. God swears by it to emphasize its authority and truthfulness, underscoring the certainty of what follows. It is described as “clear” in that its verses are lucid in language, explicit in guidance, and decisive in rulings and meanings. Its truth is manifest, and it clarifies what is otherwise ambiguous, indefinite, or inexact—distinguishing truth from falsehood and providing clear direction in matters of belief, law, and moral conduct.",
  43003: "Indeed, We have made it an Arabic Qur’an, so that you may understand.",
  43004: "And indeed, it is in the Master Book [3] with Us—exalted and full of wisdom.\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. i.e., the Preserved Tablet.The Master Book (Umm al-Kitāb, lit. “the Mother of the Book”) refers to the primordial source of divine revelation with Allāh. It denotes the preserved, authoritative register in which the Qur’an exists prior to its progressive revelation in time. Describing it as “with Us” emphasizes its exalted status, divine origin, and protection from alteration, while “highly exalted and full of wisdom” affirms its supreme rank and perfect ordering.",
  43005: "Should We then turn away from you the Reminder, disregarding you, because you are a people who exceed all bounds?",
  43006: "How many a prophet did We send among the former peoples!",
  43007: "Yet no prophet came to them without their mocking him.",
  43008: "So We destroyed those stronger than these in might, and the example of the former peoples has already passed.",
  43009: "If you ask them, ‘Who created the heavens and the earth?’ they will surely say, ‘Allāh —the All-Mighty, the All-Knowing.’",
  43010: "It is He who has made the earth a resting place for you, and made within it pathways for you, so that you may be guided.",
  43011: "It is He who sends down water from the sky in due measure; then by it We revive a dead land. Thus you will be brought forth.",
  43012: "And He is the One who created all things in pairs and made for you ships and livestock upon which you ride,",
  43013: "so that you may settle upon their backs; then remember the favor of your Lord when you have settled upon them, and say, ‘Glory be to the One who has subjected this to us, for we could not have done so.",
  43014: "And indeed, to our Lord we will return.’",
  43015: "Yet they attribute to Him some of His servants as offspring [4]. Indeed, the human being is clearly ungrateful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. This refers to the claim that some beings—such as angels or others whom Allāh Himself created—are His offspring. The Qur’an condemns this belief as a grave error, for it undermines Allāh’s absolute oneness and transcendence. Those beings whom people attribute as offspring to Allāh are, in fact, His servants, wholly dependent upon Him. Attributing progeny to Allāh reflects ingratitude for His favors and a failure to recognize His unique, incomparable nature..",
  43016: "Or has He taken for Himself daughters from what He created and chosen for you sons?",
  43017: "When one of them is given glad tidings of a daughter, which he attributes to the Most Compassionate, his face darkens, and he is filled with grief.",
  43018: "Is one who is raised in adornment and unable to express clearly in dispute [to be associated with Allāh]?",
  43019: "Yet they regard the angels—who are servants of the Most Compassionate—as females. Did they witness their creation? Their testimony will be recorded, and they will be questioned.",
  43020: "They say, “If the Most Compassionate had willed, we would never have worshiped them [5].” They have no knowledge of that; they do nothing but conjecture.\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. “Them” refers to the angels, whom the pagans regarded as daughters of God and worshiped alongside Him. Their claim—“If the Most Compassionate had willed…”—is a false justification, blaming divine will for their own idolatry.",
  43021: "Or did We give them a Scripture before it to which they hold fast?",
  43022: "Rather, they say, ‘We found our fathers upon a tradition, and indeed, we are following in their footsteps.’",
  43023: "Thus, We did not send any warner before you in any town except that its affluent ones said, ‘Indeed, we found our fathers upon a tradition, and indeed, we are following in their footsteps.’",
  43024: "He said, ‘Even if I bring you better guidance than what you found your fathers following?’ They said, ‘Indeed, we deny what you have been sent with.’",
  43025: "So We took retribution from them; then see how the end of the deniers was.",
  43026: "And [remember] when Abraham said to his father and his people, “Indeed, I disavow all that you worship,",
  43027: "Except the One who created me [6]; for indeed He will guide me.\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. i.e., Allāh —the One who alone created and guides. Abraham’s exception affirms exclusive devotion to the Creator and rejects all forms of idolatry. By grounding guidance in creation itself, the verse emphasizes that the One who brought him into existence is also the only One capable of rightly guiding him. This statement expresses complete trust in divine guidance and underscores the principle of pure monotheism (tawḥīd).",
  43028: "And he made it [7] an enduring word among his descendants, so that they might return [to Allāh]. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. That is, Abraham established the declaration of pure monotheism—that none is to be worshiped besides Allāh—as an enduring principle among his descendants. This “word” refers to the creed of monotheism, passed down as a lasting spiritual legacy, serving as a constant (standard) by which truth and falsehood are distinguished across generations.\n[8]. That is, so that they may turn back to Allāh whenever they stray. The verse points to the enduring availability of repentance and guidance: when people slip into error, heedlessness, or deviation, they are called to return through repentance, remembrance, and renewed commitment upon receiving admonition. This highlights Allāh’s mercy and the continual openness of the path back to Him.",
  43029: "Rather, I granted enjoyment to these and their fathers until the truth and a clear messenger came to them. [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. These” refers to the present disbelievers, along with their forefathers, who were granted worldly enjoyment until the coming of the truth—i.e., divine revelation—and a clear messenger, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ.\n“Enjoyment” here refers to temporary worldly provision and respite, not divine approval or endorsement. Allāh allowed them and their forefathers to benefit from life’s comforts while deferring judgment, until the arrival of the truth and a clear messenger, whose message was manifest, intelligible, and supported by evident proof. This underscores that accountability follows only after clear guidance has been conveyed, leaving no excuse based on ignorance or lack of warning.",
  43030: "But when the Truth came to them, they said, ‘This is sorcery, and indeed, we disbelieve in it.’",
  43031: "And they said, ‘Why was this Qur’an not sent down to a great man from one of the two towns?’ [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. The two towns refers to Makkah and Ṭāʾif, the two principal cities of western Arabia at the time. The objectors assumed that divine revelation should be granted to a wealthy, powerful, or socially prominent figure from among these cities—rather than to Muhammad, whom they regarded as an impoverished orphan. This objection reflects their belief that worldly status and lineage determine worth and authority. The verse rebukes this elitist assumption, emphasizing that Allāh alone chooses whom He wills for His mercy and message, independent of wealth, rank, or social standing.",
  43032: "Is it they who distribute the mercy of your Lord? It is We who have distributed among them their livelihood in the life of this world, and We have raised some of them above others in ranks, so that some may employ others in service. But the mercy of your Lord is better than what they amass.",
  43033: "Were it not that all people would become one community [11], We would have made for those who disbelieve in the Most Compassionate houses with ceilings of silver and stairways upon which they ascend,\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. That is, all people would become united in disbelief, drawn by the overwhelming attraction of worldly wealth. Allāh restrains such overwhelming worldly luxury for the disbelievers so that wealth is not mistaken as a sign of divine favor or truth. This verse highlights that worldly prosperity is not a measure of worth before Allāh, and that material inequality serves a moral purpose by preserving human freedom to choose faith without coercion through wealth or spectacle.",
  43034: "and for their houses doors of silver and couches upon which they recline,",
  43035: "and ornaments of gold. Yet all of this is nothing but the enjoyment of this worldly life. And the Hereafter, with your Lord, is for the righteous. [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. That is, the lasting reward, honor, and true success of the Hereafter belong to the righteous—those who are mindful of Allāh and live in obedience to Him. This verse draws a sharp contrast between the fleeting enjoyment of worldly luxury and the enduring nearness and reward found with the Lord. Material wealth, therefore, is neither a sign of divine favor nor a measure of true worth; rather, righteousness is the criterion by which ultimate success is judged.",
  43036: "Whoever turns away from the Reminder of the Most Compassionate [13], We assign to him a devil, who becomes his companion.\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. Turning away blindly refers to deliberate neglect and willful disregard of divine guidance, not mere ignorance or an unintentional lapse. The Reminder of the Most Compassionate refers to the Qur’an, which calls people to faith, reflection, and moral responsibility. When a person persistently rejects this reminder, Allāh allows a devil to become his qarīn (constant associate), reinforcing misguidance and increasing sin. This companionship is a consequence of one’s own choices, not coercion, and illustrates the Qur’anic principle that turning away from guidance leads to further moral blindness.",
  43037: "They indeed hinder them from the Path, while they think they are rightly guided.",
  43038: "Until, when he comes to Us, he will say to his companion, ‘If only there were between me and you the distance of the two easts!’ What a wretched companion! [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14] The distance of the two easts is an Arabic idiom expressing the greatest possible separation. It is commonly understood as a figurative reference to the vast distance between east and west, or between the extreme points of sunrise at different times of the year. The expression conveys intense regret and revulsion, as the person wishes to be utterly removed from the devil who accompanied and misled him. This lament underscores the tragic realization, on the Day of Judgment, of the consequences of choosing corrupt companionship over divine guidance.",
  43039: "It will not benefit you on that Day—since you committed wrongdoing—that you will share in the punishment. [15]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. That is, it will bring them no benefit or relief on that Day to share punishment with those who misled them. Wrongdoing here refers foremost to associating partners with Allāh in worship (polytheism)—the gravest of sins—as well as other major transgressions committed knowingly and persistently. Each person bears responsibility for his own actions, and shared guilt or companionship in punishment offers no mitigation. The verse underscores individual accountability and the futility of blame-shifting on the Day of Judgment.",
  43040: "Can you then make the deaf hear, or guide the blind and one who is in clear error? [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. The deaf and the blind are metaphors for those who deliberately refuse to hear or perceive the truth. The verse does not refer to physical impairment, but to spiritual obstinacy and self-imposed blindness. It reassures the Prophet that guidance ultimately rests with Allāh alone; one cannot compel understanding or faith in those who persist in manifest error.",
  43041: "Even if We take you away [in death], We will surely take retribution from them.",
  43042: "Or We will surely show you [O Prophet] what We have promised them; for indeed We have power over them.",
  43043: "So hold fast, [O Prophet], to what has been revealed to you. Indeed, you are upon a straight path.",
  43044: "It is indeed a Reminder for you and your people, and you will be questioned about it.",
  43045: "Ask those of Our messengers whom We sent before you: ‘Did We ever appoint gods to be worshiped besides Allāh, the Most Compassionate?’",
  43046: "We sent Moses with Our signs to Pharaoh and his courtiers, and he said, ‘Indeed, I am a messenger of the Lord of the worlds.’",
  43047: "When he came to them with Our signs, they at once mocked them.",
  43048: "We did not show them any sign except that it was greater than the previous one, and We seized them with punishment [17] so that they might return [in repentance].\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. That is, Allāh afflicted them with successive punishments—such as droughts, plagues, famine, crop failure, floods, and other calamities—not as acts of arbitrary vengeance, but as warnings meant to awaken conscience and prompt repentance. The Qur’an repeatedly emphasizes that such afflictions serve as corrective mercy, providing opportunities for people to return to Allāh before final judgment.",
  43049: "They said [to Moses], ‘O sorcerer [18], invoke your Lord for us by the covenant He has made with you. If He removes this punishment from us, we will surely be guided.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. The term “sorcerer” (sāḥir) is used here derisively to deny Moses’ prophethood by portraying his divinely granted signs as magic. Although the word could sometimes be used more loosely for someone of exceptional knowledge or skill, in this context it reflects mockery rather than honor. Despite this, they still acknowledged Moses’ special standing with Allāh by asking him to invoke his Lord on their behalf. This exposes their inconsistency and lack of sincere faith: they sought relief from punishment without genuine submission, promising guidance only as a temporary response to affliction.",
  43050: "When We removed the punishment from them, they at once broke their pledge.",
  43051: "And Pharaoh called out to his people, ‘O my people, does not the kingdom of Egypt belong to me, and do not these rivers flow beneath me? Do you not see?’",
  43052: "Or am I not better than this one, who is insignificant and can hardly express himself? [19]\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. Pharaoh’s claim reflects arrogance and false standards of worth. By calling Moses “lowly ” and “barely articulate,” he sought to undermine the message by attacking the messenger, appealing to social status, wealth, and outward eloquence rather than truth. Moses did experience a speech impediment prior to his mission, and he prayed to Allāh to remove it and to ease his speech; this supplication was answered before he was sent to Pharaoh (see 20:25–36). Pharaoh’s accusation therefore was either exaggerated or deliberately misleading, serving as a rhetorical tactic to discredit Moses and divert attention from the divine signs he brought. The verse exposes the fallacy of judging truth by worldly rank or rhetorical polish rather than by divine authority and moral substance.",
  43053: "Why then have bracelets of gold not been given to him, or angels sent with him to accompany him?",
  43054: "Thus he led his people astray, and they obeyed him. They were a sinful people.",
  43055: "So when they angered Us, We took retribution from them and drowned them all together.",
  43056: "Thus We made them an example and a lesson for later generations.",
  43057: "When the son of Mary was cited as an example [20], your people immediately burst into mockery. [21]\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. That is, Jesus son of Mary was cited as an example in the discussion of beings falsely worshiped alongside Allāh. His mention was not to equate him with idols, but to expose the inconsistency of those who claimed that venerating created beings—whether idols, angels, or righteous servants—was acceptable. The Qur’an repeatedly affirms that Jesus was a human messenger and servant of Allāh, never claiming divinity or inviting worship.\n[21] Their laughter and jeering arose from a polemical objection. They argued mockingly that if those worshiped besides Allāh are destined for punishment, then Jesus—whom some people worship—would also be in Hell. This argument was made in derision, not in good faith. The Prophet ﷺ responded by clarifying that the earlier verse referred specifically to lifeless idols and false gods that people knowingly worship, not to righteous servants of Allāh who neither sought worship nor approved of it. Jesus himself explicitly called people to worship Allāh alone and disavowed any claim to divinity. Their mockery thus reflected deliberate distortion rather than sincere inquiry.",
  43058: "And they said, “Are our gods better, or he—Jesus?” They did not raise this comparison except for the sake of argument; rather, they are a quarrelsome people. [22]\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. Matthew 2:13 says, \"When they had gone, an angel of the Lord appeared to Joseph in a dream. 'Get up,' he said, 'take the child (Jesus) and his mother and escape to Egypt. Stay there until I tell you, for Herod is going to search for the child to kill him.'\"\nHere, Jesus is clearly under the guidance of Allāh, and Allāh communicates instructions through His messenger (the angel). This shows obedience to Allāh, which is a key sign of servitude.\" \nThe angel warned Joseph to take Jesus to safety. This shows that Jesus was a servant of Allāh and a messenger. So, even in the Bible, we see that Jesus is both a servant and a messenger of Allāh, acting fully under Allāh’s guidance, just as the prophets are described.",
  43059: "He—Jesus—was no more than a servant upon whom We bestowed favor [Prophethood], and We made him an example for the Children of Israel. [23]\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. This verse affirms that Jesus was a servant of Allāh, honored with prophethood and divine favor, not a being worthy of worship. This understanding is consistent with Jesus’ own statements attributed to him in earlier scriptures, where he distinguished himself from God and directed worship to Allāh alone. For example, he is reported to have said, “I am ascending to Allāh, my Lord and your Lord, and to my God and your God,” affirming shared servanthood rather than divinity. Likewise, when addressed as “Good Teacher,” he reportedly replied, “Why do you call me good? None is good except Allāh,” redirecting ultimate goodness to Allāh alone. Such statements reinforce the Qur’anic portrayal of Jesus as a righteous messenger who called people to worship Allāh, lived in obedience to Him, and never claimed divinity. His life thus serves as an example for the Children of Israel in humility, devotion, and submission to Allāh.",
  43060: "Had We willed, We could have made angels succeed you upon the earth, one after another [in worship of Allāh alone].",
  43061: "He is surely a sign of the Hour [24]; so do not doubt it, and follow me—this is a straight path.\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. This verse is widely understood to refer to the second coming of Jesus, son of Mary, as one of the major signs preceding the Hour. The Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said: “There is no prophet between me and him—that is, Jesus—and he will descend.” He described him as of medium height, fair with a reddish hue, wearing two light garments, appearing as though water were dripping from his head, though it would not be wet.\nAccording to authentic sources from the Qur’an and Sunnah, Jesus will return not as a new prophet or lawgiver, but as a follower of the final revelation, confirming the truth of Islam and restoring justice. He is reported to descend near the eastern white minaret of Damascus, traditionally associated with the Umayyad Mosque.\nUpon his descent, he will join the believers in prayer and pray behind the Mahdī, affirming his adherence to the final message brought by Muḥammad ﷺ. He will then confront and kill al-Masīḥ ad-Dajjāl (the False Messiah) near Ludd (Lod) in present-day Palestine, bringing an end to widespread deception and tyranny.\nHe will rule with justice, break the cross—refuting false claims of his divinity—kill the swine, and abolish the jizyah, as people accept the truth willingly. Peace and abundance will prevail, and the Qur’an and Sunnah will govern society.\nHe will live for a period of time, then die a natural death. His return will affirm his true status as a servant and messenger of Allāh, vindicate his message of pure monotheism, and mark the final triumph of truth before the onset of the Day of Judgment.",
  43062: "Do not let Satan turn you [O people] away from the Path, for he is a clear enemy to you.",
  43063: "When Jesus came with clear proofs, he said, ‘Indeed, I have come to you with wisdom, and to make clear to you some of what you differ about. So fear Allāh and obey me.",
  43064: "Indeed, Allāh—He alone is my Lord and your Lord—so worship Him; this is the straight Path.’",
  43065: "But the factions differed among themselves concerning Jesus [25]; so woe to the wrongdoers from the punishment of a painful Day.\\n\\nFootnotes: [25]. O you who ask about Jesus—listen to the voice of truth. He was a noble servant and a chosen Prophet, never a god to be worshiped. He came with the light of pure monotheism, calling hearts to their Lord. He never claimed divinity, nor did he call people to worship anyone besides Allāh.\nHe was born as a pure miracle, to a chaste and chosen mother; by a word from his Creator, he became a sign for all mankind. He was neither killed nor crucified, but was raised in honor, as his Lord saved him from harm and preserved the truth.\nAnd he will return one day with justice—breaking the cross and falsehood—guiding humanity back to pure monotheism, and following the final Messenger, Muḥammad ﷺ.",
  43066: "Are they waiting for anything except that the Hour comes upon them suddenly, while they do not perceive?",
  43067: "On that Day, close friends will be enemies of one another—except the righteous.",
  43068: "“O My servants, there will be no fear upon you this Day, nor will you grieve.",
  43069: "—those who believed in Our verses and were Muslims [fully submitting to God].",
  43070: "Enter Paradise, you and your spouses, rejoicing in bliss.”",
  43071: "There will be circulated among them platters of gold and goblets; therein will be all that souls desire and eyes delight in. And you will abide therein forever.",
  43072: "This is the Paradise that you have been made to inherit because of what you used to do [of righteousness].",
  43073: "You will have therein abundant fruits, from which you will eat.",
  43074: "Indeed, the guilty will remain in the punishment of Jahannam [Hell] forever.",
  43075: "It will not be lightened for them, and they will be therein in utter despair.",
  43076: "We did not wrong them; rather, they themselves were the wrongdoers.",
  43077: "They will cry out, ‘O Mālik [26], let your Lord put an end to us.’ He will say, ‘Indeed, you will remain.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. Mālik is the angel appointed by Allāh as the keeper of Hellfire. In their extreme despair, the inhabitants of Hell call upon him to ask his Lord to end their existence altogether. Mālik’s response—“Indeed, you will remain”—confirms the permanence of their punishment and the finality of divine judgment. This exchange underscores the depth of their regret and the absence of any relief or escape after the truth has been knowingly rejected.",
  43078: "We have certainly brought you the Truth, but most of you are averse to it.",
  43079: "Or have they resolved on a matter [27]? Indeed, We too have resolved.\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]. That is, have they firmly resolved upon a plan or course of action—namely, a plot against you (Muḥammad) and the believers. The Arabic verb conveys decisive determination; the verse responds by affirming that Allāh, in turn, has determined the outcome, underscoring the ultimate supremacy of the divine will.",
  43080: "Or do they think that We do not hear their secrets and their private conversations? Indeed We do—and Our angel-messengers are with them, recording.",
  43081: "Say, ‘If the Most Compassionate had a son—as you claim—then I would be the first of His worshippers.’ [28]\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. This statement is rhetorical and does not affirm the possibility of God having a son. Rather, it refutes the claim: if such a thing were true, the Prophet would be the first to submit accordingly. Some scholars interpret “the first of the worshippers” as meaning the first to reject or deny such an assertion. In all cases, the verse affirms God’s absolute Oneness and negates any offspring attributed to Him.",
  43082: "Glory be to the Lord of the heavens and the earth—the Lord of the great Throne—far exalted above all that they ascribe to Him.",
  43083: "So leave them to indulge in falsehood and amuse themselves until they meet their promised Day.",
  43084: "It is He who is God in the heavens and God on the earth. He is the All-Wise, the All-Knowing.",
  43085: "Blessed is the One to whom belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth and whatever is between them. With Him is knowledge of the Hour, and to Him you will be returned [for judgment].",
  43086: "Those whom they invoke besides Him have no power to intercede, except those who bear witness to the Truth while they know. [29]\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. That is, none possess any power of intercession except by Allāh’s permission. The exception refers to those who knowingly bear witness to the Truth—namely, the Oneness of God (Islamic monotheism): that Allāh alone is worthy of worship, that He has no partner, no offspring, no equal, and no intermediary who shares in His divinity or authority. Such testimony must be given with knowledge and conviction, not ignorance or blind imitation.",
  43087: "If you ask them [30] who created them, they will surely say, ‘Allāh.’ How then are they turned away? [31]\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. That is, the pagans addressed in this passage, who invoke others besides Allāh while still acknowledging Him as their Creator.\n[31]. That is, how are they turned away from worshipping Allāh alone after admitting that He alone created them. The question is rhetorical, highlighting the inconsistency and irrationality of affirming divine creation while denying exclusive devotion.",
  43088: "And He knows [his] saying [32], ‘O my Lord, these are a people who do not believe.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [32]. This refers to the Prophet Muḥammad’s complaint to his Lord regarding the persistent disbelief of his people, expressing sorrow and concern rather than a plea for punishment. God’s knowing affirms that this supplication is heard and fully accounted for.",
  43089: "So [forbear and] overlook them and say, ‘Peace!’ [33] For they will soon know.\\n\\nFootnotes: [33]. That is, address them with words of gentleness, avoiding harsh or improper speech. This is a command to respond with dignified restraint and disengagement, not an expression of reconciliation. “Peace” here signifies withdrawal without retaliation or argument, leaving their judgment to Allāh.",
  44001: "Ḥāʾ Mīm. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2: verse 1.",
  44002: "By the clear Book. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The Clear Book refers to the Qur’an. God swears by it to emphasize its authority and truthfulness, underscoring the certainty of what follows. It is described as “clear” in that its verses are lucid in language, explicit in guidance, and decisive in rulings and meanings. Its truth is manifest, and it clarifies what is otherwise ambiguous, indefinite, or inexact—distinguishing truth from falsehood and providing clear direction in matters of belief, law, and moral conduct.",
  44003: "We sent it down on a blessed night [3]. Indeed, We are ever warning [mankind]. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. That is, the blessed night on which the Qur’an was first sent down, in the month of Ramadān, the ninth month of the lunar calendar. This night is commonly referred to as Laylat al-Qadr (the Night of Decree), and is distinguished by divine blessing, mercy, and the commencement of revelation.\n[4]. That is, We have sent revelation as a warning to mankind—calling them to faith, repentance, and awareness of divine judgment.",
  44004: "On that night, every matter of wisdom is made distinct. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. That is, on this blessed night, every matter decreed by Allāh is distinguished and set forth with wisdom, including affairs related to life, death, provision, and destiny, according to divine knowledge and will.",
  44005: "As a command from Us. Indeed, We have ever been sending [messengers].",
  44006: "As a mercy from your Lord. Indeed, He alone is the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing.",
  44007: "Lord of the heavens and the earth and whatever is between them—if you are certain.",
  44008: "There is no true god except Him. He gives life and causes death. He is your Lord and the Lord of your forefathers.",
  44009: "Rather, they are in doubt, lost in idle play.",
  44010: "So watch for a Day when the sky will come with a clear smoke. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. A reference to the severe drought that struck the pagans, causing extreme famine, after which they appealed to the Prophet ﷺ to pray to Allāh for relief.",
  44011: "It will cover the people. This is a painful punishment.",
  44012: "“Our Lord, remove this punishment from us; indeed, we now believe.” [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. This plea expresses a declaration of belief in the oneness of Allāh and in Muḥammad as His servant and Messenger, uttered under the pressure of punishment rather than from sincere conviction. It echoes earlier denials and anticipates the divine response that such belief, professed only at the moment of affliction, will not avail them.",
  44013: "How can there be remembrance for them, when a clear messenger had already come to them [Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ]?",
  44014: "Then they turned away from him and said, ‘He is taught—and possessed.’",
  44015: "Indeed, We will remove the punishment for a while, but you will surely return [to your former ways].",
  44016: "On the Day [8] when We seize with the greatest blow, We will surely take retribution.\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. That is, the Day of decisive judgment, commonly interpreted as referring to the Battle of Badr, when decisive retribution was inflicted upon the disbelievers.",
  44017: "Indeed, We tested the people of Pharaoh before them, when a noble messenger came to them [i.e., Moses],",
  44018: "[saying], ‘Release to me the servants of Allāh. Indeed, I am a trustworthy messenger for you,’",
  44019: "and do not exalt yourselves above Allāh [9]. Indeed, I have come to you with a clear authority.\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. That is, do not act arrogantly or tyrannically by defying Allāh’s command—neglecting His worship and considering yourselves above His worshippers. Exalting oneself above Allāh by rejecting His authority signifies rebellion against divine sovereignty and refusal to submit to His will.",
  44020: "And indeed, I seek refuge in Allāh—my Lord and your Lord—lest you stone me.",
  44021: "If you do not believe me, then leave me alone.",
  44022: "Then he called out to his Lord [10], saying, ‘Indeed, these are a guilty people.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. That is, Moses appealed to his Lord after persistent rejection and hostility, describing his people as guilty of disbelief, oppression, and defiance of the divine command. This supplication represents a plea for divine judgment after the exhaustion of patient warning and forbearance.\nThe verse also offers a broader lesson: when faced with hardship and injustice, believers are encouraged to turn to Allāh in supplication and to await His response with patience and trust, knowing that He is ever near and fully aware of their need.",
  44023: "[Allāh said], ‘Set out at night with My servants; indeed, you will be pursued.’",
  44024: "And leave the sea calm. Indeed, they are an army destined to be drowned.",
  44025: "How many gardens and springs they left behind!",
  44026: "and cultivated fields and noble dwellings,",
  44027: "and luxuries in which they delighted.",
  44028: "Thus it was, and We caused another people to inherit it.",
  44029: "So neither the heaven nor the earth wept for them, nor were they given respite.",
  44030: "Indeed, We saved the Children of Israel from the humiliating punishment,",
  44031: "from Pharaoh. Indeed, he was arrogant—among the transgressors.",
  44032: "And indeed, We chose them with knowledge, above all other peoples [in their time].",
  44033: "And We gave them signs in which there was a clear test.",
  44034: "Indeed, these [pagans] say:",
  44035: "“It is nothing but our first death, and we will never be resurrected,",
  44036: "then bring back our forefathers, if you are truthful.\"",
  44037: "Are they better, or the people of Tubbaʿ [11] and those before them? We destroyed them, for indeed, they were guilty ones.\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. Tubbaʿ is a royal title borne by ancient rulers of Yemen, often identified with the Himyarite kings. The “people of Tubbaʿ” refers to a powerful and prosperous civilization that was destroyed despite its strength, serving as a warning to those who deny the truth after clear signs.",
  44038: "We have not created the heavens and the earth and whatever is between them for play [without true purpose]. [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. That is, creation is not without wisdom, purpose, or moral order. The heavens and the earth were created in truth and for a just end, not as idle play or meaningless existence.\nIn divine wisdom, Allāh created them all; the heavens and the earth are filled with His creatures. The sea—vast and expansive—is teeming with creatures beyond number, both great and small. We proclaim that Allāh is the only true God, with no deity besides Him. His provision for all that He has created is generous and sustaining, and He has determined the span of life for every being.",
  44039: "We have not created them except in truth, but most of them do not know.",
  44040: "Indeed, the Day of Judgment is their appointed time—all of them.",
  44041: "The Day when no ally will avail another in anything, nor will they be helped,",
  44042: "except those whom Allāh has mercy upon. Indeed, He alone is the All-Mighty, the All-Merciful.",
  44043: "Indeed, the tree of Zaqqum,",
  44044: "will be the food of the sinful ones,",
  44045: "like molten metal, it boils in their bellies,",
  44046: "like the boiling of scalding water.",
  44047: "“Seize him, then drag him into the midst of the Blaze,",
  44048: "Then pour the torment of scalding water over his head.\"",
  44049: "“Taste it. Indeed, you are the mighty, the noble. [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. The condemned is addressed with titles of power and nobility he once claimed in worldly life—now used mockingly and contrasted with his humiliation and punishment.",
  44050: "Indeed, this is what you used to doubt.\"",
  44051: "Indeed, the righteous will be in a secure place,",
  44052: "in gardens and springs,",
  44053: "wearing garments of fine silk and brocade, facing one another in delight.",
  44054: "Thus it will be, and We shall wed them to maidens with wide, beautiful eyes. [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. Fair maidens created by Allāh in purity and beauty, described as having intensely dark irises contrasted with clear white scleras. They are chaste, untouched, and unwed. This is a Qur’anic description of one of the rewards of Paradise, referring to companions created in honor and purity, symbolizing comfort, dignity, and lasting companionship granted by Allāh. The imagery signifies joy, serenity, and divine generosity, not earthly desire, lust, or impermanence.",
  44055: "They will call for every kind of fruit therein, secure.",
  44056: "They will not taste death therein except the first death, and He will protect them from the punishment of the blazing Fire,",
  44057: "as a favor from your Lord. That indeed is the great success.",
  44058: "Indeed, We have made this [Qur’an] easy in your language, so that they may take heed.",
  44059: "So wait; indeed, they too are waiting.",
  45001: "Ḥāʾ Mīm. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2: verse 1.",
  45002: "This Book is sent down by Allāh—the Almighty, the All-Wise.",
  45003: "Indeed, in the heavens and the earth there are surely signs for the believers.",
  45004: "And in your own creation, and in what Allāh spreads throughout the earth of living creatures, are signs for a people who are certain in faith.",
  45005: "In the alternation of night and day, in the provision [2] that Allāh sends down from the sky—by which He revives the earth after its death—and in the directing of the winds [3], are signs for a people who use reason.\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. That is, rain and whatever provision Allāh causes to descend from the sky, by which crops grow, sustenance is produced, and life is maintained. This includes both physical nourishment and the means through which it is obtained.\n[3]. That is, the directing and regulation of the winds—shifting in direction, strength, and purpose—by which Allāh brings benefit, disperses clouds, moderates climate, and manifests His power and wisdom.",
  45006: "These are the verses of Allāh which We recite to you in truth. So in what discourse after Allāh and His verses will they believe?",
  45007: "Woe to every persistent liar and sinner,",
  45008: "who hears the verses of Allāh recited to him, then persists arrogantly as though he had not heard them; so announce to him a painful punishment.",
  45009: "When he comes to know of any of Our verses, he takes them as mockery. For such people, there is a humiliating punishment.",
  45010: "Ahead of them is Hell; neither what they have earned nor those they have taken as protectors besides Allāh will avail them in anything. And for them is a tremendous punishment.",
  45011: "This is guidance; and for those who disbelieve in the verses of their Lord is a punishment of painful torment.",
  45012: "It is Allāh who has subjected the sea for you, so that ships may sail upon it by His command, that you may seek His bounty, and that you may be grateful.",
  45013: "And He has subjected for you whatever is in the heavens [4] and whatever is on the earth [5]—all of it from Him [6]. Indeed, in that are signs for a people who reflect.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. That is, all celestial bodies and forces—such as the sun, moon, stars, and the laws governing them—which operate by Allāh’s command for benefit and order.\n[5]. That is, all that exists on the earth, including land, seas, rivers, animals, plants, trees, mountains, and resources, which have been made serviceable for human life by divine will.\n[6]. That is, all of this subjection and benefit originates solely from Allāh, not from human power or independent forces. The phrase emphasizes divine grace and exclusive lordship. All of this has been made for your benefit, so that the grace reaching people may lead to gratitude overflowing in praise and glorification of Allāh.",
  45014: "Tell those who believe [7] to forgive those who do not expect the Days of Allāh [8], so that He may recompense a people for what they have earned.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. That is, those who believe in Allāh and His Messenger Muḥammad ﷺ. They are the believers commanded to respond with patience and forbearance, particularly in the early period when armed struggle (jihād) had not yet been prescribed.\n[8]. The “Days of Allāh” refer to the times of divine judgment, punishment, and recompense—both in this world and in the Hereafter. Those who do not fear these days are those who deny accountability and act without concern for divine retribution.\nAccordingly, believers are instructed not to seek personal vengeance. Retaliation is only justified when it is for the sake of Allāh and in response to violations of His commands. As for personal wrongs, it is better to entrust the matter to Allāh and to refrain from avenging oneself. Let no one be misled by vain words that call one back to pre-Islamic or unethical conduct, for it is because of sins that the wrath of Allāh comes upon the wrongdoers.",
  45015: "Whoever does what is right, it is for his own good; and whoever does evil, it is against himself. Then to your Lord you will all be returned.",
  45016: "Indeed, We gave the Children of Israel the Scripture, sound judgment [9], and prophethood; We provided them with good things, and We favored them above all other peoples [of their time]. [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. That is, sound judgment and understanding in matters of religion and law, including wisdom, discernment, and the ability to judge rightly according to divine guidance.\n[10]. That is, above other peoples of their time, by virtue of the many prophets sent among them from the descendants of Abraham. This preference was neither absolute nor unconditional; rather, it was based on divine favor, responsibility, and obedience, and it did not guarantee salvation apart from faith and righteousness.",
  45017: "We gave them clear proofs concerning the matter [of religion]. Yet they did not differ except after knowledge had come to them, out of envy among themselves. Indeed, your Lord will judge between them on the Day of Resurrection regarding that over which they used to differ.",
  45018: "Then We set you [O Prophet] upon a clear path of the religion [of Islam—the religion of all the prophets]; so follow it, and do not follow the desires of those who have no knowledge.",
  45019: "They will not benefit you in anything against Allāh. Indeed, the wrongdoers are allies of one another, but Allāh is the Protector of the righteous.",
  45020: "This [Qur’an] is an insight for mankind—a guidance and a mercy for a people who are certain in faith.",
  45021: "Or do those who commit evil deeds think that We shall make them like those who believe and do good deeds—equal in their living and their dying? Evil is what they judge!",
  45022: "Allāh created the heavens and the earth in truth, so that every soul is recompensed for what it earned, and they will not be wronged.",
  45023: "Have you seen the one who takes his own desire as his god? And Allāh has left him astray despite knowledge, and has set a seal upon his hearing and his heart, and placed a veil over his sight. Who then can guide him after Allāh? Will you not take heed?",
  45024: "They say, ‘There is nothing but our worldly life: we die and we live [11], and nothing causes us to perish except time.’ They have no knowledge of that [12]; they only conjecture.\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. That is, they claim that life consists only of a continuous cycle of generations—some dying while others are born—without resurrection or accountability beyond this world.\n[12]. That is, they possess no certain or revealed knowledge for this claim. It is based solely on speculation and assumption, not proof or reasoned certainty.",
  45025: "When Our clear verses are recited to them, their only argument is to say, ‘Bring back our forefathers, if you are truthful.’",
  45026: "Say, ‘It is Allāh who gives you life, then causes you to die; then He will gather you on the Day of Resurrection, in which there is no doubt—but most people do not know.’",
  45027: "To Allāh belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth. On the Day the Hour comes to pass, the followers of falsehood will be lost.",
  45028: "You will see every nation kneeling; each nation will be summoned to its record [of deeds]: ‘Today you shall be recompensed for what you used to do.’",
  45029: "This is Our record; it speaks about you in truth. Indeed, We [13] have been recording all that you used to do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. That is, Allāh commanded His angels to record human deeds precisely and without omission. The record bears true testimony, neither adding nor subtracting anything from what was done.",
  45030: "As for those who believed and did righteous deeds, their Lord will admit them into His mercy. That is indeed the clear success.",
  45031: "But those who disbelieved [will be asked], ‘Were My verses not recited to you? Then you acted arrogantly and became a guilty people.’",
  45032: "Whenever it was said, ‘Indeed, the promise of Allāh is true, and there is no doubt about the Hour,’ you said, ‘We do not know what the Hour is. We only conjecture, and we are not certain.’",
  45033: "The evil of what they did will become apparent to them, and that which they used to mock will encompass them.",
  45034: "It will be said, ‘This Day We will forget you [14], just as you forgot the meeting of this Day of yours. Your abode is the Fire, and there will be none to help you.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. That is, Allāh will abandon them to punishment, just as they abandoned belief in, preparation for, and concern about the meeting of this Day. This “forgetting” does not imply literal forgetfulness on the part of Allāh—who is free from all deficiency—but signifies deliberate disregard and withdrawal of mercy as a form of just recompense.",
  45035: "That is because you took the verses of Allāh in jest, and worldly life deluded you.’ So this Day they will not be brought out of it, nor will they be allowed to make amends.",
  45036: "So all praise belongs to Allāh, Lord of the heavens, Lord of the earth, and Lord of all the worlds.",
  45037: "To Him belongs all grandeur in the heavens and the earth, and He is the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.",
  46001: "Ḥā Mīm. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2: verse 1.",
  46002: "The revelation of this Book is from Allāh —the All-Mighty, All-Wise.",
  46003: "We did not create the heavens and the earth and everything between them except in truth and for a fixed term. Yet those who disbelieve turn away from what they are warned of.",
  46004: "Say, “Have you considered those you invoke besides Allāh? Show me what they have created of the earth. Or do they have any share in the heavens? Bring me a scripture from before this [2], or a trace of knowledge—if you are truthful.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. This verse challenges the claim that idols or other beings are partners with Allāh by demanding evidence of three kinds: creation, authority, or revelation. Since none of these false gods can be shown to have created anything, to share in the governance of the heavens and the earth, or to be supported by any authentic scripture or remnant of reliable knowledge, their supposed divinity is exposed as baseless. The absence of proof confirms that associating partners with Allāh rests on assumption and tradition, not truth or evidence.",
  46005: "Who is more astray than one who invokes, besides Allāh, those who will not respond to him until the Day of Resurrection—and who are unaware of their being invoked?",
  46006: "When mankind are gathered, those they worshipped will turn against them as enemies and utterly reject their worship. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. That is, the beings they invoked besides Allāh —whether idols, false deities, or imagined intercessors—will renounce their worshippers on the Day of Resurrection and testify against them, becoming their enemies instead of their hoped-for allies.",
  46007: "When Our clear revelations are recited to them, those who disbelieve say of the truth when it comes to them, “This is obvious magic.”",
  46008: "Or do they say, “He has invented it”? Say, “If I have invented it, you have no power at all to protect me from Allāh. He knows best what you say about it [4]. He is sufficient as a witness between me and you—and He is the Most Forgiving, the Most Merciful.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. That is, concerning the false accusations, allegations, and slander made against the Qur’an and the Prophet Muhammad (ﷺ).",
  46009: "Say, “I am not unprecedented among the messengers, nor do I know what will be done with me or with you [5]. I follow only what is revealed to me, and I am but a clear warner.” [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. That is, the Prophet does not have independent knowledge of the unseen, except for what Allāh discloses to him through revelation.\n[6]. That is, a severe punishment awaits those who knowingly turn away from the Qur’an after its truth has been made clear to them.",
  46010: "Say, “Have you considered: if this Qur’an is from Allāh and you reject it, while a witness from the Children of Israel [7] testifies to its truth and believes, yet you persist in arrogance [8]? Surely Allāh does not guide the wrongdoing people.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. This refers to figures such as Abdullah ibn Salam, a learned Jewish scholar who testified to the truth of the Qur’an based on his knowledge of earlier scripture and embraced Islam after the Prophet ﷺ migrated to Madinah.\n[8]. Meaning: would you not, in that case, be acting unjustly and as wrongdoers?",
  46011: "Those who disbelieve say of those who believe, “If it were any good, they would not have preceded us to it [9].” And since they are not guided by it, they say, “This is an ancient lie.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. This alludes to the early believers, many of whom were poor, socially marginalized, or enslaved. Their acceptance of Islam was wrongly used by disbelievers as a pretext to belittle the truth of the message, reflecting arrogance rather than reason.",
  46012: "Before it was the Book of Moses—a guide and a mercy. This is a Book confirming it, in clear Arabic, to warn those who do wrong [10] and to give glad tidings to the doers of good.\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. That is, by worshipping others besides Allāh.",
  46013: "Indeed, those who say, “Our Lord is Allāh,” and then remain steadfast—no fear will be upon them, nor will they grieve.",
  46014: "Those are the companions of Paradise, abiding therein forever—a reward for what they used to do [of righteousness].",
  46015: "We have enjoined upon man goodness toward his parents. His mother bore him in hardship and gave birth to him in hardship, and his gestation and weaning are thirty months. Then, when he reaches maturity and attains forty years, he says, “My Lord, inspire me to be grateful for Your favor which You have bestowed upon me and upon my parents, and to do righteous deeds that please You, and make my offspring righteous [11]. Indeed, I turn to You in repentance, and indeed I am among the Muslims.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. Your father and mother rejoice when you live righteously and wisely. Let your conduct be a source of happiness to them: strive to ease their lives, especially in their old age, by serving them, caring for their needs, and showing patience and respect. Walk in their virtuous footsteps, follow their good example, and live with integrity, sobriety, and wisdom. True honor toward parents is not limited to outward respect alone, but is fulfilled by seeking the pleasure and glory of Allāh, upholding Islam, and supporting it to the best of your ability. Such conduct gladdens the hearts of righteous parents—especially the mother, who carried you with hardship, gave birth to you in pain, and raised you with immense care, sacrifice, and devotion.",
  46016: "Such are those from whom We accept the best of their deeds and overlook their misdeeds—counted among the companions of Paradise. This is the true promise they were given.",
  46017: "But one who says to his parents, “Uff to you! Do you promise me that I will be raised, when generations have passed before me [12]?” while they both call upon Allāh for help, saying, “Woe to you! Believe! Indeed, Allah’s promise is true.” Yet he replies, “This is nothing but the tales of the former peoples.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. He rejects resurrection by pointing to earlier generations who have passed away without returning, using this as a false argument. The verse strongly censures such behavior, emphasizing that mocking one’s parents and arrogantly refusing their guidance is a serious moral and spiritual offense deserving of divine punishment.",
  46018: "Those are the ones upon whom the decree has taken effect, among the nations of jinn and humankind who passed before them. Indeed, they were truly losers.",
  46019: "And for each there will be degrees according to what they have done, so that He may repay them in full for their deeds—and they will not be wronged.",
  46020: "On the Day the disbelievers are brought before the Fire, it will be said, “You exhausted your good things in your worldly life and enjoyed them. So today you are repaid with a humiliating punishment for your arrogance on the earth without right and for your defiant disobedience.”",
  46021: "And remember the brother of ‘Ād, when he warned his people in al-Aḥqāf—the sandy hills—while warners had passed on before him and after him, saying, “Worship none but Allāh. Indeed, I fear for you the punishment of a tremendous Day.” [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. Al-Aḥqāf refers to a region of sand dunes in the southern part of Arabia, traditionally associated with the dwelling place of the people of ‘Ād.",
  46022: "They said, “Have you come to turn us away from our gods? Then bring us what you threaten us with, if you are truthful.”",
  46023: "Hud said, \"Knowledge [of its timing] is only with Allāh, and I convey to you what I have been sent with, but I see that you are a people acting ignorantly.",
  46024: "So when they saw it as a cloud approaching their valleys, they said, “This is a cloud bringing us rain.” No—rather, it is what you sought to hasten: a wind carrying a painful punishment.",
  46025: "Destroying everything by the command of its Lord, so nothing could be seen except the ruins of their dwellings. Thus do We recompense the guilty people.",
  46026: "We had firmly established them in ways We have not established you, and We granted them hearing, sight, and intellects; yet neither their hearing, nor their sight, nor their intellects benefited them in the least, since they denied the signs of Allāh. And they were overtaken by what they used to mock.",
  46027: "We have already destroyed the towns around you and varied the signs, so that they might return.",
  46028: "So why did those they took as deities besides Allāh, to draw them nearer to Him, not help them? Rather, they failed them completely. That was the result of their lie and what they used to invent.",
  46029: "And [remember] when We directed toward you a group of the jinn to listen to the Qur’an—when they were present at it, they said, “Listen attentively.” Then when it was concluded, they returned to their people as warners.",
  46030: " They said, “O our people, we have heard a Book sent down after Moses, confirming what came before it, guiding to the truth and to a straight path.”",
  46031: "O our people, respond to Allah’s caller and believe in him [14]; He will forgive you your sins and grant you protection from a painful punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. This means to affirm the truth of what the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ brought from God, to believe in his prophethood, and to follow his guidance in belief, worship, and conduct, as sincere submission to God.",
  46032: "Whoever does not respond to Allah’s caller cannot escape on earth, nor will he have any protectors besides Him. Such are in evident error.",
  46033: "Have they not considered that Allāh—who created the heavens and the earth and was not wearied by their creation—is able to give life to the dead? Yes indeed, He is capable of all things. [15]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. That is, the creation of the heavens and the earth did not tire or exhaust Allāh in any way. This affirms His absolute and limitless power and categorically refutes any notion of weakness, fatigue, or need for rest. Attributes such as tiredness, weariness, fatigue, or the need for rest belong to created beings and human limitations, not to Allāh Almighty.\nThis stands in clear contrast to the description found in the Bible, which states that God rested after creation. For example, in Genesis it is said: “By the seventh day God had finished the work he had been doing; so on the seventh day he rested from all his work” (Genesis 2:2). The Qur’an explicitly rejects any such notion of divine fatigue or rest, affirming that Allāh is never wearied by creation. \nThe verse further presents a rational argument from the greater to the lesser: if creating the entire cosmos required no effort whatsoever, then reviving the dead is unquestionably within divine power. This line of reasoning is a recurring proof of resurrection throughout the Qur’an.",
  46034: "On the Day the disbelievers are brought before the Fire, it will be said, “Is this not the truth?” They will say, “Yes—by our Lord.” He will say, “Then taste the punishment for what you used to deny.”",
  46035: "So be patient, as were the messengers of firm resolve [16], and do not seek to hasten it for them. On the Day they see what they are promised, it will be as if they had not remained except an hour of a day. This is a proclamation—so will any be destroyed except the rebellious people?\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. That is, the messengers of firm resolve (ulū al-ʿazm), traditionally identified as Noah, Abraham, Moses, Jesus, and Muhammad—peace be upon them all—who were distinguished by exceptional patience, perseverance, and steadfastness in conveying God’s message despite severe trials.",
  47001: "Those who disbelieve and avert others from the way of Allāh —He will render their deeds worthless. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. Belief must include faith in the messengership of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. True belief is incomplete without affirming and following the Messenger sent by Allāh. Allâh’s Messenger ﷺ said, \"By Him (Allāh) in Whose Hand Muhammad’s soul is, there is none from amongst the Jews and the Christians (of these present nations) who hears about me and then dies without believing in the Message with which I have been sent, but he will be from the inhabitants of the Hellfire.",
  47002: "But those who believe, do righteous deeds [2], and believe in what has been sent down to Muhammad—which is the truth from their Lord—He will expiate their misdeeds and set right their condition. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The Companions relate that while they were sitting with Prophet Muhammad ﷺ in the mosque, a man arrived riding a camel. He caused the camel to kneel within the mosque, tied its foreleg, and then asked, “Which of you is Muhammad?” At that time the Prophet ﷺ was seated among them, leaning on his arm. They replied, “This fair-complexioned man reclining on his arm.”\nThe man addressed him, “O son of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib.” The Prophet ﷺ responded, “I am here.” The man said, “I will question you firmly, so do not be upset.” The Prophet ﷺ replied, “Ask whatever you wish.”\nThe man then asked, “I ask you by your Lord and the Lord of those before you: has Allāh sent you as a messenger to all mankind?” He replied, “By Allāh, yes.” The man continued, “Has Allāh commanded you to perform five prayers each day and night?” He replied, “By Allāh, yes.” He then asked, “Has Allāh commanded you to fast during this month of the year—Ramadan?” He replied, “By Allāh, yes.” Finally, he asked, “Has Allāh commanded you to take alms from our wealthy and distribute them among our poor?” The Prophet ﷺ replied, “By Allāh, yes.” Thereupon the man said, “I believe in all that with which you have been sent, and I am a messenger sent by my people.”\n[3]. The Prophet ﷺ said:\n“My likeness in relation to the prophets before me is that of a man who built a house, perfected it, and beautified it—except for the place of a single brick. People walked around it, admiring its beauty, yet saying, ‘If only this brick were placed.’ I am that brick, and I am the final prophet.”\nThis parable affirms the continuity and completion of prophethood in Muhammad ﷺ. Scholars have drawn thematic parallels between this imagery and Biblical passages that speak of a rejected stone becoming foundational, such as Psalm 118:22 and Gospel of Matthew 21:42, cited by Jesus (peace be upon him).",
  47003: "That is because those who disbelieve follow falsehood, while those who believe follow the truth from their Lord. Thus does Allāh set forth for mankind their examples.",
  47004: "So when you encounter those who disbelieve [in battle], strike the necks until, when you have subdued them thoroughly, bind them firmly. Thereafter, either release them graciously or for ransom, until the war lays down its burdens. That is the command. Had Allāh willed, He could have taken retribution from them Himself, but He does so to test some of you by means of others. And those who are killed in the cause of Allāh —He will never cause their deeds to be lost.",
  47005: "He will guide them and set right their condition.",
  47006: "And admit them into Paradise, which He has made known to them.",
  47007: "O believers, if you support Allāh, He will support you and make your footing firm.",
  47008: "But those who disbelieve—destruction is for them, and He will render their deeds in vain.",
  47009: "That is because they hated what Allāh has sent down, so He rendered their deeds worthless.",
  47010: "Have they not traveled through the land to see how the end of those before them turned out? Allāh destroyed them—and for the disbelievers is something similar.",
  47011: "That is because Allāh is the Protector of those who believe, while the disbelievers have no protector at all.",
  47012: "Indeed, Allāh will admit those who believe and do righteous deeds into Gardens beneath which rivers flow. But those who disbelieve enjoy themselves and eat as cattle eat, and the Fire will be their abode.",
  47013: "How many a town—mightier in strength than your town, which expelled you—have We destroyed, and there was no one to help them.",
  47014: "Is one who is upon a clear proof from his Lord like those whose evil deeds have been made appealing to them and who follow their own desires?",
  47015: "The parable of the Paradise promised to the righteous: therein are rivers of water that never stagnate, rivers of milk whose taste never changes, rivers of wine delightful to drink, and rivers of purified honey, clear and pure. There they will have every kind of fruit, and forgiveness from their Lord. Is one such as this like those who will abide forever in the Fire, and who are given boiling water to drink, tearing apart their bowels?",
  47016: "Among them are those who listen to you, but when they leave your presence, they say to those who have been given knowledge [4], “What did he just say?” Such are the ones whose hearts Allāh has sealed and who follow their own desires.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. From among the companions of the Prophet ﷺ.",
  47017: "But those who are rightly guided—Allah increases them in guidance [5] and grants them their righteousness.\\n\\nFootnotes: [5].  The phrase “increases them in guidance” reflects the Qur’anic principle that guidance is dynamic and cumulative: those who respond positively to truth are granted further clarity, firmness, and insight. The expression “grants them their righteousness” (ātāhum taqwāhum) signifies that Allāh enables them to actualize and preserve their moral consciousness (taqwā), not merely to know what is right, but to live by it consistently. Early exegetes note that this increase is both spiritual—in faith and understanding—and practical—in conduct and perseverance. One of the companions of the Prophet Muhammad reported that the Messenger of God (ﷺ) said to him: \"Say: O Allāh, guide me and keep me steadfast on the right path.\"\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ would also say, “O Allāh, I ask You for guidance, piety, chastity and self- sufficiency.”",
  47018: "Do they then await anything except the Hour, that it should come upon them suddenly? Its signs have already come—so what will their remembrance benefit them once it has come upon them? [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. This verse describes human heedlessness toward the Final Hour (al-sāʿah), emphasizing its sudden and unavoidable arrival. The statement “its signs have already come” refers to the appearance of both minor and major portents of the Last Day. These include: the coming of Prophet Muhammad (ﷺ), the splitting of the moon after the pagans of Makkah challenged the Prophet (ﷺ) which was fulfilled before their eyes, widespread moral decline and social upheaval, as well as the great eschatological signs described in Islamic tradition, such as the emergence of the Antichrist (al-Dajjāl), the descent of Jesus son of Mary, the onslaught of Gog and Magog, the appearance of the Beast that will address humankind, and the rising of the sun from the West.\nThe rhetorical question—“what benefit will their remembrance be?”—highlights a central Qur’anic theme: repentance, reflection, and conscious return to Allāh are meaningful only before the onset of final judgment. Once the Hour arrives, awareness comes too late to effect change, turning remembrance into regret rather than a means of salvation.",
  47019: "So know that there is no god but Allāh [7], and seek forgiveness for your sin and for the believing men and believing women. Allāh knows your movements and your resting place.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. This verse begins with an imperative to knowledge, underscoring that true faith is founded upon conscious understanding rather than blind assertion. The declaration “there is no god worthy of worship except Allāh” expresses the essence of tawḥīd—the absolute oneness of God in divinity, worship, and authority. It negates not the existence of other objects of devotion, but their legitimacy.The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: \"Whoever says: There is no god but Allāh and disbelieves in what is worshiped other than Allāh, his wealth and blood are forbidden, and his reckoning is with Allāh.\"\nWithout faith, it is impossible to please Him. For whoever seeks to draw near to Allāh must believe that He is One—Lord of all, Sovereign, the One upon Whom all depend. He neither begets nor is begotten, and none is equal or comparable to Him.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ once asked a man: “How many gods do you worship today?” He said: “Seven—six on earth and One in the heaven.” \nThe Prophet ﷺ asked: “Which of them do you call upon in fear and hope?” \nThe man replied: “The One in the heaven.” Then the Prophet ﷺ said: “Then leave the six and worship the One in the heaven.\"\nEven polytheists instinctively called upon Allāh alone in times of need and distress.\nThis hadith is a simple yet profound proof of tawḥīd (pure monotheism).\nEven idol-worshippers acknowledged Allāh alone when in fear and hope.\nThe Prophet ﷺ used their natural instinct toward the Creator as evidence for monotheism.\n“The One in the heaven” indicates Allāh’s highness, meaning above His creation, not inside the sky.\nThe fitrah (innate nature) recognizes that only Allāh responds to prayers.\nPolytheism is artificial and learned, while Monotheism is natural and intuitive.\nWhen people face danger, they abandon idols and call only on Allāh.\n\"and seek forgiveness for your sin\",. The command to seek forgiveness, addressed first to the Prophet ﷺ and then extended to believing men and women, affirms both the moral responsibility of the individual and the collective solidarity of the believing community. Early exegetes note that the Prophet’s seeking forgiveness is an act of devotion, humility, and instruction for his followers, rather than a response to wrongdoing.\nThe concluding statement—“Allāh knows well your movements and your resting place”—emphasizes Allāh’s complete and continuous knowledge of human activity, whether in public action or private repose. It serves as both a reassurance to the sincere and a warning to the heedless, reinforcing accountability before Allāh at all times.",
  47020: "The believers say, “Why has a chapter not been sent down?” But when a decisive chapter is revealed and fighting is mentioned in it, you see those in whose hearts is a sickness [of hypocrisy] looking at you like one overcome by death. So what is more fitting for them is—",
  47021: "Obedience and good words. Then, when the matter is firmly resolved, if they had been true to Allāh, it would surely have been better for them.",
  47022: "So would you then, if you turn away [8], cause corruption in the land and sever your ties of kinship? [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. The phrase “if you turn away” (in tawallaytum) is understood by early exegetes in two complementary senses:\nturning away from obedience to God and His Messenger, and withdrawing from moral responsibility once authority, strength, or freedom is attained.\nBoth meanings highlight a recurring Qur’anic warning: that abandonment of divine guidance often leads to social and moral decay, especially when people act without accountability.\n[9]. Ties of kinship (al-arḥām) refer to the God-ordained bonds of family and lineage, whose maintenance is repeatedly emphasized in the Qur’an and Sunnah. Severing these ties includes neglect, injustice, hostility, or deliberate estrangement from relatives. Islamic teachings regard the preservation of kinship as a fundamental moral duty and a pillar of social cohesion, while its violation is counted among the gravest forms of corruption (fasād) in society.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: \"Three (types of people) will not enter Paradise: (1) the habitual wine (alcohol) drinker, (2) the believer in sorcery (and astrology is among it), and (3) the one who severs the ties of kinship.\"\nThe Prophet ﷺ also said, \"Allāh created His creation, and when He finished it, the womb got up and caught hold of Allāh whereupon Allāh said, ‘What is the matter?’ On that, it said, ‘I seek refuge with you from those who sever the ties of kith and kin).’ On that Allâh said, ‘Will you accept (be satisfied) if I bestow My Favours on him who keeps your ties, and withhold My Favours from him who severs your ties?’ On that it said, ‘Yes, O my Lord!’ Then Allāh said, ‘That is for you.’ The narrator added: If you wish, you can recite: \"Would you then, if you were given the authority, do mischief in the land and sever your ties of kinship?\"",
  47023: "Such are the ones whom Allāh has cursed, making them deaf and blinding their sight to the truth.",
  47024: "Do they not then reflect upon the Qur’an, or are there locks upon their hearts? [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. The rhetorical question “are there locks upon their hearts?” employs a powerful metaphor to describe spiritual obstruction. In Qur’anic usage, the heart (qalb) is the center of understanding, moral awareness, and receptivity to truth—not merely the seat of emotion. The image of locks signifies barriers that are largely self-imposed, formed through arrogance, persistent sin, hypocrisy, or the deliberate rejection of guidance. Early exegetes explain that these locks are not arbitrary restraints imposed without cause, but the cumulative result of repeatedly turning away from truth, until the heart becomes sealed against reflection, responsiveness, and transformation.\nBecause intentions and actions flow from the heart, its condition is decisive: when corruption takes root within, it inevitably manifests outwardly in thought, speech, and behavior. The verse therefore establishes a direct link between reflection upon revelation and spiritual openness. A failure to engage the Qur’an thoughtfully is not due to any deficiency or lack of clarity in the message itself, but to inward resistance that prevents its meaning from taking hold.\nThe Qur’an thus stands as one of the most powerful means for nurturing a living relationship with Allāh. Through it, God discloses His guidance so that people may know Him more fully, learn how to live rightly in this world, and find comfort during times of hardship and sorrow. As the Word of Allāh, the Qur’an also serves as a source of protection against moral harm, offering freedom from spiritual bondage, healing for inner wounds, and enduring hope for those who approach it with open and receptive hearts.",
  47025: "Indeed, those who turned back after guidance had become clear to them—Satan enticed them and gave them false hopes.",
  47026: "That is because they say to those who hate what Allāh has sent down, “We will obey you in some matters,” while Allāh knows what they conceal.",
  47027: "So how will it be when the angels take them in death, striking their faces and their backs?",
  47028: "That is because they followed what displeased Allāh and disliked His pleasure, so He rendered their deeds void.",
  47029: "Or do those in whose hearts is a sickness [of hypocrisy] think that Allāh will never bring out their malice?",
  47030: " Had We willed, We could have shown them to you, and you would surely recognize them by their marks; yet you will recognize them by the tone of their speech. Allāh knows all your deeds.",
  47031: "We will surely test you until We make evident those among you who strive and those who are patient, and We will test your deeds.",
  47032: "Indeed, those who disbelieve, turn others away from the way of Allāh, and oppose the Messenger after guidance has become clear to them can never harm Allāh in any way, and He will render their deeds void.",
  47033: "O believers, obey Allāh and obey the Messenger, and do not nullify your deeds. [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. The command “obey Allāh and obey the Messenger” deliberately repeats the verb obey, indicating that obedience to the Messenger is neither secondary nor optional, but an essential extension of obedience to God. According to the Qur’anic, the Messenger conveys, explains, and embodies divine guidance; therefore, obedience to him safeguards sound belief and correct practice.\nThe warning “do not nullify your deeds” emphasizes that righteous actions are not merely performed once and permanently secured. Rather, they can be rendered void through persistent disobedience, hypocrisy, arrogance, or the deliberate rejection of guidance after it has become known. Early exegetes explain that deeds are not nullified by ordinary human error or occasional sin, but by attitudes and actions that undermine sincerity (ikhlāṣ) and faith itself.\nAt the same time, the Qur’an affirms a crucial principle: sins—even major sins—do not erase all of a believer’s good deeds. Nothing nullifies a believer’s deeds entirely except the denial of faith or polytheism, that is, associating partners with Allāh. \nThe verse thus balances warning with reassurance, underscoring moral responsibility, perseverance, and humility, while reminding believers that faith must be preserved through continued obedience and sincere devotion.",
  47034: "Indeed, those who disbelieve and turn others away from the way of Allāh, then die while they are disbelievers—Allāh will never forgive them.",
  47035: "So do not lose heart or seek peace when you have the upper hand, for Allāh is with you [12] and He will never deprive you of your deeds.\\n\\nFootnotes: [12].  The statement “Allāh is with you” affirms divine support, aid, and oversight, not physical proximity. In Qur’anic usage, God’s “being with” believers signifies His assistance, protection, guidance, and the strengthening of resolve—especially in moments of trial, struggle, or confrontation. The verse addresses a situation of moral and strategic advantage, warning against weakness or premature compromise that arises from fear or doubt rather than justice and principle.\nThe assurance that “He will never diminish your deeds” reinforces a foundational Qur’anic teaching: steadfastness and sincere effort are never wasted before God. Even when worldly outcomes appear uncertain or delayed, divine justice ensures that no righteous action is reduced, lost, or overlooked. Every act of faithfulness is preserved in full measure.\nTaken together, the verse balances resolve with reassurance. It calls believers to perseverance without arrogance and courage without aggression, grounding strength not in self-reliance, but in trust in God’s presence, fairness, and unfailing support. The believer is thus encouraged to remain firm, unafraid, and undiscouraged, confident that divine aid accompanies those who walk the path of truth.",
  47036: "The life of this world is only play and amusement. But if you believe [13] and are mindful of Allāh, He will give you your rewards and will not ask you for your wealth. [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. Belief (īmān) in Allāh and His messenger Muhammad is not mere intellectual assent, but a conscious commitment of heart, word, and action. It entails trust in Allāh, acceptance of His guidance, and responsiveness to moral responsibility. Here, belief is paired with mindfulness of God (taqwā), emphasizing that true faith manifests through ethical restraint, obedience, and awareness of divine accountability.\n[14]. The assurance that Allāh “will not demand your wealth” does not negate obligations such as charity or almsgiving, but affirms that Allāh does not burden believers with excessive or exhaustive demands. Early exegetes explain that Allāh requires only what is reasonable and purifying, not the total surrender of one’s possessions. The verse reassures believers that divine commands are rooted in mercy and justice: wealth is a trust to be used responsibly, not a test meant to impoverish or exploit. God’s reward, therefore, far exceeds what is asked in return.",
  47037: "If He were to ask you for it and press you, you would become miserly, and He would bring out your resentment.",
  47038: "Here you are—being called to spend in the cause of Allāh —yet among you are those who are stingy. Whoever is stingy is only stingy against himself. Allāh is Free of all need, while you are the needy. If you turn away, He will replace you with another people; then they will not be like you.",
  48001: "Indeed, We have granted you [O Prophet] a clear victory. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. I.e., the Treaty of Hudaybiyyah.\nThe Arabic expression fatḥan mubīnan, commonly translated as “a clear conquest” or “a manifest victory,” literally means “a clear opening.” In Qur’anic usage, fatḥ denotes not only military conquest but any decisive breakthrough granted by God, including political, social, or spiritual success. This verse was revealed following the Treaty of Hudaybiyyah, an agreement that appeared unfavorable to the Muslims at the time but ultimately led to the peaceful expansion of Islam and the eventual conquest of Mecca. The verse thus reframes victory as divine strategy rather than immediate outward triumph.",
  48002: "So that Allāh may forgive you your past and future sins [2], complete His favor upon you, and guide you to a straight path.\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. i.e., in worldly misjudgements, not in the conveyance of God's Message.\nThe phrase “that Allāh may forgive you for your past and your future sins ” has been understood by the scholars in a manner consistent with the Prophet Muḥammad’s moral infallibility (ʿiṣmah). The Arabic word dhanb (ذنب), while often translated “sin,” can also denote a shortcoming, burden, or that which is blamed, and in this context is interpreted as either (1) matters perceived as faults by opponents, (2) actions that occurred prior to prophethood, or (3) minor, non-deliberate matters that carry no moral culpability. The verse emphasizes divine reassurance, the completion of God’s favor, and firm guidance upon the straight path (Islam), rather than implying actual wrongdoing. It highlights God’s protection, approval, and continual guidance of the Prophet following the “clear conquest” mentioned in the previous verse.",
  48003: "And that Allāh may grant you a mighty victory.",
  48004: "He is the One who sent down tranquility into the hearts of the believers, so that they may increase in faith upon their faith [3]. To Allāh belong the hosts of the heavens and the earth. Allāh is Ever All-Knowing, All-Wise.\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. The phrase “that they may increase in faith along with their faith” affirms the Qur’anic principle that faith (īmān) is capable of increase and strengthening. \nThe scholars holds that īmān (faith) increases through obedience, certainty, and divine reassurance, and diminishes through heedlessness, sins, without negating its essential presence. The “increase” here is widely understood as growth in inner conviction, steadfastness, tranquility (sakīnah), and clarity, particularly in moments of trial. In the context of the Treaty of Hudaybiyyah, this increase in faith reflects the believers’ deepened trust in Allāh’s wisdom despite outward circumstances that initially appeared unfavorable.\nLet us fix our eyes on Allāh, the Creator of universe. When our attention and gaze are on Allāh, He will build our faith. So be encouraged and let your faith arise by reading the Qur’an and by performing righteous deeds. May Allāh support us, may his enemies be scattered; may his foes flee before Him. Allāh cares for his people. Our enemies are scattered when Allāh helps us. Allāh is promising us that the enemy that oppressed us, the enemy that took our joy away is going to be scattered in His name.",
  48005: "So that He may admit believing men and women into Gardens beneath which rivers flow, abiding therein forever, and expiate their misdeeds; and that is, with Allāh, a supreme triumph.",
  48006: "And that He may punish the hypocrite men and women and the polytheist men and women—those who hold evil assumptions about Allāh. Upon them is the evil turn of fate. Allāh’s anger is upon them, and He has cursed them and prepared Hell for them. What a wretched destination.",
  48007: "To Allāh belong the hosts of the heavens and the earth, and Allāh is Ever All-Mighty, All-Wise.",
  48008: "Indeed, We have sent you [O Prophet] [4] as a witness, a bearer of good news, and a warner.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. This Verse appears also in the Torah Isaiah 42:1 to 12, thus: \"Verily We have sent you (Muhammad) as a witness, as a bringer of glad tidings and as a warner, and as a protector for the nations. You are my slave and My messenger, and I have named you Al-Mutawakkil (one who depends upon God). You are neither hard-hearted nor of fierce character, nor one who shouts in the markets. You do not return evil for evil, but excuse and forgive. Allāh (God) will not take you unto Him till He guides through you a crocked nation on the right path by causing them to say: \"None has the right to be worshipped but Allāh.\" With such a statement He will cause to open blind eyes, deaf ears and hardened hearts.\"\nThe Isaianic passage describes a servant chosen by God, gentle in character, tasked with establishing justice, guiding nations, bringing people from darkness to light, opposing idolatry, and being associated with the descendants of Kedar (Isaiah 42:11), traditionally identified in Arab genealogy as linked to Ishmael. Scholars have understood these features—together with references to a new proclamation of praise, universal guidance, and moral reform—as corresponding to the prophetic mission of Muḥammad ﷺ.",
  48009: "So that you may believe in Allāh and His Messenger, support and honor him, and glorify Him morning and evening. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The pronoun “him” in “support and honor him” refers to the Messenger, indicating the obligation to aid, defend, respect, and uphold the prophetic mission. The final clause, “and glorify [Allāh] morning and evening,” refers exclusively to God, as glorification (tasbīḥ) is an act of worship due to Allāh alone. Scholars note that the verse deliberately distinguishes between reverence shown to the Messenger and worship directed to Allāh, affirming both prophetic authority and strict monotheism. The sequence reflects the Qur’anic pattern in which faith in Allāh is expressed through obedience and support of His Messenger, while devotion and glorification remain solely for Allāh.\nTo glorify Allāh is not to bestow glory on Allāh or add to His glory, but to recognize and acknowledge His glory. That is to praise Him with your lips, to obey His Word, to pray in Allah’s name only, to perform good deeds, and remain sexual pure by fleeing sexual immorality, glorifying Allāh with your body, to seek the good of others doing everything for the glory of Allāh, to give generously, giving to the needy, to live honorably, letting your light shine before others, so that they may see your good works and give glory to Allāh in heaven, to be faithful when persecuted, and to face death with faith.",
  48010: "Indeed, those who pledge allegiance to you [O Prophet] [6] are in fact pledging allegiance to Allāh. The Hand of Allāh is above their hands. So whoever breaks [his pledge] breaks it only against himself, and whoever fulfills what he has covenanted with Allāh—He will give him a great reward.\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. The Qur’an and authentic Sunnah affirm that Allāh has Hands, as stated explicitly in revelation, while categorically denying any resemblance to created beings. As Allāh declares: “There is nothing like unto Him” (Qur’an 42:11). Therefore we affirm what Allāh affirmed for Himself, including the attribute of two Hands, without asking how, without likening them to creation, without distortion, and without denial.\nReferences to the “Hand of Allāh ” thus signify a real divine attribute, the true nature of which is known only to Allāh, while also conveying meanings of power, generosity, authority, mercy, and care, as understood from context. When believers are called to place their trust in Allāh, they do so by affirming His attributes as revealed, relying upon Him alone, and following the example of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ in complete reliance (tawakkul), knowing that divine attributes are affirmed without comparison, imagination, or embodiment.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: “Allāh spreads out His hand at night to accept the repentance of the one who sinned during the day, and He spreads out His hand by day to accept the repentance of the one who sinned during the night, (and that will continue) until the sun rises from the west.” So we should put our trust in Him and not look to those less powerful. Whatever we face as we look toward the future, we can follow the prophet Muhammad's example and place our hope and trust in Allāh.",
  48011: "The desert Arabs who remained behind will say to you [O Prophet], ‘Our wealth and our families kept us busy, so ask forgiveness for us.’ They say with their tongues what is not in their hearts. Say, ‘Who then has any power for you against Allāh, if He intends harm for you or benefit for you?’ Rather, Allāh is Ever Aware of what you do.",
  48012: "Rather, you assumed that the Messenger and the believers would never return to their families, and that was made appealing in your hearts; and you harbored an evil assumption [7], and you were a people doomed to destruction.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. The “evil thought” refers to assuming that Allāh would abandon His Messenger ﷺ and the believers—expecting defeat, destruction, or divine neglect. This was not mere pessimism but a moral failure of trust (tawakkul) and a denial of Allāh's wisdom and promise. Such assumptions were “made fair-seeming” to their hearts, meaning they rationalized fear and self-interest until it appeared justified. The verse concludes by describing the consequence of this mindset: spiritual ruin and loss, not because of external events, but due to corrupt inner convictions.",
  48013: "Whoever does not believe in Allāh and His Messenger—indeed, We have prepared for the disbelievers a blazing Fire.",
  48014: "To Allāh alone belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth. He forgives whom He wills and punishes whom He wills. Allāh is Ever-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  48015: "Those who remained behind will say, when you set out to take the spoils, “Let us follow you.” They wish to change the words of Allāh. Say, “Never shall you follow us; thus has Allāh said before.” Then they will say, “Rather, you are jealous of us.” Rather, they understand not, except a little. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. “They want to change the Words of Allāh … they understand not except a little” \nThe phrase “to change the Words of Allāh ” refers to an attempt to circumvent a prior divine decree by seeking participation in benefit after having avoided responsibility. Scholars explain that this request did not arise from sincere repentance, but from self-interest once material gain became apparent. Their failure to comprehend the justice of God’s decree—described as “they understand not except a little”—is attributed to spiritual blindness resulting from persistent sin, hypocrisy, and insincerity, which harden the heart and impair moral discernment. Thus, their misunderstanding was not merely intellectual, but the consequence of hearts rendered unreceptive to guidance through their own actions.",
  48016: "Say to the desert Arabs who remained behind, “You will soon be called to face a people of great might: you will fight them, unless they submit. If you obey, Allāh will grant you a good reward; but if you turn away as you turned away before, He will punish you with a painful punishment.”",
  48017: "There is no blame upon the blind, nor upon the lame, nor upon the sick. Whoever obeys Allāh and His Messenger—He will admit him into Gardens beneath which rivers flow; but whoever turns away—He will punish him with a painful punishment.",
  48018: "Allāh was indeed pleased with the believers when they pledged allegiance to you [O Prophet] under the tree [9]. He knew what was in their hearts, so He sent down tranquility upon them and rewarded them with a near victory.\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. The pledge under the tree refers to the Pledge of al-Ḥudaybiyyah (Bayʿat al-Riḍwān), during which the believers pledged loyalty to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ at a moment of apparent danger and uncertainty. God’s declaration of pleasure signifies His acceptance of their sincerity, courage, and steadfast intention, not merely the outward act of pledging. The verse highlights that divine reward is tied to the inner truthfulness of the heart, which is why tranquility (sakīnah) was granted before victory. The “near victory” is understood by the majority of exegetes to refer to the conquest of Khaybar, which followed shortly thereafter.\nIn later generations, some people began visiting the site of the tree itself. When ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb saw people treating the tree with reverence, he feared that it could become a source of innovation or idolatry, and therefore ordered it to be cut down. This action reflects the Companions’ strict commitment to preserving pure monotheism (tawḥīd). The Prophet ﷺ strongly warned against assigning sanctity to physical objects such as trees or places, likening such practices to the beginnings of idol-making. Thus, honor in Islam is attached to obedience and faith, not to locations or material objects once associated with righteous events.",
  48019: "He has promised them many spoils of war that they will gain. And Allāh is Ever-Mighty, All-Wise.",
  48020: "Allāh has promised you many spoils that you will gain. He has hastened this for you and restrained the hands of people from you [10], so that it may be a sign for the believers and that He may guide you to a straight path.\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. This is widely understood to refer to the conquest of Khaybar, granted soon after al-Ḥudaybiyyah, serving as a sign that patience and obedience bring swift divine aid.\nOn the day (of the battle) of Khaibar the Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"Tomorrow I will give the flag to somebody who will be given victory (by Allāh) and who loves Allāh and His messenger and is loved by Allāh and His messenger.\" So, the people wondered all that night as to who would receive the flag and in the morning everyone hoped that he would be that person. Allah's Messenger (ﷺ) asked, \"Where is `Ali?\" He was told that `Ali was suffering from eye-trouble, so he applied saliva to his eyes and invoked Allāh to cure him. He at once got cured as if he had no ailment. The Prophet (ﷺ) gave him the flag. `Ali said, \"Should I fight them till they become like us (i.e. Muslim)?\" The Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"Go to them patiently and calmly till you enter the land. Then, invite them to Islam, and inform them what is enjoined upon them, for, by Allāh, if Allāh gives guidance to somebody through you, it is better for you than possessing red camels (which the Arabs considered the most valuable wealth at that time).",
  48021: "And other gains you have not yet been able to attain—Allāh has already encompassed them. Allāh is over all things All-Powerful.",
  48022: "If the disbelievers were to fight you, they would certainly turn their backs; then they would find neither protector nor helper.",
  48023: "Such is the way of Allāh that has passed before; and you will never find in the way of Allāh any change.",
  48024: "He is the One who restrained their hands from you and your hands from them in the valley of Makkah, after He had given you victory over them [11]. Allāh is Ever-Seeing of what you do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. This verse refers to events surrounding the Treaty of al-Ḥudaybiyyah, when Allāh restrained both the Muslims and the Quraysh from engaging in open fighting near Mecca, despite moments of tension and the believers’ ability to prevail. Some reports mention that a group of approximately eighty armed men from the Quraysh attempted a surprise attack on the Muslims near Mecca. They were captured without resistance and subsequently released by the Prophet ﷺ, demonstrating restraint and mercy.\nThe “victory” mentioned in the verse is therefore understood in two complementary ways: first, as this immediate tactical success in neutralizing an attack without bloodshed; and second, as a moral and strategic victory, manifested through restraint, divine protection, and the favorable outcome of the treaty itself rather than a full military confrontation. By preventing bloodshed within the sacred precincts, Allāh displayed His wisdom, transforming a situation of apparent conflict into enduring peace and long-term success. The verse underscores that true victory may lie in restraint when guided by divine command, and affirms that Allāh remains fully aware of all actions and intentions.",
  48025: "They are the ones who disbelieved, barred you from the Sacred Mosque, and prevented the sacrificial animals from reaching their place of sacrifice. Had it not been for believing men and women whom you did not know—whom you might have harmed unknowingly [12], thus incurring blame because of them. So that Allāh might admit into His mercy whom He wills. Had they been clearly separated one from another, We would surely have punished those who disbelieved among them with a painful punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. We would have let you march to Makkah.\nThis verse explains that one reason fighting was restrained at al-Ḥudaybiyyah was the presence of unknown believing men and women living among the Quraysh in Mecca. Had hostilities occurred, these concealed believers might have been harmed unintentionally, causing moral guilt and communal harm. God’s restraint thus served a protective mercy, safeguarding innocent believers while allowing time for faith to emerge openly. The verse demonstrates that divine wisdom may delay punishment not out of weakness, but out of mercy and justice, and that God’s decree accounts for hidden realities unknown to human judgment.",
  48026: "When those who disbelieved placed in their hearts rage—the rage of ignorance [13]—Allāh sent down His tranquility upon His Messenger and upon the believers, and made them adhere to the word of piety [14]; and they were most deserving of it and worthy of it. Allāh is Knowing of all things.\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. This verse refers to the occasion when the pagan Quraysh of Mecca prevented the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ and the believers from entering the Sacred Mosque to perform ʿUmrah. The Quraysh feared that allowing the Muslims to enter Mecca would be perceived by the Arab tribes as a humiliation and a sign of weakness. Although the believers were temporarily barred, Allāh affirmed that the Prophet’s vision was true and would be fulfilled at the appointed time. What appeared as a delay was in fact part of divine wisdom, culminating in later safe entry into Mecca and the strengthening of Islam.\n[14]. The “word of piety” is widely understood to refer to the declaration of faith (shahādah): “There is no deity worthy of worship except Allāh, and Muhammad is the Messenger of Allāh.” This statement embodies pure monotheism (tawḥīd), God-consciousness (taqwā), and steadfast obedience. Adherence to this word distinguished the believers from the arrogance of pre-Islamic ignorance and made them worthy of divine tranquility, guidance, and honor.",
  48027: "Indeed, Allāh has fulfilled the vision of His Messenger in truth: you will surely enter the Sacred Mosque, if Allāh wills, in security, with your heads shaved or your hair cut short, not fearing [15]. So He knew what you did not know, and He appointed besides that a near victory. [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. Shaving the head or shortening the hair (ḥalq or taqṣīr) is among the required rites marking the completion of pilgrimage (ʿUmrah or Ḥajj). This act symbolizes humility, obedience, and the formal conclusion of the sacred state (iḥrām), signifying that the worshipper has successfully fulfilled the prescribed rites in submission to Allāh.\n[16] The “near victory” refers to events that preceded the fulfillment of the Prophet’s vision of entering Mecca for pilgrimage. It is commonly understood to include the Treaty of al-Ḥudaybiyyah, which secured peace and recognition for the Muslims, followed shortly by the conquest of Khaybar. These events served as tangible confirmations of divine support and reassurance that the vision granted to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ would be fulfilled at its appointed time, demonstrating that apparent delay was in fact part of God’s wise plan.",
  48028: "He is the One who sent His Messenger with guidance and the religion of truth, that He may make it prevail over all religion.\nAllāh is sufficient as a Witness.",
  48029: "Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allāh [17]. Those who are with him are firm against the disbelievers, yet compassionate among themselves [18]. You see them bowing and prostrating, seeking Allāh’s grace and pleasure. Their mark is on their faces from the trace of prostration. That is their description in the Torah [19]; and their description in the Gospel [20] is like a plant that sends forth its shoots, strengthens them, grows stout, and stands firm upon its stem—delighting the farmers—so that Allāh may enrage through them the disbelievers.\nAllāh has promised those among them who believe and do righteous deeds forgiveness and a great reward.\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. There is much to learn from the character, manners, and etiquette of the final Messenger of Allāh to mankind, Muhammad ﷺ. He exemplified the highest standards of humility, kindness, and justice. He treated all believers equally, respected every individual, and openly expressed love and concern for his companions. He would ask about those who were absent, visit the sick, and care for the vulnerable. His speech was gentle, eloquent, and comprehensive—conveying deep meaning with beautiful clarity. His words captured hearts and minds, and even his opponents acknowledged his integrity, wisdom, and noble character.\n[18]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ emphasized mercy as a foundational principle of faith. He said: “He who does not show mercy to people, Allāh will not show mercy to him.” This teaching highlights that divine mercy is closely linked to showing mercy to others. He also reported that Allāh said: “My love is assured for those who love one another for My sake, meet one another for My sake, visit one another for My sake, and give to one another for My sake.” These teachings underscore the importance of compassion, forgiveness, brotherhood, and selfless care within the believing community.\n[19]. This phrase is referring to earlier scriptural descriptions of righteous servants of God found in the Hebrew Bible, which emphasize devotion, humility, and closeness to God. References such as Deuteronomy 33:13 and Numbers 16:22 highlight God’s favor upon His chosen servants and His knowledge of the hearts and souls of humankind, themes that align with the Qur’anic portrayal of the believers who followed the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ.\n[20]. The parable of gradual growth mirrors imagery found in the Gospels, such as Mark 4:26–29 and Matthew 13:31–39, where those followers are likened to a seed that grows steadily from small beginnings into something strong and established. This imagery symbolizes the believing community that began modestly, grew through faith and perseverance, and became firmly rooted—bringing joy to the faithful while frustrating those who reject the truth.",
  49001: "O believers, do not put yourselves ahead of Allāh and His Messenger [1]. And fear Allāh. Indeed, Allāh is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. Rather, believers are commanded to seek judgment, decree, and guidance from Allāh and His Messenger, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, and to show complete obedience to them. This verse establishes the foundational principle of proper conduct (adab) toward Allāh and His Messenger, prohibiting believers from placing personal opinion, preference, or initiative before divine revelation in matters of faith, law, and guidance. This includes refraining from speaking, deciding, or acting in religious matters without sound knowledge rooted in revelation. The verse concludes by reminding believers that Allāh is All-Hearing and All-Knowing, fully aware of both outward speech and inward intention.",
  49002: "O believers, do not raise your voices above the voice of the Prophet, nor speak loudly to him as you speak loudly to one another [2], lest your deeds be nullified while you perceive not.\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. This verse establishes reverence, humility, and discipline in the presence of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. It teaches that respect for the Messenger is not merely a matter of etiquette, but an essential expression of faith, the neglect of which may undermine one’s good deeds without a person even realizing it. Raising one’s voice, addressing the Prophet casually, or speaking to him as one speaks to others reflects a lack of proper adab (conduct) and diminishes the spiritual weight of actions, even in the absence of ill intention.\nThis principle reflects a broader Islamic ethic: order, restraint, and dignity in speech and behavior. One who guards the tongue protects oneself from harm, while gentleness, respect, and avoidance of arrogance are signs of true piety. Showing courtesy to elders, honoring people of knowledge, and maintaining respectful conduct toward those in authority—so long as it is in what is right—are all manifestations of reverence for Allāh.\nThe Prophet ﷺ emphasized this value when he said: “It is part of glorifying Allāh to honor the grey-haired believer, the bearer of the Qur’an who neither exaggerates nor neglects it, and to honor the just ruler.” Thus, respect shown to God’s Messenger and to others in rightful positions is, in reality, respect shown to Allāh Himself.",
  49003: "Indeed, those who lower their voices in the presence of Allāh’s Messenger are the ones whose hearts Allāh has tested for righteousness. For them is forgiveness and a great reward.",
  49004: "Indeed, those who call out to you from behind the chambers—most of them do not understand. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. This denotes not merely intellectual understanding, but sound judgment, restraint, and proper conduct. The verse censures the callers’ lack of decorum and social awareness, not their intelligence, in addressing the Prophet ﷺ loudly from outside his private chambers. Thus, the verse constitutes a clear rebuke of improper conduct and behavior.\nLove, therefore, is not limited to gentle words of affirmation, gifts, or financial provision. True love also includes firm and open rebuke when necessary. Offences are inevitable among people who live together in love. When an offence occurs once—or even a second time—it may and often should be forgiven and consciously overlooked. However, when wrongful behavior becomes repetitive, forgiveness alone is insufficient; open rebuke becomes necessary. Why? Because if a person is never confronted about their wrongdoing, they may continue in it, assuming there is nothing wrong with their attitude. We all possess character flaws, weaknesses, and shortcomings. Yet as people commanded by Allāh to live in mutual love, we must also be courageous enough to correct one another. This does not mean exposing or humiliating an offender before multitudes. Rather, when one is wronged, the Prophetic teaching is to inform the person privately of their fault. If they refuse to accept the correction, others may then be involved. The goal is reformation, not embarrassment. Forgiveness is indeed vital, but open rebuke is sometimes essential for lasting change in character. Some individuals will not abandon persistent wrongdoing unless they are confronted with it. Therefore, speak the truth in love—not to vent anger or assert superiority, but to help your brother or sister improve as a Muslim.\nThe former approach leads to the pleasure and glorification of Allāh; the latter merely reveals selfishness. Thus, walk in love with one another—a love that forgives, corrects, and reforms.",
  49005: "Had they been patient until you came out to them, it would have been better for them. Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. This verse follows the rebuke of improper conduct with a moral correction tempered by mercy. It teaches that patience and respect for boundaries are signs of sound judgment and good character. Yet, despite the fault committed, Allāh closes the passage by emphasizing His attributes of forgiveness and mercy, indicating that sincere repentance and improved conduct remain open to those who err.\nThe pairing of rebuke with mercy reinforces a central ethical principle in Islam: correction is meant to reform, not to condemn. Even when behavior warrants admonition, the door to forgiveness is never closed for those who are willing to learn, change, and return to what is better.",
  49006: "O believers, if a wicked person comes to you with news, verify it, lest you harm a people out of ignorance and then become regretful for what you have done.",
  49007: "Know that among you is Allāh’s Messenger. If he were to obey you in many matters, you would surely fall into hardship. But Allāh has endeared faith to you and made it pleasing to your hearts, and He has made ingratitude, wickedness, and disobedience hateful to you. Such are the rightly guided—",
  49008: "a bounty from Allāh and a blessing. \nAllāh is All-Knowing, All-Wise.",
  49009: "If two groups of believers fight one another, then make peace between them [5]. But if one of them transgresses against the other, fight the one that transgresses until it returns to the command of Allāh.\nThen if it returns, make peace between them with justice and act equitably. Indeed, Allāh loves those who act equitably.\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. Even after conflict, reconciliation must be carried out with fairness and equity, prohibiting revenge or partiality. Justice remains obligatory even toward a former aggressor.\nCooperating with fellow believers in prayer and service—according to how Allāh guides and empowers—enables us to accomplish far more together than we ever could alone. As it is often said, “Individually, we are one drop; together, we are an ocean.” Through Allāh’s empowerment and guidance, believers can work collectively to restore peace and advance the message of Islam, seeking His honor rather than our own.",
  49010: "The believers are but brothers [6]; so make peace between your brothers, and be mindful of Allāh, so that you may be shown mercy.\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. The declaration that believers are brothers establishes fraternity as a legal and ethical foundation of the Muslim community, making reconciliation and God-consciousness essential prerequisites for receiving divine mercy.\nBelievers are therefore called to be affectionate toward one another with brotherly love, giving preference to one another in honor, as those chosen by Allāh—righteous and beloved—clothed with mercy, kindness, humility, gentleness, and patience, bearing with one another and forgiving one another.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“The believer is a mirror to his believing brother. He protects him from loss and defends him in his absence.”\nTo be a mirror is not merely to affirm, but also to reflect truthfully. If a believer sees something wrong in his brother, he corrects him—not to shame or dominate, but to reform and preserve the bond of brotherhood. In this way, mutual correction, forgiveness, and compassion become expressions of faith and means of safeguarding the unity of the community.",
  49011: "O believers, let not some men mock others [7]—perhaps they are better than them—nor let some women mock other women—perhaps they are better than them. And do not defame one another, nor call one another by offensive nicknames. Wretched is the name of wickedness after faith. Whoever does not repent—then they who are the wrongdoers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. Mockery (sukhriyya) includes ridicule, sarcasm, belittling speech, gestures, or tones intended to demean others. The prohibition applies equally to men and women, emphasizing universal moral accountability. The warning “perhaps they are better than them” dismantles all claims to moral or spiritual superiority, reminding believers that true worth is known only to Allāh. Such behavior contradicts faith because it breeds arrogance, fractures brotherhood, and undermines the dignity that Allāh has granted to every believer. Repentance is therefore required, as persistence in mockery constitutes wrongdoing rather than a trivial social fault.\nScripture and Prophetic teaching further warn against traits that corrode communal harmony. Among the actions detested by Allāh are: looking down on others, lying, shedding innocent blood, devising wicked\nplans, hastening toward evil, giving false testimony, and sowing discord among brothers. Mockery often serves as a gateway to these vices, making its prohibition a safeguard for both individual character and communal unity.",
  49012: "O believers, avoid much suspicion; indeed, some suspicion is sin. And do not spy, nor backbite one another [8]. Would any of you like to eat the flesh of his dead brother? You would surely detest it.\nSo be mindful of Allāh. Indeed, Allāh is Accepting of repentance, Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. Backbiting (ghībah) refers to mentioning about a person—whether true or false—what they would dislike to be said in their absence. The Qur’an likens backbiting to eating the flesh of one’s dead brother, for just as the dead do not feel the pain when their flesh is consumed, the absent person does not immediately perceive the harm done to his honor. Yet the act remains morally repulsive and grievous before Allāh.\nBackbiting differs from sincere counsel or necessary warning, which are permitted when motivated by justice, protection from harm, or genuine reform, and when carried out without malice, exaggeration, or public shaming. Spying (tajassus) refers to prying into private matters or actively seeking out faults that Allāh has concealed. Together, suspicion, spying, and backbiting corrupt the heart, violate trust, and erode brotherhood. The graphic imagery employed in the verse underscores the severe moral ugliness of these sins and their destructive effect on communal bonds. Repentance is therefore emphasized, as such behaviors often become habitual, yet remain curable through God-conscious restraint and sincere reform.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“O you who have believed with your tongues while faith has not yet entered your hearts, do not backbite people and do not seek out their faults. Whoever seeks out the faults of others, Allāh will seek out his faults, and whoever Allāh seeks out his faults, He will expose him—even in his own home.”\nHe ﷺ also said:\n“Whoever conceals the faults of his brother, Allāh will conceal his faults on the Day of Resurrection. And whoever exposes the faults of his brother, Allāh will expose his faults—even those committed in the privacy of his home.”",
  49013: "O mankind, indeed We created you from a male and a female [9], and made you into nations and tribes so that you may come to know one another. Indeed, the most noble of you in the sight of Allāh is the most mindful of Him [10]. Truly, Allāh is All-Knowing, All-Aware.\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]). “From a male and a female”—that is, Adam and Eve—affirms the shared origin of all humanity, dismantling any claim of inherent superiority based on race, lineage, ethnicity, or social status. Human diversity into nations and tribes is presented not as a basis for pride or division, but as a means of mutual recognition, cooperation, and social organization. The verse thus establishes equality in origin while acknowledging diversity in identity.\n[10]. “The one who has the most taqwā among you”—that is, the one most distinguished by God-consciousness, piety, and reverent fear of Allāh. The phrase “in the sight of Allāh” shifts the standard of honor from human judgment to divine evaluation, which alone is fully informed and perfectly just. Nobility is therefore measured by taqwā—a moral quality rooted in sincerity, obedience, and restraint—rather than outward markers such as wealth, power, ethnicity, or ancestry. By concluding with All-Knowing, All-Aware, the verse affirms that true virtue may be hidden from people but is never hidden from Allāh.",
  49014: "[Some of] the desert Arabs said, :We have believed.\" Say, \"You have not yet believed; rather, say, “We have submitted,” for faith has not yet entered your hearts. But if you obey Allāh and His Messenger, He will not diminish anything of your deeds. Indeed, Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\"",
  49015: "The believers are only those who believe in Allāh and His Messenger, then do not doubt, and who strive with their wealth and their lives in the cause of Allāh. Those are the ones who are truthful. [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. “Truthful” (al-ṣādiqūn) denotes those whose profession of faith is confirmed by unwavering conviction and self-sacrificing action. Truthfulness here is not merely verbal sincerity, but the harmony of belief, intention, and conduct. The absence of doubt refers to settled commitment rather than the absence of intellectual inquiry, while striving with wealth and life signifies the willingness to place one’s resources and very self in the service of Allāh’s cause. Such people are deemed truthful because their faith is proven by endurance, obedience, and sacrifice, not by claim alone.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“If you possess four qualities, you will not grieve over what you miss of this world: fulfilling trusts, truthful speech, good character, and lawful earning—consuming only what is lawful and good, and abstaining from what is unlawful and corrupt.”",
  49016: "Say, \"Do you inform Allāh of your faith, while Allāh knows whatever is in the heavens and whatever is in the earth? And Allāh is All-Knowing of everything.”",
  49017: "They consider it a favor to you that they have submitted. Say, “Do not consider your submission a favor to me. Rather, it is Allāh who has conferred a favor upon you by guiding you to faith—if you are indeed truthful.”",
  49018: "Indeed, Allāh knows the unseen of the heavens and the earth. Allāh is Ever-Seeing of what you do. [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. This verse closes Sūrat al-Ḥujurāt by returning every claim, action, and intention to divine knowledge and sight. After correcting outward speech, inward assumptions, social conduct, and claims of faith, the sūrah ends by affirming that nothing—whether hidden belief or visible deed—escapes Allāh’s awareness. True faith, therefore, is lived before Allāh, not performed for people.",
  50001: "Qāf [1]. By the glorious Qur’an [2].\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See the footnote Qur’an chapter 2: v 1 regarding the disconnected letters (ḥurūf muqaṭṭaʿāt), whose precise meaning is known only to Allāh. They serve as a rhetorical marker, drawing attention to the divine origin and inimitability of the Qur’an.\n[2]. The oath “By the Glorious Qur’an” introduces the central message that follows: despite the denials of the disbelievers, the Messenger ﷺ has indeed been sent to warn humanity of resurrection and judgment. The description al-majīd (“Glorious”) emphasizes the Qur’an’s exalted authority, truthfulness, and decisive role in affirming life after death and accountability before Allāh.",
  50002: "Rather, they wonder that there has come to them a warner from among themselves; so the disbelievers say, \"This is a strange thing!",
  50003: "What! When we have died and become dust—shall we be resurrected? That is a far return.",
  50004: "We already know what the earth consumes of them, and with Us is a preserving record.",
  50005: "Rather, they denied the truth when it came to them, so they are in a state of confusion.",
  50006: "Do they not look at the sky above them—how We built it and adorned it, and it has no rifts?",
  50007: "And We spread out the earth, and set upon it firm mountains, and caused to grow in it every kind of pleasing plant,",
  50008: "as an insight and a reminder for every servant who turns back in devotion. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. “Who turns back in devotion” translates munīb, which denotes one who repeatedly returns to Allāh with humility, repentance, and reflection. It implies an inward disposition of responsiveness rather than mere outward belief. The signs in creation serve as insight (tabṣirah) and reminder (dhikrā) only for such hearts—those willing to reflect, repent, and realign themselves with divine guidance. Thus, guidance is not withheld, but received according to one’s openness and readiness to return.",
  50009: "And We send down blessed water from the sky [4], and by it We cause gardens to grow and grain for harvest,\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. “Blessed water” (māʾan mubārakan) denotes rain endowed with benefit, increase, and life-giving power. In the Qur’anic argument, rainfall functions as a visible sign of revival after lifelessness, illustrating how Allāh brings forth gardens and sustenance from barren land. This recurring imagery prepares the mind to accept resurrection, demonstrating that the restoration of life after decay is already witnessed repeatedly in creation. The blessing lies not merely in abundance, but in purposeful renewal and provision.\nProphet Muhammad ﷺ once led the morning prayer at Ḥudaybiyah after rainfall during the night. Turning to the people, he said: “Do you know what your Lord has said?” They replied, “Allāh and His Messenger know best.”\nHe [Allāh] said: “Some of My servants entered the morning as believers and some as unbelievers. Whoever said, ‘We received rain by the blessing and mercy of Allāh,’ is a believer in Me and a disbeliever in the stars. But whoever said, ‘We received rain because of such-and-such star,’ has disbelieved in Me and believed in the stars.”\nThis narration teaches that natural blessings are not independent causes, but manifestations of divine mercy. Allāh causes His sun to rise upon both the righteous and the wicked, and sends rain upon believers and unbelievers alike. Such universal provision reflects the vastness of Allāh’s grace, which sustains all creation regardless of gratitude or denial.\nYet this mercy does not negate accountability. While all benefit from Allāh’s generosity—air to breathe, sustenance to live—every soul will stand before Him for judgment, to receive recompense for what it has earned, whether good or evil. Glory and honor belong to Allāh alone.",
  50010: "and towering palm trees with clustered, layered fruit [5],\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The Arabic phrase ṭalʿun naḍīd refers to the early fruit clusters of the date palm (ṭalʿ), described as naḍīd—closely packed, layered, or arranged one upon another. The expression evokes both abundance and order: dates forming dense, neatly stacked clusters along the tall palm, emphasizing fertility, provision, and deliberate design rather than mere quantity.\nThis imagery resonates with broader scriptural symbolism. The righteous are likened to the palm tree—steadfast, fruitful, and enduring—bearing fruit even in old age, remaining fresh and green, and proclaiming the uprightness and goodness of Allāh, from whom all blessings flow. Likewise, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ once asked his companions to identify a tree whose leaves never fall and which resembles a believer. When he revealed the answer, he said: “It is the date-palm.” The palm thus stands as a recurring symbol of constancy, vitality, and sustained goodness in both revelation and prophetic teaching.",
  50011: "as provision for Our servants—and by it We bring a dead land to life. Thus is the resurrection.",
  50012: "Before them, the people of Noah denied, as did the people of al-Rass [6] and Thamūd,\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. Al-Rass (lit. “the well” or “deep pit”) refers to a well around which its people dwelt together with their livestock. They were idol-worshippers who rejected divine guidance. Early exegetes differ over further details: some report that the community was destroyed after denying their prophet, others relate that they cast their messenger into the well, while some identify al-Rass with a region in Arabia or its environs. The Qur’anic mention remains deliberately concise, functioning primarily as a moral exemplary —grouped with other peoples who rejected the truth and were consequently judged—rather than as a fully detailed historical account.",
  50013: "and ʿĀd, and Pharaoh, and the people of Lot,",
  50014: "and the people of the Thicket [7], and the people of Tubbaʿ—each denied the messengers, so My threat came true.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. Tubbaʿ was a royal title of the Himyarite kings of ancient Yemen. The “people of Tubbaʿ” refers to their community, cited here for rejecting the messengers sent to them, with the Qur’anic emphasis on moral accountability rather than historical detail.",
  50015: "Were We ever wearied by the first creation [8]? Rather, they are in doubt about a new creation.\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. The question is rhetorical, emphatically denying any notion of fatigue, weakness, or limitation in divine creation. Allāh was not wearied by the creation of the heavens and the earth so as to require rest—an idea explicitly rejected here and contrasted with accounts that portray God as resting after creation. Attributes such as tiredness, fatigue, or the need for rest belong to human beings, not to Allāh Almighty. The verse thus refutes both the claim that God rested after completing creation and the assumption that resurrection would be more difficult than the initial act of creation, exposing such doubt as logically inconsistent.",
  50016: "Indeed, We created man, and We know what his soul whispers within him—and We [9] are nearer to him than his jugular vein.\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. The verse affirms God’s complete knowledge of the human inner life and His absolute nearness. The phrase “nearer than his jugular vein” is an expression of divine awareness, power, and oversight—more intimate than a person’s own life-source. This nearness is one of knowledge, authority, and control, fully consistent with God’s transcendence, and is also realized through His angels who are intimately present with human beings, as made explicit in the following verse.",
  50017: "When the two receivers receive him, seated on the right and on the left. [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. The “two receivers” are the angels appointed to observe and record every word and deed of a person: one positioned on the right, recording good deeds, and one on the left, recording evil deeds. Traditional reports state that they accompany human beings at all times, except during moments of marital intimacy or when using the bathroom. Their constant presence underscores the completeness and precision of moral accountability, with nothing uttered or done escaping record.",
  50018: "He does not utter a word but that a watcher is with him, ready. [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. The “watcher” refers to the recording angel assigned to document a person’s speech. The verse emphasizes that every utterance—whether significant or seemingly trivial—is precisely recorded, reinforcing the theme of complete moral accountability introduced in the preceding verses. This meaning is illustrated in a reported narration in which a man said, “O Messenger of Allāh, such-and-such a woman is mentioned for the abundance of her prayers, charity, and fasting, yet she harms her neighbors with her tongue.” The Prophet ﷺ replied, “She is in the Hellfire.” The man then said, “Another woman is mentioned who is known for the fewness of her fasting, charity, and prayers, but she gives small pieces of dried curd in charity and does not harm her neighbors with her tongue.” He ﷺ replied, “She is in Paradise.”",
  50019: "The throes of death will come with the truth—that is what you were trying to avoid. [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. “The truth” refers to the undeniable reality unveiled at the moment of death: the certainty of God, the Hereafter, resurrection, reckoning, judgment, accountability for one’s deeds, and the realities of Paradise and Hell. At that point, all doubt, denial, and self-deception fall away. The verse confronts the human tendency to evade or postpone reflection on death and judgment, declaring that what was avoided throughout life becomes unavoidable upon its arrival.",
  50020: "And the Trumpet will be blown [13].\nThat is the Day you were warned of.\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. This refers to the second blowing of the Trumpet, which will bring all beings back to life for judgment. It signifies the divine command that inaugurates the Resurrection and the Final Judgment. Its blowing marks the end of worldly existence and the beginning of recompense, fulfilling the repeated warnings given to humanity. The event is sudden, irresistible, and universal, underscoring the certainty of the promised retribution.",
  50021: "Each soul will come, with it a driver and a witness. [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. The “driver” (sāʾiq) is the angel who leads a person to the place of judgment, while the “witness” (shahīd) is the angel who testifies to that person’s deeds. This pairing underscores the inevitability and fairness of judgment: no one arrives alone, and every individual is escorted and testified for or against with perfect justice.",
  50022: "You were in heedlessness of this; so We have removed from you your veil, and today your sight is sharp.",
  50023: "His accompanying angel will say, ‘Here is his record, ready and present with me.’",
  50024: "“Cast, both of you, into Hell every stubborn denier.”",
  50025: "Every withholder of good, transgressor, full of doubt;",
  50026: "who set up another god alongside Allāh. Cast him into severe punishment.",
  50027: "His devilish companion will say, ‘Our Lord, I did not cause him to transgress, but he was far astray.’",
  50028: "Allāh will say, ‘Do not dispute before Me, for I have already given you the warning.’",
  50029: "My word is not changed with Me [15], nor am I in the least unjust to My servants.\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. “My word is not changed with Me” refers to God’s decisive decree concerning judgment and recompense. It affirms the immutability of divine justice: what God has decreed is neither revised nor overturned. The statement simultaneously negates any notion of injustice, emphasizing that judgment follows prior warning, human choice, and full accountability, and is therefore neither arbitrary nor unjust.",
  50030: "On the Day We will say to Hell, ‘Are you filled?’ and it will reply, ‘Are there any more?’ [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. The dialogue emphasizes the vastness of Hell and the gravity of divine judgment. Hell’s response—asking whether there are more to come—does not imply desire or independent will, but underscores the abundance of those deserving punishment and the certainty of retribution. The exchange intensifies the warning to humanity, highlighting the seriousness of denial and moral rebellion. This meaning is further illustrated in a reported saying of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ: “The people will be thrown into the Hellfire and it will say, ‘Are there any more (to come)?’ until Allāh places His Foot over it, and it will say, ‘Enough! Enough!’",
  50031: "And Paradise will be brought near to the righteous—no longer distant;",
  50032: "“This is what you were promised—for every one who turns back [to God], a faithful keeper [of His covenant],",
  50033: "who feared the Most Compassionate in the unseen [17] and came with a heart turned in repentance,\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. “In the unseen” (bil-ghayb) denotes fearing Allāh without seeing Him, or while one is in private—out of sincere faith rather than outward compulsion or display. It emphasizes inner consciousness, integrity, and reverence maintained when no human observation or immediate consequence is present.",
  50034: "Enter it in peace. This is the Day of Eternity.\"",
  50035: "They will have whatever they desire there, and with Us is even more. [18]\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. That is, the vision of the Most Honorable Face of Allāh—the greatest promise and supreme reward beyond all the pleasures of Paradise. The believers will see Allāh as He truly is, without confusion, obstruction, or hardship.\nIt is authentically reported that one night the Companions were with the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ when he looked at the full moon and said that the believers will see their Lord as clearly as they see the moon, without crowding or difficulty. He further taught that steadfast observance of the morning and afternoon prayers is among the means that lead to this honor, and he recited: “And glorify the praise of your Lord before sunrise and before sunset.”\nEarlier revelation likewise affirms this promise. Jesus, God’s messenger to the Israelites, declared: “Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God” (Matthew 5:8), and its fulfillment is echoed in Revelation 22:4: “They will see His face.”\nAll veils of doubt and distortion will then be removed. The believers will behold their Lord with clear vision, transformed for eternity. The greatest joy of Paradise is not merely its delights, but being with Allāh Himself and seeing Him directly.",
  50036: "How many generations before them did We destroy—mightier than they, who ranged throughout the lands—yet was there any escape?",
  50037: "There is certainly a reminder in this for anyone with a receptive heart, or who listens with attentive presence.",
  50038: "Indeed, We created the heavens and the earth and whatever is between them in six Days [19], and no weariness touched Us. [20]\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. The Qur’an states that Allāh created the heavens and the earth in six days. While Allāh is fully capable of creating instantly by His command—“His command, when He wills a thing, is only to say to it: ‘Be,’ and it is” (Qur’an 36:82)—the creation in stages reflects divine wisdom, not limitation. Scripture likewise affirms this creative power: “For He spoke, and it was done; He commanded, and it stood fast” (Psalms 33:9).\nThe scholars explain that creation in stages served multiple purposes:\nto manifest Allāh’s power and wisdom to the angels,\nto prepare the world deliberately for Adam and his offspring, highlighting Adam’s honored status,\nto demonstrate that wisdom is shown not only in speed but also in measured order,\nto teach humanity deliberation and care in action, and\nto affirm that creation occurred by divine will and design, not by chance or accident.\nThus, staged creation reflects purposeful order and wisdom alongside absolute power.\n[20]. This verse emphatically denies any notion that Allāh experienced weariness, fatigue, or a need for rest after creation. Such attributes belong to created beings, not to the Creator. The statement affirms Allāh’s perfect power and transcendence, rejecting any belief that creation imposed strain, limitation, or exhaustion upon Him.",
  50039: "So be patient [O Prophet] with what they [21] say [of denials], and glorify your Lord with praise before sunrise [22] and before sunset [23].\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. That is, the Jews and others who denied or opposed the message.\n[22].That is, the morning prayer (Ṣalāt al-Fajr).\n[23]. That is, the midday and afternoon prayers (Ṣalāt al-Ẓuhr and Ṣalāt al-ʿAṣr)..",
  50040: "And during the night, glorify Him [24], and after the prayers [have ended]. [25]\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. \"And during the night, glorify Him—\" that is, the two evening prayers, Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ.\n[25]. \"And after the prostrations—\" that is, the supererogatory prayers and the uttering of glorifications at these times, with continuous praise of Allāh.",
  50041: "Listen out for the Day when the caller will call out from a near place. [26]\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. That is the Day of Resurrection, when the caller—understood to be the angel entrusted with the Trumpet—will call mankind forth from their graves. “A nearby place” indicates the nearness and inescapability of the call, reaching all creation without barrier or delay.",
  50042: "The Day they will hear the mighty Blast in truth—that is the Day of Emergence [from the graves]. [27]\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]. That is the Day of Resurrection, when the Trumpet will be blown in truth and certainty, and mankind will come forth from their graves for judgment. The “mighty Shout” refers to the single commanding blast that brings about the resurrection.",
  50043: "Indeed, We alone give life and cause death, and to Us is the final return.",
  50044: "On the Day when the earth will split from them as they rush forth—that is a gathering easy for Us.",
  50045: "We know best what they say, and you are not over them a compeller. So remind by the Qur’an whoever fears My warning.",
  51001: "By the winds that scatter,",
  51002: "and by the clouds that carry heavy loads,",
  51003: "and by the ships that glide with ease,",
  51004: "and by the angels who apportion the command—carrying out His decree, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. That is the angels entrusted with carrying out and distributing the commands of Allāh throughout creation—such as decrees concerning provision, life, death, and other affairs. Each angel has been appointed by Allāh to manage a specific matter of this world and the Hereafter, acting solely by His permission and never exceeding or falling short of what has been decreed and ordained.",
  51005: "Indeed, what you are promised—of resurrection—is certainly true,",
  51006: "and the recompense is certain to occur.",
  51007: "By the sky of beautifully woven paths, [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. That is the heaven marked by pathways, tracks, and orbits—referring to the ordered courses of the stars and planets, as well as the routes of angels and the execution of divine decrees. The expression points to the precision, structure, and harmony of the heavens as signs of Allāh’s power and design.",
  51008: "Indeed, you differ in what you say. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. That is you are divided and inconsistent in your statements concerning the Qur’an and the Messenger—alternately calling it truth, sorcery, poetry, or fabrication—revealing confusion and contradiction rather than certainty.",
  51009: "Turned away from the truth is he who is deluded. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. That is, turned away from the Qur’an and the truth is only one who has willingly allowed himself to be deluded—one whose heart is already inclined toward misguidance, so he is diverted from faith as a consequence of his own disposition,\narrogance and persistence in sin.",
  51010: "Perish the liars—",
  51011: "who are engulfed in heedlessness;",
  51012: "questioning, ‘When is the Day of Recompense?’",
  51013: "The Day when they are burned upon the Fire.",
  51014: "“Taste your torment—this is what you sought to hasten.”",
  51015: "Indeed, the righteous will be amid gardens and springs.",
  51016: "Enjoying what their Lord has given them; indeed, they had been doers of good before that.",
  51017: "They used to sleep only a little at night.[5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. Being constant in voluntary night prayers, never allowing a night to pass without spending a portion of it in worship. This verse admits two grammatical interpretations: if mā is taken as relative, the meaning is that they slept only a little of the night, spending most of it in prayer; if mā is taken as negative, the meaning is that they slept during the night but devoted a small portion of it to prayer. Both readings affirm their constancy in night worship.",
  51018: "Before dawn, they would seek forgiveness. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. Voluntary prayers and supplications are especially recommended in the hours before dawn, a time associated with sincerity, humility, and acceptance of repentance.",
  51019: "From their wealth they give a due share to those who ask and to the deprived.",
  51020: "On the earth are signs for those who have firm faith,",
  51021: "and within yourselves as well—will you not then perceive?",
  51022: "In the heaven is your provision and whatever you are promised.",
  51023: "So by the Lord of the heaven and the earth, it—[resurrection]—is indeed the manifest Truth as clearly as you can speak.",
  51024: "Has the story of Abraham’s honored guests reached you?",
  51025: "When they entered upon him and said, ‘Peace,’ he replied, ‘Peace—you are a people unknown.’",
  51026: "So he went quietly to his household and returned with a fat [roasted] calf. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. Thinking them to be human, Abraham hastened to honor his guests. The expression “a fat calf” signifies his generosity and eagerness to provide the best of what he had. The verb rāgha (فَرَاغَ) implies that he withdrew quietly and swiftly, without drawing attention or causing delay, while the meal was prepared and cooked—an indication of refined and thoughtful hospitality rather than mere provision.\nAllāh repeatedly calls believers to care for strangers and foreigners who are far from their homelands. He is the defender of the orphan, the widow, and the stranger, and He reminds us that we ourselves have known vulnerability and displacement. In the Qurʾān, hospitality is often a means by which Allāh blesses the host, not only the guest—just as Abraham was blessed through his three foreign visitors. Welcoming strangers into one’s home may be difficult, but it is ultimately the host who benefits most.",
  51027: "So he placed it before them and said, ‘Will you not eat?’",
  51028: "He felt fear of them. They said, ‘Do not fear,’ and gave him glad tidings of a knowledgeable son. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. Abraham’s fear arose when the guests did not eat, a breach of the norms of hospitality that suggested they were not ordinary travelers. Their reassurance—“Fear not”—marks the disclosure of their true, angelic nature. The “knowledgeable son” is understood to refer to Isaac, whose description emphasizes knowledge, wisdom, and prophetic calling rather than physical strength or worldly status. The announcement transforms Abraham’s moment of unease into divine blessing, illustrating how fear and hospitality can become gateways to revelation and grace.",
  51029: "Then his wife came forward with an astonished cry, struck her face, and said, ‘A barren old woman—me!’",
  51030: "They said, ‘Thus has said your Lord. He is indeed the All-Wise, All-Knowing.’",
  51031: "Abraham said, ‘So what is your mission, O [angelic] messengers?’",
  51032: "They said, ‘Indeed, we have been sent to a sinful people [the people of Lot],’",
  51033: "so that we may rain down upon them stones of baked clay,",
  51034: "marked by your Lord for those who transgress all bounds.",
  51035: "So We brought out whoever was therein of the believers.",
  51036: "We found there no household of Muslims except one. [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. That household consisted of Lot and his two daughters. Although the previous verse speaks of believers, this verse specifies that only one household of those who had fully submitted to God—was found in the city. The household of Lot alone, excluding his wife, combined true faith with outward submission, highlighting the near-total absence of righteousness among his people. The shift in terminology underscores that salvation is tied not to numbers, lineage, or social presence, but to sincere belief expressed through submission and obedience.",
  51037: "We left it as a sign for those who fear the painful punishment. [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. The Dead Sea region in Palestine has traditionally been identified with the lands of the people of Lot. The “sign” (āyah) refers to the enduring warning left behind after their destruction. It may be understood as the physical remains of the ruined settlements, the historical memory preserved among later peoples, and the divine account recorded in revelation. This sign is not merely evidentiary but moral in nature: it serves as a warning that benefits only those who possess reverent fear and moral awareness. Thus, the verse emphasizes that divine signs are effective not by their visibility alone, but by the disposition of those who reflect upon them.",
  51038: "And in Moses, too [there was a sign], when We sent him to Pharaoh with clear authority.",
  51039: "But he turned away—backed by his supporters—and said about Moses, \"A sorcerer, or a madman!\"",
  51040: "So We seized him and his soldiers and cast them into the sea, while he was blameworthy. [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. The phrase “while he was blameworthy” underscores that Pharaoh’s destruction was the result of his own persistent arrogance and rejection of clear signs. Despite repeated warnings and unmistakable proof, he chose tyranny and denial. The verse affirms that divine punishment is neither arbitrary nor unjust, but a consequence of willful wrongdoing, especially when power is used to oppose truth.",
  51041: "And in ʿĀd [there was a sign too], when We sent against them the barren wind.",
  51042: "It left nothing it came upon except that it reduced it to ruins.",
  51043: "And in Thamūd [there was a sign], when they were told, \"Enjoy yourselves for a while.\"",
  51044: "But they insolently defied the command of their Lord, so the thunderbolt seized them while they were looking on.",
  51045: "So they were unable to rise, nor could they save themselves.",
  51046: "Before them were the people of Noah [who were destroyed]—indeed, they were a defiantly sinful people.",
  51047: "And in the heaven there is a sign: We built it with might, and We are expanding it.",
  51048: "The earth We spread out—and excellent is the One who spreads it.",
  51049: "We created all things in pairs [12] so that you may take heed.\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. The phrase “in pairs” translates the Arabic zawjayn, which denotes pairing, complementarity, and correspondence. It encompasses:\nLiving beings: male and female\nNatural dualities: night and day, land and sea, benefit and harm\nOther complementary opposites that function together in creation.\nThe verse draws attention to the relational nature of creation, while implicitly contrasting it with the absolute Oneness of God, who alone has no mate or counterpart.\nThe purpose of this pairing is remembrance: reflection upon created duality is meant to lead to awareness of God’s Oneness and divine purpose.",
  51050: "So flee to Allāh [13]. I am a clear warner to you from Him.\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. The command “flee to Allāh” is among the most forceful calls in the Qurʾān. It signifies an urgent turning away from disbelief, injustice, sin, and self-reliance, and a wholehearted turning toward Allāh in faith, obedience, and trust. To flee to Allāh is to run from all that distances one from Him, and to pursue righteousness together with faith, love, perseverance, and gentleness. It is better to take refuge in Allāh than to place one’s trust in human strength or protection. Unlike flight from danger into uncertainty, this flight is toward safety, mercy, and true refuge. The phrase underscores a central Qurʾānic truth: there is no escape from Allāh—only escape to Him.",
  51051: "Do not set up any other god with Allāh. I have been sent by Him to you as a clear warner.",
  51052: "Similarly, no messenger [14] came to those before them without their saying, ‘A magician or a madman.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. The messengers referred to here include the prophets sent to earlier nations, such as Noah, Hūd, Ṣāliḥ, Abraham, Lot, Moses, and Jesus, among others. Despite differences in time, place, and circumstance, their core message—calling to the worship of Allāh alone and moral accountability—was met with remarkably similar rejection. Rather than engaging the substance of their message, their opponents repeatedly resorted to dismissing them as sorcerers or madmen. The verse thus situates the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ within a long, continuous line of messengers, affirming that such accusations are a recurring human response to divine truth, not an indictment of the messenger himself.",
  51053: "Have they enjoined it upon one another? No—rather, they are a people who transgress all bounds.",
  51054: "So turn away from them—you are not to be blamed. [15]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. This reassurance absolves the Prophet ﷺ of responsibility for the rejection of his message. Having faithfully conveyed the warning with clarity, he bears no blame for those who persist in denial. The command to “turn away” does not signal abandonment of the mission, but a release from engaging in fruitless argument after the truth has been made plain. It affirms a central prophetic principle: accountability lies in sincere proclamation, while acceptance rests with the hearer.",
  51055: "But continue to remind, for the reminder truly benefits the believers.",
  51056: "I created the jinn and mankind only to worship Me alone. [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. This verse defines the purpose of the creation of jinn and humankind. Worship (ʿibādah) in the Qurʾān encompasses far more than ritual observance; it includes obedience, love, humility, reliance, and living in conscious submission to Allāh in every aspect of life. The verse does not suggest that Allāh is in need of worship, but rather that worship is the means by which created beings fulfill their purpose and attain true meaning. Exclusive devotion to Allāh alone lies at the heart of this calling.\nThis meaning is further clarified in a well-known report from a Companion of the Prophet ﷺ, who said: “I was riding behind the Prophet ﷺ on a donkey when he asked me, ‘Do you know what Allāh’s right over His servants is, and what the servants’ right over Allāh is?’ I replied, ‘Allāh and His Messenger know best.’ He said, ‘Allāh’s right over His servants is that they worship Him alone and associate nothing with Him. And the servants’ right over Allāh is that He does not punish those who associate nothing with Him.’\nThis report reinforces that worship, in its truest sense, is exclusive devotion to Allāh, and that such devotion is both the foundation of faith and the source of divine mercy.",
  51057: "I seek no provision from them [17], nor do I desire that they should feed Me. [18]\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. Allāh’s declaration that He seeks no provision (rizq) from His creation—that is, provision either for themselves or for any of My creatures—affirms His absolute self-sufficiency. Unlike false deities imagined to depend on offerings, labor, or tribute, Allāh is in no need of sustenance from those He created. Worship, therefore, is not an act of supplying Allāh, but an act of recognizing one’s dependence upon Him.\n[18]. The phrase “nor do I desire that they should feed Me”—that is, feed themselves or any of My creatures—further emphasizes divine independence by negating even the most basic form of need. In the ancient world, gods were often thought to require food offerings; this verse categorically rejects such notions and prepares for the affirmation that Allāh alone is the true Provider and Sustainer of all.",
  51058: "Indeed, Allāh alone is the All-Provider [19], the Possessor of steadfast strength.\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. The All-Provider translates al-Razzāq, an intensive divine name indicating constant, abundant, and unrestricted provision. Allāh provides His creatures with everything they need for their existence and well-being. Among His provisions is guidance, by which hearts are directed and lives are ordered in truth. The paired description “the Firm Possessor of strength” (dhū al-quwwati al-matīn) affirms that Allāh’s provision does not arise from need or depletion, but from unassailable power and sufficiency. Giving does not weaken Him, nor does sustaining creation diminish His strength.",
  51059: "Indeed, the wrongdoers will have a share ˹of punishment˺ like that of their predecessors; so let them not seek to hasten it.",
  51060: "So woe to those who disbelieve when their promised Day comes.",
  52001: "By the Mount, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. The Mount (al-Ṭūr) refers to Mount Sinai, the place where Allāh spoke to Moses and revealed the Law. In the Qurʾān, the Mount is a recurring symbol of divine revelation, covenant, and moral authority. Swearing by it here invokes the certainty of God’s message and underscores continuity between the revelation given to Moses and the Qurʾānic warning that follows.",
  52002: "and by a Book inscribed, [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The phrase “an inscribed Book” translates the Arabic kitābin masṭūr (كِتَابٍ مَسْطُورٍ), literally meaning “a written or recorded book.” Early exegetes interpret this expression in several ways. Some scholars understand it as a reference to the Qur'an itself, emphasizing its preservation and transmission in written form, while others interpret it more broadly as a heavenly record or divine scripture in which revelation and decrees are inscribed. The verse occurs within a sequence of solemn oaths in Sūrat al-Ṭūr, where Allāh swears by significant signs—such as the Mount and the inscribed Book—to affirm the certainty of the message that follows.",
  52003: "on parchment spread open,",
  52004: "and by the ever-visited House, [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. The House frequently visited refers primarily to al-Bayt al-Maʿmūr, the House of worship in the heavens directly above the Kaʿbah, which is continually frequented by angels in worship. According to prophetic reports, seventy thousand angels visit it daily, each entering it once and never returning. Some exegetes have also understood the phrase to allude secondarily to the Kaʿbah in Makkah, which is likewise a House of God, constantly frequented by pilgrims and worshippers. Together, these meanings highlight the parallel between heavenly and earthly worship and underscore the constancy and universality of devotion to Allāh.",
  52005: "and by the roof raised high [i.e., the heaven],",
  52006: "and by the sea set ablaze, [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. On the Day of Resurrection, or \"by the overflowed sea.\"",
  52007: "indeed, your Lord’s punishment is inevitable—",
  52008: "there is none who can avert it.",
  52009: "the Day when the sky convulses in violent motion,",
  52010: "and the mountains will be set in motion. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The phrase “will be set in motion” translates the emphatic Arabic expression (tasīru… sayra), which conveys steady yet irresistible movement. Though mountains symbolize permanence and stability, on the Day of Judgment they will be carried away as if weightless. Elsewhere, the Qurʾān likens this to drifting clouds (27:88) and to scattered dust (56:6), underscoring the complete collapse of what once appeared immovable and enduring.",
  52011: "So woe, on that Day, to the deniers,",
  52012: "those who are absorbed in falsehood, amusing themselves, [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. The phrase “amusing themselves in falsehood” describes those who treat matters of ultimate truth lightly—engaging in denial, disbelief, evil deeds, mockery, and baseless speculation. Instead of sincere reflection, they remain absorbed in heedless diversion, disregarding the reality of the Hereafter. The Qurʾān repeatedly contrasts such frivolous play with remembrance, moral seriousness, and accountability, especially in the face of divine warning and impending judgment.",
  52013: "the Day they are driven violently into the Fire of Hell:",
  52014: "This is the Fire which you used to deny.",
  52015: "Is this magic—or do you not see?",
  52016: "Burn in it—whether you endure patiently or do not endure, it is all the same for you. You are only being recompensed for what you used to do.",
  52017: "Indeed, the righteous will be in gardens and bliss,",
  52018: "rejoicing in what their Lord has given them, and their Lord has protected them from the punishment of the blazing Fire.",
  52019: "\"Eat and drink pleasantly for what you used to do [of righteousness],”",
  52020: "reclining on couches arranged in rows; and We shall pair them with companions of wide, beautiful eyes.",
  52021: "As for those who believed, and whose offspring followed them in faith—We shall unite their offspring with them, and We shall not lessen any of their deeds. Every person is held in pledge for what he has earned. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. This verse affirms both divine mercy and individual accountability. Allāh graciously unites believing families in Paradise by elevating the rank of offspring who share their parents’ faith, even if their deeds are lesser. At the same time, no one’s reward is diminished for another’s sake, nor is anyone burdened with another’s deeds. Each person remains pledged to what he has earned, while Allāh’s generosity preserves familial bonds without compromising perfect justice.",
  52022: "And We shall provide them with whatever fruit and meat they desire.",
  52023: "They will pass a cup among themselves—free from idle talk and sin.",
  52024: "They will be served by their attendants, as though they were hidden pearls.",
  52025: "They will turn to one another, asking each other. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. This mutual exchange signifies a reflective and grateful conversation among the people of Paradise. They recall their former lives, their fears, and the trials from which Allāh delivered them. Their dialogue is neither anxious nor disputatious, but serene and filled with gratitude—an exchange that deepens their joy through remembrance of divine mercy and protection. Similar passages in the Qurʾān portray the inhabitants of Paradise conversing about Allāh’s saving grace and the path that led them to everlasting bliss.",
  52026: "They will say, ‘Indeed, we lived among our families in reverent fear [of God’s displeasure].’",
  52027: "So Allāh graciously favored us and protected us from the punishment of the scorching wind.",
  52028: "Indeed, we used to call upon Him before. Indeed, He is the Most Good, the Most Merciful.",
  52029: "So continue to remind, for by your Lord’s favor you are neither a soothsayer nor possessed.",
  52030: "Or do they say, ‘He is a poet—we await his downfall by the turns of time’? [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9].  This accusation reflects a strategy of dismissal rather than sincere engagement. By labeling the Prophet a poet and awaiting “his downfall,” his opponents implied that his message would fade with his death or be overtaken by the turns of time. The Qurʾān repeatedly refutes this claim, affirming that revelation is neither poetic invention nor subject to the uncertainties of fate, but divine speech—preserved, upheld, and ultimately fulfilled by Allāh.",
  52031: "Say, ‘Wait—I am indeed with you among those who wait.’",
  52032: "Or do their minds prompt them to say this? Or are they a transgressing people?",
  52033: "Or do they say, ‘He has invented it’? No—rather, they do not believe.",
  52034: "Let them then produce a discourse like it, if they are truthful.",
  52035: "Or were they created from nothing, or are they the creators [of themselves]?",
  52036: "Or did they create the heavens and the earth? Rather, they are not certain.",
  52037: "Or do they possess your Lord’s treasuries? Or are they in control?",
  52038: "Or do they have a stairway by which they eavesdrop [to the heavens]? Then let their listener bring a clear proof.",
  52039: "Or has He daughters, while you have sons?",
  52040: "Or do you ask them for a payment, so that they are burdened with debt?",
  52041: "Or do they possess knowledge of the unseen, so they write it down?",
  52042: "Or do they intend a plot? But those who disbelieve are themselves ensnared.",
  52043: "Or do they have a god other than Allāh? Exalted is Allāh above whatever they associate with Him.",
  52044: "If they were to see a fragment of the sky falling down [10], they would say, ‘It is but a mass of piled-up clouds.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. This verse illustrates the depth of stubborn denial. Even if the disbelievers were to witness an unmistakable sign of divine power—such as a fragment of the sky falling as a sign of the punishment they themselves had demanded—they would still rationalize it away as a natural phenomenon, calling it “a mass of piled clouds.” The verse thus exposes not doubt about the sign itself, but a willful refusal to acknowledge its meaning. It underscores that disbelief here stems not from lack of evidence, but from hardened hearts and deliberate rejection.",
  52045: "So leave them alone until they encounter their Day, on which they will be struck down in terror,",
  52046: "the Day when their plotting will not avail them at all, nor will they be helped.",
  52047: "And for the wrongdoers is another punishment besides that [11], but most of them are unaware.\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. “Another punishment besides that” is understood by many exegetes to refer to worldly punishment—such as calamities, defeat, humiliation, or spiritual hardening—as well as punishment in the grave, prior to the punishment of the Hereafter. The verse emphasizes that divine retribution is not limited to the final judgment; consequences may begin in this life, though many remain unaware of their cause or meaning.",
  52048: "So be patient for the judgment of your Lord, for you are under Our watchful Eyes [12]. And glorify your Lord with His praise [13] when you rise. [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. That is, under Our care and protection—a reassurance of constant divine oversight, guardianship, and support.\nThe term “Eyes” affirms also a real attribute of Allāh as befits His majesty—without likening it to creation and without asking how.\n[13]. That is, saying: “Subḥānallāhi wa biḥamdihī”—“Glory be to Allāh, and all praise is due to Him.” This expression combines declaring Allāh’s transcendence with affirming His perfection and grace.\n[14]. That is, when you rise from your sleeping place, from a sitting position, or when you stand to perform the noon (Ẓuhr) and afternoon (ʿAṣr) prayers. The phrase encompasses multiple moments of rising, indicating the importance of beginning actions with remembrance of Allāh.",
  52049: "And during the night [15], glorify Him, and at the retreat of the stars. [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. “During the night” refers to worship and remembrance performed after sunset. This includes the two evening prayers: Maghrib (sunset) and ʿIshāʾ (after sunset), as well as other forms of night devotion, such as voluntary prayer and remembrance. The night is singled out for its stillness and sincerity, when distractions fade and devotion is more focused.\n[16]. That is, the morning prayer (Fajr), performed at dawn when the stars fade and daylight begins to appear. This time marks the close of the night and the opening of the day with remembrance and devotion.",
  53001: "By the star when it sets. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. i.e., the star as it sets or falls—an oath invoking a sign of order and guidance in creation. Some interpret it more broadly as referring to the stars in general or to the descent of revelation. Others have specifically identified it with the constellation Pleiades (al-Thurayyā).",
  53002: "Your companion did not stray, nor did he err. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. \"Your companion\" Refers to the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ, described as their companion to emphasize familiarity: they knew his character, honesty, and sound judgment long before revelation.\nThis is a testimony to the Messenger ﷺ that he is upright, rightly guided, and a follower of the truth, not astray. The one who is astray (ḍāll) is ignorant, walking upon a path other than the correct one without knowledge; while the one who deviates (ghāwī) is aware of the truth yet deliberately turns away from it toward something else. Thus, Allāh —Exalted and Glorious—is He—declared His Messenger and his law to be far removed from resembling the people of misguidance, such as the Christians and the ways of the Jews, and from knowing the truth yet concealing it or acting contrary to it. Rather, he ﷺ and that with which Allāh sent him of the magnificent law are in the utmost state of uprightness, balance, and soundness.",
  53003: "Nor does he speak from his own desire.",
  53004: "It is but a revelation revealed,",
  53005: "taught to him by one of mighty power. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. “One of mighty power ” refers to the angel Gabriel (Jibrīl), the messenger entrusted with conveying revelation from Allāh to His prophets. He is described elsewhere as possessing great power, firmness, and trustworthiness, emphasizing that the Qurʾān was transmitted with perfect accuracy and authority, free from error or distortion.",
  53006: "Endowed with strength and soundness; then he stood upright in his true form, [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. The phrase “Endowed with strength and soundness\"  translates dhū mirrah, which denotes firmness, integrity, and perfected capability. It is understood as a description of the angel Gabriel, emphasizing his immense strength, composure, and reliability in conveying revelation. The statement “then he stood upright in his true form” indicates that Gabriel appeared fully and steadily, not in a partial or veiled manifestation, underscoring the clarity, certainty, and authority of the revelation received.",
  53007: "while he was on the highest horizon.",
  53008: "Then he drew near and came down,",
  53009: "until he was at a distance of two bow-lengths, or even nearer. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. “Two bow-lengths, or even nearer” is an Arabic idiom expressing extreme closeness, not a precise physical measurement. It conveys the immediacy and certainty of the encounter, emphasizing the direct and unmediated transmission of revelation. In this context, it describes the nearness of the angel Gabriel to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ at the moment of revelation, underscoring both the honor of the Prophet and the clarity and reliability of what was revealed.",
  53010: "Then He revealed to His servant [6] what He revealed.\\n\\nFootnotes: [6] “His servant” refers to the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ. The verse affirms that while the angel Gabriel conveyed the revelation, its source is God alone. The repetition “what He revealed” conveys reverence and restraint, emphasizing the gravity and certainty of divine communication without detailing its content.",
  53011: "The [Prophet’s] heart did not deny what it saw. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. The term “heart” (fuʾād) denotes the inner faculty of perception and certainty, not mere emotion. It affirms inner conviction fully aligned with true sight. “What he saw” refers to what the Prophet ﷺ witnessed with his own eyes, namely the true form of the angel Gabriel. This verse confirms that the Prophet’s inner perception neither denied nor distorted the reality of what was shown to him. Rather, his heart affirmed it with complete certainty, clarity, and truthfulness, rejecting any suggestion of illusion, imagination, or error in the revelatory experience.",
  53012: "Do you then dispute with him about what he saw?",
  53013: "He certainly saw him on another descent. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. “Another descent” refers to a second encounter in which the Prophet ﷺ saw the angel Gabriel, distinct from the earlier appearance described previously. Many scholars identify this with the encounter during the Night Journey, near the Lote Tree of the Utmost Boundary. The verse emphasizes repetition and certainty, affirming that the vision was real, conscious, and free from illusion or error.",
  53014: "By the lote tree at the furthest limit, [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. In the seventh heaven beyond which none may pass.",
  53015: "near which is the Garden of Abode,",
  53016: "when the lote tree was covered by what covered it [of splendor],",
  53017: " the sight did not swerve, nor did it overstep. [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. This verse highlights the Prophet’s ﷺ perfect composure and discipline during the Miʿrāj. “Did not swerve” negates distraction, confusion, or wavering, while “did not transgress” negates overstepping what was divinely permitted. Despite witnessing overwhelming splendor, his sight remained steady and restrained, exemplifying ideal prophetic adab (reverent conduct) and complete obedience.",
  53018: "He certainly saw some of the greatest signs of his Lord. [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. “Some of the greatest signs of his Lord” refers to the extraordinary manifestations shown to the Prophet ﷺ during the Night Journey and Ascension. Among what he witnessed were the angel Gabriel in his true form with six hundred wings, and from the marvels of the unseen realm, a vast green drape that filled and obscured the horizon of the heaven. These sights were among the greatest signs granted to him, affirming the reality and majesty of the unseen world.\nSuch beauty and magnificence awaken reflection on what Allāh has prepared for the believers in the Hereafter, when they will behold realities far beyond worldly perception, according to His will and mercy. When the Prophet ﷺ witnessed the marvels of the heavenly realm, he truly beheld some of the greatest signs of his Lord. The Qurʾān deliberately refrains from detailed description, indicating that human language cannot fully encompass the glory and majesty revealed to God’s final Messenger ﷺ.",
  53019: "Have you considered al-Lāt and al-ʿUzzā, [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. al-Lāt and al-ʿUzzā were among the most prominent idols worshipped by the pagan Arabs of Makkah. Al-Lāt was associated with fertility and sustenance, while al-ʿUzzā was regarded as a powerful goddess linked to strength and protection. Both were falsely believed to possess divine intercessory power. By mentioning them immediately after describing the greatest divine signs witnessed by the Prophet ﷺ, the Qurʾān exposes the stark contrast between true divine majesty and lifeless, man-made objects, highlighting the irrationality of idolatry and the baselessness of associating partners with Allāh.",
  53020: "and Manāt—the third one as well? [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. Manāt was a major pre-Islamic Arabian idol, venerated especially by tribes along the Red Sea coast and associated with fate and destiny. By referring to her as “the third”, the Qurʾān subtly diminishes her status, grouping her with al-Lāt and al-ʿUzzā as part of a fabricated triad of goddesses. This critique is not confined to Arabian paganism alone but reflects a broader human tendency to assign divinity to created beings. Similar patterns appear across cultures and eras, where revered female figures—such as Lakshmi, Saraswati, Parvati, Tara, and other widely worshipped goddesses—are venerated and invoked instead of the One true God. The verse thus exposes the universality of idolatry and rejects all forms of associating partners with Allāh, regardless of cultural context or historical setting.",
  53021: "Are the males for you, and the females for Him? [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. This verse condemns the inconsistency and arrogance of the pagans, who preferred male offspring for themselves while attributing female offspring to Allāh—despite regarding daughters as inferior. The Qurʾān exposes both the false doctrine of assigning lineage to Allāh and the moral hypocrisy underlying such claims. Allāh is exalted above offspring, gender, or division, and such attributions reflect human desire and bias rather than revealed truth.",
  53022: "That, then, is an unfair division!",
  53023: "These [idols] are nothing but names you have named—you and your fathers—for which Allāh has sent down no authority. The pagans follow nothing but conjecture and what their own souls incline to, while certainly the guidance has come to them from their Lord.",
  53024: "Or shall man have whatever he wishes? [15]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15] The pagans worshipped these idols in the hope that they would intercede for them on the Day of Resurrection (see 39:3). This verse refutes the notion that human desire determines spiritual reality. It challenges the belief that one may obtain salvation, divine favor, or intercession merely by wishing for it. In its immediate context, it rejects the assumption that idols or imagined intermediaries can intercede by personal choice. The Qurʾān affirms instead that all outcomes belong solely to Allāh’s will, wisdom, and permission, not to human conjecture or desire.",
  53025: "Rather, to Allāh alone belong the Hereafter and the present life. [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. This verse establishes absolute divine sovereignty. Since ownership of both the Hereafter and the present world belongs exclusively to Allāh, there is no need for any mediator or independent intercessor between Him and His creation. Believers are therefore called to pray directly and exclusively to Allāh, seeking all needs from Him alone. All authority, judgment, reward, and destiny rest with Him—not with idols, imagined intermediaries, or human desire. No being can grant benefit or intercession except by His permission. The verse thus directly corrects the false assumption mentioned in the preceding line: that wishes, lineage, or chosen intermediaries can influence divine outcomes. All matters, temporal and eternal, ultimately return to Allāh alone.",
  53026: "How many angels there are in the heavens whose intercession avails nothing at all, except after Allāh has granted permission to whom He wills and is pleased with.",
  53027: "Indeed, those who do not believe in the Hereafter assign feminine names to the angels. [17]\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. The pagan Arabs claimed that angels were female and referred to them as the “daughters of Allāh,” despite having no knowledge or revelation to support this belief. This attribution reflected both religious error and moral inconsistency, as they preferred sons for themselves while assigning daughters to Allāh. The verse connects such claims to disbelief in the Hereafter, indicating that denial of accountability leads to unfounded speculation about the unseen. The Qurʾān rejects all such assertions, affirming that angels are honored servants of Allāh and are neither male nor female in any human sense.",
  53028: "They have no knowledge of it; they follow only conjecture, and indeed conjecture avails nothing against the truth.",
  53029: "So turn away from whoever turns his back on Our reminder and desires nothing but the worldly life.",
  53030: "That is the limit of their knowledge. Indeed, your Lord knows best who has strayed from His path, and He knows best who is rightly guided.",
  53031: "To Allāh belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth, so that He may repay those who do evil for what they have done, and reward those who do good with the best reward. [18]\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. The “best reward” (al-ḥusnā) refers to Paradise and all that it contains of eternal bliss, nearness to Allāh, and complete satisfaction. The verse links divine ownership with divine justice: since all creation belongs to Allāh, He alone possesses the authority to judge and recompense. Evil is repaid with full justice, while good is rewarded with grace and excellence beyond measure. This establishes that accountability and recompense are grounded not in human claims or desires, but in Allāh’s perfect knowledge, wisdom, and mercy.",
  53032: "Those who avoid major sins [19] and indecencies—except for minor lapses. Indeed, your Lord is vast in forgiveness. He knows you best—when He brought you forth from the earth and when you were embryos in your mothers’ wombs. So do not claim yourselves to be pure [20]; He knows best who is truly mindful of Him.\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. Major sins (kabāʾir) are grave acts explicitly prohibited in revelation and associated with severe warning or punishment, such as idolatry, murder, adultery, theft, false testimony, and injustice. Indecencies (fawāḥish) refer to blatant immoral acts, whether committed openly or in secret. Minor lapses are lesser faults or unintentional slips that may occur despite sincere faith and effort. This verse reassures that while believers must avoid major transgressions, Allāh’s forgiveness encompasses minor shortcomings when one repents and strives to obey Him. The Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said: “Avoid the deadly sins, follow the right course, and give glad tidings.”\n[20] “Do not claim yourselves to be pure” forbids moral self-praise—elevating oneself or being wise in one’s own eyes. Since Allāh alone knows the full reality of a person—their origin, inner state, intentions, and struggles—no one is entitled to declare themselves pure or assured of righteousness. True virtue lies in humility, repentance, and taqwā (reverent awareness of Allāh), which Allāh alone fully knows.",
  53033: "Have you ˹O Prophet˺ seen the one who turned away ˹from the truth˺?",
  53034: "who gave a little, then held back.",
  53035: "Does he have knowledge of the unseen, so that he can see?",
  53036: "Or has he not been informed of what is in the scriptures of Moses?",
  53037: "And ˹of˺ Abraham, who fulfilled ˹his obligations˺?",
  53038: "That no bearer shall bear the burden of another. [21]\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. This verse establishes the foundational principle of individual moral responsibility: no person is held accountable for the sins of another. Each soul bears its own burden based on its choices and actions. The rule negates inherited guilt, collective blame, and vicarious atonement. It affirms divine justice and personal accountability, a principle reiterated throughout revelation (cf. 6:164; 17:15; 35:18). Intercession, where granted, does not transfer guilt but occurs only by Allāh’s permission without negating personal responsibility.",
  53039: "And that a person will have nothing except what they strive for. [22]\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. This verse affirms the principle of personal effort and accountability: a person attains only the outcome of what they themselves strive for. It does not deny Allāh’s grace or mercy; rather, it establishes that reward is connected to sincere effort and intention. Divine grace may multiply and elevate deeds, but it does not replace responsibility or striving. The verse rejects reliance on lineage, mere claims, or borrowed merit, and anchors success in earnest effort coupled with faith.\nThis world is a passageway to the Hereafter, and one should not become attached to it at the expense of eternal life. Muslims regard the Hereafter as their true objective because it is everlasting, and thus they strive foremost for it rather than for a life that is short and transient. This does not negate striving for lawful worldly aims or developing the earth; rather, Islam encourages productive effort in all beneficial pursuits.\nHuman progress is inseparable from effort and diligence. One who studies with dedication becomes learned, just as a farmer who tills the land, sows seeds, and waters them is rewarded with crops. In contrast, laziness and negligence lead to hardship and loss. Islam strongly discourages indolence and irresponsibility, regarding hard work as a duty and productive effort as a virtue. Working and benefiting others are beloved to Allāh, while idleness is the root of many evils.\nFor this reason, Muslims strive most in what brings them closer to Allāh and yields lasting benefit. The Prophet ﷺ said: “When a human being dies, all his deeds come to an end except three: ongoing charity, beneficial knowledge, or a righteous child who prays for him.” Thus, true striving is that which produces enduring reward in the Hereafter.",
  53040: "And that his strivings will soon be seen, [23]\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. This verse affirms that every person’s efforts—whether righteous or wrongful—will be made manifest. What is hidden in intention and action will be brought into the open, especially on the Day of Judgment, when deeds are fully displayed and assessed. The phrase “will soon be seen” underscores the certainty and nearness of accountability, reminding that no effort is lost or forgotten. Deeds may first be witnessed through their consequences in this world, and they will be fully revealed and judged in the Hereafter, where recompense is rendered with complete justice.\nThis principle operates as a universal law of effort and consequence. Those who are diligent, disciplined, and sincere in their work often rise to positions of trust, stability, and responsibility, while negligence and indolence lead to dependency, regret, and loss of dignity. Hard work earns respect and opens paths of opportunity; perseverance grants control over one’s circumstances, whereas the abandonment of effort leaves a person subject to the will of others.\nIn Islam, this law extends far beyond material life. Just as effort and persistence yield results in this world, striving in faith, worship, and service builds one’s standing in the Hereafter. A believer works with purpose—seeking a wholesome life in this world and eternal reward in Paradise. Thus, sincere and excellent striving brings honor in this life, while steadfast effort for the sake of Allāh brings everlasting joy in the next.",
  53041: "Then he will be fully repaid for it,",
  53042: "and that to your Lord is the final return.",
  53043: "And that He is the One who makes one laugh [24] and weep. [25]\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. “Makes [one] laugh” affirms that joy, relief, and moments of happiness occur by Allāh’s will. Laughter here encompasses the causes of ease, success, contentment, and delight that Allāh creates within human experience. The Qurʾān recognizes that there is a proper time for laughter, play, and lightheartedness, just as there is a time for seriousness and restraint. While it is inappropriate to laugh during moments of gravity or sorrow, laughter itself is a blessing when expressed with balance and wisdom. Allāh allows and intends joy for His servants, and encouraging wholesome cheer and ease among others can be an act of kindness—without excess or impropriety.\n[25]. “And weep” signifies sorrow, grief, fear, and emotional pain, all of which occur by Allāh’s wisdom. Tears may arise from hardship, loss, repentance, humility, or awe of Allāh. The verse teaches that both joy and sorrow are encompassed within divine decree and serve purposes of testing, purification, and return to Allāh. When His servants cry out to Him in repentance and trust, He hears them, forgives their sins, and renews their spiritual strength. From sincere tears often emerges restored hope, inner healing, and deeper closeness to Allāh.",
  53044: "And that He is the One who causes death and gives life,",
  53045: "And that He created the pairs—male and female,",
  53046: "from a drop of fluid when it is emitted.",
  53047: "and that upon Him is the final resurrection.",
  53048: "And that He is the One who enriches and suffices,",
  53049: "And that He alone is the Lord of Sirius. [26]\\n\\nFootnotes: [26]. Sirius (al-Shiʿrā) is the brightest star visible to the naked eye and was revered by certain ancient Arab groups, some of whom attributed to it divine influence or worshipped it outright. By declaring Allāh as the Lord of Sirius, the Qurʾān directly refutes astral worship and affirms that even the most luminous and awe-inspiring celestial bodies are created and governed by Allāh alone. The verse underscores Allāh’s absolute sovereignty over the heavens and dismantles any claim that stars or celestial forces possess independent power or divinity.",
  53050: "And that He destroyed the former ʿĀd, [27]\\n\\nFootnotes: [27]. ʿĀd refers to an ancient and powerful people who lived in the region of al-Aḥqāf (sand dunes) and were known for their strength and monumental constructions. They rejected their prophet Hūd عليه السلام and persisted in arrogance and denial despite clear warnings. “The former” distinguishes them from a later people also called ʿĀd, emphasizing their early place in history and the completeness of their destruction. The verse serves as a warning that neither power nor antiquity can protect a people who defy Allah’s guidance.",
  53051: "And Thamūd—He spared none of them, [28]\\n\\nFootnotes: [28]. Thamūd were the people of the Prophet Ṣāliḥ عليه السلام and are sometimes referred to in early sources as the second ʿĀd, having come after the destruction of the first ʿĀd. They inhabited the rocky region of al-Ḥijr and were granted strength and prosperity. Despite receiving clear signs—including the she-camel sent as a divine proof—they rejected their prophet and persisted in defiance. “He spared none” emphasizes the completeness of their destruction once they knowingly rebelled after guidance had come to them, illustrating that no power or lineage can avert divine justice.",
  53052: "and the people of Noah before them—indeed, they were more unjust and more transgressing.",
  53053: "And the overturned cities—He brought them down, [29]\\n\\nFootnotes: [29]. The “overturned cities” refer to the towns of the people of Lot, traditionally identified with Sodom and Gomorrah. They were destroyed for persistent moral corruption and rejection of prophetic warning. The wording emphasizes the sudden and total reversal of their settlements, underscoring the decisiveness of divine judgment after clear signs had been denied.",
  53054: "so they were covered by what covered them ˹of torment˺.",
  53055: "So which of your Lord’s favors do you doubt?",
  53056: "This ˹Prophet˺ is a warner, like the earlier warners.",
  53057: "The approaching Hour has drawn near,",
  53058: "none but Allāh can disclose it.",
  53059: "Do you then wonder at this message,",
  53060: "and you laugh and do not weep, [30]\\n\\nFootnotes: [30]. This verse censures a response of mockery and heedlessness toward divine revelation. Laughter here signifies ridicule, dismissal, and moral indifference, while “do not weep” points to the absence of humility, remorse, and reverent fear in the face of warnings about the Hereafter. The Qurʾān frequently contrasts frivolous laughter with sincere weeping as indicators of inner awareness and spiritual seriousness. A heart that truly comprehends the gravity of the message is moved to reflection, humility, and repentance—not mockery.\nThe Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ once delivered a sermon to his Companions unlike any they had heard before, saying: “If you knew what I know, you would laugh little and weep much.” Those present were so affected that they covered their faces and began to weep. He further said: “Paradise and Hellfire were shown to me, and I have never seen anything like this day in good and in evil. If you were to know what I know, you would laugh little and weep much.” The Companions were overwhelmed, and their weeping reflected the deep impact of this reminder.\nThis teaches that Paradise and Hell are real and present realities, not abstract ideas. Excessive laughter is blameworthy when it reflects heedlessness and forgetfulness of the Hereafter, whereas weeping out of fear of Allāh is praiseworthy, for it indicates a living heart, spiritual awareness, and concern for one’s final return. A believer is therefore called to remain alert, reflective, and conscious of the life to come.",
  53061: "while you amuse yourselves in heedlessness? [31]\\n\\nFootnotes: [31]. “Amuse yourselves in heedlessness” translates sāmidūn, which denotes diversion, frivolity, and deliberate distraction from serious reflection. It describes an attitude of willful inattentiveness—occupying oneself with entertainment or mockery to avoid confronting truth and accountability. In this context, it condemns responding to divine warning with indifference rather than humility, repentance, and readiness for the Hereafter.",
  53062: "So prostrate yourselves to Allāh and worship Him alone. [32]\\n\\nFootnotes: [32] This verse is a direct command that follows a series of warnings, reflections on past nations, and reminders of Allāh’s power over creation, life, death, and resurrection. The command to prostrate (sujūd) signifies the highest expression of humility, submission, and closeness to Allāh—both physically and spiritually. It reflects an acknowledgment of His absolute lordship and one’s complete dependence upon Him.\nThe instruction to worship Him alone encompasses all forms of devotion—outward and inward—including prayer, obedience, sincerity, and remembrance.\nAs the concluding response to the preceding rebuke, the verse calls one away from mockery, heedlessness, and turning aside, toward humility, submission, and sincere devotion. It also marks a point of prostration (sajdah) in recitation, where the listener is encouraged to physically prostrate, embodying the very command being given.",
  54001: "The Hour has drawn near, and the moon has split ˹in two˺. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. This verse refers to the splitting of the moon, a miraculous sign shown by Allāh in response to the demand of the Quraysh during the lifetime of the Prophet ﷺ. It is widely reported in authentic prophetic traditions that the moon was seen split into two distinct parts, visible from Makkah, before returning to its normal state. The Qurʾān presents this event as a real, observable sign, not merely a metaphor, serving as proof of the Prophet’s truthfulness and a warning of the approaching Final Hour.\nAt the same time, the event foreshadows the cosmic upheavals that will accompany the Day of Judgment, linking a past miracle with future accountability. Despite witnessing this sign, many persisted in denial, illustrating that rejection often stems not from lack of evidence but from obstinate disbelief.\nThe verse opens the surah with a powerful pairing: the approach of the Hour and a visible sign in the heavens, emphasizing both the urgency of the message and the reality of divine intervention in the natural order.",
  54002: "Yet whenever they see a sign, they turn away and say, ‘Ongoing sorcery.’ [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The word “sign” (āyah) refers to a clear miracle or divine proof, such as the splitting of the moon mentioned in the previous verse. Despite witnessing extraordinary signs, the disbelievers deliberately turned away and dismissed them as “ongoing sorcery”—meaning repeated, continuous, or habitual magic. This accusation reflects willful denial rather than genuine doubt, as truth was rebranded as deception in order to avoid moral accountability. The verse thus highlights that miracles do not benefit those whose hearts are closed to faith.\nThroughout history, Allāh supported His messengers with signs confirming their divine commission. Moses was granted manifest miracles such as the staff and the shining hand; Jesus was enabled to heal the blind and the leper and to raise the dead by Allāh’s permission. The Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ was given the Qurʾān itself as a lasting literary miracle, challenging the masters of Arabic eloquence. He was also shown many other signs, including the Night Journey and Ascension, the splitting of the moon, the multiplication of food and water to sustain entire armies, water flowing from between his fingers, and miraculous healings—among them the restoration of Qatādah’s eye after it was struck at the Battle of Uḥud.\nIn addition, the Prophet ﷺ foretold matters of the unseen, was greeted by inanimate objects, witnessed the weeping of the palm-trunk when he ceased leaning on it for sermons, and was a means of guidance, healing, and renewed purpose for countless people. Yet, as this verse illustrates, even overwhelming evidence fails to guide those who choose denial over humility and faith.",
  54003: "They rejected ˹the truth˺ and followed their desires; but every matter has its appointed end. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. This verse explains the root cause of rejection: denial of truth followed by submission to personal desires rather than guidance. “Low desires” refers to whims, passions, and self-interest that override reason, conscience, and revelation. The concluding phrase, “but every matter has its appointed end ” affirms that every affair—truth and falsehood, belief and denial—will reach its destined conclusion at the time decreed by Allāh. No injustice will remain unresolved, no truth permanently obscured. Though falsehood may appear dominant for a time, ultimate judgment and clarification are inevitable, whether in this world or the Hereafter.",
  54004: "There have already come to them reports in which there is sufficient deterrence.",
  54005: "A complete and profound wisdom—but warnings are of no benefit ˹to them˺.",
  54006: "So turn away from them ˹O Prophet˺, and await the Day when the caller will call them to a dreadful thing, [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. The “caller” refers to Isrāfīl, the angel assigned to blow the Trumpet and summon all of creation for resurrection and judgment. At his call, the dead will rise from their graves and be gathered for accountability before Allāh. The “dreadful thing” denotes the fearful summons to the Day of Judgment—when denial, mockery, and heedlessness will cease, and every soul will confront the full reality of its deeds. This verse marks the transition from worldly warning to unavoidable divine reckoning.",
  54007: "with their eyes downcast, they will emerge from the graves as if they were scattered locusts, spreading. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The phrase “with their eyes downcast” conveys a state of humiliation, fear, and shock, as the disbelievers rise from their graves fully aware of the reality they once denied. Their lowered gaze reflects loss of pride and overwhelming dread.\nThe image “as if they were scattered locusts” presents a clear picture of chaotic, countless movement—people emerging in vast numbers, disoriented and rushing in all directions. Locusts are used as a metaphor for:\ntheir multitude,\ntheir disorderly dispersal,\nand their lack of control in the face of a greater force.\nThe word “spreading” reinforces this sense of uncontrolled expansion, as humanity pours forth from the graves toward the place of gathering.\nTogether, the imagery captures the shock, scale, and confusion of resurrection—transforming what was once denied into an overwhelming and inescapable reality.",
  54008: "Rushing toward the caller, the disbelievers will say, ‘This is a difficult Day.’ [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. The phrase “rushing toward the caller” describes the compelled movement of humanity toward the summons—there is no hesitation or escape. The caller (previously mentioned) is the one who summons them to the gathering for judgment, and all respond in complete submission, whether willingly or not.\nThe disbelievers’ statement, “This is a difficult Day,” reflects a sudden realization of the gravity of what they had denied. The word “difficult” (ʿasir) conveys not merely hardship, but intense severity, distress, and inescapable consequence.\nThis moment marks a reversal:\nwhat was once dismissed as improbable or false is now experienced as undeniable and overwhelming reality.\nTheir words are not a complaint, but an admission born of fear and regret, recognizing the seriousness of the Day they failed to prepare for.",
  54009: "Before them, the people of Noah rejected ˹their messenger˺; they denied Our servant and said, ‘He is mad,’ and he was rebuked. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7] This verse shifts from the scene of the Hereafter to a historical example: the people of Noah, who persistently rejected their prophet. By recalling this earlier nation, the Qur’an draws a parallel between past denial and present disbelief.\nThe phrase “Our servant” is a mark of honor and closeness, emphasizing Noah’s status as a devoted servant of Allāh despite the accusations against him.\nTheir claim, “He is mad,” reflects a common pattern among disbelievers—dismissing divine messengers by attacking their character rather than engaging with their message. The statement “and he was rebuked” (wazdujir) can imply that he was:\nharshly rejected and driven away,\npublicly scorned, or threatened and restrained from continuing his mission.\nThe verse highlights that rejection of truth is not new; it is part of a recurring human pattern. Yet, as earlier verses indicated, every matter has its destined outcome, and past nations serve as a warning of what follows persistent denial.",
  54010: "So he called upon his Lord, ˹saying,˺\n‘I am overpowered—so grant me victory.’",
  54011: "Then We opened the gates of the sky with water pouring forth, [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. This verse marks the immediate and powerful response to the supplication of Noah. The phrase “We opened the gates of the sky” conveys a dramatic image of the heavens releasing rain not in its usual measure, but as if barriers were removed and the sky itself was unleashed.\nThe expression “pouring water” (māʾin munhamir) indicates continuous, torrential rainfall, far beyond normal patterns—intense, overwhelming, and unceasing.\nTogether, the imagery depicts a cosmic response: the sky is opened above, and (as the following verse will show) the earth will respond from below—leading to a total and inescapable flood.\nThe verse emphasizes that what follows is not a natural event alone, but a divinely commanded and controlled judgment, unfolding in direct response to the prophet’s plea.",
  54012: "We caused the earth to burst forth with springs, so the waters met for a matter already decreed.",
  54013: "We carried him upon ˹a vessel of˺ planks and nails,",
  54014: "sailing under Our [watchful] Eyes—a reward for one who had been rejected.[9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9] The phrase “sailing under Our watchful eyes” signifies complete divine protection, care, and supervision. It conveys that the ark was not merely floating, but was being guided and safeguarded by Allāh at every moment.\nThe expression “Our eyes” affirms that Allāh possesses real attributes as befits His majesty, including Eyes, without likening them to those of His creation and without asking how. This is understood in accordance with the principle of affirming what Allāh affirms for Himself while maintaining His absolute transcendence, as “there is nothing like unto Him.” Thus, it denotes perfect awareness, watchfulness, and protection, without implying any human resemblance.\nThe clause “a reward for one who had been rejected” refers to Noah, who endured persistent denial and hostility from his people. His salvation is presented not only as rescue, but as divine vindication and honor—a recompense for his patience and steadfastness. Together, the verse highlights a profound contrast: rejection by people, followed by protection and reward from Allāh. It reinforces a recurring theme: those who are rejected for upholding the truth are ultimately seen, protected, and rewarded by Allāh.",
  54015: "We have certainly left it as a sign—so is there anyone who will take heed? [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. “Left it as a sign” refers to the lasting lesson and warning derived from the story of Noah—preserved through revelation and, according to many exegetes, also through enduring traces of the Ark known to earlier generations. The “sign” is not merely historical, but moral and spiritual: a reminder of divine justice, the certainty of consequence, and the salvation granted to those who trust and obey. The closing question—“is there anyone who will take heed?”—is a call to reflection, urging the listener to learn before facing a similar reckoning.",
  54016: "Then how ˹terrible˺ were My punishment and My warnings! [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. This verse is a rhetorical exclamation emphasizing the severity and reality of divine judgment. The phrase “how were My punishment and My warnings” is not a question seeking an answer, but a forceful affirmation—drawing attention to the outcome already demonstrated in the destruction of past nations.\nThe pairing of “punishment” and “warnings” highlights a key principle: punishment does not come without prior warning. Messengers were sent, signs were shown, and opportunities for repentance were given—yet they were persistently rejected. Thus, the verse underscores: the truthfulness of the warnings, the inevitability of consequence,\nand the justice of the punishment that follows denial.\nIt serves both as a reminder of what has already occurred and as a warning of what may come, reinforcing that divine judgment is neither arbitrary nor sudden, but preceded by clear guidance and ample warning.",
  54017: "We have certainly made the Qurʾān easy for remembrance—so is there anyone who will take heed? [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. The phrase “made the Qurʾān easy for remembrance” conveys multiple layers of meaning. It indicates that the Qurʾān has been made: easy to recite, through its rhythm and linguistic clarity, easy to memorize, as evidenced by its preservation in the hearts of countless people, and accessible for reflection and understanding, for those who approach it sincerely.\nThe word “remembrance” (dhikr) includes:\nremembering Allāh, taking lessons from its message, and being mindful of guidance and warning.\nThe recurring question “so is there anyone who will take heed?” is a powerful invitation rather than a request for information. It calls the listener to respond—implying that the barrier is not in the message, but in the willingness to reflect and accept.\nThis verse, repeated throughout the surah, emphasizes a central theme: divine guidance has been made accessible—what remains is human response.",
  54018: "The people of ʿĀd denied ˹their prophet Hūd˺—then how ˹terrible˺ were My punishment and My warnings!",
  54019: "Indeed, We sent against them a screaming wind, on a day of unrelenting misfortune,",
  54020: "sweeping people away as if they were trunks of uprooted date-palms.",
  54021: "Then how [terrible] were My punishment and warnings?!",
  54022: "We have certainly made the Qurʾān easy for remembrance—so is there anyone who will take heed?",
  54023: "The people of Thamūd rejected the warnings.",
  54024: "They said, \"Is it a single mortal man among us that we should follow? Then we would surely be in error and madness.\" [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. This statement reflects the mindset of the people of Thamūd in their rejection of Salih. Their objection is not based on lack of evidence, but on arrogance and social bias—they find it unacceptable that a messenger should be an ordinary human being from among themselves.\nThe phrase “a single mortal man from among us” expresses both astonishment and disdain, implying: Why him and not one of us? How can someone like us be chosen above us? Their conclusion, “we would surely be in error and madness,” reveals a reversal of truth: they label guidance as misguidance, and obedience as irrational behavior. This reflects a recurring pattern in the Qur’an: rejecting truth not because it is unclear, but because it challenges pride, status, and expectations.\nThe verse highlights that the barrier to faith is often not intellectual, but psychological and moral—rooted in arrogance, envy, and unwillingness to submit.",
  54025: "Has the Reminder been sent down to him from among us? Rather, he is an insolent liar! [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14] The “Reminder” (adh-dhikr) refers to divine revelation—guidance sent by Allāh to instruct and warn. The people of Thamūd question why such revelation would be given to Salih instead of someone else among them, revealing their envy and sense of entitlement.\nTheir question is not a sincere inquiry, but a form of rejection disguised as doubt. It reflects the same pattern seen in other nations: disbelief rooted in pride rather than lack of evidence.\nThe accusation “an insolent liar” combines:\nfalsehood (liar), and arrogance or reckless boldness (insolent).\nThus, they portray the prophet not only as untruthful, but as someone who presumptuously claims what does not belong to him.\nThis verse highlights a recurring theme:\nwhen truth challenges ego and social hierarchy, it is often dismissed through personal attacks and character assassination, rather than thoughtful engagement with the message itself.",
  54026: "They will soon know—tomorrow—who the insolent liar is.",
  54027: "Indeed, We are sending the she-camel as a trial for them; so watch them [O Ṣāliḥ] and be patient,",
  54028: "and inform them that the water is to be shared between them—each one’s turn of drinking is to be observed. [15]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. This instruction was given to the prophet Salih regarding the miraculous she-camel. The water supply was to be divided in turns: one day for the she-camel,\nand another for the people.\nThe phrase “each one’s turn of drinking is to be observed” indicates that every allotted turn would be: clearly designated,\nfully attended, and not to be violated.\nThis arrangement was itself part of the test: Would they respect the divine command? Or would they transgress against it?\nThe sharing of water—an essential resource—made the trial practical and immediate, affecting their daily lives. It exposed not only belief or disbelief, but also discipline, restraint, and obedience.\nTheir eventual violation of this command would demonstrate their persistence in defiance, leading directly to the consequence that follows.",
  54029: "But they called their companion [16], so he took it upon himself and hamstrung her.\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. “Their companion” refers to the most defiant individual among the people of Thamūd—identified in Qurʾānic exegesis as the one who took responsibility for killing the she-camel. Although a single person carried out the act, the entire community shared in the guilt because they approved of it and supported the decision. “Hamstrung her” denotes the deliberate crippling and killing of the she-camel, a direct violation of Allāh’s command and a decisive act of rebellion after clear warning and fair testing.",
  54030: "Then how ˹terrible˺ were My punishment and My warnings!",
  54031: "Indeed, We sent against them a single blast, and they became like dry fragments of a cattle enclosure. [17]\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. The phrase “a single blast” refers to a sudden, overwhelming cry—an intense sound or shock sent by Allāh that brought instant destruction. Its singularity emphasizes both speed and decisiveness: no prolonged process, but a single, final moment.\nThe image “like dry fragments of a cattle enclosure” describes the aftermath: brittle, broken pieces of wood or vegetation,\ntrampled and scattered debris, reduced to something lifeless and insignificant.\nThis metaphor conveys: the total collapse of their strength, the fragility of what once seemed powerful, and the complete erasure of their presence. The contrast is striking: a once strong and structured people, reduced instantly to dry, scattered remnants.\nThe verse highlights the finality and suddenness of divine judgment, showing that when it comes, even the mightiest are brought to nothing in a single moment.",
  54032: "We have certainly made the Qurʾān easy for remembrance—so is there anyone who will take heed?",
  54033: "The people of Lot rejected the warnings.",
  54034: "Indeed, We sent against them a storm of stones—except the family of Lot; We delivered them before dawn,",
  54035: "as a grace from Us—thus do We reward whoever gives thanks.",
  54036: "He had certainly warned them of Our mighty grasp, but they disputed the warnings.",
  54037: "They had certainly sought to lure him away from his guests, so We obliterated their eyes—‘Then taste My punishment and My warnings!’",
  54038: "And indeed, an abiding punishment overtook them early in the morning.",
  54039: "Taste My punishment and My warnings.",
  54040: "We have certainly made the Qurʾān easy for remembrance—so is there anyone who will take heed?",
  54041: "And indeed, warnings came to the people of Pharaoh. [18]\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. This verse introduces the people of Pharaoh as another example in the recurring pattern of the surah. They were given clear and repeated warnings through the message of Moses, supported by unmistakable signs and miracles.\nThe phrase “warnings came” emphasizes that guidance was delivered fully and repeatedly—leaving no excuse for denial. These warnings were not limited to words, but were reinforced by visible signs that demonstrated divine authority.\nBy including Pharaoh’s people after earlier nations, the Qur’an reinforces a consistent historical pattern: warning is given, it is rejected, and consequence follows.\nThe verse underscores that no people are left without clear guidance, and that the stories of past nations serve as ongoing reminders for those who come after them.",
  54042: "They denied all Our signs, so We seized them with the seizure of One Exalted in Might, Perfect in Ability.",
  54043: "Are your disbelievers better than those ˹earlier ones˺, or do you have an exemption in the Scriptures? [19]\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]  This verse poses a rhetorical challenge to the disbelievers, confronting their sense of security and superiority. After recounting the destruction of past nations—such as those of Noah, ʿĀd, Thamūd, and the people of Pharaoh—the question asks: are you somehow better, such that you would escape a similar fate?\nThe phrase “or do you have an exemption in the Scriptures?” challenges the notion that they possess any divine guarantee of immunity. It implies: there is no special protection granted to them, no written assurance that they will be spared, and no justification for their confidence in avoiding consequence.\nThe verse dismantles false assumptions:\nof superiority, of safety without accountability, and of exemption from the moral pattern seen throughout history.\nIt reinforces a central message: the same principles apply to all—those who reject truth are not beyond consequence, regardless of who they are.",
  54044: "Or do they say, ‘We are a united force, bound to prevail’? [20]\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. This verse exposes another form of false confidence among the disbelievers: reliance on numbers, unity, and worldly strength. The phrase “a united force” suggests a belief that collective power will guarantee victory and protection.\nTheir claim reflects: self-assurance rooted in material strength, trust in alliances and numbers, and a dismissal of the consequences faced by earlier nations.\nHowever, the Qur’an challenges this assumption by placing it in contrast with history: past nations were often stronger, more numerous, and more established,\nyet their unity did not prevent their downfall when they rejected the truth.\nThus, the verse highlights a recurring illusion: that external strength ensures success, when in reality, outcome depends on alignment with truth and divine will.\nIt serves as a warning that unity without righteousness does not guarantee protection, and that reliance on worldly power alone cannot avert divine judgment.",
  54045: "Soon their assembly will be defeated, and they will turn their backs in flight. [21]\\n\\nFootnotes: [21] This verse is a direct response to the claim of strength and unity expressed in the previous verse. The term “assembly” refers to their gathered forces—their alliances, numbers, and collective power in which they place their confidence.\nThe statement “will be defeated” indicates a certain and imminent reversal of their expectations. The word “soon” conveys both nearness and inevitability.\nThe phrase “they will turn their backs in flight” is a clear image of defeat and humiliation: from confidence → to retreat,\nfrom unity → to dispersal, from strength → to weakness. Historically, this has been understood to refer to events such as the defeat of the Quraysh at battles like Battle of Badr, though the wording also carries a broader meaning—affirming that false confidence in worldly power will ultimately collapse.\nThe verse reinforces a central lesson:\nreliance on numbers and strength without truth leads to downfall, and what appears strong may quickly be overturned by divine decree.",
  54046: "Rather, the Hour is their appointed time—and the Hour is more grievous and more bitter. [22]\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]  This verse shifts the focus from worldly defeat to a far greater and ultimate reality: the Hour (the Day of Judgment). While earlier verses speak of their defeat in this life, this statement clarifies that their true appointed reckoning lies in the Hereafter.\nThe phrase “the Hour is their appointed time” indicates a fixed and unavoidable moment when they will face full accountability—no delay, no escape.\nThe description “more grievous and more bitter” emphasizes that: whatever punishment occurs in this world is minor in comparison, and the reality of the Hereafter is far more severe, painful, and enduring. This verse serves to: correct false assumptions that worldly outcomes are the final measure, and redirect attention to the ultimate and decisive judgment. It reinforces a central theme:\nworldly defeat is temporary, but the Hour is the true and final reckoning, far more intense and consequential.",
  54047: "Indeed, the guilty are in error and ˹destined for˺ blazing fire.",
  54048: "On the Day they are dragged into the Fire upon their faces—˹it will be said,˺ ‘Taste the touch of the blazing Fire!’",
  54049: "Indeed, We have created everything with precise measure.",
  54050: "Our command is but one—like the twinkling of an eye.",
  54051: "We have already destroyed those like you—so is there anyone who will take heed?",
  54052: "Everything they did is recorded in the registers.",
  54053: "And everything, small and great, is inscribed. [23]\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. This verse reinforces and expands the previous one by emphasizing the total precision and completeness of divine record-keeping. The phrase “small and great” indicates that: no action is too minor to be recorded, and no deed is too significant to be missed.\nThe term “inscribed” conveys that deeds are not merely noted, but carefully written, preserved, and fixed—leaving no ambiguity or loss. Together, this verse highlights:\nthe absolute comprehensiveness of accountability, the precision of divine justice, and the certainty that every action—however insignificant it may seem—has consequence. In context, it serves as a final reinforcement: nothing escapes record, nothing escapes judgment, and every detail will be brought forth on the Day of Reckoning.",
  54054: "Indeed, the righteous will be in gardens and rivers. [24]\\n\\nFootnotes: [24]. The term “the righteous” (al-muttaqūn) refers to those who possess taqwā—a conscious awareness of Allāh that leads to faith, obedience, and avoidance of wrongdoing.\nThe imagery of “gardens and rivers” represents the delights of Paradise:\ngardens symbolize beauty, peace, and abundance, rivers signify continuous provision, life, and ease.\nThis pairing conveys not only physical comfort, but also: lasting tranquility, spiritual fulfillment, and complete reward.\nIn contrast to the earlier verses describing punishment and destruction, this verse introduces a scene of mercy and reward, showing the ultimate outcome for those who respond to guidance.\nIt highlights a central Qur’anic balance:\nwarning for the rejecters, and glad tidings for the righteous.",
  54055: "In a seat of perfect truth and honor, in the presence of an All-Powerful King. [25].\\n\\nFootnotes: [25]. “A seat of truth and honor” translates maqʿadi ṣidq, which signifies a settled station characterized by absolute truth, sincerity, permanence, and dignity. It is a place free from falsehood, fear, or loss, where Allah’s promise is fully realized and never revoked. This refers to Paradise, and more specifically to a position of nearness and honor granted to the righteous.\n“In the presence of an All-Powerful King” denotes honored nearness in rank and favor. Malīk emphasizes perfect sovereignty and kingship, while Muqtadir highlights irresistible power and complete control. The verse thus concludes the sūrah by affirming that the ultimate reward of the righteous is not only eternal bliss, but exalted nearness to Allāh Himself, under His absolute authority and care.",
  55001: "The Most Compassionate—[Allāh]. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. The name “The Most Compassionate” (Ar-Raḥmān) is one of the most comprehensive names of Allāh, expressing boundless, all-encompassing mercy. It signifies a mercy that extends to all creation—believers and disbelievers alike—in this world.\nUnlike Ar-Raḥīm (The Especially Merciful), which often emphasizes mercy specific to the believers, Ar-Raḥmān denotes universal mercy, encompassing provision, care, and sustenance for all beings.\nBeginning the surah with this name sets its central theme: divine mercy as the foundation of creation, guidance as an expression of that mercy, and blessings as manifestations of it.\nIt prepares the reader for the repeated refrain that follows in the surah:\n“Which of your Lord’s favors will you deny?”\nhighlighting that all blessings ultimately stem from the mercy of Allāh.",
  55002: "He has taught the Qur’an, [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The phrase “He has taught the Qurʾān” presents revelation as one of the greatest manifestations of divine mercy. Immediately after mentioning Ar-Raḥmān (The Most Compassionate), the verse highlights that teaching the Qurʾān is a direct expression of that mercy.\nThe verb “taught” indicates that:\nthe Qurʾān is not human in origin, but is divinely imparted knowledge, conveyed with clarity and guidance.\nNotably, this verse precedes the mention of human creation, emphasizing that: guidance comes before existence in importance, and that the purpose of human life is tied to receiving and living by this revelation. Thus, the verse highlights a profound priority: the greatest gift is not merely creation, but guidance that gives meaning to creation.",
  55003: "He created man,",
  55004: "and taught him speech.",
  55005: "The sun and the moon ˹move˺ by precise calculation, [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3] The phrase “by precise calculation” indicates that the sun and the moon operate according to exact measure, order, and determination. Their movements are not random, but governed by a precise system established by Allāh.\nThis precision is evident in: the regularity of day and night, the calculation of time and seasons, and the structure of lunar and solar cycles.\nThe verse highlights a broader principle:\nthe universe is governed by order, balance, and exact proportion, reflecting divine wisdom and control.\nIn the context of the surah, it points to the harmony of creation: just as revelation is precise, and human creation is purposeful,\nthe cosmos itself is ordered and measured.\nThis serves as a sign for reflection: what is so precisely ordered cannot be without purpose or design.",
  55006: "and the stars and the trees both prostrate [4].\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. The word al-najm may refer either to the celestial stars or to low-growing plants without trunks, while al-shajar denotes trees with upright stems. Both meanings are valid and complementary, indicating that all of creation—whether in the heavens or on the earth, great or small—submits to Allāh. Their “prostration” (sujūd) is not ritual worship like that of humans, but their complete obedience to the laws, order, and purpose set by their Creator. Thus, the verse portrays a universe in total harmony and submission to Allāh’s command.",
  55007: "And the sky—He raised it high, and He set the balance [of justice],",
  55008: "so that you may not transgress in the balance.",
  55009: "Uphold the weighing with justice, and do not fall short in the balance. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. This verse completes the sequence on the balance, translating its principle into practical human conduct.\nThe phrase “uphold the weighing with justice” is a direct command to establish fairness and accuracy in all dealings. It includes: honesty in trade and measurement, fairness in judgment,\nand equity in all forms of interaction.\nThe instruction “do not fall short in the balance” (wa lā tukh’sirū al-mīzān) warns against: cheating, reducing what is due to others, or distorting fairness for personal gain. Together, the verse emphasizes:\nthe application of divine balance in human life, transforming a cosmic principle into a moral obligation.\nIn context, these verses (7–9) form a complete progression: Allāh establishes the balance in creation, humans are commanded not to violate it, and are instructed to actively uphold justice and fairness. Thus, the balance is not merely observed—it must be maintained and protected in all aspects of life.",
  55010: "And the earth—He laid it out for all living beings.",
  55011: "Therein are fruits, and palm trees with clustered dates,",
  55012: "and grain with husk, and fragrant plants.",
  55013: "Then which of the bounties of your Lord will you both deny? [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. This recurring question is addressed jointly to jinn and humankind, as indicated by the dual form in the Arabic (tukadhibān). The word \"bounties\" includes every form of divine favor: creation, sustenance, order in the universe, guidance, mercy, justice, and warning. The question is rhetorical, not seeking an answer, but rebuking denial and ingratitude. Its repetition throughout the sūrah serves to awaken conscience, reinforce accountability, and compel reflection after each mention of Allāh’s blessings or signs. Each recurrence renews the challenge: after witnessing these favors, which of them can truly be denied?",
  55014: "He created man from dry clay like pottery. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. The phrase “man” (al-insān) here is commonly understood to refer to the first human, Adam, and by extension to humanity as a whole.\nThe expression “dry clay like pottery” describes a stage in human creation: dry, hollow clay, capable of producing a sound when struck, similar to fired pottery.\nThis imagery emphasizes: the humble and earthly origin of human beings, their physical fragility, and the transformation from lifeless matter into a living being by divine command.\nIn contrast to earlier verses describing the grandeur and balance of creation, this verse reminds humans of their lowly beginnings, encouraging humility.\nIt also prepares for the contrast in the next verse: human creation from clay, and the creation of jinn from fire. Together, they highlight both diversity in creation and the power of Allāh to create from entirely different elements.",
  55015: "And He created the jinn [8] from a smokeless flame of fire.\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. The jinn are a distinct creation of Allāh, created from “a smokeless flame of fire”, indicating a subtle, penetrating, and energetic origin. Iblīs (Satan) is regarded as the forefather of the jinn, just as Adam is the forefather of humankind.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said:\n“The angels were created from light, the jinn were created from a smokeless flame of fire, and Adam was created from what has been described to you (i.e., sounding clay like the clay of pottery).”\nThis narration clarifies the differing origins of creation, highlighting Allāh’s wisdom and power in creating diverse beings from distinct substances, while all remain equally subject to His command, accountability, and judgment.\"",
  55016: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55017: "He is the Lord of the two sunrises and the Lord of the two sunsets. [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. “The two sunrises and the two sunsets” refer to the extremes of the sun’s rising and setting points over the course of the year—at the summer and winter solstices. As the seasons change, the sun rises and sets at different points on the horizon, reaching two farthest limits. By describing Himself as the Lord of both, Allāh affirms His complete control over time, seasons, celestial motion, and cosmic order. The expression also conveys totality: He is Lord of every sunrise and sunset in between, emphasizing His absolute sovereignty over the heavens and the earth.",
  55018: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55019: "He has released the two seas, meeting together, [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. “The two seas” refers to two bodies of water with distinct properties that come into contact yet remain differentiated by divine decree. It may refer to saltwater and freshwater, such as seas and rivers, or to seas with differing salinity, temperature, or density. Though they meet, Allāh has established natural laws that preserve their distinct characteristics. The verse highlights divine power, wisdom, and balance in creation, demonstrating that even vast and powerful natural forces operate within precise limits set by Allāh.",
  55020: "between them is a barrier—they do not overstep. [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. This verse completes the description of the two seas. The “barrier” (barzakh) refers to a boundary that prevents the two bodies of water from completely overwhelming one another, even as they meet. The phrase “they do not overstep” indicates that: each retains its own properties, neither transgresses the limits set for it,and both remain within the bounds established by Allāh.\nThis may be understood on multiple levels:\nphysical: distinct bodies of water maintaining differences (such as salinity and density),\nsymbolic: a broader principle of limits and boundaries within creation.\nIn context, the verse reinforces a central theme: creation operates with precision, balance, and restraint, and everything functions within divinely established limits. Just as the seas do not transgress their bounds, humans are implicitly reminded to respect the limits set for them.",
  55021: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55022: "From both of them emerge pearl and coral.",
  55023: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55024: "To Him belong the ships that sail upon the sea, towering like mountains. [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. The phrase describes large ships with raised sails moving swiftly across the sea. Their being likened to “mountains” emphasizes their immense size, stability, and majesty, especially striking given the vastness and power of the ocean. By stating that they “belong” to Allāh, the verse affirms that human mastery of navigation, shipbuilding, and maritime trade is itself a divine gift. The winds, buoyancy, materials, and laws that allow such massive vessels to move safely across the sea all operate by Allāh’s permission and design. Thus, even human technological achievement remains subject to divine sovereignty and mercy.",
  55025: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55026: "Everyone upon it will perish. [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. The phrase “everyone upon it” refers to all who are on the earth—humans, jinn, and all living beings. It affirms the universal reality of mortality: no created being is exempt from death.\nThe word “will perish” conveys:\ninevitability, finality, and the transient nature of worldly existence.\nThis verse marks a shift in tone:\nfrom descriptions of provision, beauty, and power, to a reminder of the temporary nature of all creation.\nIt emphasizes a fundamental truth:\neverything in the created world is finite,\nno matter how great, strong, or enduring it may appear. The verse prepares for the contrast in the next verse: while all creation perishes, only Allāh, the Lord remains, eternal and unchanging.",
  55027: "but the Face of your Lord—full of Majesty and Honor—will remain. [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. This verse presents a direct contrast to the previous one: while all creation perishes, “the Face of your Lord” alone remains.\nThe term “Face” affirms a real attribute of Allāh as befits His majesty—without likening it to creation and without asking how. It also conveys the meaning of:\nHis Essence, His eternal being, and that which is directed toward Him alone.\nThe description “full of Majesty and Honor” emphasizes: majesty (jalāl) greatness, power, and awe, honor (ikrām) generosity, nobility, and grace.\nTogether, they reflect the perfection of Allāh: in absolute power, and in perfect generosity.\nThe verse affirms a central truth:\neverything else is temporary and perishing,\nwhile Allāh alone is eternal and enduring.\nIt redirects focus from creation to the Creator: from what fades, to what remains forever.",
  55028: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55029: " All who are in the heavens and the earth ask of Him; every day He is engaged in a matter. [15]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. “Every day He is engaged in some matter” does not imply change, fatigue, or preoccupation on the part of Allāh. Rather, it highlights the continuous manifestation of His divine will and action. Allāh is constantly bringing about His decrees: giving life and causing death, granting provision, answering prayers, forgiving sins, raising and lowering ranks, relieving distress, and administering justice. These acts occur effortlessly and perfectly, without distraction or limitation.\nThe verse thus reinforces that Allāh is ever-present, ever-governing, and ever-merciful, while remaining transcendent above time, need, and all imperfections associated with created beings.",
  55030: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55031: "We shall soon attend to you, O you two weighty beings. [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. The address “O you two beings” refers to humankind and jinn, the two morally responsible creations endowed with choice and accountability.\nThe expression “We shall soon attend to you” does not imply distraction or preoccupation, but conveys:\na deliberate turning toward judgment,\na declaration of imminent accountability,\nand a shift from provision and mercy to reckoning and justice.\nThe term “the two weighty beings” suggests: their significance and responsibility, and the seriousness of their role in responding to divine guidance.\nThis verse marks a transition: from describing blessings and divine activity,\nto a direct warning of coming judgment.\nIt reminds both humans and jinn: their actions are not ignored, and a time will come when they will be fully called to account.",
  55032: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55033: "O assembly of jinn and humankind, if you are able to pass beyond the bounds of the heavens and the earth, then pass—but you will not pass except with authority. [17]\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. The address “O assembly of jinn and humankind” again highlights the dual audience of the surah—both groups who possess awareness, choice, and accountability.\nThe phrase “pass beyond the bounds of the heavens and the earth” refers to attempting to escape: the dominion of Allāh, His power and control, and ultimately accountability and judgment.\nThe challenge “then pass” is rhetorical—it exposes human and jinn limitation. It is not an invitation, but a declaration: such escape is beyond their ability.\nThe statement “you will not pass except with authority” indicates that: no one can transcend these bounds by their own power, only by divine permission and authority could anything occur—something not granted for escape from judgment.\nThis verse emphasizes: the absolute sovereignty of Allāh, the limits of created beings, and the impossibility of evading divine decree. It delivers a powerful message: no matter the ability or advancement, there is no escape from the dominion of Allāh or from accountability to Him.",
  55034: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55035: "There will be sent against you both a flame of fire and molten brass, and you will not be able to defend yourselves.",
  55036: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55037: "When the sky is split apart and turns rose-red, like tanned leather. [18]\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. This refers to the moment when the Day of Resurrection and Judgment occurs. The verse describes a profound cosmic upheaval at the end of time. “Rose-colored” translates wardah, suggesting a deep red or pink hue, while al-dihān refers to oiled or tanned leather, smooth, molten, and glowing. The imagery portrays a sky that loses its familiar solidity and instead appears fluid, radiant, and transformed, signaling the collapse of the present order of creation. This dramatic alteration marks the onset of the Final Judgment, when the heavens themselves are radically changed by Allāh’s command.",
  55038: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55039: "On that Day neither human nor jinn will be asked about his sin. [19]\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. This verse does not negate accountability on the Day of Judgment. Rather, it indicates that at a certain stage no questioning will be needed, because guilt will be self-evident. The sinners will be recognized by their distinct signs, and their deeds will already be known, recorded, and manifest. In other verses, questioning is mentioned at different stages of the Day of Resurrection; thus, the Qurʾān presents multiple phases of judgment. Here, the emphasis is on the overwhelming clarity of guilt—when denial is futile and interrogation unnecessary, and justice proceeds by divine knowledge and visible proof.",
  55040: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55041: "The guilty will be recognized by their marks, and they will be seized by the forelocks and the feet.",
  55042: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55043: "This is Hell which the guilty used to deny. [20]\\n\\nFootnotes: [20]. The term “Hell” refers to the Fire (Jahannam), the place of punishment in the Hereafter. The name “Jahannam” reflects a historical rendering linked to a valley associated with fire, but the Qur’anic term conveys a far greater and ultimate reality.\nThe phrase “which the guilty used to deny” highlights a central theme: what was once rejected as false or improbable, is now encountered as undeniable reality.\nThis moment represents: the collapse of denial, the exposure of truth, and the realization of what had been repeatedly warned. In context, the verse serves as:\na direct confrontation, pointing to the very consequence they dismissed. It reinforces a powerful pattern: denial in this life does not remove reality, it only delays the moment when it must be faced.",
  55044: "They will circulate between it and scalding, boiling water.",
  55045: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55046: "But for the one who fears standing before his Lord, there will be two gardens—",
  55047: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55048: "lush, abounding with spreading branches.",
  55049: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55050: "In both gardens are two flowing springs.",
  55051: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55052: "In both gardens are two kinds of every fruit.",
  55053: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55054: "Reclining on couches lined with brocade, and the fruits of both gardens are close at hand.",
  55055: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55056: "In them are companions of modest, restrained gaze [21], untouched before by any human or jinn.\\n\\nFootnotes: [21]. “Restrained gaze ” signifies exclusive devotion, chastity, and modesty. Their gaze is reserved solely for their own mates, reflecting purity of character and dignity. The phrase does not imply shyness alone, but rather moral refinement, faithfulness, and honored intimacy—free from impropriety or prior contact. This description emphasizes the complete purity and nobility of the reward prepared for the righteous in Paradise.",
  55057: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55058: "As though they were rubies and coral.",
  55059: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55060: "Is the reward for doing good anything but goodness in return?",
  55061: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55062: "And beyond these two are two other gardens.",
  55063: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55064: "Both are richly dark green. [22]\\n\\nFootnotes: [22]. “Dark green” translates mudhāmmatān, a term indicating intense, deep greenness—the color of vegetation at its fullest and most luxuriant stage. \nThis denotes gardens richly irrigated, dense with foliage, and overflowing with life, to the point that their greenness appears almost black from abundance. The description conveys freshness, vitality, and perfection, emphasizing that these gardens are not sparse or fading, but at the height of beauty and flourishing bounty prepared for the righteous.",
  55065: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55066: "In both gardens are two springs, gushing forth abundantly.",
  55067: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55068: "In both gardens are fruits, with palm-dates and pomegranates.",
  55069: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55070: "In them are companions, good and beautiful.",
  55071: " Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55072: "Maidens with wide, lovely eyes, sheltered within pavilions.",
  55073: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55074: "No human or jinn has ever touched them before.",
  55075: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55076: "Reclining on green cushions and richly beautiful carpets.",
  55077: "Then which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?",
  55078: "Blessed is the Name of your Lord, full of majesty and honor. [23]\\n\\nFootnotes: [23]. “Blessed” (tabāraka) signifies abundant, everlasting goodness and exaltation—a blessing that is limitless, and never diminished. “The Name of your Lord” points to Allāh Himself, as He is known, invoked, and worshipped, affirming that all blessing, goodness, and increase flow from Him alone.\n“Full of Majesty and Honor” unites two supreme attributes: Majesty, denoting absolute greatness, awe, and sovereignty; and Honor, denoting generosity, grace, and benevolence toward creation. Together, they balance reverent fear with loving hope.\nThe verse concludes the sūrah by directing hearts to glorification, gratitude, and exclusive devotion to the Eternal Lord—whose majesty inspires reverence and whose honor bestows mercy. \nAs we discover hidden treasures within Allāh’s creation in this world, we are reminded that far greater delights await in the Hereafter. There, believers will worship Allāh alone, their Creator, and praise Him forever, acknowledging that all existence and blessing originate by His will and endure through His grace.",
  56001: "When the Inevitable Event occurs— [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. “The Inevitable Event” translates al-wāqiʿah, a term denoting something certain to occur and impossible to avert. It refers to the Day of Resurrection, when the present order of creation ends and divine judgment begins. The wording emphasizes inevitability and certainty: what has been promised by Allāh will unquestionably come to pass. By opening the sūrah with this expression, the Qurʾān confronts the listener with the reality of the Hereafter before any description, allowing no room for denial, delay, or escape.\nThis phrase sets a solemn tone for the chapter, preparing the reader for the coming contrasts between those who are honored and those who are disgraced on that Day.",
  56002: "there is no denying its occurrence;",
  56003: "bringing some low and raising others high. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. This verse describes the radical reversal of ranks that will occur on the Day of Resurrection. Those who were arrogant, oppressive, or heedless in this world will be brought low, while those who were humble, faithful, and righteous will be raised in honor and rank. Worldly status, wealth, lineage, and power will carry no weight; only faith and deeds will matter. The expression emphasizes that the Hereafter is a realm of perfect justice, where true worth is revealed and every soul is placed in its rightful position by Allāh.",
  56004: "When the earth is shaken with a violent shaking,",
  56005: "and the mountains are ground to powder,",
  56006: "and become scattered dust,",
  56007: "And you will be stored into three groups:",
  56008: "as for those on the right—how blessed the people of the right are! [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. “Those on the right” refers to the people who will be honored and saved on the Day of Resurrection. The “right” symbolizes righteousness, success, and divine favor, as such people will receive their records in their right hands and be admitted into Paradise. The rhetorical repetition in the verse conveys admiration and exaltation, emphasizing how blessed, fortunate, and dignified their final state will be.",
  56009: "As for those on the left—how wretched the people of the left are! [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. “Those on the left” refers to the people who will be disgraced and condemned on the Day of Resurrection. The “left” symbolizes failure, misguidance, and loss, as such people will receive their records in their left hands and be destined for punishment. The rhetorical expression conveys alarm, pity, and dread, emphasizing the misery and ruin of their final outcome due to disbelief, arrogance, and persistent wrongdoing.",
  56010: "But the foremost [in faith and virtue] will be the foremost [in entering Paradise].",
  56011: "Those are the ones brought near to Allāh,",
  56012: "In gardens of bliss,",
  56013: "many from the earlier generations,",
  56014: "and a few from the later generations,",
  56015: "on couches intricately inlaid with gold,",
  56016: "reclining on them, facing one another.",
  56017: "They will be attended by immortal youths,",
  56018: "with cups, jugs, and a drink from a flowing spring,",
  56019: "that causes neither headache nor intoxication.",
  56020: "and fruits of whatever kind they choose,",
  56021: "and meat of birds that they desire.",
  56022: "And companions with wide, beautiful eyes,",
  56023: "like well-protected pearls,",
  56024: "as a reward for what they used to do.",
  56025: "They will not hear therein any vain or sinful speech,",
  56026: "only the greeting, \"Peace—peace.\"",
  56027: "As for those on the right—how blessed the people of the right are!",
  56028: "[They will be] among thornless lote trees,",
  56029: "and banana trees in clustered layers, [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The term ṭalḥ is widely interpreted as referring to the banana tree; manḍūd denotes fruit arranged in contiguous layers or clusters, emphasizing abundance and ease of access. An alternative reading identifies ṭalḥ as a fruit-laden acacia.",
  56030: "and an ever-stretched shade,",
  56031: "and water flowing forth,",
  56032: "and abundant fruit—",
  56033: "neither ending nor forbidden,",
  56034: "and raised couches,",
  56035: "Indeed, We created them [their spouses] with a distinct creation,",
  56036: "and made them virgins,",
  56037: "devoted in affection, loving and responsive, equal in age,",
  56038: "for the people of the right—",
  56039: "many from the former generations,",
  56040: "and many from the later generations.",
  56041: "As for those on the left—how miserable the people of the left are! [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. Those whose records are given in their left hand—the people of Hell. The interrogative form in Arabic expresses dread and censure, not a request for information, and intentionally echoes the earlier rhetorical praise of those on the right.",
  56042: "[They will be] amid scorching winds and scalding water,",
  56043: "and a shade of dense black smoke,",
  56044: "neither cool nor refreshing.",
  56045: "Indeed, before that, they lived in a life of indulgent luxury,",
  56046: "and they persisted in the great sin, [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. The great sin denotes obstinate persistence in grave wrongdoing, often interpreted as associating partners with Allāh, and more broadly as sustained disbelief, denial of resurrection, or habitual violation of binding religious obligations, such as neglecting the prescribed prayer and failing to pay zakāt.",
  56047: "And they would say, “After we die and become dust and bones, will we really be raised again?”",
  56048: "And our forefathers as well?\"",
  56049: "Say, “Indeed, the earlier and the later generations,",
  56050: " will surely be gathered for the appointment of a known Day.",
  56051: "Then indeed you—O misguided deniers—",
  56052: "will surely eat from the tree of Zaqqum,",
  56053: "filling your stomachs with it,",
  56054: "and then drinking boiling water over it,",
  56055: "drinking like thirsty camels.”",
  56056: "Such is their welcome on the Day of Judgment.",
  56057: "It is We who created you—so why, then, do you not believe in resurrection?",
  56058: "Have you considered the semen you emit?",
  56059: "Is it you who create it, or are We the Creator?",
  56060: "We have ordained death among you, and none can outstrip Us,",
  56061: "that We may replace you with others and raise you up in forms unknown to you.",
  56062: "You are well aware of the first creation—so will you not take heed?",
  56063: "Have you considered what you sow?",
  56064: "Is it you who make it grow, or are We the Growers? [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. The Arabic verb tazraʿūnahu denotes cultivating or causing growth, while al-zāriʿūn (“the Growers”) attributes true causation to Allāh. Neither the one who plants nor the one who waters is anything in themselves; it is Allāh alone who causes growth. Humans sow and tend, but growth itself lies beyond their control. The plural “We” is the majestic plural.",
  56065: "If We willed, We could reduce it to ruin, leaving you in bewilderment and grief,",
  56066: "[saying], “We have indeed suffered loss, [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. The term literally denotes being burdened with debt or liability, but it extends to severe loss, ruin, or an inescapable calamity. In the context of a destroyed harvest, it expresses the cry of those who see themselves financially ruined and helpless.",
  56067: "rather, we are utterly deprived.”",
  56068: "Have you considered the water you drink?",
  56069: "Is it you who bring it down from the clouds, or is it We who bring it down?",
  56070: "If We willed, We could make it bitter—so will you not be grateful?",
  56071: "Have you considered the fire you kindle?",
  56072: "Is it you who produced its tree, or are We the Ones who produced it?",
  56073: "We made it a reminder and a provision for the wayfarers. [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. The Arabic al-muqwīn originally denotes travelers or wayfarers in barren lands, especially those in need of fire for warmth, cooking, and survival. By extension, it includes all humanity, since fire serves as both a reminder—of divine power and the Hellfire—and a provision of practical benefit and mercy in worldly life.",
  56074: "So glorify the Name of your Lord, the Most Great.",
  56075: "So I swear by the positions of the stars,",
  56076: "Indeed, it is a mighty oath—if only you knew.",
  56077: "Indeed, this is a noble Qur’an, [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. The adjective “noble” (karīm) conveys both intrinsic excellence and honored status. Applied to the Qur’an, it denotes a revelation that is precious, exalted, and worthy of the highest reverence, both in its origin and in its content.\nEngagement with the Qur’an—through regular recitation and sincere adherence to its worldview and teachings—cultivates a moral transformation whereby idolatry and sin become detestable. In Qur’anic ethics, the proper fear of Allāh is inseparable from a hatred of evil: to fear Allāh is to reject pride, arrogance, corrupt behavior, and perverse speech. Thus, the Word of Allāh nurtures a healthy, reverent fear that leads to obedience, guiding believers toward actions that are pleasing to Him and causing sin to appear increasingly repulsive.",
  56078: "In a well-Preserved Record, [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. \"In a well-Preserved Record\" denotes a divinely protected record, commonly understood as the heavenly register or Preserved Tablet, underscoring the Qur’an’s safeguarded and exalted origin.",
  56079: "None touch it except the purified—[13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. The phrase al-muṭahharūn (“the purified”) is understood primarily as referring to the angels, emphasizing the Qur’an’s presence in a heavenly, safeguarded realm—specifically in the Preserved Tablet (al-Lawḥ al-Maḥfūẓ) with Allāh—rather than making a legal ruling about ritual purity for human handling. This reading highlights the Qur’an’s transcendence and protection in the unseen domain.\nSome later scholars extend the verse’s implication to include reverence and purity in one’s approach to the Qur’an, deriving an ethical or devotional dimension from the wording. However, in its immediate context, the verse underscores the Qur’an’s sanctity, elevation, and preserved origin, rather than establishing a jurisprudential regulation concerning physical contact.",
  56080: "a revelation from the Lord of the worlds.",
  56081: "Is it this discourse that you hold in such disregard?",
  56082: " and instead of giving thanks for the provision, you deny [Allāh, the Provider]?",
  56083: "Then why, when the soul reaches the throat in its ascent,",
  56084: "while at that moment you look on in helplessness,",
  56085: "when We are nearer [14] to him than you, but you do not see?\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. Allāh’s nearness here refers to His knowledge, power, and absolute authority, while the angels are nearer in actual presence. Divine “nearness” does not denote physical proximity but signifies complete control and decisive action at the moment of death, enacted by Allāh’s command and decree, often through the angels entrusted with taking the soul. The contrast highlights human limitation: people witness death outwardly, yet remain unable to perceive the unseen reality of divine action.",
  56086: "Then why, if you are not to be [resurrected and recompensed, do you not—",
  56087: "bring the soul back [to the body], if you are truthful?",
  56088: "Then if he [15] is among those drawn near [to Us], [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. The pronoun “he” refers to the dying person whose soul is being taken, as described in the preceding verses (56:83–87). The shift from direct address to the third person marks the transition from challenge to judgment and outcome.\n[16]. Arabic al-muqarrabīn (“those drawn near”) denotes a distinct rank of believers granted special closeness to Allāh. The Qur’an and the Sunnah identify them as those foremost in faith, sincerity, and obedience, whose nearness is bestowed by divine grace, not attained by human effort alone. This nearness signifies honor, acceptance, and priority in reward.",
  56089: "then for him are rest, fragrant delight, and a Garden of Bliss.",
  56090: "If he is one of the people of the right,",
  56091: "“Peace be to you—you are among the people of the right.”",
  56092: "But if he is one of the misguided deniers,",
  56093: "then for him is a welcome of boiling water,",
  56094: "and burning in the Blazing Fire.",
  56095: "Indeed, this is the absolute truth.",
  56096: "So glorify the Name of your Lord, the Most Great.",
  57001: "Whatever is in the heavens and the earth glorifies Allāh. He is the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.",
  57002: "To Him belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth. He gives life and causes death, and He is Most Powerful over all things.",
  57003: "He is the First and the Last, the Manifest [1] and the Hidden [2]. And He is the Knower of all things.\\n\\nFootnotes: [1].  al-Ẓāhir: literally “the Manifest” or “the Most High.” He is evident through His creation and revelation, with nothing above Him. The name affirms that Allāh is manifest in His signs, supreme in authority, and exalted beyond all creation. It conveys clear dominion and absolute transcendence.\n[2] al-Bāṭin: literally “the Hidden” or “the Most Near.” He is beyond human physical perception, with nothing closer than Him in knowledge or power. The name signifies that Allāh is nearer than all else in awareness and presence, though unseen—denoting perfect inward knowledge of all realities and complete nearness without any physical indwelling.",
  57004: "It is He who created the heavens and the earth in six Days, then rose over the Throne. He knows whatever enters the earth and whatever emerges from it, whatever descends from the heaven and whatever ascends to it. And He is with you wherever you may be [3]. And Allāh sees all that you do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. Divine “withness” here does not imply physical presence or indwelling. Rather, it signifies Allāh’s encompassing knowledge, awareness, authority, and protection, by which nothing escapes His sight or control. It affirms that Allāh is exalted above the Throne while being “with” His creation through His knowledge and power. The verse thus combines transcendence (“rose over the Throne”) with immediacy (“He is with you”), without contradiction.",
  57005: "To Him belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth, and to Allāh all matters are returned [for decision].",
  57006: "He merges the night into the day and merges the day into the night, and He knows what is within the chests [of people] [4].\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. The expression “what is within the chests” refers to inner thoughts, intentions, motives, and concealed beliefs. It emphasizes that Allāh’s knowledge encompasses not only outward actions but also the deepest, unspoken realities of the human heart, beyond what others can perceive or judge. Allāh alone is God; there is no deity besides Him. Those who obey His commands are truly blessed.",
  57007: "Believe in Allāh and His Messenger, and spend from what He has entrusted to you. Those among you who believe and spend [in the cause of Allāh] shall have a mighty reward [i.e., Paradise].",
  57008: "Why should you not believe in Allāh, when the Messenger calls you to believe in your Lord, and He has already taken your covenant—if you are truly believers?",
  57009: "It is He who sends down clear verses upon His servant [Muhammad], to bring you out from utter darkness into light. Indeed, Allāh is Kind and Merciful to you.",
  57010: "Why should you not spend in the cause of Allāh, when to Allāh belongs the inheritance of the heavens and the earth? Those among you who spent and fought before the Conquest [of Makkah] are not equal to those who did so afterwards. They are higher in rank than those who spent and fought later. Yet to each Allāh has promised the best. And Allāh is All-Aware of what you do.",
  57011: "Who is it that will lend to Allāh a goodly loan [5], so He will multiply it for him, and he will have a noble reward?\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The expression “a goodly loan” is a metaphor for sincere spending in the cause of Allāh—given willingly, from lawful means, without reproach, ostentation, or expectation of worldly return. The “loan” is to Allāh in the sense that He guarantees its repayment and multiplication, transforming charity into an act of trust, devotion, and certainty. The promise of multiplication underscores divine generosity, not divine need.\nKindness to the poor is thus described as a loan to Allāh, for which He Himself becomes the Guarantor and Rewarder.\nThis meaning is illustrated in a report from one of the Companions: while traveling with the Messenger of Allāh, Muhammad ﷺ, a rider appeared looking from side to side. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said: “Whoever has an extra mount should give it to one who has none, and whoever has surplus provision should give it to one without provision.” He continued naming different possessions until the Companions realized that no one had a right to surplus while another was in need.\nThis teaching establishes that goodly loan encompasses meeting essential human needs—such as food, clothing, shelter, and transport—when one has more than suffices. Whoever has two garments should share with one who has none; whoever has excess food should share with the hungry. The verse thus refers not merely to optional charity, but to ethical responsibility rooted in compassion and social justice.",
  57012: "On the Day you will see the believing men and women, their light shining before them and on their right [6]. They will be told, “Good news for you today: gardens beneath which rivers flow, where you will abide forever.” That is the great triumph.\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. The light granted to the believers represents the manifestation of faith, sincerity, and righteous deeds on the Day of Resurrection. It guides them safely across the darkness of that Day and toward their final abode. Its appearing ahead of them signifies guidance and direction, while its being on their right reflects honor and acceptance, in contrast to those whose records are given from the left. The Qur’an and the Sunnah relate this light to one’s faith and deeds in worldly life, varying in intensity according to sincerity, obedience, and moral integrity.\nAllāh has placed within the believers a light—the light of faith, Islam, truth, and goodness—and He wills that this light shine in the world. Acts of kindness, honesty, patience, humility, forgiveness, and compassion are expressions of that light. Helping those in need, restraining pride when one could boast, and speaking the truth even when it is difficult are ways in which this light reaches hearts, often without words.\nYet the purpose of this light is not self-display or praise, but that the Majesty of Allāh be recognized through one’s conduct. Good deeds become a mirror reflecting His mercy, wisdom, and compassion. Thus, believers are called to walk gently yet brightly—living in such a way that their lives become a silent invitation to Allāh, not to themselves. When goodness is witnessed, hearts are moved to remember Him, and to say: “How beautiful is the guidance of Allāh.”",
  57013: "On the Day when the hypocrite men and women will say to those who believed, “Wait for us, so we may take from your light.” It will be said, “Go back and seek a light [7].” Then a wall will be placed between them, with a gate: inside it is mercy, and outside it is torment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. That is, to where light could have been acquired—namely, in worldly life. The command “Go back behind you and seek a light” is scornful and condemnatory, not literal. It signifies that light cannot be borrowed or obtained at that moment; it must be earned beforehand through sincere faith and righteous deeds. The erection of a wall marks the final and irreversible division between genuine belief and hypocrisy: mercy lies on the side of faith, while punishment confronts those whose belief was merely outward, lacking inward sincerity.",
  57014: "They will call out to them, “Were we not with you?” They will reply, “Yes—but you tempted yourselves, and you waited and doubted, and were deluded by false hopes until the command of Allāh came, while the deceiver [Satan] deceived you about Allāh. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. “The deceiver” refers to Satan, whose deception lies not merely in urging sin, but in cultivating false hopes, procrastination, and complacency—leading people to presume upon Allāh’s mercy while neglecting repentance and obedience. The phrase “deceived you about Allāh” signifies being misled into distorted assumptions about divine forgiveness, justice, and accountability, until the moment for action had irrevocably passed.\nBelievers are therefore urged to remain spiritually sober and vigilant, exercising restraint from evil and self-deception. Satan is a persistent enemy, seeking to mislead and entangle people in false security, until they become his companions in the Blazing Fire. True safety lies in awareness, humility, repentance, and steadfast obedience to Allāh.",
  57015: "So today no ransom will be accepted from you [hypocrites], nor from those who disbelieved. Your abode is the Fire—it is most fitting for you. What a miserable destination.",
  57016: "Has the time not yet come for those who believe that their hearts should humble themselves to the remembrance of Allāh and to what has come down of the truth, and that they should not be like those who were given the Scripture before them [9]—then time grew long for them, so their hearts hardened [10], and many of them are defiantly disobedient?\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. Those who were given the Scripture before them refers primarily to earlier religious communities, especially the Jews and Christians. The verse does not criticize revelation itself, but warns against allowing the passage of time to weaken spiritual responsiveness and humility toward divine guidance.\n[10]. Their hearts hardened indicates a spiritual condition marked by diminished humility, reduced receptivity to remembrance of God, and moral insensitivity. In Qurʾānic usage, hardness of heart results from prolonged heedlessness rather than from the mere passage of time itself.",
  57017: "Know that Allāh revives the earth after its death [11]. We have made the signs clear to you, so that you may understand.\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. Reviving the earth after its death functions on two interconnected levels. Literally, it refers to the observable renewal of barren land through rain, a recurrent Qur’anic sign of divine power. Metaphorically, it is explained as the revival of dead hearts through guidance, repentance, and faith after periods of spiritual heedlessness or moral decay. The verse thus reinforces the preceding exhortation by reminding the audience that renewal—whether physical or spiritual—occurs only through divine mercy, and that the signs are made clear to awaken understanding and moral responsiveness.",
  57018: "Indeed, the charitable men and women, and those who lend to Allāh a goodly loan [12]—it will be multiplied for them, and they will have a noble reward.\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. He who is gracious to a poor man lends to Allāh, and Allāh will repay him for his bountiful deed. The expression a goodly loan (qarḍan ḥasanan) describes voluntary charity given sincerely, without reproach or harm, and solely in pursuit of Allāh’s pleasure. The metaphor emphasizes the certainty of return from Allāh and the multiplication of reward.",
  57019: "Those who believe in Allāh and His messengers—they are the truthful and the witnesses with their Lord. For them is their reward and their light. But those who disbelieve and deny Our signs—they are the people of the Fire.",
  57020: "Know that the life of this world is only play, amusement, adornment, mutual boasting among you, and rivalry in wealth and children. It is like rain whose growth delights the farmers; then it withers, and you see it turn yellow; then it becomes debris. And in the Hereafter there is either severe punishment or forgiveness from Allāh and His pleasure. And the life of this world is nothing but the enjoyment of delusion. [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. This verse cautions against allowing worldly life to dominate one’s affections or determine ultimate purpose. Rather than condemning the world in itself, it warns against conformity to its patterns of distraction, rivalry, and illusion, which obscure recognition of Allāh’s will and good pleasure. True orientation requires turning one’s affections away from the world as an end in itself and directing them toward Allāh alone, seeking His aid in deepening love and devotion. Out of His bounty, Allāh provided the best model in His final messenger, the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ, who urged a posture of detachment, saying: “Be in this world as though you were a stranger or a traveler.” He further advised making conscious use of life and health before their loss, underscoring human mortality and accountability. His own practice embodied this teaching: when marks from a simple reed mat were seen on his side, he explained that his relationship to the world was like that of a rider who rests briefly in the shade of a tree, then continues on his journey, leaving it behind. Together, these teachings reinforce the verse’s message that worldly enjoyment is fleeting, and that lasting success lies in mindful preparation for the Hereafter.",
  57021: "Race toward forgiveness from your Lord and a Paradise as vast as the heavens and the earth, prepared for those who believe in Allāh and His messengers. That is the bounty of Allāh; He grants it to whom He wills. Allāh is the Possessor of great bounty. [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. This verse redirects human competitiveness away from worldly rivalry toward spiritual striving. The command to “race” emphasizes urgency and intentional effort in seeking forgiveness and divine nearness, presenting moral excellence as a field of positive competition. The description of Paradise as vast as the heavens and the earth underscores the boundlessness of divine generosity, contrasting sharply with the limited and fleeting nature of worldly gain described in the preceding verses. By grounding this reward in belief in Allāh and His messengers, the verse affirms faith as the essential foundation of salvation, while its repeated emphasis on bounty (faḍl) makes clear that entry into Paradise is not earned by human merit alone but granted through divine grace, bestowed according to God’s will and wisdom.",
  57022: "No calamity befalls the earth or yourselves but it is in a record before We bring it into being. Indeed, that is easy for Allāh.",
  57023: "So that you may not grieve over what you have missed [15], nor exult over what He has given you. Allāh does not love every self-conceited, boastful person.\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. Do not lose hope because of the past. Allāh can create new opportunities and solutions even in the most difficult situations.",
  57024: "Those who are miserly and enjoin miserliness upon others. Whoever turns away—then indeed, Allāh is the Self-Sufficient, the Praiseworthy.",
  57025: "Indeed, We sent Our messengers with clear evidences, and We sent down with them the Book and the Balance, so that mankind may uphold justice. And We sent down iron, wherein is great might and benefits for the people, and so that Allāh may know who supports Him and His messengers in the unseen. Indeed, Allāh is All-Strong, All-Mighty.",
  57026: "We sent Noah and Abraham, and placed among their offspring prophethood and the Scripture. Some of them are rightly guided, yet many of them are defiantly disobedient.",
  57027: "Then We sent, following in their footsteps, Our messengers, and after them We sent Jesus, son of Mary, and gave him the Gospel. We placed compassion and mercy in the hearts of his followers. As for monasticism, they invented it—we did not prescribe it for them—seeking Allāh’s pleasure, yet they did not observe it properly. So We gave the believers among them their reward, but many of them are defiantly disobedient.",
  57028: "O you who believe [16], be mindful ofAllāh and believe in His Messenger. He will grant you a double share of His mercy, and make for you a light by which you shall walk, and forgive you. Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. The expression refers to the People of the Scripture, namely the believing Jewish and Christian communities, who received earlier revealed scriptures before the Qur’an.",
  57029: "So that the People of the Scripture may know that they have no power over anything of Allāh’s bounty [17], and that all bounty is in the hand of Allāh [18], He grants it to whom He wills. Allāh is the Possessor of immense bounty.\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. This clause addresses the People of the Scripture and corrects any claim to exclusive entitlement to divine favor. It affirms that lineage, prior revelation, or communal status does not grant control over Allāh’s grace; all bounty remains beyond human ownership or guarantee.\n[18]. The correct approach regarding the attributes of Allāh is to affirm its meaning in a real sense, without likening Him to His creation, as Allāh said of Himself in the Qur’an: “There is nothing like unto Him, and He is the All-Hearer, the All-Seer” [Q 42:11]. So we affirm all of the meanings that are mentioned in the Qur'an and the Sunnah according to their apparent meaning, and we reject any likening of Him to His creation. Hence we say: He, may He be glorified and exalted, hears all sounds, but not through any physical faculty comparable to those of the sons of Adam. Similarly, He sees all people with vision that is not like the vision of the sons of Adam, which is a physical faculty of theirs. He has two hands, both a right hand, and He has fingers, but not in a physical sense like those of the sons of Adam; rather His two hands are outstretched, bestowing blessings upon creation, not withholding good. And He has a face, but it is not like the physical faces of the sons of Adam that are made of flesh and blood. We say that He smiles upon whomever He will of His creation, but we do not say that this is showing teeth (like a human smile); and He descends every night to the lowest heaven. The divine attributes mentioned in the Qur'an and Sunnah are to be affirmed without discussing how or likening Him to His creation, and without denying any of His attributes or interpreting them in a way different from the apparent intended meaning. We do not differentiate between any of the divine attributes, no matter what category they come under. Every divine attribute that is mentioned in the Qur'an and the authentic Sunnah must be affirmed.",
  58001: "Indeed, Allāh has heard the statement of the woman [1] who was arguing with you, [O Prophet], concerning her husband [2] and directing her complaint to Allāh [3]. Allāh hears your mutual dialogue. Indeed, Allāh is All-Hearing, All-Seeing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. “the woman” (who was arguing with you) refers to a specific woman, traditionally identified as Khawlah bint Thaʿlabah, whose case prompted the revelation. The Qur’an preserves her voice as an example of appeal, persistence, and divine response. For Allāh knows what we need even before we ask Him. Yet He invites us to ask, to seek, and to knock in prayer. Every supplication draws us closer to Allāh, the One who alone can respond. We are not strangers to Allāh in prayer—He knows us, and He wants to hear from us. We may bring our concerns to Him at any moment. Allāh will answer. As He says, “Call upon Me, and I will respond to you.”\n[2]. “concerning her husband” refers to her husband, traditionally identified as Aws ibn al-Ṣāmit, and to a marital dispute involving the pre-Islamic practice of ẓihār, in which a husband would liken his wife to a woman permanently forbidden to him (such as his mother), thereby suspending the marriage without formally dissolving it.\n[3]. This occurred after she had not received a favorable response from the Prophet ﷺ, who informed her that no revelation had yet come to him regarding the matter. Thereupon, this passage was revealed, addressing her complaint, abolishing the pre-Islamic practice of ẓihār, and establishing justice in its place.",
  58002: "Those among you who pronounce ẓihār against their wives [4]—declaring them unlawful like their mothers—they are not their mothers. Their mothers are only those who gave birth to them. And indeed, they are saying an objectionable statement and falsehood. Yet Allāh is surely Most Pardoning, Most Forgiving.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. This refers to the statement addressed to one’s wife, “You are to me like the back of my mother,” by which she was declared unlawful for marital intimacy. This pre-Islamic practice, known as ẓihār, was subsequently abolished by the revelation of the verses that follow.",
  58003: "Those who pronounce ẓihār against their wives—likening them to their mothers—and then retract what they have said must free a slave before the two of them touch one another [5]. This is what you are admonished with. Allāh is fully aware of all that you do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. That is, engage in marital intimacy.",
  58004: "But whoever cannot find [a slave to free] must fast for two consecutive months before the two of them touch [6] one another. And if one is unable to do so, then he must feed sixty needy people. This is ordained so that you may truly believe in Allāh and His Messenger. These are the limits set by Allāh. And for the disbelievers there is a painful punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. That is, resume marital intimacy. \nAs in the previous verse, this indicates that the prescribed expiation must be completed before the resumption of marital relations, emphasizing the seriousness of the declaration and the necessity of atonement prior to reconciliation.",
  58005: "Indeed, those who oppose Allāh and His Messenger will be disgraced, just as those before them were disgraced. We have certainly sent down clear revelations. And for the disbelievers there is a humiliating punishment.",
  58006: "On the Day when Allāh will resurrect them all and inform them of what they did—Allāh has kept full account of it, though they have forgotten it. Allāh is Witness over all things.",
  58007: "Have you not seen that Allāh knows whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth? There is no private counsel of three but He is the fourth of them, nor of five but He is the sixth of them—nor of fewer than that, nor more—except that He is with them wherever they may be [7]. Then, on the Day of Resurrection, He will inform them of what they did. Indeed, Allāh is the Knower of everything. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. That is, by His perfect knowledge and all-encompassing power. Nothing escapes Him, and no gathering, thought, or whisper occurs except that it is fully known to Him.\n[8]. Allāh knows everything in complete detail—He knows where people are, what they have done, and what lies within their hearts. His being ‘with’ His creation is through His knowledge and power, while He is exalted above His creation, above His Throne, in a manner befitting His majesty. He watches over all things without limitation. Nothing is hidden from Him—neither in the heavens nor on the earth—and every matter ultimately returns to His perfect knowledge.",
  58008: "Have you not seen those who were forbidden from holding secret counsels, yet they return to what they were forbidden—conferring privately in sin, hostility, and disobedience to the Messenger? And when they come to you, they greet you with words by which Allāh does not greet you, and they say to themselves, ‘Why does Allāh not punish us for what we say [9]?’ Hell is sufficient for them; they will burn in it. What a terrible destination.\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. They said this in mockery and arrogance, assuming that Allāh would not hold them accountable for their words. Their delay in punishment was not approval, but respite. Allāh records every statement and intention, and His forbearance should never be mistaken for neglect. When judgment comes, nothing spoken or concealed will be ignored.",
  58009: "O believers, when you confer privately, do not confer in sin, hostility, or disobedience to the Messenger. Rather, confer together in righteousness and piety. And fear Allāh, to whom you will all be gathered.",
  58010: "Holding secret counsels is only from Satan, meant to grieve the believers. But he will not harm them at all except by the permission of Allāh. So in Allāh alone let the believers place their complete trust.",
  58011: "O believers, when it is said to you, ‘Make room in your assemblies,’ then make room. Allāh will make room for you [10]. And when it is said, ‘Rise up [for a good deed],’ then rise up. Allāh will raise in rank those of you who believe, and those given knowledge to higher ranks. Allāh is fully aware of all that you do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. That is, Allāh will make room for you in His mercy, favor, and provision—both in this life and in the Hereafter. Just as you show generosity and consideration to others, Allāh grants you spiritual expansion, honor, and ease.\nThis verse was revealed concerning making space in gatherings so that those who arrive may find a place to sit; in return, Allāh promises to make room for such people through His grace. However, its meaning is broader than this specific context. Whoever gives relief to someone in hardship, Allāh grants him relief from His bounty. Whoever comforts a broken heart, Allāh comforts his heart. Whoever eases the pain of a believer, Allāh eases his pain. Whoever wipes away a tear, Allāh wipes away his tears. There is none more generous or more faithful than Almighty Allāh. Acts of goodness and compassion are a means of protection from harm and calamity.",
  58012: "O believers, when you wish to consult the Messenger privately, then offer charity before your consultation. That is better for you and purer. But if you cannot find anything to give, then Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  58013: "Are you hesitant to present charities before your consultation? Since you did not do so, and Allāh has turned to you in mercy, then establish the prayer, give the poor-rate, and obey Allāh and His Messenger. Allāh is fully aware of all that you do.",
  58014: "Have you not seen those [hypocrites] who take as allies a people with whom Allāh has become angry [11]? They are neither of you nor of them, and they knowingly swear to lies. [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. This refers to those among the Jews who disbelieved and thereby earned Allah’s anger through persistent disbelief, rebellion, and opposition to His guidance. Aligning oneself with those who incur Allah’s wrath reflects corrupted loyalty and a heart detached from faith, seeking protection or benefit where none can truly be found.\n[12]. That is, they deliberately swear that they are Muslims while fully aware of their lies. Their oaths are not mistakes or slips of the tongue, but calculated acts of deception used to conceal hypocrisy and mislead others. Yet the hypocrites do not realize that nothing is hidden from Allāh.",
  58015: "Allāh has prepared for them a severe punishment. Evil indeed is what they used to do.",
  58016: "They have taken their [false] oaths as a shield [for their misdeeds], thereby turning others away from Allāh’s way. So for them there is a humiliating punishment.",
  58017: "Neither their wealth nor their children will avail them anything against Allāh. They are the people of the Hellfire; they will abide therein forever.",
  58018: "On the Day Allāh will resurrect them all, they will swear to Him just as they swear to you, thinking they have a valid basis [13]. Truly, they are the liars.\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. They imagine that their false oaths will benefit them before Allāh, just as they used them in this world to deceive people and protect themselves. But on that Day, excuses and lies will be of no avail. What they relied upon will collapse, and their deception will be fully exposed.",
  58019: "Satan has taken hold of them and made them forget the remembrance of Allāh. They are the party of Satan. Truly, it is the party of Satan who are the losers.",
  58020: "Indeed, those who oppose Allāh and His Messenger—those are among the most humiliated.",
  58021: "Allāh has written, ‘I will surely prevail—I and My messengers.’ Indeed, Allāh is Strong, Mighty.",
  58022: "You will not find people who believe in Allāh and the Last Day loving those who oppose Allāh [14] and His Messenger—even if they are their fathers, sons, brothers, or relatives. They are the ones in whose hearts Allāh has written faith and strengthened them with a spirit from Him [15]. He will admit them into Gardens beneath which rivers flow, abiding there forever. Allāh is pleased with them, and they are pleased with Him. They are the party of Allāh. Truly, it is the party of Allāh who are the successful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. That is, those who persist in opposition to Allāh and His Messenger through disbelief, hostility, or active resistance to divine guidance. This verse does not prohibit natural kindness, justice, or good conduct toward relatives who disbelieve; rather, it negates loyalty of the heart that compromises faith. True belief places allegiance to Allāh above all ties, even the closest bonds of family.\nAllāh has created a universe that bears witness to His existence and operates according to His law. To live in defiance of that law is folly. For a person to live in conflict with his Creator is to live against the very order upon which life is built.\n[15]. This refers to divine support, reassurance, and strengthening bestowed by Allāh. Many scholars understand it as guidance, tranquility, and firmness in faith, while others identify it as assistance through the angel Gabriel. It signifies a special spiritual reinforcement that stabilizes believers upon the truth, grants them endurance, and draws them nearer to Allāh through certainty and devotion.\nIt is granted by the will of Allāh, empowering believers to walk the path of righteousness. This divine guidance and influence supports them toward what is right and just. The holy spirit is another name for the angel Gabriel, whose role includes conveying revelation to the prophets and assisting the believers by Allah’s command.",
  59001: "Whatever is in the heavens and the earth glorifies Allāh, and He is the Almighty, the All-Wise.",
  59002: "It is He who expelled the disbelievers among the People of the Scripture from their homes at the first exile [1]. You did not expect them to leave, and they thought their fortresses would protect them from Allāh. But Allāh came upon them from where they did not expect and cast terror into their hearts, so they ruined their homes with their own hands and the hands of the believers. So take heed, O people of insight.\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. This refers to Banū al-Naḍīr, a Jewish tribe of Madinah who broke their treaty with the Prophet ﷺ and plotted against him. As a result, they were expelled from their dwellings—marking the first major gathering and exile of the People of the Scripture from the region. The verse highlights that their removal occurred not through a decisive battle, but through divine decree, fulfilling Allah’s promise and demonstrating that no fortress can protect against His will.",
  59003: "If Allāh had not decreed exile for them, He would certainly have punished them in this world [2]; and in the Hereafter, they will have the punishment of the Fire.\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. That is, Allāh decreed exile for them instead of immediate destruction or death. This exile was itself a form of worldly punishment and humiliation, sparing them a harsher fate at that time. Nevertheless, for those who persisted in disbelief and rebellion, a far greater punishment awaits in the Hereafter—the punishment of the Fire.",
  59004: "That is because they opposed Allāh and His Messenger. Whoever opposes Allāh—Allāh is indeed severe in punishment.",
  59005: "Whatever palm trees [3] you cut down or left standing on their roots was by Allah’s permission, so that He might disgrace the rebellious.\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. This refers to the cutting down of some date palm trees belonging to Banū al-Naḍīr during their siege and expulsion. This action was carried out by Allah’s permission and wisdom, not as wanton destruction. It served to weaken the rebellious, demonstrate divine authority, and affirm that all outcomes—whether preservation or destruction—occur by Allah’s will and for a just purpose.",
  59006: "Whatever Allāh restored to His Messenger from them—you did not spur for it any horses or camels. Rather, Allāh grants His messengers authority over whom He wills. Allāh has power over all things.",
  59007: "Whatever Allāh restores to His Messenger from the people of the towns [4] belongs to Allāh, the Messenger, his near relatives [5], the orphans, the needy, and the stranded traveler—so that it does not circulate only among the rich among you. Whatever the Messenger gives you, take it; and whatever he forbids you, abstain from it. And be mindful of Allāh. Indeed, Allāh is severe in punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. This refers to property and wealth taken from the People of the Towns without actual fighting—such as through surrender, treaty, or abandonment—classified as fayʾ. Such wealth was restored by Allāh to His Messenger and was not acquired through military combat, but by divine decree.\n[5]. That is, the close relatives of the Messenger ﷺ, specifically the family of Banū Hāshim and Banū al-Muṭṭalib, who were prohibited from receiving zakāh. Allāh allotted them a share of this wealth to honor their closeness to the Messenger and to provide for them lawfully.",
  59008: "And it is for the poor emigrants who were expelled from their homes and deprived of their wealth, seeking Allah’s bounty and pleasure, and supporting Allāh and His Messenger. They are the truthful.[6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. It was narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: “You shall gather on the Day of Judgment, and it will be asked, ‘Where are the poor and destitute of this nation?’ They shall stand up, and they will be asked, ‘What did you do in the worldly life?’ They shall reply, ‘Our Lord, You tested us (with poverty) and we bore it patiently when others were given wealth and power.’ Allāh will say, ‘You have spoken the truth.’ So they shall enter Paradise before others, and those who were endowed with wealth and power will receive a severe reckoning.”\nThe Prophet ﷺ also said:\n“The poor Muslims will enter Paradise before the rich by half a day (which is like 500 years).”\nScholars explain that this does not mean poor people are automatically better. The reason they enter earlier is:\nLess accountability (they had less wealth to answer for).\nThe rich will be questioned about how they earned and spent their wealth.\nA wealthy believer who used wealth well may still have a higher rank in Paradise than a poor person.",
  59009: "And those who settled in the home [Madinah] and embraced the faith before them [7] love those who emigrated to them [8], and find no desire in their hearts for what has been given to them. They give preference to others over themselves, even though they are in need. Whoever is protected from the greed of his own soul—those are the successful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. This refers to the Anṣār—the believers of Madinah—who had settled there and embraced Islam before the arrival of the emigrants from Makkah. They welcomed the Muhājirūn with sincerity and generosity, sharing their homes, wealth, and lives. Their early faith and steadfast support became a model of brotherhood and selflessness in Islam.\n[8]. That is, they love the emigrants with genuine affection rooted in faith, not merely outward courtesy. Their love is free of envy or resentment, even when the emigrants are given preference or material support. This love reflects purity of heart and sincere belief, demonstrating that true faith produces generosity, unity, and selfless concern for others.\nBelievers are therefore called to love one another deeply for the sake of Allāh, for such love carries immense reward and covers a multitude of sins. Love for the sake of Allāh is a noble trait that has become rare, yet it is among the highest expressions of faith. It begins in the heart—loving others because of their closeness to Allāh—and is manifested through deeds: supporting them in righteousness, honoring them, and defending them for the sake of truth. One does not love others for worldly benefit, familiarity, or appearance, but because they strive to please Allāh.\nThe rewards for loving one another for the sake of Allāh are immense. Among them is tasting the sweetness of faith, as the Prophet ﷺ said that true sweetness is found in loving Allāh and His Messenger above all else, and loving a person solely for Allah’s sake. On the Day of Resurrection, those who loved one another for Allah’s sake will be granted His shade on a Day when there is no shade but His. They will be placed upon seats of light, envied by the prophets and martyrs. And whoever loves another purely for Allah’s sake earns Allah’s love in return—a rank and honor beyond all measure.",
  59010: "And those who come after them say, “Our Lord, forgive us and our brothers who preceded us in faith, and do not place in our hearts any resentment toward those who believe. Our Lord, indeed You are Kind, Merciful.”",
  59011: "Have you not seen the hypocrites saying to their brothers among the disbelievers from the People of the Scripture [9], ‘If you are expelled, we will surely go out with you, and we will never obey anyone against you. And if you are fought, we will certainly support you.’ But Allāh testifies that they are liars.\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. This refers to the hypocrites who secretly aligned themselves with disbelievers among the People of the Scripture—particularly Banū al-Naḍīr. They made false promises of support and loyalty, claiming they would share exile with them or fight alongside them. These assurances were driven by deception and self-interest rather than sincerity. Allah exposed their falsehood by making clear that they neither fulfilled their pledges nor stood by those they claimed to support.",
  59012: "If they are expelled, they will not leave with them; and if they are fought against, they will not help them. And even if they were to help them, they would surely turn their backs—then they would not be helped.",
  59013: "Indeed, they fear you more in their hearts than they fear Allāh. That is because they are a people who do not understand. [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. That is, they lack true understanding of Allah’s greatness, power, and authority. Because their hearts are devoid of sound faith, they fear people more than their Lord. Had they truly known Allāh—His complete control over all affairs and His power to bring benefit or harm—they would not have placed fear of the believers above fear of Him. This misplaced fear reveals the weakness of their faith and the blindness of their hearts.",
  59014: "They will not fight you together except from fortified towns or from behind walls. Their hostility among themselves is intense. You think they are united, but their hearts are divided. That is because they are a people who do not understand.",
  59015: "They are like those who came just before them: they tasted the bitter consequences of their deeds, and for them there is a painful punishment. [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. This refers to the earlier expulsion of the Jewish tribe of Banū Qaynuqāʿ from Madinah, who were exiled before Banū al-Naḍīr after violating their covenant. Their fate serves as a recent historical example of the consequences of rebellion and betrayal.",
  59016: "They are like Satan, who says to a person, ‘Disbelieve,’ but when he does so, he says, ‘I am free of you. Indeed, I fear Allāh, Lord of the worlds.’",
  59017: "So the end of both will be that they will be in the Fire, abiding there forever. That is the recompense of the wrongdoers.",
  59018: "O believers, fear Allāh. Let every soul consider what it has sent ahead for tomorrow, and fear Allāh [12]. Indeed, Allāh is fully aware of what you do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [12].  The “fear of Allāh” does not mean being frightened of Him. Rather, it signifies deep reverence, awe, and conscious awareness—taking Allāh seriously and striving to live in a way that honors Him. True fear of Allāh leads a person to love what Allāh loves and to hate what Allāh hates, namely evil. To hate evil means to reject, avoid, and stand firmly against all forms of moral corruption, including:\nPride: Thinking too highly of oneself and refusing correction.\nArrogance: Looking down on others and behaving with false superiority.\nA life of wrongdoing: Persistent choices that harm oneself or others and oppose Allah’s guidance.\nCorrupt speech: Twisted, dishonest, harmful, or indecent words.\nWhoever truly honors Allāh will reject pride, arrogance, injustice, and corrupt speech, because these are traits Allāh despises.\nAllāh Almighty says in a ḥadīth qudsī:\n“Pride is My cloak and Majesty is My robe. Whoever competes with Me in either of them, I will punish him.”\nThis means that pride (kibriyāʾ) and majesty (ʿaẓamah) belong exclusively to Allāh. When a human being displays arrogance or self-exaltation, it is a form of rebellion—claiming a greatness that belongs only to the Creator. This is why Islam strongly warns against pride and arrogance and calls believers to humility, self-accountability, and reverent obedience.",
  59019: "Do not be like those who forgot Allāh, so He caused them to forget themselves [13]. They are the rebellious.\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. That is, when they neglected the remembrance of Allāh and abandoned obedience to Him, Allāh caused them to forget what truly benefits their own souls. They became heedless of their purpose, moral responsibility, and ultimate destiny. Such people lose self-awareness, self-discipline, and concern for their own salvation, pursuing desires that harm them in this world and the Hereafter. Forgetting Allāh leads inevitably to forgetting oneself—and this is among the greatest losses.",
  59020: "The companions of the Fire and the companions of Paradise are not equal. It is the companions of Paradise who are the successful.",
  59021: "Had We sent down this Qur’an upon a mountain, you would have seen it humbled, splitting apart out of awe of Allāh. These are the examples We set forth for people, so that they may reflect.",
  59022: "He is Allāh—there is no deity except Him—the Knower of the unseen and the seen. He is the Most Compassionate, the Most Merciful.",
  59023: "He is Allāh —there is no deity except Him—the Sovereign, the Most Holy, the Source of Peace [14], the Giver of security, the Watcher over all, the Almighty, the Compeller, the Supreme. Glory be to Allāh, far above what they associate with Him.\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. True peace comes only from Allāh and is rooted in remembrance of Him. When the heart is governed by Allah’s guidance and His word is embraced with wisdom, believers are able to teach, remind, and encourage one another with sincerity, gratitude, and humility. Such inner peace produces harmony in faith and conduct.\nThe Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would affirm this truth after completing the prayer, saying: “O Allāh, You are Peace, and from You comes peace. Blessed are You, O Possessor of Majesty and Honor.”\nThis supplication reminds believers that all tranquility, safety, and perfection originate from Allāh alone, and that lasting peace is found through nearness to Him.",
  59024: "He is Allāh —the Creator, the Originator, the Fashioner. To Him belong the Most Beautiful Names. Whatever is in the heavens and the earth glorifies Him. And  He is the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.",
  60001: "O believers, do not take My enemies and your enemies as allies, showing them affection while they have rejected the truth that has come to you—driving out the Messenger and yourselves because you believe in Allāh, your Lord. If you have gone forth striving in My path and seeking My pleasure, then do not show them affection. You confide in them with love, while I know best what you conceal and what you reveal. Whoever among you does so has indeed strayed from the straight path.",
  60002: "If they were to gain power over you, they would become enemies to you, extending their hands and their tongues against you with harm, and they would wish that you too would disbelieve.",
  60003: "Neither your relatives nor your children will benefit you. On the Day of Resurrection, He will separate between you. Allāh sees all that you do.",
  60004: "There has certainly been for you an excellent example in Abraham and those with him, when they said to their people, ‘We are free from you and from whatever you worship besides Allāh. We reject you, and there has arisen between us and you enmity and hatred forever—until you believe in Allāh alone.’ Except for Abraham’s statement to his father, ‘I will surely ask forgiveness for you, but I have no power to avail you anything against Allāh.’ ‘Our Lord, in You we trust, and to You we turn, and to You is the final return.’",
  60005: "Our Lord, do not make us a trial for those who disbelieve, and forgive us, our Lord. Indeed, You are the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.",
  60006: "There is indeed for you in them a good example for anyone who hopes in Allāh and the Last Day. Whoever turns away—then indeed, Allāh is the Self-Sufficient, the Praiseworthy.",
  60007: "It may be that Allāh will place affection between you and those you now regard as enemies. Allāh is All-Capable, and Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  60008: "Allāh does not forbid you from dealing kindly and justly with those who did not fight you because of your religion or drive you out of your homes. Allāh loves those who are just.",
  60009: " Allāh only forbids you from taking as allies those who fought you because of your religion, expelled you from your homes, and supported your expulsion. Whoever takes them as allies—it is they who are the wrongdoers.",
  60010: "O believers, when believing women come to you as emigrants, then examine them. Allāh knows best about their faith. If you find them to be believers, do not send them back to the disbelievers. They are not lawful for them, nor are they lawful for them. But give the disbelievers what they have spent [1]. There is no blame upon you in marrying them when you give them their dowries. And do not hold on to marriage ties with disbelieving women [2], but ask for what you have spent, and let them ask for what they have spent [3]. That is the judgment of Allāh —He judges between you. And Allāh is All-Knowing, All-Wise.\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. That is, compensate the disbelieving husbands for the dowries they had paid, so that no financial injustice is done.\n[2]. That is, do not maintain marital bonds with women who persist in disbelief by keeping them as wives while they choose allegiance with the disbelievers.\n[3] . If a disbelieving wife chose to join the disbelievers, a Muslim husband was entitled to reclaim the equivalent of the dowry he had paid. Likewise, if a believing woman joined the Muslims, the disbelieving husband was entitled to compensation for his dowry. These rulings—and those that follow—were revealed after the Treaty of al-Ḥudaybiyyah, establishing just and reciprocal regulations between the two parties.",
  60011: "If any of your wives go over to the disbelievers [4], and then you receive compensation, give those whose wives have departed the equivalent of what they had spent. And be mindful of Allāh, in whom you believe.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. That is, if any believing woman apostatized or chose to join the disbelievers, thereby dissolving her marriage to a Muslim. In such cases, when Muslims later obtained lawful compensation through victory or agreement, the affected husbands were to be reimbursed the dowries they had paid. This ruling ensured justice and prevented financial loss resulting from circumstances beyond their control.",
  60012: "O Prophet, when believing women come to you pledging that they will not associate anything with Allāh [5], nor steal, nor commit adultery, nor kill their children, nor bring forth any slander they have fabricated ˹such as falsely attributing children to their husbands˺ [6], nor disobey you in what is right [7]—then accept their pledge and seek forgiveness for them from Allāh. Indeed, Allāh is Most Forgiving, Most Merciful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. This pledge begins with pure monotheism—refraining from associating anything with Allāh in worship—which is the foundation of all faith and the greatest obligation upon a believer.\nA companion of the Prophet (may Allāh be pleased with him) narrated that he once walked with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ at night. During that encounter, the Prophet ﷺ informed him that those who possess great wealth will have little reward on the Day of Resurrection, except those who spend it generously in all directions and use it in good causes. Later that night, the Prophet ﷺ spoke words that he did not understand until he returned and explained that Gabriel had come to him with glad tidings: “Whoever dies without associating anything with Allāh will enter Paradise.”\nWhen the Prophet ﷺ asked—repeatedly—“Even if he steals? Even if he commits adultery?” Jibrīl replied each time, “Yes,” and finally added, “Even if he drinks intoxication.”\nThis hadith highlights several truths:\nWealth is a test, and salvation lies in generosity.\nAllah’s mercy is vast, and no sin is unforgivable if one dies upon pure monotheism.\nMajor sins do not nullify faith, though they may entail punishment before entry into Paradise.\nThis glad tidings was conveyed three times for emphasis, underscoring its importance.\n[6] That is, falsely attributing a child—whether born from adultery or otherwise—to a woman’s husband. Such slander violates lineage, honor, and social trust, and is a grave moral offense in Islam.\n[7] That is, they must not disobey the Prophet ﷺ in what is right—meaning commands that conform to obedience to Allāh. This includes refraining from practices of ignorance such as wailing loudly in grief, tearing garments, pulling out hair, scratching the face, or other forms of unlawful lamentation. Obedience here signifies adherence to righteous conduct, moral discipline, and the prophetic guidance in all lawful matters.",
  60013: "O believers, do not take as allies a people upon whom Allah’s wrath has fallen. They have despaired of the Hereafter, just as the disbelievers have despaired of those in the graves.",
  61001: "Whatever is in the heavens and the earth glorifies Allāh. And He is the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.",
  61002: "O believers, why do you say what you do not do? [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. This verse rebukes those who make claims, promises, or declarations without the intention—or follow-through—of acting upon them. It condemns hypocrisy in speech, especially when words are used to project righteousness, commitment, or courage without sincere resolve. Allāh calls believers to integrity, urging that speech and action be aligned, for empty words weaken faith, erode trust, and invite divine displeasure.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ warned sternly against such hypocrisy, saying:\n“A man will be brought on the Day of Resurrection and cast into Hell. His intestines will spill out, and he will go around them as a donkey goes around a millstone. The people of Hell will gather around him and say, ‘Did you not used to command us to do good and forbid us from evil?’ He will reply, ‘Yes, I used to command you to do good but did not do it myself, and I used to forbid you from evil but practiced it myself.’”\nThis ḥadīth illustrates the grave danger of preaching righteousness without embodying it, and it reinforces the Qur’anic demand for sincerity, accountability, and consistency between belief, speech, and action.",
  61003: "It is most hateful in the sight of Allāh that you say what you do not do.",
  61004: "Indeed, Allāh loves those who fight in His cause in ranks, as though they are a solid structure.",
  61005: "And [remember] when Moses said to his people, ‘O my people, why do you hurt me when you know that I am the Messenger of Allāh to you?’ So when they turned away, Allāh caused their hearts to turn away. Allāh does not guide the rebellious people.",
  61006: "And when Jesus, son of Mary, said, ‘O Children of Israel, I am the Messenger of Allāh to you [2], confirming what came before me of the Torah and giving glad tidings of a Messenger to come after me, whose name is Ahmad [3].’ But when he came to them with clear proofs, they said, ‘This is clear sorcery.’ [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. That is, Jesus عليه السلام affirms his role as a messenger sent by Allāh specifically to the Children of Israel. He did not claim divinity, but servitude and prophethood—calling his people to worship Allāh alone and to uphold the guidance revealed before him.\nEven within the New Testament, Jesus is repeatedly identified as a prophet and teacher, not as God. For example:\n“The crowds answered, ‘This is the prophet Jesus, from Nazareth in Galilee.’ (Matthew 21:11)\n“We know that you are a teacher who has come from God.” (John 3:2)\n“When the people saw the sign that he had done, they said, ‘This is indeed the Prophet who is to come into the world.’ (John 6:14)\nMany of the people, when they heard these words, said, ‘This really is the Prophet.’ (John 7:40)\nFurthermore, the Gospels describe Jesus praying to God in complete humility. In one instance:\nHe went a little farther, fell on his face, and prayed, saying, ‘My God, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me; yet not as I will, but as You will.’ (Matthew 26:39)\nThis act of prostration and supplication demonstrates servitude and dependence upon God. One who prays, submits, and seeks the will of God cannot himself be God. Jesus consistently attributes his actions and authority to God’s will, never to his own independent divinity.\nThese evidences affirm that Jesus was a noble prophet and messenger of God, not the son of God. Islam honors him highly while rejecting later religious claims that contradict pure monotheism.\nWhoever sincerely declares and believes:\nThere is no god but Allāh; Muḥammad is the servant and Messenger of Allāh; and Jesus is the servant and Messenger of Allāh —has affirmed true faith and is promised salvation by Allah’s mercy.\n[3]. Ahmad is another name of the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ, meaning “the most praised, the Parakletos.\" Jesus’s glad tidings point to the final Messenger who would come after him, completing the chain of prophethood. This verse affirms the continuity of divine revelation and the fulfillment of earlier scriptures through the mission of Muḥammad ﷺ.\n[4]. When the religious leaders witnessed the miraculous works performed by Jesus عليه السلام, they were compelled to explain the source of his power. Rather than accept the truth, they chose to attribute his miracles to sorcery and demonic influence. In doing so, they rejected clear signs and undeniable evidence—not out of reasoned inquiry, but out of arrogance and denial. This has been the recurring pattern of disbelieving communities when divine truth threatens entrenched falsehoods.\nJesus, however, affirmed that his power came solely from Allāh, the Creator of all beings, and not from himself. Their accusation that his miracles were the result of demonic forces led Jesus to warn them against committing a grave sin—falsely attributing divine acts to other than Allāh. Such claims amount to blasphemy and idolatry: inventing lies against Allāh and associating partners with Him in what belongs to Him alone.\nThis response illustrates how truth is often resisted not because it lacks evidence, but because accepting it requires humility, repentance, and surrender to Allah’s authority.",
  61007: "Who is more unjust than one who invents a lie against Allāh while he is being invited to Islam [5]? Allāh does not guide the wrongdoing people.\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. Islam means peace attained through complete submission to Allāh.\nThere is no injustice greater than deliberately fabricating falsehood about Allāh—whether by denying His signs, distorting His message, or attributing lies to His messengers—while one is being openly invited to Islam, the path of truth and wholehearted submission to Allāh alone. Such rejection is not born of ignorance, but of arrogance and conscious denial. Because true guidance requires sincerity, humility, and a willingness to submit, Allāh does not guide those who persist in wrongdoing by their own choice.",
  61008: "They seek to extinguish the Light of Allāh with their mouths, but Allāh will perfect His Light, even though the disbelievers may detest it.",
  61009: "It is He who sent His Messenger with guidance and the religion of truth, to make it prevail over all other religions—even though the polytheists may detest it.",
  61010: "O believers, shall I guide you to a transaction that will save you from a painful punishment?",
  61011: "That you believe in Allāh and His Messenger, and strive in the cause of Allāh with your wealth and your lives—that is better for you, if only you knew.",
  61012: "He will forgive you your sins and admit you into gardens beneath which rivers flow, and into good dwellings in Gardens of Eden. That is the great triumph.",
  61013: "And another [blessing] that you love: help from Allāh and a near victory. So give glad tidings to the believers.",
  61014: "O believers, be supporters of Allāh, just as Jesus, son of Mary, said to the disciples, ‘Who are my supporters for Allāh?’ The disciples said, ‘We are the supporters of Allāh.’ Then a group of the Children of Israel believed and a group disbelieved. So We supported those who believed against their enemies, and they became victorious.",
  62001: "Whatever is in the heavens and the earth glorifies Allāh —the Sovereign, the Most Holy, the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.",
  62002: "He is the One who sent among the unlettered a Messenger from among themselves [1], reciting to them His verses, purifying them, and teaching them the Book and wisdom—though before that they were in clear error.\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. Unlettered refers to those without prior scripture—commonly the Arabs at the time. A Messenger refers to the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ. The Book is the Qur’an, and wisdom is understood as the Sunnah.\nAllāh sent the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ from among the very people to whom he was sent—sharing their language, lineage, and lived experience. This made the message clear and accessible, leaving no excuse for rejection based on unfamiliarity. Through his mission, a people who had largely been without revealed guidance were transformed into a community shaped by revelation, knowledge, and moral refinement—through the recitation of the Qur’an, purification of hearts, and instruction in divine wisdom.\nThis mission is also understood to correspond to earlier prophecies found in previous scriptures. In the Book of Deuteronomy (18:15, 18), Moses says to the Children of Israel: “The Lord your God will raise up for you a prophet like me from among your brethren… I will put My words in his mouth, and he shall speak to them all that I command him.”\nMany Muslim scholars—and some others—have seen this description as corresponding most fully to Muḥammad ﷺ, who conveyed revelation verbatim and established a comprehensive moral and legal framework.\nMuḥammad ﷺ is often compared more closely to Moses than to Jesus in certain aspects: both were born through natural means, led communities, established law, and were accepted as prophets by their followers during their lifetimes despite initial opposition. Moses faced resistance from the Israelites, and Muḥammad ﷺ endured persecution in Makkah and migration to Madinah, yet both ultimately established communities grounded in revelation.\nBy contrast, the Gospel of John (1:11) states regarding Jesus: “He came to his own, but his own people did not receive him.”\nThese parallels are seen as supporting the understanding that Muḥammad ﷺ is the prophet foretold in earlier revelation—sent as guidance and mercy to humanity, and as the final messenger in the line of prophethood.",
  62003: "And to others among them who have not yet joined them [2]. And He is the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. That is, others beyond the first recipients—including later generations and non-Arabs—who would enter Islam after the initial community of believers. This affirms the universal and ongoing mission of the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ, whose message is not limited by time, place, or ethnicity, but extends to all peoples until the Day of Resurrection.",
  62004: "That is the bounty of Allāh; He grants it to whom He wills, and Allāh is the Possessor of great bounty.",
  62005: "The example of those who were entrusted with the Torah, but then did not carry it, is like that of a donkey carrying books [3]. How evil is the example of a people who deny the revelations of Allāh. Allāh does not guide the unjust people.\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. This parable illustrates the condition of those who were given divine revelation yet failed to understand it, live by it, or uphold its guidance. Like a donkey carrying books it neither comprehends nor benefits from, they bore the Scripture outwardly without internalizing its meaning or acting upon it. The likeness condemns not knowledge itself, but knowledge devoid of practice, showing that revelation is a trust that brings honor only when it is believed, understood, and obeyed.",
  62006: "Say, ‘O you who are Jews, if you claim that you are favored by Allāh above all other people, then long for death—if you are truthful.’",
  62007: "But they will never wish for it, because of what their hands have sent forth [4]. And Allāh is All-Aware of the wrongdoers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. Such as disobedience to Allāh; distorting the scriptures; killing the prophets; falsely claiming to have killed Jesus; accusing Mary of fornication; unlawfully consuming people’s wealth; and engaging in usury, among other grave sins.",
  62008: "Say, ‘Indeed, the death from which you flee will surely meet you. Then you will be returned to Allāh, the Knower of the unseen and the seen, and He will inform you of what you used to do.’",
  62009: "O believers, when the call to prayer is proclaimed on Friday, then hasten [5] to the remembrance of Allāh and leave off trade. That is better for you, if only you knew.\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. Hasten does not mean rushing physically, but responding promptly with seriousness of heart and intention. The remembrance of Allāh refers primarily to the Friday sermon (khuṭbah) and the congregational prayer, which together form the essence of Jumuʿah worship. This command establishes the obligation of attending the Friday prayer for those upon whom it is required, and it necessitates setting aside trade and worldly distractions—affirming that obedience to Allāh takes precedence over all material pursuits.\nBelievers are thus exhorted not to neglect gathering in the mosques for congregational prayer, Qur’an recitation, and the Sunnah. Rather, they are encouraged to strengthen one another through communal worship, to recognize themselves as true members of the mosque—the House of Allāh—and to enter His mercy with confidence through prayer and remembrance. This collective devotion nurtures closeness to Allāh, deepens mutual affection among believers, and sustains a life lived consciously for His sake.",
  62010: "Then when the prayer is concluded, disperse through the land and seek of Allah’s bounty, and remember Allāh often so that you may prosper. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. True prosperity lies not merely in material gain, but in success granted by Allāh through obedience and constant remembrance. While believers are encouraged to seek lawful provision after prayer, they are reminded to remain mindful of Allāh in all circumstances. Abundant remembrance preserves faith, purifies intention, and ensures that worldly pursuits do not distract from the ultimate goal—success in this life and the Hereafter.\nBelievers are therefore called to abandon sinful habits and corrupt ways of living, and to allow Allāh to renew their hearts, thoughts, and conduct. This includes truthfulness instead of deceit, patience instead of uncontrolled anger, pure speech instead of abuse, and lawful work instead of theft, begging, or exploitation. Hands are to be used for honest labor and generosity, not wrongdoing.\nWhen the call to prayer is made, all worldly activity is to be set aside so that one may respond to Allah’s summons and join the congregation. In this balance between worship and work lies a life of righteousness, dignity, and true prosperity under Allah’s guidance.",
  62011: "But when they see a trade or an amusement, they rush toward it and leave you standing. Say, ‘What is with Allāh is better than amusement and trade, and Allāh is the Best of Providers.’",
  63001: "When the hypocrites [1] come to you, they say, ‘We testify that you are indeed the Messenger of Allāh.’ Allāh knows that you are indeed His Messenger, but Allāh testifies that the hypocrites are truly liars.\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. The hypocrites (al-munāfiqūn) are those who outwardly profess faith while concealing disbelief in their hearts. Their testimony was factually correct—Muḥammad ﷺ is indeed the Messenger of Allāh —but it was uttered without sincerity or true belief. Thus, Allāh declares them liars, not because the statement itself was false, but because it was spoken deceptively, devoid of faith and conviction. Hypocrisy of this kind is more dangerous than open disbelief, as it corrupts trust, undermines the community from within, and cloaks falsehood in the appearance of truth.\nThe Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ described the signs of hypocrisy, saying: “Whoever possesses four traits is a pure hypocrite, and whoever possesses one of them has a trait of hypocrisy until he abandons it:\n(1) when he is entrusted, he betrays;\n(2) when he speaks, he lies;\n(3) when he makes a promise, he breaks it;\n(4) and when he quarrels, he behaves in an immoral and abusive manner.”\nHe ﷺ also warned: “The most evil people in the sight of Allāh on the Day of Resurrection are the two-faced—those who approach some people with one face and others with another.”\nThese prophetic warnings show that hypocrisy is not merely a hidden belief, but a destructive pattern of conduct that erodes integrity, sincerity, and moral accountability.",
  63002: "They have taken their oaths as a shield, thereby hindering others from the way of Allāh. Indeed, evil is what they do.",
  63003: "That is because they believed, then disbelieved; so their hearts were sealed, and they do not understand.",
  63004: "When you see them, their appearance impresses you, and when they speak, you listen to their words. Yet they are like propped-up pieces of wood. They think every cry is against them. They are the enemy, so beware of them. May Allāh destroy them—how deluded they are.",
  63005: "When they are told, ‘Come, so that the Messenger of Allāh may seek forgiveness for you,’ they turn their heads aside, and you see them turning away arrogantly.",
  63006: "It is the same for them whether you seek forgiveness for them or not—Allāh will never forgive them. Indeed, Allāh does not guide the rebellious people.",
  63007: "They are the ones who say, ‘Do not spend on those who are with the Messenger of Allāh until they disperse.’ But to Allāh belong the treasures of the heavens and the earth, and the hypocrites do not understand.",
  63008: "They say, ‘When we return to Madinah, the more honored [2] will surely drive out the more humble [3] from it.’ But honor belongs to Allāh, and to His Messenger and the believers—yet the hypocrites do not know.\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. Referring to ʿAbdullāh ibn Ubayy ibn Salūl, the leader of the hypocrites in Madinah at that time. He arrogantly considered himself and his followers to be “the mightier,” claiming social and political superiority over the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ and the believers, despite lacking true honor in the sight of Allāh.\n[3]. That is, they contemptuously referred to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ and the believers as the more humble, intending weakness or inferiority. In reality, Allāh declares that true honor, dignity, and strength belong to Him alone—and consequently to His Messenger and the believers—while humiliation awaits those who oppose the truth.",
  63009: "O believers, do not let your wealth or your children distract you from the remembrance of Allāh [4]. Whoever does so—it is they who are the losers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. The remembrance of Allāh includes faith, prayer, recitation of the Qur’an, obedience to His commands, and constant mindfulness of Him in one’s heart and actions. Wealth and children are blessings and trusts from Allāh, but when attachment to them leads to neglect of worship, moral duty, or obedience, they become a cause of loss rather than benefit. True success lies in keeping Allāh foremost, using worldly blessings as means to draw nearer to Him—not as distractions that turn one away from His remembrance and the five obligatory prayers.",
  63010: "Spend from what We have provided you before death comes to one of you and he says, ‘My Lord, if only You would delay me for a short time so that I might give charity and be among the righteous.’ [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. This verse portrays the regret of a person at the moment of death, when the opportunity for repentance and righteous deeds has come to an end. Realizing the truth too late, he longs for even a brief delay so that he might give charity and live righteously—but such a return is no longer granted. The verse serves as a solemn warning to act while time remains, for wealth spent in obedience before death brings lasting benefit, whereas regret afterward brings none.\nTo be “among the righteous” means to live in conformity with the Qur’an and the Sunnah. The righteous are those who have entered into a covenant with Allāh through faith and strive to live according to His guidance. Though they may falter at times, they turn back in repentance, seeking forgiveness and desiring what is right.\nIn contrast, the wicked are those who reject Allāh, disregard His laws, or persist in oppression and wrongdoing. It is essential to distinguish between righteous living and righteous standing: righteous standing is the state of acceptance Allāh grants through faith, while righteous living is the outward conduct that flows from it. Righteous living without faith—merely adhering to a moral code—does not lead to righteous standing. But righteous standing, grounded in sincere faith in Allāh and His Messenger Muḥammad ﷺ, naturally leads to righteous living. Since no person is sinless, true righteousness is not earned by deeds alone, but granted by Allāh to those who believe and strive in obedience.",
  63011: "But Allāh will never delay a soul when its appointed term has come [6]. Allāh is All-Aware of what you do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. That is, when the time decreed by Allāh for a person’s death arrives, it cannot be postponed or delayed for repentance, charity, or righteous deeds. This verse affirms the absolute certainty of the divine decree and urges believers to act before the opportunity ends. Life is a trust with a fixed term known only to Allāh, and once it expires, accountability begins and the opportunity for action ceases.\nThe Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said:\n“Remember often the destroyer of pleasures.” (i.e., death)\nHe ﷺ also said:\n“I had forbidden you from visiting graves. Now, visit them, for they remind you of death and the Hereafter.”\nRemembering death does not lead to despair; rather, it leads to clarity. It awakens the heart, disciplines the soul, and urges sincere action before the door of deeds is closed forever.",
  64001: "Whatever is in the heavens and the earth glorifies Allāh. To Him belongs all dominion, and to Him belongs all praise. And He has power over all things.",
  64002: "It is He who created you; and among you are disbelievers, and among you are believers. Allāh is All-Seeing of what you do.",
  64003: "He created the heavens and the earth for a true purpose, and He formed you and perfected your forms. And to Him is the final return.",
  64004: "He knows whatever is in the heavens and the earth, and He knows what you conceal and what you reveal. And Allāh is All-Knowing of what is within the chests.",
  64005: "Has there not come to you the news of those who disbelieved before? They tasted the evil consequence of their affair, and for them is a painful punishment.",
  64006: "That was because their messengers came to them with clear proofs, but they said, ‘Shall a mere human being guide us?’ So they disbelieved and turned away. Allāh had no need of them, and Allāh is Self-Sufficient, Praiseworthy.",
  64007: "Those who disbelieve claim that they will never be resurrected. Say, ‘Yes—by my Lord—you will certainly be resurrected, then you will be informed of what you have done. And that is easy for Allāh.’",
  64008: "So believe in Allah and His Messenger and the Light [1] which We have sent down. And Allah is All-Aware of what you do.\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. The Light refers to the Qur’an—the divine revelation sent down as guidance for humanity. It is described as “light” because it illuminates the path of truth, distinguishes right from wrong, and removes the darkness of ignorance, doubt, and misguidance. Through it, hearts are guided, minds are clarified, and lives are directed toward righteousness.\nBelief in the “Light” is mentioned alongside belief in Allāh and His Messenger to emphasize that true faith requires accepting not only the One who reveals and the one who conveys, but also the message itself. The Qur’an serves as a comprehensive source of guidance—providing spiritual direction, moral principles, and a framework for individual and communal life.\nThis verse follows the affirmation of resurrection and accountability, reminding that the appropriate response is to believe and follow the revealed guidance before the Day of Judgment. The closing statement—“Allāh is All-Aware of what you do”—reinforces that faith is not merely a claim, but must be reflected in one’s actions, which are fully known to Allāh.",
  64009: " On the Day when He will gather you for the Day of Gathering—that is the Day of mutual loss and gain. Whoever believes in Allāh and does righteous deeds, He will remove from him his misdeeds and admit him into gardens beneath which rivers flow, to abide therein forever. That is the great triumph.",
  64010: "But those who disbelieve and deny Our revelations—they are the people of the Fire, abiding therein forever. What a wretched destination.",
  64011: "No calamity befalls except by Allah’s permission. Whoever believes in Allāh, He guides his heart [2]. Allāh is All-Knowing of all things.\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. That is, to accept Allah’s decree with patience and a sound heart, without resentment or objection to His will. Allāh grants inner guidance, tranquility, and contentment to the heart of the believer who knows that every calamity occurs by His decree. Such a person responds with patience, trust, and submission, recognizing that what has occurred could not have missed him, and what missed him could not have occurred. This guidance of the heart enables the believer to face trials with faith rather than despair, and to find wisdom, meaning, and reward in adversity.\nThe Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said:\n“He who slaps the cheeks, tears the clothes, and calls to the ways of the Days of Ignorance is not from us.”\nHe ﷺ also said:\n“How wonderful is the affair of the believer! All of his affairs are good—and this is only for the believer. If something pleasing befalls him, he is grateful, and that is good for him; and if hardship befalls him, he is patient, and that is better for him.”\nThese teachings show that true faith transforms hardship into spiritual growth, and calamity into a means of closeness to Allāh.",
  64012: "Obey Allāh and obey the Messenger. But if you turn away, then the duty of Our Messenger is only clear conveyance.",
  64013: "Allāh—there is no god but Him. And upon Allah let the believers place their trust.",
  64014: "O believers, indeed among your wives and your children are some who are enemies to you [3], so beware of them. But if you pardon, overlook, and forgive—then Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. That is, since they may turn you away from following the Divine Path. Some spouses or children may become enemies in the sense that they distract one from obedience to Allāh, encourage disobedience, weaken resolve in faith, or pressure a believer to compromise religious duties for worldly comfort or emotional attachment. This does not refer to literal hostility, but to spiritual harm that can arise when love for family supersedes loyalty to Allāh and His guidance.\n[4]. That is, if you choose to pardon their mistakes, overlook their shortcomings, and forgive their harm—rather than respond with harshness or retaliation—you reflect a higher moral character. Allāh encourages mercy within the family, reminding believers that forgiveness brings divine forgiveness in return, and that patience and compassion can transform trials into means of reward and closeness to Him.\nForgiveness is a noble trait and a sign of true strength. If anger overtakes us, let forgiveness elevate us. If we wish for our own sins to be erased, let us forgive others. Believers are servants of Allāh, honored and loved by Him—so let life reflect that sacred standing. Clothe yourselves daily with mercy, gentleness, patience, and humility. Respond with kindness rather than anger, endure hardship with calm trust, and persist in good even when results are slow. In doing so, the heart becomes a place of peace, actions inspire goodness, and life reflects the beauty Allāh intends.",
  64015: "Your wealth and your children are only a trial, but with Allāh is a great reward.",
  64016: "So fear Allāh as much as you are able; listen and obey, and spend—that is better for yourselves. Whoever is protected from the greed of his own soul—it is they who are the successful. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. That is, obedience, generosity, and self-discipline ultimately benefit one’s own soul—not Allāh, who is free of all need. What a person spends sincerely for Allāh purifies the heart from attachment to wealth, trains the soul in selflessness, and protects it from greed. Being saved from the greed of the soul means restraining selfish desire, miserliness, and excessive attachment to worldly gain. Those who overcome this inner greed through faith, obedience, and generosity are the truly successful—both in this life and in the Hereafter.\nAllāh is generous, merciful, and kind—not only to the righteous, but to all, even the ungrateful and the wicked. The Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ instructed those who wish to reflect divine character to love, to do good, and to give without expecting anything in return.\nThe Prophet ﷺ said: “Indeed, Allāh is Generous; He loves generosity and noble character, and He detests pettiness.”\nAnd he ﷺ said: “The believer is simple and generous, whereas the wicked person is deceitful and ignoble.”\nThese teachings show that generosity is not merely a financial act, but a spiritual quality that purifies the heart, reflects faith, and draws one nearer to Allāh.",
  64017: "If you lend to Allāh a goodly loan [6], He will multiply it for you and forgive you. Allāh is Most Appreciative, Most Forbearing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. Whoever gives to the poor lends to Allāh, and Allāh repays it many times over. This goodly loan is not limited to material charity alone, though that is among its clearest forms. It also extends to caring for the inner condition of others. Some people are poor in spirit, lacking hope, peace, or faith—so lending to Allāh includes helping restore hope, strengthen faith, ease sorrow, and increase joy through kindness, compassion, and sincere presence.\nMaterial giving is often easier, for one may simply part with wealth or goods. But tending to a person’s emotional and spiritual well-being requires more: time, patience, empathy, and the willingness to walk alongside another soul. This form of giving involves pouring out one’s care and attention to meet needs that cannot be touched or measured—yet it is among the most beloved acts to Allāh, and among the most richly rewarded.",
  64018: "He is the Knower of the unseen and the seen, the All-Mighty, the All-Wise.",
  65001: "O Prophet ˹tell the believers:˺ When you divorce women, divorce them at the beginning of their prescribed waiting period [1], and count the waiting period accurately [2], and fear Allāh, your Lord. Do not expel them from their homes, nor should they leave, unless they commit a clear indecency [3]. These are the limits set by Allāh. Whoever transgresses the limits of Allāh has certainly wronged himself. You do not know—perhaps Allāh will bring about after that a new situation.\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. That is, divorce should be pronounced at the start of the wife’s waiting period (ʿiddah), meaning during a time of purity in which no marital relations have occurred. This ensures that the waiting period is clear, just, and free from confusion. Islam prohibits divorcing a woman during menstruation or in a period of purity after intimacy, as such timing prolongs hardship and violates the wisdom and fairness intended in the law.\nThis regulation protects both spouses by preserving the possibility of reconciliation and preventing impulsive or emotionally driven decisions. It also ensures accurate determination of the waiting period, particularly in relation to pregnancy. Thus, divorce in Islam is not left to arbitrary action, but is governed by careful timing and ethical restraint, reflecting both justice and mercy in family law.\n[2]. That is, three menstrual periods for a woman who menstruates. The waiting period (ʿiddah) is the legally prescribed time a divorced woman must observe before she may remarry. It serves multiple purposes: to confirm the absence of pregnancy, to preserve lineage, to allow space for reflection and possible reconciliation, and to protect the rights and dignity of both spouses. Counting it accurately is a moral and legal obligation, emphasizing responsibility, restraint, and fear of Allāh in marital matters.\n[3]. Clear indecency refers to manifest and proven immoral conduct, such as adultery or behavior that flagrantly violates Islamic moral and legal boundaries. According to many scholars, it may also include actions that make continued residence harmful or unsafe, such as severe misconduct or abuse. This exception is narrowly defined and does not permit expulsion based on suspicion, personal dislike, or minor disputes. The rule emphasizes protection, dignity, and fairness for the woman during her waiting period, while allowing limited exceptions where clear wrongdoing has been established.",
  65002: "Then when they are about to reach the end of their waiting period, retain them in a fair manner or part with them in a fair manner [4]. And take as witnesses two just men from among you [5], and establish the testimony for Allāh. That is what is instructed to whoever believes in Allāh and the Last Day. And whoever fears Allāh —He will make for him a way out.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. A husband may take back his wife after the first or second divorce, which are considered revocable divorces, either during or at the end of her waiting period (ʿiddah), provided this is done honorably and with the intention of reconciliation. However, if he divorces her a third time, the divorce becomes irrevocable, and the marriage is fully terminated after the completion of her waiting period. In that case, she is not lawful for him again unless she lawfully marries another man, the marriage is consummated, and then ends naturally through divorce or death—without any prior arrangement or intention to make her permissible for her former husband.\n[5]. A single witness is generally insufficient to establish a charge in matters of crime or wrongdoing. Islamic law requires the testimony of two witnesses in most cases, and four witnesses in cases involving adultery or fornication. In certain legal matters, a single witness accompanied by an oath may be accepted, according to established juristic principles.\nIf a malicious witness comes forward to accuse someone falsely, both parties are to appear before qualified judges, who must investigate the matter carefully and impartially. Should the testimony be proven false, the witness who made the false accusation is subject to punishment for false testimony. These safeguards are intended to protect justice, prevent oppression, and preserve the honor and rights of individuals within society.",
  65003: "And He will provide for him from where he does not expect. And whoever places his trust in Allāh—He is sufficient for him. Indeed, Allāh will accomplish His purpose. And Allāh has already set for everything a decreed measure.",
  65004: "As for those of your women who have despaired of menstruation—if you are in doubt—their waiting period is three months, as well as for those who have not menstruated. And for those who are pregnant, their term is until they deliver their burden. And whoever fears Allāh —He will make matters easy for him.",
  65005: "That is the command of Allāh which He has sent down to you. And whoever fears Allāh—He will remove from him his misdeeds and increase for him his reward.",
  65006: "Lodge them where you dwell, according to your means, and do not harm them so as to make life difficult for them [6]. And if they are pregnant, then provide for them until they deliver their burden. Then if they breastfeed for you, give them their due payment, and consult together in a fair manner. But if you disagree, then another woman may breastfeed for him.\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. That is, do not harm, pressure, or mistreat them in order to force them out of the home or compel them to surrender their rights. This includes emotional abuse, intimidation, withholding maintenance, or creating hardship so as to make their stay unbearable. Islam strictly forbids such conduct during the waiting period, emphasizing dignity, fairness, and compassion. The command protects women from exploitation and ensures that divorce is not used as a means of oppression, but is carried out with responsibility and fear of Allāh.",
  65007: " Let the one of means spend according to his means, and the one whose provision is restricted—let him spend from what Allāh has given him. Allāh does not burden any soul beyond what He has given it. Allāh will bring about ease after hardship.",
  65008: "And how many a town rebelled against the command of its Lord and His messengers, so We called it to a severe account and punished it with a dreadful punishment.",
  65009: "So it tasted the evil consequence of its conduct, and the outcome of its affair was loss.",
  65010: "Allāh has prepared for them a severe punishment. So fear Allāh, O people of understanding who have believed. Indeed, Allāh has sent down to you a Reminder.",
  65011: "A Messenger [7] reciting to you the clear verses of Allāh, so that He may bring those who believe and do righteous deeds out of darknesses into light. And whoever believes in Allāh and does righteous deeds—He will admit him into gardens beneath which rivers flow, to abide therein forever. Allāh has indeed granted him an excellent provision.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. That is, the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ, sent by Allāh as a bearer of revelation and guidance. Through reciting the clear verses of the Qur’an, he conveys Allah’s message faithfully, calling people from ignorance, disbelief, and moral confusion into the light of faith, knowledge, and righteous conduct. His mission is both to inform and to transform—guiding hearts and societies by Allah’s permission from darkness into light.",
  65012: "Allāh is the One who created seven heavens, and of the earth, the like of them. His command descends among them, so that you may know that Allāh has power over all things, and that Allāh indeed encompasses all things in knowledge.",
  66001: "O Prophet, why do you forbid for yourself what Allāh has made lawful for you, seeking to please your wives? And Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.",
  66002: "Allāh has already ordained for you the dissolution of your oaths. Allāh is your Protector; He is the All-Knowing, the All-Wise.",
  66003: "And when the Prophet confided to one of his wives a matter [1], and she disclosed it [2], Allāh made it known to him. He informed her of part of it and overlooked part. Then when he informed her of it, she said, ‘Who informed you of this?’ He said, ‘The All-Knowing, the All-Aware informed me.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. That is, one of the wives of the Prophet ﷺ—identified in reports as Ḥafṣah bint ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with her). The Qurʾān does not name her explicitly, preserving dignity and directing attention to the moral lesson rather than to the individual.\n[2]. That is, she disclosed the private matter to another wife, identified in reports as ʿĀ’ishah (may Allāh be pleased with her), contrary to the trust placed in her. The Qurʾān does not name her explicitly, preserving discretion and directing attention to the moral lesson rather than personal details. The disclosure was not malicious, but it did constitute a breach of confidentiality. Allāh then informed the Prophet ﷺ of what had occurred, demonstrating His complete knowledge of what is concealed and what is revealed.",
  66004: "If you both [3] repent to Allāh—for your hearts have indeed inclined [4]—then that is best. But if you support one another against him, then indeed Allāh is his Protector, and Gabriel, and the righteous among the believers; and the angels, after that, are his supporters.\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. This refers to two of the Prophet’s wives, identified as ʿĀ’ishah and Ḥafṣah (may Allāh be pleased with them both). The Qur’an addresses them in the dual form, yet refrains from naming them explicitly, preserving honor and directing attention to the moral lesson rather than the individuals.\n[4]. That is, your hearts inclined or wavered slightly from what was most appropriate in that situation. The expression does not imply disbelief, hypocrisy, or deliberate wrongdoing, but a momentary lapse in judgment or emotional inclination. The wording reflects Allah’s gentleness, calling to repentance while affirming the essential faith, sincerity, and noble rank of the Prophet’s wives.",
  66005: "Perhaps, if he were to divorce you, his Lord would give him in your place wives better than you—submissive, believing, devoutly obedient, repentant, devoted to worship and fasting [5]—previously married and virgins.\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The expression is understood by some scholars to include women who strive in the cause of faith, such as traveling or exerting effort for noble purposes like daʿwah and service to the religion. In Islam, the responsibility of calling to Allāh, enjoining what is right, and forbidding what is wrong applies to both men and women, each according to their proper conduct and circumstances. The Qur’an and Sunnah address both genders, and women share in the duty of moral guidance, righteousness, and reform.\nA woman engaged in daʿwah must observe Islamic decency and modesty, including chastity, proper hijab in the presence of non-maḥram men, avoidance of inappropriate mixing, and refraining from anything that could compromise her dignity or the credibility of her message. When addressing men, this must be done without seclusion (khalwah), with proper hijab, and within ethical boundaries established by Islam. Her life and conduct should reflect the message she conveys.\nDue to the challenges and risks often associated with outreach and missionary efforts, scholars have noted that structured arrangements—such as husband-and-wife teams or coordinated efforts among women, or women working alongside men in appropriate settings—are generally safer and more effective. However, this should never discourage women from participating in daʿwah, for Allāh calls both men and women, married and unmarried, to serve Him. Throughout history, Allāh has used women in profound and impactful ways in spreading guidance, nurturing faith, and strengthening the community.",
  66006: "O believers, protect yourselves and your families from a Fire whose fuel is people and stones [6], over which are angels—stern and severe—who do not disobey Allāh in what He commands them, but carry out whatever they are commanded.\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. “People and stones” indicates the extreme intensity and horror of the Fire. Scholars explain that the stones include idols and objects worshipped besides Allāh, which will be cast into Hell along with their worshippers, increasing their torment and regret. The imagery underscores that Hell is not symbolic but real, severe, and prepared for those who persist in disbelief and disobedience. This warning urges believers to protect themselves and their families through faith, righteous conduct, and proper guidance.",
  66007: "O disbelievers, make no excuses today. You will only be recompensed for what you used to do.",
  66008: "O believers, turn to Allāh in sincere repentance. Perhaps your Lord will remove from you your misdeeds and admit you into Gardens beneath which rivers flow—on the Day when Allāh will not disgrace the Prophet and those who believed with him. Their light will proceed before them and on their right [7]. They will say, ‘Our Lord, perfect our light [8] for us and forgive us. Indeed, You are Able to do all things.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. The light represents the believers’ faith, guidance, and righteous deeds, made manifest on the Day of Judgment. It will lead them safely across the darkness of that Day—especially over the Ṣirāṭ—serving as a visible sign of their salvation and honor. Its appearance before them and on their right symbolizes forward guidance and the reception of their records in the right hand, both signs of success and divine favor.\n[8]. This supplication reflects the believers’ humility and fear despite their honor. They ask Allāh to complete and preserve their light, lest it diminish or be extinguished, as happens to the hypocrites. “Perfect our light” means to grant them full guidance, security, and safe passage until entry into Paradise, along with forgiveness that removes all remaining shortcomings.",
  66009: "O Prophet, strive against the disbelievers and the hypocrites and be firm with them. Their abode is Hell—what a miserable destination.",
  66010: "Allāh sets forth an example for those who disbelieve: the wife of Noah and the wife of Lot. They were under two of Our righteous servants, but they betrayed them [9], so their husbands availed them nothing against Allāh. And it was said, ‘Enter the Fire with those who enter.’\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. The “betrayal” mentioned here does not refer to marital unfaithfulness. The wives of prophets are unanimously regarded by scholars as chaste. Rather, the betrayal was in faith and allegiance: they rejected the message of their husbands, opposed their mission, and sided with disbelievers. The wife of Noah is reported to have accused him of madness, while the wife of Lot informed his people about his guests. This illustrates that family ties, even marriage to a prophet, do not avail without true belief and obedience to Allāh. Each soul is accountable for its own faith and deeds.",
  66011: "And Allāh sets forth an example for those who believe: the wife of Pharaoh, when she said, ‘My Lord, build for me a house near You in Paradise, and deliver me from Pharaoh and his deeds, and deliver me from the wrongdoing people.’ [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. This refers to Āsiyah bint Muzāḥim (may Allāh be pleased with her), the wife of Pharaoh, who believed in Allāh despite living under one of the most tyrannical rulers in history. Her supplication reflects complete faith, courage, and detachment from worldly power. She did not ask merely for deliverance from punishment, but for nearness to Allāh—“a house near You in Paradise”—placing divine closeness above comfort, wealth, or status. Her plea for rescue from Pharaoh and the wrongdoing people signifies her rejection of disbelief, oppression, and injustice. This example demonstrates that true faith can flourish even in the harshest environments, and that honor before Allāh is based on belief and righteousness, not lineage, marriage, or worldly position.\nThe Prophet ﷺ taught that human beings differ in degrees of moral and spiritual perfection. Many men attained righteousness and excellence, and some reached the highest level as prophets. Among women, however, only a few attained exceptional perfection. Foremost among them are Āsiyah bint Muzāḥim, Maryam bint ʿImrān (the mother of Jesus), Khadījah bint Khuwaylid, Fāṭimah bint Muḥammad, and ʿĀ’ishah bint Abī Bakr (may Allāh be pleased with them all). Their status reflects faith, sacrifice, and devotion, not worldly rank.",
  66012: "And Mary [11], the daughter of ʿImrān [12], who guarded her chastity; so We breathed into her through Our Spirit. And she believed in the Words of her Lord and His Books, and she was among the devoutly obedient.\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. Mary (may Allāh be pleased with her) is one of the most honored women in Islam. She was chosen, purified, and preferred above the women of her time. The Qur’an praises her for her chastity, faith, and complete devotion to Allāh. She is the only woman mentioned by name in the Qur’an and is upheld as a lasting example of sincerity, patience, and obedience.\n[12]. ʿImrān refers to Maryam’s father, Amram i.e., Joachim, a righteous man from the lineage of the Children of Israel. This ʿImrān is not the same as ʿImrān, the father of Moses and Aaron. The Qur’an affirms Mary's noble lineage while emphasizing that her true honor lies in her faith and righteousness, not merely in ancestry.",
  67001: "Blessed is He in Whose Hand is the dominion, and He has power over all things.",
  67002: "He is the One who created death and life [1] to test you—which of you is best in deed [2]. He is the All-Mighty, the All-Forgiving.\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. Death is mentioned before life in the Arabic (al-mawt wa-al-ḥayāh), a deliberate ordering that underscores human mortality and the transient nature of worldly existence. Death is described as a created state, not mere non-existence, affirming divine authority and control over both life and death. Humanity is thus set between life and death, prosperity and adversity, blessing and loss, and is called to choose the life of the Hereafter over the fleeting life of this world. Whoever follows Muhammad, God’s final messenger ﷺ, and believes in Allāh who sent him, is promised eternal life and will not perish. The life of this world is temporary, whereas the Home of the Hereafter is the true and everlasting life.\n[2]. This refers to deeds performed sincerely for the sake of Allāh alone and in accordance with the teachings of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. The phrase emphasizes the quality of actions rather than their quantity. “Best” (aḥsan) has traditionally been understood to denote deeds characterized by sincerity of intention and correctness in practice, not merely an abundance of outward acts.",
  67003: "He is the One who created the seven heavens, one above another. You will find no inconsistency [3] in the creation of the Most Compassionate. So return the gaze—do you see any flaw?\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. The Arabic term tafāwut denotes disparity, disharmony, or lack of proportion. It does not merely imply error, but any form of imbalance, contradiction, or incoherence. The verse invites repeated reflection upon creation, asserting that no defect or irregularity will be found in the work of the Most Compassionate. The command to “return the gaze” underscores both the perfection of divine creation and the limitations of human perception.\nAll parts of the universe are interconnected and operate in harmony and precise coordination; nothing in creation is disordered, discordant, or out of proportion. Phenomena such as birth defects, genetic mutations, and apparent irregularities do not negate this harmony, but occur within the broader perfection of creation. Such contrasts allow perfection to be recognized by comparison and also serve as a test for humankind.",
  67004: "Then look again, and yet again; the gaze will return to you abased and weary.",
  67005: "We have adorned the lowest heaven with lamps, and made them missiles against the devils [who eavesdrop]; and for them We have prepared the punishment of the blazing Fire.",
  67006: "For those who disbelieve in their Lord is the punishment of Jahannam [Hell], and wretched is the final destination.",
  67007: "When they are cast into it, they will hear it roaring as it boils up.",
  67008: "It almost bursts apart with rage. Whenever a group is cast into it, its keepers will ask them, ‘Did no warner come to you?’",
  67009: " They will say, ‘Yes’, a warner did come to us, but we denied and said, ‘Allāh has not sent down anything; you are only in great error.’",
  67010: "They will say, ‘If only we had listened or used our reason, we would not have been among the companions of the blazing Fire.’",
  67011: "Thus they will confess their sin. So away [4] with the companions of the blazing Fire!\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. Away from all good and from Allah’s mercy. The expression fa-suḥqan is an idiomatic Arabic formula of condemnation and exclusion, signifying being driven far away from divine mercy rather than mere physical removal. Occurring here after the acknowledgment of sin, it marks a final verdict: confession without repentance comes too late to avert punishment, underscoring the irrevocability of the judgment.",
  67012: "Indeed, those who stand in awe of their Lord, though unseen, will have forgiveness and a great reward.",
  67013: "Whether you keep your speech secret or declare it openly, He truly has full knowledge of what is within the chests [of mankind and jinn].",
  67014: "Does He who created not know [5]? And He is the Most Subtle, the All-Aware.\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. Or does He not know those whom He created?",
  67015: "It is He who made the earth manageable for you; so walk in its paths and eat of His provision; and to Him is the Resurrection.",
  67016: "Do you feel secure that He Who is above the heaven will not cause the earth to sink with you. Then lo! it quakes [6]?\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. The phrase “He who is above the heaven” affirms divine transcendence, exalted highness and loftiness. It serves as a warning against false security, reminding humanity that the earth itself remains subject to divine command and may become an instrument of punishment.\nThe transcendence and loftiness of Allāh is reflected in a well-known report from one of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ. He relates that he struck a slave-girl in anger after a wolf carried off one of his sheep. Disturbed by this, he came to the Prophet, who reproached him and instructed him to bring the girl. The Prophet ﷺ asked her, “Where is Allāh?” She replied, “Above the heavens.” He then asked, “Who am I?” She said, “You are Muhammad ﷺ, the Messenger of God.” Thereupon the Prophet ﷺ said, “Free her, for she is a believer.”\nThis report has been cited as affirming Allah’s transcendence, exaltedness and loftiness above creation.",
  67017: " Or do you feel secure that He who is above the heavens will not send against you a sandstorm? Then you will know how My warning is.",
  67018: "Indeed, those before them also denied [the messengers]; then how terrible was My reproach!",
  67019: "Have they not seen the birds above them, spreading their wings and folding them in? None holds them aloft except Allāh, the Most Compassionate. Indeed, He is the All-Seer of everything.",
  67020: "Or who could be an army for you, helping you apart from the Most Compassionate [Allāh]? The disbelievers are only in delusion.",
  67021: "Or who could provide for you if He were to withhold His provision? No—they persist in arrogance and aversion.",
  67022: "Is one who walks facedown and stumbling better guided, or one who walks upright on a straight path?",
  67023: "Say, “It is He who brought you into being and gave you hearing, sight, and intellects—yet little is the gratitude you show.”",
  67024: "Say, “It is He who has scattered you in the earth, and to Him you will be gathered.”",
  67025: "And they say, “When is this promise, if you are truthful?”",
  67026: "Say, “The knowledge of it is only with Allāh, and I am only a clear warner.”",
  67027: "But when they see it approaching near, the faces of those who disbelieved will turn gloomy, and it will be said, “This is what you used to demand.”",
  67028: "Say, “Have you considered: if Allāh causes me to perish and those with me, or has mercy upon us—then who will protect the disbelievers from a painful punishment?”",
  67029: "Say, “He is the Most Compassionate; in Him we believe and upon Him we rely. You will come to know who is in manifest error.”",
  67030: "Say, “Have you considered: if your water were to sink deep into the earth, who then could bring you pure, flowing water [7]?”\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. No one is able to bring it forth except Almighty Allāh. The Arabic term māʾin denotes water that is visible, accessible, and continuously flowing—clear and life-sustaining. By contrasting it with water that has sunk beyond reach (ghawr), the verse underscores humanity’s complete dependence on divine provision. The sūrah closes by drawing attention to an everyday necessity, reminding that even the most basic means of life remain entirely under Allāh’s control.",
  68001: "Nūn [1]. By the Pen, and what they inscribe [i.e., the angels].\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. See footnote Qur’an chapter 2: verse 1.",
  68002: "By the grace of your Lord, you are not [O Prophet] insane. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The accusation of insanity, as claimed by the pagans, was employed to undermine the Prophet’s message. The verse decisively rejects this charge, grounding his soundness of mind and integrity in divine grace, and affirming that revelation is a sign of God’s favor rather than delusion.",
  68003: "Indeed, for you is an uninterrupted reward.",
  68004: "You [O Prophet] are surely of great moral character. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. The term khuluq encompasses moral character, conduct, and habitual disposition, while ʿaẓīm (“great”) denotes exceptional excellence. The phrase “upon a great character” indicates steadfast embodiment of virtue rather than occasional moral acts. This verse thus serves as a definitive divine commendation of the ethical integrity of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, following the refutation of false accusations and the promise of an unfailing reward.\nIt is narrated that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ was so gentle and humble with people that if a servant-girl were to take him by the hand, seeking his help in some matter, he would not release her hand until he had fulfilled her need, even if she stopped him in the streets of Madīnah.\nThis report illustrates the perfection of the Prophet’s humility despite his exalted rank. Though he was the most honored of creation and free of all worldly need, he rendered himself in service to every soul—high or low, noble or poor. His compassion was not a sign of weakness, but of mercy and a sincere desire to guide hearts to the truth. He visited the sick, comforted the afflicted, supported the oppressed, relieved the distressed, and bore patiently the burdens of others.\nThus, in his example it becomes clear that true honor lies not in domination but in service; not in being served but in serving; not in asserting status but in sacrificing it for the good of others. This is from the perfection of his character, as Allāh Himself attests: “And indeed, you are of a great character.” For the height of honor is not in dominion, but in compassionate humility.",
  68005: "Soon you will see and they will see,",
  68006: "which of you is the afflicted one.",
  68007: "Indeed, your Lord alone knows best who has gone astray from His way, and He knows best who are rightly guided.",
  68008: "So do not obey the deniers.",
  68009: "They wish you would compromise [4], so they would too.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. That is, compromise in matters concerning the fundamentals of Islam—the true religion of God—so as to meet in the middle between belief and unbelief. The verb tud’hin derives from id’hān, meaning to yield, flatter, or compromise in matters of principle. The verse does not refer to gentleness or courtesy, but to doctrinal and moral concession. The deniers sought a mutual accommodation whereby truth would be diluted in exchange for social acceptance. The command that follows makes clear that such compromise in matters of revelation is impermissible.",
  68010: "Do not obey [O Prophet] any contemptible habitual swearer, [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The expression combines two condemnatory traits: ḥallāf denotes one who swears oaths excessively, often to mask falsehood or gain advantage, while mahīn signifies one who is base, contemptible, or morally insignificant. In Qur’anic usage, frequent oath-taking is associated with dishonesty rather than piety. The verse thus warns against yielding to the influence of such individuals, whose outward religiosity conceals corrupt character.\nThis meaning is reinforced by a well-known report from the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, who said: “There are three types of people to whom Allāh will not speak on the Day of Resurrection, nor purify them, and for whom there is a painful punishment: a man who has surplus water in the desert and refuses it to a traveler; a man who sells goods after the afternoon prayer and swears by Allāh that he bought them at a certain price, deceiving the buyer; and a man who gives allegiance to a ruler for worldly gain—if he is given, he fulfills it, and if he is not given, he breaks it.”\nThe report illustrates that excessive or manipulative oath-taking is a mark of moral corruption, not devotion, and stands in direct contrast to integrity and truthfulness.",
  68011: "a defamer, going about spreading gossip,",
  68012: "withholder of good, transgressor, sinful,",
  68013: "coarse—and beyond all that, base-born.",
  68014: "Just because he has wealth and sons—”",
  68015: "When Our verses are recited to him, he says, “Tales of the ancients.”",
  68016: "We will soon brand him on the snout.",
  68017: "Indeed, We tested them—as We tested the owners of the garden—when they swore they would harvest its yield at dawn.",
  68018: "And they made no exception. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. That is, they made no exception either by saying “if Allāh wills” (in shāʾAllāh), or by intending to leave a share of the harvest for the poor. This omission reflects both disregard for divine will and greed toward the needy. In Qur’anic usage, “making an exception” (istithnāʾ) signifies acknowledging God’s sovereignty over outcomes and fulfilling social responsibility. Their failure in both respects reveals arrogance and self-reliance, ultimately leading to their loss.",
  68019: "So a calamity from your Lord struck the garden while they were asleep.",
  68020: "So it became like a field stripped bare.",
  68021: "At dawn, they called out to one another,",
  68022: "“go early to your field if you intend to harvest it.”",
  68023: "So they set out, whispering to one another,",
  68024: "[Saying], “Don’t let any poor person approach you today asking for help.”",
  68025: "They went out early, fully resolved and confident in their ability. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. That is, they set out confident in their ability to carry out their plan to keep the poor away, believing they had the power to prevent anyone from taking any of the fruit. The term qāridīn conveys firm resolve combined with a sense of self-assured power and control. It implies not merely determination, but confidence that one’s plan will succeed without hindrance. In this context, it reflects their false assumption that they were fully capable of executing their scheme—harvesting the garden and excluding the poor—independent of divine will. The verse thus highlights arrogant self-reliance, which is immediately exposed as illusory in the verses that follow.",
  68026: "But when they saw it [the garden], they said, “We must have gone astray.”",
  68027: "Rather, we have been deprived [of its fruits].",
  68028: "The most reasonable among them said, “Did I not tell you, ‘Why do you not glorify Allāh?’",
  68029: "They said, ‘Glory be to Allāh our Lord—we have been unjust.’",
  68030: "Then they turned on each other, throwing blame.",
  68031: "They said, ‘Woe to us! Indeed, we were transgressors.’",
  68032: "Perhaps our Lord will give us something better in its place. We turn to our Lord in hope. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. That is, in hope of His mercy, forgiveness, and bounty. The phrase innā ilā rabbinā rāghibūn expresses hopeful turning marked by longing, repentance, and reliance. It does not imply entitlement, but humble expectation grounded in acknowledgment of wrongdoing. Their hope is directed not merely toward material replacement, but toward divine mercy and acceptance, signifying sincere repentance after recognition of sin.",
  68033: "Such is the punishment [of this life], but the punishment of the Hereafter is greater—if only they knew.",
  68034: "Indeed, the righteous will have gardens of Bliss with their Lord.",
  68035: "Shall We treat the Muslims [9] like the wicked.\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. Muslims here denotes those who submit to God’s will in faith and obedience, not merely a communal label. The verse affirms the principle of divine justice: submission and righteousness are not equated with guilt and wrongdoing.",
  68036: "What is the matter with you? How do you judge?",
  68037: "Or do you [O pagans] have a Scripture in which you read,",
  68038: "that you will indeed find in it whatever you choose?",
  68039: "Or do you have binding oaths from Us, extending until the Day of Resurrection, that you will have whatever you decide?",
  68040: "Ask them which of them can guarantee that claim.",
  68041: "Or do they have partners [with God]? Then let them bring their partners [10], if they are truthful.\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. That is, those to whom they attribute divinity besides Allāh, or whom they claim as partners with Him—whether from among themselves or others—in authority, judgment, or intercession. The verse challenges them to produce these alleged associates in support of their claims. The demand is rhetorical, exposing the emptiness of polytheistic belief: no such partner can appear, testify, or assume responsibility before Allāh. This completes the dismantling of all false sources of authority—scripture, covenant, guarantor, and intercessor.",
  68042: "On the Day when the Shin will be bared [11], and they will be called to prostrate, but they will not be able, [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. In accordance with authentic ḥadīths: ‘The Shin’ here refers to that of Allāh, before which every believer will prostrate on the Day of Judgement. The wording is to be affirmed as it appears, without likening it to creation or inquiring into its modality, in accordance with the approach of the early Muslims.\nAnother meaning of the verse: The phrase yukshafu ʿan sāq is an expression denoting the uncovering of a moment of extreme severity, dread, and reality on the Day of Judgment. It signifies the removal of all veils and pretenses, when matters become manifest and unavoidable. \n[12]. That is, they will be rendered incapable of prostration, though they are commanded to do so. In the life of this world, they refused to humble themselves in worship despite having the ability. On the Day of Judgment, when submission is demanded, their backs will be made rigid, and they will be unable to respond. This incapacity is a form of punishment, exposing the consequences of their prior arrogance.",
  68043: "Their eyes will be humbled, humiliation covering them; and indeed they used to be called to prostrate while they were sound.",
  68044: "So leave Me [to deal] with whoever denies this message; We shall gradually draw them on to their ruin from where they do not realize.",
  68045: "I will give them respite. Indeed, My plan is firm. [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. The respite granted by Allāh is not a sign of approval or neglect, but part of a deliberate and wise divine plan. “My plan is firm” signifies that Allāh’s decree is unyielding, precise, and impossible to escape. Those who persist in denial may be allowed time to continue, yet this delay only serves to establish proof against them and to fulfill divine justice at the appointed moment. The verse reinforces that Allāh is never overtaken, deceived, or thwarted—His judgment comes exactly when and how He wills.",
  68046: "Or do you ask them for payment, so they are weighed down with debt?",
  68047: " Or do they have access to the unseen, so they record it?",
  68048: "So be patient for the decision of your Lord, and do not be like the companion of the whale, when he cried out while in distress. [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. “The companion of the whale” refers to Prophet Jonah (Yūnus), who left his people prematurely before being commanded to do so. When he was swallowed by the whale, he called out to Allāh in intense distress and repentance from the depths of darkness. His supplication—acknowledging Allāh’s perfection and his own shortcoming—was answered, and he was delivered.\nThe verse does not criticize Jonah’s repentance, but gently reminds the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ to remain patient and steadfast, even in the face of rejection and hardship. Unlike Jonah’s momentary haste, the Prophet is instructed to persevere calmly under divine command, trusting fully in Allāh’s timing and wisdom.",
  68049: "Had it not been for a grace from his Lord that overtook him, he would indeed have been cast upon the barren shore while he was blameworthy. [15]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. This verse continues the account of Jonah (Yūnus). The “grace” refers to Allāh’s mercy in accepting Jonah’s repentance and relieving him of blame before his deliverance. Although he was cast onto the shore after being released from the whale, he was no longer blameworthy, having turned back to Allāh in humility and supplication.\nThe verse highlights two key lessons:\nDivine mercy precedes punishment—repentance can avert lasting disgrace.\nEven prophets are in need of Allāh’s grace at every moment.\nBy mentioning what could have happened without divine grace, the Qurʾān underscores that salvation, honor, and restoration come only by Allāh’s favor, not by one’s status or deeds alone.",
  68050: "But his Lord chose him and made him one of the righteous.",
  68051: "Indeed, the disbelievers would almost make you slip with their eyes [through hatred] when they hear the Qur’an, and they say, \"He is certainly insane.\"",
  68052: "But it is only a reminder for all people.",
  69001: "The Inevitable Truth!",
  69002: "What is the Inevitable Truth?",
  69003: "And what can make you know what the Inevitable Truth is?",
  69004: "[The people of] Thamūd and ʿĀd denied the Striking Calamity [of the Hour].",
  69005: "As for Thamūd, they were destroyed by the violent blast.",
  69006: "And as for ʿĀd, they were destroyed by a screaming, violent wind.",
  69007: "Which He unleashed upon them for seven nights and eight days unceasingly, so that you would see the people lying lifeless, as if they were hollow trunks of palm trees.",
  69008: "So do you see any remnant of them now?",
  69009: "And Pharaoh, and those before him, and the overturned cities came with persistent sin.",
  69010: "They disobeyed the messenger of their Lord, and He seized them with a severe taking.",
  69011: "Indeed, when the water overflowed, We carried you in the sailing ship. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. The plural pronoun “We” in the Qur’an is a majestic form (royal We) used exclusively for Allāh, signifying greatness, authority, and power without implying plurality. The verse refers to the Ark of Prophet Noah. Although the event predates later generations, the address “We carried you” encompasses all of humanity, as those who lived afterward are descendants of the survivors whom Allāh saved. The description of the water as “overflowing” conveys a flood that exceeded all bounds, covering the earth as a decisive manifestation of both judgment and mercy. The passage thus serves as a reminder of divine preservation: human survival is a gift from Allāh, and history testifies to His power to destroy and to save.",
  69012: "So that We might make it a reminder for you, and that a receptive ear might retain it.",
  69013: "When the Trumpet is blown in a single blowing,",
  69014: "And the earth and the mountains are lifted up, then crushed with a single crushing.",
  69015: "On that Day, the Inevitable Event [of Resurrection] will occur.",
  69016: "And the sky will split asunder, becoming frail on that Day.",
  69017: "And the angels will be on its borders; and there will carry the Throne of your Lord above them, that day, eight [of them] .",
  69018: "That Day you will be exposed—no secret of yours will remain hidden.",
  69019: "As for the one who is given his record in his right hand, he will say, \"Here—read my record!\"",
  69020: "Indeed, I was certain that I would face my account.",
  69021: "So he will live a life well-pleasing,",
  69022: "in a lofty garden,",
  69023: "its fruits are near at hand.",
  69024: "“Eat and drink pleasantly for what you sent forth in the days gone by.”",
  69025: "But as for the one who is given his record in his left hand, he will say, \"Oh, would that I had not been given my record!\"",
  69026: "And had not known what my reckoning was.",
  69027: "Oh, would that death had finished me once and for all! [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. This cry expresses utter despair and irreversible regret. Faced with judgment and punishment, the disbeliever wishes that physical death had been complete annihilation—ending all existence and accountability. The verse powerfully refutes the notion that death is an escape or final rest. Instead, it affirms the Qurʾānic reality that death is a passage to resurrection, where deeds are exposed and recompense is unavoidable. The wish itself underscores the severity of the Hereafter and the tragic consequence of denying it during worldly life.",
  69028: "My wealth has not benefited me at all.",
  69029: "All my authority is gone.",
  69030: "\"Arrest him and fetter him,",
  69031: "then throw him into the blazing Fire,",
  69032: "then into a chain whose length is seventy cubits, bind him.",
  69033: "Indeed, he did not believe in Allāh, the Most Great,",
  69034: "nor did he urge the feeding of the needy.",
  69035: "So today he has here no intimate friend,",
  69036: "nor any food except foul discharge,",
  69037: "none will consume it except the guilty.",
  69038: "But rather, I swear by what you see,",
  69039: "and by what you do not see.",
  69040: "Indeed, this is truly the word [of God] conveyed by a noble Messenger. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. The phrase “the noble Messenger” here refers to the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ, who faithfully conveyed the words of God to humanity. The verse does not attribute authorship of the Qurʾān to any created being; rather, it affirms that the Messenger’s role was to transmit the revelation with complete integrity, accuracy, and trustworthiness. By describing him as “noble,” the verse emphasizes his exalted character, reliability, and honor in fulfilling the divine trust of conveying God’s revelation without addition, omission, or distortion.",
  69041: "It is not the word of a poet—little is that you believe.",
  69042: "Nor is it the word of a soothsayer [4]—little do you take heed.\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. A soothsayer (kāhin) in pre-Islamic Arabia was a figure who claimed access to hidden knowledge through jinn, omens, or conjecture. Their speech was typically obscure, fragmented, and speculative, lacking moral coherence or divine authority. By denying that the Qurʾān is the word of a soothsayer, the verse refutes the notion that revelation is based on guesswork, occult influence, or vague prediction. Instead, it affirms that the Qurʾān is clear, purposeful, morally consistent, and grounded in divine truth. The rebuke, “little do you take heed,” indicates that the failure lies not in the clarity of the message, but in the listeners’ unwillingness to reflect upon it sincerely.\nThe Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ further warned against the soothsayers, saying:\n“He is not a believer who seeks omens or has omens interpreted for him, who practices fortunetelling or has his fortune told, or who practices magic or has it practiced for him. Whoever goes to a soothsayer and believes what he says has disbelieved in what was revealed to Muḥammad ﷺ.”\nOmens and superstitions are not from Allāh and carry no truth. Islam therefore rejects all beliefs, traditions, customs, or authorities—whether religious, cultural, or ancestral—that attribute legitimacy to omens or soothsaying. Guidance and knowledge of the unseen belong to Allāh alone.",
  69043: "This is a revelation sent down by Allāh, the Lord of all the worlds.",
  69044: "Had he falsely ascribed any words to Us,",
  69045: "We would surely have seized him with a mighty grasp, [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. Another interpretation renders the phrase as “by [Our] Right Hand,” signifying might, power, and absolute authority—without implying any likeness between Allāh and His creation. Whether understood literally in expression or idiomatically in meaning, the phrase conveys a decisive and overpowering seizure. It underscores that even the most honored messenger would not be exempt from accountability if he were to falsely attribute words to Allāh. This conditional statement therefore powerfully affirms the Prophet Muḥammad’s ﷺ truthfulness and confirms the divine origin of the Qurʾān.",
  69046: "then We would certainly have cut off his aorta.",
  69047: "Then none of you could have protected him from Us.",
  69048: "This Qur’an is indeed a reminder for the righteous.",
  69049: "We certainly know that among you are deniers.",
  69050: "Indeed, it will be a source of deep regret for the disbelievers.",
  69051: "This Qur’an is indeed the absolute truth.",
  69052: "So glorify the Name of Allāh, your Lord, the Most Great. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. To “glorify the Name” of Allāh means to declare Him absolutely free from all imperfection and deficiency, and to exalt Him in belief, speech, and action. It encompasses remembrance (dhikr), praise, obedience, humility, and living in a manner that reflects reverence for His majesty. The title “the Most Great” (al-ʿAẓīm) affirms Allāh’s absolute greatness in His essence, attributes, and authority—greater than all that can be imagined or compared.\nThis closing command is the natural response to the certainty just affirmed: since the Qurʾān is the absolute truth, the fitting conclusion is humble glorification and devoted worship of the Lord who revealed it.\nWhen this verse—“So glorify the Name of your Lord, the Most Great”—was revealed, the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said: “Say it in your bowing.”\nAnd when the verse “Glorify the Name of your Lord, the Most High” (87:1) was revealed, he said, 'Say it in your prostration.'\nAccordingly, the Prophet ﷺ would say while bowing in prayer: Subḥāna Rabbiyal-ʿAẓīm (Glory be to my Lord, the Most Great),\nand while prostrating: Subḥāna Rabbiyal-Aʿlā (Glory be to my Lord, the Most High).\nExalting Allāh—the true Lord of mankind, the Sovereign King of mankind, the only God of mankind—is central to worship. He alone is most worthy of praise, and His greatness cannot be encompassed by any mind or tongue.\nIt is also narrated that the Prophet ﷺ would supplicate between the two prostrations, saying: “O Allāh, forgive me, have mercy on me, guide me, grant me well-being, and provide for me.”\nSupplication is likewise encouraged in prostration and before concluding the prayer, seeking forgiveness, mercy, guidance, and deliverance from evil—affirming that to Allāh alone belong sovereignty, power, and eternal glory.",
  70001: "A questioner [mockingly] asked about a punishment bound to occur,",
  70002: "upon the disbelievers—there is none who can avert it,",
  70003: "from Allāh, Lord of the ascending paths.",
  70004: "The angels and the spirit [Gabriel] ascend to Him on a Day whose measure is fifty thousand years. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. This verse describes the immense magnitude of the Day of Judgment. The “Spirit” refers to the angel Gabriel, mentioned separately as an honor and distinction. The phrase “fifty thousand years” conveys the overwhelming length and gravity of that Day when measured by worldly standards. While it will be extraordinarily long and severe for the disbelievers, it will be made light and swift for the believers, feeling no longer than the time of an obligatory prayer—by Allāh’s mercy, ease, and grace.",
  70005: "So be patient with a beautiful patience. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. “Beautiful patience” (ṣabran jamīlā) denotes patience without complaint, bitterness, or resentment—a calm, dignified perseverance that entrusts the outcome entirely to Allāh. It is not passive resignation, but steadfast endurance coupled with restraint of the tongue and heart. In the context of mockery and denial, the Prophet ﷺ is instructed to respond not with retaliation, but with composed forbearance, confident that divine justice will unfold in its appointed time.",
  70006: "Indeed, they see it as far off, [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. “They” refers to the disbelievers, who regard the promised punishment or the Day of Judgment as distant, unlikely, or unreal. Their perception is shaped by denial and heedlessness, leading them to mock divine warning and delay repentance. The verse highlights a contrast between human misjudgment and divine certainty, preparing for the following verse, which affirms that what they deem far off is, in reality, near.",
  70007: "yet We see it as very near.",
  70008: "On the Day when the sky will be like molten copper,",
  70009: "and the mountains will be like tufts of wool,",
  70010: "and no close friend will ask about a close friend,",
  70011: "though they will see one another. The guilty person will wish he could ransom himself from the punishment of that Day by offering his children,",
  70012: "and his spouse, and his brother,",
  70013: "and his close family that sheltered him,",
  70014: "and everyone on the earth altogether—just to deliver himself.",
  70015: "By no means! It is a raging flame!",
  70016: "Stripping away the skin, [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. This verse describes the Hellfire as violently tearing away the outer parts of the body, including the skin, scalp, and extremities. It conveys extreme physical torment and humiliation, emphasizing that the punishment is direct, painful, and relentless. The imagery is not presented for mere description, but as a grave warning—underscoring the severity of divine justice for those who persist in denial, arrogance, and rejection of truth.",
  70017: "calling out to whoever turned his back and turned away [from the Truth], [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. That is, the Hellfire will pick out and seize such people from the vast gathering of humanity on the Day of Resurrection, just as a bird picks up a grain from the earth with its beak and swallows it. This vivid imagery conveys inevitability and precision—none who deserve it will escape.\nThe verse portrays Hellfire as calling or drawing toward itself those who deliberately turned away from faith and truth. “Turning one’s back” signifies conscious rejection and arrogance, while “turning away” emphasizes persistence in denial rather than momentary doubt or ignorance. The punishment is therefore not arbitrary; it corresponds directly to a person’s willful refusal of guidance after it had clearly come to them.",
  70018: "and amassed and hoarded wealth. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. That is, refraining from paying from one’s wealth what is due to others—especially the poor and those entitled to assistance. This verse condemns the practice of greedily accumulating wealth and withholding it from rightful obligations such as charity and social responsibility. Hoarding here does not refer to lawful possession itself, but to obsessive accumulation driven by arrogance, self-sufficiency, and disregard for accountability before Allāh.\nIn the Qurʾānic context, such behavior reflects a heart deeply attached to worldly gain, confident in material wealth, and dismissive of the Hereafter—traits that often accompany rejection of divine guidance. The verse therefore links moral corruption and economic injustice with spiritual denial.",
  70019: "Indeed, man is created impatient,",
  70020: "when misfortune strikes him, he is restless and distressed,",
  70021: "but when good touches him, he becomes miserly,",
  70022: "except those who pray [i.e., the believers],",
  70023: "who are steadfast in their prayer,",
  70024: "those in whose wealth there is a known right.",
  70025: "for the one who asks and for the deprived.",
  70026: "and who firmly believe in the Day of Recompense,",
  70027: "and who are apprehensive of their Lord’s punishment,",
  70028: "for none can feel secure from their Lord’s punishment,",
  70029: "and those who guard their chastity, [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. To “guard one’s chastity” means to restrain oneself from all unlawful sexual behavior, whether in action, speech, or intention. It includes avoiding adultery, fornication, and all paths that lead to them, while channeling desire through lawful marriage. In the Qurʾānic ethic, chastity is not repression but moral discipline, preserving personal dignity, family stability, and spiritual integrity. This quality reflects self-control rooted in awareness of Allāh and accountability before Him.\nEach of you must know how to control his own body being aware of your physical impulses, desires, and actions.\nIt is all about chastity, avoiding forbidden acts, and maintaining moral integrity in spiritual purity and honor living in a way that is pleasing to Allāh, avoiding sin and immoral actions, maintaining dignity, integrity, and respect for oneself and others.\nThe bodily control is not just about abstaining from sin, but also about living with spiritual nobility, being available for Allah’s purpose and separated from things profane.\nThis explains the reason for controlling the body: to dedicate oneself fully to Allāh, avoiding actions, desires, or distractions that are contrary to Allah’s guidance.\nEssentially, the body is being treated as a tool for worship and good deeds, not for indulgence in what is forbidden or spiritually harmful.\nTrue devotion starts with self-control and bodily discipline.\nA person should live in moral purity and honor, not letting base desires dominate.\nThis prepares one to serve Allāh fully, free from the distractions or corruption of worldly temptations.",
  70030: "except with their wives or those whom their right hands possess—for then they are not blameworthy.",
  70031: "But whoever exceeds those bounds—they are the transgressors.",
  70032: "And those who faithfully honor their trusts and covenants, [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. Trusts (amānāt) include all responsibilities placed upon a person—toward Allāh and toward people—such as religious obligations, rights, property, positions of authority, confidential matters, and moral duties. Covenants refer to promises, agreements, oaths, and commitments, whether made with Allāh or with others. Honoring them reflects integrity, reliability, and faithfulness, and is a defining mark of true belief. Betrayal of trust and violation of covenants are repeatedly condemned in the Qurʾān as signs of moral corruption and hypocrisy.\nThe Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said:\n“Guarantee me six things and I will guarantee you Paradise: when you speak, speak the truth; when you make a promise, fulfill it; when you are entrusted with something, discharge the trust; guard your private parts; lower your gaze; and restrain your hands from wrongdoing.”\nHe ﷺ also said:\n“The Muslim is the one from whose tongue and hand people are safe; the believer is the one from whom people’s lives and wealth are safe; and the emigrant is the one who abandons what Allāh has forbidden.”\nAccordingly, a true Muslim does not insult, curse, backbite, or spread corruption among people. Others are safe from his hand—he does not harm them, usurp their property, or violate their rights. The true emigrant (muhājir) is therefore not merely one who relocates physically, but one who abandons sin and disobedience for the sake of Allāh. Upholding trusts and covenants thus forms the foundation of ethical conduct, social harmony, and sincere faith.",
  70033: "and those who stand firm by their testimonies, [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. Testimonies (shahādāt) include bearing witness truthfully in legal, social, and moral matters, as well as affirming the truth in word and action whenever required. Standing by one’s testimony means truthfulness without distortion, courage without concealment, and consistency without compromise—whether the testimony benefits oneself or not. The Qurʾān repeatedly commands justice in testimony, even against one’s own interests or those of family and kin. False testimony and concealment of truth are grave sins, as they corrupt justice and undermine trust. Upholding truthful witness reflects integrity, God-consciousness (taqwā), and loyalty to divine truth over personal gain.\nAt the highest level, testimony includes the testimony of faith (shahādatayn)—bearing witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allāh and that Muḥammad ﷺ is the Messenger of Allāh. Whoever affirms this testimony sincerely, and lives in accordance with its demands, is promised entry into Paradise by Allāh’s mercy; while whoever knowingly rejects Allāh and His Messenger is excluded from that promise. Thus, truthful testimony is not merely spoken—it is lived, upheld through belief, conduct, and fidelity to divine guidance.",
  70034: "and those who carefully maintain their prayer, [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. To maintain the prayer means more than performing it outwardly. It includes observing its times, fulfilling its conditions and pillars, praying with humility and presence of heart, and guarding it from neglect or distraction. Prayer here crowns the list of virtues in this passage—showing that sincere worship is both the foundation and culmination of righteous character. Those who truly maintain prayer allow it to shape their conduct, restrain wrongdoing, and nurture continuous awareness of Allāh.",
  70035: "they will be in Gardens of Bliss, honored.",
  70036: "So what is the matter with those who disbelieve, that they rush toward you,",
  70037: "from the right and the left, gathering in groups? [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. Some of the pagan leaders of Makkah used to assemble around the Prophet ﷺ from different directions—not to seek guidance, but to mock, dispute, and oppose him. They would gather in factions, deriding the message and claiming arrogantly that if there were truly a Hereafter, they would deserve Paradise before the believers. This verse exposes the emptiness of their assumption: mere proximity, numbers, or social status grant no privilege before Allāh. True honor and salvation belong only to faith, humility, and obedience—not to mockery or false confidence.",
  70038: "Does every one of them aspire to be admitted into a Garden of Bliss?",
  70039: "By no means! Indeed, We created them from what they know. [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. The phrase “from what they know” refers to their humble origin—from a despised fluid (nuṭfah, a sperm-drop). The rebuke dismantles arrogance and entitlement: how can those created from such a lowly beginning presume automatic admission into Paradise while rejecting faith and obedience? The verse reminds them that honor before Allāh does not arise from origin, status, or desire, but from humility, belief, and righteousness. Human weakness at creation stands in stark contrast to their inflated claims, exposing the emptiness of pride and false hope.",
  70040: "Nay—by the Lord of all the easts and the wests [13], We are fully able—\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. And what is in them, of the sun and the moon and the constellations: i.e., Allāh swearing by Himself. \n“The easts and the wests” refers to all points of sunrise and sunset, across seasons and locations, highlighting Allāh’s complete dominion over time, space, and the cosmos. The oath underscores divine omnipotence—specifically His ability to create, replace, resurrect, and judge. The opening “Rather/Nay” rebukes false expectations of entitlement to Paradise and redirects attention to Allāh’s absolute authority, which none can resist or escape.",
  70041: "to replace them with others better than them; and none can outstrip Us.",
  70042: "So leave them to engage in idle talk and diversion until they encounter their promised Day.",
  70043: "The Day they will come forth from the graves swiftly, as if they were rushing toward an idol. [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. The comparison draws on the image of idolaters running eagerly toward their idols or cultic markers during rituals and festivals. On the Day of Resurrection, the idolaters will rise from their graves with the same frantic urgency—but now driven by fear and compulsion, not devotion or enthusiasm. The irony is striking: they once rushed willingly toward false objects of worship, and now they rush unwillingly toward judgment. The verse emphasizes the suddenness, inevitability, and humiliating urgency of the Resurrection, when denial gives way to forced confrontation with the truth.",
  70044: "Their eyes downcast, humiliation covering them—this is the Day they were promised.",
  71001: "Indeed, We sent Noah to his people, saying, ‘Warn your people before a painful punishment comes upon them.’",
  71002: "He said, ‘My people, I am indeed a plain and clear warner to you.’",
  71003: "that you worship Allāh alone, be mindful of Him, and obey me,",
  71004: "He will forgive your sins and grant you reprieve until an appointed time. Indeed, when Allah’s appointed time comes, it cannot be delayed—if only you knew.",
  71005: "Noah said, ‘My Lord, I have been calling my people night and day,’",
  71006: "Yet my calling only drove them farther from the truth.",
  71007: "Whenever I call them so that You may forgive them, they put their fingers in their ears, cover themselves with their garments, persist in stubbornness, and grow ever more arrogant. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. The act of thrusting fingers into the ears signifies deliberate refusal to listen—not inability to hear. Covering themselves with their garments symbolizes conscious avoidance and self-concealment, as though shielding themselves from the truth itself. This physical imagery reflects inner rejection: they blocked hearing, hid from confrontation, and refused engagement. Their persistence indicates stubborn continuity in denial despite repeated calls, while “growing arrogantly proud” denotes willful pride—rejecting guidance not out of ignorance, but out of self-exaltation. The verse thus portrays layered resistance: physical, psychological, moral, and spiritual.",
  71008: "Then I openly declared the message to them,",
  71009: "then I addressed them publicly and spoke to them privately.",
  71010: "I said, ‘Seek your Lord’s forgiveness—indeed, He is Most Forgiving.’",
  71011: "He will send you abundant rain from the sky,",
  71012: "and provide you with wealth and children, and bless you with gardens and rivers.",
  71013: "What is the matter with you that you do not hold Allāh in due reverence, acknowledging His greatness? [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The phrase “that you do not hold Allāh in due reverence,” combines reverent awe, recognition of majesty, and hope grounded in respect. It does not mean mere optimism, but a dignified expectation shaped by awareness of Allāh’s greatness. The rebuke challenges a moral contradiction: those who benefit from Allāh’s favors yet fail to honor Him appropriately—by humility, obedience, and awe—are out of harmony with reason and gratitude. The verse calls the heart to recognize divine grandeur and respond with reverence that transforms belief into conduct.",
  71014: "When He has indeed created you through successive stages? [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. “Created you through successive stages” refers to the gradual process of human creation and development—from conception, embryonic phases, birth, growth, maturity, and onward through life. It also encompasses stages of physical, intellectual, and moral development. The verse appeals to reflection: recognizing this ordered, purposeful progression should awaken reverence for Allāh’s wisdom and power, countering denial and heedlessness.",
  71015: "Do you not see how Allāh has created the seven heavens, one above another,",
  71016: "and placed the moon therein as a light, and made the sun a burning lamp?",
  71017: "Allāh brought you forth from the earth as a plant, [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. That is, your father Adam, whom Allāh created from clay, and from whom humanity originates. Thereafter, human life is sustained by what the earth produces—such as grain, fruits, and other provisions—by Allāh’s decree.\nThe imagery of growing like a plant emphasizes gradual, nurtured development under divine care. It points first to human origin—created from earthly elements—and then to the stages of growth, sustenance, and increase governed entirely by Allāh. Just as plants depend on soil, water, light, and precise conditions, so human life depends wholly on divine provision and wisdom. The comparison also alludes to return and renewal: as plants emerge from the earth, so humanity will be brought forth again after death. The verse thus calls to humility, gratitude, and reflection on resurrection and accountability.",
  71018: "Then He will return you to it and bring you forth again. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The verse affirms the full cycle of human existence by divine decree: origin, return, and resurrection. “Return you to it” refers to burial in the earth after death, while “bring you forth again” clearly denotes resurrection. The repetition emphasizes certainty and ease—just as the first creation occurred, the second is no more difficult. The verse thus grounds belief in the Hereafter in observable origins and invites confidence in Allāh’s power to recreate.",
  71019: "Allāh has made the earth a broad expanse for you,",
  71020: "so that you may traverse its broad ways, [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. That is, in pursuit of a lawful livelihood.\n“Broad ways” refers to the wide, open routes Allāh has made across the earth—natural paths, valleys, plains, and later developed roads—that enable human movement, travel, trade, migration, and communication. The phrase highlights how the earth’s design facilitates ease rather than confinement, allowing people to journey, seek provision, reflect, and settle across lands. It also points to divine wisdom in making the world navigable, orderly, and suited for human civilization, reminding that even ordinary mobility and livelihood are signs of Allāh’s care and provision.",
  71021: "Noah said, ‘My Lord, they have disobeyed me and followed those whose wealth and children only increase them in loss.’",
  71022: "They devised a mighty plot. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. The phrase “a mighty plot” refers to the organized, deliberate efforts of the leaders and elites among Noah’s people to oppose the truth. Their plotting included coordinated deception, public misrepresentation of the message, intimidation of followers, and persistent efforts to preserve idolatry and social control. The term a mighty plot indicates not a momentary act, but a sustained, strategic campaign of resistance. The verse underscores that disbelief was not passive ignorance, but an intentional conspiracy against guidance—making their accountability all the more severe.",
  71023: "They said, ‘Never abandon your gods—especially Wadd, Suwāʿ, Yaghūth, Yaʿūq, and Nasr.’ [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. These names refer to righteous individuals among the people of Noah. When they passed away, Satan inspired their people to erect images in their honor and place them in their gathering areas, naming the images after those pious figures. At that time, these images were not worshipped; they were intended only as memorials to remind people of their righteousness. However, when those generations passed away and knowledge of the original intent was forgotten, later generations began to worship them, thus falling into idolatry.\nMost early scholars and commentators explain that after the deaths of these righteous individuals, people remained near their graves, made images of them, and over time—through ignorance and gradual deviation—veneration turned into worship. This verse exposes the historical roots of idolatry and serves as a warning against exaggeration in honoring the righteous, showing how sincere remembrance can devolve into false worship when knowledge and guidance are lost.",
  71024: "They have led many astray; so, O my Lord, let the persistent wrongdoers increase only in error. [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. The phrase “do not increase the persistent wrongdoers except in error” reflects a rhetorical supplication found in prophetic speech. It does not imply a desire for injustice, but rather expresses a recognition of the moral consequence of persistent rejection. Those described as “persistent wrongdoers” are not merely mistaken, but willfully continue in disbelief and misguidance despite repeated warnings.\nIn this context, “increase … in error” signifies that, as a result of their continued obstinacy, they are left to stray further, their deviation becoming more evident and self-inflicted. It underscores a principle found in early religious discourse: when guidance is repeatedly rejected, misguidance deepens as a consequence.\nThus, the statement conveys both a plea for divine justice and an acknowledgment that continued rebellion leads to further estrangement from truth—not by arbitrary\ndecree, but as a result of one’s own persistent choices.",
  71025: "Because of their sins, they were drowned and then made to enter the Fire, and they found no helpers besides Allāh.[10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. This verse links cause and consequence with striking clarity. Their drowning refers to the punishment in this world—the Flood that overtook the people of Noah—while being made to enter the Fire refers to their punishment in the Hereafter. The immediate sequence underscores that divine justice spans both realms, worldly and eternal. The statement that they found no helpers besides Allāh nullifies every false reliance: idols, leaders, wealth, lineage, or alliances offered no protection. The verse thus affirms absolute divine sovereignty and serves as a warning that salvation and aid come from Allāh alone, and only through faith and obedience.",
  71026: "Noah said, ‘My Lord, do not leave upon the earth any of the disbelievers.’ [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. This supplication reflects the culmination of a long and patient struggle. After persistent calling by Noah—carried out openly and privately, night and day—his people continued in deliberate rejection and led others astray. His plea is not born of haste or personal anger, but follows enduring resistance to guidance and the spread of corruption.\nThe request “do not leave… any of the disbelievers” is understood in this context as a call for the removal of a society that had become wholly resistant to truth, such that its continued existence would only perpetuate injustice and misguidance. It reflects a moment where mercy, having been repeatedly offered and refused, gives way to divine justice.\nAt a deeper level, the verse highlights the gravity of collective moral decay: when wrongdoing becomes entrenched and self-perpetuating, its consequences extend beyond individuals to entire communities.\nThe supplication thus conveys both the sorrow of a messenger and the seriousness of persistent rejection.",
  71027: "If You spare them, they will lead Your servants astray and produce only wicked, ungrateful offspring. [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. This statement reflects a prophetic assessment shaped by prolonged experience rather than a general judgment on humanity. After sustained دعوت (calling) by Noah—met with persistent rejection and active misguidance—this supplication expresses the concern that, if left unchecked, such a community would continue to corrupt others and perpetuate disbelief across generations.\nThe phrase “beget none but wicked, ungrateful offspring” is not a deterministic claim about all descendants, but a description of a society so deeply entrenched in فساد (corruption) that it reproduces its values through upbringing and environment. In this sense, the verse highlights the powerful role of social influence and moral conditioning in shaping future generations.\nThus, the supplication underscores both the gravity of collective wrongdoing and the far-reaching consequences of unchecked misguidance—where corruption is not only sustained but inherited.",
  71028: "My Lord, forgive me, my parents, and whoever enters my home as a believer, and all believing men and women; and do not increase the wrongdoers except in ruin. [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. This closing supplication reflects a shift from warning to mercy. After a long mission marked by calling, patience, and rejection, the prayer of Noah turns to forgiveness—for himself, his closest family, and the wider community of believers. The inclusion of “whoever enters my home as a believer” has been understood both literally and symbolically: referring not only to physical entry, but also to those who accept the message and join the household of faith.\nThe mention of “believing men and women” broadens the supplication universally, emphasizing the shared spiritual bond that transcends lineage and place. It highlights a key theme: that faith, not kinship, defines true belonging.\nThe final phrase, “do not increase the wrongdoers except in ruin,” contrasts with the earlier plea for forgiveness. It reflects the moral divide between those who accept guidance and those who persist in rejection. While mercy is sought for the believers, the supplication acknowledges that continued wrongdoing leads inevitably to downfall—underscoring both divine justice and the consequences of persistent corruption.",
  72001: "Say [O Prophet], “It has been revealed to me that a group of the jinn listened [to the Qurʾān], and said, “Indeed, we have heard an amazing recitation,” [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. The “jinn” are a distinct creation made from smokeless fire, unseen to humans, yet morally accountable like mankind. This verse refers to a specific group of jinn who listened attentively to the recitation of the Qurʾān by the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ and were deeply moved by it. Their description of the Qurʾān as “an amazing recitation” (ʿajaban) reflects astonishment at its clarity, power, truthfulness, and guidance—qualities they immediately recognized as divine. The verse affirms that the message of the Qurʾān is universal, addressed not only to humans but also to jinn, and that sincere listeners—regardless of their nature—can recognize truth when they encounter it.",
  72002: "that guides to what is right, so we have believed in it, and we will never associate anyone with Allāh, our Lord.",
  72003: "And that He, our Lord—exalted and glorified—has never taken a wife or a son.[2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. Lit. “Exalted is the grandeur (or majesty) of our Lord” (taʿālā jaddu rabbinā). The Arabic phrase is an inserted declaration affirming God’s absolute transcendence and sovereignty. Jadd here signifies majesty, greatness, or exalted standing—not lineage, fortune, or progeny. The formulation “has never taken” reflects the emphatic Qur’anic negation of any attribution of familial relations to God, addressing both pagan conceptions of divine consorts and Christian claims of divine sonship.",
  72004: "And that our foolish one has been speaking outrageous lies about Allāh. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. By falsely attributing to Him a wife or a son or daughters or other partners or wrong attributes.\nThe Arabic safīhunā (“our foolish one”) is commonly understood as a reference to Satan (Iblīs), though some also interpret it more generally as foolish or impious speakers among the jinn. The term shaṭaṭā denotes extreme deviation, outrageous excess, or gross falsehood—here describing statements that violate God’s absolute transcendence by attributing to Him what is unbefitting.",
  72005: "We had thought that humans and jinn would never speak lies about Allāh.",
  72006: "And there were men among humankind who sought refuge with men from the jinn, but they only increased them in burden.",
  72007: "They thought, just as you 'jinn' thought, that Allāh would never send a messenger.",
  72008: "We sought access to the heaven, but found it filled with mighty guards and blazing flames.",
  72009: "We used to sit there in positions to eavesdrop; but whoever listens now will find a flaming fire lying in wait for him.",
  72010: "We do not know whether harm is intended for those on the earth, or whether their Lord intends to guide them aright.",
  72011: "Among us are the righteous, and among us are those below that; we are of differing ways.",
  72012: "We have realized that we can never overpower Allāh on earth, nor can we escape Him by fleeing.",
  72013: "When we heard the guidance, we believed in it. Whoever believes in his Lord will not fear deprivation or injustice.",
  72014: "Among us are Muslims ˹those who submit to Allāh˺, and among us are the deviant. Whoever submits—those are the ones who seek right guidance. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. Muslims (lit. “those who submit”) here refers to those among the jinn who have willingly submitted themselves to God in faith and obedience. In this context, the term carries its primary Qur’anic sense of submission to Allāh, not an ethnic or communal designation. The verse contrasts al-muslimūn with al-qāsiṭūn (“the inequitable,” those who deviate from justice), highlighting moral and spiritual alignment rather than species or origin.",
  72015: "But as for those who deviate, they will be fuel for Hellfire.",
  72016: "Had they remained steadfast on the right Way, We would surely have granted them abundant water—˹that is, provision and prosperity˺.",
  72017: "So that We might test them thereby [5]. Whoever turns away from the Remembrance of his Lord [6]—He will cause him to enter an ever-increasing torment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The phrase “that We might test them thereby”. The “test” here refers to trial through ease and abundance rather than hardship, examining gratitude, obedience, and moral response in the presence of divine favor.\n[6]. “Remembrance” (dhikr) denotes God’s revelation and admonition—here understood as the Qur’an and the call to faith it conveys. Turning away implies conscious disregard rather than mere ignorance, and the resulting “escalating torment” signifies a punishment that intensifies without relief.",
  72018: "The mosques ˹places of worship˺ are for Allāh alone, so do not call upon anyone besides Him. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. “The term al-masājid (mosques) refers primarily to places of prostration and worship, but it also extends to every place of devotion—even the earth as a whole. It emphasizes that all acts of worship—supplication, prayer, and obedience—are due exclusively to Allāh. The command “do not invoke anyone besides Allāh ” affirms absolute monotheism (tawḥīd) in worship, rejecting any form of intermediary invocation, whether directed toward angels, jinn, saints, or any other beings.",
  72019: "When the servant of Allāh stood up invoking Him alone, they almost crowded around him. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. “The servant of Allāh” refers to the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ in his role of complete devotion and servitude. The pronoun “they” refer to the jinn, who gathered closely upon hearing the Qur’an recited; some also include people among the audience. The expression “crowded” conveying the sense of a tightly packed, overlapping crowd drawn intensely toward the act of exclusive worship.",
  72020: "Say, ‘I only call upon my Lord ˹alone˺, and I do not associate anyone with Him.’",
  72021: "Say, ‘Indeed, I have no power to harm or guide you—except by Allah’s will.’",
  72022: "Say, ‘No one can protect me from Allāh, nor can I find any refuge apart from Him.’",
  72023: "I only convey what I receive from Allāh and His messages; and whoever disobeys Allāh and His messenger—for him is the Fire of Hell, wherein he will abide forever.",
  72024: "When they finally see what they were promised, they will realize who is weaker in helpers and fewer in number.",
  72025: "Say, ‘I do not know whether what you are promised is near, or whether my Lord will appoint for it a distant term.’",
  72026: "He is the Knower of the unseen; and He does not disclose His [knowledge of the] unseen to anyone,",
  72027: "except a messenger whom He has chosen; He places guards ˹angels˺ before him and behind him,",
  72028: "so that He may make evident that they have indeed delivered the messages of their Lord. And He encompasses all that is with them and has enumerated everything in number.",
  73001: "O you who are wrapped ˹in your cloak˺, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. That is, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, who had wrapped himself in his cloak after his first encounter with the angel Gabriel, seeking reassurance and composure following the sudden and overwhelming experience of revelation.",
  73002: "rise at night ˹to pray˺, except for a little,",
  73003: "˹pray˺ half of it, or a little less,",
  73004: "or add to it a little more, and recite the Qur’an with measured recitation. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The command \"recite the Qur’an distinctly\" instructs deliberate, measured recitation marked by clear pronunciation, unhurried pacing, and reflective pauses, aiming at understanding and attentiveness rather than melodic performance.",
  73005: "Indeed, We will send down to you a weighty Word. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. That is, a revelation of great gravity and responsibility, bearing moral, spiritual, and practical weight upon its recipient. The Qur’an, when revealed to the Prophet ﷺ, bore heavily upon him due to its unparalleled excellence, the depth and perfection of its meanings, and the nobility of its ordinances.",
  73006: "Indeed, rising at night ˹for prayer˺ is more effective for strengthening the soul and more conducive to sound recitation. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. That is, night prayer brings about greater harmony and firmness between the heart and the tongue, making one more focused and the recitation clearer, steadier, and more deliberate.",
  73007: "Indeed, during the day you are occupied with prolonged activity.",
  73008: "Remember the Name of your Lord, and devote yourself to Him in wholehearted devotion. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The expression \"and devote yourself to him\" denotes complete dedication and exclusive turning toward Allāh, involving inward detachment from all else and sustained devotion in worship and remembrance.",
  73009: "He is the Lord of the East and the West; there is no god but Him—so take Him as your Disposer of your affairs.",
  73010: "Bear patiently what they say, and part from them graciously.",
  73011: "Leave to Me the deniers—those endowed with abundance—and grant them a little respite.",
  73012: "Indeed, We have prepared for them heavy chains and Hellfire,",
  73013: "and food that chokes, and a painful punishment,",
  73014: "on the Day when the earth and the mountains quake, and the mountains become like heaps of loose sand.",
  73015: "Indeed, We have sent you a messenger [6] as a witness over you, just as We sent a messenger to Pharaoh.\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. This refers to Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, who was sent as a witness over all people—conveying the divine message faithfully and bearing testimony to their response, whether acceptance or rejection. His role parallels that of Moses, who was sent to Pharaoh as a messenger, calling him and his people to the truth and standing as a witness against their denial.\nThe concept of “witness” here encompasses both the delivery of revelation and accountability before God: the messenger establishes the proof, leaving no excuse for those who receive the message. Thus, the verse highlights a recurring pattern in divine guidance—messengers are sent to make the truth clear, and their communities are judged according to their response.\nIn this context, salvation is rooted in affirming the oneness of Allāh (tawḥīd), sincerely acknowledging Him as the sole Lord and object of worship, and believing in His messengers. This includes faith in Muhammad ﷺ as the final Prophet sent to all people, as well as in earlier messengers such as Moses and Jesus—peace be upon them all—who were sent to the Children of Israel with the same essential call: to worship God alone.",
  73016: "But Pharaoh disobeyed the Messenger [7], so We seized him with a severe punishment.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. That is, Pharaoh rejected the messenger sent to him, namely Moses. Likewise, the Children of Israel later rejected Jesus, God’s messenger to them, and sought to put him to death, though God saved him from them and raised him up. In the same pattern, many now reject God’s final Messenger, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. The verse thus warns that rejecting God’s messengers leads to grave consequences.",
  73017: "How then will you guard yourselves, if you persist in disbelief, against a Day that will make children grey-haired?",
  73018: "The sky will be torn apart by it; His promise will surely be fulfilled.",
  73019: "Indeed, this is a reminder, so whoever wills may take a way to his Lord.",
  73020: "Indeed, your Lord knows that you ˹O Prophet˺ rise ˹for prayer˺ nearly two-thirds of the night, or half of it, or a third of it, and so do a group of those with you. And Allāh determines the measure of the night and the day. He knows that you will not be able to keep count of it, so He has turned to you in mercy [8]. So recite what is easy of the Qur’an. He knows that among you are those who are ill, others who travel through the land seeking the bounty of Allāh, and others who strive in the cause of Allāh. So recite what is easy of it, and establish prayer, give the poor-rate, and lend to Allāh a goodly loan [9]. Whatever good you send forth for yourselves, you will find it with Allāh —better and greater in reward [10]. And seek the forgiveness of Allāh [11]. Indeed, Allāh is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.”\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. That is, Allāh has shown mercy and granted concession by lightening the obligation of prolonged night prayer, recognizing human limitation and the difficulty of maintaining precise calculation of its duration. The ruling mentioned at the beginning of this chapter is thus eased here: believers are no longer required to adhere to the specific portions previously prescribed, but may instead pray at night whatever they are able. This concession reflects Allāh’s mercy toward His servants and His will that they not be burdened with hardship.\n[9]. A “goodly loan” refers to spending in charity sincerely for the sake of Allāh, without expectation of worldly return. It includes helping those in need without seeking profit or charging interest. Such giving is described as a “loan” only by way of encouragement, for Allāh guarantees recompense multiplied in the Hereafter.\n[10]. That is, any good deeds performed with sincerity will be preserved by Allāh and returned in the Hereafter with greater benefit and multiplied reward. Charity and assistance to the needy are especially honored: reward may be multiplied tenfold, seven hundredfold, or even more, as Allāh wills. The expression “lend” signifies divine assurance that generosity will be repaid—not only through material means, but also through spiritual blessing and lasting good.\n[11]. A command to continually seek Allāh’s forgiveness for shortcomings and sins, even while striving in obedience—emphasizing humility and reliance upon His mercy. Allāh forgives those who turn to Him sincerely and seek His pardon.",
  74001: "O you who are covered ˹in your cloak˺, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. Arabic al-muzzammil (“the one who is covered or wrapped up”), referring to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, who wrapped himself in his cloak after his first encounter with revelation, having seen the angel Gabriel for the first time, before being commanded to rise for night prayer.",
  74002: "arise and warn, [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. That is, arise to publicly proclaim the divine message and warn people of accountability and judgment. This command marks the beginning of the Prophet Muhammad’s open mission, calling him from personal preparation to active warning and guidance, summoning humanity away from unrighteousness and toward faith and obedience. Out of His mercy, Allāh sent His final Messenger, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, so that whoever believes in Allāh and follows His Messenger may be guided and attain salvation in the Hereafter.",
  74003: "and your Lord—glorify and exalt Him,",
  74004: "and purify your garments, [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. Literally, “purify your garments.” This includes physical cleanliness required for prayer and worship, and it signifies purification of conduct, character, and intentions from sin and moral defilement.",
  74005: "and shun idols, [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. Al-rujz is interpreted by many commentators as idols and false objects of worship, and by others more broadly as all forms of spiritual and moral impurity linked to idolatry. The command calls for complete dissociation from idolatry in belief, worship, and conduct.",
  74006: "and do not confer favor seeking a greater return, [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. That is, do not bestow favors or acts of generosity with the intention of gaining greater benefit, status, or recompense in return. The command calls for sincerity and selflessness, particularly in conveying the divine message, without seeking worldly advantage or recognition. True generosity is grounded in seeking Allāh's pleasure alone, with the assurance that any lasting reward comes from Him and is far greater than what people can offer.",
  74007: "and be patient for the sake of your Lord.",
  74008: "When the Trumpet is blown, [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. The “Trumpet” refers to the great blast announcing the onset of the Day of Resurrection, signaling the end of the present world and the beginning of divine judgment, as described repeatedly in the Qur’an.",
  74009: "that Day will be a hard day,",
  74010: "—not easy for the disbelievers.",
  74011: "Leave to Me the one whom I created alone, [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. That is, the one whom Allāh created alone—without wealth, power, or supporters. The expression underscores human dependence at the moment of creation and contrasts it with later arrogance and denial of divine truth. It serves as a reminder that all subsequent wealth, status, and influence are granted by Allāh and may be withdrawn by Him.",
  74012: "and granted him abundant wealth,",
  74013: "and sons ever present with him,",
  74014: "and spread out everything before him, easing his life,",
  74015: "Yet he greedily desires that I should give him more! [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. That is, despite the many favors already granted, he remains greedy and dissatisfied, presuming entitlement to further blessings without gratitude or submission.",
  74016: "No indeed! He has been stubborn toward Our verses. [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. That is, he denied the Qur’an revealed to My Messenger, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. The term denotes deliberate obstinacy: a willful resistance to Allāh’s revealed signs, not arising from ignorance, but from pride and defiance.",
  74017: "I will burden him with a relentless climb. [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. That is, to climb a slippery mountain in the Hell-fire called As-Sa‘ûd, or face a severe punishment! It refers to a steep, exhausting ascent. It symbolizes a form of punishment that grows increasingly burdensome, allowing no rest or relief, and conveys relentless hardship rather than a single moment of pain.",
  74018: "Indeed, he thought and plotted. [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. That is, he deliberately reflected and calculated how to oppose the Qur’an, scheming to devise an explanation or accusation that would undermine its message.",
  74019: "May he perish—how he plotted!",
  74020: "Again, may he perish—how he plotted!",
  74021: "Then he looked about,",
  74022: "then he frowned and scowled,",
  74023: "then he turned away and grew arrogant, [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. That is, he deliberately turned away from the truth and responded with arrogance, refusing to humble himself before God’s message despite recognizing its force.",
  74024: "and said, “This is nothing but magic passed down ˹from others˺.",
  74025: "This is nothing but the word of a human being.”",
  74026: "Soon I will consign him to Saqar. [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. Saqar is a name for Hell, mentioned here to convey intense and inescapable punishment. The term is associated with scorching, consuming fire and is later described in the passage as sparing nothing and leaving nothing intact. The Qur’an deliberately retains the proper name to emphasize the gravity and certainty of divine judgment.",
  74027: "What will make you realize what Saqar is?",
  74028: " It spares nothing and leaves nothing unburned, [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. That is, Saqar utterly consumes the disbelievers for whom it is prepared, sparing no one among them and leaving nothing intact. The absolute phrasing emphasizes the totality and severity of the punishment, allowing no exception or relief.",
  74029: "scorching and blackening human skin.",
  74030: "Over it are nineteen ˹angels˺. [15]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. That is, nineteen angels appointed over Hell, entrusted with its guardianship and execution of punishment.",
  74031: "We have appointed none but angels as wardens of the Fire, and We have made their number only a trial for those who disbelieve—so that those given the Scripture may attain certainty, those who believe may increase in faith, and both the People of the Scripture and the believers may have no doubt; and so that those in whose hearts is a disease and the disbelievers may say, ‘What does Allāh mean by this example?’ Thus Allāh leaves astray whom He wills and guides whom He wills. None knows the hosts of your Lord except Him. This is but a reminder for humankind.",
  74032: "No! By the moon,",
  74033: "and by the night as it departs,",
  74034: "and by the morning as it brightens;",
  74035: " Indeed, it is one of the greatest portents [16] —\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. That is, Hellfire is among the greatest signs and warnings of Allāh, pointing to the reality of judgment and accountability.",
  74036: "a warning to all humankind,",
  74037: "for whoever among you wills to go forward or remain behind.",
  74038: "Every soul is held in pledge for what it has earned,",
  74039: "except the people of the right, [17]\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. The phrase “the people of the right” refers to those who will receive their record of deeds in their right hands—a symbol of acceptance, success, and divine favor on the Day of Judgment. In Qur’anic usage, the “right” signifies blessing, righteousness, and honor, in contrast to the “left,” which denotes loss and rejection.\nThe people of the right are characterized by faith and righteous action. They believe in Allāh, affirm His oneness, and follow His guidance with sincerity. Their lives are marked by moral integrity, compassion, humility, and obedience to divine commands. They avoid major wrongdoing, seek forgiveness for their shortcomings, and strive for spiritual growth.\nIn broader Qur’anic descriptions, they are associated with qualities such as truthfulness, patience, charity, and mindfulness of God. As a result, they are promised ease in reckoning, security from punishment, and entry into Paradise. Thus, the expression not only identifies their final outcome but also reflects a life lived in conscious devotion and moral responsibility.",
  74040: "in gardens of bliss, asking one another,",
  74041: "about the guilty,",
  74042: "What brought you into Saqar ˹Hell˺?",
  74043: "They will say, ‘We were not among those who observed prayer,’",
  74044: "nor did we feed the poor,",
  74045: "and we used to engage in falsehood with those who engaged in it,",
  74046: "and we used to deny the Day of Judgment,",
  74047: "until the certainty ˹of death˺ came to us.",
  74048: "So the intercession of intercessors will not benefit them.",
  74049: "Then what is the matter with them that they turn away from the Reminder,",
  74050: "as though they were frightened zebras,",
  74051: "fleeing from a lion? [18]\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. The term qaswarah refers to a fierce predator, commonly understood as a lion. The simile evokes a scene of sudden panic and instinctive flight—describing how they recoil from the Reminder with fear and aversion, as though it were a deadly threat rather than a source of guidance and salvation.",
  74052: "Rather, each one of them desires that he be given scrolls laid open.",
  74053: "No! Rather, they do not fear the Hereafter.",
  74054: "No indeed! It is truly a reminder. [19]\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. The term “Reminder” refers to the Qur’an as a message that awakens what is already known within the human soul—the recognition of truth, accountability, and the reality of the Hereafter. It is called a reminder because it does not introduce an unfamiliar truth, but rather calls people back to their innate awareness of God and moral responsibility.\nThus, the verse emphasizes that the message is clear, accessible, and sufficient in itself. Rejection, therefore, is not due to lack of evidence, but to heedlessness or unwillingness to respond. The Qur’an serves as both a guidance for those who seek truth and a proof against those who turn away.",
  74055: "So whoever wills may take heed.",
  74056: "Yet they will not take heed unless Allāh wills. He is most worthy to be feared and most worthy to forgive.",
  75001: "I do swear by the Day of Resurrection,",
  75002: "And I swear by the self-reproaching soul, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. The self-reproaching soul refers to the human conscience that reproaches itself for wrongdoing and moral failure. It is cited here as evidence of innate moral awareness and accountability, pointing to the reality of judgment and resurrection. The Qur’an frequently appeals to this inner moral witness, which was emphasized in the message of the Prophet Muhammad (ﷺ), who called people to repentance and moral reform.",
  75003: "Does man think that We cannot restore his bones? [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. This rhetorical question challenges denial of the Resurrection. By specifically mentioning bones—often thought of as the last remnants of the human body—the verse emphasizes that reassembling the body is fully within Allāh’s power, and that resurrection after death is neither impossible nor improbable.",
  75004: "Yes indeed! We are fully able to restore even his fingertips.",
  75005: "Rather, man desires to persist in sin ahead of him, [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. This refers to the disbeliever who denies the Day of Resurrection and Judgment. He desires to persist in sin and moral transgression, deliberately disregarding the accountability that lies ahead of him. The expression denotes willful defiance and conscious denial of future judgment, not ignorance or lack of evidence.",
  75006: "He asks ˹mockingly˺, ‘When is the Day of Resurrection?’",
  75007: "So when the sight is dazed,",
  75008: "and the moon is eclipsed,",
  75009: "and the sun and the moon are brought together, [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. That is, the sun and the moon—normally separate and governed by a fixed cosmic order—are brought together as part of the upheaval of the Resurrection. Some reports mention the loss of their light or the overturning of their usual courses. The image signifies the collapse of the familiar natural order and the approach of final judgment.",
  75010: "On that Day, man will say, ‘Where is the escape?’",
  75011: "No! There is no refuge. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The phrase lā wazar literally means “there is no refuge or place of escape.” Early commentators explain that wazar originally referred to a protective shelter or mountain stronghold to which one might flee in danger. In the context of Sūrat al-Qiyāmah, the verse emphasizes the impossibility of escaping divine judgment on the Day of Resurrection.",
  75012: "To your Lord on that Day is the final destination.",
  75013: "Man will be informed on that Day of all that he sent forth and left behind. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. That is, man will be fully informed of all his deeds—both those he committed and those he neglected or delayed. Nothing, whether action or omission, good or bad, will be hidden on that Day.",
  75014: "Rather, man will be a witness against his own self. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. A person becomes a witness against himself through his own awareness, testimony, and the undeniable evidence of his deeds. His conscience, his record of actions, and even his own limbs will testify, leaving no room for denial. Thus, the human being carries within himself the proof of his own accountability.",
  75015: "Even as he offers his excuses.",
  75016: "Do not move your tongue ˹O Prophet˺ with it to hasten it ˹i.e., the recitation of the Qur’an˺. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. This instruction was addressed to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, who would move his tongue quickly to repeat the revelation as it was being conveyed, out of eagerness and concern to preserve it. Allāh reassures him that the Qur’an will be fully gathered and recited to him without the need for haste. The verse conveys reassurance rather than rebuke, emphasizing divine care and the guaranteed preservation of revelation, so that he need not rush or fear forgetting any part of it.",
  75017: "Indeed, it is upon Us to make you memorize and recite it.",
  75018: "Then when We recite it, follow its recitation. [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. That is, when the revelation is conveyed through the angel Gabriel, the Prophet ﷺ is instructed to listen attentively and follow the divinely guided recitation, without hastening to repeat it prematurely.",
  75019: "Then indeed, upon Us rests its explanation. [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. That is, Allāh has taken upon Himself the responsibility of clarifying the meaning of the revelation to the Prophet ﷺ—whether through further verses, divine instruction, or inspired understanding—so that it may be conveyed and understood correctly.",
  75020: "No! Rather, you love the present life,",
  75021: "and neglect the Hereafter.",
  75022: "On that Day, some faces will shine with radiance,",
  75023: "looking at their Lord. [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. That is, the believers will be granted the honor of gazing toward their Lord on the Day of Resurrection. This vision is understood as a real and special reward bestowed upon the believers, in a manner befitting His majesty.",
  75024: "And other faces on that Day will be shrouded in gloom,",
  75025: "knowing that a crushing calamity will befall them.",
  75026: "No! When the soul reaches the collarbones,",
  75027: "and it is said, ‘Is there any healer?’ [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. That is, those present at the time of death ask in desperation whether there is anyone who can heal or save the dying person. The question reflects the final human attempt to find a remedy, though none remains once the soul has reached this stage. The word rāq is related to ruqyah, a form of supplication and healing through Qur’ānic verses and prescribed invocations taught by the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. However, at this moment, even such means can no longer avert death, underscoring the inevitability of the soul’s departure.",
  75028: "And he knows for certain that it is the moment of parting,",
  75029: "and the leg is entwined with the leg, [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. An image describing the final moments of death, when the body becomes powerless and the legs are drawn together. It is commonly understood as a reference to the dying person being prepared for burial, including the binding or shrouding of the legs, and signifies the complete loss of bodily control and the imminence of departure from this world.\nIt has also been interpreted metaphorically to mean that one hardship is joined to another: the agony of death combined with the distress of leaving the worldly life, the anxiety over what awaits in the Hereafter, and the simultaneous presence of people preparing the body for burial and angels taking charge of the soul.",
  75030: "to your Lord on that Day will be the procession. [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. The expression “the procession” conveys the inevitable movement of every soul toward its final standing before Allāh. It evokes the image of being driven or led onward—whether willingly or unwillingly—toward the ultimate encounter with one’s Lord. In this sense, it signifies not merely physical movement, but the final return and accountability after death.\nThe verse underscores the certainty of this journey: no one can escape it, delay it, or divert from it. All human striving culminates in this moment, where each person stands before Allāh to face the outcome of their deeds. Thus, the “procession” reflects both the inevitability of return and the solemn reality of judgment.",
  75031: "So he neither believed nor prayed,",
  75032: "but rather, he denied and turned away,",
  75033: "then he went to his people, swaggering in conceit,",
  75034: "Woe to you, and woe!",
  75035: "Again, woe to you, and woe!",
  75036: "Does man think that he will be left neglected? [15]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. That is, left without purpose, responsibility, accountability, or command. The verse rebukes the notion that human beings are created aimlessly or will not be called to account for their deeds.",
  75037: "Was he not once a drop of emitted sperm?",
  75038: "Then he became a clinging clot, and He created and proportioned him,",
  75039: "and made from him the two mates, male and female.",
  75040: "Is He not able to bring the dead back to life? [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. That is, the One who created man from an insignificant drop, formed him in stages, and determined his nature and form, is surely able to resurrect the dead. The verse affirms divine power over resurrection and refutes any doubt about life after death.",
  76001: "Has there come upon man a span of time when he was not even a thing mentioned?",
  76002: "Indeed, We created the human being from a mingled drop [1] to test him [2], and so We made him hearing and seeing.\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. That is, the mixed reproductive fluids of the man and the woman. The expression emphasizes the humble and complex origin of human life.\n[2]. That is, Allāh created man to test him through moral responsibility and free choice. The granting of hearing and sight equips him with the faculties necessary to perceive guidance, distinguish truth from falsehood, and be held accountable for his actions.",
  76003: "Indeed, We guided him to the path—whether he be grateful [3] or ungrateful [4].\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. That is, Allāh made the path of guidance clear to the human being by granting him reason, an innate moral disposition, and revealed instruction—enabling him to recognize truth and choose rightly. Gratitude in the Qur’an is not merely verbal thanks, but a conscious acknowledgment expressed through faith, obedience, and the right use of blessings. It reflects an inner recognition of divine guidance and a corresponding outward response in one’s actions.\n[4]. The term kafūr conveys intense ingratitude, often extending to denial or rejection of truth. It is not simple forgetfulness, but a willful turning away despite having been shown the path.\nIngratitude here signifies deliberate denial of Allāh's guidance and favors after they have been made known. In the Qur’an, ingratitude is not merely lack of thankfulness, but a moral and spiritual rejection that accompanies disbelief, rebellion, and corruption. Gratitude, by contrast, is inseparable from faith and acknowledgment of Allah’s lordship and guidance, while ingratitude reflects hard-heartedness, pride, and refusal to recognize divine grace.",
  76004: "Indeed, We have prepared for the disbelievers chains, shackles, and a blazing Fire. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. “chains, shackles, and a blazing Fire”\nThese images evoke restraint, humiliation, and punishment. The “chains” and “shackles” symbolize not only physical confinement but also the consequences of moral and spiritual bondage in worldly life. The “blazing Fire” (saʿīr) conveys an intensely kindled, raging punishment. Together, the imagery underscores the gravity of persistent disbelief and rejection of guidance after it has been made clear.",
  76005: "Indeed, the righteous will drink from a cup infused with camphor, [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. “camphor” (كَافُورًا)\nCamphor (kāfūr) here refers to a fragrant, cooling, and refreshing substance, used to describe the drink of the righteous in Paradise. The imagery conveys purity, serenity, and delight, contrasting with the burning punishment mentioned earlier. Early commentators note that it may symbolize not only physical refreshment but also spiritual ease and clarity, reflecting the inner purity of those who are rewarded.",
  76006: "a spring from which the servants of Allāh will drink, making it flow abundantly. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. “causing it to gush forth abundantly”\nThe verb conveys forceful, abundant flowing, suggesting not only plentiful supply but also ease and responsiveness—the spring flows as they wish. Classical commentators note that this imagery reflects a state of complete satisfaction and honor, where the righteous are granted effortless access to their reward. It contrasts sharply with worldly scarcity and restraint, symbolizing both physical delight and spiritual fulfillment.",
  76007: "They fulfill their vows and fear a Day whose evil will be widespread. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. That is, they are faithful to their obligations toward Allāh and live with awareness of the Day of Judgment, whose consequences will extend to all and spare no one except those granted divine mercy.\nWhen you make a vow to Allāh, do not delay fulfilling it. Fulfill what you vow. Better that you do not vow than that you vow and not fulfill it.",
  76008: "They give food, out of love for Allāh [9], to the needy, the orphan, and the captive, [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. That is, they give food despite their own need or desire for it, purely for the sake of Allāh. The phrase indicates sincerity and selflessness, not charity given for recognition or return.\nThe Prophet ﷺ said, \"O people, spread peace 'and love to one another', give food to the poor, uphold the ties of kinship, pray during the night while people 'normally' sleep, and you will enter Paradise in peace.\"\n[10]. The mention of captives highlights the breadth of their compassion, extending even to those who may be socially distant or hostile. It underscores mercy, generosity, and moral excellence beyond kinship, status, or expectation of reciprocity.\nHelp the poor, the orphan and the prisoned as you would a foreigner and stranger, so they can continue to live among you. Treat each other well and fairly, especially those in need. Out of love for Allāh, help those who fall on hard times and are not able to take care of themselves. Treat them just as you would treat a foreigner and stranger with hospitality and love. Love each other with genuine affection, and take delight in honoring each other and feeding the poor.",
  76009: "“We feed you only for the Countenance of Allāh; we desire from you neither reward nor gratitude. [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. The Countenance of Allāh signifies seeking His pleasure alone. The statement expresses pure sincerity: acts of charity are performed solely for the sake of Allāh, without any desire for praise, recognition, gratitude, or worldly return. Such giving reflects devotion that is free from self-interest and motivated entirely by faith.\nThis meaning is reinforced by the teaching of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, who said: “Feed the hungry, visit the sick, and free the captive.” His instruction emphasizes compassion, social responsibility, and solidarity—caring for those in need, comforting the afflicted, and assisting the vulnerable purely for the sake of Allāh, not for personal gain.",
  76010: "Indeed, we fear from our Lord a Day severe and distressful.” [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. That is, they are deeply conscious of the severity of the Day of Judgment and the accountability before their Lord. Their fear reflects reverence and responsibility, motivating sincere obedience and selfless deeds rather than despair or hopelessness.",
  76011: "So Allāh will protect them from the evil of that Day and grant them radiance and joy,",
  76012: "and will reward them for their patience with a garden and fine silk garments.",
  76013: "Reclining therein on raised couches, they will see neither scorching sun nor bitter cold.",
  76014: "Its shade will draw close over them, and its fruit clusters will be lowered within easy reach.",
  76015: "And vessels of silver and crystal cups will be circulated among them,",
  76016: "crystal-clear goblets of silver, filled with perfect measure.",
  76017: "There they will be served a cup delicately infused with ginger,",
  76018: "from a spring there called Salsabīl.",
  76019: "They will be served by eternal youths; when you behold them, you would think them scattered pearls.",
  76020: "When you look around, you will see bliss and a great kingdom. [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. “bliss and a vast dominion” denotes abundant, continuous delight, encompassing both physical enjoyment and inner contentment. Mulk kabīr (a great dominion) suggests not political rule, but a state of honor, dignity, and expansive possession, where the inhabitants of Paradise experience a sense of fullness, freedom, and sovereignty within their domain. Together, they portray a reality of complete felicity and elevated status.",
  76021: "They will be clothed in fine green silk and luxuriant brocade, adorned with bracelets of silver, and their Lord will give them a pure drink.",
  76022: "“This is your reward, and your striving has been appreciated.”",
  76023: "Indeed, We have sent down to you the Qur’an in successive stages.",
  76024: "So be patient with your Lord’s decree, and do not obey any sinner or ungrateful one among them. [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. “Decree” (ḥukm) here refers to God’s commanding judgment and unfolding will. The terms “a sinner” (āthim) and “an ungrateful one” (kafūr) are indefinite and generic, indicating any person characterized by persistent wrongdoing or profound ingratitude. Kafūr denotes one who habitually denies or covers over divine favor and truth and is broader in scope than “disbeliever,” encompassing moral and spiritual ingratitude as well as outright denial. The verse thus calls for warning the undisciplined, comforting the fainthearted, supporting the weak, and exercising patience toward all.",
  76025: "And remember the Name of your Lord morning and evening, [15]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. i.e., referring to the morning, noon, and afternoon prescribed prayers.",
  76026: "and during the night [16], prostrate to Him and glorify Him for a long part of the night.\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. i.e., the sunset and evening prayers.",
  76027: "Indeed, these love the fleeting life and neglect a weighty Day ahead of them. [17]\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. I.e., they neglect the remembrance of the Day of Judgment that awaits them, failing to work toward it and their own salvation.",
  76028: "We created them and strengthened their constitution; and if We will, We can replace them with others like them entirely.",
  76029: "Indeed, this is a reminder—so whoever wills may take a path to his Lord.",
  76030: "Yet you cannot will unless Allāh wills; indeed, Allāh is ever Knowing and Wise.",
  76031: "He admits whom He wills into His mercy; but the wrongdoers—He has prepared for them a painful punishment. [18]\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. I.e., those who commit injustice and wrongdoing, including idolaters; murderers; the sexually immoral; thieves; extortioners who seize from others—through strife, greed, or oppression—what does not lawfully belong to them; those who practice magic arts; those who utter curses or engage in verbally abusive speech; and all liars.",
  77001: "By the winds sent forth in succession, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. Lit. “those sent forth.” The referent is understood by many commentators as the winds, sent successively by Allāh; others interpret it as angels dispatched with divine commands, or as revelations sent in measured sequence. The wording deliberately allows for layered meaning, unified by the idea of purposeful dispatch under divine command.",
  77002: "and the winds that blow violently,",
  77003: "and the winds that spread [rainclouds] far and wide,",
  77004: "and those who bring criterion, [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. “Criterion” (al-furqān) denotes that which decisively distinguishes between truth and falsehood, right and wrong. It is commonly understood as divine revelation conveyed by the angels, though it may also encompass judgment, moral discernment, or authoritative command. The term is deliberately broad, emphasizing decisive separation rather than a single form of delivery.",
  77005: "And those [angels] who deliver a message,",
  77006: "as an excuse or a warning, [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. i.e., to remove any excuse before Allāh by clear communication of the truth, or to serve as a warning of the consequences of rejection and wrongdoing. Revelation thus functions either to absolve from blame through guidance or to warn through admonition.",
  77007: "Indeed, what you are promised will surely come to pass.",
  77008: "So when the stars are obliterated,",
  77009: "and when the sky is split open,",
  77010: "when the mountains are blown away, [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. “the mountains are blown away”\nThe verb conveys the idea of being uprooted, blasted, and scattered, as if reduced to dust and dispersed. It depicts a total dismantling of what is normally solid and immovable, symbolizing the collapse of the physical world’s stability at the onset of the Day of Judgment.",
  77011: "and when the messengers are appointed their time, [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. “the messengers are appointed their time”  The phrase refers to the appointed moment when the messengers are gathered and called forward, often understood as the time they will bear witness regarding their communities. It signifies a stage in the unfolding of the Day of Judgment, where accountability is established with clarity and order.",
  77012: "for what Day has all this been delayed?",
  77013: "For the Day of Final Decision! [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. Lit. “the Day of Final Decision”, denoting the Day on which matters are decisively separated and judged—between truth and falsehood, belief and denial, reward and punishment.",
  77014: "What will make you realize what the Day of Final Decision is?",
  77015: "Woe [7], on that Day, to the deniers.\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. “Woe… to the deniers”\nThe expression “woe” conveys a sense of ruin, destruction, and severe loss. It is not merely a word of lament, but a declaration of doom and impending punishment, reflecting a state of utter misery.\nThe term “the deniers” refers to those who reject, deny, or declare false the truth, especially the message of revelation and the reality of the Hereafter. It implies not mere doubt, but deliberate and active rejection after the truth has been made clear.",
  77016: "Did We not destroy the former peoples? [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. “the former peoples” this refers to earlier nations who rejected their messengers and were consequently destroyed, such as the peoples of ʿĀd, Thamūd, and others. The question serves as a rhetorical reminder, calling attention to historical precedent as evidence of divine justice and the consequences of denial.",
  77017: "Then We will cause the later ones to follow them [in destruction]. [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. “the later ones” refers to those who come after earlier destroyed nations and persist in similar denial and wrongdoing. Their being made to “follow” indicates sharing the same fate and consequence, continuing the pattern of divine justice across generations.",
  77018: "Thus do We deal with the guilty.",
  77019: "Woe, on that Day, to the deniers.",
  77020: "Did We not create you from a humble fluid [semen]?",
  77021: "Then We placed it in a secure resting place [the womb],",
  77022: "for a known term [appointed period],",
  77023: "So We determined—and how excellent are We in determining. [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. “We determined” The verb conveys the act of measuring, ordaining, and precisely determining all aspects of creation. It reflects both power and precision, indicating that everything unfolds according to divine measure.\n“how excellent are We in determining”\nThis expression affirms the perfection of divine determination—flawless, exact, and beyond deficiency. It is not self-praise in a human sense, but a declaration of absolute competence and mastery in creation.",
  77024: "Woe, on that Day, to the deniers.",
  77025: "Have We not made the earth a receptacle, [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. “a receptacle” the term conveys the idea of something that gathers, contains, and holds together. It refers to the earth as a place that encompasses both the living and the dead, serving as a dwelling, support, and final resting place. The expression highlights the earth’s role in sustaining life and enclosing all within it.",
  77026: "for the living and the dead, [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. “the living and the dead” this phrase clarifies the function of the earth as a comprehensive receptacle, containing both the living upon its surface and the dead within it. It emphasizes the earth’s role as a place of life, burial, and return, encompassing all stages of human existence.",
  77027: "Did We not set therein towering mountains and give you fresh water to drink?",
  77028: "Woe, on that Day, to the deniers.",
  77029: "Proceed to that which you used to deny! [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. “Proceed to that which you used to deny” this command is directed to those who denied the truth, instructing them to move toward the reality they once rejected. It carries a tone of irony and inevitability, as what was denied is now encountered directly. The statement marks a transition from disbelief to inescapable confrontation with the consequences.",
  77030: "Proceed to a shadow [of smoke] with three columns.",
  77031: "neither providing shade nor availing against the flames.",
  77032: "It hurls sparks as large as palaces,",
  77033: "as though they were yellowish-black camels.",
  77034: "Woe, on that Day, to the deniers.",
  77035: "This is a Day when they will not speak, [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]? “they will not speak” this indicates a moment on the Day of Judgment when the deniers are struck silent, unable to speak in their own defense. It reflects a state of helplessness and accountability, where no argument or excuse can be offered.",
  77036: "nor will they be allowed to make excuses.",
  77037: "Woe, on that Day, to the deniers.",
  77038: "This is the Day of Decision. We have gathered you and the former peoples.",
  77039: "So if you have any scheme [15], then plot it against Me. [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. “Scheme” denotes any planned stratagem, plot, or calculated attempt to oppose, evade, or undermine divine truth or judgment.\n[16]. “Against Me” expresses a direct divine challenge, emphasizing Allāh’s absolute authority and the futility of all opposition. The wording underscores that any scheme against Allāh is ultimately powerless.",
  77040: "Woe, on that Day, to the deniers.",
  77041: "Indeed, the righteous will be amid shades and springs,",
  77042: "and fruits of whatever they desire.",
  77043: "“Eat and drink in ease for what you used to do.”",
  77044: "Thus do We reward the doers of good.",
  77045: "Woe, on that Day, to the deniers.",
  77046: "Eat and enjoy yourselves for a little while; indeed, you are guilty. [17]\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. The phrase “for a little while” emphasizes the brief and fleeting nature of worldly enjoyment in contrast to the permanence of the Hereafter.\nThe term “you are guilty” refers to those who are culpable in wrongdoing, especially through denial and persistent sin. It conveys not only moral culpability but a deeper state of existential guilt and accountability before divine judgment.",
  77047: "Woe, on that Day, to the deniers.",
  77048: "When they are told, ‘Bow down in prayer,’ they do not bow down.",
  77049: "Woe, on that Day, to the deniers.",
  77050: "So in what message, after this ˹Qur’an˺, will they believe? [18]\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. “Message” translates ḥadīth, denoting an authoritative message, discourse or proclamation. The rhetorical question asserts that if this Qur’an is rejected, no other message could warrant belief, underscoring the finality and sufficiency of its guidance.",
  78001: "What are they asking one another about? [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. This opening verse introduces the central theme of the sūrah through a rhetorical question that captures the curiosity and dispute of the disbelievers. The expression “they ask one another” suggests not a sincere search for truth, but a circulating conversation marked by doubt, speculation, and often mockery. Early commentators explain that the subject of their questioning is the “Great News” mentioned in the following verse—commonly understood to be the reality of resurrection and the Day of Judgment.\nBy beginning in this way, the Qurʾān draws attention to the irony of their behavior: they discuss a matter of ultimate importance among themselves, yet fail to seek its truth with openness. The question also functions as a subtle rebuke, exposing the superficial nature of their inquiry. Rather than directly answering them, the verse sets the stage for a decisive clarification, shifting the discourse from human speculation to divine certainty.\nThe opening thus creates a sense of anticipation while highlighting a recurring Qurʾānic theme: that denial is often rooted not in ignorance alone, but in reluctance to accept the consequences of belief.",
  78002: "About the momentous news, [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The “momentous news” refers primarily to the reality of resurrection and the Day of Judgment, though it also encompasses the Qur’an itself and the divine message it proclaims. The phrase denotes a momentous truth of immense consequence, disputed by those who deny accountability.",
  78003: "that over which they differ among themselves. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. This phrase completes the description of the “Great News” by emphasizing the difference that surrounds it within their own ranks. The wording suggests an internal circulation of views—debate, doubt, and denial—rather than a unified stance. Their disagreement is not over minor details, but over a matter of ultimate consequence.\nEarly commentators identify this difference as centering on the reality of resurrection and the Day of Judgment. Despite the clarity and seriousness of this message, their responses remain divided: some deny it outright, others question it, and some waver without firm conviction.",
  78004: "No indeed—soon they will come to know. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. This verse marks a decisive shift from description to emphatic rebuke. The particle kallā is a forceful expression used in the Qurʾān to reject false assumptions and to arrest the listener’s attention. It signals that their questioning and difference are not merely mistaken, but fundamentally misplaced.\nThe statement “they will come to know” points to a future moment of undeniable realization, when the truth of the “Great News” will become clear beyond dispute. Early commentators explain that this knowledge will manifest either at death or on the Day of Judgment, when belief is no longer a matter of choice but of direct witnessing.\nThe brevity and force of the verse heighten its impact. It does not argue or elaborate, but delivers a stark warning: what is now treated as a subject of debate will soon become a matter of certain knowledge. This shift from speculation to inevitability reinforces the central theme of the passage—human doubt cannot alter the reality of what is to come.",
  78005: "Again, no indeed—soon they will come to know.",
  78006: "Have We not made the earth a resting place?",
  78007: "And the mountains as stabilizing pegs,",
  78008: "and created you in pairs,",
  78009: "and made your sleep for rest,",
  78010: "and made the night a covering, [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. This verse presents the night through the evocative metaphor of a “garment” (libās), suggesting something that envelops, conceals, and provides comfort. Just as clothing covers the body, the night covers the earth in darkness, offering a natural setting for rest and withdrawal from activity. Early commentators highlight that this imagery conveys both physical and experiential dimensions: the darkness of night shields from visibility, quiets movement, and brings a sense of stillness. It is not merely an absence of light, but a purposeful condition designed to facilitate repose.\nWithin the sequence of verses, this sign continues the pattern of divine arrangement in human life. After pointing to creation and structure, the Qurʾān now draws attention to the rhythm of time—alternating between night and day. The night, as a covering, prepares for the day’s activity, reinforcing the idea of balance and intentional design. This ordered cycle further supports the central argument: the One who governs such precise patterns in creation is fully capable of bringing about resurrection and final accountability.",
  78011: "and made the day for earning a livelihood. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. This verse completes the contrast with the previous one by presenting the day as a time designated for maʿāsh—the pursuit of livelihood and the means of sustaining life. While the night is described as a covering that brings rest, the day is characterized by movement, effort, and engagement with the world. Early commentators explain that maʿāsh encompasses not only earning provision but all forms of activity through which life is maintained and developed. The alternation between night and day thus reflects a deliberate rhythm: rest followed by action, stillness followed by striving.\nWithin the broader passage, this pairing reinforces the theme of balance and purposeful design in creation. Human life is structured within this cycle, benefiting from both repose and productivity. The verse, like those before it, directs attention to an ordered system that sustains existence—pointing to the wisdom and power of the Creator, and by extension, His ability to bring about resurrection and final reckoning.",
  78012: "and built above you seven mighty [heavens, one above the other], [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. This verse draws attention to the structure above humanity, describing the creation of “seven mighty” commonly understood as the seven heavens. The term shidād conveys strength, firmness, and durability, highlighting the greatness and stability of this elevated construction. Early exegesis interprets the “seven” as a real and ordered arrangement, each heaven layered above the other in a structured system. The use of the verb “built” suggests deliberate construction, reinforcing the idea of precision and intentional design in the cosmos.\nIn the flow of the passage, the argument expands from what lies beneath and around human beings to what is above them. The great scale and order of the heavens serve as further evidence of divine power and control. The verse thus continues to build a cumulative case: the One who has constructed such a vast and ordered system is fully capable of bringing about resurrection and the final realization previously denied.",
  78013: "and made [the sun] a radiant, blazing lamp, [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. This verse describes the sun through the vivid imagery of a “blazing lamp” combining the notion of illumination with intense heat and radiance. The term sirāj evokes a source of light that gives visibility and guidance, while wahhāj emphasizes its brilliance and burning intensity. Early commentators identify this as a reference to the sun, whose light and heat are essential for life on earth. The metaphor of a lamp suggests purposeful placement, as something set to provide both light and benefit within a larger system.\nWithin the sequence of verses, this sign completes a broader portrayal of the natural order: the earth below, the mountains within it, human creation, the rhythm of night and day, the heavens above, and now the source of light that sustains life. Each element points to deliberate arrangement and harmony. The One who has established such a balanced and life-sustaining system is fully capable of bringing about resurrection, reinforcing the certainty of the “Great News” first introduced at the beginning of the sūrah.",
  78014: "and sent down from the rainclouds pouring water in abundance—",
  78015: "so that We may bring forth by it grain and vegetation, [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. This verse completes the sequence of vegetation by describing gardens as alfāf—a term that conveys dense, intertwined, and abundant growth. The imagery evokes lush landscapes where plants are closely woven together, creating both richness and visual fullness. Early commentators explain that this description highlights not only the quantity of growth, but its density and beauty. The gardens represent the culmination of the process initiated by rainfall: from water to grain, from vegetation to flourishing, interwoven life.\nWithin the passage, this final image reinforces the theme of provision and renewal. What begins as water descending from the sky results in a vibrant and sustained ecosystem. This progression mirrors the broader Qurʾānic argument: just as barren earth is revived into layered, living abundance, so too will life be restored after death. The verse thus closes this section of signs with a clear picture of growth, abundance, and divine generosity, pointing beyond itself to the reality of resurrection.",
  78016: "and lush, densely interwoven gardens,",
  78017: "Indeed, the Day of Decision [10] has been set for an appointed time—\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. This verse returns to the central theme introduced at the beginning of the sūrah, now stated with certainty rather than questioned. The “Day of Decision” (Yawm al-Faṣl) refers to the moment when truth is decisively separated from falsehood, and every soul is judged with complete justice. The term carries the sense of finality—an ultimate distinction in which all ambiguity is removed.\nThe description of this Day as a mīqāt (appointed time) emphasizes that it is not random or uncertain, but fixed and precisely determined. Early commentators note that this appointed time is known only to Allāh, yet its occurrence is inevitable and cannot be delayed or hastened.\nWithin the flow of the passage, this verse marks a transition from signs of creation to the reality they point toward. After presenting evidence in the natural world—earth, sky, life, and provision—the Qurʾān now declares the conclusion: a Day has been set in which all will be brought to account. What was once debated and denied is here affirmed as an unavoidable and precisely scheduled reality.",
  78018: "The Day the trumpet is blown [11], then you will come forth in multitudes,\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. The “trumpet” (ṣūr) refers to the horn blown by an angel at Allāh’s command to signal the resurrection and gathering for judgment. Qur’anic passages describe multiple blasts—one bringing the present order to an end and another initiating resurrection—underscoring divine power and the certainty of the Hereafter.",
  78019: "and the sky is opened, becoming gateways, [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. This verse describes a dramatic transformation of the sky on the Day of Judgment. The familiar, continuous expanse of the heavens is described as being “opened,” becoming gateways or doors. This imagery conveys a fundamental disruption of the natural order, as what once appeared sealed and stable is now parted and reconfigured.\nEarly commentators explain that this opening signifies the removal of barriers between the seen and unseen worlds. The heavens, which once concealed the realities beyond human perception, are transformed into accessible passages, marking the unfolding of divine events and the descent of angels.\nWithin the sequence of verses, this image intensifies the portrayal of cosmic upheaval. Following the gathering of humanity, the very structure of the sky is altered, reinforcing the greatness of the Day. The verse thus underscores that the reality long denied will not only affect human beings, but will encompass the entire cosmos, as creation itself is reshaped in preparation for final judgment.",
  78020: "and the mountains will be set in motion, becoming a mirage. [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. This verse completes the sequence of cosmic transformations by describing the fate of the mountains, once presented as symbols of stability and firmness. They are “set in motion” indicating their uprooting and displacement, as the fixed order of the world is undone.\nThey then become a mirage, an image that conveys illusion, insubstantiality, and disappearance. What once appeared solid and enduring is reduced to something fleeting and unreal, emphasizing the total reversal of familiar reality on the Day of Judgment.\nEarly commentators note the deliberate contrast with earlier verses in the sūrah, where mountains were described as “pegs” anchoring the earth. Here, that same stability is dissolved, demonstrating that even the most seemingly permanent aspects of creation are temporary. The verse thus reinforces a central Qurʾānic message: that all worldly solidity will give way to a greater, ultimate reality, where only what is true and enduring remains.",
  78021: "Indeed, Hell lies in ambush, [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. This verse describes Hell (Jahannam) as a place of ambush—conveying vigilant readiness and inevitability. The imagery evokes something lying in wait along a path, not encountered by chance but certain to confront those who proceed toward it. Early commentators explain that this does not imply motion, but preparedness: Hell is already established and awaiting those whose actions lead them to it. The use of kānat (“has been”) further suggests that this reality is not merely future, but already in existence.\nWithin the structure of the sūrah, this marks a transition from the events of the Day of Judgment to their consequences. Just as the Day itself is fixed, so too are its outcomes prepared in advance. The verse thus reinforces a central Qurʾānic theme: accountability is not speculative, but inevitable, with consequences already set in readiness.",
  78022: "a home for the transgressors, [15]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. This verse describes Hell as a “home” for the transgressors, those who exceed the bounds set by Allāh and persist in rejection and wrongdoing. The term home conveys the idea of a final abode to which one ultimately returns, emphasizing permanence and inevitability.\nEarly commentators explain that this “home” is not arbitrary, but the outcome of one’s own actions. Those who live in defiance of divine limits are led to a destination that reflects their choices, reinforcing the principle of just recompense.\nWithin the flow of the sūrah, this verse clarifies the consequence hinted at in the previous one. Hell, described as lying in ambush, is now identified as the destined end for a specific group. The verse thus underscores a central Qurʾānic theme: that ultimate return is not random, but determined by one’s response to truth, leading either to reward or to a fitting and enduring consequence.",
  78023: "abiding therein for long periods,",
  78024: "They will neither taste therein any coolness nor any drink,",
  78025: "except boiling water and foul purulence,",
  78026: "a fitting recompense. [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. Lit. “a recompense in exact measure” (wifāq), i.e., a requital precisely corresponding to their disbelief and evil deeds—neither excessive nor unjust—underscoring the principle of proportional divine justice.",
  78027: "Indeed, they did not expect a reckoning,",
  78028: "and they denied Our signs with utter denial,",
  78029: "and We have recorded everything in a written record.",
  78030: "So taste—for We will only increase you in punishment.",
  78031: "Indeed, for the righteous is attainment, [17]\\n\\nFootnotes: [17]. This verse marks a decisive transition from the fate of the transgressors to the reward of the righteous. The term attainment conveys a dual meaning: it signifies both deliverance from danger and attainment of success. Thus, it encompasses not only escape from punishment, but the achievement of a desired and secure outcome. \nEarly commentators explain that taqwā (God-consciousness) involves awareness of accountability, leading to restraint from wrongdoing and commitment to what is right. Those characterized by this quality are contrasted with the transgressors described earlier, whose denial led to loss.\nWithin the structure of the sūrah, this verse introduces the balance that follows warning: just as punishment is certain for those who reject, so too is reward assured for those who remain mindful of their Lord. The verse thus reinforces a central Qurʾānic theme—that ultimate success lies not merely in avoidance of harm, but in attaining a state of security, fulfillment, and divine favor.",
  78032: "orchards and grapevines,",
  78033: "and maidens of equal age, [18]\\n\\nFootnotes: [18]. This verse continues the description of the rewards prepared for the righteous, mentioning kawāʿib atrābā—youthful companions of matching age. The term kawāʿib traditionally refers to maidens in the prime of youth, while atrāb emphasizes equality in age, suggesting harmony, balance, and mutual suitability.\nEarly commentators understand this as part of the broader imagery of delight and fulfillment in the hereafter, where companionship is free from the imperfections and imbalances of worldly life. The emphasis is not merely on physical description, but on completeness, compatibility, and enduring contentment.\nWithin the passage, this verse adds a dimension of personal and relational fulfillment to the material blessings already described. Just as the righteous are granted gardens and provision, they are also granted companionship characterized by harmony and ease. The verse thus reinforces a central Qurʾānic theme: that the rewards of the hereafter encompass both external favor and inner satisfaction, reflecting a state of perfected wellbeing.",
  78034: "and a cup filled full [of pure drink].",
  78035: "They will not hear therein any idle talk nor lying—",
  78036: "a reward from your Lord, a gift adequately calculated,",
  78037: "from the Lord of the heavens and the earth and all that is between them—the Most Compassionate. None may speak or plead except by His permission.",
  78038: "On the Day the Spirit [Gabriel] and the angels stand in rows; they will not speak except one whom the Most Gracious permits, and who speaks what is right.",
  78039: "That Day is the Truth. So whoever wills may take a path back to his Lord. [19]\\n\\nFootnotes: [19]. I.e., to take recourse to Allāh by turning back to Him through faith, repentance, and obedience before the inevitable Day arrives. The verse affirms human moral choice while underscoring that the opportunity to return exists only prior to judgment.",
  78040: "Indeed, We have warned you of a near punishment—the Day when a person will behold what his two hands have put forward, and the disbeliever will say, ‘Woe to me! Would that I were mere dust!’",
  79001: "By those [angels] who violently pull out [evil souls at death], [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. This verse opens the sūrah with an oath, referring to those who extract with force. Early commentators widely interpret this as referring to the angels who seize the souls of the wicked, drawing them out with force and harshness at the moment of death.\nThe expression conveys intensity and struggle, contrasting with later verses that describe a gentler extraction for the righteous. The imagery emphasizes the gravity of the moment when life is taken and the transition to the unseen begins.\nSome interpretations understand the term more generally as forces that pull or remove, but the dominant view among early commentators identifies them as angels carrying out divine command. Within the structure of the sūrah, this opening oath sets a solemn tone, directing attention to the reality of death as the first stage of accountability, and preparing for the themes of resurrection and judgment that follow.",
  79002: "and by those [angels] who draw out [good souls] gently [at death],",
  79003: "and by those gliding through space swiftly, [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. I.e., angels who move swiftly and freely through the heavens and the earth, carrying out Allāh’s commands with complete obedience and precision. The imagery conveys speed, ease, and unhindered motion, emphasizing their readiness and efficiency in executing divine decrees.",
  79004: "then those who outstrip one another, racing ahead, [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. I.e., angels who hasten to carry out Allah’s commands, striving to outpace one another in obedience and execution. The imagery conveys eagerness, swiftness, and precedence in fulfilling divine decrees, emphasizing their perfect submission and readiness.",
  79005: "and those who arrange each matter, [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. I.e., angels entrusted with administering and executing divine decrees in the cosmos, arranging matters in accordance with Allāh’s command. The phrase emphasizes order, precision, and delegated authority, while affirming that ultimate control belongs solely to Allāh.\nBy all of this, Allāh swore that He will surely resurrect people for reckoning and recompense.",
  79006: "On the Day when [the world] convulses with a mighty blast, [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. I.e., the first great convulsion of the cosmos, commonly understood as the first blast of the Trumpet, by which the present order is violently shaken and brought to an end, preceding the subsequent blast that initiates resurrection.",
  79007: "Followed by the subsequent blast. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. I.e., the second blast of the Trumpet, which follows the first convulsion and brings about resurrection and the gathering for judgment. Together, the two blasts mark the end of the present order and the beginning of the Hereafter.",
  79008: "On that Day, hearts will be trembling, [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. I.e., the hearts of those gripped by fear and dread at the realization of judgment—commonly understood as the disbelievers and evildoers who denied the Resurrection. The trembling reflects intense inner terror and anxiety, in contrast to the security granted to the righteous.",
  79009: "their eyes, humbled,",
  79010: "They say [mockingly], ‘Shall we really be brought back to our former state of life,",
  79011: "when we have become crumbling bones?”",
  79012: "They say, ‘That then would be a return entailing total loss.’",
  79013: "But it will be only a single shout, [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. The second blowing of the Trumpet—i.e., a single divine command or blast by which resurrection occurs instantly. The expression emphasizes the ease and immediacy of Allah’s power: what is deemed impossible by human reckoning happens with one decisive call.",
  79014: "Then suddenly, they are all upon the open plain. [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. I.e., resurrected and standing upon the exposed surface of the earth, fully conscious and present for gathering and judgment. The term conveys sudden emergence, clarity, and public exposure after death.",
  79015: "Has the story of Moses reached you,",
  79016: "when his Lord called out to him at the Sacred valley of Tuwa?",
  79017: "“Go to Pharaoh—he has transgressed all bounds.”",
  79018: "Say, “Would you like to purify yourself,",
  79019: "and guide you to your Lord, so that you may fear Him?”",
  79020: "Then he showed him the greatest sign.[10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. I.e., his staff becoming a manifest snake.",
  79021: "But he denied it and disobeyed.",
  79022: "Then he turned away, striving [against the truth].",
  79023: "He gathered his people and proclaimed,",
  79024: "saying, ‘I am your lord, the Most High.’",
  79025: "So Allāh seized him—a deterrent example [11] for the first and the last. [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. By drowning him in the sea, and in the Hereafter by subjecting him to the most severe punishment.\n[12]. Transgression i.e., for Pharaoh's setting himself as a deity and for his previous oppression of the people and denial of Moses's Message.",
  79026: "Indeed, in that is surely a lesson for whoever stands in awe of Allāh.",
  79027: "Are you harder to create, or the heaven? He built it—",
  79028: "He raised its height and perfected it.",
  79029: "He darkened its night and brought out its daylight.",
  79030: "And the earth—after that—He spread it out.",
  79031: "He brought forth from it its water and its pasture,",
  79032: "and set the mountains firmly upon it,",
  79033: "as a provision for you and for your livestock.",
  79034: "But when the great overwhelming calamity [13] comes—\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. I.e., the second blowing of the Trumpet, by which all are raised from the dead for resurrection and judgment. The Arabic expression ٱلطَّآمَّةُ ٱلْكُبْرَىٰ (al-ṭāmmah al-kubrā) denotes an overwhelming, all-engulfing catastrophe, described here in its absolute and final form. The root conveys the sense of something that covers, swamps, or overtakes everything, leaving nothing unaffected. This is a reference to the Day of Resurrection, when the established order of the world collapses and ultimate judgment takes place. The Qur’anic phrasing deliberately avoids technical detail, emphasizing instead the total, inescapable nature of the event and its gravity, rather than its precise chronology or mechanics.",
  79035: "on the Day when every person will recall all that they strove for,",
  79036: "and Hellfire will be displayed for all who see.",
  79037: "Then as for the one who transgressed—",
  79038: "and preferred the worldly life, [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. The worldly life translates ٱلْحَيَوٰةَ ٱلدُّنْيَا (al-ḥayāh al-dunyā). The adjective dunyā derives from a root meaning “nearer” or “lower,” indicating immediacy and transience rather than intrinsic evil. In the Qur’anic moral framework, the present life is not condemned in itself; rather, blame falls on preferring it over accountability, restraint, and the life to come. The verse thus identifies ethical failure not as material existence, but as a deliberate prioritization of short-term gain over enduring consequence and moral responsibility.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: \n“By Allāh, the life of this world compared to the Hereafter is nothing more than like one of you dipping his finger into the sea — let him see what it brings back.”\nThe ocean represents the Hereafter — vast, endless, and beyond human comprehension.\nThe drop of water that clings to the finger is the worldly life — short, fleeting, and insignificant in comparison.\nThis clear image urges believers to not be deceived by the temporary pleasures of this world, but to strive for what is eternal.\n The pleasures and trials of this world, however long they last, are as nothing compared to the endless bliss or punishment of the Hereafter.",
  79039: "then indeed Hellfire is the final abode.",
  79040: "But as for the one who stands in awe before their Lord and restrains the soul from its impulses—",
  79041: "then indeed Paradise is the final abode.",
  79042: "They ask you [O Prophet] about the Hour: when will it be? [15]\\n\\nFootnotes: [15]. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said, \"When honesty is lost, then wait for the Hour [of Doom].\" It was asked, \"How will honesty be lost, O God's Messenger ﷺ?\" He said, \"When authority is given to those who do not deserve it, then wait for the Hour.\"\nThe Prophet ﷺ said when trust is lost and the unworhty lead, the Final Hour draws near.",
  79043: "What have you to do with its mention? [16]\\n\\nFootnotes: [16]. Meaning that he ﷺ had no knowledge of it, though he is one of its Signs. The rhetorical question here emphatically denies the Prophet any role in determining or announcing the timing of the Hour. “What concern of yours is it to mention it?” The term dhikrā (“mention,” “reminder”) refers not to warning about the Hour itself—which remains central to the Prophet’s mission—but specifically to knowledge of its appointed time. The verse thus redirects attention away from speculative progression toward moral preparedness, reinforcing that ultimate knowledge of the Hour rests exclusively with Allāh, while the Prophet’s role is to warn and remind, not to disclose hidden decrees.",
  79044: "To your Lord is its ultimate end.",
  79045: "You are only a warner to those who fear it.",
  79046: "On the Day they see it, it will be as though they had not remained [in the world] except an evening or its morning.",
  80001: "He frowned and turned away, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. The verse opens with two terse verbs—“frowned and turned away”—without naming the subject. It is understood that the referent is the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, yet the Qur’an’s indirect phrasing preserves his dignity while delivering a gentle but unmistakable correction. The action described is minor and unspoken, underscoring the Qur’an’s ethical precision: even subtle expressions and shifts of attention carry moral weight. The episode establishes a central lesson of the sūrah—that sincere seekers of guidance take precedence over social status or influence, and that no individual, however eminent, is beyond moral refinement.",
  80002: "when the blind man [2] came to him.\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The “blind man” refers to ʿAbdullāh ibn Umm Maktūm, an early believer who was physically blind but spiritually earnest. He approached the Prophet ﷺ seeking guidance while the Prophet was engaged in discourse with influential leaders of Quraysh. The Qur’an mentions his blindness not to diminish him, but to underscore his social vulnerability and to invert prevailing norms of status: outward limitation does not preclude spiritual insight, nor does social prominence guarantee moral priority. The episode serves as a corrective lesson, emphasizing that sincerity and receptivity to guidance outweigh rank, power, or appearance in divine evaluation.",
  80003: "What would make you know? Perhaps he might purify himself, [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. Purify himself denotes purification, growth, and moral refinement. The term refers not to ritual cleansing, but to inner transformation through receptivity to guidance. The verse underscores the limits of human judgment: outward status, social prominence, or the manner of approach do not determine spiritual potential. Divine evaluation instead prioritizes sincerity, humility, and openness to moral growth, even when such potential is not yet outwardly visible.",
  80004: "or be reminded, and the reminder will benefit him?",
  80005: "But as for the one who considers himself self-sufficient—[4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. “Considering himself free from need (of Divine guidance),” here refers to an influential pagan of the Quraysh whom the Prophet ﷺ had hoped would embrace Islam.\nThe term does not imply true independence, but rather a perceived lack of need for guidance—an attitude of self-satisfaction and moral complacency. In Qur’anic usage, such self-assurance is a spiritual liability: it closes one off from reminder and reform.\nThe contrast here is deliberate—those most receptive to guidance may appear insignificant, while those who feel no need for it may command attention. The verse critiques not wealth or status in themselves, but the inner posture of indifference that blinds one to dependence on Allāh.",
  80006: "you give him your attention,",
  80007: "though you are not to blame if he does not purify himself.",
  80008: "And as for the one who came to you striving—",
  80009: "while he is in awe of Allāh—",
  80010: "you are distracted from him.",
  80011: "No indeed; it is a reminder –",
  80012: "so whoever wills may remember it, [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. “Of what is in this Qur’an.” The phrase “may remember it” signifies not mere recollection, but remembrance in the fuller sense of mindfulness, reflection, and conscious moral awareness.\nThe verse affirms human responsibility: the reminder is offered openly, yet its benefit depends on the individual’s willingness to attend, reflect, and respond. Guidance is neither imposed nor restricted; rather, it becomes effective only through deliberate receptivity and moral engagement.",
  80013: "in honored pages,",
  80014: "exalted and purified,",
  80015: "in the hands of angelic scribes,",
  80016: "noble and virtuous. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. “Noble” denotes nobility, honor, and elevated rank, while “virtuous” signifies moral excellence, righteousness, and devoted obedience to Allāh. Together, these paired adjectives emphasize both status and character, affirming that the bearers of revelation are not only exalted in rank but also impeccable in integrity. This underscores the trustworthiness, purity, and dignity of the transmission of the Qur’an.",
  80017: "Perished is man—how ungrateful he is! [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. “Perished is man” denotes condemnation or rebuke, not a literal statement of death. It conveys the sense of “woe to” or “how ruinous is”. The exclamation “How ungrateful he is!” highlights human ingratitude and denial in the face of evident blessings and guidance. The verse censures not mere ignorance, but a willful failure to acknowledge dependence upon Allāh, setting the stage for the ensuing reminder of human origin, fragility, and divine provision.",
  80018: "From what did He create him? [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. The verse condemns human beings who show arrogance on earth and deny Allāh. The question “From what thing did He create him?” is rhetorical, not inquisitive: it confronts human arrogance by directing attention to humble origins, preparing for the answer given in the following verse. The Qur’an employs this interrogative form to dismantle claims of self-sufficiency and ingratitude, reminding the human being that status, power, and pride stand in stark contrast to the fragility and dependence inherent in one’s creation.",
  80019: "He created him from a mere drop of fluid, then proportioned him, [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. That is, his creation, physical form, capacities, provision, lifespan, and appointed course. The expression “then determined him” refers to Allāh’s precise apportioning of every aspect of human existence—how one is formed, sustained, enabled, limited, and ultimately brought to an end. It affirms that human life unfolds not by chance or self-direction, but according to divine measure and wisdom, encompassing both material circumstances and the broader course of one’s life.",
  80020: "then He made the way easy for him, [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. That is, He made the way easy for him in multiple, complementary senses. This includes his emergence from his mother’s womb, the facilitation of his course through life—with its means of movement, sustenance, and survival—and the opening of the path to true guidance through intellect, conscience, and revelation. The Qur’anic wording intentionally encompasses all these layers, emphasizing that every stage of human existence is preceded by divine enablement, even as moral choice and responsibility remain.",
  80021: "then He causes him to die and grants him burial;",
  80022: "then, when He wills, He will resurrect him.",
  80023: "No indeed—he has yet to fulfill what his Lord commanded him.",
  80024: "So let man consider his food.",
  80025: "How We pour down rain in abundance,",
  80026: "then We split the earth—splitting it open.",
  80027: "and cause grain to grow in it,",
  80028: "as well as grapes and fresh greens,",
  80029: "and olive trees and date-palms,",
  80030: "and dense, luxuriant orchards,",
  80031: "and fruits and grass—",
  80032: "as provision for you and your livestock.",
  80033: "But when the deafening blast [11] strikes—\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. The deafening blast refers to that which deafens the ears, identified as the second blowing of the Trumpet. This blast marks the moment when all the dead are raised for resurrection and brought forth for judgment. The Qur’anic expression emphasizes not the mechanics of the event, but its sudden, inescapable, and all-encompassing impact, shattering all remaining ties to worldly order and initiating the final reckoning.",
  80034: "on the Day when a person will flee from his brother,",
  80035: "and from his mother and father,",
  80036: "and from his wife and children.",
  80037: "every one of them, that Day, will have enough concern of his own. [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. The sequence—brother, mother, father—progressively intensifies the portrayal of relational collapse on the Day of Judgment. These are among the strongest natural human bonds, yet each is rendered powerless in the face of personal accountability. The verse does not suggest hostility or rejection, but overwhelming preoccupation and fear, as every individual is consumed by concern for their own standing. The imagery underscores a central Qur’anic principle: on that Day, responsibility is entirely individual, and no kinship, affection, or shared history can mitigate one’s reckoning before Allāh.",
  80038: "Faces, that Day, will be bright,",
  80039: "cheerful and rejoicing;",
  80040: "while other faces on that Day will be dust-stained,",
  80041: "darkness will cover them,",
  80042: "such are the disbelievers, the wicked ones.",
  81001: "When the sun is folded up and darkened, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. The expression “the sun is folded up” translates كُوِّرَتْ (kuwwirat), which literally means to wrap, roll up, or fold together, as a turban is wrapped. It signifies the collapse of the sun’s function and radiance at the end of the world. The verse thus marks the dissolution of the most dominant source of light and order known to human experience, signaling the onset of cosmic upheaval and final reckoning. In this connection, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: “The sun and the moon will be folded up (or joined together and deprived of their light) on the Day of Resurrection,” reinforcing the Qur’anic imagery of their extinguishing and loss of worldly function.",
  81002: "when the stars fall, dispersing,",
  81003: "when the mountains are set in motion,",
  81004: "when pregnant camels are abandoned, [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. Pregnant camels translates, referring specifically to she-camels in the final stage of pregnancy, traditionally among the most valuable possessions in Arab society. Such animals would never be neglected under normal circumstances. Their abandonment signifies total societal collapse and overwhelming terror, in which even the most prized worldly assets are ignored. The verse thus conveys the absolute disintegration of human priorities at the onset of the Final Hour.",
  81005: "when wild beasts are gathered together, [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. The gathering of wild beasts signifies the collapse of natural order and instinctive boundaries. Creatures that normally flee from one another in fear are driven together, reflecting a state of overwhelming terror that suspends their natural behavior. It is also understood as a sign of universal accountability, in which all creatures are drawn into the events of the Final Hour, underscoring the totality and inescapability of the upheaval. In this regard, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: “Rights will surely be restored to those entitled to them on the Day of Resurrection, until retaliation is taken for the hornless sheep from the horned sheep.”",
  81006: "when the seas are set ablaze, [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. The phrase “the seas are set ablaze” conveys the kindling of fire, as well as violent boiling or overflowing. It is understood as a reference to the combustion, eruption, or catastrophic upheaval of the seas at the end of the world—whether through ignition, evaporation, or the collapse of their natural boundaries. The image signifies the dissolution of one of creation’s most stable and life-sustaining systems, reinforcing the total cosmic disintegration that accompanies the Final Hour.",
  81007: "when the souls are paired, [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The pairing of souls has been understood in multiple, complementary ways. It may refer to the reuniting of souls with their bodies at resurrection, the grouping of individuals with their like—believers with believers, wrongdoers with wrongdoers—or the joining of each person with their deeds and moral counterparts. The Qur’anic expression deliberately leaves the pairing open-ended, emphasizing not the mechanics of the process but the restoration of order, correspondence, and accountability at the Final Hour.",
  81008: "and when the baby girl buried alive is asked, [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. This refers to the practice of female infanticide carried out by some pagan Arabs during the pre-Islamic Age of Ignorance (Jāhiliyyah), in which baby girls were sometimes buried alive shortly after birth out of fear of poverty, shame, or perceived social burden. The verse strikingly portrays the victim herself as being questioned, not the killer, underscoring her complete innocence and exposing the moral bankruptcy of the act. This scene exemplifies the Qur’an’s broader insistence on absolute justice, in which even the most powerless are given a voice and every injustice is brought fully to account.",
  81009: "for what crime was she killed,",
  81010: "when the records of deeds are spread open,",
  81011: "when the sky is stripped away,",
  81012: "when Hellfire is set ablaze,",
  81013: "and when Paradise is brought near –",
  81014: "then each soul will know what it has brought forth, [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. “What it has brought forth” refers to faith or disbelief, and good or evil deeds, prepared for that Day. The expression conveys the sense of actively presenting or producing one’s deeds, not merely having them recorded externally. The verse emphasizes that actions are carried forward by the soul itself and confronted directly at the time of judgment. This moment of recognition marks the culmination of accountability, when every individual becomes fully aware of the moral weight and consequences of what they have done.",
  81015: "I swear by the receding stars—",
  81016: "that run their courses and disappear,",
  81017: "and by the night as it falls,",
  81018: "and by the morning when it breathes—",
  81019: "Indeed, this is surely the word of God conveyed by a noble messenger [Gabriel],",
  81020: "endowed with power, held in high rank with the Lord of the Throne—",
  81021: "obeyed there and trustworthy.",
  81022: "Your companion is not insane. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. This verse directly refutes the charge of madness as claimed by the pagan opponents of Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. The expression “your fellow” underscores his long-standing presence among them and their intimate knowledge of his character, truthfulness, and sound judgment. The denial is categorical: revelation is not the product of delusion or frenzy, but of conscious, sober reception. The verse thus anchors the Qur’an’s authority in the Prophet’s known integrity and mental clarity, appealing to lived experience rather than abstract argument.",
  81023: "Indeed, he has seen Gabriel on the clear horizon,",
  81024: "nor does he withhold any knowledge of\n the unseen.",
  81025: "nor is this the word of an outcast devil.",
  81026: "So where are you going? [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. In your denial of the Qur’an and turning away from it. This rhetorical question is not a request for direction, but a sharp moral challenge. Having established that the Qur’an is true revelation—neither madness, deception, nor satanic speech—the verse confronts the listener with the absence of any credible alternative. It exposes the illogicality of denial and calls for self-examination and decisive response: once the truth is clear, turning away is itself a deliberate choice, but one that leads nowhere except to Hellfire.",
  81027: "It is only a Reminder to all peoples,",
  81028: "for whoever wills among you to be upright,",
  81029: "but you cannot will unless Allāh, the Lord of the worlds, so wills.",
  82001: "When the sky is split apart,",
  82002: "when the stars scatter,",
  82003: "when the seas burst forth and overflow,",
  82004: "and when the graves are overturned,",
  82005: "then each soul will know what it has put forth and what it has left behind. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. “What it has put forth ” refers to a person’s deeds, intentions, and moral choices carried forward for judgment, while “what it has left behind” includes both neglected duties and lasting effects—such as consequences, influence, or harm that continue after one’s death. The verse emphasizes comprehensive accountability: not only for what one actively does, but also for what one fails to do or leaves unresolved. On that Day, every individual will gain full awareness of the total moral imprint of their life.",
  82006: "O mankind, what has deluded you concerning your Lord, the Most Generous?",
  82007: "Who created you, then shaped you, and balanced you,",
  82008: "in whatever form He willed, He assembled you?",
  82009: "No indeed! Rather, you deny the Judgment.",
  82010: "Yet there are watchers over you,",
  82011: "noble recording scribes,",
  82012: "who know all that you do. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. Their knowledge encompasses all outward actions and inward intentions insofar as they manifest in deeds. Nothing is overlooked, forgotten, or misrepresented. The verse emphasizes that accountability is neither arbitrary nor reflective guesswork; rather, it is grounded in precise, continuous awareness and faithful recording. This certainty reinforces the reality of Judgement denied in the preceding verses.",
  82013: "Indeed, the virtuous will be in bliss,",
  82014: "And the wicked will surely be in Hellfire,",
  82015: "which they will enter and burn in on the Day of Judgment,",
  82016: "and will never be absent from it.",
  82017: "What will make you realize what the Day of Judgment is?",
  82018: "Again, what will make you realize what the Day of Judgment is?",
  82019: "It is the Day when no soul will have power to do anything for another soul. The command on that Day is entirely for Allāh.",
  83001: "Woe to the defrauders, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. Those who give short measure and balance—that is, those who cheat people in sale transactions by giving less than what is due, often by an amount so small as to escape immediate notice. Defrauders translates ٱلْمُطَفِّفِينَ (al-muṭaffifīn), referring specifically to those who diminish weights or measures while demanding full measure for themselves. Although the term originates in commercial malpractice, the Qur’anic rebuke extends beyond trade to all forms of injustice, double standards, and moral dishonesty. It condemns any conduct in which one seeks advantage through subtle wrongdoing while outwardly appearing fair, underscoring that even minor acts of fraud carry grave ethical weight before Allāh —especially when they reflect a deeper disregard for accountability and justice.",
  83002: "those who, when they take measure from people, take in full;",
  83003: "but when they measure or weigh for them, they give less than due.",
  83004: "Do they not think that they will be resurrected,",
  83005: "for a tremendous Day,",
  83006: "the Day when mankind will stand before Allāh, the Lord of the worlds. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. Will stand before Allāh signifies the moment of universal accountability, when all people are raised from the dead and made to stand in humility and submission for judgment. No rank, wealth, or power retains any consequence; every individual stands equally before the Lord of all creation. The expression underscores the gravity and impartiality of that Day, confronting human injustice and moral negligence with the certainty of divine justice and recompense.\nThis standing is for judgment and testimony against those guilty of grave wrongdoing: idolaters, murderers, sorcerers, adulterers; liars and those who swear falsely; cheaters in measure and trade; and those who oppress or exploit the hired worker, widows, and orphans. It also includes those who deny justice to the resident alien and the foreigner. Such acts are singled out because they reveal a failure to fear and honor Allāh, replacing reverence for divine accountability with arrogance, exploitation, and moral disregard.",
  83007: "No indeed! The record of the wicked is surely in Sijjīn. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. I.e., the lowest depths of the earth.\nSijjīn is mentioned here without immediate explanation, heightening its gravity. Linguistically, the term is associated with confinement, restriction, and something intensely constricted, and it is commonly understood as a lowest register or place of imprisonment for the records of the wicked. It signifies degradation, humiliation, and inescapable accountability.\nIt is appointed for men to die, then to come to the Judgement. At death, the soul separates from the body and goes before Allāh for the personal judgement, to see what it became. Then the soul, if perfectly good, goes to heaven. If imperfectly good but repentant, it needs pardoning or purification before entering heaven. If irretrievably wicked, unrepentant, it will go to that place where Allāh is not, along with the others like it, with demons and humans similar to it. In a Hadith: \"When the denier is about to leave the world and proceed to the next world, angels with black faces come down to him from heaven with hair-cloth and sit away from him as far as the eye can see. Then the angel of death comes and sits at his head and says, ‘Wicked soul, come out to displeasure from God.’ Then it becomes dissipated in his body, and he draws it out as a spit is drawn out from moistened wool. He then seizes it, and when he does so they do not leave it in his hand for an instant, but put it in that hair-cloth and from it there comes forth a stench like the most offensive stench of a corpse found on the face of the earth. They then take it up and do not bring it past a company of angels without their asking, ‘Who is this wicked soul?’ to which they reply, ‘So and so, the son of so and so,’ using the worst names he was called in the world. When he is brought to the lowest heaven request is made that the gate be opened for him, but it is not opened for him. God’s messenger then recited, ‘The gates of heaven will not be opened for them and they will not enter paradise until a camel can pass through the eye of a needle.' (Qur'an, chapter 7, verse 40) Allāh who is great and glorious then says, ‘Record his book in Sijjin in the lowest earth,’ and his soul is thrown down. He then recited, ‘He who assigns partners to Allāh is as if he had fallen down from heaven and been snatched up by birds, or made to fall by the wind in a place far distant.' (Qur’an, chapter 22, verse 31). His soul is then restored to his body, two angels come to him, and making him sit up say to him, ‘Who is your Lord?’ He replies, ‘Alas, alas, I do not know.’ They ask, ‘What is your religion?' and he replies, ‘Alas, alas, I do not know.’ They ask, ‘Who is this man who was sent among you?' and he replies, ‘Alas, alas, I do not know.’ Then one calls out from heaven, ‘He has lied, so spread out carpets from hell for him, and open a gate for him into hell.’ Then some of its heat and hot air comes to him, his grave is made narrow for him so that his ribs are pressed together in it, and a man with an ugly, face, ugly garments and an offensive odour come to him and says, ‘Be grieved with what displeases you, for this is your day which you have been promised.’ He asks, ‘Who are you, for your face is most ugly and brings evil?' He replies, ‘I am your wicked deeds.’ He then says, ‘My Lord, do not bring the Resurrection Day.\"",
  83008: "What will make you realize what Sijjin is? -",
  83009: "It is a clearly inscribed record.",
  83010: "Woe on that Day to the deniers,",
  83011: "those who deny the Day of Judgment!",
  83012: "None denies it except every transgressor steeped in sin.",
  83013: "When Our verses are recited to him, he says, ‘Tales of the ancients.’",
  83014: "No indeed! Rather, what they used to earn has rusted their hearts.",
  83015: "No indeed! On that Day they will surely be veiled from their Lord.",
  83016: "Then indeed, they will surely burn in the Blazing Fire,",
  83017: "then it will be said, ‘This is what you used to deny.’",
  83018: "No indeed! The record of the virtuous is surely in ʿIlliyyīn.",
  83019: "What will make you realize what ʿIlliyyīn is? [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. ʿIlliyyīn is mentioned without immediate explanation to magnify its honor. Linguistically, the term conveys height, elevation, and exalted rank, and it is commonly understood as the highest register in which the records of the virtuous are preserved.\nRather than denoting merely a physical location, ʿIlliyyīn signifies nearness to Allāh, honor, and acceptance. The deeds recorded there are elevated, witnessed, and affirmed, in contrast to the constriction and humiliation associated with Sijjīn. The question “What will make you realize…?” underscores that this rank transcends ordinary comprehension and can only be known through divine disclosure, highlighting the immense reward awaiting those of righteousness and moral integrity.\nA companion of the Prophet (ﷺ) said: We went out with the Prophet to the funeral of a man of the Ansar and came to the grave. It had not yet been dug, so God’s messenger sat down and we sat down around him quietly. He had in his hand a stick with which he was making marks on the ground. Then he raised his head and said, “Seek refuge in Allāh from the punishment of the grave saying it twice or thrice. He then said, “When a believer is about to leave the world and go forward to the next world, angels with faces white as the sun come down to him from heaven with one of the shrouds of paradise and some of the perfume of paradise and sit away from him as far as the eye can see. Then the angel of death comes and sits at his head and says, 'Good soul, come out to forgiveness and acceptance from Allāh.' It then comes out as a drop flows from a water-skin and he seizes it; and when he does so, they do not leave it in his hand for an instant, but take it and place it in that shroud and that perfume, and from it there comes forth a fragrance like that of the sweetest musk found on the face of the earth. They then take it up and do not bring it past a company of angels without their asking, “Who is this good soul?' to which they reply, ‘So and so, the son of so and so,’ using the best of his names by which people called him on the earth. They then bring him to the lowest heaven and ask that the gate should be opened for him. This is done, and from every heaven its archangels escort him to the next heaven till he is brought to the seventh heaven, and Allāh who is great and glorious says, ‘Record the book of my servant in ‘Illiyiin and take him back to earth, for I created mankind from it, I shall return them into it, and from it I shall bring them forth another time.' His soul is then restored to his body, two angels come to him, and making him sit up say to him, ‘Who is your Lord?’ He replies, ‘My Lord is Allāh.’ They ask, ‘What is your religion?’ and he replies, ‘My religion is Islam.’ They ask, ‘Who is this man who was sent among you?’ and he replies, ‘He is Muhammad, God’s messenger.’ They ask, ‘What is your [source of] knowledge?’ and he replies, ‘I have read God’s Book, the Qur’an, believed in it and declared it to be true.’ Then one calls out from heaven, ‘My servant has spoken the truth, so spread out carpets from paradise for him, clothe him from paradise, and open a gate for him into paradise.’ Then some of its joy and fragrance comes to him, his grave is made spacious for him as far as the eye can see, and a man with a beautiful face, beautiful garments and a sweet odour comes to him and says, ‘Rejoice in what pleases you for this is your day which you have been promised.' He asks, ‘Who are you, for your face is perfectly beautiful and brings good?' He replies, ‘I am your good deeds.’ He then says, ‘My Lord, bring the last hour; my Lord, bring the last hour, so that I may return to my people and my property.’",
  83020: "It is a clearly inscribed record,",
  83021: "witnessed by those closest to Allāh.",
  83022: "Indeed, the virtuous will surely be in bliss,",
  83023: "on couches, gazing.",
  83024: "You will recognize in their faces the radiance of bliss.",
  83025: "They will be given to drink from a sealed nectar,",
  83026: "its seal is musk—so for this let the competitors compete–",
  83027: "and its mixture is of Tasnīm, [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. Tasnīm is described in the Qur’an as a lofty spring in Paradise, from which the drink of the righteous is mixed, while those closest to Allāh drink from it directly. Linguistically, the name conveys the sense of elevation and flowing from above, indicating both physical height and spiritual rank. Its mention signifies honor, purity, and closeness to Allāh. The blending of the sealed nectar with Tasnīm underscores the refinement of the reward granted to the virtuous, while the exclusive access of the foremost highlights degrees of reward in accordance with faith, sincerity, and excellence in deeds.",
  83028: "a spring from which those closest to Allāh will drink.",
  83029: "Indeed, the guilty used to laugh at the believers,",
  83030: "when they passed by them, they would exchange mocking winks,",
  83031: "and when they returned to their people, they would return jesting,",
  83032: "and when they saw the believers, they would say, ‘These are indeed astray.’",
  83033: "Yet they were not sent to be guardians over them.",
  83034: "So today the believers will laugh at the disbelievers;",
  83035: "On couches, gazing.",
  83036: "Have the disbelievers been repaid for what they used to do?",
  84001: "When the sky is split open,",
  84002: "and obeys its Lord, as it is bound to do.",
  84003: "when the earth is stretched out,",
  84004: "and casts out whatever is within it [1] and becomes empty,\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. This refers to the earth expelling everything concealed within it—traditionally understood to include the dead, buried bodies, treasures, and all hidden contents—so that it is left completely empty. The imagery underscores total disclosure and finality at the time of judgment.",
  84005: "and obeys its Lord, as it is bound to do.",
  84006: "O humanity, you are toiling toward your Lord with relentless effort, and you will surely meet Him. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2].  That is, meeting one’s Lord through death and resurrection, culminating in standing before Him for judgment. This “meeting” is understood as encountering the outcome of one’s deeds—reward or punishment—rather than a physical meeting in a worldly sense. Accordingly, one is called to practice righteousness and charity, in awareness that we will indeed meet Allāh and receive just recompense for both our deeds and our intentions.",
  84007: "As for him who is given his record in his right hand,",
  84008: "He will receive an easy reckoning; [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. This is a lenient accounting (ḥisāb yasīr), involving presentation and pardon rather than an exhaustive examination of deeds. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: “Whoever is called to account will surely be punished.” When asked, “Does not Allāh say, ‘He will receive an easy reckoning’?”—referring to the Qur’an 84:8—the Prophet ﷺ replied: “That refers only to the presentation of the account; but whoever is interrogated over his account will certainly be ruined.”",
  84009: "and he will return to his people joyfully.",
  84010: "But as for him who is given his record from behind his back,",
  84011: "he will cry out for destruction,",
  84012: "and he will burn in a blazing fire.",
  84013: "Indeed, he had been among his people joyful.",
  84014: "He thought he would never return [to Us].",
  84015: "But yes—indeed, his Lord was ever seeing him.",
  84016: "So I swear by the twilight,",
  84017: "and by the night and all it encloses,",
  84018: "and by the moon as it grows full—",
  84019: "you will surely pass from stage to stage. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. That is, passing through successive stages decreed by Allāh—i.e., from sperm-drop to clinging clot to lump, and from life to death to resurrection. This is also understood as encompassing continual transitions in human states and conditions, both in this world and in the Hereafter.",
  84020: "So what is the matter with them? Why do they not believe?",
  84021: "And when the Qur’an is recited to them, why do they not prostrate in submission?",
  84022: "Rather, those who disbelieve persist in denial,",
  84023: "but Allāh knows best what they conceal within themselves.",
  84024: "So give them tidings of a painful punishment;",
  84025: "except those who believe and do righteous deeds—for them is a reward that will never be cut off.",
  85001: "By the heaven marked by the constellations, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. God swears here by the heaven adorned with constellations.",
  85002: "and the Promised Day, [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. i.e., the Day of Judgment.",
  85003: "and a witness, and the witnessed. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. “The witnessed” is understood in as referring to what is testified over—most commonly the nation to whom a messenger was sent, their deeds, or the Day of Judgment itself. Thus, Prophet Muhammad ﷺ is the witness, and what is witnessed encompasses humanity’s response to divine guidance.",
  85004: "Cursed were the people of the trench, [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. This refers to the “People of the Trench,” a group who persecuted believers by burning them alive in pits of fire because of their faith. They are often identified with a historical persecution in southern Arabia. According to early interpretation, they acted in service of a tyrannical king and sought to prevent people from believing in Allāh. Their blind obedience to unjust authority and participation in religious persecution incurred divine condemnation.",
  85005: "the fire, filled with fuel— [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. That is, a fire kept intensely burning by abundant fuel. The phrase emphasizes the deliberate preparation and sustained nature of the blaze, underscoring the cruelty and calculated intent of those who carried out the persecution of believers because of their faith.",
  85006: "as they sat beside it, [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. As executioners and spectators, they sat by the fire they had prepared, watching as the believers were punished. The phrase highlights their deliberate presence and passive observation, emphasizing the cruelty, indifference, and moral responsibility of those who oversaw the persecution.",
  85007: "and they themselves were witnesses to what they were doing to the believers.",
  85008: "Their only resentment against them was that they believed in Allāh—the All-Mighty, the Praiseworthy—",
  85009: "To Him belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth. Allāh is Witness over all things.",
  85010: "Indeed, those who persecuted the believing men and women and then did not repent—for them is the punishment of Hell, and for them is the punishment of the blazing Fire.",
  85011: "Indeed, those who believe and do righteous deeds will have gardens beneath which rivers flow—that is the great success.",
  85012: "Indeed, the punishment of your Lord is surely severe. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. “Punishment” renders the Arabic baṭsh, which denotes a forceful seizure, vengeance, or decisive act of grasp. It conveys Allāh’s overwhelming power to seize wrongdoers suddenly and irresistibly, whether in this world or in the Hereafter.",
  85013: "Indeed, He is the One who originates and then resurrects, [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. That is, Allāh is the One who brings creation into existence from nothing and then brings it back after death. This is affirming both the initial act of creation and the certainty of resurrection, demonstrating divine power over life, death, and renewal.",
  85014: "and He is the Oft-Forgiving, the Ever-Loving, [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. Allāh is the Creator whose nature is marked by forgiveness and loving-kindness. The divine name al-Wadūd denotes constant, devoted love, while al-Ghafūr signifies abundant and repeated forgiveness. Love and mercy originate from Him, and He bestows them upon His creation in varying degrees. Believers are therefore called to embody patience, kindness, and compassion, recognizing that righteous deeds are perfected by sincerity, humility, and mercy. Allāh, our Lord, is All-Forgiving and all-embracing in His love.",
  85015: "Possessor of the Glorious Throne;",
  85016: "Doer of whatever He intends.",
  85017: "Has there come to you the story of the hosts",
  85018: "Of Pharaoh and Thamūd?",
  85019: "Rather, those who disbelieve persist in denial.",
  85020: "Yet Allāh encompasses them from behind. [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. That is, Allāh fully surrounds them from all sides with His knowledge, power, and authority, and will requite them for their misdeeds. The expression conveys inescapability: despite their denial, they remain completely within His grasp, unable to flee His punishment or His judgment.",
  85021: "Rather, this is a glorious Qur’an,",
  85022: "in a Preserved Tablet. [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. “Preserved Tablet” renders the Arabic al-Lawḥ al-Maḥfūẓ, a term in the Qur'an referring to a divinely preserved register in which all things are recorded. It is understood in Islamic religion to contain the complete decree of Allāh, encompassing everything that has occurred and will occur—creation, events, destinies, and revelation. The Qur’ān itself is described as being inscribed therein, signifying its ultimate preservation from alteration.\nThe exact nature and location of the Preserved Tablet are part of the unseen (al-ghayb), known only to Allāh. However, early scholars commonly describe it as being in the highest realm of creation, above the heavens, near the Divine Throne (al-ʿArsh). It is not associated with any human or angelic ownership; rather, it is under the exclusive dominion of Allāh, though certain angels may be entrusted with aspects of recording and conveying what is decreed.\nThe concept emphasizes two central themes: the absolute knowledge of Allāh—nothing escapes His awareness—and the perfect preservation of His revelation. Thus, describing the Qur’ān as being in a “Preserved Tablet” underscores its divine origin, protection, and unalterable truth.",
  86001: "By the sky and the night-comer – [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. “The night-comer” (al-ṭāriq) refers to that which comes or appears by night, commonly understood as a bright, striking star. It emphasizes sudden appearance in the darkness and visibility at night, drawing attention as a sign in the heavens. In this verse, Allāh swears by the sky and by this night-appearing star, a Qur’anic rhetorical device used to underscore the importance and certainty of what follows.",
  86002: "what will make you realize what the night-comer is?",
  86003: "It is the piercing star.",
  86004: "There is no soul but that over it is a guardian. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. That is, angels appointed over each human being who guard them by Allāh’s command and record their deeds. These guardians as including angels who watch over a person’s life and conduct, as well as those who attentively record every good and evil action, so that nothing is overlooked in the divine reckoning.",
  86005: "So let man consider from what he was created—",
  86006: "He was created from a gushing fluid, [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. The phrase “a gushing fluid” translates the Arabic māʾ dāfiq, describing fluid that is forcefully emitted. This is referring to the reproductive fluid involved in human creation, emphasizing its dynamic movement and humble physical origin. The expression draws attention to the contrast between the human being’s lowly beginning and Allāh’s power to create, sustain, and resurrect.",
  86007: "emerging from between the backbone and the ribs. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. The phrase “between the backbone and the ribs” is understood as referring to the backbone or loins and to the chest or rib area. Many scholars explained the expression as alluding to human creation arising from both parents, while others understood it as a general description of the inner region of the body from which reproductive fluid emerges.\nIt may also be noted that, in human anatomy, both the testes and the ovaries are sustained by arteries that originate high in the abdominal region, between the backbone and the ribcage, before descending to the reproductive organs. This observation accords with the verse’s imagery without reducing it to a technical anatomical statement. The primary purpose of the wording remains reflective, emphasizing humanity’s humble origin and Allāh’s power to recreate and resurrect.",
  86008: "Indeed, He is surely Able to bring him back to life.",
  86009: "On the Day when all secrets will be exposed,",
  86010: "so he will have neither strength nor any helper.",
  86011: "By the heaven with its recurring return, [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. That is consider the heaven ever-revolving with whatever is in it and recurring patterns of rain, heat, sound waves, etc.",
  86012: "and the earth that cracks open. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. The phrase “the earth that cracks open” refers to the earth’s splitting and opening for the sprouting of plants. It evokes the image of the ground breaking open to allow vegetation to emerge after rainfall, highlighting the earth’s responsiveness to divine command and its role in sustaining life. In the broader context, this imagery complements the preceding oath by the heaven, together pointing to Allāh’s power to bring forth, renew, and ultimately resurrect.",
  86013: "Indeed, it is a decisive word—the Qur’an,",
  86014: "and it is not a matter of amusement.",
  86015: "Indeed, they are devising a scheme,",
  86016: "but I too am planning a plan.",
  86017: "So give respite to the disbelievers—give them respite for a little while. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. That is, do not be hasty in seeking their doom. It refers to a temporary reprieve granted by Allāh, not an indication of approval or escape from accountability. Scholars explain this respite as a delay by which matters unfold according to divine wisdom, culminating in judgment at the appointed time. The expression underscores the certainty of reckoning despite apparent postponement.",
  87001: "Glorify the Name of your Lord, the Most High. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. That is, to exalt and sanctify Him by praising His Name and giving thanks. To glorify the Name of Allāh entails affirming His transcendence, perfection, and supreme greatness in belief, speech, and action. His goodness, love, and mercy are constant and enduring, extending to all creation, and believers are called to respond with gratitude, remembrance, humble devotion, and obedience.",
  87002: "Who created and perfectly proportioned,",
  87003: "and Who determined and then guided,",
  87004: "and Who brings forth the pasture,",
  87005: "then makes it dry, dark stubble.",
  87006: "We will make you recite the Qur’an, [O Prophet], so you will not forget,",
  87007: "except what Allāh wills; indeed, He knows what is manifest and what is hidden.",
  87008: "We will ease you toward ease. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. That is, Allāh will facilitate for the Prophet ﷺ the path that is easy and upright. Scholars explain “the ease” as the way of obedience, guidance, and righteousness, made accessible through divine aid rather than hardship. It also conveys reassurance that the Prophet’s mission, practice, and conveyance of revelation will be supported and made\nmanageable by Allāh’s grace.",
  87009: "So remind, if the reminder is beneficial.",
  87010: "He who fears Allāh will take heed,",
  87011: "but the most wretched will avoid it.",
  87012: "who will enter and burn in the great Fire,",
  87013: "where he will neither die nor live.",
  87014: "Successful indeed is the one who purifies himself,",
  87015: "and remembers his Lord’s Name and prays. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. That is, he engages in the remembrance of Allāh by mentioning and glorifying His Name, and he establishes prayer. This encompasses both verbal remembrance (dhikr) and prescribed acts of worship—particularly the five obligatory prayers—as well as other prayers associated with purification and sincere obedience, such as the Witr prayer, the ʿĪd prayers (ʿĪd al-Fiṭr and ʿĪd al-Aḍḥā), and regular Sunnah and devotional prayers. The verse thus links inner purification with outward worship as signs of true success.",
  87016: "But you prefer the life of this world, [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. That is, you give priority to the immediate comforts, desires, and attractions of worldly life over what is lasting. This preference reflects choosing what is present and fleeting rather than what is enduring and eternal. The verse serves as a moral reproof, reminding that attachment to this world distracts from preparation for the Hereafter, which is superior and everlasting.\nThis meaning is illustrated by a report that the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ once passed through a marketplace and came upon a dead goat with small ears. Holding it by the ear, he asked those present whether they would like to have it for a small price. When they replied that it was worthless even if given freely, he said: “By Allāh, this world is more insignificant in the sight of Allāh than this is to you.” The narration highlights the insignificance of worldly life in comparison to the Hereafter. Just as no one desires a decaying carcass, worldly possessions have no inherent value apart from righteous action, faith, and seeking the pleasure of Allāh.",
  87017: "while the Hereafter is far better and more enduring.",
  87018: "Indeed, this is mentioned in the earlier Scriptures,",
  87019: "the scriptures of Abraham and Moses. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. That is, the scrolls given to Abraham—traditionally understood as ten scrolls—and the Torah revealed to Moses. Scholars explain that the essential teachings emphasized in this sūrah, such as faith in Allāh, moral responsibility, purification of the soul, and the reality of the Hereafter, were already present in earlier revelations. The verse thus affirms the continuity and unity of divine guidance across prophetic history, even though the specific texts of those earlier scriptures are no longer exist in their original form.",
  88001: "Has there come to you the news of the Overwhelming Event?",
  88002: "˹Some˺ faces, that Day, will be humbled, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. That is, faces marked by humiliation and submission as a result of fear, regret, and disgrace. This abasement reflects the inner state of those who rejected guidance in worldly life and now face the consequences of their deeds. The outward expression on their faces mirrors their inward loss and sorrow on the Day of Judgment.",
  88003: "toiling and weary, [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. That is, engaged in exhausting toil that brings no benefit. This is referring to those who exerted themselves in false beliefs, misguided practices, or futile efforts in worldly life, only to find their labor unrewarded in the Hereafter.",
  88004: "burning in a blazing Fire,",
  88005: "they will be given drink from a boiling spring.",
  88006: "They will have no food except from a bitter thorny plant, [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. The term translated as “bitter thorny plant” renders the Arabic ḍarīʿ, which scholars describe as a dry, thorny, and bitter poisonous plant that is neither nourishing nor pleasant to consume. It is understood as a form of punishment food in the Hereafter—causing pain without providing sustenance.",
  88007: "that neither nourishes nor avails against hunger.",
  88008: "˹Other˺ faces on that Day will be blissful,",
  88009: "well pleased with their efforts,",
  88010: "in a lofty Paradise,",
  88011: "there you will hear no idle talk.",
  88012: "Therein is a flowing spring,",
  88013: "and couches raised,",
  88014: "and cups laid out,",
  88015: "and fine cushions lined up,",
  88016: "and splendid carpets spread out.",
  88017: "Do they not look at the camel—how it is created,",
  88018: "and the sky—how it is raised,",
  88019: "And the mountains—how they are firmly set?",
  88020: "And the earth—how it is spread out?",
  88021: "So remind—for you are only a reminder.",
  88022: "You are not one to compel them.",
  88023: "But whoever turns away and disbelieves,",
  88024: "Then Allāh will punish him with the greatest punishment.",
  88025: "Indeed, to Us is their return,",
  88026: "then indeed upon Us is their reckoning. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. That is, Allāh alone will call them to account for their deeds. This reckoning encompasses the full assessment of actions, intentions, and beliefs, leading to just recompense. The verse emphasizes exclusive divine authority in judgment, affirming that no soul will be wronged and that ultimate accountability rests solely with Allāh.\nIt is reported that Prophet Muhammad ﷺ would supplicate in some of his prayers: “O Allāh, bring me to account with an easy reckoning.”",
  89001: "By the dawn, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. “The dawn” (al-fajr) refers to the break of day when light first emerges after the darkness of night. It signifies a moment of transition, renewal, and awakening, when the world is brought from stillness into life. In the Qur’an, Allāh swears by such observable phenomena to draw attention to their significance as signs of His power and wisdom. Dawn, in particular, symbolizes clarity after obscurity, guidance after misguidance, and hope after hardship. Some early commentators also connected this oath to specific sacred times—such as the dawn of certain blessed days or the time of the dawn prayer—highlighting its spiritual importance. Overall, the oath by the dawn prepares the reader for a message of profound certainty, urging reflection on both the natural order and the moral truths that follow.",
  89002: "and the ten nights, [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The “ten nights” have been interpreted in several ways. Many scholars held that they refer to the first ten nights of Dhū al-Ḥijjah, which are regarded as especially virtuous due to the rites of pilgrimage and the increase in acts of worship. Others understood them as the last ten nights of Ramaḍān, distinguished by intensified devotion and the presence of the Night of Decree. In either case, the phrase signifies a period of heightened spiritual significance and divine favor.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: “There are no days in which righteous deeds are more beloved to Allāh than these ten days.” He was asked, “Not even jihād in the way of Allāh?” He replied: “Not even jihād in the way of Allāh, except for a man who goes out with his life and wealth and returns with nothing.”\nIt is also reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ used to observe retreat (iʿtikāf) during the last ten nights of Ramaḍān and would say: “Seek the Night of Decree in the last ten nights of Ramaḍān.”",
  89003: "and the even and the odd, [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. The phrase “the even and the odd” has been understood in multiple complementary ways. Some interpreted it as referring to all created things in pairs (the even) and to what is singular (the odd), pointing to the order of creation and the uniqueness of Allāh. Others understood it more specifically as a reference to acts of worship, such as the even and odd numbers of ritual prayers, including the odd-numbered witr prayer. Some also held that it alludes to specific days, such as the Day of Sacrifice (even) and the Day of ʿArafah (odd). In all interpretations, the expression emphasizes divine order, balance, and the contrast between plurality and oneness.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ practiced the witr prayer regularly and encouraged others to perform it at night after Isha (night-time prayer) or before fajr (dawn prayer). As such, praying witr comes under semi obligatory sunnah. The Prophet ﷺ would perform the witr prayer and say: 'O you people (followers) of the Qur'an, perform the witr prayer, for Allāh is one and He loves the witr (the odd number).'",
  89004: "and by the night as it passes,  [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. “The night as it passes” (al-layl idhā yasrī) refers to the gradual departure of the night as it moves toward dawn. The verb suggests motion and quiet progression, evoking the image of darkness receding and giving way to light. In Qur’anic rhetoric, the night often symbolizes stillness, concealment, or hardship, while its passing signifies relief, renewal, and the unfolding of clarity. By swearing by the night in its passing, the verse draws attention to the ordered cycles of time as signs of divine wisdom and power. It also complements the earlier oath “by the dawn,” together highlighting the transition from darkness to light—a recurring Qur’anic theme pointing to guidance after misguidance and ease after difficulty.",
  89005: "Is there not in all this an oath for those of understanding? [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. “For those of understanding” (dhī ḥijr), literally “one of restraint,” refers to a person endowed with sound reason and discernment. The term ḥijr denotes an intellect that restrains its possessor from folly, error, and wrongdoing—combining understanding with self-control and moral awareness. It implies the capacity to reflect deeply, recognize truth when presented with clear signs, and act accordingly. The verse is rhetorical, affirming that the preceding oaths—by the dawn, the nights, and the passing of time—are sufficient proof for anyone who truly reflects. Their underlying message is that human beings will inevitably be recompensed for their deeds; thus, it is not a lack of evidence but a failure of disciplined reasoning and heedfulness that leads to denial. Accordingly, the verse warns against persisting in disbelief, polytheism, and sin, as divine accountability is certain.",
  89006: "Have you not considered how your Lord dealt with ˹the people of˺ ‘Aad, [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. ʿĀd was an ancient Arabian people renowned for their power and monumental constructions. They were sent the prophet Hūd but rejected his call, persisting in arrogance and injustice. As a result, Allāh destroyed them with a devastating wind. Their mention here serves as a warning that worldly strength and civilization do not avert divine judgment.",
  89007: "Iram—possessors of lofty pillars, [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. Iram refers to the tribe of ʿĀd, described here as builders of great height and strength. “Lofty towers” conveys their remarkable architecture and imposing stature, symbolizing both their material power and their arrogance, which ultimately did not protect them from divine punishment.",
  89008: "the like of which had never been created in the lands.",
  89009: "and ˹the people of˺ Thamūd, who carved out the rocks in the valley;",
  89010: "and Pharaoh—possessor of the stakes? [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. “The stakes” (al-awtād) has been interpreted in several complementary ways: as a reference to Pharaoh’s immense power and firm dominion; to the stakes or pegs of his military camps and monumental constructions; or to instruments of torture by which he punished opponents. The expression conveys severity, entrenched authority, and cruelty, all of which failed to save him from divine judgment.",
  89011: "Who transgressed in the lands, [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. That is, each of these peoples exceeded all bounds through arrogance, oppression, and rebellion against divine guidance. The phrase generalizes their crimes across different lands and eras, emphasizing that tyranny and moral corruption, wherever they occur, lead inevitably to divine reckoning.",
  89012: "and spread therein much corruption;",
  89013: "so your Lord poured upon them a scourge of punishment.",
  89014: "Indeed your Lord is Ever-Watchful. [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. Over human beings, He observes them and tests them through the blessings with which He favors them. The Arabic bil-mirṣād denotes being in a position of watch and readiness, that is, vigilant oversight with inevitable requital. The point is not that Allāh “waits” as humans do, but that nothing escapes His knowledge, and wrongdoing will be met with just accountability at the appointed time.",
  89015: "As for man, when his Lord tests him by honoring him and bestowing favor upon him, he says, ‘My Lord has honored me.’",
  89016: "But when He tests him by restricting his provision, he says, ‘My Lord has humiliated me.’ [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. The verse censures the mistaken assumption that material hardship signifies divine humiliation. Restriction of provision, like its abundance, is a test, not a measure of a person’s worth before Allāh. Thus, one should not be ungrateful in prosperity nor impatient in adversity. Rather, be generous in times of ease and patient in times of trial. Be worthy of the trust of your neighbor, and look upon him with a bright and smiling face. Be a treasure to the poor, an admonisher to the rich, an answerer of the cry of the needy, and a preserver of the sanctity of your pledge. Be fair in judgment and guarded in speech. Be unjust to no one, and show humility toward all. Be a lamp for those who walk in darkness, a joy to the sorrowful, a sea for the thirsty, and a haven for the distressed—an upholder and defender of the victim of oppression. Let integrity and uprightness distinguish all your deeds. Be a home for the stranger, a balm to the suffering, a tower of strength for the fugitive; eyes for the blind, and a guiding light for the feet of the erring.",
  89017: "No indeed! Rather, you do not honor the orphan,",
  89018: "and you do not encourage one another to feed the poor,",
  89019: "and you consume inheritance, devouring it altogether, [12]\\n\\nFootnotes: [12]. The verse condemns the unjust appropriation of inheritance, particularly the consumption of the shares of the weak, such as orphans and women. The emphatic phrasing underscores unrestrained greed and moral callousness in the handling of others’ rights.",
  89020: "and you love wealth with immense love. [13]\\n\\nFootnotes: [13]. This verse condemns excessive attachment to wealth when it leads to neglect of moral responsibility, injustice toward others, and disregard for accountability before Allāh. Love of wealth in itself is not censured, but it becomes blameworthy when it grows all-consuming and displaces higher ethical and spiritual obligations. The verse thus calls for moderation, contentment, and a reordering of priorities, reminding that true security lies not in possessions but in reliance upon Allāh. Those who place their trust in Him and act with integrity are not forsaken; rather, they are guided toward a path of balance, righteousness, and lasting peace.",
  89021: "No indeed! When the earth is crushed—pounded over and over,",
  89022: "and your Lord comes [for Judgment], and the angels are arrayed rank after rank,",
  89023: "and Hell is brought within view on that Day. On that Day, man will take heed—but how will such heed then avail him?",
  89024: "He will say, ‘Oh, would that I had sent forth ˹some good˺ for my life.’ [14]\\n\\nFootnotes: [14]. That is, deeds performed in worldly life that would avail a person in the Hereafter. The verse underscores the tragic realization that what was treated as secondary—the life to come—was in fact the true and enduring life.",
  89025: "So on that Day, none will punish as He punishes,",
  89026: "and none will bind as He binds.",
  89027: " “O the tranquil soul!",
  89028: "Return to your Lord, well-pleased and pleasing to Him.",
  89029: "Enter among My honoured servants,",
  89030: "and enter My Paradise.”",
  90001: "I do swear by this city– [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. “This city” refers to Makkah, the sacred city and sanctuary established by Abraham. The oath emphasizes its unique sanctity, historical significance, and the gravity of the message revealed there. The expression lā uqsimu (“No, I swear”) is a rhetorical form in Arabic used to affirm and intensify what follows, not to negate the oath. Accordingly, this sacred city is to be regarded as a sign of hope and love by both those who dwell therein and those who visit it from afar. Believers are thus called to stand in solidarity with all who come to Makkah, to show kindness and goodwill, and to embody the values of the Qur’an and the Sunnah, so that they may become a living heart of guidance for the world.",
  90002: "and you are free of restriction [for a while] in this city– [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The phrase ḥillun bihādhā al-balad has been understood to mean that the Prophet is, for a time, released from the customary inviolability of the city, indicating a temporary permission connected with future events. It has also been read more generally as affirming his lawful presence and mission in Makkah despite opposition. Both readings underscore the gravity of the oath and the exceptional circumstances surrounding the Prophet’s role in the sacred city.\nThis meaning is illuminated by the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ on the day of the liberation of Makkah, when he declared: “This town was sanctified by Allāh when He created the heavens and the earth, and it is sanctified by Allāh until the Day of Judgment. It was not permitted for anyone before me to fight in it, and it was permitted for me only for part of a day. It remains sanctified by the sanctification of Allāh until the Day of Judgment: its thorny plants are not to be cut, its game is not to be frightened, lost property is not to be taken except by one who announces it, and its trees are not to be cut.”\nThis declaration affirms both the enduring sanctity of Makkah and the uniquely limited nature of the temporary permission granted to the Prophet.",
  90003: "and by a father and what was born of him! [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. The oath “by a father and what was born of him” has been interpreted broadly to encompass parenthood and progeny as a universal human reality. Some scholars understand it as referring specifically to Adam and his descendants, or to Abraham and his progeny, while others take it in a general sense. The expression highlights the continuity of human generation, struggle, and responsibility, fitting the sūrah’s theme of human toil and moral trial.",
  90004: "Indeed, We have created the human being in constant struggle.",
  90005: "Does he think that no one will ever have power over him?",
  90006: "He says [boastfully], ‘I have squandered abundant wealth.’ [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. In display of glory and grandeur. The statement “I have spent abundant wealth” reflects boastful self-regard and moral blindness. The Arabic ahlaktu literally means “I have destroyed” or “exhausted,” implying reckless expenditure undertaken for pride, rivalry, or opposition to truth rather than for righteousness. The speaker boasts either of squandering wealth in sinful pursuits or of expending it in hostility toward the divine message, assuming that material power places him beyond accountability. The verse exposes the delusion that lavish spending, when detached from moral purpose, constitutes virtue or grants immunity from divine judgment.",
  90007: "Does he think that no one sees him?",
  90008: "Have We not given him two eyes,",
  90009: "and a tongue and two lips,",
  90010: "and shown him the two paths—of good and evil?",
  90011: "Yet he has not undertaken the steep path. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The “Steep path” translates al-ʿaqabah, literally a steep and constricted mountain pass. The verb iqtaḥama conveys forceful effort—pressing forward despite resistance—indicating that this path requires deliberate moral struggle rather than passive assent. The Qur’an immediately explains this Path through concrete acts of self-sacrifice and social responsibility, such as freeing the enslaved, feeding the needy, and standing with the oppressed. Thus, the verse rebukes those who recognize moral truth yet shrink from the costly effort required to live by it.\nTraversing this Path entails learning and embodying the guidance of the Qur’an: affirming pure Islamic monotheism, establishing daily prayer, paying the prescribed poor-rate, fasting, performing ʿUmrah and Ḥajj, striving in the cause of Allāh, serving people, and caring for orphans and widows in their distress. These are not mere rituals, but expressions of commitment to the demanding moral way the verse calls for.",
  90012: "What will make you realize what the steep path is? [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. Paths differ not merely in width, but in the values and ends they embody. The path to destruction is described as wide because, in a fallen world, sin is abundant and readily accessible. People are often encouraged to prioritize self-interest over responsibility toward others, and to regard greed, pride, and the pursuit of power as virtues. Many follow this path because it is easy, enticing, and appears to satisfy immediate desires, even if only temporarily.\nBy contrast, the steep path is difficult and narrow, and few choose it. It is the path that leads to salvation—marked by surrender to Allāh, moral discipline, and commitment to what is right, even when it is demanding. Though challenging, this path yields spiritual growth and lasting fulfillment, revealing that what the world offers through the wide path is ultimately shallow in comparison. As the Prophet ﷺ said: “Paradise is surrounded by adversity, and Hellfire is surrounded by lusts.”",
  90013: "It is to free a slave, [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. This includes freeing a person from bondage in its various forms—slavery, crushing debt, unlawful captivity, or other conditions that strip human dignity—as well as aiding stranded foreigners and vulnerable strangers. “Freeing of a slave” translates fakku raqabah, literally “the release of a neck,” a Qur’anic expression for emancipation. It signifies the restoration of human dignity and freedom, and represents one of the highest forms of moral action in the Qur’an.\nSlavery was a deeply entrenched institution in the ancient world, and the Qur’an consistently works to dismantle it by encouraging manumission, prescribing it as expiation for certain sins, and placing it at the forefront of the “steep path.” Its mention here underscores that true righteousness begins with costly acts of justice and compassion that liberate others, not merely with inward belief or personal piety.\nThe Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said: “Whoever frees a slave, Allāh will save every part of his body from the Fire, in proportion to the parts he has freed from the slave.”\nHelp other people with their struggles.\nWhen someone is going through hardship—emotional pain, problems, temptations, grief, or practical difficulties—Muslims are called to support, comfort, and help them.\nBy doing this, they live out the teaching of Islam.",
  90014: "or to give food on a day of famine—",
  90015: "to an orphan who is a close relative,",
  90016: "or a destitute person in the dust. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. When people are starving, sick, displaced, or threatened, Allāh calls His servants to be the first to respond—by providing food, medicine, shelter, and safety, all in His name. The verse condemns any form of religiosity that withdraws from human suffering while claiming devotion. The Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ rebuked those who imagined that fasting and prayer alone sufficed while neglecting the concrete compassion demanded by crisis: feeding the hungry, sheltering the wanderer, and clothing the destitute.\nThe Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ reported that Allāh will say on the Day of Judgment: “Son of Adam, I was sick and you did not visit Me.” The person will reply, “My Lord, how could I visit You when You are the Lord of all beings?” He will say, “Did you not know that My servant so-and-so was sick and you did not visit him? Had you visited him, you would have found Me there.”\nThen He will say: “Son of Adam, I asked you for food and you did not feed Me.” He will reply, “My Lord, how could I feed You when You are the Lord of all beings?” He will say, “Did you not know that My servant so-and-so was hungry and you did not feed him? Had you fed him, you would have found its reward with Me.”\nThen He will say: “Son of Adam, I asked you for drink and you did not give Me to drink.” He will reply, “My Lord, how could I give You drink when You are the Lord of all beings?” He will say, “Did you not know that My servant so-and-so was thirsty and you did not give him drink? Had you given him drink, you would have found its reward with Me.”\nThus, Allāh wills that the hungry be fed—both physically and spiritually—and He brings relief to the afflicted through the hands of those who walk the steep path. Compassionate action is not secondary to faith; it is one of its clearest proofs.",
  90017: "Then to be among those who believe and urge one another to patience, and urge one another to compassion. [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. Faith is presented here not as an isolated conviction but as a shared moral commitment. The verb tawāṣaw denotes mutual and continual exhortation, implying a community in which believers actively encourage one another to remain steadfast (ṣabr) in hardship and to practice compassion (marḥamah) toward others. Patience without compassion risks becoming harsh endurance, while compassion without patience lacks resilience. Together, they form the ethical balance required to walk the steep path—a path sustained by communal responsibility, moral perseverance, and merciful concern for human dignity.\nThe Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said: “While a man was walking along a road, he found a thorny branch on the path and removed it. Allāh appreciated his deed and forgave him.” This teaching illustrates how even small acts of care for others, when rooted in faith and compassion, are weighty before Allāh.",
  90018: "Those are the people of the right. [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. “The people of the right” translates aṣḥābu al-maymanah, an expression denoting blessing, felicity, and a favorable outcome. In Qur’anic usage, the “right” signifies honor, success, and acceptance, in contrast to the “left,” which denotes loss and misfortune. It refers, in particular, to those who receive the record of their deeds in their right hand and are admitted to Paradise. These are people who combined true faith with perseverance and compassion, translating belief into concrete acts of justice and mercy. Their position reflects not mere affiliation, but the moral consequence of choosing the steep path and remaining steadfast upon it.",
  90019: "As for those who reject Our signs, they are the people of the left, [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. “The people of the left” translates aṣḥābu al-mashʾamah, a term that conveys misfortune, loss, and an evil end. In Qur’anic imagery, the “left” symbolizes rejection, disgrace, and exclusion from divine favor, in contrast to the “right,” which signifies blessing and acceptance. It refers to those who deny God’s signs in both belief and conduct—those who are given the record of their deeds in their left hand and who proceed toward Hell rather than Paradise.",
  90020: "The Fire will be sealed shut over them.",
  91001: "By the sun and its morning brightness, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. God swears by the sun and its morning brightness. Divine oaths by created things are not independent of God Himself; rather, they ultimately refer back to Him, for He is the Lord, Creator, and Sustainer of all beings. Thus, when Allāh swears by aspects of His creation, He is drawing attention both to their significance as signs of His power and wisdom and to the gravity of the truth being affirmed. In this sense, His swearing by creation is an indirect affirmation of His own perfection, greatness, and lordship.\nThrough such oaths, Allāh calls the reader of Divine Revelation to reflect deeply on His signs in the natural world as evidence of His existence, unity, and purpose. While Allāh may swear by whatever He wills, it is not permitted for human beings to swear by anything other than Allāh. Swearing by created beings—such as prophets, angels, righteous persons, the Kaʿbah, or one’s life or honor—is prohibited.\nThe Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said: “Whoever swears by anything other than Allāh has committed disbelief or idolatry.” He also said: “Do not swear by your fathers, nor by idols.”",
  91002: "and by the moon as it follows it— [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The phrase “as it follows it” refers to the moon following the sun in its ordained course. The moon appears after the sun’s setting and reflects its light, highlighting the harmony, order, and dependence within creation. This succession points to precise divine regulation: each celestial body has its appointed role and timing. By swearing by the moon in its following of the sun, Allāh draws attention to the balance and coherence of the cosmos as signs of His wisdom, power, and purposeful design.",
  91003: "and by the day as it reveals it, [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. “as it reveals it” translates jallāhā, meaning to make something clear, manifest, or fully visible. The verse describes the day as unveiling the sun in its full brightness, bringing its light into complete clarity. This continues the sequence of natural signs—sun, moon, and day—each reflecting a distinct aspect of illumination and order.",
  91004: "and by the night as it covers it, [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. I.e., the earth or the sun with darkness.",
  91005: "and by the sky and the One who built it,",
  91006: "and by the earth and the One who spread it,",
  91007: "and by the human self and the One who perfectly proportioned it, [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The “human self” (nafs) refers to the inner moral faculty endowed with awareness, inclination, and responsibility. The verb sawwāhā (“proportioned it”) indicates that the human self was created in a state of balance and harmony, capable of recognizing truth and falsehood. This innate moral disposition is later clarified by the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ, who said: \n“Every newborn is born upon the natural disposition (Islam). Then his parents make him a Jew, or a Christian, or a Magian—just as an animal gives birth to a whole, sound animal. Do you find any among them that are mutilated?”\nThe Prophet ﷺ also said:\n“Allāh, Mighty and Exalted, says: ‘Indeed, I created My servants upon pure monotheism (upright, inclining to truth), but the devils came to them and turned them away from their religion of Islam.’",
  91008: "then He inspired it with its wickedness and its righteousness.",
  91009: "Successful is the one who purifies it,",
  91010: "and failed is the one who corrupts it.",
  91011: "The people of Thamūd rejected [their messenger] because of their rebellion,",
  91012: "when the most wretched among them rose up. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. That is, to hamstring the she-camel that had been sent by God as a sign to them. This act represented the peak of their rebellion, as the she-camel was a clear divine sign whose violation symbolized their collective defiance of God’s command.",
  91013: "So God’s messenger said to them, ‘God’s she-camel—and her share of water.’ [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. That is, the she-camel was granted a divinely assigned and inviolable right to drink from the community’s water source, with a specified share or turn that was not to be infringed upon. This arrangement served as a clear test of obedience, restraint, and justice. Respecting her right signified acceptance of God’s sign and submission to His command, while violating it amounted to open rebellion against God and rejection of His messenger.",
  91014: "Yet they rejected him and hamstrung her, so their Lord crushed them for their sin and leveled them all alike. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. That is, they were leveled to the ground, or the punishment was made equal upon all of them. God’s punishment descended upon the entire community without distinction. Although the she-camel was physically hamstrung by a single individual, the people of Thamūd shared collective responsibility through approval, silence, and complicity. The verb sawwāhā (“leveled them all alike”) signifies that none were spared—no rank, lineage, or status offered protection. The verse thus underscores a Qur’anic principle: when corruption becomes communal and a clear divine sign is willfully violated, accountability and ruin likewise become collective.",
  91015: "And He has no fear of the consequences.",
  92001: "By the night when it covers [the earth], [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. The night is described as covering what daylight reveals, enveloping the world in darkness and stillness. This alternation between concealment and disclosure is a recurring Qur’anic sign of divine order and balance. By swearing by the night in its covering, God draws attention to the rhythm of rest and restraint that complements the activity and clarity of the day—preparing for the sūrah’s reflection on moral balance within the human self.",
  92002: "and by the day as it appears,",
  92003: "and by the One who created male and female,",
  92004: "Indeed, your striving is surely diverse.",
  92005: "As for the one who gives and is mindful of Allāh,",
  92006: "and affirms the best,",
  92007: "We will ease him toward ease. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. That is, the way of salvation. Allāh will gently facilitate for him the path that leads to ease in both this life and the Hereafter. Ease (al-yusrā) does not signify a life free of effort or trial, but a divinely assisted way in which obedience, generosity, and righteousness become natural rather than burdensome. Allāh’s guidance aligns a person’s inner disposition with what is good, so that moral striving is met with spiritual openness, clarity, and eventual felicity.",
  92008: "But as for the one who is stingy and considers himself self-sufficient,",
  92009: "and denies the best,",
  92010: "We will ease him toward hardship. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3].That is, the way of ruin and perdition. Allāh allows such a person to proceed along a path that becomes progressively difficult in this life and culminates in loss in the Hereafter. Hardship (al-ʿusrā) does not mean the absence of divine power, but the withdrawal of divine facilitation. When a person chooses stinginess, arrogance, and denial of moral truth, the road of life itself becomes constricted—good deeds feel heavy, wrongdoing feels easy, and moral blindness deepens until ruin becomes the natural end.",
  92011: "What will his wealth benefit him when he perishes?",
  92012: "It is certainly upon Us to give guidance, [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. To guide and show the right from wrong.",
  92013: "and indeed, to Us belong the Hereafter and the present life.",
  92014: "So I warn you of a blazing Fire,",
  92015: "none will enter it except the most wretched,",
  92016: "who denies [the truth] and turns away.",
  92017: "But the most pious will be spared from it—",
  92018: "who gives his wealth to purify himself, [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. Generosity is a path to true prosperity: whoever helps others will themselves be helped. The Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said: “Whoever relieves a believer of hardship in this world, Allāh will relieve him of hardship on the Day of Resurrection. Whoever eases one in difficulty, Allāh will make it easy for him in this world and the Hereafter. Whoever conceals the faults of a believer, Allāh will conceal his faults in this world and the Hereafter. Allāh continues to help the servant as long as the servant helps his brother. Whoever travels a path in search of knowledge, Allāh will make easy for him a path to Paradise. No people gather in the houses of Allāh, reciting the Book of Allāh and studying it together, except that tranquility descends upon them, mercy envelops them, the angels surround them, and Allāh mentions them among those near Him. And whoever is slowed by his deeds will not be hastened by his lineage.”\nThis teaching illustrates that giving in charity purifies the self not only by removing attachment to wealth, but by cultivating mercy, humility, and reliance upon Allāh—virtues that draw divine assistance in both this life and the Hereafter.",
  92019: "and not in return for any favor done to him,",
  92020: "but only to seek the Face of his Lord, the Most High.",
  92021: "He will surely be well pleased. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. That is, he will attain complete and lasting satisfaction. This pleasure is not merely emotional contentment in this world, but the fulfillment granted by Allāh in the Hereafter—through acceptance, reward, and nearness to Him. Having given sincerely, without seeking repayment or recognition, such a person is granted a state in which the soul’s deepest longing is answered. The verse affirms that pure intention and selfless devotion culminate in true happiness, a contentment that neither wealth nor worldly success can provide.",
  93001: "By the morning sunlight, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. God swears by the early hours of the day (al-ḍuḥā), a time marked by clarity, renewal, and the return of light after darkness. The oath draws attention to reassurance and hope following hardship, setting the tone for the message that follows.",
  93002: "and by the night as it grows still, [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The night symbolizes stillness and obscurity, yet it is never permanent. Just as darkness gives way to daybreak, hardship is followed by ease. This oath reassures that moments of difficulty and silence are part of a divine rhythm, not signs of abandonment. With patience and trust in Allāh, relief and clarity inevitably follow, as light follows the night.",
  93003: "your Lord has not forsaken you, nor is He displeased; [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. This verse was revealed in response to the taunts of the pagans, who said to the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ that his Lord had forsaken him when revelation paused for a time. Allāh emphatically refutes this claim, affirming that the temporary cessation of revelation was neither abandonment nor displeasure, but part of divine wisdom and timing.",
  93004: "the life of the Hereafter is far better for you than this present life. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. Another meaning is: what lies ahead for you is better than what has passed before. This reassurance affirms that future stages of the Prophet’s mission—and ultimately the Hereafter—will surpass earlier hardships, emphasizing divine wisdom, progression, and hope rather than loss or decline.",
  93005: "And your Lord will certainly give you more, and you will be well pleased.",
  93006: "Did He not find you orphaned and provide for you? [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ was orphaned at a young age, having lost his father before his birth and his mother soon thereafter. Allāh granted him shelter, care, and protection through successive guardians—his grandfather ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib and then his uncle Abū Ṭālib—and ultimately through divine support and honor. This verse reminds the Prophet ﷺ that his early vulnerability was met with Allāh’s providence, care, and preservation, affirming that past hardship was never a sign of abandonment but part of a divinely guided path.\nIslam, the pure and undefiled religion before God, strongly encourages the care of orphans and widows. The Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said: “I and the believer who sponsors an orphan will be in Paradise like these two,” and he raised his index finger and the one next to it, holding them close together.",
  93007: "Did He not find you unguided and then guide you?",
  93008: "Did He not find you poor, and then enrich you?",
  93009: "So as for the orphan—do not oppress him, [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. The command “do not oppress him” translates lā taqhar, which denotes more than physical harm; it includes humiliating, intimidating, exploiting, or exercising unjust dominance over one who is vulnerable. Having been an orphan himself, the Prophet ﷺ is reminded—and the wider community is instructed—that gratitude for divine care must be expressed through mercy toward those in similar need. The verse establishes a moral principle: past blessing entails present responsibility, and strength is to be used for protection, not oppression.\nA man once came to the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ, lamenting the hardness of his heart. The Prophet ﷺ said: “If you wish to soften your heart, feed the poor and gently pat the head of the orphan.”",
  93010: "and as for the one who asks—do not repel him, [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. “The one who asks” includes both those who seek material help and those who seek knowledge or guidance. The Qur’an instructs believers to respond to such requests with kindness, dignity, and generosity, forbidding harsh dismissal or rebuke. Even when one is unable to give, the response should remain gentle and respectful. The principle is to give to those who ask and not turn away those who seek assistance or wish to borrow, upholding compassion and social responsibility.",
  93011: "and proclaim the grace of your Lord. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. That is, the gift of Prophethood, the Qur’an, and the perfect religion of Islam. To “proclaim the grace of your Lord” means to acknowledge, remember, and speak of God’s blessings with gratitude—without pride or self-display. This includes praising Allāh for His guidance, mercy, provision, and protection, and making those blessings evident through righteous conduct and service to others. Proclamation here is not boastful speech, but thankful testimony: gratitude expressed by the tongue, affirmed in the heart, and embodied in action. By recounting Allah’s favors, the believer strengthens faith, inspires hope in others, and fulfills the duty of thankfulness.",
  94001: "Did We not expand your chest for you? [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. The phrase “expand your chest” translates sharḥ al-ṣadr, a Qur’anic expression denoting inner openness, reassurance, and spiritual capacity. It signifies that Allāh granted the Prophet ﷺ clarity of heart, firmness of faith, patience in adversity, and the strength to bear the weight of revelation and opposition. This expansion primarily is a spiritual endowment—an inward illumination and tranquility—though some traditions also allude to a miraculous physical sign. In either case, the meaning affirms divine preparation and support for the Prophet’s mission.",
  94002: "And removed from you your burden, [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The “burden” (wizr) refers to the weight of distress, anxiety, and responsibility that pressed upon the Prophet ﷺ before and during the early stages of his mission. Allāh lifted this burden by granting reassurance, strengthening his resolve, clarifying his path, and supporting him through revelation and eventual success. It does not imply sin—for the Prophet ﷺ is protected from moral fault—but rather the heaviness of concern, opposition, and the immense responsibility of conveying the divine message. The verse affirms that Allāh does not leave His messenger crushed beneath the weight of duty, but actively lightens the load through divine aid.",
  94003: "that weighed down your back?",
  94004: "and raised high your renown for you?",
  94005: "For indeed, with hardship comes ease. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. This verse affirms a divine promise: hardship is never solitary, for ease accompanies it. The Arabic maʿa (“with”) indicates simultaneity rather than mere succession—relief exists alongside difficulty, even if it is not immediately perceived. Al-ʿusr (“the hardship”) is definite and singular, while yusr (“ease”) is indefinite, implying that a single hardship is accompanied by multiple avenues of ease. The repetition of this promise in the following verse reinforces certainty, reassurance, and hope, especially in times of trial.\nThe Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said: “The most affluent person in the world among the people of Hell will be brought on the Day of Resurrection and dipped into the Fire once. Then it will be said to him, ‘O son of Adam, did you ever experience any good? Did you ever have any pleasure?’ He will say, ‘No, by Allāh, O Lord.’ Then the most destitute person in the world among the people of Paradise will be brought and dipped into Paradise once, and it will be said to him, ‘O son of Adam, did you ever experience any hardship or pain?’ He will say, ‘No, by Allāh, O Lord, I never experienced any hardship, nor did I suffer any pain.’”\nThis narration illustrates how fleeting worldly hardship becomes when measured against eternal ease, affirming the Qur’anic promise that patience is never without ultimate relief.",
  94006: "Indeed, with hardship comes ease. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. That is, Allah’s plan is one of mercy and purpose, not abandonment or failure. He knows the course He has set for the Prophet ﷺ and for the believers—a plan leading to growth, support, and ultimate success, not loss or forsakenness. What may appear as delay or hardship is part of a divinely guided process that culminates in a good life in this world and a far greater future in the Hereafter. The verse reassures that Allah’s care is constant, His promise trustworthy, and His outcome always good.",
  94007: "So when you have finished [your tasks], then devote yourself fully,",
  94008: "and to your Lord turn with devoted longing. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The verb farghab (from raghiba ilā) conveys eager desire, longing, and hopeful turning toward something with intention and aspiration. The command directs the Prophet ﷺ—and by extension the believer—to orient all hope, reliance, and deep yearning exclusively toward Allāh. It implies not mere devotion, but heartfelt aspiration, seeking, and earnest turning to Him alone after completing one’s duties.",
  95001: "By the fig and the olive, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. God swears by the fig and the olive—two fruits long associated with nourishment, healing, and blessing. Many scholars also interpret them symbolically as references to blessed lands tied to revelation and prophetic history. The oath invites reflection on divine provision and the moral order that follows.",
  95002: "and Mount Sinai, [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. Mount Sinai is the mountain where Allāh spoke to the Prophet Moses عليه السلام and revealed the Torah. It symbolizes divine revelation, covenant, and moral guidance, standing as a witness to Allāh’s communication with humanity.",
  95003: "and this secure city. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. This refers to Makkah, the sacred city made secure by God and established as a sanctuary. Its security is both physical and spiritual: violence is forbidden within it, and it is the place of pilgrimage, worship, and the final revelation. By swearing an oath by this town, God emphasizes its sanctity and the gravity of the message revealed there.\nThe preceding verses together allude to the lands associated with three of the greatest prophets of God. Jesus lived and preached in the land of the fig and the olive; Moses communicated with God on Mount Sinai; and Muḥammad ﷺ was born in Makkah. A parallel reference appears in Deuteronomy 33:1: “The Lord came from Sinai, rose from Seir upon us, and shone forth from Mount Paran.” Seir is associated with the region near Jerusalem, while Paran is identified with a mountainous area near Makkah. Together, these references underscore a continuous chain of divine revelation across sacred lands and prophetic history.",
  95004: "Indeed, We have created the human being in the best form. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. “The best form” translates aḥsan taqwīm, meaning the most balanced and excellent constitution. It refers not only to the human being’s upright physical form, but also to the harmony of intellect, moral awareness, and spiritual capacity. Human beings are endowed with the ability to distinguish right from wrong, to seek truth, and to bear moral responsibility. This excellence, therefore, is not merely physical beauty, but a complete integration of body, mind, and soul—making the human being uniquely suited for guidance, accountability, and closeness to Allāh.\nThis is further reflected in the Prophetic teaching that Allāh created mankind “in His image.” This does not imply that Allāh possesses a human form. Rather, it points to the distinctive qualities granted to human beings, such as moral and rational awareness, a sense of responsibility, and a role of stewardship on earth. In this way, being created by Allāh affirms the inherent dignity and worth of every human being.",
  95005: "Then We return him to the lowest of the low,  [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. “The lowest of the low” translates asfala sāfilīn, denoting the deepest state of degradation and loss. While some scholars note that this can include physical decline in extreme old age, the primary meaning in context is moral and spiritual descent—the corruption of the human being who fails to live in accordance with the purpose for which they were created. Having been endowed with the best form—intellect, conscience, and capacity for faith—those who reject guidance and persist in wrongdoing are reduced to a state beneath their intended dignity, culminating in humiliation in the Hereafter. The verse thus underscores that true elevation or abasement depends not on form or status, but on faith and righteous conduct.",
  95006: "except those who believe and do righteous deeds—for them is a never-ending reward.",
  95007: "So what causes you, after this, to deny the Day of Recompense?",
  95008: "Is not Allāh the most just of all judges? [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. By appointing the Day of Judgment for requital, Allāh does not leave His servants in vain—without judgment or moral consequence. He will judge between them with perfect justice, rewarding the doer of good for his good and requiting the evildoer for his evil. This rhetorical question affirms, rather than asks about, Allāh’s absolute justice. Having established human dignity, moral responsibility, accountability, and recompense, the sūrah concludes by grounding judgment in Allāh’s complete wisdom and fairness. No injustice, imbalance, or error enters His judgment; every deed is weighed with full knowledge and equity. The verse thus reassures the believer and confronts the denier: ultimate judgment belongs to the One who is the most just of all judges, and His verdict is final and flawless.",
  96001: "Recite in the Name of your Lord Who created—",
  96002: "created man from a clinging clot.",
  96003: "Recite, and your Lord is the Most Generous,",
  96004: "Who taught by the pen,",
  96005: "taught man what he did not know.",
  96006: "No! Indeed, the human being transgresses, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. The Arabic yaṭghā denotes more than wrongdoing; it signifies exceeding all bounds with arrogance and disregard for restraint, including rebellion and tyranny. Here it applies especially to the denier, whose rejection of truth drives such transgression. It reflects a recurring human tendency, most evident when one sees oneself as self-sufficient and free of accountability (96:7).",
  96007: "when he sees himself as self-sufficient. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. In terms of wealth, honor, and rank.\nThat is, when a person imagines himself independent of Allāh. The phrase “sees himself self-sufficient” reflects a delusion, not a reality; human beings remain utterly dependent on Allāh for existence, provision, guidance, and life itself. This false sense of self-sufficiency—whether due to wealth, power, knowledge, or status—breeds arrogance and rebellion, leading one to overstep moral bounds. The verse thus identifies perceived independence from Allāh as the root cause of transgression and denial.",
  96008: "Indeed, to your Lord is the final Return.",
  96009: "Have you seen the one who forbids,",
  96010: "a servant when he prays?",
  96011: "What if he is upon right guidance,",
  96012: "Or enjoins righteousness?",
  96013: "What if he rejects the truth and turns away?",
  96014: "Does he not know that Allāh sees? [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. This rhetorical question affirms Allāh’s complete and constant awareness. Nothing—neither outward action nor inward intention—escapes His sight. The verse serves as a direct warning to those who arrogantly obstruct worship or reject truth: even if they feel unaccountable before people, they remain fully seen by Allāh. Divine watchfulness here implies not mere observation, but inevitable accountability; every act of injustice, denial, or oppression will be brought to judgment. The reminder dismantles the illusion of impunity and calls the conscience back to awareness of Allāh’s ever-present oversight.\nThe Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said: “Do not commit sin in secrecy while you would be ashamed for people to see you committing it openly.”",
  96015: "No indeed! If he does not desist, We will surely seize him by the forelock,",
  96016: "a lying, sinful forelock.",
  96017: "So let him summon his council—",
  96018: "We will summon the guards of Hell. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. “The guards of Hell” translates al-zabāniyah, the stern and powerful angels appointed to execute divine punishment. They are described in the Qur’an as relentless, obedient servants of Allāh who do not disobey His commands. This verse contrasts human authority with divine power: while the arrogant oppressor may summon his worldly council and supporters, Allāh will summon forces beyond human resistance. The warning underscores the futility of relying on social power, influence, or intimidation in defiance of Allāh, and affirms that ultimate authority belongs solely to Allāh.",
  96019: "No indeed! Do not obey him; rather, prostrate yourself and draw near [to Allāh].",
  97001: "Indeed, We sent the Qur'an down during the Night of Decree. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. “The Night of Glory and Decree ” translates Laylat al-Qadr, a night of immense honor, divine determination, and surpassing worth. It is the night in which the Qur’an was first sent down from the Preserved Tablet to the lowest heaven, marking the beginning of revelation to the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ. The term qadr conveys multiple meanings, including divine decree, precise measure, power, and exalted status. On this night, the affairs of the coming year are released—provisions, lifespans, births, deaths, calamities, and all other matters—according to Allāh’s perfect wisdom. Thus, it is a night in which divine mercy descends and Allāh’s wise decisions are set forth. Its greatness lies not in the passage of time itself, but in the revelation and decrees issued within it, making it greater than any span of time measured by human standards.",
  97002: "What will make you realize what the Night of Decree is?",
  97003: "The Night of Decree is better than a thousand months [2].\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. That is, worship and righteous deeds performed during the Night of Decree surpass those performed over a thousand months outside of it—more than eighty-three years of continuous devotion—for whoever stands in prayer with sincere faith and in hopeful expectation of reward from Allāh. The comparison is not intended to restrict the Night’s value to a numerical measure, but to convey its immeasurable excellence and divine generosity. This superiority arises from the descent of the Qur’an, the angels, and divine mercy on this Night, making even a single moment within it greater than a lifetime of ordinary worship. It is a manifestation of Allāh’s grace, through which He multiplies reward beyond all human calculation.",
  97004: "The angels and the Spirit descend in it, by their Lord’s permission, with every matter. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. The verb tanazzalu conveys repeated and abundant descent, indicating that the angels come down continuously throughout the night. The “Spirit” (al-Rūḥ) is commonly understood to refer to the angel Jibrīl. The phrase “for every matter” (min kulli amr) signifies that they descend carrying out all that Allāh has decreed—His commands for the coming period. This includes all affairs: provision, life spans, births, deaths, calamities, mercy, and guidance, each determined according to His wisdom. Their descent signifies peace, blessing, and divine order, as the unseen realm engages with the affairs of creation in perfect obedience to their Lord. The verse thus portrays a night filled with divine activity, in which heavenly beings descend by Allāh’s permission to carry out His will.",
  97005: "It is peace—until the rise of dawn. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. That is, the Night of Decree is wholly characterized by peace and safety until the break of dawn. It is a night free from harm, in which mercy descends, sins are forgiven, and supplications are answered. The angels greet the believers with peace, and no evil is decreed to occur within it. This peace endures from sunset until dawn, marking the completion of a night distinguished by tranquility, divine protection, and abundant blessing.\nThe Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said: “Seek the Night of Decree in the odd nights of the last ten nights of Ramaḍān.”",
  98001: "Those who disbelieved among the People of the Scripture and the polytheists were not to abandon [their disbelief] until clear evidence came to them: [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. “Clear evidence” (al-bayyinah) refers to unmistakable divine proof that leaves no excuse for denial. It is understood to mean either Muḥammad ﷺ himself—whose truthfulness, character, and message testified to his prophethood—or the Qur’an revealed to him as his enduring miracle. The verse highlights that disbelief and adherence to falsehood persisted not because guidance was unclear, but because hearts resisted the truth even after decisive proof had been established. This underscores a central Qur’anic principle: accountability follows clarity, and rejection after clear evidence is a moral choice rather than an intellectual failure.",
  98002: "a messenger from Allāh [2], reciting purified scriptures,\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The “messenger” refers to the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ, the final Messenger sent by Allāh to all humanity. The phrase “reciting purified scriptures” (ṣuḥuf muṭahharah) describes the revelation as wholly pure—free from error, falsehood, and corruption. It also conveys a sense of spiritual purity, as the message itself purifies hearts and guides them to the truth.\nThe Prophet ﷺ recites the revelation entrusted to him—the Qur’an—conveying it faithfully, clearly, and without alteration. His mission itself constitutes the clear evidence (al-bayyinah), through which divine guidance was made manifest, distinguishing truth from falsehood and confirming the message of earlier revelations in its final, preserved form.",
  98003: "containing upright rulings. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. “Upright ordinances” translates kutubun qayyimah, denoting teachings and commands that are straight, just, and firmly established. They are upright in belief, ethics, and law, free from contradiction or deviation, and enduring in that they remain valid and applicable across time and circumstance. This refers to the core doctrines, moral principles, and legal rulings contained in divine revelation—guidance that rectifies human conduct and establishes justice. The phrase affirms that the message recited by the Messenger is not arbitrary or temporary, but divinely grounded, balanced, and lasting.",
  98004: "Yet those who were given the Scripture did not split ˹into sects˺ until after clear evidence had come to them, [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. This verse condemns willful division, not sincere ignorance. Those who were given earlier revelation did not disagree due to lack of guidance, but after clear evidence had come to them. “The clear evidence” (al-bayyinah) refers to either the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ himself—whose character, message, and truthfulness testified to his prophethood—or the Qur’ān, sent as his enduring miracle, containing unmistakable guidance and upright ordinances. Scholars explain that their disagreement arose from envy, pride, and worldly rivalry rather than uncertainty about the truth. The verse thus exposes a moral failing: clarity of evidence does not guarantee unity when hearts are corrupted by self-interest. It serves as a warning that knowledge, when divorced from humility and sincerity, can become a cause of fragmentation rather than guidance.",
  98005: "They were commanded only to worship Allāh, devoting the religion sincerely to Him, inclining to truth, to establish prayer, and to give charity. That is the upright religion. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. This verse defines the essence of true religion. To worship Allāh “with sincere devotion” means pure monotheism (ikhlāṣ), directing all acts of worship to Him alone without partners. “Being upright” translates ḥunafāʾ—inclining away from falsehood toward the natural disposition of pure faith. Establishing the prayer represents continual devotion to Allāh, while paying the poor-rate (zakāh) embodies social responsibility and justice. Together, these commands summarize the core of divine religion across all revelations: sincere belief, upright moral orientation, regular worship, and care for others. “That is the right religion” affirms that this way is straight, balanced, and enduring, free from distortion and excess.\nThe Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ said: “Verily, your Lord Allāh said: ‘I created all of My servants inclined to worship Me alone, but devils came to them and turned them away from their religion. They made unlawful what I had made lawful for them, and commanded them to associate partners with Me for which I sent down no authority.’”",
  98006: "Indeed, those who disbelieve among the People of the Scripture and the polytheists will be in the Fire of Hell, abiding therein forever. They are the worst of all creatures. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. The phrase “the worst of creatures” (sharr al-bariyyah) refers to those who persist in disbelief after clear evidence has been established. It is a moral and spiritual judgment based on the willful rejection of truth, not a denial that they are still created beings endowed with inherent human dignity. The verse thus emphasizes accountability: once guidance has been made clear, to reject it is a grave moral failure.",
  98007: "Indeed, those who believe and do good—they are the best of all creatures. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. The phrase “the best of creatures” (khayr al-bariyyah) refers to those who attain the highest moral and spiritual rank through faith and righteous action. It signifies not inherent superiority by origin, but a distinction earned through belief, sincerity, and obedience. In contrast to the previous verse, it shows that human worth is determined by one’s response to divine guidance—those who accept and live by it are elevated to the highest rank.",
  98008: "Their reward with their Lord is Gardens of Eden, with rivers flowing beneath, where they will abide forever. Allāh is well-pleased with them, and they are well-pleased with Him. That is for the one who is in awe of his Lord. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. The phrase “Allāh is well-pleased with them, and they are well-pleased with Him” expresses the highest state of mutual رضى (pleasure): divine acceptance and human contentment. This reflects complete harmony between the servant and his Lord. The concluding phrase, “for the one who stands in awe of his Lord” (khashiyah), indicates that such a reward is for those whose reverent awareness of Allāh leads them to faith, obedience, and sincerity.",
  99001: "When the earth is shaken with its ultimate earthquake, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. The phrase “its earthquake” (zilzālahā) emphasizes the earth’s final and destined convulsion. The repetition of the root in the Arabic intensifies the meaning, conveying a complete and overwhelming upheaval unlike any before—one associated with the Resurrection, when the natural order is entirely undone.",
  99002: "and the earth brings forth its burdens, [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The term “its burdens” (athqālahā) refers to all that the earth contains and carries—most notably the dead, who are brought forth for resurrection, as well as everything hidden within it. The imagery conveys a complete emptying of the earth as it releases its contents in preparation for judgment. Nothing buried, concealed, or forgotten will remain hidden on that Day; the earth itself will testify by bringing forth all that it once held.",
  99003: "and man will say, ‘What is the matter with it?’ [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. This question expresses shock, fear, astonishment and utter bewilderment at the unprecedented transformation of the earth. Humanity, accustomed to the earth’s stability and silence, is stunned as it convulses and releases what it had concealed. This cry is not one of inquiry seeking an answer, but an involuntary reaction of terror upon witnessing the collapse of the familiar world and the arrival of the Day of Resurrection. It reflects the sudden realization that divine judgment has begun and that worldly certainties have come to an end.",
  99004: "On that Day, it will report its news, [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. That is, the earth will testify—by Allāh’s command—concerning all that occurred upon it. It will recount the deeds committed on its surface, both good and evil, serving as a witness against or in favor of those who acted upon it. This testimony leaves no act concealed: places, times, and actions will all be made known. The verse underscores the totality of accountability on the Day of Resurrection, when even inanimate creation is made to speak the truth by divine will.",
  99005: "because your Lord has inspired it. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The phrase “has inspired it” (awḥā lahā) refers to Allāh’s direct command and enabling of the earth to speak. Here, waḥy signifies not prophetic revelation, but divine instruction by which the earth is made to testify. The verse emphasizes that even the earth acts in full obedience to its Lord, bearing witness as commanded.",
  99006: "On that Day, people will emerge in scattered groups to be shown their deeds. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6].  The phrase “in separate groups” (ashtātan) indicates that people will be divided and distinguished according to their deeds and states. No longer gathered as a single community, they will be separated for judgment, each group reflecting its beliefs and actions. The purpose is “that they may be shown their deeds,” meaning that every individual will be made fully aware of what they have done.",
  99007: "Whoever does even an atom’s weight of good will see it, [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. The phrase “an atom’s weight” (mithqāla dharrah) refers to the smallest conceivable measure of something. It emphasizes that no deed, however small, escapes notice. Even the slightest act of good will be brought forth and seen, underscoring the completeness and precision of divine justice.",
  99008: "and whoever does an atom’s weight of evil will see it. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. This verse completes the balance established in the previous one: just as no act of good is overlooked, no act of evil escapes account. The phrase “an atom’s weight” (mithqāla dharrah) emphasizes the smallest conceivable measure, underscoring the precision and completeness of divine justice. Every deed, however slight, will be made manifest.",
  100001: "By the charging horses, breathing heavily, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. The phrase evokes war horses charging at full speed, their breath audibly panting or snorting (ḍabḥan). The imagery conveys power, urgency, and intensity, setting the tone for a vivid and dynamic oath. Allāh swears by these galloping horses as they are driven into battle, bearing their riders with speed, exertion, and resolve. It evokes disciplined striving, sacrifice, and obedience, as the horses expend their strength entirely at the command of their riders. The oath thus draws attention to unwavering dedication in service and prepares for the moral contrast that follows—between such loyalty and human ingratitude toward their Lord.",
  100002: "striking sparks of fire with their hooves,[2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The phrase describes the horses’ hooves striking against hard ground, producing visible sparks (qad-ḥan). It intensifies the imagery of speed, power, and force, suggesting movement in darkness where the sparks become visible. The scene adds to the dynamic sequence of motion, highlighting relentless forward drive and the intensity of the charge.",
  100003: "then launching raids at dawn, [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. This refers to swift charges or raids carried out at daybreak, a time chosen for its element of surprise and strategic advantage, when the enemy is least aware. The Arabic al-mughīrāti ṣubḥā conveys sudden, decisive action undertaken with resolve and discipline. Dawn raids exemplify determination, courage, and complete readiness, as horses and riders press forward without hesitation. The image intensifies the oath’s momentum and underscores total commitment to a commanded cause, setting up the sūrah’s contrast between such devoted striving and human ingratitude toward Allāh.",
  100004: "stirring up thereby clouds of dust, [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. The phrase describes the horses stirring up dense clouds of dust (naqʿan) as they charge forward. The image conveys speed, force, and the intensity of movement, as the ground itself is disturbed by their force and momentum. It adds to the clear progression of the scene, portraying a forceful and coordinated advance.",
  100005: "penetrating as one body into the midst of the enemy lines. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The phrase describes the horses and their riders penetrating directly into the midst of a gathered force (jamʿan), indicating a bold and unified charge. The imagery conveys قوة, coordination, and resolve, as they advance as one body into the heart of the enemy lines. It completes the sequence of motion—galloping, striking sparks, raiding at dawn, raising dust, and finally plunging into the center—before the passage turns to its moral contrast.",
  100006: "Indeed, the human being is deeply ungrateful to his Lord. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. “Ungrateful” translates kanūd, a strong Arabic term describing one who persistently denies or disregards blessings. It refers to a person who enjoys Allāh’s favors yet fails to acknowledge them with gratitude, obedience, or humility. This is not mere forgetfulness, but a settled disposition of ingratitude—complaining in hardship, claiming credit in times of ease, and neglecting the Giver while enjoying the gifts. The verse thus exposes a central moral flaw in human nature when left unchecked by faith: despite abundant blessings from Allāh, man often responds with denial rather than thankfulness.",
  100007: "And he himself bears witness to it. [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. The verse indicates that the human being is himself a witness to his ingratitude, whether through inner awareness or outward conduct. It suggests that this condition is not hidden or unknown; rather, one’s own actions and conscience testify to it, reinforcing personal accountability.",
  100008: "Indeed, he is extreme in his love of wealth. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8]. The phrase describes an intense and excessive attachment to wealth. Although al-khayr literally means “good,” it is understood here as material wealth. The term shadīd conveys severity and intensity, indicating a deep, consuming love that can lead to heedlessness and ingratitude. It explains the underlying cause of the man’s ingratitude—an overpowering attachment to worldly gain.",
  100009: "Does he not know that when the contents of the graves are overturned, [9]\\n\\nFootnotes: [9]. The verb buʿthira conveys the overturning and scattering of what lies within the graves, bringing forth the dead for resurrection. It suggests a forceful and complete exposure, as all that was hidden is brought out into the open in preparation for judgment.",
  100010: "and what is in the hearts is brought forth and exposed? [10]\\n\\nFootnotes: [10]. The phrase “what is in the hearts is brought forth” refers to the full disclosure of inner realities—intentions, motives, and concealed thoughts. Nothing remains hidden; what was inward and unseen is extracted and made manifest, completing the exposure that began with the overturning of the graves.",
  100011: "Indeed, their Lord, on that Day, is fully aware of them. [11]\\n\\nFootnotes: [11]. This verse affirms Allāh’s complete and encompassing knowledge at the moment of final judgment. His awareness includes outward actions and inward realities alike—intentions, motives, and secrets of the heart—everything that has been exposed from the graves and the hearts in the preceding verses. The phrase “on that Day” does not imply that Allah’s knowledge is limited to the Day of Resurrection; rather, it emphasizes the moment when His perfect knowledge is fully manifested through judgment and recompense. Nothing will be overlooked or forgotten, and every soul will be judged with absolute justice and clarity.",
  101001: "The Striking Calamity. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. “The Striking Calamity” of the final Hour translates al-Qāriʿah, a Qur’anic name for the Day of Resurrection. The term derives from a root meaning “to strike” or “to knock violently,” evoking an event that crashes upon hearts with overwhelming force and terror. It signifies the suddenness, severity, and inescapability of the Hour, which will shatter worldly illusions and awaken all to judgment. The verse opens the sūrah with a jarring tone, preparing the listener for a depiction of total upheaval and ultimate accountability.",
  101002: "What is the Striking Calamity?",
  101003: "What will make you realize what the Striking Calamity is?",
  101004: "It is the Day when people will be like scattered moths, moving in confusion, [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The comparison to “scattered moths” (al-farāsh al-mabthūth) evokes utter confusion, fragility, and helpless motion. Moths swarm chaotically, colliding without direction—instinctively drawn yet unable to orient themselves—an image that clearly portrays humanity’s disarray at the shock of the Resurrection. The simile highlights the collapse of worldly order: social ranks vanish, confidence dissolves, and people move aimlessly in panic and fear. The verse thus underscores the overwhelming terror of the Hour, when human strength, control, and composure are utterly undone.",
  101005: "and the mountains will be like carded wool. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. “Carded wool” translates al-ʿihn al-manfūsh, referring to dyed wool that has been combed, loosened, and fluffed until it becomes light, scattered, and without cohesion. The simile conveys the complete loss of solidity and stability: mountains—once symbols of strength, permanence, and immovability—will be reduced to something weightless and drifting. This image emphasizes the total upheaval of the natural order on the Day of Resurrection, when even the most massive and enduring features of creation are rendered fragile and insubstantial by the command of Allāh.",
  101006: "Then whoever’s scales are heavy [with good deeds], [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. The “scales” (mawāzīn) represent the weighing of deeds on the Day of Judgment. Their “heaviness” signifies the abundance and weight of good deeds. The imagery conveys precise and just evaluation, where one’s actions determine one’s ultimate outcome.",
  101007: "he will be in a pleasant life. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. The phrase “a pleasant life in Paradise” denotes a life of complete contentment, satisfaction, and ease. It suggests not only external comfort, but inner fulfillment and peace, reflecting the reward of one whose good deeds have outweighed their sins.",
  101008: "But as for the one whose scales of good deeds are light, [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. The “lightness” of the scales signifies a deficiency in good deeds. It reflects a state in which one’s deeds do not carry sufficient weight to secure success, standing in direct contrast to the one whose scales are heavy. The imagery underscores the decisive nature of the balance in determining one’s final outcome.",
  101009: "his abode will be a deep abyss, [7]\\n\\nFootnotes: [7]. “A deep abyss” translates Hāwiyah, a name for Hell denoting a deep, bottomless pit into which one falls helplessly. The term comes from a root meaning “to plunge” or “to collapse,” conveying total loss, humiliation, and despair. Calling it a “home” is bitter irony: what should have been a place of safety becomes a place of ruin. The verse emphasizes that when righteous deeds are weightless, there remains no shelter except punishment—underscoring the gravity of moral failure and the justice of divine recompense.",
  101010: "What will make you realize what it is?",
  101011: "It is a Fire, intensely hot. [8]\\n\\nFootnotes: [8] The phrase “a Fire, intensely hot” (nārun ḥāmiyah) denotes a fire of extreme and consuming heat. The term ḥāmiyah conveys intensity and fierceness, suggesting a blaze that is relentless and all-consuming. It serves as the final clarification of the “abyss” (hāwiyah), revealing it to be a fire of overwhelming severity.",
  102001: "The pursuit of outdoing one another in worldly gains has distracted you ˹from Allah˺, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. “Competition in worldly increase” translates at-takāthur—a term denoting mutual rivalry in accumulation and boasting over abundance, whether in wealth, children, possessions, status, lineage, numbers, or influence. The verb alhākum (“distracts you”) conveys a state of preoccupation so absorbing that it diverts one from what truly matters: remembrance of Allāh, moral responsibility, and preparation for the Hereafter. The verse does not condemn lawful provision itself, but rather the heedless fixation on comparison and accumulation that hardens the heart and obscures ultimate accountability.\nEarthly possessions, by their nature, are unstable—subject to decay, loss, and theft—and thus incapable of providing lasting security or fulfillment. What truly endures are the “treasures” secured through righteous deeds, sincere faith, generosity, and moral integrity—qualities whose effects transcend this life and shape one’s eternal outcome.\nThis meaning is reinforced in the teaching of the Prophet Muhammad, who said:\n“The son of Adam says: ‘My wealth, my wealth!’ But what of your wealth truly belongs to you, O son of Adam, except what you ate and consumed, what you wore and wore out, or what you gave in charity and thus sent forward?”\nThe hadith clarifies that most of what is claimed as “one’s own” is, in reality, transient and ultimately lost; only what is meaningfully used or given for the sake of good is truly preserved. Together, the verse and the hadith call for a reordering of priorities: rather than anchoring the heart in material accumulation or social distinction, one is urged to invest in what yields enduring benefit. In this light, true wealth is not measured by possession, but by what is carried forward through faith, generosity, and righteous living.",
  102002: "until you end up in the graves. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The phrase “until you end up in the graves” is a Qur’anic indirect expression for death. It employs deliberate irony: the grave is described as a visit, not a permanent residence, underscoring the temporary nature of worldly life and the certainty of return to Allāh. People remain absorbed in competition and accumulation until death abruptly interrupts them, at which point all competition ends and accountability begins. The verse rebukes heedlessness and reminds that awareness often comes too late—only when one is carried to the grave does the illusion of worldly permanence collapse.",
  102003: "No indeed! You will come to know.",
  102004: "Again no! You will come to know.",
  102005: "No indeed! If only you knew with certain knowledge,",
  102006: "you will surely see the Hellfire,",
  102007: "then you will surely see it with your own eyes, in full certainty.",
  102008: "Then on that Day you will surely be questioned about your worldly blessings.[3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. “The blessings ” translates an-naʿīm, referring to the worldly comforts, pleasures, and provisions granted by Allāh—such as health, wealth, time, security, sustenance, and every form of ease enjoyed in this life. The verse affirms that these blessings are not morally neutral; they entail responsibility. People will be questioned about how they acquired them, how they used them, and whether they expressed gratitude through obedience, generosity, and restraint. The warning does not condemn lawful enjoyment itself, but heedless indulgence that leads to ingratitude, arrogance, or neglect of duty. As the Prophet ﷺ said: “A servant’s feet will not move on the Day of Resurrection until he is asked about his life and how he spent it, his knowledge and what he did with it, his wealth—how he earned it and how he spent it—and his body and how he used it.”",
  103001: "By the ˹passing of˺ time, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. “The time” translates al-ʿAṣr, a term that denotes time in its passing, compressing, and diminishing nature. It encompasses the flow of human life, history, and opportunity as it steadily moves toward its end. By swearing an oath by time, Allāh draws attention to its immense significance as the container of all human actions—obedience and disobedience, gain and loss. Scholars explain that time itself testifies against humanity, for every moment that passes is irretrievable, and it is within time that people either invest in faith and righteousness or squander their lives in heedlessness. The oath prepares the listener for the central message of the sūrah: that most people are in loss precisely because they fail to use time as Allāh intended.",
  103002: "Indeed, mankind is surely in complete loss, [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The phrase “in complete loss” translates la-fī khusr, an emphatic construction indicating complete, ongoing, and universal loss. It signifies not a partial setback, but a state of ruin in which life, time, and effort are being wasted with no lasting return. This loss refers to spiritual failure, moral bankruptcy, and ultimate regret in the Hereafter—unless one meets the conditions outlined in the following verse. The verse establishes a stark premise: by default, human beings are losing their most precious capital—time—unless they consciously invest it in faith, righteous action, truth, and patience.",
  103003: "except those who believe, do righteous deeds, and encourage one another to the truth [3] and encourage one another to patience. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. In this concise sūrah, Allāh sets forth the complete path to salvation and clarifies what He requires of humanity. Salvation lies in true belief in Allāh alone, living a life of humility and submission to His will, and following His final revelation—the Qur’an—and His messengers, the last of whom is the Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ as a living and practical example. It requires performing righteous deeds, such as establishing regular prayer, paying the poor-rate (zakāh), and engaging in all acts of goodness. It further entails acting justly, treating others with fairness and integrity, and cultivating devotion marked by loving-kindness, compassion, service, and preferring others over oneself. Believers are also commanded to mutually encourage obedience to all that Allāh has ordained in the Qur’an and through His Messenger, while warning one another against idolatry and sin—everything Allāh and His Messenger have forbidden—so that they may be safeguarded from loss and admitted into Paradise.\n[4]. Slow to anger and abounding in steadfast love and faithfulness: This expression highlights the virtue of patience as a central moral quality. It signifies restraint in anger, constancy in mercy, and unwavering commitment to truth. Patience here encompasses perseverance in performing righteous deeds, steadfast self-control in resisting sinful desires, and endurance in the face of suffering and harm encountered in worldly life. Through such patience, a person maintains moral integrity and spiritual balance despite trials and provocations.",
  104001: "Woe to every backbiter and slanderer, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. “Woe to every backbiter and slanderer”\nArabic: waylun likulli humazatin lumazah\nThe word “woe” is a severe expression of warning and threat, understood by many scholars as indicating impending punishment. The paired terms humazah and lumazah describe habitual offenders who harm others through ridicule and contempt.\nHumazah refers to one who backbites, mocks, or slanders people—often behind their backs—through speech, insinuation, or subtle gestures. Lumazah refers to one who openly insults, defames, or finds fault in others, sometimes through facial expressions, body language, or direct verbal attack.\nTogether, the terms encompass a wide range of social harm: from private gossip to public humiliation, including sarcasm, ridicule, and character assassination. The verse condemns not isolated acts, but a repeated disposition of belittling others, especially when tied to arrogance or a sense of superiority.",
  104002: "who amasses wealth ˹greedily˺ and counts it ˹repeatedly˺, [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. “Who amasses wealth greedily and counts it repeatedly” refers to one who accumulates wealth obsessively and measures it repeatedly, taking excessive delight and false security in its quantity. This expression condemns greed, miserliness, and attachment to material possessions, where wealth is hoarded rather than used responsibly or charitably. Such counting symbolizes a mindset that assumes wealth guarantees permanence, power, or protection, while neglecting moral accountability and the transient nature of worldly riches.",
  104003: "thinking that his wealth will make him immortal. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. The verse exposes the underlying illusion of one who is obsessed with wealth: the belief that it grants permanence, security, or lasting significance. The verb yaḥsabu suggests a mistaken assumption, not a reality. The phrase “will make him live forever” does not necessarily imply a literal claim to immortality, but rather a mindset in which wealth creates a sense of invulnerability—leading one to act as though death, accountability, or decline will not reach him. Early commentators explain that such a person relies on wealth for status and permanence, neglecting the transient nature of life and the inevitability of judgment.",
  104004: "No indeed! He will surely be cast into the Crushing Fire. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. The term al-Ḥuṭamah (literally “the Crusher”) derives from a root meaning to break, crush, or shatter into pieces. It is understood as a name of Hellfire that not only burns but also destroys and breaks down completely. The verb yunbadhanna (“will surely be thrown”) conveys force and humiliation, suggesting a violent casting away. The verse sharply overturns the earlier illusion of permanence through wealth: instead of being preserved, the person is cast into a realm of total destruction.",
  104005: "What will make you realize what the Crushing Fire truly is?",
  105001: "Have you not considered how your Lord dealt with the people of the Elephant? [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. “The people of the Elephant” refers to the army led by Abraha, the Abyssinian ruler of Yemen, who marched with elephants toward Makkah intending to destroy the Kaʿbah. The verse appeals to reflection rather than mere historical recall, reminding the listener of a well-known event that demonstrates God’s power, protection of His sacred House, and the futility of arrogance and aggression against divine will.",
  105002: "Did He not bring their scheme to complete ruin? [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. Causing them to perish.\n“Their scheme” denotes the deliberate and calculated plan to attack and destroy the Kaʿbah. Despite their military strength and careful preparation, Allāh caused their plot to collapse completely, bringing about total failure and loss. The expression “utter ruin” underscores the futility of arrogance before divine power and serves as a warning that no scheme opposed to Allāh’s will can succeed.",
  105003: "He sent against them birds in flocks, [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. The phrase “birds in flocks” describes groups of birds sent by divine command as an instrument of punishment. The wording emphasizes their number, succession, and coordinated movement, underscoring that the destruction of a powerful army occurred through means that appeared weak and insignificant, thereby manifesting the absolute power and wisdom of Allāh.",
  105004: "pelting them with stones of baked clay, [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. The phrase “stones of baked clay” refers to small, hardened pellets prepared by divine command and carried by the birds. Though seemingly insignificant in size and material, they became a means of devastating punishment. This highlights that destruction does not depend on the magnitude of the means but on the will of Allāh, and it reinforces the lesson that overwhelming power can be brought low by the smallest of instruments when Allāh so decrees.",
  105005: "so He made them like eaten straw. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5.] This illustrates that arrogance and oppression ultimately lead to ruin; those who rely on power without justice are brought low.",
  106001: "For the accustomed security of the Quraysh, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. “The accustomed security of the Quraysh” refers to the state of safety, stability, and protection long enjoyed by the Quraysh tribe, particularly in their trade, travel, and residence in Makkah. This security was neither accidental nor self-produced but a favor granted by Allāh, reinforced by the protection of the Sacred House and events such as the defeat of the People of the Elephant. The verse reminds Quraysh that their prosperity and safety are divine blessings that require gratitude and worship rather than arrogance or neglect.",
  106002: "their accustomed security in the caravans of winter and summer, [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. This phrase refers to the regular trade journeys undertaken by the Quraysh: the winter caravan to Yemen and the summer caravan to Greater Syria (al-Shām). These journeys were made possible by the safety, alliances, and respect granted to the Quraysh because of their custodianship of the Kaʿbah. The repetition of “accustomed security” underscores the continuity and reliability of this divine provision, reminding them that their economic prosperity and seasonal livelihoods were sustained by Allāh’s favor rather than their own power alone.",
  106003: "so let them worship the Lord of this House ˹alone˺, [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. The Kaʿbah holds significance only because Allāh has assigned it that role. It symbolizes unity and solidarity, as Muslims throughout the world face the same direction in prayer. Its sanctity does not surpass that of a believer, for the Prophet ﷺ declared that the life and honor of a believer are greater in the sight of Allāh than the sanctity of the Kaʿbah.\nIslam strictly rejects all intermediaries in worship: every prayer, vow, and act of devotion must be directed to Allāh alone. As ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb stated upon kissing the Black Stone, it neither benefits nor harms; such acts are performed solely in obedience to Allāh and His Messenger ﷺ. Thus, genuine worship belongs to Allāh alone.",
  107001: "Have you seen the one who denies the Day of Recompense? [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1] This verse introduces a key lesson: belief in the Day of Recompense is not abstract—it shapes behavior. Its denial is reflected in how one treats others.",
  107002: "That is the one who mistreats the orphan [2],\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. “Mistreats the orphan” refers to harshness, neglect, or denial of an orphan’s rights. The verse links denial of the Day of Recompense with moral cruelty, showing that rejection of accountability manifests in social injustice, especially toward the most vulnerable. Care for orphans and widows is repeatedly emphasized in the Qurʾān as a hallmark of true faith and moral responsibility. Those who oppress them are warned that if the wronged cry out to Allāh, He will surely hear their plea, and divine retribution will follow.",
  107003: "and does not encourage feeding the poor. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. This phrase condemns not only personal neglect of the poor but also the absence of social responsibility. True faith encourages concern for the needy and motivates others toward acts of compassion and generosity. The verse highlights that indifference to poverty—whether through action or silence—is a sign of moral failure rooted in denial of accountability before Allāh.\nFeed the hungry, and help those in need. Then your light will shine out from the darkness, and the darkness around you will be as bright as noon. Allāh will guide you continually, blessing you abundantly.\nOnce a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, “O Messenger of Allāh, who are the people most beloved to Allāh?”\nHe ﷺ replied: “The most beloved of people to Allāh are those who are most beneficial to others. And the most beloved of deeds to Allāh Almighty is bringing happiness to a believer —or relieving him of a distress,\nor paying off a debt on his behalf, or driving away hunger from him. And that I walk with my brother to fulfill his need is more beloved to me than to seclude myself (for voluntary worship) in this mosque — meaning the Mosque of Madinah — for a month.”",
  107004: "So woe to those who pray,",
  107005: "who are heedless of their prayer, [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. “Heedless of their prayer” refers to neglecting prayer in its proper spirit and responsibility—whether by delaying it beyond its prescribed time, performing it carelessly, or treating it as a mere outward form without sincerity or awareness. The verse criticizes not the act of prayer itself, but the lack of mindfulness, devotion, and moral consistency that should accompany it.",
  107006: "those who only show off, [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. “Those who only show off” refers to performing acts of worship or good deeds for the sake of being seen and praised by others rather than seeking the pleasure of Allāh. This is a form of hypocrisy that empties worship of sincerity and spiritual value. The verse condemns ostentation (riyāʾ), emphasizing that outward religious appearance without inner devotion and moral integrity is unacceptable before Allāh.",
  108001: "Indeed, We have given you ˹O Prophet˺\nal-Kawthar—abundant, ever-flowing good, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. “Al-Kawthar” literally means abundance and overflowing good. It includes every form of divine favor granted to the Prophet\nMuhammad ﷺ: prophethood, revelation, honor, a lasting legacy, numerous followers, and immense good in this world and the Hereafter. It also specifically refers to a river in Paradise promised to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, distinguished by its great abundance and purity. A river whose banks had tents made of pearls. It is never going to stop. It is deeply comforting, bright as crystal, whiter than milk, and sweeter than honey, and of the strongest smell of musk.\nWhen the Prophet Muhammad (ﷺ) was made to ascend to the heavens. He said (after his return), \"I came upon a river (in Paradise) the banks of which were made of tents of hollow pearls. I asked Gabriel, ‘What is this (river)?’ He replied, ‘This is the Kauthar’. The verse is a declaration of divine generosity and consolation, affirming that despite opposition or mockery, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ has been endowed with limitless blessing from Allāh.",
  108002: "So pray to your Lord and offer sacrifice ˹to Him alone˺. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. This command directs the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ—and by extension all believers—to respond to divine blessing with exclusive devotion. Prayer and sacrifice are among the greatest acts of worship and must be offered solely to Allāh, without partners or intermediaries, in direct contrast to the practices of polytheists who dedicate sacrifices to idols or graves. The verse affirms that gratitude for al-Kawthar is expressed not through words alone, but through sincere worship, humility, and obedience.\nThe Prophet ﷺ warned of the gravity of violating this principle, stating that Allāh’s curse is upon: the one who sacrifices to other than Allāh; the one who curses his own parents; the one who shelters a religious innovator; and the one who alters established boundaries. This underscores the seriousness of preserving pure worship and moral order in Islam.",
  108003: "Indeed, your antagonist is the one cut off [3].\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. “The one cut off” means one who is severed from all lasting good, honor, and legacy. This verse overturns the insult aimed at the Prophet ﷺ by his opponents, who claimed that he would be forgotten or left without continuation. Allāh declares instead that it is the Prophet’s enemies who will be deprived of true posterity, remembrance, and success in both this world and the Hereafter, while the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ is granted enduring honor and abundant blessings.",
  109001: "Say, ‘O disbelievers— [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. “O disbelievers” addresses those who knowingly reject the message of Islām after it has been made clear to them. The verse marks a declaration of principle rather than hostility, establishing a clear distinction between faith and disbelief and introducing a firm rejection of religious compromise in matters of worship.",
  109002: "I do not worship what you worship, [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. This is a clear and immediate disavowal of all forms of worship directed to anything other than Allāh. It rejects any proposed compromise between monotheism and polytheism. The statement is firm and absolute, emphasizing that the Prophet ﷺ does not participate in their objects or modes of worship in any way.\n“What you worship” refers to false deities, idols, and inherited polytheistic practices. The verse establishes a complete distinction in worship, affirming that the Prophet ﷺ and the believers neither share in nor compromise with such practices. It categorically rejects all forms of syncretism or partial devotion, making clear that true worship must be directed solely to Allāh, without mixture or association.",
  109003: "nor do you worship what I worship. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. This clause affirms the complete and mutual distinction in worship: just as the Prophet ﷺ does not worship what they worship, they do not truly worship what he worships. Even if they claim belief in Allāh, their practices are mixed with idolatry, rendering their worship fundamentally different from the pure monotheism he brings.\nTrue worship is defined not merely by intention or acknowledgment, but by sincerity, correctness, and exclusivity—directed solely to Allāh without partners. The verse underscores the impossibility of overlap between pure monotheism and polytheistic practice, emphasizing that this separation remains unless sincere monotheistic belief is embraced.",
  109004: "Nor will I ever worship what you worship,",
  109005: "nor do you worship what I worship, [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. This statement affirms that the disbelievers do not worship Allāh alone in the manner He has prescribed. Even if they acknowledge God, their worship is mixed with partners, intermediaries, or false practices. The verse emphasizes that true worship requires exclusivity, sincerity, and obedience, and that monotheistic devotion cannot coexist with idolatry or compromise.",
  110001: "When the help of Allāh comes, and the victory, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. This verse refers to the decisive help and victory granted by Allāh, culminating in the conquest of Makkah. It marks a turning point in the mission of the Prophet ﷺ, when opposition diminished and the truth became widely manifest.\nThe term “victory” (al-fatḥ) is understood primarily as the Conquest of Makkah, while also carrying the broader sense of divine opening, triumph, and the spread of Islam. The verse signifies not only outward success, but the fulfillment of a long struggle through divine support rather than human effort alone.",
  110002: "and you see people entering the religion of Allāh in multitudes, [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The reference to people entering God's true religion “in multitudes” alludes to the period following the Conquest of Makkah, after which numerous Arabian tribes embraced Islam collectively. With the collapse of Quraysh’s political and religious dominance, tribal delegations (wufūd) from across Arabia pledged allegiance to the Prophet ﷺ, marking the decisive victory of Islam as both a religious message and a socio-political order. The verse thus reflects a historical culmination: Islam’s triumph manifested not through coercion, but through the removal of barriers that had previously prevented widespread acceptance.",
  110003: "then glorify your Lord with praise, and seek His forgiveness; indeed, He is ever accepting of repentance. [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. This verse directs the Prophet ﷺ—and by extension all believers—to respond to victory not with pride, but with humility, gratitude, and repentance. Despite the apparent completion of the mission, the command is to glorify Allāh (tasbīḥ) with praise (ḥamd) and to seek His forgiveness (istighfār), acknowledging that all success comes from Him alone.\nThe pairing of praise and repentance reflects a profound spiritual balance: even at the height of achievement, one remains conscious of human limitation and in need of divine mercy. The verse also carries an implicit indication of the nearing completion of the Prophet’s mission, as understood by many companions.\n“Ever accepting of repentance” (tawwāb) emphasizes Allāh’s constant readiness to forgive, inviting believers to return to Him continually—especially in moments of triumph, when the heart is most vulnerable\nto heedlessness.",
  111001: "May the hands of Abū Lahab perish—and he himself has perished. [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. Abū Lahab, an uncle of the Prophet ﷺ, was among his most persistent and outspoken opponents, openly rejecting the message and actively working to hinder it. His mention here is both historical and paradigmatic, representing deliberate and sustained hostility to the prophetic message and, by extension, those who consciously oppose it.\nThe phrase “his hands” symbolizes his efforts, power, and deeds, all of which are declared doomed. The repetition intensifies the meaning, conveying both a declaration of ruin and the certainty of its fulfillment. Despite his status and influence, his opposition to the truth results not in success, but in total loss in this world and the Hereafter.",
  111002: "His wealth and all that he earned will not benefit him.",
  111003: "He will burn in a fiercely blazing Fire. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. This sūrah was among the earliest revealed in Makkah, yet Abū Lahab persisted in disbelief. Its pronouncement of his fate underscores the certainty of the message: had he accepted Islam, he could have outwardly challenged it, yet his continued opposition confirmed what was revealed.\nThe verse declares the inevitable consequence of his persistent rejection and hostility—entry into a blazing Fire. The term conveys an intense, consuming flame, emphasizing both the severity and certainty of the punishment.\nIt also reflects a striking contrast with his name—“Abū Lahab” (father of flame)—as the identity he bore in this world becomes a fitting reality in the Hereafter. The verse thus underscores that deliberate opposition to the truth leads not to honor, but to enduring loss and punishment.",
  111004: "And his wife too—the carrier of firewood, [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. This refers to the wife of Abū Lahab, who likewise opposed the Prophet ﷺ and supported her husband in hostility. The phrase “carrier of firewood” has both a literal and figurative meaning.\nLiterally, it is reported that she would gather and place thorns in the Prophet’s path to harm him. Figuratively, it symbolizes one who fuels conflict, spreads harm, and incites hostility, as firewood feeds a flame.\nThe description thus reflects her active role in sustaining opposition, portraying her not merely as a bystander, but as a participant in the wrongdoing and its consequences.",
  111005: "Around her neck will be a rope of palm-fibre. [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. This verse describes the fitting and humiliating consequence of her actions. The “rope of palm-fibre” refers to a coarse, twisted cord, associated with burden, restraint, and hardship.\nIt reflects a correspondence between deed and consequence: as she once carried harm and fueled hostility, she is portrayed as bearing a degrading burden. The imagery conveys both punishment and disgrace, emphasizing that those who persist in supporting wrongdoing will share in its outcome.",
  112001: "Say, ‘He is Allāh—One, alone.’ [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. This verse affirms the absolute oneness and uniqueness of Allāh. The term Aḥad (One) conveys not merely numerical oneness, but complete singularity and incomparability—He is One in His essence, His attributes, and His exclusive right to be worshipped, without equal or partner.\nThe structure emphasizes both identity and exclusivity: “He—Allāh—is One,” declaring that the One worthy of worship is none other than Allāh alone. He has no partner, no second, and no division, and is utterly distinct from all creation. His oneness is absolute—beyond composition, plurality, or likeness.\nThis verse stands as a foundational declaration of pure monotheism (tawḥīd). It calls not only for belief, but for reverence: Allāh is exalted above all comparison, and His greatness surpasses all comprehension. He alone is most worthy of praise for His perfect nature and His mighty deeds.",
  112002: "Allāh—the Eternal Refuge of all. [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. The term al-Ṣamad conveys that Allāh is the Absolute, Perfect, and Self-Sufficient One, upon whom all creation depends while He depends on none. He is the One to whom all turn in need, at all times and in every circumstance.\n“Eternal Refuge” captures both dimensions of the term: His complete independence—free of all need—and His role as the ultimate source of support and reliance. He is complete in Himself—unchanging, enduring, and without deficiency—while all existence relies upon Him, and to Him every matter ultimately returns.\nThus, the verse deepens the meaning of His oneness: not only is He One, but He is the One eternally sought by all and in need of none.",
  112003: "He neither begets nor is begotten, [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. This verse negates any notion of lineage, origin, or dependency in relation to Allāh. He does not beget—having no offspring, partner, or continuation—and He is not begotten—having no origin, cause, or beginning. His existence is neither derived nor transmitted, and He is utterly distinct from the created order.\nIt refutes all claims that attribute familial relationships to God. He has no father, mother, spouse, children, or partner, nor is He in need of any such relationship. Accordingly, it is not appropriate to describe Him with human relational terms such as “father.” The proper names used are Allāh and Rabb—the Lord, Creator, Sustainer, and Ruler—denoting absolute sovereignty rather than lineage.\nThe verse thus directly rejects religious claims that ascribe offspring to God, including the belief that Jesus is the son of God, or that angels are daughters of God, as held in certain traditions. It affirms instead that Allāh is eternal, self-subsisting, and incomparable, without likeness or association.\nIn the broader scriptural context, even within the Bible, language describing God as “Father” is often understood metaphorically rather than biologically, emphasizing authority and care rather than literal descent. This verse, however, removes all ambiguity by categorically denying any form of divine parenthood.\nThus, it reinforces the purity of monotheism by denying any resemblance between Allāh and His creation—whether in nature, relationship, or origin.",
  112004: "And none is comparable to Him.” [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. This verse affirms that nothing is equal, comparable, or similar to Allāh in any respect. The term used conveys complete negation of likeness—whether in essence, attributes, actions, or authority.\nIt establishes that Allāh is utterly unique and beyond all comparison, unlike anything in creation. No being shares in His nature, His qualities, or His divinity. This final statement completes the sūrah’s message: absolute oneness, self-sufficiency, and transcendence.\nThus, it closes the door to all forms of likening God to creation, association, or equivalence, reinforcing that Allāh alone is the One without peer or equal.",
  113001: "Say, ‘I seek refuge in the Lord of the daybreak,  [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. This verse commands seeking protection and refuge in Allāh alone. The phrase “Lord of the daybreak” (Rabb al-falaq) refers to the One who brings forth light from darkness, splitting the night and ushering in the dawn.\nIt signifies His power over all creation—bringing relief after hardship, clarity after confusion, and safety after fear. By invoking Him as the Lord of daybreak, the verse directs the believer to the One who dispels darkness in all its forms, both physical and spiritual.\nAllāh alone is the Lord—there is no deity besides Him. He controls all opposites: light and darkness, ease and hardship. Since all affairs originate from Him, refuge is sought in Him alone. He is the One who strengthens, protects, and knows those who turn to Him in times of ease and difficulty—so seek His protection at all times.",
  113002: "from the evil of all that He has created,\n[2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. This verse teaches seeking protection from the evil present within creation. While Allāh is the Creator of all things, evil is not attributed to Him in essence, but arises from the actions and effects of created beings.\nIt encompasses every form of harm—whether seen or unseen, physical or spiritual—including that caused by people, creatures, or circumstances. Although all creation originates from Allāh, refuge is sought from the harm that may emerge within it.\nThe verse thus establishes a comprehensive scope: protection is sought not from specific threats alone, but from any source of harm within creation, known or unknown.",
  113003: "and from the evil of the night when it grows dark, [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. “Grows dark” renders ghāsiq, denoting intense, enveloping darkness, commonly understood as the night. The phrase “when it grows dark” (idhā waqab) refers to the moment when darkness fully sets in and spreads, marking a time when its effects are most complete.\nThis period is associated with increased vulnerability, as diminished visibility allows certain harms to arise more easily. Creatures move more freely, and those intending harm often act under the cover of darkness.\nBeyond its literal sense, night also symbolizes uncertainty and hidden danger—when what is unseen becomes more difficult to detect and guard against. The verse therefore directs the believer to seek refuge in Allāh from all that may arise within this state of concealment, whether physical threats or unseen harms.",
  113004: "from the evil of the sorceresses who blow on knots, [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. This verse refers to practices associated with sorcery, in which knots are tied and blown upon as part of incantations intended to cause harm. The expression reflects a known form of occult practice in which words and actions are used to influence others in hidden ways.\nThe wording may be understood either specifically—referring to practitioners of such acts—or generally, encompassing all forms of harmful manipulation carried out through concealed means. The act of “blowing on knots” symbolizes attempts to bind, influence, or harm others through unseen forces.\nThe verse thus directs the believer to seek refuge in Allāh from all such hidden and malicious influences, whether through sorcery or other subtle forms of harm that operate beyond immediate perception.",
  113005: "and from the evil of the envier when he envies. [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. This verse refers to the harm caused by envy (ḥasad), a destructive inner state in which one resents the blessings of others and may wish for their loss. The phrase “when he envies” indicates that the harm lies not merely in feeling envy, but when it is acted upon or manifests outwardly.\nEnvy can lead to various forms of harm—whether through words, actions, or subtle influence—and is among the most deeply rooted and damaging human tendencies. It may affect both the one who harbors it and the one toward whom it is directed.\nThe verse thus directs the believer to seek refuge in Allāh from the active effects of envy, whether visible or unseen, recognizing that such harm ultimately falls under His protection and control.\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said: “Do not envy one another, do not inflate prices against one another, do not hate one another, do not turn away from one another, and do not undercut one another in trade. Be servants of Allāh, as brothers. A believer is the brother of a believer: he does not wrong him, abandon him, or look down upon him.” He then said, pointing to his chest three times, “Piety is here.” He added: “It is sufficient evil for a person to belittle his Muslim brother. The blood, property, and honor of a believer are sacred to another believer.”",
  114001: "Say, ‘I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind, [1]\\n\\nFootnotes: [1]. This verse commands seeking refuge and protection in Allāh alone, specifically as the Lord of mankind (Rabb al-nās). It emphasizes His role as the Creator, Sustainer, and Guardian of all people.\nBy invoking Allāh in this way, the verse highlights His authority over human life, directing the believer to turn to Him for protection from harms that affect the heart and mind. It establishes the foundation for what follows, focusing on internal and unseen influences.\nAllāh is the One in whom we find strength, support, and protection—a secure refuge in times of fear, weakness, and distress. He shields from harm, guides through hardship, and preserves by His mercy. In Him is found not only outward safety, but also inner peace, assurance, and stability of heart.\nTo turn to Him is to enter a state of trust and security, where reliance upon Him\nreplaces anxiety and brings strength beyond one’s own. Thus, He is both a protector from external harm and a sanctuary for the soul.",
  114002: "the Sovereign King of mankind, [2]\\n\\nFootnotes: [2]. This verse affirms Allāh as the King (al-Malik) of mankind—the One who possesses absolute authority, dominion, and control over all human affairs. It complements the previous verse: not only is He their Lord who sustains them, but also their King who governs them.\nHis kingship is perfect and unmatched, unlike worldly rulers whose authority is limited and temporary. All people are under His dominion, subject to His command, and accountable before Him.\nBy invoking Allāh as King, the verse strengthens the sense of refuge: the One sought for protection is not only caring and sustaining, but also all-powerful and sovereign, capable of protecting from every form of harm.",
  114003: "the only God of mankind, [3]\\n\\nFootnotes: [3]. “The only God of mankind” affirms that Allāh alone is the true deity worthy of worship, devotion, and ultimate allegiance. The term points beyond authority or sovereignty to His exclusive right to be worshipped—recognized, loved, and obeyed as the One to whom all hearts turn.\nWhile He is the Lord and King over all people, this title emphasizes a more intimate and defining relationship: He is the One for whom humanity was created to worship. It highlights the purpose of human existence—to know Him, serve Him sincerely, and direct all acts of devotion to Him alone without association.\nThus, it distinguishes between merely acknowledging His rule and truly submitting in worship. Beyond recognizing His power, one is called to devotion—offering Him alone trust, reverence, love, and obedience.\nThis calls for uprightness before Him: living with constant awareness of His presence, maintaining sincerity and integrity in both outward actions and inward states. True devotion is marked by humility, reverence, and a conscious turning away from all that displeases Him—so seek refuge in Him alone.",
  114004: "from the harm of the whisperer, the withdrawing tempter ˹the devil˺, [4]\\n\\nFootnotes: [4]. “The whisperer, the withdrawing one” renders the Arabic expression al-waswās al-khannās, which conveys the idea of one who repeatedly and subtly insinuates thoughts, doubts, or temptations into the heart, then retreats and conceals himself. The term al-waswās denotes persistent, quiet whispering, while al-khannās refers to one who withdraws, recedes, or shrinks back.\nThis description is widely understood to refer to Satan, whose method is not overt coercion but subtle suggestion—planting fleeting impulses or uncertainties that may lead a person astray if entertained. Yet his influence is neither constant nor irresistible; rather, he withdraws when Allāh is remembered. Thus, the phrase highlights both the recurring nature of such whisperings and their inherent weakness in the presence of sincere remembrance (dhikr), awareness, and reliance upon Allāh.\nIn essence, the expression underscores an ongoing spiritual dynamic: temptation arises quietly and repeatedly, but it is repelled through conscious remembrance of Allāh and steadfastness of heart.",
  114005: "who whispers into the hearts of mankind, [5]\\n\\nFootnotes: [5]. Evil thoughts and suggestions. “into the chests ” refers to the inner being of a person—the heart, mind, and seat of intention—where thoughts, doubts, and impulses arise. The whispering described here denotes subtle, repeated insinuations that seek to influence belief, intention, and behavior, in contrast to overt compulsion.",
  114006: "from among jinn and mankind. [6]\\n\\nFootnotes: [6]. This verse clarifies that whispering does not come from a single source, but from both jinn and human beings. While the jinn are unseen and may influence thoughts directly, human beings can also act as whisperers through speech, suggestion, and influence, leading others toward harm or misguidance.\nIt expands the scope of what one must guard against: not only internal or unseen temptations, but also external influences that affect belief, thought, and behavior. Harmful whispering may come through words, ideas, or pressures that shape the inner state of a person.\nThus, the believer is taught to seek refuge in Allāh from every source of misguidance, whether hidden or apparent, recognizing that protection lies in awareness, discernment, and reliance upon Him.",
};

String? getTafsirYacobEnglish(int surah, int ayah) => _yacobEnglish[surah * 1000 + ayah];
